pportingFindings": ["On statin therapy (CoQ10 depletion)", "LDL 102 mg/dL", "HDL 49 mg/dL (low)", "Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)", "Age 41 (declining CoQ10 production)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"apoBValue": 87, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "normal", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 0.49949626186061413, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": false}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": "CAC = 0 supports deferring statin therapy short-term in selected low-intermediate risk patients. Reassess risk factors in 5-10 years."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacInterpretation": {"score": 0, "interpretation": "CAC = 0: No detectable coronary artery calcium.", "clinicalImplication": "Supports deferring statin short-term in selected patients. However, high Lp(a) still warrants close follow-up as it reflects genetic risk not captured by CAC."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "consider", "rationale": "Lipid levels meet threshold for CAC consideration. CAC may be useful if risk level is uncertain or patient is hesitant about statin.", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\nYour 10-year ASCVD (heart attack/stroke) risk is 0.5% and your 10-year total CVD risk is 0.7% — both LOW risk. That means your short-term risk of a major cardiovascular event is very small. Many tests are in good range, but we have two findings to watch: HDL cholesterol 49 mg/dL (not ideal) and hematocrit 45.2% (slightly elevated, could reflect mild dehydration).\\n\\nPositive findings (specific values)\\n- TSH 1.02 mIU/L — normal (thyroid function reassuring).  \\n- Vitamin D (25-OH) 66.4 ng/mL — optimal for bone and hormone health.  \\n- Hemoglobin 14.7 g/dL and Platelets 285 K/uL — within normal range.  \\n- Triglycerides 70 mg/dL, LDL 102 mg/dL, ApoB 87 mg/dL — near-optimal levels.  \\n- AST 17 U/L, ALT 14 U/L, Creatinine 0.64 mg/dL, eGFR 113 mL/min — liver and kidney tests normal.  \\n- Vitamin B12 592 pg/mL — adequate.\\n\\nAreas to improve (values + concrete actions)\\n- HDL cholesterol 49 mg/dL (labeled low). To raise HDL and support heart health:  \\n  - Add 25–30 g fiber daily: oatmeal for breakfast, beans with lunch, vegetables at dinner.  \\n  - Include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).  \\n  - Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter and high-fat cheeses.  \\n  - Daily exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking or equivalent; aim for at least 150 minutes moderate aerobic activity weekly.  \\n- LDL 102 mg/dL is near optimal — continue healthy habits to keep or lower it.  \\n- Hematocrit 45.2% (slightly above typical female midpoint) — may indicate mild dehydration. Action: increase fluid intake for 48–72 hours (aim for ~8 cups/ day unless otherwise instructed), then recheck hematocrit. If you have headaches, dizziness, or unusual symptoms, contact us sooner.\\n\\nAdditional lifestyle notes (female-specific)\\n- Bone health: maintain Vitamin D 66.4 ng/mL, aim for 1,000–1,200 mg calcium daily from diet or supplements as needed, and include weight-bearing exercise 2–3x/week.  \\n- If you have heavy menstrual bleeding or fatigue despite hemoglobin 14.7 g/dL, ask about a ferritin check.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Re-check lipid panel and hematocrit in 3–6 months after implementing hydration and lifestyle changes (earlier if symptoms).  \\n- No statin recommended now given your 10-year ASCVD risk 0.5%, but we’ll revisit if risk or numbers change.  \\n- Contact us for any new chest pain, breathlessness, palpitations, fainting, heavy bleeding, or persistent fatigue.\\n\\nYou’re doing well overall — small targeted changes (fiber, fish, exercise, hydration) can improve your heart and bone health. We’re here to support you.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 14.7, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 45.2, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Slightly elevated hematocrit - may indicate dehydration.", "recommendation": "Encourage hydration. Repeat if persistent.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 285, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.02, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 66.4, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D optimal (60-100 ng/mL). Supports hormone, bone, and immune health.", "recommendation": "Maintain current regimen. Monitor annually.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 592, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 102, "status": "borderline", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Near optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet and exercise.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 49, "status": "abnormal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Low HDL - increased cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Increase aerobic exercise. Consider niacin if very low.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 70, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.6926424100984084, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 0.7% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.49949626186061413, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 0.5%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.3374712853548267, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.3%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 6.025418435895934, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 6.0%, ASCVD 4.0%, Heart Failure 3.4%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 17, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 14, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 113, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.64, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 87, "status": "normal", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Apo B at optimal level.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}], "aiRecommendations": "Clinical context note for staff: Labs show largely normal panel with three borderline/abnormal findings: hematocrit 45.2% (slightly high), LDL 102 mg/dL (near-optimal/borderline), HDL 49 mg/dL (low for female). There is no testosterone level or explicit HRT status provided. Recommendations below assume routine ambulatory female patient unless otherwise specified. Modify if patient is on testosterone or other HRT — see testosterone-specific section.\\n\\n1) Overall clinical assessment\\n- No immediate red flags. Most metabolic, hepatic, renal, thyroid, vitamin B12/D, and CBC indices are within normal limits.\\n- Hematocrit mildly elevated at 45.2% — likely minimal clinical significance (possible mild hemoconcentration/dehydration). Not consistent with polycythemia at this level.\\n- Lipid profile: LDL 102 mg/dL (near-optimal) and HDL 49 mg/dL (below desirable for female; increases long-term CVD risk modestly). Triglycerides normal.\\n- 10-year cardiovascular risk scores are within acceptable ranges per PREVENT results — no indication for statin initiation based solely on these values.\\n\\n2) Priority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL (immediate physician notification)\\n- None required based on current values.\\n\\nURGENT (notify physician if any of the following occur or if patient is on testosterone)\\n- If patient is on exogenous testosterone: notify physician if hematocrit increases to ≥50% (consider dose adjustment) or ≥54% (hold/stop per protocol and evaluate for venous thromboembolism risk).\\n- If symptoms of thrombosis, dyspnea, chest pain, visual changes, focal neurologic signs, or syncope occur — contact physician immediately.\\n\\nROUTINE (staff actions to implement)\\n- Hematocrit 45.2%:\\n  * Encourage oral hydration now and counsel on adequate fluid intake.\\n  * Repeat CBC (hematocrit/hemoglobin) in 4–6 weeks to confirm persistence vs transient hemoconcentration.\\n  * Sooner recheck (within 1–2 weeks) if clinical dehydration suspected or symptoms present.\\n  * If repeat Hct rises above ≥50%, notify physician per testosterone/hematocrit protocol.\\n\\n- Lipids:\\n  * No statin indicated at LDL 102 mg/dL with low 10-year ASCVD risk — document shared decision-making if patient requests treatment.\\n  * Arrange repeat fasting lipid panel in 3 months after lifestyle interventions to assess response.\\n  * If patient fasting status unknown, order fasting lipid panel for the repeat test.\\n\\n- HDL 49 mg/dL:\\n  * Provide lifestyle counseling to raise HDL (see lifestyle section).\\n  * Reassess with repeat lipid panel in 3 months. Consider primary care or cardiology referral if HDL remains low with other risk factors or if ASCVD risk becomes elevated.\\n\\n3) Testosterone dose recommendations (if applicable)\\n- If patient is NOT receiving testosterone: no testosterone dosing actions indicated. Obtain baseline total testosterone only if clinically indicated (e.g., symptoms of hyperandrogenism, transgender care initiation).\\n- If patient IS receiving testosterone (transgender or HRT protocol):\\n  * Baseline and monitoring guidance:\\n    - Baseline CBC including hematocrit prior to initiation, then at 3 months, 6 months, and annually thereafter (or more frequently if abnormal).\\n  * Dose adjustment criteria (clinic protocol):\\n    - If hematocrit ≥50%: consider holding dose or reducing dose; discuss with prescribing physician. Evaluate for dehydration and repeat CBC in 1–2 weeks.\\n    - If hematocrit ≥54%: stop testosterone immediately and notify prescribing physician; evaluate for polycythemia complications and consider hematology referral.\\n  * No change recommended solely for Hct 45.2% — continue current regimen and monitor per schedule.\\n  * When assessing testosterone levels for dose titration, document menstrual cycle day (if premenopausal) and time since last dose; obtain trough level (just before next dose) when applicable.\\n\\n4) Lifestyle interventions needed (specific, staff to counsel)\\n- Hydration:\\n  * Encourage at least 1.5–2 L/day fluid intake unless contraindicated; reinforce rehydration prior to repeat CBC.\\n\\n- Aerobic exercise:\\n  * Target ≥150 minutes/week moderate-intensity or 75 minutes/week vigorous aerobic exercise + 2 sessions/week resistance training.\\n  * Emphasize aerobic exercise specifically to help raise HDL.\\n\\n- Diet:\\n  * Recommend Mediterranean-style diet: increase fruits/vegetables, whole grains, legumes, fatty fish (omega-3s), nuts; reduce saturated fats and trans fats.\\n  * Increase soluble fiber (oats, beans) to help LDL reduction.\\n  * Limit refined carbohydrates and added sugars.\\n\\n- Weight and tobacco:\\n  * Encourage weight loss if BMI >25 (5–10% weight reduction can improve HDL and LDL).\\n  * Strongly advise smoking cessation (if applicable) — smoking lowers HDL.\\n\\n- Alcohol:\\n  * If patient drinks alcohol, advise moderation; small amounts may raise HDL, but risks often outweigh benefits — follow counseling per clinic policy.\\n\\n- Supplements/medications:\\n  * Do not initiate niacin without physician review: consider only if HDL remains very low despite lifestyle modification and after cardiology/physician approval due to side effects (flushing, hepatotoxicity, glucose intolerance).\\n\\n5) Additional testing or referrals to consider\\n- Repeat CBC (hematocrit) in 4–6 weeks after hydration.\\n- Repeat fasting lipid panel in 3 months.\\n- If patient is on or being considered for testosterone:\\n  * Obtain baseline total testosterone, estradiol (if indicated), and schedule ongoing monitoring per hormonal therapy protocol.\\n- If low HDL persists and multiple risk factors present:\\n  * Consider referral to cardiology or preventive medicine for advanced risk stratification and management.\\n- If heavy or irregular menses or concern for iron deficiency despite current labs:\\n  * Order ferritin, iron studies if symptomatic or if hemoglobin/Hct trend downward.\\n- Dietitian referral:\\n  * Consider referral if patient requires structured dietary intervention for lipids/weight management.\\n- If any new symptoms concerning for thrombotic event or cardiac symptoms — urgent evaluation/emergency referral.\\n\\n6) Summary of next steps and timeline (staff action checklist)\\n- Immediate (today/at visit):\\n  * Counsel patient on hydration and initiate lifestyle counseling (exercise, diet, smoking cessation).\\n  * Document current HRT/testosterone status in chart.\\n\\n- Short-term (1–6 weeks):\\n  * If dehydration suspected: repeat CBC in 1–2 weeks after hydration; otherwise repeat in 4–6 weeks.\\n  * If symptomatic (chest pain, dyspnea, neurologic symptoms) — escalate immediately.\\n\\n- Mid-term (3 months):\\n  * Repeat fasting lipid panel to reassess LDL and HDL after lifestyle measures.\\n  * If on testosterone, ensure scheduled 3-month CBC and testosterone level per protocol.\\n\\n- Ongoing (3–12 months):\\n  * Continue annual monitoring of lipids and CBC per routine care and HRT protocols.\\n  * Consider dietitian or cardiology referral if nonresponsive or risk profile changes.\\n\\nPhysician notification criteria for staff\\n- Notify prescribing physician immediately if:\\n  * Hematocrit ≥50% (if on testosterone) or ≥54% (critical).\\n  * New symptoms suggesting thrombotic event or cardiac ischemia.\\n  * LDL ≥190 mg/dL or sudden significant worsening of lipid profile.\\n  * Persistent HDL <40 mg/dL with other risk factors — discuss specialist referral.\\n\\nDocumentation reminders for staff\\n- Record counseling provided, recommended timeline for lab rechecks, and any plan for medication adjustments.\\n- If ordering repeat testing, specify fasting status for lipid panel and note cycle day/time of last HRT dose if applicable.\\n\\nIf you want, provide the patient’s HRT/testosterone status and whether the lipid panel was fasting so recommendations can be individualized (dose adjustments, monitoring cadence).", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 1.43, "detail": "Triglycerides 70 / HDL 49 = 1.43", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 14, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "87 mg/dL", "detail": "Normal atherogenic particle count", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": []}	\N	2026-03-10 18:46:35.623455	2026-03-10 18:46:35.623455
34	17	2026-03-19 00:00:00	{"acne": false, "onHRT": false, "anxiety": false, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "hairLoss": false, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": false, "lowLibido": false, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2026-03-19", "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "L Robinson", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 20, "bmi": 37, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 125, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "restlessLegs": false, "lowMotivation": false, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management\\n- History of present illness: Patient is a 20-year-old female presenting for review of screening results. No symptoms suggestive of obstructive sleep apnea (no excessive daytime sleepiness, no witnessed apneas, no new loud snoring, no nocturnal choking/gasping reported). No new cardiopulmonary complaints.\\n- Menstrual/hormone history: No new menstrual complaints documented. Current use of testosterone: none reported at time of chart review. If patient is on or plans to start testosterone, additional baseline testing and monitoring will be required (see plan).\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vital signs: None recorded at time of lab/review. BMI not documented in this encounter.\\n- Screening / Labs:\\n  - Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP‑BANG): 0 points — Normal / Low risk for obstructive sleep apnea (STOP‑BANG 0–2)\\n- Abnormal/Critical findings: None\\n- Borderline findings: None\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Low risk for obstructive sleep apnea (STOP‑BANG = 0).\\n   - Interpretation: Screening score indicates low risk for clinically significant OSA in the absence of other concerning features. No immediate red flags from this screening result alone.\\n   - Differential/considerations: Screening test only — does not exclude OSA in patients with new symptoms, rapid weight gain, or other risk factors (e.g., starting or escalating testosterone therapy, development of polycythemia, sedative use). Clinical context (BMI, neck circumference, reported symptoms) should inform further evaluation.\\n\\n2. No abnormal laboratory findings identified on this screening review.\\n   - Interpretation: No abnormal or critical values identified related to sleep apnea screening. Routine surveillance recommended.\\n\\n3. Testosterone therapy status: none reported.\\n   - Interpretation: No testosterone-related monitoring required at this time. If patient is started on testosterone in the future, follow baseline and surveillance recommendations below.\\n\\nPLAN:\\n1. Low risk OSA (STOP‑BANG = 0)\\n   - Document result in chart: STOP‑BANG low risk.\\n   - Patient education/counseling provided regarding OSA symptoms to watch for: excessive daytime sleepiness, witnessed apneas, new loud snoring, nocturnal choking/gasping, morning headaches. Advised to contact clinic/physician promptly if symptoms develop.\\n   - Re‑screening: Routine re‑screen in 3–6 months (routine follow‑up) or sooner if clinical change (weight gain, new symptoms, medication changes, initiation/escalation of testosterone).\\n   - Red flags requiring immediate physician notification: STOP‑BANG ≥3, new/worsening OSA symptoms, development of polycythemia (Hct ≥48% or increase >5% from baseline), or any acute cardio‑respiratory concerns.\\n\\n2. Testosterone-related considerations (patient not on testosterone currently)\\n   - If patient begins or plans to initiate testosterone therapy: obtain baseline morning total testosterone and hematocrit/hemoglobin prior to initiation.\\n   - Monitoring schedule if testosterone initiated: repeat morning total testosterone and Hct 6–12 weeks after start or dose change, then every 3–6 months during the first year, then annually if stable.\\n   - Action thresholds: notify prescriber and consider dose reduction/interval change if STOP‑BANG increases to ≥3, if patient develops OSA symptoms, or if Hct rises to ≥48% (or increases >5% from baseline). Escalate per physician judgment if Hct ≥52–54%.\\n\\n3. Lifestyle and preventive counseling (provided verbally)\\n   - Sleep hygiene: regular sleep schedule, limit late‑night heavy meals, reduce screen time before bed.\\n   - Avoid alcohol and sedative‑hypnotics within 4–6 hours of bedtime.\\n   - Positional measures: encourage side‑sleeping if snoring develops.\\n   - Weight management: referral to weight‑management resources if BMI elevated; even modest weight loss can reduce OSA risk.\\n   - Smoking cessation counseling as indicated.\\n\\n4. Additional testing / referrals (conditional)\\n   - If STOP‑BANG increases to ≥3 or patient becomes symptomatic: refer to sleep medicine for diagnostic testing (home sleep apnea test or in‑lab polysomnography as appropriate).\\n   - Consider overnight pulse oximetry as an interim test if access to formal testing is delayed and patient has other risk factors or is on testosterone.\\n   - If polycythemia develops, coordinate with primary care/hematology per clinic protocol.\\n\\n5. Follow‑up and orders\\n   - Immediate (today): Documented STOP‑BANG low risk in chart and provided patient counseling.\\n   - Short term (4–12 weeks): If initiating testosterone, order baseline and 6–12 week labs (morning total testosterone, CBC with Hct/Hb). (No baseline labs ordered today since testosterone not reported.)\\n   - Ongoing: Routine re‑screening of STOP‑BANG in 3–6 months or sooner with clinical change. If patient remains off testosterone and asymptomatic, continue routine surveillance.\\n   - Follow‑up appointment: Routine follow‑up in 3–6 months or sooner if symptoms develop or if testosterone is initiated/changed.\\n\\nDocumentation:\\n- Results reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow‑up as above.\\n\\nDate: 03/19/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\n- Your sleep apnea screening result is 0 points on the STOP-BANG (0/8), which is LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea. This is a positive result — it means you are unlikely to have moderate-to-severe sleep apnea based on this screen.\\n\\nPositive findings (specific values)\\n- Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG): 0 points (LOW RISK, 0/8).\\n\\nAreas to improve / things to watch (with specific, actionable steps)\\n- While no other lab values were reported here, for women’s health I recommend routine checks that are important even when this screen is normal:\\n  - Iron status: Consider checking a CBC and ferritin if you have heavy periods, fatigue, or hair thinning. In the meantime, eat iron-rich foods: 3–4 servings weekly of lean red meat or 1–2 cups weekly of cooked lentils/beans, plus dark leafy greens. Pair iron foods with vitamin C (a small glass of orange juice) and avoid tea/coffee at meals to improve absorption.\\n  - Thyroid function: If you have fatigue, weight changes, or hair loss, ask for a TSH test. Until then, prioritize consistent sleep (aim for 7–8 hours a night) and moderate exercise.\\n  - Bone health: Aim for 30 minutes of weight-bearing exercise most days and 2–3 strength-training sessions per week. Aim for about 1,000–1,200 mg calcium daily (diet + supplements if needed) and 600–800 IU vitamin D daily; consider a vitamin D level check if you have limited sun exposure.\\n\\nGeneral lifestyle tips to keep your sleep and overall health strong\\n- Sleep: Aim for 7–8 hours nightly, keep a regular bedtime, avoid heavy meals/alcohol within 2–3 hours of bed, and limit screens 30–60 minutes before sleep.\\n- Activity: 30 minutes brisk walking most days + strength training 2x/week.\\n- Weight: Maintaining a healthy weight supports sleep and lowers future risk of sleep apnea.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- No urgent action required for sleep apnea. If you have daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or choking/gasping at night, contact your provider for further testing.\\n- Discuss routine screening labs at your next visit: CBC/ferritin, TSH, lipid panel, A1c, and vitamin D. For most people, yearly or every 1–3 years depending on age and symptoms is reasonable.\\n- If you’d like, schedule a follow-up in 4–12 weeks to review any new symptoms and to arrange the labs suggested above.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- STOP-BANG reported as \\"Normal\\" — interpreted as low risk for obstructive sleep apnea (STOP-BANG score 0–2) unless additional clinical information suggests otherwise.\\n- No immediate red flags from this screening result alone. Continue routine surveillance, especially if patient is receiving or will receive testosterone therapy, which can increase OSA risk.\\n\\nPriority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\n- CRITICAL\\n  - Notify physician immediately if patient develops new/worsening symptoms of OSA (daytime somnolence, witnessed apneas, gasping/choking at night, new loud snoring) or if subsequent STOP-BANG score rises to ≥3.\\n- URGENT\\n  - If patient is on testosterone and has any of the above symptoms or new polycythemia (see hematocrit thresholds below), contact physician for same‑day review and consider holding/reducing dose.\\n- ROUTINE\\n  - Document current STOP-BANG score and interpretation in the chart.\\n  - Re-screen and reassess risk with any clinical change (weight gain, new medications, dose escalation of testosterone).\\n\\nTestosterone-specific recommendations (if applicable)\\n- If patient is NOT on testosterone: no testosterone-related action required; counsel on OSA symptoms and routine re-screening.\\n- If patient is currently on testosterone or planning to initiate:\\n  - Baseline: ensure baseline hematocrit (Hct) and morning total testosterone recorded prior to initiation.\\n  - Monitoring schedule: obtain Hct and total testosterone 6–12 weeks after initiation or dose change, then every 3–6 months during the first year, then annually if stable.\\n  - Dose modification triggers:\\n    - If new clinically significant OSA symptoms or STOP-BANG increases to ≥3: notify physician; consider reducing testosterone dose by 25–50% or lengthening dosing interval pending physician review.\\n    - If Hct rises to ≥48% (or increases by >5% from baseline), notify physician; consider dose reduction and evaluation for polycythemia. Escalate further if Hct ≥52–54% per physician judgment.\\n  - If initiating higher-than-physiologic doses (e.g., supraphysiologic regimens), strongly consider baseline sleep evaluation and closer monitoring.\\n\\nLifestyle interventions to implement and counsel patients on\\n- Weight management: recommend referral to weight-management resources if BMI elevated; even modest weight loss reduces OSA risk.\\n- Sleep hygiene: consistent sleep schedule, avoid late-night heavy meals, reduce screen time before bed.\\n- Alcohol and sedative avoidance: advise avoiding alcohol and sedative-hypnotics within 4–6 hours of bedtime.\\n- Positional measures: encourage side-sleeping if snoring noted.\\n- Smoking cessation counseling as needed.\\n- Educate patient on warning symptoms warranting immediate contact (witnessed apneas, choking/gasping, excessive daytime sleepiness).\\n\\nAdditional testing or referrals to consider\\n- Sleep medicine referral and diagnostic testing:\\n  - If STOP-BANG ≥3 or patient becomes symptomatic → refer to sleep medicine for diagnostic testing (home sleep apnea testing if appropriate or in-lab polysomnography).\\n  - Consider overnight pulse oximetry as an interim test if access to formal testing is delayed and patient is on testosterone or has other risk factors.\\n- Primary care or pulmonology referral for comorbid conditions that increase OSA risk (uncontrolled hypertension, heart failure, COPD).\\n- Hematology or primary care consult if polycythemia develops.\\n- If initiating testosterone and intermediate/high OSA risk or multiple risk factors (BMI >30, age >50, large neck circumference): consider sleep medicine consult prior to dose escalation.\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (clear actionable items)\\n- Immediate (today)\\n  - Document \\"STOP-BANG low risk\\" in chart; counsel patient briefly on OSA symptoms to watch for.\\n- Short term (within 4–12 weeks)\\n  - If initiating or changing testosterone dose: order baseline and 6–12 week labs (morning total testosterone, hematocrit/Hb).\\n  - Reassess STOP-BANG if clinical circumstances change; repeat sooner if dose change.\\n- Ongoing (3–12 months)\\n  - Routine monitoring of Hct and testosterone at 3–6 month intervals during first year of therapy, then annually if stable.\\n  - Repeat STOP-BANG annually or sooner with new symptoms, weight change, or testosterone dose change.\\n- Trigger points for immediate physician notification:\\n  - STOP-BANG score ≥3\\n  - New/worsening OSA symptoms\\n  - Hct ≥48% or rise >5% from baseline (or other clinic-specific polycythemia thresholds)\\n  - Any acute cardio-respiratory concerns\\n\\nNotes for staff\\n- Interpret STOP-BANG in clinical context (age, BMI, medications, menstrual status, current HRT/testosterone).\\n- Do not make a diagnosis of OSA from screening; refer for formal testing if indicated.\\n- Tailor the above monitoring and dose‑adjustment steps to clinic-specific testosterone protocols and physician orders.", "clinicalPhenotypes": []}	\N	2026-03-19 19:09:06.402036	2026-03-19 19:09:06.402036
