ron (e.g., ferrous sulfate 325 mg once daily) — discuss dosing and GI side effects. Recheck CBC and ferritin in 6–8 weeks after starting.\\n\\n2. Vitamin D deficiency — 25-OH vitamin D 17.3 ng/mL (severe, ≤20 ng/mL).\\n   - Discuss treatment with your clinician: common options are vitamin D3 50,000 IU weekly for 8–12 weeks or vitamin D3 2,000–4,000 IU daily, then recheck level in 8–12 weeks.\\n   - Add weight-bearing exercise (walking, light weights) 3–4x/week and ensure dietary calcium (~1,200 mg/day through dairy or fortified alternatives). Consider a bone density (DEXA) scan if not done recently.\\n\\n3. Cardiovascular risk — 10-year Total CVD risk 15.3% (INTERMEDIATE), ASCVD 8.1%, Heart failure risk 18.0%.\\n   - Increase aerobic exercise to 200–300 minutes weekly (brisk walking, cycling, swimming) and add strength training 2x/week.\\n   - Focus on heart-healthy fats: fatty fish 3x weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines), olive oil, nuts.\\n   - Reduce sodium to <2,300 mg/day, limit processed foods, and aim for 25–30 g fiber/day (oatmeal at breakfast, beans at lunch, vegetables at dinner).\\n   - Because triglycerides are 151 mg/dL, also: add 25–30 g fiber daily, include 2–3 servings of fatty fish weekly, reduce saturated fat (choose lean meats, limit butter/cheese), and aim for daily 30-minute brisk walks.\\n   - Your LDL goal is a 30–50% reduction; moderate-to-high intensity statin is recommended after shared decision-making — discuss starting a statin at your next visit.\\n\\nHormones (menopause phase)\\n- Estradiol 55 pg/mL, FSH 9.25 mIU/mL, progesterone 0.33 ng/mL. Progesterone is consistent with postmenopausal status; FSH is lower than typical for postmenopause and estradiol is mildly elevated. Because hormone patterns depend on age and history, review these with your provider to confirm menopausal status and symptoms.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Schedule a visit in 2–4 weeks to discuss starting vitamin D and iron (if needed) and the statin decision.\\n- 