rs from Estrovera in that it addresses the underlying estrogen metabolism rather than vasomotor symptom relief.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected; Weight gain (estrogen metabolism factor)", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement helps your body process and balance estrogen more effectively. It supports healthy hormone metabolism, which can help with PMS symptoms, bloating, mood changes, and hormonal balance during life transitions.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected", "Weight gain (estrogen metabolism factor)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support; Vaginal dryness (mucosal barrier support)", "phenotypes": ["Gut-Microbiome Support"], "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support", "Vaginal dryness (mucosal barrier support)"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 6, "apoBValue": 107, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "borderline", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 0.6539116285609928, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": true}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "generally_recommended", "indicated": true, "rationale": "ApoB ≥90 mg/dL (107 mg/dL) with risk enhancers (elevated hs-CRP, elevated ApoB). Statin discussion recommended."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\nYour results show some important areas to address and many things looking good. Notable concerns are low luteal-phase progesterone (0.56 ng/mL), low vitamin D (25-OH 20.8 ng/mL), high LDL cholesterol (162 mg/dL), elevated hs-CRP (3.5 mg/L), low SHBG (16.4 nmol/L) with elevated free testosterone (16 pg/mL). Your 10-year ASCVD (heart attack/stroke) risk is 0.7% — very low — but individual markers (LDL, ApoB 107 mg/dL, hs-CRP) suggest we should act on lifestyle now.\\n\\nPositive findings (things that are OK)\\n- Hemoglobin 14.5 g/dL and ferritin 116 ng/mL — iron stores are good for bone and reproductive health.\\n- TSH 2.71 mIU/L — thyroid function is in the normal range.\\n- Estradiol 56 pg/mL fits with the luteal phase.\\n- Hemoglobin A1c 5.1% and triglycerides 101 mg/dL — blood sugar and triglycerides are well controlled.\\n- eGFR 70 mL/min and liver enzymes (AST 25 U/L, ALT 23 U/L) are normal.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + concrete actions)\\n- Progesterone 0.56 ng/mL (low in luteal phase): If you’re trying to conceive or have luteal phase symptoms (spotting, short cycle), discuss with your clinician about repeating mid-luteal progesterone next cycle and options such as progesterone supplementation. If not trying to conceive, still discuss with your provider.\\n- Vitamin D 25-OH 20.8 ng/mL (low): Start vitamin D3 2,000–4,000 IU daily and take with a fat-containing meal; recheck level in 8–12 weeks. Add weight-bearing exercise (walking, stair climbing) and ensure dietary calcium (dairy or fortified plant milks) for bone health.\\n- LDL 162 mg/dL and ApoB 107 mg/dL (high/borderline): Add 25–30 g fiber daily — e.g., oatmeal for breakfast, beans with lunch, vegetables at dinner. Include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel), reduce saturated fat (choose lean meats, limit butter/cheese), and do 30 minutes brisk walking daily.\\n- hs-CRP 3.5 mg/L (elevated): Follow anti-inflammatory steps — Mediterranean-style eating, regular exercise, maintain healthy weight, and avoid smoking. Repeat hs-CRP after 3 months of lifestyle changes.\\n- SHBG 16.4 nmol/L and free testosterone 16 pg/mL (low SHBG, elevated free T): Prioritize 7–8 hours quality sleep, add strength training 2–3 times weekly, manage stress (meditation, walks), and aim for modest weight loss if overweight. If you have acne, excess hair, or irregular periods, ask about evaluation for PCOS and further endocrine testing.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Discuss progesterone and fertility implications with your clinician; repeat mid-luteal progesterone next cycle.\\n- Start vitamin D3 2,000–4,000 IU daily now and recheck 25-OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Begin the cholesterol and lifestyle steps above; repeat fasting lipid panel and ApoB in ~3 months; if LDL remains ≥130 mg/dL despite lifestyle, talk about statin options.\\n- Recheck hs-CRP after 3 months of changes. Consider referral to endocrine or gynecology if androgen symptoms or menstrual concerns persist.\\n\\nYou’re doing many things right (iron, thyroid, A1c). Small targeted changes now (vitamin D, diet, exercise, sleep) can improve hormones, bone, and heart health. If you’d like, we can make a step-by-step plan together.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 14.5, "status