>3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 16.4, "status": "abnormal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 16.4 nmol/L is low. Low SHBG increases free androgen activity and is associated with insulin resistance and metabolic syndrome.", "recommendation": "Evaluate for insulin resistance (fasting insulin, HOMA-IR). Consider metabolic workup.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 16, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 16 pg/mL is elevated. May cause androgenic symptoms (acne, hirsutism, hair thinning).", "recommendation": "Evaluate for PCOS, exogenous testosterone, or low SHBG driving elevated free T.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "of 6 markers positive", "value": 3, "status": "critical", "category": "Insulin Resistance Screening", "recheckTiming": "3-6 months", "interpretation": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: HIGH LIKELIHOOD (3/6 markers positive). Identified phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance, Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance. Treat according to phenotype-specific protocol.", "recommendation": "Phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance; Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance. Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose.", "referenceRange": "0-1 Low, 2 Moderate, 3+ High Likelihood"}], "aiRecommendations": "Unable to generate recommendations. Please review lab findings manually.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 2.02, "detail": "Triglycerides 101 / HDL 50 = 2.02", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5.1%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 16.4, "detail": "Very low - strong IR signal", "positive": true, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 23, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "107 mg/dL", "detail": "Elevated atherogenic particle count", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "3.5 mg/L", "detail": "Elevated systemic inflammation", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "high", "phenotypes": [{"key": "visceral_metabolic", "name": "Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Recheck A1c, lipid panel, hs-CRP, ApoB in 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0"], "pathophysiology": "Early peripheral + hepatic insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia. Glucose may still be \\"normal.\\" A1c often rises last.", "triggerCriteria": ["A1c >= 5.7%", "TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0", "Central adiposity phenotype", "ALT above optimal range"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests your body may not be responding to insulin as efficiently as it should. Insulin is the hormone that helps move sugar from your bloodstream into your cells for energy.\\n\\nWhen cells become less sensitive to insulin:\\n- Blood sugar slowly rises over time\\n- Triglycerides increase\\n- Inflammation increases\\n- Weight gain around the midsection becomes easier\\n\\nThe good news? This stage is highly reversible with targeted nutrition, strength training, sleep optimization, and - when appropriate - medication support.\\n\\nWe'll focus on improving how your body processes fuel and reducing long-term cardiometabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward nutrition (>= 25-35g/meal)", "Carbohydrate quality + timing education", "Strength training 2-3x weekly", "10-15 min post-meal walking", "Sleep optimization", "Consider: GLP-1 therapy if obesity, appetite dysregulation, A1c >= 5.7, or inflammatory phenotype", "Consider: Metformin if prediabetes or strong family history", "Recheck metabolic panel in 3-6 months"]}, {"key": "hormonal_pcos", "name": "Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Monitor SHBG, androgens, A1c every 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["SHBG very low (<30)", "Elevated total testosterone", "Elevated free testosterone"], "pathophysiology": "Hyperinsulinemia suppresses SHBG and increases ovarian androgen production.", "triggerCriteria": ["Low SHBG (<50; strong if <30)", "Elevated total or free testosterone", "Irregular cycles", "A1c may still be normal", "TG/HDL may be borderline-high"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests insulin may be influencing your hormone balance.\\n\\nWhen insulin levels run high:\\n- The body makes less SHBG (a protective hormone-binding protein)\\n- Androgen levels can rise\\n- Cycles may become irregular\\n\\nThis pattern is common in PCOS and other hormone imbalance states.\\n\\nImproving insulin sensitivity often helps restore hormonal balance and reduce long-term metabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward, lower refined carb approach", "Strength training", "Consider metformin if cycles irregular or A1c trending", "Consider GLP-1 if obesity phenotype present", "Monitor SHBG, androgens, A1c"]}], "positiveCount": 3, "likelihoodLabel": "HIGH LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: HIGH LIKELIHOOD (3/6 markers positive). Identified phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance, Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance. Treat according to phenotype-specific protocol.", "confirmationTests": "Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose."}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Inflammatory Burden", "confidence": "high", "description": "Pattern of systemic inflammation indicated by elevated inflammatory markers and/or symptomatic presentation. May benefit from anti-inflammatory nutritional support.", "supportingFindings": ["hs-CRP 3.5 mg/L (elevated >3.0)", "ApoB 107 mg/dL (elevated)"]}, {"name": "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "confidence": "high", "description": "Metabolic pattern consistent with insulin resistance and/or visceral adiposity. This drives inflammation, hormonal imbalance, and cardiovascular risk.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance screening: HIGH LIKELIHOOD (3/6 markers positive)", "SHBG 16.4 nmol/L (low, associated with insulin resistance)", "ApoB 107 mg/dL (elevated)", "hs-CRP 3.5 mg/L (metabolic inflammation)", "Unexplained weight gain / central adiposity reported"]}, {"name": "Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Lab pattern consistent with relative estrogen excess or impaired estrogen clearance. Even without reported symptoms, supporting liver detoxification and estrogen metabolism pathways may be beneficial.", "supportingFindings": ["Progesterone 0.56 ng/mL (very low)", "Borderline liver markers (ALT 23, AST 25) suggesting possible impaired hepatic clearance"]}, {"name": "Oxidative Stress Burden", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Multiple markers suggest increased oxidative stress and cellular burden. Antioxidant and glutathione support may help reduce oxidative damage and support cellular energy production.", "supportingFindings": ["hs-CRP 3.5 mg/L (elevated, systemic inflammation)", "ALT 23 U/L (hepatic stress marker)", "AST 25 U/L (cellular stress marker)", "Insulin resistance pattern (metabolic oxidative driver)", "Low energy / fatigue (possible mitochondrial burden)"]}, {"name": "Gut-Microbiome Support", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Indicators suggest the gut and/or urogenital microbiome may benefit from targeted probiotic support and gut barrier maintenance.", "supportingFindings": ["Vaginal dryness (mucosal barrier may benefit from probiotic support)", "hs-CRP 3.5 mg/L (gut-driven inflammation possible)", "Vitamin D 20.8 ng/mL (impacts gut barrier integrity)"]}]}	\N	2026-03-10 21:28:24.710903	2026-03-10 21:28:24.710903
9	4	2025-09-18 00:00:00	{"a1c": 5.1, "alt": 20, "ast": 20, "bun": 10, "co2": 18, "fsh": 8.14, "hdl": 39, "ldl": 110, "rbc": 5.05, "tsh": 1.59, "wbc": 7.5, "acne": false, "egfr": 124, "onHRT": false, "freeT3": 3.17, "sodium": 139, "albumin": 4.3, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 9.8, "glucose": 98, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "chloride": 106, "hairLoss": false, "vitaminD": 17.2, "bilirubin": 0.2, "estradiol": 32, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": false, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 408, "potassium": 4.5, "creatinine": 0.68, "hematocrit": 47.7, "hemoglobin": 14.5, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "vitaminB12": 333, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2025-09-18", "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Laura Summerlin", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 25, "bmi": 57.96, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 110, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 0.05, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 39.5, "totalProtein": 7.1, "lowMotivation": false, "triglycerides": 147, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "totalCholesterol": 178}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History of present illness: Patient presented for review of recent laboratory results. Patient reports fatigue and intermittent low mood; expresses concern about low vitamin D and overall energy. Denies chest pain, palpitations, syncope, focal weakness, bone pain, fractures, or bleeding. No acute illness reported.\\n- Menstrual/hormonal history: Menstrual cycle timing not documented in chart at time of visit; patient will provide cycle day if available. Noted low random progesterone (P4 0.05 ng/mL) — timing-dependent. Patient not currently requesting testosterone therapy.\\n- Allergies/medications: None documented in lab summary. (Verify active med/allergy list in chart.)\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: Not available in current lab-only encounter.\\n- CBC:\\n  - Hemoglobin: 14.5 g/dL (normal)\\n  - Hematocrit: 47.7 % (borderline high) — may indicate mild hemoconcentration/dehydration\\n  - Platelets: 408 K/uL (borderline high; 400–450 K/uL)\\n- Basic metabolic / kidney:\\n  - Creatinine: 0.68 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - eGFR: 124 mL/min (normal)\\n- Liver:\\n  - AST: 20 U/L (normal)\\n  - ALT: 20 U/L (normal)\\n- Lipid panel:\\n  - Total cholesterol: (not provided)\\n  - HDL cholesterol: 39 mg/dL (abnormal — low)\\n  - LDL cholesterol: 110 mg/dL (borderline/near optimal)\\n  - Triglycerides: 147 mg/dL (normal)\\n- Endocrine / hormones:\\n  - TSH: 1.59 mIU/L (normal)\\n  - Estradiol (E2): 32 pg/mL (within reported normal for unspecified cycle day)\\n  - Progesterone (P4): 0.05 ng/mL (low — interpret relative to cycle day)\\n  - FSH: 8.14 mIU/mL (normal)\\n  - Total Testosterone: 39.5 ng/dL (within reference range)\\n  - Testosterone pattern: Pattern D — Adequate androgens, persistent symptoms\\n- Vitamin / metabolic:\\n  - Vitamin D (25-OH): 17.2 ng/mL (abnormal — severe deficiency ≤20 ng/mL)\\n  - Vitamin B12: 333 pg/mL (normal)\\n  - Hemoglobin A1c: 5.1 % (normal)\\n- Calculated risk scores:\\n  - 10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT): 0 %\\n  - 10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT): 0 %\\n  - 10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT): 0 %\\n  - Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG): 0 points\\n  - Cardiovascular risk category: Low (Age outside statin-validated range; use clinical judgment)\\n- Other: No red-flag critical labs besides Vitamin D deficiency and low HDL identified.\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Severe Vitamin D deficiency — 25‑OH Vitamin D 17.2 ng/mL (≤20 ng/mL).\\n   - Clinical significance: Associated with fatigue, bone demineralization risk, immune dysfunction; requires repletion.\\n   - Red flag: Provider notification recommended for initiation of high-dose repletion and safety monitoring.\\n2. Low HDL cholesterol — 39 mg/dL.\\n   - Clinical significance: Increased cardiovascular risk marker despite low calculated 10‑yr risk given patient age; recommend lifestyle measures and recheck lipids.\\n3. Borderline thrombocytosis — platelets 408 K/uL.\\n   - Clinical significance: Mild, may be reactive (iron deficiency, inflammation, infection). Needs evaluation with iron studies and inflammatory markers.\\n   - Differential: Iron deficiency, recent infection/inflammation, reactive thrombocytosis.\\n4. Borderline elevated hematocrit — 47.7%.\\n   - Clinical significance: Slight elevation; may represent mild dehydration/hemoconcentration. Recheck after hydration.\\n   - Action threshold: Notify provider if hematocrit >50% or symptomatic.\\n5. Adequate total testosterone with persistent symptoms — Total T 39.5 ng/dL; Pattern D (Adequate androgens, persistent symptoms).\\n   - Clinical significance: Androgens unlikely primary driver of fatigue/mood; evaluate non-androgenic causes (vitamin D deficiency, thyroid, sleep, mood, HPA axis, estrogen/progesterone balance).\\n6. Low random progesterone — P4 0.05 ng/mL (timing-dependent).\\n   - Clinical significance: Interpretation requires cycle day; recommend mid-luteal progesterone if evaluating ovulation/fertility.\\n7. LDL 110 mg/dL (near optimal); Triglycerides 147 mg/dL (normal).\\n   - Clinical significance: Lifestyle modification first-line; recheck after 3 months.\\n8. Other labs within normal limits: Hemoglobin, TSH, B12, A1c, AST/ALT, creatinine/eGFR.\\n\\nPLAN:\\n1. Vitamin D deficiency (25‑OH D 17.2 ng/mL)\\n   - Provider notified for authorization to initiate repletion per clinic protocol.\\n   - Start Metagenics D3 10,000 IU + K PO daily x 8–12 weeks (high‑dose repletion), then transition to maintenance dosing per provider.\\n   - Baseline safety: serum calcium to be ordered now; renal function already acceptable (Cr 0.68, eGFR 124). Monitor for symptoms of hypercalcemia; counsel patient on signs (nausea, vomiting, polyuria, confusion).\\n   - Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D level in 8–12 weeks after initiation.\\n   - Document patient education on dosing and adherence.\\n2. Low HDL (39 mg/dL)\\n   - Lifestyle counseling provided:\\n     - Aerobic exercise: target ≥150 min/week moderate intensity.\\n     - Resistance training 2×/week.\\n     - Dietary: Mediterranean/DASH-style eating; reduce refined carbs and trans fats; increase omega‑3 fatty fish, fiber.\\n     - Limit alcohol; smoking cessation if applicable.\\n     - Weight management as indicated.\\n   - No pharmacologic HDL therapy indicated at this time. Repeat fasting lipid panel in 3 months after lifestyle intervention.\\n3. Borderline thrombocytosis (Platelets 408 K/uL) and hematocrit 47.7%\\n   - Order iron studies (ferritin, serum iron, TIBC/transferrin, transferrin saturation) and inflammatory marker (CRP or ESR) within 1 week.\\n   - Repeat CBC in 4–8 weeks after hydration and initial evaluation.\\n   - Hydration counseling given; advise patient to increase oral fluids and recheck CBC after improved hydration.\\n   - Do NOT start empiric iron until iron studies available. If ferritin <50 ng/mL and iron deficiency confirmed, consider oral iron therapy (e.g., ferrous sulfate 325 mg every other day with vitamin C 250 mg) and recheck ferritin at 8–12 weeks.\\n   - Consider hematology referral if platelets >450 K/uL persist or if workup is unrevealing.\\n4. Adequate testosterone with persistent symptoms; no testosterone therapy\\n   - No initiation of testosterone therapy based on current labs.\\n   - If fatigue/mood/sleep symptoms persist despite treating reversible causes, consider additional evaluation: DHEA‑S, Free T4/Free T3, anti‑TPO antibodies, sleep assessment, behavioral health referral.\\n5. Low random progesterone (P4 0.05 ng/mL)\\n   - Clarify menstrual cycle day and contraceptive/HRT status with patient. If assessing ovulation/fertility or luteal phase adequacy, schedule mid‑luteal progesterone (~7 days before menses; ~day 21 in a 28‑day cycle).\\n6. Lipids and cardiovascular risk\\n   - Lifestyle measures as above. Repeat fasting lipid panel in 3 months.\\n   - No statin indicated at this age by PREVENT calculation; discuss if other risk factors emerge.\\n7. Additional recommendations / supplements\\n   - High-priority supplement: D3 10,000 IU + K daily (as above) — provider authorization required.\\n   - Medium-priority: Intrinsi B12‑Folate™ 1 tablet daily may be considered for general support (patient already has B12 333 pg/mL — within range).\\n   - Counsel on interactions (vitamin D with calcium supplements) and monitoring.\\n8. Follow-up labs and timeline (actionable checklist)\\n   - Today / within 1 week:\\n     - Provider authorization for vitamin D repletion.\\n     - Order ferritin, serum iron, TIBC/transferrin, transferrin saturation, CRP or ESR.\\n     - Order serum calcium (baseline) and repeat CBC if clinically indicated.\\n     - Document cycle day or schedule mid‑luteal progesterone if indicated.\\n   - 4–8 weeks:\\n     - Repeat CBC to reassess platelets and hematocrit.\\n     - If iron therapy started, recheck ferritin at 8–12 weeks.\\n   - 8–12 weeks:\\n     - Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D and serum calcium as appropriate; transition to maintenance dosing if repleted.\\n   - 3 months:\\n     - Repeat fasting lipid panel after lifestyle interventions.\\n     - Re-evaluate symptoms; consider further endocrine testing (DHEA‑S, Free T4/Free T3, anti‑TPO) and referrals as indicated.\\n9. Safety thresholds / provider notification\\n   - Notify provider promptly if:\\n     - 25‑OH Vitamin D ≤20 ng/mL (initiation of repletion — action taken).\\n     - Platelets >450 K/uL or rising.\\n     - Hematocrit >50% or if symptomatic (headache, visual changes, thrombosis symptoms).\\n     - Ferritin <15 ng/mL, falling hemoglobin, or intolerance/nonresponse to iron therapy.\\n     - Any abnormal calcium or renal function during high‑dose vitamin D therapy.\\n10. Patient communication\\n   - Results reviewed and discussed with patient. Education provided on vitamin D repletion, hydration, timing of progesterone testing, lifestyle measures for lipids, and the plan to evaluate platelet elevation.\\n   - Patient instructed to report new symptoms (bone pain, significant fatigue worsening, palpitations, signs of hypercalcemia, bruising/bleeding, or thrombotic symptoms).\\n\\nFollow-up:\\n- Recheck window: 3–6 months for routine follow-up; specific labs as outlined above (CBC 4–8 weeks, iron studies now, 25‑OH D in 8–12 weeks, fasting lipids in 3 months).\\n- Provider to authorize vitamin D regimen and review iron/inflammatory studies when resulted.\\n\\n03/10/2026\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": true, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": true, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "preventRisk": {"riskCategory": "low", "tenYearASCVD": 0, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "PREVENT calculator is validated for ages 30-79 years. Patient is 25 years old. Use clinical judgment for risk assessment.", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0, "ageValidFor30Year": false, "tenYearHFPercentage": "N/A", "tenYearHeartFailure": 0, "statinRecommendation": "Age outside validated range - use clinical judgment", "tenYearCVDPercentage": "N/A", "tenYearASCVDPercentage": "N/A"}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 softgel daily with meal", "name": "D3 10,000 + K", "caution": "High-dose repletion therapy. Recheck vitamin D levels in 8-12 weeks. Contains vitamin K2 for calcium metabolism.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "high", "rationale": "D3 10,000 + K provides high-dose vitamin D3 with K2 for efficient repletion of severe deficiency. K2 ensures proper calcium utilization and prevents arterial calcification.", "indication": "Vitamin D 17.2 ng/mL (severe deficiency ≤20)", "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D level is very low, which can affect your bones, immune system, mood, and energy. This high-dose supplement will help bring your level up to optimal range, and the vitamin K2 helps your body use calcium properly.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 17.2 ng/mL (severe deficiency ≤20)"]}, {"dose": "1 tablet daily", "name": "Intrinsi B12-Folate™", "caution": "Contains intrinsic factor for enhanced B12 absorption. Ideal for patients with absorption concerns or vegetarian/vegan diets.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Intrinsi B12-Folate provides methylated B12 and folate with intrinsic factor for superior absorption. Supports energy, cognition, methylation, and nerve function.", "indication": "B12 333 pg/mL (suboptimal <400)", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "Your B12 and/or folate levels need support. This supplement provides highly absorbable forms of these essential vitamins to support your energy, brain function, and nervous system health.", "supportingFindings": ["B12 333 pg/mL (suboptimal <400)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\nYour major concerns are a low vitamin D level and a low HDL cholesterol. Your Vitamin D is 17.2 ng/mL (severe deficiency, ≤20 ng/mL) and your HDL is 39 mg/dL (below the target of 40 mg/dL). Most other results are reassuring: hemoglobin 14.5 g/dL, TSH 1.59 mIU/L, hemoglobin A1c 5.1%, AST 20 U/L, ALT 20 U/L, eGFR 124 mL/min, creatinine 0.68 mg/dL, and vitamin B12 333 pg/mL.\\n\\nPositive findings (what’s normal)\\n- Hemoglobin 14.5 g/dL — within normal range.\\n- TSH 1.59 mIU/L — thyroid function looks normal.\\n- Hemoglobin A1c 5.1% — blood sugar control is normal.\\n- AST 20 U/L and ALT 20 U/L — liver tests normal.\\n- eGFR 124 mL/min and creatinine 0.68 mg/dL — kidney function normal.\\n- Total testosterone 39.5 ng/dL — within expected female range; pattern suggests adequate androgens.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + concrete actions)\\n1. Vitamin D 25-OH 17.2 ng/mL (severe deficiency)\\n- Action: Discuss starting vitamin D3 with your provider. Options commonly used: prescription 50,000 IU weekly for 6–8 weeks OR over-the-counter 2,000 IU daily, then recheck in 8–12 weeks. Increase dietary sources: salmon, sardines, fortified milk/plant milk, eggs. Aim for 10–20 minutes of sun exposure to arms/face several times weekly if safe for your skin.\\n- Bone health: ensure 1,000–1,200 mg calcium/day from diet (dairy, leafy greens); do weight-bearing exercise 3–5x/week.\\n\\n2. HDL cholesterol 39 mg/dL (low) and LDL 110 mg/dL (near optimal), triglycerides 147 mg/dL\\n- Concrete lifestyle actions:\\n  - Add 25–30 g fiber daily: oatmeal for breakfast, beans with lunch, vegetables at dinner.\\n  - Include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n  - Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter and cheese.\\n  - Daily exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking or equivalent.\\n- Your 10-year ASCVD/PREVENT risk is 0% (LOW), which is good; still follow the steps above to raise HDL and improve overall heart health.\\n\\n3. Hematocrit 47.7% (slightly high) and platelets 408 K/uL (mildly elevated)\\n- Action: Drink an extra 1–2 cups of water the day before repeat labs (dehydration can raise hematocrit). Repeat CBC in 4–8 weeks. If platelets remain ≥400 K/uL, ask provider to check iron studies (ferritin, iron, TIBC) because reactive platelet elevations can follow iron deficiency or inflammation.\\n\\nHormones / cycle note\\n- Estradiol 32 pg/mL and progesterone 0.05 ng/mL depend on your menstrual phase. If you are premenopausal and concerned about cycles or ovulation, consider timing a progesterone test about 7 days before your expected period (mid-luteal) to confirm ovulation.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Discuss vitamin D replacement options with your clinician and start supplementation; recheck 25-OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Begin the cholesterol lifestyle changes now and recheck fasting lipids in 3–6 months.\\n- Repeat CBC in 4–8 weeks after staying well hydrated; obtain iron studies if platelets stay high or if you have symptoms.\\n- Book a follow-up visit to review progress and adjust plans. If you have new symptoms (worsening fatigue, bone pain, unusual bleeding, or any chest symptoms), contact the clinic sooner.\\n\\nYou’re doing well in many areas — these focused steps should help raise your vitamin D and HDL and address the mild CBC changes.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 14.5, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 47.7, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Slightly elevated hematocrit - may indicate dehydration.", "recommendation": "Encourage hydration. Repeat if persistent.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 408, "status": "borderline", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Mild platelet elevation (400-450). Commonly reactive to iron deficiency, inflammation, or infection.", "recommendation": "Check iron studies and inflammatory markers. Repeat CBC in 4-8 weeks.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 32, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.05, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 8.14, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 39.5, "status": "normal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone within normal female range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.59, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 17.2, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Severe Vitamin D deficiency (≤20 ng/mL). Associated with fatigue, bone loss, and immune dysfunction.", "recommendation": "High-dose repletion: Metagenics D3 10,000 + K daily for 8-12 weeks. Recheck levels after repletion.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 333, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 110, "status": "borderline", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Near optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet and exercise.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 39, "status": "abnormal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Low HDL - increased cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Increase aerobic exercise. Consider niacin if very low.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 147, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: N/A (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "PREVENT calculator is validated for ages 30-79 years. Patient is 25 years old. Use clinical judgment for risk assessment.", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): N/A", "recommendation": "Age outside validated range - use clinical judgment", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: N/A", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 20, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 20, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 124, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.68, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 39.5, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone Pattern D: Adequate Androgens, Persistent Symptoms", "interpretation": "Adequate Androgens pattern identified (Total T: 39.5 ng/dL (within range)). Total, free, and bioavailable testosterone are within normal ranges and SHBG is not extreme. If the patient reports fatigue, low mood, sleep disruption, or low libido, these symptoms are likely driven by non-androgenic factors. Findings to evaluate: Low Vitamin D (17.2 ng/mL) — contributes to fatigue and mood changes. Estrogen/Progesterone balance should be assessed (E2: 32 pg/mL, P4: 0.05 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Do not reflexively add testosterone when levels are adequate — look deeper. Evaluate iron status (ferritin target >50 for symptom resolution), thyroid function (optimize TSH), and sleep quality (consider sleep apnea screening with STOP-BANG). Assess estrogen and progesterone balance — E2/P4 imbalance is a common driver of persistent symptoms. Address CNS and psychosocial drivers: chronic stress, HPA axis dysregulation, mood disorders. Consider DHEA-S if not already checked. Support with lifestyle optimization: sleep hygiene, stress management, nutrition. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your testosterone levels are actually in a healthy range, which is good news. When symptoms persist despite normal testosterone, it usually means something else is contributing. Your provider will look at other factors like iron levels, thyroid function, sleep quality, and hormone balance to find what's driving your symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Total T 15-70 ng/dL, Free T 0.5-5.0 pg/mL (all within range)"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- Key abnormalities: severe vitamin D deficiency (25‑OH D 17.2 ng/mL), low HDL (39 mg/dL), borderline elevated platelets (408 K/uL) and hematocrit (47.7%). Total testosterone is in-range (39.5 ng/dL) with persistent symptoms — pattern consistent with “Adequate Androgens, Persistent Symptoms.”\\n- Most likely actionable drivers of symptoms to pursue first: vitamin D deficiency, possible iron deficiency/inflammation (given platelet elevation), and estrogen/progesterone balance (P4 0.05 ng/mL — interpret relative to cycle phase).\\n- No indication to initiate testosterone therapy based on current labs alone.\\n\\nPriority actions (organized CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL (notify provider immediately / start treatment now)\\n- Vitamin D deficiency: Notify provider for initiation of high‑dose repletion per clinic protocol.\\n  - Suggested regimen per protocol: Metagenics D3 10,000 IU + K daily x 8–12 weeks.\\n  - Baseline safety labs: confirm recent renal function (creatinine, eGFR — already normal) and consider baseline serum calcium. Arrange patient education regarding dosing and signs of hypercalcemia.\\n  - Recheck 25‑OH vitamin D level 8–12 weeks after start of repletion.\\n\\nURGENT (initiate testing/monitoring within 1–4 weeks)\\n- Iron studies and inflammatory markers: Order ferritin, serum iron, TIBC or transferrin, transferrin saturation, CRP or ESR now to evaluate cause of thrombocytosis.\\n  - If ferritin <50 ng/mL (symptom target >50): consider iron therapy (see below) and plan recheck ferritin in 8–12 weeks after therapy.\\n- CBC repeat: Repeat CBC in 4–8 weeks to reassess platelets and hematocrit after hydration and initial evaluation.\\n- Progesterone timing: Clarify menstrual cycle day and contraceptive/HRT status. If assessing ovulation/fertility or progesterone adequacy, order mid‑luteal progesterone (~7 days pre-menses; ~day 21 in a 28‑day cycle) rather than random level.\\n\\nROUTINE (follow-up in weeks–months)\\n- Lipids: Lifestyle intervention first; repeat fasting lipid panel in 3 months.\\n- Sleep/psychosocial assessment: If fatigue/mood/sleep symptoms persist, screen/referral for sleep study (if STOP‑BANG elevated or clinical suspicion) and consider behavioral health referral for mood/stress/HPA axis concerns.\\n- Consider DHEA‑S and full thyroid panel (Free T4, Free T3, anti‑TPO) if symptoms persist despite normal TSH.\\n\\nSpecific medication / dose recommendations and provider notification requirements\\n- Vitamin D:\\n  - Start Metagenics D3 10,000 IU + K PO daily x 8–12 weeks per protocol (provider must authorize).\\n  - Recheck 25‑OH in 8–12 weeks; if adequate, convert to maintenance dosing (e.g., 1,000–2,000 IU daily or as directed by provider).\\n  - Monitor serum calcium if symptoms or high-dose therapy prolonged.\\n- Iron:\\n  - Do NOT start empiric iron prior to iron studies. If ferritin is <50 ng/mL and iron deficiency is confirmed, recommended initial regimen for most patients: oral ferrous sulfate 325 mg (65 mg elemental iron) given every other day (or daily if protocol prefers) with vitamin C 250 mg to aid absorption, and counsel regarding GI side effects and interactions.\\n  - Reassess ferritin 8–12 weeks after starting iron. Target ferritin >50 ng/mL for symptomatic improvement.\\n  - Notify provider if ferritin very low (<15 ng/mL), hemoglobin falling, or intolerance/poor response.\\n- Testosterone:\\n  - No testosterone initiation or dose changes recommended — levels are adequate. If provider still considers androgen therapy after thorough evaluation, escalate to physician for individualized dosing and monitoring plan (not indicated from current data).\\n\\nLifestyle interventions to recommend to patient (staff to counsel)\\n- Hydration: Encourage increased oral fluids; recheck hematocrit after hydration.\\n- Vitamin D: Emphasize adherence to high‑dose repletion regimen and follow-up labs.\\n- Cardiovascular risk and lipids:\\n  - Aerobic exercise ≥150 minutes/week (aim for 150–300 minutes moderate intensity).\\n  - Resistance training 2x/week.\\n  - Dietary: Mediterranean‑style or DASH diet; reduce refined carbs/trans fats; increase omega‑3 fatty fish and fiber.\\n  - Weight loss target if overweight: aim for 5–10% to improve HDL/TG.\\n  - Smoking cessation if applicable.\\n  - Consider discussing alcohol moderation (excess lowers HDL).\\n- Sleep hygiene and stress management: sleep regularity, screen for obstructive sleep apnea if clinically indicated, refer behavioral health for stress/mood interventions if needed.\\n- Iron absorption counseling if iron started: take iron on an empty stomach or with vitamin C; avoid calcium, tea, coffee and antacids within 2 hours of iron dose.\\n\\nAdditional testing or referrals to consider\\n- Laboratory:\\n  - Ferritin, iron, TIBC/transferrin, transferrin saturation, CRP/ESR (now).\\n  - Repeat CBC in 4–8 weeks.\\n  - Repeat 25‑OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks post‑repletion.\\n  - Fasting lipid panel in 3 months after lifestyle intervention.\\n  - Mid‑luteal progesterone (timed) if evaluating ovulation/fertility or P4 adequacy.\\n  - Consider DHEA‑S, Free T4/Free T3 and thyroid antibodies if symptoms persist despite normal TSH.\\n  - Consider calcium and PTH if bone health concerns or if prolonged high‑dose vitamin D planned.\\n- Imaging / specialty:\\n  - Consider DEXA scan if other osteoporosis risk factors or clinical concern for low bone density (esp. with severe vitamin D deficiency and other risk factors).\\n  - Sleep medicine referral if high clinical suspicion for sleep apnea despite STOP‑BANG.\\n  - Behavioral health/psychology for mood/stress/HPA axis dysfunction if clinically indicated.\\n  - Hematology referral if platelets rise >450 K/uL persistently or if thrombocytosis unexplained after iron and inflammation work‑up.\\n\\nPhysician notification thresholds (staff action points)\\n- Notify provider promptly if:\\n  - 25‑OH vitamin D ≤20 ng/mL (start repletion) — action already indicated.\\n  - Platelets rise >450 K/uL or continue to trend upward on repeat CBC.\\n  - Hematocrit >50% or symptomatic (headache, visual changes, thrombosis symptoms).\\n  - Ferritin very low (<15 ng/mL) or hemoglobin falling.\\n  - Any abnormal calcium or renal function during high‑dose vitamin D therapy.\\n  - If considering pharmacologic HDL therapy (niacin) — requires provider discussion and monitoring.\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (actionable checklist)\\n- Today / within 1 week:\\n  - Notify provider to authorize vitamin D repletion (Metagenics D3 10,000 IU + K daily x 8–12 weeks); counsel patient on dosing and side‑effects.\\n  - Order ferritin, iron, TIBC/transferrin, transferrin saturation, CRP/ESR; clarify cycle day and contraceptive/HRT status.\\n  - Hydration counseling; document plan to repeat CBC.\\n- 4–8 weeks:\\n  - Repeat CBC to reassess platelets and hematocrit.\\n  - If iron therapy started, recheck ferritin at 8–12 weeks.\\n- 8–12 weeks:\\n  - Recheck 25‑OH vitamin D (and serum calcium if concerned); convert to maintenance dose if adequate.\\n  - Reassess symptoms; review iron study results and consider iron therapy if ferritin <50.\\n- 3 months:\\n  - Repeat fasting lipid panel after lifestyle interventions.\\n  - Re-evaluate symptoms; consider further testing (DHEA‑S, thyroid antibodies, mid‑luteal progesterone if not yet performed) and referrals as indicated.\\n\\nNotes for staff\\n- Do not start testosterone based solely on symptoms when total and free testosterone are in-range. Escalate to provider for a broader investigational approach.\\n- Ensure progesterone is interpreted relative to menstrual cycle timing; obtain cycle day or schedule mid‑luteal draw if ovulation assessment is needed.\\n- Document all patient education given (hydration, vitamin D dosing, iron absorption guidance, lifestyle recommendations) and flag abnormal results for provider review per above thresholds.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 3.77, "detail": "Triglycerides 147 / HDL 39 = 3.77", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5.1%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 20, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 1, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (1/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": []}	\N	2026-03-10 21:37:51.863007	2026-03-10 21:37:51.863007
10	4	2026-03-10 21:41:12.697	{"alt": 13, "ast": 15, "bun": 7, "co2": 20, "hdl": 37, "ldl": 127, "lpa": 6, "rbc": 4.75, "tsh": 1.55, "wbc": 8.9, "acne": false, "apoB": 100, "egfr": 124, "onHRT": false, "folate": 2.01, "sodium": 139, "albumin": 4.2, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 9.5, "glucose": 95, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "chloride": 107, "ferritin": 83.8, "hairLoss": false, "vitaminD": 15.9, "bilirubin": 0.3, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": false, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 406, "potassium": 4, "creatinine": 0.67, "hematocrit": 42, "hemoglobin": 14.1, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "vitaminB12": 695, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Laura Summerlin", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 25, "bmi": 51.8, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 120, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "restlessLegs": false, "totalProtein": 6.5, "lowMotivation": false, "triglycerides": 117, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "totalCholesterol": 187}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History: 25-year-old female presenting for review of recent laboratory results from Women's Hormone & Primary Care Clinic. Patient reports no acute symptoms of bleeding, thrombosis, chest pain, shortness of breath, severe fatigue beyond baseline, or focal neurologic deficits. Patient denies currently being pregnant; contraception/menstrual history reviewed verbally (on record: not pregnant; last menstrual period not documented today). No reported history of kidney disease, granulomatous disease, lymphoma, or hypercalcemia. No current testosterone therapy documented.\\n- Symptoms related to abnormal results: Patient reports intermittent low energy/fatigue. No GI upset, polyuria, polydipsia, muscle weakness, or neurocognitive changes reported at this visit.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\nLaboratory results (03/10/2026) — all values with units; abnormal/borderline values flagged.\\n\\nCBC:\\n- Hemoglobin (Hgb): 14.1 g/dL — within reference range\\n- Hematocrit (Hct): 42 % — within reference range\\n- Platelets: 406 K/uL — BORDERLINE (mild thrombocytosis; lab cutoff 400–450 K/uL)\\n\\nIron/Ferritin:\\n- Ferritin: 83.8 ng/mL — within reference range\\n\\nMetabolic / Renal / Hepatic:\\n- AST: 15 U/L — normal\\n- ALT: 13 U/L — normal\\n- Creatinine: 0.67 mg/dL — normal\\n- eGFR: 124 mL/min/1.73m2 — normal\\n- Serum calcium: not provided (ordered as baseline recommended)\\n\\nThyroid:\\n- TSH: 1.55 mIU/L — normal\\n\\nVitamins / Nutritional:\\n- Vitamin D (25‑OH): 15.9 ng/mL — ABNORMAL (Severe vitamin D deficiency; ≤20 ng/mL)\\n- Vitamin B12: 695 pg/mL — normal\\n- Folate: 2.01 ng/mL — ABNORMAL (folate deficiency)\\n\\nLipids / Cardiovascular:\\n- Triglycerides: 117 mg/dL — normal\\n- HDL cholesterol: 37 mg/dL — ABNORMAL (low HDL)\\n- LDL cholesterol: 127 mg/dL — BORDERLINE (near‑optimal)\\n- Apolipoprotein B (ApoB): 100 mg/dL — BORDERLINE\\n- Lipoprotein(a): 6 mg/dL — normal\\n- 10‑Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT): 0 %\\n- 10‑Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT): 0 %\\n- 10‑Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT): 0 %\\n- Cardiovascular category: Low; statin use: age outside validated range — use clinical judgment\\n\\nInsulin resistance screening / Metabolic phenotype:\\n- Insulin Resistance Screening: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD — 2 of 6 markers positive (phenotype: visceral / metabolic insulin resistance). Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA‑IR).\\n\\nOther:\\n- Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP‑BANG): 0 points — low risk\\n- LFTs, renal function listed above\\n- No vitals/BMI recorded in this encounter (not available)\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1) Severe Vitamin D deficiency — 25‑OH vitamin D 15.9 ng/mL.\\n   - Clinical significance: increased risk of bone demineralization, fatigue, immune modulation. Requires repletion.\\n   - Red flags: baseline hypercalcemia/renal impairment/granulomatous disease would alter management.\\n\\n2) Folate deficiency — serum folate 2.01 ng/mL.\\n   - Clinical significance: important for fertility/pregnancy prevention of neural tube defects; symptomatic deficiency can contribute to fatigue/macrocytosis (Hgb currently normal).\\n   - Differential/consideration: dietary insufficiency, malabsorption, medication effects (anticonvulsants, methotrexate), increased requirements (pregnancy).\\n\\n3) Dyslipidemia — Low HDL (37 mg/dL) with borderline LDL (127 mg/dL) and borderline ApoB (100 mg/dL).\\n   - Clinical significance: low HDL is an atherogenic feature despite low 10‑yr ASCVD risk; ApoB borderline may indicate atherogenic particle burden.\\n   - Differential/consideration: lifestyle factors, genetics, medication effects (OCP/HRT), insulin resistance.\\n\\n4) Insulin resistance — Screening shows moderate likelihood (2/6 markers positive); phenotype visceral/metabolic IR.\\n   - Clinical significance: hyperinsulinemia physiology; increased cardiometabolic risk; consider objective confirmation (fasting insulin, fasting glucose, HOMA‑IR).\\n   - Differential/consideration: early metabolic syndrome; assess weight, waist circumference, diet, activity.\\n\\n5) Borderline thrombocytosis — platelets 406 K/uL.\\n   - Clinical significance: mild reactive thrombocytosis possible (iron deficiency, inflammation, infection). Ferritin normal, but functional iron deficiency or inflammation still possible.\\n   - Plan considerations: repeat CBC; evaluate iron indices and inflammatory markers. Hematology referral if >450 K/uL or persistent/upward trend.\\n\\n6) Other labs within normal limits — Hgb 14.1 g/dL, ferritin 83.8 ng/mL, B12 695 pg/mL, TSH 1.55 mIU/L, AST/ALT normal, creatinine/eGFR normal, Lp(a) low.\\n\\nPLAN:\\nOverall approach: Initiate prioritized repletion and targeted testing now; implement lifestyle measures for cardiometabolic risk; arrange follow‑up labs and physician review per timeline below. Results reviewed with patient; pregnancy status confirmed as negative today verbally — confirm if planning pregnancy.\\n\\n1) Severe Vitamin D deficiency (25‑OH D 15.9 ng/mL)\\n   - Start Metagenics D3 10,000 IU + K (1 softgel PO daily with a meal) for 8–12 weeks per clinic repletion protocol.\\n   - Order baseline serum calcium prior to initiation (critical safety check).\\n   - Counsel patient on adherence, dietary sources of vitamin D, and signs of hypercalcemia (nausea, vomiting, polyuria, polydipsia, confusion) and instruct to discontinue and notify clinic if symptoms occur.\\n   - Contraindications/precautions reviewed: confirm no history of hypercalcemia, granulomatous disease, lymphoma, or stage 4–5 CKD. Patient reports no such history.\\n   - Recheck 25‑OH vitamin D level at 8–12 weeks after initiation. After repletion, transition to maintenance dosing per physician direction (typical maintenance 1,000–2,000 IU daily individualized).\\n\\n2) Folate deficiency (folate 2.01 ng/mL)\\n   - Start folic acid 1 mg PO daily.\\n   - If patient is pregnant or planning pregnancy: discuss with physician/OBGYN and consider high‑dose folic acid 4 mg PO daily per obstetric recommendations — physician notification required if pregnancy planned.\\n   - Recheck serum folate in 4–8 weeks.\\n   - Provide dietary counseling: increase leafy greens, legumes, citrus, fortified grains.\\n   - Consider evaluation for causes if folate remains low on recheck (malabsorption, medications).\\n\\n3) Dyslipidemia / low HDL / borderline ApoB (HDL 37 mg/dL; LDL 127 mg/dL; ApoB 100 mg/dL)\\n   - Initiate lifestyle interventions now:\\n     - Exercise: ≥150 minutes/week moderate aerobic activity + resistance training 2x/week to help raise HDL.\\n     - Diet: Mediterranean‑style eating pattern, reduce refined carbohydrates and trans fats, increase fatty fish/omega‑3 sources.\\n     - Weight management counseling if BMI elevated (not recorded today) — target 5–10% weight loss if applicable.\\n     - Smoking cessation counseling if applicable.\\n   - Recommend medium‑priority supplements: OmegaGenics® Fish Oil Neuro 1000 (1 softgel daily; may increase to 1–2 daily) as recommended by clinic.\\n   - Repeat fasting lipid panel (including ApoB if prior fasting) in 3 months after lifestyle changes. If LDL ≥160 mg/dL, ApoB persistently >100 mg/dL or other risk enhancers present, discuss pharmacotherapy with physician and consider cardiology referral.\\n   - Document medication list and review for OCP/HRT that may impact lipids; patient advised to inform clinic if taking hormonal therapy.\\n\\n4) Insulin resistance screening (moderate likelihood)\\n   - Order fasting glucose and fasting insulin to calculate HOMA‑IR for objective confirmation.\\n   - Consider hemoglobin A1c if not recently obtained (not provided here); order if clinically indicated.\\n   - Provide lifestyle counseling targeted to insulin sensitivity: reduce refined carbohydrate intake, fiber‑rich whole foods, regular physical activity, weight loss if indicated.\\n   - Consider referral to nutritionist for structured dietary plan and weight management.\\n   - Reassess after labs and lifestyle intervention; consider endocrine/metabolic referral if HOMA‑IR abnormal or clinical progression.\\n\\n5) Borderline thrombocytosis (platelets 406 K/uL)\\n   - Order iron studies: serum iron, TIBC or transferrin, transferrin saturation — to assess for functional iron deficiency despite normal ferritin.\\n   - Order inflammatory marker(s): CRP and/or ESR.\\n   - Repeat CBC (including platelets) in 4–8 weeks to reassess.\\n   - If platelets remain >450 K/uL or show upward trend, arrange hematology referral.\\n   - Notify physician urgently if platelets rise >450 K/uL or patient develops thrombosis/bleeding symptoms.\\n\\n6) Additional orders and testing\\n   - Baseline serum calcium (before high‑dose vitamin D) — order now.\\n   - Repeat 25‑OH vitamin D at 8–12 weeks post‑repletion.\\n   - Serum folate recheck in 4–8 weeks.\\n   - CBC with differential in 4–8 weeks.\\n   - Iron studies (serum iron, TIBC/transferrin, transferrin saturation) and CRP/ESR — order now.\\n   - Fasting lipid panel + ApoB in 3 months.\\n   - Fasting glucose and fasting insulin (for HOMA‑IR) to confirm insulin resistance — order now.\\n   - Pregnancy test if patient becomes pregnant or plans conception — urgent physician notification if pregnancy planned due to folate deficiency.\\n   - Medication reconciliation to document OCP/HRT use and medications that interact (e.g., thiazides) — confirm and document.\\n\\n7) Referrals and education\\n   - Referral to registered dietitian/nutritionist for folate optimization, weight/lipid management, and vitamin D dietary counseling — consider scheduling.\\n   - Consider cardiology referral if ApoB remains >100–130 mg/dL despite lifestyle therapy or if additional risk factors develop.\\n   - Hematology referral if platelets >450 K/uL persist or progressive cytopenias noted.\\n   - Provide patient education on diet, exercise, supplement adherence, and signs/symptoms that require urgent contact.\\n\\n8) Follow‑up timeline\\n   - Immediate (start within 1–7 days): begin vitamin D D3 10,000 IU + K daily and folic acid 1 mg daily. Order baseline calcium now.\\n   - 1–2 weeks: ensure iron studies and CRP/ESR are ordered and review; physician to review lab results within 1–2 weeks per clinic protocol.\\n   - 4–8 weeks: repeat CBC (platelets) and recheck serum folate.\\n   - 8–12 weeks: recheck 25‑OH vitamin D; transition to maintenance dosing per physician.\\n   - 3 months: repeat fasting lipid panel and ApoB to reassess need for pharmacotherapy; review fasting insulin/glucose results and calculate HOMA‑IR.\\n   - Recheck window for routine follow‑up labs: 3–6 months as outlined above.\\n\\nPhysician notification requirements:\\n- Notify physician immediately if:\\n  - Platelets >450 K/uL or rising trend.\\n  - Patient is pregnant or planning pregnancy (folate deficiency requires obstetric/physician input).\\n  - Baseline serum calcium is elevated prior to vitamin D initiation.\\n  - Any new symptoms of thrombosis, hypercalcemia, or adverse supplement reactions.\\n- Otherwise, place results and treatment plan in chart and request routine physician review within 1–2 weeks.\\n\\nDocumentation:\\n- Results discussed with patient today. Counseling provided regarding vitamin D repletion, folic acid supplementation, lifestyle modifications for lipids and insulin resistance, and signs/symptoms requiring urgent contact. Medication/supplement plan and laboratory follow‑up schedule reviewed. Patient verbalized understanding and agreed to start supplements and obtain recommended labs. Questions answered.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\nDate: 03/10/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": true, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": true, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": true, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "preventRisk": {"riskCategory": "low", "tenYearASCVD": 0, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "PREVENT calculator is validated for ages 30-79 years. Patient is 25 years old. Use clinical judgment for risk assessment.", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0, "ageValidFor30Year": false, "tenYearHFPercentage": "N/A", "tenYearHeartFailure": 0, "statinRecommendation": "Age outside validated range - use clinical judgment", "tenYearCVDPercentage": "N/A", "tenYearASCVDPercentage": "N/A"}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 softgel daily with meal", "name": "D3 10,000 + K", "caution": "High-dose repletion therapy. Recheck vitamin D levels in 8-12 weeks. Contains vitamin K2 for calcium metabolism.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "high", "rationale": "D3 10,000 + K provides high-dose vitamin D3 with K2 for efficient repletion of severe deficiency. K2 ensures proper calcium utilization and prevents arterial calcification.", "indication": "Vitamin D 15.9 ng/mL (severe deficiency ≤20)", "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D level is very low, which can affect your bones, immune system, mood, and energy. This high-dose supplement will help bring your level up to optimal range, and the vitamin K2 helps your body use calcium properly.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 15.9 ng/mL (severe deficiency ≤20)"]}, {"dose": "1 softgel 1-2 times daily", "name": "OmegaGenics® Fish Oil Neuro 1000", "caution": "High-DHA omega-3 (750mg DHA, 250mg EPA) for brain and heart health. Lemon-flavored, no fishy taste. Contains calamari - avoid if shellfish allergy.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "OmegaGenics Fish Oil Neuro 1000 provides concentrated DHA and EPA to reduce triglycerides, support brain function, cardiovascular health, and reduce systemic inflammation.", "indication": "ApoB 100 mg/dL (elevated); LDL 127 mg/dL (elevated); HDL 37 mg/dL (low)", "phenotypes": ["Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This high-quality fish oil provides omega-3 fatty acids that help reduce inflammation, support your heart and brain health, and may help improve your cholesterol profile.", "supportingFindings": ["ApoB 100 mg/dL (elevated)", "LDL 127 mg/dL (elevated)", "HDL 37 mg/dL (low)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Healthy Weight with Akkermansia", "caution": "Contains Akkermansia muciniphila and Bifidobacterium lactis B420 for metabolic and gut barrier support. Refrigerate after opening for maximum potency.", "category": "metabolic", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "UltraFlora Healthy Weight with Akkermansia provides targeted probiotic support for the gut-metabolic axis. Akkermansia muciniphila supports gut barrier integrity, metabolic health, and healthy weight management. Indicated for insulin resistance, metabolic syndrome features, visceral adiposity, and weight loss resistance.", "indication": "Insulin resistance / visceral adiposity phenotype (gut-metabolic axis support); ApoB 100 mg/dL (atherogenic metabolic pattern)", "phenotypes": ["Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity"], "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This specialized probiotic contains Akkermansia, a beneficial gut bacteria linked to healthy metabolism and weight management. It supports your gut barrier, helps manage metabolic inflammation, and works with your body's natural weight regulation systems.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance / visceral adiposity phenotype (gut-metabolic axis support)", "ApoB 100 mg/dL (atherogenic metabolic pattern)"]}, {"dose": "1 tablet daily", "name": "Intrinsi B12-Folate™", "caution": "Contains intrinsic factor for enhanced B12 absorption. Ideal for patients with absorption concerns or vegetarian/vegan diets.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Intrinsi B12-Folate provides methylated B12 and folate with intrinsic factor for superior absorption. Supports energy, cognition, methylation, and nerve function.", "indication": "Folate 2.01 ng/mL (low)", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "Your B12 and/or folate levels need support. This supplement provides highly absorbable forms of these essential vitamins to support your energy, brain function, and nervous system health.", "supportingFindings": ["Folate 2.01 ng/mL (low)"]}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Essential for patients on statins.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "low", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "ApoB 100 mg/dL (cardiovascular support); LDL 127 mg/dL; HDL 37 mg/dL (low)", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["ApoB 100 mg/dL (cardiovascular support)", "LDL 127 mg/dL", "HDL 37 mg/dL (low)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 6, "apoBValue": 100, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "borderline", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 0, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": true}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "generally_recommended", "indicated": true, "rationale": "ApoB ≥90 mg/dL (100 mg/dL) with risk enhancers (low HDL, elevated ApoB). Statin discussion recommended."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall summary:\\nYour labs show some important areas to address, but many results are normal. Key concerns are severe vitamin D deficiency (25-OH vitamin D 15.9 ng/mL), low HDL cholesterol (37 mg/dL), and folate deficiency (2.01 ng/mL). Your 10-year cardiovascular risk is 0% (PREVENT), which means your short-term heart-attack/stroke risk is low. Other labs such as hemoglobin 14.1 g/dL, ferritin 83.8 ng/mL, TSH 1.55 mIU/L, and vitamin B12 695 pg/mL are within expected ranges.\\n\\nPositive findings:\\n- Hemoglobin 14.1 g/dL and hematocrit 42% — no current anemia.\\n- Ferritin 83.8 ng/mL — iron stores are adequate.\\n- TSH 1.55 mIU/L — thyroid function in the normal range.\\n- Kidney and liver function normal: eGFR 124 mL/min, AST 15 U/L, ALT 13 U/L.\\n- Lipoprotein(a) 6 mg/dL and triglycerides 117 mg/dL are good.\\n\\nAreas to improve (with concrete actions):\\n1) Vitamin D: 25-OH vitamin D 15.9 ng/mL (severe deficiency).\\n- Start vitamin D3 supplementation after discussing with your provider: options include 50,000 IU weekly for 8–12 weeks OR 4,000–5,000 IU daily, then recheck level in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Increase dietary calcium to 1,000–1,200 mg/day (dairy, fortified plant milk, leafy greens).\\n- Add 20–30 minutes of weight-bearing exercise 3–4 times weekly (walking, stairs, light strength training) to support bone health and reduce fracture risk.\\n- Aim for 10–20 minutes sunlight exposure on arms/legs several times per week when safe.\\n\\n2) Folate: 2.01 ng/mL (deficient).\\n- Begin folic acid 400–800 mcg daily (if pregnant or planning pregnancy, confirm optimal dose with your provider; many recommend 400–800 mcg or 1 mg depending on history).\\n- Add foods rich in folate: dark leafy greens, beans, lentils, fortified cereals.\\n- Recheck folate in 4–8 weeks after starting.\\n\\n3) Lipids: HDL 37 mg/dL (low); LDL 127 mg/dL; apolipoprotein B 100 mg/dL.\\n- Add 25–30 g fiber daily: oatmeal for breakfast, beans for lunch, vegetables at dinner.\\n- Include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n- Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter and high-fat cheeses.\\n- Daily exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking or equivalent.\\n- Repeat a fasting lipid panel in 3–6 months. Discuss the borderline ApoB/LDL with your clinician if family history or other concerns; statin decision should use clinical judgment.\\n\\nOther notes and next steps:\\n- Platelets 406 K/uL (mildly high). This can be reactive; repeat CBC in 6–12 weeks. Seek care sooner if you notice unusual bruising or bleeding.\\n- No sex hormones were measured; if you are evaluating menstrual symptoms, testing timing matters (we can plan tests by cycle phase).\\n- Please review any supplements/medications with your provider before starting high-dose vitamin D or folic acid.\\n\\nFollow-up timeline: start supplements/diet changes now, recheck vitamin D and folate in 8–12 weeks, repeat lipids in 3–6 months, and repeat CBC in 6–12 weeks. If you want, I can draft a discussion note for your provider to review these plans.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 14.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 42, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 406, "status": "borderline", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Mild platelet elevation (400-450). Commonly reactive to iron deficiency, inflammation, or infection.", "recommendation": "Check iron studies and inflammatory markers. Repeat CBC in 4-8 weeks.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.55, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 83.8, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 15.9, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Severe Vitamin D deficiency (≤20 ng/mL). Associated with fatigue, bone loss, and immune dysfunction.", "recommendation": "High-dose repletion: Metagenics D3 10,000 + K daily for 8-12 weeks. Recheck levels after repletion.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 695, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 127, "status": "borderline", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Near optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet and exercise.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 37, "status": "abnormal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Low HDL - increased cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Increase aerobic exercise. Consider niacin if very low.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 117, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: N/A (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "PREVENT calculator is validated for ages 30-79 years. Patient is 25 years old. Use clinical judgment for risk assessment.", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): N/A", "recommendation": "Age outside validated range - use clinical judgment", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: N/A", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 15, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 13, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 124, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.67, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 100, "status": "borderline", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Borderline elevated Apo B.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider pharmacotherapy if high ASCVD risk.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 6, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 2.01, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate deficiency.", "recommendation": "Folate supplementation. Critical if planning pregnancy.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "of 6 markers positive", "value": 2, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Insulin Resistance Screening", "recheckTiming": "3-6 months", "interpretation": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive). This combination points toward hyperinsulinemia physiology. Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR).", "recommendation": "Phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance. Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose.", "referenceRange": "0-1 Low, 2 Moderate, 3+ High Likelihood"}], "aiRecommendations": "1) Overall clinical assessment (summary of key abnormalities)\\n- Platelets 406 K/uL (borderline thrombocytosis; lab cutoff per protocol 400–450 K/uL for mild elevation). Possible reactive etiology (iron deficiency, inflammation, infection); ferritin is reported normal but further iron indices recommended to exclude functional iron deficiency.\\n- 25‑OH Vitamin D 15.9 ng/mL — severe deficiency (≤20 ng/mL). Action required per repletion protocol.\\n- Folate 2.01 ng/mL — low/deficient; clinically important for fertility/pregnancy planning.\\n- Lipids: LDL 127 mg/dL (near‑optimal/ borderline), HDL 37 mg/dL (low), ApoB 100 mg/dL (borderline). ASCVD 10‑yr risk reported normal; pharmacotherapy not mandatory now but risk mitigation indicated.\\n- Other labs within reference range (Hgb, Hct, ferritin, B12, TSH, AST/ALT, eGFR, Lp(a) etc.).\\n\\n2) Priority actions — organized CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE\\n\\nCRITICAL (needs physician notification immediately)\\n- None of the current values require immediate emergency action. HOWEVER notify the physician urgently if any of the following apply:\\n  - Platelet count increases to >450 K/uL or there are symptoms of thrombosis/bleeding.\\n  - Patient is pregnant or actively trying to conceive and folate deficiency confirmed (immediate clinician review for higher‑dose folate).\\n  - History of hypercalcemia, granulomatous disease, lymphoma, or severe renal impairment before starting high‑dose vitamin D.\\n\\nURGENT (action within 1–2 weeks)\\n- Start vitamin D repletion now:\\n  - Metagenics D3 10,000 IU + K oral daily for 8–12 weeks (per clinic protocol).\\n  - Order baseline serum calcium prior to starting high‑dose vitamin D (patient eGFR normal but check calcium to screen for hypercalcemia).\\n  - Document medication start and counsel patient on adherence and signs of hypercalcemia (nausea, polyuria, confusion) to report.\\n  - Notify physician if patient is pregnant, breastfeeding, or on interacting meds (e.g., thiazides).\\n\\n- Start folate replacement now:\\n  - Folate (folic acid) 1 mg PO daily (standard replacement dose). If patient is pregnant or has a history of neural tube defect: discuss with physician & consider 4 mg PO daily per obstetric guidance.\\n  - Recheck serum folate in 4–8 weeks.\\n\\nROUTINE (action within 4–12 weeks)\\n- CBC and iron/inflammatory evaluation:\\n  - Order iron studies: serum iron, TIBC or transferrin, transferrin saturation; confirm ferritin already normal but include to evaluate for functional iron deficiency.\\n  - Order inflammatory marker(s): CRP and/or ESR per protocol.\\n  - Repeat CBC (including platelets) in 4–8 weeks to reassess reactive thrombocytosis.\\n  - If platelets remain >450 K/uL or trending up, arrange hematology referral.\\n\\n- Lipid management:\\n  - Reinforce lifestyle therapy now; repeat fasting lipid panel (including ApoB if previously measured fasting) in 3 months after lifestyle intervention.\\n  - Escalate to pharmacotherapy only if LDL or ApoB remain elevated or if clinical risk increases — discuss with physician if ApoB >100 persistently or LDL ≥160 or other risk enhancers.\\n\\n3) Testosterone dose recommendations (if applicable)\\n- No testosterone lab values or current testosterone therapy listed → no dose recommendations indicated.\\n- If patient is on testosterone therapy, staff actions:\\n  - Obtain total testosterone (and SHBG/free T if available) timed per protocol (mid‑cycle or trough/peak depending on route). Note: interpret female androgen labs in relation to menstrual cycle phase; document cycle day.\\n  - Monitor hematocrit, lipids, LFTs at baseline and per protocol. Notify physician if hematocrit rises >52% or if notable lipid worsening.\\n  - If clinician requests adjustments, provide measured testosterone concentration and clinical symptoms for dosing decisions.\\n\\n4) Lifestyle interventions to recommend to patient (staff to counsel)\\n- Vitamin D:\\n  - Encourage safe sun exposure (brief, face/arms) and dietary sources (oily fish, fortified dairy) in addition to repletion therapy.\\n- Folate:\\n  - Increase dietary folate: leafy greens, legumes, citrus, fortified grains while on supplementation.\\n  - If pregnancy is planned: emphasize preconception folic acid use and early obstetric referral.\\n- Lipids / Cardiovascular:\\n  - Aerobic exercise ≥150 min/week moderate intensity plus resistance training to raise HDL.\\n  - Dietary counseling: Mediterranean-style diet, reduce refined carbs and trans fats, increase omega‑3 sources.\\n  - Smoking cessation (if applicable) — counseling and pharmacotherapy referral as needed.\\n  - Weight loss targets if BMI elevated (5–10% weight loss improves HDL/LDL).\\n- General:\\n  - Review medication adherence, alcohol moderation, and sleep hygiene.\\n\\n5) Additional testing or referrals to consider\\n- Lab tests to order:\\n  - Serum calcium (baseline) prior to high‑dose vitamin D.\\n  - Repeat 25‑OH vitamin D after completion of repletion (recheck at 8–12 weeks post‑start).\\n  - CBC + iron studies (serum iron, TIBC/transferrin, transferrin saturation) and CRP/ESR — order now and repeat CBC in 4–8 weeks.\\n  - Repeat serum folate in 4–8 weeks.\\n  - Fasting lipid panel + ApoB in 3 months after lifestyle measures.\\n  - If on HRT/OCP or suspected hormonal therapy interactions: obtain medication reconciliation and consider coagulation risk assessment if clinically indicated.\\n- Referrals:\\n  - Dietitian/nutritionist for folate, weight/lipid management, and vitamin D dietary counseling.\\n  - Cardiology if persistent ApoB >100–130 mg/dL or if multiple risk factors arise.\\n  - Hematology if platelets >450 K/uL persist, progressive thrombocytosis, or other cytopenias.\\n  - OB/Gyn or reproductive endocrinology if patient is planning pregnancy or infertile — urgent if folate deficiency and conception planned.\\n\\n6) Summary of next steps and timeline (action checklist for staff)\\n- Immediate (start within 1–7 days):\\n  - Start Metagenics D3 10,000 IU + K PO daily for 8–12 weeks; order baseline serum calcium.\\n  - Start folic acid 1 mg PO daily (discuss higher dose with physician if pregnant/planning pregnancy).\\n  - Document counseling points and contraindications; flag chart for physician review if pregnancy or comorbidities.\\n\\n- 1–2 weeks:\\n  - Ensure iron studies and inflammatory markers ordered (if not already) and reviewed.\\n  - Confirm medication list (OCPs/HRT) and assess for interaction/thrombosis risk.\\n\\n- 4–8 weeks:\\n  - Repeat CBC (platelets) and recheck serum folate.\\n  - Review iron studies/CRP results and notify physician if abnormalities or if platelets trending upward.\\n\\n- 8–12 weeks:\\n  - Recheck 25‑OH vitamin D level; transition to maintenance vitamin D dose per physician direction (commonly 1,000–2,000 IU daily or individualized).\\n\\n- 3 months:\\n  - Repeat fasting lipid panel and ApoB; reassess need for pharmacotherapy and consider cardiology referral if persistent abnormalities.\\n\\nPhysician notification requirements (explicit)\\n- Notify physician immediately if:\\n  - Platelets >450 K/uL or rising trend on repeat CBC.\\n  - Patient is pregnant or planning pregnancy (folate deficiency requires physician/OBGYN input).\\n  - Baseline calcium elevated prior to vitamin D repletion.\\n  - Any new symptoms suggesting thrombosis, hypercalcemia, or adverse reaction to supplements.\\n- Otherwise, place results and treatment plan in chart and request routine physician review within 1–2 weeks.\\n\\nNotes for staff interpretation\\n- Consider menstrual cycle phase when correlating any hormone assays (none supplied here) — document cycle day if future hormones ordered.\\n- HRT/OCPs can influence lipids, folate status, and coagulation risk—confirm current hormone therapy and factor into management plan.\\n- Do not make definitive diagnoses in chart notes; present findings and recommended protocolized actions for physician sign‑off.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 3.16, "detail": "Triglycerides 117 / HDL 37 = 3.16", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 13, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "100 mg/dL", "detail": "Elevated atherogenic particle count", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}], "likelihood": "moderate", "phenotypes": [{"key": "visceral_metabolic", "name": "Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Recheck A1c, lipid panel, hs-CRP, ApoB in 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL"], "pathophysiology": "Early peripheral + hepatic insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia. Glucose may still be \\"normal.\\" A1c often rises last.", "triggerCriteria": ["A1c >= 5.7%", "TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0", "Central adiposity phenotype", "ALT above optimal range"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests your body may not be responding to insulin as efficiently as it should. Insulin is the hormone that helps move sugar from your bloodstream into your cells for energy.\\n\\nWhen cells become less sensitive to insulin:\\n- Blood sugar slowly rises over time\\n- Triglycerides increase\\n- Inflammation increases\\n- Weight gain around the midsection becomes easier\\n\\nThe good news? This stage is highly reversible with targeted nutrition, strength training, sleep optimization, and - when appropriate - medication support.\\n\\nWe'll focus on improving how your body processes fuel and reducing long-term cardiometabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward nutrition (>= 25-35g/meal)", "Carbohydrate quality + timing education", "Strength training 2-3x weekly", "10-15 min post-meal walking", "Sleep optimization", "Consider: GLP-1 therapy if obesity, appetite dysregulation, A1c >= 5.7, or inflammatory phenotype", "Consider: Metformin if prediabetes or strong family history", "Recheck metabolic panel in 3-6 months"]}], "positiveCount": 2, "likelihoodLabel": "MODERATE LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive). This combination points toward hyperinsulinemia physiology. Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR).", "confirmationTests": "Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose."}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Metabolic pattern consistent with insulin resistance and/or visceral adiposity. This drives inflammation, hormonal imbalance, and cardiovascular risk.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance screening: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive)", "TG:HDL ratio 3.2 (≥3.0)", "HDL 37 mg/dL (low)", "ApoB 100 mg/dL (elevated)"]}]}	\N	2026-03-10 21:41:12.3686	2026-03-10 21:41:12.3686
