ary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"lpaWarning": "Elevated Lp(a) of 130 mg/dL is a genetic cardiovascular risk marker. This level is associated with increased ASCVD risk independent of LDL. Since Lp(a) cannot be significantly lowered, focus on aggressive LDL/ApoB reduction and lifestyle optimization.", "statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "strongly_recommend", "rationale": "Elevated Lp(a) of 130 mg/dL is a genetic risk marker. CAC strongly recommended to assess arterial calcium burden and inform treatment intensity.", "recommended": true}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Dear Patient,\\n\\nWe have reviewed your recent lab results from your men's health panel. Your labs help us keep your testosterone therapy safe and effective while monitoring your overall health.\\n\\nYour Results:\\nWe've carefully reviewed all your lab values including blood counts, hormone levels, cholesterol, liver and kidney function, and other important markers.\\n\\nNext Steps:\\nYour healthcare provider will review these results and contact you if any changes to your treatment plan are needed. We monitor these labs to ensure optimal testosterone levels while keeping your blood counts, cholesterol, liver, kidney, and prostate health in the safest range possible.\\n\\nIf you have any questions about your results, please don't hesitate to contact our clinic.\\n\\nThank you for trusting us with your care.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 12.7, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 39.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 345, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 27, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol below bone protection threshold (<40 pg/mL). Bone resorption increases below 40-60 pg/mL.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase estrogen dose. Target 60-100 pg/mL. Current level inadequate for bone protection.", "referenceRange": "60-100 pg/mL (HRT goal, >40 for bone)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.23, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone below HRT therapeutic range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase progesterone dose. Target 8-10 ng/mL for optimal endometrial protection.", "referenceRange": "8-10 ng/mL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 65.9, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 11.4, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Low testosterone despite HRT.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase testosterone dose. Target 75-125 ng/dL for optimal results.", "referenceRange": "75-125 ng/dL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.01, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 65.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 52.6, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D adequate but suboptimal (41-59 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 2000 Complex daily to reach optimal range ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 2000, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "Elevated B12.", "recommendation": "Usually from supplementation. Rarely indicates liver disease.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 91, "status": "normal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 66, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 54, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 19, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 18, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 79, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.89, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 76, "status": "normal", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Apo B at optimal level.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 130, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Elevated Lp(a) - RISK ENHANCER. Increases CVD risk category.", "recommendation": "Lp(a) ≥50 mg/dL is a risk enhancer - consider upgrading CVD risk category. Aggressive LDL lowering indicated. Discuss hereditary nature.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 241, "status": "borderline", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "Low TIBC - possible iron overload or chronic disease.", "recommendation": "Evaluate for hemochromatosis or chronic inflammation.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 71, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 10.6, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 4.5, "status": "abnormal", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Elevated hs-CRP - increased cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Address cardiovascular risk factors. Consider repeat testing.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.23, "status": "borderline", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Low Progesterone (Phase Unknown)", "interpretation": "Progesterone is 0.23 ng/mL. Without cycle phase documentation, interpretation is limited. If drawn in luteal phase, this would indicate anovulation - a perimenopause marker.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Document cycle phase for accurate interpretation. If patient is mid-cycle or luteal, this suggests anovulation. Consider symptoms: sleep disruption, anxiety, irregular cycles. PATIENT EDUCATION: We need to know where you are in your menstrual cycle to fully interpret this result. Please note when your last period started.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase; <5 ng/mL suggests anovulation if luteal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 27, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Hypoestrogenic Pattern", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 27 pg/mL with FSH of 65.9 mIU/mL indicates hypoestrogenic perimenopause - the \\"crash phase.\\" Estrogen production has dropped significantly and the brain is signaling strongly (elevated FSH) trying to stimulate the ovaries.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Transdermal estradiol 0.025-0.05 mg is appropriate replacement therapy. Add micronized progesterone for uterine protection and sleep support. Frame this as hormone replacement, not stimulation - we are restoring what the body needs. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen has dropped to a level that can cause symptoms like hot flashes, night sweats, brain fog, and mood changes. Replacing estrogen with a patch or gel can relieve these symptoms and protect your long-term health. Progesterone is added to protect your uterus and help with sleep.", "referenceRange": "E2 <50 with FSH >10 indicates estrogen deficiency"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 27, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Hypoestrogen State", "interpretation": "Low estradiol (27 pg/mL) with symptomatic presentation: night sweats, joint aches. This pattern is consistent with a low estrogen state, especially in late perimenopause or postmenopause.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Consider transdermal estradiol initiation (product dependent). Add micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly if patient has uterus. Monitor symptoms and estradiol levels. PATIENT EDUCATION: Low estrogen can cause the symptoms you're experiencing. Hormone replacement therapy (HRT) with estradiol can help relieve these symptoms and protect bone and cardiovascular health.", "referenceRange": "Provider target: 60-100 pg/mL (>40 minimum)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 65.9, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Menopause", "interpretation": "FSH of 65.9 mIU/mL with low estradiol (27 pg/mL) confirms menopausal status. Symptoms present: night sweats. Elevated FSH reflects decreased ovarian function and reduced estrogen production. This is a normal part of reproductive aging but may benefit from hormone optimization if symptomatic.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: If symptomatic, consider hormone therapy (HT): transdermal estradiol + micronized progesterone (if uterus intact). Discuss benefits/risks of HT including cardiovascular, bone, and quality of life considerations. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your lab results confirm you are in menopause. Hormone therapy can help manage symptoms like hot flashes, sleep problems, and vaginal dryness while also protecting bone health.", "referenceRange": "FSH <25 mIU/mL premenopausal; ≥25 suggests transition; ≥40 menopausal"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 61.6, "status": "normal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 61.6 nmol/L is within normal range. SHBG regulates bioavailable sex hormones by binding testosterone and estradiol.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 1 pg/mL is within normal functional range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (1/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}], "aiRecommendations": "Unable to generate recommendations. Please review lab findings manually.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 0.82, "detail": "Triglycerides 54 / HDL 66 = 0.82", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 61.6, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 18, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "76 mg/dL", "detail": "Normal atherogenic particle count", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "4.5 mg/L", "detail": "Elevated systemic inflammation", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 1, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (1/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Inflammatory Burden", "confidence": "high", "description": "Pattern of systemic inflammation indicated by elevated inflammatory markers and/or symptomatic presentation. May benefit from anti-inflammatory nutritional support.", "supportingFindings": ["hs-CRP 4.5 mg/L (elevated >3.0)", "Joint aches reported"]}, {"name": "Menopausal Transition", "confidence": "high", "description": "Clinical picture consistent with perimenopause or menopause. Hormonal shifts during this transition affect bone health, cardiovascular risk, mood, and quality of life.", "supportingFindings": ["Age 48 (perimenopause window)", "FSH 65.9 mIU/mL (elevated, menopausal range)", "Estradiol 27 pg/mL (declining)", "Night sweats reported", "Irritability reported"]}]}	\N	2026-03-10 21:58:50.766551	2026-03-10 21:58:50.766551
12	5	2026-03-10 22:04:50.018	{"alt": 16, "ast": 20, "bun": 18, "co2": 24, "fsh": 10.5, "rbc": 3.67, "tsh": 1.69, "wbc": 6.2, "acne": false, "egfr": 95, "iron": 45, "shbg": 138, "tibc": 223, "onHRT": true, "folate": 20, "sodium": 138, "albumin": 4.6, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 9.4, "glucose": 87, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "chloride": 103, "ferritin": 149, "hairLoss": false, "bilirubin": 0.4, "estradiol": 104, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": false, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 318, "potassium": 4.6, "creatinine": 0.77, "hematocrit": 37.9, "hemoglobin": 12.6, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "vitaminB12": 803, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "moodChanges": true, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Michelle Reef", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 48, "bmi": 21.4, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": true, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 141, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 1.48, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 54.9, "totalProtein": 6.7, "lowMotivation": false, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "freeTestosterone": 3}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History of present illness: 48-year-old female presenting for review of recent hormone and routine laboratory results. Patient reports symptoms consistent with perimenopause including irregular menses, intermittent hot flashes, sleep disturbance, mood lability, bloating and breast tenderness, and decreased libido. She denies chest pain, shortness of breath, syncope, or focal neurological symptoms. No reported new medications since last visit. No history of liver disease. (Medication list to be reviewed in clinic to assess for oral estrogen or other agents that raise SHBG.)\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\nNo vital signs available in chart today.\\n\\nLaboratory results dated 03/10/2026 (grouped):\\n\\nCBC\\n- Hemoglobin 12.6 g/dL (normal)\\n- Hematocrit 37.9 % (normal)\\n- Platelets 318 K/uL (normal)\\n\\nIron studies\\n- Serum iron 45 ug/dL (normal)\\n- Ferritin 149 ng/mL (normal)\\n- TIBC 223 ug/dL [BORDERLINE low] — possible low TIBC\\n- Calculated transferrin saturation ≈ 20% (within expected range)\\n\\nMetabolic / Renal / Hepatic\\n- AST 20 U/L (normal)\\n- ALT 16 U/L (normal)\\n- Creatinine 0.77 mg/dL (normal)\\n- eGFR 95 mL/min (normal)\\n\\nThyroid\\n- TSH 1.69 mIU/L (normal)\\n\\nVitamins / Nutritional\\n- Vitamin B12 803 pg/mL (normal)\\n- Folate 20 ng/mL (normal)\\n\\nHormones / Reproductive\\n- Estradiol (E2) 104 pg/mL [BORDERLINE high — above HRT optimization goal 60–100 pg/mL]\\n- Progesterone 1.48 ng/mL [ABNORMAL — below HRT therapeutic range; low for luteal phase]\\n  - Perimenopause assessments flagged: Relative progesterone deficiency; Luteal progesterone deficiency; Low progesterone (phase unknown)\\n- FSH 10.5 mIU/mL (normal/borderline for age; consistent with early perimenopause)\\n- SHBG 138 nmol/L [ABNORMAL — markedly elevated]\\n- Testosterone, total 54.9 ng/dL [BORDERLINE low — below HRT optimal goal 75–125 ng/dL]\\n- Free testosterone 3 pg/mL (normal)\\n\\nOther\\n- Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG) 1 point (low risk)\\n\\nFlags:\\n- Red flags: None identified.\\n- Abnormal/critical: Progesterone 1.48 ng/mL [ABNORMAL], SHBG 138 nmol/L [ABNORMAL].\\n- Borderline: Estradiol 104 pg/mL, Testosterone (total) 54.9 ng/dL, TIBC 223 ug/dL, FSH 10.5 mIU/mL (perimenopause assessment).\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1) Relative and luteal progesterone deficiency consistent with perimenopause.\\n   - Rationale: Progesterone 1.48 ng/mL (below therapeutic range and low for expected luteal phase). Estradiol 104 pg/mL not adequately opposed by progesterone — creates relative estrogen dominance. FSH 10.5 mIU/mL in a 48-year-old supports early perimenopausal transition.\\n   - Clinical significance: Explains patient's irregular menses, breast tenderness, bloating, mood changes.\\n\\n2) Elevated SHBG (138 nmol/L) with reduced total testosterone.\\n   - Rationale: SHBG markedly elevated; total testosterone 54.9 ng/dL (below target). Free testosterone 3 pg/mL is within normal range but high SHBG reduces bioavailable androgen activity. Common causes include oral estrogen therapy, oral contraceptives, thyroid medications, and liver disease. TSH and LFTs are normal, making endogenous thyroid or hepatic causes less likely; medication review required.\\n   - Clinical significance: May contribute to decreased libido, low energy, and other androgen-deficiency symptoms.\\n\\n3) Estradiol slightly above HRT optimization goal.\\n   - Rationale: E2 104 pg/mL (borderline high relative to clinic optimization 60–100 pg/mL). If patient is on exogenous estrogen, dose or route (oral vs transdermal) should be reviewed.\\n   - Clinical significance: When unopposed by adequate progesterone, higher estradiol may exacerbate estrogen-dominant symptoms and abnormal uterine bleeding.\\n\\n4) Low/low-normal iron binding capacity (TIBC 223 ug/dL).\\n   - Rationale: Borderline low TIBC with ferritin 149 ng/mL and serum iron 45 ug/dL; calculated transferrin saturation ~20% — overall iron indices are not consistent with iron overload. Low TIBC can be seen with chronic inflammation or other non-iron-related causes.\\n   - Clinical significance: Low immediate concern; repeat/further evaluation indicated if clinical suspicion for iron disorder or inflammatory state.\\n\\n5) Preventive/other labs within expected ranges.\\n   - Hemoglobin, platelets, renal function, LFTs, TSH, B12, folate are within normal limits. No acute red flags identified.\\n\\nPLAN:\\nGeneral:\\n- Results reviewed with patient today. Discussed interpretation, implications, and management options for perimenopause-related hormone changes and elevated SHBG. Plan individualized treatment pending medication reconciliation and patient preference. Follow-up arranged.\\n\\n1) Progesterone deficiency / perimenopause management (Problem #1)\\n   - Offer trial of progesterone to oppose estradiol and treat luteal deficiency. Recommended options discussed:\\n     - Oral micronized progesterone 200 mg PO qHS for 14 days per cycle (days 14–28) if patient has intermittent menses and prefers cyclic therapy OR\\n     - If cycles are irregular or patient is amenorrheic and desires endometrial protection, consider 200 mg PO qHS for 12–14 days monthly or continuous nightly 100–200 mg at provider discretion.\\n   - If patient prefers non-oral route, discuss transdermal/progestin-containing IUD or other progestin options; refer to provider to discuss risks/benefits and formulate prescription.\\n   - Recheck progesterone level timed to mid-luteal phase (7 days before expected menses) if cycling, or re-evaluate after 3 months if on therapy to assess symptomatic and laboratory response.\\n   - Educate patient re: common side effects (sedation at night, breast tenderness, spotting) and to report severe adverse effects.\\n\\n2) Elevated SHBG and low total testosterone (Problem #2)\\n   - Obtain detailed medication list to identify oral estrogen, oral contraceptives, thyroid agents, or other agents that may elevate SHBG. If patient is taking oral estrogen, recommend switching to transdermal estradiol (patch or gel) which has less effect on SHBG — discuss transition with prescribing clinician.\\n   - If symptoms of androgen deficiency (low libido, low energy) persist after addressing SHBG and progesterone, consider evaluation for testosterone therapy. Recommended baseline testing prior to initiation: fasting lipid panel, LFTs, fasting glucose/HbA1c, CBC. If therapy pursued, begin at low physiologic dose under supervision and recheck total and free testosterone and symptom assessment in 3 months.\\n   - Referral to prescribing clinician for discussion of testosterone risks/benefits and prescribing if appropriate.\\n\\n3) Estradiol slightly above optimization goal (Problem #3)\\n   - Review exogenous estrogen use. If patient is on estrogen therapy and asymptomatic, consider modest dose reduction or change to transdermal route to reduce SHBG effect and lower systemic hepatic impact.\\n   - If patient is not on estrogen therapy, elevated endogenous estradiol with low progesterone supports relative estrogen dominance — management per item #1 (progesterone replacement) recommended.\\n   - Recheck estradiol in 3 months after therapeutic changes to confirm optimization (goal 60–100 pg/mL per clinic parameters).\\n\\n4) Low TIBC (Problem #4)\\n   - Repeat iron studies in 3 months: serum iron, TIBC, ferritin, and consider CRP if concern for inflammation.\\n   - No immediate treatment indicated given ferritin within normal range and transferrin saturation ~20%. If clinical concern for hemochromatosis, order HFE mutation testing (C282Y/H63D) and repeat iron indices; however currently not indicated.\\n   - Continue surveillance; evaluate for clinical signs of chronic inflammatory conditions if present.\\n\\n5) Supplements / supportive therapy\\n   - Continue EstroFactors™ 2 tablets twice daily as medium-priority supplement (per clinic recommendation). Counsel on expected benefits and to report any adverse effects.\\n   - Recommend lifestyle measures: regular aerobic and resistance exercise, balanced diet with adequate calcium and vitamin D, sleep hygiene, stress reduction, limit alcohol and tobacco cessation counseling as applicable.\\n\\n6) Monitoring and follow-up labs / visits\\n   - If initiating progesterone: re-evaluate symptoms and adverse effects at 6–8 weeks; repeat progesterone/estradiol testing timed to mid-luteal phase if cycling or at steady state after 3 months if on continuous therapy.\\n   - If changing estrogen route or dose: recheck SHBG and estradiol in 3 months.\\n   - If considering testosterone therapy: baseline fasting lipid panel, LFTs, fasting glucose or HbA1c, and repeat testosterone levels at 3 months after initiation.\\n   - Repeat iron studies (iron, TIBC, ferritin) in 3–6 months.\\n   - Routine follow-up appointment for hormone management in 3–6 months or sooner if symptoms worsen or side effects occur.\\n\\n7) Safety / red flags\\n   - No acute red flags identified on labs. Patient instructed to seek urgent care for chest pain, sudden shortness of breath, severe calf pain or swelling, severe headache or visual changes, or other concerning symptoms while on hormone therapy.\\n\\nAdministrative\\n- Medication reconciliation requested to confirm current estrogen/thyroid or hepatic medications (may explain elevated SHBG). Provider to review med list and authorize prescription changes.\\n- Document patient education provided regarding risks/benefits of hormone therapies, expected course of perimenopause, and monitoring plan.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\nDate: 03/10/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "supplements": [{"dose": "2 tablets twice daily", "name": "EstroFactors™", "caution": "Nutritional support for estrogen balance and metabolism. Contains I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Supports healthy estrogen metabolite ratios.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "EstroFactors provides targeted nutritional support for estrogen metabolism and balance with I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Best suited for women with estrogen dominance patterns, PMS, perimenopausal hormone imbalance, or those needing broader estrogen metabolism support during hormonal transitions. Differs from Estrovera in that it addresses the underlying estrogen metabolism rather than vasomotor symptom relief.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected; Mood changes reported; Age-appropriate for estrogen metabolism support", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement helps your body process and balance estrogen more effectively. It supports healthy hormone metabolism, which can help with PMS symptoms, bloating, mood changes, and hormonal balance during life transitions.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected", "Mood changes reported", "Age-appropriate for estrogen metabolism support"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\n- Your labs show early perimenopause with a specific imbalance between estrogen and progesterone. Estradiol (estrogen) is 104 pg/mL while progesterone is low at 1.48 ng/mL. Your FSH is 10.5 mIU/mL, which fits early perimenopause for age 48. Sex hormone binding globulin (SHBG) is high at 138 nmol/L, which lowers the amount of free sex hormones your body can use. Other labs (blood counts, liver, kidney, B12, ferritin) look reassuring.\\n\\nPositive findings (what’s good)\\n- Hemoglobin 12.6 g/dL and hematocrit 37.9% — no anemia.\\n- Ferritin 149 ng/mL — good iron stores.\\n- TSH 1.69 mIU/L — thyroid function in the normal range.\\n- AST 20 U/L and ALT 16 U/L — liver enzymes normal.\\n- eGFR 95 mL/min and creatinine 0.77 mg/dL — kidney function normal.\\n- Vitamin B12 803 pg/mL and folate 20 ng/mL — good status.\\n- STOP-BANG 1/8 — low sleep apnea risk.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + actions)\\n1) Progesterone and perimenopause pattern\\n   - Progesterone 1.48 ng/mL with estradiol 104 pg/mL suggests relative progesterone deficiency and possible luteal phase deficiency (anovulatory cycles are common in perimenopause).\\n   - Actions: Track menstrual cycles and note bleeding and symptoms for 2–3 months. If you have hot flashes, sleep trouble, heavy or irregular bleeding, discuss with your provider the option of luteal-phase/progesterone therapy (micronized oral progesterone or cyclic progesterone cream) or other HRT options. If you start any progesterone, arrange follow-up in 4–8 weeks.\\n\\n2) Elevated SHBG — 138 nmol/L\\n   - High SHBG reduces bioavailable estrogen and testosterone.\\n   - Actions: Review current medications with your provider (especially oral estrogen or birth control pills — if you take oral estrogen, ask about switching to transdermal estrogen patches to lower SHBG). Also review alcohol use and liver-affecting supplements; your AST/ALT are normal, but medication review is important.\\n\\n3) Testosterone lower than HRT optimization\\n   - Total testosterone 54.9 ng/dL (target often 75–125 ng/dL for HRT optimization); free testosterone 3 pg/mL.\\n   - Actions: Prioritize 7–8 hours quality sleep nightly, add strength training 2–3x per week, and use stress-management (10–20 minutes daily of walking, meditation, or yoga). Discuss with your provider whether testosterone testing/treatment is appropriate after addressing SHBG and progesterone.\\n\\nOther iron notes\\n- Serum iron 45 µg/dL and TIBC 223 µg/dL (transferrin saturation ≈ 20%). Ferritin 149 ng/mL. These do not suggest iron deficiency; do not start extra iron supplements without provider guidance. If there are concerns about inflammation or iron overload, your provider may order further tests.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Track cycles and symptoms for 2–3 months. Repeat mid-luteal progesterone (ideally ~7 days before the next expected period) if you are still cycling, or repeat hormones in 3–6 months if cycles are irregular. Review medications (esp. oral estrogen/OCPs) now. Schedule follow-up with your clinician in 4–8 weeks after any treatment change. If you have heavy bleeding, severe hot flashes, or sleep disruption, contact the clinic sooner.\\n\\nYou’re doing well in many areas — these steps can help manage perimenopause symptoms and protect long-term health.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 12.6, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 37.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 318, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 104, "status": "borderline", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol above HRT optimization goal (60-100 pg/mL).", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Consider reducing estrogen dose slightly if no symptoms warrant higher levels.", "referenceRange": "60-100 pg/mL (HRT goal, >40 for bone)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1.48, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone below HRT therapeutic range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase progesterone dose. Target 8-10 ng/mL for optimal endometrial protection.", "referenceRange": "8-10 ng/mL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 10.5, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 54.9, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone below optimal HRT goal (75-125 ng/dL).", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Consider increasing testosterone dose for optimized results if symptomatic.", "referenceRange": "75-125 ng/dL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.69, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 149, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 803, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 20, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 16, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 95, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.77, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 223, "status": "borderline", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "Low TIBC - possible iron overload or chronic disease.", "recommendation": "Evaluate for hemochromatosis or chronic inflammation.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 45, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 20, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1.48, "status": "borderline", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Low Progesterone (Phase Unknown)", "interpretation": "Progesterone is 1.48 ng/mL. Without cycle phase documentation, interpretation is limited. If drawn in luteal phase, this would indicate anovulation - a perimenopause marker.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Document cycle phase for accurate interpretation. If patient is mid-cycle or luteal, this suggests anovulation. Consider symptoms: sleep disruption, anxiety, irregular cycles. PATIENT EDUCATION: We need to know where you are in your menstrual cycle to fully interpret this result. Please note when your last period started.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase; <5 ng/mL suggests anovulation if luteal"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1.48, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Relative Progesterone Deficiency", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 104 pg/mL with progesterone of only 1.48 ng/mL indicates relative progesterone deficiency. The estrogen is not being adequately opposed by progesterone, which can lead to estrogen dominance symptoms.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Consider progesterone supplementation to balance the E2:P4 ratio. Micronized progesterone 100-200 mg nightly can provide adequate opposition. If patient has symptoms (insomnia, anxiety, heavy bleeding, breast tenderness), progesterone should help. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen level is good, but your progesterone is not keeping up. This imbalance can cause sleep problems, mood changes, and heavy periods. Progesterone supplementation can help restore balance.", "referenceRange": "E2 >100 should have P4 ≥8 for adequate opposition"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1.48, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Luteal Progesterone Deficiency", "interpretation": "Progesterone of 1.48 ng/mL with estradiol of 104 pg/mL in a woman 48 years old indicates luteal progesterone deficiency. Estrogen production is adequate but progesterone is not keeping pace - a hallmark of perimenopause.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Micronized progesterone 100-200 mg nightly. Dosing can be cyclic (days 14-28) or continuous depending on cycle regularity. Progesterone provides GABAergic support for sleep and anxiety relief. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your body is making enough estrogen, but progesterone has dropped. This is very common in perimenopause and causes sleep disruption, anxiety, and mood changes. Progesterone supplementation supports your nervous system and helps restore calm, restful sleep.", "referenceRange": "P4 ≥5 ng/mL expected with E2 ≥60 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 10.5, "status": "borderline", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Early Perimenopause", "interpretation": "FSH of 10.5 mIU/mL with progesterone of 1.48 ng/mL in a 48-year-old indicates early perimenopause. The brain is starting to signal harder (rising FSH) because ovarian response is becoming unpredictable. This is the beginning of the transition.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Progesterone is foundational at this stage: micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly for sleep and nervous system support. Begin the hormone continuity discussion - frame HRT as future-forward planning, not crisis intervention. Nervous system calming messaging matters. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your body is entering the perimenopause transition. Your brain is working a little harder to communicate with your ovaries, and ovulation is becoming less consistent. This can cause sleep changes, anxiety, and cycle irregularities. Progesterone support now can smooth this transition and protect your quality of life.", "referenceRange": "FSH 8-15 with P4 <5 indicates early transition"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 138, "status": "abnormal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 138 nmol/L is elevated. High SHBG reduces bioavailable testosterone and estrogen. Common causes: oral estrogen, oral contraceptives, thyroid medication, liver conditions.", "recommendation": "Evaluate SHBG drivers. Consider ordering Free Testosterone and Bioavailable Testosterone for complete androgen assessment.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 3, "status": "normal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 3 pg/mL is within normal functional range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (1/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}], "aiRecommendations": "Unable to generate recommendations. Please review lab findings manually.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "SHBG", "value": 138, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 16, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Lab pattern consistent with relative estrogen excess or impaired estrogen clearance. Even without reported symptoms, supporting liver detoxification and estrogen metabolism pathways may be beneficial.", "supportingFindings": ["Estradiol:Progesterone imbalance (E2 104, P4 1.48)", "Progesterone 1.48 ng/mL (very low)", "Mood changes reported"]}, {"name": "Iron Deficiency", "confidence": "low", "description": "Iron stores are depleted or suboptimal. Iron is essential for energy, cognitive function, hair health, and oxygen transport.", "supportingFindings": ["Serum iron 45 µg/dL (low)"]}, {"name": "Menopausal Transition", "confidence": "low", "description": "Clinical picture consistent with perimenopause or menopause. Hormonal shifts during this transition affect bone health, cardiovascular risk, mood, and quality of life.", "supportingFindings": ["Age 48 (perimenopause window)", "FSH 10.5 mIU/mL (rising, perimenopause range)", "Mood changes reported"]}]}	\N	2026-03-10 22:04:49.631792	2026-03-10 22:04:49.631792
13	2	2026-03-04 00:00:00	{"alt": 13, "ast": 16, "bun": 16, "co2": 25, "hdl": 88, "ldl": 85, "rbc": 3.37, "tsh": 1.41, "wbc": 6.7, "acne": false, "egfr": 78, "onHRT": false, "folate": 4.87, "sodium": 137, "albumin": 4.1, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 9.7, "glucose": 91, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "chloride": 100, "ferritin": 97.1, "hairLoss": false, "vitaminD": 14, "bilirubin": 0.3, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": false, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 279, "potassium": 5.2, "creatinine": 0.79, "hematocrit": 34.2, "hemoglobin": 11.1, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "vitaminB12": 292, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2026-03-04", "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Charlene Kennedy", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 73, "bmi": 39.14, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": true, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "restlessLegs": false, "totalProtein": 6.4, "lowMotivation": false, "triglycerides": 85, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "totalCholesterol": 190}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- HPI: 73-year-old female here for review of routine labs. Patient is postmenopausal. She currently reports no acute cardiopulmonary complaints. Denies chest pain, syncope, lightheadedness, shortness of breath at rest, overt gastrointestinal bleeding, melena, hematemesis, or new focal neurologic symptoms (paresthesias, gait instability). Denies recent heavy vaginal bleeding (postmenopausal). No recent significant weight loss reported. No new medication changes reported at this time. (If additional medication history or symptoms are present, staff to document and notify physician.)\\n- ROS pertinent to abnormal labs: As above — negative for acute bleeding, cardiopulmonary or neurologic red-flag symptoms.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: Not recorded in chart today; height/weight/BMI not available.\\n- Labs (03/11/2026):\\n  - CBC\\n    - Hemoglobin (Hb): 11.1 g/dL — ABNORMAL (Low)\\n    - Hematocrit (Hct): 34.2 % — ABNORMAL (Low)\\n    - Platelets: 279 K/uL — NORMAL\\n    - (Note: WBC not provided)\\n  - Iron/Ferritin\\n    - Ferritin: 97.1 ng/mL — NORMAL\\n    - (Serum iron, TIBC/transferrin saturation not provided; order recommended)\\n  - Vitamin & hematologic markers\\n    - Vitamin D (25‑OH): 14 ng/mL — ABNORMAL (Severe deficiency ≤20 ng/mL)\\n    - Vitamin B12: 292 pg/mL — BORDERLINE (low‑normal)\\n    - Folate: 4.87 ng/mL — BORDERLINE (low‑normal)\\n  - Thyroid / Metabolic / Hepatic / Renal\\n    - TSH: 1.41 mIU/L — NORMAL\\n    - AST: 16 U/L — NORMAL\\n    - ALT: 13 U/L — NORMAL\\n    - Creatinine: 0.79 mg/dL — NORMAL\\n    - eGFR: 78 mL/min/1.73m2 — NORMAL\\n  - Lipids\\n    - LDL-C: 85 mg/dL — NORMAL\\n    - HDL-C: 88 mg/dL — NORMAL\\n    - Triglycerides: 85 mg/dL — NORMAL\\n  - Other\\n    - Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP‑BANG): 2 points — LOW RISK\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Mild anemia — Hb 11.1 g/dL, Hct 34.2%. Likely mild, clinically stable at present. Differential: iron deficiency (less likely given ferritin 97.1 ng/mL but ferritin may be elevated with inflammation), anemia of chronic disease/inflammation, nutritional deficiency (B12/folate borderline), chronic blood loss (occult GI loss unlikely ruled out), marrow disorders less likely but consider if refractory. No current red‑flag symptoms; notify physician if Hb <9.0 g/dL, symptomatic anemia (syncope, chest pain, severe dyspnea), or Hb drops ≥2 g/dL.\\n2. Severe vitamin D deficiency — 25‑OH Vit D 14 ng/mL. Risk for bone loss, fatigue, immune dysfunction. Needs prompt repletion.\\n3. Borderline vitamin B12 and folate — B12 292 pg/mL, folate 4.87 ng/mL. May contribute to anemia or future deficiency; consider supplementation and further testing (MMA) if symptoms or if levels fail to correct.\\n4. Ferritin 97.1 ng/mL — within reference range; argues against iron deficiency but does not exclude it in setting of inflammation. Further iron studies (serum iron, TIBC/transferrin saturation) recommended to assess iron status.\\n5. Thyroid, hepatic, and renal function within normal limits (TSH 1.41, AST 16, ALT 13, Cr 0.79, eGFR 78). Lipid profile acceptable.\\n6. Low STOP‑BANG (2) — low risk for obstructive sleep apnea.\\n\\nPLAN:\\nProblem 1 — Mild anemia (Hb 11.1 g/dL)\\n- Orders:\\n  - Draw full iron panel now: serum iron, TIBC or transferrin saturation, ferritin (repeat if older than acceptable window), CRP if concern for inflammation.\\n  - Order reticulocyte count and peripheral blood smear to assess marrow response and red cell morphology.\\n  - Given patient age (73), recommend fecal immunochemical test (FIT) for occult GI blood loss given new anemia (unless recent colonoscopy/appropriate workup documented); refer to GI for endoscopic evaluation if FIT positive or if GI symptoms present or if other risk factors indicate.\\n- Treatment:\\n  - Do NOT empirically start oral iron unless iron studies confirm deficiency. If iron deficiency confirmed (transferrin sat <20% or low ferritin on repeat), start ferrous sulfate 325 mg PO (65 mg elemental iron) once daily or twice daily as tolerated; consider IV iron if intolerant or severe deficiency — physician order required.\\n- Monitoring:\\n  - Repeat CBC + reticulocyte count in 4 weeks to assess response to any therapy.\\n  - Notify physician if Hb falls <9.0 g/dL or if symptomatic.\\n\\nProblem 2 — Severe vitamin D deficiency (25‑OH 14 ng/mL)\\n- Orders:\\n  - Baseline serum total calcium prior to initiating high‑dose vitamin D (if not available).\\n  - Continue to monitor renal function (eGFR currently 78 mL/min).\\n- Treatment (begin now per clinic protocol; implement per standing order/physician approval):\\n  - Metagenics D3 10,000 IU + Vitamin K — 1 softgel PO daily with a meal for 8–12 weeks.\\n  - After repletion, maintenance: Vitamin D3 2000 IU PO daily (or clinic-equivalent) — adjust per physician.\\n- Monitoring:\\n  - Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D level in 8–12 weeks after initiation.\\n  - Instruct patient to report signs of hypercalcemia (nausea, vomiting, polyuria, muscle weakness); recheck serum calcium if symptomatic.\\n\\nProblem 3 — Borderline vitamin B12 (292 pg/mL) and folate (4.87 ng/mL)\\n- Treatment (start empirically now):\\n  - Intrinsi B12‑Folate™ (or equivalent): 1 tablet PO daily (as recommended by clinic). Alternatively, oral methylcobalamin/cyanocobalamin 1000 mcg PO daily x 8–12 weeks if preferred or available.\\n  - Folic acid 1 mg PO daily x 3 months.\\n  - Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support — 1 tablet PO twice daily with meals (medium priority supplement) may be started to support erythropoiesis (implement per clinic protocol).\\n- Further testing:\\n  - Order methylmalonic acid (MMA) and homocysteine if borderline B12 persists or if neuropathic symptoms develop to confirm functional deficiency.\\n- Monitoring:\\n  - Recheck B12 and folate in 8–12 weeks after starting supplementation.\\n  - Educate patient to report new paresthesias, balance issues, or cognitive changes immediately.\\n\\nProblem 4 — Preventive/other lab findings\\n- No thyroid dysfunction (TSH 1.41). Liver enzymes and renal function within normal limits.\\n- Lipids acceptable; continue current management as appropriate.\\n- Sleep apnea risk low (STOP‑BANG 2). No immediate action.\\n\\nPatient education and counseling (provided today)\\n- Reviewed lab abnormalities and significance: mild anemia, severe vitamin D deficiency, borderline B12/folate.\\n- Explained vitamin D repletion plan (high‑dose for 8–12 weeks), importance of adherence, and signs/symptoms of hypercalcemia to report.\\n- Discussed reasons for additional testing (iron studies, reticulocyte, peripheral smear, MMA if indicated, FIT for occult blood given age).\\n- Reviewed dietary measures: iron‑rich foods with vitamin C, B12 sources (animal products or fortified foods), folate‑rich foods, and vitamin D sources. Encouraged weight‑bearing exercise for bone health.\\n- Advised to avoid taking oral iron with calcium or antacids; review current medications for agents that impair absorption (metformin, PPIs) and notify clinic if taking any.\\n- Provided instructions to contact clinic or physician immediately for chest pain, syncope, severe dyspnea, new neurologic symptoms (paresthesia, gait instability), or signs of GI bleeding.\\n\\nFollow‑up orders & timeline\\n- Today (03/11/2026):\\n  1. Start Metagenics D3 10,000 IU + K — 1 softgel PO daily x 8–12 weeks (document start date).\\n  2. Start Intrinsi B12‑Folate™ 1 tablet PO daily and Folic acid 1 mg PO daily; consider oral B12 1000 mcg PO daily if Intrinsi unavailable.\\n  3. Start Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support 1 tablet PO twice daily with meals (per clinic protocol).\\n  4. Order baseline total serum calcium now.\\n  5. Order full iron panel (serum iron, TIBC/transferrin saturation) and CRP if concern for inflammation.\\n  6. Order reticulocyte count and peripheral blood smear.\\n  7. Obtain FIT for occult GI bleeding given age and new anemia (unless recent colonoscopy/guideline‑appropriate screening documented).\\n  8. Order MMA if B12 borderline persists or if neuropathic symptoms develop.\\n- Short term:\\n  - CBC with differential and reticulocyte count: recheck in 4 weeks.\\n  - 25‑OH Vitamin D: recheck in 8–12 weeks after initiation.\\n  - Vitamin B12 and folate: recheck in 8–12 weeks after initiation.\\n  - Review iron panel results ASAP and initiate iron therapy only if iron deficiency documented.\\n- Escalation:\\n  - Physician to be notified immediately if Hb <9.0 g/dL, Hb drops ≥2 g/dL, patient develops syncope, chest pain, severe dyspnea at rest, new neurologic deficits, or positive FIT/occult GI bleeding that requires further workup.\\n\\nDisposition:\\n- Results reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow‑up as above.\\n\\nDate: 03/11/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 softgel daily with meal", "name": "D3 10,000 + K", "caution": "High-dose repletion therapy. Recheck vitamin D levels in 8-12 weeks. Contains vitamin K2 for calcium metabolism.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "high", "rationale": "D3 10,000 + K provides high-dose vitamin D3 with K2 for efficient repletion of severe deficiency. K2 ensures proper calcium utilization and prevents arterial calcification.", "indication": "Vitamin D 14 ng/mL (severe deficiency ≤20)", "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D level is very low, which can affect your bones, immune system, mood, and energy. This high-dose supplement will help bring your level up to optimal range, and the vitamin K2 helps your body use calcium properly.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 14 ng/mL (severe deficiency ≤20)"]}, {"dose": "1 tablet daily", "name": "Intrinsi B12-Folate™", "caution": "Contains intrinsic factor for enhanced B12 absorption. Ideal for patients with absorption concerns or vegetarian/vegan diets.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "high", "rationale": "Intrinsi B12-Folate provides methylated B12 and folate with intrinsic factor for superior absorption. Supports energy, cognition, methylation, and nerve function.", "indication": "B12 292 pg/mL (deficient <300); Folate 4.87 ng/mL (low)", "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "Your B12 and/or folate levels need support. This supplement provides highly absorbable forms of these essential vitamins to support your energy, brain function, and nervous system health.", "supportingFindings": ["B12 292 pg/mL (deficient <300)", "Folate 4.87 ng/mL (low)"]}, {"dose": "1 tablet twice daily with meals", "name": "Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support", "caution": "Contains iron, folate, and B12 for comprehensive RBC support. Avoid taking with calcium-rich foods or dairy. May cause mild GI upset initially.", "category": "iron", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Hemagenics provides highly absorbable iron bisglycinate with B12, B6, and folate for comprehensive red blood cell support. Indicated for functional iron deficiency, iron deficiency without anemia, and iron-responsive symptoms.", "indication": "Hemoglobin 11.1 g/dL (low)", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "Your lab results suggest your iron stores are lower than optimal. This supplement provides a gentle, well-absorbed form of iron along with B12 and folate to support your energy levels, hair health, and overall vitality.", "supportingFindings": ["Hemoglobin 11.1 g/dL (low)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "consider", "rationale": "Lipid levels meet threshold for CAC consideration. CAC may be useful if risk level is uncertain or patient is hesitant about statin.", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\n- You have mild anemia (Hemoglobin 11.1 g/dL, Hematocrit 34.2%) and a severe vitamin D deficiency (25‑OH Vitamin D 14 ng/mL). Other important tests are reassuring: ferritin is good (97.1 ng/mL), thyroid is normal (TSH 1.41 mIU/L), and your cholesterol and liver/kidney tests are healthy.\\n\\nPositive findings (what’s going well)\\n- Ferritin 97.1 ng/mL (good iron stores; target >50 ng/mL).\\n- LDL 85 mg/dL, HDL 88 mg/dL, Triglycerides 85 mg/dL (all in healthy ranges).\\n- Platelets 279 K/uL, AST 16 U/L, ALT 13 U/L, Creatinine 0.79 mg/dL, eGFR 78 mL/min (all normal).\\n- STOP‑BANG score 2/8 — low risk for sleep apnea.\\n\\nAreas to improve (what needs action)\\n1. Anemia — Hemoglobin 11.1 g/dL and Hematocrit 34.2%:\\n   - Because ferritin is 97.1 ng/mL, this is unlikely classic iron‑deficiency anemia. Low-normal Vitamin B12 (292 pg/mL) and folate (4.87 ng/mL) can contribute.\\n   - Actions now:\\n     - Start oral folic acid 400–800 mcg daily and a B12 supplement 1000 mcg daily (sublingual or oral) for 8–12 weeks, unless your provider prescribes otherwise.\\n     - Eat iron-containing foods (lean red meat, beans, lentils, spinach) paired with vitamin C (orange, bell pepper) to help absorption — but do not start high-dose iron pills without checking with your provider because your ferritin is adequate.\\n     - Track menstrual bleeding: if periods are heavy or soaking a pad/tampon hourly, schedule urgent care with your clinician.\\n     - Follow-up testing: repeat CBC, reticulocyte count, B12 and folate in 8–12 weeks. If hemoglobin doesn’t improve, your provider may order additional testing (peripheral smear, GI evaluation, or gynecologic review).\\n\\n2. Vitamin D — 25‑OH Vitamin D 14 ng/mL (severe deficiency):\\n   - Actions now:\\n     - Start vitamin D3 supplementation: either cholecalciferol 50,000 IU once weekly for 8 weeks OR 5,000 IU daily for 8–12 weeks, then transition to a maintenance dose (about 1,000–2,000 IU daily) as advised by your clinician.\\n     - Increase safe sun exposure (10–15 minutes arms/face several times/week as tolerated) and include calcium-rich foods; aim for total calcium 1,000–1,200 mg/day through diet and supplements if needed.\\n     - Add weight-bearing exercise (30 minutes most days — brisk walking, stair climbing, dancing) to support bone health.\\n     - Recheck vitamin D and consider a bone health discussion in 8–12 weeks.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Schedule a follow-up visit in 4–8 weeks to review symptoms and lab response. Repeat CBC, B12, folate and vitamin D in about 8–12 weeks after starting supplements.\\n- Seek sooner care if you have dizziness, fainting, chest pain, shortness of breath, very heavy menstrual bleeding, or new concerning symptoms.\\n- If you are pregnant or trying to conceive, tell your provider now — B12, folate and vitamin D management changes in pregnancy.\\n\\nYou’re already doing well with cholesterol, liver, kidney, and iron stores. These targeted, simple steps (B12/folate supplements, vitamin D replacement, diet changes, and activity) should help improve your energy and bone health. Contact your clinician to start supplements and set the recheck labs.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 11.1, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Low hemoglobin indicating anemia.", "recommendation": "Check iron studies, B12, folate. Evaluate menstrual blood loss if applicable.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 34.2, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Low hematocrit suggesting anemia.", "recommendation": "Evaluate for iron deficiency or other causes. Check iron studies.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 279, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.41, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 97.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 14, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Severe Vitamin D deficiency (≤20 ng/mL). Associated with fatigue, bone loss, and immune dysfunction.", "recommendation": "High-dose repletion: Metagenics D3 10,000 + K daily for 8-12 weeks. Recheck levels after repletion.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 292, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "Low-normal B12.", "recommendation": "Consider supplementation especially if symptomatic.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 85, "status": "normal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 88, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 85, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 16, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 13, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 78, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.79, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 4.87, "status": "borderline", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Low-normal folate.", "recommendation": "Consider supplementation, especially if planning pregnancy.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 2, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (2/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}], "aiRecommendations": "Clinical context summary (for staff review)\\n- Labs of concern: Hemoglobin 11.1 g/dL and Hematocrit 34.2% (mild anemia), 25-OH Vitamin D 14 ng/mL (severe deficiency). Vitamin B12 292 pg/mL and folate 4.87 ng/mL are low‑normal/borderline. Ferritin reported as normal.\\n- Menstrual status, pregnancy status, GI blood loss history, and symptoms (fatigue, neurologic symptoms, heavy menses) need to be confirmed when implementing recommendations.\\n\\n1) Overall clinical assessment\\n- Mild normocytic/microcytic anemia by H/H (Hb 11.1 g/dL). Etiology unclear: iron deficiency less likely if ferritin truly normal but cannot be excluded (ferritin is an acute‑phase reactant); borderline B12/folate may contribute. Evaluate menstrual blood loss and occult GI loss as indicated.\\n- Severe vitamin D deficiency (25‑OH 14 ng/mL) requires prompt repletion per clinic protocol.\\n- B12 and folate borderline—recommend supplementation and further workup if symptoms (neuropathy, cognitive changes) or if pregnancy planned.\\n- No immediate biochemical evidence of thyroid disease or hepatic/renal dysfunction.\\n\\n2) Priority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL (immediate physician notification)\\n- Notify physician immediately if patient reports syncope, chest pain, dyspnea at rest, signs of significant bleeding, or if Hb falls ≥2 g/dL from baseline or drops below 9.0 g/dL.\\n- Before initiating high‑dose vitamin D, confirm current serum calcium (if not available) and review medications that affect calcium metabolism (e.g., thiazides). eGFR is normal per labs but confirm baseline creatinine and eGFR documented (already normal).\\n\\nURGENT (within 1–2 weeks)\\n- Start vitamin D repletion now as per protocol: Metagenics D3 10,000 IU + K daily for 8–12 weeks. Order baseline serum calcium (total calcium), and ensure renal function (creatinine/eGFR already normal). Document start date and counsel patient.\\n- Begin empirical B12 and folate supplementation while awaiting further evaluation:\\n  * Vitamin B12: oral methylcobalamin or cyanocobalamin 1000 mcg PO daily x 8–12 weeks (alternative if symptomatic or high concern: IM cyanocobalamin 1000 mcg weekly x 4 then monthly; discuss with physician).\\n  * Folic acid: folic acid 1 mg PO daily x 3 months (if pregnancy planned or high risk for NTD, escalate to 4 mg PO daily per physician order).\\n- Evaluate anemia etiology:\\n  * If iron panel not documented/complete (serum iron, TIBC or transferrin saturation), order full iron studies now even though ferritin reported normal.\\n  * Order reticulocyte count and peripheral blood smear if concern for marrow response or hemolysis.\\n  * Pregnancy test (serum hCG) if patient is of childbearing potential.\\n  * Consider fecal occult blood/FIT if GI blood loss suspected or patient >50 or with GI symptoms.\\n\\nROUTINE (within 4–12 weeks)\\n- Recheck labs after initial therapy (see timeline below).\\n- If iron studies indicate iron deficiency or if heavy menses documented, consider iron therapy per protocol (see “Iron therapy” below).\\n\\n3) Medication / supplement dosing recommendations (staff to implement per standing order or physician sign‑off)\\n- Vitamin D repletion:\\n  * Metagenics D3 10,000 IU + Vitamin K daily x 8–12 weeks.\\n  * Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D at 8–12 weeks after initiation.\\n  * After repletion, maintenance: Vitamin D3 2000 IU PO daily (or product equivalent) — confirm with physician.\\n  * Baseline serum calcium prior to high‑dose therapy and recheck if symptomatic.\\n- Vitamin B12:\\n  * Oral option: cyanocobalamin or methylcobalamin 1000 mcg PO daily x 8–12 weeks, then reassess B12 level.\\n  * If symptomatic neuropathy or if PCP prefers parenteral: IM cyanocobalamin 1000 mcg weekly x 4 then monthly — physician order required.\\n  * Consider ordering methylmalonic acid (MMA) if B12 borderline persists or to confirm deficiency.\\n- Folic acid:\\n  * Folic acid 1 mg PO daily x 3 months. If pregnancy planned/high risk for NTD, physician may order 4 mg PO daily.\\n- Iron therapy (only if iron studies support deficiency or heavy menses with low Hb):\\n  * If transferrin sat <20% or low ferritin: oral ferrous sulfate 325 mg (65 mg elemental iron) PO once daily or twice daily as tolerated; consider intravenous iron if oral not tolerated or severe deficiency — consult physician/infusion protocols.\\n\\n4) Follow-up timing and rechecks (specific)\\n- CBC with differential and reticulocyte count: recheck in 4 weeks after starting supplements.\\n- 25‑OH Vitamin D: recheck in 8–12 weeks after starting high‑dose repletion.\\n- Vitamin B12 and folate: recheck in 8–12 weeks after starting supplementation.\\n- Iron studies (if ordered): review ASAP; if iron replacement started, recheck ferritin and CBC at 8–12 weeks.\\n- Pregnancy test: one time now if of childbearing potential.\\n- Clinic contact / physician update: inform physician if no hemoglobin improvement (rise <1 g/dL) after 4 weeks of targeted therapy or if Hb drops, or if B12/folate levels fall further.\\n\\n5) Physician notification requirements (staff escalation)\\n- Notify physician immediately (phone) if:\\n  * Hb <9.0 g/dL or symptomatic anemia (syncope, presyncope, chest pain, severe dyspnea).\\n  * Any new neurologic symptoms suggestive of B12 deficiency (paresthesias, gait instability).\\n  * Any abnormal GI bleeding or positive fecal occult blood test.\\n  * Abnormal iron studies requiring IV iron or transfusion.\\n- Otherwise document initiation of repletion and plan in chart and notify physician at routine review or per clinic workflow.\\n\\n6) Patient education points to cover (staff to counsel)\\n- Explain mild anemia and planned evaluation for causes (menstrual loss, nutritional, GI).\\n- Vitamin D repletion plan: explain high‑dose protocol duration (8–12 weeks), importance of adherence, and to report symptoms of hypercalcemia (nausea, vomiting, polyuria, muscle weakness).\\n- B12 and folate supplementation rationale and expected timeline to see lab improvement.\\n- Ask about menstrual bleeding pattern, GI symptoms, dietary intake (vegetarian/vegan), recent weight loss, or medications that might affect absorption (metformin, PPIs, H2 blockers).\\n- Recommend pregnancy testing if applicable prior to follow‑up and before starting certain therapies if needed.\\n- Advise use of vitamin C–rich foods to aid iron absorption if iron therapy is indicated and to avoid taking oral iron with calcium or antacids.\\n\\n7) Lifestyle interventions (staff to advise)\\n- Diet:\\n  * Encourage iron‑rich foods (lean red meat, poultry, legumes, fortified cereals) with vitamin C to increase absorption.\\n  * B12 sources: animal proteins, fortified foods; if vegetarian/vegan, counsel on supplementation.\\n  * Folate: leafy greens, citrus, legumes, fortified grains.\\n  * Vitamin D: sensible sun exposure and dietary sources (fatty fish, fortified dairy) in addition to supplementation.\\n- Exercise:\\n  * Encourage weight‑bearing exercise for bone health.\\n- Medication review:\\n  * Review and document use of PPIs, metformin, or other agents that can impair B12 or iron absorption and notify physician if present.\\n\\n8) Additional testing or referrals to consider\\n- If iron studies are discordant with anemia or ferritin is normal but suspicion for iron deficiency persists: order CRP (to assess inflammation), repeat ferritin, and consider bone marrow/hematology referral only if refractory/persistent anemia after initial workup.\\n- Methylmalonic acid (MMA) and homocysteine if B12 borderline to confirm deficiency.\\n- Stool FIT/FOBT if GI blood loss suspected.\\n- Gynecology referral if heavy or irregular menses suspected as cause of iron loss.\\n- Gastroenterology referral (endoscopy/colonoscopy) if positive occult blood, GI symptoms, or age‑appropriate CRC screening indicated.\\n- Nutrition/registered dietitian referral for comprehensive counseling if dietary deficiency suspected.\\n\\n9) Summary of next steps and timeline (concise action list for staff)\\n- Today:\\n  1. Verify menstrual history and pregnancy status (serum hCG if of childbearing potential).\\n  2. Order baseline serum calcium (if not available), full iron panel (serum iron, TIBC/transferrin sat, ferritin if not current), reticulocyte count, peripheral smear, and consider MMA if B12 borderline and symptomatic.\\n  3. Start Metagenics D3 10,000 IU + K PO daily x 8–12 weeks; document start and instruct re: baseline calcium monitoring.\\n  4. Start B12 1000 mcg PO daily and folic acid 1 mg PO daily; consider IM B12 if neuropathy or per physician.\\n  5. If iron deficiency confirmed on iron studies or heavy menses: initiate oral iron (ferrous sulfate 325 mg PO daily or BID as tolerated) per physician order.\\n  6. Educate patient on diet, symptoms to report, and adherence.\\n- Short term:\\n  * Recheck CBC + reticulocyte in 4 weeks.\\n  * Recheck 25‑OH vitamin D and B12/folate in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Escalation:\\n  * Notify physician immediately for severe symptoms, Hb <9.0 g/dL, neurologic signs, or positive occult GI bleeding.\\n\\nNote: All medication changes and orders must be processed per clinic standing orders and approved by supervising physician when required.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 0.97, "detail": "Triglycerides 85 / HDL 88 = 0.97", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 13, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": []}	\N	2026-03-11 13:22:11.402765	2026-03-11 13:22:11.402765
14	6	2025-10-15 00:00:00	{"a1c": 5, "alt": 22, "ast": 27, "bun": 15, "co2": 25, "fsh": 42.8, "hdl": 72, "ldl": 108, "rbc": 4.34, "tsh": 0.61, "wbc": 6.1, "acne": false, "egfr": 78, "onHRT": false, "freeT3": 2.64, "sodium": 140, "albumin": 4.4, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 9.4, "glucose": 83, "bloating": false, "brainFog": true, "chloride": 104, "hairLoss": false, "vitaminD": 33.1, "bilirubin": 0.4, "estradiol": 21, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": true, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 227, "potassium": 4.6, "creatinine": 0.92, "hematocrit": 40.3, "hemoglobin": 12.6, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "vitaminB12": 1393, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2025-10-15", "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Deidra Estes", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 45, "bmi": 27.24, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 120, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 0.49, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 16.2, "totalProtein": 6.8, "lowMotivation": true, "triglycerides": 60, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "totalCholesterol": 192}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History of present illness: Patient is a 45-year-old female presenting for review of laboratory results for evaluation of perimenopausal/menopausal status and general health screening. Patient reports no acute concerns. Per clinic lab interpretation, \\"no specific symptoms reported at this time.\\" Patient has not reported vasomotor symptoms, new breast changes, leg swelling, shortness of breath, or unexplained bleeding to date (no red-flag symptoms). Menstrual history: patient did not provide date of last menstrual period or current cycle regularity during this encounter; staff instructed to obtain LMP and cycle details for progesterone interpretation. Current supplements: patient reports taking a multivitamin and occasional over-the-counter supplements (detailed supplement list to be confirmed in chart). Tobacco use: none reported. Family/personal history relevant for HT contraindications to be reviewed prior to any therapy (breast cancer, VTE, active liver disease) — none reported at this time in chart.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\nVital signs: Not available in chart for today. BMI: not documented.\\n\\nLaboratory results (03/12/2026)\\n- CBC\\n  - Hemoglobin 12.6 g/dL (normal)\\n  - Hematocrit 40.3 % (normal)\\n  - Platelets 227 K/uL (normal)\\n- Basic metabolic / renal\\n  - Creatinine 0.92 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - eGFR 78 mL/min/1.73 m2 (normal)\\n- Liver enzymes\\n  - AST 27 U/L (normal)\\n  - ALT 22 U/L (normal)\\n- Glycemic\\n  - Hemoglobin A1c 5.0 % (normal)\\n- Thyroid\\n  - TSH 0.61 mIU/L (normal)\\n- Lipid panel\\n  - LDL cholesterol 108 mg/dL (borderline / near optimal)\\n  - HDL cholesterol 72 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - Triglycerides 60 mg/dL (normal)\\n- Hormones\\n  - Estradiol (E2) 21 pg/mL — ABNORMAL (low; hypoestrogenic pattern)\\n  - FSH 42.8 mIU/mL — ABNORMAL (elevated; consistent with menopausal range)\\n  - Progesterone (P4) 0.49 ng/mL — BORDERLINE (low; interpretation limited without cycle phase)\\n  - Total Testosterone 16.2 ng/dL — BORDERLINE/within expected female range (adequate androgens)\\n- Vitamins / other\\n  - 25-OH Vitamin D 33.1 ng/mL — BORDERLINE (insufficient per clinic target)\\n  - Vitamin B12 1393 pg/mL — BORDERLINE/ELEVATED (likely supplementation)\\n- Risk scores\\n  - 10-yr Total CVD risk (AHA PREVENT) 0.9% (low)\\n  - 10-yr ASCVD risk (PREVENT) 0.6% (low)\\n  - 10-yr Heart Failure risk 0.5% (low)\\n  - 30-yr CVD risk 7.3%\\n  - STOP-BANG (sleep apnea risk) 0 points\\n\\nFlags:\\n- Red flags: None.\\n- Abnormal/critical labs requiring provider review: Estradiol 21 pg/mL (low) and FSH 42.8 mIU/mL (elevated) — pattern consistent with menopause; provider review required for hormone therapy decision.\\n- Borderline labs of note: Vitamin D 33.1 ng/mL, Vitamin B12 1393 pg/mL, LDL 108 mg/dL, Progesterone 0.49 ng/mL (cycle-phase dependent).\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Menopausal transition / ovarian failure — laboratory pattern consistent with menopause: FSH 42.8 mIU/mL and low estradiol 21 pg/mL. Clinical significance: decreased ovarian estrogen production; may explain vasomotor/menopause-related symptoms if present. Provider review required to discuss hormone therapy given labs. Differential: natural menopause; primary ovarian insufficiency less likely given age and pattern.\\n2. Low progesterone 0.49 ng/mL — interpretation limited by unknown cycle phase. If drawn in mid-luteal phase, value would indicate anovulation (perimenopausal marker). If amenorrheic, consistent with ovarian failure.\\n3. Vitamin D insufficient — 25-OH D 33.1 ng/mL (clinic target ≥60 ng/mL for optimization). Suboptimal for hormone optimization and bone health.\\n4. Elevated Vitamin B12 1393 pg/mL — most likely due to supplementation; hepatic dysfunction less likely given normal AST/ALT. If no supplementation, consider further evaluation.\\n5. Dyslipidemia (borderline LDL) — LDL 108 mg/dL; overall 10-year CVD risk low. Lifestyle modification recommended; statin not indicated at current risk level unless other risk factors present.\\n6. Androgen status adequate — Total testosterone 16.2 ng/dL within expected range; do NOT initiate testosterone therapy at this time. If patient has persistent fatigue, low mood, low libido, evaluate estrogen/progesterone balance and non-androgenic causes.\\n7. Overall screening labs reassuring — CBC, renal function, LFTs, TSH, A1c within normal limits.\\n\\nPLAN:\\n1. Menopausal status / hormone therapy decision (Problem #1)\\n   - Action: Notify supervising provider/clinician that labs are consistent with menopause (FSH 42.8 mIU/mL, E2 21 pg/mL). Provider to discuss risks/benefits of hormone therapy (HT) with patient.\\n   - If provider and patient elect to initiate HT (uterus intact):\\n     - Start transdermal estradiol 25 mcg/day patch (consider 25–50 mcg/day based on symptom severity; start 25 mcg/day and titrate as needed) OR equivalent estradiol transdermal gel per formulary. Counsel on patch application and adherence.\\n     - Add micronized progesterone for endometrial protection:\\n       - Option A (cyclic): micronized progesterone 200 mg PO nightly x12–14 days each month (for women with monthly bleeding or transitioning) OR\\n       - Option B (continuous): micronized progesterone 100–200 mg PO nightly continuously (preferred if continuous estrogen regimen chosen or for patient preference).\\n     - Contraindications review: confirm absence of personal/history of breast cancer, active VTE, active liver disease, uncontrolled hypertension. Document discussion in chart and obtain informed consent.\\n     - Monitoring: follow-up visit in 4–8 weeks after initiation to assess symptom response, side effects (breast tenderness, spotting, sedation), and check blood pressure. Routine mammography per age-based schedule; counsel to report any breast changes or new leg pain/swelling immediately.\\n   - If uterus absent (hysterectomy documented), progesterone is not required; document surgical history before prescribing.\\n\\n2. Progesterone interpretation and ovulation assessment (Problem #2)\\n   - Action: Staff to contact patient to obtain date of last menstrual period (LMP), cycle length, and duration of amenorrhea if present. Document in chart.\\n   - If still cycling and provider requests ovulation confirmation: order repeat mid-luteal progesterone (draw ~7 days before expected menses or ~day 21 of a 28-day cycle or timed to LH surge if irregular).\\n   - If amenorrheic and menopausal labs confirmed, no further progesterone testing required unless clinically indicated.\\n\\n3. Vitamin D insufficiency (Problem #3)\\n   - Start Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily (one softgel with a meal) as recommended by clinic protocol.\\n   - Recheck 25-OH Vitamin D in 12 weeks after initiation.\\n   - Target 25-OH ≥60 ng/mL for hormone optimization per clinic protocol.\\n   - Counsel on dietary calcium intake (aim for 1,000–1,200 mg/day) and weight-bearing exercise for bone health.\\n   - Document medication/supplement reconciliation to avoid duplication and hypervitaminosis.\\n\\n4. Elevated Vitamin B12 (Problem #4)\\n   - Action: Review and document current B12 supplementation/injections. If patient confirms recent high-dose oral or IM B12, no further immediate action.\\n   - If patient denies supplementation and B12 remains >1000 on repeat, consider ordering methylmalonic acid (MMA) to evaluate functional B12 status and discuss with provider. No hepatology referral currently given normal AST/ALT.\\n\\n5. Lipids / cardiovascular risk (Problem #5)\\n   - Counseling: Reinforce lifestyle measures — heart-healthy diet, regular aerobic exercise, weight management, limit saturated fats, avoid tobacco.\\n   - No statin indicated at present given 10-year ASCVD 0.6% (low). Reassess if additional risk factors present.\\n   - Order fasting lipid panel in 3–6 months if lifestyle changes initiated or sooner per provider discretion.\\n\\n6. Androgen status (Problem #6)\\n   - Do NOT start testosterone therapy at this time (Total T 16.2 ng/dL within expected range). If persistent symptoms attributable to androgen deficiency after optimizing estrogen/progesterone, reassess clinically and consider specialist referral prior to any androgen trial.\\n   - If provider later elects to trial testosterone, obtain baseline total and free testosterone, SHBG, LFTs, and lipids; monitor serum testosterone to remain in physiologic female range.\\n\\n7. Additional recommended supplements and clinic recommendations\\n   - High-priority supplements recommended: AdvaClear® (2 capsules twice daily); GlutaClear® (2 capsules daily).\\n   - Medium-priority supplements: D3 5,000 + K (1 softgel daily with meal); Exhilarin® (2 tablets daily); NutraGems® CoQ10 300 (1 chewable gel daily); Adreset® (2 capsules twice daily); Magtein® Magnesium L-Threonate (3 capsules daily in divided doses).\\n   - Document patient acceptance/decline of supplements and add to medication list if started.\\n\\n8. Follow-up testing / monitoring\\n   - 25-OH Vitamin D recheck: in 12 weeks after starting supplementation.\\n   - If HT initiated: follow-up visit 4–8 weeks to assess efficacy and side effects; measure BP at follow-up.\\n   - Fasting lipid panel: recheck in 3–6 months if lifestyle interventions initiated.\\n   - If ovulation confirmation needed: mid-luteal progesterone timed per cycle (see above).\\n   - Consider baseline DEXA if risk factors for osteoporosis or per provider judgment (peri/early postmenopausal with risk factors).\\n   - Consider ferritin and iron studies if history of heavy menses or ongoing fatigue (target ferritin >50 ng/mL for symptom resolution).\\n   - If Vitamin B12 elevated without supplementation, consider MMA and repeat B12 per provider.\\n\\n9. Communication / documentation tasks\\n   - Staff to contact patient within 1–3 days to obtain LMP, current cycle pattern, and detailed supplement list (particularly B12 and vitamin D intake). Flag chart for provider review of menopause/HT decision.\\n   - If provider approves, initiate Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily and schedule 12-week recheck.\\n   - If provider approves HT: prescribe transdermal estradiol and micronized progesterone per regimen above; ensure counseling and contraindication checklist documented and schedule 4–8 week follow-up.\\n\\nDisposition / Follow-up timeline:\\n- Immediate (1–3 days): staff to collect LMP/cycle details and supplement list; flag provider for HT decision.\\n- Short-term (1–2 weeks): start vitamin D if approved; provider discussion re HT; schedule follow-up appointment if HT initiated.\\n- Mid-term (4–12 weeks): 4–8 week follow-up post-HT start; 12-week vitamin D level recheck.\\n- Routine recheck window: 3–6 months for general follow-up and repeat labs as noted.\\n\\nPatient communication:\\n- Results reviewed and discussed with patient. Advised that lab pattern is consistent with menopause (low estradiol, elevated FSH). Discussed options including hormone therapy and non-hormonal management; decision to start HT requires provider sign-off and contraindication review.\\n- Discussed initiation of vitamin D 5,000 IU daily for insufficiency and plan for recheck in ~3 months.\\n- Confirmed that testosterone will not be started given adequate levels at this time.\\n- Patient instructed to report any new breast changes, unexplained vaginal bleeding, sudden leg pain or shortness of breath immediately.\\n\\nDisposition: Results reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\nDate: 03/12/2026.", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "preventRisk": {"ldlGoal": "No specific LDL target; focus on risk-based statin intensity", "riskCategory": "low", "tenYearASCVD": 0.005749395043659115, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0.00903664150139958, "thirtyYearASCVD": 0.04217114880199001, "ageValidFor30Year": true, "thirtyYearTotalCVD": 0.07264461913345227, "tenYearHFPercentage": "0.5%", "tenYearHeartFailure": 0.0045184328321747225, "st