ingFindings": ["Low energy reported", "Brain fog reported"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "consider", "rationale": "Lipid levels meet threshold for CAC consideration. CAC may be useful if risk level is uncertain or patient is hesitant about statin.", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\n- Your hormones show a menopausal/hypoestrogenic pattern: estradiol (E2) is 21 pg/mL and FSH is 42.8 mIU/mL. This combination (E2 21 pg/mL, FSH 42.8 mIU/mL) confirms reduced ovarian estrogen production consistent with menopause or the “crash” phase of perimenopause. Progesterone is low at 0.49 ng/mL (phase not documented), which could mean anovulatory cycles if this was drawn in the luteal phase. You have no urgent lab abnormalities: hemoglobin 12.6 g/dL and hematocrit 40.3% are normal, and TSH is normal at 0.61 mIU/L.\\n\\nPositive findings (what’s good)\\n- Hemoglobin 12.6 g/dL and hematocrit 40.3% are within normal range.\\n- TSH 0.61 mIU/L indicates normal thyroid function.\\n- A1c 5.0% shows normal blood sugar control.\\n- HDL is strong at 72 mg/dL and triglycerides are 60 mg/dL.\\n- LDL is near optimal at 108 mg/dL.\\n- 10-year ASCVD risk is very low: 0.6% (10-year total CVD risk 0.9%), meaning low short-term risk of heart attack or stroke.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + actions)\\n1. Low estrogen / menopause (E2 21 pg/mL, FSH 42.8 mIU/mL, P4 0.49 ng/mL)\\n   - Lifestyle for symptoms & bone health: Strength/weight-bearing exercise 2–3x weekly (resistance training + brisk walking 30 minutes most days), and balance training.\\n   - Calcium: aim for 1,000–1,200 mg/day from diet (dairy, fortified plant milk, canned salmon). \\n   - Consider bone density testing (DEXA) within 12 months given menopausal status.\\n   - If bothersome symptoms (hot flashes, sleep, vaginal dryness), discuss hormone therapy options with your clinician.\\n\\n2. Vitamin D borderline: 25-OH vitamin D = 33.1 ng/mL (insufficient)\\n   - Start vitamin D3 1,000–2,000 IU daily and retest 25-OH vitamin D in 3 months.\\n   - Continue weight-bearing exercise and calcium as above for bone support.\\n\\n3. Elevated B12: 1,393 pg/mL\\n   - Review supplements: stop extra B12 supplement (if taking >500 mcg daily) and recheck B12 in 3 months. With normal liver tests and CBC, high B12 often reflects supplementation.\\n\\nHeart health (your risk and what it means)\\n- Your 10-year ASCVD risk is 0.6% (very low). Maintain heart health with:\\n  - 150 minutes/week moderate aerobic activity (brisk walking, cycling) and 2 strength sessions weekly.\\n  - Mediterranean-style eating: vegetables, whole grains, olive oil, nuts, fatty fish 2–3x weekly.\\n  - Limit processed foods and added sugars; keep alcohol moderate.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Discuss menopausal symptoms and options with your provider; consider DEXA referral within 12 months.\\n- Start vitamin D3 1,000–2,000 IU daily; recheck 25-OH D in 3 months.\\n- Stop extra B12 supplement if applicable and recheck B12 in 3 months.\\n- Maintain exercise and diet for bone and heart health; follow up in 3 months or sooner if symptoms worsen.\\n\\nYou’re doing well in many areas—small targeted changes (vitamin D, bone-strengthening exercise, reviewing supplements) will help protect your bones, hormones, and overall health.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 12.6, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 40.3, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 227, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 21, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.49, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 42.8, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 16.2, "status": "normal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone within normal female range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 0.61, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 33.1, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D insufficient (31-40 ng/mL). Suboptimal for hormone optimization.", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 5,000 + K daily. Target ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 1393, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "Elevated B12.", "recommendation": "Usually from supplementation. Rarely indicates liver disease.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 108, "status": "borderline", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Near optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet and exercise.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 72, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 60, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.9036641501399579, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 0.9% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.5749395043659116, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 0.6%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.45184328321747225, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.5%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 7.264461913345227, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 7.3%, ASCVD 4.2%, Heart Failure 4.5%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 27, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 22, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 78, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.92, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.49, "status": "borderline", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Low Progesterone (Phase Unknown)", "interpretation": "Progesterone is 0.49 ng/mL. Without cycle phase documentation, interpretation is limited. If drawn in luteal phase, this would indicate anovulation - a perimenopause marker.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Document cycle phase for accurate interpretation. If patient is mid-cycle or luteal, this suggests anovulation. Consider symptoms: sleep disruption, anxiety, irregular cycles. PATIENT EDUCATION: We need to know where you are in your menstrual cycle to fully interpret this result. Please note when your last period started.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase; <5 ng/mL suggests anovulation if luteal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 21, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Hypoestrogenic Pattern", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 21 pg/mL with FSH of 42.8 mIU/mL indicates hypoestrogenic perimenopause - the \\"crash phase.\\" Estrogen production has dropped significantly and the brain is signaling strongly (elevated FSH) trying to stimulate the ovaries.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Transdermal estradiol 0.025-0.05 mg is appropriate replacement therapy. Add micronized progesterone for uterine protection and sleep support. Frame this as hormone replacement, not stimulation - we are restoring what the body needs. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen has dropped to a level that can cause symptoms like hot flashes, night sweats, brain fog, and mood changes. Replacing estrogen with a patch or gel can relieve these symptoms and protect your long-term health. Progesterone is added to protect your uterus and help with sleep.", "referenceRange": "E2 <50 with FSH >10 indicates estrogen deficiency"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 42.8, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Menopause", "interpretation": "FSH of 42.8 mIU/mL with low estradiol (21 pg/mL) confirms menopausal status. No specific symptoms reported at this time. Elevated FSH reflects decreased ovarian function and reduced estrogen production. This is a normal part of reproductive aging but may benefit from hormone optimization if symptomatic.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: If symptomatic, consider hormone therapy (HT): transdermal estradiol + micronized progesterone (if uterus intact). Discuss benefits/risks of HT including cardiovascular, bone, and quality of life considerations. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your lab results confirm you are in menopause. Hormone therapy can help manage symptoms like hot flashes, sleep problems, and vaginal dryness while also protecting bone health.", "referenceRange": "FSH <25 mIU/mL premenopausal; ≥25 suggests transition; ≥40 menopausal"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 16.2, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone Pattern D: Adequate Androgens, Persistent Symptoms", "interpretation": "Adequate Androgens pattern identified (Total T: 16.2 ng/dL (within range)). Total, free, and bioavailable testosterone are within normal ranges and SHBG is not extreme. If the patient reports fatigue, low mood, sleep disruption, or low libido, these symptoms are likely driven by non-androgenic factors. Findings to evaluate: Estrogen/Progesterone balance should be assessed (E2: 21 pg/mL, P4: 0.49 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Do not reflexively add testosterone when levels are adequate — look deeper. Evaluate iron status (ferritin target >50 for symptom resolution), thyroid function (optimize TSH), and sleep quality (consider sleep apnea screening with STOP-BANG). Assess estrogen and progesterone balance — E2/P4 imbalance is a common driver of persistent symptoms. Address CNS and psychosocial drivers: chronic stress, HPA axis dysregulation, mood disorders. Consider DHEA-S if not already checked. Support with lifestyle optimization: sleep hygiene, stress management, nutrition. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your testosterone levels are actually in a healthy range, which is good news. When symptoms persist despite normal testosterone, it usually means something else is contributing. Your provider will look at other factors like iron levels, thyroid function, sleep quality, and hormone balance to find what's driving your symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Total T 15-70 ng/dL, Free T 0.5-5.0 pg/mL (all within range)"}], "aiRecommendations": "Critical overall assessment\\n- Labs are consistent with ovarian failure/menopause: FSH 42.8 mIU/mL with low estradiol 21 pg/mL. Progesterone 0.49 ng/mL is low but interpretation requires cycle-phase documentation if menses persist. No acute red-flag lab abnormalities (CBC, LFTs, renal function normal).\\n- Borderline vitamin D insufficiency (25-OH 33.1 ng/mL) — suboptimal for hormone optimization per clinic protocol.\\n- Vitamin B12 elevated (1393 pg/mL) most commonly reflects supplementation; LFTs are normal so less likely hepatic pathology.\\n- Lipids: LDL 108 mg/dL — near optimal; manage with lifestyle.\\n\\nPriority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\n\\nCRITICAL (requires provider notification/decision)\\n- Menopausal status and HT decision:\\n  - Notify provider: FSH 42.8 mIU/mL + E2 21 pg/mL → lab pattern consistent with menopause. Provider review required to decide on hormone therapy based on symptoms, contraindications (personal Hx breast cancer, VTE, active liver disease, unexplained vaginal bleeding), and patient preference.\\n  - If uterus intact and provider chooses HT, plan to start transdermal estradiol plus micronized progesterone for endometrial protection (see dosing below). Document discussion of risks/benefits and VTE/breast cancer screening status in chart.\\n\\nURGENT (address within 1–2 weeks)\\n- Cycle-phase documentation for progesterone interpretation:\\n  - If patient is still cycling, staff must obtain and record date of last menstrual period and cycle length. If patient is amenorrheic, document duration.\\n  - If provider needs confirmation of ovulation, order repeat mid-luteal progesterone: draw ~7 days before expected menses (approx. day 21 of 28-day cycle) or use LH surge tracking if cycles irregular.\\n\\nROUTINE (standard follow-up)\\n- Vitamin D:\\n  - Start Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily per protocol. Target 25-OH ≥60 ng/mL.\\n  - Recheck 25-OH in 12 weeks after initiation.\\n  - Notify provider if patient is on other high-dose vitamin D or has hypercalcemia risk.\\n\\n- Vitamin B12:\\n  - Review medication/supplement list for recent B12 injections or high-dose oral supplements. If patient reports supplementation, no immediate action.\\n  - If no supplementation and B12 remains >1000 on repeat, discuss with provider — consider checking methylmalonic acid (MMA) or further hepatic workup only if clinically indicated. No immediate hepatology referral given normal AST/ALT.\\n\\n- Lipids:\\n  - Reinforce lifestyle measures; recheck fasting lipid panel in 3–6 months if initiating lifestyle changes. If additional CV risk factors present, escalate to provider for statin consideration per risk calculators.\\n\\nSpecific medication recommendations and monitoring (for staff to action on provider order)\\n\\n- Hormone therapy (if provider elects HT and uterus intact):\\n  - Transdermal estradiol: start 25–50 mcg/day (0.025–0.05 mg/day) via patch or equivalent gel. Choose product per clinic formulary and instruct patient on product-specific application frequency (e.g., Vivelle/Climara directions).\\n  - Micronized progesterone (for endometrial protection): options\\n    - Cyclic: micronized progesterone 200 mg nightly x12–14 days each month (for patients transitioning with monthly bleeding) OR\\n    - Continuous: micronized progesterone 100–200 mg nightly continuously (preferred when continuous estrogen recommended for symptom control or patient preference).\\n  - Provide provider with contraindication checklist (personal/strong family history of breast cancer, active VTE, uncontrolled hypertension, active liver disease) prior to initiating.\\n  - Monitoring: follow-up visit at 4–8 weeks to assess symptom response and side effects (breast tenderness, spotting, sedation from progesterone). Routine labs are not required to titrate E2; check blood pressure at follow-up. Continue standard mammography screening per age/interval.\\n\\n- Vitamin D:\\n  - Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily. Recheck 25-OH in 12 weeks.\\n\\n- Testosterone:\\n  - DO NOT START testosterone at this time — total testosterone 16.2 ng/dL is within expected range and symptoms, if present, are more likely due to estrogen/progesterone imbalance or non-androgenic causes.\\n  - If provider later considers androgen therapy despite current levels, obtain baseline total and free testosterone, SHBG, LFTs, lipids; start low and monitor serum testosterone to remain in female physiologic range. (Provide specific dosing only after provider indicates intent to trial and signs a testosterone protocol.)\\n\\nLifestyle interventions to counsel patients (staff education points)\\n- Address symptom drivers: sleep hygiene, cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia if present, assessment for mood disorders and stress reduction strategies.\\n- Nutrition: encourage balanced diet with adequate calcium (≥1,000–1,200 mg/day) and vitamin D; emphasize iron-rich foods if menses heavy (check ferritin).\\n- Exercise: weight-bearing exercise 3×/week for bone health; aerobic activity for CV risk reduction.\\n- Smoking cessation if applicable (critical for HT risk profile).\\n- Sleep apnea: if STOP-BANG elevated or symptoms present, proceed with sleep medicine referral.\\n\\nAdditional testing or referrals to consider\\n- If menstruating and ovulation assessment needed: repeat mid-luteal progesterone (draw ~7 days before expected menses) or use LH surge testing.\\n- Ferritin and iron studies (if history of heavy menses, fatigue) — target ferritin >50 ng/mL for symptom resolution.\\n- Thyroid: TSH is reported normal; if clinical suspicion persists (symptoms of hypothyroidism/hyperthyroidism), consider reflex free T4, free T3, and anti-TPO.\\n- Bone health: DEXA scan if risk factors for osteoporosis or per established age-based screening—consider baseline DEXA if patient is peri/early postmenopausal with risk factors.\\n- Mental health/sleep referral if significant mood symptoms or suspected sleep apnea.\\n- Gynecology referral for complex bleeding history, contraindications to HT, or if patient prefers specialist consultation.\\n\\nPhysician notification triggers for staff\\n- Initiation of hormone therapy (estradiol ± progesterone) — requires provider sign-off and documentation of counseling/contraindications.\\n- Elevated B12 (>1000 pg/mL) without known supplementation — notify provider to consider further evaluation.\\n- Patient reports new/unexplained vaginal bleeding while on HT — urgent provider notification.\\n- Any new symptoms suggestive of VTE, stroke, or breast changes — immediate provider notification.\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (for clinic staff workflow)\\n1. Immediate (within 1–3 days)\\n   - Contact patient to obtain last menstrual period date and cycle regularity; add to chart.\\n   - Review and document current supplements (especially B12, vitamin D) and nicotine use.\\n   - Flag chart for provider review of menopausal HT decision.\\n\\n2. Short-term (within 1–2 weeks)\\n   - If provider approves: initiate Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily; schedule 12-week 25-OH recheck.\\n   - If provider approves HT: start transdermal estradiol 25–50 mcg/day + micronized progesterone per chosen regimen. Provide medication instructions and contraindication counseling; schedule 4–8 week follow-up visit.\\n\\n3. Mid-term (6–12 weeks)\\n   - Reassess symptoms and side effects at 4–8 weeks after HT start; document blood pressure and any adverse events.\\n   - Recheck vitamin D at 12 weeks.\\n   - Repeat fasting lipid panel in 3–6 months if lifestyle changes initiated.\\n\\n4. Contingent testing\\n   - If still cycling and provider requests ovulation confirmation: arrange mid-luteal progesterone draw (~7 days before expected menses).\\n   - Order ferritin and additional thyroid testing if clinically indicated.\\n\\nConcise patient education points for staff to deliver (documented in chart)\\n- Explain that labs are consistent with menopause (not a diagnosis statement for patient; staff should phrase as “your hormone testing shows low estrogen and high FSH consistent with menopause”) and that estrogen replacement can relieve vasomotor and sleep symptoms and protect bone health. Document that risks and benefits were discussed with provider if HT started.\\n- Vitamin D is low-moderate; starting high-dose D3 (5,000 IU daily) is recommended and levels will be rechecked in ~3 months.\\n- Elevated B12 is most likely from supplementation; confirm current supplements.\\n- Testosterone is adequate — do not start testosterone given current levels; provider will evaluate other causes for persistent symptoms.\\n\\nNotes/clinic protocol reminders\\n- If uterus absent (hysterectomy), progesterone is not required for endometrial protection — document surgical history before prescribing.\\n- Ensure contraception counseling if any residual fertility and patient is on HT or other hormones — although labs suggest menopause, document last menstrual period and contraceptive status.\\n- All medication starts must be signed off by provider and counseling documented in the chart.\\n\\nEnd of recommendations.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 0.83, "detail": "Triglycerides 60 / HDL 72 = 0.83", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 22, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Menopausal Transition", "confidence": "high", "description": "Clinical picture consistent with perimenopause or menopause. Hormonal shifts during this transition affect bone health, cardiovascular risk, mood, and quality of life.", "supportingFindings": ["Age 45 (perimenopause window)", "FSH 42.8 mIU/mL (elevated, menopausal range)", "Estradiol 21 pg/mL (declining)"]}, {"name": "Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Lab pattern consistent with relative estrogen excess or impaired estrogen clearance. Even without reported symptoms, supporting liver detoxification and estrogen metabolism pathways may be beneficial.", "supportingFindings": ["Progesterone 0.49 ng/mL (very low)", "Borderline liver markers (ALT 22, AST 27) suggesting possible impaired hepatic clearance"]}, {"name": "Oxidative Stress Burden", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Multiple markers suggest increased oxidative stress and cellular burden. Antioxidant and glutathione support may help reduce oxidative damage and support cellular energy production.", "supportingFindings": ["ALT 22 U/L (hepatic stress marker)", "AST 27 U/L (cellular stress marker)", "Low energy / fatigue (possible mitochondrial burden)", "Brain fog reported (possible oxidative CNS burden)", "Age 45 (increased oxidative stress risk)"]}, {"name": "Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Pattern consistent with HPA-axis stress and cortisol dysregulation. Chronic stress depletes DHEA-S, disrupts sleep, and impairs cognitive and emotional function.", "supportingFindings": ["Low energy / fatigue reported", "Low motivation reported", "Brain fog reported"]}]}	\N	2026-03-12 13:59:21.592509	2026-03-12 13:59:21.592509
