se or pellet placement; staff to confirm current HRT formulations, routes, and doses prior to any changes.\\n- Review of systems pertinent to lab abnormalities: No documentation of chest pain, shortness of breath, visual changes, neurologic deficits, or symptoms of thrombotic events. No documented report of acne, hirsutism, or hair thinning in chart at time of note; patient will be queried further about androgenic symptoms.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: Not documented in lab report (BP, HR, BMI not available in result summary).\\n- Labs (03/12/2026):\\n  - CBC:\\n    - Hemoglobin: 15.4 g/dL (Normal)\\n    - Hematocrit: 44.5 % (Borderline high) — may reflect mild hemoconcentration/dehydration\\n    - Platelets: 255 K/uL (Normal)\\n  - Basic metabolic / renal / hepatic:\\n    - Creatinine: 0.88 mg/dL (Normal)\\n    - eGFR: 85 mL/min (Normal)\\n    - AST: 21 U/L (Normal)\\n    - ALT: 21 U/L (Normal)\\n  - Iron studies:\\n    - Ferritin: 145 ng/mL (Normal)\\n    - Serum Iron: 110 ug/dL (Normal)\\n    - TIBC: 256 ug/dL (Normal)\\n  - Endocrine / Hormones:\\n    - Estradiol (E2): 61 pg/mL (Normal)\\n    - Progesterone: 0.33 ng/mL (Abnormal — LOW)\\n      - Perimenopause/luteal assessment: Luteal progesterone deficiency reported (0.33 ng/mL)\\n    - Testosterone, Total: 55.9 ng/dL (Borderline low relative to clinic HRT target 75–125 ng/dL)\\n    - Free Testosterone: 7 pg/mL (Abnormal — ELEVATED)\\n    - SHBG: 54.2 nmol/L (Normal)\\n    - TSH: 1.21 mIU/L (Normal)\\n    - Vitamin D (25-OH): 71.7 ng/mL (Normal)\\n  - Other:\\n    - Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG): 1 point (Low risk)\\n- Flags:\\n  - Abnormal: Progesterone 0.33 ng/mL (subtherapeutic for HRT endometrial protection), Free testosterone 7 pg/mL (elevated).\\n  - Borderline: Hematocrit 44.5%, Total testosterone 55.9 ng/dL.\\n- Red flags: None immediate (no critical/urgent laboratory value such as severe anemia, renal failure, transaminitis, markedly abnormal electrolytes). However, progesterone subtherapeutic for endometrial protection in HRT requires provider review and action.\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1) Luteal progesterone deficiency / probable perimenopausal ovulatory dysfunction.\\n   - Finding: Progesterone 0.33 ng/mL (measured 03/12/2026). Estradiol 61 pg/mL with low progesterone in a 39 y female is consistent with luteal phase progesterone deficiency / anovulation and suggests perimenopausal transition if the sample was obtained in luteal phase.\\n   - Clinical significance: Progesterone well below clinic therapeutic target for endometrial protection (approx goal serum 8–10 ng/mL for HRT). Risk of inadequate endometrial protection if estrogen therapy continued without adequate progestogen coverage. Progesterone deficiency can contribute to sleep disturbance, anxiety, and bleeding irregularities.\\n\\n2) Elevated free testosterone with discordant low/low-normal total testosterone.\\n   - Findings: Free T 7 pg/mL (elevated), Total T 55.9 ng/dL (borderline low for clinic HRT targets), SHBG 54.2 nmol/L (normal).\\n   - Clinical significance & differential: Discordant results may reflect assay variability/timing, exogenous testosterone contamination (topical transfer), altered SHBG dynamics, laboratory artifact, or adrenal androgen contribution. Elevated free T can cause androgenic symptoms (acne, hirsutism, androgenic alopecia). Do NOT empirically increase testosterone dosing until provider review and repeat morning labs/documentation of exogenous sources.\\n\\n3) Borderline elevated hematocrit.\\n   - Finding: Hct 44.5% (borderline high).\\n   - Clinical significance: May reflect mild dehydration/hemoconcentration. Monitor if testosterone is initiated or dose increased because testosterone can increase Hct. No immediate intervention required unless rising or symptomatic.\\n\\n4) Other routine labs within expected limits.\\n   - TSH, ferritin, vitamins, LFTs, renal function normal. No immediate metabolic or hepatic contraindications to recommended interventions.\\n\\nPLAN:\\nOverall: Provider notified today (03/12/2026) for review of subtherapeutic progesterone and discordant androgen panel. Do not change testosterone without provider sign-off. Staff to confirm cycle timing, pregnancy status, and current HRT formulations/doses prior to ordering changes. Patient education provided regarding hydration and next steps; patient to be contacted for additional history.\\n\\n1) Luteal progesterone deficiency / perimenopause\\n   - Immediate provider action (CRITICAL): Provider to review and sign order for micronized progesterone per clinic protocol.\\n     - Recommend micronized progesterone 100–200 mg PO nightly.\\n       - If patient desires cyclic therapy and cycles are regular: consider 200 mg nightly on days 14–28 of cycle (or clinic’s standard cyclic regimen) to provide luteal support.\\n       - If cycles irregular or continuous endometrial protection preferred: consider 100–200 mg nightly continuous.\\n     - Rationale: Aim for serum progesterone ~8–10 ng/mL for endometrial protection and symptomatic benefit.\\n   - Before initiating: If patient of reproductive potential, obtain pregnancy test (urine or serum) prior to starting progesterone as indicated.\\n   - Monitoring: Recheck progesterone 4 weeks after initiation/change. If on cyclic dosing, obtain mid-luteal progesterone (~7 days before expected menses). If continuous dosing, obtain random trough at 4 weeks per clinic protocol.\\n   - Documentation: Staff to add progesterone order to chart after provider signs; document patient counseling regarding sedation risk with nighttime dosing and sleep benefits.\\n\\n2) Elevated free testosterone with discordant total testosterone\\n   - Do NOT empirically increase testosterone; provider decision required.\\n   - Immediate actions:\\n     - Provider notified to review current testosterone formulation, route (topical vs pellet vs injectable), exact dose, dosing timing, and any recent applications or pellet procedures. If topical products are used, advise patient on hygiene and consider temporary discontinuation only if directed by provider.\\n     - Staff to confirm and document LMP/cycle day and whether sample was a morning lab (AM fasting) — schedule repeat morning labs.\\n   - Diagnostic/repeat labs:\\n     - Repeat total testosterone, free testosterone, and SHBG in a morning (AM) sample 4–6 weeks after any testosterone dose change or sooner if no change to clarify discordance (if no change, repeat in 6–8 weeks).\\n     - If elevated free T persists: order DHEA-S and androstenedione to evaluate adrenal contribution.\\n     - If PCOS suspected (cycle irregularity or hyperandrogenic signs): consider early follicular (cycle days 2–5) FSH/LH, fasting insulin and glucose or HbA1c, and pelvic ultrasound.\\n   - Management considerations:\\n     - If clinical androgen excess confirmed and exogenous testosterone suspected, consider dose reduction/hold rather than dose increase.\\n     - If provider elects to optimize testosterone after excluding causes of elevated free T, follow clinic conditional dose-adjustment guidance and recheck labs 4–6 weeks after change.\\n   - Patient counseling: Educate regarding signs of androgen excess and the importance of confirming topical product use to prevent inadvertent exposure to others.\\n\\n3) Borderline hematocrit 44.5%\\n   - Plan: Advise increased oral hydration now. Reassess hematocrit/Hb in 2–4 weeks if hydration suboptimal or if symptoms suggest dehydration.\\n   - If testosterone is increased or initiated: recheck Hct at baseline and periodically per clinic protocol; notify provider if Hct >50–52% or if patient develops symptoms of polycythemia.\\n   - Document hydration counseling in chart.\\n\\n4) Additional testing and referrals\\n   - Order after provider review / as clinically indicated:\\n     - Pregnancy test if reproductive potential and prior to progesterone initiation/changes.\\n     - Repeat morning total T, free T, SHBG (4–6 weeks after any change or 6–8 weeks if no change).\\n     - DHEA-S, androstenedione if elevated free T persists.\\n     - Early-follicular FSH/LH, AMH if fertility evaluation desired or PCOS workup indicated.\\n     - Fasting glucose and insulin or HbA1c if insulin resistance suspected.\\n     - Pelvic ultrasound if PCOS suspected or indicated.\\n   - Referrals:\\n     - Consider Endocrinology or Reproductive Endocrinology referral if discordant androgen labs persist or for complex management.\\n     - Dermatology consultation as needed for acne/hirsutism management.\\n\\n5) Supplements and lifestyle recommendations\\n   - Supplements recommended (per clinic lab interpretation):\\n     - High priority: AdvaClear® 2 capsules twice daily.\\n     - Medium priority: GlutaClear® 2 capsules daily; EstroFactors™ 2 tablets twice daily; Magtein® (Magnesium L-Threonate) 3 capsules daily divided doses.\\n     - (Note: Supplements documented as recommendations; initiate only per provider/patient preference and reconcile with current medications.)\\n   - Lifestyle:\\n     - Encourage oral hydration and recheck Hct as above.\\n     - Sleep hygiene and anxiety management counseling; discuss that progesterone may improve sleep/anxiety and sedation potential with nighttime dosing.\\n     - If hyperandrogenic symptoms suspected, counsel on weight management, regular exercise, and low-glycemic diet to improve insulin sensitivity.\\n     - If topical testosterone used, review application hygiene (wash hands after application, avoid skin contact with others) to reduce transfer risk.\\n\\n6) Communication, documentation, and follow-up timeline\\n   - Actions completed today (03/12/2026):\\n     - Results reviewed in chart.\\n     - Provider notified via EHR message for urgent review of progesterone and testosterone discordance.\\n     - Staff to contact patient to obtain LMP/cycle day, confirm current HRT products (formulation/route/dose), pregnancy status, and presence/absence of androgenic symptoms; document responses.\\n     - Patient counseled to increase fluids and to expect follow-up testing.\\n   - Orders to be placed after provider sign-off:\\n     - Micronized progesterone 100–200 mg nightly (cyclic vs continuous per provider decision).\\n     - Pregnancy test if applicable.\\n     - Repeat hormone panel (AM total T, free T, SHBG) per timing above.\\n     - Progesterone recheck at 4 weeks after progesterone initiation/change (timing based on cyclic vs continuous regimen).\\n     - Hct/Hb recheck in 2–4 weeks if dehydration suspected or sooner if clinically indicated.\\n     - Additional labs (DHEA-S, androstenedione, early-follicular FSH/LH, fasting glucose/HbA1c) if clinically indicated.\\n   - Follow-up appointment: schedule hormone follow-up in 4–6 weeks or sooner after lab repeats/medication changes. Recheck window recommended 3–6 months for routine follow-up.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\n03/12/2026\\nProvider notified in chart for order/signature.", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "supplements": [{"dose": "2 capsules twice daily", "name": "AdvaClear®", "caution": "Broad-spectrum phase I/II liver detoxification support. Contains NAC, green tea extract, milk thistle, and B-vitamins for biotransformation. Best taken with food.", "category": "detox", "priority": "high", "rationale": "AdvaClear provides broad-spectrum phase I and phase II liver detoxification support with NAC, green tea extract, watercress, and B-vitamins. Designed for metabolic clearance support, biotransformation of hormones and environmental compounds, and hepatic stress. Appropriate when liver markers are borderline, estr