Burden"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement boosts your body's master antioxidant, glutathione, which protects your cells from damage. Your results suggest increased oxidative stress, and this formula helps your body defend and repair at the cellular level.", "supportingFindings": ["Oxidative stress phenotype (glutathione/antioxidant support indicated)", "ALT 21 U/L (hepatic glutathione demand)", "Low energy (mitochondrial antioxidant support)", "Brain fog (CNS oxidative burden)"]}, {"dose": "2 tablets twice daily", "name": "EstroFactors™", "caution": "Nutritional support for estrogen balance and metabolism. Contains I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Supports healthy estrogen metabolite ratios.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "EstroFactors provides targeted nutritional support for estrogen metabolism and balance with I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Best suited for women with estrogen dominance patterns, PMS, perimenopausal hormone imbalance, or those needing broader estrogen metabolism support during hormonal transitions. Differs from Estrovera in that it addresses the underlying estrogen metabolism rather than vasomotor symptom relief.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected; Age-appropriate for estrogen metabolism support", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement helps your body process and balance estrogen more effectively. It supports healthy hormone metabolism, which can help with PMS symptoms, bloating, mood changes, and hormonal balance during life transitions.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected", "Age-appropriate for estrogen metabolism support"]}, {"dose": "3 capsules daily (divided doses)", "name": "Magtein® Magnesium L-Threonate", "caution": "L-Threonate form crosses blood-brain barrier for cognitive and sleep support. Well-tolerated; gentle on GI system.", "category": "mineral", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Magtein is the only magnesium form clinically shown to cross the blood-brain barrier. Supports quality sleep, cognitive function, GABA activity, and stress resilience.", "indication": "Low energy reported; Brain fog reported", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This special form of magnesium is designed to reach the brain, where it supports better sleep quality, mental clarity, and a calmer nervous system.", "supportingFindings": ["Low energy reported", "Brain fog reported"]}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Essential for patients on statins.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "low", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)", "phenotypes": ["Oxidative Stress Burden"], "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\n- Your hormone tests suggest early perimenopausal changes: progesterone is low at 0.33 ng/mL while estradiol is 61 pg/mL, which fits a pattern called luteal progesterone deficiency. Free testosterone is mildly high at 7 pg/mL while total testosterone is 55.9 ng/dL (below the HRT target of 75–125 ng/dL). Most other labs are reassuring.\\n\\nPositive findings (what’s good)\\n- Estradiol: 61 pg/mL (in the target HRT range 60–100 pg/mL).\\n- Ferritin: 145 ng/mL (good iron stores; goal >50 ng/mL).\\n- Vitamin D (25-OH): 71.7 ng/mL (optimal; goal 60–100 ng/mL).\\n- TSH: 1.21 mIU/L (euthyroid).\\n- Hemoglobin: 15.4 g/dL and Platelets: 255 K/uL (both normal).\\n- Liver enzymes AST 21 U/L and ALT 21 U/L are normal.\\n- Kidney function eGFR 85 mL/min and Creatinine 0.88 mg/dL are normal.\\n- SHBG 54.2 nmol/L is in the normal range.\\n- Sleep apnea risk (STOP-BANG) = 1 (low risk).\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + concrete actions)\\n- Progesterone: 0.33 ng/mL — this is below the expected luteal level and below typical HRT therapeutic range. Action: If your cycle was in the luteal phase when this was drawn, recheck mid-luteal (about 7 days before your expected period; day 21 for a 28-day cycle) to confirm anovulation. If low progesterone is confirmed and you have symptoms (irregular periods, heavy bleeding, sleep or mood changes), discuss cyclical bioidentical progesterone or other progestogen options with your clinician. If you start progesterone therapy, plan follow-up labs or symptom review in 6–12 weeks.\\n- Free Testosterone: 7 pg/mL (elevated) and Total Testosterone: 55.9 ng/dL (below HRT goal). Elevated free testosterone can cause acne, hair thinning, or unwanted hair. Actions you can start now:\\n  - Sleep: aim for 7–8 hours nightly to support hormone balance.\\n  - Strength training: 2–3 sessions weekly (bodyweight squats, lunges, resistance bands) to improve muscle, metabolism, and testosterone balance.\\n  - Stress management: 10–20 minutes daily of meditation, walking, or breathing exercises.\\n  - Review with your provider about hormone adjustments (estrogen/progesterone balance, possible anti-androgen options) rather than starting any medication yourself.\\n- Hematocrit: 44.5% (slightly high). Action: hydrate well before repeat blood draw and recheck in 4–8 weeks. If it remains high, we’ll evaluate for other causes.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- If this progesterone draw was not timed to your cycle, repeat a mid-luteal progesterone (about 7 days before expected period) in your next cycle.\\n- If low progesterone is confirmed and symptoms persist, schedule a visit within 4–8 weeks to discuss treatment options.\\n- Repeat hematocrit in 4–8 weeks after hydrating before the draw.\\n- Continue Vitamin D at current dosing and aim for dietary calcium 1,000–1,200 mg/day plus weight-bearing exercise 3x/week for bone health.\\n- Keep a symptom diary (period dates, flow, acne, hair changes, sleep, mood) and bring it to your follow-up.\\n\\nYou’re doing well on many measures — these targeted steps can help balance hormones and protect bone and heart health. Please call to schedule the follow-up lab timing or a clinic visit to review treatment options.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 15.4, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 44.5, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Slightly elevated hematocrit - may indicate dehydration.", "recommendation": "Encourage hydration. Repeat if persistent.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 255, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 61, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol at optimal HRT goal (60-100 pg/mL).", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Optimal level for HRT. Maintain current dosing. Bone protection achieved.", "referenceRange": "60-100 pg/mL (HRT goal, >40 for bone)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.33, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone below HRT therapeutic range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase progesterone dose. Target 8-10 ng/mL for optimal endometrial protection.", "referenceRange": "8-10 ng/mL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 55.9, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone below optimal HRT goal (75-125 ng/dL).", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Consider increasing testosterone dose for optimized results if symptomatic.", "referenceRange": "75-125 ng/dL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.21, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 145, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 71.7, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D optimal (60-100 ng/mL). Supports hormone, bone, and immune health.", "recommendation": "Maintain current regimen. Monitor annually.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 21, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 21, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 85, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.88, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 256, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 110, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.33, "status": "borderline", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Low Progesterone (Phase Unknown)", "interpretation": "Progesterone is 0.33 ng/mL. Without cycle phase documentation, interpretation is limited. If drawn in luteal phase, this would indicate anovulation - a perimenopause marker.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Document cycle phase for accurate interpretation. If patient is mid-cycle or luteal, this suggests anovulation. Consider symptoms: sleep disruption, anxiety, irregular cycles. PATIENT EDUCATION: We need to know where you are in your menstrual cycle to fully interpret this result. Please note when your last period started.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase; <5 ng/mL suggests anovulation if luteal"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.33, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Luteal Progesterone Deficiency", "interpretation": "Progesterone of 0.33 ng/mL with estradiol of 61 pg/mL in a woman 39 years old indicates luteal progesterone deficiency. Estrogen production is adequate but progesterone is not keeping pace - a hallmark of perimenopause.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Micronized progesterone 100-200 mg nightly. Dosing can be cyclic (days 14-28) or continuous depending on cycle regularity. Progesterone provides GABAergic support for sleep and anxiety relief. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your body is making enough estrogen, but progesterone has dropped. This is very common in perimenopause and causes sleep disruption, anxiety, and mood changes. Progesterone supplementation supports your nervous system and helps restore calm, restful sleep.", "referenceRange": "P4 ≥5 ng/mL expected with E2 ≥60 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 54.2, "status": "normal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 54.2 nmol/L is within normal range. SHBG regulates bioavailable sex hormones by binding testosterone and estradiol.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 7, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 7 pg/mL is elevated. May cause androgenic symptoms (acne, hirsutism, hair thinning).", "recommendation": "Evaluate for PCOS, exogenous testosterone, or low SHBG driving elevated free T.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (1/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}], "aiRecommendations": "Clinical context note (for staff): These recommendations are for clinician review and action. Do not implement medication changes without provider sign-off. Confirm current HRT formulation, route, and exact doses from the chart before ordering or changing therapy.\\n\\n1) Overall clinical assessment\\n- Progesterone 0.33 ng/mL: markedly below the clinic therapeutic target (8–10 ng/mL) for endometrial protection in HRT. In the context of a 39-year-old with estradiol 61 pg/mL, this pattern suggests luteal progesterone deficiency / anovulation and is consistent with perimenopause if the blood draw was in the luteal phase.\\n- Total testosterone 55.9 ng/dL: below the clinic optimal HRT target (75–125 ng/dL).\\n- Free testosterone 7 pg/mL: elevated relative to expected female range and discordant with low total testosterone — warrants further evaluation for assay timing/technique, exogenous testosterone exposure, SHBG dynamics, or adrenal contribution.\\n- Hematocrit 44.5%: borderline-high; likely dehydration or hemoconcentration — not an immediate red flag at this value but should be reassessed if persistent or if patient on testosterone.\\n- Other labs (TSH, ferritin, vitamin D, hepatic/renal panels, platelets, hemoglobin) are within expected limits per report.\\n\\n2) Priority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL\\n- Provider notification required now (phone or electronic message) to review and order progesterone dose change given subtherapeutic progesterone with target goal for endometrial protection.\\n- Do NOT increase testosterone dose before provider review due to discordant total vs free testosterone and elevated free T. Provider must confirm route/dose of current testosterone and review for exogenous exposure.\\n\\nURGENT (within 1–2 weeks)\\n- Staff: Document menstrual cycle timing in chart and confirm date of last menstrual period (LMP) and whether the blood draw was timed to a cycle phase (follicular, mid-cycle, luteal). If unknown, contact patient for cycle-day data ASAP — cycle context is essential for interpreting progesterone.\\n- If patient reports current use of topical testosterone creams, gels, or recent pellet implants, notify provider urgently (risk of elevated free T or contamination).\\n\\nROUTINE (next 2–8 weeks)\\n- Encourage hydration now; repeat hematocrit/Hb in 2–4 weeks if clinically indicated or if patient reports dehydration symptoms.\\n- Plan for endocrine/hormone follow-up per recommendations below.\\n\\n3) Specific medication/dose recommendations (for provider review and order)\\nProgesterone (PROVIDER TO SIGN ORDER)\\n- Micronized progesterone 100–200 mg nightly is recommended per protocol.\\n  - If cycles are regular and patient prefers cyclic dosing: prescribe micronized progesterone 200 mg nightly on days 14–28 of cycle (or adjust to provider’s standard cyclic regimen).\\n  - If cycles are irregular or continuous endometrial protection is desired: prescribe micronized progesterone 100–200 mg nightly continuous.\\n- Rationale: target serum progesterone approx 8–10 ng/mL for optimal endometrial protection and symptomatic benefit (sleep/anxiety).\\n\\nTestosterone (PROVIDER DECISION — DO NOT CHANGE WITHOUT REVIEW)\\n- Do not empirically increase testosterone because free T is elevated. Provider should:\\n  - Verify current testosterone formulation, dose, timing, and recent adherence.\\n  - If provider elects to optimize testosterone after review and after excluding causes of elevated free T, use one of the following conditional adjustments (choose based on current route):\\n    - Transdermal gel/cream: consider increasing by 25–50% of the current daily dose, then recheck labs. (Example: if patient is on 0.5 mg/day, consider increasing to ~0.6–0.75 mg/day.) \\n    - Transdermal patch: consider increasing by one incremental patch strength per manufacturer guidance.\\n    - Injectable/subcutaneous preparations: consider small incremental increases (e.g., +10–20 mg per dose or increase frequency modestly) guided by current regimen and tolerability.\\n  - Recheck total testosterone, free testosterone and SHBG 4–6 weeks after any dose change.\\n- If patient has clinical androgen excess (acne, hirsutism, hair loss) or if free T remains elevated, consider decreasing or holding exogenous testosterone instead of increasing.\\n\\n4) Laboratory follow-up timing and monitoring\\n- Progesterone: recheck level 4 weeks after initiating/increasing progesterone. If menstrual cycles present, preferentially obtain a mid-luteal progesterone (approx 7 days before expected menses) to confirm luteal adequacy; if dosing continuous, obtain random trough level at 4 weeks to ensure levels approaching therapeutic range.\\n- Testosterone panel: repeat total testosterone, free testosterone, and SHBG 4–6 weeks after any testosterone dose adjustment. If no dose change, repeat in 6–8 weeks for clarification.\\n- Hematocrit/Hgb: recheck in 2–4 weeks if hydration was suboptimal or if testosterone dose is escalated (testosterone can raise hematocrit). Notify provider if Hct rises above clinic safety thresholds (e.g., >50–52% per local protocol).\\n- Additional endocrine labs (see next section) — order timing as clinically appropriate; try to obtain morning (AM), fasting samples and document cycle day where relevant.\\n\\n5) Additional testing and referrals to consider\\n- Immediate lab/reflex testing:\\n  - Repeat total T, free T, and SHBG in a morning sample; document timing and fasting status.\\n  - Serum DHEA-S and androstenedione to evaluate adrenal contribution if free T remains elevated.\\n  - If PCOS suspected (irregular cycles, oligomenorrhea, hyperandrogenic signs): order early-follicular (cycle days 2–5) FSH/LH, fasting insulin and glucose or HbA1c, and consider pelvic ultrasound for polycystic ovarian morphology.\\n  - Consider pregnancy test (urine or serum) if patient of reproductive potential and before initiating/enhancing progesterone or making HRT changes.\\n- Consults/referrals:\\n  - Endocrinology or Reproductive Endocrinology if discordant testosterone values persist, complex androgen excess, or if fertility preservation/assistance is a concern.\\n  - Dermatology or cosmetology referral for management of hirsutism/acne if clinically indicated.\\n- Fertility assessment:\\n  - If fertility assessment desired or patient is concerned: add AMH and early follicular FSH if not already available.\\n- Medication reconciliation: confirm topical HRT products (creams/patches/pellets) and OTC androgen-containing supplements; remove any exogenous testosterone exposure if contributing to elevated free T.\\n\\n6) Lifestyle interventions to communicate to patient (staff to provide as education points; document in chart)\\n- Hydration: advise increased oral fluid intake and reassessment of Hct in 2–4 weeks.\\n- Sleep and anxiety management: advise sleep hygiene measures and discuss that progesterone can improve sleep and anxiety; counsel on sedation risk with nighttime dosing.\\n- Androgen/PCOS risk reduction (if hyperandrogenic signs or elevated free T): recommend weight management, regular moderate exercise, low-glycemic diet to improve insulin sensitivity, and avoidance of androgenic over-the-counter supplements.\\n- Skin/hair management: discuss topical skin care for acne and referral options for hirsutism management if present.\\n- Medication/application hygiene: if using topical testosterone, remind patient to wash hands after application and avoid skin contact with others to reduce transfer.\\n\\n7) Summary of next steps and timeline (ordered by priority)\\n- Immediate (today)\\n  - Provider notified to review progesterone result and to order progesterone dose increase (micronized progesterone 100–200 mg nightly; cyclic vs continuous per provider decision).\\n  - Staff to contact patient to document last menstrual period and cycle day of blood draw; confirm current HRT products, routes, doses, and any topical testosterone use or pellet history.\\n  - Advise patient to increase fluids; document hydration counseling.\\n\\n- Short term (within 1–2 weeks)\\n  - If pregnancy possible: perform pregnancy test before making HRT changes.\\n  - If topical testosterone exposure suspected, advise temporary discontinuation until provider review (only if ordered by provider).\\n\\n- Near term (4 weeks)\\n  - Recheck progesterone level 4 weeks after new progesterone dosing (timing: mid-luteal if cyclic; trough/random if continuous per provider guidance).\\n  - Recheck Hct/Hb if clinically indicated or if symptoms of dehydration persist.\\n\\n- Near–medium term (4–6 weeks after any testosterone change)\\n  - Repeat total testosterone, free testosterone, and SHBG (morning sample). Adjust therapy based on results and symptoms.\\n\\n- Further evaluation (within 4–8 weeks as indicated)\\n  - Order DHEA-S, androstenedione ± early-follicular FSH/LH, fasting insulin or HbA1c, and pelvic ultrasound if free T remains elevated or PCOS suspected.\\n  - Refer to Endocrinology/Reproductive Endocrinology if discordant labs persist or patient has complex symptoms.\\n\\nPhysician notification triggers (staff)\\n- Notify provider immediately: progesterone 0.33 ng/mL (subtherapeutic for endometrial protection) — provider to order dose adjustment.\\n- Notify provider prior to any testosterone dose increase because of discordant low total vs elevated free testosterone.\\n- Notify provider urgently if hematocrit rises >50% or if patient develops symptoms suggestive of polycythemia (headache, visual changes, thrombosis) after testosterone adjustment.\\n\\nDocumentation checklist for staff (prior to provider action)\\n- Confirm and document: LMP/cycle day; current HRT products with exact doses & route; symptoms of androgen excess; pregnancy status; hydration status.\\n- Add lab recheck orders to chart per provider plan and schedule follow-up visit or telehealth for results review.\\n\\nEnd of recommendations.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "SHBG", "value": 54.2, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 21, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Lab pattern consistent with relative estrogen excess or impaired estrogen clearance. Even without reported symptoms, supporting liver detoxification and estrogen metabolism pathways may be beneficial.", "supportingFindings": ["Progesterone 0.33 ng/mL (very low)", "Borderline liver markers (ALT 21, AST 21) suggesting possible impaired hepatic clearance"]}, {"name": "Oxidative Stress Burden", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Multiple markers suggest increased oxidative stress and cellular burden. Antioxidant and glutathione support may help reduce oxidative damage and support cellular energy production.", "supportingFindings": ["ALT 21 U/L (hepatic stress marker)", "AST 21 U/L (cellular stress marker)", "Low energy / fatigue (possible mitochondrial burden)", "Brain fog reported (possible oxidative CNS burden)"]}]}	\N	2026-03-12 15:42:23.731531	2026-03-12 15:42:23.731531
