Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support", "caution": "Contains iron, folate, and B12 for comprehensive RBC support. Avoid taking with calcium-rich foods or dairy. May cause mild GI upset initially.", "category": "iron", "priority": "high", "rationale": "Hemagenics provides highly absorbable iron bisglycinate with B12, B6, and folate for comprehensive red blood cell support. Indicated for functional iron deficiency, iron deficiency without anemia, and iron-responsive symptoms.", "indication": "Ferritin 21.8 ng/mL (iron deficiency ≤30); Serum iron 49 µg/dL (low <60)", "phenotypes": ["Iron Deficiency"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "Your lab results suggest your iron stores are lower than optimal. This supplement provides a gentle, well-absorbed form of iron along with B12 and folate to support your energy levels, hair health, and overall vitality.", "supportingFindings": ["Ferritin 21.8 ng/mL (iron deficiency ≤30)", "Serum iron 49 µg/dL (low <60)"]}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Essential for patients on statins.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "ApoB 105 mg/dL (cardiovascular support); hs-CRP 3 mg/L; LDL 133 mg/dL; TC 216 mg/dL", "phenotypes": ["Inflammatory Burden", "Oxidative Stress Burden"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["ApoB 105 mg/dL (cardiovascular support)", "hs-CRP 3 mg/L", "LDL 133 mg/dL", "TC 216 mg/dL", "Lp(a) 74 (elevated)"]}, {"dose": "2 capsules twice daily", "name": "AdvaClear®", "caution": "Broad-spectrum phase I/II liver detoxification support. Contains NAC, green tea extract, milk thistle, and B-vitamins for biotransformation. Best taken with food.", "category": "detox", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "AdvaClear provides broad-spectrum phase I and phase II liver detoxification support with NAC, green tea extract, watercress, and B-vitamins. Designed for metabolic clearance support, biotransformation of hormones and environmental compounds, and hepatic stress. Appropriate when liver markers are borderline, estrogen clearance is sluggish, or metabolic dysfunction is present.", "indication": "Oxidative stress burden (liver biotransformation support); Inflammatory burden (metabolic clearance support); Insulin resistance / metabolic dysfunction (hepatic metabolic support); hs-CRP 3 mg/L (inflammatory metabolic burden)", "phenotypes": ["Oxidative Stress Burden", "Inflammatory Burden", "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement supports your liver's natural detoxification processes. Your results suggest your body could benefit from help processing hormones, metabolic byproducts, and other compounds. It provides nutrients that support both phases of your liver's detox pathways.", "supportingFindings": ["Oxidative stress burden (liver biotransformation support)", "Inflammatory burden (metabolic clearance support)", "Insulin resistance / metabolic dysfunction (hepatic metabolic support)", "hs-CRP 3 mg/L (inflammatory metabolic burden)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Healthy Weight with Akkermansia", "caution": "Contains Akkermansia muciniphila and Bifidobacterium lactis B420 for metabolic and gut barrier support. Refrigerate after opening for maximum potency.", "category": "metabolic", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "UltraFlora Healthy Weight with Akkermansia provides targeted probiotic support for the gut-metabolic axis. Akkermansia muciniphila supports gut barrier integrity, metabolic health, and healthy weight management. Indicated for insulin resistance, metabolic syndrome features, visceral adiposity, and weight loss resistance.", "indication": "Insulin resistance / visceral adiposity phenotype (gut-metabolic axis support); Inflammatory phenotype (gut-immune axis); ApoB 105 mg/dL (atherogenic metabolic pattern); hs-CRP 3 mg/L (metabolic inflammation)", "phenotypes": ["Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "Inflammatory Burden"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This specialized probiotic contains Akkermansia, a beneficial gut bacteria linked to healthy metabolism and weight management. It supports your gut barrier, helps manage metabolic inflammation, and works with your body's natural weight regulation systems.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance / visceral adiposity phenotype (gut-metabolic axis support)", "Inflammatory phenotype (gut-immune axis)", "ApoB 105 mg/dL (atherogenic metabolic pattern)", "hs-CRP 3 mg/L (metabolic inflammation)"]}, {"dose": "2 tablets twice daily", "name": "EstroFactors™", "caution": "Nutritional support for estrogen balance and metabolism. Contains I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Supports healthy estrogen metabolite ratios.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "EstroFactors provides targeted nutritional support for estrogen metabolism and balance with I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Best suited for women with estrogen dominance patterns, PMS, perimenopausal hormone imbalance, or those needing broader estrogen metabolism support during hormonal transitions. Differs from Estrovera in that it addresses the underlying estrogen metabolism rather than vasomotor symptom relief.", "indication": "PMS symptoms reported; Age-appropriate for estrogen metabolism support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This supplement helps your body process and balance estrogen more effectively. It supports healthy hormone metabolism, which can help with PMS symptoms, bloating, mood changes, and hormonal balance during life transitions.", "supportingFindings": ["PMS symptoms reported", "Age-appropriate for estrogen metabolism support"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 74, "apoBValue": 105, "lpaStatus": "elevated", "apoBStatus": "borderline", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 0.781492084493848, "hasElevatedLpa": true, "statinGuidance": "Possibly consider statin therapy given risk enhancement; shared decision-making recommended.", "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "reclassified_upward", "clinicalGuidance": "Lp(a) 74 mg/dL - RISK ENHANCER (genetic risk factor). RECLASSIFIED TO BORDERLINE RISK: Risk enhancer present. Shared decision-making regarding statin therapy.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "cacRecommendation": "CAC reasonable especially with strong family history and risk enhancement present.", "hasBorderlineApoB": true}, "cacStatinRec": {"lpaWarning": "Elevated Lp(a) of 74 mg/dL is a genetic cardiovascular risk marker. This level is associated with increased ASCVD risk independent of LDL. Since Lp(a) cannot be significantly lowered, focus on aggressive LDL/ApoB reduction and lifestyle optimization.", "statinDiscussion": {"strength": "generally_recommended", "indicated": true, "rationale": "ApoB ≥90 mg/dL (105 mg/dL) with risk enhancers (elevated Lp(a), elevated ApoB). Statin discussion recommended."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "6-8 weeks (iron supplementation monitoring)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\nYour 10-year ASCVD risk is low at 0.8% and your 10-year total CVD risk is 1.2%, which is reassuring. You have a few important findings to address: low iron stores (ferritin 21.8 ng/mL), an elevated lipoprotein(a) (Lp(a) 74 mg/dL), and high SHBG (360 nmol/L). Your hemoglobin is normal (12.7 g/dL), so you do not have anemia right now. Your vitamin D is optimal (60.9 ng/mL) and thyroid function (TSH 2.51 mIU/L) is normal. Your hormone levels (estradiol 54 pg/mL, progesterone 0.39 ng/mL, FSH 7.64 mIU/mL) are appropriate for the follicular/pre‑ovulatory phase of your cycle.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Hemoglobin 12.7 g/dL and hematocrit 39.6% – no anemia.\\n- Vitamin D 60.9 ng/mL – supports bone and immune health.\\n- HDL 65 mg/dL and triglycerides 89 mg/dL – favorable lipid components.\\n- Kidney and liver tests normal (eGFR 80 mL/min; AST 20 U/L; ALT 17 U/L).\\n\\nAreas to improve (with concrete actions)\\n1) Iron stores low: Ferritin 21.8 ng/mL (iron deficiency without anemia).\\n   - Diet: Eat 1 serving (~3–4 oz) lean red meat or poultry 2–3× weekly, add iron-rich plant foods (beans, lentils, fortified cereal), and include vitamin C (orange juice, bell pepper) with iron meals to boost absorption.\\n   - Timing: Avoid coffee or tea with iron-rich meals; wait 1–2 hours.\\n   - Supplements: Discuss starting oral iron (typical options provide ~45–65 mg elemental iron daily or 60–65 mg every other day to reduce side effects) with your clinician.\\n   - Follow-up: Repeat ferritin and CBC in 8–12 weeks after starting treatment.\\n\\n2) Heart risk markers: LDL 133 mg/dL, ApoB 105 mg/dL, hs‑CRP 3 mg/L, Lp(a) 74 mg/dL.\\n   - Diet: Add 25–30 g fiber daily (oatmeal for breakfast, beans at lunch, vegetables at dinner). Follow a Mediterranean-style diet, limit processed foods and saturated fat (choose lean meats, limit butter/cheese).\\n   - Fish: Include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n   - Exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking daily (or 150 minutes moderate activity weekly).\\n   - Discuss family history of early heart disease with your clinician; Lp(a) is mostly genetic and may prompt specialist review.\\n\\n3) Hormone binding: SHBG 360 nmol/L (elevated).\\n   - Review medications: discuss oral estrogen/oral contraceptives, thyroid meds, or supplements with your provider as possible contributors.\\n   - If you have symptoms (low energy, low libido), discuss further hormone testing and management.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Discuss starting iron therapy and review medications that may raise SHBG at your next visit within 2–4 weeks.\\n- Repeat ferritin and CBC in 8–12 weeks after any iron start.\\n- Repeat fasting lipid panel in 3–6 months after lifestyle changes.\\n- If you have family history of early CVD or symptoms, consider referral to cardiology for Lp(a) counseling.\\n\\nYou’re doing well in many areas—small diet and exercise steps and treating low iron can make a big difference. If you’d like, we can schedule a follow-up to make a specific plan.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 12.7, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 39.6, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 95.4, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 95.4 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 270, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 54, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol appropriate for follicular phase.", "recommendation": "Normal finding. Continue monitoring if needed for fertility.", "referenceRange": "20-150 pg/mL (follicular phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.39, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone appropriate for pre-ovulatory phase.", "recommendation": "Normal finding.", "referenceRange": "<1.5 ng/mL (pre-ovulation)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 7.64, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH appropriate for follicular phase.", "recommendation": "Normal finding.", "referenceRange": "3-10 mIU/mL (follicular phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 54.7, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Upper normal testosterone - may be elevated.", "recommendation": "Correlate with clinical signs (hirsutism, acne). Consider PCOS workup.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 2.51, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 21.8, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Iron deficiency without anemia (ferritin ≤30).", "recommendation": "Provider protocol: Metagenics Hemagenics OR Prescription 65mg Elemental Iron (take every other day, on empty stomach, avoid caffeine and dairy 2 hours before/after).", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 60.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D optimal (60-100 ng/mL). Supports hormone, bone, and immune health.", "recommendation": "Maintain current regimen. Monitor annually.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 703, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 4.7, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 133, "status": "borderline", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Borderline high LDL.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider statin if additional risk factors.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 65, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 89, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 1.224518119575854, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 1.2% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.781492084493848, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 0.8%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.5046192575667449, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.5%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (1/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 9.325132400040731, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 9.3%, ASCVD 5.4%, Heart Failure 5.0%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 20, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 17, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 80, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.93, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 105, "status": "borderline", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Borderline elevated Apo B.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider pharmacotherapy if high ASCVD risk.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 74, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Elevated Lp(a) - RISK ENHANCER. Increases CVD risk category.", "recommendation": "Lp(a) ≥50 mg/dL is a risk enhancer - consider upgrading CVD risk category. Aggressive LDL lowering indicated. Discuss hereditary nature.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 409, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 49, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 17.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 3, "status": "borderline", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Borderline hs-CRP - moderate cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Continue monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 360, "status": "abnormal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 360 nmol/L is elevated. High SHBG reduces bioavailable testosterone and estrogen. Common causes: oral estrogen, oral contraceptives, thyroid medication, liver conditions.", "recommendation": "Evaluate SHBG drivers (oral estrogen, OCP, thyroid medication). Consider ordering Bioavailable Testosterone for complete androgen assessment.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 1 pg/mL is within normal functional range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "of 6 markers positive", "value": 2, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Insulin Resistance Screening", "recheckTiming": "3-6 months", "interpretation": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive). This combination points toward hyperinsulinemia physiology. Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR).", "recommendation": "Phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance. Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose.", "referenceRange": "0-1 Low, 2 Moderate, 3+ High Likelihood"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- Key abnormalities: borderline-high Total Testosterone 54.7 ng/dL; markedly elevated SHBG 360 nmol/L; ferritin low at 21.8 ng/mL (iron deficiency without anemia); LDL 133 mg/dL and ApoB 105 mg/dL (borderline); Lp(a) 74 mg/dL (clinically significant risk enhancer); hs-CRP 3 mg/L (low-grade inflammation).\\n- Interpretation: SHBG is markedly elevated and likely lowers bioavailable androgen despite borderline total testosterone; free testosterone is reported normal. Ferritin indicates iron deficiency requiring treatment. Lipid profile with elevated Lp(a) and borderline LDL/ApoB increases lifetime CVD risk; consider more aggressive lipid management if clinical risk factors or family history present.\\n- Correlate androgen findings with clinical signs (hirsutism, acne, menstrual pattern). Consider PCOS only if clinical criteria (oligo/anovulation, hyperandrogenism) or supporting labs (e.g., elevated free/bioavailable T, AMH, ultrasound) are present.\\n\\nPriority actions (organized CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL (notify MD immediately)\\n- Notify physician promptly about Lp(a) 74 mg/dL (≥50 mg/dL considered a risk enhancer) and ApoB 105 mg/dL. These findings may change CVD risk categorization and influence decisions about pharmacologic lipid-lowering therapy.\\n- Notify physician if patient has history of premature ASCVD in first-degree relative or other high-risk features — escalate lipid management (possible immediate statin discussion).\\n\\nURGENT (action within 1–2 weeks)\\n- Initiate iron therapy per clinic protocol for ferritin 21.8 ng/mL:\\n  * Metagenics Hemagenics OR prescription elemental iron 65 mg every other day (e.g., ferrous sulfate 325 mg PO every other day) on an empty stomach. Counsel to avoid caffeine and dairy 2 hours before/after dosing and to take with vitamin C or orange juice to enhance absorption. Advise on constipation side effects and stool changes; consider stool softener if needed.\\n  * Order recheck ferritin and CBC in 8–12 weeks after starting iron therapy.\\n- Review current medication list for drivers of high SHBG (oral estrogen, combined oral contraceptive, oral estrogen replacement, thyroid hormone dosing, hepatic disease). Flag for physician review if patient is taking oral estrogen/OCP or thyroid replacement.\\n\\nROUTINE (action within 4–12 weeks unless escalated)\\n- Lipid management:\\n  * Provide lifestyle counseling now (see Lifestyle section). Reassess lipids and ApoB in 3 months after lifestyle changes or sooner (4–6 weeks) if pharmacotherapy is initiated.\\n  * If clinician elects pharmacotherapy due to Lp(a) and ApoB elevation or family history/premature ASCVD, consider initiating moderate- to high-intensity statin per physician decision (examples for provider consideration: atorvastatin 20–40 mg nightly or rosuvastatin 10–20 mg nightly). Discuss with physician — do not initiate without MD order.\\n  * Consider cardiology referral if Lp(a) very high or family/personal history of premature CVD, or if LDL remains above goal despite statin (discuss PCSK9 inhibitor or other advanced therapies).\\n- Inflammation: Repeat hs-CRP after 3 months of lifestyle interventions or sooner if clinical changes/infection.\\n\\nTest-specific and physician-notify triggers\\n- Notify physician: Lp(a) ≥50 mg/dL (here 74 mg/dL), ApoB ≥100 mg/dL, ferritin ≤30 ng/mL (here 21.8), markedly elevated SHBG (360 nmol/L).\\n- Order confirmation tests and targeted assessments as below.\\n\\nTestosterone-specific guidance and dosing recommendations (if applicable)\\n- Do not initiate or increase testosterone therapy at this time based on these labs alone.\\n- Rationale: Total testosterone borderline-high but free testosterone and other androgen-related labs reported normal; SHBG is markedly elevated which reduces bioavailable testosterone. Correlate with symptoms before any therapy change.\\n- Recommended next labs before any testosterone dosing adjustment:\\n  * Order bioavailable (calculated or direct) testosterone and repeat free testosterone if not contemporaneous with cycle timing.\\n  * Time repeat androgen testing in early follicular phase when feasible (cycle days 3–5) if patient is menstruating and not on exogenous androgens, and document cycle day.\\n- If patient is already on exogenous testosterone:\\n  * Do not empirically alter dose. Notify physician to consider measuring bioavailable T and symptom review; if dose adjustment is considered, recheck levels 6–8 weeks after any change.\\n- If after symptom correlation and bioavailable T confirms deficiency, discuss testosterone replacement with physician; example monitoring schedule (for MD): check total and free T 6–8 weeks after dose change, then every 3–6 months once stable.\\n\\nLifestyle interventions (staff to counsel and document)\\n- Diet: Recommend Mediterranean-style diet emphasizing vegetables, fruits, whole grains, lean protein, and reduced saturated fat and processed foods to help LDL and inflammation. Counsel on iron-rich foods (heme iron sources: red meat, poultry, fish; non-heme: legumes, fortified cereals) and pairing with vitamin C to improve iron absorption.\\n- Physical activity: Target ≥150 min/week moderate-intensity aerobic exercise + 2 sessions/week strength training.\\n- Weight management: Aim for 5–10% weight reduction if overweight — will improve lipid profile, insulin sensitivity, and possibly androgen profile.\\n- Smoking cessation if applicable.\\n- Alcohol: moderate intake; avoid heavy alcohol which raises triglycerides and may affect liver and SHBG.\\n- Sleep and stress: counsel on sleep hygiene and stress reduction; chronic inflammation and cardiometabolic risk are impacted by poor sleep and high stress.\\n- Iron-specific counseling: advise to take iron every other day on empty stomach when possible; take with vitamin C; avoid calcium, tea/coffee within 2 hours.\\n\\nAdditional testing, monitoring and referrals to consider\\n- Androgen workup:\\n  * Order bioavailable testosterone (direct or calculated) and repeat free testosterone if needed. Consider measuring SHBG drivers (review med list).\\n  * If clinical hyperandrogenism or menstrual irregularity suspected → check AMH, fasting insulin/glucose or 2-hr OGTT if insulin resistance suspected, pelvic ultrasound (ovarian morphology) and consider endocrine or reproductive specialist referral for PCOS evaluation.\\n- Confirmatory/repeat tests:\\n  * Lp(a): single elevated result typically reflects genetic predisposition; repeat once to confirm if clinically indicated, but usually one measurement is sufficient.\\n  * Lipids & ApoB: recheck in 3 months after lifestyle changes or 4–6 weeks after initiating statin.\\n  * Ferritin/CBC: recheck in 8–12 weeks after starting iron.\\n  * hs-CRP: repeat in 3 months or sooner if symptoms/infection.\\n- Specialty referrals:\\n  * Cardiology: consider if Lp(a) elevated with family history of premature ASCVD, or if considering advanced lipid-lowering therapy or if LDL/ApoB not controlled despite statin.\\n  * Endocrinology/Reproductive endocrinology: if persistent hyperandrogenic symptoms, abnormal ovarian imaging, or complex endocrine interactions (thyroid therapy adjustments).\\n  * Hematology or GI if iron therapy fails or there's evidence of bleeding — evaluate for source of blood loss (GI referral if warranted).\\n- Medication review: pharmacy/clinician to review current prescriptions for oral estrogens/OCPs or thyroid replacement that may elevate SHBG; discuss risk/benefit of changing formulation (e.g., transdermal estrogen has less effect on SHBG than oral estrogens).\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (staff checklist)\\n- Immediate (today/within 48–72 hrs)\\n  1. Notify physician about Lp(a) 74 mg/dL, ApoB 105 mg/dL, ferritin 21.8 ng/mL, and SHBG 360 nmol/L for clinical review and potential escalation.\\n  2. Start iron per protocol: Metagenics Hemagenics OR prescription elemental iron 65 mg every other day, empty stomach; counsel on interactions and side effects. Document counseling.\\n  3. Review med list for oral estrogen/OCP/thyroid meds and flag for MD review.\\n\\n- Short term (1–2 weeks)\\n  1. Order bioavailable testosterone and repeat free testosterone if needed; document menstrual cycle day when drawn.\\n  2. Arrange patient education on diet, exercise, iron dosing, and CVD risk (Lp[a] family implications).\\n\\n- Near term (4–12 weeks)\\n  1. Recheck fasting lipid panel + ApoB in 3 months or sooner if statin is started. If statin started, consider baseline LFTs per MD preference and follow-up lipid panel at 4–6 weeks.\\n  2. Recheck ferritin and CBC at 8–12 weeks after iron initiation.\\n  3. Repeat hs-CRP in 3 months after lifestyle measures.\\n\\n- Referral timeline\\n  1. Consider cardiology referral if family or personal premature CVD or if clinician plans advanced lipid therapy (refer within 4 weeks).\\n  2. Refer to endocrinology/reproductive specialist if clinical signs of hyperandrogenism persist or PCOS workup is positive (timing as clinically appropriate).\\n\\nDocumentation/communication points for provider\\n- Include lab values and note that Lp(a) 74 mg/dL is a risk enhancer and may change management despite otherwise normal 10-year ASCVD risk estimates.\\n- Note SHBG 360 nmol/L and request medication review (oral estrogen/OCP/oral thyroid) and bioavailable testosterone measurement prior to any androgen therapy decisions.\\n- Flag ferritin 21.8 ng/mL and that iron therapy has been initiated per clinic protocol; recheck planned at 8–12 weeks.\\n\\nLimitations / clinical caveats for MD review\\n- One elevated Lp(a) is usually sufficient (genetic), but clinical interpretation must consider family history and total risk.\\n- SHBG can be labile with exogenous hormones and thyroid dosing; labs should be interpreted in context of current meds and cycle timing.\\n- Do not start testosterone therapy based solely on total testosterone when SHBG is markedly elevated and free/bioavailable measures are normal — correlate clinically first.\\n\\nEnd of recommendations.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 1.37, "detail": "Triglycerides 89 / HDL 65 = 1.37", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name"