", "Oxidative Stress Burden"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["ApoB 123 mg/dL (cardiovascular support)", "Triglycerides 540 mg/dL", "HDL 40 mg/dL (low)", "TC 226 mg/dL", "Age 41 (declining CoQ10 production)"]}, {"dose": "2 capsules twice daily", "name": "AdvaClear®", "caution": "Broad-spectrum phase I/II liver detoxification support. Contains NAC, green tea extract, milk thistle, and B-vitamins for biotransformation. Best taken with food.", "category": "detox", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "AdvaClear provides broad-spectrum phase I and phase II liver detoxification support with NAC, green tea extract, watercress, and B-vitamins. Designed for metabolic clearance support, biotransformation of hormones and environmental compounds, and hepatic stress. Appropriate when liver markers are borderline, estrogen clearance is sluggish, or metabolic dysfunction is present.", "indication": "Oxidative stress burden (liver biotransformation support); Inflammatory burden (metabolic clearance support); Insulin resistance / metabolic dysfunction (hepatic metabolic support)", "phenotypes": ["Oxidative Stress Burden", "Inflammatory Burden", "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement supports your liver's natural detoxification processes. Your results suggest your body could benefit from help processing hormones, metabolic byproducts, and other compounds. It provides nutrients that support both phases of your liver's detox pathways.", "supportingFindings": ["Oxidative stress burden (liver biotransformation support)", "Inflammatory burden (metabolic clearance support)", "Insulin resistance / metabolic dysfunction (hepatic metabolic support)"]}, {"dose": "1 soft chew during times of stress", "name": "HerWellness™ Rapid Stress Relief", "caution": "Fast-acting L-Theanine and Lactium formula. Non-drowsy. Promotes calm within 1 hour. Contains milk.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "low", "rationale": "HerWellness Rapid Stress Relief provides fast-acting stress support with L-Theanine (200mg) and Lactium. Promotes calm within 1 hour without drowsiness.", "indication": "Irritability reported", "phenotypes": ["Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation"], "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This fast-acting supplement helps your body respond to stress more calmly. It promotes relaxation within about an hour and can be taken as needed without causing drowsiness.", "supportingFindings": ["Irritability reported"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 6, "apoBValue": 123, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "borderline", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 2.1150703746797577, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": true}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "generally_recommended", "indicated": true, "rationale": "ApoB ≥90 mg/dL (123 mg/dL) with risk enhancers (low HDL, elevated triglycerides, elevated ApoB). Statin discussion recommended."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": true, "severity": "very_high", "recommendation": "Triglycerides ≥500 mg/dL (540 mg/dL) - URGENT: High risk for pancreatitis. Fibrate therapy indicated. Rule out secondary causes (diabetes, alcohol, medications)."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "consider", "rationale": "Borderline lipid levels with multiple risk enhancers (low HDL, elevated triglycerides, elevated ApoB). Consider CAC to refine risk assessment.", "recommended": true}}, "recheckWindow": "6-8 weeks (iron supplementation monitoring)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\n- Some tests are normal, but there are a few important problems to address right away: very high triglycerides (540 mg/dL), low iron stores (ferritin 20.6 ng/mL), and severe vitamin D deficiency (25-OH vitamin D 10.3 ng/mL). Your hormone pattern (progesterone 0.11 ng/mL in the luteal phase) suggests this cycle was likely anovulatory, which is common in perimenopause and can explain symptoms like night sweats.\\n\\nPositive findings (what’s OK)\\n- Hemoglobin 13.5 g/dL and hematocrit 42.3% are within normal range.\\n- TSH 2.34 mIU/L shows normal thyroid function.\\n- Hemoglobin A1c 5.2% indicates normal blood sugar control.\\n- eGFR 113 mL/min and creatinine 0.66 mg/dL show healthy kidney function.\\n- hs-CRP 0.9 mg/L indicates low inflammation.\\n- 10-year ASCVD risk 2.1% and 10-year total CVD risk 3.1% (LOW risk).\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + concrete actions)\\n- Triglycerides 540 mg/dL (VERY HIGH — risk of pancreatitis): Contact your provider today or within 24–48 hours. Until seen: stop alcohol completely, cut simple carbs and sugary drinks, follow a very low-fat meal plan, and avoid crash diets. Your clinician may start prescription therapy (fibrate or high-dose EPA) and order an urgent repeat fasting lipid panel.\\n- Ferritin 20.6 ng/mL (iron deficiency without anemia): Eat iron-rich foods (red meat, poultry, beans, lentils, iron-fortified cereal). If approved by your clinician, start an oral iron supplement (take with vitamin C, avoid taking with calcium or coffee/tea). Recheck ferritin and CBC in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Vitamin D 25-OH 10.3 ng/mL (severe deficiency): Discuss starting high-dose therapy with your clinician (common approaches: prescription high-dose vitamin D or 2,000–5,000 IU daily if approved). Take with a meal containing fat. Recheck level in 8–12 weeks. Add weight-bearing exercise and aim for 1,000–1,200 mg calcium daily for bone health.\\n- Lipids and heart health: HDL 40 mg/dL (low/borderline) and ApoB 123 mg/dL with 30-year CVD risk 20.8% (higher long-term risk) — intensify lifestyle now: add 25–30 g fiber daily (oatmeal, beans, vegetables), include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly, reduce saturated fat (choose lean meats, limit butter/cheese), and aim for 30–60 minutes moderate activity most days. Statin not automatically required now; discuss with your provider given long-term risk.\\n- Hormones: Progesterone 0.11 ng/mL and estradiol 42 pg/mL suggest anovulation/luteal insufficiency. Track cycles and symptoms; discuss perimenopause management (non-hormonal or hormonal options) with your clinician. For low testosterone (total 12.7 ng/dL), prioritize 7–8 hours sleep, strength training 2–3× weekly, and stress reduction.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Contact clinic within 24–48 hours for triglycerides 540 mg/dL (urgent). Book follow-up within 1–2 weeks for treatment planning (lipid meds, iron, vitamin D). Recheck fasting lipid panel soon after any medication change; repeat ferritin/CBC and vitamin D in 8–12 weeks. If you take B12 supplements/injections, review with your clinician given B12 1245 pg/mL.\\n\\nYou’re doing well in many areas — these focused steps can significantly reduce short-term risks and improve long-term health.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 13.5, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 42.3, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 89.2, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 89.2 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 347, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 42, "status": "borderline", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Low estradiol in luteal phase.", "recommendation": "May indicate corpus luteum dysfunction. Correlate with progesterone.", "referenceRange": "50-250 pg/mL (luteal phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.11, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Low progesterone in luteal phase - luteal phase defect.", "recommendation": "May affect fertility/early pregnancy. Consider progesterone support if TTC.", "referenceRange": "5-20 ng/mL (luteal phase)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 7.59, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 12.7, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Low testosterone.", "recommendation": "May contribute to low libido or fatigue. Consider HRT discussion.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 2.34, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 20.6, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Iron deficiency without anemia (ferritin ≤30).", "recommendation": "Provider protocol: Metagenics Hemagenics OR Prescription 65mg Elemental Iron (take every other day, on empty stomach, avoid caffeine and dairy 2 hours before/after).", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 10.3, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Severe Vitamin D deficiency (≤20 ng/mL). Associated with fatigue, bone loss, and immune dysfunction.", "recommendation": "High-dose repletion: Metagenics D3 10,000 + K daily for 8-12 weeks. Recheck levels after repletion.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 1245, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "Elevated B12.", "recommendation": "Usually from supplementation. Rarely indicates liver disease.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.2, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 40, "status": "abnormal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Low HDL - increased cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Increase aerobic exercise. Consider niacin if very low.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 540, "status": "critical", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Very high triglycerides - pancreatitis risk.", "recommendation": "Strict diet. Consider fibrate therapy. Avoid alcohol.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 3.1137722939264796, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 3.1% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 2.1150703746797577, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 2.1%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.6883928899140677, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.7%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (1/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 20.765185514591806, "status": "borderline", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 20.8%, ASCVD 13.2%, Heart Failure 6.2%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 18, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 13, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 113, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.66, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 123, "status": "borderline", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Borderline elevated Apo B.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider pharmacotherapy if high ASCVD risk.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 6, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 411, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 48, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 8.39, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 0.9, "status": "normal", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Low cardiovascular inflammation risk.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.11, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Luteal Progesterone", "interpretation": "Luteal progesterone of 0.11 ng/mL indicates anovulation or luteal failure. This cycle was likely anovulatory - a common perimenopause finding.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Anovulatory cycle confirmed. If recurrent, this signals advancing perimenopause. Consider progesterone for symptom management: micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly continuously, or cyclically if patient prefers withdrawal bleeds. Discuss HRT options if symptomatic. PATIENT EDUCATION: This level indicates you likely did not ovulate this cycle, which is common in perimenopause. This can cause irregular periods, heavy bleeding, and other symptoms. Treatment is available to help regulate your cycles and relieve symptoms.", "referenceRange": "≥10 ng/mL optimal, 8-10 borderline, <8 low, <5 anovulatory"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 42, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Vasomotor Symptoms with Fluctuating Estrogen", "interpretation": "Vasomotor symptoms (night sweats) present despite estradiol level of 42 pg/mL. In perimenopause, estrogen can be spiky and fluctuating, causing vasomotor symptoms even when levels appear normal or high at time of draw.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Consider steady delivery: transdermal estradiol patch starting at 0.0375 mg/day (common starting dose for twice-weekly systems). Add micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly if patient has uterus. Goal is symptom control through stable hormone delivery. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your hot flashes and night sweats may be caused by fluctuating hormone levels rather than consistently low estrogen. A steady-release estrogen patch can help stabilize your levels and reduce symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Symptoms indicate hormonal instability despite E2 level"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 49.8, "status": "normal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 49.8 nmol/L is within normal range. SHBG regulates bioavailable sex hormones by binding testosterone and estradiol.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 2, "status": "normal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 2 pg/mL is within normal functional range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "of 6 markers positive", "value": 3, "status": "critical", "category": "Insulin Resistance Screening", "recheckTiming": "3-6 months", "interpretation": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: HIGH LIKELIHOOD (3/6 markers positive). Identified phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance, Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance. Treat according to phenotype-specific protocol.", "recommendation": "Phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance; Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance. Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose.", "referenceRange": "0-1 Low, 2 Moderate, 3+ High Likelihood"}], "aiRecommendations": "Unable to generate recommendations. Please review lab findings manually.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 13.5, "detail": "Triglycerides 540 / HDL 40 = 13.5", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5.2%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 49.8, "detail": "Low - hyperinsulinemia suppresses SHBG", "positive": true, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 13, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "123 mg/dL", "detail": "Elevated atherogenic particle count", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "0.9 mg/L", "detail": "Low inflammatory burden", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "high", "phenotypes": [{"key": "visceral_metabolic", "name": "Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Recheck A1c, lipid panel, hs-CRP, ApoB in 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL"], "pathophysiology": "Early peripheral + hepatic insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia. Glucose may still be \\"normal.\\" A1c often rises last.", "triggerCriteria": ["A1c >= 5.7%", "TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0", "Central adiposity phenotype", "ALT above optimal range"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests your body may not be responding to insulin as efficiently as it should. Insulin is the hormone that helps move sugar from your bloodstream into your cells for energy.\\n\\nWhen cells become less sensitive to insulin:\\n- Blood sugar slowly rises over time\\n- Triglycerides increase\\n- Inflammation increases\\n- Weight gain around the midsection becomes easier\\n\\nThe good news? This stage is highly reversible with targeted nutrition, strength training, sleep optimization, and - when appropriate - medication support.\\n\\nWe'll focus on improving how your body processes fuel and reducing long-term cardiometabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward nutrition (>= 25-35g/meal)", "Carbohydrate quality + timing education", "Strength training 2-3x weekly", "10-15 min post-meal walking", "Sleep optimization", "Consider: GLP-1 therapy if obesity, appetite dysregulation, A1c >= 5.7, or inflammatory phenotype", "Consider: Metformin if prediabetes or strong family history", "Recheck metabolic panel in 3-6 months"]}, {"key": "hormonal_pcos", "name": "Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Monitor SHBG, androgens, A1c every 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["SHBG low (<50)", "TG/HDL borderline-high"], "pathophysiology": "Hyperinsulinemia suppresses SHBG and increases ovarian androgen production.", "triggerCriteria": ["Low SHBG (<50; strong if <30)", "Elevated total or free testosterone", "Irregular cycles", "A1c may still be normal", "TG/HDL may be borderline-high"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests insulin may be influencing your hormone balance.\\n\\nWhen insulin levels run high:\\n- The body makes less SHBG (a protective hormone-binding protein)\\n- Androgen levels can rise\\n- Cycles may become irregular\\n\\nThis pattern is common in PCOS and other hormone imbalance states.\\n\\nImproving insulin sensitivity often helps restore hormonal balance and reduce long-term metabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward, lower refined carb approach", "Strength training", "Consider metformin if cycles irregular or A1c trending", "Consider GLP-1 if obesity phenotype present", "Monitor SHBG, androgens, A1c"]}], "positiveCount": 3, "likelihoodLabel": "HIGH LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: HIGH LIKELIHOOD (3/6 markers positive). Identified phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance, Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance. Treat according to phenotype-specific protocol.", "confirmationTests": "Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose."}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "confidence": "high", "description": "Metabolic pattern consistent with insulin resistance and/or visceral adiposity. This drives inflammation, hormonal imbalance, and cardiovascular 