re your levels to support bone health, immune function, and overall wellness.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 23.4 ng/mL (deficient)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 115, "apoBValue": 94, "lpaStatus": "elevated", "apoBStatus": "borderline", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 0.25479505005970404, "hasElevatedLpa": true, "statinGuidance": "Possibly consider statin therapy given risk enhancement; shared decision-making recommended.", "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "reclassified_upward", "clinicalGuidance": "Lp(a) 115 mg/dL - RISK ENHANCER (genetic risk factor). RECLASSIFIED TO BORDERLINE RISK: Risk enhancer present. Shared decision-making regarding statin therapy.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "cacRecommendation": "CAC reasonable especially with strong family history and risk enhancement present.", "hasBorderlineApoB": true}, "cacStatinRec": {"lpaWarning": "Elevated Lp(a) of 115 mg/dL is a genetic cardiovascular risk marker. This level is associated with increased ASCVD risk independent of LDL. Since Lp(a) cannot be significantly lowered, focus on aggressive LDL/ApoB reduction and lifestyle optimization.", "statinDiscussion": {"strength": "generally_recommended", "indicated": true, "rationale": "ApoB ≥90 mg/dL (94 mg/dL) with risk enhancers (prediabetes/metabolic syndrome, elevated Lp(a), elevated ApoB). Statin discussion recommended."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "strongly_recommend", "rationale": "Elevated Lp(a) of 115 mg/dL is a genetic risk marker. CAC strongly recommended to assess arterial calcium burden and inform treatment intensity.", "recommended": true}}, "recheckWindow": "3 months (glycemic monitoring)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\nYour tests show that you are in menopause (Estradiol 5 pg/mL, FSH 122 mIU/mL) and experiencing symptoms consistent with a low-estrogen state (night sweats). Cardiovascular risk is low (10-year ASCVD risk 0.3%), but you have two important findings to address: low Vitamin D (25-OH 23.4 ng/mL) and a high Lipoprotein(a) (Lp(a) 115 mg/dL), which is a risk enhancer. A1c is 5.7% (prediabetes range). Many other results are reassuring.\\n\\nPositive findings (what’s healthy right now)\\n- Hemoglobin 13.2 g/dL and hematocrit 39.7% — no anemia.\\n- HDL 139 mg/dL and triglycerides 64 mg/dL — protective lipid picture.\\n- TSH 0.96 mIU/L — thyroid function normal.\\n- Vitamin B12 520 pg/mL and folate 8.47 ng/mL — adequate.\\n- eGFR 97 mL/min and creatinine 0.76 mg/dL — normal kidney function.\\n- 10-year total CVD risk 0.4% (ASCVD 0.3%) — overall low short-term heart risk.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + actions)\\n- Vitamin D 25-OH 23.4 ng/mL (deficient/suboptimal): Start Vitamin D3 2,000 IU daily, eat fatty fish (salmon, sardines), and get 10–20 minutes sun exposure on arms/legs several times weekly. Recheck 25-OH in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Lipoprotein(a) 115 mg/dL (elevated): This raises lifetime CVD risk even though your 10-year risk is low. Discuss Lp(a) with your clinician—consider family history review and possible referral to a lipid specialist. Maintain heart-healthy lifestyle: 25–30 g fiber/day (oatmeal breakfast, beans lunch, veggies dinner), 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly, reduce saturated fat (lean meats, limit butter/cheese), and 30 minutes daily brisk walking.\\n- A1c 5.7% (prediabetes): Reduce refined carbs (swap white bread for whole grain, limit sugary drinks), include protein with each meal, and walk 10–15 minutes after meals. Recheck A1c in 3–6 months.\\n- ALT 40 U/L (mildly high): Limit alcohol to <2 drinks/week, review supplements/medications that stress the liver, and work on weight management; repeat liver tests in 3 months.\\n- Ferritin 277 ng/mL and MCV 96.8 fL: Ferritin is high—stop any iron supplements until reviewed, and repeat ferritin and iron panel in 3 months to evaluate for inflammation or iron overload (hs-CRP is low at 0.7 mg/L).\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Discuss menopausal symptom options (non-drug measures and possible hormone therapy) with your clinician within 4–12 weeks.\\n- Start Vitamin D3 2,000 IU/day now and recheck in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Implement diet/exercise steps above today; recheck A1c in 3–6 months.\\n- Repeat ALT and ferritin in ~3 months; consider lipid specialist referral for Lp(a) discussion.\\n- Consider bone density (DEXA) given menopause and low Vitamin D—discuss scheduling within the next 3–6 months.\\n\\nYou’re doing well in many areas; these targeted steps can improve symptoms, bone health, and long-term heart health. If you’d like, we can help set up the vitamin re-checks and referrals.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 13.2, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 39.7, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 96.8, "status": "borderline", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 96.8 fL is in the high-normal range. Early macrocytosis can precede overt B12 or folate deficiency.", "recommendation": "Ensure B12 and folate levels are within optimal range. Monitor CBC at next visit.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 243, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 5, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol appropriate for postmenopausal status.", "recommendation": "Consider HRT if symptomatic for vasomotor symptoms, bone health, or quality of life.", "referenceRange": "<20 pg/mL (postmenopausal without HRT)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.19, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone appropriate for postmenopausal status.", "recommendation": "Expected finding.", "referenceRange": "<1 ng/mL (postmenopausal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 122, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "Elevated FSH consistent with menopause.", "recommendation": "Expected finding for postmenopausal women.", "referenceRange": ">25 mIU/mL (postmenopausal)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 31.7, "status": "normal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone within normal female range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 0.96, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 277, "status": "borderline", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Elevated ferritin.", "recommendation": "May indicate inflammation, hemochromatosis, or liver disease. Correlate clinically.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 23.4, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D deficiency (21-30 ng/mL). Suboptimal for hormone and bone health.", "recommendation": "Repletion: Metagenics D3 5,000 + K daily. Target ≥60 ng/mL. Recheck in 8-12 weeks.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 520, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.7, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Prediabetes range (5.7-6.4%).", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider metformin if high risk.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 129, "status": "borderline", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Near optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet and exercise.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 139, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 64, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.3752477731577082, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 0.4% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.25479505005970404, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 0.3%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.5077437191718024, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.5%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 2.951466919425463, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 3.0%, ASCVD 1.8%, Heart Failure 4.5%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 29, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 40, "status": "borderline", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "Mild ALT elevation.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle changes. Repeat in 4-6 weeks.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "score", "value": 0.91, "status": "normal", "category": "FIB-4 Score (Liver Fibrosis)", "recheckTiming": "", "interpretation": "FIB-4 score 0.91 indicates LOW risk of advanced fibrosis (F0-F1). Negative predictive value >90%.", "recommendation": "Advanced fibrosis unlikely. Continue lifestyle modifications and routine LFT monitoring. Address underlying cause of LFT elevation.", "referenceRange": "<1.30 low risk, 1.30-2.67 indeterminate, >2.67 high risk"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 97, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.76, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 94, "status": "borderline", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Borderline elevated Apo B.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider pharmacotherapy if high ASCVD risk.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 115, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Elevated Lp(a) - RISK ENHANCER. Increases CVD risk category.", "recommendation": "Lp(a) ≥50 mg/dL is a risk enhancer - consider upgrading CVD risk category. Aggressive LDL lowering indicated. Discuss hereditary nature.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 320, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 147, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 8.47, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 0.7, "status": "normal", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Low cardiovascular inflammation risk.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 5, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Hypoestrogenic Pattern", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 5 pg/mL with FSH of 122 mIU/mL indicates hypoestrogenic perimenopause - the \\"crash phase.\\" Estrogen production has dropped significantly and the brain is signaling strongly (elevated FSH) trying to stimulate the ovaries.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Transdermal estradiol 0.025-0.05 mg is appropriate replacement therapy. Add micronized progesterone for uterine protection and sleep support. Frame this as hormone replacement, not stimulation - we are restoring what the body needs. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen has dropped to a level that can cause symptoms like hot flashes, night sweats, brain fog, and mood changes. Replacing estrogen with a patch or gel can relieve these symptoms and protect your long-term health. Progesterone is added to protect your uterus and help with sleep.", "referenceRange": "E2 <50 with FSH >10 indicates estrogen deficiency"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 5, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Hypoestrogen State", "interpretation": "Low estradiol (5 pg/mL) with symptomatic presentation: night sweats. This pattern is consistent with a low estrogen state, especially in late perimenopause or postmenopause.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Consider transdermal estradiol initiation (product dependent). Add micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly if patient has uterus. Monitor symptoms and estradiol levels. PATIENT EDUCATION: Low estrogen can cause the symptoms you're experiencing. Hormone replacement therapy (HRT) with estradiol can help relieve these symptoms and protect bone and cardiovascular health.", "referenceRange": "Provider target: 60-100 pg/mL (>40 minimum)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 122, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Menopause", "interpretation": "FSH of 122 mIU/mL with low estradiol (5 pg/mL) confirms menopausal status. Symptoms present: night sweats. Elevated FSH reflects decreased ovarian function and reduced estrogen production. This is a normal part of reproductive aging but may benefit from hormone optimization if symptomatic.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: If symptomatic, consider hormone therapy (HT): transdermal estradiol + micronized progesterone (if uterus intact). Discuss benefits/risks of HT including cardiovascular, bone, and quality of life considerations. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your lab results confirm you are in menopause. Hormone therapy can help manage symptoms like hot flashes, sleep problems, and vaginal dryness while also protecting bone health.", "referenceRange": "FSH <25 mIU/mL premenopausal; ≥25 suggests transition; ≥40 menopausal"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 31.7, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone Pattern D: Adequate Androgens, Persistent Symptoms", "interpretation": "Adequate Androgens pattern identified (Total T: 31.7 ng/dL (within range); Free T: 5 pg/mL; SHBG: 46.4 nmol/L). Total, free, and bioavailable testosterone are within normal ranges and SHBG is not extreme. If the patient reports fatigue, low mood, sleep disruption, or low libido, these symptoms are likely driven by non-androgenic factors. Findings to evaluate: Low Vitamin D (23.4 ng/mL) — contributes to fatigue and mood changes. Estrogen/Progesterone balance should be assessed (E2: 5 pg/mL, P4: 0.19 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Do not reflexively add testosterone when levels are adequate — look deeper. Evaluate iron status (ferritin target >50 for symptom resolution), thyroid function (optimize TSH), and sleep quality (consider sleep apnea screening with STOP-BANG). Assess estrogen and progesterone balance — E2/P4 imbalance is a common driver of persistent symptoms. Address CNS and psychosocial drivers: chronic stress, HPA axis dysregulation, mood disorders. Consider DHEA-S if not already checked. Support with lifestyle optimization: sleep hygiene, stress management, nutrition. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your testosterone levels are actually in a healthy range, which is good news. When symptoms persist despite normal testosterone, it usually means something else is contributing. Your provider will look at other factors like iron levels, thyroid function, sleep quality, and hormone balance to find what's driving your symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Total T 15-70 ng/dL, Free T 0.5-5.0 pg/mL (all within range)"}, {"unit": "of 6 markers positive", "value": 4, "status": "critical", "category": "Insulin Resistance Screening", "recheckTiming": "3-6 months", "interpretation": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: HIGH LIKELIHOOD (4/6 markers positive). Identified phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance, Hepatic (Liver-Driven) Insulin Resistance, Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance. Treat according to phenotype-specific protocol.", "recommendation": "Phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance; Hepatic (Liver-Driven) Insulin Resistance; Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance. Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose.", "referenceRange": "0-1 Low, 2 Moderate, 3+ High Likelihood"}], "aiRecommendations": "Unable to generate recommendations. Please review lab findings manually.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 0.46, "detail": "Triglycerides 64 / HDL 139 = 0.46", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5.7%", "detail": "Prediabetic range", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 46.4, "detail": "Low - hyperinsulinemia suppresses SHBG", "positive": true, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 40, "detail": "Above optimal range - may indicate hepatic insulin resistance", "positive": true, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "94 mg/dL", "detail": "Elevated atherogenic particle count", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "0.7 mg/L", "detail": "Low inflammatory burden", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "high", "phenotypes": [{"key": "visceral_metabolic", "name": "Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Recheck A1c, lipid panel, hs-CRP, ApoB in 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["A1c >= 5.7%", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "ALT above optimal range"], "pathophysiology": "Early peripheral + hepatic insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia. Glucose may still be \\"normal.\\" A1c often rises last.", "triggerCriteria": ["A1c >= 5.7%", "TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0", "Central adiposity phenotype", "ALT above optimal range"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests your body may not be responding to insulin as efficiently as it should. Insulin is the hormone that helps move sugar from your bloodstream into your cells for energy.\\n\\nWhen cells become less sensitive to insulin:\\n- Blood sugar slowly rises over time\\n- Triglycerides increase\\n- Inflammation increases\\n- Weight gain around the midsection becomes easier\\n\\nThe good news? This stage is highly reversible with targeted nutrition, strength training, sleep optimization, and - when appropriate - medication support.\\n\\nWe'll focus on improving how your body processes fuel and reducing long-term cardiometabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward nutrition (>= 25-35g/meal)", "Carbohydrate quality + timing education", "Strength training 2-3x weekly", "10-15 min post-meal walking", "Sleep optimization", "Consider: GLP-1 therapy if obesity, appetite dysregulation, A1c >= 5.7, or inflammatory phenotype", "Consider: Metformin if prediabetes or strong family history", "Recheck metabolic panel in 3-6 months"]}, {"key": "hepatic", "name": "Hepatic (Liver-Driven) Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Monitor ALT, TG, ApoB, A1c every 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["ALT above optimal (>25 U/L)", "ApoB elevated", "A1c mildly elevated"], "pathophysiology": "Hepatic insulin resistance leads to increased hepatic glucose output + VLDL overproduction. Often early MASLD (metabolic-associated steatotic liver disease) physiology.", "triggerCriteria": ["ALT above optimal (>= 25 U/L)", "TG elevated or TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB elevated", "A1c may be normal or mildly elevated"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour labs suggest your liver may be under metabolic strain. The liver plays a major role in managing blood sugar and fat metabolism.\\n\\nWhen the liver becomes less responsive to insulin:\\n- Triglycerides rise\\n- Liver enzymes increase\\n- Fat can accumulate in the liver over time\\n\\nThis is common and often related to nutrition, stress, sleep, and weight distribution.\\n\\nThe encouraging part: liver-related metabolic changes are very responsive to targeted lifestyle changes and, if needed, medication support.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Weight reduction goal 5-10% if overweight", "Eliminate sugary beverages", "Alcohol reduction", "Protein-first nutrition", "Consider GLP-1 if weight loss appropriate", "Omega-3 if TG elevated"]}, {"key": "hormonal_pcos", "name": "Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Monitor SHBG, androgens, A1c every 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["SHBG low (<50)", "A1c elevated"], "pathophysiology": "Hyperinsulinemia suppresses SHBG and increases ovarian androgen production.", "triggerCriteria": ["Low SHBG (<50; strong if <30)", "Elevated total or free testosterone", "Irregular cycles", "A1c may still be normal", "TG/HDL may be borderline-high"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests insulin may be influencing your hormone balance.\\n\\nWhen insulin levels run high:\\n- The body makes less SHBG (a protective hormone-binding protein)\\n- Androgen levels can rise\\n- Cycles may become irregular\\n\\nThis pattern is common in PCOS and other hormone imbalance states.\\n\\nImproving insulin sensitivity often helps restore hormonal balance and reduce long-term metabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward, lower refined carb approach", "Strength training", "Consider metformin if cycles irregular or A1c trending", "Consider GLP-1 if obesity phenotype present", "Monitor SHBG, androgens, A1c"]}], "positiveCount": 4, "likelihoodLabel": "HIGH LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: HIGH LIKELIHOOD (4/6 markers positive). Identified phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance, Hepatic (Liver-Driven) Insulin Resistance, Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance. Treat according to phenotype-specific protocol.", "confirmationTests": "Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose."}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "confidence": "high", "description": "Metabolic pattern consistent with insulin resistance and/or visceral adiposity. This drives inflammation, hormonal imbalance, and cardiovascular risk.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance screening: HIGH LIKELIHOOD (4/6 markers positive)", "A1c 5.7% (prediabetic range)", "SHBG 46.4 nmol/L (low, associated with insulin resistance)", "ApoB 94 mg/dL (elevated)", "Unexplained weight gain / central adiposity reported", "ALT 40 U/L (above optimal for women >25)"]}, {"name": "Menopausal Transition", "confidence": "high", "description": "Clinical picture consistent with perimenopause or menopause. Hormonal shifts during this transition affect bone health, cardiovascular risk, mood, and quality of life.", "supportingFindings": ["Postmenopausal status", "FSH 122 mIU/mL (elevated, menopausal range)", "Estradiol 5 pg/mL (declining)", "Night sweats reported", "Mood changes reported"]}, {"name": "Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Lab pattern consistent with relative estrogen excess or impaired estrogen clearance. Even without reported symptoms, supporting liver detoxification and estrogen metabolism pathways may be beneficial.", "supportingFindings": ["Borderline liver markers (ALT 40, AST 29) suggesting possible impaired hepatic clearance", "ALT 40 U/L (elevated, may impair estrogen metabolism)", "Mood changes reported"]}, {"name": "Oxidative Stress Burden", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Multiple markers suggest increased oxidative stress and cellular burden. Antioxidant and glutathione support may help reduce oxidative damage and support cellular energy production.", "supportingFindings": ["ALT 40 U/L (hepatic stress marker)", "AST 29 U/L (cellular stress marker)", "Insulin resistance pattern (metabolic oxidative driver)", "A1c 5.7% (glycemic stress)", "Age 48 (increased oxidative stress risk)"]}]}	\N	2026-03-18 17:40:23.360319	2026-03-18 17:40:23.360319
30	15	2026-03-12 00:00:00	{"fsh": 22.3, "acne": false, "shbg": 75.3, "onHRT": true, "anxiety": true, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "hairLoss": false, "estradiol": 10, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": false, "lowLibido": false, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2026-03-12", "moodChanges": true, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Missy Maranto", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 45, "bmi": 23.9, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 130, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 2.98, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 13.8, "lowMotivation": false, "menstrualPhase": "follicular", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "freeTestosterone": 1}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History of present illness: Patient is a 45-year-old female presenting for review of recent hormone laboratory results and management of perimenopausal symptoms. She reports symptoms consistent with hypoestrogenia including intermittent hot flashes, irregular menstrual bleeding pattern over recent months, worsening sleep fragmentation, and decreased libido. No acute chest pain, shortness of breath, focal neurologic symptoms, or signs of thrombosis reported. No history of active liver disease or prior venous thromboembolism documented in chart today (confirm in chart review).\\n- Gynecologic/reproductive: Perimenopausal symptoms as above. Unknown current HRT regimen and last dose timing in chart — staff to document prior to any dose changes. Uterine status: not documented in chart today — confirm intact uterus prior to estrogen adjustments.\\n- Allergies: None documented in chart today.\\n- Medications: Not documented in chart today — verify current systemic or topical hormone therapies and other meds prior to any changes.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: None recorded in chart at time of encounter (BP, HR, weight/BMI not available; to be recorded at next visit).\\n- Risk scores: STOP-BANG for OSA = 0 (low risk).\\n- Hormone laboratory results (most recent):\\n  - Estradiol (E2): 10 pg/mL — ABNORMAL. (Clinic bone-protection threshold <40 pg/mL.)\\n  - FSH: 22.3 mIU/mL — BORDERLINE/ELEVATED.\\n  - Progesterone: 2.98 ng/mL — ABNORMAL (below clinic HRT therapeutic target when being used for endometrial protection).\\n  - Total Testosterone: 13.8 ng/dL — ABNORMAL (below clinic target for symptomatic benefit).\\n  - SHBG: 75.3 nmol/L — within reported normal range.\\n  - Free Testosterone: 1 pg/mL — within reported normal range.\\n- Other labs: CBC, CMP, LFTs, lipid panel, vitamin D, AMH, and DEXA results not available in this report — order as indicated below.\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1) Hypoestrogenic perimenopausal state (\\"crash phase\\"): Estradiol 10 pg/mL with FSH 22.3 mIU/mL consistent with markedly reduced ovarian estrogen production. Estradiol at this level is below bone-protective threshold and likely contributing to patient symptoms (hot flashes, sleep disturbance) and increased longer-term bone loss risk.\\n2) Inadequate progestogen exposure for endometrial protection: Progesterone 2.98 ng/mL is below clinic therapeutic target for progestin protection when uterus is intact; risk of endometrial stimulation if estrogen is increased without adequate progestogen.\\n3) Low total testosterone: Total T 13.8 ng/dL — below clinic symptomatic target; may contribute to low libido, low energy. SHBG and free T reported as normal but total T remains low.\\n4) Elevated FSH / diminished ovarian reserve: FSH 22.3 mIU/mL may indicate declining ovarian reserve consistent with perimenopause.\\n5) Bone health risk: Estradiol <40 pg/mL poses increased risk of accelerated bone resorption; consider bone density assessment if not recent.\\n6) Safety/administrative: Current HRT regimen, last dose timing, pregnancy status (if relevant) and contraindications (VTE, active liver disease, uncontrolled HTN) are not documented in chart today and must be verified prior to any medication changes.\\nRed flags requiring immediate physician review: decision to increase estrogen and progesterone doses (provider sign-off required). Contraindications to estrogen/progestogen must be ruled out prior to escalation; stop and notify provider for any new chest pain, neurologic deficit, or signs of VTE after dose change.\\n\\nPLAN:\\nGeneral:\\n- Results reviewed with patient. Discussed that labs show a hypoestrogenic perimenopausal pattern with estradiol 10 pg/mL and FSH 22.3 mIU/mL, inadequate progesterone level for endometrial protection, and low total testosterone. Discussed risks/benefits of treatment escalation, need for provider authorization, and safety monitoring. Patient verbalized understanding.\\n\\nImmediate actions (require provider sign-off prior to dispensing/titration):\\n1) Verify baseline information before any changes:\\n   - Document current HRT regimen (formulation, dose, route, exact last dose timing).\\n   - Confirm uterine status (intact vs hysterectomy).\\n   - Confirm pregnancy status if of childbearing potential.\\n   - Review contraindications to estrogen/progestogen/testosterone (personal/family VTE history, stroke, CHD, uncontrolled HTN, active liver disease, active breast cancer).\\n   - Obtain baseline CBC, CMP (including LFTs), and lipid panel if not recently available.\\n\\n2) Estradiol management (provider to authorize):\\n   - Goal: clinic target estradiol 60–100 pg/mL.\\n   - If patient is currently on transdermal 0.025 mg (25 mcg) patch → increase to transdermal 0.05 mg (50 mcg) patch.\\n   - If currently on 0.05 mg and still hypoestrogenic → consider 0.1 mg (100 mcg) patch.\\n   - If using transdermal gel, document product and dose; consider equivalent increase or switch to patch per provider preference.\\n   - Timing for recheck: recheck serum estradiol 4 weeks after dose change. Document last patch change and time of blood draw (for patches, standard clinic draw at trough 24 hours after patch change unless otherwise specified).\\n   - Provider to authorize escalation and confirm progestogen plan if uterus intact.\\n\\n3) Progesterone (endometrial protection) (provider to authorize):\\n   - If patient is on micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly or 100 mg x14 days/month → increase to 200 mg nightly (either cyclic 12–14 days/month for cyclic regimen or continuous 200 mg nightly if a continuous combined regimen is chosen).\\n   - Note: serum progesterone is timing-dependent — for therapeutic monitoring, draw level ~3–4 hours after an oral bedtime dose to approximate peak. Recheck progesterone 4 weeks after dosing change with timing documented.\\n   - If patient declines or has contraindication to micronized progesterone, discuss alternative progestogen options with provider.\\n\\n4) Testosterone management (provider to authorize if symptomatic and after review of contraindications):\\n   - If patient is already on testosterone: increase current dose by 25–50% as an initial titration; document current formulation and dose prior to change.\\n   - If initiating testosterone: provider consideration options:\\n     - IM: testosterone cypionate/enanthate 25–50 mg every 10–14 days (or 20–40 mg weekly), titratable to effect.\\n     - Transdermal: increase topical dose by approximately 25–50% per clinic product equivalence (document product).\\n   - Monitoring: recheck total testosterone, free T, SHBG, CBC (Hgb/Hct), LFTs 6–8 weeks after initiation/titration. Notify provider if total testosterone >150 ng/dL or if virilizing signs develop (voice change, clitoromegaly, severe acne, rapid hair growth).\\n\\nSafety monitoring and labs:\\n- Recheck schedule:\\n  - Estradiol and progesterone: 4 weeks after dose adjustments (document exact timing of last dose relative to blood draw).\\n  - Testosterone panel (total T, free T, SHBG), CBC, CMP/LFTs: 6–8 weeks after testosterone initiation/titration.\\n- Baseline labs if not available: pregnancy test (if applicable), CBC, CMP including LFTs, lipid panel, vitamin D level, TSH/free T4.\\n- Bone health: order DEXA if no DEXA within past 2 years or if fracture risk factors present.\\n- Consider AMH if fertility preservation or further ovarian reserve assessment desired.\\n\\nLifestyle and adjunctive measures:\\n- Bone health: recommend weight-bearing and resistance exercise, smoking cessation, limit alcohol to ≤1 drink/day. Ensure adequate calcium intake and vitamin D supplementation per clinic standards (check vitamin D level and supplement as indicated).\\n- Sleep: counsel on sleep hygiene; discuss that micronized progesterone can be sedating—advise bedtime dosing.\\n- Topical hormone safety education: if using transdermal hormones, advise on application sites, avoid skin-to-skin transfer, wash hands after application.\\n- Menstrual bleeding counseling: counsel that changes in HRT can alter bleeding patterns; instruct patient to report heavy bleeding or new severe pelvic pain. Consider endometrial evaluation only for abnormal bleeding after changes.\\n\\nFollow-up and timeline:\\n- Provider to review and authorize estrogen and progesterone dose changes today (03/19/2026).\\n- Implement dose changes within 1–2 weeks after provider approval and after verifying contraindications and current dosing/timing documentation.\\n- Recheck estradiol and progesterone 4 weeks after changes (ensure blood draw timed relative to last dose: estradiol trough for patch ~24 hours after change; progesterone ~3–4 hours after oral dose).\\n- Recheck testosterone panel and safety labs 6–8 weeks after testosterone change.\\n- Arrange DEXA within 4–8 weeks if indicated.\\n- Schedule clinician follow-up to review repeat lab results and symptoms at 4–8 weeks post-adjustment.\\n- Provider notification criteria: any contraindications identified, estradiol remains <20 pg/mL after two appropriate escalations, total T >150 ng/dL or virilization, new chest pain/neurologic deficits/shortness of breath or suspected VTE, significant abnormal LFTs or Hct rise >5% or Hct >52%.\\n\\nOrders placed / to be placed:\\n- Provider to sign orders for:\\n  - Estradiol/progesterone/testosterone dose changes as above (provider directive).\\n  - Labs: Estradiol, progesterone, total testosterone, free T, SHBG, CBC, CMP (incl LFTs), lipid panel, pregnancy test if applicable, vitamin D, TSH/free T4 as indicated.\\n  - DEXA if not recent.\\n  - AMH if fertility assessment desired.\\n\\nDocumentation notes:\\n- Staff to document current HRT regimen, last dose timing, uterine status, and pregnancy status prior to lab-based dose changes.\\n- All hormone dose changes require prescribing provider authorization and individualized risk assessment.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\nDate: 03/19/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 soft chew during times of stress", "name": "HerWellness™ Rapid Stress Relief", "caution": "Fast-acting L-Theanine and Lactium formula. Non-drowsy. Promotes calm within 1 hour. Contains milk.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "low", "rationale": "HerWellness Rapid Stress Relief provides fast-acting stress support with L-Theanine (200mg) and Lactium. Promotes calm within 1 hour without drowsiness.", "indication": "Anxiety reported; Mood changes reported", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This fast-acting supplement helps your body respond to stress more calmly. It promotes relaxation within about an hour and can be taken as needed without causing drowsiness.", "supportingFindings": ["Anxiety reported", "Mood changes reported"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\nYour hormones show a hypoestrogenic perimenopause pattern — an “ovarian crash” phase. Key numbers: estradiol 10 pg/mL and FSH 22.3 mIU/mL. Estradiol at 10 pg/mL is well below the bone-protection threshold (<40 pg/mL), which raises risk for increased bone breakdown. Progesterone is 2.98 ng/mL (below typical HRT therapeutic range), and total testosterone is low at 13.8 ng/dL despite current HRT. SHBG is 75.3 nmol/L (within normal range) and free testosterone is 1 pg/mL (within functional range). STOP-BANG sleep apnea score 0 points (low risk).\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- SHBG: 75.3 nmol/L — within normal limits, which helps regulate hormone availability.\\n- Free testosterone: 1 pg/mL — currently within a functional range.\\n- STOP-BANG: 0/8 — low risk for obstructive sleep apnea.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + actionable steps)\\n- Estradiol 10 pg/mL (target to reduce bone loss is >40–60 pg/mL):\\n  - Discuss adjusting estrogen therapy with your clinician soon — restoring estradiol above ~40 pg/mL helps protect bone.\\n  - Start bone-support actions now: weight-bearing aerobic activity 30 minutes most days (brisk walking), plus strength training 2–3x weekly.\\n  - Take calcium 1,000–1,200 mg/day via diet/supplements, and vitamin D 1,000–2,000 IU/day (check level).\\n  - Limit alcohol to <2 drinks/week and avoid smoking.\\n- Progesterone 2.98 ng/mL (below HRT therapeutic range):\\n  - Review timing/dose/form of progesterone with provider; consider adjusting HRT regimen.\\n- Total testosterone 13.8 ng/dL (low despite HRT):\\n  - Prioritize 7–8 hours quality sleep nightly.\\n  - Continue strength training 2–3x weekly to boost muscle and support testosterone function.\\n  - Manage stress with daily practices (10–20 minutes meditation, yoga, or brisk walking).\\n  - Ensure adequate protein each meal to support muscle (20–30 g/meal).\\n\\nAdditional female-specific checks to order\\n- Ferritin and CBC to screen for iron deficiency.\\n- TSH and free T4 to check thyroid function.\\n- 25-OH vitamin D.\\n- DEXA bone density scan given low estradiol (bone protection concern).\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Contact your provider within 2–4 weeks to review results and discuss HRT adjustments.\\n- If HRT is changed, recheck estradiol, FSH, progesterone, and total testosterone in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Arrange DEXA within 6–12 months and order ferritin/TSH/Vit D in the next 4–6 weeks.\\nYou’re taking important steps by reviewing these numbers — we can adjust therapy and lifestyle to protect your bones, hormones, and overall health.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 10, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol below bone protection threshold (<40 pg/mL). Bone resorption increases below 40-60 pg/mL.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase estrogen dose. Target 60-100 pg/mL. Current level inadequate for bone protection.", "referenceRange": "60-100 pg/mL (HRT goal, >40 for bone)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 2.98, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone below HRT therapeutic range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase progesterone dose. Target 8-10 ng/mL for optimal endometrial protection.", "referenceRange": "8-10 ng/mL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 22.3, "status": "borderline", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "Elevated FSH may indicate diminished ovarian reserve.", "recommendation": "Consider AMH testing if fertility is a concern.", "referenceRange": "3-10 mIU/mL (follicular phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 13.8, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Low testosterone despite HRT.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase testosterone dose. Target 75-125 ng/dL for optimal results.", "referenceRange": "75-125 ng/dL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 10, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Hypoestrogenic Pattern", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 10 pg/mL with FSH of 22.3 mIU/mL indicates hypoestrogenic perimenopause - the \\"crash phase.\\" Estrogen production has dropped significantly and the brain is signaling strongly (elevated FSH) trying to stimulate the ovaries.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Transdermal estradiol 0.025-0.05 mg is appropriate replacement therapy. Add micronized progesterone for uterine protection and sleep support. Frame this as hormone replacement, not stimulation - we are restoring what the body needs. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen has dropped to a level that can cause symptoms like hot flashes, night sweats, brain fog, and mood changes. Replacing estrogen with a patch or gel can relieve these symptoms and protect your long-term health. Progesterone is added to protect your uterus and help with sleep.", "referenceRange": "E2 <50 with FSH >10 indicates estrogen deficiency"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 75.3, "status": "normal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 75.3 nmol/L is within normal range. SHBG regulates bioavailable sex hormones by binding testosterone and estradiol.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 1 pg/mL is within normal functional range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- Labs indicate a hypoestrogenic perimenopausal pattern with estradiol 10 pg/mL and FSH 22.3 mIU/mL consistent with low ovarian estrogen production. Estradiol at this level is below the bone-protective threshold and is likely contributing to symptoms and long-term bone risk.\\n- Progesterone 2.98 ng/mL is below the clinic HRT therapeutic range for endometrial protection.\\n- Total testosterone 13.8 ng/dL is below the clinic target for symptomatic benefit.\\n- SHBG, free T, and STOP-BANG were within normal limits per the reported results.\\n- Action is required to restore estradiol to the clinic target (60–100 pg/mL), increase progesterone to therapeutic range (aim 8–10 ng/mL when measured appropriately), and raise testosterone toward the target (75–125 ng/dL). Timing of blood draws relative to HRT dosing must be documented for correct interpretation.\\n\\nPriority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL (requires provider sign-off before dispensing/titration)\\n- Estradiol: Patient estradiol 10 pg/mL → provider review and authorize estrogen dose increase to achieve target 60–100 pg/mL. If patient has intact uterus, ensure progestogen plan approved concurrently.\\n- Progesterone: Current value 2.98 ng/mL → provider approval to increase micronized progesterone for endometrial protection (see dosing options below).\\n- Safety checks before increases: confirm pregnancy status if of childbearing potential; review thromboembolic history, active liver disease, uncontrolled hypertension; notify provider immediately if any contraindications present.\\n\\nURGENT (to implement once provider approves)\\n- Estradiol dosing adjustment (transdermal preference per protocol):\\n  - If patient is currently on a 0.025 mg (25 mcg) transdermal patch → increase to 0.05 mg (50 mcg) patch.\\n  - If already on 0.05 mg (50 mcg) and still hypoestrogenic → consider 0.1 mg (100 mcg) patch.\\n  - If using transdermal gel: increase to the equivalent dose (clinic-specific conversion), or switch to patch per provider preference.\\n  - Recheck serum estradiol 4 weeks after dose change (draw timing: document last patch change/time of gel dose; for transdermal patches, steady state by 48–72 hours; standard practice is blood draw at trough 24 hours after patch change—document clinic protocol).\\n- Progesterone dosing adjustment:\\n  - If current micronized progesterone is 100 mg nightly or 100 mg x14 days/month → increase to 200 mg nightly (either cyclic 12–14 days/month if cyclic HRT, or continuous 200 mg nightly if a continuous combined regimen is chosen by provider).\\n  - Recheck progesterone 4 weeks after change. Note: serum progesterone is time-dependent—draw ~3–4 hours after an oral bedtime dose to approximate peak when measuring level for therapeutic monitoring (document time of last dose).\\n- Testosterone:\\n  - If patient already on testosterone: increase current dose by 25–50% and recheck levels.\\n  - If initiating or changing route, consider common clinic regimens (provider decision):\\n    - IM option: testosterone cypionate/enanthate 25–50 mg every 10–14 days (or 20–40 mg weekly) titratable to effect.\\n    - Transdermal option: increase topical dose per product equivalence by ~25–50% (clinic-specific topical concentrations vary—document current product and dose).\\n  - Recheck total testosterone (and free T, SHBG) 6–8 weeks after dose adjustment. Notify provider if total testosterone exceeds 150 ng/dL or if patient develops virilizing symptoms.\\n\\nROUTINE\\n- Bone health: order DEXA if no baseline within the last 2 years or if fracture risk factors present.\\n- Vitamin D, calcium counseling and supplementation as appropriate.\\n- Routine labs: CBC, CMP, lipid panel, LFTs at baseline and after testosterone escalation per clinic protocol.\\n- If patient desires fertility or fertility preservation counseling → order AMH and consider reproductive endocrine referral.\\n\\nTestosterone dose recommendations (staff-facing, for provider review)\\n- If patient already on testosterone:\\n  - Increase current dose by 25–50% as an initial step; document current formulation and dose prior to change.\\n  - Reassess labs 6–8 weeks after change; titrate further only under provider direction to reach total T 75–125 ng/dL.\\n- If patient not currently on testosterone and provider elects to start:\\n  - IM option for provider consideration: testosterone cypionate/enanthate 25–50 mg q10–14 days (or 20–40 mg weekly); recheck 6–8 weeks.\\n  - Transdermal option for provider consideration: increase topical formulation to a dose expected to achieve target total T (clinic-specific product dosing—start low and titrate).\\n- Monitoring: total testosterone, free testosterone, SHBG, LFTs, CBC (Hgb/Hct) at baseline and 6–8 weeks after initiation/titration. Watch for virilization; hold and notify provider if signs occur.\\n\\nLifestyle interventions to communicate to patient (staff to educate)\\n- Bone health:\\n  - Encourage weight-bearing exercise (resistance training, walking), smoking cessation, limit alcohol to ≤1 drink/day.\\n  - Ensure adequate calcium intake (dietary ± supplements) and vitamin D supplementation per clinic guidelines; consider vitamin D level if not recently checked.\\n- Cardiometabolic health:\\n  - Encourage Mediterranean-style diet, weight management, regular aerobic activity.\\n- Sleep and mood:\\n  - Discuss sleep hygiene; progesterone may help sleep but counsel on timing of dose (bedtime) and possible sedation.\\n- Safety with topical hormones:\\n  - If using transdermal/ topical testosterone or estrogen, counsel on application site, avoid skin-to-skin contact with others, wash hands after application.\\n- Menstrual bleeding:\\n  - Counsel that changes in HRT may alter bleeding patterns; instruct patient to report heavy bleeding or new severe pelvic pain.\\n\\nAdditional testing or referrals to consider\\n- AMH (anti-Müllerian hormone) if fertility is a current concern or to further assess ovarian reserve.\\n- DEXA scan for bone density baseline if not performed in past 2 years.\\n- Pregnancy test before increasing estrogen/testosterone if childbearing potential.\\n- Baseline and follow-up: CBC, CMP (incl. LFTs), lipid panel.\\n- TSH and free T4 if not recently checked (thyroid disease can impact symptoms).\\n- Reproductive endocrinology referral if fertility preservation or assisted reproduction is a patient goal.\\n- Endometrial evaluation (TVUS or consult) only if abnormal bleeding develops after HRT changes.\\n\\nPhysician notification criteria (immediate)\\n- Any contraindications to estrogen/progesterone identified (active VTE, stroke, uncontrolled HTN, active liver disease) — halt changes and notify provider.\\n- Estradiol remains <20 pg/mL after two appropriate escalations.\\n- Total testosterone >150 ng/dL or any virilizing symptoms (voice change, clitoromegaly, severe acne, rapid hair growth).\\n- New/worsening chest pain, neurologic deficits, shortness of breath, or signs of VTE after hormone dose change.\\n- Significant abnormal labs after dose change (elevated LFTs, Hct rise >5% or Hct >52%, markedly abnormal lipids).\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (concise)\\n- Immediate (today/after provider sign-off):\\n  - Provider to review and authorize estrogen increase and progesterone increase.\\n  - Verify pregnancy status and contraindications.\\n- Within 1–2 weeks:\\n  - Implement dose changes once provider approves (increase patch to 0.05 mg if appropriate; increase micronized progesterone to 200 mg nightly if indicated).\\n  - Document exact time of last HRT dose in chart for lab interpretation.\\n- 4 weeks after estrogen/progesterone adjustment:\\n  - Recheck serum estradiol and serum progesterone (draw timed relative to last dose—document timing).\\n  - Symptom review and adverse effects check.\\n- 6–8 weeks after testosterone adjustment:\\n  - Recheck total testosterone, free T, SHBG, CBC, LFTs; adjust dose per lab and symptoms.\\n- Within 4–8 weeks:\\n  - Order/arrange DEXA if indicated; order AMH if fertility concerned.\\n- Follow-up visit:\\n  - Schedule clinician follow-up after lab results (4–8 weeks) to review hormone levels, symptoms, bleeding pattern, and safety labs.\\n\\nDocumenting timing and current regimen\\n- IMPORTANT for staff: before changing doses or interpreting labs, document current HRT regimen (dose, route, last dose timing), contraceptive status, menstrual phase (if applicable), and whether the patient has an intact uterus. These details are necessary for correct lab interpretation and safe regimen adjustments.\\n\\nNote: These are clinic protocol–based management recommendations for provider review. Changes must be authorized by the prescribing provider and individualized to the patient’s full medical history and contraindications.", "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Menopausal Transition", "confidence": "high", "description": "Clinical picture consistent with perimenopause or menopause. Hormonal shifts during this transition affect bone health, cardiovascular risk, mood, and quality of life.", "supportingFindings": ["Age 45 (perimenopause window)", "FSH 22.3 mIU/mL (rising, perimenopause range)", "Estradiol 10 pg/mL (declining)", "Mood changes reported"]}]}	\N	2026-03-19 13:57:49.97929	2026-03-19 13:57:49.97929
