 could benefit from help processing hormones, metabolic byproducts, and other compounds. It provides nutrients that support both phases of your liver's detox pathways.", "supportingFindings": ["Oxidative stress burden (liver biotransformation support)", "Inflammatory burden (metabolic clearance support)", "Insulin resistance / metabolic dysfunction (hepatic metabolic support)", "Borderline liver markers (ALT 21, AST 25) suggesting hepatic biotransformation load", "hs-CRP 41 mg/L (inflammatory metabolic burden)"]}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Essential for patients on statins.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "ApoB 98 mg/dL (cardiovascular support); hs-CRP 41 mg/L; LDL 120 mg/dL; Lp(a) 132 (elevated)", "phenotypes": ["Inflammatory Burden", "Oxidative Stress Burden"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["ApoB 98 mg/dL (cardiovascular support)", "hs-CRP 41 mg/L", "LDL 120 mg/dL", "Lp(a) 132 (elevated)", "Age 44 (declining CoQ10 production)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Healthy Weight with Akkermansia", "caution": "Contains Akkermansia muciniphila and Bifidobacterium lactis B420 for metabolic and gut barrier support. Refrigerate after opening for maximum potency.", "category": "metabolic", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "UltraFlora Healthy Weight with Akkermansia provides targeted probiotic support for the gut-metabolic axis. Akkermansia muciniphila supports gut barrier integrity, metabolic health, and healthy weight management. Indicated for insulin resistance, metabolic syndrome features, visceral adiposity, and weight loss resistance.", "indication": "Insulin resistance / visceral adiposity phenotype (gut-metabolic axis support); Inflammatory phenotype (gut-immune axis); ApoB 98 mg/dL (atherogenic metabolic pattern); hs-CRP 41 mg/L (metabolic inflammation)", "phenotypes": ["Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "Inflammatory Burden"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This specialized probiotic contains Akkermansia, a beneficial gut bacteria linked to healthy metabolism and weight management. It supports your gut barrier, helps manage metabolic inflammation, and works with your body's natural weight regulation systems.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance / visceral adiposity phenotype (gut-metabolic axis support)", "Inflammatory phenotype (gut-immune axis)", "ApoB 98 mg/dL (atherogenic metabolic pattern)", "hs-CRP 41 mg/L (metabolic inflammation)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 132, "apoBValue": 98, "lpaStatus": "elevated", "apoBStatus": "borderline", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 1.9797779156748418, "hasElevatedLpa": true, "statinGuidance": "Possibly consider statin therapy given risk enhancement; shared decision-making recommended.", "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "reclassified_upward", "clinicalGuidance": "Lp(a) 132 mg/dL - RISK ENHANCER (genetic risk factor). RECLASSIFIED TO BORDERLINE RISK: Risk enhancer present. Shared decision-making regarding statin therapy.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "cacRecommendation": "CAC reasonable especially with strong family history and risk enhancement present.", "hasBorderlineApoB": true}, "cacStatinRec": {"lpaWarning": "Elevated Lp(a) of 132 mg/dL is a genetic cardiovascular risk marker. This level is associated with increased ASCVD risk independent of LDL. Since Lp(a) cannot be significantly lowered, focus on aggressive LDL/ApoB reduction and lifestyle optimization.", "statinDiscussion": {"strength": "generally_recommended", "indicated": true, "rationale": "ApoB ≥90 mg/dL (98 mg/dL) with risk enhancers (elevated hs-CRP, elevated Lp(a), elevated ApoB). Statin discussion recommended."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "strongly_recommend", "rationale": "Elevated Lp(a) of 132 mg/dL is a genetic risk marker. CAC strongly recommended to assess arterial calcium burden and inform treatment intensity.", "recommended": true}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\n- You have several important findings to address: severe vitamin D deficiency (25-OH vitamin D 13.8 ng/mL), a high sleep apnea risk (STOP-BANG 5/8), very high inflammation (hs-CRP 41 mg/L), and an elevated Lipoprotein(a) of 132 mg/dL. Your 10-year ASCVD risk is low at 1.98% but your 30-year CVD risk is 19.4%, so focused lifestyle changes and a few follow-ups are appropriate. You are menopausal based on FSH 75.7 mIU/mL and estradiol (E2) 17 pg/mL — this affects bone and symptom management.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Hemoglobin 15.2 g/dL, hematocrit 46.2% (slightly high), MCV 84.8 fL, platelets 357 K/uL.\\n- Ferritin 84.3 ng/mL (good iron stores).\\n- Hemoglobin A1c 5.2% (normal blood sugar).\\n- Lipids: LDL 120 mg/dL, HDL 52 mg/dL, triglycerides 91 mg/dL.\\n- Kidney and liver: eGFR 94 mL/min, creatinine 0.79 mg/dL, AST 25 U/L, ALT 21 U/L.\\n- Testosterone total 22.5 ng/dL (within female range).\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + actions)\\n- Vitamin D 25-OH = 13.8 ng/mL (severe deficiency). Start vitamin D3 therapy — discuss either prescription 50,000 IU once weekly for 8–12 weeks or vitamin D3 2,000–5,000 IU daily, then recheck level in 8–12 weeks. Increase dietary calcium and weight-bearing exercise (walks, stairs, resistance training) to support bone health; consider bone density (DEXA) discussion given menopausal status (FSH 75.7 mIU/mL, E2 17 pg/mL).\\n- hs-CRP = 41 mg/L (markedly elevated). Contact clinic within 1 week for urgent evaluation — this may reflect infection or inflammation. Recheck after any acute illness; if persistently high, work-up for sources of inflammation.\\n- STOP-BANG = 5 (high risk). Arrange a sleep medicine referral for home sleep apnea testing or polysomnography. In the meantime: avoid alcohol and sedatives before bed, sleep on your side, and aim for weight loss if applicable (target 5–10% weight reduction to help OSA).\\n- Lipoprotein(a) = 132 mg/dL (elevated). Discuss with your clinician/cardiologist — Lp(a) raises lifetime CVD risk and may change prevention strategies even with LDL 120 mg/dL.\\n- Vitamin B12 = 2000 pg/mL (high). Review supplements; stop high-dose B12 if unnecessary and recheck in 3 months.\\n- TSH = 3.95 mIU/L (upper-normal). If you have symptoms (fatigue, cold intolerance), recheck TSH with free T4 in 6–8 weeks.\\n\\nCardiovascular prevention (you): 10-year ASCVD risk 1.98% (low), 30-year CVD 19.4% (notable). Continue heart-healthy habits: aim for 150 minutes/week moderate aerobic activity (brisk walking), follow a Mediterranean-style diet (vegetables, whole grains, olive oil, nuts, fatty fish 2–3x weekly), limit processed foods and added sugars, and add 25–30 g fiber daily (oatmeal, beans, vegetables). These reduce long-term risk and help with Lp(a) impact.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Contact clinic within 1 week for hs-CRP evaluation.\\n- Start vitamin D per plan and recheck 8–12 weeks.\\n- Sleep medicine referral now for OSA testing.\\n- Review supplements (B12) now and recheck in 3 months.\\n- Consider cardiology discussion for Lp(a) and long-term prevention; consider bone density testing within 3 months given menopause and low vitamin D.\\n\\nYou’re doing well in many areas; these focused steps can improve energy, bone, sleep, and heart health. Please book follow-up to start these plans — we’re here to help.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 15.2, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 46.2, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Slightly elevated hematocrit - may indicate dehydration.", "recommendation": "Encourage hydration. Repeat if persistent.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 84.8, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 84.8 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 357, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 17, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol appropriate for postmenopausal status.", "recommendation": "Consider HRT if symptomatic for vasomotor symptoms, bone health, or quality of life.", "referenceRange": "<20 pg/mL (postmenopausal without HRT)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.26, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone appropriate for postmenopausal status.", "recommendation": "Expected finding.", "referenceRange": "<1 ng/mL (postmenopausal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 75.7, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "Elevated FSH consistent with menopause.", "recommendation": "Expected finding for postmenopausal women.", "referenceRange": ">25 mIU/mL (postmenopausal)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 22.5, "status": "normal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone within normal female range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 3.95, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 84.3, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 13.8, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Severe Vitamin D deficiency (≤20 ng/mL). Associated with fatigue, bone loss, and immune dysfunction.", "recommendation": "High-dose repletion: Metagenics D3 10,000 + K daily for 8-12 weeks. Recheck levels after repletion.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 2000, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "Elevated B12.", "recommendation": "Usually from supplementation. Rarely indicates liver disease.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.2, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 120, "status": "borderline", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Near optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet and exercise.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 52, "status": "borderline", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "HDL could be higher for optimal protection.", "recommendation": "Increase exercise. Moderate alcohol if appropriate.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 91, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 3.1425883074245125, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 3.1% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 1.9797779156748418, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 2.0%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 2.367140782003801, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 2.4%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 5, "status": "critical", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Follow-up in 1-3 months after sleep study", "interpretation": "HIGH RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (5/8 points)", "recommendation": "High probability of moderate-severe OSA. STRONGLY RECOMMEND sleep study (polysomnography or HSAT). Evaluate for CPAP therapy. Address cardiovascular risk factors. Consider holding or reducing TRT dose if elevated hematocrit.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 19.433261500477922, "status": "borderline", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 19.4%, ASCVD 11.6%, Heart Failure 16.2%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 25, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 21, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 94, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.79, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 98, "status": "borderline", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Borderline elevated Apo B.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider pharmacotherapy if high ASCVD risk.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 132, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Elevated Lp(a) - RISK ENHANCER. Increases CVD risk category.", "recommendation": "Lp(a) ≥50 mg/dL is a risk enhancer - consider upgrading CVD risk category. Aggressive LDL lowering indicated. Discuss hereditary nature.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 324, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 44, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 6.56, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 41, "status": "critical", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Markedly elevated hs-CRP - acute inflammation.", "recommendation": "Evaluate for infection or inflammatory condition.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 17, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Hypoestrogenic Pattern", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 17 pg/mL with FSH of 75.7 mIU/mL indicates hypoestrogenic perimenopause - the \\"crash phase.\\" Estrogen production has dropped significantly and the brain is signaling strongly (elevated FSH) trying to stimulate the ovaries.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Transdermal estradiol 0.025-0.05 mg is appropriate replacement therapy. Add micronized progesterone for uterine protection and sleep support. Frame this as hormone replacement, not stimulation - we are restoring what the body needs. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen has dropped to a level that can cause symptoms like hot flashes, night sweats, brain fog, and mood changes. Replacing estrogen with a patch or gel can relieve these symptoms and protect your long-term health. Progesterone is added to protect your uterus and help with sleep.", "referenceRange": "E2 <50 with FSH >10 indicates estrogen deficiency"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 75.7, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Menopause", "interpretation": "FSH of 75.7 mIU/mL with low estradiol (17 pg/mL) confirms menopausal status. No specific symptoms reported at this time. Elevated FSH reflects decreased ovarian function and reduced estrogen production. This is a normal part of reproductive aging but may benefit from hormone optimization if symptomatic.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: If symptomatic, consider hormone therapy (HT): transdermal estradiol + micronized progesterone (if uterus intact). Discuss benefits/risks of HT including cardiovascular, bone, and quality of life considerations. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your lab results confirm you are in menopause. Hormone therapy can help manage symptoms like hot flashes, sleep problems, and vaginal dryness while also protecting bone health.", "referenceRange": "FSH <25 mIU/mL premenopausal; ≥25 suggests transition; ≥40 menopausal"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 22.5, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone Pattern D: Adequate Androgens, Persistent Symptoms", "interpretation": "Adequate Androgens pattern identified (Total T: 22.5 ng/dL (within range); Free T: 3 pg/mL; SHBG: 50.2 nmol/L). Total, free, and bioavailable testosterone are within normal ranges and SHBG is not extreme. If the patient reports fatigue, low mood, sleep disruption, or low libido, these symptoms are likely driven by non-androgenic factors. Findings to evaluate: Thyroid: TSH 3.95 mIU/L — evaluate thyroid function. Low Vitamin D (13.8 ng/mL) — contributes to fatigue and mood changes. Estrogen/Progesterone balance should be assessed (E2: 17 pg/mL, P4: 0.26 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Do not reflexively add testosterone when levels are adequate — look deeper. Evaluate iron status (ferritin target >50 for symptom resolution), thyroid function (optimize TSH), and sleep quality (consider sleep apnea screening with STOP-BANG). Assess estrogen and progesterone balance — E2/P4 imbalance is a common driver of persistent symptoms. Address CNS and psychosocial drivers: chronic stress, HPA axis dysregulation, mood disorders. Consider DHEA-S if not already checked. Support with lifestyle optimization: sleep hygiene, stress management, nutrition. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your testosterone levels are actually in a healthy range, which is good news. When symptoms persist despite normal testosterone, it usually means something else is contributing. Your provider will look at other factors like iron levels, thyroid function, sleep quality, and hormone balance to find what's driving your symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Total T 15-70 ng/dL, Free T 0.5-5.0 pg/mL (all within range)"}, {"unit": "of 6 markers positive", "value": 2, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Insulin Resistance Screening", "recheckTiming": "3-6 months", "interpretation": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive). This combination points toward hyperinsulinemia physiology. Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR).", "recommendation": "Phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance. Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose.", "referenceRange": "0-1 Low, 2 Moderate, 3+ High Likelihood"}], "aiRecommendations": "Unable to generate recommendations. Please review lab findings manually.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 1.75, "detail": "Triglycerides 91 / HDL 52 = 1.75", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5.2%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 50.2, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 21, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "98 mg/dL", "detail": "Elevated atherogenic particle count", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "41 mg/L", "detail": "Elevated systemic inflammation", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "moderate", "phenotypes": [{"key": "visceral_metabolic", "name": "Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Recheck A1c, lipid panel, hs-CRP, ApoB in 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0"], "pathophysiology": "Early peripheral + hepatic insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia. Glucose may still be \\"normal.\\" A1c often rises last.", "triggerCriteria": ["A1c >= 5.7%", "TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0", "Central adiposity phenotype", "ALT above optimal range"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests your body may not be responding to insulin as efficiently as it should. Insulin is the hormone that helps move sugar from your bloodstream into your cells for energy.\\n\\nWhen cells become less sensitive to insulin:\\n- Blood sugar slowly rises over time\\n- Triglycerides increase\\n- Inflammation increases\\n- Weight gain around the midsection becomes easier\\n\\nThe good news? This stage is highly reversible with targeted nutrition, strength training, sleep optimization, and - when appropriate - medication support.\\n\\nWe'll focus on improving how your body processes fuel and reducing long-term cardiometabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward nutrition (>= 25-35g/meal)", "Carbohydrate quality + timing education", "Strength training 2-3x weekly", "10-15 min post-meal walking", "Sleep optimization", "Consider: GLP-1 therapy if obesity, appetite dysregulation, A1c >= 5.7, or inflammatory phenotype", "Consider: Metformin if prediabetes or strong family history", "Recheck metabolic panel in 3-6 months"]}], "positiveCount": 2, "likelihoodLabel": "MODERATE LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive). This combination points toward hyperinsulinemia physiology. Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR).", "confirmationTests": "Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose."}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Inflammatory Burden", "confidence": "high", "description": "Pattern of systemic inflammation indicated by elevated inflammatory markers and/or symptomatic presentation. May benefit from anti-inflammatory nutritional support.", "supportingFindings": ["hs-CRP 41 mg/L (elevated >3.0)", "WBC 11.1 K/uL (elevated)", "ApoB 98 mg/dL (elevated)"]}, {"name": "Menopausal Transition", "confidence": "high", "description": "Clinical picture consistent with perimenopause or menopause. Hormonal shifts during this transition affect bone health, cardiovascular risk, mood, and quality of life.", "supportingFindings": ["Postmenopausal status", "FSH 75.7 mIU/mL (elevated, menopausal range)", "Estradiol 17 pg/mL (declining)"]}, {"name": "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Metabolic pattern consistent with insulin resistance and/or visceral adiposity. This drives inflammation, hormonal imbalance, and cardiovascular risk.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance screening: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive)", "ApoB 98 mg/dL (elevated)", "hs-CRP 41 mg/L (metabolic inflammation)"]}, {"name": "Oxidative Stress Burden", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Multiple markers suggest increased oxidative stress and cellular burden. Antioxidant and glutathione support may help reduce oxidative damage and support cellular energy production.", "supportingFindings": ["hs-CRP 41 mg/L (elevated, systemic inflammation)", "ALT 21 U/L (hepatic stress marker)", "AST 25 U/L (cellular stress marker)", "Insulin resistance pattern (metabolic oxidative driver)"]}, {"name": "Iron Deficiency", "confidence": "low", "description": "Iron stores are depleted or suboptimal. Iron is essential for energy, cognitive function, hair health, and oxygen transport.", "supportingFindings": ["Serum iron 44 µg/dL (low)"]}]}	\N	2026-03-19 19:07:39.559115	2026-03-19 19:07:39.559115
33	17	2026-03-19 00:00:00	{"acne": false, "onHRT": false, "anxiety": false, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "hairLoss": false, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": false, "lowLibido": false, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2026-03-19", "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "L Robinson", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 20, "bmi": 37, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 125, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "restlessLegs": false, "lowMotivation": false, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management\\n- History of present illness: Patient is a 20-year-old female presenting for review of screening results. No symptoms suggestive of obstructive sleep apnea (no excessive daytime sleepiness, no witnessed apneas, no new loud snoring, no nocturnal choking/gasping reported). No new cardiopulmonary complaints.\\n- Menstrual/hormone history: No new menstrual complaints documented. Current use of testosterone: none reported at time of chart review. If patient is on or plans to start testosterone, additional baseline testing and monitoring will be required (see plan).\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vital signs: None recorded at time of lab/review. BMI not documented in this encounter.\\n- Screening / Labs:\\n  - Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP‑BANG): 0 points — Normal / Low risk for obstructive sleep apnea (STOP‑BANG 0–2)\\n- Abnormal/Critical findings: None\\n- Borderline findings: None\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Low risk for obstructive sleep apnea (STOP‑BANG = 0).\\n   - Interpretation: Screening score indicates low risk for clinically significant OSA in the absence of other concerning features. No immediate red flags from this screening result alone.\\n   - Differential/considerations: Screening test only — does not exclude OSA in patients with new symptoms, rapid weight gain, or other risk factors (e.g., starting or escalating testosterone therapy, development of polycythemia, sedative use). Clinical context (BMI, neck circumference, reported symptoms) should inform further evaluation.\\n\\n2. No abnormal laboratory findings identified on this screening review.\\n   - Interpretation: No abnormal or critical values identified related to sleep apnea screening. Routine surveillance recommended.\\n\\n3. Testosterone therapy status: none reported.\\n   - Interpretation: No testosterone-related monitoring required at this time. If patient is started on testosterone in the future, follow baseline and surveillance recommendations below.\\n\\nPLAN:\\n1. Low risk OSA (STOP‑BANG = 0)\\n   - Document result in chart: STOP‑BANG low risk.\\n   - Patient education/counseling provided regarding OSA symptoms to watch for: excessive daytime sleepiness, witnessed apneas, new loud snoring, nocturnal choking/gasping, morning headaches. Advised to contact clinic/physician promptly if symptoms develop.\\n   - Re‑screening: Routine re‑screen in 3–6 months (routine follow‑up) or sooner if clinical change (weight gain, new symptoms, medication changes, initiation/escalation of testosterone).\\n   - Red flags requiring immediate physician notification: STOP‑BANG ≥3, new/worsening OSA symptoms, development of polycythemia (Hct ≥48% or increase >5% from baseline), or any acute cardio‑respiratory concerns.\\n\\n2. Testosterone-related considerations (patient not on testosterone currently)\\n   - If patient begins or plans to initiate testosterone therapy: obtain baseline morning total testosterone and hematocrit/hemoglobin prior to initiation.\\n   - Monitoring schedule if testosterone initiated: repeat morning total testosterone and Hct 6–12 weeks after start or dose change, then every 3–6 months during the first year, then annually if stable.\\n   - Action thresholds: notify prescriber and consider dose reduction/interval change if STOP‑BANG increases to ≥3, if patient develops OSA symptoms, or if Hct rises to ≥48% (or increases >5% from baseline). Escalate per physician judgment if Hct ≥52–54%.\\n\\n3. Lifestyle and preventive counseling (provided verbally)\\n   - Sleep hygiene: regular sleep schedule, limit late‑night heavy meals, reduce screen time before bed.\\n   - Avoid alcohol and sedative‑hypnotics within 4–6 hours of bedtime.\\n   - Positional measures: encourage side‑sleeping if snoring develops.\\n   - Weight management: referral to weight‑management resources if BMI elevated; even modest weight loss can reduce OSA risk.\\n   - Smoking cessation counseling as indicated.\\n\\n4. Additional testing / referrals (conditional)\\n   - If STOP‑BANG increases to ≥3 or patient becomes symptomatic: refer to sleep medicine for diagnostic testing (home sleep apnea test or in‑lab polysomnography as appropriate).\\n   - Consider overnight pulse oximetry as an interim test if access to formal testing is delayed and patient has other risk factors or is on testosterone.\\n   - If polycythemia develops, coordinate with primary care/hematology per clinic protocol.\\n\\n5. Follow‑up and orders\\n   - Immediate (today): Documented STOP‑BANG low risk in chart and provided patient counseling.\\n   - Short term (4–12 weeks): If initiating testosterone, order baseline and 6–12 week labs (morning total testosterone, CBC with Hct/Hb). (No baseline labs ordered today since testosterone not reported.)\\n   - Ongoing: Routine re‑screening of STOP‑BANG in 3–6 months or sooner with clinical change. If patient remains off testosterone and asymptomatic, continue routine surveillance.\\n   - Follow‑up appointment: Routine follow‑up in 3–6 months or sooner if symptoms develop or if testosterone is initiated/changed.\\n\\nDocumentation:\\n- Results reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow‑up as above.\\n\\nDate: 03/19/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\n- Your sleep apnea screening result is 0 points on the STOP-BANG (0/8), which is LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea. This is a positive result — it means you are unlikely to have moderate-to-severe sleep apnea based on this screen.\\n\\nPositive findings (specific values)\\n- Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG): 0 points (LOW RISK, 0/8).\\n\\nAreas to improve / things to watch (with specific, actionable steps)\\n- While no other lab values were reported here, for women’s health I recommend routine checks that are important even when this screen is normal:\\n  - Iron status: Consider checking a CBC and ferritin if you have heavy periods, fatigue, or hair thinning. In the meantime, eat iron-rich foods: 3–4 servings weekly of lean red meat or 1–2 cups weekly of cooked lentils/beans, plus dark leafy greens. Pair iron foods with vitamin C (a small glass of orange juice) and avoid tea/coffee at meals to improve absorption.\\n  - Thyroid function: If you have fatigue, weight changes, or hair loss, ask for a TSH test. Until then, prioritize consistent sleep (aim for 7–8 hours a night) and moderate exercise.\\n  - Bone health: Aim for 30 minutes of weight-bearing exercise most days and 2–3 strength-training sessions per week. Aim for about 1,000–1,200 mg calcium daily (diet + supplements if needed) and 600–800 IU vitamin D daily; consider a vitamin D level check if you have limited sun exposure.\\n\\nGeneral lifestyle tips to keep your sleep and overall health strong\\n- Sleep: Aim for 7–8 hours nightly, keep a regular bedtime, avoid heavy meals/alcohol within 2–3 hours of bed, and limit screens 30–60 minutes before sleep.\\n- Activity: 30 minutes brisk walking most days + strength training 2x/week.\\n- Weight: Maintaining a healthy weight supports sleep and lowers future risk of sleep apnea.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- No urgent action required for sleep apnea. If you have daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or choking/gasping at night, contact your provider for further testing.\\n- Discuss routine screening labs at your next visit: CBC/ferritin, TSH, lipid panel, A1c, and vitamin D. For most people, yearly or every 1–3 years depending on age and symptoms is reasonable.\\n- If you’d like, schedule a follow-up in 4–12 weeks to review any new symptoms and to arrange the labs suggested above.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- STOP-BANG reported as \\"Normal\\" — interpreted as low risk for obstructive sleep apnea (STOP-BANG score 0–2) unless additional clinical information suggests otherwise.\\n- No immediate red flags from this screening result alone. Continue routine surveillance, especially if patient is receiving or will receive testosterone therapy, which can increase OSA risk.\\n\\nPriority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\n- CRITICAL\\n  - Notify physician immediately if patient develops new/worsening symptoms of OSA (daytime somnolence, witnessed apneas, gasping/choking at night, new loud snoring) or if subsequent STOP-BANG score rises to ≥3.\\n- URGENT\\n  - If patient is on testosterone and has any of the above symptoms or new polycythemia (see hematocrit thresholds below), contact physician for same‑day review and consider holding/reducing dose.\\n- ROUTINE\\n  - Document current STOP-BANG score and interpretation in the chart.\\n  - Re-screen and reassess risk with any clinical change (weight gain, new medications, dose escalation of testosterone).\\n\\nTestosterone-specific recommendations (if applicable)\\n- If patient is NOT on testosterone: no testosterone-related action required; counsel on OSA symptoms and routine re-screening.\\n- If patient is currently on testosterone or planning to initiate:\\n  - Baseline: ensure baseline hematocrit (Hct) and morning total testosterone recorded prior to initiation.\\n  - Monitoring schedule: obtain Hct and total testosterone 6–12 weeks after initiation or dose change, then every 3–6 months during the first year, then annually if stable.\\n  - Dose modification triggers:\\n    - If new clinically significant OSA symptoms or STOP-BANG increases to ≥3: notify physician; consider reducing testosterone dose by 25–50% or lengthening dosing interval pending physician review.\\n    - If Hct rises to ≥48% (or increases by >5% from baseline), notify physician; consider dose reduction and evaluation for polycythemia. Escalate further if Hct ≥52–54% per physician judgment.\\n  - If initiating higher-than-physiologic doses (e.g., supraphysiologic regimens), strongly consider baseline sleep evaluation and closer monitoring.\\n\\nLifestyle interventions to implement and counsel patients on\\n- Weight management: recommend referral to weight-management resources if BMI elevated; even modest weight loss reduces OSA risk.\\n- Sleep hygiene: consistent sleep schedule, avoid late-night heavy meals, reduce screen time before bed.\\n- Alcohol and sedative avoidance: advise avoiding alcohol and sedative-hypnotics within 4–6 hours of bedtime.\\n- Positional measures: encourage side-sleeping if snoring noted.\\n- Smoking cessation counseling as needed.\\n- Educate patient on warning symptoms warranting immediate contact (witnessed apneas, choking/gasping, excessive daytime sleepiness).\\n\\nAdditional testing or referrals to consider\\n- Sleep medicine referral and diagnostic testing:\\n  - If STOP-BANG ≥3 or patient becomes symptomatic → refer to sleep medicine for diagnostic testing (home sleep apnea testing if appropriate or in-lab polysomnography).\\n  - Consider overnight pulse oximetry as an interim test if access to formal testing is delayed and patient is on testosterone or has other risk factors.\\n- Primary care or pulmonology referral for comorbid conditions that increase OSA risk (uncontrolled hypertension, heart failure, COPD).\\n- Hematology or primary care consult if polycythemia develops.\\n- If initiating testosterone and intermediate/high OSA risk or multiple risk factors (BMI >30, age >50, large neck circumference): consider sleep medicine consult prior to dose escalation.\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (clear actionable items)\\n- Immediate (today)\\n  - Document \\"STOP-BANG low risk\\" in chart; counsel patient briefly on OSA symptoms to watch for.\\n- Short term (within 4–12 weeks)\\n  - If initiating or changing testosterone dose: order baseline and 6–12 week labs (morning total testosterone, hematocrit/Hb).\\n  - Reassess STOP-BANG if clinical circumstances change; repeat sooner if dose change.\\n- Ongoing (3–12 months)\\n  - Routine monitoring of Hct and testosterone at 3–6 month intervals during first year of therapy, then annually if stable.\\n  - Repeat STOP-BANG annually or sooner with new symptoms, weight change, or testosterone dose change.\\n- Trigger points for immediate physician notification:\\n  - STOP-BANG score ≥3\\n  - New/worsening OSA symptoms\\n  - Hct ≥48% or rise >5% from baseline (or other clinic-specific polycythemia thresholds)\\n  - Any acute cardio-respiratory concerns\\n\\nNotes for staff\\n- Interpret STOP-BANG in clinical context (age, BMI, medications, menstrual status, current HRT/testosterone).\\n- Do not make a diagnosis of OSA from screening; refer for formal testing if indicated.\\n- Tailor the above monitoring and dose‑adjustment steps to clinic-specific testosterone protocols and physician orders.", "clinicalPhenotypes": []}	\N	2026-03-19 19:08:51.974457	2026-03-19 19:08:51.974457
