ion": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 60, "apoBValue": 66, "lpaStatus": "elevated", "apoBStatus": "normal", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 0, "hasElevatedLpa": true, "statinGuidance": "Possibly consider statin therapy given risk enhancement; shared decision-making recommended.", "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "reclassified_upward", "clinicalGuidance": "Lp(a) 60 mg/dL - RISK ENHANCER (genetic risk factor). RECLASSIFIED TO BORDERLINE RISK: Risk enhancer present. Shared decision-making regarding statin therapy.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "cacRecommendation": "CAC reasonable especially with strong family history and risk enhancement present.", "hasBorderlineApoB": false}, "cacStatinRec": {"lpaWarning": "Elevated Lp(a) of 60 mg/dL is a genetic cardiovascular risk marker. This level is associated with increased ASCVD risk independent of LDL. Since Lp(a) cannot be significantly lowered, focus on aggressive LDL/ApoB reduction and lifestyle optimization.", "statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall summary (plain language)\\n- Several important results are normal (good news) but a few require follow-up. Key concerns: elevated total testosterone 86.4 ng/dL and free testosterone 17 pg/mL, low vitamin D 25-OH at 20.9 ng/mL, elevated Lipoprotein(a) 60 mg/dL, and hs-CRP 3.6 mg/L. Your calculated 10-year ASCVD risk is 0% (LOW risk), which means your short-term risk of heart attack or stroke is low, but the elevated Lp(a) and hs-CRP are “risk enhancers” that deserve attention.\\n\\nPositive findings (what’s good right now)\\n- Hemoglobin 14.6 g/dL, MCV 89.4 fL, platelets 274 K/uL (all within normal range).\\n- LDL 75 mg/dL, triglycerides 71 mg/dL, Apolipoprotein B 66 mg/dL (all in optimal ranges).\\n- TSH 1.29 mIU/L (euthyroid), Hemoglobin A1c 4.9% (normal glucose), eGFR 88 mL/min (normal kidney function).\\n\\nAreas to improve (exact values + concrete actions)\\n- Testosterone: Total 86.4 ng/dL and Free 17 pg/mL (both elevated). Actions: prioritize 7–8 hours sleep nightly; add strength training 2–3× weekly; use stress reduction (10–20 minutes daily meditation, brisk walks, or yoga). Discuss symptoms (acne, hair changes, irregular periods) with your provider; repeat testing timed to your menstrual cycle (tell clinic which cycle day) and consider endocrine referral if symptoms persist.\\n- Vitamin D: 25-OH 20.9 ng/mL (low). Actions: start vitamin D3 2000 IU daily and eat vitamin D–rich foods (fatty fish, fortified dairy). Aim for 10–15 minutes sun exposure on arms/hands few times weekly if safe. Recheck 25-OH in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Cardiovascular inflammation and Lp(a): Lp(a) 60 mg/dL (elevated), hs-CRP 3.6 mg/L (elevated). Actions: heart-healthy diet and activity despite 0% 10-year risk: add 25–30 g fiber daily (oatmeal breakfast, beans at lunch, vegetables at dinner); include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines); reduce saturated fat (choose lean meats, limit butter/cheese); daily exercise 30 minutes brisk walking or equivalent; stop smoking if applicable. Discuss Lp(a) with your clinician—family history and specialist referral may be appropriate.\\n- Iron stores: Ferritin 37 ng/mL (low-normal; optimal >50). Actions: increase iron-rich foods (lean red meat, beans, spinach) + vitamin C with meals to improve absorption; consider oral iron supplement (discuss dose with provider) if symptoms or repeat ferritin remains low. Recheck ferritin and CBC in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Hydration: Hematocrit 45.4% (slightly high) — drink an extra 1–1.5 L water/day for a week and repeat CBC.\\n\\nOther notes\\n- Vitamin B12 902 pg/mL likely from supplements—review supplements with your clinician. Folate 4.41 ng/mL is low-normal; if pregnancy is possible, take 400–800 mcg folic acid daily.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Start vitamin D 2000 IU/day now; recheck 25-OH in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Improve diet/exercise as above and repeat lipid/hs-CRP if recommended in 3–6 months.\\n- Repeat CBC and ferritin in 8–12 weeks after hydration and dietary changes.\\n- Schedule follow-up with your provider in 4–8 weeks to review symptoms (period timing for hormone testing), consider endocrine or lipid specialist referral (for elevated testosterone and Lp(a)), and adjust plan.\\n\\nYou’re doing many things well. These targeted, manageable steps can lower symptoms and long-term risk — please contact the clinic to book the follow-up testing and discuss any supplements or prescriptions.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 14.6, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 45.4, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Slightly elevated hematocrit - may indicate dehydration.", "recommendation": "Encourage hydration. Repeat if persistent.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 89.4, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 89.4 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 274, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 55, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.42, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 6.6, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 86.4, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Elevated testosterone for females.", "recommendation": "Evaluate for PCOS, adrenal hyperplasia, or androgen-secreting tumor.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.29, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 37, "status": "borderline", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Iron insufficiency (ferritin 31-50). Optimal is >50.", "recommendation": "Provider protocol: Metagenics Hemagenics if symptomatic (fatigue, hair loss, restless legs, exercise intolerance).", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 20.9, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D deficiency (21-30 ng/mL). Suboptimal for hormone and bone health.", "recommendation": "Repletion: Metagenics D3 5,000 + K daily. Target ≥60 ng/mL. Recheck in 8-12 weeks.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 902, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "Elevated B12.", "recommendation": "Usually from supplementation. Rarely indicates liver disease.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 4.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 75, "status": "normal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 43, "status": "abnormal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Low HDL - increased cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Increase aerobic exercise. Consider niacin if very low.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 71, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: N/A (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "PREVENT calculator is validated for ages 30-79 years. Patient is 20 years old. Use clinical judgment for risk assessment.", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): N/A", "recommendation": "Age outside validated range - use clinical judgment", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: N/A", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 20, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 16, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 88, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.96, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 66, "status": "normal", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Apo B at optimal level.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 60, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Elevated Lp(a) - RISK ENHANCER. Increases CVD risk category.", "recommendation": "Lp(a) ≥50 mg/dL is a risk enhancer - consider upgrading CVD risk category. Aggressive LDL lowering indicated. Discuss hereditary nature.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 333, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 92, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 4.41, "status": "borderline", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Low-normal folate.", "recommendation": "Consider supplementation, especially if planning pregnancy.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 3.6, "status": "abnormal", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Elevated hs-CRP - increased cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Address cardiovascular risk factors. Consider repeat testing.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 25.4, "status": "normal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 25.4 nmol/L is within normal range. SHBG regulates bioavailable sex hormones by binding testosterone and estradiol.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 17, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 17 pg/mL is elevated. May cause androgenic symptoms (acne, hirsutism, hair thinning).", "recommendation": "Evaluate for PCOS, exogenous testosterone, or low SHBG driving elevated free T.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "of 6 markers positive", "value": 2, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Insulin Resistance Screening", "recheckTiming": "3-6 months", "interpretation": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive). This combination points toward hyperinsulinemia physiology. Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR).", "recommendation": "Phenotype(s): Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance. Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose.", "referenceRange": "0-1 Low, 2 Moderate, 3+ High Likelihood"}], "aiRecommendations": "Clinical purpose: staff-facing recommendations for provider review. Organized by priority with specific actions, doses, timelines, and when to notify the physician.\\n\\n1) Overall clinical assessment (concise)\\n- Key abnormalities: elevated total testosterone 86.4 ng/dL and free testosterone 17 pg/mL (both above female reference ranges), vitamin D deficiency 20.9 ng/mL, ferritin low-normal 37 ng/mL, elevated Lp(a) 60 mg/dL, hs-CRP 3.6 mg/L, low HDL 43 mg/dL, borderline folate 4.41 ng/mL, hematocrit 45.4% (borderline high), vitamin B12 high 902 pg/mL.\\n- Clinical concern priorities: hyperandrogenism (endogenous vs exogenous source) and cardiovascular risk enhancers (Lp(a) and hs-CRP). Iron and vitamin D insufficiency are addressable and may affect symptoms/long-term health.\\n\\n2) CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE prioritized actions\\n\\nCRITICAL (notify provider immediately)\\n- Elevated testosterone (total 86.4 ng/dL; free 17 pg/mL)\\n  - Notify provider now if patient reports virilization (voice changes, clitoromegaly, rapid/unusual hair growth), sudden worsening of acne, or rapid symptom onset.\\n  - If patient is currently using any exogenous androgen/testosterone (topical, intramuscular, pellets, compounded gels): instruct patient to HOLD further doses until provider review. Document route/dose/frequency and time of last dose for provider.\\n- Lipoprotein(a) 60 mg/dL and hs-CRP 3.6 mg/L\\n  - Notify provider for consideration of CVD risk reclassification and early specialist referral (lipid clinic/cardiology) if personal/family premature ASCVD.\\n\\nURGENT (action within 1–2 weeks; provider review)\\n- Vitamin D deficiency 20.9 ng/mL\\n  - Start repletion per clinic protocol: Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + vitamin K daily. Target 25-OH ≥60 ng/mL. Recheck level in 8–12 weeks.\\n  - Document supplementation history and calcium intake.\\n- Ferritin 37 ng/mL (iron insufficiency)\\n  - If symptomatic (fatigue, hair loss, RLS, exercise intolerance) or pregnancy/fertility planning: start iron per provider protocol (Metagenics Hemagenics as per clinic protocol). If asymptomatic, discuss benefits of empiric supplementation vs dietary measures.\\n  - Recheck ferritin in 8–12 weeks after starting supplementation.\\n- Free and total testosterone elevated — diagnostic evaluation:\\n  - If patient is not on exogenous testosterone: order targeted endocrine workup (see detailed testing section). Schedule in early follicular phase (cycle days 3–7) when possible for reproducible results. Consider pelvic ultrasound if PCOS suspected.\\n  - If on testosterone therapy: follow the “hold/reduce” guidance above and recheck levels (see monitoring).\\n\\nROUTINE (address within 4–12 weeks)\\n- HDL low (43 mg/dL): implement lifestyle measures (aerobic exercise, weight management, smoking cessation). Consider pharmacologic options only if indicated after risk reassessment.\\n- Folate low-normal (4.41 ng/mL): start folic acid 400–800 mcg daily if pregnancy planned or recommend standard multivitamin/prenatal. Recheck if clinically indicated.\\n- hs-CRP 3.6 mg/L: repeat after lifestyle interventions or treatment of any acute inflammation in 6–12 weeks to confirm persistent elevation before escalating therapy.\\n- Hematocrit 45.4%: encourage hydration, repeat CBC at next lab draw; notify provider if it increases or patient has polycythemia symptoms.\\n\\n3) Testosterone-specific recommendations (dose/action guidance for staff)\\n- If patient is receiving exogenous testosterone (any formulation)\\n  - Immediate action: HOLD next dose(s) and notify provider.\\n  - Suggested interim dosing plan for provider consideration:\\n    - For topical/gel/low-dose formulations: consider reducing dose by 50% or stopping temporarily pending re-evaluation and repeat levels in 4 weeks.\\n    - For long-acting IM injections or pellets: inform provider—levels may remain elevated for weeks; plan for monitoring and end-organ symptom assessment.\\n  - Monitoring after change: recheck total and free testosterone (morning draw) 4 weeks after stopping/reducing exogenous therapy. If therapy was stopped and levels normalize, discuss re-initiation at lower dose only after provider decision.\\n- If patient is NOT on exogenous testosterone\\n  - Do NOT initiate testosterone. Proceed with diagnostic evaluation for endogenous causes prior to any therapy.\\n  - Consider anti-androgen therapy only after provider-endocrine assessment and confirmation of source.\\n\\n4) Lifestyle and patient-education points for staff to cover (to be delivered by clinician/staff)\\n- Hydration: encourage increased oral fluids; repeat hematocrit with next labs.\\n- Vitamin D: start prescribed D3 5,000 IU + K daily; advise on safe sunlight exposure and dietary sources.\\n- Iron: if started on iron supplement per protocol, discuss GI side effects, take with vitamin C and between meals or per product directions; avoid concurrent calcium within 2 hours of iron.\\n- Cardiovascular risk: explain that elevated Lp(a) is often hereditary; emphasize smoking cessation, aerobic exercise (≥150 min/wk moderate), weight management, blood pressure control, and LDL optimization.\\n- Reproductive planning: if planning pregnancy, start folate 400–800 mcg/day and correct iron/vitamin D as above; avoid exogenous testosterone while attempting conception.\\n- Medication/supplement review: confirm and document all OTC supplements (especially B12, D3, and any testosterone products).\\n\\n5) Additional testing and referrals to consider\\n- Immediate/additional labs (timing in early follicular phase if patient is cycling):\\n  - Repeat total and free testosterone (morning fasting sample), SHBG (already normal per results), DHEA-S, 17-hydroxyprogesterone (AM), morning cortisol if clinical suspicion of adrenal source.\\n  - Pelvic ultrasound (transvaginal) if menstrual irregularity or clinical suspicion for PCOS.\\n  - If very rapid virilization or markedly higher testosterone (>150–200 ng/dL) or adrenal enzyme abnormalities: consider abdominal/pelvic imaging (CT/MRI) for androgen-secreting tumor—notify provider immediately.\\n- Cardiology/lipid clinic:\\n  - Refer for lipid specialist or cardiology consult if family or personal history of premature ASCVD, or if provider elects aggressive LDL lowering based on Lp(a) and hs-CRP.\\n  - Consider genetic counseling if strong family history of elevated Lp(a) or premature CVD.\\n- Endocrinology referral:\\n  - If endogenous hyperandrogenism confirmed or if diagnostic workup is unclear/abnormal, or if virilization present or resistant to initial management.\\n\\n6) Monitoring, re-check timeline, and who to notify\\n- Immediate (same day–48 hours)\\n  - Notify provider about elevated testosterone (86.4 ng/dL and free 17 pg/mL), Lp(a) 60 mg/dL, hs-CRP 3.6 mg/L, vitamin D deficiency.\\n  - If on exogenous testosterone: instruct patient to hold doses until provider direction; document and communicate to provider.\\n- Short-term (2–4 weeks)\\n  - If exogenous testosterone held/reduced: recheck total and free testosterone in 4 weeks (morning draw) and assess symptoms.\\n  - If starting iron for symptomatic ferritin 37: clinical follow-up and ferritin recheck in 8–12 weeks (but document start and schedule).\\n- Intermediate (6–12 weeks)\\n  - Vitamin D recheck at 8–12 weeks after starting D3 5,000 IU.\\n  - Ferritin recheck 8–12 weeks after starting iron.\\n  - Repeat hs-CRP in 6–12 weeks after addressing reversible causes/lifestyle changes.\\n  - If folate supplementation started for pregnancy planning, recheck per provider discretion.\\n- Routine (3–6 months)\\n  - Lipid panel and cardiovascular risk reassessment if lipid-lowering therapy is started or adjusted.\\n  - If diagnostic workup for hyperandrogenism is negative and symptoms persist, schedule endocrinology referral.\\n\\n7) Documentation and provider notification requirements\\n- Document: current medications/supplements (including topical/compounded testosterone gels), symptom inventory (hirsutism, acne, menstrual irregularity, voice changes), last menstrual period and cycle phase, pregnancy status/plans, family history of premature CVD.\\n- Mandatory notification to provider: elevated testosterone (both values), Lp(a) ≥50, hs-CRP >3, vitamin D <30, ferritin <50 with symptoms, and any report of virilization.\\n- Escalate to provider/emergency referral for rapid virilization or testosterone levels suggesting tumor (provider judgment; immediate imaging referral if concerned).\\n\\nConcise next-step summary for scheduling and orders\\n- Today:\\n  - Notify provider of testosterone, Lp(a), hs-CRP, vitamin D, ferritin results.\\n  - If on exogenous testosterone: instruct patient to hold medication pending provider review.\\n  - Start vitamin D3 5,000 IU + K daily per protocol; document start.\\n  - Begin folate 400–800 mcg if pregnancy planning.\\n- Orders to place per provider:\\n  - Repeat total and free testosterone (morning, fasting) — schedule in morning; if cycling, order for cycle day 3–7.\\n  - DHEA-S, 17-OH progesterone (AM), morning cortisol if indicated.\\n  - Pelvic ultrasound if PCOS suspected.\\n  - Ferritin recheck in 8–12 weeks if iron started.\\n  - Vitamin D 25-OH recheck in 8–12 weeks.\\n  - Repeat hs-CRP in 6–12 weeks.\\n  - Consider referral to lipid clinic/cardiology and/or endocrinology based on provider review.\\n\\nNote: Do not initiate androgen therapy. All medication starts/stops and imaging referrals require provider sign-off.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 1.65, "detail": "Triglycerides 71 / HDL 43 = 1.65", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "4.9%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 25.4, "detail": "Very low - strong IR signal", "positive": true, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 16, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "66 mg/dL", "detail": "Normal atherogenic particle count", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "3.6 mg/L", "detail": "Elevated systemic inflammation", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "moderate", "phenotypes": [{"key": "hormonal_pcos", "name": "Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Monitor SHBG, androgens, A1c every 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["SHBG very low (<30)", "Elevated total testosterone", "Elevated free testosterone"], "pathophysiology": "Hyperinsulinemia suppresses SHBG and increases ovarian androgen production.", "triggerCriteria": ["Low SHBG (<50; strong if <30)", "Elevated total or free testosterone", "Irregular cycles", "A1c may still be normal", "TG/HDL may be borderline-high"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests insulin may be influencing your hormone balance.\\n\\nWhen insulin levels run high:\\n- The body makes less SHBG (a protective hormone-binding protein)\\n- Androgen levels can rise\\n- Cycles may become irregular\\n\\nThis pattern is common in PCOS and other hormone imbalance states.\\n\\nImproving insulin sensitivity often helps restore hormonal balance and reduce long-term metabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward, lower refined carb approach", "Strength training", "Consider metformin if cycles irregular or A1c trending", "Consider GLP-1 if obesity phenotype present", "Monitor SHBG, androgens, A1c"]}], "positiveCount": 2, "likelihoodLabel": "MODERATE LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive). This combination points toward hyperinsulinemia physiology. Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR).", "confirmationTests": "Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose."}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Inflammatory Burden", "confidence": "high", "description": "Pattern of systemic inflammation indicated by elevated inflammatory markers and/or symptomatic presentation. May benefit from anti-inflammatory nutritional support.", "supportingFindings": ["hs-CRP 3.6 mg/L (elevated >3.0)", "WBC 13.5 K/uL (elevated)"]}, {"name": "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Metabolic pattern consistent with insulin resistance and/or visceral adiposity. This drives inflammation, hormonal imbalance, and cardiovascular risk.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance screening: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive)", "HDL 43 mg/dL (low)", "SHBG 25.4 nmol/L (low, associated with insulin resistance)", "hs-CRP 3.6 mg/L (metabolic inflammation)"]}, {"name": "Oxidative Stress Burden", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Multiple markers suggest increased oxidative stress and cellular burden. Antioxidant and glutathione support may help reduce oxidative damage and support cellular energy production.", "supportingFindings": ["hs-CRP 3.6 mg/L (elevated, systemic inflammation)", "Insulin resistance pattern (metabolic oxidative driver)"]}, {"name": "Iron Deficiency", "confidence": "low", "description": "Iron stores are depleted or suboptimal. Iron is essential for energy, cognitive function, hair health, and oxygen transport.", "supportingFindings": ["Ferritin 37 ng/mL (suboptimal ≤50)"]}]}	\N	2026-03-19 19:14:42.989788	2026-03-19 19:14:42.989788
