ion": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 60, "apoBValue": 66, "lpaStatus": "elevated", "apoBStatus": "normal", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 0, "hasElevatedLpa": true, "statinGuidance": "Possibly consider statin therapy given risk enhancement; shared decision-making recommended.", "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "reclassified_upward", "clinicalGuidance": "Lp(a) 60 mg/dL - RISK ENHANCER (genetic risk factor). RECLASSIFIED TO BORDERLINE RISK: Risk enhancer present. Shared decision-making regarding statin therapy.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "cacRecommendation": "CAC reasonable especially with strong family history and risk enhancement present.", "hasBorderlineApoB": false}, "cacStatinRec": {"lpaWarning": "Elevated Lp(a) of 60 mg/dL is a genetic cardiovascular risk marker. This level is associated with increased ASCVD risk independent of LDL. Since Lp(a) cannot be significantly lowered, focus on aggressive LDL/ApoB reduction and lifestyle optimization.", "statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall summary (plain language)\\n- Several important results are normal (good news) but a few require follow-up. Key concerns: elevated total testosterone 86.4 ng/dL and free testosterone 17 pg/mL, low vitamin D 25-OH at 20.9 ng/mL, elevated Lipoprotein(a) 60 mg/dL, and hs-CRP 3.6 mg/L. Your calculated 10-year ASCVD risk is 0% (LOW risk), which means your short-term risk of heart attack or stroke is low, but the elevated Lp(a) and hs-CRP are “risk enhancers” that deserve attention.\\n\\nPositive findings (what’s good right now)\\n- Hemoglobin 14.6 g/dL, MCV 89.4 fL, platelets 274 K/uL (all within normal range).\\n- LDL 75 mg/dL, triglycerides 71 mg/dL, Apolipoprotein B 66 mg/dL (all in optimal ranges).\\n- TSH 1.29 mIU/L (euthyroid), Hemoglobin A1c 4.9% (normal glucose), eGFR 88 mL/min (normal kidney function).\\n\\nAreas to improve (exact values + concrete actions)\\n- Testosterone: Total 86.4 ng/dL and Free 17 pg/mL (both elevated). Actions: prioritize 7–8 hours sleep nightly; add strength training 2–3× weekly; use stress reduction (10–20 minutes daily meditation, brisk walks, or yoga). Discuss symptoms (acne, hair changes, irregular periods) with your provider; repeat testing timed to your menstrual cycle (tell clinic which cycle day) and consider endocrine referral if symptoms persist.\\n- Vitamin D: 25-OH 20.9 ng/mL (low). Actions: start vitamin D3 2000 IU daily and eat vitamin D–rich foods (fatty fish, fortified dairy). Aim for 10–15 minutes sun exposure on arms/hands few times weekly if safe. Recheck 25-OH in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Cardiovascular inflammation and Lp(a): Lp(a) 60 mg/dL (elevated), hs-CRP 3.6 mg/L (elevated). Actions: heart-healthy diet and activity despite 0% 10-year risk: add 25–30 g fiber daily (oatmeal breakfast, beans at lunch, vegetables at dinner); include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines); reduce saturated fat (choose lean meats, limit butter/cheese); daily exercise 30 minutes brisk walking or equivalent; stop smoking if applicable. Discuss Lp(a) with your clinician—family history and specialist referral may be appropriate.\\n- Iron stores: Ferritin 37 ng/mL (low-normal; optimal >50). Actions: increase iron-rich foods (lean red meat, beans, spinach) + vitamin C with meals to improve absorption; consider oral iron supplement (discuss dose with provider) if symptoms or repeat ferritin remains low. Recheck ferritin and CBC in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Hydration: Hematocrit 45.4% (slightly high) — drink an extra 1–1.5 L water/day for a week and repeat CBC.\\n\\nOther notes\\n- Vitamin B12 902 pg/mL likely from supplements—review supplements with your clinician. Folate 4.41 ng/mL is low-normal; if pregnancy is possible, take 400–800 mcg folic acid daily.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Start vitamin D 2000 IU/day now; recheck 25-OH in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Improve diet/exercise as above and repeat lipid/hs-CRP if recommended in 3–6 months.\\n- Repeat CBC and ferritin in 8–12 weeks after hydration and dietary changes.\\n- Schedule follow-up with your provider in 4–8 weeks to review symptoms (period timing for hormone testing), consider endocrine or lipid specialist referral (for elevated testosterone and Lp(a)), and adjust plan.\\n\\nYou’re doing many things well. These targeted, manageable steps can lower symptoms and long-term risk — please contact the clinic to book the follow-up testing and discuss any supplements or prescriptions.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 14.6, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 45.4, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Sligh