44	22	2026-03-26 19:28:33.843	{"fsh": 11.9, "mcv": 89.2, "rbc": 4.17, "wbc": 9.9, "acne": false, "shbg": 43.8, "onHRT": true, "anxiety": true, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "ferritin": 8.8, "hairLoss": false, "vitaminD": 31.3, "estradiol": 181, "headaches": true, "lowEnergy": true, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 279, "hematocrit": 37.2, "hemoglobin": 12, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Amanda Richardson", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 36, "bmi": 24, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 110, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 0.6, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 22.1, "lowMotivation": false, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "freeTestosterone": 3}	{"redFlags": [{"action": "Metagenics Hemagenics OR Prescription 65mg Elemental Iron. Evaluate for occult blood loss.", "message": "Ferritin is 8.8 ng/mL (<10 threshold).", "category": "Iron - Severe Deficiency", "severity": "urgent"}], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History/context: 36-year-old female established in Women's Hormone & Primary Care Clinic. Patient presents for review of recent laboratory results related to hormone therapy monitoring and general health screening.\\n- Reported symptoms consistent with lab abnormalities (patient endorses / was queried regarding typical estrogen-dominant symptoms): insomnia with early-morning waking (3 a.m.), intermittent anxiety, breast fullness/tenderness, and irregular/heavier menses. No syncope, chest pain, shortness of breath, melena, hematochezia, or overt GI bleeding reported. No known pregnancy. No current report of severe fatigue beyond baseline. Patient understands need for further evaluation for iron deficiency.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\nVital signs: BMI and BP not provided in lab packet. STOP-BANG sleep apnea risk: 0 points.\\n\\nLaboratory results (03/26/2026)\\n- Complete Blood Count (CBC)\\n  - Hemoglobin: 12.0 g/dL — within reference range (NORMAL)\\n  - Hematocrit: 37.2 % — NORMAL\\n  - MCV: 89.2 fL — NORMAL\\n  - Platelets: 279 K/uL — NORMAL\\n- Iron studies\\n  - Ferritin: 8.8 ng/mL — CRITICAL / Severe iron deficiency (FLAGGED; reference threshold for concern <10 ng/mL)\\n  - (No additional iron panel values provided)\\n- Hormones\\n  - Estradiol (E2): 181 pg/mL — ABNORMAL (elevated relative to typical HRT target range; consistent with estrogen dominance pattern)\\n  - Progesterone: 0.6 ng/mL — ABNORMAL (below expected HRT therapeutic/mid-luteal range)\\n  - Testosterone, Total: 22.1 ng/dL — ABNORMAL (low for age despite HRT)\\n  - Free Testosterone: 3 pg/mL — NORMAL (per report)\\n  - SHBG: 43.8 nmol/L — NORMAL\\n  - FSH: 11.9 mIU/mL — NORMAL for perimenopausal transition range\\n- Vitamin / Other\\n  - Vitamin D, 25-OH: 31.3 ng/mL — BORDERLINE / Insufficient (31–40 ng/mL)\\n- Risk scores: STOP-BANG 0 (low risk for obstructive sleep apnea)\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1) Severe iron deficiency — ferritin 8.8 ng/mL (CRITICAL). Iron stores severely depleted despite preserved hemoglobin (Hgb 12.0 g/dL) — iron deficiency without anemia at present. This is a high-priority red flag requiring timely treatment and evaluation for source of blood loss.\\n   - Clinical significance: symptomatic or progressive iron depletion; risk for developing iron-deficiency anemia; needs replacement and investigation for occult blood loss (GI vs gynecologic).\\n   - Differential: heavy menstrual blood loss (menorrhagia), occult GI bleeding, dietary insufficiency, malabsorption (less likely without other history), pregnancy (unlikely but must be excluded if applicable).\\n\\n2) Estrogen dominance pattern consistent with ovulatory dysfunction — Estradiol 181 pg/mL with low progesterone 0.6 ng/mL indicates continued estrogenic exposure without adequate luteal-phase progesterone. Correlates with reported symptoms (insomnia/3am waking, anxiety, breast tenderness, irregular/heavy cycles).\\n   - Clinical significance: symptoms of estrogen dominance; potential endometrial protection needed if on unopposed estrogen; abnormal uterine bleeding risk.\\n   - Differential: exogenous estrogen dose too high or inappropriate timing relative to progesterone; anovulatory cycles; perimenopausal endocrine variability.\\n\\n3) Low total testosterone — 22.1 ng/dL despite HRT (Free T 3 pg/mL reported as normal). Possible inadequate androgen replacement or altered bioavailability (SHBG 43.8 nmol/L is within normal range).\\n   - Clinical significance: may contribute to low libido, decreased energy, or mood changes (patient-reported symptoms correlate partially).\\n   - Differential: inadequate dosing/administration of testosterone therapy, medication interactions, lab variability.\\n\\n4) Vitamin D insufficiency — 25-OH Vitamin D 31.3 ng/mL (borderline insufficient).\\n   - Clinical significance: suboptimal for hormone optimization, bone health; supplementation indicated.\\n\\n5) Other labs normal — CBC indices otherwise normal (Hgb/Hct/MCV), platelets normal, SHBG and FSH are within expected ranges for perimenopausal transition. STOP-BANG low risk for sleep apnea.\\n\\nPLAN:\\nGeneral: Results reviewed and discussed with patient today (03/26/2026). Patient counseled on urgency of iron deficiency and need for further evaluation and prompt treatment. Follow-up and monitoring plans outlined below.\\n\\n1) Severe iron deficiency (Ferritin 8.8 ng/mL)\\n   - Treatment: Begin iron replacement promptly.\\n     - Preferred (non-prescription supplement option): Metagenics Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support — 1 tablet PO twice daily with meals.\\n     - Prescription option (if patient prefers Rx): Oral ferrous sulfate 325 mg (contains ~65 mg elemental iron) — dosing to be determined by prescribing clinician; common regimen is one tablet PO once to twice daily as tolerated. Recommend discussion with prescribing clinician for definitive Rx dosing.\\n   - Counseling: Take iron with vitamin C or orange juice to improve absorption; avoid calcium, tea, coffee, and high-fiber foods close to dosing. Expect GI side effects (constipation, nausea); consider stool softener or polyethylene glycol PRN for constipation. If Hemagenics chosen, review specific product directions and interactions.\\n   - Diagnostic evaluation for source of iron loss:\\n     - Order fecal immunochemical test (FIT) x1 (or stool guaiac) to evaluate occult GI bleeding. If positive or persistent iron deficiency, arrange GI referral for further evaluation (consider endoscopic workup as clinically indicated).\\n     - Arrange gynecologic evaluation for possible heavy menstrual bleeding (pelvic exam and transvaginal ultrasound as indicated, assessment for fibroids or structural causes). Consider menstrual blood loss quantification and review current contraception/HRT effects on bleeding.\\n   - Monitoring: Repeat labs in 2–4 weeks (urgent recheck window) — CBC (Hgb/Hct), ferritin, and iron studies to confirm response. Continue treatment until ferritin >50 ng/mL (typical target for repletion) and clinical improvement; adjust plan per results.\\n\\n2) Estrogen dominance / low progesterone (Estradiol 181 pg/mL; Progesterone 0.6 ng/mL)\\n   - Immediate: Discussed need to optimize progesterone to protect endometrium and address symptoms.\\n   - Management recommendation (to be implemented by HRT prescriber):\\n     - Consider increasing progesterone exposure. Options include micronized progesterone (e.g., 100–200 mg PO nightly for luteal support or cyclic dosing such as days 14–28 depending on regimen and patient preference). Alternatively, consider continuous combined therapy if appropriate.\\n     - Reassess estradiol regimen: consider lowering estradiol dose or altering route of administration if symptomatic or if clinician determines estradiol is supratherapeutic for patient goals.\\n   - Monitoring: Re-check serum estradiol and progesterone in 2–4 weeks after any HRT dose adjustments to confirm therapeutic progesterone and avoid sustained unopposed estrogen. If changes not made, re-check hormone panel in 2–4 weeks given abnormal values.\\n   - Additional: If heavy bleeding persists or endometrial concerns arise, obtain transvaginal ultrasound (endometrial thickness) and consider referral to gynecology.\\n\\n3) Low total testosterone (22.1 ng/dL) despite HRT\\n   - Plan: Review current testosterone formulation, dose, application technique/adherence, and timing of blood draw relative to dosing. Consider increasing dose or changing formulation if clinically indicated.\\n   - Action: Discuss with prescribing clinician/endocrinologist for potential adjustment. Repeat testosterone (total and free) with clear timing instructions relative to dosing in 2–4 weeks after any adjustment.\\n   - Consider checking additional labs if adjusting therapy (lipids, LFTs, CBC) per testosterone monitoring guidelines.\\n\\n4) Vitamin D insufficiency (25-OH 31.3 ng/mL)\\n   - Start Vitamin D3 5,000 IU daily with a meal plus K (as recommended): D3 5,000 + K — 1 softgel PO daily with a meal (medium-priority).\\n   - Re-check 25-OH Vitamin D in 8–12 weeks to confirm repletion; then adjust dose to maintenance (e.g., 1,000–2,000 IU daily) as appropriate.\\n\\n5) Adjunct supplement recommendations provided and counseling\\n   - Magtein® Magnesium L-Threonate — 3 capsules daily divided doses (medium priority); useful for sleep/mood/neurologic support.\\n   - EstroFactors™ — 2 tablets twice daily (medium priority) — as recommended by clinic for symptom management.\\n   - AdvaClear® — 2 capsules twice daily (medium priority) — for detoxification/adverse estrogen metabolites support per clinic protocol.\\n   - Review for possible interactions with iron and other meds/supplements; counsel on timing (separate iron and calcium/magnesium by 2 hours).\\n\\n6) Safety and red-flag actions\\n   - Urgent red flag: ferritin <10 ng/mL — requires prompt treatment and evaluation for blood loss. Ordered FIT stool test today (or will order per clinic protocol) and gynecologic referral as indicated. If patient develops melena, hematemesis, hematochezia, syncope, or progressive weakness, instructed to present to ER immediately.\\n   - Documented recommendation for GI evaluation if occult blood loss suspected or if iron deficiency persists despite supplementation.\\n\\n7) Follow-up and monitoring\\n   - Urgent recheck window for critical values: 2–4 weeks.\\n     - Labs to repeat at 2–4 weeks: CBC (Hgb/Hct), ferritin, iron studies (if available), estradiol, progesterone, total testosterone (timed appropriately relative to dosing), and basic metabolic panel if on oral iron and symptomatic.\\n   - Vitamin D recheck at 8–12 weeks.\\n   - Gynecology referral to be scheduled within 2–4 weeks for evaluation of heavy/irregular menses if patient agrees.\\n   - GI referral if FIT positive or if iron deficiency persists despite supplementation.\\n   - Clinic follow-up appointment / telephone check-in in 2–4 weeks to review repeat labs and finalize HRT adjustments.\\n\\nCommunication:\\n- Results reviewed and discussed with patient today (03/26/2026). Risks, benefits, and rationale for iron repletion and for adjusting HRT/progesterone were explained. Patient verbalized understanding and agrees with planned steps. Questions answered.\\n\\nDisposition:\\n- Start Hemagenics RBC Support 1 tablet PO twice daily with meals today (or prescription oral iron 65 mg elemental per prescribing clinician).\\n- Start Vitamin D3 5,000 IU + K daily.\\n- Begin other recommended supplements as desired and after reviewing interactions.\\n- Arrange FIT/stool occult blood testing and gynecology referral.\\n- Reassess labs in 2–4 weeks and follow up as above.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\nDate: 03/26/2026.", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 tablet twice daily with meals", "name": "Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support", "caution": "Contains iron, folate, and B12 for comprehensive RBC support. Avoid taking with calcium-rich foods or dairy. May cause mild GI upset initially.", "category": "iron", "priority": "high", "rationale": "Hemagenics provides highly absorbable iron bisglycinate with B12, B6, and folate for comprehensive red blood cell support. Indicated for functional iron deficiency, iron deficiency without anemia, and iron-responsive symptoms.", "indication": "Ferritin 8.8 ng/mL (severely depleted ≤20); Fatigue with iron depletion", "phenotypes": ["Iron Deficiency"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "Your lab results suggest your iron stores are lower than optimal. This supplement provides a gentle, well-absorbed form of iron along with B12 and folate to support your energy levels, hair health, and overall vitality.", "supportingFindings": ["Ferritin 8.8 ng/mL (severely depleted ≤20)", "Fatigue with iron depletion"]}, {"dose": "1 softgel daily with meal", "name": "D3 5,000 + K", "caution": "Repletion dose for deficiency/insufficiency. Contains vitamin K2 for optimal calcium metabolism. Recheck levels in 8-12 weeks.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "D3 5,000 + K provides vitamin D3 with K2 for repletion of deficiency/insufficiency. Target ≥60 ng/mL for bone, immune, and cardiovascular support.", "indication": "Vitamin D 31.3 ng/mL (insufficient)", "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D is below optimal range. This supplement will help restore your levels to support bone health, immune function, and overall wellness.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 31.3 ng/mL (insufficient)"]}, {"dose": "3 capsules daily (divided doses)", "name": "Magtein® Magnesium L-Threonate", "caution": "L-Threonate form crosses blood-brain barrier for cognitive and sleep support. Well-tolerated; gentle on GI system.", "category": "mineral", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Magtein is the only magnesium form clinically shown to cross the blood-brain barrier. Supports quality sleep, cognitive function, GABA activity, and stress resilience.", "indication": "Low energy reported; Anxiety reported (magnesium supports GABA); Headaches reported", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This special form of magnesium is designed to reach the brain, where it supports better sleep quality, mental clarity, and a calmer nervous system.", "supportingFindings": ["Low energy reported", "Anxiety reported (magnesium supports GABA)", "Headaches reported"]}, {"dose": "2 tablets twice daily", "name": "EstroFactors™", "caution": "Nutritional support for estrogen balance and metabolism. Contains I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Supports healthy estrogen metabolite ratios.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "EstroFactors provides targeted nutritional support for estrogen metabolism and balance with I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Best suited for women with estrogen dominance patterns, PMS, perimenopausal hormone imbalance, or those needing broader estrogen metabolism support during hormonal transitions. Differs from Estrovera in that it addresses the underlying estrogen metabolism rather than vasomotor symptom relief.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected; Headaches reported (hormone-related)", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement helps your body process and balance estrogen more effectively. It supports healthy hormone metabolism, which can help with PMS symptoms, bloating, mood changes, and hormonal balance during life transitions.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected", "Headaches reported (hormone-related)"]}, {"dose": "2 capsules twice daily", "name": "AdvaClear®", "caution": "Broad-spectrum phase I/II liver detoxification support. Contains NAC, green tea extract, milk thistle, and B-vitamins for biotransformation. Best taken with food.", "category": "detox", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "AdvaClear provides broad-spectrum phase I and phase II liver detoxification support with NAC, green tea extract, watercress, and B-vitamins. Designed for metabolic clearance support, biotransformation of hormones and environmental compounds, and hepatic stress. Appropriate when liver markers are borderline, estrogen clearance is sluggish, or metabolic dysfunction is present.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance pattern (hepatic detox pathways needed for clearance); Low energy (biotransformation burden)", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance"], "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This supplement supports your liver's natural detoxification processes. Your results suggest your body could benefit from help processing hormones, metabolic byproducts, and other compounds. It provides nutrients that support both phases of your liver's detox pathways.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance pattern (hepatic detox pathways needed for clearance)", "Low energy (biotransformation burden)"]}, {"dose": "1 soft chew during times of stress", "name": "HerWellness™ Rapid Stress Relief", "caution": "Fast-acting L-Theanine and Lactium formula. Non-drowsy. Promotes calm within 1 hour. Contains milk.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "low", "rationale": "HerWellness Rapid Stress Relief provides fast-acting stress support with L-Theanine (200mg) and Lactium. Promotes calm within 1 hour without drowsiness.", "indication": "Anxiety reported", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This fast-acting supplement helps your body respond to stress more calmly. It promotes relaxation within about an hour and can be taken as needed without causing drowsiness.", "supportingFindings": ["Anxiety reported"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "2-4 weeks (urgent values present)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\nYour labs show a pattern called “estrogen dominance” with an elevated estradiol of 181 pg/mL and a low progesterone of 0.6 ng/mL. This usually reflects ovulatory dysfunction in perimenopause rather than too much estrogen production. You also have severely low iron stores (ferritin 8.8 ng/mL) even though your hemoglobin is still normal (12 g/dL). Vitamin D is a bit low at 31.3 ng/mL. Your total testosterone is 22.1 ng/dL (reported as low despite HRT); free testosterone is 3 pg/mL and SHBG is 43.8 nmol/L.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Hemoglobin 12 g/dL and hematocrit 37.2% are within the normal range.\\n- MCV 89.2 fL, platelets 279 K/uL, and FSH 11.9 mIU/mL are within expected ranges.\\n- STOP-BANG score 0 = low risk for sleep apnea.\\n\\nAreas to improve (with specific actions)\\n1) Estradiol 181 pg/mL / Progesterone 0.6 ng/mL — estrogen dominance:\\n   - Urgent: physician review required before any hormone changes.\\n   - Lifestyle: prioritize sleep hygiene (7–8 hours nightly), reduce excess alcohol, avoid caffeine late afternoon (may lower nighttime anxiety/3am waking), and track bleeding patterns and symptoms (breast tenderness, migraines, heavy/irregular cycles).\\n   - If you have heavy or irregular bleeding, ask your clinician about progesterone (cyclic) to protect the uterine lining.\\n\\n2) Ferritin 8.8 ng/mL (severely low):\\n   - Start oral iron: aim for ~60–65 mg elemental iron (for example ferrous sulfate 325 mg = 65 mg elemental) every other day with 250–500 mg vitamin C or a glass of orange juice to improve absorption. Take away from calcium, coffee/tea. Continue for at least 8–12 weeks and recheck ferritin and CBC.\\n   - Eat iron-rich foods daily: lean red meat, lentils/beans, spinach, iron-fortified cereal; cook in cast-iron pans.\\n\\n3) Vitamin D 31.3 ng/mL (insufficient):\\n   - Start vitamin D3 1,000–2,000 IU daily and recheck 25-OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks.\\n\\n4) Testosterone 22.1 ng/dL (low):\\n   - Lifestyle to support testosterone: 7–8 hours quality sleep, strength training 2–3x weekly, manage stress with exercise, meditation or hobbies, and maintain healthy body weight.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Physician review is needed urgently for the hormone pattern (estradiol/progesterone) before changing HRT.\\n- Begin iron therapy now and recheck ferritin + CBC in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Start vitamin D3 and recheck in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Consider a follow-up hormone panel after any HRT adjustment in ~6–12 weeks and continue tracking symptoms and bleeding; reach out sooner if you develop heavy bleeding, severe headaches, or new symptoms.\\n\\nIf you’d like, we can schedule a follow-up visit to review these results and make a specific treatment plan.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 12, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 37.2, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 89.2, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 89.2 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 279, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 181, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol elevated above typical HRT range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Evaluate estrogen dose. Monitor for estrogen excess symptoms.", "referenceRange": "60-100 pg/mL (HRT goal, >40 for bone)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.6, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone below HRT therapeutic range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase progesterone dose. Target 8-10 ng/mL for optimal endometrial protection.", "referenceRange": "8-10 ng/mL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 11.9, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 22.1, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Low testosterone despite HRT.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase testosterone dose. Target 75-125 ng/dL for optimal results.", "referenceRange": "75-125 ng/dL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 8.8, "status": "critical", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Severely depleted iron stores without anemia.", "recommendation": "Provider protocol: Metagenics Hemagenics OR Prescription 65mg Elemental Iron (take every other day, on empty stomach, avoid caffeine and dairy 2 hours before/after). Evaluate for blood loss.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 31.3, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D insufficient (31-40 ng/mL). Suboptimal for hormone optimization.", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 5,000 + K daily. Target ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 181, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Estrogen Dominance Pattern", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 181 pg/mL with progesterone of only 0.6 ng/mL represents estrogen dominance. This is not excess estrogen production — it reflects ovulatory dysfunction where estrogen continues but ovulation (and thus progesterone) fails. Associated symptoms include insomnia or 3am waking, anxiety, breast tenderness, migraines, and heavy or irregular cycles.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Add progesterone first — do not attempt to suppress estrogen. Micronized progesterone 100–200 mg nightly provides opposition and symptom relief. Avoid language about \\"too much estrogen\\" — the issue is inadequate progesterone from anovulatory cycles. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen is in the higher range, but the real issue is that progesterone hasn't kept up. This happens when ovulation becomes irregular in perimenopause. Adding progesterone will help balance things out and relieve symptoms like heavy periods, breast tenderness, and mood swings.", "referenceRange": "E2 >150 with P4 <5 indicates unopposed estrogen"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 43.8, "status": "normal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 43.8 nmol/L is within normal range. SHBG regulates bioavailable sex hormones by binding testosterone and estradiol.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 3, "status": "normal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 3 pg/mL is within normal functional range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}], "aiRecommendations": "Unable to generate recommendations. Please review lab findings manually.", "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Iron Deficiency", "confidence": "high", "description": "Iron stores are depleted or suboptimal. Iron is essential for energy, cognitive function, hair health, and oxygen transport.", "supportingFindings": ["Ferritin 8.8 ng/mL (severely depleted ≤20)", "Low energy/fatigue reported"]}, {"name": "Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance", "con