health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 7, "apoBValue": 109, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "borderline", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 1.6379454598848506, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": true}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "generally_recommended", "indicated": true, "rationale": "ApoB ≥90 mg/dL (109 mg/dL) with risk enhancers (elevated hs-CRP, elevated ApoB). Statin discussion recommended."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "consider", "rationale": "Borderline lipid levels with multiple risk enhancers (elevated hs-CRP, elevated ApoB). Consider CAC to refine risk assessment.", "recommended": true}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\n- You are postmenopausal (FSH 44.7 mIU/mL) and have a few important findings to address: high LDL cholesterol (171 mg/dL), elevated inflammation (hs-CRP 3.2 mg/L), and testosterone higher than the HRT target (Total T 179 ng/dL). Progesterone is 1.84 ng/mL (below the typical HRT therapeutic range), and estradiol is 57 pg/mL (at the low end of bone-protective levels). Many other results are reassuring.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Hemoglobin 13.1 g/dL, hematocrit 39.1%, ferritin 73.2 ng/mL — good iron stores and no anemia.\\n- Vitamin D 25-OH 69.7 ng/mL — optimal for bone and immune health.\\n- HDL 111 mg/dL and triglycerides 54 mg/dL — protective lipid values.\\n- TSH 0.7 mIU/L — thyroid function is normal.\\n- 10-year ASCVD risk 1.6% — overall 10-year heart risk is low.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + concrete actions)\\n1) LDL cholesterol 171 mg/dL and ApoB 109 mg/dL; hs-CRP 3.2 mg/L:\\n   - Add 25–30 g fiber daily: oatmeal for breakfast, beans or lentils at lunch, vegetables at dinner.\\n   - Include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n   - Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter and high-fat cheeses.\\n   - Daily exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking (or equivalent) most days.\\n   - Because LDL is 171 mg/dL and hs-CRP is 3.2 mg/L, discuss lipid management with your clinician; with persistent LDL >160 despite lifestyle changes, a statin may be considered even if 10-year risk is low. Recheck fasting lipids in 6–12 weeks after lifestyle changes.\\n\\n2) Testosterone: Total T 179 ng/dL (HRT optimization goal 75–125 ng/dL); SHBG 158 nmol/L; Free T 10 pg/mL:\\n   - Priority: discuss reducing testosterone dose with your provider.\\n   - Lifestyle to support healthy hormones: prioritize 7–8 hours sleep nightly, strength training 2–3×/week, and daily stress management (walks, meditation, hobbies).\\n\\n3) Progesterone 1.84 ng/mL:\\n   - This is below typical HRT therapeutic targets but is unexpectedly above typical postmenopausal endogenous levels—please bring a current medication/supplement list to your appointment. Your clinician may repeat the test or adjust HRT dosing.\\n\\n4) MCV 96.1 fL (high-normal) and B12 2000 pg/mL (elevated):\\n   - If you take high-dose B12 supplements, consider lowering dose and review with your clinician. If macrocytosis progresses, testing methylmalonic acid may help.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Schedule a visit in the next 4–8 weeks to review HRT (reduce testosterone dose; review progesterone source) and to plan lipid strategy. Recheck lipids and hs-CRP in ~6–12 weeks after lifestyle changes. Continue vitamin D and bone-supporting habits (weight-bearing exercise, calcium ~1,000–1,200 mg/day from diet/supplements as advised). If you want, bring current supplements/medications to the visit so we can reconcile causes of the progesterone and high B12. You’re doing many things well — these targeted steps can lower heart risk and optimize hormone balance.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 13.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 39.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 96.1, "status": "borderline", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 96.1 fL is in the high-normal range. Early macrocytosis can precede overt B12 or folate deficiency.", "recommendation": "Ensure B12 and folate levels are within optimal range. Monitor CBC at next visit.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 377, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 57, "status": "borderline", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol at minimum bone protection level (40-60 pg/mL) but below optimal HRT goal.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Consider increasing estrogen dose. Target 60-100 pg/mL for optimized symptom relief.", "referenceRange": "60-100 pg/mL (HRT goal, >40 for bone)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1.84, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone below HRT therapeutic range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase progesterone dose. Target 8-10 ng/mL for optimal endometrial protection.", "referenceRange": "8-10 ng/mL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 44.7, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "Elevated FSH consistent with menopause.", "recommendation": "Expected finding for postmenopausal women.", "referenceRange": ">25 mIU/mL (postmenopausal)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 179, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone above HRT optimization goal (75-125 ng/dL).", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Consider reducing testosterone dose. Monitor for androgenic side effects.", "referenceRange": "75-125 ng/dL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 0.7, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 73.2, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 69.7, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D optimal (60-100 ng/mL). Supports hormone, bone, and immune health.", "recommendation": "Maintain current regimen. Monitor annually.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 2000, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "Elevated B12.", "recommendation": "Usually from supplementation. Rarely indicates liver disease.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.2, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 171, "status": "abnormal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "High LDL.", "recommendation": "Consider statin based on ASCVD risk. Lifestyle modifications.", "