efit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\n- Most results are healthy. Your 10-year ASCVD risk is very low (0.2%), and routine heart disease prevention is appropriate. There are two borderline items to address: Vitamin D is slightly low (insufficient) and your hormone pattern suggests early perimenopause. I’ve included clear steps you can start right away.\\n\\nPositive findings (specific values)\\n- Heart/lipids: LDL 66 mg/dL, HDL 66 mg/dL, triglycerides 41 mg/dL — excellent.\\n- Blood sugar: Hemoglobin A1c 5.0% — normal.\\n- Thyroid: TSH 2.0 mIU/L — euthyroid.\\n- Blood counts: Hemoglobin 12.8 g/dL, hematocrit 40.6%, MCV 92.7 fL, platelets 302 K/uL — within normal ranges.\\n- Liver/kidneys: AST 15 U/L, ALT 11 U/L, creatinine 0.83 mg/dL, eGFR 94 mL/min — normal.\\n- Vitamin B12 502 pg/mL — adequate.\\n\\nAreas to improve (exact values + actions)\\n1) Vitamin D: 25-OH = 31.6 ng/mL (insufficient; 31–40 ng/mL). Actionable plan:\\n   - Start vitamin D3 2,000 IU daily (or as directed by your clinician). Recheck 25-OH vitamin D in 3 months.\\n   - Add 2 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel), 10–20 minutes sunlight on arms/face several times per week, and include calcium-rich foods (aim for total 1,000–1,200 mg calcium/day) for bone health.\\n   - Begin weight-bearing exercise (brisk walking or stair climbing) 30 minutes most days + strength training 2x/week.\\n\\n2) Reproductive hormones / perimenopause signs:\\n   - FSH 9.26 mIU/mL, estradiol (E2) 25 pg/mL, progesterone 0.44 ng/mL (age 35). These numbers plus symptoms indicate early perimenopause — the ovaries are becoming less predictable.\\n   - Actionable steps: Track cycles and symptoms for 3 months (note hot flashes, sleep changes, irregular bleeding, libido changes). If you want clearer evaluation of ovulation, repeat progesterone mid-luteal (~7 days before expected period) or repeat FSH on day 3 of a cycle per provider instructions.\\n   - For symptoms (fatigue, low mood, low libido): prioritize 7–8 hours sleep, strength training 2–3x weekly, daily 10–20 min stress management (breathing, meditation), and consider referral to menopause specialist if symptoms affect quality of life.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Start vitamin D 2,000 IU/day now; recheck 25-OH D in 3 months.\\n- Keep a 3-month cycle/symptom diary; repeat hormone testing with cycle timing as recommended, or sooner if symptoms worsen (heavy bleeding, severe hot flashes, sudden mood changes).\\n- Routine follow-up with your clinician in 3–6 months to review results and adjust plan. You’re doing well overall — these targeted steps can help protect bone health and manage early perimenopausal changes.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 12.8, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 40.6, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 92.7, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 92.7 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 302, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 25, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.44, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 9.26, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 24.8, "status": "normal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone within normal female range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 2, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 31.6, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D insufficient (31-40 ng/mL). Suboptimal for hormone optimization.", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 5,000 + K daily. Target ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 502, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 66, "status": "normal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 66, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 41, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.2314921049834827, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 0.2% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.15056795247813518, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 0.2%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.1275401626272042, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.1%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 1.8964568077423207, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 1.9%, ASCVD 1.1%, Heart Failure 1.2%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 15, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 11, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 94, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.83, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 9.26, "status": "borderline", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Early Perimenopause", "interpretation": "FSH of 9.26 mIU/mL with progesterone of 0.44 ng/mL in a 35-year-old indicates early perimenopause. The brain is starting to signal harder (rising FSH) because ovarian response is becoming unpredictable. This is the beginning of the transition.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Progesterone is foundational at this stage: micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly for sleep and nervous system support. Begin the hormone continuity discussion — frame HRT as future-forward planning, not crisis intervention. Nervous system calming messaging matters. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your body is entering the perimenopause transition. Your brain is working a little harder to communicate with your ovaries, and ovulation is becoming less consistent. This can cause sleep changes, anxiety, and cycle irregularities. Progesterone support now can smooth this transition and protect your quality of life.", "referenceRange": "FSH 8–15 with P4 <5 indicates early transition"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 24.8, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone Pattern D: Adequate Androgens, Persistent Symptoms", "interpretation": "Adequate Androgens pattern identified (Total T: 24.8 ng/dL (within range)). Total, free, and bioavailable testosterone are within normal ranges and SHBG is not extreme. If the patient reports fatigue, low mood, sleep disruption, or low libido, these symptoms are likely driven by non-androgenic factors. Findings to evaluate: Estrogen/Progesterone balance should be assessed (E2: 25 pg/mL, P4: 0.44 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Do not reflexively add testosterone when levels are adequate — look deeper. Evaluate iron status (ferritin target >50 for symptom resolution), thyroid function (optimize TSH), and sleep quality (consider sleep apnea screening with STOP-BANG). Assess estrogen and progesterone balance — E2/P4 imbalance is a common driver of persistent symptoms. Address CNS and psychosocial drivers: chronic stress, HPA axis dysregulation, mood disorders. Consider DHEA-S if not already checked. Support with lifestyle optimization: sleep hygiene, stress management, nutrition. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your testosterone levels are actually in a healthy range, which is good news. When symptoms persist despite normal testosterone, it usually means something else is contributing. Your provider will look at other factors like iron levels, thyroid function, sleep quality, and hormone balance to find what's driving your symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Total T 15-70 ng/dL, Free T 0.5-5.0 pg/mL (all within range)"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- Labs indicate early perimenopausal physiology in a 35-year-old: FSH 9.26 mIU/mL with low progesterone (P4 0.44 ng/mL) → ovarian response becoming less predictable. Estradiol 25 pg/mL and low luteal-phase progesterone suggest anovulatory or follicular-phase sampling; correlate with cycle day.\\n- Vitamin D insufficient at 31.6 ng/mL by clinic protocol (target ≥60 ng/mL for hormone optimization).\\n- Total testosterone 24.8 ng/dL is within reference range; pattern consistent with “Adequate Androgens, Persistent Symptoms.” Do not initiate androgen therapy based on current numbers alone.\\n- CBC, lipids, A1c, renal/hepatic markers, TSH and B12 are normal per provided data.\\n- No immediate lab red flags (no severe anemia, thyroid dysfunction, marked lipid or metabolic derangement shown).\\n\\nPriority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL\\n- None emergent based on provided labs (no severe anemia, no hypercalcemia documented, no markedly abnormal chemistry). No immediate inpatient or emergency action required.\\n\\nURGENT (action within 1–2 weeks / notify provider)\\n- Initiate micronized progesterone 100 mg PO nightly as the protocol-recommended foundational therapy for early perimenopause — provider should review and sign order prior to start.\\n  * Physician notification requirement: provider must confirm there are no contraindications (pregnancy risk, active liver disease, known hypersensitivity, history of hormone-sensitive malignancy where progesterone is contraindicated) and approve the Rx.\\n  * Pre-initiation checklist for staff to confirm/document: last menstrual period, pregnancy test if pregnancy possible/unknown, current contraception use and desire for future fertility, active breast or uterine pathology, current estrogen use (if patient on transdermal/other HRT, need combined regimen considerations).\\n- Begin Vitamin D repletion: Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily per protocol — provider to sign order and verify no hypercalcemia or granulomatous disease history.\\n  * Notify provider if patient is on high-dose calcium supplements, hypercalcemia, or taking medications interacting with vitamin D.\\n\\nROUTINE (to be completed in next 4–12 weeks)\\n- Do NOT start testosterone therapy at this time. Document rationale and patient counseling. Escalation to consider testosterone only if objective low androgen levels and persistent symptoms after addressing other reversible causes — discuss with provider if symptoms persist despite interventions.\\n- Targeted assessments (see testing/referral section) and lifestyle interventions.\\n\\nSpecific medication/dose recommendations (for staff to implement after provider approval)\\n- Micronized progesterone: 100 mg PO at bedtime nightly — start immediately after provider sign-off. Reassess benefit/sleep response at 8–12 weeks.\\n- Vitamin D3 + K: Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily — recheck 25-OH D in ~12 weeks.\\n- Testosterone: No dose indicated. Document “Not indicated — Total T 24.8 ng/dL adequate.”\\n\\nFollow-up timing and monitoring\\n- Progesterone symptom follow-up: telephone or clinic check at 4 weeks for tolerance (sedation, mood effects), in-person or telehealth at 8–12 weeks for efficacy review. Reassess need for continued nightly dosing vs cyclic approach at 3 months.\\n- Vitamin D: recheck 25-OH vitamin D at 12 weeks after starting 5,000 IU/day. Aim for ≥60 ng/mL per clinic protocol.\\n- Re-evaluate perimenopause labs (FSH, estradiol, progesterone) in 6–12 months or sooner if symptoms progress, cycles become markedly irregular, or if fertility is a concern.\\n- If new/worsening symptoms (heavy bleeding, severe mood changes, chest pain, thrombotic symptoms, signs of liver dysfunction), notify physician immediately.\\n\\nPatient education points for staff to cover (documented counseling)\\n- Explain perimenopause in neutral, forward-planning terms: brain signals to ovaries are increasing, ovulation is becoming less predictable; symptoms may include sleep changes, anxiety, cycle irregularity.\\n- Rationale for progesterone: to support sleep, normalize nervous system symptoms, and improve cycle-related symptoms; expected onset of benefit often within weeks for sleep, up to 3 months for cycle/somatic symptoms.\\n- Vitamin D rationale: suboptimal level can blunt hormone optimization and general health; high-dose D3 recommended; discuss sun exposure, dietary sources, and calcium intake.\\n- Contraception and fertility: patient at age 35 can still ovulate — discuss contraception if pregnancy is not desired; if fertility is desired, refer to provider/REI sooner rather than later.\\n- Side effects to counsel: progesterone may cause drowsiness, vivid dreams, mood fluctuations — advise taking at bedtime and caution with driving/machinery until tolerated. Instruct patient to report severe mood changes, new bleeding patterns, or signs of thrombosis (even though progesterone alone is lower risk than some estrogen/progestin therapies).\\n\\nLifestyle interventions (clinic staff to advise and document)\\n- Sleep hygiene: regular sleep schedule, reduce evening stimulants/caffeine, limit screens before bed; consider CBT-i referral if chronic insomnia.\\n- Stress/HPA axis: recommend stress-reduction strategies (mindfulness, guided breathing, behavioral therapy) and consider referral to behavioral health if anxiety/depression significant.\\n- Nutrition: ensure adequate dietary calcium and vitamin D; encourage iron-rich diet if menses heavy; target ferritin >50 ng/mL if symptomatic (order ferritin).\\n- Exercise: regular weight-bearing exercise to support bone health and mood.\\n- Weight management: advise on moderate exercise and balanced diet to optimize metabolic and cardiovascular health.\\n\\nAdditional testing and referrals to consider (prioritized)\\n- Ferritin + iron studies (ferritin, iron, TIBC) — target ferritin >50 ng/mL if symptomatic or heavy menses.\\n- Pregnancy test (urine or serum) prior to initiating progesterone if pregnancy possible.\\n- DHEA-S level if adrenal contribution to symptoms is suspected.\\n- Repeat sex-hormone testing (FSH, LH, estradiol, progesterone) timed to cycle if fertility evaluation or ovulatory status is required — consider mid-luteal progesterone (~7 days pre-menses or day ~21 in a regular 28-day cycle) if confirming ovulation.\\n- Free T3 / Free T4 if clinical suspicion for non-euthyroid symptoms despite normal TSH.\\n- Mental health screen (PHQ-9/GAD-7) and referral to behavioral health if positive.\\n- Sleep study referral only if STOP-BANG elevated or clinical suspicion for obstructive sleep apnea despite current STOP-BANG result (current STOP-BANG reported normal).\\n- Gyn/REI referral for contraception counseling, persistent bleeding, or fertility planning.\\n- Endocrinology referral if complex hypo/hyperandrogenism, abnormal DHEA-S, or unresolved metabolic/endocrine issues.\\n\\nWhen to notify provider immediately\\n- New heavy vaginal bleeding, syncope, chest pain, shortness of breath, signs of thromboembolism, severe mood or behavior change after starting progesterone or vitamin D.\\n- Any abnormal test results from the additional labs that require physician action (e.g., ferritin extremely low, pregnancy positive).\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (concise checklist for staff)\\n1. Provider sign-off: micronized progesterone 100 mg PO qHS and Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily (urgent — within 1–2 business days).\\n2. Pre-initiation checks before progesterone: pregnancy test if applicable; document contraception status; review contraindications (complete within 24–48 hours).\\n3. Start medications once provider approves; document counseling and side-effect education.\\n4. Reassess tolerance by phone at 4 weeks; in-person/telehealth visit at 8–12 weeks for symptom/effectiveness review and decision about continuation.\\n5. Recheck 25-OH vitamin D at 12 weeks aiming for ≥60 ng/mL.\\n6. Order ferritin/iron studies now (routine) — act on results: if ferritin <50 or symptomatic with heavy menses, initiate iron therapy per protocol and consider recheck in 8–12 weeks.\\n7. Repeat perimenopause hormonal panel in 6–12 months or sooner if clinical change.\\n8. Document rationale for no testosterone initiation and plan to reassess after reversible contributors (iron, sleep, thyroid, mood) are optimized.\\n\\nClinician note\\n- Do not initiate testosterone at this time; address progesterone foundation, optimize vitamin D, and investigate reversible contributors (iron, sleep, thyroid, mental health). Reevaluate symptoms and labs per timeline above for any future consideration of androgen therapy.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 0.62, "detail": "Triglycerides 41 / HDL 66 = 0.62", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 11, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": []}	\N	2026-03-28 01:15:44.36128	2026-03-28 01:15:44.36128
53	27	2026-01-05 00:00:00	{"alt": 12, "ast": 21, "bun": 13, "co2": 21, "fsh": 9.64, "hdl": 64, "ldl": 74, "lpa": 8, "mcv": 91.8, "rbc": 4.61, "tsh": 1.94, "wbc": 7, "acne": false, "apoB": 57, "egfr": 106, "iron": 87, "shbg": 64.2, "tibc": 290, "hsCRP": 0.2, "onHRT": false, "folate": 8.39, "freeT4": 1.14, "sodium": 139, "albumin": 4.8, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 8.8, "glucose": 68, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "chloride": 104, "ferritin": 96.5, "hairLoss": false, "vitaminD": 30, "bilirubin": 1.7, "estradiol": 50, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": true, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 330, "potassium": 4.5, "creatinine": 0.75, "hematocrit": 42.3, "hemoglobin": 13.6, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "vitaminB12": 677, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2026-01-05", "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Brittany C", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 35, "bmi": 19, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 110, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 0.36, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 28.3, "totalProtein": 6.9, "lowMotivation": false, "triglycerides": 41, "menstrualPhase": "luteal", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "freeTestosterone": 3, "totalCholesterol": 146}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History/context: 35-year-old female; labs reviewed for evaluation of menstrual/hormone status and general health. Patient counseled about abnormal luteal progesterone and low vitamin D. Reports (per chart): possible irregular menses/perimenopausal-type symptoms noted in prior history (anovulatory pattern suspected). No acute red-flag symptoms reported in chart (no syncope, heavy uncontrolled bleeding documented at this visit). Patient not known to be pregnant at time of review (verify if TTC).\\n- Symptoms to monitor (based on results): irregular menses, potential menstrual cycle changes, possible perimenopausal symptoms (mood, sleep, libido) though testosterone panel is within acceptable range.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: No vital signs (BP, HR, BMI) available in chart at time of entry.\\n- CBC:\\n  - Hemoglobin 13.6 g/dL (normal)\\n  - Hematocrit 42.3% (normal)\\n  - MCV 91.8 fL (normal)\\n  - Platelets 330 K/uL (normal)\\n- CMP / Renal & Hepatic:\\n  - Creatinine 0.75 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - eGFR 106 mL/min (normal)\\n  - AST 21 U/L (normal)\\n  - ALT 12 U/L (normal)\\n- Iron studies & related:\\n  - Ferritin 96.5 ng/mL (normal)\\n  - Serum Iron 87 ug/dL (normal)\\n  - TIBC 290 ug/dL (normal)\\n  - Folate 8.39 ng/mL (normal)\\n  - Vitamin B12 677 pg/mL (normal)\\n- Lipids / CV risk:\\n  - LDL cholesterol 74 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - HDL cholesterol 64 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - Triglycerides 41 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - Apolipoprotein B 57 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - Lipoprotein(a) 8 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - AHA PREVENT scores: 10-yr Total CVD 0.24% (0.2%), 10-yr ASCVD 0.16% (0.2%), 10-yr HF 0.12% (0.1%); 30-yr CVD 1.96% (2.0%). Category: Low risk.\\n  - hs-CRP 0.2 mg/L (normal)\\n- Hormones:\\n  - Progesterone (luteal): 0.36 ng/mL — ABNORMAL (low; consistent with luteal phase defect/anovulation)\\n  - Estradiol (E2): 50 pg/mL (within reported reference)\\n  - FSH: 9.64 mIU/mL (within reported reference)\\n  - Testosterone (Total): 28.3 ng/dL (within range)\\n  - Free Testosterone: 3 pg/mL\\n  - SHBG: 64.2 nmol/L\\n  - Perimenopause assessment: Luteal Progesterone 0.36 ng/mL — ABNORMAL; suggests anovulatory or luteal failure pattern this cycle.\\n- Vitamin / other labs:\\n  - 25-OH Vitamin D: 30 ng/mL — ABNORMAL (insufficiency; clinic target ≥60 ng/mL)\\n- Other normal labs (selected): TSH 1.94 mIU/L, Ferritin noted above, AMPLE metabolic/lipid values as listed.\\n- Risk scores / screening:\\n  - STOP-BANG (Sleep Apnea Risk): 0 points (low)\\n- Flags:\\n  - RED FLAGS: None documented at time of review.\\n  - ABNORMAL: Progesterone 0.36 ng/mL (luteal), Vitamin D 30 ng/mL (insufficient).\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1) Luteal phase progesterone low — probable anovulation / luteal phase defect.\\n   - Data: Progesterone 0.36 ng/mL in luteal window. Perimenopausal pattern possible. Clinical significance: may produce irregular menses, impaired fertility if attempting conception.\\n   - Differential: Perimenopausal anovulation, hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis dysfunction, recent anovulatory cycle, exogenous hormone interference (if on), early pregnancy (if applicable—unlikely but verify).\\n\\n2) Vitamin D insufficiency.\\n   - Data: 25-OH Vitamin D 30 ng/mL (clinic classifies 21–30 ng/mL as insufficiency; target ≥60 ng/mL for bone/hormone health).\\n   - Clinical significance: suboptimal for bone and hormonal health; repletion recommended.\\n\\n3) Androgen status — adequate / borderline but not low.\\n   - Data: Total T 28.3 ng/dL, Free T 3 pg/mL, SHBG 64.2 nmol/L — categorized as “Adequate Androgens; Persistent Symptoms” pattern.\\n   - Clinical significance: Testosterone therapy NOT indicated based on labs. If symptoms (fatigue, low libido, mood) persist, evaluate non-androgen causes (sleep, thyroid, iron, psychosocial).\\n\\n4) Routine labs otherwise within normal limits.\\n   - Data: CBC, ferritin, TSH, liver enzymes, creatinine, lipids, inflammatory markers all within acceptable ranges.\\n   - Clinical significance: low cardiovascular risk; statin not indicated at this time; no biochemical evidence of anemia or thyroid dysfunction to explain fatigue.\\n\\n5) Fertility/trying to conceive (TTC) status — needs confirmation.\\n   - Data: Anovulatory pattern on labs. If patient is TTC, this is clinically important and requires timely intervention.\\n   - Red flag: Notify physician immediately if patient is attempting conception or if pregnancy status unknown.\\n\\nPLAN:\\nFor Assessment 1 — Luteal phase defect / anovulation:\\n- Immediate actions:\\n  - Verify patient’s reproductive intentions (TTC vs not TTC) at next contact. If TTC, notify physician immediately and consider fertility-directed management.\\n  - If patient is TTC: obtain pregnancy test prior to any hormonal therapy as indicated.\\n- Therapeutic options (provider decision required):\\n  - Consider micronized progesterone for luteal support or symptom control per provider:\\n    - Micronized progesterone 100 mg PO qHS — options:\\n      - Continuous: 100 mg PO qHS nightly continuously for perimenopausal symptom control/bleeding stabilization.\\n      - Cyclical: 100 mg PO qHS for specified days each cycle per shared decision-making to induce withdrawal bleeding (timing individualized).\\n    - If used for fertility/luteal support, discuss duration with provider (often continued through 1st trimester if pregnancy achieved per provider discretion).\\n  - If patient declines or provider does not start progesterone, plan to re-evaluate.\\n- Monitoring:\\n  - Order repeat mid-luteal progesterone in the next cycle to confirm ovulation if not on exogenous progesterone: draw approximately 7 days post-ovulation or ~cycle day 21 in a 28-day cycle (adjust per cycle length).\\n  - Reassess menstrual bleeding pattern and symptoms in 4–12 weeks.\\n- Referral/testing if persistent anovulation or fertility concerns:\\n  - Consider REI referral, AMH and repeat FSH/E2 if pursuing fertility evaluation.\\n  - Consider pelvic ultrasound for structural evaluation if abnormal bleeding.\\n\\nFor Assessment 2 — Vitamin D insufficiency:\\n- Start supplementation per clinic protocol:\\n  - Metagenics Vitamin D3 5,000 IU + K1 daily with a meal (1 softgel PO daily).\\n  - Advise adherence and counsel regarding expected timeline for repletion.\\n- Monitoring:\\n  - Recheck 25-OH Vitamin D in 8–12 weeks to document response and aim for clinic target ≥60 ng/mL.\\n- Counseling:\\n  - Discuss dietary calcium/vitamin D sources and weight-bearing exercise for bone health.\\n\\nFor Assessment 3 — Androgen status adequate; no testosterone therapy indicated:\\n- Do NOT initiate testosterone at this time.\\n- Counseling:\\n  - Explain that total and free testosterone are within acceptable ranges and that symptoms are more likely due to estrogen/progesterone imbalance, sleep, mood, thyroid/iron status, or psychosocial factors.\\n- If symptoms persist despite addressing other causes, discuss reassessment and formal shared decision-making with provider prior to any androgen therapy.\\n- If provider still considering testosterone, require baseline lipids, LFTs, informed consent, and pregnancy status check; schedule close monitoring.\\n\\nFor Assessment 4 — Routine normal labs and preventive care:\\n- No statin indicated; lifestyle modifications recommended for primary prevention given low CV risk.\\n- Encourage routine preventive measures: diet, exercise, smoking cessation if applicable.\\n- Consider DEXA only if additional osteoporosis risk factors present; not indicated solely based on current labs.\\n\\nOther plan elements / workflow:\\n- Physician notification checklist for staff:\\n  - Notify physician immediately if patient confirms she is trying to conceive, has heavy/abnormal bleeding, plans to start progesterone, or requests testosterone despite counseling.\\n- Supplement recommendations:\\n  - Primary: Vitamin D3 5,000 IU + K daily (Metagenics) — start now.\\n  - Optional/clinic-suggested: AdvaClear® and EstroFactors™ noted as medium-priority supplements; discuss with provider before initiation.\\n- Lifestyle interventions:\\n  - Sleep hygiene reinforcement; refer to sleep medicine if persistent insomnia or suspected obstructive sleep apnea.\\n  - Weight-bearing exercise 3×/week for bone health.\\n  - Nutrition: ensure adequate calcium intake and balanced diet; re-evaluate iron intake if menses become heavy.\\n  - Stress management: consider CBT, mindfulness, or behavioral health referral if mood/stress symptoms persist.\\n- Follow-up labs / timing:\\n  - Mid-luteal progesterone: next naturally timed mid-luteal draw (7 days post-ovulation or adjusted per cycle length) if not on exogenous progesterone — within next cycle.\\n  - 25-OH Vitamin D: recheck in 8–12 weeks after starting supplementation.\\n  - Reassess progesterone-related symptoms and bleeding pattern 4–12 weeks after any progesterone start.\\n- Follow-up visit:\\n  - Routine follow-up in 3–6 months (per clinic recheck window). Sooner visits arranged if TTC, abnormal bleeding, or symptom progression.\\n- Documentation:\\n  - Provider to sign off on any medication starts (progesterone, vitamin D) and referrals.\\n  - All counseling items documented in chart.\\n\\nPatient communication:\\n- Results reviewed and discussed with patient. Counseling provided regarding anovulation/luteal deficiency, vitamin D insufficiency and the rationale for not starting testosterone at this time.\\n- Patient instructed to notify clinic immediately if attempting conception, if pregnancy is possible, or if heavy bleeding or other red-flag symptoms occur.\\n\\nDate: 03/28/2026\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "preventRisk": {"ldlGoal": "No specific LDL target; focus on risk-based statin intensity", "riskCategory": "low", "tenYearASCVD": 0.0015880036233748903, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0.0024183404834260537, "thirtyYearASCVD": 0.011826043429419973, "ageValidFor30Year": true, "thirtyYearTotalCVD": 0.019614667235524217, "tenYearHFPercentage": "0.1%", "tenYearHeartFailure": 0.0012198763786252466, "statinRecommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "tenYearCVDPercentage": "0.2%", "tenYearASCVDPercentage": "0.2%", "thirtyYearHFPercentage": "1.1%", "thirtyYearHeartFailure": 0.010974929630910046, "thirtyYearCVDPercentage": "2.0%", "thirtyYearASCVDPercentage": "1.2%"}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 softgel daily with meal", "name": "D3 5,000 + K", "caution": "Repletion dose for deficiency/insufficiency. Contains vitamin K2 for optimal calcium metabolism. Recheck levels in 8-12 weeks.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "D3 5,000 + K provides vitamin D3 with K2 for repletion of deficiency/insufficiency. Target ≥60 ng/mL for bone, immune, and cardiovascular support.", "indication": "Vitamin D 30 ng/mL (deficient)", "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D is below optimal range. This supplement will help restore your levels to support bone health, immune function, and overall wellness.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 30 ng/mL (deficient)"]}, {"dose": "2 capsules twice daily", "name": "AdvaClear®", "caution": "Broad-spectrum phase I/II liver detoxification support. Contains NAC, green tea extract, milk thistle, and B-vitamins for biotransformation. Best taken with food.", "category": "detox", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "AdvaClear provides broad-spectrum phase I and phase II liver detoxification support with NAC, green tea extract, watercress, and B-vitamins. Designed for metabolic clearance support, biotransformation of hormones and environmental compounds, and hepatic stress. Appropriate when liver markers are borderline, estrogen clearance is sluggish, or metabolic dysfunction is present.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance pattern (hepatic detox pathways needed for clearance); Borderline liver markers (ALT 12, AST 21) suggesting hepatic biotransformation load; Low energy (biotransformation burden)", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement supports your liver's natural detoxification processes. Your results suggest your body could benefit from help processing hormones, metabolic byproducts, and other compounds. It provides nutrients that support both phases of your liver's detox pathways.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance pattern (hepatic detox pathways needed for clearance)", "Borderline liver markers (ALT 12, AST 21) suggesting hepatic biotransformation load", "Low energy (biotransformation burden)"]}, {"dose": "2 tablets twice daily", "name": "EstroFactors™", "caution": "Nutritional support for estrogen balance and metabolism. Contains I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Supports healthy estrogen metabolite ratios.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "EstroFactors provides targeted nutritional support for estrogen metabolism and balance with I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Best suited for women with estrogen dominance patterns, PMS, perimenopausal hormone imbalance, or those needing broader estrogen metabolism support during hormonal transitions. Differs from Estrovera in that it addresses the underlying estrogen metabolism rather than vasomotor symptom relief.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance"], "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This supplement helps your body process and balance estrogen more effectively. It supports healthy hormone metabolism, which can help with PMS symptoms, bloating, mood changes, and hormonal balance during life transitions.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 8, "apoBValue": 57, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "normal", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 0.15880036233748904, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": false}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\n- Your labs show several strengths (good iron stores, healthy cholesterol, normal thyroid) and one clear area to address: low luteal progesterone and a borderline low vitamin D. Key numbers: Progesterone 0.36 ng/mL (low for the luteal phase), Vitamin D (25‑OH) 30 ng/mL (21–30 ng/mL = deficient/suboptimal), Estradiol 50 pg/mL, FSH 9.64 mIU/mL, Ferritin 96.5 ng/mL, TSH 1.94 mIU/L, LDL 74 mg/dL, HDL 64 mg/dL, Triglycerides 41 mg/dL, 10‑year ASCVD risk 0.2% (very low).\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Good iron and blood counts: Hemoglobin 13.6 g/dL, Hematocrit 42.3%, Ferritin 96.5 ng/mL — no iron deficiency.\\n- Thyroid normal: TSH 1.94 mIU/L.\\n- Heart health excellent: LDL 74 mg/dL, HDL 64 mg/dL, Triglycerides 41 mg/dL, 10‑year ASCVD risk 0.2% (meaning about 2 in 1,000 chance of a heart attack or stroke in the next 10 years).\\n- Liver and kidney tests normal (AST 21 U/L, ALT 12 U/L, eGFR 106 mL/min).\\n\\nAreas to improve (with specific actions)\\n1) Low luteal progesterone: Progesterone 0.36 ng/mL suggests this cycle was likely anovulatory or had luteal phase insufficiency. Actions:\\n   - Track cycles: use a period calendar and luteal-phase ovulation kits (LH urine tests) for 1–3 cycles.\\n   - Lifestyle to support ovulation: maintain a healthy weight, aim for 7–8 hours sleep nightly, manage stress (10–20 minutes daily meditation or gentle exercise), and include strength training 2–3x/week.\\n   - If you are trying to conceive or have symptoms (irregular cycles, heavy bleeding, infertility), schedule a visit in the next 4–8 weeks to discuss options (timed progesterone support or fertility evaluation).\\n\\n2) Vitamin D suboptimal: 25‑OH Vitamin D 30 ng/mL (deficient range 21–30 ng/mL).\\n   - Start vitamin D3 1,000–2,000 IU daily and recheck level in 3 months.\\n   - Bone health: aim for 1,000–1,200 mg calcium daily from diet (dairy, fortified plant milk, leafy greens), and weight‑bearing exercise 30 minutes, 3–5x/week.\\n\\nOther symptom guidance\\n- Androgens: Total Testosterone 28.3 ng/dL, Free T 3 pg/mL, SHBG 64.2 nmol/L — levels are adequate. If you have fatigue, low mood, sleep disruption, or low libido, focus on sleep (7–8 hours), strength training 2–3x/week, and stress reduction rather than testosterone therapy.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Start vitamin D 1,000–2,000 IU daily now; recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D in 3 months.\\n- Repeat luteal progesterone test mid‑luteal (about 7 days before expected period or ~day 21 in a 28‑day cycle) for 1–2 cycles; if cycles are irregular, we will plan LH tracking or referral.\\n- Schedule follow-up visit in 4–8 weeks to review tracking and symptoms. If trying to conceive or symptoms worsen, call sooner.\\n\\nYou’re doing well in many areas — these focused steps can help improve hormones and bone health.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 13.6, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 42.3, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 91.8, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 91.8 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 330, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 50, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol appropriate for luteal phase.", "recommendation": "Normal finding. Continue monitoring.", "referenceRange": "50-250 pg/mL (luteal phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.36, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Low progesterone in luteal phase - luteal phase defect.", "recommendation": "May affect fertility/early pregnancy. Consider progesterone support if TTC.", "referenceRange": "5-20 ng/mL (luteal phase)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 9.64, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 28.3, "status": "normal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone within normal female range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.94, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 96.5, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 30, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D deficiency (21-30 ng/mL). Suboptimal for hormone and bone health.", "recommendation": "Repletion: Metagenics D3 5,000 + K daily. Target ≥60 ng/mL. Recheck in 8-12 weeks.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 677, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 74, "status": "normal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 64, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 41, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.24183404834260536, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 0.2% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.15880036233748904, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 0.2%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.12198763786252466, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.1%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 1.9614667235524217, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 2.0%, ASCVD 1.2%, Heart Failure 1.1%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 21, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 12, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 106, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.75, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 57, "status": "normal", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Apo B at optimal level.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 8, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 290, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 87, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 8.39, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 0.2, "status": "normal", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Low cardiovascular inflammation risk.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.36, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Luteal Progesterone", "interpretation": "Luteal progesterone of 0.36 ng/mL indicates anovulation or luteal failure. This cycle was likely anovulatory — a common perimenopause finding.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Anovulatory cycle confirmed. If recurrent, this signals advancing perimenopause. Consider progesterone for symptom management: micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly continuously, or cyclically if patient prefers withdrawal bleeds. Discuss HRT options if symptomatic. PATIENT EDUCATION: This level indicates you likely did not ovulate this cycle, which is common in perimenopause. This can cause irregular periods, heavy bleeding, and other symptoms. Treatment is available to help regulate your cycles and relieve symptoms.", "referenceRange": "≥10 ng/mL optimal, 8–10 borderline, <8 low, <5 anovulatory"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 28.3, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone Pattern D: Adequate Androgens, Persistent Symptoms", "interpretation": "Adequate Androgens pattern identified (Total T: 28.3 ng/dL (within range); Free T: 3 pg/mL; SHBG: 64.2 nmol/L). Total, free, and bioavailable testosterone are within normal ranges and SHBG is not extreme. If the patient reports fatigue, low mood, sleep disruption, or low libido, these symptoms are likely driven by non-androgenic factors. Findings to evaluate: Estrogen/Progesterone balance should be assessed (E2: 50 pg/mL, P4: 0.36 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Do not reflexively add testosterone when levels are adequate — look deeper. Evaluate iron status (ferritin target >50 for symptom resolution), thyroid function (optimize TSH), and sleep quality (consider sleep apnea screening with STOP-BANG). Assess estrogen and progesterone balance — E2/P4 imbalance is a common driver of persistent symptoms. Address CNS and psychosocial drivers: chronic stress, HPA axis dysregulation, mood disorders. Consider DHEA-S if not already checked. Support with lifestyle optimization: sleep hygiene, stress management, nutrition. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your testosterone levels are actually in a healthy range, which is good news. When symptoms persist despite normal testosterone, it usually means something else is contributing. Your provider will look at other factors like iron levels, thyroid function, sleep quality, and hormone balance to find what's driving your symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Total T 15-70 ng/dL, Free T 0.5-5.0 pg/mL (all within range)"}], "aiRecommendations": "1) Overall clinical assessment\\n- Current labs indicate an anovulatory/luteal-failure pattern this cycle: progesterone 0.36 ng/mL in the luteal window → consistent with luteal phase defect/anovulation (perimenopausal pattern).\\n- Vitamin D 25-OH = 30 ng/mL → insufficiency (21–30 ng/mL); suboptimal for bone and hormonal health. Target per clinic protocol ≥60 ng/mL with repletion.\\n- Androgen panel (Total T 28.3 ng/dL, Free T 3 pg/mL, SHBG 64.2 nmol/L) consistent with “Adequate Androgens” pattern. Do NOT reflexively start testosterone based on these values.\\n- Other routine labs listed as normal (including ferritin, TSH, estradiol), so alternate causes of fatigue/low libido should be prioritized before androgen therapy.\\n\\n2) Priority actions (organized CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL\\n- Physician notification required immediately if any of the following apply:\\n  * Patient is currently trying to conceive (TTC) — anovulation has direct fertility implications and may prompt immediate luteal/ovulation support and fertility referral.\\n  * Patient has acute heavy uterine bleeding, syncope, or other red-flag symptoms.\\n- If TTC and pregnancy is possible, confirm pregnancy status prior to initiating any hormonal therapy where clinically relevant (pregnancy test as appropriate).\\n\\nURGENT (action within 1–2 weeks)\\n- For infertility/TTC:\\n  * Discuss and consider initiating luteal support per protocol if advised by provider (see dosing below). Provider decision required for start/route/duration.\\n  * Order mid-luteal progesterone in the next cycle to confirm ovulation if the patient is not on exogenous progesterone: time specimen 7 days after confirmed ovulation or approximately cycle day 21 in a regular 28-day cycle (adjust per cycle length/ovulation timing).\\n- For symptomatic perimenopause (irregular menses, heavy bleeding, vasomotor or sleep disturbance) — notify provider to discuss HRT options; consider starting micronized progesterone for cycle regulation/symptom control per provider direction.\\n\\nROUTINE (action within 4–12 weeks)\\n- Vitamin D repletion per clinic protocol (Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily). Recheck 25-OH in 8–12 weeks to document rise toward target ≥60 ng/mL.\\n- If progesterone support is NOT started, re-evaluate menstrual pattern and repeat mid-luteal P4 in 1–3 cycles if irregularity persists to document ongoing anovulation.\\n- Document and counsel patient that testosterone therapy is not indicated based on current labs.\\n\\n3) Testosterone dose recommendations (if applicable)\\n- Recommendation: Testosterone therapy is NOT indicated at this time.\\n  * Rationale: Total and free testosterone are in acceptable ranges (Total T 28.3 ng/dL, Free T 3 pg/mL, SHBG 64.2 nmol/L) and symptoms are more likely related to estrogen/progesterone imbalance, sleep, iron/thyroid status, or psychosocial/CNS factors.\\n- If provider still considers androgen therapy later, require full reassessment (symptom correlation, baseline lipids, liver tests, pregnancy status if applicable, and informed consent) and discuss risks/monitoring—do not start without provider signoff.\\n\\n4) Specific medication recommendations / dose adjustments (per clinic protocol)\\n- Progesterone (provider decision; for symptomatic control or fertility support):\\n  * Micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly (oral) — clinic protocol option.\\n  * Dosing options to present to provider/patient:\\n    - Continuous: 100 mg PO qHS nightly continuously (used for perimenopausal symptom control/bleeding stabilization).\\n    - Cyclical: 100 mg PO qHS for a scheduled interval each month to induce withdrawal bleeds (discuss cycle days with patient; typical cycle-day scheduling should be individualized).\\n  * If used for luteal support in known conception/TTC, discuss duration with provider (common practice: continue through first trimester per provider decision; clinic protocol specifics to be followed).\\n  * Monitor bleeding pattern and side effects; reassess at 4–12 weeks.\\n- Vitamin D:\\n  * Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily as per protocol. Check 25-OH 8–12 weeks after initiation.\\n- Notify provider for any medication starts so they can document prescription and counseling.\\n\\n5) Patient education points to cover (staff to review with patient)\\n- Explain that this cycle’s progesterone (0.36 ng/mL) indicates probable anovulation — common in perimenopause and can cause irregular periods and fertility challenges.\\n- If TTC: explain options (luteal support, ovulation induction, referral) and the need for timely follow-up.\\n- If not TTC: explain progesterone therapy options (purpose, regimen alternatives — continuous vs cyclic, expected effects, side effects).\\n- Vitamin D: explain insufficiency, recommended supplement dose, expected timeline to recheck levels, and importance for bone and hormone health.\\n- Reassure patient that testosterone levels are within expected range and starting testosterone is not recommended at present.\\n- Lifestyle advice: sleep hygiene, stress mitigation, weight management and nutrition to help symptomatic improvement.\\n\\n6) Lifestyle interventions needed (specific, staff-facing)\\n- Sleep: reinforce sleep hygiene; if symptoms include daytime sleepiness or snoring despite low STOP-BANG, consider sleep medicine referral or further testing.\\n- Nutrition:\\n  * Maintain adequate dietary calcium and vitamin D; review calcium intake if patient is on replacement.\\n  * Ensure iron-rich diet if heavy menses present despite normal ferritin; recheck ferritin if bleeding pattern changes.\\n- Exercise: encourage weight-bearing exercise 3×/week for bone health and mood.\\n- Stress management: recommend CBT, mindfulness, or referral to behavioral health if chronic stress/mood symptoms.\\n- Substance use: counsel on smoking cessation and alcohol moderation if applicable.\\n\\n7) Additional testing or referrals to consider\\n- Recheck labs:\\n  * 25-OH Vitamin D in 8–12 weeks after supplementation.\\n  * Mid-luteal progesterone in next cycle (7 days post-ovulation or adjusted by cycle length) if not on exogenous progesterone, or 1 cycle after stopping progesterone if verifying endogenous ovulation.\\n- Consider if symptomatic or fertility concerns:\\n  * AMH, repeat FSH/E2 if fertility evaluation desired (age-dependent).\\n  * DHEA-S if adrenal androgen contribution suspected (not checked).\\n  * Pelvic ultrasound if abnormal uterine bleeding or suspicion of structural cause.\\n  * Endocrinology or REI referral for recurrent anovulation, infertility, or if ovulation induction is being considered.\\n  * Gynecology or menopause specialist consult for discussion of full HRT regimen (estrogen ± progesterone) if perimenopausal symptoms significant.\\n- Bone density (DEXA) assessment if additional risk factors for osteoporosis or as clinically indicated by age/FRAX/BMD guidelines.\\n\\n8) Summary of next steps and timeline (clear actionable checklist)\\n- Immediate:\\n  * Notify provider if patient is TTC or has heavy bleeding/other red flags.\\n  * If provider authorizes, initiate micronized progesterone 100 mg qHS (continuous or cyclic per shared decision-making) for symptomatic control or luteal support — document prescription and counseling.\\n  * Start Vitamin D 5,000 IU + K daily per protocol.\\n- Within 1–2 weeks:\\n  * If TTC: plan fertility counseling/possible referral; order pregnancy test if appropriate.\\n- Within next cycle (timing individualized):\\n  * Draw mid-luteal progesterone 7 days after ovulation (or cycle day 21 if regular 28-day cycle) to confirm ovulation if not on exogenous progesterone.\\n- 4–12 weeks:\\n  * Reassess progesterone-related symptoms and bleeding pattern if progesterone started.\\n  * Recheck Vitamin D 25-OH at 8–12 weeks.\\n- 3 months:\\n  * If anovulation persists or fertility goals unmet, discuss REI referral; if perimenopausal symptoms persist despite progesterone, discuss broader HRT options with provider.\\n- Ongoing:\\n  * No testosterone therapy at this time; reassess if new data or persistent symptoms after addressing thyroid, iron, sleep, and psychosocial factors.\\n\\nPhysician notification checklist for staff\\n- Notify physician now if: patient is actively trying to conceive, has significant/worrisome bleeding, plans to start progesterone, or requests testosterone despite normal labs.\\n- Document counseling provided about anovulation, progesterone options, Vitamin D therapy, and rationale for not starting testosterone.\\n\\nEnd of recommendations — please review and sign off for any medication starts (progesterone, Vitamin D) and for any referrals or additional testing.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 0.64, "detail": "Triglycerides 41 / HDL 64 = 0.64", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 64.2, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 12, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "57 mg/dL", "detail": "Normal atherogenic particle count", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "0.2 mg/L", "detail": "Low inflammatory burden", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Lab pattern consistent with relative estrogen excess or impaired estrogen clearance. Even without reported symptoms, supporting liver detoxification and estrogen metabolism pathways may be beneficial.", "supportingFindings": ["Progesterone 0.36 ng/mL (very low)", "Borderline liver markers (ALT 12, AST 21) suggesting possible impaired hepatic clearance"]}]}	\N	2026-03-28 01:19:30.005489	2026-03-28 01:19:30.005489
