 in 3-6 months.", "referenceRange": "70-99 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mEq/L", "value": 136, "status": "normal", "category": "Sodium", "interpretation": "Sodium within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Normal electrolyte balance. Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "136-145 mEq/L"}, {"unit": "mEq/L", "value": 4.5, "status": "normal", "category": "Potassium", "interpretation": "Potassium within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Normal electrolyte balance. Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "3.5-5.0 mEq/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 55.9, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D adequate but suboptimal (41-59 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 2000 Complex daily to reach optimal range ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 690, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 optimal (400-1000 pg/mL). Adequate for neurological and red blood cell function.", "recommendation": "Continue current intake. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "400-1000 pg/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "of 6 markers positive", "value": 3, "status": "critical", "category": "Insulin Resistance Screening", "recheckTiming": "3-6 months", "interpretation": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: HIGH LIKELIHOOD (3/6 markers positive). Identified phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance, Hepatic (Liver-Driven) Insulin Resistance. Treat according to phenotype-specific protocol.", "recommendation": "Phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance; Hepatic (Liver-Driven) Insulin Resistance. Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose.", "referenceRange": "0-1 Low, 2 Moderate, 3+ High Likelihood"}], "aiRecommendations": "1) Overall clinical assessment\\n- Key abnormalities: total testosterone 337 ng/dL (below clinic goal 600–1200), STOP-BANG 5 (high risk for moderate–severe OSA), triglycerides 253 mg/dL, A1c 6.0% (prediabetes range), low HDL 32 mg/dL, mild ALT elevation 42 U/L, creatinine 1.38 mg/dL, hemoglobin 13.2 g/dL (low), estradiol 15 pg/mL (low), vitamin D suboptimal 55.9 ng/mL.\\n- Clinical priorities: urgent evaluation/treatment of suspected OSA before initiating or escalating TRT; metabolic risk optimization (triglycerides, A1c, HDL, lifetime CVD risk); investigate causes of low testosterone and low estradiol (including medications such as aromatase inhibitors); monitor and re-evaluate borderline renal and hepatic markers.\\n\\n2) Priority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL (immediate)\\n- Sleep apnea: Arrange urgent referral for sleep study (HSAT or in-lab polysomnography). Document STOP-BANG = 5 and request study within 2–4 weeks. Notify physician immediately of high OSA risk.\\n- TRT management: Do NOT initiate or escalate TRT until OSA is evaluated and managed. If patient is currently on TRT, discuss with physician about holding or reducing dose if clinically indicated until OSA evaluation complete.\\n- Medication review: Immediately check medication list for aromatase inhibitor (e.g., anastrozole). If patient is using an AI and estradiol is 15 pg/mL, discuss stopping or reducing AI with physician.\\n\\nURGENT (within 1–4 weeks)\\n- Creatinine 1.38 mg/dL: Reinforce hydration, repeat BMP (including creatinine, eGFR, electrolytes) in 2–4 weeks. Notify physician now for review of baseline renal function and concurrent nephrotoxic medications (NSAIDs, ACEi/ARB dose changes, supplements).\\n- ALT 42 U/L: Counsel on alcohol moderation and hepatotoxic agents; repeat LFTs in 4–6 weeks. Notify physician if ALT rises or other LFTs abnormal.\\n- Triglycerides 253 mg/dL: Implement immediate dietary counseling (reduce refined carbs and alcohol), consider omega-3 prescription or OTC (prescribe per clinic protocol) and assess need for lipid-lowering therapy in physician review. Repeat fasting lipid panel in 3 months.\\n- Hemoglobin 13.2 g/dL: Recheck CBC in 4–6 weeks to evaluate for anemia workup per protocol.\\n\\nROUTINE (within 1–3 months and ongoing)\\n- Glycemic risk: A1c 6.0% and fasting glucose 115 mg/dL — enroll in lifestyle/dietary intervention; consider referral to diabetes prevention program. Recheck A1c and fasting glucose in 3 months.\\n- Vitamin D: Continue Metagenics D3 2000 IU daily (per protocol) and recheck 25-OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks aiming for ≥60 ng/mL.\\n- Cardiovascular risk: Address risk factors and plan annual risk re-assessment. Coordinate primary care/cardiology follow-up as appropriate.\\n\\n3) Testosterone dose recommendations (IF APPLICABLE)\\n- If patient is NOT currently on TRT:\\n  - Defer initiation until OSA evaluation completed and metabolic/renal/hepatic issues addressed.\\n  - If after OSA management and patient meets criteria for TRT, consider initiating standard starting regimen per clinic protocol (examples):\\n    - Injectable option: testosterone cypionate 100 mg IM weekly (or 50–100 mg SC weekly) with follow-up labs at 6–8 weeks; OR\\n    - Topical option: testosterone gel 50–100 mg daily (dose per product) with follow-up labs at 6–8 weeks.\\n  - Target trough/steady-state total testosterone 600–900 ng/dL; titrate based on labs and symptoms.\\n- If patient IS currently on TRT:\\n  - Do not increase dose given high OSA risk and low estradiol. Maintain current dose pending sleep study results.\\n  - If on anastrozole/an AI and estradiol 15 pg/mL, discuss stopping/reducing AI with physician (suggest stopping AI and rechecking E2 in 4–6 weeks).\\n  - If hematocrit/hemoglobin were elevated would consider dose reduction; current Hgb is low (13.2 g/dL) so polycythemia is not present.\\n\\nMonitoring if initiating or adjusting TRT (staff checklist)\\n- Baseline and follow-up labs: total testosterone, estradiol, CBC (Hgb/Hct), PSA, CMP (including AST/ALT, creatinine), lipid panel.\\n- Recheck schedule: 6–8 weeks after initiation/change for testosterone/estradiol and CBC; 3 months for CMP and lipids; then every 3–6 months until stable, then every 6–12 months per protocol.\\n- Immediate physician notification for Hct >54% or Hgb increases >1.5 g/dL or marked LFT/creatinine rise.\\n\\n4) Lifestyle interventions to implement (staff to counsel and document)\\n- Sleep hygiene and urgent sleep study referral; advise avoidance of sedatives/alcohol before bed.\\n- Weight loss target: aim for 7–10% weight reduction if overweight; refer to nutrition/weight management program.\\n- Exercise: at least 150 min/week of moderate aerobic activity plus resistance training.\\n- Diet: reduce refined carbohydrates and added sugars; limit alcohol intake (especially given elevated triglycerides and ALT); increase dietary fiber and omega-3 rich foods.\\n- Smoking cessation if applicable.\\n- Diabetes prevention counseling: calorie reduction, increased activity; consider metformin discussion with physician for high-risk patients.\\n\\n5) Additional testing or referrals to consider\\n- Sleep medicine: urgent HSAT or in-lab polysomnogram; refer to sleep clinic.\\n- Endocrinology or Urology for complex hypogonadism evaluation if secondary causes suspected or if planning long-term TRT.\\n- Nephrology if creatinine remains elevated or eGFR declines on repeat testing.\\n- Cardiology if symptomatic or if clinic physician deems necessary given elevated lifetime CVD risk and multiple risk factors.\\n- Nutrition/Diabetes prevention program or Diabetes Educator referral.\\n- If anemia persists: iron studies (ferritin, iron, TIBC), reticulocyte count, stool occult blood per anemia protocol.\\n- If low estradiol persists after stopping AI: consider bone density (DEXA) if risk factors for low bone density present.\\n\\n6) Summary of next steps and timeline (staff action checklist)\\n- Immediate (today–2 days)\\n  - Notify physician of STOP-BANG 5, testosterone 337 ng/dL, triglycerides 253 mg/dL, creatinine 1.38 mg/dL, ALT 42 U/L, A1c 6.0%.\\n  - Verify current medications (specifically aromatase inhibitors, opioids, glucocorticoids) and document.\\n  - Place referral order for sleep study (HSAT or polysomnography) with priority scheduling (target within 2–4 weeks).\\n\\n- Short term (2–6 weeks)\\n  - Repeat BMP including creatinine in 2–4 weeks.\\n  - Repeat CBC (Hgb/Hct) in 4–6 weeks.\\n  - If on AI, discuss stopping with physician and recheck estradiol in 4–6 weeks.\\n  - Start vitamin D 2000 IU daily (if not already) and recheck 25-OH in 8–12 weeks.\\n  - Begin lifestyle counseling (diet, exercise, alcohol reduction) and refer to nutrition/weight loss program.\\n\\n- Intermediate (6–12 weeks)\\n  - If TRT initiated after OSA addressed: check total testosterone, estradiol, CBC, CMP at 6–8 weeks post-initiation or dose change.\\n  - Repeat fasting lipid panel and fasting glucose in ~3 months.\\n  - Reassess A1c in 3 months.\\n\\n- Ongoing (3–12 months)\\n  - Annual CV risk reassessment; ongoing management of lipids, glycemia, weight.\\n  - Repeat LFTs and renal function periodically; notify physician for upward trends.\\n\\nPhysician notification requirements (must alert MD)\\n- STOP-BANG ≥5 (immediate)\\n- New or worsening creatinine elevation or eGFR decline on repeat testing\\n- Triglycerides persistently >500 mg/dL (or persistent >200 mg/dL for management decision)\\n- ALT >2× ULN or rising trend\\n- Any decision to start, stop, or alter TRT dose or to stop aromatase inhibitor\\n- Hemoglobin or hematocrit changes meeting polycythemia thresholds or symptomatic anemia\\n\\nDocument all patient education provided and orders placed. Follow clinic TRT and anemia protocols for any medication changes.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 7.91, "detail": "Triglycerides 253 / HDL 32 = 7.91", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "6%", "detail": "Prediabetic range", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 42, "detail": "Above optimal range - may indicate hepatic insulin resistance", "positive": true, "threshold": "> 35 U/L (optimal)"}], "likelihood": "high", "phenotypes": [{"key": "visceral_metabolic", "name": "Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Recheck A1c, lipid panel, hs-CRP, ApoB in 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["A1c >= 5.7%", "TG/HDL >= 3", "ALT above optimal range"], "pathophysiology": "Early peripheral + hepatic insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia. Glucose may still be \\"normal.\\" A1c often rises last.", "triggerCriteria": ["A1c >= 5.7%", "TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0", "Central adiposity phenotype", "ALT above optimal range"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests your body may not be responding to insulin as efficiently as it should. Insulin is the hormone that helps move sugar from your bloodstream into your cells for energy.\\n\\nWhen cells become less sensitive to insulin:\\n- Blood sugar slowly rises over time\\n- Triglycerides increase\\n- Inflammation increases\\n- Weight gain around the midsection becomes easier\\n\\nThe good news? This stage is highly reversible with targeted nutrition, strength training, sleep optimization, and - when appropriate - medication support.\\n\\nWe'll focus on improving how your body processes fuel and reducing long-term cardiometabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward nutrition (>= 25-35g/meal)", "Carbohydrate quality + timing education", "Strength training 2-3x weekly", "10-15 min post-meal walking", "Sleep optimization", "Consider: GLP-1 therapy if obesity, appetite dysregulation, A1c >= 5.7, or inflammatory phenotype", "Consider: Metformin if prediabetes or strong family history", "Recheck metabolic panel in 3-6 months"]}, {"key": "hepatic", "name": "Hepatic (Liver-Driven) Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Monitor ALT, TG, ApoB, A1c every 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["ALT above optimal (>35 U/L)", "TG elevated / TG:HDL >= 3", "A1c mildly elevated"], "pathophysiology": "Hepatic insulin resistance leads to increased hepatic glucose output + VLDL overproduction. Often early MASLD (metabolic-associated steatotic liver disease) physiology.", "triggerCriteria": ["ALT above optimal (>= 35 U/L)", "TG elevated or TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB elevated", "A1c may be normal or mildly elevated"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour labs suggest your liver may be under metabolic strain. The liver plays a major role in managing blood sugar and fat metabolism.\\n\\nWhen the liver becomes less responsive to insulin:\\n- Triglycerides rise\\n- Liver enzymes increase\\n- Fat can accumulate in the liver over time\\n\\nThis is common and often related to nutrition, stress, sleep, and weight distribution.\\n\\nThe encouraging part: liver-related metabolic changes are very responsive to targeted lifestyle changes and, if needed, medication support.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Weight reduction goal 5-10% if overweight", "Eliminate sugary beverages", "Alcohol reduction", "Protein-first nutrition", "Consider GLP-1 if weight loss appropriate", "Omega-3 if TG elevated"]}], "positiveCount": 3, "likelihoodLabel": "HIGH LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: HIGH LIKELIHOOD (3/6 markers positive). Identified phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance, Hepatic (Liver-Driven) Insulin Resistance. Treat according to phenotype-specific protocol.", "confirmationTests": "Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose."}}	\N	2026-03-28 20:20:42.571657	2026-03-28 20:20:42.571657
59	22	2026-03-18 00:00:00	{"fsh": 11.9, "mcv": 89.2, "rbc": 4.17, "wbc": 9.9, "acne": false, "shbg": 43.8, "onHRT": true, "anxiety": true, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "ferritin": 8.8, "hairLoss": false, "vitaminD": 31.3, "estradiol": 181, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": true, "lowLibido": true, "platelets": 279, "hematocrit": 37.2, "hemoglobin": 12, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2026-03-18", "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Amanda Richardson", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 36, "bmi": 24, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 120, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 0.6, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 22.1, "lowMotivation": false, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "freeTestosterone": 3}	{"redFlags": [{"action": "Metagenics Hemagenics OR Prescription 65mg Elemental Iron. Evaluate for occult blood loss.", "message": "Ferritin is 8.8 ng/mL (<10 threshold).", "category": "Iron - Severe Deficiency", "severity": "urgent"}], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History/Symptoms: Patient reports symptoms consistent with estrogen dominance pattern (per lab interpretation) including nocturnal insomnia/3am waking, intermittent anxiety, and breast tenderness. Reports irregular/heavy menses. No syncope, chest pain, shortness of breath, or overt GI bleeding reported. No current pregnancy. Patient is motivated to address low iron and optimize hormone regimen.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: Not available in chart today. BMI not provided.\\n- Risk scores: Sleep apnea risk (STOP-BANG): 0 points.\\n\\nLaboratory results (03/30/2026)\\n- Complete Blood Count (CBC)\\n  - Hemoglobin: 12.0 g/dL (Normal)\\n  - Hematocrit: 37.2 % (Normal)\\n  - MCV: 89.2 fL (Normal)\\n  - Platelets: 279 K/uL (Normal)\\n\\n- Iron studies\\n  - Ferritin: 8.8 ng/mL — CRITICAL / Severe iron deficiency (reference threshold <10 ng/mL flagged) — URGENT finding\\n\\n- Hormones\\n  - Estradiol (E2): 181 pg/mL — ABNORMAL; elevated above typical HRT range\\n  - Progesterone: 0.6 ng/mL — ABNORMAL; below HRT therapeutic range\\n  - Testosterone, total: 22.1 ng/dL — ABNORMAL; low for patient on HRT\\n  - Free testosterone: 3 pg/mL (Normal)\\n  - SHBG: 43.8 nmol/L (Normal)\\n  - FSH: 11.9 mIU/mL (Normal)\\n\\n- Micronutrients\\n  - Vitamin D (25-OH): 31.3 ng/mL — BORDERLINE / insufficient (31–40 ng/mL)\\n\\n- Other: No CMP, lipid panel, or LFTs available in this lab set.\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Severe iron deficiency — ferritin 8.8 ng/mL (critical). Although hemoglobin is currently 12.0 g/dL (not anemic), ferritin <10 indicates severely depleted iron stores. Urgent evaluation recommended for source of blood loss (occult GI bleeding, heavy menstrual loss). Risk: progression to iron deficiency anemia if untreated.\\n   - Differential: heavy menstrual bleeding (patient reports irregular/heavy cycles), occult GI blood loss (colonic/polyps/ulcer), malabsorption (celiac), inadequate dietary iron intake.\\n\\n2. Estrogen dominance pattern / ovulatory dysfunction: Estradiol 181 pg/mL with progesterone 0.6 ng/mL indicates ongoing estrogen exposure without adequate ovulation/progesterone production. Correlates with patient symptoms (insomnia, anxiety, breast tenderness, irregular/heavy menses).\\n   - Differential: perimenopausal anovulatory cycles, exogenous estradiol dosing on HRT that is excessive for current needs, luteal phase deficiency, or timing of blood draw relative to cycle/HRT.\\n\\n3. Low total testosterone: 22.1 ng/dL while on HRT — low relative to expected therapeutic range. May contribute to decreased libido, low energy, or decreased muscle mass if symptomatic.\\n   - Differential: insufficient testosterone dosing, increased SHBG (though SHBG is within reference here), lab variability.\\n\\n4. Vitamin D insufficiency: 25-OH vitamin D 31.3 ng/mL — suboptimal for hormone optimization and overall health; needs repletion.\\n\\n5. Other routine labs stable: CBC indices within normal limits at present (Hgb 12.0 g/dL, Hct 37.2%, MCV 89.2 fL, platelets 279 K/uL). FSH and SHBG within reference for context.\\n\\nRed flags requiring immediate physician review:\\n- Ferritin 8.8 ng/mL (severe deficiency) — evaluate for occult blood loss (GI workup, stool testing) and initiate iron repletion urgently. Consider GI referral if occult blood present or if ferritin fails to improve on therapy.\\n\\nPLAN:\\n1. Severe iron deficiency (ferritin 8.8 ng/mL)\\n   - Start iron repletion immediately:\\n     - First-line options: Metagenics/Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support — 1 tablet twice daily with meals (clinic supplement recommendation).\\n     - Alternative prescription: Ferrous sulfate 325 mg PO once daily (contains 65 mg elemental iron) if preferred/covered; adjust dosing per tolerability and prescriber preference.\\n   - Administration counseling: Take iron on an empty stomach for best absorption if tolerated; if GI upset, take with food. Avoid calcium-containing foods/supplements, tea, coffee within 1–2 hours of iron. Consider concurrent vitamin C 500 mg with iron to enhance absorption unless contraindicated.\\n   - Manage side effects: Discuss constipation — consider stool softener/prn (e.g., docusate) if needed.\\n   - Evaluate for source of blood loss:\\n     - Order fecal immunochemical test (FIT) or fecal occult blood test x1 promptly.\\n     - If FIT positive or ongoing heavy menses, refer to GI for colonoscopy evaluation and to Gyn for evaluation of menstrual bleeding (pelvic exam, transvaginal ultrasound) as indicated.\\n   - Labs and follow-up:\\n     - Re-check ferritin, CBC (Hgb/Hct), and iron panel (serum iron, TIBC, transferrin saturation) in 2–4 weeks (urgent window) to document response.\\n     - If no significant ferritin improvement or persistent low ferritin despite adherence after 6–8 weeks, consider hematology referral and evaluate for IV iron therapy.\\n\\n2. Estrogen dominance pattern / ovulatory dysfunction (E2 181 pg/mL, progesterone 0.6 ng/mL)\\n   - Do NOT make unilateral changes to current prescription without clinician/physician review.\\n   - Recommendations for clinician to consider:\\n     - Review current estradiol formulation and dose; consider lowering estradiol dose if above intended therapeutic target.\\n     - Increase/provide adequate progestogen coverage to address low progesterone and protect endometrium — options include oral micronized progesterone (e.g., 100–200 mg nightly x12–14 days per month for cyclic dosing) or alternate clinic-specific progesterone formulations/doses depending on current HRT regimen and patient goals. Exact dose and schedule to be determined by prescribing clinician.\\n     - If bleeding is heavy/irregular, arrange gynecologic evaluation for structural causes (fibroids, polyps) and to guide endometrial protection strategy.\\n   - Monitoring:\\n     - After any HRT dose adjustments, repeat estradiol and progesterone levels in approximately 4 weeks to assess response (earlier if symptomatic).\\n     - Consider tracking menstrual pattern and symptom diary.\\n\\n3. Low total testosterone (22.1 ng/dL)\\n   - Correlate with symptoms (libido, energy). If symptomatic and on prior testosterone therapy, discuss dose optimization with prescriber.\\n   - If considering testosterone dose adjustment, obtain baseline LFTs, lipid panel, and repeat total testosterone with free testosterone and SHBG in 8–12 weeks after adjustment to monitor efficacy and safety.\\n   - No immediate change without prescriber consultation.\\n\\n4. Vitamin D insufficiency (25-OH 31.3 ng/mL)\\n   - Start Vitamin D3 5,000 IU + K (1 softgel daily with meal) as recommended.\\n   - Re-check 25-OH vitamin D level in 8–12 weeks after starting supplementation.\\n\\n5. Adjunct supplements recommended for hormone optimization / methylation / magnesium\\n   - AdvaClear® 2 capsules twice daily (per clinic guidance for estrogen metabolism) — discuss with prescribing clinician.\\n   - Magtein® Magnesium L-Threonate: 3 capsules daily divided doses.\\n   - EstroFactors™: 2 tablets twice daily.\\n   - Patient counseled that supplements are adjunctive; clinician to review potential interactions with medications.\\n\\n6. Lifestyle and symptomatic management\\n   - Diet: Increase dietary heme iron sources (red meat, poultry, fish) and iron-fortified foods; pair iron-containing meals with vitamin C-rich foods; avoid excessive caffeine at meals.\\n   - Menstrual management: Track bleeding; use sanitary product logs to quantify heaviness; seek urgent care if saturating >1 pad/hour or with syncope.\\n   - Sleep hygiene and stress management for insomnia/anxiety (sleep routine, CBT-I referral if needed).\\n   - Avoid NSAID overuse which can worsen GI bleeding.\\n\\n7. Referrals and safety monitoring\\n   - Urgent clinician/physician review requested due to ferritin <10 ng/mL.\\n   - GYN referral for evaluation of heavy/irregular menses and for endometrial protection decisions.\\n   - GI referral if FIT positive or if occult GI blood loss suspected.\\n   - Hematology referral if iron deficiency fails to correct or if workup shows alternative etiology.\\n\\n8. Follow-up and labs\\n   - Urgent recheck window: 2–4 weeks for ferritin and CBC and iron studies.\\n   - Hormone recheck (estradiol, progesterone) approximately 4 weeks after any HRT adjustments.\\n   - Vitamin D recheck in 8–12 weeks after starting supplementation.\\n   - Additional monitoring labs (LFTs, lipids) as clinically indicated prior to testosterone adjustments.\\n\\nCommunication:\\n- Results reviewed and discussed with patient today (03/30/2026). Urgent nature of low ferritin and need for iron repletion and evaluation for blood loss explained. Treatment options (Hemagenics Red Blood Cell Support OR prescription ferrous sulfate 325 mg daily) reviewed along with GI/GYN evaluation pathway. Risks, benefits, and monitoring plan discussed. Patient verbalized understanding and is agreeable to start recommended iron therapy/supplements and follow-up labs.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\nDate: 03/30/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 tablet twice daily with meals", "name": "Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support", "caution": "Contains iron, folate, and B12 for comprehensive RBC support. Avoid taking with calcium-rich foods or dairy. May cause mild GI upset initially.", "category": "iron", "priority": "high", "rationale": "Hemagenics provides highly absorbable iron bisglycinate with B12, B6, and folate for comprehensive red blood cell support. Indicated for functional iron deficiency, iron deficiency without anemia, and iron-responsive symptoms.", "indication": "Ferritin 8.8 ng/mL (severely depleted ≤20); Fatigue with iron depletion", "phenotypes": ["Iron Deficiency"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "Your lab results suggest your iron stores are lower than optimal. This supplement provides a gentle, well-absorbed form of iron along with B12 and folate to support your energy levels, hair health, and overall vitality.", "supportingFindings": ["Ferritin 8.8 ng/mL (severely depleted ≤20)", "Fatigue with iron depletion"]}, {"dose": "1 softgel daily with meal", "name": "D3 5,000 + K", "caution": "Repletion dose for deficiency/insufficiency. Contains vitamin K2 for optimal calcium metabolism. Recheck levels in 8-12 weeks.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "D3 5,000 + K provides vitamin D3 with K2 for repletion of deficiency/insufficiency. Target ≥60 ng/mL for bone, immune, and cardiovascular support.", "indication": "Vitamin D 31.3 ng/mL (insufficient)", "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D is below optimal range. This supplement will help restore your levels to support bone health, immune function, and overall wellness.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 31.3 ng/mL (insufficient)"]}, {"dose": "2 capsules twice daily", "name": "AdvaClear®", "caution": "Broad-spectrum phase I/II liver detoxification support. Contains NAC, green tea extract, milk thistle, and B-vitamins for biotransformation. Best taken with food.", "category": "detox", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "AdvaClear provides broad-spectrum phase I and phase II liver detoxification support with NAC, green tea extract, watercress, and B-vitamins. Designed for metabolic clearance support, biotransformation of hormones and environmental compounds, and hepatic stress. Appropriate when liver markers are borderline, estrogen clearance is sluggish, or metabolic dysfunction is present.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance pattern (hepatic detox pathways needed for clearance); Low energy (biotransformation burden)", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance"], "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This supplement supports your liver's natural detoxification processes. Your results suggest your body could benefit from help processing hormones, metabolic byproducts, and other compounds. It provides nutrients that support both phases of your liver's detox pathways.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance pattern (hepatic detox pathways needed for clearance)", "Low energy (biotransformation burden)"]}, {"dose": "3 capsules daily (divided doses)", "name": "Magtein® Magnesium L-Threonate", "caution": "L-Threonate form crosses blood-brain barrier for cognitive and sleep support. Well-tolerated; gentle on GI system.", "category": "mineral", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Magtein is the only magnesium form clinically shown to cross the blood-brain barrier. Supports quality sleep, cognitive function, GABA activity, and stress resilience.", "indication": "Low energy reported; Anxiety reported (magnesium supports GABA)", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This special form of magnesium is designed to reach the brain, where it supports better sleep quality, mental clarity, and a calmer nervous system.", "supportingFindings": ["Low energy reported", "Anxiety reported (magnesium supports GABA)"]}, {"dose": "2 tablets twice daily", "name": "EstroFactors™", "caution": "Nutritional support for estrogen balance and metabolism. Contains I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Supports healthy estrogen metabolite ratios.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "EstroFactors provides targeted nutritional support for estrogen metabolism and balance with I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Best suited for women with estrogen dominance patterns, PMS, perimenopausal hormone imbalance, or those needing broader estrogen metabolism support during hormonal transitions. Differs from Estrovera in that it addresses the underlying estrogen metabolism rather than vasomotor symptom relief.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance"], "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This supplement helps your body process and balance estrogen more effectively. It supports healthy hormone metabolism, which can help with PMS symptoms, bloating, mood changes, and hormonal balance during life transitions.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected"]}, {"dose": "1 soft chew during times of stress", "name": "HerWellness™ Rapid Stress Relief", "caution": "Fast-acting L-Theanine and Lactium formula. Non-drowsy. Promotes calm within 1 hour. Contains milk.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "low", "rationale": "HerWellness Rapid Stress Relief provides fast-acting stress support with L-Theanine (200mg) and Lactium. Promotes calm within 1 hour without drowsiness.", "indication": "Anxiety reported", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This fast-acting supplement helps your body respond to stress more calmly. It promotes relaxation within about an hour and can be taken as needed without causing drowsiness.", "supportingFindings": ["Anxiety reported"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "2-4 weeks (urgent values present)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\n- Your labs show three main issues to address: very low iron stores (ferritin 8.8 ng/mL), an “estrogen dominance” pattern (estradiol 181 pg/mL with low progesterone 0.6 ng/mL suggesting ovulatory dysfunction), and low total testosterone (22.1 ng/dL) despite ongoing HRT. Vitamin D is borderline (31.3 ng/mL). Your complete blood count is still within the normal range.\\n\\nPositive findings (exact values)\\n- Hemoglobin 12.0 g/dL (normal)\\n- Hematocrit 37.2% (normal)\\n- MCV 89.2 fL (normal-sized red blood cells)\\n- Platelets 279 K/uL (normal)\\n- FSH 11.9 mIU/mL\\n- SHBG 43.8 nmol/L\\n- Free testosterone 3 pg/mL\\n- STOP-BANG sleep apnea risk 0/8 (LOW risk)\\n\\nAreas to improve (values + concrete actions)\\n1. Ferritin 8.8 ng/mL (severely depleted)\\n   - Action: Discuss starting oral iron with your clinician (common option: ferrous sulfate 325 mg = ~65 mg elemental iron). Current best practice often uses every‑other‑day dosing to improve absorption and tolerance (e.g., one tablet every other day) or daily if recommended by your clinician.\\n   - Take iron with vitamin C (a glass of orange juice) and avoid tea/coffee or calcium within 1–2 hours of doses.\\n   - Eat iron-rich foods: 2–3 servings/week of lean red meat or iron-fortified cereals, add beans and dark leafy greens, and include vitamin C at meals.\\n   - If you have GI symptoms or can't tolerate oral iron, ask about IV iron. Repeat ferritin and CBC in 8–12 weeks after starting treatment. Also discuss testing for GI blood loss if no clear cause.\\n\\n2. Estradiol 181 pg/mL and Progesterone 0.6 ng/mL — “estrogen dominance”\\n   - Note cycle timing: if you are in the luteal phase we expect progesterone to be much higher; low progesterone suggests anovulatory cycles.\\n   - Action: Track ovulation (LH kits or basal body temp) and bring cycle information to your provider. Lifestyle: improve sleep hygiene (7–8 hrs nightly), reduce late-night caffeine/alcohol, stress reduction (meditation, yoga), and regular moderate exercise. Discuss with your provider about cyclic/progestogen therapy to balance estrogen and reduce symptoms (heavy/irregular flow, breast tenderness, insomnia).\\n\\n3. Testosterone (total) 22.1 ng/dL (low)\\n   - Action: Prioritize 7–8 hours sleep, start strength training 2–3x/week, and use stress management (walking, breathing exercises). Discuss testosterone assessment/therapy options with your clinician if symptoms persist.\\n\\n4. Vitamin D 25‑OH 31.3 ng/mL (insufficient)\\n   - Action: Start vitamin D3 2000 IU daily and recheck level in 8–12 weeks; add 10–20 minutes sunlight and fatty fish 1–2x/week.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- These critical findings require physician review before any medication changes. Schedule a visit within the next 2–4 weeks to review iron treatment and hormone adjustments. Recheck ferritin, CBC, and vitamin D in 8–12 weeks after starting therapy, and consider mid‑luteal progesterone testing if you are tracking ovulation. If you develop dizziness, rapid heartbeat, passage of large clots, or worsening symptoms, contact the clinic sooner.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 12, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 37.2, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 89.2, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 89.2 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 279, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 181, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol elevated above typical HRT range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Evaluate estrogen dose. Monitor for estrogen excess symptoms.", "referenceRange": "60-100 pg/mL (HRT goal, >40 for bone)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.6, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone below HRT therapeutic range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase progesterone dose. Target 8-10 ng/mL for optimal endometrial protection.", "referenceRange": "8-10 ng/mL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 11.9, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 22.1, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Low testosterone despite HRT.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase testosterone dose. Target 75-125 ng/dL for optimal results.", "referenceRange": "75-125 ng/dL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 8.8, "status": "critical", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Severely depleted iron stores without anemia.", "recommendation": "Provider protocol: Metagenics Hemagenics OR Prescription 65mg Elemental Iron (take every other day, on empty stomach, avoid caffeine and dairy 2 hours before/after). Evaluate for blood loss.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 31.3, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D insufficient (31-40 ng/mL). Suboptimal for hormone optimization.", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 5,000 + K daily. Target ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 181, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Estrogen Dominance Pattern", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 181 pg/mL with progesterone of only 0.6 ng/mL represents estrogen dominance. This is not excess estrogen production — it reflects ovulatory dysfunction where estrogen continues but ovulation (and thus progesterone) fails. Associated symptoms include insomnia or 3am waking, anxiety, breast tenderness, migraines, and heavy or irregular cycles.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Add progesterone first — do not attempt to suppress estrogen. Micronized progesterone 100–200 mg nightly provides opposition and symptom relief. Avoid language about \\"too much estrogen\\" — the issue is inadequate progesterone from anovulatory cycles. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen is in the higher range, but the real issue is that progesterone hasn't kept up. This happens when ovulation becomes irregular in perimenopause. Adding progesterone will help balance things out and relieve symptoms like heavy periods, breast tenderness, and mood swings.", "referenceRange": "E2 >150 with P4 <5 indicates unopposed estrogen"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 43.8, "status": "normal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 43.8 nmol/L is within normal range. SHBG regulates bioavailable sex hormones by binding testosterone and estradiol.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 3, "status": "normal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 3 pg/mL is within normal functional range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}], "aiRecommendations": "Unable to generate recommendations. Please review lab findings manually.", "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Iron Deficiency", "confidence": "high", "description": "Iron stores are depleted or suboptimal. Iron is essential for energy, cognitive function, hair health, and oxygen transport.", "supportingFindings": ["Ferritin 8.8 ng/mL (severely depleted ≤20)", "Low energy/fatigue reported"]}, {"name": "Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Lab pattern consistent with relative estrogen excess or impaired estrogen clearance. Even without reported symptoms, supporting liver detoxification and estrogen metabolism pathways may be beneficial.", "supportingFindings": ["Estradiol:Progesterone imbalance (E2 181, P4 0.6)", "Progesterone 0.6 ng/mL (very low)"]}]}	\N	2026-03-30 13:42:25.200764	2026-03-30 13:42:25.200764
60	23	2026-03-20 00:00:00	{"a1c": 5.2, "alt": 10, "ast": 24, "bun": 12, "co2": 25, "fsh": 44.7, "hdl": 111, "ldl": 171, "lpa": 7, "mcv": 96.1, "rbc": 4.07, "tsh": 0.7, "wbc": 3.8, "acne": false, "apoB": 109, "egfr": 79, "iron": 42, "shbg": 158, "tibc": 371, "hsCRP": 3.2, "onHRT": true, "folate": 19.2, "freeT4": 1.13, "sodium": 133, "albumin": 5, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 10.2, "glucose": 82, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "chloride": 96, "ferritin": 73.2, "hairLoss": false, "vitaminD": 69.7, "bilirubin": 0.2, "estradiol": 57, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": false, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 377, "potassium": 4.4, "creatinine": 0.85, "hematocrit": 39.1, "hemoglobin": 13.1, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "vitaminB12": 2000, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2026-03-20", "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Colleen Bowman", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 58, "bmi": 23.59, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": true, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 136, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 1.84, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 179, "totalProtein": 8, "lowMotivation": false, "triglycerides": 54, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "freeTestosterone": 10, "totalCholesterol": 293}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Laboratory review and follow-up for hormone management and cardiovascular risk assessment.\\n- History / symptoms: Patient is a 58-year-old female. No specific symptoms were reported in the lab summary provided. Menopausal status suggested by labs (FSH elevated); document last menstrual period/amenorrhea duration (requested). No report of androgenic symptoms (acne, hair loss, voice changes) in current data; confirm presence/absence at follow-up. Patient reports taking B12 supplementation (dose/timing to be documented). No reported statin use in provided data.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: Not provided in lab summary (BP, HR, weight, BMI unavailable—document at next visit).\\n- CBC:\\n  - Hemoglobin 13.1 g/dL (normal)\\n  - Hematocrit 39.1 % (normal)\\n  - MCV 96.1 fL (high-normal / borderline)\\n  - Platelets 377 K/uL (normal)\\n- Iron studies:\\n  - Serum iron 42 ug/dL (normal)\\n  - TIBC 371 ug/dL (normal)\\n  - Ferritin 73.2 ng/mL (normal)\\n- Metabolic / Renal / Hepatic:\\n  - Creatinine 0.85 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - eGFR 79 mL/min (normal)\\n  - AST 24 U/L (normal)\\n  - ALT 10 U/L (normal)\\n- Diabetes / Metabolic:\\n  - Hemoglobin A1c 5.2% (normal)\\n  - Insulin resistance screen: 2/6 markers positive — MODERATE LIKELIHOOD of insulin resistance (phenotype: visceral/metabolic). Consider fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR) for confirmation.\\n- Lipids / Cardiovascular markers:\\n  - Total cholesterol: (value not provided)\\n  - HDL 111 mg/dL (normal / protective)\\n  - Triglycerides 54 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - LDL 171 mg/dL [ABNORMAL]\\n  - Apolipoprotein B 109 mg/dL [BORDERLINE]\\n  - Lipoprotein(a) 7 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - hs-CRP 3.2 mg/L [ABNORMAL — elevated cardiovascular inflammation marker]\\n- Hormone panel:\\n  - FSH 44.7 mIU/mL [ABNORMAL — consistent with menopause]\\n  - Estradiol 57 pg/mL [BORDERLINE — low end of bone protection; below HRT optimal goal]\\n  - Progesterone 1.84 ng/mL [ABNORMAL / low for luteal protection]\\n  - Testosterone (Total) 179 ng/dL [CRITICAL / ABNORMAL — above HRT optimization goal 75–125 ng/dL]\\n  - SHBG 158 nmol/L\\n  - Free Testosterone 10 pg/mL (elevated)\\n- Micronutrients:\\n  - Vitamin B12 2000 pg/mL [BORDERLINE / high — consistent with supplementation]\\n  - Folate 19.2 ng/mL (normal)\\n  - Vitamin D (25-OH) 69.7 ng/mL (normal)\\n- Risk scores:\\n  - AHA PREVENT: 10-yr Total CVD 3.1% (low), 10-yr ASCVD 2.0% (low), 10-yr HF 2.7% (low)\\n  - 30-year CVD risk: 17.4% [BORDERLINE / elevated lifetime risk]\\n  - 10-year / 30-year ASCVD and HF as in clinic summary above\\n  - STOP-BANG sleep apnea risk: 2 points (low)\\n- Other: DEXA status not provided.\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Menopausal status — laboratory evidence of menopause: FSH 44.7 mIU/mL consistent with ovarian insufficiency/menopause. Estradiol low-normal at 57 pg/mL (below HRT optimization goal). Clinical correlation with symptoms and menstrual history required. Differential: natural menopause vs. prior oophorectomy or other causes; confirm LMP/amenorrhea duration.\\n2. Supratherapeutic testosterone on HRT — Total testosterone 179 ng/dL (goal 75–125 ng/dL); Free T elevated; SHBG high. This is a critical finding with risk of androgenic/adverse effects. Reducing testosterone exposure is required. Red flag: notify physician STAT.\\n3. Insufficient progesterone for endometrial protection (if uterus intact) — progesterone 1.84 ng/mL. If uterus present, current progesterone level is inadequate to protect endometrium. If patient still cycling, need cycle-phase documentation (mid-luteal vs. follicular). Consider anovulation/perimenopause if drawn in luteal phase.\\n4. Atherogenic lipid profile with elevated inflammatory marker — LDL 171 mg/dL, ApoB 109 mg/dL borderline-high, hs‑CRP 3.2 mg/L. Although 10-year risk is low, LDL ≥160 and elevated hs‑CRP/ApoB increase lifetime CVD risk; consider primary prevention pharmacotherapy. Red flag: notify physician STAT.\\n5. Moderate likelihood of insulin resistance — 2/6 markers positive; phenotype suggests visceral/metabolic insulin resistance. Requires confirmatory testing (fasting insulin + glucose for HOMA-IR) and lifestyle interventions.\\n6. Elevated Vitamin B12 (2000 pg/mL) — likely due to supplementation. No immediate toxicity likely but document supplementation dose/route; consider reducing supplemental dose if unnecessary.\\n7. Borderline macrocytosis — MCV 96.1 fL. Correlate with B12/folate (folate normal, B12 elevated) and monitor CBC trend. Consider alcohol, liver disease, or medication causes if MCV rises.\\n8. Estradiol borderline low for bone protection — target E2 60–100 pg/mL for bone and symptom control if HRT is indicated; consider estrogen dose adjustment.\\n9. Other labs stable/normal — HDL high (111 mg/dL), triglycerides low (54 mg/dL), hemoglobin A1c normal (5.2%), LFTs and renal function normal.\\n\\nPLAN:\\n(Immediate / STAT actions)\\n- Notify supervising provider immediately regarding:\\n  - Supratherapeutic testosterone (Total T 179 ng/dL).\\n  - Atherogenic lipid profile: LDL 171 mg/dL with ApoB 109 mg/dL and hs‑CRP 3.2 mg/L.\\n- Document in chart and obtain/confirm current testosterone formulation and dosing (topical cream/gel dose and frequency, IM dose and interval, pellet use with dates, or other). Do not make medication changes without provider order.\\n\\n(Testosterone management — provider action required)\\n- Recommend urgent reduction/hold of testosterone per provider decision:\\n  - If topical (cream/gel): reduce daily dose by ~30% initially (e.g., from current dose X → ~0.7X) or consider holding 1–2 days per week; recheck levels at trough in ~4 weeks.\\n  - If intramuscular: reduce dose by 25–50% and/or increase interval between injections by 25–50%; recheck trough level ~4 weeks after change.\\n  - If pellets: plan to delay next pellet insertion and consider transition to titratable formulation; discuss specifics with provider (pellets cannot be removed).\\n- Orders post-adjustment:\\n  - Recheck Total Testosterone, Free T, SHBG 4 weeks after dose change (timed at trough based on formulation).\\n  - Monitor CBC (baseline and q3–6 months), lipids and LFTs per protocol while adjusting testosterone.\\n- Symptom monitoring: assess for androgenic symptoms (acne, hair thinning, virilization) at next visit; consider adjunctive therapies if needed.\\n\\n(Estrogen / Progesterone management)\\n- Document uterine status (intact vs. hysterectomy) and last menstrual period / amenorrhea duration immediately.\\n- If uterus intact:\\n  - Progesterone currently inadequate for endometrial protection (1.84 ng/mL). Provider to adjust route/dose.\\n  - Suggested empiric options for provider consideration: micronized progesterone 100 mg at bedtime (qHS) initially; if needed for protection increase to 100–200 mg qHS or cyclic 200 mg qHS ×12–14 days/month depending on regimen. Provider to choose dose and schedule. Recheck progesterone 4–6 weeks after adjustment (document cycle timing).\\n- Estradiol:\\n  - Goal estradiol 60–100 pg/mL. If transdermal patch and currently on 0.025 mg/day, consider increasing to 0.05 mg/day per provider decision; if oral formulation, consider dose increase or switch to transdermal (preferred when CVD risk is a concern). Recheck estradiol 4–6 weeks after dose change.\\n- Note: All hormone changes require provider order and documentation of current regimen prior to change.\\n\\n(Cardiovascular risk / Lipid management)\\n- Provider consideration: initiate moderate-intensity statin for primary prevention given LDL ≥160 mg/dL and ApoB 109 mg/dL with elevated hs‑CRP.\\n  - Suggested options for provider discussion: atorvastatin 10–20 mg daily or rosuvastatin 5–10 mg daily. Shared decision-making with patient required.\\n- Orders if statin initiated:\\n  - Baseline LFTs (ALT, AST) — ALT already 10 U/L and AST 24 U/L documented; repeat if not within 3 months.\\n  - Consider baseline CK per clinic protocol (if desired).\\n  - Recheck fasting lipid panel and LFTs 6–12 weeks after initiation/dose change; then every 6–12 months.\\n- Lifestyle counseling (see below) to be documented and reinforced.\\n\\n(Insulin resistance / metabolic)\\n- Order fasting insulin and fasting glucose to calculate HOMA-IR to confirm hyperinsulinemia physiology.\\n- Recommend lifestyle interventions: dietary counseling (Mediterranean/DASH, reduce refined carbs, increase soluble fiber), physical activity goal ≥150 min/week moderate aerobic + 2x weekly resistance training, weight loss 5–10% if overweight, limit alcohol, smoking cessation if applicable.\\n- Consider referral to nutritionist/diabetes prevention program if available.\\n\\n(Other labs / monitoring)\\n- CBC repeat in 3 months to monitor MCV trend (MCV 96.1 fL borderline).\\n- Repeat hs‑CRP in 3 months after lifestyle intervention or pharmacologic therapy to reassess inflammatory risk.\\n- Repeat hormone panel (Total T, Free T, SHBG, Estradiol, Progesterone) 4 weeks after any HRT dose change (timed appropriately); sooner if changes urgent.\\n- If statin started: fasting lipid panel and LFTs 6–12 weeks post-initiation.\\n- Consider DEXA scan if not up to date given menopausal status and borderline-low estrogen (arrange per provider).\\n- If elevated B12 persists without clear indication, consider methylmalonic acid if concern for functional B12 deficiency; otherwise document supplementation and consider dose reduction.\\n- Document and confirm current B12 supplementation dose/route and last dose.\\n\\n(Supplement recommendations)\\n- Recommended high-priority supplements per clinic protocols (if patient elects and provider concurs):\\n  - AdvaClear® 2 capsules twice daily\\n  - GlutaClear® 2 capsules daily\\n  - OmegaGenics® Fish Oil Neuro 1000: 1 softgel 1–2 times daily\\n- Medium-priority:\\n  - NutraGems® CoQ10 300: 1 chewable gel daily\\n  - UltraFlora® Healthy Weight with Akkermansia: 1 capsule daily\\n  - EstroFactors™: 2 tablets twice daily\\n- Note: Supplements should be reviewed with provider for interactions with medications (e.g., statin) and patient preference.\\n\\n(Lifestyle counseling)\\n- Documented counseling to be given and provided in writing:\\n  - Diet: Mediterranean/DASH, reduce saturated fat and refined carbohydrates, increase fiber and plant-based foods.\\n  - Exercise: ≥150 min/wk moderate aerobic + resistance training twice weekly.\\n  - Weight: aim for 5–10% weight reduction if overweight.\\n  - Alcohol: ≤1 drink/day.\\n  - Tobacco cessation resources if applicable.\\n  - Sleep and stress management as adjunctive measures.\\n\\n(Referrals and additional considerations)\\n- Consider endocrinology referral for complex hormone management if discordant labs or difficulty titrating therapy.\\n- Consider cardiology referral if patient/prescriber prefers specialist input for primary prevention given LDL 171 mg/dL and elevated hs-CRP.\\n- Gynecology referral if uterus intact and hormone regimen adjustments needed or for abnormal uterine bleeding.\\n- Hepatology referral if unexplained abnormal LFTs or other hepatic concerns.\\n\\n(Follow-up / timeline)\\n- Immediate (within 24–72 hours): Notify provider of supratherapeutic testosterone and elevated LDL/hs‑CRP. Document current HRT regimen and LMP/menstrual status.\\n- 1–2 weeks: Provider implements testosterone dose reduction/hold and decides on estrogen/progesterone adjustments.\\n- 4 weeks after hormone dose change: Recheck Total T, Free T, SHBG (trough), Estradiol, Progesterone (with documented cycle phase as applicable). Clinical follow-up for symptoms.\\n- 6–12 weeks: If statin started, recheck fasting lipid panel and LFTs.\\n- 3 months: Repeat CBC (monitor MCV), repeat hs‑CRP, reassess metabolic and cardiovascular risk and response to interventions.\\n- Ongoing: Monitor CBC, lipids, LFTs, and clinical response per clinic HRT and cardiovascular follow-up protocols.\\n\\nDOCUMENTATION AND PATIENT COMMUNICATION:\\n- Results reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Next steps: provider to be notified urgently for testosterone and lipid issues; patient will have hormone regimen and/or statin discussion with provider and follow-up labs as per timeline above.\\n\\nDate: 03/30/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": true, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": true, "high_nonHD