arance support); Insulin resistance / metabolic dysfunction (hepatic metabolic support)", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance", "Oxidative Stress Burden", "Inflammatory Burden", "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "This supplement supports your liver's natural detoxification processes. Your results suggest your body could benefit from help processing hormones, metabolic byproducts, and other compounds. It provides nutrients that support both phases of your liver's detox pathways.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance pattern (hepatic detox pathways needed for clearance)", "Oxidative stress burden (liver biotransformation support)", "Inflammatory burden (metabolic clearance support)", "Insulin resistance / metabolic dysfunction (hepatic metabolic support)", "Borderline liver markers (ALT 10, AST 24) suggesting hepatic biotransformation load", "hs-CRP 3.2 mg/L (inflammatory metabolic burden)"]}, {"dose": "2 capsules daily", "name": "GlutaClear®", "caution": "Glutathione and antioxidant support formula. Contains NAC, alpha-lipoic acid, and green tea catechins. Supports cellular defense against oxidative damage.", "category": "detox", "priority": "high", "rationale": "GlutaClear supports glutathione production and antioxidant defense with NAC, alpha-lipoic acid, and green tea catechins. Indicated when there is evidence of oxidative stress, inflammatory burden, or metabolic dysfunction requiring enhanced cellular defense. While AdvaClear provides broader liver detoxification support, GlutaClear specifically targets glutathione pathways and antioxidant capacity.", "indication": "Oxidative stress phenotype (glutathione/antioxidant support indicated); Inflammatory burden (antioxidant defense support); Insulin resistance (oxidative metabolic stress); hs-CRP 3.2 mg/L (high inflammatory/oxidative burden)", "phenotypes": ["Oxidative Stress Burden", "Inflammatory Burden", "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "This supplement boosts your body's master antioxidant, glutathione, which protects your cells from damage. Your results suggest increased oxidative stress, and this formula helps your body defend and repair at the cellular level.", "supportingFindings": ["Oxidative stress phenotype (glutathione/antioxidant support indicated)", "Inflammatory burden (antioxidant defense support)", "Insulin resistance (oxidative metabolic stress)", "hs-CRP 3.2 mg/L (high inflammatory/oxidative burden)", "Age 58 (declining glutathione production)"]}, {"dose": "1 softgel 1-2 times daily", "name": "OmegaGenics® Fish Oil Neuro 1000", "caution": "High-DHA omega-3 (750mg DHA, 250mg EPA) for brain and heart health. Lemon-flavored, no fishy taste. Contains calamari - avoid if shellfish allergy.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "high", "rationale": "OmegaGenics Fish Oil Neuro 1000 provides concentrated DHA and EPA to reduce triglycerides, support brain function, cardiovascular health, and reduce systemic inflammation.", "indication": "hs-CRP 3.2 mg/L (inflammatory); ApoB 109 mg/dL (elevated); LDL 171 mg/dL (elevated); Total cholesterol 293 mg/dL (elevated)", "phenotypes": ["Inflammatory Burden", "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "This high-quality fish oil provides omega-3 fatty acids that help reduce inflammation, support your heart and brain health, and may help improve your cholesterol profile.", "supportingFindings": ["hs-CRP 3.2 mg/L (inflammatory)", "ApoB 109 mg/dL (elevated)", "LDL 171 mg/dL (elevated)", "Total cholesterol 293 mg/dL (elevated)"]}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Essential for patients on statins.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "ApoB 109 mg/dL (cardiovascular support); hs-CRP 3.2 mg/L; LDL 171 mg/dL; TC 293 mg/dL", "phenotypes": ["Inflammatory Burden", "Oxidative Stress Burden"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["ApoB 109 mg/dL (cardiovascular support)", "hs-CRP 3.2 mg/L", "LDL 171 mg/dL", "TC 293 mg/dL", "Age 58 (declining CoQ10 production)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Healthy Weight with Akkermansia", "caution": "Contains Akkermansia muciniphila and Bifidobacterium lactis B420 for metabolic and gut barrier support. Refrigerate after opening for maximum potency.", "category": "metabolic", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "UltraFlora Healthy Weight with Akkermansia provides targeted probiotic support for the gut-metabolic axis. Akkermansia muciniphila supports gut barrier integrity, metabolic health, and healthy weight management. Indicated for insulin resistance, metabolic syndrome features, visceral adiposity, and weight loss resistance.", "indication": "Insulin resistance / visceral adiposity phenotype (gut-metabolic axis support); Inflammatory phenotype (gut-immune axis); ApoB 109 mg/dL (atherogenic metabolic pattern); hs-CRP 3.2 mg/L (metabolic inflammation)", "phenotypes": ["Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "Inflammatory Burden"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This specialized probiotic contains Akkermansia, a beneficial gut bacteria linked to healthy metabolism and weight management. It supports your gut barrier, helps manage metabolic inflammation, and works with your body's natural weight regulation systems.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance / visceral adiposity phenotype (gut-metabolic axis support)", "Inflammatory phenotype (gut-immune axis)", "ApoB 109 mg/dL (atherogenic metabolic pattern)", "hs-CRP 3.2 mg/L (metabolic inflammation)"]}, {"dose": "2 tablets twice daily", "name": "EstroFactors™", "caution": "Nutritional support for estrogen balance and metabolism. Contains I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Supports healthy estrogen metabolite ratios.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "EstroFactors provides targeted nutritional support for estrogen metabolism and balance with I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Best suited for women with estrogen dominance patterns, PMS, perimenopausal hormone imbalance, or those needing broader estrogen metabolism support during hormonal transitions. Differs from Estrovera in that it addresses the underlying estrogen metabolism rather than vasomotor symptom relief.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected; Age-appropriate for estrogen metabolism support", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement helps your body process and balance estrogen more effectively. It supports healthy hormone metabolism, which can help with PMS symptoms, bloating, mood changes, and hormonal balance during life transitions.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected", "Age-appropriate for estrogen metabolism support"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 7, "apoBValue": 109, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "borderline", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 2.0222013427927963, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": true}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "generally_recommended", "indicated": true, "rationale": "ApoB ≥90 mg/dL (109 mg/dL) with risk enhancers (elevated hs-CRP, elevated ApoB). Statin discussion recommended."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "consider", "rationale": "Borderline lipid levels with multiple risk enhancers (elevated hs-CRP, elevated ApoB). Consider CAC to refine risk assessment.", "recommended": true}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\nYour labs show menopause-level hormones (FSH 44.7 mIU/mL) with low progesterone (1.84 ng/mL) and a testosterone level above the usual HRT target (Total T 179 ng/dL; Free T 10 pg/mL; SHBG 158 nmol/L). Your LDL cholesterol is elevated at 171 mg/dL and your hs-CRP is 3.2 mg/L (both increase cardiovascular risk). Your 10-year ASCVD risk is low at 2.0%, but your 30-year CVD risk is higher at 17.4% — this suggests low short-term but meaningful long-term cardiovascular risk.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Hemoglobin 13.1 g/dL and hematocrit 39.1% are within normal range.\\n- Ferritin 73.2 ng/mL indicates good iron stores.\\n- TSH 0.7 mIU/L (euthyroid).\\n- Vitamin D 69.7 ng/mL (optimal).\\n- HDL 111 mg/dL, triglycerides 54 mg/dL, Lp(a) 7 mg/dL — all favorable.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + concrete actions)\\n1) Cholesterol and heart risk: LDL 171 mg/dL, ApoB 109 mg/dL, hs-CRP 3.2 mg/L, 30-year CVD risk 17.4% (intermediate long‑term risk).\\n- Diet: Add 25–30 g fiber daily (oatmeal for breakfast, beans at lunch, vegetables at dinner).\\n- Fish: Eat 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n- Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter and cheese.\\n- Exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking daily; aim for 200–300 minutes aerobic weekly plus strength training 2x/week.\\n- Reduce sodium to <2,300 mg/day and limit processed foods.\\n- Discuss statin therapy with your provider given LDL 171 mg/dL and ApoB 109 mg/dL.\\n\\n2) Hormones and HRT optimization: Progesterone 1.84 ng/mL (below HRT therapeutic range) and Total Testosterone 179 ng/dL (above HRT optimization goal 75–125 ng/dL).\\n- Contact your HRT prescriber to prioritize lowering testosterone dose; avoid DIY dose changes.\\n- Lifestyle to support hormones: prioritize 7–8 hours sleep, strength training 2–3x/week, and stress management (meditation, walking, hobbies).\\n\\n3) Blood/other: MCV 96.1 fL (high-normal) and Vitamin B12 2000 pg/mL (elevated).\\n- If you take B12 supplements, review dose with your clinician; we may repeat B12 and MCV in 3 months.\\n\\nBone health and next steps\\n- Estradiol 57 pg/mL is at the lower end for bone protection. With FSH 44.7 mIU/mL (menopause), consider DEXA scan within 6–12 months and keep Vitamin D 69.7 ng/mL and adequate calcium (dietary 1,000–1,200 mg/day).\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Contact your HRT prescriber within 1–2 weeks to adjust testosterone/progesterone.\\n- Start cholesterol lifestyle changes now; recheck fasting lipids and hs-CRP in 6–12 weeks.\\n- Review B12 supplement and repeat B12/MCV in ~3 months.\\n- Discuss DEXA for bone density in the next 6–12 months.\\nIf you want, I can help draft questions for your visit or a short message to your provider.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 13.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 39.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 96.1, "status": "borderline", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 96.1 fL is in the high-normal range. Early macrocytosis can precede overt B12 or folate deficiency.", "recommendation": "Ensure B12 and folate levels are within optimal range. Monitor CBC at next visit.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 377, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 57, "status": "borderline", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol at minimum bone protection level (40-60 pg/mL) but below optimal HRT goal.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Consider increasing estrogen dose. Target 60-100 pg/mL for optimized symptom relief.", "referenceRange": "60-100 pg/mL (HRT goal, >40 for bone)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1.84, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone below HRT therapeutic range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase progesterone dose. Target 8-10 ng/mL for optimal endometrial protection.", "referenceRange": "8-10 ng/mL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 44.7, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 179, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone above HRT optimization goal (75-125 ng/dL).", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Consider reducing testosterone dose. Monitor for androgenic side effects.", "referenceRange": "75-125 ng/dL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 0.7, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 73.2, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 69.7, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D optimal (60-100 ng/mL). Supports hormone, bone, and immune health.", "recommendation": "Maintain current regimen. Monitor annually.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 2000, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "Elevated B12.", "recommendation": "Usually from supplementation. Rarely indicates liver disease.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.2, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 171, "status": "abnormal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "High LDL.", "recommendation": "Consider statin based on ASCVD risk. Lifestyle modifications.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 111, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 54, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 3.1407156072663827, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 3.1% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 2.0222013427927963, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 2.0%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 2.7257346051844964, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 2.7%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 2, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (2/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 17.36331039786597, "status": "borderline", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 17.4%, ASCVD 10.3%, Heart Failure 16.8%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 24, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 10, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 79, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.85, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 109, "status": "borderline", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Borderline elevated Apo B.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider pharmacotherapy if high ASCVD risk.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 7, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 371, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 42, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 19.2, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 3.2, "status": "abnormal", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Elevated hs-CRP - increased cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Address cardiovascular risk factors. Consider repeat testing.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1.84, "status": "borderline", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Low Progesterone (Phase Not Documented)", "interpretation": "Progesterone is 1.84 ng/mL. Without documented cycle phase or sufficient estradiol/FSH context to identify a specific pattern, interpretation is limited. If drawn in the luteal phase, this level would indicate anovulation — a perimenopause marker.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Document cycle phase for accurate interpretation. If the patient is mid-cycle or in the luteal phase, this suggests anovulation. Consider symptoms: sleep disruption, anxiety, irregular cycles. If symptomatic, empiric progesterone (100 mg nightly) is reasonable. PATIENT EDUCATION: Knowing where you are in your menstrual cycle helps us fully interpret this result. Please note when your last period started so we can better understand your hormone picture.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase; <5 ng/mL suggests anovulation if luteal"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 44.7, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Menopause", "interpretation": "FSH of 44.7 mIU/mL is consistent with menopause. No specific symptoms reported at this time. Elevated FSH reflects decreased ovarian function and reduced estrogen production. This is a normal part of reproductive aging but may benefit from hormone optimization if symptomatic.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: If symptomatic, consider hormone therapy (HT): transdermal estradiol + micronized progesterone (if uterus intact). Discuss benefits/risks of HT including cardiovascular, bone, and quality of life considerations. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your lab results confirm you are in menopause. Hormone therapy can help manage symptoms like hot flashes, sleep problems, and vaginal dryness while also protecting bone health.", "referenceRange": "FSH <25 mIU/mL premenopausal; ≥25 suggests transition; ≥40 menopausal"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 179, "status": "critical", "category": "Testosterone Pattern C: Above HRT Optimization Goal", "interpretation": "Testosterone above HRT optimization goal (Total T: 179 ng/dL (above HRT optimization goal of 75–125 ng/dL); SHBG: 158 nmol/L; Free T: 10 pg/mL (elevated)). The target range for testosterone on HRT is 75–125 ng/dL. At 179 ng/dL, the patient is above goal and significantly elevated — dose reduction should be prioritized.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Testosterone is above the HRT optimization goal of 75–125 ng/dL. Reduce the testosterone dose — do NOT add additional testosterone. At >150 ng/dL, this is significantly elevated; prioritize dose reduction promptly. Manage downstream symptoms if present: spironolactone for acne or hair thinning, sleep support for insomnia, anxiety management as needed. If using pellet therapy, consider transitioning to a titratable form (transdermal cream or gel) to allow precise dose adjustments. Monitor CBC, lipids, and liver function. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your testosterone is above the target range for your hormone therapy. Your provider will adjust your dose to bring it back into the optimal range (75–125 ng/dL) and manage any related symptoms.", "referenceRange": "HRT optimization goal: 75–125 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "of 6 markers positive", "value": 2, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Insulin Resistance Screening", "recheckTiming": "3-6 months", "interpretation": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive). This combination points toward hyperinsulinemia physiology. Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR).", "recommendation": "Phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance. Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose.", "referenceRange": "0-1 Low, 2 Moderate, 3+ High Likelihood"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- Summary: Labs consistent with menopause (FSH 44.7 mIU/mL). Key actionable abnormalities: supratherapeutic testosterone (Total T 179 ng/dL, Free T elevated), low/low‑normal sex steroids for HRT goals (Estradiol 57 pg/mL; Progesterone 1.84 ng/mL), atherogenic lipid profile (LDL 171 mg/dL; ApoB 109 mg/dL) with elevated inflammatory marker (hs‑CRP 3.2 mg/L), borderline macrocytosis (MCV 96.1 fL), and very high B12 (2000 pg/mL) consistent with supplementation. Many hormone interpretations limited without documented cycle phase; FSH supports menopausal status.\\n\\nPriority actions (organized CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\n\\nCRITICAL (notify provider immediately)\\n- Supratherapeutic testosterone: Total T 179 ng/dL (>150 ng/dL) — notify provider STAT (risk of androgenic adverse effects). Recommendation: hold or reduce testosterone dose promptly (see dosing guidance below). Do NOT add more testosterone.\\n- Atherogenic lipids with LDL 171 mg/dL and ApoB 109 mg/dL and hs‑CRP 3.2 mg/L — notify provider to consider primary prevention pharmacotherapy (statin) given LDL ≥160 mg/dL and elevated lifetime CVD risk. Order baseline ALT and consider baseline CK if initiating statin per clinic protocol.\\n\\nURGENT (action within 1–2 weeks)\\n- Estradiol/progesterone optimization for menopausal HT:\\n  - Estradiol 57 pg/mL at low end of bone protection and below HRT target (goal 60–100 pg/mL) — discuss increasing estrogen dose with provider.\\n  - Progesterone 1.84 ng/mL — inadequate for endometrial protection if uterus intact (goal 8–10 ng/mL). Provider to adjust progesterone dosing.\\n- Document menstrual/cycle status immediately in chart (date of last menstrual period or amenorrhea duration) to aid interpretation of progesterone and any residual cycling.\\n- Elevated hs‑CRP 3.2 mg/L — address modifiable risks and plan repeat hs‑CRP after risk reduction measures (3 months).\\n\\nROUTINE (action in weeks–months)\\n- MCV 96.1 fL (high‑normal): monitor CBC at next visit (3 months). Correlate with B12/folate/ferritin (current folate and ferritin reported normal).\\n- Vitamin B12 2000 pg/mL: document supplementation history (dose, route, last dose). No immediate action if asymptomatic and supplementing; consider reducing supplemental dose if extremely high and not indicated.\\n- Reassess lipids and inflammatory markers after interventions (see timeline).\\n\\nSpecific testosterone recommendations (for provider review)\\n- Goal: Total testosterone 75–125 ng/dL (as clinic protocol).\\n- Immediate action: reduce testosterone exposure now — urgent provider decision.\\n  - If topical (cream/gel): reduce daily dose by ~30% initially (example: if currently X mg/day, reduce to ~0.7X) or hold 1–2 days per week while rechecking levels; plan to remeasure in 4 weeks.\\n  - If intramuscular injection: reduce dose by 25–50% and/or increase injection interval (e.g., extend interval by 25–50%); recheck level at trough ~4 weeks after dose change.\\n  - If subcutaneous/intravaginal pellets: prioritize transition to a titratable form (transdermal cream/gel) for precise control; for current pellet users, plan to reduce next pellet dose (delay next insertion) — discuss timing with provider because pellets cannot be removed easily.\\n- Monitoring after adjustment:\\n  - Recheck Total T, Free T, SHBG in 4 weeks after dose change (trough timing per formulation).\\n  - Monitor CBC (baseline and then q3–6 months), lipids and LFTs periodically while dose being adjusted.\\n- Symptom management: if androgenic symptoms (acne, hair thinning), consider adjuncts (e.g., topical management, spironolactone) per provider.\\n\\nHormone (estradiol/progesterone) dose recommendations (for provider review)\\n- Estradiol:\\n  - Goal 60–100 pg/mL for symptom control and bone protection.\\n  - If on transdermal patch: common step-up is 0.025 mg/day → 0.05 mg/day. If current dose unknown, provider to adjust to achieve target range and reassess in 4–6 weeks.\\n  - If oral estradiol: increase dose per provider judgment; consider transdermal route for lower hepatic effect if CVD risk elevated.\\n- Progesterone (if uterus intact):\\n  - Current progesterone 1.84 ng/mL is inadequate for luteal protection. For endometrial protection target mid‑luteal 8–10 ng/mL.\\n  - Empiric option per protocol: micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly (qHS) — consider increasing to 100–200 mg nightly or cyclic 200 mg qHS ×12–14 days/month depending on regimen. Provider to set regimen and recheck progesterone level 4–6 weeks after initiating/changing.\\n  - Ensure documentation of cycle phase if patient still cycling; if draws were luteal and level still low, treat as anovulatory/perimenopausal.\\n\\nCardiovascular risk management and lipid recommendations (staff actions and provider discussion)\\n- LDL 171 mg/dL, ApoB 109 mg/dL, hs‑CRP 3.2 mg/L — elevate primary prevention concern.\\n- Provider consideration:\\n  - Initiate moderate‑intensity statin if agreed (e.g., atorvastatin 10–20 mg or rosuvastatin 5–10 mg) given LDL ≥160 mg/dL even if 10‑year risk is low — discuss risks/benefits with patient.\\n  - Order baseline LFTs prior to initiation; check fasting lipid panel 6–12 weeks after start/change, then every 6–12 months.\\n- Lifestyle: institute aggressive lifestyle counseling (see below).\\n- Consider cardiology referral if patient has multiple risk enhancers, symptomatic CVD, or patient/prescriber preference.\\n\\nLifestyle interventions (to convey to patient by staff)\\n- Diet: Mediterranean/DASH style diet, reduce saturated fats, increase soluble fiber, limit refined carbs and trans fats.\\n- Exercise: ≥150 minutes/week moderate aerobic activity plus twice‑weekly resistance training.\\n- Weight: aim for 5–10% weight reduction if overweight.\\n- Smoking cessation if applicable.\\n- Alcohol: limit to ≤1 drink/day.\\n- Sleep hygiene and stress reduction as adjuncts (also relevant for perimenopausal symptoms).\\n- Document counseling in chart and provide written resources per clinic protocol.\\n\\nAdditional testing and referrals to consider\\n- Repeat hormone panel with documented timing:\\n  - Document last menstrual period/last bleeding date. If cycling, draw progesterone mid‑luteal (7 days after ovulation or ~day 21 of 28‑day cycle).\\n  - Repeat Total T, Free T, SHBG, Estradiol, Progesterone 4 weeks after any dose change (test timing per formulation).\\n- CBC repeat in 3 months to monitor MCV trends.\\n- If considering statin: baseline ALT ± AST (if not already done) and fasting lipid panel in 6–12 weeks after initiation.\\n- Repeat hs‑CRP in 3 months after risk modification.\\n- DEXA scan: if not up to date, consider bone density testing given menopausal status (discuss with provider).\\n- Specialty referrals as indicated:\\n  - Endocrinology if complex hormone management or discordant labs.\\n  - Cardiology for high lifetime risk or complexity.\\n  - Gynecology if abnormal uterine bleeding, if uterus intact and progesterone adjustment needed, or for long‑term HT planning.\\n  - Hepatology if unexplained very high B12 with abnormal LFTs.\\n\\nPatient education points for staff to cover (brief, actionable)\\n- Request and document date of last menstrual period/menstrual status to improve interpretation of hormone labs.\\n- Explain that testosterone is above target and the provider will reduce the dose to avoid side effects; expect recheck in ~4 weeks.\\n- Explain need to discuss cholesterol and heart disease risk with provider; may consider statin.\\n- Review current B12 supplementation (dose/frequency) and plan to document; reassure that high B12 usually from supplements.\\n- If initiating or changing HT, review potential benefits/risks and need for periodic monitoring and symptom follow-up.\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (actionable checklist)\\n- Immediate (within 24–72 hours)\\n  - Notify provider about supratherapeutic testosterone (Total T 179 ng/dL) — urgent dose reduction decision.\\n  - Notify provider about LDL 171 mg/dL, ApoB 109 mg/dL, hs‑CRP 3.2 mg/L — consider statin discussion.\\n  - Document last menstrual period / menopausal status in chart.\\n\\n- 1–2 weeks\\n  - Implement provider decision on testosterone dose reduction (hold/reduce/modify regimen).\\n  - Provider to decide on estrogen/progesterone adjustments; begin changes as ordered.\\n\\n- 4 weeks after hormone dose change\\n  - Recheck Total T, Free T, SHBG (timed at trough), Estradiol and Progesterone (with cycle phase documented if cycling).\\n  - Review symptoms and side effects.\\n\\n- 6–12 weeks\\n  - If statin started: recheck fasting lipid panel and LFTs at ~6–12 weeks post initiation or dose change.\\n  - If estrogen/progesterone adjusted, re-evaluate symptoms and labs (can also be done at 4–6 weeks per provider).\\n\\n- 3 months\\n  - Repeat CBC (monitor MCV), repeat hs‑CRP to assess response to risk modification.\\n  - Reassess need for DEXA if not previously performed.\\n\\n- Ongoing\\n  - Monitor CBC, lipids, LFTs, and clinical response per clinic HRT and cardiovascular follow-up protocols.\\n\\nPhysician notification requirements (summary)\\n- Notify provider immediately for:\\n  - Testosterone >150 ng/dL (Total T 179 ng/dL).\\n  - LDL ≥160 mg/dL with elevated ApoB and hs‑CRP (LDL 171 mg/dL; ApoB 109 mg/dL; hs‑CRP 3.2 mg/L).\\n  - Any new/worsening symptoms suggestive of androgen excess, liver disease, or statin adverse effects.\\n\\nNotes/considerations for charting\\n- Record current hormone delivery method and doses (transdermal, oral, IM, pellets) before making or implementing adjustments.\\n- Clearly document cycle phase/amenorrhea length and recent supplementation (B12) to aid interpretation.\\n- All medication changes should be approved by the provider and documented in the plan.\\n\\n(End of staff-facing recommendations)", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 0.49, "detail": "Triglycerides 54 / HDL 111 = 0.49", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5.2%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 158, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 10, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "109 mg/dL", "detail": "Elevated atherogenic particle count", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "3.2 mg/L", "detail": "Elevated systemic inflammation", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "moderate", "phenotypes": [{"key": "visceral_metabolic", "name": "Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Recheck A1c, lipid panel, hs-CRP, ApoB in 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0"], "pathophysiology": "Early peripheral + hepatic insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia. Glucose may still be \\"normal.\\" A1c often rises last.", "triggerCriteria": ["A1c >= 5.7%", "TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0", "Central adiposity phenotype", "ALT above optimal range"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests your body may not be responding to insulin as efficiently as it should. Insulin is the hormone that helps move sugar from your bloodstream into your cells for energy.\\n\\nWhen cells become less sensitive to insulin:\\n- Blood sugar slowly rises over time\\n- Triglycerides increase\\n- Inflammation increases\\n- Weight gain around the midsection becomes easier\\n\\nThe good news? This stage is highly reversible with targeted nutrition, strength training, sleep optimization, and - when appropriate - medication support.\\n\\nWe'll focus on improving how your body processes fuel and reducing long-term cardiometabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward nutrition (>= 25-35g/meal)", "Carbohydrate quality + timing education", "Strength training 2-3x weekly", "10-15 min post-meal walking", "Sleep optimization", "Consider: GLP-1 therapy if obesity, appetite dysregulation, A1c >= 5.7, or inflammatory phenotype", "Consider: Metformin if prediabetes or strong family history", "Recheck metabolic panel in 3-6 months"]}], "positiveCount": 2, "likelihoodLabel": "MODERATE LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive). This combination points toward hyperinsulinemia physiology. Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR).", "confirmationTests": "Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose."}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Inflammatory Burden", "confidence": "high", "description": "Pattern of systemic inflammation indicated by elevated inflammatory markers and/or symptomatic presentation. May benefit from anti-inflammatory nutritional support.", "supportingFindings": ["hs-CRP 3.2 mg/L (elevated >3.0)", "ApoB 109 mg/dL (elevated)"]}, {"name": "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Metabolic pattern consistent with insulin resistance and/or visceral adiposity. This drives inflammation, hormonal imbalance, and cardiovascular risk.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance screening: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive)", "ApoB 109 mg/dL (elevated)", "hs-CRP 3.2 mg/L (metabolic inflammation)"]}, {"name": "Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Lab pattern consistent with relative estrogen excess or impaired estrogen clearance. Even without reported symptoms, supporting liver detoxification and estrogen metabolism pathways may be beneficial.", "supportingFindings": ["Progesterone 1.84 ng/mL (very low)", "Borderline liver markers (ALT 10, AST 24) suggesting possible impaired hepatic clearance"]}, {"name": "Oxidative Stress Burden", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Multiple markers suggest increased oxidative stress and cellular burden. Antioxidant and glutathione support may help reduce oxidative damage and support cellular energy production.", "supportingFindings": ["hs-CRP 3.2 mg/L (elevated, systemic inflammation)", "AST 24 U/L (cellular stress marker)", "Insulin resistance pattern (metabolic oxidative driver)", "Age 58 (increased oxidative stress risk)"]}, {"name": "Iron Deficiency", "confidence": "low", "description": "Iron stores are depleted or suboptimal. Iron is essential for energy, cognitive function, hair health, and oxygen transport.", "supportingFindings": ["Serum iron 42 µg/dL (low)"]}, {"name": "Menopausal Transition", "confidence": "low", "description": "Clinical picture consistent with perimenopause or menopause. Hormonal shifts during this transition affect bone health, cardiovascular risk, mood, and quality of life.", "supportingFindings": ["Age 58 (perimenopause window)", "FSH 44.7 mIU/mL (elevated, menopausal range)"]}]}	\N	2026-03-30 15:12:33.297966	2026-03-30 15:12:33.297966
