neurologic symptoms. No report of heavy or prolonged menstrual bleeding in chart; menstrual phase noted as luteal on labs (see Objective). No current use of exogenous androgens reported in chart. Alcohol use, smoking status, and exact cycle day to be confirmed and documented for further correlation.\\n- Relevant ROS (pertinent to labs): Fatigue and occasional low mood (as above). No fevers, night sweats, jaundice, abdominal pain, or bruising reported.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vital signs: Not provided in chart today (BP/HR/weight/BMI unavailable).\\n- Labs (03/30/2026). All values listed with units; abnormal/borderline values flagged.\\n\\nCBC:\\n- Hemoglobin (Hgb): 15.9 g/dL (normal)\\n- Hematocrit (Hct): 46.8 % — BORDERLINE (slightly elevated; may reflect dehydration)\\n- MCV: 94.7 fL (normal)\\n- Platelets: 276 K/uL (normal)\\n\\nIron studies:\\n- Serum Iron: 145 µg/dL (normal)\\n- TIBC (Total Iron Binding Capacity): 324 µg/dL (normal)\\n- Transferrin saturation (calculated TSAT = serum iron / TIBC ×100): 44.8% (calculated) — borderline, just below 45% threshold\\n- Ferritin: 204 ng/mL — BORDERLINE (elevated)\\n\\nComprehensive metabolic / renal / hepatic:\\n- AST: 19 U/L (normal)\\n- ALT: 18 U/L (normal)\\n- Creatinine: 0.95 mg/dL (normal)\\n- eGFR: 80 mL/min (normal)\\n\\nLipid panel / cardiovascular markers:\\n- Total cholesterol: (not provided) [not listed]\\n- LDL cholesterol: 104 mg/dL — BORDERLINE (near optimal)\\n- HDL cholesterol: 91 mg/dL (normal, favorable)\\n- Triglycerides: 46 mg/dL (normal)\\n- Apolipoprotein B: 85 mg/dL (normal)\\n- Lipoprotein(a): 17 mg/dL (normal)\\n- hs-CRP: 0.6 mg/L (normal)\\n\\nGlycemic:\\n- Hemoglobin A1c: 4.6 % (normal)\\n\\nThyroid / adrenal:\\n- TSH: 1.45 mIU/L (normal)\\n- DHEA-S: 135 µg/dL (normal)\\n\\nHormones (reproductive):\\n- Estradiol (E2): 111 pg/mL (normal); sample interpreted as luteal-phase context\\n- Progesterone (P4): 10.8 ng/mL (normal) — documented as luteal/progesterone consistent with ovulation\\n- FSH: 3.99 mIU/mL (normal)\\n- Total Testosterone: 20.6 ng/dL (normal)\\n- Free Testosterone: 2 pg/mL (normal)\\n- SHBG: 73.9 nmol/L (normal)\\n- Testosterone pattern interpretation: Pattern D — Adequate androgens, persistent symptoms likely non-androgenic (BORDERLINE pattern flagged in summary due to symptoms despite adequate labs)\\n\\nVitamins / micronutrients:\\n- Vitamin B12: 762 pg/mL (normal)\\n- Folate: 12.9 ng/mL (normal)\\n- Vitamin D (25‑OH): 44.1 ng/mL — BORDERLINE (adequate but below clinic target range 41–59 ng/mL; suboptimal per clinic target)\\n\\nOther:\\n- Hemoglobin A1c: 4.6% (normal)\\n- Perimenopause assessment: Luteal Progesterone: 10.8 ng/mL (consistent with ovulatory luteal sample)\\n\\nRisk scores (AHA PREVENT 2023 / STOP‑BANG):\\n- 10‑year Total CVD risk: 0.2% (0.1818%) — low\\n- 10‑year ASCVD risk: 0.1% (0.1238%) — low\\n- 10‑year Heart Failure risk: 0.1% (0.1342%) — low\\n- 30‑year CVD risk: 1.5% (1.4920%) — low\\n- Sleep apnea risk (STOP‑BANG): 0 points — low\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1) Borderline elevated hematocrit 46.8% (Hct) — likely due to relative hemoconcentration / inadequate hydration. Hemoglobin 15.9 g/dL and MCV normal. Differential: dehydration, physiologic variation, smoking or exogenous androgen use (none documented), early polycythemia vera unlikely but consider if Hct rises or symptoms develop.\\n   - Red flags: Hct >52% or symptoms of hyperviscosity (headache, visual changes, thrombotic events) would prompt urgent notification.\\n\\n2) Elevated ferritin 204 ng/mL with calculated TSAT ≈44.8% (borderline just below 45%) — possible iron loading vs. acute phase reactant vs. metabolic/liver contribution.\\n   - Correlation: AST/ALT and hs‑CRP normal, making active inflammation or overt hepatic injury less likely. Ferritin elevated but <300 ng/mL; TSAT marginally below 45% threshold.\\n   - Differential: iron repletion/overload (hereditary hemochromatosis if TSAT increases), metabolic/inflammatory condition, alcohol use, NAFLD (LFTs currently normal).\\n\\n3) Vitamin D 25‑OH 44.1 ng/mL — adequate but below clinic target; repletion recommended to reach clinic target range.\\n   - No immediate critical issue.\\n\\n4) LDL cholesterol 104 mg/dL — near‑optimal; overall 10‑year ASCVD risk low, statin not indicated at present.\\n   - Address with lifestyle modification.\\n\\n5) Testosterone pattern: Total T 20.6 ng/dL, Free T 2 pg/mL, SHBG 73.9 nmol/L — consistent with adequate androgen status (Pattern D). Patient reports fatigue/low mood; labs do not support androgen deficiency as primary driver.\\n   - Differential: symptoms likely non‑androgenic—consider thyroid functional testing beyond TSH (free T4/free T3), sleep disturbance, mood disorder, iron dysregulation, vitamin D insufficiency, or other medical/behavioral causes.\\n\\n6) Other labs within normal limits and low cardiovascular risk profile — see Objective for full list (HDL high, triglycerides low, LFTs normal, renal function normal, HbA1c normal).\\n\\nPLAN:\\n(Overall: results reviewed with patient; plan to address borderline Hct, elevated ferritin, vitamin D, LDL; no testosterone therapy indicated at this time.)\\n\\nProblem 1 — Borderline elevated hematocrit 46.8%:\\n- Plan:\\n  - Counsel patient to increase oral hydration immediately and before repeat labs.\\n  - Order repeat CBC (Hgb/Hct/MCV/platelets) in 4–6 weeks to reassess when euvolemic.\\n  - Instruct patient to avoid heavy exercise and very diuretic states prior to repeat labs.\\n  - Provider notification triggers: notify provider if repeat Hct >52% or if patient develops symptoms of hyperviscosity (headache, visual disturbance, focal deficit, thrombotic symptoms).\\n\\nProblem 2 — Ferritin 204 ng/mL with calculated TSAT ≈44.8%:\\n- Plan:\\n  - TSAT calculated today at 44.8% from available serum iron (145 µg/dL) and TIBC (324 µg/dL). This is marginally below 45% threshold.\\n  - If lab system has not auto‑reported TSAT, document calculation in chart and flag for monitoring.\\n  - Repeat ferritin and CRP in 8–12 weeks to evaluate persistence (order ferritin, CRP). If TSAT remains ≤45% and ferritin decreases or stabilizes, continue monitoring.\\n  - If TSAT >45% on repeat or ferritin persistently >300 ng/mL → order HFE gene testing (C282Y, H63D) and consider hematology/hepatology referral for iron overload evaluation and possible phlebotomy.\\n  - Evaluate and document alcohol use, family history of hemochromatosis, hepatomegaly, and other metabolic risk factors in chart.\\n  - Consider hepatic ultrasound only if LFTs become abnormal or clinical suspicion for NAFLD/other hepatic disease increases.\\n\\nProblem 3 — Vitamin D 25‑OH 44.1 ng/mL (suboptimal per clinic target):\\n- Plan:\\n  - Start Metagenics D3 2000 Complex PO once daily (per clinic protocol). Counsel on taking with a meal for absorption.\\n  - Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D level in 12 weeks to document response to supplementation.\\n  - Discuss dietary sources and safe sun exposure as adjuncts.\\n\\nProblem 4 — LDL 104 mg/dL with low 10‑yr ASCVD risk:\\n- Plan:\\n  - Reinforce lifestyle interventions: Mediterranean‑style diet, reduce saturated fat, increase fiber, weight management as appropriate.\\n  - Exercise recommendation: at least 150 minutes per week of moderate aerobic activity + resistance training twice weekly.\\n  - Recheck fasting lipid panel in 6–12 months (sooner if clinical changes or new risk factors).\\n  - No statin therapy indicated at this time given low calculated 10‑yr ASCVD risk. Document shared decision‑making.\\n\\nProblem 5 — Androgen status / reported fatigue and low mood:\\n- Plan:\\n  - Do NOT initiate testosterone therapy: current total and free testosterone and SHBG indicate adequate androgen status.\\n  - Address likely non‑androgenic contributors to fatigue/low mood:\\n    - Confirm menstrual cycle details and that progesterone/estradiol were luteal-phase draws (P4 10.8 ng/mL and E2 111 pg/mL consistent with luteal phase).\\n    - Evaluate thyroid function further if symptoms persist: order free T4 and free T3 to complement normal TSH.\\n    - Screen for mood disorder / depression and anxiety; consider PHQ‑9/GAD‑7 screening and behavioral health referral if positive.\\n    - Assess sleep quality: although STOP‑BANG low, consider sleep study if high clinical suspicion for sleep apnea or nonrestorative sleep.\\n    - Consider assessment for chronic fatigue contributors (iron studies monitored as above, B12/folate are adequate).\\n  - If future consideration of testosterone therapy arises, obtain baseline LFTs, CBC, lipid panel and pregnancy test if applicable and obtain informed consent per clinic protocol.\\n\\nProblem 6 — General preventive care and supplements:\\n- Plan:\\n  - Recommend Magtein® Magnesium L‑Threonate (medium‑priority supplement per clinic): 3 capsules daily in divided doses (as recommended by clinic).\\n  - Review alcohol moderation and smoking cessation (if applicable); counsel that excess alcohol may raise ferritin and affect liver.\\n  - Reinforce sleep hygiene, stress reduction strategies; refer to CBT or behavioral health if warranted.\\n\\nOrders / Follow-up labs (with timing):\\n- Today / immediate:\\n  - Start Metagenics D3 2000 Complex PO daily.\\n  - Start Magtein® Magnesium L‑Threonate, 3 capsules daily divided doses.\\n  - Document TSAT calculation in chart (TSAT ≈44.8%). If clinic lab does not auto‑report TSAT, place stat note.\\n  - Patient education provided re: hydration, diet, exercise, and when to seek urgent care.\\n- Within 4–6 weeks:\\n  - Repeat CBC (Hgb/Hct/MCV/platelets) — patient to hydrate before sampling.\\n  - Notify provider if Hct >52% or patient becomes symptomatic.\\n- Within 8–12 weeks:\\n  - Repeat ferritin and CRP. Recalculate TSAT if repeat iron/TIBC ordered.\\n  - If ferritin remains >300 ng/mL or TSAT >45% → order HFE gene panel (C282Y/H63D) and consider referral to hematology/hepatology.\\n- At 12 weeks:\\n  - Recheck Vitamin D 25‑OH level to assess response to supplementation.\\n- 6–12 months:\\n  - Repeat fasting lipid panel (sooner if new risk factors develop).\\n- Additional as indicated:\\n  - Free T4 and free T3 if fatigue/low mood persist despite above workup.\\n  - PHQ‑9/GAD‑7 screening today or at next visit if not already completed.\\n  - Consider sleep medicine referral or polysomnography if clinically warranted.\\n\\nCommunication:\\n- Results reviewed and discussed with patient today (03/30/2026). Discussion covered borderline hematocrit and need for hydration and repeat CBC, elevated ferritin with calculated TSAT ≈44.8% and plan for repeat ferritin/CRP in 8–12 weeks, initiation of vitamin D supplementation, LDL lifestyle counseling, and decision not to initiate testosterone therapy based on current labs. Patient education provided regarding lifestyle measures and when to seek urgent care.\\n- Patient verbalized understanding and agreed with plan. Questions answered.\\n\\nProvider notification triggers (explicit):\\n- TSAT >45% or ferritin >300 ng/mL → order genetic testing (HFE) and consider hematology/hepatology referral.\\n- Hematocrit >52% or hyperviscosity/thrombotic symptoms → immediate provider notification.\\n- New abnormal LFTs or clinical signs of liver disease → notify provider.\\n\\nFollow‑up appointment:\\n- Return visit or lab review 4–6 weeks for repeat CBC; subsequent lab review at 8–12 weeks for ferritin/CRP and at 12 weeks for vitamin D result. Routine clinic follow up in 3–6 months or sooner as clinically indicated.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\nDate: 03/30/2026.", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "preventRisk": {"ldl