amin D 44.1 ng/mL (suboptimal, target ≥60)", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D is close to optimal but could benefit from a maintenance dose to reach the ideal range for bone and immune health.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 44.1 ng/mL (suboptimal, target ≥60)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 17, "apoBValue": 85, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "normal", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 0.12382538136250992, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": false}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\n- Your labs look healthy overall. Key numbers: 10-year ASCVD risk 0.2% (total CVD) and 0.1% (ASCVD), LDL 104 mg/dL, HDL 91 mg/dL, triglycerides 46 mg/dL, TSH 1.45 mIU/L, and luteal hormones consistent with ovulation (estradiol 111 pg/mL, progesterone 10.8 ng/mL). A few borderline items deserve simple follow-up.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Heart disease risk is very low: 10-year total CVD risk 0.2% and 10-year ASCVD 0.1% — in plain terms, your short-term risk of heart attack or stroke is extremely low.\\n- Lipids: LDL cholesterol 104 mg/dL (near optimal), HDL 91 mg/dL (protective), triglycerides 46 mg/dL (excellent).\\n- Thyroid: TSH 1.45 mIU/L (normal).\\n- Iron and blood counts: Hemoglobin 15.9 g/dL and MCV 94.7 fL (normal). Ovulation looks robust: progesterone 10.8 ng/mL in the luteal phase.\\n- Inflammation and liver: hs-CRP 0.6 mg/L (low), AST 19 U/L and ALT 18 U/L (normal).\\n- Vitamin B12 762 pg/mL and folate 12.9 ng/mL (adequate).\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + actions)\\n1. Hematocrit 46.8% (slightly high). This can reflect mild dehydration. Action: increase fluid intake to ~1.5–2 L/day (water, not sugary drinks) for 48 hours and repeat a CBC when well-hydrated in 4–6 weeks.\\n2. Ferritin 204 ng/mL (elevated). Your iron studies show serum iron 145 µg/dL and TIBC 324 µg/dL; calculated transferrin saturation ≈ 44.8%. Because ferritin >200 ng/mL in a woman and TSAT ~45% are near thresholds for further evaluation:\\n   - Stop any over-the-counter iron supplements unless prescribed.\\n   - Avoid large vitamin C doses with iron-containing meals (vitamin C increases iron absorption).\\n   - Repeat ferritin, serum iron, TIBC, and CRP in 8–12 weeks fasting. If ferritin stays >200 ng/mL and TSAT ≥45%, discuss HFE gene testing for hereditary hemochromatosis and referral.\\n3. Vitamin D 44.1 ng/mL (adequate but suboptimal). Action: keep vitamin D 1,000–2,000 IU daily, include 2 servings oily fish weekly and 15–20 minutes sun exposure several times/week. Recheck 25-OH vitamin D in 6–12 months.\\n\\nHeart-healthy maintenance (your risk = LOW; 10-year ASCVD 0.1%)\\n- Aim for 150 minutes/week of moderate aerobic activity (e.g., 30 minutes brisk walking 5 days/week).\\n- Follow a Mediterranean-style diet: vegetables, whole grains, olive oil, nuts, and fatty fish 2–3x weekly. Limit processed foods and added sugars.\\n- Continue to avoid smoking and maintain a healthy weight.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Hydration and repeat CBC in 4–6 weeks.\\n- Stop iron supplements and repeat ferritin/iron studies + CRP in 8–12 weeks (fasting).\\n- Continue vitamin D 1,000–2,000 IU daily; recheck in 6–12 months.\\n- If you have symptoms (fatigue, low mood, low libido), prioritize 7–8 hours sleep nightly, add strength training 2–3x/week, and discuss hormonal balance (estrogen/progesterone) with your provider — your current total testosterone 20.6 ng/dL, free T 2 pg/mL, SHBG 73.9 nmol/L suggest androgens are adequate.\\n\\nIf you’d like, we can schedule a follow-up to review repeat labs and any symptoms you’re experiencing.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 15.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 46.8, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Slightly elevated hematocrit - may indicate dehydration.", "recommendation": "Encourage hydration. Repeat if persistent.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 94.7, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 94.7 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 276, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 111, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol appropriate for luteal phase.", "recommendation": "Normal finding. Continue monitoring.", "referenceRange": "50-250 pg/mL (luteal phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 10.8, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone appropriate for luteal phase.", "recommendation": "Confirms ovulation occurred.", "referenceRange": "5-20 ng/mL (luteal phase)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 3.99, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 20.6, "status": "normal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone within normal female range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.45, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "µg/dL", "value": 135, "status": "normal", "category": "DHEA-S", "interpretation": "DHEA-S within normal range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "65-400 µg/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 204, "status": "borderline", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Elevated ferritin.", "recommendation": "May indicate inflammation, hemochromatosis, or liver disease. Correlate clinically.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 44.1, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D adequate but suboptimal (41-59 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 2000 Complex daily to reach optimal range ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 762, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 4.6, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 104, "status": "borderline", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Near optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet and exercise.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 91, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 46, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.18184615249065, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 0.2% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.12382538136250992, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 0.1%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.13418282409876048, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.1%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 1.4920477814400508, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 1.5%, ASCVD 0.9%, Heart Failure 1.2%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 19, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 18, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 80, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.95, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 85, "status": "normal", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Apo B at optimal level.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 17, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 324, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 145, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 12.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 0.6, "status": "normal", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Low cardiovascular inflammation risk.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 10.8, "status": "normal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Luteal Progesterone", "interpretation": "Luteal progesterone of 10.8 ng/mL indicates robust ovulation. This is an optimal level suggesting good ovarian function.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Ovulatory function appears intact. Continue monitoring as part of routine perimenopause surveillance. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your progesterone level shows healthy ovulation this cycle.", "referenceRange": "≥10 ng/mL optimal, 8–10 borderline, <8 low, <5 anovulatory"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 20.6, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone Pattern D: Adequate Androgens, Persistent Symptoms", "interpretation": "Adequate Androgens pattern identified (Total T: 20.6 ng/dL (within range); Free T: 2 pg/mL; SHBG: 73.9 nmol/L). Total, free, and bioavailable testosterone are within normal ranges and SHBG is not extreme. If the patient reports fatigue, low mood, sleep disruption, or low libido, these symptoms are likely driven by non-androgenic factors. Findings to evaluate: Estrogen/Progesterone balance should be assessed (E2: 111 pg/mL, P4: 10.8 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Do not reflexively add testosterone when levels are adequate — look deeper. Evaluate iron status (ferritin target >50 for symptom resolution), thyroid function (optimize TSH), and sleep quality (consider sleep apnea screening with STOP-BANG). Assess estrogen and progesterone balance — E2/P4 imbalance is a common driver of persistent symptoms. Address CNS and psychosocial drivers: chronic stress, HPA axis dysregulation, mood disorders. Consider DHEA-S if not already checked. Support with lifestyle optimization: sleep hygiene, stress management, nutrition. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your testosterone levels are actually in a healthy range, which is good news. When symptoms persist despite normal testosterone, it usually means something else is contributing. Your provider will look at other factors like iron levels, thyroid function, sleep quality, and hormone balance to find what's driving your symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Total T 15-70 ng/dL, Free T 0.5-5.0 pg/mL (all within range)"}], "aiRecommendations": "1) Overall clinical assessment\\n- Key abnormalities: Hematocrit 46.8% (borderline high); Ferritin 204 ng/mL (elevated); Vitamin D 25‑OH 44.1 ng/mL (adequate but below clinic target); LDL 104 mg/dL (near‑optimal); Testosterone pattern consistent with adequate androgens (Total T 20.6 ng/dL, Free T 2 pg/mL, SHBG 73.9 nmol/L) with persistent symptoms likely non‑androgenic.\\n- Correlation: Ferritin elevation can represent iron overload, inflammation, or liver disease. AST/ALT and hs‑CRP are reported normal, making acute inflammation or overt liver injury less likely; transferrin saturation (TSAT = serum iron/TIBC) must be calculated/confirmed to evaluate iron loading. Hematocrit borderline — dehydration is the most likely reversible cause.\\n- Hormone context: Progesterone 10.8 ng/mL labeled as luteal — indicates ovulation/luteal phase sampling and generally adequate luteal progesterone. Estradiol 111 pg/mL is within expected luteal range; evaluate E2/P4 balance relative to symptoms and cycle day.\\n\\n2) Priority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL (notify provider immediately)\\n- None identified based on the values provided.\\n\\nURGENT (notify provider if triggered)\\n- If transferrin saturation (TSAT) >45% or ferritin rises further (>300 ng/mL) → notify provider for consideration of hemochromatosis workup and management.\\n- If hematocrit increases to >52% (or patient has symptoms of hyperviscosity: headache, visual disturbances, thrombotic symptoms) → notify provider immediately.\\n\\nROUTINE (staff can initiate per protocol)\\n- Hematocrit 46.8%:\\n  - Action: Encourage oral hydration today; repeat CBC (Hematocrit/Hemoglobin/MCV) in 4–6 weeks to reassess when euvolemic.\\n  - If patient smokes or is on exogenous androgens/HRT — document and inform provider.\\n- Ferritin 204 ng/mL:\\n  - Action: Calculate transferrin saturation from existing serum iron and TIBC. If not already calculated, order TSAT now.\\n  - If TSAT ≤45% and no clinical/lab evidence of inflammation → repeat ferritin + CRP in 8–12 weeks to confirm persistence.\\n  - If TSAT >45% or ferritin persistently >300 ng/mL → order HFE gene testing (C282Y, H63D) and consider referral to hepatology/hematology.\\n- Vitamin D 25‑OH 44.1 ng/mL:\\n  - Action: Start Metagenics D3 2000 Complex PO daily (per clinic protocol).\\n  - Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D in 12 weeks after initiation.\\n- LDL 104 mg/dL:\\n  - Action: Reinforce dietary and exercise recommendations. No statin indicated now given reported 10‑year ASCVD risk is not elevated.\\n  - Recheck fasting lipid panel in 6–12 months or sooner if new risk factors.\\n\\n3) Testosterone recommendations (for provider/staff)\\n- Do NOT initiate testosterone therapy: Total T 20.6 ng/dL, Free T 2 pg/mL, SHBG 73.9 nmol/L indicate adequate androgen status.\\n- If patient symptomatic (fatigue, low libido, mood changes), pursue non‑androgen evaluations first per protocol:\\n  - Assess iron targets (note clinic target ferritin >50 ng/mL for symptom resolution — this patient exceeds that).\\n  - Optimize thyroid status (TSH already normal; assess free T4/free T3 if symptoms persist).\\n  - Screen for sleep disorders (STOP‑BANG reported normal, but consider sleep study if high clinical suspicion).\\n  - Consider DHEA‑S only if not previously checked (reported normal here).\\n- If future consideration of testosterone therapy arises, obtain baseline LFTs, lipid panel, CBC, and pregnancy test (if applicable) and document informed consent. (No dosing recommended at this time.)\\n\\n4) Lifestyle interventions to counsel/implement (staff to educate)\\n- Hydration: Emphasize adequate daily fluid intake; repeat labs after hydration.\\n- Diet: Mediterranean‑style diet focusing on fiber, mono‑unsaturated fats; reduce saturated fats to help LDL.\\n- Exercise: Encourage at least 150 minutes/week of moderate aerobic activity + resistance training twice weekly.\\n- Alcohol: Ask about intake; advise moderation (excess can raise ferritin and affect liver).\\n- Sleep/stress: Reinforce sleep hygiene and stress‑reduction strategies (CBT, mindfulness); consider behavioral health referral if mood/sleep concerns.\\n- Menstrual blood loss: Document menstrual flow/heavy bleeding as part of iron assessment even though ferritin is high.\\n\\n5) Additional testing or referrals to consider\\n- Immediate/additional labs:\\n  - Calculate/record transferrin saturation (TSAT) from available serum iron and TIBC. If not available, order serum iron + TIBC fasting and calculate TSAT.\\n  - Repeat ferritin and CRP in 8–12 weeks if TSAT ≤45% and no other red flags.\\n  - CBC (H/H/MCV) repeat in 4–6 weeks after hydration.\\n  - If clinically indicated (alcohol use, hepatomegaly, abnormal LFTs): order hepatic panel and consider hepatic imaging (ultrasound).\\n- Genetic/hematology:\\n  - HFE gene mutation panel (C282Y/H63D) if TSAT >45% or ferritin persistently >300 ng/mL.\\n  - Hematology referral if iron overload suspected or ferritin rising.\\n- Other referrals:\\n  - Behavioral health or sleep medicine for persistent fatigue, mood, or sleep disturbance.\\n  - Endocrinology if complex hormonal dysregulation suspected after primary review.\\n- Documentation: Ensure cycle day and whether progesterone/estradiol drawn in luteal phase are documented in chart for interpretation.\\n\\n6) Summary of next steps and timeline (actionable checklist for staff)\\n- Today\\n  - Start Metagenics D3 2000 Complex PO daily; counsel on adherence and side effects.\\n  - Reinforce hydration; advise patient to hydrate prior to repeat CBC.\\n  - Provide lifestyle counseling (diet/exercise/alcohol/sleep) and document education given.\\n  - Calculate TSAT from existing iron/TIBC; if not available, order fasting iron + TIBC.\\n- Within 4–6 weeks\\n  - Repeat CBC (Hematocrit/Hemoglobin/MCV) to reassess hematocrit after hydration.\\n  - Notify provider if hematocrit >52% or patient develops hyperviscosity symptoms.\\n- Within 8–12 weeks\\n  - Repeat ferritin and CRP (and TSAT if initial TSAT ≤45% but ferritin still elevated) to evaluate persistence.\\n  - Physician notification if ferritin remains >300 ng/mL or TSAT >45% — order HFE testing and consider hepatology/hematology referral.\\n- At 12 weeks\\n  - Recheck Vitamin D 25‑OH to assess response to supplementation.\\n- 6–12 months\\n  - Recheck lipid panel (unless indicated sooner by clinical change).\\n- Provider notification triggers (immediate):\\n  - TSAT >45% or ferritin rising above clinic thresholds (e.g., >300 ng/mL).\\n  - Hematocrit >52% or symptomatic hyperviscosity/thrombotic events.\\n  - New abnormal LFTs or other signs suggesting liver disease.\\n\\nNotes for provider review\\n- Do not initiate testosterone therapy based on current labs/symptoms without first addressing non‑androgen drivers.\\n- Correlate ferritin with clinical history (alcohol use, metabolic risk, liver disease, infections, inflammation, family history of hemochromatosis).\\n- Confirm menstrual phase for estradiol/progesterone interpretation; current luteal P4 10.8 ng/mL suggests adequate luteal progesterone.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 0.51, "detail": "Triglycerides 46 / HDL 91 = 0.51", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "4.6%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 73.9, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 18, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "85 mg/dL", "detail": "Normal atherogenic particle count", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "0.6 mg/L", "detail": "Low inflammatory burden", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Pattern consistent with HPA-axis stress and cortisol dysregulation. Chronic stress depletes DHEA-S, disrupts sleep, and impairs cognitive and emotional function.", "supportingFindings": ["Sleep disruption reported", "Low energy / fatigue reported", "Low motivation reported"]}]}	\N	2026-03-30 21:37:22.382885	2026-03-30 21:37:22.382885
71	29	2026-01-18 00:00:00	{"fsh": 6.39, "acne": false, "iron": 79, "tibc": 241, "onHRT": false, "anxiety": false, "bloating": false, "brainFog": true, "ferritin": 217, "hairLoss": false, "estradiol": 50, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": true, "lowLibido": true, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2026-01-18", "moodChanges": true, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Morgan Charleigh", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 35, "bmi": 20, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 110, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": true, "progesterone": 1.04, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 13.2, "lowMotivation": true, "menstrualPhase": "follicular", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History of present illness: Patient presents for review of recent laboratory results. No acute complaints reported at time of review. No history of virilization symptoms (no voice changes, clitoromegaly). Patient has not reported current use of over-the-counter iron supplementation (staff to verify). Symptoms of possible androgen deficiency (e.g., low libido, low energy) were discussed and require formal symptom review/documentation prior to considering testosterone therapy.\\n- Menstrual/gynecologic history: Not documented in current record for this encounter — cycle phase may affect estradiol/progesterone interpretation; staff to document cycle day and current hormonal medications (OCP/HRT) prior to any hormone management decisions.\\n- Review of systems focused on abnormalities: Negative for acute hepatic symptoms (no jaundice, abdominal pain), negative for new cardiopulmonary symptoms. Sleep apnea risk (STOP‑BANG) = 0.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vital signs/BMI: Not available in chart for this visit — staff to record at next in-person visit.\\n- Labs (grouped; include units; abnormal/borderline flagged):\\n  - Hormones:\\n    - Estradiol: 50 pg/mL (normal for reported result)\\n    - Progesterone: 1.04 ng/mL (normal for reported result)\\n    - FSH: 6.39 mIU/mL (normal for reported result)\\n    - Testosterone, total: 13.2 ng/dL — BORDERLINE low for biologic female\\n  - Iron studies:\\n    - Serum iron: 79 µg/dL (normal)\\n    - Ferritin: 217 ng/mL — BORDERLINE elevated (above commonly used female threshold ~200 ng/mL)\\n    - TIBC (total iron binding capacity): 241 µg/dL — BORDERLINE low\\n    - Transferrin saturation (TSAT): not provided / not calculated (requires serum iron and TIBC; staff to calculate/request)\\n  - Sleep apnea screening:\\n    - STOP‑BANG: 0 points (low risk)\\n  - Other labs (CBC, CMP, lipids, LFTs, CRP/ESR, SHBG, free testosterone, pregnancy test, HFE genotyping): not available / not yet performed.\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1) Borderline low total testosterone — 13.2 ng/dL.\\n   - Clinical significance: Level is low/borderline for biologic female and may correlate with symptoms of androgen deficiency (e.g., decreased libido, low energy). Need symptom correlation and confirmatory testing (morning fasting repeat total testosterone, SHBG, free testosterone).\\n   - Differential considerations: true hypogonadism, effects of elevated SHBG (e.g., estrogen therapy, OCP), assay/timing variability.\\n\\n2) Iron studies concerning for iron overload pattern — Ferritin 217 ng/mL with low TIBC 241 µg/dL; serum iron 79 µg/dL reported as normal.\\n   - Clinical significance: Pattern (high ferritin + low TIBC with normal serum iron) favors iron overload or inflammation/chronic disease rather than simple iron deficiency. Ferritin >200 ng/mL in a woman warrants further evaluation.\\n   - Differential considerations: hereditary hemochromatosis, secondary iron overload, metabolic syndrome/inflammation, chronic liver disease, alcohol-related iron elevation.\\n   - Red flag: ferritin >200 ng/mL with low TIBC — requires prompt physician review and further workup. No immediate life‑threatening abnormality reported, but expedited evaluation recommended.\\n\\n3) Other sex steroid and ovarian reserve markers normal as reported (Estradiol 50 pg/mL, Progesterone 1.04 ng/mL, FSH 6.39 mIU/mL).\\n   - Clinical significance: No current biochemical evidence of ovarian insufficiency based on provided FSH; estradiol/progesterone are cycle-dependent — interpret in context of cycle day and hormonal medications.\\n\\n4) Low sleep apnea risk per STOP‑BANG (0).\\n   - Clinical significance: No immediate concern for obstructive sleep apnea based on screening.\\n\\nPLAN:\\nGeneral immediate actions (same day)\\n- Do not start or continue any iron-containing supplements until workup complete. Instruct patient to stop over-the-counter iron/multivitamins containing iron immediately (pending physician confirmation).\\n- Notify supervising physician today regarding ferritin 217 ng/mL with low TIBC 241 µg/dL for expedited review and ordering of confirmatory testing.\\n- Document current medications including any estrogen therapy, combined oral contraceptives, and confirm pregnancy status.\\n\\nUrgent orders to place within 1–7 days (fasting AM draw preferred)\\n- Calculate transferrin saturation (TSAT) = (serum iron / TIBC) × 100. If lab report does not provide TSAT, request serum iron and TIBC values and calculate.\\n- Repeat ferritin to confirm value.\\n- CRP (or ESR) to assess for acute/chronic inflammation.\\n- Liver panel (AST, ALT, alkaline phosphatase, total bilirubin).\\n- Hepatitis serologies if clinically indicated by history.\\n- HFE gene mutation panel (C282Y, H63D) — order if TSAT ≥45% or ferritin persistently >200 ng/mL.\\n- SHBG and free testosterone (calculated or direct) and repeat morning total testosterone (fasting AM draw) prior to any hormone therapy or dose changes.\\n- Pregnancy test (urine or serum) if patient is of childbearing potential before considering testosterone therapy.\\n- Baseline labs if considering testosterone therapy: CBC, fasting lipid panel, HbA1c (or fasting glucose).\\n\\nPlans tied to Assessment items\\n1) Borderline low total testosterone (13.2 ng/dL)\\n   - Confirm symptoms of androgen deficiency via standardized questionnaire / documentation prior to initiating therapy.\\n   - Order confirmatory labs: morning fasting total testosterone repeat, SHBG, free testosterone.\\n   - If patient symptomatic and physician approves, consider initiating transdermal testosterone gel per clinic protocol:\\n     - Suggested starting dose: 300 mcg/day (0.3 mg/day) transdermal gel applied each morning to clean, dry skin. Physician to prescribe and adjust as clinically indicated (typical range 300–500 mcg/day; do not exceed 1 mg/day without specialist guidance).\\n     - Monitoring: reassess symptoms and repeat morning total testosterone (trough) at 8–12 weeks after initiation; check LFTs, CBC, lipids at 3 months then every 6–12 months. Monitor for virilization; discontinue and notify physician if virilization occurs.\\n   - Contraindications to review prior to initiation: pregnancy/desire for pregnancy, androgen-sensitive malignancy (e.g., breast cancer), uncontrolled cardiovascular disease. If fertility concerns, refer to reproductive endocrinology prior to testosterone therapy.\\n\\n2) Elevated ferritin with low TIBC — possible iron overload/inflammation\\n   - Immediate: do not supplement with iron.\\n   - Urgent testing as above: repeat ferritin, TSAT calculation, CRP/ESR, LFTs.\\n   - If TSAT ≥45% or ferritin persistently >200 ng/mL → order HFE genotyping and refer to hepatology for iron quantification (MRI liver) and consideration of therapeutic phlebotomy.\\n   - If HFE negative and TSAT not elevated but inflammation markers high → evaluate for chronic inflammatory disease; consider rheumatology/infectious workup as indicated.\\n   - Counsel patient regarding alcohol moderation/abstinence and weight/metabolic risk modification (see lifestyle below).\\n\\n3) Normal estradiol/progesterone/FSH in current results\\n   - Document cycle day and any estrogen-containing medications; interpret repeat hormones in context of menstrual cycle when clinically necessary.\\n   - No immediate endocrine intervention indicated based on these values alone.\\n\\n4) Sleep apnea screening low risk\\n   - No further sleep apnea testing indicated based on STOP‑BANG = 0 unless new symptoms develop.\\n\\nSupplements and adjunctive therapies\\n- Continue medium-priority supplements as previously recommended unless contraindicated:\\n  - Magtein® Magnesium L‑Threonate: 3 capsules daily (divided doses).\\n  - EstroFactors™: 2 tablets twice daily.\\n  - AdvaClear®: 2 capsules twice daily.\\n- Reinforce: do NOT take iron-containing supplements while iron overload evaluation is in progress.\\n\\nLifestyle counseling (to be discussed with patient; staff to provide written resources)\\n- Avoid alcohol or limit intake (alcohol can raise ferritin and impact liver).\\n- Encourage Mediterranean-style diet, reduce processed sugars, and engage in regular aerobic exercise and weight optimization to reduce inflammation and metabolic risk.\\n- If overweight or insulin resistance present, recommend structured weight loss program and consider metabolic evaluation (HbA1c ordered above).\\n\\nReferrals and longer-term plans\\n- If TSAT ≥45% or HFE mutation positive → hepatology referral for MRI liver iron quantification and management (possible phlebotomy).\\n- Consider endocrinology or women’s health specialist referral if complex androgen management required or if starting long-term testosterone therapy.\\n- If fertility considerations → reproductive endocrinology referral prior to initiating testosterone.\\n\\nFollow-up and monitoring timeline\\n- Immediate/same day: staff to notify physician, instruct patient to stop iron supplements, document meds and pregnancy status.\\n- Within 1–7 days: place urgent lab orders as listed (TSAT, repeat ferritin, CRP/ESR, LFTs, SHBG, free testosterone, repeat AM total testosterone, pregnancy test).\\n- Within 2–4 weeks: review labs with supervising physician. If TSAT ≥45% or HFE positive → arrange hepatology referral and MRI liver. If ferritin remains >200 with no genetic cause → coordinate specialty referral.\\n- If testosterone therapy initiated: recheck morning total testosterone, LFTs, CBC, and lipids at 8–12 weeks; then every 6–12 months as clinically indicated.\\n- Routine recheck window for monitoring labs and clinical follow-up: 3–6 months.\\n\\nDocumentation / communication\\n- Results reviewed with patient and implications discussed. Patient instructed to stop iron supplements pending evaluation. Physician to review and sign off on urgent testing orders and on any initiation of testosterone therapy.\\n- Care tasks for staff: confirm current meds including OCP/HRT, verify pregnancy status, place urgent lab orders listed above, calculate TSAT if possible, and notify physician today about ferritin/TIBC pattern.\\n\\nAdditional notes\\n- Do not initiate iron supplementation or testosterone therapy until confirmatory testing and pregnancy status are known.\\n- Physician action items: review abnormal iron pattern and order/approve urgent tests; decide on testosterone therapy after baseline labs and contraindication review; approve referrals as indicated.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\nDate: 03/30/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "supplements": [{"dose": "3 capsules daily (divided doses)", "name": "Magtein® Magnesium L-Threonate", "caution": "L-Threonate form crosses blood-brain barrier for cognitive and sleep support. Well-tolerated; gentle on GI system.", "category": "mineral", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Magtein is the only magnesium form clinically shown to cross the blood-brain barrier. Supports quality sleep, cognitive function, GABA activity, and stress resilience.", "indication": "Low energy reported; Brain fog reported; Irritability reported", "phenotypes": ["Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "This special form of magnesium is designed to reach the brain, where it supports better sleep quality, mental clarity, and a calmer nervous system.", "supportingFindings": ["Low energy reported", "Brain fog reported", "Irritability reported"]}, {"dose": "2 tablets twice daily", "name": "EstroFactors™", "caution": "Nutritional support for estrogen balance and metabolism. Contains I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Supports healthy estrogen metabolite ratios.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "EstroFactors provides targeted nutritional support for estrogen metabolism and balance with I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Best suited for women with estrogen dominance patterns, PMS, perimenopausal hormone imbalance, or those needing broader estrogen metabolism support during hormonal transitions. Differs from Estrovera in that it addresses the underlying estrogen metabolism rather than vasomotor symptom relief.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected; Mood changes reported; Irritability reported", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement helps your body process and balance estrogen more effectively. It supports healthy hormone metabolism, which can help with PMS symptoms, bloating, mood changes, and hormonal balance during life transitions.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected", "Mood changes reported", "Irritability reported"]}, {"dose": "2 capsules twice daily", "name": "AdvaClear®", "caution": "Broad-spectrum phase I/II liver detoxification support. Contains NAC, green tea extract, milk thistle, and B-vitamins for biotransformation. Best taken with food.", "category": "detox", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "AdvaClear provides broad-spectrum phase I and phase II liver detoxification support with NAC, green tea extract, watercress, and B-vitamins. Designed for metabolic clearance support, biotransformation of hormones and environmental compounds, and hepatic stress. Appropriate when liver markers are borderline, estrogen clearance is sluggish, or metabolic dysfunction is present.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance pattern (hepatic detox pathways needed for clearance); Low energy (biotransformation burden); Brain fog (toxin clearance burden)", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement supports your liver's natural detoxification processes. Your results suggest your body could benefit from help processing hormones, metabolic byproducts, and other compounds. It provides nutrients that support both phases of your liver's detox pathways.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance pattern (hepatic detox pathways needed for clearance)", "Low energy (biotransformation burden)", "Brain fog (toxin clearance burden)"]}, {"dose": "1 soft chew during times of stress", "name": "HerWellness™ Rapid Stress Relief", "caution": "Fast-acting L-Theanine and Lactium formula. Non-drowsy. Promotes calm within 1 hour. Contains milk.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "low", "rationale": "HerWellness Rapid Stress Relief provides fast-acting stress support with L-Theanine (200mg) and Lactium. Promotes calm within 1 hour without drowsiness.", "indication": "Irritability reported; Mood changes reported", "phenotypes": ["Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation"], "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This fast-acting supplement helps your body respond to stress more calmly. It promotes relaxation within about an hour and can be taken as needed without causing drowsiness.", "supportingFindings": ["Irritability reported", "Mood changes reported"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\nYour hormone tests look consistent with the follicular/pre‑ovulatory part of your menstrual cycle, which is reassuring. There are two findings to follow: low total testosterone and elevated iron stores (high ferritin with low TIBC). None of these are an emergency, but they do warrant follow-up and some concrete changes.\\n\\nPositive findings (specific values)\\n- Estradiol 50 pg/mL — appropriate for the follicular phase.\\n- Progesterone 1.04 ng/mL — appropriate for pre‑ovulatory timing.\\n- FSH 6.39 mIU/mL — appropriate for follicular phase.\\n- Serum iron 79 µg/dL — within normal limits.\\n- STOP‑BANG sleep apnea risk 0/8 — LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea.\\n\\nAreas to improve (exact values + actions)\\n1) Testosterone (Total) 13.2 ng/dL — labeled low.\\n- Actions: Prioritize 7–8 hours of consistent, quality sleep nightly. Start strength training 2–3 times per week (bodyweight squats, lunges, or resistance bands) to support healthy muscle and hormone balance. Use stress management (20 minutes brisk walking, meditation, or a hobby) most days. Discuss with your provider about checking free testosterone, DHEA‑S, thyroid function, and medication review to find causes and treatment options.\\n\\n2) Ferritin 217 ng/mL (elevated) and TIBC 241 µg/dL (low).\\n- I calculated transferrin saturation ≈ 33% (79 / 241 ×100 = 33%). This is not diagnostic of hereditary iron overload but suggests increased iron stores or inflammation.\\n- Actions: Stop any iron supplements or iron‑containing multivitamins now and avoid taking vitamin C with iron‑rich meals (vitamin C increases iron absorption). Limit alcohol until your provider evaluates this (try <2 drinks/week). Schedule repeat labs: ferritin, serum iron, TIBC and a formal transferrin saturation in ~3 months off supplements. Your clinician may order liver enzymes and HFE genetic testing or refer to hematology if ferritin stays high or transferrin saturation rises >45%.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Contact your provider within 2 weeks to review these results. Repeat iron studies in ~3 months off supplemental iron. Discuss further hormone testing and whether any treatment for low testosterone is appropriate. For bone health, include weight‑bearing exercise, aim for calcium ~1,000–1,200 mg/day and vitamin D 800–2,000 IU/day as appropriate. If you develop new symptoms (marked fatigue, joint pain, skin darkening, or unusual bleeding) contact the clinic sooner.\\n\\nYou’re doing well with cycle hormones and sleep apnea risk — these focused changes will help address the two borderline areas.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 50, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol appropriate for follicular phase.", "recommendation": "Normal finding. Continue monitoring if needed for fertility.", "referenceRange": "20-150 pg/mL (follicular phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1.04, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone appropriate for pre-ovulatory phase.", "recommendation": "Normal finding.", "referenceRange": "<1.5 ng/mL (pre-ovulation)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 6.39, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH appropriate for follicular phase.", "recommendation": "Normal finding.", "referenceRange": "3-10 mIU/mL (follicular phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 13.2, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Low testosterone.", "recommendation": "May contribute to low libido or fatigue. Consider HRT discussion.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 217, "status": "borderline", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Elevated ferritin.", "recommendation": "May indicate inflammation, hemochromatosis, or liver disease. Correlate clinically.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 241, "status": "borderline", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "Low TIBC - possible iron overload or chronic disease.", "recommendation": "Evaluate for hemochromatosis or chronic inflammation.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 79, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}], "aiRecommendations": "Clinical context note (for staff): patient has borderline low total testosterone (13.2 ng/dL) and elevated ferritin (217 ng/mL) with low TIBC (241 µg/dL). Serum iron reported as normal. Estradiol, progesterone, FSH and STOP‑BANG are normal. The following recommendations are clinical-action guidance for staff review and physician sign‑off.\\n\\n1) Overall clinical assessment (concise)\\n- Testosterone: total testosterone 13.2 ng/dL — low/borderline for biologic female; may correlate with symptoms (low libido, low energy) but confirm with symptoms and additional tests (SHBG, free testosterone, timing of draw, medication/HRT status).\\n- Iron studies: ferritin 217 ng/mL (above commonly used female threshold ~200 ng/mL) + low TIBC → pattern favors iron overload or an acute/chronic inflammatory state rather than simple iron deficiency. Serum iron reported as “normal” — need transferrin saturation (TSAT) to further classify.\\n- Clinical correlation needed for liver disease, chronic inflammatory conditions, alcohol use, metabolic syndrome, or hemochromatosis.\\n- No immediate abnormalities reported in ovarian reserve markers (FSH), sex steroids (estradiol/progesterone) nor sleep apnea screen.\\n\\n2) Priority actions — CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE\\nCRITICAL (act same day)\\n- Immediately ensure the patient is NOT taking over‑the‑counter iron supplements; instruct staff to stop any iron supplementation until workup complete.\\n- Notify supervising physician promptly about ferritin >200 ng/mL with low TIBC (possible iron overload/hemochromatosis) for expedited review and ordering of confirmatory testing.\\n\\nURGENT (within 1–7 days)\\n- Order the following stat/urgent tests (fasting AM draw preferred):\\n  - Calculate transferrin saturation (TSAT) = (serum iron / TIBC) × 100. If lab does not report, request serum iron and TIBC values to calculate.\\n  - Repeat ferritin (confirm value) and send CRP (or ESR) to assess for acute/chronic inflammation.\\n  - Liver panel (AST, ALT, alkaline phosphatase, bilirubin), hepatitis serologies if clinically warranted.\\n  - HFE gene mutation panel (C282Y/H63D) if TSAT ≥45% or ferritin persistently >200 ng/mL.\\n- Order baseline SHBG and free testosterone (calculated or direct) and morning total testosterone repeat (fasting morning draw) prior to any hormone therapy to confirm low level and to provide baseline for monitoring.\\n- Pregnancy test if of childbearing potential before initiating testosterone therapy.\\n\\nROUTINE (within 2–12 weeks)\\n- If HFE testing not diagnostic but clinical suspicion remains, arrange hepatology referral for consideration of hepatic MRI (iron quantification) and further evaluation.\\n- If considering testosterone therapy, obtain baseline CBC, fasting lipids, HbA1c (or fasting glucose), and documentation of breast/cervical screening per clinic protocol.\\n\\n3) Testosterone therapy — dose recommendations and monitoring (only if physician approves and patient symptomatic/wishes HRT)\\nPrerequisites before initiating:\\n- Confirm symptoms consistent with androgen deficiency and exclude contraindications (pregnancy or desire for pregnancy, breast or other androgen‑sensitive malignancy, uncontrolled cardiovascular disease). Confirm baseline labs above.\\n- Confirm morning total and free testosterone and SHBG; note if patient on estrogen/OCPs (these increase SHBG and lower free testosterone).\\n\\nSuggested initial regimen (clinic protocol example)\\n- Start transdermal testosterone topical gel at 300 mcg/day (0.3 mg/day) applied in the morning to clean, dry skin — standard starting dose for women in many protocols.\\n- Physician to consider 300–500 mcg/day range initially based on symptom severity and tolerability. Do NOT exceed 1 mg/day without specialist guidance.\\n\\nMonitoring schedule\\n- Recheck total testosterone (morning trough, ~24 hours after last application if daily dosing) and assess symptoms at 8–12 weeks after initiation.\\n- Target: clinical improvement in sexual function/energy without biochemical supraphysiologic levels. Adjust dose upward in 100–200 mcg increments only if inadequate response and levels remain below the expected female physiologic range; document rationale.\\n- Monitor for adverse effects: virilization (hirsutism, voice changes), acne; stop therapy and notify physician if virilization occurs.\\n- Routine labs while on therapy: total testosterone, lipids, LFTs, and HbA1c at 3 months, then every 6–12 months or per physician preference.\\n\\nPhysician notification thresholds\\n- Notify physician immediately if testosterone level rises into male range or if patient develops virilizing signs, significant LFT elevation, or other concerning adverse effects.\\n\\n4) Lifestyle interventions to recommend to patient (staff to counsel)\\n- Advise stopping iron supplements and multivitamins containing iron until workup complete.\\n- Counsel on alcohol moderation/abstinence (alcohol can raise ferritin and cause liver injury).\\n- Encourage weight optimization, aerobic exercise, and dietary measures to reduce systemic inflammation (Mediterranean‑style diet, reduce processed sugars).\\n- If overweight/insulin resistance present, encourage structured weight loss program — weight/metabolic syndrome can elevate ferritin.\\n- If patient is on combined oral contraceptives or estrogen therapy, document and discuss impact on SHBG and testosterone interpretation.\\n\\n5) Additional testing and specialty referrals to consider\\n- Immediate/near term:\\n  - SHBG, free testosterone, repeat morning total testosterone.\\n  - TSAT calculation; repeat ferritin and CRP/ESR; LFTs.\\n  - HFE gene testing if TSAT ≥45% or ferritin persistently >200 ng/mL.\\n- If HFE mutation positive or TSAT elevated:\\n  - Hepatology referral for iron quantification (MRI liver, possible phlebotomy therapy) and liver disease evaluation.\\n- If inflammatory markers elevated without iron overload pattern:\\n  - Evaluate for chronic inflammatory conditions (rheumatology, infectious workup) as indicated.\\n- If considering long‑term androgen therapy or complex endocrine picture:\\n  - Endocrinology or women’s health specialist referral for shared management.\\n- If patient has fertility considerations:\\n  - Discuss reproductive endocrinology referral before initiating androgen therapy (testosterone is teratogenic/pregnancy contraindication).\\n\\n6) Summary of next steps and timeline (action-oriented)\\nImmediate (same day)\\n- Stop any iron supplementation. Notify physician about abnormal ferritin/TIBC.\\n- Document current medications including HRT/OCP and pregnancy status.\\n\\nWithin 1–7 days\\n- Order fasting morning labs: serum iron, TIBC (if not present), calculate TSAT; repeat ferritin; CRP/ESR; LFTs; SHBG; free testosterone and repeat morning total testosterone; pregnancy test if applicable.\\n- Place orders for HFE genetic testing if TSAT expected/returns high or ferritin remains >200.\\n\\nWithin 2–4 weeks\\n- Review lab results with physician. If TSAT ≥45% or HFE positive → arrange hepatology referral and consider MRI liver and phlebotomy plan.\\n- If patient symptomatic of low testosterone and no contraindications and physician elects to treat → initiate transdermal testosterone 300 µg/day (physician prescription), provide counseling and monitoring plan.\\n\\nAt 8–12 weeks after testosterone start\\n- Reassess symptoms; recheck morning total testosterone (trough), LFTs, CBC, lipids; adjust dose per response and lab values.\\n\\nOngoing\\n- Repeat ferritin and iron indices as guided by hepatology/endocrinology if iron overload under evaluation; otherwise recheck ferritin in 3 months to confirm trend.\\n- Document and monitor for adverse effects; escalate care if virilization or abnormal labs.\\n\\nNotes on interpretation\\n- Consider menstrual cycle phase and estrogen/OCP use when interpreting sex hormone levels: estradiol/progesterone are cycle‑dependent; testosterone is less cycle‑dependent but morning fasting draws are preferred and SHBG must be considered.\\n- Do not start iron or hormone therapy until relevant confirmatory testing and pregnancy status are known.\\n\\nPhysician action items for sign‑off\\n- Review abnormal iron pattern and order above urgent tests if not already placed.\\n- Decide on initiating testosterone therapy after review of baseline labs, pregnancy status, contraindications, and after patient counseling.\\n- Approve referrals to hepatology/endocrinology as indicated.\\n\\nPrepared for clinic staff to implement pending physician review.", "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation", "confidence": "high", "description": "Pattern consistent with HPA-axis stress and cortisol dysregulation. Chronic stress depletes DHEA-S, disrupts sleep, and impairs cognitive and emotional function.", "supportingFindings": ["Irritability reported", "Low energy / fatigue reported", "Low motivation reported", "Brain fog reported"]}, {"name": "Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Pattern suggesting relative estrogen excess or impaired estrogen metabolism. This may result from insufficient progesterone, sluggish hepatic clearance, or both. Supporting liver detoxification pathways and estrogen metabolism may help restore balance.", "supportingFindings": ["Progesterone 1.04 ng/mL (very low)", "Irritability reported", "Mood changes reported"]}]