"Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": true}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "strongly_indicated", "indicated": true, "rationale": "LDL-C ≥190 mg/dL (204 mg/dL). High-intensity statin therapy strongly indicated per ACC/AHA guidelines. Consider familial hypercholesterolemia evaluation."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "consider", "rationale": "Lipid levels meet threshold for CAC consideration. CAC may be useful if risk level is uncertain or patient is hesitant about statin.", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall summary in plain language:\\nYou have some important findings to address and many healthy results. Your LDL (“bad”) cholesterol is high at 204 mg/dL, and your vitamin D is low at 23.1 ng/mL. Your total testosterone is elevated at 82.9 ng/dL with free testosterone 8 pg/mL, and your progesterone is low at 1 ng/mL while estradiol is 119 pg/mL — this pattern is consistent with luteal progesterone deficiency and perimenopause. Your 10‑year cardiovascular risk is low (Total CVD 1.3%, ASCVD 0.9%), but the very high LDL means we should act now.\\n\\nPositive findings (what’s good right now):\\n- Hemoglobin 15.1 g/dL, ferritin 97.7 ng/mL, MCV 94 fL — iron stores and red blood cells look healthy.\\n- TSH 0.96 mIU/L — thyroid function is in the normal range.\\n- Hemoglobin A1c 5.1% and triglycerides 92 mg/dL — blood sugar and triglycerides are normal.\\n- hs-CRP 0.4 mg/L — low inflammation for heart disease risk.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + actions):\\n1. LDL cholesterol 204 mg/dL (very high). Your cardiology note recommends a high‑intensity statin (Atorvastatin 40–80 mg or Rosuvastatin 20–40 mg) because LDL ≥190 mg/dL. Also start these lifestyle steps now:\\n   - Add 25–30 g fiber daily: oatmeal for breakfast, beans or lentils with lunch, plenty of vegetables at dinner.\\n   - Eat fatty fish 2–3 times weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n   - Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter, fatty cheeses.\\n   - Daily exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking (or equivalent) most days.\\n   Please discuss statin options with your clinician as soon as possible.\\n\\n2. Vitamin D 23.1 ng/mL (suboptimal for bone/hormone health). Actions:\\n   - Aim for brief sun exposure when safe (10–20 minutes several times/week).\\n   - Consider vitamin D3 supplementation (discuss dose with your clinician; common starting doses are 1,000–2,000 IU/day or a higher short course if advised).\\n   - Add weight‑bearing exercise (walking, squats) and calcium‑rich foods to protect bone health.\\n   Recheck 25‑OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks after starting supplements.\\n\\n3. Reproductive hormones: Progesterone 1 ng/mL with estradiol 119 pg/mL and FSH 10.5 mIU/mL — suggests luteal progesterone deficiency (perimenopause). Elevated testosterone (total 82.9 ng/dL; free 8 pg/mL) may cause acne, hair thinning, or excess hair. Actions:\\n   - Prioritize 7–8 hours sleep, add strength training 2–3×/week, and use stress‑management (walking, meditation).\\n   - Discuss with your gynecologist/endocrinologist about hormone management (bioidentical progesterone, cyclic progesterone, or other options) and testing timed to your cycle.\\n\\nOther notes:\\n- Hematocrit 46.9% slightly high — may reflect dehydration. Increase fluids and recheck in 1–2 weeks.\\n- HDL 51 mg/dL could be higher — exercise and healthy fats help. ApoB 121 mg/dL is borderline.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline:\\n1. Schedule a physician visit urgently to review LDL 204 mg/dL and discuss starting a high‑intensity statin.\\n2. Start the cholesterol lifestyle changes immediately and plan a lipid recheck 4–12 weeks after any medication start (or 3 months after lifestyle changes).\\n3. Begin vitamin D measures and recheck in 8–12 weeks.\\n4. Book follow‑up with your gynecologist/endocrinologist to address perimenopausal symptoms and elevated testosterone; they may repeat hormones timed to your cycle and discuss treatment.\\n5. Recheck hematocrit after hydrating in 1–2 weeks.\\n\\nYou’re doing many things well; these targeted steps can improve symptoms and long‑term health. Please review these results with your clinician before making medication changes.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 15.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 46.9, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Slightly elevated hematocrit - may indicate dehydration.", "recommendation": "Encourage hydration. Repeat if persistent.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 94, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 94 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 249, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 119, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone level recorded.", "recommendation": "For 