f new menopausal symptoms at this time.\\n- Allergies: none documented in chart.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\nVitals: None recorded at time of lab review in chart today.\\n\\nLaboratory results (03/30/2026)\\n- Complete Blood Count (CBC)\\n  - Hemoglobin (Hgb): 14.7 g/dL — within normal limits\\n  - Hematocrit (Hct): 45.2 % — BORDERLINE (mildly elevated for female)\\n  - Mean corpuscular volume (MCV): 93.4 fL — within normal limits\\n  - Platelets: 285 K/uL — within normal limits\\n\\n- Basic Metabolic / Renal / Hepatic\\n  - Creatinine: 0.64 mg/dL — within normal limits\\n  - eGFR: 113 mL/min/1.73m2 — within normal limits\\n  - AST: 17 U/L — within normal limits\\n  - ALT: 14 U/L — within normal limits\\n\\n- Lipid panel / lipoproteins\\n  - HDL cholesterol: 49 mg/dL — ABNORMAL (low; female threshold <50 mg/dL)\\n  - LDL cholesterol: 102 mg/dL — BORDERLINE (near-optimal)\\n  - Triglycerides: 70 mg/dL — within normal limits\\n  - Apolipoprotein B (ApoB): 87 mg/dL — within normal limits\\n\\n- Endocrine / Vitamins\\n  - TSH: 1.02 mIU/L — within normal limits\\n  - Vitamin D (25-OH): 66.4 ng/mL — within normal limits / sufficient\\n  - Vitamin B12: 592 pg/mL — within normal limits\\n\\n- Risk scores / other\\n  - 10-year Total CVD Risk (AHA PREVENT 2023): 0.81% — low\\n  - 10-year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT): 0.53% — low\\n  - 10-year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT): 0.32% — low\\n  - 30-year CVD Risk (PREVENT): 6.5%\\n  - Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG): 0 points\\n  - Statin recommendation per PREVENT: Lifestyle modifications recommended; statin generally not indicated at current risk level.\\n\\n- Red flags: None identified on current labs.\\n\\n- Supplements recommended by clinic: NutraGems® CoQ10 300, 1 chewable gel daily (medium priority).\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Low HDL cholesterol — 49 mg/dL (female threshold <50 mg/dL). Modestly increased relative cardiovascular risk marker despite low calculated 10-year risk. Lifestyle-modifiable contributor suspected. No indication for pharmacologic HDL-raising therapy at this time.\\n   - Differential considerations: genetic lipid pattern, diet, physical inactivity, smoking (patient reports non-smoker), medications.\\n\\n2. Borderline elevated hematocrit — 45.2% (female). Hemoglobin and MCV normal. Mild hemoconcentration/relative dehydration is most likely; therapy-related erythrocytosis (e.g., androgen/testosterone) unlikely based on patient report of no current systemic testosterone use, but must be confirmed and trended.\\n   - Differential considerations: preanalytical hemoconcentration (dehydration), smoking, exogenous androgens, undiagnosed hypoxia, primary/secondary polycythemia (less likely given single mildly elevated value).\\n\\n3. Borderline LDL cholesterol — 102 mg/dL. Near-optimal; with low absolute 10-year ASCVD risk, statin therapy not indicated at this time. Focus on lifestyle measures to optimize LDL/HDL and overall risk reduction.\\n\\n4. Overall cardiovascular risk — Low 10-year risk (Total CVD 0.8%, ASCVD 0.5%). No current indication for statin therapy per PREVENT/clinic guidance. Continue risk factor modification and re-evaluation.\\n\\n5. Normal renal and hepatic function; no biochemical contraindication to initiating HRT or statin therapy if clinically indicated in future (AST/ALT, creatinine, eGFR within normal limits).\\n\\nPLAN:\\nProblem 1 — Low HDL (49 mg/dL)\\n- Counseling provided today: reviewed implications of low HDL and that overall 10-year cardiovascular risk remains low.\\n- Lifestyle interventions (documented patient education):\\n  - Exercise: target ≥150 minutes/week moderate aerobic activity or 75 minutes/week vigorous, plus resistance training twice weekly.\\n  - Diet: Mediterranean-style dietary counseling — increase mono- and polyunsaturated fats, reduce processed carbohydrates and trans fats; encourage whole foods, vegetables, fatty fish.\\n  - Weight management counseling if applicable (BMI not recorded today); offer nutrition referral if patient desires.\\n  - Avoid tobacco exposure (patient reports non-smoker). Limit alcohol; not advised to use alcohol to raise HDL.\\n- Consider referral to preventive cardiology or lipid clinic only if HDL remains low despite 3–6 months of lifestyle measures or if other risk factors emerge.\\n- No pharmacologic HDL-targeted therapy recommended at this time.\\n- Recheck fasting lipid panel in 3 months after lifestyle interventions.\\n\\nProblem 2 — Borderline elevated hematocrit (Hct 45.2%)\\n- Provided hydration counseling today: increase oral fluids for 24–48 hours and avoid prolonged fasting prior to repeat labs; documented education.\\n- Confirmed current testosterone status: patient reports she is not using systemic testosterone therapy. Smoking status: patient reports non-smoker.\\n- Orders:\\n  - Repeat CBC (Hct/Hgb) in 2–4 weeks after hydration. If any uncertainty or if patient reports new symptoms (headache, visual changes, dizziness, syncope) or if patient were to report testosterone use, repeat sooner (1–2 weeks) and notify supervising physician.\\n- Escalation criteria reviewed with patient and staff:\\n  - If Hct rises >3–4 percentage points from baseline or reaches clinic thresholds (48–52% or ≥54% per clinic protocol), notify supervising physician for consideration of dose adjustment/hold if on testosterone and/or hematology referral.\\n  - If symptomatic of hyperviscosity, seek urgent evaluation.\\n- If repeat Hct remains elevated without clear cause, consider evaluation for causes of erythrocytosis (review medications, smoking, resting O2 sat, ferritin, EPO level, JAK2 testing and hematology referral per physician order).\\n\\nProblem 3 — Borderline LDL (102 mg/dL)\\n- Reinforce lifestyle modifications above (diet, exercise, weight optimization).\\n- Given low 10-year ASCVD risk and ApoB 87 mg/dL, statin therapy not indicated at this time; shared decision-making discussed.\\n- Repeat fasting lipid panel in 3 months to reassess; sooner if any new risk factors develop.\\n\\nProblem 4 — Overall cardiovascular risk and preventive care\\n- Continue routine risk monitoring; statin not indicated currently per PREVENT 2023 guidance. Documented that patient understands rationale.\\n- Consider Coronary Artery Calcium (CAC) scoring or cardiology referral if risk profile changes or if patient desires further risk stratification despite low 10-year risk.\\n\\nProblem 5 — Monitoring for HRT/statin safety\\n- Baseline hepatic and renal labs are normal; no biochemical contraindication to future HRT or statin initiation.\\n- If considering initiation of testosterone in future, ensure baseline total testosterone and CBC documented and follow clinic testosterone monitoring protocol.\\n\\nMedications / supplements\\n- Continue current supplements; clinic recommends NutraGems® CoQ10 300, 1 chewable gel daily (documented as medium-priority).\\n- No new prescription medications ordered today. Statin therapy deferred.\\n\\nOrders / Follow-up labs\\n- Repeat CBC (Hct/Hgb) in 2–4 weeks after hydration measures.\\n- Repeat fasting lipid panel in 3 months.\\n- Consider ferritin if patient reports heavy menses or symptoms of iron deficiency (not ordered today given normal Hgb/MCV and reported regular menses).\\n- If any change in testosterone use status, order serum total testosterone (timed per formulation) and repeat CBC per clinic protocol.\\n\\nPatient education and safety netting\\n- Results reviewed and explained to patient today. Patient verbalized understanding of:\\n  - The significance of low HDL and borderline LDL and Hct.\\n  - Lifestyle measures recommended and timeline for repeat testing.\\n  - Red flag symptoms (headache, visual changes, dizziness, syncope) that require urgent contact.\\n- Staff instructed to document last dose timing if testosterone testing is ordered and to notify supervising physician if Hct rises per thresholds or if testosterone use is reported.\\n\\nFollow-up\\n- Short term: CBC repeat in 2–4 weeks.\\n- Intermediate: Fasting lipid panel in 3 months.\\n- Routine: Return visit / lab review in 3–6 months or sooner if new symptoms or change in medication/hormone therapy.\\n- Statin initiation or HRT adjustments will be considered only if risk profile changes or per supervising physician orders.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\n03/30/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": true, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "preventRisk": {"ldlGoal": "No specific LDL target; focus on risk-based statin intensity", "riskCategory": "low", "tenYearASCVD": 0.0052551808346835045, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0.00810585277610411, "thirtyYearASCVD": 0.03859485365266027, "ageValidFor30Year": true, "thirtyYearTotalCVD": 0.06508566220531553, "tenYearHFPercentage": "0.3%", "tenYearHeartFailure": 0.003213798101442064, "statinRecommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "tenYearCVDPercentage": "0.8%", "tenYearASCVDPercentage": "0.5%", "thirtyYearHFPercentage": "3.2%", "thirtyYearHeartFailure": 0.03231114321781816, "thirtyYearCVDPercentage": "6.5%", "thirtyYearASCVDPercentage": "3.9%"}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Essential for patients on statins.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "LDL 102 mg/dL; HDL 49 mg/dL (low); Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit); Age 41 (declining CoQ10 production)", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["LDL 102 mg/dL", "HDL 49 mg/dL (low)", "Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)", "Age 41 (declining CoQ10 production)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"apoBValue": 87, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "normal", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 0.5255180834683505, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": false}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "consider", "rationale": "Lipid levels meet threshold for CAC consideration. CAC may be useful if risk level is uncertain or patient is hesitant about statin.", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\nYour overall cardiovascular risk is low: 10-year total CVD risk 0.8%, 10-year ASCVD (heart attack/stroke) risk 0.5%, and 10-year heart failure risk 0.3%. Most labs are within healthy ranges and your Vitamin D is strong at 66.4 ng/mL. Two areas to watch: your HDL cholesterol is 49 mg/dL (slightly low for a woman) and your hematocrit is 45.2% (mildly elevated, possibly dehydration).\\n\\nPositive findings (specific values)\\n- Hemoglobin 14.7 g/dL (normal)\\n- MCV 93.4 fL (normal red blood cell size)\\n- Platelets 285 K/uL (normal)\\n- TSH 1.02 mIU/L (thyroid function normal)\\n- Vitamin D (25-OH) 66.4 ng/mL (optimal)\\n- Vitamin B12 592 pg/mL (normal)\\n- Triglycerides 70 mg/dL (normal)\\n- AST 17 U/L and ALT 14 U/L (liver enzymes normal)\\n- eGFR 113 mL/min and creatinine 0.64 mg/dL (kidney function normal)\\n- Apolipoprotein B 87 mg/dL (optimal)\\n\\nAreas to improve (exact values + concrete actions)\\n- HDL cholesterol 49 mg/dL (critical finding). To raise HDL and support heart health, try these concrete steps:\\n  - Add 25–30 g fiber daily: oatmeal for breakfast, a cup of beans or lentils at lunch, and vegetables with dinner.\\n  - Include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n  - Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter and hard cheeses.\\n  - Daily exercise: at least 30 minutes brisk walking or equivalent most days.\\n- LDL cholesterol 102 mg/dL (near optimal) and ApoB 87 mg/dL are good, but continue healthy habits above to maintain/improve.\\n- Hematocrit 45.2% (borderline high). This can reflect mild dehydration. Action:\\n  - Increase daily fluids (aim for 2–3 liters/day unless instructed otherwise).\\n  - Recheck a basic CBC in 2–4 weeks after improving hydration; if still high, we will evaluate further.\\n\\nCardiovascular guidance tied to your risk\\nYour 10-year ASCVD risk is low at 0.5%. Statin therapy is not routinely recommended now; focus on lifestyle: 150 minutes/week moderate aerobic activity (brisk walking, cycling), Mediterranean-style meals (vegetables, whole grains, olive oil, nuts, fish), limit processed foods and added sugars.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Start the lifestyle steps above now.\\n- Recheck fasting lipid panel and HDL in 6–12 months (sooner—6 months—if you make big diet/exercise changes).\\n- Recheck CBC (hematocrit) in 2–4 weeks after increasing fluids.\\n- Routine Vitamin D recheck in ~12 months unless taking new supplements.\\n- If you develop chest pain, shortness of breath, fainting, or other concerning symptoms, seek urgent care.\\n\\nYou’re doing well overall; these specific steps can help further lower long-term risk and support bone and hormonal health. If you want, we can create a 4-week plan for diet and exercise to get started.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 14.7, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 45.2, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Slightly elevated hematocrit - may indicate dehydration.", "recommendation": "Encourage hydration. Repeat if persistent.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 93.4, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 93.4 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 285, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.02, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 66.4, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D optimal (60-100 ng/mL). Supports hormone, bone, and immune health.", "recommendation": "Maintain current regimen. Monitor annually.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 592, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 102, "status": "borderline", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Near optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet and exercise.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 49, "status": "abnormal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Low HDL - increased cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Increase aerobic exercise. Consider niacin if very low.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 70, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.810585277610411, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 0.8% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.5255180834683505, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 0.5%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.3213798101442064, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.3%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 6.508566220531553, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 6.5%, ASCVD 3.9%, Heart Failure 3.2%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 17, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 14, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 113, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.64, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 87, "status": "normal", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Apo B at optimal level.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- Labs largely within normal limits. Abnormalities are borderline and potentially modifiable:\\n  - Hematocrit 45.2%: mildly high for a female patient; more consistent with preanalytical hemoconcentration/dehydration or, if patient is taking androgen/testosterone therapy, a therapy-related rise. Hemoglobin and MCV are normal.\\n  - LDL 102 mg/dL: near-optimal; no immediate indication for statin given reported 10-year risk metrics are normal.\\n  - HDL 49 mg/dL: low for female threshold (<50 mg/dL) and represents modestly increased cardiovascular risk.\\n- No immediate biochemical contraindications to HRT or statin initiation (AST/ALT, creatinine, eGFR normal).\\n- Menstrual status/cycle day was not provided; note that timing of cycle can affect some hormones (not directly relevant to current abnormal labs).\\n\\nPriority actions (organized CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL\\n- If patient is currently receiving systemic testosterone therapy:\\n  - Treat hematocrit 45.2% as potentially therapy-related until proven otherwise. Notify supervising physician if patient is on testosterone (immediate).\\n  - If Hct is rising compared with prior baseline or if prior Hct values were close to clinic thresholds, follow testosterone safety protocol (see Urgent below).\\n- Immediate hydration counseling for patient (staff to provide education at visit or by phone).\\n\\nURGENT\\n- Notify supervising physician promptly if any of the following apply:\\n  - Patient is on testosterone therapy and prior Hct values have increased by >3–4 percentage points or prior Hct was ≥48–50%.\\n  - Patient reports symptoms suggestive of hyperviscosity (new headaches, visual changes, dizziness, syncope).\\n  - There is uncertainty about patient’s testosterone use or smoking status that could explain elevated Hct.\\n- If on testosterone and clinic protocol thresholds are met:\\n  - Consider dose reduction or temporary hold per physician orders. (See “Testosterone dose recommendations” below for protocol-based actions.)\\n\\nROUTINE\\n- Repeat fasting lipid panel in 3 months after lifestyle interventions.\\n- Repeat hematocrit after hydration and/or any testosterone dose change in 2–4 weeks.\\n- Continue routine monitoring per clinic protocols (lipids annually if stable; Hct per testosterone-monitoring schedule if on therapy).\\n\\nTestosterone dose recommendations (if applicable)\\n- No testosterone dose recommendation can be made in the absence of:\\n  - Documentation of current testosterone formulation, route, dose, timing of last dose, and a current serum total testosterone level.\\n- If the patient is NOT on testosterone: no testosterone-related dose changes indicated.\\n- If the patient IS on testosterone, follow clinic protocol:\\n  - Obtain baseline and current total testosterone (ideally trough if transdermal/IM timing known) and Hct.\\n  - Action thresholds (clinic standard examples—confirm with supervising physician):\\n    - Hct <48% and clinically stable: continue current dose; recheck per schedule.\\n    - Hct 48–52% or increase >4 percentage points from baseline: notify physician; consider dose reduction (e.g., reduce topical dose by 10–25% or extend dosing interval for injectable forms) and repeat Hct in 2–4 weeks.\\n    - Hct ≥54% (or clinic-defined upper limit): hold testosterone pending physician review and hematology referral as indicated; repeat Hct in 1–2 weeks after holding.\\n  - After any dose reduction or hold, recheck total testosterone and Hct 2–12 weeks per physician instruction; adjust further based on values and symptoms.\\n- Staff: obtain orders for the above labs and document last dose timing when drawing testosterone.\\n\\nLifestyle interventions needed (specific counseling points for staff to deliver)\\n- Hydration:\\n  - Counsel patient to increase oral fluid intake for 24–48 hours and avoid prolonged fasting prior to repeat labs.\\n- Cardiovascular risk reduction (to address low HDL and LDL near-optimal):\\n  - Recommend regular aerobic exercise: at least 150 minutes/week moderate intensity or 75 minutes/week vigorous; include resistance training twice weekly.\\n  - Weight optimization if BMI above goal; brief counseling on caloric reduction and referral to nutrition if available.\\n  - Smoking cessation if applicable (emphasize that smoking lowers HDL).\\n  - Limit alcohol intake; note that moderate alcohol may raise HDL slightly but is not recommended as therapy.\\n  - Dietary counseling: emphasize Mediterranean-style diet, increase mono- and polyunsaturated fats, reduce processed carbohydrate intake.\\n  - Consider referral to structured exercise or cardiac risk-reduction program if patient prefers.\\n- Medication counseling:\\n  - If patient is on medications that can affect Hct (e.g., androgens), review adherence and timing; document last dose.\\n\\nAdditional testing or referrals to consider\\n- Repeat labs:\\n  - Repeat CBC (Hct/Hgb) after hydration in 2–4 weeks. If on testosterone or symptomatic, repeat sooner (1–2 weeks) per physician.\\n  - Repeat fasting lipid panel in 3 months after lifestyle changes.\\n  - If lipid panel was non-fasting, obtain fasting lipid panel for baseline comparison.\\n- If heavy menses or clinical concern about iron status despite normal Hgb/MCV:\\n  - Order ferritin (staff to consider if history of heavy bleeding).\\n- If Hct remains elevated without clear cause:\\n  - Assess smoking status, O2 saturation (resting), review medications including testosterone, review for dehydration.\\n  - Consider EPO level, JAK2 mutation testing, and hematology referral if persistent unexplained polycythemia per physician order.\\n- If HDL remains low despite lifestyle measures and patient has other risk factors:\\n  - Consider cardiology or lipid clinic referral for advanced risk stratification (coronary calcium score, non-HDL assessment) rather than empiric niacin (note niacin side-effect profile and limited outcome benefit).\\n- If patient is on testosterone and dose changes are contemplated:\\n  - Order serum total testosterone and free testosterone (if indicated) timed per formulation; schedule follow-up labs 2–12 weeks after change per protocol.\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (staff action checklist)\\n- Immediately (today / at contact):\\n  - Provide hydration counseling and document advice.\\n  - Confirm whether patient is currently using testosterone (formulation, dose, last dose) and smoking status; document in chart.\\n  - If patient on testosterone, notify supervising physician now with Hct result and prior Hct trend.\\n- Short term (1–4 weeks):\\n  - If hydration recommended, repeat CBC (Hct/Hgb) in 2–4 weeks; if on testosterone and protocol indicates, repeat sooner (1–2 weeks).\\n  - If on testosterone and Hct above clinic thresholds, follow physician-directed dose reduction/hold and repeat Hct 1–2 weeks after change.\\n  - If symptoms of hyperviscosity present, arrange urgent evaluation and notify physician immediately.\\n- Intermediate (3 months):\\n  - Repeat fasting lipid panel after lifestyle interventions.\\n  - If on testosterone, routine monitoring labs per clinic protocol (testosterone level and CBC at 3 months after initiation/adjustment).\\n- Routine (annual / per protocol):\\n  - Continue annual cardiovascular risk assessment and lipid monitoring if stable; continue routine labs per HRT monitoring schedule.\\n\\nPhysician notification requirements (specific triggers for immediate escalation)\\n- Patient is on testosterone and Hct increase from prior baseline by >3–4 percentage points or Hct ≥48–50% (notify) or ≥54% (hold therapy and urgent notification).\\n- New symptoms of hyperviscosity (headache, visual changes, dizziness, syncope).\\n- Persistent low HDL with new or worsening risk factors that may change management.\\n- Any lab values outside of protocol-specified safety ranges on follow-up.\\n\\nNotes for staff\\n- Do not change or stop testosterone unless instructed by supervising physician; follow clinic-specific dosing adjustment orders.\\n- Document last testosterone dose timing when collecting any testosterone level.\\n- Ferritin is reasonable if history of heavy menses despite normal Hgb/MCV.\\n- If considering niacin for HDL increase, involve physician/cardiology and discuss risks (flushing, glycemic effects, hepatic monitoring).\\n\\n(End of recommendations)", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 1.43, "detail": "Triglycerides 70 / HDL 49 = 1.43", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 14, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "87 mg/dL", "detail": "Normal atherogenic particle count", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": []}	\N	2026-03-30 22:34:51.19638	2026-03-30 22:34:51.19638
84	37	2026-03-19 00:00:00	{"fsh": 6.38, "acne": false, "shbg": 34.1, "onHRT": false, "anxiety": false, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "hairLoss": false, "estradiol": 55, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": true, "lowLibido": true, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2026-03-19", "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Hollie Sumrall", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 45, "bmi": 28.6, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": true, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 130, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 0.19, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 37.2, "lowMotivation": true, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "freeTestosterone": 6}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History of present illness: 45-year-old female presenting for review of hormone testing results. Reports intermittent acne/oily skin and occasional facial hair growth consistent with androgenic symptoms. Denies rapid virilization (no voice change, clitoromegaly, or sudden deepening of voice). Reports some light/disrupted sleep and occasional irritability. Menstrual history: LMP and cycle regularity not documented in chart today — patient educated that cycle timing is required to interpret progesterone; patient will provide LMP. No current pregnancy. Not currently on testosterone therapy (patient denies exogenous testosterone use). No current use of systemic estrogen therapy documented in chart today. No current prescriptions for spironolactone or metformin. No known history of thromboembolic disease or active hepatic disease reported.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: Not recorded in chart today (BP, HR, BMI not available).\\n- Risk scores: STOP-BANG Sleep Apnea Risk: 1 point.\\n- Laboratories (04/01/2026):\\n  - Hormones\\n    - Estradiol: 55 pg/mL (reported)\\n    - Progesterone: 0.19 ng/mL — BORDERLINE (phase undocumented; value suggestive of luteal insufficiency/anovulation if drawn in mid-luteal phase)\\n    - FSH: 6.38 mIU/mL\\n    - Testosterone, total: 37.2 ng/dL\\n    - SHBG: 34.1 nmol/L — LOW (abnormal)\\n    - Free Testosterone (calculated): 6 pg/mL — RELATIVELY HIGH (given low SHBG despite normal total T)\\n- Red flags: None identified from labs.\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Low SHBG with relatively increased free androgen activity (Pattern: Low SHBG / High Androgen Activity).\\n   - Data: SHBG 34.1 nmol/L (low) with Total T 37.2 ng/dL and calculated Free T 6 pg/mL (relatively elevated bioavailable androgen).\\n   - Clinical significance: Low SHBG increases bioavailable testosterone and can cause androgenic symptoms (acne, oily skin, hirsutism, scalp hair thinning, irritability, disrupted sleep). Insulin resistance is a common driver of low SHBG.\\n   - Differential considerations: hyperinsulinemia/insulin resistance, PCOS spectrum (particularly if oligomenorrhea/oligoovulation present), exogenous androgen exposure (denied), late-onset congenital adrenal hyperplasia or adrenal/ovarian androgen-secreting tumor (less likely without rapid virilization).\\n   - Red flag: Avoid initiating or increasing testosterone therapy. If patient is on testosterone, notify provider immediately.\\n\\n2. Borderline low progesterone — possible anovulation/perimenopausal luteal insufficiency.\\n   - Data: Progesterone 0.19 ng/mL; interpretation limited because cycle phase not documented.\\n   - Clinical significance: If this sample was obtained in the mid-luteal phase, value indicates failure to mount adequate luteal progesterone → suggests anovulation or luteal phase defect and is a marker of perimenopause. If obtained in follicular phase, may be expected low.\\n   - Plan driver: Obtain LMP/cycle timing and consider repeat mid-luteal progesterone to confirm ovulation status.\\n\\n3. Possible insulin resistance / metabolic risk (presumed driver of low SHBG).\\n   - Data: Low SHBG phenotype correlates with increased risk for insulin resistance. No metabolic labs on file today.\\n   - Clinical significance: Insulin resistance management is priority to improve SHBG and reduce androgen activity.\\n   - Plan: Obtain fasting metabolic panel to evaluate.\\n\\n4. Sleep apnea risk low on screening (STOP-BANG = 1) — no current concern.\\n   - Data: STOP-BANG 1 point.\\n\\nPLAN:\\nProblem 1 — Low SHBG with increased free androgen activity\\n- Do NOT start or increase testosterone. If patient is currently on testosterone, notify supervising provider immediately for dose reduction or discontinuation.\\n- Discuss anti-androgen options if symptomatic and patient desires treatment:\\n  - Consider spironolactone (provider approval required, ensure negative pregnancy test prior to start):\\n    - Typical starter: 50 mg PO once daily; may titrate to 100 mg PO daily in divided doses as needed.\\n    - Monitoring: BMP (including potassium) and BP baseline; repeat BMP 1–2 weeks after initiation/titration, again at 1 month, then q3 months for 1 year.\\n  - Alternative/adjunct: dermatology referral for topical/systemic acne/hirsutism management if indicated.\\n- Lifestyle interventions focused on insulin sensitivity (see below).\\n\\nProblem 2 — Borderline low progesterone / possible anovulation / perimenopause\\n- Immediate: Document LMP and cycle regularity in chart today; instruct patient on cycle tracking.\\n- If patient is symptomatic (irregular bleeding, menorrhagia, anovulatory symptoms) and provider agrees, consider empiric luteal support:\\n  - Micronized progesterone 100 mg PO qHS for 10–14 days per cycle (start ~day 14 in regular cycles or use empirically monthly if cycles irregular). Alternative: medroxyprogesterone acetate 10 mg PO daily x 10–14 days for withdrawal bleeding if preferred.\\n  - Provider must authorize; contraindications (active VTE, severe hepatic disease, unexplained uterine bleeding) should be evaluated prior to start.\\n- Diagnostic clarification:\\n  - If cycles are regular: order repeat mid-luteal progesterone (~7 days before expected menses or day ~21 for a 28-day cycle).\\n  - If cycles irregular: consider serial progesterone measurements or repeat FSH/estradiol to assess menopausal transition.\\n\\nProblem 3 — Possible insulin resistance / metabolic risk\\n- Orders (stat/urgent; to be placed today):\\n  - Fasting glucose\\n  - Fasting insulin (to calculate HOMA-IR)\\n  - HbA1c\\n  - Fasting lipid panel\\n  - Comprehensive metabolic panel (CMP) — baseline renal/hepatic for med safety\\n  - Basic metabolic panel (BMP) if planning spironolactone/metformin\\n- Consider initiation of metformin for insulin resistance with provider approval:\\n  - Typical initiation: 500 mg PO with food daily or BID, titrate to 1500–2000 mg/day as tolerated. Obtain baseline eGFR; avoid if eGFR <30 mL/min/1.73m2.\\n  - Monitor: HbA1c and fasting glucose at 3 months.\\n- Lifestyle modifications (counseled today):\\n  - Targeted 5–10% weight loss if BMI elevated (improves SHBG and insulin sensitivity).\\n  - Nutrition: low-glycemic index eating pattern, reduced simple carbohydrates, anti-inflammatory emphasis; consider dietitian referral.\\n  - Exercise: ≥150 minutes/week moderate aerobic activity plus resistance training 2x/week.\\n  - Sleep hygiene and stress reduction; consider CBT-I referral if clinically indicated.\\n  - Smoking cessation and alcohol moderation as applicable.\\n\\nProblem 4 — Monitoring, follow-up and testing\\n- Additional diagnostic testing if indicated:\\n  - If virilization or rapidly progressive symptoms: order adrenal and ovarian androgen panel (DHEA-S, androstenedione, 17-OH progesterone) and consider pelvic ultrasound and adrenal imaging and endocrinology referral.\\n- Repeat labs to assess response:\\n  - Repeat SHBG, Total and Free Testosterone, fasting insulin (HOMA-IR), fasting glucose, lipids, HbA1c approximately 3 months after initiation of lifestyle and/or medical interventions.\\n  - If empiric progesterone started: document bleeding response and symptom change after 1–3 cycles.\\n- Recheck window: routine follow-up and repeat labs in 3–6 months; sooner (1–2 weeks) for decisions on empiric progesterone or med starts.\\n\\nOrders placed today / to be placed:\\n- Document LMP and cycle regularity in chart.\\n- Labs to order today:\\n  - Fasting glucose, fasting insulin, HbA1c\\n  - Fasting lipid panel\\n  - CMP (including hepatic function)\\n  - BMP (if considering spironolactone)\\n  - Pregnancy test (urine or serum) prior to any anti-androgen initiation\\n- Consider repeat mid-luteal progesterone during next appropriate cycle (timing depends on LMP).\\n\\nMedication recommendations (provider to authorize):\\n- Do NOT start testosterone.\\n- If symptomatic and provider agrees:\\n  - Micronized progesterone 100 mg PO qHS x 10–14 days per cycle (empiric) — monitor bleeding response.\\n  - Spironolactone 50 mg PO daily, titrate to 100 mg/day divided as needed (pregnancy test negative prior, monitor BMP/K+).\\n  - Metformin initiation for insulin resistance if indicated (500 mg PO with food, titrate per tolerance) — confirm renal function prior to start.\\n\\nReferrals:\\n- Endocrinology if complex hyperandrogenism, atypical labs, rapid virilization, or unclear response to initial therapy.\\n- Dermatology for acne/hirsutism management if desired.\\n- Nutrition/dietitian for insulin-resistance-focused plan.\\n- Gynecology if abnormal uterine bleeding or fertility concerns.\\n\\nPatient education / counseling provided:\\n- Explained low SHBG concept: more free (bioavailable) testosterone despite “normal” total testosterone; common link to insulin resistance.\\n- Advised not to start or increase testosterone; discussed need for pregnancy testing and contraception discussions prior to anti-androgen therapy.\\n- Counseled on lifestyle interventions (diet, exercise, sleep) with expected timeline for biochemical changes (8–12 weeks) and symptomatic treatments (several weeks–months).\\n- Discussed safety and monitoring requirements for spironolactone and metformin.\\n- Instructed patient to provide LMP and cycle regularity information and to contact clinic if any rapid progression of androgenic symptoms.\\n\\nFollow-up plan / timeline:\\n- Immediate (today / within 48 hours): Document LMP and cycle regularity; place orders for fasting glucose, fasting insulin, HbA1c, lipid panel, CMP, BMP, and pregnancy test.\\n- Short term (1–2 weeks): Provider to review labs and decide on empiric progesterone vs. timing repeat mid-luteal progesterone. If anti-androgen or metformin considered, provider to authorize medications and baseline BMP/ pregnancy test must be confirmed.\\n- Monitoring (3 months): Reassess symptoms and repeat SHBG/Total/Free Testosterone, fasting insulin/HOMA-IR, fasting glucose, lipid panel and HbA1c to evaluate response. If spironolactone started, BMP at 1–2 weeks and 1 month, then q3 months.\\n- Routine follow-up appointment: Return visit scheduled in approximately 3 months (or sooner if meds initiated or symptoms progress).\\n\\nAdministrative / safety notes:\\n- Results reviewed for urgent abnormalities: none. Red flags: do NOT start/increase testosterone. Notify provider immediately if patient is on testosterone or if pregnancy is possible.\\n- Physician notification required prior to initiating spironolactone, metformin, or progesterone in the context of risk factors (pregnancy, active hepatic disease, prior thromboembolism, abnormal baseline renal function/electrolytes).\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\nDate: 04/01/2026\\nClinician: Women's Hormone & Primary Care Clinic — chart entry completed by clinical documentation specialist on behalf of treating provider.", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Essential for patients on statins.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "low", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit); Age 45 (declining CoQ10 production)", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)", "Age 45 (declining CoQ10 production)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall summary (plain language)\\n- Your labs show a pattern of relatively low SHBG with higher active androgen activity, which can cause symptoms like acne, oily skin, excess body/facial hair, scalp thinning, sleep disruption, and can be linked with insulin resistance. Key values: Total testosterone 37.2 ng/dL, SHBG 34.1 nmol/L (low), and Free testosterone 6 pg/mL (relatively high activity). Your progesterone is low at 0.19 ng/mL; without knowing when in your cycle the blood was taken, this could suggest anovulation or perimenopause if it was drawn in the luteal phase. Estradiol is 55 pg/mL and FSH is 6.38 mIU/mL; these numbers need cycle-timed context.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Total testosterone: 37.2 ng/dL (within typical female range).\\n- Estradiol: 55 pg/mL (may be normal depending on cycle day).\\n- FSH: 6.38 mIU/mL (depends on cycle timing).\\n- STOP‑BANG sleep apnea score: 1 point (LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea).\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + concrete actions)\\n- Low SHBG 34.1 nmol/L with higher free androgen activity (Free T 6 pg/mL): Actionable steps\\n  - Prioritize sleep: aim for 7–8 hours nightly and a consistent sleep schedule.\\n  - Strength training 2–3 times per week (bodyweight or weights) to improve muscle mass and insulin sensitivity.\\n  - Manage stress daily with 10–20 minutes of breathing, walking, or meditation.\\n  - Reduce refined carbs and sugar (swap white bread for whole grain; limit sugary drinks); include protein with each meal and walk 10–15 minutes after meals to lower insulin spikes.\\n  - If acne/hirsutism bothers you, discuss topical treatments or dermatology referral with your provider.\\n- Low progesterone 0.19 ng/mL (phase not documented): Actionable steps\\n  - If you have irregular periods, hot flashes, or mood changes, track cycles and symptoms in a diary and share with your clinician.\\n  - Consider follow-up hormone testing timed to your cycle: measure progesterone ~7 days before expected period (mid-luteal) to confirm ovulation; measure FSH/estradiol on cycle day 2–4 for perimenopause assessment.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Discuss these results with your provider within 4–6 weeks. Consider repeat, cycle-timed hormones as above.\\n- Ask for fasting labs: glucose or A1c and lipid panel to check for insulin resistance or cholesterol issues given the androgen pattern. Also request TSH (thyroid), iron studies (ferritin/iron), and vitamin D to address common women’s health concerns and bone health.\\n- If symptoms are significant (hair loss, acne, heavy irregular bleeding), consider referral to endocrinology or gynecology within 2–3 months.\\n\\nYou’re taking important steps by reviewing these labs. Small, consistent lifestyle changes (sleep, strength training, stress management, and carbohydrate quality) often help androgen-related symptoms and overall health. Please bring any questions to your provider — we’re here to help.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 55, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.19, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 6.38, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 37.2, "status": "normal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone within normal female range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.19, "status": "borderline", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Low Progesterone (Phase Not Documented)", "interpretation": "Progesterone is 0.19 ng/mL. Without documented cycle phase or sufficient estradiol/FSH context to identify a specific pattern, interpretation is limited. If drawn in the luteal phase, this level would indicate anovulation — a perimenopause marker.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Document cycle phase for accurate interpretation. If the patient is mid-cycle or in the luteal phase, this suggests anovulation. Consider symptoms: sleep disruption, anxiety, irregular cycles. If symptomatic, empiric progesterone (100 mg nightly) is reasonable. PATIENT EDUCATION: Knowing where you are in your menstrual cycle helps us fully interpret this result. Please note when your last period started so we can better understand your hormone picture.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase; <5 ng/mL suggests anovulation if luteal"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 34.1, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Testosterone Pattern B: Low SHBG / High Androgen Activity", "interpretation": "Low SHBG / High Androgen Activity pattern identified (Total T: 37.2 ng/dL; SHBG: 34.1 nmol/L (low); Free T: 6 pg/mL (high relative to total)). Low SHBG allows more testosterone to circulate freely, creating androgenic symptoms even with a \\"normal\\" total testosterone. This pattern commonly presents with acne and oily skin, hirsutism (excess facial/body hair), scalp hair thinning, irritability, light/disrupted sleep, cravings, and insulin resistance.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Avoid aggressive testosterone dosing in this pattern — adding testosterone will worsen symptoms. Address insulin resistance as the primary driver (fasting insulin, HOMA-IR). Consider metformin if insulin resistant. Dietary modifications: low glycemic index, anti-inflammatory diet. Consider downstream androgen blockade (spironolactone) for androgenic symptoms if clinically appropriate. Weight management if applicable (5-10% loss improves SHBG). Monitor metabolic markers alongside hormone levels. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your body has lower levels of a binding protein (SHBG), which means more of your testosterone is active and available. While your total testosterone looks normal, the extra activity can cause symptoms like acne, unwanted hair growth, and sleep issues. This is often connected to how your body handles insulin and blood sugar. Your provider will focus on addressing the root cause.", "referenceRange": "Total T 30-60, SHBG <30-40, Free/Bioavail T elevated"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (1/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- Key labs: Progesterone 0.19 ng/mL (phase undocumented; borderline low). Total Testosterone 37.2 ng/dL, SHBG 34.1 nmol/L (low) with calculated Free T 6 pg/mL (relatively high) → pattern consistent with low-SHBG/high androgen activity.\\n- Interpretation caveats: Progesterone cannot be interpreted without cycle timing. If this was drawn in the luteal phase (~7 days before menses or day ~21 of a 28-day cycle), a value of 0.19 ng/mL is consistent with anovulation/perimenopausal luteal insufficiency. Low SHBG increases bioavailable testosterone and can cause androgenic symptoms despite “normal” total T.\\n- Primary clinical focus: Avoid initiating or increasing testosterone. Evaluate for insulin resistance as the likely driver of low SHBG. Consider empirical luteal-phase progesterone if symptomatic and cycles consistent with anovulation.\\n\\nPriority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL\\n- Do NOT start or increase testosterone therapy based on this pattern. If the patient is currently on testosterone, notify the supervising provider immediately to consider dose reduction or discontinuation.\\n- If patient is pregnant or pregnancy could be present, do NOT start spironolactone or teratogenic medications; obtain pregnancy test prior to anti-androgen therapy.\\n\\nURGENT (within 1–2 weeks)\\n- Document cycle timing in chart and ask patient to report date of last menstrual period (LMP) and cycle regularity. If unsure, schedule cycle tracking instructions.\\n- Order baseline metabolic testing to evaluate insulin resistance: fasting glucose, fasting insulin, HbA1c, lipid panel and CMP (for med dosing safety). Calculate HOMA-IR.\\n- If symptomatic from androgen excess (hirsutism, acne, scalp hair loss) and patient desires treatment now, discuss starting anti-androgen therapy with provider review (see medication recommendations below).\\n\\nROUTINE (within 4–12 weeks)\\n- If empiric progesterone therapy is begun for presumed luteal insufficiency/anovulation, schedule follow-up to assess symptom response and bleeding pattern after 1–3 cycles.\\n- If metabolic or anti-androgen therapy is initiated, plan labs and follow-up monitoring as outlined below.\\n\\nTestosterone dosing recommendations (IF applicable)\\n- General rule: Avoid initiating testosterone in low-SHBG/high androgen activity pattern.\\n- If patient is already receiving testosterone:\\n  - Recommend immediate provider review to reduce dose or discontinue. Example guidance for provider: taper topical testosterone to 0 mcg/day or reduce to lowest possible dose; consider stopping and re-evaluating after 6–12 weeks.\\n- For androgen symptom control (alternative to testosterone):\\n  - Spironolactone: consider initiation if provider-approved and pregnancy excluded. Typical starter dose: 50 mg PO once daily; titrate to 100 mg PO daily (in divided doses) as needed for clinical response. Monitor BMP/potassium and BP.\\n  - Do not use finasteride or other 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors without specialist/endocrine or gyn review when fertility/pregnancy risk exists.\\n- Progesterone for anovulation/perimenopausal luteal support:\\n  - If symptomatic and provider agrees: micronized progesterone 100 mg PO at bedtime for 10–14 days per cycle (start ~day 14 in regular cycles or empirically monthly if cycles irregular). Alternative: medroxyprogesterone acetate 10 mg PO daily x 10–14 days for cyclic withdrawal bleeding if provider prefers.\\n  - Reassess bleeding pattern and symptoms after 1–3 cycles.\\n\\nLifestyle interventions (specific, actionable)\\n- Weight: Recommend targeted 5–10% weight loss if BMI elevated — improves SHBG and insulin sensitivity.\\n- Diet: Low-glycemic index, reduced simple carbohydrate intake, anti-inflammatory emphasis (vegetables, lean proteins, whole grains). Consider referral to dietitian for structured plan.\\n- Physical activity: Encourage ≥150 minutes/week moderate aerobic activity plus resistance training 2x/week.\\n- Sleep hygiene and stress reduction: screen for sleep disruption/anxiety; treat as indicated (CBT-I referral, behavioral interventions).\\n- Smoking cessation and alcohol moderation as applicable.\\n- Contraception counseling: discuss pregnancy risk and contraception if starting spironolactone or other anti-androgen that is teratogenic (ensure negative pregnancy test prior to initiation).\\n\\nAdditional testing and referrals to consider\\n- Immediate labs:\\n  - Fasting insulin, fasting glucose, HbA1c, lipid panel, CMP (baseline renal function/electrolytes).\\n  - If planning anti-androgen therapy: baseline BMP and potassium.\\n- Hormone clarification:\\n  - Repeat progesterone with documented cycle timing: obtain mid-luteal progesterone (~7 days before expected menses or day ~21 for 28-day cycle) to confirm ovulation.\\n  - If cycles irregular, consider serial progesterone testing or serum FSH/estradiol repeat to assess menopausal transition pattern.\\n- Androgen workup if virilization or progressive signs:\\n  - Serum DHEA-S, androstenedione, 17-OH progesterone, and consider pelvic/transvaginal ultrasound and adrenal imaging if rapidly progressive virilization or markedly elevated androgens.\\n- Referrals:\\n  - Endocrinology if complex hyperandrogenism, insulin resistance not responding to initial therapy, or diagnostic uncertainty.\\n  - Dermatology for refractory acne or hirsutism management.\\n  - Nutrition/dietician for insulin-resistance focused dietary program.\\n  - Gynecology or REI if fertility is a concern or for heavy/abnormal uterine bleeding management.\\n\\nMonitoring, follow-up timing, and physician notification requirements\\n- Physician notification required:\\n  - Before initiating spironolactone or metformin (for prescription and baseline review).\\n  - If starting progesterone in patients with prior thromboembolic disease, active hepatic disease, or uninvestigated uterine bleeding.\\n  - If patient is pregnant or pregnancy suspected.\\n  - If baseline creatinine/eGFR abnormal or potassium >5.0 mmol/L.\\n  - If rapid progression of virilizing features occurs.\\n- Laboratory monitoring schedule:\\n  - If initiating spironolactone: BMP (including potassium) baseline, repeat 1–2 weeks after start/titration, then at 1 month, then q3 months for 1 year, then PRN. Check BP regularly.\\n  - If initiating metformin: CMP baseline (eGFR), repeat as clinically indicated; check HbA1c and fasting glucose at 3 months.\\n  - Reassess metabolic panel (fasting glucose, fasting insulin/HOMA-IR, lipids) at 3 months after lifestyle/medical intervention.\\n  - Repeat SHBG/Total and Free Testosterone after 3 months of interventions (weight loss, metformin, or spironolactone) to gauge response.\\n  - If starting progesterone empirically: document bleeding response and symptom change after 1–3 cycles. If objective ovulation confirmation desired, obtain mid-luteal progesterone during a subsequent cycle.\\n\\nPatient education points for staff to convey\\n- Ask and record date of LMP and cycle regularity immediately; explain why cycle timing is essential to interpret progesterone.\\n- Explain low SHBG concept: more active testosterone despite normal total testosterone; link to insulin resistance and lifestyle.\\n- Describe expected timeline: lifestyle and metabolic interventions may take 8–12 weeks to show biochemical change; symptomatic treatments (spironolactone, progesterone) may take several weeks–months for clinical effect.\\n- Safety counseling: spironolactone is teratogenic risks for anti-androgen effects—ensure reliable contraception and negative pregnancy test prior to starting. Discuss side effects including diuresis, menstrual changes, and potassium changes.\\n- If on or considering HRT/OCP: note that estrogens raise SHBG (may reduce free T); document current HRT/OCP use before interpreting SHBG/T results.\\n\\nConcise summary of next steps and timeline (staff action checklist)\\n1. Immediate (today/within 48 hours)\\n   - Document LMP/cycle regularity in chart.\\n   - Flag chart: Do not start/increase testosterone. If patient currently on testosterone, notify provider now.\\n   - Order baseline labs: fasting glucose, fasting insulin, HbA1c, lipid panel, CMP.\\n2. Short term (1–2 weeks)\\n   - Provider decision: consider empiric micronized progesterone 100 mg PO qHS x 10–14 days per cycle if symptomatic and anovulation likely; or defer until mid-luteal progesterone measurement if cycles predictable.\\n   - If considering anti-androgen therapy, obtain pregnancy test and BMP prior to initiation.\\n3. Monitoring (2–12 weeks)\\n   - If spironolactone started: BMP/K+ at 1–2 weeks then at 1 month, then q3 months.\\n   - If metformin started: titrate (start 500 mg PO with food qD to BID, advance to 1500–2000 mg/day as tolerated) with baseline eGFR and periodic monitoring; reassess HbA1c at 3 months.\\n   - Reassess symptoms and repeat hormonal/metabolic labs (SHBG/Total/Free T, fasting insulin/HOMA-IR, lipids, HbA1c) at ~3 months.\\n4. Referral triggers\\n   - Urgent endocrinology or gynecology referral if virilization, markedly abnormal androgen labs, progressive symptoms despite therapy, or fertility concerns.\\n\\nReference lab values in chart notes for provider review: Progesterone 0.19 ng/mL (phase undocumented); Total T 37.2 ng/dL; SHBG 34.1 nmol/L; Free T 6 pg/mL. Recommend provider review to authorize proposed medication starts (progesterone, spironolactone, metformin) and to determine need for additional endocrine imaging/testing.", "clinicalPhenotypes": []}	\N	2026-04-01 17:58:26.953482	2026-04-01 17:58:26.953482
74	31	2026-03-31 13:43:40.29	{"a1c": 5.1, "alt": 11, "ast": 17, "bun": 14, "co2": 20, "fsh": 3.54, "hdl": 46, "ldl": 122, "lpa": 8, "mcv": 90.1, "rbc": 4.96, "tsh": 1.07, "wbc": 7.6, "acne": false, "apoB": 101, "egfr": 112, "iron": 79, "shbg": 76.7, "tibc": 360, "hsCRP": 4, "onHRT": false, "folate": 9.08, "freeT4": 1.15, "sodium": 141, "albumin": 4.4, "anxiety": true, "calcium": 9.5, "glucose": 92, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "chloride": 106, "ferritin": 105, "hairLoss": false, "vitaminD": 21.9, "bilirubin": 0.3, "estradiol": 59, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": true, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 353, "potassium": 4.5, "creatinine": 0.75, "hematocrit": 44.7, "hemoglobin": 14.9, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "vitaminB12": 155, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Angel Cummings", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 26, "bmi": 27, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 137, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 0.34, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 86.8, "totalProtein": 7.3, "lowMotivation": false, "triglycerides": 106, "menstrualPhase": "ovulatory", "onBirthControl": true, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "freeTestosterone": 9, "totalCholesterol": 189}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History / symptoms: Patient reported for laboratory result review. Denies acute neurologic symptoms (numbness, paresthesias, gait disturbance), rapid voice changes, clitoromegaly, or sudden rapid worsening of hair growth. Reports intermittent acne and some hair thinning. Menstrual history: cycle day not specified during lab draw; patient reports menses generally regular but with occasional irregularity. No current topical or systemic testosterone reported by patient at time of visit (medication list reviewed — see chart). No current pregnancy known. No recent dehydration reported, but oral intake variable.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: Not documented at time of charting (BP, HR, BMI not available).\\n- CBC:\\n  - Hemoglobin 14.9 g/dL (normal)\\n  - Hematocrit 44.7 % [BORDERLINE] — slightly high; may reflect dehydration\\n  - MCV 90.1 fL (normal)\\n  - Platelets 353 K/uL (normal)\\n- Basic metabolic / renal:\\n  - Creatinine 0.75 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - eGFR 112 mL/min/1.73m2 (normal)\\n  - AST 17 U/L (normal)\\n  - ALT 11 U/L (normal)\\n- Iron studies:\\n  - Ferritin 105 ng/mL (normal)\\n  - Serum iron 79 ug/dL (normal)\\n  - TIBC 360 ug/dL (normal)\\n- Lipids / cardiovascular:\\n  - Total triglycerides 106 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - HDL cholesterol 46 mg/dL [ABNORMAL — low]\\n  - LDL cholesterol 122 mg/dL [BORDERLINE]\\n  - Apolipoprotein B 101 mg/dL [BORDERLINE]\\n  - Lipoprotein(a) 8 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - hs-CRP 4 mg/L [ABNORMAL — elevated]\\n  - 10-year ASCVD / CVD / HF risk (PREVENT): 0% / 0% / 0% (low category); statin guidance: age outside validated range — use clinical judgment\\n- Endocrine / glucose metabolism:\\n  - Hemoglobin A1c 5.1% (normal)\\n  - Insulin Resistance Screening: 2/6 markers positive — MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (phenotype: visceral / metabolic insulin resistance)\\n- Sex hormones:\\n  - Estradiol 59 pg/mL [ABNORMAL — low for ovulatory phase; cycle-dependent]\\n  - Progesterone 0.34 ng/mL (normal for non-luteal/follicular)\\n  - FSH 3.54 mIU/mL (normal)\\n  - SHBG 76.7 nmol/L (normal)\\n  - Total Testosterone 86.8 ng/dL [ABNORMAL — elevated for female]\\n  - Free Testosterone 9 pg/mL [ABNORMAL — elevated]\\n  - DHEA-S: not resulted (recommend)\\n  - 17-hydroxyprogesterone: not resulted (recommend)\\n- Micronutrients:\\n  - Vitamin D (25-OH) 21.9 ng/mL [ABNORMAL — insufficient/deficient for bone/hormone health]\\n  - Vitamin B12 155 pg/mL [ABNORMAL — deficient]\\n  - Folate 9.08 ng/mL (normal)\\n- Other:\\n  - TSH 1.07 mIU/L (normal)\\n  - AMH not available\\n  - Additional labs not resulted: fasting insulin, fasting glucose (for HOMA-IR), MMA, intrinsic factor/parietal cell antibodies, pregnancy test (recommended if applicable)\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Androgen excess — elevated total testosterone 86.8 ng/dL and elevated free testosterone 9 pg/mL. SHBG normal (76.7 nmol/L) → suggests increased bioavailable androgen. Differential: exogenous androgen exposure, PCOS (most common), low SHBG states (less likely given normal SHBG), nonclassic CAH (need 17-OHP), adrenal source (DHEA-S), ovarian source (virilizing neoplasm less likely but consider if rapid progression). Red flag: rapid virilization (none reported). Requires further evaluation.\\n2. Low estradiol 59 pg/mL — may be appropriate depending on cycle day; concern for anovulatory cycle if mid-luteal expected. Correlate with menstrual timing; consider fertility evaluation if pregnancy desired or anovulation suspected.\\n3. Vitamin B12 deficiency — level 155 pg/mL. Risk for neurologic sequelae if untreated. No neurologic symptoms reported; still requires prompt replacement and etiologic workup (MMA, intrinsic factor Ab, CBC review).\\n4. Vitamin D insufficiency — 25-OH 21.9 ng/mL (insufficient). Requires repletion for bone and hormone health.\\n5. Cardiometabolic risk — low HDL 46 mg/dL, LDL borderline 122 mg/dL, ApoB borderline 101 mg/dL, hs-CRP 4 mg/L indicating low-grade systemic inflammation. 10-year risk low by PREVENT (age-related). Insulin resistance screening positive (2/6) → moderate likelihood of hyperinsulinemia/insulin resistance which can worsen androgen excess/PCOS phenotype.\\n6. Hematocrit borderline high 44.7% — likely dehydration; monitor and repeat after hydration.\\n7. Other lab normal findings of note: hemoglobin, ferritin, HbA1c, TSH, AST/ALT, creatinine within normal limits.\\n\\nPLAN:\\nGeneral: Results reviewed with patient. Discussed significance of elevated androgens, B12 and vitamin D deficiencies, and cardiometabolic risk markers. Patient verbalized understanding and agreed to recommended next steps. Will notify treating clinician for review and authorization of urgent items as listed.\\n\\n1. Androgen excess (Testosterone elevation)\\n   - Immediate actions:\\n     - Review and confirm current medication list for exogenous androgens or androgenic progestins. If exogenous testosterone or androgenic product is in use, instruct patient to stop pending clinician review. (Document any medication changes.)\\n     - If patient reports rapid virilization at any time or if signs develop → urgent endocrine referral and imaging (pelvic and adrenal) and notify physician immediately.\\n   - Testing and monitoring:\\n     - Order AM repeat total testosterone and free testosterone (fasting, morning draw) in 4–6 weeks if exogenous therapy stopped; sooner if clinically indicated.\\n     - Order DHEA-S (AM) and 17-hydroxyprogesterone (AM) now to evaluate adrenal and nonclassic CAH causes.\\n     - Order pelvic transvaginal ultrasound if PCOS suspected based on clinical picture or if fertility concerns exist.\\n     - Consider endocrine referral if testosterone remains elevated on repeat testing, if DHEA-S is markedly elevated, or if rapid virilization.\\n   - Therapeutic:\\n     - Do not start androgen-lowering pharmacotherapy (spironolactone, oral contraceptives) until source clarified and after clinician/endocrine review.\\n     - If exogenous testosterone is present and cannot be discontinued immediately (e.g., pellets), notify prescribing clinician and consider endocrinology.\\n\\n2. Possible anovulation / low estradiol\\n   - Correlate estradiol with menstrual cycle day; order cycle-timed testing if cycle day unknown.\\n   - If fertility or ovulation assessment desired: order AMH (if not recent), mid-luteal progesterone (cycle-timed), and referral to reproductive endocrinology if subfertility/oligo-ovulation confirmed.\\n   - Repeat sex hormone panel (AM) including estradiol and progesterone per cycle timing as indicated.\\n\\n3. Vitamin B12 deficiency (B12 155 pg/mL)\\n   - Urgent: Discussed replacement options with patient.\\n     - If no neurologic symptoms and clinician prefers oral therapy: start cyanocobalamin 1,000–2,000 mcg PO daily.\\n     - If any neurologic symptoms or malabsorption suspected: start cyanocobalamin 1,000 mcg IM weekly ×4 then 1,000 mcg IM monthly (notify physician for IM authorization).\\n   - Order methylmalonic acid (MMA) and intrinsic factor antibody and/or parietal cell antibody to assess etiology.\\n   - Review CBC (macrocytosis) and repeat B12 and MMA in 4–8 weeks after initiation.\\n   - Notify physician within 24–72 hours to confirm replacement route.\\n\\n4. Vitamin D insufficiency (25-OH 21.9 ng/mL)\\n   - Start vitamin D3 repletion: cholecalciferol 5,000 IU PO daily (Metagenics D3 5,000 + K as per clinic protocol) with a meal; order baseline serum calcium.\\n   - Recheck 25‑OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks after starting therapy.\\n\\n5. Cardiometabolic risk / Insulin resistance\\n   - Lifestyle interventions:\\n     - Mediterranean-style diet, reduce saturated fat and added sugars, increase fiber.\\n     - Exercise: ≥150 min/week moderate aerobic activity plus 2 sessions/week resistance training.\\n     - Weight management counseling if BMI elevated (goal 5–10% weight loss if applicable).\\n     - Smoking cessation and limit alcohol; sleep hygiene and stress reduction.\\n   - Labs and monitoring:\\n     - Order fasting insulin and fasting glucose (for HOMA-IR) now to quantify hyperinsulinemia.\\n     - Repeat fasting lipid panel and hs-CRP in ~3 months after lifestyle interventions.\\n   - Pharmacologic:\\n     - No statin initiated at this time given low 10‑yr ASCVD risk and age; consider future therapy if ApoB/LDL/hs-CRP increase or other risk factors develop. Discuss with clinician if warranted.\\n\\n6. Hematocrit 44.7% (borderline)\\n   - Instruct patient to increase oral hydration and recheck CBC/hematocrit in 1–2 weeks.\\n   - If persistently elevated on repeat testing, evaluate for polycythemia / hemoconcentration causes.\\n\\n7. Supplements (as recommended)\\n   - High-priority (discussed and recommended):\\n     - GlutaClear®: 2 capsules daily (per clinic guidance)\\n     - OmegaGenics® Fish Oil Neuro 1000: 1 softgel once daily (may increase to 1 softgel twice daily based on clinician preference)\\n     - UltraFlora® Healthy Weight with Akkermansia: 1 capsule daily\\n     - AdvaClear®: 2 capsules twice daily\\n     - Intrinsi B12-Folate™: 1 tablet daily (adjunct only; replacement dose per B12 deficiency plan above — supplements do not replace therapeutic B12 dosing)\\n   - Medium-priority:\\n     - NutraGems® CoQ10 300: 1 chewable gel daily\\n     - Vitamin D3 5,000 IU + K: 1 softgel daily with meal (as above)\\n     - Magtein® Magnesium L-Threonate: 3 capsules daily (divided doses)\\n   - Note: Supplements discussed as adjunctive; document allergies and verify no contraindications with current medications. Obtain clinician approval for prescription-grade products if clinic protocol requires.\\n\\n8. Orders and scheduling\\n   - Labs to order now:\\n     - AM repeat total testosterone and free testosterone (fasting, morning) — repeat in 4–6 weeks if exogenous androgen stopped sooner.\\n     - DHEA-S (AM), 17-hydroxyprogesterone (AM)\\n     - Fasting insulin and fasting glucose (HOMA-IR calculation)\\n     - Methylmalonic acid (MMA), intrinsic factor antibody (± parietal cell Ab)\\n     - Pregnancy test (if patient of childbearing potential and status unknown)\\n     - Baseline serum calcium (before vitamin D repletion)\\n     - Repeat 25‑OH vitamin D at 8–12 weeks after starting therapy\\n     - Repeat lipid panel and hs-CRP in ~3 months\\n     - CBC for hematocrit in 1–2 weeks\\n   - Referrals:\\n     - Endocrinology: if testosterone remains elevated, DHEA-S abnormal, or rapid virilization/concern for neoplasm.\\n     - Reproductive endocrinology / OB-GYN: if anovulation confirmed and fertility desired.\\n     - GI or hematology: if malabsorption suspected or uncertainty about B12 replacement route.\\n     - Imaging: pelvic ultrasound (transvaginal) if PCOS or ovarian pathology suspected; adrenal/pelvic CT or MRI urgently if rapid virilization or imaging indicated by endocrine.\\n   - Documentation & communication:\\n     - Notify treating clinician for confirmation/authorization of B12 replacement route, initiation of vitamin D repletion (if required), and any plan to stop exogenous testosterone (if present).\\n     - If urgent red-flag findings develop (rapid virilization or neurologic deficits), contact physician immediately and arrange urgent imaging/referral.\\n\\nFOLLOW-UP:\\n- Immediate (today / within 24–48 hours):\\n  - Begin vitamin D repletion (D3 5,000 IU PO daily) and baseline serum calcium.\\n  - Begin B12 replacement per clinician-approved route (oral cyanocobalamin 1,000–2,000 mcg PO daily if no neuro signs; IM protocol if neuro signs or malabsorption).\\n  - Increase oral hydration; repeat hematocrit in 1–2 weeks.\\n  - Obtain ordered labs listed above.\\n- Short term (1–6 weeks):\\n  - Repeat androgen panel (AM) 4–6 weeks after stopping exogenous androgens or after medication change.\\n  - Review MMA and intrinsic factor Ab results and adjust B12 plan as indicated.\\n  - Review fasting insulin/glucose results and determine need for further metabolic management.\\n- Intermediate (8–12 weeks):\\n  - Recheck 25‑OH vitamin D.\\n  - Reassess lipids and hs-CRP ~3 months after lifestyle interventions.\\n- Clinic follow-up: schedule return visit or telehealth appointment in 4–8 weeks to review repeat labs and adjust treatment plan; sooner if abnormal results require expedited action.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\nDate: 03/31/2026.", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": true, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": true, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": true, "high_nonHDL": true, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "preventRisk": {"riskCategory": "low", "tenYearASCVD": 0, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "PREVENT calculator is validated for ages 30-79 years. Patient is 26 years old. Use clinical judgment for risk assessment.", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0, "ageValidFor30Year": false, "tenYearHFPercentage": "N/A", "tenYearHeartFailure": 0, "statinRecommendation": "Age outside validated range - use clinical judgment", "tenYearCVDPercentage": "N/A", "tenYearASCVDPercentage": "N/A"}, "supplements": [{"dose": "2 capsules daily", "name": "GlutaClear®", "caution": "Glutathione and antioxidant support formula. Contains NAC, alpha-lipoic acid, and green tea catechins. Supports cellular defense against oxidative damage.", "category": "detox", "priority": "high", "rationale": "GlutaClear supports glutathione production and antioxidant defense with NAC, alpha-lipoic acid, and green tea catechins. Indicated when there is evidence of oxidative stress, inflammatory burden, or metabolic dysfunction requiring enhanced cellular defense. While AdvaClear provides broader liver detoxification support, GlutaClear specifically targets glutathione pathways and antioxidant capacity.", "indication": "Oxidative stress phenotype (glutathione/antioxidant support indicated); Inflammatory burden (antioxidant defense support); Insulin resistance (oxidative metabolic stress); hs-CRP 4 mg/L (high inflammatory/oxidative burden)", "phenotypes": ["Oxidative Stress Burden", "Inflammatory Burden", "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "This supplement boosts your body's master antioxidant, glutathione, which protects your 