loss resistance.", "indication": "Gut-microbiome support phenotype; Weight gain / weight loss resistance; Bloating reported (gut-metabolic dysfunction)", "phenotypes": ["Gut-Microbiome Support"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This specialized probiotic contains Akkermansia, a beneficial gut bacteria linked to healthy metabolism and weight management. It supports your gut barrier, helps manage metabolic inflammation, and works with your body's natural weight regulation systems.", "supportingFindings": ["Gut-microbiome support phenotype", "Weight gain / weight loss resistance", "Bloating reported (gut-metabolic dysfunction)"]}, {"dose": "1 tablet daily", "name": "HerWellness™ Estrovera®", "caution": "Clinically studied ERr 731 rhubarb extract for vasomotor symptom relief. Non-hormonal option. Effects typically noticed within 4 weeks.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Estrovera provides clinically studied ERr 731 Siberian rhubarb extract specifically for vasomotor symptom relief (hot flashes, night sweats). Best suited for women with active vasomotor symptoms who need non-hormonal support.", "indication": "Age 42 (perimenopause window); Mood changes reported; Irritability reported", "phenotypes": ["Menopausal Transition"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This plant-based supplement is clinically proven to help with hot flashes, night sweats, and related sleep and mood changes. It works without hormones and most women notice improvement within 4 weeks.", "supportingFindings": ["Age 42 (perimenopause window)", "Mood changes reported", "Irritability reported"]}, {"dose": "2 capsules twice daily", "name": "AdvaClear®", "caution": "Broad-spectrum phase I/II liver detoxification support. Contains NAC, green tea extract, milk thistle, and B-vitamins for biotransformation. Best taken with food.", "category": "detox", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "AdvaClear provides broad-spectrum phase I and phase II liver detoxification support with NAC, green tea extract, watercress, and B-vitamins. Designed for metabolic clearance support, biotransformation of hormones and environmental compounds, and hepatic stress. Appropriate when liver markers are borderline, estrogen clearance is sluggish, or metabolic dysfunction is present.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance pattern (hepatic detox pathways needed for clearance); Weight gain (metabolic clearance factor); Bloating (GI-hepatic axis)", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement supports your liver's natural detoxification processes. Your results suggest your body could benefit from help processing hormones, metabolic byproducts, and other compounds. It provides nutrients that support both phases of your liver's detox pathways.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance pattern (hepatic detox pathways needed for clearance)", "Weight gain (metabolic clearance factor)", "Bloating (GI-hepatic axis)"]}, {"dose": "1 soft chew during times of stress", "name": "HerWellness™ Rapid Stress Relief", "caution": "Fast-acting L-Theanine and Lactium formula. Non-drowsy. Promotes calm within 1 hour. Contains milk.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "low", "rationale": "HerWellness Rapid Stress Relief provides fast-acting stress support with L-Theanine (200mg) and Lactium. Promotes calm within 1 hour without drowsiness.", "indication": "Irritability reported; Mood changes reported", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This fast-acting supplement helps your body respond to stress more calmly. It promotes relaxation within about an hour and can be taken as needed without causing drowsiness.", "supportingFindings": ["Irritability reported", "Mood changes reported"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 8, "apoBValue": 58, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "normal", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 0.5529939815043807, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": false}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "consider", "rationale": "Lipid levels meet threshold for CAC consideration. CAC may be useful if risk level is uncertain or patient is hesitant about statin.", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\nYour results show a perimenopausal, hypoestrogenic pattern: estradiol is low at 32 pg/mL with FSH 39.3 mIU/mL, which fits the “crash phase” of the menopausal transition and explains symptoms like vaginal dryness. You also have a low progesterone (0.41 ng/mL) and low total testosterone (16.7 ng/dL) despite HRT. You have severe vitamin D deficiency (25-OH vitamin D 18.3 ng/mL). Cardiac risk is low (10-year ASCVD risk 0.6%, 10-year total CVD risk 0.9%, 30-year CVD risk 7.1%), but hs-CRP is 1.7 mg/L (borderline inflammation). Hematocrit is mildly high at 45.7% (could reflect mild dehydration). Overall your thyroid and iron stores look good.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Hemoglobin 15.1 g/dL (normal)\\n- Ferritin 82.8 ng/mL (adequate iron stores)\\n- TSH 1.95 mIU/L (thyroid function normal)\\n- LDL 81 mg/dL, HDL 61 mg/dL, Triglycerides 45 mg/dL (lipids favorable)\\n- Hemoglobin A1c 4.9% (normal blood sugar)\\n- eGFR 93 mL/min (kidney function normal)\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + actions)\\n1) Low estrogen and bone health: Estradiol 32 pg/mL (below ~40 pg/mL bone-protection threshold). Actions: discuss estrogen optimization with your clinician (transdermal or other HRT options) because estrogen helps prevent bone loss; start weight-bearing and resistance exercise 3x/week (walking, stair-climbing, squats) and ensure dietary calcium ~1,000–1,200 mg/day from dairy or supplements.\\n2) Progesterone 0.41 ng/mL and anovulation risk: If you were in the luteal phase when tested, this suggests anovulatory cycles. Action: document cycle day for future tests and review HRT dosing with your provider within 4–8 weeks.\\n3) Low testosterone: Total testosterone 16.7 ng/dL. Actions: prioritize 7–8 hours sleep nightly, add strength training 2–3x/week, and use stress reduction (15–20 minutes/day meditation or brisk walking). Discuss hormone options with your clinician if symptoms persist.\\n4) Vitamin D deficiency: 25-OH Vitamin D 18.3 ng/mL. Action: start repletion—either vitamin D3 50,000 IU weekly for 6–8 weeks OR 5,000–6,000 IU daily, then maintenance (1,000–2,000 IU/day). Recheck level in 8–12 weeks.\\n5) Hematocrit 45.7%: hydrate well (aim for 2–3 L fluids/day) and repeat CBC in 2–4 weeks.\\n6) B12 232 pg/mL (low-normal): consider oral B12 1,000 mcg daily if fatigue or deficiency symptoms; recheck in 8–12 weeks.\\n\\nCardiovascular plan (low risk 10-year ASCVD 0.6%): Continue heart-healthy habits—aim for 150 minutes/week moderate aerobic activity (brisk walking), Mediterranean-style eating (veg, whole grains, olive oil, fish), limit processed foods, maintain healthy weight.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Review HRT and bone protection with clinician in 4–8 weeks.\\n- Start vitamin D repletion now; recheck 25-OH D in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Repeat hormones with documented cycle day or after any HRT changes in 6–12 weeks.\\n- Repeat CBC after hydration in 2–4 weeks; check B12 after supplementation in 8–12 weeks.\\n\\nYou’re doing many things well—these targeted steps can help your symptoms, bone health, and energy. Please call to schedule a follow-up to make a clear plan.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 15.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 45.7, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Slightly elevated hematocrit - may indicate dehydration.", "recommendation": "Encourage hydration. Repeat if persistent.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 93.6, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 93.6 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 209, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 32, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol below bone protection threshold (<40 pg/mL). Bone resorption increases below 40-60 pg/mL.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase estrogen dose. Target 60-100 pg/mL. Current level inadequate for bone protection.", "referenceRange": "60-100 pg/mL (HRT goal, >40 for bone)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.41, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone below HRT therapeutic range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase progesterone dose. Target 8-10 ng/mL for optimal endometrial protection.", "referenceRange": "8-10 ng/mL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 39.3, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 16.7, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Low testosterone despite HRT.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase testosterone dose. Target 75-125 ng/dL for optimal results.", "referenceRange": "75-125 ng/dL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.95, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 82.8, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 18.3, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Severe Vitamin D deficiency (≤20 ng/mL). Associated with fatigue, bone loss, and immune dysfunction.", "recommendation": "High-dose repletion: Metagenics D3 10,000 + K daily for 8-12 weeks. Recheck levels after repletion.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 232, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "Low-normal B12.", "recommendation": "Consider supplementation especially if symptomatic.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 4.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 81, "status": "normal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 61, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 45, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.8845888179334978, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 0.9% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.5529939815043807, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 0.6%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.40171438727796677, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.4%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 7.057428116327981, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 7.1%, ASCVD 4.0%, Heart Failure 3.8%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 14, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 17, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 93, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.81, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 58, "status": "normal", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Apo B at optimal level.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 8, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 338, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 140, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 6.57, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 1.7, "status": "borderline", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Borderline hs-CRP - moderate cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Continue monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.41, "status": "borderline", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Low Progesterone (Phase Not Documented)", "interpretation": "Progesterone is 0.41 ng/mL. Without documented cycle phase or sufficient estradiol/FSH context to identify a specific pattern, interpretation is limited. If drawn in the luteal phase, this level would indicate anovulation — a perimenopause marker.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Document cycle phase for accurate interpretation. If the patient is mid-cycle or in the luteal phase, this suggests anovulation. Consider symptoms: sleep disruption, anxiety, irregular cycles. If symptomatic, empiric progesterone (100 mg nightly) is reasonable. PATIENT EDUCATION: Knowing where you are in your menstrual cycle helps us fully interpret this result. Please note when your last period started so we can better understand your hormone picture.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase; <5 ng/mL suggests anovulation if luteal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 32, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Hypoestrogenic Pattern", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 32 pg/mL with FSH of 39.3 mIU/mL indicates hypoestrogenic perimenopause — the \\"crash phase.\\" Estrogen production has dropped significantly and the brain is signaling strongly (elevated FSH) trying to stimulate the ovaries.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Transdermal estradiol 0.025–0.05 mg is appropriate replacement therapy. Add micronized progesterone for uterine protection and sleep support. Frame this as hormone replacement, not stimulation — we are restoring what the body needs. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen has dropped to a level that can cause symptoms like hot flashes, night sweats, brain fog, and mood changes. Replacing estrogen with a patch or gel can relieve these symptoms and protect your long-term health. Progesterone is added to protect your uterus and help with sleep.", "referenceRange": "E2 <50 with FSH >10 indicates estrogen deficiency"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 39.3, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Perimenopause Transition", "interpretation": "FSH of 39.3 mIU/mL indicates perimenopause (menopausal transition). Symptoms present: vaginal dryness. Elevated FSH reflects decreased ovarian function and reduced estrogen production. This is a normal part of reproductive aging but may benefit from hormone optimization if symptomatic.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: If symptomatic, consider low-dose transdermal estradiol + micronized progesterone (if uterus intact). Perimenopause is characterized by fluctuating hormones. Steady hormone delivery via patch can smooth out peaks and valleys. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your lab results show you are in perimenopause — the transition phase before menopause. Hormone levels can swing widely during this time, causing symptoms. Treatment options are available to help you feel better.", "referenceRange": "FSH <25 mIU/mL premenopausal; ≥25 suggests transition; ≥40 menopausal"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 57.6, "status": "normal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 57.6 nmol/L is within normal range. SHBG regulates bioavailable sex hormones by binding testosterone and estradiol.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 2, "status": "normal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 2 pg/mL is within normal functional range.", "recommendation": "C