each the ideal range for bone and immune health.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 43.2 ng/mL (suboptimal, target ≥60)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "generally_recommended", "indicated": true, "rationale": "Persistent non-HDL ≥160 mg/dL (167 mg/dL) indicates elevated atherogenic lipoprotein burden. Statin discussion recommended."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": true, "severity": "high", "recommendation": "Triglycerides 200-499 mg/dL (249 mg/dL). Recommend intensive lifestyle modification first. Rule out secondary causes (diabetes, hypothyroidism, medications, alcohol). If on statin and TG persists ≥150, consider prescription omega-3 (icosapent ethyl) for patients with established ASCVD or diabetes with additional risk factors."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "consider", "rationale": "Borderline lipid levels with multiple risk enhancers (low HDL, elevated triglycerides). Consider CAC to refine risk assessment.", "recommended": true}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall summary (plain English)\\n- Your labs show that you are in menopause: FSH is 70.9 mIU/mL and estradiol is 5 pg/mL, which confirms decreased ovarian estrogen production. Hemoglobin is good at 13.8 g/dL and blood sugar control is normal (A1c 5.0%). Your 10‑year heart risk is low (10‑year ASCVD 2.4%, 10‑year total CVD 4.0%), but your 30‑year CVD risk is higher (23.7%), so addressing cholesterol and lifestyle now can lower long‑term risk.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Hemoglobin 13.8 g/dL and hematocrit 43.4% (normal).\\n- Vitamin B12 751 pg/mL (good).\\n- Hemoglobin A1c 5.0% (normal blood sugar).\\n- Liver tests AST 31 U/L and ALT 22 U/L (normal).\\n- Kidney function eGFR 95 mL/min, creatinine 0.75 mg/dL (normal).\\n- Vitamin D 25‑OH 43.2 ng/mL (adequate but suboptimal).\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific numbers + actions)\\n1) Lipids: triglycerides 249 mg/dL (high) and HDL 44 mg/dL (slightly low). LDL 117 mg/dL (near optimal).\\n   - Diet: Add 25–30 g fiber daily (example: oatmeal for breakfast, beans/legumes at lunch, vegetables at dinner).\\n   - Omega‑3s: Eat 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n   - Lower saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter and full‑fat cheese.\\n   - Exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking (or equivalent) daily.\\n   - These steps are recommended because your triglycerides >150 mg/dL increase long‑term risk despite a low 10‑year ASCVD (2.4%). Statin is generally not indicated now; lifestyle first.\\n\\n2) Hormones & bone health: FSH 70.9 mIU/mL, estradiol 5 pg/mL, progesterone 0.19 ng/mL, total testosterone 6.9 ng/dL (low).\\n   - Menopause: Low estrogen can raise bone loss risk. Start weight‑bearing exercise (walking, hiking, dancing) 30 minutes most days + strength training 2×/week.\\n   - Bone support: Aim for ~1,000–1,200 mg calcium/day from diet and continue vitamin D (your 43.2 ng/mL is OK); consider 800–1,000 IU vitamin D daily if your provider recommends.\\n   - Low testosterone actions: prioritize 7–8 hours sleep nightly, strength training 2–3×/week, and stress reduction (walking, meditation).\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Start the lifestyle steps above now. Recheck a fasting lipid panel in 3 months to assess triglycerides and HDL.\\n- Discuss menopause symptoms and options (including hormone therapy) with your provider if you develop hot flashes, sleep trouble, or vaginal symptoms.\\n- Consider a bone density (DEXA) discussion within 6–12 months because of confirmed menopause.\\n- Repeat vitamin D in 12 months if supplementing; repeat TSH only if symptoms develop (current TSH 3.95 mIU/L is within range).\\n\\nIf you want, we can make a 3‑month plan together (meals, exercise schedule, and follow‑up labs).", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 13.8, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 43.4, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 95.4, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 95.4 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 237, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 5, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.19, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpr