inflammation. Pregnancy status and current use of combined oral contraceptives, systemic estrogen, or other HRT not documented today — confirm prior to hormone decisions. Menstrual cycle day at time of draw not provided; patient presumed premenopausal. No current complaints reported to staff related to libido, energy, or virilization in chart; patient to be queried regarding symptoms (low libido, sexual dysfunction, persistent fatigue) prior to any androgen therapy.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vital signs: none recorded in chart at time of lab review.\\n- Laboratory results (04/01/2026):\\n  - CBC:\\n    - Hemoglobin 13.7 g/dL (normal)\\n    - Hematocrit 41.1 % (normal)\\n    - MCV 86.5 fL (normal)\\n    - Platelets 301 K/uL (normal)\\n  - Basic metabolic / renal:\\n    - Creatinine 0.73 mg/dL (normal)\\n    - eGFR 111 mL/min/1.73m2 (normal)\\n  - Liver tests:\\n    - AST 13 U/L (normal)\\n    - ALT 14 U/L (normal)\\n  - Iron studies:\\n    - Ferritin 52.1 ng/mL (normal)\\n    - Serum iron 43 ug/dL (normal)\\n    - TIBC 378 ug/dL (normal)\\n  - Diabetes / metabolic:\\n    - Hemoglobin A1c 4.9 % (normal)\\n    - Insulin Resistance Screening: 2/6 markers positive — Moderate likelihood of insulin resistance (Phenotype: Visceral/Metabolic Insulin Resistance) [ABNORMAL]\\n  - Lipids / cardiovascular:\\n    - LDL cholesterol 90 mg/dL (normal)\\n    - HDL cholesterol 61 mg/dL (normal)\\n    - Triglycerides 61 mg/dL (normal)\\n    - Lipoprotein(a) 20 mg/dL (normal)\\n    - Apolipoprotein B 117 mg/dL — Borderline elevated [BORDERLINE]\\n  - Inflammatory:\\n    - hs-CRP 3.3 mg/L — Elevated; low-grade systemic inflammation; increased cardiovascular risk [ABNORMAL]\\n  - Hormones:\\n    - Estradiol 38 pg/mL (normal)\\n    - Progesterone 0.25 ng/mL (normal)\\n    - FSH 10.3 mIU/mL (normal)\\n    - TSH 1.0 mIU/L (normal)\\n    - Total testosterone 13.3 ng/dL — Borderline low [BORDERLINE]\\n    - Free testosterone 1 pg/mL (normal)\\n    - SHBG 66.9 nmol/L (normal)\\n  - Vitamins / micronutrients:\\n    - Vitamin D (25‑OH) 37.2 ng/mL — Insufficient per clinic target (31–40 ng/mL) [BORDERLINE]\\n    - Vitamin B12 763 pg/mL (normal)\\n    - Folate 20 ng/mL (normal)\\n  - Other:\\n    - Ferritin as above (normal)\\n  - Risk scores:\\n    - 10‑yr Total CVD risk (AHA PREVENT): 0.3% (low)\\n    - 10‑yr ASCVD: 0.2% (low)\\n    - 10‑yr Heart Failure risk: 0.1% (low)\\n    - 30‑yr CVD risk (PREVENT): 2.3%\\n    - Sleep apnea risk (STOP-BANG): 0 points (low)\\n- Flags:\\n  - Abnormal: hs-CRP 3.3 mg/L; Insulin Resistance Screening: moderate likelihood (2/6 markers).\\n  - Borderline: Total testosterone 13.3 ng/dL; Vitamin D 25‑OH 37.2 ng/mL; ApoB 117 mg/dL.\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Elevated hs-CRP 3.3 mg/L — low-grade systemic inflammation; correlates with increased cardiovascular inflammatory risk despite low calculated 10‑yr ASCVD. Must exclude acute/extrinsic causes (infection, dental work, injury). If persistent, indicates need for intensified CV risk modification and possible specialist input. (Physician notification recommended if no acute cause.)\\n2. Moderate likelihood insulin resistance (2/6 markers positive; phenotype: visceral/metabolic) — suggests hyperinsulinemia physiology and metabolic risk despite normal A1c. Consider confirmatory fasting insulin and fasting glucose for HOMA-IR calculation. Initiate lifestyle measures to reduce insulin resistance.\\n3. Apolipoprotein B 117 mg/dL — borderline elevated atherogenic particle burden despite acceptable LDL; represents residual cardiovascular risk marker. Lifestyle modification first-line; reassess fasting lipid panel with ApoB in 3 months. Consider lipid specialist if persists ≥110–120 mg/dL or if additional risk emerges.\\n4. Total testosterone borderline low (13.3 ng/dL) with normal free testosterone and normal SHBG — biochemical androgen deficiency not established. Treatment should be symptom-driven. If patient reports persistent clinically significant symptoms (low libido, sexual dysfunction, refractory fatigue) and desires therapy, discuss a physician-authorized trial of low-dose transdermal testosterone with informed consent and monitoring. Contraindicated until pregnancy status and contraception reviewed.\\n5. Vitamin D insufficient for clinic hormone-optimization target (25‑OH 37.2 ng/mL) — suboptimal; supplementation recommended.\\n6. Routine labs otherwise within normal limits (CBC, LFTs, kidney function, A1c, standard lipid panel, thyroid, B12, folate).\\n7. No red-flag acute laboratory findings at this time. Urgent physician notification recommended if hs-CRP persists >3 mg/L without an acute cause, prior to initiating testosterone, or if ApoB remains elevated despite interventions.\\n\\nPLAN:\\n1. Elevated hs-CRP 3.3 mg/L\\n   - Immediate: Ask patient about recent infection, dental procedures, trauma, or inflammatory symptoms. Document review.\\n   - If any acute trigger present → defer CV interpretation and repeat hs-CRP after recovery.\\n   - If no acute trigger → notify supervising physician for review and counseling.\\n   - Repeat hs-CRP in 6–12 weeks after addressing possible acute causes and initiating lifestyle changes.\\n   - Reinforce CV risk reduction (see #4). Consider earlier lipid/ApoB reassessment if clinician directs.\\n2. Insulin resistance (moderate likelihood)\\n   - Order fasting insulin and fasting glucose with calculation of HOMA-IR to confirm hyperinsulinemia physiology (physician to sign order).\\n   - Implement lifestyle interventions now: target 5–10% weight loss if applicable; Mediterranean-style diet (reduce processed/refined carbs), caloric quality improvement, increased fiber, limit sugar and refined carbohydrates.\\n   - Exercise: ≥150 minutes/week moderate aerobic + ≥2 resistance sessions/week.\\n   - Recommend UltraFlora® Healthy Weight with Akkermansia 1 capsule daily (medium-priority supplement) as per clinic suggestions.\\n   - Start GlutaClear® 2 capsules daily and OmegaGenics® Fish Oil Neuro 1000 one softgel daily (high-priority supplements) per clinic suggestions — document allergies and obtain patient acceptance; supplements are adjunctive to lifestyle.\\n   - Reassess metabolic markers and symptoms in 3 months.\\n3. Apolipoprotein B 117 mg/dL (borderline)\\n   - Lifestyle-first: dietary counseling (Mediterranean-style), weight management, exercise, smoking cessation if applicable. Offer dietitian referral.\\n   - Recheck fasting lipid panel with ApoB in ~3 months (ensure fasting status).\\n   - If ApoB persists ≥110–120 mg/dL or additional risk factors present, consider cardiology/lipidology referral for discussion of pharmacotherapy (statin ± other agents) per physician decision.\\n4. Testosterone borderline low (13.3 ng/dL) with normal free T and SHBG\\n   - Counseling: Explained that total testosterone is borderline low but free testosterone and SHBG are normal; biochemical androgen deficiency not proven. Treatment only if patient symptomatic and after physician discussion/consent.\\n   - If patient symptomatic and physician-authorized trial desired:\\n     - Recommended starting regimen (physician order required): transdermal testosterone approximately 300 mcg/day (0.3 mg/day) applied to abdomen/thigh/gluteal area per clinic HRT protocol. Alternative starting range 300–600 mcg/day depending on protocol.\\n     - Contraindications/precautions: confirm nonpregnant status, discuss contraception/breastfeeding risk; rule out active hormone-sensitive malignancy and uncontrolled CVD. Physician to counsel on risks including virilization.\\n     - Monitoring: check total testosterone, free testosterone, SHBG 8–12 weeks after initiation or dose change, then at 6 months. Clinical review at 4–8 weeks for adverse effects. If no symptomatic improvement by 12 weeks with appropriate levels, reassess and consider endocrinology referral.\\n   - If not pursuing therapy now: re-evaluate symptoms and consider repeating total testosterone in 3 months or sooner if symptoms change; document cycle day and OCP/HRT status prior to recheck.\\n5. Vitamin D insufficiency (25‑OH 37.2 ng/mL)\\n   - Start Vitamin D3 5,000 IU + K daily (Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K) with a meal, unless contraindicated. Document patient medication/supplement allergies and counsel on adherence.\\n   - Recheck 25‑OH vitamin D level in 8–12 weeks after initiation. Clinic target for hormone optimization: ≥60 ng/mL.\\n6. Preventive CV risk measures and lifestyle counseling\\n   - Provide lifestyle counseling today: Mediterranean-style diet, increased physical activity, weight loss goals if applicable, sleep hygiene, stress reduction, limit alcohol, smoking cessation support if active smoker.\\n   - Provide/offer referrals: dietitian for structured nutrition plan; behavioral health/CBT for stress/sleep if needed.\\n7. Follow-up testing and timeline (place orders as physician directs)\\n   - Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (for HOMA‑IR) — ASAP.\\n   - Repeat hs-CRP in 6–12 weeks (earlier if clinician recommends).\\n   - Repeat 25‑OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks after starting supplementation.\\n   - If testosterone started: Total testosterone, free testosterone, SHBG at 8–12 weeks after initiation or dose change, then at 6 months.\\n   - Fasting lipid panel + ApoB in 3 months.\\n   - Repeat any labs sooner if new symptoms develop.\\n8. Referrals / escalation\\n   - Cardiology/lipidology referral if ApoB remains elevated despite lifestyle measures or if persistent hs-CRP >3 mg/L without a reversible cause (physician discretion).\\n   - Endocrinology if complex androgen evaluation needed or difficulty with testosterone titration.\\n   - Gynecology for contraception/fertility counseling prior to HRT initiation if applicable.\\n9. Communication and documentation\\n   - Confirm and document current medications (including OCP/HRT, supplements), pregnancy status, and menstrual cycle day prior to any hormone therapy decision.\\n   - Notify ordering physician/supervising clinician regarding elevated hs-CRP and ApoB and request sign-off for any testosterone prescription or additional testing as outlined.\\n\\nDisposition / Follow-up:\\n- Immediate: Confirm pregnancy status and current estrogen/HRT/OCP use; provide vitamin D prescription/supplement instructions and lifestyle counseling today. Notify physician of hs-CRP 3.3 mg/L and ApoB 117 mg/dL for review.\\n- Short-term (within 2 weeks): Physician decision regarding fasting insulin/HOMA-IR order and whether to trial testosterone if patient symptomatic and desires therapy.\\n- Recheck schedule:\\n  - 6–12 weeks: hs-CRP repeat if indicated; symptom check.\\n  - 8–12 weeks: vitamin D recheck after supplementation; testosterone panel if started/changed.\\n  - 3 months: fasting lipid panel + ApoB recheck; metabolic follow-up.\\n- Routine follow-up appointment: 3–6 months per clinic protocol or sooner based on physician plan.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\nDate: 04/01/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": true, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": true, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "preventRisk": {"ldlGoal": "No specific LDL target; focus on risk-based statin intensity", "riskCategory": "low", "tenYearASCVD": 0.002030036544259925, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0.002957308862613175, "thirtyYearASCVD": 0.01445380019661985, "ageValidFor30Year": true, "thirtyYearTotalCVD": 0.022937468244724202, "tenYearHFPercentage": "0.1%", "tenYearHeartFailure": 0.001409410250136067, "statinRecom