on": "High-DHA omega-3 (750mg DHA, 250mg EPA) for brain and heart health. Lemon-flavored, no fishy taste. Contains calamari - avoid if shellfish allergy.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "high", "rationale": "OmegaGenics Fish Oil Neuro 1000 provides concentrated DHA and EPA to reduce triglycerides, support brain function, cardiovascular health, and reduce systemic inflammation.", "indication": "hs-CRP 3.3 mg/L (inflammatory); ApoB 117 mg/dL (elevated)", "phenotypes": ["Inflammatory Burden", "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "This high-quality fish oil provides omega-3 fatty acids that help reduce inflammation, support your heart and brain health, and may help improve your cholesterol profile.", "supportingFindings": ["hs-CRP 3.3 mg/L (inflammatory)", "ApoB 117 mg/dL (elevated)"]}, {"dose": "1 softgel daily with meal", "name": "D3 5,000 + K", "caution": "Repletion dose for deficiency/insufficiency. Contains vitamin K2 for optimal calcium metabolism. Recheck levels in 8-12 weeks.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "D3 5,000 + K provides vitamin D3 with K2 for repletion of deficiency/insufficiency. Target ≥60 ng/mL for bone, immune, and cardiovascular support.", "indication": "Vitamin D 37.2 ng/mL (insufficient)", "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D is below optimal range. This supplement will help restore your levels to support bone health, immune function, and overall wellness.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 37.2 ng/mL (insufficient)"]}, {"dose": "2 capsules twice daily", "name": "AdvaClear®", "caution": "Broad-spectrum phase I/II liver detoxification support. Contains NAC, green tea extract, milk thistle, and B-vitamins for biotransformation. Best taken with food.", "category": "detox", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "AdvaClear provides broad-spectrum phase I and phase II liver detoxification support with NAC, green tea extract, watercress, and B-vitamins. Designed for metabolic clearance support, biotransformation of hormones and environmental compounds, and hepatic stress. Appropriate when liver markers are borderline, estrogen clearance is sluggish, or metabolic dysfunction is present.", "indication": "Oxidative stress burden (liver biotransformation support); Inflammatory burden (metabolic clearance support); Insulin resistance / metabolic dysfunction (hepatic metabolic support); hs-CRP 3.3 mg/L (inflammatory metabolic burden)", "phenotypes": ["Oxidative Stress Burden", "Inflammatory Burden", "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement supports your liver's natural detoxification processes. Your results suggest your body could benefit from help processing hormones, metabolic byproducts, and other compounds. It provides nutrients that support both phases of your liver's detox pathways.", "supportingFindings": ["Oxidative stress burden (liver biotransformation support)", "Inflammatory burden (metabolic clearance support)", "Insulin resistance / metabolic dysfunction (hepatic metabolic support)", "hs-CRP 3.3 mg/L (inflammatory metabolic burden)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Healthy Weight with Akkermansia", "caution": "Contains Akkermansia muciniphila and Bifidobacterium lactis B420 for metabolic and gut barrier support. Refrigerate after opening for maximum potency.", "category": "metabolic", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "UltraFlora Healthy Weight with Akkermansia provides targeted probiotic support for the gut-metabolic axis. Akkermansia muciniphila supports gut barrier integrity, metabolic health, and healthy weight management. Indicated for insulin resistance, metabolic syndrome features, visceral adiposity, and weight loss resistance.", "indication": "Insulin resistance / visceral adiposity phenotype (gut-metabolic axis support); Inflammatory phenotype (gut-immune axis); ApoB 117 mg/dL (atherogenic metabolic pattern); hs-CRP 3.3 mg/L (metabolic inflammation)", "phenotypes": ["Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "Inflammatory Burden"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This specialized probiotic contains Akkermansia, a beneficial gut bacteria linked to healthy metabolism and weight management. It supports your gut barrier, helps manage metabolic inflammation, and works with your body's natural weight regulation systems.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance / visceral adiposity phenotype (gut-metabolic axis support)", "Inflammatory phenotype (gut-immune axis)", "ApoB 117 mg/dL (atherogenic metabolic pattern)", "hs-CRP 3.3 mg/L (metabolic inflammation)"]}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Essential for patients on statins.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "ApoB 117 mg/dL (cardiovascular support); hs-CRP 3.3 mg/L", "phenotypes": ["Inflammatory Burden", "Oxidative Stress Burden"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["ApoB 117 mg/dL (cardiovascular support)", "hs-CRP 3.3 mg/L"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 20, "apoBValue": 117, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "borderline", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 0.20300365442599252, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": true}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "generally_recommended", "indicated": true, "rationale": "ApoB ≥90 mg/dL (117 mg/dL) with risk enhancers (elevated hs-CRP, elevated ApoB). Statin discussion recommended."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\nYour labs show many strengths but a few areas to improve. Your 10-year ASCVD risk is very low at 0.2% and 10-year total CVD risk is 0.3%, which is reassuring. However, hs-CRP is elevated at 3.3 mg/L (this is a marker of inflammation and is associated with increased cardiovascular risk). You also have borderline low total testosterone at 13.3 ng/dL and vitamin D is slightly insufficient at 37.2 ng/mL. Apolipoprotein B is borderline elevated at 117 mg/dL, which can reflect the number of atherogenic cholesterol particles even though your LDL is good.\\n\\nPositive findings (what’s working well)\\n- Hemoglobin 13.7 g/dL and hematocrit 41.1% — iron status looks healthy.\\n- Ferritin 52.1 ng/mL — iron stores are in the optimal range (>50).\\n- TSH 1.0 mIU/L — thyroid function is normal.\\n- Hemoglobin A1c 4.9% — excellent blood sugar control.\\n- LDL 90 mg/dL, HDL 61 mg/dL, triglycerides 61 mg/dL — favorable cholesterol profile.\\n- Lp(a) 20 mg/dL and eGFR 111 mL/min — good kidney function and low genetic risk marker.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + actions)\\n- hs-CRP 3.3 mg/L (elevated): Adopt anti-inflammatory habits — aim for 150 minutes/week of moderate exercise (brisk walking, cycling), follow a Mediterranean-style diet (vegetables, whole grains, olive oil, fish, nuts), reduce processed foods and added sugars, prioritize 7–8 hours sleep, and manage stress (10–20 minutes daily meditation or brisk walking). Repeat hs-CRP in 3 months; if still >3 mg/L, discuss further evaluation with your provider.\\n- Apolipoprotein B 117 mg/dL (borderline): Increase soluble fiber (aim 25–30 g/day — oatmeal for breakfast, beans at lunch, vegetables at dinner), include fatty fish 2–3x weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines), reduce saturated fat (choose lean meats, limit butter/cheese), and continue daily activity (30 minutes brisk walk).\\n- Total Testosterone 13.3 ng/dL (low): If you have symptoms (low libido, low energy), try sleep hygiene (7–8 hours), strength training 2–3x/week, and stress reduction; recheck testosterone in the morning and consider specialist referral if symptoms persist.\\n- Vitamin D 37.2 ng/mL (insufficient): Start vitamin D3 1,000–2,000 IU daily, get 10–20 minutes sun exposure several times a week, and recheck 25-OH vitamin D in 3 months. For bone health, ensure calcium intake ~1,000–1,200 mg/day and weight-bearing exercise.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Start the lifestyle changes above now. Begin vitamin D supplement today and implement fiber/fish/sat-fat changes.\\n- Repeat hs-CRP, ApoB, and vitamin D in ~3 months.\\n- Reassess testosterone if you have related symptoms; time tests with provider to match your cycle and morning hours.\\n- Discuss any persistent inflammation or symptoms with your clinician for possible further testing or referrals.\\n\\nYou’re doing many things right — these targeted steps can lower inflammation and optimize hormones and heart health.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 13.7, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 41.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 86.5, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 86.5 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 301, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 38, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.25, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 10.3, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 13.3, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Low testosterone.", "recommendation": "May contribute to low libido or fatigue. Consider HRT discussion.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 52.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 37.2, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D insufficient (31-40 ng/mL). Suboptimal for hormone optimization.", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 5,000 + K daily. Target ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 763, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 4.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 90, "status": "normal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 61, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 61, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.2957308862613175, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 0.3% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.20300365442599252, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 0.2%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.1409410250136067, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.1%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 2.29374682447242, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 2.3%, ASCVD 1.4%, Heart Failure 1.4%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 13, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 14, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 111, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.73, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 117, "status": "borderline", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Borderline elevated Apo B.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider pharmacotherapy if high ASCVD risk.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 20, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 378, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 43, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 20, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 3.3, "status": "abnormal", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Elevated hs-CRP - increased cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Address cardiovascular risk factors. Consider repeat testing.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 66.9, "status": "normal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 66.9 nmol/L is within normal range. SHBG regulates bioavailable sex hormones by binding testosterone and estradiol.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 1 pg/mL is within normal functional range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "of 6 markers positive", "value": 2, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Insulin Resistance Screening", "recheckTiming": "3-6 months", "interpretation": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive). This combination points toward hyperinsulinemia physiology. Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR).", "recommendation": "Phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance. Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose.", "referenceRange": "0-1 Low, 2 Moderate, 3+ High Likelihood"}], "aiRecommendations": "Clinical context (for staff review)\\n- Labs of concern: Total testosterone 13.3 ng/dL (borderline low), 25-OH vitamin D 37.2 ng/mL (insufficient per clinic targets), ApoB 117 mg/dL (borderline elevated), hs-CRP 3.3 mg/L (elevated → higher CV inflammatory risk).\\n- Other relevant normals: SHBG and free testosterone normal, estradiol/progesterone/FSH normal, ferritin and CBC normal, TSH normal, lipid panel LDL/HDL/triglycerides normal, 10-year ASCVD risk normal. Confirm whether patient is taking systemic estrogen, combined OCP, or other HRT (affects interpretation of testosterone and SHBG).\\n\\n1) Overall clinical assessment (concise)\\n- No acute/critical laboratory emergency.\\n- Findings suggest: borderline low total testosterone (but free T and SHBG normal → biochemical androgen deficiency not definitive); suboptimal vitamin D for hormone optimization; evidence of low-grade systemic inflammation (hs-CRP >3 mg/L) and atherogenic particle signal (ApoB 117 mg/dL) that warrant cardiovascular risk optimization despite normal calculated 10-year ASCVD score.\\n- Management options: conservative risk-factor modification and re-evaluation; consider a symptom-guided, physician-approved trial of low-dose testosterone if patient has clinically significant symptoms (low libido, decreased sexual function, persistent fatigue) after counseling.\\n\\n2) Priority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL (immediate physician notification)\\n- Notify physician if:\\n  - New or worsening constitutional symptoms, signs of infection/inflammation, or any acute cardiovascular symptoms.\\n  - Patient is pregnant, breastfeeding, or of childbearing potential without reliable contraception and testosterone therapy is being considered.\\n\\nURGENT (within 1–2 weeks)\\n- Review elevated hs-CRP (3.3 mg/L) with physician:\\n  - Check for acute causes of inflammation (recent infection, dental work, injury, autoimmune flare). If symptoms/signs present, defer CV-risk interpretation until resolved and repeat hs-CRP after recovery.\\n  - If no acute trigger, escalate CV risk counseling and consider earlier re-check and lipid/ApoB reassessment.\\n\\nROUTINE (initiate within 0–4 weeks)\\n- Start vitamin D supplementation per protocol: Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily. Document patient allergy/intolerance review and counsel about adherence and sun exposure.\\n- Lifestyle counseling for CV risk and ApoB reduction (see section 4).\\n- If patient symptomatic and interested in androgen therapy, discuss trial with physician; obtain informed consent including side effects and monitoring plan.\\n\\n3) Testosterone recommendations (staff-facing; physician approval required)\\n- Indication: Consider only if patient has persistent, clinically significant symptoms attributable to androgen deficiency (e.g., decreased libido, sexual dysfunction, refractory fatigue) and after discussion of risks/benefits.\\n- Recommended initial regimen (follow clinic HRT order set; physician must sign):\\n  - Preferred first-line: low‑dose transdermal testosterone trial.\\n    - Clinic example starting dose: transdermal testosterone 300 mcg (0.3 mg) daily (apply to abdomen/thigh/gluteal area per product instructions). If clinic formulary uses a different concentration, match to an approximate delivered dose of 300 mcg/day.\\n    - Alternative transdermal starting range (if protocol permits): 300–600 mcg/day.\\n  - Injectable alternative (only if clinic protocol supports): testosterone cypionate 10–25 mg IM or subcutaneous every 7–14 days as a low-dose protocol (physician to order exact dose/frequency).\\n- Monitoring and titration:\\n  - Re-check total testosterone, free testosterone, SHBG 8–12 weeks after initiation or dose change, and again at 6 months. Aim for symptomatic improvement without supraphysiologic levels; avoid levels that produce virilization.\\n  - Assess for adverse effects (acne, hirsutism, voice change, hair loss, mood changes) at 4–8 week intervals initially; document virilizing signs at each visit.\\n  - If no symptomatic improvement by 12 weeks and levels in target range, re-evaluate diagnosis and consider referral to endocrinology.\\n- Contraindications/precautions to review before starting:\\n  - Pregnancy/breastfeeding → do not start; ensure effective contraception if applicable.\\n  - Active hormone-sensitive malignancy → discuss with physician.\\n  - Liver disease, uncontrolled CVD → consult physician.\\n\\n4) Lifestyle interventions (specific staff instructions)\\n- Cardiovascular risk reduction (priority given elevated hs-CRP and ApoB):\\n  - Diet: Recommend Mediterranean-style diet (reduce processed carbs/trans fats; emphasize vegetables, fatty fish, fiber, nuts). Consider referral to dietitian.\\n  - Weight: Target 5–10% weight loss if BMI elevated; document baseline weight and set realistic goals.\\n  - Exercise: At least 150 minutes/week moderate-intensity aerobic activity + 2 sessions/week resistance training.\\n  - Smoking cessation: Offer counseling and pharmacotherapy if applicable.\\n  - Alcohol reduction: Limit to guideline amounts.\\n  - Sleep and stress: address sleep hygiene and stress reduction (CBT, mindfulness referrals as needed).\\n- Vitamin D: Instruct patient to take Metagenics D3 5000 IU + K daily with a meal; counsel on signs of vitamin D excess (rare) and calcium intake.\\n- General hormone health: If symptomatic and considering testosterone, counsel on potential side effects (acne, oily skin, hirsutism, voice deepening) and on expected timeline for benefit (~8–12 weeks for libido/fatigue improvement).\\n\\n5) Additional testing or referrals to consider (ordered based on physician direction)\\n- Repeat testing:\\n  - Vitamin D 25-OH: recheck in 8–12 weeks after starting supplementation. Target per clinic: ≥60 ng/mL.\\n  - Total testosterone, free testosterone, SHBG: recheck 8–12 weeks after any testosterone start/change; otherwise repeat in 3 months if untreated but symptomatic.\\n  - hs-CRP: repeat in 6–12 weeks after addressing acute causes/lifestyle changes to confirm persistence.\\n  - ApoB + fasting lipid panel: re-evaluate in 3 months; ensure fasting status for accurate triglyceride assessment.\\n- Additional labs if indicated:\\n  - High-sensitivity troponin/ECG only if cardiac symptoms.\\n  - Evaluate inflammatory sources if hs-CRP persists (CBC, ESR, consider dental/ENT/GI review).\\n  - If patterns suggest adrenal contribution to androgen status (e.g., signs of hyperandrogenism despite low total T), consider DHEA-S and 17‑OH progesterone.\\n- Referrals:\\n  - Cardiology or lipidology if ApoB remains ≥110–120 mg/dL or if there is persistent hs-CRP elevation >3 despite lifestyle measures and patient has other risk factors — for consideration of statin therapy or PCSK9/other lipid-lowering strategies.\\n  - Endocrinology if complex androgen deficiency, unclear source of androgen changes, or difficulty titrating testosterone.\\n  - Gynecology if fertility concerns or contraception counseling before HRT initiation.\\n  - Dietitian and behavioral health as needed for lifestyle interventions.\\n\\n6) Summary of next steps and timeline (actionable checklist)\\n- Immediate (today)\\n  - Confirm documentation of current medications (OCP/HRT, supplements) and pregnancy status; notify physician of hs-CRP 3.3 and ApoB 117.\\n  - Counsel patient on starting Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily (if no contraindication) and document counseling.\\n  - Provide cardiovascular lifestyle counseling and offer referrals (dietitian, smoking cessation) as appropriate.\\n\\n- Short term (within 2 weeks)\\n  - Physician to decide on trial of testosterone therapy if patient symptomatic and consents.\\n  - If testosterone to be started: place prescription per clinic protocol (transdermal 300 mcg/day suggested starting dose) and schedule monitoring.\\n\\n- Follow-up testing schedule (if interventions started)\\n  - 6–12 weeks: repeat hs-CRP (if no acute infection), symptom check.\\n  - 8–12 weeks: recheck total testosterone, free testosterone, SHBG after starting or changing testosterone dose.\\n  - 8–12 weeks: recheck 25-OH vitamin D level after supplementation.\\n  - 3 months: fasting lipid panel + ApoB recheck; review need for lipid-lowering therapy if ApoB remains elevated.\\n  - Ongoing: clinic visit/calls at 4–8 weeks for adverse effect screening if on testosterone; reassess need to continue/titrate at 12 weeks.\\n\\nPhysician notification requirements (explicit)\\n- Notify ordering physician or supervising clinician:\\n  - Prior to initiating testosterone therapy (required sign-off).\\n  - For persistent hs-CRP >3.0 mg/L without an acute explanation.\\n  - If ApoB remains ≥110–120 mg/dL despite lifestyle changes or if family history/personal history raises risk.\\n  - For any adverse events or signs of virilization after starting testosterone.\\n\\nPatient education points to cover (staff script items)\\n- Explain borderline low total testosterone vs normal free testosterone and that treatment is symptom-driven; risks/benefits of a trial of low-dose testosterone.\\n- Explain vitamin D insufficiency, rationale for 5,000 IU daily, expected timeline to normalization (8–12 weeks) and need for re-check.\\n- Communicate the meaning of elevated hs-CRP and ApoB as markers of inflammation and atherogenic particle burden and emphasize lifestyle modification as first-line.\\n- Discuss contraception and pregnancy avoidance if testosterone therapy is being considered.\\n\\nNotes specific to cycle/HRT interactions\\n- Document cycle day when hormones drawn; if premenopausal, interpretation of testosterone/estradiol/progesterone can vary by cycle phase—note for physician review.\\n- If patient is taking estrogen-containing HRT or OCPs, SHBG may be elevated and total testosterone interpretation altered; confirm medication use prior to decisions.\\n\\nEndorsement\\n- All therapeutic decisions (initiation/titration of testosterone, starting statin or other lipid-lowering therapy) require physician ordering and informed consent. Follow clinic HRT and lipid management protocols for exact medication selection and dose adjustments.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 1, "detail": "Triglycerides 61 / HDL 61 = 1", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "4.9%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 66.9, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 14, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "117 mg/dL", "detail": "Elevated atherogenic particle count", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "3.3 mg/L", "detail": "Elevated systemic inflammation", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "moderate", "phenotypes": [{"key": "visceral_metabolic", "name": "Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Recheck A1c, lipid panel, hs-CRP, ApoB in 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0"], "pathophysiology": "Early peripheral + hepatic insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia. Glucose may still be \\"normal.\\" A1c often rises last.", "triggerCriteria": ["A1c >= 5.7%", "TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0", "Central adiposity phenotype", "ALT above optimal range"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests your body may not be responding to insulin as efficiently as it should. Insulin is the hormone that helps move sugar from your bloodstream into your cells for energy.\\n\\nWhen cells become less sensitive to insulin:\\n- Blood sugar slowly rises over time\\n- Triglycerides increase\\n- Inflammation increases\\n- Weight gain around the midsection becomes easier\\n\\nThe good news? This stage is highly reversible with targeted nutrition, strength training, sleep optimization, and - when appropriate - medication support.\\n\\nWe'll focus on improving how your body processes fuel and reducing long-term cardiometabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward nutrition (>= 25-35g/meal)", "Carbohydrate quality + timing education", "Strength training 2-3x weekly", "10-15 min post-meal walking", "Sleep optimization", "Consider: GLP-1 therapy if obesity, appetite dysregulation, A1c >= 5.7, or inflammatory phenotype", "Consider: Metformin if prediabetes or strong family history", "Recheck metabolic panel in 3-6 months"]}], "positiveCount": 2, "likelihoodLabel": "MODERATE LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive). This combination points toward hyperinsulinemia physiology. Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR).", "confirmationTests": "Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose."}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Inflammatory Burden", "confidence": "high", "description": "Pattern of systemic inflammation indicated by elevated inflammatory markers and/or symptomatic presentation. May benefit from anti-inflammatory nutritional support.", "supportingFindings": ["hs-CRP 3.3 mg/L (elevated >3.0)", "ApoB 117 mg/dL (elevated)"]}, {"name": "Menopausal Transition", "confidence": "high", "description": "Clinical picture consistent with perimenopause or menopause. Hormonal shifts during this transition affect bone health, cardiovascular risk, mood, and quality of life.", "supportingFindings": ["FSH 10.3 mIU/mL (rising, perimenopause range)", "Estradiol 38 pg/mL (declining)"]}, {"name": "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Metabolic pattern consistent with insulin resistance and/or visceral adiposity. This drives inflammation, hormonal imbalance, and cardiovascular risk.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance screening: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive)", "ApoB 117 mg/dL (elevated)", "hs-CRP 3.3 mg/L (metabolic inflammation)"]}, {"name": "Oxidative Stress Burden", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Multiple markers suggest increased oxidative stress and cellular burden. Antioxidant and glutathione support may help reduce oxidative damage and support cellular energy production.", "supportingFindings": ["hs-CRP 3.3 mg/L (elevated, systemic inflammation)", "Insulin resistance pattern (metabolic oxidative driver)"]}, {"name": "Iron Deficiency", "confidence": "low", "description": "Iron stores are depleted or suboptimal. Iron is essential for energy, cognitive function, hair health, and oxygen transport.", "supportingFindings": ["Serum iron 43 µg/dL (low)"]}]}	\N	2026-04-01 14:16:49.964997	2026-04-01 14:16:49.964997
79	35	2024-04-04 00:00:00	{"a1c": 5.4, "alt": 52, "ast": 37, "bun": 13, "co2": 25, "fsh": 104, "hdl": 144, "ldl": 105, "mcv": 95.4, "rbc": 4.12, "tsh": 1.17, "wbc": 5.5, "acne": false, "egfr": 92, "onHRT": false, "sodium": 140, "albumin": 4.9, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 9.9, "glucose": 84, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "chloride": 101, "hairLoss": false, "vitaminD": 31.5, "bilirubin": 0.3, "estradiol": 5, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": true, "lowLibido": true, "platelets": 275, "potassium": 4, "creatinine": 0.8, "hematocrit": 39.3, "hemoglobin": 13, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "vitaminB12": 600, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2024-04-04", "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Amanda Taylor", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 46, "bmi": 20, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 115, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": true, "progesterone": 0.05, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 24.3, "totalProtein": 7.9, "lowMotivation": false, "triglycerides": 60, "menstrualPhase": "postmenopausal", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "totalCholesterol": 261}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History of present illness: Patient Amanda Taylor is a 46-year-old female presenting for review of hormone and routine laboratory results. No specific menopausal symptoms reported at this time. Patient reports no new abdominal pain, jaundice, or other acute concerns. No complaints of chest pain, shortness of breath, or focal neurologic symptoms.\\n- Menstrual/hormonal history: Patient has biochemical findings consistent with menopause (see Objective). Patient did not report specific details of menses or menopausal symptoms during today’s encounter; no new bleeding reported.\\n- Medications/supplements: Not specifically provided in chart. (Recommend reconciliation at provider visit.)\\n- Allergies: Not provided.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vital signs: Not available in chart for today.\\n- Relevant calculated scores:\\n  * 10-year Total CVD risk (PREVENT): 0.3% (low)\\n  * 10-year ASCVD risk (PREVENT): 0.2%\\n  * 10-year Heart Failure risk (PREVENT): 0.4%\\n  * 30-year CVD risk (PREVENT): 2.1%\\n  * STOP‑BANG (sleep apnea risk): 0 points\\n  * FIB‑4 score: 0.86\\n\\nLaboratory data (04/01/2026):\\n- CBC\\n  * Hemoglobin 13 g/dL — normal\\n  * Hematocrit 39.3% — normal\\n  * MCV 95.4 fL — normal\\n  * Platelets 275 K/uL — normal\\n- Metabolic / Renal / Liver\\n  * Creatinine 0.8 mg/dL — normal\\n  * eGFR 92 mL/min/1.73 m2 — normal\\n  * AST 37 U/L — borderline high (reference-dependent; mild elevation)\\n  * ALT 52 U/L — borderline high (mild elevation) — provider review recommended prior to oral estrogen therapy\\n  * FIB‑4 score 0.86 — low (low likelihood advanced fibrosis)\\n- Lipid panel\\n  * LDL‑C 105 mg/dL — borderline (near optimal)\\n  * HDL‑C 144 mg/dL — high/optimal\\n  * Triglycerides 60 mg/dL — normal\\n- Glycemic / Metabolic\\n  * Hemoglobin A1c 5.4% — normal\\n- Micronutrients / Other labs\\n  * 25‑OH Vitamin D 31.5 ng/mL — borderline insufficient (31–40 ng/mL)\\n  * Vitamin B12 600 pg/mL — normal\\n- Hormones\\n  * Estradiol (E2) 5 pg/mL — low/ABNORMAL (hypoestrogenic)\\n  * FSH 104 mIU/mL — elevated/ABNORMAL (consistent with menopause)\\n  * Progesterone (P4) 0.05 ng/mL — low/consistent with postmenopausal state\\n  * Total Testosterone 24.3 ng/dL — within expected range (adequate androgen status)\\n  * TSH 1.17 mIU/L — normal\\n\\nASSESSMENT (numbered problem list):\\n1. Biochemical menopause / hypoestrogenic state (\\"crash\\" perimenopausal → menopausal pattern).\\n   - Basis: Estradiol 5 pg/mL (low) with FSH 104 mIU/mL (markedly elevated) — consistent with ovarian failure/menopause.\\n   - Clinical significance: Explains decreased estrogen production; patient is at risk for vasomotor symptoms, genitourinary syndrome of menopause, bone density loss long-term. Patient currently reports no specific menopausal symptoms.\\n   - Red flag: None immediate, but estrogen therapy decisions require review given mild LFT abnormalities (see #3).\\n2. Vitamin D insufficiency.\\n   - Basis: 25‑OH Vitamin D 31.5 ng/mL (borderline insufficient; target for hormone optimization higher, goal ≥60 ng/mL per clinic protocol).\\n   - Clinical significance: Suboptimal for bone health and hormone optimization.\\n3. Mild transaminitis (borderline AST/ALT elevations).\\n   - Basis: AST 37 U/L, ALT 52 U/L (ALT mildly elevated).\\n   - Clinical significance: Mild hepatic enzyme elevations; FIB‑4 low and eGFR normal. Possible etiologies to consider: nonalcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD), medication/supplement effect, alcohol use, viral hepatitis; requires follow-up.\\n   - Red flag: Provider notification required prior to initiating any oral estrogen therapy. If ALT/AST rise >2× baseline or patient develops hepatic symptoms, urgent evaluation/referral needed.\\n4. Borderline elevated LDL cholesterol.\\n   - Basis: LDL 105 mg/dL (near optimal but above ideal for some risk profiles).\\n   - Clinical significance: Global 10-year CVD risk is low (ASCVD 0.2%); statin generally not indicated at this risk level. Lifestyle measures recommended.\\n5. Adequate androgen status / testosterone not low.\\n   - Basis: Total testosterone 24.3 ng/dL within expected range; SHBG not extreme per report.\\n   - Clinical significance: Symptoms such as fatigue, low libido, low mood are unlikely to be primarily due to androgen deficiency. Avoid initiating testosterone therapy now.\\n6. Other routine labs normal.\\n   - Basis: CBC, creatinine, eGFR, HbA1c, B12, triglycerides, HDL, FIB‑4 within acceptable ranges.\\n\\nPLAN:\\nGeneral:\\n- Discussed results with patient today. Reviewed that labs confirm menopausal biochemical status and described implications and management options. Patient verbalized understanding. Provider-staff coordination items below for orders and provider decision-making.\\n\\n1) Management of biochemical menopause / hypoestrogenic state\\n   - If patient is symptomatic and desires hormone therapy (HT), recommend transdermal estradiol as first-line because it bypasses first-pass hepatic metabolism given the mild ALT elevation.\\n     * Suggested regimen for provider consideration: Transdermal estradiol patch 25–50 mcg/day (0.025–0.05 mg patch) — provider decision required.\\n     * If uterus intact, add micronized progesterone for endometrial protection:\\n       - Option A (continuous): Micronized progesterone 100–200 mg PO qHS continuous.\\n       - Option B (cyclic): Micronized progesterone 100–200 mg PO qHS x12–14 days/month.\\n     * Provider to document chosen regimen and counsel on expected benefits/side effects.\\n   - Do NOT initiate oral estrogen without provider approval and after reviewing LFT trend.\\n   - If patient is asymptomatic and prefers no HT, discuss non‑hormonal options and monitoring for bone health and genitourinary symptoms.\\n\\n2) Vitamin D replacement\\n   - Start Vitamin D3 5,000 IU + K daily (Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K or equivalent) — order placed.\\n   - Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D in 8–12 weeks aiming for ≥60 ng/mL; adjust dose as needed.\\n   - Counsel on dietary calcium intake and safe sun exposure.\\n\\n3) Elevated AST/ALT — hepatic evaluation and monitoring\\n   - Repeat AST, ALT in 4–6 weeks to assess trend prior to/after HT initiation.\\n   - If ALT/AST increase >2× baseline or become >2–3× ULN, or if patient develops symptoms (jaundice, RUQ pain), notify provider immediately and arrange hepatitis panel (HBsAg, HCV Ab) and hepatic imaging (ultrasound) as indicated; consider GI/hepatology referral.\\n   - Lifestyle counseling: reduce alcohol intake, review and stop hepatotoxic supplements/medications, weight optimization, Mediterranean-style diet, limit added sugars.\\n   - Document orders: repeat LFTs scheduled in 4–6 weeks.\\n\\n4) Lipid management\\n   - Lifestyle modifications recommended: Mediterranean-style diet, reduce saturated fat, increase fiber, regular aerobic exercise (≥150 min/week) + resistance training 2×/week.\\n   - Repeat fasting lipid panel in 3 months after lifestyle interventions. Statin therapy not indicated at present given 10-year ASCVD risk low; reassess if risk factors change.\\n\\n5) Androgen status / testosterone\\n   - No testosterone therapy indicated at this time given total testosterone 24.3 ng/dL within range.\\n   - If persistent sexual dysfunction or low libido after estrogen/progesterone optimization, pursue broader evaluation (iron studies, thyroid function, sleep, mood) before considering testosterone. Consider DHEA‑S if adrenal contribution suspected and per provider discretion.\\n\\n6) Iron studies and fatigue evaluation\\n   - Order ferritin, iron, TIBC now to evaluate for iron deficiency as contributor to fatigue/low libido; target ferritin >50 ng/mL if symptomatic.\\n   - If ferritin low and iron deficiency confirmed, consider oral iron therapy per provider decision and recheck in 6–8 weeks after initiation.\\n\\n7) Additional testing / referrals\\n   - Order ferritin/iron studies today.\\n   - Order repeat AST/ALT in 4–6 weeks.\\n   - If transaminases remain elevated, order hepatitis B and C serologies and consider liver ultrasound for steatosis.\\n   - Consider nutritionist referral for weight and lipid optimization if indicated.\\n   - Consider behavioral health referral if mood, anxiety, or sleep symptoms become prominent.\\n\\n8) Supplements (per clinic recommendations)\\n   - Start Vitamin D3 5,000 IU + K daily (as above).\\n   - Optional medium-priority supplements for consideration: CoQ10 300 mg chewable gel daily; AdvaClear® 2 capsules twice daily — discuss with provider and reconcile with current medications prior to starting.\\n\\n9) Follow-up and timeline\\n   - Immediate (today): Orders placed for Vitamin D supplement, ferritin/iron studies, and to schedule repeat LFTs in 4–6 weeks. Flag chart for provider decision regarding HT (transdermal estradiol ± progesterone).\\n   - 1–2 weeks: Provider to decide on initiating HT and document chosen regimen and contraindications review.\\n   - 4–6 weeks: Repeat AST/ALT. If persistent elevation, proceed with hepatitis panel and imaging as indicated.\\n   - 6–12 weeks after HT initiation: Clinical follow-up to assess symptom response, side effects, blood pressure, and consider further labs as needed.\\n   - 8–12 weeks: Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D and adjust dosing to achieve target ≥60 ng/mL.\\n   - 3 months: Repeat lipid panel and routine metabolic assessment.\\n   - Routine follow-up window: 3–6 months for ongoing management.\\n\\n10) Notifications / safety triggers\\n   - Notify provider prior to initiating any oral estrogen.\\n   - Notify provider immediately if ALT/AST rise >2× baseline, show sustained elevation, or if patient develops hepatic symptoms.\\n   - Notify provider if ferritin <50 with symptomatic anemia.\\n\\nDocumentation:\\n- Results were reviewed with the patient today and discussed. Counseling provided on lifestyle interventions (alcohol moderation, diet, exercise), rationale for transdermal estrogen preference given LFTs, and vitamin D replacement. Risks, benefits, and need for follow-up labs were reviewed.\\n- Orders placed: Vitamin D3 5,000 IU + K daily (supplement recommendation), ferritin/iron studies, repeat AST/ALT in 4–6 weeks, lipid panel in 3 months (per clinic protocol). Provider flagged for HT decision.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\n04/01/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_L