rocesses. Your results suggest your body could benefit from help processing hormones, metabolic byproducts, and other compounds. It provides nutrients that support both phases of your liver's detox pathways.", "supportingFindings": ["Borderline liver markers (ALT 52, AST 37) suggesting hepatic biotransformation load", "ALT 52 U/L (elevated, liver detox support indicated)", "Low energy (biotransformation burden)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "consider", "rationale": "Lipid levels meet threshold for CAC consideration. CAC may be useful if risk level is uncertain or patient is hesitant about statin.", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Dear Patient,\\n\\nWe have reviewed your recent lab results from your men's health panel. Your labs help us keep your testosterone therapy safe and effective while monitoring your overall health.\\n\\nYour Results:\\nWe've carefully reviewed all your lab values including blood counts, hormone levels, cholesterol, liver and kidney function, and other important markers.\\n\\nNext Steps:\\nYour healthcare provider will review these results and contact you if any changes to your treatment plan are needed. We monitor these labs to ensure optimal testosterone levels while keeping your blood counts, cholesterol, liver, kidney, and prostate health in the safest range possible.\\n\\nIf you have any questions about your results, please don't hesitate to contact our clinic.\\n\\nThank you for trusting us with your care.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 13, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 39.3, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 95.4, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 95.4 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 275, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 5, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol appropriate for postmenopausal status.", "recommendation": "Consider HRT if symptomatic for vasomotor symptoms, bone health, or quality of life.", "referenceRange": "<20 pg/mL (postmenopausal without HRT)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.05, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone appropriate for postmenopausal status.", "recommendation": "Expected finding.", "referenceRange": "<1 ng/mL (postmenopausal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 104, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "Elevated FSH consistent with menopause.", "recommendation": "Expected finding for postmenopausal women.", "referenceRange": ">25 mIU/mL (postmenopausal)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 24.3, "status": "normal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone within normal female range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.17, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 31.5, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D insufficient (31-40 ng/mL). Suboptimal for hormone optimization.", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 5,000 + K daily. Target ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 600, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.4, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 105, "status": "borderline", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Near optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet and exercise.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 144, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 60, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.2644062144138707, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 0.3% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.17420263766603375, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 0.2%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.42669257677978395, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.4%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 2.132337390875393, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 2.1%, ASCVD 1.3%, Heart Failure 3.9%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 37, "status": "borderline", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "Mild AST elevation.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle counseling. Repeat in 4-6 weeks.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 52, "status": "borderline", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "Mild ALT elevation.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle changes. Repeat in 4-6 weeks.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "score", "value": 0.86, "status": "normal", "category": "FIB-4 Score (Liver Fibrosis)", "recheckTiming": "", "interpretation": "FIB-4 score 0.86 indicates LOW risk of advanced fibrosis (F0-F1). Negative predictive value >90%.", "recommendation": "Advanced fibrosis unlikely. Continue lifestyle modifications and routine LFT monitoring. Address underlying cause of LFT elevation.", "referenceRange": "<1.30 low risk, 1.30-2.67 indeterminate, >2.67 high risk"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 92, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.8, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 5, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Hypoestrogenic Pattern", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 5 pg/mL with FSH of 104 mIU/mL indicates hypoestrogenic perimenopause — the \\"crash phase.\\" Estrogen production has dropped significantly and the brain is signaling strongly (elevated FSH) trying to stimulate the ovaries.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Transdermal estradiol 0.025–0.05 mg is appropriate replacement therapy. Add micronized progesterone for uterine protection and sleep support. Frame this as hormone replacement, not stimulation — we are restoring what the body needs. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen has dropped to a level that can cause symptoms like hot flashes, night sweats, brain fog, and mood changes. Replacing estrogen with a patch or gel can relieve these symptoms and protect your long-term health. Progesterone is added to protect your uterus and help with sleep.", "referenceRange": "E2 <50 with FSH >10 indicates estrogen deficiency"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 104, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Menopause", "interpretation": "FSH of 104 mIU/mL with low estradiol (5 pg/mL) confirms menopausal status. No specific symptoms reported at this time. Elevated FSH reflects decreased ovarian function and reduced estrogen production. This is a normal part of reproductive aging but may benefit from hormone optimization if symptomatic.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: If symptomatic, consider hormone therapy (HT): transdermal estradiol + micronized progesterone (if uterus intact). Discuss benefits/risks of HT including cardiovascular, bone, and quality of life considerations. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your lab results confirm you are in menopause. Hormone therapy can help manage symptoms like hot flashes, sleep problems, and vaginal dryness while also protecting bone health.", "referenceRange": "FSH <25 mIU/mL premenopausal; ≥25 suggests transition; ≥40 menopausal"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 24.3, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone Pattern D: Adequate Androgens, Persistent Symptoms", "interpretation": "Adequate Androgens pattern identified (Total T: 24.3 ng/dL (within range)). Total, free, and bioavailable testosterone are within normal ranges and SHBG is not extreme. If the patient reports fatigue, low mood, sleep disruption, or low libido, these symptoms are likely driven by non-androgenic factors. Findings to evaluate: Estrogen/Progesterone balance should be assessed (E2: 5 pg/mL, P4: 0.05 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Do not reflexively add testosterone when levels are adequate — look deeper. Evaluate iron status (ferritin target >50 for symptom resolution), thyroid function (optimize TSH), and sleep quality (consider sleep apnea screening with STOP-BANG). Assess estrogen and progesterone balance — E2/P4 imbalance is a common driver of persistent symptoms. Address CNS and psychosocial drivers: chronic stress, HPA axis dysregulation, mood disorders. Consider DHEA-S if not already checked. Support with lifestyle optimization: sleep hygiene, stress management, nutrition. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your testosterone levels are actually in a healthy range, which is good news. When symptoms persist despite normal testosterone, it usually means something else is contributing. Your provider will look at other factors like iron levels, thyroid function, sleep quality, and hormone balance to find what's driving your symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Total T 15-70 ng/dL, Free T 0.5-5.0 pg/mL (all within range)"}], "aiRecommendations": "Note: FOR CLINIC STAFF ONLY — recommendations are clinical guidance for provider review and patient management. Do not present as definitive diagnosis; flag provider for approval where indicated.\\n\\n1) Overall clinical assessment\\n- Menopausal state confirmed: FSH 104 mIU/mL with estradiol 5 pg/mL → biochemical menopause / hypoestrogenic perimenopausal “crash” pattern.\\n- Vitamin D borderline insufficiency (25-OH D 31.5 ng/mL) — suboptimal for hormone optimization.\\n- Mild transaminase elevations (AST 37 U/L, ALT 52 U/L) — borderline; FIB‑4 is normal.\\n- LDL mildly elevated (105 mg/dL) but near-optimal; global CVD risk markers reportedly normal.\\n- Total testosterone 24.3 ng/dL within range — androgens adequate; persistent symptoms (if present) are unlikely to be primarily androgen deficiency.\\n\\n2) Priority actions — organized CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE\\n\\nCRITICAL (immediate provider notification required)\\n- Notify provider before initiating any oral estrogen therapy given ALT 52 U/L and ongoing LFT abnormalities. Recommend transdermal estradiol as first-line because it bypasses first-pass hepatic metabolism.\\n- Notify provider if ALT or AST rises >2× baseline or >2–3× ULN, or if patient develops signs of hepatic dysfunction (jaundice, right‑upper-quadrant pain, new coagulopathy).\\n\\nURGENT (action within 1–2 weeks)\\n- Initiate Vitamin D replacement per protocol: Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily. Order follow-up 25‑OH D in 8–12 weeks.\\n- If patient is symptomatic from menopause and provider elects HT:\\n  * Start transdermal estradiol 25–50 mcg/day (0.025–0.05 mg patch) — provider decision required.\\n  * Add micronized progesterone for endometrial protection: suggest 100–200 mg oral qHS. Specify regimen choice for provider:\\n    - Continuous combined: progesterone 100–200 mg nightly continuous if adopting continuous estradiol.\\n    - Cyclic: progesterone 100–200 mg nightly x12–14 days/month if patient prefers cyclic regimen.\\n  * Before initiating HT, confirm provider approval given mild LFT elevations and review contraindications (history of estrogen-sensitive cancer, thromboembolism, uncontrolled BP, etc.).\\n\\nROUTINE (within 4–12 weeks)\\n- Repeat LFTs (AST, ALT) in 4–6 weeks to assess trend after lifestyle interventions and before/after HT initiation.\\n- Recheck Vitamin D in 8–12 weeks aiming for ≥60 ng/mL.\\n- Repeat lipid profile in 3 months after lifestyle interventions; consider statin only if 10-year ASCVD risk and provider deem it indicated.\\n- If starting progesterone/estradiol, follow-up visit at 6–12 weeks to assess symptom response, side effects, and blood pressure.\\n\\n3) Testosterone recommendations\\n- No testosterone therapy indicated at this time: total testosterone 24.3 ng/dL is within expected range and SHBG not extreme.\\n- Provider guidance: do not initiate testosterone given adequate androgen levels. If sexual dysfunction or severe low‑libido persists after optimizing estrogen/progesterone, consider broader evaluation (iron, thyroid, sleep, mood) and reassess need for androgen therapy only after documented deficiency and specialist consultation.\\n- If provider later elects trial of testosterone, document baseline lipid, LFTs, and follow-up schedule; but do not start without provider approval.\\n\\n4) Lifestyle interventions to recommend (staff to counsel and document)\\n- Hepatic risk modification:\\n  * Reduce alcohol intake (aim for abstinence or minimal consumption) and review hepatotoxic medications/supplements.\\n  * Counsel weight loss if BMI elevated; target 5–10% weight loss if NAFLD suspected.\\n  * Encourage Mediterranean-style diet, limit added sugars and refined carbohydrates.\\n- Cardiometabolic:\\n  * Aerobic exercise ≥150 min/week + 2 sessions/week resistance training.\\n  * Dietary counseling for LDL reduction: reduce saturated fats, increase fiber and plant-based foods.\\n- Bone and vitamin D:\\n  * Encourage safe sun exposure and dietary calcium intake; continue vitamin D supplementation as above.\\n- Sleep/stress:\\n  * Assess and optimize sleep hygiene; screen for obstructive sleep apnea if clinical suspicion increases (STOP‑BANG was reported normal).\\n  * Address stress management and mental health resources if symptoms of mood/fatigue are present.\\n- Reproductive-uterine care:\\n  * If uterus intact and starting estrogen, counsel on progesterone necessity for endometrial protection and its side effects (sedation, spotting).\\n\\n5) Additional testing / referrals to consider\\n- Iron studies: ferritin, iron, TIBC — target ferritin >50 ng/mL if symptomatic (fatigue/low libido). Order now.\\n- Repeat LFTs (AST/ALT) in 4–6 weeks. If persistent elevation:\\n  * Order hepatitis B surface Ag, hepatitis C antibody.\\n  * Consider liver ultrasound for steatosis if ALT remains elevated or other features of NAFLD.\\n  * Gastroenterology/hepatology referral if ALT persistently >2× ULN, evidence of liver disease, or abnormal imaging.\\n- DHEA‑S if sexual symptoms persist and provider requests adrenal androgen assessment.\\n- Mental health evaluation or behavioral health referral if mood, anxiety, or chronic stress likely contributing.\\n- Nutritionist referral for targeted weight loss/LDL management if indicated.\\n- If initiating HT: ensure up-to-date screening per age (mammography, cervical screening as appropriate) — remind provider to confirm.\\n\\n6) Summary of next steps and timeline (specific actionable items for staff)\\n- Immediate (today / prior to leaving visit)\\n  * Place orders/referrals for: Vitamin D Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily; ferritin and iron studies; baseline LFTs already done but schedule repeat in 4–6 weeks.\\n  * Flag chart for provider discussion/approval to start transdermal estradiol 25–50 mcg/day + micronized progesterone 100–200 mg qHS (continuous vs cyclic per provider).\\n  * Counsel patient on alcohol reduction, diet, exercise, and rationale for vitamin D replacement; document counseling points.\\n\\n- 1–2 weeks\\n  * Provider to decide on HT initiation given menopause confirmation and LFTs; if initiating, document chosen estradiol dose and progesterone regimen.\\n\\n- 4–6 weeks\\n  * Repeat AST/ALT. If values elevated further or not improving, notify provider and arrange hepatitis panel and possible hepatology referral.\\n  * If ferritin low, consider iron therapy (oral iron) per provider decision and plan recheck in 6–8 weeks after starting.\\n\\n- 8–12 weeks\\n  * Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D aiming for ≥60 ng/mL; adjust dose if target not reached.\\n  * Reassess symptoms after HT initiation at ~6–12 weeks; schedule follow-up visit for symptom review and BP/labs as indicated.\\n\\n- 3 months\\n  * Repeat lipid panel and broader metabolic assessment; consider statin only if indicated by ASCVD risk and provider decision.\\n\\nProvider notification triggers (staff action)\\n- Notify provider immediately if ALT/AST increase >2× baseline or >2–3× ULN, new hepatic symptoms, or labs suggest active viral hepatitis.\\n- Notify provider before initiating any oral estrogen, and confirm transdermal regimen selection and progesterone protocol.\\n- Notify provider if ferritin <50 with symptomatic anemia or if iron studies show iron deficiency.\\n\\nConcise clinical bottom line for provider review\\n- Biochemical menopause with low estradiol — transdermal estradiol (25–50 mcg/day) + micronized progesterone (100–200 mg qHS; continuous or cyclic per plan) is an appropriate HT option if patient is symptomatic and no contraindications — provider approval required.\\n- Start Vitamin D 5,000 IU + K daily and recheck in 8–12 weeks aiming for ≥60 ng/mL.\\n- LFTs are mildly elevated; use transdermal estrogen, repeat LFTs in 4–6 weeks, and pursue hepatitis/NAFLD workup if not improving. Do not start testosterone at this time.\\n- Order ferritin/iron studies and address other reversible contributors to fatigue/low libido (iron, thyroid optimization, sleep, psychosocial factors).", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 0.42, "detail": "Triglycerides 60 / HDL 144 = 0.42", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5.4%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 52, "detail": "Above optimal range - may indicate hepatic insulin resistance", "positive": true, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 1, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (1/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Menopausal Transition", "confidence": "high", "description": "Clinical picture consistent with perimenopause or menopause. Hormonal shifts during this transition affect bone health, cardiovascular risk, mood, and quality of life.", "supportingFindings": ["Postmenopausal status", "FSH 104 mIU/mL (elevated, menopausal range)", "Estradiol 5 pg/mL (declining)", "Irritability reported"]}]}	\N	2026-04-01 14:31:52.679831	2026-04-01 14:31:52.679831
