nt protection. Essential for patients on statins.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "low", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit); Age 49 (declining CoQ10 production)", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)", "Age 49 (declining CoQ10 production)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\nYour labs show a pattern consistent with menopause (FSH 40.1 mIU/mL) with some hormone levels that may need adjustment while you’re on hormone therapy, plus low circulating iron. Most safety labs look OK, but a few targeted changes could improve energy, symptoms, and bone health.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- FSH 40.1 mIU/mL: consistent with menopause (expected).\\n- Ferritin 69 ng/mL and TIBC 282 ug/dL: iron stores are in the acceptable range (ferritin >50).\\n- Free testosterone 2 pg/mL: within a functional range.\\n- STOP‑BANG 0 points: low risk for obstructive sleep apnea.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + concrete actions)\\n- Estradiol 171 pg/mL (elevated above typical HRT range): Discuss lowering or changing the form of estrogen with your clinician. If you are taking oral estrogen, switching to a transdermal patch or gel often reduces liver-driven increases in SHBG.\\n- Progesterone 1.88 ng/mL (below typical HRT therapeutic range but unexpectedly high for a postmenopausal baseline): Review timing/dose of exogenous progesterone with your provider; consider re-testing levels after dosing changes.\\n- Total testosterone 33.2 ng/dL (low despite HRT) and SHBG 132 nmol/L (high): High SHBG reduces available testosterone. Actions: prioritize 7–8 hours sleep nightly; add resistance/strength training 2–3 times per week; practice stress reduction (walking, yoga, meditation). Talk with your clinician about HRT route (transdermal vs oral) and whether testosterone supplementation is appropriate.\\n- Serum iron 38 ug/dL (low): Even though ferritin is 69 ng/mL, low serum iron can cause fatigue. Concrete steps: eat iron‑rich meals (2–3 servings of lean red meat or iron-fortified cereal weekly, add beans and spinach); take vitamin C with iron-rich meals (glass of orange juice) to boost absorption; avoid tea/coffee with meals. Consider starting an oral iron supplement after discussing with your provider (example option: ferrous sulfate 325 mg = 65 mg elemental iron every other day or daily as tolerated). Avoid taking iron with calcium/antacids—separate by 2 hours.\\n\\nBone and thyroid health\\n- Menopause increases bone loss risk. Start calcium 1,000–1,200 mg/day and vitamin D 1,000–2,000 IU/day and continue resistance exercise. If you have not had a DEXA bone density scan in the past 2 years, discuss scheduling one.\\n- SHBG can be raised by thyroid meds or liver issues—ask your clinician to check TSH and basic liver tests.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Discuss HRT dosing/route and possible testosterone options with your clinician within 2–4 weeks.\\n- If starting iron, recheck serum iron and ferritin in 6–8 weeks.\\n- Repeat comprehensive hormone panel 6–12 weeks after any HRT changes.\\n- Check TSH and liver tests now; arrange DEXA if not recent.\\n\\nYou’re doing important work by checking these labs; small changes (diet, exercise, HRT adjustments) can make a big difference. Contact your provider to plan the specific medication or supplement changes.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 171, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol elevated above typical HRT range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Evaluate estrogen dose. Monitor for estrogen excess symptoms.", "referenceRange": "60-100 pg/mL (HRT goal, >40 for bone)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1.88, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone below HRT therapeutic range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase progesterone dose. Target 8-10 ng/mL for optimal endometrial protection.", "referenceRange": "8-10 ng/mL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 40.1, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "Elevated FSH consistent with menopause.", "recommendation": "Expected finding for postmenopausal women.", "referenceRange": ">25 mIU/mL (postmenopausal)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 33.2, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Low testosterone despite HRT.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase testosterone dose. Target 75-125 ng/dL for optimal results.", "referenceRange": "75-125 ng/dL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 69, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 282, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 38, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Low serum iron - iron deficiency.", "recommendation": "Iron supplementation. Evaluate for blood loss.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1.88, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Elevated Postmenopausal Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone of 1.88 ng/mL is unexpectedly elevated for postmenopausal status. This may indicate exogenous progesterone use, adrenal source, or lab timing issue.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Confirm patient is not on progesterone therapy. If no exogenous source, consider adrenal evaluation. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your progesterone level is higher than expected after menopause. We should confirm you're not taking any progesterone supplements.", "referenceRange": "<1 ng/mL expected postmenopausal"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 40.1, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Menopause", "interpretation": "FSH of 40.1 mIU/mL is consistent with menopause. No specific symptoms reported at this time. Elevated FSH reflects decreased ovarian function and reduced estrogen production. This is a normal part of reproductive aging but may benefit from hormone optimization if symptomatic.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: If symptomatic, consider hormone therapy (HT): transdermal estradiol + micronized progesterone (if uterus intact). Discuss benefits/risks of HT including cardiovascular, bone, and quality of life considerations. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your lab results confirm you are in menopause. Hormone therapy can help manage symptoms like hot flashes, sleep problems, and vaginal dryness while also protecting bone health.", "referenceRange": "FSH <25 mIU/mL premenopausal; ≥25 suggests transition; ≥40 menopausal"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 132, "status": "abnormal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 132 nmol/L is elevated. High SHBG reduces bioavailable testosterone and estrogen. Common causes: oral estrogen, oral contraceptives, thyroid medication, liver conditions.", "recommendation": "Evaluate SHBG drivers (oral estrogen, OCP, thyroid medication). Consider ordering Bioavailable Testosterone for complete androgen assessment.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 2, "status": "normal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 2 pg/mL is within normal functional range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}], "aiRecommendations": "Unable to generate recommendations. Please review lab findings manually.", "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Menopausal Transition", "confidence": "high", "description": "Clinical picture consistent with perimenopause or menopause. Hormonal shifts during this transition affect bone health, cardiovascular risk, mood, and quality of life.", "supportingFindings": ["Postmenopausal status", "FSH 40.1 mIU/mL (elevated, menopausal range)"]}, {"name": "Iron Deficiency", "confidence": "low", "description": "Iron stores are depleted or suboptimal. Iron is essential for energy, cognitive function, hair health, and oxygen transport.", "supportingFindings": ["Serum iron 38 µg/dL (low)", "Low energy/fatigue reported"]}]}	\N	2026-04-01 17:23:49.275477	2026-04-01 17:23:49.275477
