ellular defense against oxidative damage.", "category": "detox", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "GlutaClear supports glutathione production and antioxidant defense with NAC, alpha-lipoic acid, and green tea catechins. Indicated when there is evidence of oxidative stress, inflammatory burden, or metabolic dysfunction requiring enhanced cellular defense. While AdvaClear provides broader liver detoxification support, GlutaClear specifically targets glutathione pathways and antioxidant capacity.", "indication": "Insulin resistance (oxidative metabolic stress); ALT 28 U/L (hepatic glutathione demand); Low energy (mitochondrial antioxidant support); Age 58 (declining glutathione production)", "phenotypes": ["Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement boosts your body's master antioxidant, glutathione, which protects your cells from damage. Your results suggest increased oxidative stress, and this formula helps your body defend and repair at the cellular level.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance (oxidative metabolic stress)", "ALT 28 U/L (hepatic glutathione demand)", "Low energy (mitochondrial antioxidant support)", "Age 58 (declining glutathione production)"]}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Essential for patients on statins.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "LDL 159 mg/dL; TC 270 mg/dL; Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit); Age 58 (declining CoQ10 production)", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["LDL 159 mg/dL", "TC 270 mg/dL", "Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)", "Age 58 (declining CoQ10 production)"]}, {"dose": "1 softgel 1-2 times daily", "name": "OmegaGenics® Fish Oil Neuro 1000", "caution": "High-DHA omega-3 (750mg DHA, 250mg EPA) for brain and heart health. Lemon-flavored, no fishy taste. Contains calamari - avoid if shellfish allergy.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "OmegaGenics Fish Oil Neuro 1000 provides concentrated DHA and EPA to reduce triglycerides, support brain function, cardiovascular health, and reduce systemic inflammation.", "indication": "LDL 159 mg/dL (elevated); Total cholesterol 270 mg/dL (elevated)", "phenotypes": ["Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This high-quality fish oil provides omega-3 fatty acids that help reduce inflammation, support your heart and brain health, and may help improve your cholesterol profile.", "supportingFindings": ["LDL 159 mg/dL (elevated)", "Total cholesterol 270 mg/dL (elevated)"]}, {"dose": "1 tablet daily with meal", "name": "D3 2000 Complex", "caution": "Maintenance dose for suboptimal levels. Comprehensive vitamin D support with cofactors.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "low", "rationale": "D3 2000 Complex provides maintenance vitamin D with cofactors to reach and maintain optimal range ≥60 ng/mL.", "indication": "Vitamin D 52.9 ng/mL (suboptimal, target ≥60)", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D is close to optimal but could benefit from a maintenance dose to reach the ideal range for bone and immune health.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 52.9 ng/mL (suboptimal, target ≥60)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "generally_recommended", "indicated": true, "rationale": "Persistent non-HDL ≥160 mg/dL (184 mg/dL) indicates elevated atherogenic lipoprotein burden. Statin discussion recommended."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "consider", "rationale": "Lipid levels meet threshold for CAC consideration. CAC may be useful if risk level is uncertain or patient is hesitant about statin.", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\nYou have laboratory results consistent with menopause (FSH 41.6 mIU/mL) and a borderline heart risk profile (10-year total CVD risk 7.1%). Many values are reassuring (normal TSH 0.98 mIU/L, hemoglobin 14.2 g/dL, HDL 86 mg/dL). A few areas deserve follow-up and concrete lifestyle steps now: LDL cholesterol 159 mg/dL, vitamin B12 276 pg/mL (low‑normal), MCV 98.5 fL (high‑normal), AST 34 U/L (mildly high), hematocrit 45.1% (slightly high—possible dehydration), and estradiol 56 pg/mL with postmenopausal FSH.\\n\\nPositive findings (what’s good)\\n- TSH 0.98 mIU/L — normal thyroid function.\\n- Hemoglobin 14.2 g/dL and platelets 320 K/uL — good blood counts.\\n- HDL 86 mg/dL and triglycerides 123 mg/dL — protective lipid components.\\n- Hemoglobin A1c 5.4% — normal blood sugar control.\\n- eGFR 78 mL/min and creatinine 0.86 mg/dL — normal kidney function.\\n- FIB‑4 1.16 — low risk of advanced liver fibrosis.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + actions)\\n- LDL cholesterol 159 mg/dL (above 130): start these dietary and activity steps now:\\n  - Add 25–30 g fiber daily: oatmeal for breakfast, beans at lunch, vegetables at dinner.\\n  - Include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n  - Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter and cheese.\\n  - Daily exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking (aim for 150 minutes/week total).\\n  - Because your 10‑year CVD risk is 7.1% (BORDERLINE), follow a Mediterranean-style diet, limit processed foods/added sugars, and discuss statin therapy with your provider; coronary calcium (CAC) scoring can help guide that choice.\\n- Vitamin B12 276 pg/mL and MCV 98.5 fL: increase B12 intake (eggs, dairy, fortified cereals, lean meat) and consider an oral B12 1,000 mcg daily for 8–12 weeks or discuss injection if you have symptoms (fatigue, numbness). Repeat CBC and B12 in 3 months.\\n- Hematocrit 45.1%: drink adequate fluids (aim for 1.5–2 L/day) and recheck labs when well‑hydrated.\\n- AST 34 U/L (mild): limit alcohol to <2 drinks/week, review medications/supplements that affect the liver, and maintain healthy weight. Recheck AST/ALT in 3 months.\\n- Estradiol 56 pg/mL with FSH 41.6 mIU/mL confirms menopause; if you develop hot flashes, sleep or mood changes, discuss hormone therapy options with your clinician.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Start the diet/exercise steps today. Recheck fasting lipid panel, B12, CBC, and liver tests in ~3 months. Consider bone health screening (DEXA) and discuss menopause symptom management and whether statin therapy or CAC testing is right for you at your next visit. If new symptoms occur (numbness, worsening fatigue, heavy bleeding), contact your clinician sooner.\\n\\nYou’re doing well in many areas — these targeted changes can lower your heart and nutrient risks.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 14.2, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 45.1, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Slightly elevated hematocrit - may indicate dehydration.", "recommendation": "Encourage hydration. Repeat if persistent.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 98.5, "status": "borderline", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 98.5 fL is in the high-normal range. Early macrocytosis can precede overt B12 or folate deficiency.", "recommendation": "Ensure B12 and folate levels are within optimal range. Monitor CBC at next visit.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 320, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 56, "status": "borderline", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol elevated for postmenopausal status without HRT.", "recommendation": "Evaluate for exogenous estrogen source or ovarian pathology. Consider HRT if symptomatic.", "referenceRange": "<20 pg/mL (postmenopausal without HRT)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.26, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone appropriate for postmenopausal status.", "recommendation": "Expected finding.", "referenceRange": "<1 ng/mL (postmenopausal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 41.6, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "Elevated FSH consistent with menopause.", "recommendation": "Expected finding for postmenopausal women.", "referenceRange": ">25 mIU/mL (postmenopausal)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 29.2, "status": "normal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone within normal female range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 0.98, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 52.9, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D adequate but suboptimal (41-59 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 2000 Complex daily to reach optimal range ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 276, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "Low-normal B12.", "recommendation": "Consider supplementation especially if symptomatic.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.4, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 159, "status": "borderline", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Borderline high LDL.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider statin if additional risk factors.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 86, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 123, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 7.08675620577023, "status": "borderline", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 7.1% (BORDERLINE RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nBORDERLINE RISK (5-7.5%): Consider coronary artery calcium (CAC) scoring\\n\\nStatin therapy based on shared decision-making if risk-enhancing factors present", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 4.334949694469105, "status": "borderline", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 4.3%", "recommendation": "Consider statin if risk-enhancing factors present. CAC scoring may help guide decision.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 4.8712277364110195, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 4.9%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 2, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (2/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 28.232419115579845, "status": "borderline", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 28.2%, ASCVD 16.7%, Heart Failure 23.4%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 34, "status": "borderline", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "Mild AST elevation.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle counseling. Repeat in 4-6 weeks.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 28, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "score", "value": 1.16, "status": "normal", "category": "FIB-4 Score (Liver Fibrosis)", "recheckTiming": "", "interpretation": "FIB-4 score 1.16 indicates LOW risk of advanced fibrosis (F0-F1). Negative predictive value >90%.", "recommendation": "Advanced fibrosis unlikely. Continue lifestyle modifications and routine LFT monitoring. Address underlying cause of LFT elevation.", "referenceRange": "<1.30 low risk, 1.30-2.67 indeterminate, >2.67 high risk"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 78, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.86, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 41.6, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Menopause", "interpretation": "FSH of 41.6 mIU/mL is consistent with menopause. No specific symptoms reported at this time. Elevated FSH reflects decreased ovarian function and reduced estrogen production. This is a normal part of reproductive aging but may benefit from hormone optimization if symptomatic.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: If symptomatic, consider hormone therapy (HT): transdermal estradiol + micronized progesterone (if uterus intact). Discuss benefits/risks of HT including cardiovascular, bone, and quality of life considerations. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your lab results confirm you are in menopause. Hormone therapy can help manage symptoms like hot flashes, sleep problems, and vaginal dryness while also protecting bone health.", "referenceRange": "FSH <25 mIU/mL premenopausal; ≥25 suggests transition; ≥40 menopausal"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 29.2, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone Pattern D: Adequate Androgens, Persistent Symptoms", "interpretation": "Adequate Androgens pattern identified (Total T: 29.2 ng/dL (within range)). Total, free, and bioavailable testosterone are within normal ranges and SHBG is not extreme. If the patient reports fatigue, low mood, sleep disruption, or low libido, these symptoms are likely driven by non-androgenic factors. Findings to evaluate: Estrogen/Progesterone balance should be assessed (E2: 56 pg/mL, P4: 0.26 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Do not reflexively add testosterone when levels are adequate — look deeper. Evaluate iron status (ferritin target >50 for symptom resolution), thyroid function (optimize TSH), and sleep quality (consider sleep apnea screening with STOP-BANG). Assess estrogen and progesterone balance — E2/P4 imbalance is a common driver of persistent symptoms. Address CNS and psychosocial drivers: chronic stress, HPA axis dysregulation, mood disorders. Consider DHEA-S if not already checked. Support with lifestyle optimization: sleep hygiene, stress management, nutrition. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your testosterone levels are actually in a healthy range, which is good news. When symptoms persist despite normal testosterone, it usually means something else is contributing. Your provider will look at other factors like iron levels, thyroid function, sleep quality, and hormone balance to find what's driving your symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Total T 15-70 ng/dL, Free T 0.5-5.0 pg/mL (all within range)"}], "aiRecommendations": "Unable to generate recommendations. Please review lab findings manually.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 1.43, "detail": "Triglycerides 123 / HDL 86 = 1.43", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5.4%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 28, "detail": "Above optimal range - may indicate hepatic insulin resistance", "positive": true, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 1, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (1/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Menopausal Transition", "confidence": "high", "description": "Clinical picture consistent with perimenopause or menopause. Hormonal shifts during this transition affect bone health, cardiovascular risk, mood, and quality of life.", "supportingFindings": ["Postmenopausal status", "FSH 41.6 mIU/mL (elevated, menopausal range)"]}, {"name": "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Metabolic pattern consistent with insulin resistance and/or visceral adiposity. This drives inflammation, hormonal imbalance, and cardiovascular risk.", "supportingFindings": ["Unexplained weight gain / central adiposity reported", "ALT 28 U/L (above optimal for women >25)"]}]}	\N	2026-04-02 16:06:03.829327	2026-04-02 16:06:03.829327
90	40	2026-01-10 00:00:00	{"a1c": 5.4, "alt": 19, "ast": 25, "bun": 12, "co2": 25, "fsh": 67.7, "hdl": 80, "ldl": 145, "lpa": 6, "mcv": 95.2, "rbc": 4.38, "tsh": 0.98, "wbc": 9.1, "acne": false, "apoB": 94, "egfr": 100, "iron": 114, "shbg": 128, "tibc": 293, "onHRT": false, "freeT4": 1.06, "sodium": 137, "albumin": 4.4, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 9.3, "glucose": 88, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "chloride": 100, "ferritin": 74, "hairLoss": false, "vitaminD": 52.9, "bilirubin": 0.9, "estradiol": 40, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": true, "lowLibido": true, "platelets": 277, "potassium": 4.5, "creatinine": 0.69, "hematocrit": 41.7, "hemoglobin": 13.9, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "vitaminB12": 856, "weightGain": true, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2026-01-10", "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Stephanie Sinclair", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 58, "bmi": 26.4, "race": "white", "smoker": true, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": true, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 132, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 0.39, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 28.7, "totalProtein": 6.8, "lowMotivation": false, "triglycerides": 85, "menstrualPhase": "postmenopausal", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "freeTestosterone": 2, "totalCholesterol": 242}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History of present illness: 58-year-old female presenting for review of laboratory results related to menopausal evaluation and cardiovascular risk assessment. No acute complaints documented in lab data. Patient reports no current episodes of abnormal vaginal bleeding, severe pelvic pain, or other acute gynecologic symptoms per intake (if symptoms develop, advised to notify clinic urgently).\\n- Menstrual/gynecologic history: Labs consistent with menopause; confirm uterine status (intact vs prior hysterectomy) in chart (action item).\\n- Medications/supplements: Review required today for possible exogenous estrogen sources (oral estrogen, OCPs, topical creams, compounded products, herbal/phytoestrogen supplements). Staff to confirm medication/supplement list and report to provider same day.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\nVital signs: Not available in chart today. (BMI/BP not provided.)\\n\\nLaboratory results (04/02/2026) — grouped and flagged:\\n\\nCBC\\n- Hemoglobin 13.9 g/dL (normal)\\n- Hematocrit 41.7 % (normal)\\n- MCV 95.2 fL (normal)\\n- Platelets 277 K/uL (normal)\\n\\nCMP / Renal / Hepatic\\n- Creatinine 0.69 mg/dL (normal)\\n- eGFR 100 mL/min/1.73m2 (normal)\\n- AST 25 U/L (normal)\\n- ALT 19 U/L (normal)\\n\\nIron studies / related\\n- Ferritin 74 ng/mL (normal)\\n- TIBC 293 ug/dL (normal)\\n- Serum iron 114 ug/dL (normal)\\n\\nHematologic / metabolic\\n- Hemoglobin A1c 5.4 % (normal)\\n- Vitamin B12 856 pg/mL (normal)\\n\\nLipids / Cardiovascular biomarkers\\n- Total cholesterol: (not provided)\\n- LDL cholesterol 145 mg/dL (BORDERLINE HIGH)\\n- HDL cholesterol 80 mg/dL (normal)\\n- Triglycerides 85 mg/dL (normal)\\n- Apolipoprotein B (ApoB) 94 mg/dL (BORDERLINE)\\n- Lipoprotein(a) 6 mg/dL (normal)\\n\\nHormones\\n- Estradiol (E2) 40 pg/mL (ABNORMAL / borderline for postmenopausal — hypoestrogenic “crash” pattern noted)\\n- FSH 67.7 mIU/mL (ABNORMAL — consistent with menopause)\\n- Progesterone 0.39 ng/mL (normal for postmenopausal)\\n- Total testosterone 28.7 ng/dL (normal)\\n- Free testosterone 2 pg/mL (normal)\\n- SHBG 128 nmol/L (ABNORMAL — markedly elevated)\\n- TSH 0.98 mIU/L (normal)\\n\\nOther\\n- 25-OH Vitamin D 52.9 ng/mL (BORDERLINE — adequate but suboptimal per clinic target)\\n- Lipid risk scores (AHA PREVENT 2023):\\n  - 10-year Total CVD risk: 6.7% (BORDERLINE)\\n  - 10-year ASCVD risk: 3.9% (BORDERLINE)\\n  - 10-year Heart Failure risk: 4.1%\\n  - 30-year CVD risk: 26.8% (30-yr ASCVD 15.0%, 30-yr HF 19.5%)\\n- Sleep apnea risk (STOP-BANG): 2 points (low)\\n- Red flags: None identified on lab review.\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1) Menopausal status with hypoestrogenic pattern — lab evidence of menopause/crash phase:\\n   - FSH 67.7 mIU/mL (consistent with menopause).\\n   - Estradiol 40 pg/mL — low for premenopausal, borderline/high for untreated postmenopausal; interpreted as hypoestrogenic “crash” pattern.\\n   - Clinical significance: decreased ovarian function, symptoms (vasomotor, sleep, mood, urogenital) may occur; patient may benefit from estrogen therapy if symptomatic.\\n   - Differential: physiologic menopause vs exogenous estrogen exposure vs less likely ovarian pathology if estradiol unexpectedly elevated for postmenopausal status.\\n\\n2) Elevated SHBG 128 nmol/L:\\n   - Clinical significance: reduces bioavailable testosterone/estrogen; may mask androgen deficiency symptoms; common causes include oral estrogen, OCPs, thyroid medication, liver disease.\\n   - Liver enzymes (AST/ALT) normal, TSH normal — medication review required to identify exogenous drivers.\\n\\n3) Lipid abnormalities / ASCVD risk borderline:\\n   - LDL 145 mg/dL, ApoB 94 mg/dL; 10-year Total CVD risk 6.7%, 10-year ASCVD 3.9% (borderline risk).\\n   - Clinical significance: lifestyle modification indicated; consider Coronary Artery Calcium (CAC) scoring and shared decision-making regarding statin if risk-enhancing factors present.\\n\\n4) Vitamin D insufficient per clinic target:\\n   - 25-OH Vitamin D 52.9 ng/mL — adequate but suboptimal (clinic target ≥60 ng/mL).\\n   - Clinical significance: supplementation recommended to optimize bone and metabolic health.\\n\\n5) Androgen status — total and free testosterone within reference range but bioavailable testosterone likely reduced due to high SHBG:\\n   - Do not initiate testosterone replacement until bioavailable testosterone measured and symptoms correlate; consider bioavailable testosterone testing.\\n\\n6) Preventive and safety considerations:\\n   - No acute laboratory red flags. If patient reports vaginal bleeding, pelvic pain, or if medication review does not reveal exogenous estrogen and estradiol elevation is unexplained, urgent gynecology evaluation and transvaginal ultrasound recommended.\\n   - Shared-decision required prior to initiation of HRT or statin; physician notification required for these decisions.\\n\\nPLAN:\\n(Ordered actions prioritized with timing)\\n\\nImmediate / Same day (staff actions)\\n1. Medication and supplement reconciliation (critical):\\n   - Contact patient / review EHR med list today to document all prescription, OTC, topical, compounded, herbal/phytoestrogen, and weight-loss supplements. Report findings to provider immediately.\\n   - Confirm uterine status (intact vs hysterectomy) in chart.\\n\\n2. Patient education today:\\n   - Explain lab results: menopausal status, elevated FSH, low estradiol, high SHBG, borderline LDL/risk.\\n   - Instruct patient to report any new vaginal bleeding or pelvic pain immediately.\\n   - Begin lifestyle counseling: Mediterranean-style diet, weight optimization, exercise (≥150 min/week aerobic + resistance training), limit alcohol, reinforce smoking cessation if applicable.\\n\\nNear term (within 1–2 weeks)\\n3. Additional testing (order):\\n   - Morning bioavailable testosterone (or calculated bioavailable testosterone) and repeat SHBG if indicated — fasting AM draw preferred. (Order within 1 week.)\\n   - If no exogenous estrogen exposure identified and estradiol elevation remains unexplained or patient has gynecologic symptoms → order transvaginal pelvic ultrasound and refer to gynecology urgently.\\n   - Consider Coronary Artery Calcium (CAC) scoring referral if patient and provider elect shared decision-making for statin initiation given 10-year CVD risk 6.7%.\\n\\n4. Vitamin D supplementation:\\n   - Start Vitamin D3 (Metagenics D3) 2000 IU PO daily with food. Recheck 25-OH Vitamin D in 8–12 weeks.\\n\\n5. Lipid and cardiovascular management:\\n   - Implement lifestyle modifications (dietary counseling, exercise, weight loss target if BMI >25).\\n   - Order fasting lipid panel and ApoB in 3 months after lifestyle measures or 6–12 weeks after statin initiation if started.\\n   - Discuss statin therapy only after provider review and consideration of CAC and risk-enhancing factors; do not start statin without provider sign-off.\\n\\n6. Hormone therapy considerations:\\n   - If patient is symptomatic from hypoestrogenism and provider elects treatment, recommended regimen (provider must authorize and document informed consent):\\n     - Transdermal estradiol 25–50 mcg/day (patch) preferred (especially given high SHBG and thrombotic risk profile).\\n     - If uterus intact: add micronized progesterone 100–200 mg nightly for endometrial protection.\\n     - If prior hysterectomy: progesterone not required.\\n   - Do NOT initiate HRT until provider review and confirmation that no contraindications exist (history of VTE, active estrogen-dependent malignancy, uncontrolled hypertension, etc.). Notify provider prior to initiation.\\n\\n7. Androgen therapy considerations:\\n   - Do not start testosterone replacement at this time.\\n   - If bioavailable testosterone is low and symptoms persist after addressing estrogen deficiency: consider transdermal testosterone trial beginning at 300 mcg/day (0.3 mg) per clinic protocol only after physician approval and informed consent.\\n   - If trial initiated: baseline and 3-month total testosterone, monitor for virilization, lipids, LFTs.\\n\\nRoutine / Follow-up\\n8. Follow-up appointments:\\n   - If HRT or testosterone started: schedule clinic follow-up at 6–12 weeks to assess symptoms and side effects; check labs as above.\\n   - If no hormone therapy started: routine follow-up and reassess labs in 3–6 months per clinic protocol (re-check lipids in ~3 months after lifestyle changes; re-check vitamin D at 8–12 weeks).\\n   - Re-evaluate need for DEXA scan if criteria met (age, risk factors) — order per provider discretion.\\n\\n9. Supplement recommendations (per clinic AI report):\\n   - High priority: UltraFlora® Healthy Weight with Akkermansia — 1 capsule daily (consider per patient preference and tolerance).\\n   - Medium priority options (discuss with patient and provider): NutraGems® CoQ10 300 (1 chewable gel daily); AdvaClear® (2 capsules twice daily); OmegaGenics® Fish Oil Neuro 1000 (1 softgel 1–2 times daily); GlutaClear® (2 capsules daily).\\n   - Document any new supplement starts and review for interactions.\\n\\n10. Orders placed / to be placed (staff to enter or confirm with provider):\\n   - Bioavailable testosterone (AM sample), SHBG (if repeat), fasting lipid panel with ApoB in 3 months, 25-OH Vitamin D recheck in 8–12 weeks.\\n   - Consider orders: transvaginal pelvic ultrasound (if indicated), CAC referral (if pursuing statin decision), DEXA (if indicated).\\n   - Hold prescription of HRT or statin until provider signs off.\\n\\nPhysician notification requirements\\n- Notify provider immediately if:\\n  - Patient denies any exogenous estrogen yet estradiol elevation unexplained.\\n  - Patient reports vaginal bleeding, pelvic pain, or other concerning symptoms.\\n  - Staff recommends initiating HRT or testosterone, or wishes to start a statin — provider sign-off required.\\n\\nAdministrative\\n- Recheck window: routine follow-up recommended in 3–6 months; specific testing per timeline above.\\n\\nDiscussion and patient understanding:\\n- Results reviewed and discussed with patient. Risks/benefits of potential hormone therapy and lipid interventions explained at a high level; plan for medication/supplement reconciliation and further testing discussed.\\n- Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding of plan and instructions to report vaginal bleeding or new concerning symptoms.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\nDate: 04/02/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": true, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": true, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": true}, "preventRisk": {"ldlGoal": "No specific LDL target; focus on risk-based statin intensity", "riskCategory": "borderline", "tenYearASCVD": 0.03883700304601588, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nBORDERLINE RISK (5-7.5%): Consider coronary artery calcium (CAC) scoring\\n\\nStatin therapy based on shared decision-making if risk-enhancing factors present", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0.06733174146948975, "thirtyYearASCVD": 0.15006728887632254, "ageValidFor30Year": true, "thirtyYearTotalCVD": 0.268117257892958, "tenYearHFPercentage": "4.1%", "tenYearHeartFailure": 0.04093634564874444, "statinRecommendation": "Consider statin if risk-enhancing factors present. CAC scoring may help guide decision.", "tenYearCVDPercentage": "6.7%", "tenYearASCVDPercentage": "3.9%", "thirtyYearHFPercentage": "19.5%", "thirtyYearHeartFailure": 0.19520273225001974, "thirtyYearCVDPercentage": "26.8%", "thirtyYearASCVDPercentage": "15.0%"}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Healthy Weight with Akkermansia", "caution": "Contains Akkermansia muciniphila and Bifidobacterium lactis B420 for metabolic and gut barrier support. Refrigerate after opening for maximum potency.", "category": "metabolic", "priority": "high", "rationale": "UltraFlora Healthy Weight with Akkermansia provides targeted probiotic support for the gut-metabolic axis. Akkermansia muciniphila supports gut barrier integrity, metabolic healt