"referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 3.883700304601588, "status": "borderline", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 3.9%", "recommendation": "Consider statin if risk-enhancing factors present. CAC scoring may help guide decision.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 4.093634564874444, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 4.1%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 2, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (2/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 26.8117257892958, "status": "borderline", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 26.8%, ASCVD 15.0%, Heart Failure 19.5%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 25, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 19, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 100, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.69, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 94, "status": "borderline", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Borderline elevated Apo B.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider pharmacotherapy if high ASCVD risk.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 6, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 293, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 114, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 40, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Hypoestrogenic Pattern", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 40 pg/mL with FSH of 67.7 mIU/mL indicates hypoestrogenic perimenopause — the \\"crash phase.\\" Estrogen production has dropped significantly and the brain is signaling strongly (elevated FSH) trying to stimulate the ovaries.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Transdermal estradiol 0.025–0.05 mg is appropriate replacement therapy. Add micronized progesterone for uterine protection and sleep support. Frame this as hormone replacement, not stimulation — we are restoring what the body needs. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen has dropped to a level that can cause symptoms like hot flashes, night sweats, brain fog, and mood changes. Replacing estrogen with a patch or gel can relieve these symptoms and protect your long-term health. Progesterone is added to protect your uterus and help with sleep.", "referenceRange": "E2 <50 with FSH >10 indicates estrogen deficiency"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 67.7, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Menopause", "interpretation": "FSH of 67.7 mIU/mL is consistent with menopause. No specific symptoms reported at this time. Elevated FSH reflects decreased ovarian function and reduced estrogen production. This is a normal part of reproductive aging but may benefit from hormone optimization if symptomatic.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: If symptomatic, consider hormone therapy (HT): transdermal estradiol + micronized progesterone (if uterus intact). Discuss benefits/risks of HT including cardiovascular, bone, and quality of life considerations. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your lab results confirm you are in menopause. Hormone therapy can help manage symptoms like hot flashes, sleep problems, and vaginal dryness while also protecting bone health.", "referenceRange": "FSH <25 mIU/mL premenopausal; ≥25 suggests transition; ≥40 menopausal"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 128, "status": "abnormal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 128 nmol/L is elevated. High SHBG reduces bioavailable testosterone and estrogen. Common causes: oral estrogen, oral contraceptives, thyroid medication, liver conditions.", "recommendation": "Evaluate SHBG drivers (oral estrogen, OCP, thyroid medication). Consider ordering Bioavailable Testosterone for complete androgen assessment.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 2, "status": "normal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 2 pg/mL is within normal functional range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}], "aiRecommendations": "FOR CLINIC STAFF ONLY — Clinical synthesis and action plan\\n\\n1) Overall clinical assessment (concise)\\n- Labs consistent with menopause/perimenopause: FSH 67.7 mIU/mL (menopausal range) with an estradiol of 40 pg/mL — hypoestrogenic “crash” pattern. Patient likely menopausal and may benefit from hormone therapy if symptomatic.\\n- Estradiol 40 pg/mL is borderline-high for a postmenopausal patient who is not on HRT → must exclude exogenous estrogen exposure and consider ovarian source if medication/supplement review is negative.\\n- SHBG markedly elevated (128 nmol/L) — will reduce bioavailable sex steroids; common causes include oral estrogen, OCPs, thyroid meds, hepatic dysfunction. Liver enzymes are normal (AST/ALT normal), but medication review is essential.\\n- Lipid risk: LDL 145 mg/dL, ApoB 94 mg/dL, 10-yr CVD risk 6.7% (borderline). Lifestyle management recommended; consider CAC and statin shared decision-making if risk-enhancers present.\\n- Vitamin D 25-OH 52.9 ng/mL — adequate but suboptimal for clinic protocol; supplementation recommended to target ≥60 ng/mL.\\n\\n2) Priority actions — CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE\\n\\nCRITICAL (do now / immediate provider notification)\\n- Confirm current medication and supplement list immediately for any exogenous estrogen sources (oral estrogen, combined OCPs, topical/creams, compounded products, phytoestrogen supplements). Staff: call patient or review EHR med list; report findings to provider today.\\n- If patient is not taking exogenous estrogen and has unexplained vaginal bleeding, pelvic pain, or clinical signs concerning for ovarian/uterine pathology → urgent gynaecology referral and pelvic/transvaginal ultrasound; notify provider immediately.\\n- Notify provider prior to any initiation of HRT (transdermal estradiol ± progesterone) or if planning to start statin.\\n\\nURGENT (within 1–2 weeks)\\n- Order Bioavailable Testosterone (or calculated bioavailable testosterone) and re-check SHBG drivers (confirm current thyroid medication dosing, liver function recently normal but review for hepatic disease). Send morning, fasting sample if lab requires.\\n- If patient is symptomatic and provider elects to treat hypoestrogenic symptoms, prepare for initiation of transdermal estradiol 25–50 mcg/day (see dosing below) and micronized progesterone for uterine protection — obtain consent and document counseling on risks/benefits. Provider review required before prescribing.\\n- For cardiovascular risk borderline (10-yr 6.7%), discuss CAC scoring as next step in shared decision-making; arrange referral/order if provider and patient agree.\\n\\nROUTINE (within 4–12 weeks)\\n- Start Metagenics D3 2000 IU daily; recheck 25-OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Lifestyle counseling (diet, exercise, smoking cessation) and schedule follow-up lipid panel in 3 months after lifestyle interventions or 6–12 weeks after any statin start.\\n- If starting HRT, schedule follow-up visit at 6–12 weeks post-initiation to assess symptoms, side effects, and consider labs per protocol.\\n\\n3) Testosterone (androgen) recommendations (if applicable)\\n- Current Total and Free testosterone listed as normal; SHBG high may reduce bioavailable testosterone — do NOT start testosterone replacement until bioavailable testosterone is measured and symptoms correlate.\\n- If bioavailable testosterone is low and patient has persistent clinical symptoms (low libido, low energy, reduced well-being) after addressing estrogen deficiency:\\n  - Consider a therapeutic trial of transdermal testosterone. Suggested starting dose (clinic protocol): 300 mcg (0.3 mg) daily transdermal gel. Alternative starting ranges 300–600 mcg/day depending on symptom severity and monitoring capability.\\n  - Monitoring: total testosterone at baseline and 3 months (draw morning sample), evaluate for virilizing effects (acne, hirsutism, voice change), monitor lipids and LFTs. Discontinue if adverse effects.\\n- Physician must approve testosterone therapy; document informed consent and follow-up plan.\\n\\n4) Lifestyle interventions to implement now (staff to counsel and document)\\n- Diet: Mediterranean-style diet, reduce saturated fat, emphasize vegetables, whole grains, legumes, fish, olive oil. Specific target: reduce LDL through diet and weight optimization.\\n- Exercise: ≥150 minutes/week moderate aerobic activity + twice-weekly resistance training.\\n- Weight: support weight loss if BMI >25 (5–10% weight loss improves lipids).\\n- Smoking cessation counseling and referral if positive.\\n- Sleep hygiene and alcohol moderation.\\n- Bone health: encourage calcium intake (dietary) and weight-bearing exercise; consider DEXA per age/risk factors (see additional testing).\\n- Vitamin D: instruct take Metagenics D3 2000 IU daily with food; document adherence plan.\\n\\n5) Additional testing and referrals to consider (staff order items per provider)\\n\\nImmediate/near-term\\n- Medication/supplement review documented and reported to provider (critical).\\n- Bioavailable testosterone (and repeating SHBG if indicated) — morning draw.\\n- If unexplained elevated estradiol and no exogenous source: transvaginal pelvic ultrasound (evaluate ovaries/uterus) and consider gynecology consult.\\n- If symptomatic and planning HRT: ensure up-to-date mammogram per age/guidelines before initiation if due.\\n- CBC and ferritin already normal; order DEXA if age ≥65 or <65 with risk factors for osteoporosis (fragility fracture, long-term glucocorticoids, smoking, low body weight).\\n- Consider Coronary Artery Calcium (CAC) score if shared decision-making for statin initiation given 10-yr CVD risk 6.7% (borderline).\\n\\nRoutine monitoring\\n- Fasting lipid panel (and ApoB) in 3 months after lifestyle interventions or 6–12 weeks after statin initiation.\\n- Vitamin D recheck 8–12 weeks after starting D3 2000 IU.\\n- If HRT started: symptom review and safety check at 6–12 weeks; estradiol level not routinely required but can be rechecked at provider discretion if clinical concern. Re-check FSH not necessary for routine follow-up.\\n- If testosterone trial started: total testosterone and safety labs at baseline and 3 months, then as clinically indicated.\\n\\n6) Physician notification requirements\\n- Notify provider immediately if:\\n  - Patient denies exogenous estrogen but estradiol elevation unexplained.\\n  - Patient reports vaginal bleeding, pelvic pain, or other concerning symptoms.\\n  - Staff proposes initiation of HRT (transdermal estradiol ± progesterone) or testosterone — provider sign-off required.\\n  - Considering starting statin therapy (provider decision) or ordering CAC.\\n- Provide provider with medication/supplement list and bioavailable testosterone/SHBG results when available.\\n\\n7) Summary of next steps and timeline (clear, actionable)\\n- Today (same day):\\n  - Review and document full medication/supplement list; report to provider.\\n  - Confirm presence/absence of uterine status (intact vs hysterectomy) in chart.\\n- Within 1 week:\\n  - Order morning Bioavailable Testosterone and re-check SHBG drivers; order pelvic ultrasound only if provider directs or if symptoms present.\\n  - Start Metagenics D3 2000 IU daily (if not contraindicated); counsel patient on adherence.\\n  - Begin lifestyle counseling and provide written resources/referrals.\\n- 1–2 weeks:\\n  - Provider decision point: initiate HRT (if symptomatic) — transdermal estradiol 25–50 mcg/day + micronized progesterone 100–200 mg nightly (if uterus intact). Document counseling and contraindications. If patient has hysterectomy, progesterone not required.\\n  - If starting testosterone trial (only after bioavailable testosterone low and provider approves): initiate transdermal testosterone 300 mcg/day.\\n- 6–12 weeks:\\n  - If HRT started: clinic follow-up to assess symptom response and adverse effects; consider estradiol level if clinically indicated.\\n  - If testosterone started: measure total testosterone, review for adverse effects, check lipids/LFTs.\\n- 8–12 weeks:\\n  - Recheck 25-OH Vitamin D.\\n- 3 months:\\n  - Recheck fasting lipid panel and ApoB after lifestyle changes or 6–12 weeks after statin start if initiated. Reassess need for CAC if still undecided on statin.\\n- Annual:\\n  - Annual lipid risk assessment and ASCVD risk recalculation; routine preventive care per age.\\n\\nPatient education points for staff to cover (scripted, to be delivered by clinician/staff)\\n- Explain menopausal status and that estradiol has fallen; if symptomatic, HRT can relieve vasomotor and sleep symptoms and protect bone health — discuss risks and benefits with provider.\\n- Ask about current medications/supplements explicitly (including topical creams and compounded products).\\n- Advise to report any new vaginal bleeding immediately.\\n- Explain rationale for vitamin D supplementation and timeline for rechecking.\\n- Discuss lifestyle changes and resources for diet/exercise/smoking cessation.\\n\\nNote on contraindications/caveats\\n- Do not start oral estrogen if risk factors for VTE, active or past estrogen-dependent malignancy, uncontrolled hypertension, or other contraindications — provider must review history.\\n- Transdermal estrogen preferred over oral when concerned about SHBG and thrombotic risk.\\n- All medication starts require provider sign-off and documentation of informed consent.\\n\\nIf you want, I can prepare:\\n- A checklist for the intake call to confirm med/supplement exposures today.\\n- Pre-populated order sets: bioavailable testosterone, pelvic ultrasound, CAC referral, D3 refill, and transdermal estradiol ± micronized progesterone prescription for provider signature.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 1.06, "detail": "Triglycerides 85 / HDL 80 = 1.06", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5.4%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 128, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 19, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "94 mg/dL", "detail": "Elevated atherogenic particle count", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 1, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (1/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Menopausal Transition", "confidence": "high", "description": "Clinical picture consistent with perimenopause or menopause. Hormonal shifts during this transition affect bone health, cardiovascular risk, mood, and quality of life.", "supportingFindings": ["Postmenopausal status", "FSH 67.7 mIU/mL (elevated, menopausal range)", "Estradiol 40 pg/mL (declining)"]}, {"name": "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Metabolic pattern consistent with insulin resistance and/or visceral adiposity. This drives inflammation, hormonal imbalance, and cardiovascular risk.", "supportingFindings": ["ApoB 94 mg/dL (elevated)", "Unexplained weight gain / central adiposity reported"]}]}	\N	2026-04-02 16:11:34.907772	2026-04-02 16:11:34.907772
