 "high", "rationale": "UltraFlora Healthy Weight with Akkermansia provides targeted probiotic support for the gut-metabolic axis. Akkermansia muciniphila supports gut barrier integrity, metabolic health, and healthy weight management. Indicated for insulin resistance, metabolic syndrome features, visceral adiposity, and weight loss resistance.", "indication": "Insulin resistance / visceral adiposity phenotype (gut-metabolic axis support); Inflammatory phenotype (gut-immune axis); Triglycerides 363 mg/dL (elevated, metabolic); ApoB 91 mg/dL (atherogenic metabolic pattern)", "phenotypes": ["Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "Inflammatory Burden"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "This specialized probiotic contains Akkermansia, a beneficial gut bacteria linked to healthy metabolism and weight management. It supports your gut barrier, helps manage metabolic inflammation, and works with your body's natural weight regulation systems.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance / visceral adiposity phenotype (gut-metabolic axis support)", "Inflammatory phenotype (gut-immune axis)", "Triglycerides 363 mg/dL (elevated, metabolic)", "ApoB 91 mg/dL (atherogenic metabolic pattern)", "hs-CRP 3.3 mg/L (metabolic inflammation)", "A1c 5.7% (glycemic dysregulation)"]}, {"dose": "1 tablet twice daily with meals", "name": "Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support", "caution": "Contains iron, folate, and B12 for comprehensive RBC support. Avoid taking with calcium-rich foods or dairy. May cause mild GI upset initially.", "category": "iron", "priority": "high", "rationale": "Hemagenics provides highly absorbable iron bisglycinate with B12, B6, and folate for comprehensive red blood cell support. Indicated for functional iron deficiency, iron deficiency without anemia, and iron-responsive symptoms.", "indication": "Ferritin 42.8 ng/mL (suboptimal ≤50, optimal >50); Fatigue with iron depletion", "phenotypes": ["Iron Deficiency"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "Your lab results suggest your iron stores are lower than optimal. This supplement provides a gentle, well-absorbed form of iron along with B12 and folate to support your energy levels, hair health, and overall vitality.", "supportingFindings": ["Ferritin 42.8 ng/mL (suboptimal ≤50, optimal >50)", "Fatigue with iron depletion"]}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Essential for patients on statins.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "ApoB 91 mg/dL (cardiovascular support); Triglycerides 363 mg/dL; hs-CRP 3.3 mg/L; TC 210 mg/dL", "phenotypes": ["Inflammatory Burden", "Oxidative Stress Burden"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["ApoB 91 mg/dL (cardiovascular support)", "Triglycerides 363 mg/dL", "hs-CRP 3.3 mg/L", "TC 210 mg/dL", "Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)", "Age 70 (declining CoQ10 production)"]}, {"dose": "2 tablets twice daily", "name": "EstroFactors™", "caution": "Nutritional support for estrogen balance and metabolism. Contains I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Supports healthy estrogen metabolite ratios.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "EstroFactors provides targeted nutritional support for estrogen metabolism and balance with I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Best suited for women with estrogen dominance patterns, PMS, perimenopausal hormone imbalance, or those needing broader estrogen metabolism support during hormonal transitions. Differs from Estrovera in that it addresses the underlying estrogen metabolism rather than vasomotor symptom relief.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected; Age-appropriate for estrogen metabolism support", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement helps your body process and balance estrogen more effectively. It supports healthy hormone metabolism, which can help with PMS symptoms, bloating, mood changes, and hormonal balance during life transitions.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected", "Age-appropriate for estrogen metabolism support"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 6, "apoBValue": 91, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "borderline", "riskCategory": "intermediate", "baseASCVDRisk": 7.634769702540388, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "statinGuidance": "Moderate-intensity statin reasonable based on shared decision-making.", "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "intermediate", "clinicalGuidance": "Intermediate risk (7.5-19.9%) without risk enhancers. Standard shared decision-making for statin therapy.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "cacRecommendation": "CAC scoring may help refine treatment decision if patient uncertain.", "hasBorderlineApoB": true}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "generally_recommended", "indicated": true, "rationale": "ApoB ≥90 mg/dL (91 mg/dL) with risk enhancers (elevated hs-CRP, elevated triglycerides, prediabetes/metabolic syndrome, elevated ApoB). Statin discussion recommended."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": true, "severity": "high", "recommendation": "Triglycerides 200-499 mg/dL (363 mg/dL). Recommend intensive lifestyle modification first. Rule out secondary causes (diabetes, hypothyroidism, medications, alcohol). If on statin and TG persists ≥150, consider prescription omega-3 (icosapent ethyl) for patients with established ASCVD or diabetes with additional risk factors."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "recommend", "rationale": "Metabolic syndrome pattern (prediabetes/diabetes with dyslipidemia). CAC recommended to assess cardiovascular risk and guide therapy intensity.", "recommended": true}}, "recheckWindow": "6-8 weeks (thyroid monitoring)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\nYour labs show some strengths (good vitamin D and HDL) but several important areas to address: macrocytic red blood cells (MCV 101.9 fL), thyroid underactivity (TSH 4.67 mIU/L), very high triglycerides (363 mg/dL), an intermediate 10-year total cardiovascular risk (13.9%), and an “estrogen dominance” hormone pattern with estradiol 170 pg/mL and very low progesterone 0.07 ng/mL. These results are actionable and many changes you can start right away.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Hemoglobin 13.9 g/dL and hematocrit 41.9% are within normal ranges.\\n- Vitamin D 25-OH is 71.8 ng/mL (optimal).\\n- Vitamin B12 655 pg/mL and folate 20 ng/mL are adequate.\\n- LDL 70 mg/dL and HDL 67 mg/dL are good.\\n- eGFR 60 mL/min, AST 24 U/L, ALT 18 U/L are normal.\\n\\nAreas to improve (with specific actions)\\n- MCV 101.9 fL (macrocytosis): Possible causes include low iron stores, hypothyroidism, alcohol, or meds. Ferritin is 42.8 ng/mL (low-normal; optimal >50). Action: add iron-rich foods (spinach, lentils, lean red meat), consider an oral iron supplement if recommended by your provider, and recheck ferritin in 8–12 weeks. Limit alcohol (≤2 drinks/week) as it can raise MCV and triglycerides.\\n- Thyroid: TSH 4.67 mIU/L (elevated). Action: discuss starting or adjusting thyroid therapy with your clinician; check free T4 now and repeat TSH in 6–8 weeks after any change.\\n- Triglycerides 363 mg/dL (very high): Action now — reduce refined carbs (swap white bread for whole grain, limit sugary drinks), add 25–30 g fiber daily (oatmeal breakfast, beans at lunch, vegetables at dinner), include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel), reduce saturated fat (lean meats, limit butter/cheese), and aim for daily 30 minutes brisk walking. Discuss prescription omega-3 or fibrate with your clinician if levels remain >300 mg/dL.\\n- Blood sugar: A1c 5.7% (prediabetes). Action: reduce refined carbs, include protein with each meal, and walk 10–15 minutes after meals.\\n- Cardiovascular risk: 10-year total CVD risk 13.9% (INTERMEDIATE). This means a higher-than-average risk over 10 years. Actions: increase to 200–300 minutes aerobic exercise weekly plus strength training 2×/week, follow a Mediterranean-style diet (olive oil, nuts, fish 3×/week), reduce sodium <2,300 mg/day, and discuss moderate-to-high intensity statin therapy with your provider to reach a 30–50% LDL reduction.\\n- Hormones: Estradiol 170 pg/mL and progesterone 0.07 ng/mL suggest ovulatory dysfunction/perimenopause (if you are still having cycles, progesterone in a true luteal sample should be much higher). SHBG is high at 132 nmol/L (often from oral estrogen), and total testosterone is low at 8.5 ng/dL. Action: track cycle timing and symptoms, discuss changing oral estrogen to transdermal estrogen to lower SHBG, and consider progesterone therapy or ovulation support with your clinician. For low testosterone effects, prioritize 7–8 hours sleep, add strength training 2–3× weekly, and manage stress (meditation, hobbies).\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Book a follow-up with your clinician within 4 weeks to review thyroid (TSH/free T4), discuss HRT form and possible progesterone, and consider treatment for triglycerides and statin therapy.\\n- Repeat fasting lipid panel and triglycerides in 4–12 weeks after lifestyle or medication changes.\\n- Recheck ferritin in 8–12 weeks after dietary changes or iron supplement.\\nYou’re doing well in some areas and there are clear, concrete steps to lower risks — your care team can help prioritize these changes and any medications.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 13.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 41.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 101.9, "status": "abnormal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "Elevated MCV (101.9 fL) indicates macrocytic red blood cells. Common causes include B12 deficiency, folate deficiency, hypothyroidism, alcohol use, or certain medications (methotrexate, hydroxyurea).", "recommendation": "check serum B12, folate, TSH, reticulocyte count, and medication list. Rule out alcohol use..", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 248, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 170, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol elevated above typical HRT range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Evaluate estrogen dose. Monitor for estrogen excess symptoms.", "referenceRange": "60-100 pg/mL (HRT goal, >40 for bone)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.07, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone below HRT therapeutic range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase progesterone dose. Target 8-10 ng/mL for optimal endometrial protection.", "referenceRange": "8-10 ng/mL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 16.7, "status": "borderline", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH lower than expected for postmenopausal status.", "recommendation": "Confirm menopausal status. Rule out pituitary dysfunction.", "referenceRange": ">25 mIU/mL (postmenopausal)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 8.5, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Low testosterone despite HRT.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase testosterone dose. Target 75-125 ng/dL for optimal results.", "referenceRange": "75-125 ng/dL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 4.67, "status": "abnormal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "Elevated TSH - hypothyroidism.", "recommendation": "Check Free T4, TPO antibodies. Consider levothyroxine if symptomatic.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 42.8, "status": "borderline", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Iron insufficiency (ferritin 31-50). Optimal is >50.", "recommendation": "Provider protocol: Metagenics Hemagenics if symptomatic (fatigue, hair loss, restless legs, exercise intolerance).", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 71.8, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D optimal (60-100 ng/mL). Supports hormone, bone, and immune health.", "recommendation": "Maintain current regimen. Monitor annually.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 655, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.7, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Prediabetes range (5.7-6.4%).", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider metformin if high risk.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 70, "status": "normal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 67, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 363, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "High triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Dietary changes. Reduce refined carbs and alcohol.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 13.91116660578757, "status": "abnormal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 13.9% (INTERMEDIATE RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nINTERMEDIATE-HIGH RISK (7.5-20%): Moderate-to-high intensity statin recommended\\n\\nRisk-enhancing factors may favor more aggressive therapy", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 7.634769702540388, "status": "abnormal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 7.6%", "recommendation": "MODERATE-TO-HIGH intensity statin based on shared decision-making", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 7.98570042293201, "status": "borderline", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 8.0%", "recommendation": "Consider SGLT2 inhibitor or GLP-1 agonist for cardioprotection if diabetic or high-risk.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 2, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (2/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 24, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 18, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 60, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 91, "status": "borderline", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Borderline elevated Apo B.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider pharmacotherapy if high ASCVD risk.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 6, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 425, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 107, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 20, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 3.3, "status": "abnormal", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Elevated hs-CRP - increased cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Address cardiovascular risk factors. Consider repeat testing.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 170, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Estrogen Dominance Pattern", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 170 pg/mL with progesterone of only 0.07 ng/mL represents estrogen dominance. This is not excess estrogen production — it reflects ovulatory dysfunction where estrogen continues but ovulation (and thus progesterone) fails. Associated symptoms include insomnia or 3am waking, anxiety, breast tenderness, migraines, and heavy or irregular cycles.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Add progesterone first — do not attempt to suppress estrogen. Micronized progesterone 100–200 mg nightly provides opposition and symptom relief. Avoid language about \\"too much estrogen\\" — the issue is inadequate progesterone from anovulatory cycles. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen is in the higher range, but the real issue is that progesterone hasn't kept up. This happens when ovulation becomes irregular in perimenopause. Adding progesterone will help balance things out and relieve symptoms like heavy periods, breast tenderness, and mood swings.", "referenceRange": "E2 >150 with P4 <5 indicates unopposed estrogen"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 132, "status": "abnormal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 132 nmol/L is elevated. High SHBG reduces bioavailable testosterone and estrogen. Common causes: oral estrogen, oral contraceptives, thyroid medication, liver conditions.", "recommendation": "Evaluate SHBG drivers (oral estrogen, OCP, thyroid medication). Consider ordering Bioavailable Testosterone for complete androgen assessment.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 1 pg/mL is within normal functional range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "of 6 markers positive", "value": 4, "status": "critical", "category": "Insulin Resistance Screening", "recheckTiming": "3-6 months", "interpretation": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: HIGH LIKELIHOOD (4/6 markers positive). Identified phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance. Treat according to phenotype-specific protocol.", "recommendation": "Phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance. Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose.", "referenceRange": "0-1 Low, 2 Moderate, 3+ High Likelihood"}], "aiRecommendations": "Unable to generate recommendations. Please review lab findings manually.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 5.42, "detail": "Triglycerides 363 / HDL 67 = 5.42", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5.7%", "detail": "Prediabetic range", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 132, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 18, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "91 mg/dL", "detail": "Elevated atherogenic particle count", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "3.3 mg/L", "detail": "Elevated systemic inflammation", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "high", "phenotypes": [{"key": "visceral_metabolic", "name": "Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Recheck A1c, lipid panel, hs-CRP, ApoB in 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["A1c >= 5.7%", "TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0"], "pathophysiology": "Early peripheral + hepatic insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia. Glucose may still be \\"normal.\\" A1c often rises last.", "triggerCriteria": ["A1c >= 5.7%", "TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0", "Central adiposity phenotype", "ALT above optimal range"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests your body may not be responding to insulin as efficiently as it should. Insulin is the hormone that helps move sugar from your bloodstream into your cells for energy.\\n\\nWhen cells become less sensitive to insulin:\\n- Blood sugar slowly rises over time\\n- Triglycerides increase\\n- Inflammation increases\\n- Weight gain around the midsection becomes easier\\n\\nThe good news? This stage is highly reversible with targeted nutrition, strength training, sleep optimization, and - when appropriate - medication support.\\n\\nWe'll focus on improving how your body processes fuel and reducing long-term cardiometabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": [