g / GI symptoms reported", "Vaginal dryness (mucosal barrier support)"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "generally_recommended", "indicated": true, "rationale": "ApoB ≥90 mg/dL (122 mg/dL) with risk enhancers (low HDL, elevated triglycerides, elevated ApoB). Statin discussion recommended."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": true, "severity": "borderline", "recommendation": "Triglycerides 150-199 mg/dL (184 mg/dL). Lifestyle modifications recommended (weight loss, exercise, reduce refined carbs/alcohol)."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "2-4 weeks (urgent values present)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\nYour labs show mild iron deficiency anemia and several cardiovascular risk factors that we should address together. Key abnormal numbers are below; your doctor needs to review these critical results before any medication changes.\\n\\nPositive findings (within expected ranges)\\n- MCV 86.2 fL (normal cell size)\\n- Platelets 337 K/µL (normal)\\n- TSH 0.81 mIU/L (thyroid function in the normal range)\\n- Vitamin B12 444 pg/mL, Folate 7.62 ng/mL (both normal)\\n- Hemoglobin A1c 5.6% (near normal blood sugar control)\\n- AST 20 U/L, ALT 15 U/L (liver tests normal)\\n- eGFR 71 mL/min, Creatinine 0.95 mg/dL (kidney function adequate)\\n- SHBG 59 nmol/L, Free testosterone 1 pg/mL (SHBG and free T in expected range)\\n\\nAreas to improve (exact values + actions)\\n- Iron and anemia: Hemoglobin 10.9 g/dL and Hematocrit 33% with Ferritin 33.2 ng/mL (ferritin 31–50 indicates iron insufficiency with anemia). Actions: eat 2–3 servings daily of iron-rich foods (lean red meat, poultry, beans, lentils, spinach), add vitamin C (orange or kiwi) with iron-rich meals to improve absorption, avoid tea/coffee with meals, and discuss starting oral iron with your clinician (a common option is ferrous sulfate 325 mg [~65 mg elemental iron] once daily or every other day—confirm dose with your provider). Recheck CBC and ferritin in 8–12 weeks after treatment starts.\\n- Cholesterol and heart risk: LDL 167 mg/dL (high), HDL 42 mg/dL (low), Triglycerides 184 mg/dL (borderline), Apolipoprotein B 122 mg/dL (borderline), Lipoprotein(a) 41 mg/dL (elevated), hs-CRP 1.3 mg/L (moderate). Actions (required): add 25–30 g fiber daily (oatmeal breakfast, beans at lunch, vegetables at dinner), include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines), reduce saturated fat (choose lean meats, limit butter/cheese), and do 30 minutes brisk walking daily. Discuss ASCVD risk score and possible statin therapy with your provider; elevated Lp(a) is often genetic—ask about family history and cardiology referral if early heart disease in family.\\n- Vitamin D: 25-OH vitamin D 32.6 ng/mL (insufficient for hormone optimization). Action: consider 1,000–2,000 IU vitamin D3 daily and recheck in 3 months; ensure calcium intake and weight-bearing exercise for bone health.\\n- Testosterone: Total testosterone 6.2 ng/dL (low). Actions: prioritize 7–8 hours sleep nightly, strength train 2–3x weekly, and manage stress (walking, meditation). Discuss symptoms and options with your clinician.\\n\\nHormone note: Estradiol 7 pg/mL, Progesterone 0.19 ng/mL, FSH 77.6 mIU/mL—these values are consistent with menopause. If you are still cycling or on hormones, tell your provider as interpretation changes.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Physician review urgently (critical results).\\n- If starting iron or vitamin D, follow up labs in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Lipid follow-up and ASCVD risk discussion within 4–8 weeks; consider cardiology referral if family history or provider recommends.\\nYou’re off to a good start — these are treatable concerns and small diet/activity changes can make a big difference.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 10.9, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation":