", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 1.3, "status": "borderline", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Borderline hs-CRP - moderate cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Continue monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 59, "status": "normal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 59 nmol/L is within normal range. SHBG regulates bioavailable sex hormones by binding testosterone and estradiol.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 1 pg/mL is within normal functional range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "of 6 markers positive", "value": 2, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Insulin Resistance Screening", "recheckTiming": "3-6 months", "interpretation": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive). This combination points toward hyperinsulinemia physiology. Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR).", "recommendation": "Phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance. Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose.", "referenceRange": "0-1 Low, 2 Moderate, 3+ High Likelihood"}], "aiRecommendations": "Clinical context: staff-facing synthesis of labs and protocol-driven actions. Prioritize acute metabolic risk (hyperglycemia), iron-deficiency anemia, dyslipidemia/cardiovascular risk, borderline vitamin D and low total testosterone. Actions below are for clinician review/ordering and staff implementation.\\n\\nCRITICAL — Physician notification required (Immediate)\\n- Fasting glucose 157 mg/dL (≥126 mg/dL diabetes threshold).\\n  - Action: Notify physician immediately for review.\\n  - Rationale: Single fasting glucose in diabetic range requires confirmation and urgent evaluation for diabetes mellitus and initiation of management plan if confirmed.\\n\\nURGENT — Actions to implement after physician notification\\n1. Confirm hyperglycemia\\n  - Order a repeat fasting plasma glucose (FPG) on a separate day (≥8 hours fasting) as soon as possible (preferably next available morning).\\n  - Order Hemoglobin A1c repeat and/or fasting/random plasma glucose if discordance persists. Note: current chart lists A1c as normal — document discordance and consider hemoglobin variants or acute stress/medication effect.\\n  - Order BMP (basic metabolic panel) if not current (eGFR/creatinine reported normal but reconfirm for medication safety).\\n  - If repeat testing confirms diabetes:\\n    - Prepare orders for initiation of first-line therapy (for physician to sign): Metformin starting 500 mg PO with evening meal daily, titrate per tolerance to 500 mg BID after 1 week then up to 1000 mg BID as indicated; check eGFR prior to initiation and avoid if eGFR <30 mL/min/1.73m2.\\n    - Baseline orders: fasting lipid panel (already available), urine albumin-creatinine ratio, referral/education for diabetes self-management.\\n  - Patient education points to cover at first contact:\\n    - Need to repeat fasting test to confirm.\\n    - If confirmed, lifestyle measures (dietary carbohydrate reduction, weight loss, increase activity) and medication options will be discussed.\\n    - Review current medications (steroids, atypical antipsychotics) that may raise glucose.\\n\\nPRIORITY — Iron-deficiency anemia (Start now per protocol)\\n- Labs: Hemoglobin 10.9 g/dL, Hematocrit 33%, Ferritin 33.2 ng/mL (iron-insufficient with anemia per protocol).\\n- Protocol prescription (staff may prepare for physician signature):\\n  - Elemental iron 65 mg PO every other day, empty stomach; instruct to avoid caffeine and dairy 2 hours before and after dose.\\n  - Add advice to take with 250–500 mg vitamin C or orange juice to increase absorption if tolerated (confirm if protocol allows).\\n- Monitoring and follow-up:\\n  - Repeat CBC and ferritin in 8–12 weeks after starting therapy.\\n  - If symptoms severe or Hgb <10 g/dL, or if intolerance to oral iron, notify physician urgently for IV iron consideration.\\n- Clinical questions to address:\\n  - Obtain menstrual history: quantify bleeding/heavy menses (Pictorial Blood Assessment Chart or patient report); if heavy menses, consider gynecology referral.\\n  - Pregnancy test if of reproductive potential before initiating therapy or further work-up.\\n\\nCARDIOVASCULAR RISK — Lipids and Lp(a)\\n- Abnormal labs: LDL 167 mg/dL, ApoB 122 mg/dL, Lp(a) 41 mg/dL, HDL 42 mg/dL, Triglycerides 184 mg/dL, hs-CRP 1.3 mg/L.\\n- Immediate staff actions:\\n  - Calculate 10-year ASCVD risk (use pooled cohort equations) and document age, BP, smoking status, diabetes status once confirmed to guide statin decisions.\\n  - Notify physician with lipid summary and calculated ASCVD risk.\\n- Pharmacotherapy (physician decision):\\n  - If ASCVD risk ≥7.5–10% (or higher depending on age/comorbidities), consider initiating statin:\\n    - Example order for physician: Atorvastatin 20–40 mg PO nightly (moderate-to-high intensity) or Rosuvastatin 10–20 mg PO nightly. Select dose based on risk and target LDL reduction.\\n  - If statin is started: baseline LFTs already normal; recheck lipids and LFTs 4–12 weeks after initiation and then every 3–12 months.\\n- Lp(a) and ApoB considerations:\\n  - Note Lp(a) 41 mg/dL is elevated; document genetic risk and consider more aggressive LDL lowering if borderline risk.\\n  - Consider coronary artery calcium (CAC) scoring if ASCVD risk uncertain — order referral for CAC imaging per physician decision.\\n- Lifestyle counseling to provide (see Lifestyle section).\\n\\nHRT / Testosterone (If applicable)\\n- Lab: Total testosterone 6.2 ng/dL (borderline low), Free testosterone and SHBG reported normal.\\n- Staff assessment and options to present to physician:\\n  - If patient is symptomatic (low libido, severe fatigue) and desires therapy, consider trial of testosterone therapy after shared decision-making and baseline counseling.\\n  - Suggested starting regimen for symptomatic patients (physician order required):\\n    - Transdermal testosterone gel/cream low-dose trial (typical female dosing: start at 300 mcg [0.3 mg] to 400 mcg daily); alternative preparations require specialist oversight. Use lowest effective dose.\\n    - Monitor total testosterone 3 months after initiation (measure mid-therapy level), adjust dose to achieve mid-normal female range and clinical response.\\n  - Monitoring while on therapy:\\n    - Check total testosterone, lipids, liver function, fasting glucose, and signs of virilization at 3 months and then every 6–12 months.\\n  - If asymptomatic, recommend conservative management and reassessment; consider optimizing anemia, vitamin D, and mental health before initiating testosterone.\\n- Hormone testing considerations:\\n  - Note cycle phase can affect sex-hormone interpretation. If rechecking testosterone for diagnosis, schedule blood draw in early follicular phase (cycle days 1–7) when feasible and ensure washout of exogenous hormones per protocol.\\n\\nROUTINE / Other protocol-driven actions\\n- Vitamin D insufficiency 25-OH 32.6 ng/mL (target ≥60 ng/mL per protocol):\\n  - Start Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily per protocol (physician sign-off).\\n  - Recheck 25-OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks.\\n  - Document baseline calcium and review hypercalcemia risk; if patient on thiazides or hypercalcemia history, notify physician.\\n- Triglycerides 184 mg/dL and low HDL:\\n  - Lifestyle interventions (see below). If persistent >175–199 mg/dL, focus on TG-lowering lifestyle; pharmacotherapy typically reserved for higher levels or ASCVD risk.\\n- hs-CRP 1.3 mg/L: monitor; reinforce lifestyle interventions.\\n\\nAdditional testing/referrals to consider\\n- Gynecology referral if heavy menses suspected or abnormal uterine bleeding.\\n- Nutrition/dietary counseling referral for weight management, diabetes prevention/management, and lipid optimization.\\n- Cardiology referral or CAC scan if ASCVD risk uncertain and Lp(a) elevated.\\n- Endocrinology referral if complex hormonal management needed (e.g., initiating testosterone, discordant glycemic markers, abnormal pituitary concerns).\\n- If diabetes confirmed: diabetes education program and consideration of CGM or primary care referral.\\n\\nPatient education points to cover (staff to deliver)\\n- Explain need to repeat fasting glucose to confirm results and that A1c may be discordant; emphasize urgency.\\n- If starting iron: dosing schedule (65 mg elemental every other day), expected timeline to see Hgb improvement (4–8 weeks), common GI side effects and strategies (take with food if needed; vitamin C to help absorption), and the need for follow-up labs in 8–12 weeks.\\n- If starting vitamin D: take daily, recheck levels in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Lifestyle counseling: reduce refined carbohydrates and alcohol, increase aerobic exercise, Mediterranean-style diet, smoking cessation if applicable.\\n- If testosterone is discussed: counsel on benefits, risks, monitoring requirements, and signs of virilization to report.\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (action checklist)\\n1. Immediate (today)\\n  - Notify physician now about fasting glucose 157 mg/dL and discordant normal A1c.\\n  - Prepare and present diabetes confirmation orders (repeat FPG next morning; repeat A1c if requested).\\n  - Prepare iron prescription per protocol (65 mg elemental every other day) and notify physician to sign/approve.\\n  - Initiate vitamin D supplement order per protocol pending physician sign-off.\\n\\n2. Within 1 week\\n  - Repeat fasting plasma glucose (fasting ≥8 hours).\\n  - Review medication list for agents raising glucose; document acute illness/stressors.\\n  - Calculate and document ASCVD 10-year risk; notify physician for statin consideration.\\n\\n3. 4–12 weeks\\n  - If iron started: repeat CBC and ferritin at 8–12 weeks.\\n  - If vitamin D started: recheck 25-OH vitamin D at 8–12 weeks.\\n  - If statin started: check lipids and LFTs at 4–12 weeks after initiation.\\n  - If testosterone started: check total testosterone and adverse effects at 3 months.\\n\\n4. Ongoing\\n  - Document menstrual history and consider gynecology referral if heavy bleeding.\\n  - If diabetes confirmed: initiate diabetic baseline monitoring (urine ACR, eye exam referral as indicated) and diabetes education.\\n\\nNotes and caveats\\n- Do not diagnose in the chart without physician sign-off — present these recommendations to the supervising physician for orders.\\n- Consider cycle timing and exogenous hormones when interpreting sex steroids; retest hormones in early follicular phase if more precise assessment required.\\n- Confirm all prescription orders are within clinic protocol scope and obtain physician signature prior to dispensing or initiation.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 4.38, "detail": "Triglycerides 184 / HDL 42 = 4.38", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5.6%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 59, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 15, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "122 mg/dL", "detail": "Elevated atherogenic particle count", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "1.3 mg/L", "detail": "Low inflammatory burden", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "moderate", "phenotypes": [{"key": "visceral_metabolic", "name": "Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Recheck A1c, lipid panel, hs-CRP, ApoB in 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL"], "pathophysiology": "Early peripheral + hepatic insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia. Glucose may still be \\"normal.\\" A1c often rises last.", "triggerCriteria": ["A1c >= 5.7%", "TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0", "Central adiposity phenotype", "ALT above optimal range"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests your body may not be responding to insulin as efficiently as it should. Insulin is the hormone that helps move sugar from your bloodstream into your cells for energy.\\n\\nWhen cells become less sensitive to insulin:\\n- Blood sugar slowly rises over time\\n- Triglycerides increase\\n- Inflammation increases\\n- Weight gain around the midsection becomes easier\\n\\nThe good news? This stage is highly reversible with targeted nutrition, strength training, sleep optimization, and - when appropriate - medication support.\\n\\nWe'll focus on improving how your body processes fuel and reducing long-term cardiometabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward nutrition (>= 25-35g/meal)", "Carbohydrate quality + timing education", "Strength training 2-3x weekly", "10-15 min post-meal walking", "Sleep optimization", "Consider: GLP-1 therapy if obesity, appetite dysregulation, A1c >= 5.7, or inflammatory phenotype", "Consider: Metformin if prediabetes or strong family history", "Recheck metabolic panel in 3-6 months"]}], "positiveCount": 