ning for maximum potency.", "category": "metabolic", "priority": "high", "rationale": "UltraFlora Healthy Weight with Akkermansia provides targeted probiotic support for the gut-metabolic axis. Akkermansia muciniphila supports gut barrier integrity, metabolic health, and healthy weight management. Indicated for insulin resistance, metabolic syndrome features, visceral adiposity, and weight loss resistance.", "indication": "Insulin resistance / visceral adiposity phenotype (gut-metabolic axis support); Gut-microbiome support phenotype; Inflammatory phenotype (gut-immune axis); Weight gain / weight loss resistance", "phenotypes": ["Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "Gut-Microbiome Support", "Inflammatory Burden"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "This specialized probiotic contains Akkermansia, a beneficial gut bacteria linked to healthy metabolism and weight management. It supports your gut barrier, helps manage metabolic inflammation, and works with your body's natural weight regulation systems.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance / visceral adiposity phenotype (gut-metabolic axis support)", "Gut-microbiome support phenotype", "Inflammatory phenotype (gut-immune axis)", "Weight gain / weight loss resistance", "Triglycerides 184 mg/dL (elevated, metabolic)", "ApoB 122 mg/dL (atherogenic metabolic pattern)", "Bloating reported (gut-metabolic dysfunction)"]}, {"dose": "1 tablet twice daily with meals", "name": "Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support", "caution": "Contains iron, folate, and B12 for comprehensive RBC support. Avoid taking with calcium-rich foods or dairy. May cause mild GI upset initially.", "category": "iron", "priority": "high", "rationale": "Hemagenics provides highly absorbable iron bisglycinate with B12, B6, and folate for comprehensive red blood cell support. Indicated for functional iron deficiency, iron deficiency without anemia, and iron-responsive symptoms.", "indication": "Ferritin 33.2 ng/mL (suboptimal ≤50, optimal >50); Serum iron 42 µg/dL (low <60); Hemoglobin 10.9 g/dL (low); Fatigue with iron depletion", "phenotypes": ["Iron Deficiency"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "Your lab results suggest your iron stores are lower than optimal. This supplement provides a gentle, well-absorbed form of iron along with B12 and folate to support your energy levels, hair health, and overall vitality.", "supportingFindings": ["Ferritin 33.2 ng/mL (suboptimal ≤50, optimal >50)", "Serum iron 42 µg/dL (low <60)", "Hemoglobin 10.9 g/dL (low)", "Fatigue with iron depletion"]}, {"dose": "2 capsules daily", "name": "GlutaClear®", "caution": "Glutathione and antioxidant support formula. Contains NAC, alpha-lipoic acid, and green tea catechins. Supports cellular defense against oxidative damage.", "category": "detox", "priority": "high", "rationale": "GlutaClear supports glutathione production and antioxidant defense with NAC, alpha-lipoic acid, and green tea catechins. Indicated when there is evidence of oxidative stress, inflammatory burden, or metabolic dysfunction requiring enhanced cellular defense. While AdvaClear provides broader liver detoxification support, GlutaClear specifically targets glutathione pathways and antioxidant capacity.", "indication": "Oxidative stress phenotype (glutathione/antioxidant support indicated); Inflammatory burden (antioxidant defense support); Insulin resistance (oxidative metabolic stress); Low energy (mitochondrial antioxidant support)", "phenotypes": ["Oxidative Stress Burden", "Inflammatory Burden", "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "This supplement boosts your body's master antioxidant, glutathione, which protects your cells from damage. Your results suggest increased oxidative stress, and this formula helps your body defend and repair at the cellular level.", "supportingFindings": ["Oxidative stress phenotype (glutathione/antioxidant support indicated)", "Inflammatory burden (antioxidant defense support)", "Insulin resistance (oxidative metabolic stress)", "Low energy (mitochondrial antioxidant support)", "Brain fog (CNS oxidative burden)", "Age 53 (declining glutathione production)"]}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Essential for patients on statins.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "ApoB 122 mg/dL (cardiovascular support); Triglycerides 184 mg/dL; LDL 167 mg/dL; HDL 42 mg/dL (low)", "phenotypes": ["Inflammatory Burden", "Oxidative Stress Burden"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["ApoB 122 mg/dL (cardiovascular support)", "Triglycerides 184 mg/dL", "LDL 167 mg/dL", "HDL 42 mg/dL (low)", "TC 246 mg/dL", "Lp(a) 41 (elevated)", "Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)", "Age 53 (declining CoQ10 production)"]}, {"dose": "3 capsules daily (divided doses)", "name": "Magtein® Magnesium L-Threonate", "caution": "L-Threonate form crosses blood-brain barrier for cognitive and sleep support. Well-tolerated; gentle on GI system.", "category": "mineral", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Magtein is the only magnesium form clinically shown to cross the blood-brain barrier. Supports quality sleep, cognitive function, GABA activity, and stress resilience.", "indication": "Sleep disruption reported; Low energy reported; Anxiety reported (magnesium supports GABA); Brain fog reported", "phenotypes": ["Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "This special form of magnesium is designed to reach the brain, where it supports better sleep quality, mental clarity, and a calmer nervous system.", "supportingFindings": ["Sleep disruption reported", "Low energy reported", "Anxiety reported (magnesium supports GABA)", "Brain fog reported", "Headaches reported", "Irritability reported"]}, {"dose": "1 soft chew during times of stress", "name": "HerWellness™ Rapid Stress Relief", "caution": "Fast-acting L-Theanine and Lactium formula. Non-drowsy. Promotes calm within 1 hour. Contains milk.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "HerWellness Rapid Stress Relief provides fast-acting stress support with L-Theanine (200mg) and Lactium. Promotes calm within 1 hour without drowsiness.", "indication": "Anxiety reported; Sleep disruption (stress-related); Irritability reported; Mood changes reported", "phenotypes": ["Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This fast-acting supplement helps your body respond to stress more calmly. It promotes relaxation within about an hour and can be taken as needed without causing drowsiness.", "supportingFindings": ["Anxiety reported", "Sleep disruption (stress-related)", "Irritability reported", "Mood changes reported"]}, {"dose": "1 softgel daily with meal", "name": "D3 5,000 + K", "caution": "Repletion dose for deficiency/insufficiency. Contains vitamin K2 for optimal calcium metabolism. Recheck levels in 8-12 weeks.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "D3 5,000 + K provides vitamin D3 with K2 for repletion of deficiency/insufficiency. Target ≥60 ng/mL for bone, immune, and cardiovascular support.", "indication": "Vitamin D 32.6 ng/mL (insufficient)", "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D is below optimal range. This supplement will help restore your levels to support bone health, immune function, and overall wellness.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 32.6 ng/mL (insufficient)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support; Bloating / GI symptoms reported", "phenotypes": ["Gut-Microbiome Support"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support", "Bloating / GI symptoms reported"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 41, "apoBValue": 122, "lpaStatus": "elevated", "apoBStatus": "borderline", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 2.942599452410798, "hasElevatedLpa": true, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Lp(a) 41 mg/dL - RISK ENHANCER (genetic risk factor). Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": true}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "generally_recommended", "indicated": true, "rationale": "ApoB ≥90 mg/dL (122 mg/dL) with risk enhancers (low HDL, elevated triglycerides, elevated ApoB). Statin discussion recommended."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": true, "severity": "borderline", "recommendation": "Triglycerides 150-199 mg/dL (184 mg/dL). Lifestyle modifications recommended (weight loss, exercise, reduce refined carbs/alcohol)."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "consider", "rationale": "Borderline lipid levels with multiple risk enhancers (low HDL, elevated triglycerides, elevated ApoB). Consider CAC to refine risk assessment.", "recommended": true}}, "recheckWindow": "2-4 weeks (urgent values present)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\nYou are in menopause: your estradiol is 7 pg/mL and FSH is 77.6 mIU/mL, which confirms menopausal status and explains symptoms (hot flashes, night sweats, sleep disruption). You have mild iron-deficiency anemia (hemoglobin 10.9 g/dL, hematocrit 33%, ferritin 33.2 ng/mL). Your LDL is high at 167 mg/dL with triglycerides 184 mg/dL and HDL 42 mg/dL; Lipoprotein(a) is elevated at 41 mg/dL. Your 10-year ASCVD risk is low at 2.9%, but your 30-year CVD risk is 25.5% (higher long-term risk).\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- TSH 0.81 mIU/L (normal), Vitamin B12 444 pg/mL (normal), MCV 86.2 fL (normal cell size).\\n- eGFR 71 mL/min and liver tests AST 20 U/L, ALT 15 U/L are normal.\\n- Hemoglobin A1c 5.6% (normal glycemic control).\\n\\nAreas to improve (with concrete actions)\\n1) Iron deficiency anemia (Hgb 10.9 g/dL, Hct 33%, ferritin 33.2 ng/mL)\\n- Start oral iron (example: ferrous sulfate 325 mg ≈65 mg elemental iron) once daily or every other day — take with a glass of orange juice or 250 mg vitamin C to boost absorption; avoid taking with calcium, tea/coffee, or high-fiber meals.\\n- Eat iron-rich meals: 2–3 servings/week of red meat or poultry; add beans or lentils and iron-fortified cereals; include vitamin C (citrus, bell peppers) at meals.\\n- Recheck CBC and ferritin in 6–8 weeks. If symptoms persist or levels do not improve, call clinic.\\n\\n2) Cholesterol & cardiovascular risk (LDL 167 mg/dL, TG 184 mg/dL, HDL 42 mg/dL, Lp(a) 41 mg/dL)\\n- Add 25–30 g fiber daily: oatmeal for breakfast, beans with lunch, vegetables at dinner.\\n- Include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n- Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter and cheese.\\n- Daily exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking (or equivalent) most days.\\n- Because your 10-year ASCVD risk is low (2.9%), statin is not automatically recommended now, but because LDL is 167 mg/dL, Lp(a) is 41 mg/dL, and your 30-year risk is 25.5%, follow-up and possible medication discussion is advised if lifestyle changes do not lower LDL—discuss with your provider in 3 months.\\n\\n3) Vitamin D and testosterone\\n- Vitamin D 25‑OH 32.6 ng/mL (insufficient): take vitamin D3 1,000–2,000 IU daily and recheck in 3 months.\\n- Total testosterone 6.2 ng/dL (low): prioritize 7–8 hours sleep, strength training 2–3×/week, and stress management (walks, meditation).\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Schedule visit to review results and menopausal symptom options (hormone therapy can be discussed given estradiol 7 pg/mL, FSH 77.6 mIU/mL and bothersome symptoms).\\n- Start iron and lifestyle measures now. Recheck CBC/ferritin in 6–8 weeks, lipids in ~3 months, vitamin D in 3 months.\\n- If symptoms worsen or you have chest pain, shortness of breath, or fainting, seek care immediately.\\n\\nYou’re taking the right first steps — we’ll review progress and adjust treatment together.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 10.9, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Low hemoglobin indicating anemia.", "recommendation": "Check iron studies, B12, folate. Evaluate menstrual blood loss if applicable.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 33, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Low hematocrit suggesting anemia.", "recommendation": "Evaluate for iron deficiency or other causes. Check iron studies.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 86.2, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 86.2 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 337, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 7, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol appropriate for postmenopausal status.", "recommendation": "Consider HRT if symptomatic for vasomotor symptoms, bone health, or quality of life.", "referenceRange": "<20 pg/mL (postmenopausal without HRT)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.19, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone appropriate for postmenopausal status.", "recommendation": "Expected finding.", "referenceRange": "<1 ng/mL (postmenopausal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 77.6, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "Elevated FSH consistent with menopause.", "recommendation": "Expected finding for postmenopausal women.", "referenceRange": ">25 mIU/mL (postmenopausal)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 6.2, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Low testosterone.", "recommendation": "May contribute to low libido or fatigue. Consider HRT discussion.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 0.81, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 33.2, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Iron insufficiency WITH anemia (ferritin 31-50).", "recommendation": "Provider protocol: Prescription 65mg Elemental Iron (take every other day, on empty stomach, avoid caffeine and dairy 2 hours before/after).", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 32.6, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D insufficient (31-40 ng/mL). Suboptimal for hormone optimization.", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 5,000 + K daily. Target ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 444, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.6, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 167, "status": "abnormal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "High LDL.", "recommendation": "Consider statin based on ASCVD risk. Lifestyle modifications.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 42, "status": "abnormal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Low HDL - increased cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Increase aerobic exercise. Consider niacin if very low.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 184, "status": "borderline", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Borderline high triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Reduce sugar and alcohol.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 4.174492716839115, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 4.2% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 2.942599452410798, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 2.9%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 1.2811823425441884, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 1.3%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (1/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 25.53691143547517, "status": "borderline", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 25.5%, ASCVD 17.0%, Heart Failure 10.1%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 20, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 15, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 71, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.95, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 122, "status": "borderline", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Borderline elevated Apo B.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider pharmacotherapy if high ASCVD risk.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 41, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Elevated Lp(a) - genetic cardiovascular risk factor.", "recommendation": "Lp(a) is genetically determined. Consider more aggressive LDL lowering. CAC scoring may help refine risk.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 439, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 42, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 7.62, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 1.3, "status": "borderline", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Borderline hs-CRP - moderate cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Continue monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 7, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Hypoestrogenic Pattern", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 7 pg/mL with FSH of 77.6 mIU/mL indicates hypoestrogenic perimenopause — the \\"crash phase.\\" Estrogen production has dropped significantly and the brain is signaling strongly (elevated FSH) trying to stimulate the ovaries.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Transdermal estradiol 0.025–0.05 mg is appropriate replacement therapy. Add micronized progesterone for uterine protection and sleep support. Frame this as hormone replacement, not stimulation — we are restoring what the body needs. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen has dropped to a level that can cause symptoms like hot flashes, night sweats, brain fog, and mood changes. Replacing estrogen with a patch or gel can relieve these symptoms and protect your long-term health. Progesterone is added to protect your uterus and help with sleep.", "referenceRange": "E2 <50 with FSH >10 indicates estrogen deficiency"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 77.6, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Menopause", "interpretation": "FSH of 77.6 mIU/mL with low estradiol (7 pg/mL) confirms menopausal status. Symptoms present: hot flashes, night sweats, sleep disruption. Elevated FSH reflects decreased ovarian function and reduced estrogen production. This is a normal part of reproductive aging but may benefit from hormone optimization if symptomatic.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: If symptomatic, consider hormone therapy (HT): transdermal estradiol + micronized progesterone (if uterus intact). Discuss benefits/risks of HT including cardiovascular, bone, and quality of life considerations. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your lab results confirm you are in menopause. Hormone therapy can help manage symptoms like hot flashes, sleep problems, and vaginal dryness while also protecting bone health.", "referenceRange": "FSH <25 mIU/mL premenopausal; ≥25 suggests transition; ≥40 menopausal"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 59, "status": "normal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 59 nmol/L is within normal range. SHBG regulates bioavailable sex hormones by binding testosterone and estradiol.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 1 pg/mL is within normal functional range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "of 6 markers positive", "value": 2, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Insulin Resistance Screening", "recheckTiming": "3-6 months", "interpretation": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive). This combination points toward hyperinsulinemia physiology. Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR).", "recommendation": "Phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance. Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose.", "referenceRange": "0-1 Low, 2 Moderate, 3+ High Likelihood"}], "aiRecommendations": "Unable to generate recommendations. Please review lab findings manually.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 4.38, "detail": "Triglycerides 184 / HDL 42 = 4.38", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5.6%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 59, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 15, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "122 mg/dL", "detail": "Elevated atherogenic particle count", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "1.3 mg/L", "detail": "Low inflammatory burden", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "moderate", "phenotypes": [{"key": "visceral_metabolic", "name": "Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Recheck A1c, lipid panel, hs-CRP, ApoB in 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL"], "pathophysiology": "Early peripheral + hepatic insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia. Glucose may still be \\"normal.\\" A1c often rises last.", "triggerCriteria": ["A1c >= 5.7%", "TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0", "Central adiposity phenotype", "ALT above optimal range"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests your body may not be responding to insulin as efficiently as it should. Insulin is the hormone that helps move sugar from your bloodstream into your cells for energy.\\n\\nWhen cells become less sensitive to insulin:\\n- Blood sugar slowly rises over time\\n- Triglycerides increase\\n- Inflammation increases\\n- Weight gain around the midsection becomes easier\\n\\nThe good news? This stage is highly reversible with targeted nutrition, strength training, sleep optimization, and - when appropriate - medication support.\\n\\nWe'll focus on improving how your body processes fuel and reducing long-term cardiometabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward nutrition (>= 25-35g/meal)", "Carbohydrate quality + timing education", "Strength training 2-3x weekly", "10-15 min post-meal walking", "Sleep optimization", "Consider: GLP-1 therapy if obesity, appetite dysregulation, A1c >= 5.7, or inflammatory phenotype", "Consider: Metformin if prediabetes or strong family history", "Recheck metabolic panel in 3-6 months"]}], "positiveCount": 2, "likelihoodLabel": "MODERATE LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive). This combination points toward hyperinsulinemia physiology. Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR).", "confirmationTests": "Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose."}, "clinicalPhenoty