nce, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}]	\N	2026-01-14 00:00:00	2026-03-30 21:41:45.495199	Goal: Support heart health by lowering LDL, reduce inflammation/iron-related burden from elevated ferritin, and improve hydration to normalize hematocrit.\n\nDiet: Mediterranean-style, anti-inflammatory diet\n\nFoods to Emphasize:\nSalmon - (3-4 oz, 2-3x/week) - to help lower LDL and provide dietary vitamin D to support your 25‑OH vitamin D (44.1 ng/mL)\nOats - (1/2–1 cup cooked, daily) - soluble fiber helps reduce LDL cholesterol (LDL 104 mg/dL)\nExtra virgin olive oil - (1 Tbsp, daily) - swap for saturated fats to lower LDL and reduce inflammation linked to elevated ferritin\nBroccoli (or other cruciferous veggies) - (1 cup cooked, daily) - antioxidant and liver-supporting nutrients can help lower inflammation that may be raising ferritin (204 ng/mL)\nCucumber or watermelon - (1 cup, daily) - water-rich foods to support hydration and help normalize borderline-high hematocrit (46.8%)\nPlain low-fat yogurt or milk - (1 cup, daily) - dietary calcium can modestly reduce intestinal iron absorption if iron overload is a concern and supports overall nutrition	\N
