1	Amanda Richardson	female	2026-01-05 21:08:15.122	{"a1c": 5.4, "alt": 8, "ast": 9, "bun": 9, "co2": 24, "fsh": 7.29, "hdl": 34, "ldl": 99, "lpa": 6, "rbc": 4.38, "tsh": 0.99, "wbc": 7.4, "apoB": 84, "egfr": 91, "iron": 81, "shbg": 48.2, "tibc": 350, "hsCRP": 0.3, "onHRT": false, "folate": 15.9, "sodium": 143, "albumin": 4.4, "calcium": 9.1, "glucose": 115, "chloride": 104, "ferritin": 34.6, "vitaminD": 30.4, "bilirubin": 0.4, "estradiol": 38, "lowEnergy": true, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 286, "potassium": 4, "creatinine": 0.85, "hematocrit": 38.1, "hemoglobin": 12.3, "hotFlashes": true, "jointAches": false, "vitaminB12": 782, "nightSweats": true, "patientName": "Amanda Richardson", "demographics": {"age": 36, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 118, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "progesterone": 0.24, "testosterone": 12.7, "totalProtein": 6.8, "lowMotivation": false, "triglycerides": 116, "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": true, "freeTestosterone": 2, "totalCholesterol": 156}	{"redFlags": [], "ascvdRisk": {"tenYearRisk": 0, "riskCategory": "low", "riskPercentage": "N/A", "recommendations": "ASCVD calculator is validated for ages 40-79 years. Patient is 36 years old. Use clinical judgment for risk assessment.", "statinRecommendation": "Consider if LDL ≥190 mg/dL or familial hypercholesterolemia"}, "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": true, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 tablet twice daily with meals", "name": "Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support", "caution": "Contains iron, folate, and B12 for comprehensive RBC support. Avoid with calcium-rich foods. May cause mild GI upset initially.", "category": "iron", "priority": "high", "rationale": "Hemagenics provides comprehensive red blood cell support with highly absorbable iron, folate, and B12. Indicated for functional iron deficiency or iron deficiency without anemia.", "indication": "Iron deficiency without anemia: Ferritin 34.6 ng/mL"}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\nYour labs are mostly reassuring, but there are a few areas to address to lower future health risks and improve symptoms. Key concerns: low HDL cholesterol (34 mg/dL), borderline iron stores (ferritin 34.6 ng/mL), vitamin D insufficiency (30.4 ng/mL), and low total testosterone (12.7 ng/dL). Thyroid function (TSH 0.99 mIU/L) and blood counts (hemoglobin 12.3 g/dL, hematocrit 38.1%) are currently normal.\\n\\nPositive findings (things going well)\\n- LDL cholesterol: 99 mg/dL (optimal)\\n- Triglycerides: 116 mg/dL (normal)\\n- Hemoglobin A1c: 5.4% (normal blood sugar control)\\n- TSH: 0.99 mIU/L (euthyroid)\\n- hs-CRP: 0.3 mg/L (low inflammation)\\n- Platelets 286 K/uL, creatinine 0.85 mg/dL, eGFR 91 mL/min — all within normal range.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + concrete actions)\\n1) Low HDL: HDL 34 mg/dL (optimal is ≥50 for women)\\n   - Add 25–30 g fiber daily: oatmeal for breakfast, beans with lunch, vegetables at dinner.\\n   - Eat 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n   - Reduce saturated fats: choose lean meats, limit butter/cheese.\\n   - Daily exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking (or equivalent).\\n   - Repeat fasting lipid panel in ~3 months.\\n\\n2) Iron stores: Ferritin 34.6 ng/mL (insufficient; goal >50 ng/mL)\\n   - Eat iron-rich foods: lean red meat 2–3x/week, beans, lentils, spinach.\\n   - Take vitamin C with iron-rich meals (orange juice) to boost absorption.\\n   - If symptoms of fatigue or heavy menses, discuss oral iron (e.g., 30–65 mg elemental iron daily) with your provider and recheck ferritin in 8–12 weeks.\\n\\n3) Vitamin D: 25-OH 30.4 ng/mL (insufficient; target 60–80 ng/mL)\\n   - Start vitamin D3 (discuss dose; commonly 2000–4000 IU daily) and recheck level in 8–12 weeks.\\n   - Weight-bearing exercise and 1,000–1,200 mg/day calcium from diet or supplements support bone health.\\n   - Consider DEXA bone density testing if postmenopausal or additional risk factors.\\n\\n4) Low testosterone: Total testosterone 12.7 ng/dL\\n   - Prioritize 7–8 hours sleep, add strength training 2–3x/week, and use stress-reduction (meditation, walking).\\n   - Reassess symptoms (low libido, low energy) with provider to discuss options.\\n\\n5) Reproductive hormones: Progesterone 0.24 ng/mL and estradiol 38 pg/mL — interpretation depends on menstrual phase. If you were in the mid-luteal phase, progesterone should be higher; if follicular, low is expected. If you have irregular cycles, hot flashes, or PMS/sleep issues, consider repeat progesterone timed to mid-luteal phase or discuss menopause evaluation.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Begin dietary/exercise changes now; repeat fasting lipid panel in ~3 months.\\n- Start vitamin D3 and recheck 25-OH in 8–12 weeks.\\n- If considering iron supplement or if heavy menses/fatigue, discuss starting oral iron and recheck ferritin in 8–12 weeks.\\n- If vasomotor symptoms or menstrual concerns are affecting daily life, schedule a visit to discuss menopause management or further hormone testing.\\n\\nYou’re doing many things well — these targeted steps can help improve energy, bone health, and long-term heart health. If you’d like, we can help create a 4-week plan for diet, exercise, and supplements.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 12.3, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 38.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 286, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 38, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.24, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 7.29, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 12.7, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Low testosterone.", "recommendation": "May contribute to low libido or fatigue. Consider HRT discussion.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 0.99, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 34.6, "status": "borderline", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Iron insufficiency (ferritin 31-50). Optimal is >50.", "recommendation": "Provider protocol: Metagenics Hemagenics if symptomatic (fatigue, hair loss, restless legs, exercise intolerance).", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 30.4, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D insufficiency (≤40 ng/mL). Goal is 60-80 ng/mL.", "recommendation": "Provider protocol: 5,000 IU D3+K daily. Recheck in 8-12 weeks.", "referenceRange": "60-80 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 782, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.4, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 99, "status": "normal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 34, "status": "abnormal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Low HDL - increased cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Increase aerobic exercise. Consider niacin if very low.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 116, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": null, "status": "normal", "category": "ASCVD Cardiovascular Risk", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year risk of heart attack or stroke: N/A (LOW RISK)", "recommendation": "ASCVD calculator is validated for ages 40-79 years. Patient is 36 years old. Use clinical judgment for risk assessment.", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 9, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 8, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 91, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.85, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 84, "status": "normal", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Apo B at optimal level.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 6, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<30 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 350, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 81, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 15.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 0.3, "status": "normal", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Low cardiovascular inflammation risk.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<1 low risk, 1-3 moderate, >3 high risk"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.24, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Low Progesterone (Phase Unknown)", "interpretation": "Progesterone is very low (0.24 ng/mL). Without cycle phase documentation, this may reflect follicular timing (expected) or anovulatory cycle. If symptoms of sleep fragmentation, anxiety, or PMS are present, this finding is clinically relevant.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Document cycle phase for accurate interpretation. If patient reports sleep disruption, anxiety, or PMS symptoms, consider trial of micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly. PATIENT EDUCATION: Progesterone levels naturally vary throughout your cycle. If you're experiencing sleep problems or anxiety, discuss progesterone supplementation with your provider.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 38, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Vasomotor Symptoms with Fluctuating Estrogen", "interpretation": "Vasomotor symptoms (hot flashes, night sweats) present despite estradiol level of 38 pg/mL. In perimenopause, estrogen can be spiky and fluctuating, causing vasomotor symptoms even when levels appear normal or high at time of draw.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Consider steady delivery: transdermal estradiol patch starting at 0.0375 mg/day (common starting dose for twice-weekly systems). Add micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly if patient has uterus. Goal is symptom control through stable hormone delivery. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your hot flashes and night sweats may be caused by fluctuating hormone levels rather than consistently low estrogen. A steady-release estrogen patch can help stabilize your levels and reduce symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Symptoms indicate hormonal instability despite E2 level"}], "aiRecommendations": "1) Overall clinical assessment (synthesis)\\n- Labs show borderline low total testosterone (12.7 ng/dL), borderline ferritin (34.6 ng/mL), insufficient vitamin D (25-OH 30.4 ng/mL), low HDL (34 mg/dL), very low measured progesterone (0.24 ng/mL) with unknown cycle phase, and vasomotor symptoms despite an estradiol of 38 pg/mL. Other screening labs (CBC, A1c, TSH, lipids except HDL, LFTs, renal panel) are within reference.\\n- Clinical implications for staff: possible symptomatic androgen insufficiency (low libido/fatigue), iron insufficiency related to menstruation or depletion risk, suboptimal vitamin D for bone/metabolic health, cardiovascular risk factor from low HDL, and probable perimenopausal hormone instability causing vasomotor symptoms. Cycle timing must be documented to interpret progesterone.\\n\\n2) Priority actions — organized CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE\\n\\nCRITICAL (notify provider immediately / start now)\\n- If patient is pregnant, breastfeeding, or desires pregnancy within short term → do NOT start testosterone or systemic estrogen without provider discussion. Confirm pregnancy status before HRT.\\n- Provider notification triggers:\\n  - Ferritin falls <30 ng/mL or hemoglobin drops ≥2 g/dL from baseline.\\n  - Vitamin D <20 ng/mL or >100 ng/mL on follow-up.\\n  - Any clinical signs of virilization after testosterone start (acne, clitoromegaly, deepening voice) or testosterone level above female reference range on therapy.\\n  - New abnormal LFTs >2x ULN after initiating hormone therapy.\\n\\nURGENT (initiate within 1–2 weeks if patient symptomatic / provider review)\\n- Document menstrual history and exact cycle day of blood draw in chart (essential for interpreting progesterone and planning cyclic vs continuous progesterone).\\n- Address iron insufficiency if symptomatic (fatigue, hair loss, restless legs, heavy menses): start therapeutic oral iron or provider-directed Hemagenics per protocol. Recheck ferritin and CBC in 8–12 weeks.\\n- If patient has vasomotor symptoms affecting quality of life and desires HRT, discuss initiating steady-dose transdermal estradiol ± progesterone per protocol (see dosing below). If uterus present, progesterone required.\\n\\nROUTINE (standard follow-up, labs, lifestyle counseling)\\n- Start vitamin D replacement per protocol (5,000 IU D3 + vitamin K daily) and recheck 25-OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Lifestyle interventions for low HDL and general cardiovascular risk reduction (exercise, smoking cessation, diet).\\n- Monitor labs after any new hormone therapy (see monitoring timeline below).\\n\\n3) Testosterone recommendations (for provider consideration; staff to implement on provider order)\\n- Indication: symptomatic low libido/fatigue consistent with low total testosterone and after discussion of risks/benefits.\\n- Suggested starting regimen (provider order required):\\n  - Transdermal testosterone gel/cream starting at 300 mcg/day (0.3 mg/day) applied topically once daily (common low starting dose in female-specific protocols).\\n  - Alternative: If protocol offers a different commercially/compounded product, choose equivalent low-dose transdermal formulation aiming for physiologic mid-female range.\\n- Monitoring and titration:\\n  - Check total testosterone 6–8 weeks after initiation (draw trough, ~24 hours after last application if gel used) to guide dose adjustments.\\n  - Target: clinic protocol target range (provider to confirm) — adjust dose upward in small increments only if symptoms persist and level below target without signs of androgen excess.\\n  - Reassess symptoms and adverse effects at 6–8 weeks and every 3 months once stable; thereafter every 6–12 months.\\n  - Baseline LFTs and lipid panel already available; repeat lipids at 3–6 months after initiation and LFTs if clinically indicated.\\n\\n4) Progesterone and Estradiol recommendations (for provider consideration)\\n- Required documentation before interpretation: record last menstrual period and menstrual cycle day for progesterone interpretation.\\n- If symptomatic (sleep fragmentation, anxiety, PMS) or if starting transdermal estradiol and uterus present:\\n  - Micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly is acceptable per clinic protocol for endometrial protection and symptom control; consider cyclic dosing (e.g., 100–200 mg nightly for 12–14 days/month) vs continuous nightly 100 mg if combined with continuous estradiol—decision by provider.\\n- For vasomotor symptoms with fluctuating estrogen:\\n  - Consider transdermal estradiol patch starting at 0.0375 mg/day (follow clinic prescribing guidelines for frequency as per patch system). If uterus present, add progesterone as above.\\n  - Counsel about risks/contraindications (smoking status, thromboembolism history).\\n\\n5) Iron management (ferritin 34.6 ng/mL)\\n- If symptomatic or ongoing menstrual losses:\\n  - Start therapeutic oral iron OR clinic protocol supplement (Metagenics Hemagenics) per provider order.\\n  - Common oral dosing example (provider to confirm): ferrous sulfate 325 mg PO once daily or every other day to improve tolerance; consider ferrous gluconate or heme iron if GI intolerance. Note: staff to follow clinic formulary.\\n  - Give counseling: take with vitamin C, avoid concurrent calcium/antacids within 2 hours, expect GI side effects; encourage adherence for 8–12 weeks.\\n- Monitoring:\\n  - Recheck ferritin and CBC 8–12 weeks after starting supplementation. Goal ferritin >50 ng/mL per clinic protocol.\\n- Escalation:\\n  - If ferritin fails to rise or patient has heavy bleeding, notify provider for GI workup/referral (occult GI blood loss evaluation) or OB/GYN referral for abnormal uterine bleeding.\\n\\n6) Vitamin D (25-OH 30.4 ng/mL)\\n- Per clinic protocol: start vitamin D3 5,000 IU daily with vitamin K (as available in clinic supplement) — provider order required.\\n- Recheck 25-OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks. Aim 60–80 ng/mL.\\n- Notify provider if level remains <30 after 12 weeks or rises >100 ng/mL.\\n\\n7) Low HDL (34 mg/dL)\\n- Immediate actions:\\n  - Document smoking status (if smoker, prioritize cessation).\\n  - Start structured lifestyle plan: increase aerobic activity to ≥150 minutes/week moderate intensity or ≥75 minutes vigorous; incorporate resistance training 2x/week; weight loss if overweight/obese; adopt Mediterranean-style diet; limit alcohol.\\n- Medication considerations:\\n  - Do not initiate niacin routinely — consider only after provider review if HDL remains very low and symptoms/risk profile warrant, recognizing limited outcome benefit and side effects.\\n  - No immediate statin indicated given LDL and ASCVD risk provided as normal; re-evaluate if risk changes.\\n- Monitoring:\\n  - Repeat fasting lipid panel in 3–6 months after lifestyle intervention and any hormonal therapy change (testosterone/estrogen can affect lipids).\\n\\n8) Additional testing / referrals to consider\\n- Mandatory: Document cycle day and LMP now; obtain pregnancy test if reproductive potential not excluded.\\n- If heavy or irregular menses: OB/GYN referral for evaluation (pelvic ultrasound, endometrial assessment) and management.\\n- If ferritin continues low or GI risk factors present (age >50, GI symptoms, family history): consider GI referral for occult blood testing/colonoscopy as per provider.\\n- If fertility concerns: consider AMH and formal fertility referral.\\n- Consider DEXA scan if patient is perimenopausal/postmenopausal or has additional bone risk factors (age, steroid use, low BMI).\\n- Consider endocrine or menopause specialist referral for complex HRT decisions or if adverse effects occur.\\n\\n9) Patient education points for staff to cover (scripted, concise)\\n- Explain that progesterone and testosterone levels vary with cycle — document cycle day and discuss timing.\\n- Review goals: improve symptoms (libido, energy, hot flashes, sleep) and lab targets (ferritin >50, vitamin D 60–80).\\n- Iron counseling: how to take oral iron, expected timeline to symptom improvement (4–12 weeks), and side effects.\\n- Vitamin D counseling: take daily 5,000 IU D3 + K; recheck in 8–12 weeks.\\n- HRT counseling: benefits/risks of transdermal estradiol and testosterone; contraceptive implications and pregnancy precautions; signs to report (vaginal bleeding, severe headaches, calf swelling, jaundice, virilization).\\n- Lifestyle counseling: exercise prescription, smoking cessation, diet, alcohol limits.\\n\\n10) Summary of next steps and timeline (actionable checklist for staff)\\n- Immediately/documentation:\\n  - Record LMP/cycle day in chart and verify pregnancy status.\\n  - Notify provider now if pregnancy possible or patient desires pregnancy.\\n- Within 1–2 weeks (after provider order):\\n  - If symptomatic iron insufficiency → start Metagenics Hemagenics or oral iron per provider.\\n  - If patient elects HRT for vasomotor symptoms → place orders for transdermal estradiol 0.0375 mg/day ± micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly (if uterus present) per provider.\\n  - If patient elects testosterone therapy → place order for transdermal testosterone 300 mcg/day per protocol (provider to sign).\\n- Monitoring schedule:\\n  - Testosterone level: recheck 6–8 weeks after start; then every 3 months until stable, then every 6–12 months.\\n  - Ferritin + CBC: recheck 8–12 weeks after starting iron.\\n  - 25-OH vitamin D: recheck 8–12 weeks after starting supplementation.\\n  - Fasting lipid panel: recheck in 3–6 months after lifestyle/HRT changes.\\n  - LFTs: repeat only if clinically indicated or signs/symptoms suggest hepatotoxicity.\\n- Follow-up appointment:\\n  - Schedule review visit at 6–8 weeks to assess symptom response, adherence, adverse effects, and lab results; sooner if concerning symptoms.\\n- Escalation criteria:\\n  - Notify provider for ferritin <30, Hgb fall ≥2 g/dL, testosterone adverse effects or supraphysiologic levels, vitamin D <20 or >100 ng/mL, new abnormal LFTs >2x ULN, or any thrombotic symptoms on estrogen.\\n\\nNote: All medication starts/changes require explicit provider orders and documentation of informed consent about risks/benefits. Review contraception/pregnancy status prior to initiating systemic sex steroid therapy."}	2026-01-05 21:08:15.131092	\N
