 "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": true, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": true}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 tablet twice daily with meals", "name": "Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support", "caution": "Contains iron, folate, and B12 for comprehensive RBC support. Avoid with calcium-rich foods. May cause mild GI upset initially.", "category": "iron", "priority": "high", "rationale": "Hemagenics provides comprehensive red blood cell support with highly absorbable iron, folate, and B12. Indicated for functional iron deficiency or iron deficiency without anemia.", "indication": "Functional iron deficiency: Serum iron 35 µg/dL"}, {"dose": "1 tablet daily", "name": "HerWellness™ Estrovera®", "caution": "Rhubarb extract for menopausal symptom relief. Non-hormonal option. Effects typically noticed within 4 weeks.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Estrovera provides clinically studied ERr 731® rhubarb extract for relief of menopausal symptoms including hot flashes, sleep disturbances, and mood changes without hormones.", "indication": "Low estradiol (5 pg/mL)"}, {"dose": "1 softgel 1-2 times daily", "name": "OmegaGenics® Fish Oil Neuro 1000", "caution": "High-DHA omega-3 (750mg DHA, 250mg EPA) for brain and heart health. Lemon-flavored, no fishy taste. Contains calamari - avoid if shellfish allergy.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "OmegaGenics Fish Oil Neuro 1000 provides concentrated DHA and EPA to reduce triglycerides, support brain function, cardiovascular health, and reduce inflammation for joint comfort.", "indication": "TG 222 mg/dL, LDL 198 mg/dL, HDL 44 mg/dL (low), hs-CRP 12.2 mg/L"}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Non-GMO, gluten-free.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statins.", "indication": "LDL 198 mg/dL, HDL 44 mg/dL (low), TG 222 mg/dL, TC 286 mg/dL, ApoB 149 mg/dL, hs-CRP 12.2 mg/L"}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "general", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Women's health foundation"}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "strongly_indicated", "indicated": true, "rationale": "LDL-C ≥190 mg/dL (198 mg/dL). High-intensity statin therapy strongly indicated per ACC/AHA guidelines. Consider familial hypercholesterolemia evaluation."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": true, "severity": "high", "recommendation": "Triglycerides 200-499 mg/dL (222 mg/dL). Recommend intensive lifestyle modification first. Rule out secondary causes (diabetes, hypothyroidism, medications, alcohol). If on statin and TG persists ≥150, consider prescription omega-3 (icosapent ethyl) for patients with established ASCVD or diabetes with additional risk factors."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\nYour labs show several important concerns that need follow-up: very high LDL cholesterol (198 mg/dL), high triglycerides (222 mg/dL), elevated ApoB (149 mg/dL), low serum iron (35 µg/dL) with normal ferritin (108 ng/mL), and a markedly elevated hs-CRP (12.2 mg/L) indicating active inflammation. Your hemoglobin is normal at 13 g/dL and your thyroid (TSH 0.94 mIU/L) and kidney tests (eGFR 94 mL/min) are fine.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Hemoglobin 13 g/dL and hematocrit 40% are within normal ranges.\\n- Ferritin 108 ng/mL indicates adequate iron stores.\\n- Platelets 248 K/µL, Vitamin B12 626 pg/mL, folate 20 ng/mL are all normal.\\n- TSH 0.94 mIU/L—your thyroid is functioning normally.\\n- AST 21 U/L and ALT 23 U/L are normal.\\n- Lipoprotein(a) 23 mg/dL is in the optimal range.\\n\\nAreas to improve (with specific values and actions)\\n- LDL cholesterol 198 mg/dL (very high) and ApoB 149 mg/dL (elevated): Discuss statin therapy with your clinician (guideline: consider if LDL ≥190 mg/dL). Also start lifestyle changes now:\\n  - Add 25–30 g fiber daily: oatmeal for breakfast, beans with lunch, vegetables at dinner.\\n  - Include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n  - Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter and cheese.\\n  - Daily exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking or equivalent.\\n- Triglycerides 222 mg/dL (high): in addition to the above, limit alcohol, reduce refined carbs and added sugars, and consider walking 10–15 minutes after meals.\\n- HDL 44 mg/dL (low): same lifestyle measures above plus weight-bearing exercise and regular aerobic activity.\\n- hs-CRP 12.2 mg/L (markedly elevated): this suggests active inflammation or infection. Make a same-week appointment with your provider to evaluate sources (infection, dental issue, recent injury, autoimmune). Repeat hs-CRP after the cause is addressed.\\n- Serum iron 35 µg/dL (low) with ferritin 108 ng/mL: this pattern can occur with inflammation (high CRP). Because hemoglobin is 13 g/dL and ferritin is adequate, do not start iron pills without clinician input. Actions: eat iron-rich foods (lean red meat, beans, lentils), include vitamin C with meals to boost absorption (orange or bell pepper), avoid taking calcium at the same time as iron, and repeat iron studies in 4–8 weeks after inflammation is addressed.\\n- Vitamin D 25-OH 41.8 ng/mL (suboptimal; goal 60–80 ng/mL): consider vitamin D3 supplementation (commonly 2000–4000 IU daily—discuss dose with your clinician) and recheck in 8–12 weeks. Add weight-bearing exercise and adequate calcium for bone health.\\n\\nHormones and menstrual note\\n- Estradiol 5 pg/mL, progesterone 0.18 ng/mL, FSH 1.81 mIU/mL: interpretation depends on where you are in your menstrual cycle. If these were intended to assess ovarian function, repeat testing on specific cycle days per your clinician’s instructions.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Call your clinician this week to review LDL 198 mg/dL and hs-CRP 12.2 mg/L. Discuss starting a statin and investigate the cause of inflammation.\\n- Begin the lifestyle steps above immediately. Recheck lipids, ApoB, and hs-CRP in 6–12 weeks after changes or after starting medication; recheck vitamin D in 8–12 weeks if you start supplements; repeat iron studies in 4–8 weeks after treating inflammation.\\nYou’re taking an important step by reviewing these results—small, consistent changes (diet, exercise, sleep, and follow-up testing) will make a big difference.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 13, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 40, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 248, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 5, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.18, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 1.81, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 6.8, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Low testosterone.", "recommendation": "May contribute to low libido or fatigue. Consider HRT discussion.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 0.94, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 108, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 41.8, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D suboptimal. Goal is 60-80 ng/mL.", "recommendation": "Consider 2,000-5,000 IU D3+K daily to reach optimal range of 60-80 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "60-80 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 626, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 198, "status": "abnormal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Very high LDL - high cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Statin therapy recommended. Intensive lifestyle changes.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 44, "status": "abnormal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Low HDL - increased cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Increase aerobic exercise. Consider niacin if very low.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 222, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "High triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Dietary changes. Reduce refined carbs and alcohol.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": null, "status": "normal", "category": "ASCVD Cardiovascular Risk", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year risk of heart attack or stroke: N/A (LOW RISK)", "recommendation": "ASCVD calculator is validated for ages 40-79 years. Patient is 33 years old. Use clinical judgment for risk assessment.", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 21, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 23, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 94, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.84, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 149, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Elevated Apo B - increased cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Consider statin therapy. Intensive lifestyle modifications.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 23, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<30 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 348, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 35, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Low serum iron - iron deficiency.", "recommendation": "Iron supplementation. Evaluate for blood loss.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 20, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 12.2, "status": "critical", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Markedly elevated hs-CRP - acute inflammation.", "recommendation": "Evaluate for infection or inflammatory condition.", "referenceRange": "<1 low risk, 1-3 moderate, >3 high risk"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- Significant atherogenic dyslipidemia with very high LDL (198 mg/dL) and elevated ApoB (149 mg/dL) indicating high lifetime ASCVD risk despite a currently reported ASCVD risk calculation — requires immediate provider review.\\n- Markedly elevated hs‑CRP (12.2 mg/L) suggestive of acute inflammation or infection; may be transient but requires prompt evaluation prior to risk stratification.\\n- Borderline low total testosterone (6.8 ng/dL) that may contribute to symptoms (low libido, fatigue); consideration for therapeutic trial of testosterone after confirmation and counseling.\\n- Suboptimal vitamin D (25‑OH 41.8 ng/mL) — below clinic target 60–80 ng/mL.\\n- Low serum iron (35 µg/dL) with normal ferritin reported — possible iron deficiency or functional iron deficiency; warrants evaluation for menstrual blood loss or other sources.\\n- Triglycerides elevated (222 mg/dL) and HDL low (44 mg/dL) — additional modifiable CV risk factors.\\n\\nPriority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL (Physician notification required now)\\n- Notify provider immediately for LDL 198 mg/dL (≥190 mg/dL threshold) — statin therapy consideration and urgent lipid management planning.\\n- Notify provider immediately for hs‑CRP 12.2 mg/L — evaluate for acute infection/inflammation and determine need for urgent workup/treatment.\\n- Flag ApoB 149 mg/dL to provider as additional evidence of high atherogenic particle burden.\\n\\nURGENT (staff actions to initiate pending physician sign‑off)\\n- Hold immediate patient counseling about pregnancy status/contraception if of childbearing potential prior to initiating statin or testosterone. Obtain pregnancy test prior to starting statin or testosterone if applicable.\\n- Obtain repeat hs‑CRP and basic infectious/inflammatory assessment (symptom screen, vitals, CRP/ESR if available) same day or within 48–72 hours to determine persistence vs transient elevation.\\n- Order fasting lipid panel and direct LDL if prior sample was non‑fasting or if confirmation needed; if confirmed LDL ≥190, initiate high‑intensity statin per protocol (see medication dosing below) pending provider approval.\\n- Check baseline LFTs (AST/ALT already normal) and consider baseline CK if history of muscle disease or myalgias, as safety monitoring prior to statin start.\\n\\nROUTINE (to implement within 1–4 weeks)\\n- Arrange follow‑up labs and visits per timeline below.\\n- Begin lifestyle counseling interventions (see section below) immediately.\\n\\nMedication/dose recommendations (specific)\\n- Statin (provider decision required):\\n  - Recommend initiating a high‑intensity statin given LDL 198 mg/dL and ApoB 149:\\n    - Atorvastatin 40–80 mg PO once daily OR rosuvastatin 20–40 mg PO once daily.\\n  - If starting statin: obtain baseline LFTs (if not recent), counsel on potential side effects, check pregnancy status in women of reproductive potential; consider CK only if symptomatic or high risk.\\n  - Reassess LDL at 4–12 weeks after initiation or dose adjustment; goal LDL reduction ≥50% (or as directed by provider).\\n- Iron supplementation for low serum iron (pending provider confirmation and assessment of ferritin and blood loss):\\n  - If treating empirically: ferrous sulfate 325 mg PO once daily to three times daily (elemental iron 65 mg/tab); start with once daily or twice daily if GI intolerance and titrate.\\n  - Recommend taking with vitamin C (e.g., 250 mg vitamin C or orange juice) to enhance absorption; avoid co‑administration with calcium, tea/coffee.\\n  - Recheck CBC + iron studies (serum iron, ferritin, TIBC/TSAT) in 4–8 weeks after starting therapy.\\n  - Evaluate for gynecologic causes of blood loss; consider referral to GYN if heavy menses.\\n- Vitamin D:\\n  - Recommend vitamin D3 2,000–5,000 IU PO daily plus vitamin K2 100 mcg PO daily until target 60–80 ng/mL achieved (provider order required).\\n  - Recheck 25‑OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks after initiation/change in dose.\\n- Testosterone (if provider elects to trial for symptoms)\\n  - Suggest confirmatory morning total testosterone prior to initiation (repeat AM level; consider cycle timing — obtain in follicular phase if premenopausal).\\n  - If initiating transdermal therapy (preferred route): start low-dose regimen, e.g. 300 mcg (0.3 mg) transdermal daily (typical starting dose for female HRT protocols); adjust based on symptoms and serum levels.\\n  - Recheck total (and free if available) testosterone 8–12 weeks after initiation and again at 6 months; monitor for virilizing effects, lipids, LFTs.\\n  - Contraindications: active pregnancy, untreated hormone‑sensitive malignancy — confirm pregnancy status and cancer history prior to initiation.\\n\\nLifestyle interventions (specific, to communicate to patient per provider direction)\\n- Diet:\\n  - Start Mediterranean‑style diet: increase vegetables, whole grains, legumes, nuts, fish; reduce saturated fat and dietary cholesterol; prioritize fiber.\\n  - Reduce refined carbohydrates and simple sugars to lower triglycerides.\\n  - Limit alcohol intake (even moderate alcohol can raise TGs).\\n- Exercise:\\n  - Target ≥150 minutes/week moderate‑intensity aerobic activity + 2 strength sessions/week; emphasize regular aerobic exercise to raise HDL and lower TGs.\\n- Weight:\\n  - Encourage weight loss if BMI elevated—target 5–10% weight reduction to improve lipids and metabolic profile.\\n- Smoking cessation if applicable.\\n- For iron: advise on dietary iron sources (heme iron: red meat, poultry; nonheme iron plus vitamin C), manage heavy menstrual bleeding per GYN referral.\\n- For vitamin D: safe sun exposure counseling as appropriate.\\n\\nAdditional testing/referrals to consider\\n- Repeat labs:\\n  - Repeat hs‑CRP in 2–4 weeks after evaluating for infection/inflammation to determine persistence.\\n  - Fasting lipid panel 4–12 weeks after statin initiation or dose change; then every 3–12 months as indicated.\\n  - Repeat morning total testosterone (confirmatory) prior to initiating therapy; measure free testosterone if available.\\n  - Recheck iron studies (serum iron, ferritin, TIBC/TSAT) and CBC in 4–8 weeks after starting iron therapy.\\n  - Recheck 25‑OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks after supplementation adjustment.\\n- Referrals:\\n  - Urgent cardiology or lipid specialist referral for consideration of genetic dyslipidemia management given LDL ≥190 mg/dL and elevated ApoB — especially if LDL remains ≥190 on repeat testing or family history of premature ASCVD.\\n  - Gynecology referral for evaluation of menstrual blood loss if iron deficiency suspected or if abnormal uterine bleeding.\\n  - Consider infectious disease/rheumatology workup only if hs‑CRP elevation persists and no infectious source identified.\\n  - Consider GI referral/occult blood testing if iron deficiency persists or if patient age/risks indicate.\\n\\nMonitoring and safety (to staff)\\n- Before starting statin or testosterone in reproductive‑aged women: verify negative pregnancy test and contraception plan; document counseling.\\n- Monitor LFTs 6–12 weeks after statin initiation and as clinically indicated; consider CK only if myalgias.\\n- Monitor for adverse effects of iron (constipation, GI upset); suggest dose adjustments for intolerance.\\n- Document baseline and follow‑up symptom assessment for testosterone therapy (libido, energy, mood) and adverse effects (acne, hirsutism).\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (actionable checklist)\\n- Immediate (same day/24–48 hours)\\n  - Notify provider of LDL 198 mg/dL, ApoB 149, and hs‑CRP 12.2 mg/L — urgent review and decision on statin and immediate evaluation for inflammation/infection.\\n  - If patient of childbearing potential, obtain urine pregnancy test before any statin or testosterone initiation.\\n  - Screen patient for acute infection symptoms and obtain repeat hs‑CRP/CRP or ESR as ordered.\\n- Within 1 week\\n  - Order fasting lipid panel if previous sample nonfasting or to confirm; order baseline LFTs (if not recent).\\n  - Initiate lifestyle counseling and document education points (diet, exercise, alcohol reduction, smoking cessation).\\n  - If iron supplementation is to be started, begin ferrous sulfate regimen and give iron counseling.\\n- 4–12 weeks\\n  - Recheck fasting lipid panel 4–12 weeks after statin start or dose change.\\n  - Recheck CBC and iron studies 4–8 weeks after starting iron.\\n  - Recheck vitamin D in 8–12 weeks after initiating/adjusting supplementation.\\n  - Recheck testosterone 8–12 weeks after therapeutic start (or obtain repeat baseline prior to start).\\n  - Repeat hs‑CRP in 2–4 weeks to confirm persistence; if still >2 mg/L with no acute cause, escalate workup.\\n- 3–6 months\\n  - Ongoing follow‑up for symptom response if testosterone trialed; adjust dose per provider.\\n  - Arrange cardiology/lipidology referral if LDL remains ≥190 or inadequate response to statin.\\n\\nPhysician notification triggers for staff (repeat)\\n- LDL ≥190 mg/dL — immediate.\\n- hs‑CRP ≥10 mg/L — immediate.\\n- ApoB significantly elevated (≥130 mg/dL) — notify.\\n- Any plans to start statin or testosterone in women of childbearing potential without documented negative pregnancy test — notify.\\n\\nNote: These are protocol‑based management suggestions for provider review. Any medication initiation or changes require physician authorization and documentation."}	2026-01-06 22:10:16.983176	\N
