 "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "general", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Women's health foundation"}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\nYour labs show mostly healthy results, but a few areas could use attention to lower long-term risk and support energy, bone, and reproductive health. Key numbers to note: HDL cholesterol 34 mg/dL (low), LDL 99 mg/dL (good), ferritin 34.6 ng/mL (low-normal), vitamin D 25-OH 30.4 ng/mL (insufficient), total testosterone 12.7 ng/dL (low), progesterone 0.24 ng/mL (very low), estradiol 38 pg/mL, and TSH 0.99 mIU/L (normal).\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Hemoglobin 12.3 g/dL, hematocrit 38.1%, platelets 286 K/uL — no current anemia.\\n- LDL 99 mg/dL, triglycerides 116 mg/dL, ApoB 84 mg/dL, Lp(a) 6 mg/dL — heart-related lipids otherwise favorable.\\n- TSH 0.99 mIU/L — thyroid function is normal.\\n- Hemoglobin A1c 5.4% — blood sugar well controlled.\\n- hs-CRP 0.3 mg/L — low inflammation.\\n\\nAreas to improve (with concrete actions)\\n1. Low HDL cholesterol — HDL 34 mg/dL (goal >40 mg/dL):\\n   - Add 25–30 g fiber daily: oatmeal for breakfast, beans with lunch, lots of vegetables at dinner.\\n   - Include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n   - Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter and high-fat cheeses.\\n   - Exercise daily: 30 minutes brisk walking or equivalent; add 2 sessions of strength training weekly.\\n\\n2. Iron stores — ferritin 34.6 ng/mL (optimal >50 ng/mL):\\n   - Increase dietary iron: red meat, lentils, spinach, iron-fortified cereal.\\n   - Consider oral iron: e.g., ferrous sulfate 325 mg (≈65 mg elemental iron) every other day or 1 tablet daily if tolerated; take with orange juice (vitamin C) and avoid taking with calcium or coffee/tea. Discuss exact dosing with your clinician.\\n   - Recheck ferritin in 8–12 weeks. If you have heavy periods or fatigue, report this—further evaluation may be needed.\\n\\n3. Vitamin D — 25-OH 30.4 ng/mL (goal 60–80 ng/mL for bone health):\\n   - Start vitamin D3 2000–4000 IU daily and increase calcium-rich foods or supplements as needed; recheck level in 8–12 weeks to reach 60–80 ng/mL.\\n   - Add weight-bearing exercise for bone strength (walking, jogging, squats).\\n\\n4. Sex hormones:\\n   - Progesterone 0.24 ng/mL and estradiol 38 pg/mL: interpretation depends on cycle day. If you’re trying to confirm ovulation or have symptoms (PMS, sleep problems), get a mid-luteal progesterone (about 7 days after ovulation) or track ovulation and repeat testing. Low total testosterone 12.7 ng/dL: prioritize 7–8 hours sleep, strength training 2–3× weekly, and stress reduction (meditation, hobbies); discuss symptoms with your provider for possible further testing or treatment.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Begin the cholesterol and lifestyle actions now. Start vitamin D3 2000–4000 IU/day and iron strategy as discussed; recheck vitamin D and ferritin in 8–12 weeks. Repeat lipid panel in 3 months after lifestyle changes. If menstrual timing for hormones is unclear or you have symptoms (hot flashes, heavy periods, low libido, sleep issues), schedule follow-up to plan cycle-timed testing and discuss treatment options. Your TSH is normal; no thyroid action needed now.\\n\\nYou’re doing many things well — these targeted steps can improve symptoms, bone health, and long-term heart health. If you want, I can help you draft questions for your provider or a checklist to track supplements, exercise, and symptoms.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 12.3, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 38.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 286, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 38, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.24, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 7.29, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 12.7, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Low testosterone.", "recommendation": "May contribute to low libido or fatigue. Consider HRT discussion.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 0.99, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 34.6, "status": "borderline", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Iron insufficiency (ferritin 31-50). Optimal is >50.", "recommendation": "Provider protocol: Metagenics Hemagenics if symptomatic (fatigue, hair loss, restless legs, exercise intolerance).", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 30.4, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D insufficiency (31-40 ng/mL). Goal is 60-80 ng/mL.", "recommendation": "Metagenics Vitamin D3 5,000 + K: 1 softgel daily with meal. Recheck in 8-12 weeks.", "referenceRange": "60-80 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 782, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.4, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 99, "status": "normal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 34, "status": "abnormal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Low HDL - increased cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Increase aerobic exercise. Consider niacin if very low.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 116, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": null, "status": "normal", "category": "ASCVD Cardiovascular Risk", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year risk of heart attack or stroke: N/A (LOW RISK)", "recommendation": "ASCVD calculator is validated for ages 40-79 years. Patient is 36 years old. Use clinical judgment for risk assessment.", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 9, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 8, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 91, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.85, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 84, "status": "normal", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Apo B at optimal level.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 6, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<30 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 350, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 81, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 15.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 0.3, "status": "normal", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Low cardiovascular inflammation risk.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<1 low risk, 1-3 moderate, >3 high risk"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.24, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Low Progesterone (Phase Unknown)", "interpretation": "Progesterone is very low (0.24 ng/mL). Without cycle phase documentation, this may reflect follicular timing (expected) or anovulatory cycle. If symptoms of sleep fragmentation, anxiety, or PMS are present, this finding is clinically relevant.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Document cycle phase for accurate interpretation. If patient reports sleep disruption, anxiety, or PMS symptoms, consider trial of micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly. PATIENT EDUCATION: Progesterone levels naturally vary throughout your cycle. If you're experiencing sleep problems or anxiety, discuss progesterone supplementation with your provider.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 38, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Vasomotor Symptoms with Fluctuating Estrogen", "interpretation": "Vasomotor symptoms (hot flashes) present despite estradiol level of 38 pg/mL. In perimenopause, estrogen can be spiky and fluctuating, causing vasomotor symptoms even when levels appear normal or high at time of draw.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Consider steady delivery: transdermal estradiol patch starting at 0.0375 mg/day (common starting dose for twice-weekly systems). Add micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly if patient has uterus. Goal is symptom control through stable hormone delivery. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your hot flashes and night sweats may be caused by fluctuating hormone levels rather than consistently low estrogen. A steady-release estrogen patch can help stabilize your levels and reduce symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Symptoms indicate hormonal instability despite E2 level"}], "aiRecommendations": "Clinical context: staff-facing summary and action plan based on labs provided. Reference values cited in parentheses.\\n\\n1) Overall clinical assessment (concise)\\n- Low/low‑normal sex hormones consistent with symptomatic perimenopausal profile: total testosterone low at 12.7 ng/dL, estradiol 38 pg/mL with vasomotor symptoms (fluctuating levels), and very low progesterone 0.24 ng/mL (cycle phase not specified).  \\n- Borderline iron stores (ferritin 34.6 ng/mL) consistent with iron insufficiency risk in menstruating females.  \\n- Vitamin D insufficiency (25‑OH D 30.4 ng/mL).  \\n- Atherogenic risk marker: low HDL 34 mg/dL. Other metabolic and inflammatory markers reported normal.\\n\\n2) Priority actions — CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE\\nCRITICAL (require immediate provider notification)\\n- Any plan to initiate systemic estrogen and/or progesterone: notify provider before starting. Provider must confirm absence of contraindications (history of breast cancer, active thromboembolic disease, uncontrolled BP, etc.) and document informed consent.  \\n- Consider testosterone therapy? Notify provider to review benefits/risks and approve order prior to initiation. Document pregnancy status if premenopausal.\\n\\nURGENT (actions to complete within 1–2 weeks)\\n- Iron management for ferritin 34.6 ng/mL if patient symptomatic (fatigue, hair loss, restless legs, heavy menses): start iron replacement per clinic protocol and schedule follow-up labs. Provider to decide between Metagenics Hemagenics (clinic formulary) or oral elemental iron.  \\n- Vitamin D insufficiency: start replacement now (see dosing below). Recheck in 8–12 weeks.\\n\\nROUTINE (actions to complete within 4–12 weeks unless symptoms dictate sooner)\\n- If considering HRT for vasomotor symptoms: initiate transdermal estradiol and add progesterone if uterus present (see dosing below) after provider approval. Follow symptom response and safety monitoring.  \\n- Document menstrual cycle day and last menstrual period to interpret progesterone and other cycle‑dependent hormones. If ovulatory status unclear and fertility/ovulation assessment needed, order mid‑luteal progesterone.\\n\\n3) Specific medication/dose recommendations and monitoring (staff must obtain provider sign‑off)\\nA. Vitamin D\\n- Start: Metagenics Vitamin D3 5,000 IU + K1/K2, 1 softgel PO daily with a meal.  \\n- Recheck: 25‑OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks. Target: 60–80 ng/mL per clinic goal.\\n\\nB. Iron (ferritin 34.6 ng/mL)\\n- Per clinic protocol, if symptomatic: start Metagenics Hemagenics (use clinic formulary dosing). If Hemagenics unavailable or provider prefers standard iron: ferrous sulfate 325 mg PO q48h (provides ~65 mg elemental iron) OR ferrous gluconate/ferrous fumarate equivalent daily dosing — consider alternate‑day dosing to improve absorption/tolerability.  \\n- Supplementation duration: replete for 8–12 weeks then recheck ferritin and CBC.  \\n- Recheck: ferritin and CBC in 8–12 weeks. If ongoing heavy menses, consider gynecology referral.\\n\\nC. Progesterone (if symptoms or for endometrial protection with estrogen)\\n- If provider elects trial for sleep/anxiety/PMS or for endometrial protection with estrogen and patient has uterus: micronized progesterone 100 mg PO qHS nightly.  \\n- If used for cyclical support only, provider may specify cyclical vs continuous dosing — document plan.  \\n- Reassess symptoms in 4–8 weeks. Monitor for sedation, mood changes. If measuring progesterone to assess ovulation: obtain mid‑luteal progesterone ~7 days before expected menses.\\n\\nD. Estradiol (vasomotor symptoms with fluctuating estrogen)\\n- If provider elects systemic therapy for vasomotor symptoms: transdermal estradiol patch starting 0.0375 mg/day (apply twice weekly per product instructions) — start lowest effective dose.  \\n- If uterus present: add micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly for endometrial protection (see above).  \\n- Follow-up: assess symptom control in 4–8 weeks. Provider to re-evaluate need for dose escalation or alternative therapy. Prior to initiation, ensure up‑to‑date breast/cervical screening per age guidelines and document contraindications.\\n\\nE. Testosterone (total 12.7 ng/dL — borderline low)\\n- Provider discussion required before starting. If provider elects to treat for decreased libido/fatigue: recommended starting regimen (low‑dose, conservative): transdermal testosterone delivering 300 mcg (0.3 mg) daily (e.g., topical gel/cream or patch as available in clinic formulary). Acceptable starting range: 300–600 mcg/day depending on symptoms and provider preference.  \\n- Monitoring: measure total testosterone (and SHBG/free testosterone if available) 6–8 weeks after initiation and with each dose change; target mid‑female reference range without exceeding upper female range. Monitor for adverse effects (acne, hirsutism, voice changes, clitoromegaly) and stop if virilization.  \\n- Labs to monitor after initiation: total testosterone 6–8 weeks, then q3 months x1 year then q6–12 months if stable; baseline CBC, LFTs, fasting lipids at baseline and periodically. Notify provider for any supraphysiologic levels or signs of virilization.\\n\\n4) Lifestyle interventions (to counsel patient)\\n- Cardiovascular health (HDL 34 mg/dL): emphasize regular aerobic exercise (≥150 minutes moderate intensity/week + resistance training twice weekly), smoking cessation if applicable, weight loss if BMI elevated, Mediterranean‑style diet (increase monounsaturated fats, omega‑3s, fiber), limit alcohol. Reassess lipids in 3–6 months. Consider referral to dietitian/exercise program.  \\n- Iron optimization: advise food sources high in heme iron (red meat, poultry) and iron absorption enhancers (vitamin C with iron), avoid concurrent calcium/tea/coffee with iron dosing. Discuss tracking menstrual bleeding.  \\n- Vitamin D: encourage safe sun exposure and dietary sources (fatty fish, fortified foods) while on replacement therapy.  \\n- Sleep and vasomotor symptom hygiene: cooling strategies, layered clothing, bedtime cooling, avoidance of triggers (hot beverages, spicy foods, alcohol). If progesterone being trialed for sleep, counsel on sedating effects.\\n\\n5) Additional testing and referrals to consider\\n- Document cycle day and last menstrual period now. If ovulatory status unclear: order mid‑luteal progesterone (day ~21 in a 28‑day cycle or 7 days pre‑menses). Consider AMH if fertility preservation or diminished ovarian reserve concern.  \\n- Gynecology referral if heavy or irregular menses, persistent iron deficiency, or if patient desires fertility evaluation.  \\n- Cardiology or lipid specialist referral if HDL remains <35 mg/dL despite lifestyle measures and/or ASCVD risk profile changes.  \\n- Baseline mammogram and pelvic exam per age/screening history prior to initiating systemic estrogen (confirm on chart and notify provider if overdue).  \\n- If testosterone therapy considered: baseline SHBG and free testosterone if available; consider endocrine consult if complex or if prior androgen therapy issues.\\n\\n6) Summary of next steps and timeline (action checklist for staff)\\n- Immediate (today/ASAP)\\n  - Notify provider about intent to start any hormone therapy (estradiol, progesterone, testosterone) and request orders/clearance. Confirm contraindications and pregnancy status.  \\n  - Document last menstrual period and cycle day in chart.\\n\\n- Within 1 week\\n  - If symptomatic iron insufficiency: start iron per clinic protocol (Metagenics Hemagenics) or ferrous sulfate q48h as directed by provider. Provide patient education on dosing/tolerance and interactions. Order ferritin + CBC in 8–12 weeks.  \\n  - Start vitamin D3 5,000 IU daily per protocol. Schedule 25‑OH D recheck in 8–12 weeks.\\n\\n- Within 2 weeks (if provider approves HRT)\\n  - If starting transdermal estradiol: place order for patch 0.0375 mg/day and add progesterone 100 mg qHS if uterus present. Provide patient counseling on use, side effects, and safety monitoring. Schedule 4–8 week follow-up.  \\n  - If starting testosterone: obtain provider order for low‑dose transdermal testosterone (suggest 300 mcg/day starting dose), baseline testosterone/SHBG and counseling; schedule testosterone recheck in 6–8 weeks.\\n\\n- 4–8 weeks\\n  - Symptom check (vasomotor, sleep, libido), adverse effect surveillance. Adjust therapy per provider.  \\n  - Recheck testosterone (if started) at 6–8 weeks; recheck vit D at 8–12 weeks; recheck ferritin and CBC at 8–12 weeks.\\n\\n- Ongoing\\n  - Monitor for virilization with testosterone; routine labs as above. Reassess HDL after 3–6 months with lifestyle interventions; consider pharmacologic therapy only after diet/exercise fail and provider/cardiology review.\\n\\nPhysician notification triggers (must call/notify provider)\\n- Any initiation of systemic estrogen, progesterone, or testosterone.  \\n- Any new or worsening virilization, severe adverse effects, or lab values rising above female reference ranges.  \\n- Ferritin dropping or symptomatic anemia or heavy uterine bleeding.  \\n- HDL <35 mg/dL persistent despite lifestyle measures and/or change in ASCVD risk.\\n\\nPatient education points to cover at prescription/discussion\\n- Explain cycle variability of progesterone and estradiol and that a single snapshot can be misleading; document cycle day.  \\n- Vitamin D and iron replacement rationales, expected timeline to repletion, dosing and common side effects (GI upset with iron; sedation with progesterone).  \\n- For HRT: expected symptom improvement timeline (4–8 weeks); common side effects; when to stop and call clinic (breast pain, leg swelling, sudden dyspnea, abnormal bleeding).  \\n- For testosterone: possible benefits for libido/fatigue and risks (acne, hair growth, voice change), monitoring plan, and requirement to stop if virilization.\\n\\nNotes / clinic protocol reminders\\n- Always obtain provider sign‑off before initiating hormone therapy.  \\n- Interpret progesterone relative to cycle day; order mid‑luteal progesterone if assessing ovulation.  \\n- Use lowest effective estrogen dose for vasomotor symptom control; add progesterone for uterine protection when indicated.\\n\\nPrepared for provider review and sign‑off."}	2026-01-07 20:17:14.561462	\N
5	Courtney Creel	female	2026-01-09 14:28:58.695	{"a1c": 4.9, "alt": 17, "ast": 14, "bun": 11, "co2": 23, "fsh": 1.23, "hdl": 70, "ldl": 127, "lpa": 62, "rbc": 4.45, "tsh": 3.15, "wbc": 6.9, "apoB": 110, "egfr": 123, "iron": 85, "shbg": 239, "tibc": 446, "hsCRP": 35, "onHRT": false, "folate": 11.3, "freeT4": 1.15, "sodium": 137, "albumin": 4.2, "calcium": 9.4, "glucose": 115, "chloride": 100, "ferritin": 68, "vitaminD": 41.9, "bilirubin": 0.7, "estradiol": 16, "lowEnergy": false, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 322, "potassium": 4.3, "creatinine": 0.67, "hematocrit": 41.2, "hemoglobin": 13.5, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "vitaminB12": 482, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Courtney Creel", "demographics": {"age": 26, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 126, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "progesterone": 0.38, "testosterone": 26.7, "totalProtein": 7, "lowMotivation": false, "triglycerides": 207, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "freeTestosterone": 1, "totalCholesterol": 238}	{"redFlags": [], "ascvdRisk": {"tenYearRisk": 0, "riskCategory": "low", "riskPercentage": "N/A", "recommendations": "ASCVD calculator is validated for ages 40-79 years. Patient is 26 years old. Use clinical judgment for risk assessment.", "statinRecommendation": "Consider if LDL ≥190 mg/dL or familial hypercholesterolemia"}, "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": true, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": true, "high_Lp_a": true, "hsCRP_high": true, "high_nonHDL": true, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": true, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": true}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 tablet daily", "name": "HerWellness™ Estrovera®", "caution": "Rhubarb extract for menopausal symptom relief. Non-hormonal option. Effects typically noticed within 4 weeks.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Estrovera provides clinically studied ERr 731® rhubarb extract for relief of menopausal symptoms including hot flashes, sleep disturbances, and mood changes without hormones.", "indication": "Low estradiol (16 pg/mL)"}, {"dose": "1 softgel 1-2 times daily", "name": "OmegaGenics® Fish Oil Neuro 1000", "caution": "High-DHA omega-3 (750mg DHA, 250mg EPA) for brain and heart health. Lemon-flavored, no fishy taste. Contains calamari - avoid if shellfish allergy.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "OmegaGenics Fish Oil Neuro 1000 provides concentrated DHA and EPA to reduce triglycerides, support brain function, cardiovascular health, and reduce inflammation for joint comfort.", "indication": "TG 207 mg/dL, LDL 127 mg/dL, hs-CRP 35 mg/L, Lp(a) 62 nmol/L"}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Non-GMO, gluten-free.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statins.", "indication": "LDL 127 mg/dL, TG 207 mg/dL, TC 238 mg/dL, Lp(a) 62 nmol/L, ApoB 110 mg/dL, hs-CRP 35 mg/L"}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "general", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Women's health foundation"}], "cacStatinRec": {"lpaWarning": "Elevated Lp(a) of 62 mg/dL is a genetic cardiovascular risk marker. This level is associated with increased ASCVD risk independent of LDL. Since Lp(a) cannot be significantly lowered, focus on aggressive LDL/ApoB reduction and lifestyle optimization.", "statinDiscussion": {"strength": "generally_recommended", "indicated": true, "rationale": "ApoB ≥90 mg/dL (110 mg/dL) with risk enhancers (elevated hs-CRP, elevated triglycerides, elevated Lp(a), elevated ApoB). Statin discussion recommended."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": true, "severity": "high", "recommendation": "Triglycerides 200-499 mg/dL (207 mg/dL). Recommend intensive lifestyle modification first. Rule out secondary causes (diabetes, hypothyroidism, medications, alcohol). If on statin and TG persists ≥150, consider prescription omega-3 (icosapent ethyl) for patients with established ASCVD or diabetes with additional risk factors."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\n- Important abnormalities: triglycerides 207 mg/dL (high), lipoprotein(a) 62 mg/dL (elevated), and hs-CRP 35 mg/L (markedly elevated). These three findings raise concern for inflammation and increased cardiovascular risk and should be addressed promptly. Your 10-year ASCVD risk is listed as N/A and your clinic assessment is LOW risk, but the abnormal tests above still warrant timely follow-up and lifestyle changes.\\n\\nPositive findings (what’s good right now)\\n- Hemoglobin 13.5 g/dL, hematocrit 41.2%, platelets 322 K/uL — normal, no current iron-deficiency anemia.\\n- Ferritin 68 ng/mL — adequate iron stores (>50 ng/mL).\\n- HDL 70 mg/dL — protective (“good”) cholesterol.\\n- Hemoglobin A1c 4.9% — normal blood sugar control.\\n- TSH 3.15 mIU/L, AST 14 U/L, ALT 17 U/L, creatinine 0.67 mg/dL, eGFR 123 mL/min — thyroid, liver, and kidney tests within expected ranges.\\n- Vitamin B12 482 pg/mL, folate 11.3 ng/mL — adequate.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + concrete actions)\\n- Triglycerides 207 mg/dL and LDL 127 mg/dL, ApoB 110 mg/dL:\\n  - Add 25-30g fiber daily: oatmeal for breakfast, beans with lunch, vegetables at dinner.\\n  - Include 2-3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines) for omega-3s.\\n  - Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter and cheese.\\n  - Daily exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking or equivalent.\\n  - Re-check fasting lipid panel in 8–12 weeks after lifestyle changes.\\n- Lipoprotein(a) 62 mg/dL: this is a genetic risk-enhancing factor. Discuss with your provider or cardiology for risk discussion; Lp(a) does not reliably respond to diet, but it informs treatment decisions.\\n- hs-CRP 35 mg/L (markedly elevated): this typically indicates acute inflammation or infection. Action:\\n  - Contact the clinic within 48 hours to report symptoms (fever, sore throat, cough, pain).\\n  - If you have infection symptoms, treat/resolve and repeat hs-CRP in 2–4 weeks to see if it falls.\\n  - General anti-inflammatory habits: prioritize sleep, stop smoking if applicable, follow a Mediterranean-style diet, and avoid excess alcohol.\\n- Vitamin D 25-OH 41.9 ng/mL (goal 60–80 ng/mL): consider vitamin D3 supplementation (discuss dose with provider; typical starting doses are 1,000–2,000 IU/day or higher if recommended), sensible sun exposure, and re-check in ~3 months.\\n\\nHormones\\n- Estradiol 16 pg/mL, progesterone 0.38 ng/mL, FSH 1.23 mIU/mL — interpretation depends on where you are in your menstrual cycle. If these are for fertility or cycle assessment, plan testing at specific cycle days (discuss timing with your provider).\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Contact clinic within 48 hours for hs-CRP result review. Repeat hs-CRP in 2–4 weeks if you had symptoms.\\n- Start the cholesterol lifestyle steps now; re-check fasting lipid panel and ApoB in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Start vitamin D supplementation if advised and re-check in ~3 months.\\n- Consider cardiology referral if Lp(a) remains elevated or lipids don’t improve.\\n\\nYou’re doing many things well (good HDL, iron, sugar control). The steps above are practical and effective — small consistent changes often lead to big improvements. If you want, we can create a 4-week meal and exercise plan together.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 13.5, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 41.2, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 322, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 16, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.38, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 1.23, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 26.7, "status": "normal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone within normal female range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 3.15, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 68, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 41.9, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D suboptimal. Goal is 60-80 ng/mL.", "recommendation": "Consider 2,000-5,000 IU D3+K daily to reach optimal range of 60-80 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "60-80 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 482, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 4.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 127, "status": "borderline", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Near optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet and exercise.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 70, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 207, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "High triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Dietary changes. Reduce refined carbs and alcohol.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": null, "status": "normal", "category": "ASCVD Cardiovascular Risk", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year risk of heart attack or stroke: N/A (LOW RISK)", "recommendation": "ASCVD calculator is validated for ages 40-79 years. Patient is 26 years old. Use clinical judgment for risk assessment.", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 14, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 17, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 123, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.67, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 110, "status": "borderline", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Borderline elevated Apo B.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider pharmacotherapy if high ASCVD risk.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 62, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Elevated Lp(a) - cardiovascular risk enhancing factor.", "recommendation": "Genetic cardiovascular risk factor. Consider aggressive LDL lowering and lifestyle optimization.", "referenceRange": "<30 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 446, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 85, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 11.3, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 35, "status": "critical", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Markedly elevated hs-CRP - acute inflammation.", "recommendation": "Evaluate for infection or inflammatory condition.", "referenceRange": "<1 low risk, 1-3 moderate, >3 high risk"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- Key issues: markedly elevated hs-CRP (35 mg/L) indicating acute systemic inflammation/infection; hypertriglyceridemia (TG 207 mg/dL) with borderline LDL (127 mg/dL) and borderline ApoB (110 mg/dL); elevated Lp(a) (62 mg/dL) — a fixed, atherogenic risk enhancer; suboptimal vitamin D (25-OH 41.9 ng/mL; clinic goal 60–80 ng/mL).\\n- Sex-hormone labs (estradiol, progesterone, FSH, total testosterone) are reported as within reference range. Confirm cycle phase and exogenous hormone (HRT/OC) status when documenting these results.\\n- No current evidence in labs for anemia, thyroid dysfunction, renal or hepatic impairment.\\n\\nPriority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL (immediate)\\n- hs-CRP 35 mg/L: notify supervising physician immediately. This value suggests active inflammation/infection rather than chronic low-grade risk. Actions for staff to perform now:\\n  - Contact patient same day to screen for acute symptoms: fever/chills, cough, dyspnea, dysuria, dental pain, skin/soft tissue infection, recent procedures/vaccination, or other focal complaints.\\n  - If symptomatic (fever, acute focal sx): arrange urgent in-clinic or urgent-care/ER evaluation and consider empiric testing (CBC with diff, blood cultures if febrile, urine analysis ± culture, respiratory viral panel/COVID influenza if respiratory symptoms, chest x-ray if respiratory signs).\\n  - If asymptomatic: order repeat hs-CRP and ESR within 1–2 weeks to distinguish transient vs persistent inflammation; obtain CBC with differential at same time.\\n  - Document and escalate to MD for assessment and plan for targeted work-up.\\n\\nURGENT (within 1–2 weeks)\\n- Triglycerides 207 mg/dL and ApoB 110 mg/dL; Lp(a) 62 mg/dL:\\n  - Confirm fasting status of lipid draw. If non‑fasting, obtain fasting lipid panel for accurate TG assessment.\\n  - If fasting TG still >200 mg/dL after confirming fasting status: notify physician for consideration of medical therapy vs aggressive lifestyle management.\\n  - Consider short-term interventions (see below) and plan recheck of fasting lipids in 6–12 weeks after interventions.\\n  - Given Lp(a) 62 mg/dL (elevated): flag chart for cardiovascular risk counseling and consider cardiology referral, particularly if personal/family premature ASCVD or intermediate-to-high calculated risk.\\n\\nROUTINE (within 4–12 weeks)\\n- Vitamin D 25-OH 41.9 ng/mL:\\n  - Start cholecalciferol (vitamin D3) 2,000–5,000 IU PO daily with vitamin K2 100 mcg PO daily to reach clinic goal 60–80 ng/mL.\\n  - Recheck 25-OH in 8–12 weeks after starting supplementation.\\n- Lipids and cardiometabolic:\\n  - Lifestyle interventions (below); recheck fasting lipid panel and ApoB in 6–12 weeks after interventions or 6–12 weeks after any medication initiation.\\n  - Lp(a) typically measured once; repeat only if clinically indicated (change in risk stratification or prior lab variability).\\n\\nTest, medication, and physician-notification checklist (specific next steps)\\n- Immediate physician notification:\\n  - hs-CRP 35 mg/L — immediate.\\n  - If fasting TG >500 mg/dL (not present here) — urgent.\\n  - Lp(a) >50 mg/dL and ApoB >100 mg/dL — notify for cardiology consideration if ASCVD risk/family history present.\\n- Labs to order now:\\n  - CBC with differential, ESR, repeat hs-CRP in 1–2 weeks, urine analysis ± culture, fasting lipid panel (if prior nonfasting), pregnancy test if of childbearing potential before initiating lipid-lowering agents.\\n  - Consider respiratory viral panel / chest x‑ray if respiratory symptoms; blood cultures if febrile.\\n- Medication recommendations (for physician consideration):\\n  - Vitamin D: cholecalciferol 2,000–5,000 IU PO daily + vitamin K2 100 mcg PO daily. Recheck 8–12 weeks.\\n  - For persistent TG >200 after lifestyle changes: consider prescription omega‑3 ethyl esters 4 g/day (e.g., EPA/DHA product as per formulary) OR high‑EPA product (icosapent ethyl) per clinician decision — consult MD/PCP/Cardiology before start. Ensure pregnancy test prior to starting.\\n  - For LDL/ApoB elevation in the context of elevated Lp(a) or higher ASCVD risk: discuss initiation/intensification of statin with physician (examples: atorvastatin 20–40 mg daily or rosuvastatin 10–20 mg daily) — individualized by risk and contraindications. Document contraception status before statin initiation in reproductive‑aged patients.\\n  - Do NOT initiate lipid-lowering therapy without MD approval and pregnancy status check.\\n\\nTestosterone dose recommendations\\n- Not applicable: total testosterone reported within reference range and no indication to initiate or adjust testosterone therapy based on the provided labs.\\n- If patient is on testosterone therapy, confirm dose, route, timing of trough/peak lab draw and correlate clinically; consider endocrinology/MD review if symptomatic despite “normal” level.\\n\\nLifestyle interventions (specific, staff to counsel)\\n- Diet:\\n  - Reduce intake of refined carbohydrates and simple sugars (including sugar‑sweetened beverages), and reduce alcohol intake (critical for TG control).\\n  - Adopt Mediterranean-style diet: increase non‑starchy vegetables, whole grains in moderation, legumes, fatty fish (2x/week), nuts, and olive oil.\\n  - Advise weight optimization if BMI elevated; modest weight loss (5–10%) can reduce TGs and ApoB.\\n- Activity:\\n  - Encourage ≥150 min/week moderate aerobic activity + resistance training twice weekly, per tolerance.\\n- Specific to triglycerides:\\n  - Avoid high‑fat meals with alcohol; minimize fructose and rapid‑absorption carbs.\\n  - Consider over-the-counter fish oil supplements only after MD review if patient prefers non-prescription; prefer prescription products if substantial TG reduction needed.\\n- Vitamin D:\\n  - Recommend adherence to supplementation; discuss safe sunlight exposure but avoid tanning; review calcium intake.\\n- Smoking cessation:\\n  - If applicable, offer counseling and pharmacotherapy referrals — smoking increases cardiovascular risk and inflammation.\\n- Patient education points to cover (staff to deliver):\\n  - Explain hs-CRP significance as marker of inflammation; immediate evaluation is needed to rule out infection.\\n  - Review signs/symptoms that should prompt urgent medical attention (fever, chest pain, dyspnea, new focal pain or swelling).\\n  - Explain that Lp(a) is genetic and not lowered by lifestyle; it informs intensity of LDL-lowering strategies.\\n  - Discuss contraception/pregnancy considerations before starting lipid-lowering or fibrate/omega‑3 therapies.\\n\\nAdditional testing or referrals to consider\\n- Immediate/near-term:\\n  - Repeat hs-CRP and ESR, CBC with diff.\\n  - Fasting lipid panel (confirm fasting), ApoB repeat if starting therapy (baseline and 6–12 weeks after therapy).\\n  - Pregnancy test prior to any lipid-lowering therapy in reproductive‑aged women.\\n- Specialty referrals:\\n  - Cardiology: if elevated Lp(a) (>50 mg/dL) with family/personal premature ASCVD or if considering aggressive LDL lowering beyond primary-care scope.\\n  - Infectious disease or urgent care/ER evaluation if active infection suspected.\\n  - Rheumatology: if recurrent/persistent elevated inflammatory markers without infection or if autoimmune symptoms present.\\n  - Nutrition/dietitian: for structured dietary plan to lower TGs and LDL and weight loss support.\\n- Consider secondary causes evaluation for TG elevation: review medication list (estrogen therapy, glucocorticoids, antipsychotics, etc.), alcohol use, hepatic disease, uncontrolled diabetes (A1c currently normal).\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (staff action checklist)\\n- Immediate (today/same day):\\n  - Notify MD of hs-CRP 35 mg/L.\\n  - Call patient to assess for acute symptoms; arrange urgent evaluation if symptomatic.\\n  - Order CBC with diff, urine analysis, and targeted tests based on symptoms (respiratory panel/CXR, blood cultures).\\n- Short-term (within 1–2 weeks):\\n  - Repeat hs-CRP and ESR; perform fasting lipid panel if prior nonfasting.\\n  - Start vitamin D3 2,000–5,000 IU PO daily + vitamin K2 100 mcg PO daily (physician-approved), recheck 8–12 weeks.\\n  - Provide lifestyle counseling now and schedule dietitian referral as needed.\\n- Intermediate (6–12 weeks):\\n  - Recheck fasting lipid panel and ApoB after lifestyle or medication changes.\\n  - Recheck vitamin D level at 8–12 weeks.\\n  - If TGs remain >200 or ApoB remains elevated, escalate to MD for consideration of prescription omega‑3 or statin initiation and cardiology referral for Lp(a) management if indicated.\\n- Documentation and escalation:\\n  - Document all patient contacts, symptoms, and test orders promptly.\\n  - Escalate to MD for immediate sign-off on any medication initiation (statin, fibrate, prescription omega‑3) and ensure pregnancy testing where applicable.\\n\\nNote: These are staff-facing clinical action items for physician review. Do not initiate prescription lipid-lowering therapy or chronic medications without MD authorization and appropriate baseline checks (including pregnancy status in reproductive‑aged women)."}	2026-01-09 14:29:04.96535	\N
6	Kimberly Walker	female	2026-01-16 16:12:48.484	{"alt": 15, "ast": 25, "bun": 9, "co2": 25, "hdl": 92, "ldl": 121, "lpa": 6, "rbc": 4.62, "tsh": 0.76, "wbc": 6.9, "apoB": 79, "egfr": 99, "onHRT": true, "sodium": 136, "albumin": 4.4, "calcium": 9.7, "glucose": 77, "chloride": 98, "bilirubin": 0.4, "estradiol": 21, "lowEnergy": true, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 311, "potassium": 4.2, "creatinine": 0.72, "hematocrit": 42.7, "hemoglobin": 14.1, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": true, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Kimberly Walker", "demographics": {"age": 55, "bmi": 21.4, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": true, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 123, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "progesterone": 1.24, "testosterone": 142, "totalProtein": 7.1, "lowMotivation": false, "triglycerides": 51, "menstrualPhase": "postmenopausal", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": true, "totalCholesterol": 223}	{"redFlags": [], "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": true, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "preventRisk": {"ldlGoal": "No specific LDL target; focus on risk-based statin intensity", "riskCategory": "low", "tenYearASCVD": 0.013569696628816625, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0.02406082016223276, "thirtyYearASCVD": 0.0766491920608003, "ageValidFor30Year": true, "thirtyYearTotalCVD": 0.1466275828934459, "tenYearHFPercentage": "1.7%", "tenYearHeartFailure": 0.016691715474730348, "statinRecommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "tenYearCVDPercentage": "2.4%", "tenYearASCVDPercentage": "1.4%", "thirtyYearHFPercentage": "11.6%", "thirtyYearHeartFailure": 0.11577764250931978, "thirtyYearCVDPercentage": "14.7%", "thirtyYearASCVDPercentage": "7.7%"}, "supplements": [{"dose": "3 capsules daily (divided doses)", "name": "Magtein® Magnesium L-Threonate", "caution": "L-Threonate form crosses blood-brain barrier for cognitive and sleep support. Well-tolerated; gentle on GI system.", "category": "mineral", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Magtein is the only magnesium form shown to effectively cross the blood-brain barrier. Supports quality sleep, cognitive function, and energy levels.", "indication": "Sleep disturbances and low energy"}, {"dose": "1 softgel 1-2 times daily", "name": "OmegaGenics® Fish Oil Neuro 1000", "caution": "High-DHA omega-3 (750mg DHA, 250mg EPA) for brain and heart health. Lemon-flavored, no fishy taste. Contains calamari - avoid if shellfish allergy.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "OmegaGenics Fish Oil Neuro 1000 provides concentrated DHA and EPA to reduce triglycerides, support brain function, cardiovascular health, and reduce inflammation for joint comfort.", "indication": "Joint discomfort, LDL 121 mg/dL"}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Non-GMO, gluten-free.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statins.", "indication": "LDL 121 mg/dL, TC 223 mg/dL"}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "general", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Women's health foundation"}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 6, "apoBValue": 79, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "normal", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 1.3569696628816625, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": false}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "consider", "rationale": "Lipid levels meet threshold for CAC consideration. CAC may be useful if risk level is uncertain or patient is hesitant about statin.", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\nYour labs show a low-estrogen pattern with an estradiol of 21 pg/mL, which is below the bone-protection threshold (<40 pg/mL). This pattern (estradiol 21 pg/mL; progesterone 1.24 ng/mL; total testosterone 142 ng/dL) fits a hypoestrogen state consistent with late perimenopause or postmenopause and may explain joint aches and sleep disruption you reported. Your cardiovascular risk is low: 10-year ASCVD risk 1.4% and total 10-year CVD risk 2.4%.\\n\\nPositive findings (what’s good right now)\\n- Hemoglobin 14.1 g/dL and hematocrit 42.7% — iron/oxygen carrying capacity is normal.\\n- TSH 0.76 mIU/L — thyroid function looks normal.\\n- HDL 92 mg/dL and triglycerides 51 mg/dL — great lipid pattern.\\n- ApoB 79 mg/dL and Lp(a) 6 mg/dL — favorable cholesterol particle markers.\\n- Kidney and liver: eGFR 99 mL/min, creatinine 0.72 mg/dL, AST 25 U/L, ALT 15 U/L — normal.\\n- Sleep apnea risk low (STOP-BANG 2/8).\\n\\nAreas to improve (with specific values and actions)\\n1. Low estrogen — Estradiol 21 pg/mL (below 40 pg/mL; bone resorption increases under 40–60 pg/mL)\\n   - Actionable: Schedule a discussion with your provider about menopausal hormone therapy (HRT) within 4 weeks if symptoms bother you; HRT can protect bone and improve sleep/joints. If you prefer non-hormonal, ask about alternatives.\\n   - Bone health actions now: take calcium 1,000–1,200 mg/day (food + supplements as needed) and vitamin D 800–2,000 IU/day (check with provider). Start weight-bearing exercise (brisk walking or stair climbing 30 minutes, 3–5x weekly) and resistance training 2x/week.\\n   - Arrange a DEXA bone density scan within 3 months to assess fracture risk.\\n\\n2. Low progesterone — Progesterone 1.24 ng/mL (below typical HRT therapeutic range)\\n   - Actionable: If you are considering HRT, discuss cyclic or continuous progesterone options with your clinician so progesterone dosing can be optimized.\\n\\n3. Testosterone mildly high — Total testosterone 142 ng/dL (above HRT optimization goal 75–125 ng/dL)\\n   - Actionable lifestyle steps: prioritize 7–8 hours quality sleep nightly, include strength training 2–3x weekly, and use stress reduction (daily 10–20 min meditation, walking, or hobbies). If you have symptoms of excess and/or are on testosterone therapy, discuss dose adjustment with your provider.\\n\\n4. Lipids — LDL 121 mg/dL (near-optimal)\\n   - Actionable: Maintain heart-healthy habits: aim for 150 minutes/week moderate aerobic activity (brisk walking, cycling, or swimming) and a Mediterranean-style diet (vegetables, whole grains, olive oil, fish, nuts). Continue to monitor.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Book a visit within 4 weeks to review HRT options and symptoms. If HRT starts or dose changes: repeat estradiol, progesterone, and testosterone in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Schedule a DEXA scan within 3 months.\\n- Continue current healthy habits; repeat routine labs (lipid panel, CMP) in 6–12 months or sooner if therapy changes.\\n\\nIf you’d like, I can help draft questions for your visit or a checklist for the DEXA and follow-up labs.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 14.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 42.7, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 311, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 21, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol below bone protection threshold (<40 pg/mL). Bone resorption increases below 40-60 pg/mL.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase estrogen dose. Target 60-100 pg/mL. Current level inadequate for bone protection.", "referenceRange": "60-100 pg/mL (HRT goal, >40 for bone)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1.24, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone below HRT therapeutic range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase progesterone dose. Target 8-10 ng/mL for optimal endometrial protection.", "referenceRange": "8-10 ng/mL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 142, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone above HRT optimization goal (75-125 ng/dL).", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Consider reducing testosterone dose. Monitor for androgenic side effects.", "referenceRange": "75-125 ng/dL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 0.76, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 121, "status": "borderline", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Near optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet and exercise.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 92, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 51, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 2.4060820162232757, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 2.4% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 1.3569696628816625, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 1.4%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 1.6691715474730349, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 1.7%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 2, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (2/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 14.662758289344591, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 14.7%, ASCVD 7.7%, Heart Failure 11.6%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 25, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 15, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 99, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.72, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 79, "status": "normal", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Apo B at optimal level.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 6, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<30 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 21, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Hypoestrogen State", "interpretation": "Low estradiol (21 pg/mL) with symptomatic presentation: joint aches, sleep disruption. This pattern is consistent with a low estrogen state, especially in late perimenopause or postmenopause.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Consider transdermal estradiol initiation (product dependent). Add micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly if patient has uterus. Monitor symptoms and estradiol levels. PATIENT EDUCATION: Low estrogen can cause the symptoms you're experiencing. Hormone replacement therapy (HRT) with estradiol can help relieve these symptoms and protect bone and cardiovascular health.", "referenceRange": "Provider target: 60-100 pg/mL (>40 minimum)"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- Hormonal pattern: Hypoestrogen state with estradiol 21 pg/mL (below bone-protection threshold). Patient symptomatic (joint aches, sleep disruption) consistent with perimenopausal/postmenopausal low-estrogen physiology.\\n- Progesterone 1.24 ng/mL is below therapeutic range for endometrial protection on HRT.\\n- Total testosterone 142 ng/dL is above the clinic’s optimization goal (75–125 ng/dL) and should be reduced to avoid androgenic effects.\\n- Lipids: LDL 121 mg/dL—borderline; overall 10-year risk markers are normal.\\n- Other routine labs (CBC, TSH, LFTs, renal function, ApoB, Lp[a]) are within normal limits.\\n\\nPriority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL (require provider sign-off before implementation)\\n- Start/increase estradiol to achieve target estradiol 60–100 pg/mL for symptom relief and bone protection.\\n  * Recommended initial regimen for provider consideration: transdermal estradiol 0.05 mg/day (50 mcg) patch (product-dependent: e.g., Vivelle/Climara) — adjust product and frequency per formulary. Provider must approve initiation/increase.\\n- If uterus present: start micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly for endometrial protection (protocol-specified). Provider approval required.\\n- Reduce testosterone replacement dose to reach target 75–125 ng/dL; provider must authorize the specific adjustment.\\n\\nURGENT (notify provider within 48 hours)\\n- Notify provider to approve: (1) estradiol initiation/increase; (2) progesterone initiation if uterus present; (3) specific testosterone dose reduction plan.\\n- If patient reports signs of virilization (voice change, clitoromegaly), acute chest pain, new focal neurologic symptoms, or suspected thromboembolism → immediate provider notification and standard urgent workup.\\n\\nROUTINE (can be implemented by clinic staff per standing orders once provider approves)\\n- Lifestyle counseling for LDL management and bone health.\\n- Arrange follow-up laboratory monitoring per timeline below.\\n\\nSpecific medication / dose recommendations (for provider review and order)\\n- Estradiol (transdermal) — provider order recommended:\\n  * Start transdermal estradiol 0.05 mg/day (50 mcg) patch. Choose product per clinic formulary (note patch application schedule differs by product: Vivelle typically twice weekly, Climara weekly). Document product and dosing schedule.\\n  * If already on transdermal and on a lower dose, increase to 0.05 mg/day equivalent.\\n  * Goal: serum estradiol 60–100 pg/mL.\\n- Progesterone — if patient has a uterus, provider order recommended:\\n  * Micronized progesterone 100 mg PO nightly (bedtime). If prior bleeding or endometrial concerns, provider may prefer 200 mg nightly or cyclical 200 mg x12 days/month; follow provider guidance.\\n  * Goal: serum progesterone 8–10 ng/mL (check level as specified below).\\n- Testosterone — provider order recommended:\\n  * If on testosterone replacement, reduce dose by 25–50% as an initial adjustment (example options for provider to select based on formulation):\\n    - Transdermal gel/cream: reduce daily dose by 25–50% or change application frequency (e.g., every other day) depending on current dosing.\\n    - Intramuscular injections: extend injection interval (e.g., from q2–3 weeks to q3–4 weeks) or reduce per-injection dose by 25–50%.\\n  * Recheck total testosterone 4–8 weeks after dose change with target 75–125 ng/dL.\\n  * Monitor for androgenic side effects; if persistent elevation or virilization, consider further reduction or endocrinology referral.\\n\\nTiming for follow-up labs and re-evaluation\\n- Hormone panel (estradiol, progesterone, total testosterone): recheck 4–8 weeks after any dose change to assess response and titrate to targets.\\n  * Ensure consistent timing relative to cycle/HRT and dosing:\\n    - If premenopausal and cycling, document cycle day. For endogenous progesterone interpretation, obtain mid-luteal sample (approx. day 21 in a 28-day cycle) unless on continuous HRT.\\n    - If on nightly oral micronized progesterone, document time of last dose. For clinic consistency, draw labs in the morning and record time since last dose; aim to measure at a consistent interval after dosing on follow-up (clinic protocol should specify exact timing).\\n    - For transdermal estradiol, obtain sample at a consistent time relative to patch application (e.g., morning at steady state; document product and time since last patch application).\\n- Lipid panel: repeat fasting lipid profile in 3 months to assess LDL response to lifestyle changes and HRT.\\n- DEXA scan: if not performed within 2 years or no prior baseline, order bone mineral density (DEXA) given estradiol below bone-protection threshold.\\n- Routine labs (CBC, LFTs): if starting oral estrogen or changing dose, consider LFTs in 8–12 weeks per provider discretion (transdermal has less hepatic effect).\\n\\nPhysician notification requirements\\n- Provider must review and sign orders to initiate/increase estradiol and to start progesterone if uterus present.\\n- Provider must review and authorize testosterone dose reduction plan.\\n- Notify provider promptly if follow-up labs show:\\n  * Estradiol not in target range after 2 dose adjustments.\\n  * Testosterone remains >150–200 ng/dL or if androgenic signs develop.\\n  * New abnormal LFTs, unexplained bleeding, or acute cardiopulmonary symptoms.\\n\\nPatient education points for staff to communicate (for provider-approved plan)\\n- Explain low-estrogen symptoms and rationale for HRT (symptom relief, bone protection).\\n- Review HRT route choice: transdermal estradiol recommended to lower thromboembolic and hepatic risk compared with oral.\\n- If uterus present, emphasize necessity of nightly micronized progesterone for endometrial protection.\\n- Discuss expected timeline: symptomatic improvement typically within weeks; hormone levels checked at 4–8 weeks.\\n- Review potential side effects (breast tenderness, spotting, mood changes) and instruct patient to report severe symptoms (chest pain, shortness of breath, leg swelling, sudden vision changes, severe abdominal pain).\\n- Discuss testosterone dose reduction rationale (reduce androgenic effects) and potential transient symptom changes.\\n\\nLifestyle interventions (staff to counsel and document)\\n- Bone health: calcium 1000–1200 mg/day from diet/supplement and vitamin D 800–2000 IU/day as appropriate (check 25-OH vitamin D if not recently measured).\\n- Exercise: weight-bearing and resistance exercise 3+ times weekly.\\n- Cardiovascular health / LDL reduction: Mediterranean-style diet, reduce saturated fats, increase soluble fiber, weight optimization, 150 min/week moderate-intensity aerobic exercise.\\n- Tobacco cessation (if applicable) and limit alcohol.\\n- Sleep hygiene measures for sleep disruption.\\n\\nAdditional testing / referrals to consider\\n- DEXA scan for bone density (if not recent).\\n- Reproductive endocrine or gynecology referral if:\\n  * Unexplained uterine bleeding after starting HRT, difficulty achieving target progesterone level, or complex dosing considerations.\\n- Endocrinology referral if testosterone remains elevated despite dose reduction or if secondary causes of hyperandrogenism suspected.\\n- Lipid clinic or primary care for persistent LDL elevation or if 10-year risk changes significantly.\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (action checklist)\\n- Immediately (today / within 48 hours)\\n  1. Notify provider for approval of:\\n     - Start/increase transdermal estradiol to 0.05 mg/day equivalent.\\n     - Start micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly if uterus present.\\n     - Reduce testosterone dose by 25–50% (specific adjustment based on formulation).\\n- After provider approval (initiate per orders)\\n  2. Educate patient using the points above; document counseling.\\n  3. Order baseline DEXA if none within 2 years.\\n  4. Schedule lab recheck (estradiol, progesterone, total testosterone) at 4–8 weeks after medication changes with clear documentation of timing relative to dose/cycle.\\n  5. Repeat fasting lipid panel in 3 months and counsel on lifestyle modifications now.\\n- Ongoing\\n  6. Provider to reassess labs and symptoms at 4–8 weeks; adjust doses to target estradiol 60–100 pg/mL, progesterone 8–10 ng/mL, testosterone 75–125 ng/dL.\\n  7. Escalate to endocrine/gynecology if targets not met, abnormal bleeding occurs, or androgen excess persists.\\n\\nNote on cycle-phase interpretation and lab timing\\n- Document menstrual cycle day for premenopausal patients when drawing progesterone (mid-luteal recommended for endogenous assessment).\\n- Ensure consistent timing relative to HRT dosing for follow-up lab comparisons; include time-since-last-dose on lab requisition and in chart notes.\\n\\n(Provider approval required for all hormone initiation/adjustment orders; these recommendations are for clinician review and implementation only.)"}	2026-01-16 16:12:49.125844	\N
