isk": {"lpaValue": 8, "apoBValue": 57, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "normal", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 0.21068592439664938, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": false}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\nYour results are mainly very good. Your 10-year cardiovascular risk is low (Total CVD 0.3%, ASCVD 0.2%, Heart Failure 0.2%) and many blood tests are in healthy ranges. Two items to note: your Vitamin D is on the low side (30 ng/mL), and hormone testing suggests early perimenopause (FSH 9.64 mIU/mL with progesterone 0.36 ng/mL in a 35-year-old).\\n\\nPositive findings (numbers)\\n- Hemoglobin 13.6 g/dL, Hematocrit 42.3%, Platelets 330 K/uL — normal.\\n- Ferritin 96.5 ng/mL — good iron stores (>50 ng/mL).\\n- Vitamin B12 677 pg/mL, Folate 8.39 ng/mL — adequate.\\n- TSH 1.94 mIU/L — thyroid function in normal range.\\n- LDL 74 mg/dL, HDL 64 mg/dL, Triglycerides 41 mg/dL — excellent lipid profile.\\n- hs-CRP 0.2 mg/L — low inflammation.\\n- Kidney (eGFR 106 mL/min, Creatinine 0.75 mg/dL) and liver (AST 21 U/L, ALT 12 U/L) are normal.\\n- Sleep apnea risk 0/8 — low risk.\\n\\nAreas to improve (exact values + actions)\\n1) Vitamin D: Your 25-OH Vitamin D is 30 ng/mL (defined here as low/suboptimal for bone and hormone health). Actionable steps:\\n   - Start vitamin D3 1,000–2,000 IU daily (discuss exact dose with us). \\n   - Eat vitamin D foods (fatty fish, fortified milk/yogurt) and aim for 10–20 minutes sunlight on arms/face several times/week when safe.\\n   - Weight-bearing exercise 2–3x weekly (walking, jogging, squats) and aim for 30 minutes most days to support bone health.\\n   - Retest Vitamin D in 3 months to confirm improvement.\\n\\n2) Early perimenopause signs: FSH 9.64 mIU/mL and progesterone 0.36 ng/mL (values drawn in the follicular/pre-ovulatory phase). This suggests the transition has begun.\\n   - Track menstrual cycle length, flow, and symptoms (hot flashes, sleep, mood) for the next 3–6 months.\\n   - Lifestyle: strength training 2–3x/week, prioritize 7–8 hours sleep, and stress management (yoga, meditation).\\n   - If symptoms become disruptive (heavy bleeding, very irregular cycles, severe hot flashes), schedule a visit — consider repeat hormones in 6–12 months or sooner.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Start Vitamin D supplementation now and retest 25-OH Vitamin D in ~3 months.\\n- Continue routine annual checks for lipids, thyroid, and CBC (or sooner if symptoms).\\n- If menstrual symptoms worsen, book an appointment; otherwise repeat perimenopause labs in 6–12 months.\\nYou’re doing well overall — these steps should support bone, hormone, and heart health. If you want, we can set up reminders or a follow-up visit to review Vitamin D recheck and cycle tracking.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 13.6, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 42.3, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 330, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 50, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol appropriate for follicular phase.", "recommendation": "Normal finding. Continue monitoring if needed for fertility.", "referenceRange": "20-150 pg/mL (follicular phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.36, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone appropriate for pre-ovulatory phase.", "recommendation": "Normal finding.", "referenceRange": "<1.5 ng/mL (pre-ovulation)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 9.64, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH appropriate for follicular phase.", "recommendation": "Normal finding.", "referenceRange": "3-10 mIU/mL (follicular phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 28.3, "status": "normal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone within normal female range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.94, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 96.5, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 30, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D deficiency (21-30 ng/mL). Suboptimal for hormone and bone health.", "recommendation": "Repletion: Metagenics D3 5,000 + K daily. Target ≥60 ng/mL. Recheck in 8-12 weeks.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 677, "status": "nor