r energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statins.", "indication": "LDL 103 mg/dL, Lp(a) 122 nmol/L"}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "general", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Women's health foundation"}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 122, "apoBValue": 75, "lpaStatus": "elevated", "apoBStatus": "normal", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 1.2829184322480702, "hasElevatedLpa": true, "statinGuidance": "Possibly consider statin therapy given risk enhancement; shared decision-making recommended.", "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "reclassified_upward", "clinicalGuidance": "Lp(a) 122 mg/dL - RISK ENHANCER (genetic risk factor). RECLASSIFIED TO BORDERLINE RISK: Risk enhancer present. Shared decision-making regarding statin therapy.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "cacRecommendation": "CAC reasonable especially with strong family history and risk enhancement present.", "hasBorderlineApoB": false}, "cacStatinRec": {"lpaWarning": "Elevated Lp(a) of 122 mg/dL is a genetic cardiovascular risk marker. This level is associated with increased ASCVD risk independent of LDL. Since Lp(a) cannot be significantly lowered, focus on aggressive LDL/ApoB reduction and lifestyle optimization.", "statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "strongly_recommend", "rationale": "Elevated Lp(a) of 122 mg/dL is a genetic risk marker. CAC strongly recommended to assess arterial calcium burden and inform treatment intensity.", "recommended": true}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall summary (plain language)\\nYour labs show that you are in the perimenopausal transition with a hypoestrogenic pattern: estradiol (E2) is 20 pg/mL and FSH is 31.3 mIU/mL, which indicates falling ovarian estrogen production. Progesterone is low at 0.31 ng/mL. You also have an elevated Lipoprotein(a) at 122 mg/dL (a cardiovascular risk enhancer) and a supraphysiologic testosterone pattern with Total T 120 ng/dL, SHBG 63.3 nmol/L, and Free T 14 pg/mL. Many other results are reassuring (see positives below).\\n\\nPositive findings (specific values)\\n- Hemoglobin 13.5 g/dL and hematocrit 40% — normal.\\n- Ferritin 68.6 ng/mL — good iron stores.\\n- Vitamin D 25-OH 69.9 ng/mL — optimal for bone health.\\n- TSH 0.85 mIU/L — thyroid function within normal range.\\n- HDL 64 mg/dL and triglycerides 42 mg/dL — favorable lipid pattern.\\n- hs-CRP 0.3 mg/L — low inflammation.\\n- 10-year ASCVD risk 1.3% (low); 10-year total CVD risk 2.3% (low).\\n\\nAreas to improve (numbers and concrete actions)\\n1. Low estrogen and bone protection risk\\n   - Estradiol 20 pg/mL (below bone protection threshold <40 pg/mL). Bone resorption increases below 40–60 pg/mL.\\n   - Actions: Discuss hormone options with your clinician (transdermal estradiol is often used to raise E2 above 40–60 pg/mL for bone protection). Start/continue weight-bearing exercise 30 minutes, 3–5x weekly (walking, stair climbing, resistance training). Ensure calcium 1,000–1,200 mg/day from diet or supplements and maintain vitamin D (you are 69.9 ng/mL — keep current dose). Consider a DEXA bone density scan within 6–12 months.\\n\\n2. Low progesterone / anovulation\\n   - Progesterone 0.31 ng/mL. If this draw was in the luteal phase, this suggests anovulation (common in perimenopause).\\n   - Actions: Track cycles and symptoms; discuss cyclic progesterone or micronized progesterone with your clinician if indicated to protect the uterus when using estrogen.\\n\\n3. Testosterone pattern\\n   - Total T 120 ng/dL, Free T 14 pg/mL and SHBG 63.3 — pattern consistent with testosterone pellet therapy and supraphysiologic free T.\\n   - Actions: If experiencing insomnia, acne, hair changes, irritability, discuss lowering or removing testosterone therapy. Lifestyle: prioritize 7–8 hours sleep nightly, strength train 2–3x/week, and use stress-management (meditation, walking, hobbies).\\n\\n4. Cardiovascular risk enhancer: Lp(a) 122 mg/dL\\n   - Lp(a) is elevated and increases lifetime CVD risk despite a low 10-year risk.\\n   - Actions: Because your 30-year CVD risk is 15.9% (intermediate), follow intermediate CVD prevention steps: increase aerobic exercise to 200–300 minutes/week plus strength training 2x/week; follow a Mediterranean-style diet (vegetables, whole grains, olive oil, nuts, fatty fish 3x weekly); reduce sodium <2,300 mg/day; avoid processed foods; discuss Lp(a) and long-term risk with a cardiology provider to consider specialized testing or therapies.\\n\\nOther notes and next steps (timeline)\\n- Review supplements: B12 is 2000 pg/mL and serum iron 177 µg/dL — if you are taking iron or high-dose B12 supplements, stop them and recheck iron studies and B12 in 6–8 weeks. TIBC 318 µg/dL and ferritin 68.6 ng/mL are currently reassuring.\\n- Schedule a visit within 4 weeks to review symptoms and current hormone/ supplement use. Recheck estradiol, progesterone, and testosterone 8–12 weeks after any therapy change. Consider DEXA in 6–12 months and cardiology referral to discuss elevated Lp(a).\\nYou’re doing many things well (vitamin D, lipids, inflammation). Small targeted changes now can protect bone and heart health as you transition through perimenopause.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 13.5, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "He