 "priority": "medium", "rationale": "OmegaGenics Fish Oil Neuro 1000 provides concentrated DHA and EPA to reduce triglycerides, support brain function, cardiovascular health, and reduce inflammation for joint comfort.", "indication": "LDL 138 mg/dL, hs-CRP 2.2 mg/L"}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Non-GMO, gluten-free.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statins.", "indication": "LDL 138 mg/dL, TC 221 mg/dL, ApoB 102 mg/dL, hs-CRP 2.2 mg/L"}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "general", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Women's health foundation"}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 12, "apoBValue": 102, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "borderline", "riskCategory": "borderline", "baseASCVDRisk": 5.62912110578415, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "statinGuidance": "Statin not routinely indicated; focus on lifestyle modifications.", "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "borderline", "clinicalGuidance": "Borderline risk (5-7.4%) without risk enhancers. Lifestyle modifications primary; reassess risk factors.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "cacRecommendation": "CAC may help clarify risk if other risk-enhancing factors present.", "hasBorderlineApoB": true}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "generally_recommended", "indicated": true, "rationale": "ApoB ≥90 mg/dL (102 mg/dL) with risk enhancers (elevated ApoB). Statin discussion recommended."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "consider", "rationale": "Lipid levels meet threshold for CAC consideration. CAC may be useful if risk level is uncertain or patient is hesitant about statin.", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "2-4 weeks (urgent values present)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\nYour labs show a few important areas to address and many that are reassuring. Key concerns: elevated red cell measures (hematocrit 49.4% and hemoglobin 16.5 g/dL), low vitamin D (25-OH 20.3 ng/mL), borderline cholesterol risk (LDL 138 mg/dL; ApoB 102 mg/dL; hs-CRP 2.2 mg/L), and menopausal hormone pattern (estradiol 7 pg/mL and FSH 62.4 mIU/mL). Your 10-year total CVD risk is 9.3% (INTERMEDIATE risk) and 10-year ASCVD risk is 5.6%.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Ferritin 123 ng/mL (good iron stores)\\n- TSH 2.07 mIU/L (thyroid function normal)\\n- HbA1c 5.5% (blood sugar well controlled)\\n- HDL 68 mg/dL and triglycerides 73 mg/dL (protective lipid numbers)\\n- Kidney and liver tests normal (eGFR 102 mL/min; AST 18 U/L; ALT 22 U/L)\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + concrete actions)\\n- Vitamin D 25-OH 20.3 ng/mL (low): start vitamin D3 2,000 IU daily and review calcium intake (aim 1,000–1,200 mg/day). Add weight-bearing exercise (walking, stair-climbing 30 min, 3x/week). Recheck 25-OH in 8–12 weeks.\\n- LDL 138 mg/dL, ApoB 102 mg/dL, hs-CRP 2.2 mg/L: raise heart-healthy activity and diet. Because your 10-year CVD risk is 9.3% (INTERMEDIATE), aim for 200–300 minutes of aerobic activity weekly plus strength training 2×/week; include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines); add 25–30 g fiber daily (oatmeal for breakfast, beans at lunch, vegetables at dinner); choose lean meats and limit butter/cheese; reduce sodium to <2,300 mg/day; consider discussing a moderate-to-high intensity statin with your clinician to reach a 30–50% LDL reduction.\\n- Hematocrit 49.4% and hemoglobin 16.5 g/dL (elevated): ensure good hydration today (drink 1–2 extra glasses of water), repeat CBC in 4 weeks. If still high, your clinician may check for causes (smoking, dehydration, sleep-disordered breathing, or rare conditions) and consider further testing.\\n\\nMenopause and bone health\\n- Estradiol 7 pg/mL, FSH 62.4 mIU/mL — confirms menopausal status. If you have symptoms (hot flashes, mood, sleep, vaginal dryness) discuss menopause care options including hormone therapy. Due to menopause + low vitamin D, consider DEXA bone density screening if not done recently.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Schedule a visit in 4–8 weeks to review symptoms and statin discussion.\\n- Repeat CBC in 4 weeks; recheck lipids and hs-CRP in ~3 months after lifestyle changes or sooner if starting a statin.\\n- Recheck vitamin D in 8–12 weeks after starting supplements.\\nIf anything feels urgent (dizziness, chest pain, new shortness of breath), contact your clinician or urgent care right away.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 16.5, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Elevated hemoglobin - evaluate for dehydration or polycythemia.", "recommendation": "Check hydration status. Consider hematology referral if persistent.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 49.4, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Elevated hematocrit - evaluate for polycythemia.", "recommendation": "Consider hematology referral. Evaluate for underlying causes.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 280, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 7, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.4, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 62.4, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 38.3, "status": "normal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone within normal female range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 2.07, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 123, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 20.3, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D deficiency (21-30 ng/mL). Suboptimal for hormone and bone health.", "recommendation": "Repletion: Metagenics D3 5,000 + K daily. Target ≥60 ng/mL. Recheck in 8-12 weeks.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 448, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.5, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 138, "status": "borderline", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Borderline high LDL.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider statin if additional risk factors.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 68, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 73, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 9.271758133260649, "status": "abnormal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 9.3% (INTERMEDIATE RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nINTERMEDIATE-HIGH RISK (7.5-20%): Moderate-to-high intensity statin recommended\\n\\nRisk-enhancing factors may favor more aggressive therapy", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.62912110578415, "status": "abnormal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 5.6%", "recommendation": "MODERATE-TO-HIGH intensity statin based on shared decision-making", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.306243814857211, "status": "borderline", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 5.3%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 2, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (2/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 32.897861321761226, "status": "abnormal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 32.9%, ASCVD 19.9%, Heart Failure 23.1%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 18, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 22, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 102, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.63, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 102, "status": "borderline", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Borderline elevated Apo B.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider pharmacotherapy if high ASCVD risk.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 12, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 319, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 106, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 9.07, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 2.2, "status": "borderline", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Borderline hs-CRP - moderate cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Continue monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.4, "status": "borderline", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Low Progesterone (Phase Unknown)", "interpretation": "Progesterone is 0.4 ng/mL. Without cycle phase documentation, interpretation is limited. If drawn in luteal phase, this would indicate anovulation - a perimenopause marker.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Document cycle phase for accurate interpretation. If patient is mid-cycle or luteal, this suggests anovulation. Consider symptoms: sleep disruption, anxiety, irregular cycles. PATIENT EDUCATION: We need to know where you are in your menstrual cycle to fully interpret this result. Please note when your last period started.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase; <5 ng/mL suggests anovulation if luteal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 7, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Hypoestrogenic Pattern", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 7 pg/mL with FSH of 62.4 mIU/mL indicates hypoestrogenic perimenopause - the \\"crash phase.\\" Estrogen production has dropped significantly and the brain is signaling strongly (elevated FSH) trying to stimulate the ovaries.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Transdermal estradiol 0.025-0.05 mg is appropriate replacement therapy. Add micronized progesterone for uterine protection and sleep support. Frame this as hormone replacement, not stimulation - we are restoring what the body needs. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen has dropped to a level that can cause symptoms like hot flashes, night sweats, brain fog, and mood changes. Replacing estrogen with a patch or gel can relieve these symptoms and protect your long-term health. Progesterone is added to protect your uterus and help with sleep.", "referenceRange": "E2 <50 with FSH >10 indicates estrogen deficiency"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 62.4, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Menopause", "interpretation": "FSH of 62.4 mIU/mL with low estradiol (7 pg/mL) confirms menopausal status. No specific symptoms reported at this time. Elevated FSH reflects decreased ovarian function and reduced estrogen production. This is a normal part of reproductive aging but may benefit from hormone optimization if symptomatic.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: If symptomatic, consider hormone therapy (HT): transdermal estradiol + micronized progesterone (if uterus intact). Discuss benefits/risks of HT including cardiovascular, bone, and quality of life considerations. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your lab results confirm you are in menopause. Hormone therapy can help manage symptoms like hot flashes, sleep problems, and vaginal dryness while also protecting bone health.", "referenceRange": "FSH <25 mIU/mL premenopausal; ≥25 suggests transition; ≥40 menopausal"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 38.3, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone Pattern D: Adequate Androgens, Persistent Symptoms", "interpretation": "Adequate Androgens pattern identified (Total T: 38.3 ng/dL (within range); Free T: 4 pg/mL; SHBG: 62.5 nmol/L). Total, free, and bioavailable testosterone are within normal ranges and SHBG is not extreme. If the patient reports fatigue, low mood, sleep disruption, or low libido, these symptoms are likely driven by non-androgenic factors. Findings to evaluate: Low Vitamin D (20.3 ng/mL) — contributes to fatigue and mood changes. Estrogen/Progesterone balance should be assessed (E2: 7 pg/mL, P4: 0.4 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Do not reflexively add testosterone when levels are adequate — look deeper. Evaluate iron status (ferritin target >50 for symptom resolution), thyroid function (optimize TSH), and sleep quality (consider sleep apnea screening with STOP-BANG). Assess estrogen and progesterone balance — E2/P4 imbalance is a common driver of persistent symptoms. Address CNS and psychosocial drivers: chronic stress, HPA axis dysregulation, mood disorders. Consider DHEA-S if not already checked. Support with lifestyle optimization: sleep hygiene, stress management, nutrition. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your testosterone levels are actually in a healthy range, which is good news. When symptoms persist despite normal testosterone, it usually means something else is contributing. Your provider will look at other factors like iron levels, thyroid function, sleep quality, and hormone balance to find what's driving your symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Total T 15-70 ng/dL, Free T 0.5-5.0 pg/mL (all within range)"}, {"unit": "of 6 markers positive", "value": 2, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Insulin Resistance Screening", "recheckTiming": "3-6 months", "interpretation": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive). This combination points toward hyperinsulinemia physiology. Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR).", "recommendation": "Phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance. Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose.", "referenceRange": "0-1 Low, 2 Moderate, 3+ High Likelihood"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- Labs consistent with biochemical menopause: FSH 62.4 mIU/mL, estradiol 7 pg/mL, progesterone 0.4 ng/mL. Patient is hypoestrogenic and may benefit from hormone replacement if symptomatic and no contraindications.\\n- Concerning polycythemia pattern: Hematocrit 49.4% and hemoglobin 16.5 g/dL (both at or above clinic thresholds). This requires immediate physician notification and workup for secondary and primary causes.\\n- Cardiovascular risk is intermediate (10‑yr CVD risk ~9.3%; 10‑yr ASCVD 5.6%); LDL 138 mg/dL and ApoB 102 mg/dL are borderline. Consider statin therapy per protocol after shared decision‑making.\\n- Vitamin D deficiency (25‑OH D 20.3 ng/mL) — repletion recommended.\\n- Testosterone levels adequate (Total T 38.3 ng/dL, Free T 4 pg/mL, SHBG 62.5 nmol/L); do not initiate androgen therapy based on current data; evaluate other drivers of symptoms.\\n\\nPriority Actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL — Physician notification required now\\n- Notify covering physician immediately about polycythemia: Hematocrit 49.4% and Hgb 16.5 g/dL (≥48% threshold for women).\\n- Request immediate orders as appropriate (see URGENT below).\\n\\nURGENT — within 24–72 hours\\n- Hydration and repeat CBC:\\n  - Instruct patient to hydrate well and present for repeat CBC in 1 week (sooner if symptomatic: headache, visual changes, dizziness, thrombotic symptoms).\\n  - Repeat CBC should be drawn fasting and after adequate hydration to reduce hemoconcentration artifact.\\n- Baseline oxygenation and review of secondary causes:\\n  - Check resting pulse oximetry; document smoking status, recent altitude exposure, and any pulmonary disease.\\n  - Review medications (including over-the-counter/herbal testosterone, erythropoietin, diuretics) and ask about recent heavy exercise or dehydration.\\n- If Hct remains ≥48% on repeat or Hgb persistently elevated:\\n  - Order hematology referral and consider JAK2 V617F mutation testing, serum erythropoietin level, and peripheral smear per hematology guidance.\\n  - Consider immediate hematology referral if symptoms of hyperviscosity or suspected PV.\\n\\nROUTINE — within 1–12 weeks\\n- Vitamin D repletion:\\n  - Start Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily (or equivalent cholecalciferol 5,000 IU daily) and recheck 25‑OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks. Target per protocol: ≥60 ng/mL.\\n- Cardiovascular risk management:\\n  - Discuss moderate-to-high intensity statin therapy. Suggested starting options for shared decision‑making:\\n    - Atorvastatin 20 mg PO nightly (moderate intensity) — consider 40 mg if higher intensity warranted.\\n    - Rosuvastatin 10 mg PO nightly (moderate intensity).\\n  - Obtain baseline fasting lipid panel (if prior was nonfasting), BMP/LFTs as baseline before initiation (AST/ALT currently normal but confirm values).\\n  - Recheck fasting lipid panel 6–12 weeks after statin initiation, then at least annually.\\n- Menopausal hormone therapy (if patient symptomatic and no contraindications):\\n  - Consider transdermal estradiol 25–50 mcg/day (0.025–0.05 mg/day = 25–50 mcg/day patch). Use lowest effective dose for symptom control.\\n  - If uterus intact, add oral micronized progesterone 100–200 mg nightly (commonly 100 mg at bedtime; 200 mg can be used if needing greater endometrial protection or sleep benefit) — discuss continuous vs cyclic regimen with provider.\\n  - Before initiating HT, confirm absence of absolute contraindications (active/recent VTE, active breast cancer, undiagnosed genital bleeding, severe liver disease). Document informed shared decision‑making.\\n  - If HT started: schedule follow-up in 6–8 weeks for symptom check, BP, side effects; re-evaluate need for dose adjustment. Consider baseline mammogram and DEXA per age/risk if not up to date.\\n- Progesterone/estradiol lab contextualization:\\n  - Document day/phase of menstrual cycle (date of last menstrual period) in chart for accurate interpretation. If drawn in luteal phase, P4 of 0.4 ng/mL suggests anovulation; if luteal timing unknown, use menopausal pattern (high FSH, low E2) to guide management.\\n\\nSpecific next-step orders for staff to place/prompt:\\n- Immediate telephone or EHR alert to physician for polycythemia lab values.\\n- Order repeat CBC (CBC with differential) in 1 week after hydration instructions.\\n- Order resting pulse oximetry and document smoking status; order JAK2 V617F and EPO only if directed by hematology or if repeat Hct remains elevated.\\n- Order Vitamin D supplement and recheck 25‑OH D in 8–12 weeks.\\n- If starting statin: order baseline LFTs and fasting lipid panel, then lipid panel at 6–12 weeks.\\n- If HT initiated: place HT prescription pending physician signoff and schedule 6–8 week follow-up visit.\\n\\nTestosterone dose recommendations (staff-facing)\\n- Current data: Total T 38.3 ng/dL, Free T 4 pg/mL — within acceptable range.\\n- Recommendation: Do NOT initiate testosterone therapy at this time.\\n- If provider later elects a therapeutic trial for refractory symptoms after other causes addressed:\\n  - Consider a low-dose trial under specialist guidance: transdermal testosterone 300 mcg/day (0.3 mg/day) titrating to symptomatic response and serum testosterone within female physiologic range. Alternatives: 1% topical gel at low dose per formulary.\\n  - Monitoring if started: total testosterone at baseline and at 3 months (draw 24 hours after dose for gels), clinical assessment for virilization, lipid panel, LFTs. Discontinue if virilizing symptoms occur.\\n- Document clear prescriber order and monitoring schedule before initiation.\\n\\nLifestyle interventions (to counsel patients; staff to reinforce)\\n- Cardiovascular risk reduction:\\n  - Mediterranean-style diet, reduce saturated fat and simple sugars, increase vegetables, whole grains, lean protein.\\n  - Aim for 150 minutes/week of moderate-intensity exercise plus strength training twice weekly.\\n  - Tobacco cessation support and referral to counseling/medications as appropriate.\\n  - Weight optimization and referral to nutrition if BMI elevated.\\n- Sleep and pulmonary health:\\n  - Screen for obstructive sleep apnea if symptoms (snoring, daytime somnolence, witnessed apneas); consider STOP‑BANG referral if indicated despite prior normal screen.\\n- Bone health:\\n  - Ensure adequate calcium intake (dietary + supplemental as needed) and weight-bearing exercise; consider DEXA per age/risk.\\n- Vitamin D and general:\\n  - Reinforce adherence to vitamin D repletion and safe sun exposure.\\n- Hydration:\\n  - Counsel on adequate hydration prior to repeat blood draw to reduce spurious hemoconcentration.\\n\\nAdditional testing and referrals to consider\\n- Hematology referral if repeat Hct ≥48% or Hgb persistently elevated, or if symptoms of hyperviscosity: order JAK2 V617F mutation, serum EPO, and peripheral smear per hematology guidance.\\n- Cardiology or preventive cardiology consult if multiple risk-enhancing factors or provider concern (ApoB elevated, hs-CRP borderline, lifetime CVD risk high).\\n- DEXA scan if not up-to-date given hypoestrogenism / menopause and bone health risk.\\n- Sleep medicine referral if OSA symptoms present or pulse oximetry low.\\n- Behavioral health, sleep medicine, or endocrinology referral for persistent fatigue, mood, or sleep disruption after hormonal optimization.\\n- If thrombosis risk or contraindications to estrogen are present, discuss non-hormonal symptom management with provider.\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (staff checklist)\\n- Immediate (same day):\\n  - Notify physician of Hct 49.4% / Hgb 16.5 g/dL.\\n  - Instruct patient to hydrate; document smoking status and oxygenation if available.\\n- 1 week:\\n  - Repeat CBC (after hydration). Obtain pulse oximetry reading.\\n- If repeat CBC normalizes (<48% and Hgb lower): continue routine follow-up.\\n- If repeat CBC remains elevated:\\n  - Order hematology referral and consider JAK2 V617F, EPO, peripheral smear per hematology recommendations — urgent referral if persistent.\\n- Within 1–2 weeks:\\n  - Discuss statin therapy with provider; if agreed, start atorvastatin 20 mg nightly (or rosuvastatin 10 mg) after shared decision-making and baseline LFTs.\\n  - Begin Vitamin D 5,000 IU daily.\\n  - Document cycle phase / date of last menstrual period in chart; if unsure, note menopausal labs indicate low estrogen status.\\n- 6–12 weeks:\\n  - Recheck vitamin D (8–12 wks) and fasting lipid panel after lifestyle/statin initiation (6–12 wks).\\n- 6–8 weeks after HT initiation (if ordered):\\n  - Visit for symptom reassessment, BP, side effects; adjust therapy per provider.\\n- Longer term:\\n  - Annual risk reassessment: lipids, ASCVD risk, CBC if clinically indicated, and routine monitoring per HT protocol.\\n\\nPatient education points for staff to cover (scripted, concise)\\n- Explain that labs show low estrogen and high FSH consistent with menopause; discuss symptom-relief options including transdermal estrogen + progesterone if uterus intact.\\n- Emphasize the elevated hematocrit/hemoglobin requires repeat blood testing to rule out dehydration or other causes and may need hematology evaluation.\\n- Instruct on vitamin D supplementation and follow-up testing.\\n- Counsel on heart-healthy lifestyle and that cholesterol management (statin) may be recommended given intermediate 10‑yr risk.\\n- Ask the patient to document and report date of last menstrual period and any current hormone or supplement use.\\n\\nDocumentation and physician notification requirements (staff)\\n- Enter an urgent message in chart and call/notify clinician immediately regarding Hct ≥48% and Hgb 16.5 g/dL.\\n- Document hydration instructions, smoking status, and timing of repeat CBC order.\\n- Flag the chart for follow-up labs: CBC in 1 week, vitamin D recheck 8–12 weeks, lipid panel 6–12 weeks if therapy started.\\n- Record informed shared decision-making conversations for any statin or HT initiation.\\n\\nNote: This guidance is for clinic staff review and to prompt physician decision‑making. Final treatment decisions (statin initiation, HT, hematology referral, or testosterone therapy) require provider authorization and patient shared decision‑making.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 1.07, "detail": "Triglycerides 73 / HDL 68 = 1.07", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5.5%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 62.5, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 22, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "102 mg/dL", "detail": "Elevated atherogenic particle count", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "2.2 mg/L", "detail": "Elevated systemic inflammation", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "moderate", "phenotypes": [{"key": "visceral_metabolic", "name": "Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Recheck A1c, lipid panel, hs-CRP, ApoB in 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0"], "pathophysiology": "Early peripheral + hepatic insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia. Glucose may still be \\"normal.\\" A1c often rises last.", "triggerCriteria": ["A1c >= 5.7%", "TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0", "Central adiposity phenotype", "ALT above optimal range"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests your body may not be responding to insulin as efficiently as it should. Insulin is the hormone that helps move sugar from your bloodstream into your cells for energy.\\n\\nWhen cells become less sensitive to insulin:\\n- Blood sugar slowly rises over time\\n- Triglycerides increase\\n- Inflammation increases\\n- Weight gain around the midsection becomes easier\\n\\nThe good news? This stage is highly reversible with targeted nutrition, strength training, sleep optimization, and - when appropriate - medication support.\\n\\nWe'll focus on improving how your body processes fuel and reducing long-term cardiometabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward nutrition (>= 25-35g/meal)", "Carbohydrate quality + timing education", "Strength training 2-3x weekly", "10-15 min post-meal walking", "Sleep optimization", "Consider: GLP-1 therapy if obesity, appetite dysregulation, A1c >= 5.7, or inflammatory phenotype", "Consider: Metformin if prediabetes or strong family history", "Recheck metabolic panel in 3-6 months"]}], "positiveCount": 2, "likelihoodLabel": "MODERATE LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive). This combination points toward hyperinsulinemia physiology. Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR).", "confirmationTests": "Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose."}}	2026-03-05 16:21:11.899263	\N
17	Charlene Kennedy	female	2026-03-10 15:28:05.947	{"alt": 13, "ast": 16, "bun": 16, "co2": 25, "hdl": 88, "ldl": 85, "rbc": 3.37, "tsh": 1.41, "wbc": 6.7, "acne": false, "egfr": 78, "onHRT": false, "folate": 4.87, "sodium": 137, "albumin": 4.1, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 9.7, "glucose": 91, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "chloride": 100, "ferritin": 97.1, "hairLoss": false, "vitaminD": 14, "bilirubin": 0.3, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": false, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 279, "potassium": 5.2, "creatinine": 0.79, "hematocrit": 34.2, "hemoglobin": 11.1, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "vitaminB12": 292, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Charlene Kennedy", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 73, "bmi": 38.04, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": true, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 145, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "restlessLegs": false, "totalProtein": 6.4, "lowMotivation": false, "triglycerides": 85, "menstrualPhase": "postmenopausal", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "totalCholesterol": 190}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History of present illness: Reviewed laboratory results with patient today. Patient is a 73-year-old postmenopausal female presenting for routine lab review and hormone/primary care follow-up. She reports mild, progressive fatigue over the past several months but denies syncope, chest pain, palpitations, significant dyspnea at rest, melena, hematochezia, or overt bleeding. No new neurologic complaints (no paresthesias, gait instability, or memory decline reported). No recent changes in medications. Patient is postmenopausal (age 73) and has no menses for many decades.\\n- Medications: not documented in the lab data provided. (Med list to be verified in chart.)\\n- Allergies: not documented in the lab data provided.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\nVitals:\\n- Not documented in lab dataset provided / vitals not available at time of charting.\\n\\nLaboratory results (03/10/2026) — organized by category. Abnormal values flagged.\\n\\nComplete Blood Count (CBC)\\n- Hemoglobin (Hgb): 11.1 g/dL — Low [ABNORMAL]\\n- Hematocrit (Hct): 34.2 % — Low [ABNORMAL]\\n- Platelets: 279 K/uL — Normal\\n\\nIron / Iron Stores\\n- Ferritin: 97.1 ng/mL — Normal\\n\\nVitamin / Nutritional Labs\\n- Vitamin D (25-OH): 14 ng/mL — Low [SEVERE DEFICIENCY]\\n- Vitamin B12: 292 pg/mL — Low-normal / Borderline\\n- Folate: 4.87 ng/mL — Low-normal / Borderline\\n\\nThyroid / Metabolic\\n- TSH: 1.41 mIU/L — Normal\\n\\nLiver / Renal Function\\n- AST: 16 U/L — Normal\\n- ALT: 13 U/L — Normal\\n- Creatinine: 0.79 mg/dL — Normal\\n- eGFR: 78 mL/min/1.73 m2 — Normal\\n\\nLipid Panel\\n- LDL cholesterol: 85 mg/dL — Normal\\n- HDL cholesterol: 88 mg/dL — Normal\\n- Triglycerides: 85 mg/dL — Normal\\n\\nCardiometabolic / Risk Scores\\n- 10-year Total CVD Risk (AHA PREVENT 2023): 15.7% — Intermediate risk [ABNORMAL]\\n- 10-year ASCVD Risk: 7.7% — (elevated borderline for statin consideration) [ABNORMAL]\\n- 10-year Heart Failure Risk: 14.7% — [ABNORMAL]\\n- Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG): 2 points — Low risk\\n\\nRed flags: None identified on lab review.\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1) Anemia — mild, non-severe (Hgb 11.1 g/dL, Hct 34.2%).\\n   - Clinical significance: symptomatic correlate is mild fatigue. Differential includes iron deficiency (less likely given ferritin 97.1 ng/mL), anemia of chronic disease/inflammation, nutritional deficiency (borderline B12/folate), occult blood loss (GI), or less likely renal insufficiency (renal function normal).\\n   - No immediate red-flag laboratory values (no critically low hemoglobin), but given postmenopausal status, evaluation for occult GI blood loss is appropriate.\\n\\n2) Severe Vitamin D deficiency (25-OH D = 14 ng/mL).\\n   - Clinical significance: increased risk for bone loss, falls, muscle weakness, and possible impaired immune function. Needs repletion.\\n\\n3) Borderline Vitamin B12 (292 pg/mL) and borderline folate (4.87 ng/mL).\\n   - Clinical significance: low-normal values may contribute to anemia and fatigue. No reported neurologic symptoms; however, borderline B12 warrants supplementation and/or further testing (MMA) if symptoms develop.\\n\\n4) Intermediate 10-year cardiovascular risk (Total CVD 15.7%; ASCVD 7.7%; HF 14.7%).\\n   - Clinical significance: places patient in intermediate risk category where moderate-to-high intensity statin therapy should be considered following shared decision-making. Review of other modifiable risk factors (BP, smoking, activity, diet) recommended.\\n\\n5) Other labs otherwise within expected limits: platelets, ferritin, TSH, LFTs, renal function, and lipid profile (LDL 85 mg/dL, HDL 88 mg/dL, triglycerides 85 mg/dL). No immediate hepatic or renal contraindication noted to initiate recommended supplements/statin.\\n\\nPLAN:\\n1) Anemia (Hgb 11.1 g/dL; Hct 34.2%).\\n   - Diagnostic actions:\\n     - Order iron studies: serum iron, TIBC, transferrin saturation.\\n     - Order reticulocyte count and peripheral blood smear.\\n     - Order inflammatory markers (CRP and/or ESR) as clinically indicated.\\n     - Obtain fecal immunochemical test (FIT) x1 for occult GI blood loss. If FIT positive or if high clinical concern or not up-to-date with colorectal cancer screening, refer to GI for evaluation/colonoscopy.\\n     - Review medication list for agents causing bleeding (antiplatelets, anticoagulants, NSAIDs).\\n   - Therapeutic actions:\\n     - Start Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support, 1 tablet twice daily with meals (medium-priority supplement recommended), pending clinician review and patient tolerance. Discussed that ferritin is currently not low; supplement is supportive but further workup is recommended rather than empiric high-dose iron therapy.\\n     - If iron studies ultimately indicate iron deficiency, consider oral iron therapy or IV iron per GI/hematology guidance.\\n   - Monitoring:\\n     - If Hemagenics or iron therapy initiated: repeat CBC and reticulocyte count in 6–8 weeks.\\n     - If no iron therapy: repeat CBC and review labs at 3 months.\\n     - If anemia persists or worsens, refer to hematology/GI for further evaluation.\\n   - Patient education: advised to report any new GI bleeding, melena, hematochezia, or worsening fatigue/dyspnea.\\n\\n2) Severe Vitamin D deficiency (25-OH D = 14 ng/mL).\\n   - Therapeutic actions:\\n     - Start Vitamin D3 + K softgel (D3 10,000 IU with vitamin K) — 1 softgel daily with a meal (high-priority supplement recommended). (Per clinic recommendation.)\\n     - Counsel on dietary calcium; consider calcium supplementation if dietary intake inadequate.\\n     - Discuss fall prevention and weight-bearing exercise to support bone health.\\n     - Order baseline DEXA scan if not done within the last 1–2 years to assess bone mineral density given age 73 and severe vitamin D deficiency.\\n   - Monitoring:\\n     - Recheck 25-OH vitamin D level in 12 weeks (3 months) after starting supplementation.\\n     - Reassess calcium, renal function if clinically indicated prior to prolonged high-dose supplementation.\\n     - Re-evaluate fracture risk and consider osteoporosis therapy per DEXA results and clinical risk.\\n\\n3) Borderline Vitamin B12 (292 pg/mL) and Folate (4.87 ng/mL).\\n   - Therapeutic actions:\\n     - Start Intrinsi B12-Folate™ (1 tablet daily) as recommended (high-priority supplement).\\n     - If patient develops neurologic symptoms or if B12 remains low/declines, obtain methylmalonic acid (MMA) and intrinsic factor antibody testing as indicated; consider parenteral B12 if deficiency or malabsorption documented.\\n   - Monitoring:\\n     - Recheck B12 and folate levels in 3 months.\\n     - If symptoms of neuropathy develop, expedite evaluation.\\n\\n4) Cardiovascular risk (10-yr Total CVD 15.7%; ASCVD 7.7%).\\n   - Therapeutic actions:\\n     - Discussed statin therapy as per AHA PREVENT 2023 guidance. Recommend consideration of a moderate-intensity statin; patient preference and shared decision-making to determine initiation. Example option: atorvastatin 20 mg nightly (moderate intensity) — discuss risks/benefits and potential need for dose adjustment to moderate-to-high intensity if indicated.\\n     - If patient elects to start statin: obtain baseline hepatic function tests (AST/ALT are currently normal) and fasting lipid panel in 6–12 weeks after initiation, then periodically thereafter.\\n   - Lifestyle:\\n     - Counsel on heart-healthy diet (Mediterranean/DASH type), regular aerobic exercise (per baseline fitness and mobility), smoking cessation if applicable, and weight management.\\n     - Assess and optimize blood pressure control (BP values not available today — to be checked at next visit).\\n   - Monitoring:\\n     - Fasting lipid panel and LFTs 6–12 weeks after statin initiation, then every 6–12 months or as clinically indicated.\\n\\n5) Preventive and follow-up items:\\n   - DEXA bone density scan recommended given age and vitamin D deficiency (order if not obtained within 1–2 years).\\n   - Ensure colorectal cancer screening status is up-to-date; order FIT and GI referral if FIT positive or if screening incomplete given new anemia.\\n   - Sleep apnea risk low (STOP-BANG 2); no further sleep testing indicated based solely on this score.\\n   - Medication reconciliation and review of allergies at next visit.\\n   - Coordinate with PCP/primary clinic for statin initiation decision if not directly managed at this clinic.\\n\\nOrders placed / recommended:\\n- Iron studies (serum iron, TIBC, transferrin saturation), reticulocyte count, peripheral smear.\\n- Fecal immunochemical test (FIT) x1.\\n- CRP and/or ESR as indicated.\\n- 25-OH Vitamin D level recheck in 12 weeks.\\n- Vitamin B12 and folate recheck in 3 months.\\n- CBC repeat in 6–8 weeks if starting Hemagenics/iron; otherwise repeat CBC in 3 months.\\n- Baseline fasting lipid panel and hepatic panel if initiating statin; repeat lipid panel 6–12 weeks after statin initiation.\\n- DEXA scan order if not current.\\n- Consider MMA and intrinsic factor antibody testing if B12 remains low or neurologic symptoms develop.\\n\\nCounseling / patient discussion:\\n- Results and significance reviewed with patient today (anemia, severe vitamin D deficiency, borderline B12/folate, and intermediate cardiovascular risk).\\n- Discussed recommended supplement regimen: D3 10,000 + K (1 softgel daily with meal), Intrinsi B12-Folate™ (1 tablet daily), and Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support (1 tablet twice daily with meals) as per lab-priority recommendations; patient counseled on rationale and side effects.\\n- Discussed potential need for further diagnostic testing (iron studies, FIT, reticulocyte, smear, DEXA) and possible statin therapy; shared decision-making offered regarding statin initiation.\\n- Patient verbalized understanding and agrees with the plan to proceed with testing and supplements; will review statin decision after discussion with PCP and based on shared decision-making.\\n\\nFollow-up:\\n- Return/phone follow-up with results review in 3 months (routine), sooner if FIT positive or if CBC declines or patient develops new/worsening symptoms.\\n- If starting any new prescription medication (statin) or if lab-directed therapy initiated, follow-up per above monitoring intervals.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\nDate: 03/10/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "preventRisk": {"ldlGoal": "Goal: 30-50% LDL reduction", "riskCategory": "intermediate", "tenYearASCVD": 0.07725129564155353, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nINTERMEDIATE-HIGH RISK (7.5-20%): Moderate-to-high intensity statin recommended\\n\\nRisk-enhancing factors may favor more aggressive therapy", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0.1573142421047283, "ageValidFor30Year": false, "tenYearHFPercentage": "14.7%", "tenYearHeartFailure": 0.14650089384428927, "statinRecommendation": "MODERATE-TO-HIGH intensity statin based on shared decision-making", "tenYearCVDPercentage": "15.7%", "tenYearASCVDPercentage": "7.7%"}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 softgel daily with meal", "name": "D3 10,000 + K", "caution": "High-dose repletion therapy. Recheck vitamin D levels in 8-12 weeks. Contains vitamin K2 for calcium metabolism.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "high", "rationale": "D3 10,000 + K provides high-dose vitamin D3 with K2 for efficient repletion of severe deficiency. K2 ensures proper calcium utilization and prevents arterial calcification.", "indication": "Vitamin D 14 ng/mL (severe deficiency ≤20)", "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D level is very low, which can affect your bones, immune system, mood, and energy. This high-dose supplement will help bring your level up to optimal range, and the vitamin K2 helps your body use calcium properly.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 14 ng/mL (severe deficiency ≤20)"]}, {"dose": "1 tablet daily", "name": "Intrinsi B12-Folate™", "caution": "Contains intrinsic factor for enhanced B12 absorption. Ideal for patients with absorption concerns or vegetarian/vegan diets.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "high", "rationale": "Intrinsi B12-Folate provides methylated B12 and folate with intrinsic factor for superior absorption. Supports energy, cognition, methylation, and nerve function.", "indication": "B12 292 pg/mL (deficient <300); Folate 4.87 ng/mL (low)", "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "Your B12 and/or folate levels need support. This supplement provides highly absorbable forms of these essential vitamins to support your energy, brain function, and nervous system health.", "supportingFindings": ["B12 292 pg/mL (deficient <300)", "Folate 4.87 ng/mL (low)"]}, {"dose": "1 tablet twice daily with meals", "name": "Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support", "caution": "Contains iron, folate, and B12 for comprehensive RBC support. Avoid taking with calcium-rich foods or dairy. May cause mild GI upset initially.", "category": "iron", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Hemagenics provides highly absorbable iron bisglycinate with B12, B6, and folate for comprehensive red blood cell support. Indicated for functional iron deficiency, iron deficiency without anemia, and iron-responsive symptoms.", "indication": "Hemoglobin 11.1 g/dL (low)", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "Your lab results suggest your iron stores are lower than optimal. This supplement provides a gentle, well-absorbed form of iron along with B12 and folate to support your energy levels, hair health, and overall vitality.", "supportingFindings": ["Hemoglobin 11.1 g/dL (low)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "consider", "rationale": "Lipid levels meet threshold for CAC consideration. CAC may be useful if risk level is uncertain or patient is hesitant about statin.", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\nYour labs show three main issues to address: mild anemia (Hemoglobin 11.1 g/dL, Hematocrit 34.2%), a severe Vitamin D deficiency (25-OH Vitamin D 14 ng/mL), and an INTERMEDIATE 10-year cardiovascular risk (Total CVD risk 15.7%, ASCVD risk 7.7%, Heart Failure risk 14.7%). Other important tests are reassuring: TSH 1.41 mIU/L, ferritin 97.1 ng/mL, LDL 85 mg/dL, HDL 88 mg/dL, triglycerides 85 mg/dL, platelets 279 K/uL, AST 16 U/L, ALT 13 U/L, creatinine 0.79 mg/dL, eGFR 78 mL/min.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Ferritin 97.1 ng/mL (good iron stores).\\n- LDL 85 mg/dL (within goal), HDL 88 mg/dL (protective), triglycerides 85 mg/dL.\\n- TSH 1.41 mIU/L (thyroid in normal range).\\n- Liver (AST 16 U/L, ALT 13 U/L) and kidney function (creatinine 0.79 mg/dL, eGFR 78 mL/min) are normal.\\n- Low sleep apnea risk: STOP-BANG 2/8 (low).\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + concrete actions)\\n1) Anemia: Hemoglobin 11.1 g/dL, Hematocrit 34.2% (low). Ferritin 97.1 ng/mL suggests iron stores are adequate; possible causes include menstrual blood loss or low B12/folate (B12 292 pg/mL, folate 4.87 ng/mL are low-normal).\\n- Actions: Track menstrual bleeding (pad/tampon count) and report heavy periods. Eat iron-rich meals (2 servings lean red meat or poultry weekly, beans, spinach) plus vitamin C (citrus) with meals to help absorption. Add folate-rich foods (leafy greens, fortified grains) and consider an over-the-counter folic acid 400–800 mcg daily. For B12, include fortified cereal, dairy or take oral B12 500–1,000 mcg daily; discuss B12 injection if levels fall or symptoms persist.\\n- Recheck CBC and reticulocyte in 4–6 weeks.\\n\\n2) Vitamin D: 25-OH 14 ng/mL (severe deficiency ≤20 ng/mL).\\n- Actions: Start vitamin D3 supplementation—discuss with your provider either high-dose therapy (vitamin D3 50,000 IU once weekly for 6–8 weeks) or daily 2,000–4,000 IU. Increase weight-bearing exercise (walking, strength training 2×/week) and dietary calcium (aim 1,000–1,200 mg/day). Recheck Vitamin D in 8–12 weeks.\\n\\n3) Cardiovascular risk: Total CVD 15.7% (INTERMEDIATE), ASCVD 7.7% (7.7% = intermediate risk).\\n- Actions (INTERMEDIATE): Aim for 200–300 minutes aerobic activity weekly plus strength training 2×/week; focus on heart-healthy fats (avocado, nuts, olive oil, fatty fish 3×/week); reduce sodium to <2,300 mg/day; if you smoke, plan to quit. Work toward LDL reduction goal (30–50% reduction) and discuss moderate-to-high intensity statin therapy with your provider soon.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Discuss Vitamin D dosing and statin decision at your next visit (within 1 month).\\n- Repeat CBC in 4–6 weeks, Vitamin D in 8–12 weeks, and B12/folate in 3 months.\\n- Contact clinic sooner for worsening fatigue, heavy bleeding, chest pain, shortness of breath, or new symptoms. You’re doing well in many areas; these steps can make a big difference—we’ll partner with you to follow up.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 11.1, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Low hemoglobin indicating anemia.", "recommendation": "Check iron studies, B12, folate. E