18	Elizabeth O'Brien	female	2026-03-10 16:09:20.127	{"alt": 15, "ast": 19, "bun": 17, "co2": 27, "fsh": 36.3, "rbc": 4.41, "tsh": 1.62, "wbc": 5.5, "acne": false, "egfr": 100, "shbg": 52.1, "onHRT": true, "sodium": 139, "albumin": 4.6, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 9.9, "glucose": 80, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "chloride": 101, "hairLoss": false, "bilirubin": 0.3, "estradiol": 105, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": false, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 414, "potassium": 5.1, "creatinine": 0.75, "hematocrit": 42.7, "hemoglobin": 14, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Elizabeth O'Brien", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 45, "bmi": 21.35, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 111, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 3.42, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 254, "totalProtein": 7.5, "lowMotivation": false, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "freeTestosterone": 36}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History of present illness: Elizabeth O'Brien is a 45-year-old female whose routine hormone panel and basic labs were reviewed today. Patient reports no acute complaints and reports no new or worsening symptoms at this time. She denies chest pain, shortness of breath, syncope, new hirsutism, deepening voice, or recent severe acne. No documentation of current testosterone formulation or dosing in chart at time of review; cycle day/last menstrual period was not recorded with the laboratory draw. Patient was advised to report any new symptoms (rapid-onset hirsutism, marked acne, voice changes, significant hair loss, severe mood change) immediately.\\n- Menstrual/menopausal history: Labs indicate menopausal transition; patient age 45. Cycle phase at time of draw unknown — LMP/cycle day needs documentation to interpret progesterone.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vital signs: Not recorded in lab review note.\\n- Risk scores: STOP-BANG (sleep apnea risk) 0 points.\\n\\nLaboratory results (03/10/2026) — grouped by category:\\n\\nCBC\\n- Hemoglobin: 14.0 g/dL (normal)\\n- Hematocrit: 42.7% (normal)\\n- Platelets: 414 K/uL — borderline/mild thrombocytosis (400–450 K/uL) [BORDERLINE]\\n\\nComprehensive metabolic panel / basic chemistries\\n- Creatinine: 0.75 mg/dL (normal)\\n- eGFR: 100 mL/min (normal)\\n- AST: 19 U/L (normal)\\n- ALT: 15 U/L (normal)\\n\\nThyroid\\n- TSH: 1.62 mIU/L (normal)\\n\\nHormones\\n- Estradiol (E2): 105 pg/mL — slightly above HRT optimization goal (60–100 pg/mL) [BORDERLINE]\\n- Progesterone (P4): 3.42 ng/mL — low for luteal-phase expectation; below HRT therapeutic range [ABNORMAL]\\n- Total Testosterone: 254 ng/dL — supraphysiologic; well above female physiologic/HRT optimization range [CRITICAL]\\n- SHBG: 52.1 nmol/L\\n- Free Testosterone: 36 pg/mL — elevated (consistent with supraphysiologic total T) [CRITICAL]\\n- FSH: 36.3 mIU/mL — consistent with perimenopausal transition (menopausal transition) [ABNORMAL]\\n\\nOther\\n- Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG): 0 points\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Supraphysiologic androgen excess — Total Testosterone 254 ng/dL with elevated Free T (36 pg/mL) and SHBG 52.1 nmol/L. This meets clinic criteria for critical androgen excess. Differential: exogenous testosterone therapy (pellet/injection/gel) — pattern consistent with pellet therapy; if no exogenous source, consider androgen-secreting neoplasm (ovarian or adrenal). Red flag: immediate provider review required to prevent further dosing and evaluate for urgent causes. Potential clinical sequelae: insomnia, heat intolerance, acne, alopecia/hair shedding, mood changes, cardiovascular/metabolic effects.\\n\\n2. Relative progesterone deficiency / luteal-phase progesterone deficiency — Progesterone 3.42 ng/mL with Estradiol 105 pg/mL. Relative P4 deficiency may indicate inadequate luteal support, anovulation, or perimenopausal luteal insufficiency and predispose to estrogen-dominant symptoms and unopposed endometrial stimulation if on exogenous estrogen.\\n\\n3. Perimenopausal transition — FSH 36.3 mIU/mL consistent with menopausal transition. Correlates with age (45) and variable ovulatory cycles; cycle day not documented limiting correlation to progesterone.\\n\\n4. Estradiol mildly above clinic optimization goal — E2 105 pg/mL (goal 60–100 pg/mL). If patient symptomatic on current E2 dose consider modest reduction; if clinically acceptable, may continue with recheck.\\n\\n5. Mild thrombocytosis — Platelets 414 K/uL (reactive range). Differential: reactive thrombocytosis from iron deficiency, inflammation, infection, or other reactive causes. Evaluate iron stores and inflammatory markers.\\n\\n6. Other labs reassuring — Hemoglobin/hematocrit, creatinine/eGFR, LFTs, TSH within normal limits.\\n\\nRed flags requiring immediate action: supraphysiologic testosterone (see Assessment #1). Cycle day not documented — limits interpretation of progesterone and ovulatory status.\\n\\nPLAN:\\n(Immediate/Critical actions — done today)\\n1. Testosterone management (Assess #1)\\n   - Patient instructed to STOP any exogenous testosterone products (gels, patches, injections, pellets) pending provider review. Patient verbalized understanding.\\n   - Provider notified urgently of supraphysiologic Total T 254 ng/dL and elevated Free T (documentation placed in chart; provider to contact patient for next steps).\\n   - Do NOT administer further testosterone or schedule pellet insertion until provider directs changes.\\n   - If patient confirms current use of testosterone pellets: do NOT schedule next pellet; discuss transition to titratable transdermal route with provider.\\n   - If patient denies exogenous testosterone: urgent imaging and endocrine evaluation recommended (see orders below).\\n\\n(Orders to place / diagnostics)\\n2. Immediate labs to order (today/within 48 hours unless recent)\\n   - CBC with differential (repeat within 4–8 weeks if platelets persist or sooner per provider)\\n   - Ferritin, serum iron, TIBC (evaluate for iron deficiency as cause of reactive thrombocytosis)\\n   - CRP (or ESR) — evaluate inflammation\\n   - CMP / LFTs (if not performed in last 3 months) — baseline for hormone therapy safety\\n   - Fasting lipids and fasting glucose (or A1c) — baseline metabolic risk panel given androgen excess\\n   - Repeat Total Testosterone, Free T, SHBG in 2–4 weeks after any dose reduction/hold (or sooner if symptoms severe)\\n   - If progesterone therapy initiated: recheck P4 4 weeks after start/titration (timed mid-luteal if cycling)\\n   - Recheck Estradiol 6–8 weeks after any E2 dose change\\n\\n3. Imaging / specialty referral (urgent if exogenous T not confirmed)\\n   - If patient not on exogenous testosterone: order pelvic/ovarian ultrasound and consider adrenal imaging (CT abdomen/pelvis without and with contrast per provider discretion) to evaluate for androgen-secreting tumor. Provider to decide urgency.\\n   - Consider endocrinology referral if persistent unexplained hyperandrogenism or complex management.\\n\\n(Progesterone / Estradiol management — Assess #2–#4)\\n4. Progesterone replacement (provider decision)\\n   - Consider starting micronized progesterone for relative luteal deficiency:\\n     * Option A (initial): Micronized progesterone 100 mg PO at bedtime nightly; reassess symptom response and consider increase to 200 mg PO qHS if inadequate or for endometrial protection.\\n     * Option B (if patient has regular cycles and provider chooses cyclic): progesterone 100–200 mg PO qHS days 14–28 of cycle (or last 14 days).\\n     * Option C (if irregular cycles or continuous estrogen): progesterone 100–200 mg PO qHS continuously.\\n   - Reassess bleeding pattern and sedation profile; counsel patient on possible drowsiness at night.\\n   - Order repeat P4 4 weeks after initiation/titration, ideally timed mid-luteal if patient is ovulatory.\\n\\n5. Estradiol adjustment (Assess #4)\\n   - E2 is slightly above optimization goal (105 pg/mL). If patient is asymptomatic on current regimen, provider may elect to continue current dose and recheck in 6–8 weeks. If symptomatic or provider prefers optimization, consider modest dose reduction (e.g., lower patch strength or oral dose per provider).\\n   - Document cycle day/LMP to guide timing and decision-making.\\n\\n(Platelets / iron evaluation — Assess #5)\\n6. Platelet elevation management\\n   - Order ferritin, iron, TIBC and CRP (ordered above).\\n   - If ferritin <50 ng/mL or iron studies consistent with iron deficiency, recommend iron replacement per provider (common option: ferrous sulfate 325 mg PO once daily to twice daily or alternative per tolerance; IV iron if indicated) — provider order required.\\n   - Repeat CBC in 4–8 weeks after iron repletion or sooner if abnormal CRP/clinical concern.\\n\\n(Monitoring and follow-up schedule)\\n7. Monitoring timeline and follow-up (documented and discussed with patient)\\n   - Provider appointment/telehealth within 48–72 hours to review testosterone results and determine immediate management (hold/reduce/transition).\\n   - If testosterone dose reduced/held: recheck Total T, Free T, SHBG in 2–4 weeks.\\n   - Recheck CBC and LFTs 2–4 weeks after testosterone adjustment, then every 3–6 months as indicated.\\n   - Reassess progesterone 4 weeks after initiation/titration; recheck P4 timed to cycle if applicable.\\n   - Recheck Estradiol 6–8 weeks after any dose change.\\n   - Repeat CBC (platelets) in 4–8 weeks.\\n\\n(Medication/supplement recommendations)\\n8. Supplements\\n   - EstroFactors™ recommended as medium-priority: 2 tablets twice daily as previously suggested (patient advised this is adjunctive; continue only if tolerated and per provider approval).\\n   - Counsel regarding iron supplementation only after iron studies reviewed and provider order obtained.\\n\\n(Lifestyle and counseling)\\n9. Counseling provided to patient (documented)\\n   - Patient instructed to stop any additional testosterone products and to expect provider contact. Asked to report any new androgenic symptoms urgently.\\n   - Discussed sleep hygiene, stress reduction, reduced alcohol intake, weight management and exercise, and iron-rich diet if ferritin low.\\n   - Advised on potential progesterone sedation and bleeding pattern changes if started.\\n\\n(Documentation)\\n10. Charting and communication\\n    - Provider notified of critical testosterone result and need for urgent review.\\n    - Staff to document last menstrual period / cycle day at next contact to aid hormone interpretation.\\n    - Orders for labs and imaging drafted and available for provider signature if approved.\\n\\nFollow-up: Provider visit within 48–72 hours for testosterone management. Routine rechecks as above: 2–4 weeks for testosterone, 4 weeks for progesterone reassessment, 6–8 weeks for estradiol recheck, 4–8 weeks for CBC/platelet follow-up.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\n03/10/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "supplements": [{"dose": "2 tablets twice daily", "name": "EstroFactors™", "caution": "Nutritional support for estrogen balance and metabolism. Contains I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Supports healthy estrogen metabolite ratios.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "EstroFactors provides targeted nutritional support for estrogen metabolism and balance with I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Best suited for women with estrogen dominance patterns, PMS, perimenopausal hormone imbalance, or those needing broader estrogen metabolism support during hormonal transitions. Differs from Estrovera in that it addresses the underlying estrogen metabolism rather than vasomotor symptom relief.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected; Age-appropriate for estrogen metabolism support", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement helps your body process and balance estrogen more effectively. It supports healthy hormone metabolism, which can help with PMS symptoms, bloating, mood changes, and hormonal balance during life transitions.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected", "Age-appropriate for estrogen metabolism support"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall summary (plain language)\\n- Your labs show that you are in the perimenopausal transition (FSH 36.3 mIU/mL) with relatively high estradiol (105 pg/mL) but low progesterone (3.42 ng/mL). You also have a high total testosterone (254 ng/dL) and elevated free testosterone (36 pg/mL). These hormone patterns suggest luteal/progesterone deficiency common in perimenopause and a supraphysiologic testosterone level that needs prompt review.\\n\\nPositive findings (what is normal)\\n- Hemoglobin 14 g/dL and hematocrit 42.7% — normal.\\n- Thyroid: TSH 1.62 mIU/L — normal/euthyroid.\\n- Liver enzymes: AST 19 U/L and ALT 15 U/L — normal.\\n- Kidney function: eGFR 100 mL/min and creatinine 0.75 mg/dL — normal.\\n- Sleep apnea risk (STOP-BANG) 0 points — low risk.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + concrete actions)\\n- Progesterone 3.42 ng/mL (below HRT therapeutic goal): Discuss progesterone optimization. Action: schedule a visit to review options (oral or topical micronized progesterone or cyclic progesterone dosing). If this sample was not drawn in the luteal phase, repeat progesterone in the luteal phase (about day 21 of a 28-day cycle or 7 days before expected period) to confirm ovulation.\\n- Estradiol 105 pg/mL (above HRT optimization target 60–100 pg/mL): We may adjust estrogen dosing if you are on HRT. Action: review current estrogen treatment and consider small dose change; recheck level 6–8 weeks after any change.\\n- Testosterone total 254 ng/dL (goal 75–125 ng/dL) and free T 36 pg/mL, SHBG 52.1 nmol/L — supraphysiologic and flagged for urgent evaluation. Action (urgent): If you are using testosterone therapy (especially pellets), contact us within 1 week to review dosing and symptoms. Do not stop prescribed medication without talking to us. We may need to reduce or pause therapy and recheck levels in 4–6 weeks.\\n- Platelets 414 K/uL (mildly high; 400–450): This can be reactive to low iron. Action: check ferritin and iron studies. Meanwhile add iron-rich foods (lean red meat, beans, spinach), pair with vitamin C (citrus) to improve absorption, avoid tea/coffee with meals, and consider iron supplement only after ferritin checked.\\n\\nBone health and lifestyle (concrete actions)\\n- Perimenopause increases bone risk over time. Actions: weight-bearing exercise 30 minutes most days + strength training 2x/week, calcium 1,000–1,200 mg/day from diet and/or supplements, vitamin D 800–2,000 IU/day (check 25-OH vitamin D level), avoid smoking, limit alcohol (<2 drinks/week).\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Call to schedule an appointment within 1 week for urgent testosterone review.\\n- Repeat luteal progesterone (next cycle) and check ferritin/iron studies within 2–4 weeks.\\n- If therapy is changed, recheck hormones (estradiol, progesterone, testosterone) in 6–8 weeks.\\n- We’re here to help you interpret symptoms and adjust treatment—please book a visit and bring any questions.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 14, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 42.7, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 414, "status": "borderline", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Mild platelet elevation (400-450). Commonly reactive to iron deficiency, inflammation, or infection.", "recommendation": "Check iron studies and inflammatory markers. Repeat CBC in 4-8 weeks.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 105, "status": "borderline", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol above HRT optimization goal (60-100 pg/mL).", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Consider reducing estrogen dose slightly if no symptoms warrant higher levels.", "referenceRange": "60-100 pg/mL (HRT goal, >40 for bone)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 3.42, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone below HRT therapeutic range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase progesterone dose. Target 8-10 ng/mL for optimal endometrial protection.", "referenceRange": "8-10 ng/mL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 36.3, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 254, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone above HRT optimization goal (75-125 ng/dL).", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Consider reducing testosterone dose. Monitor for androgenic side effects.", "referenceRange": "75-125 ng/dL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.62, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 19, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 15, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 100, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.75, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 3.42, "status": "borderline", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Low Progesterone (Phase Unknown)", "interpretation": "Progesterone is 3.42 ng/mL. Without cycle phase documentation, interpretation is limited. If drawn in luteal phase, this would indicate anovulation - a perimenopause marker.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Document cycle phase for accurate interpretation. If patient is mid-cycle or luteal, this suggests anovulation. Consider symptoms: sleep disruption, anxiety, irregular cycles. PATIENT EDUCATION: We need to know where you are in your menstrual cycle to fully interpret this result. Please note when your last period started.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase; <5 ng/mL suggests anovulation if luteal"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 3.42, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Relative Progesterone Deficiency", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 105 pg/mL with progesterone of only 3.42 ng/mL indicates relative progesterone deficiency. The estrogen is not being adequately opposed by progesterone, which can lead to estrogen dominance symptoms.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Consider progesterone supplementation to balance the E2:P4 ratio. Micronized progesterone 100-200 mg nightly can provide adequate opposition. If patient has symptoms (insomnia, anxiety, heavy bleeding, breast tenderness), progesterone should help. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen level is good, but your progesterone is not keeping up. This imbalance can cause sleep problems, mood changes, and heavy periods. Progesterone supplementation can help restore balance.", "referenceRange": "E2 >100 should have P4 ≥8 for adequate opposition"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 3.42, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Luteal Progesterone Deficiency", "interpretation": "Progesterone of 3.42 ng/mL with estradiol of 105 pg/mL in a woman 45 years old indicates luteal progesterone deficiency. Estrogen production is adequate but progesterone is not keeping pace - a hallmark of perimenopause.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Micronized progesterone 100-200 mg nightly. Dosing can be cyclic (days 14-28) or continuous depending on cycle regularity. Progesterone provides GABAergic support for sleep and anxiety relief. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your body is making enough estrogen, but progesterone has dropped. This is very common in perimenopause and causes sleep disruption, anxiety, and mood changes. Progesterone supplementation supports your nervous system and helps restore calm, restful sleep.", "referenceRange": "P4 ≥5 ng/mL expected with E2 ≥60 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 36.3, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Perimenopause Transition", "interpretation": "FSH of 36.3 mIU/mL indicates perimenopause (menopausal transition). No specific symptoms reported at this time. Elevated FSH reflects decreased ovarian function and reduced estrogen production. This is a normal part of reproductive aging but may benefit from hormone optimization if symptomatic.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: If symptomatic, consider low-dose transdermal estradiol + micronized progesterone (if uterus intact). Perimenopause is characterized by fluctuating hormones. Steady hormone delivery via patch can smooth out peaks and valleys. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your lab results show you are in perimenopause - the transition phase before menopause. Hormone levels can swing widely during this time, causing symptoms. Treatment options are available to help you feel better.", "referenceRange": "FSH <25 mIU/mL premenopausal; ≥25 suggests transition; ≥40 menopausal"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 254, "status": "critical", "category": "Testosterone Pattern C: Supraphysiologic / Pellet Pattern", "interpretation": "Supraphysiologic testosterone pattern identified (Total T: 254 ng/dL (supraphysiologic); SHBG: 52.1 nmol/L; Free T: 36 pg/mL (elevated)). Total testosterone is well above the physiologic range for women. At >150 ng/dL, this requires urgent evaluation. This pattern is commonly seen with testosterone pellet therapy and may initially produce an energy and libido boost, but typically leads to insomnia, heat intolerance, acne, hair shedding, irritability, and anxiety as levels remain elevated.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Do NOT stack testosterone — do not add more testosterone on top of existing supraphysiologic levels. Manage downstream symptoms: consider spironolactone for acne/hair loss, sleep support, anxiety management. If NOT on testosterone therapy: Rule out androgen-secreting tumor (pelvic ultrasound, CT adrenals). If pellet-related: transition to titratable testosterone therapy (transdermal cream/gel) to allow dose adjustments. Target physiologic range (Total T 30-70 ng/dL naturally, or per HRT protocol). Monitor CBC, lipids, liver function. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your testosterone level is much higher than what the body typically needs. This can happen with certain forms of testosterone therapy like pellets. While it may feel good initially, sustained high levels can cause side effects. Your provider may recommend transitioning to a form of testosterone that allows more precise dosing control.", "referenceRange": "Total T >100-150+ (supraphysiologic for women)"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- Key labs: Platelets 414 K/uL; Estradiol (E2) 105 pg/mL; Progesterone (P4) 3.42 ng/mL; Total Testosterone 254 ng/dL; SHBG 52.1 nmol/L; Free T 36 pg/mL; FSH 36.3 mIU/mL.\\n- Synthesized interpretation for staff (for provider review):\\n  * Patient is biochemically in the menopausal transition (FSH 36.3 mIU/mL) with evidence of relative progesterone deficiency (P4 3.42 ng/mL) relative to E2 (105 pg/mL).\\n  * Testosterone is supraphysiologic (Total T 254 ng/dL; Free T elevated) — this is flagged as CRITICAL per clinic protocol and requires immediate provider notification and management.\\n  * Platelet count mildly elevated (414 K/uL) — likely reactive; needs evaluation for iron deficiency/inflammation.\\n  * Cycle timing was not documented — limits interpretation of progesterone. If the draw was expected to be luteal, this P4 indicates luteal deficiency/anovulation.\\n\\nPriority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\n\\nCRITICAL (Immediate)\\n- Notify the supervising provider immediately about the supraphysiologic testosterone (Total T 254 ng/dL, Free T 36 pg/mL).\\n- Do NOT administer or add any further testosterone (no stacking). Hold any scheduled testosterone administrations (injection/patch/next pellet insertion) until provider reviews.\\n- If patient is not on exogenous testosterone, arrange urgent evaluation to rule out an androgen-secreting tumor (see testing/referrals).\\n\\nURGENT (within 1 week)\\n- Provider review and decision on testosterone management:\\n  * If on testosterone therapy (pellet/injection/TD), plan to reduce/hold dose now and discuss transition to titratable formulation if pellet-related.\\n  * If not on therapy, order imaging (pelvic ultrasound ± adrenal imaging) per provider discretion.\\n- Baseline safety labs to order now/within 1 week if not recently performed: CBC, CMP (incl. LFTs), fasting lipids, fasting glucose or A1c.\\n- Arrange appointment for physician follow-up within 1 week to discuss androgen management and plan.\\n\\nROUTINE (timed)\\n- Platelets: Order iron studies (ferritin, iron, TIBC) and an inflammatory marker (CRP or ESR). Repeat CBC in 4–8 weeks.\\n- Progesterone/Estradiol management: document menstrual cycle phase immediately (date of last menstrual period or Day of cycle at time of draw). If patient is luteal-phase eligible, obtain repeat mid-luteal progesterone (7 days post-ovulation or day 21–23 for regular 28‑day cycles) if provider requests.\\n- Re-check hormones per adjustments (details below in \\"Follow-up timing\\").\\n\\nTestosterone-specific recommendations (for provider decision and staff implementation)\\n- Immediate: Stop any further testosterone dosing until provider review. Do NOT add any testosterone on top of current level.\\n- If patient is on transdermal testosterone:\\n  * Consider reducing current daily dose by 50% or holding dosing for several days to weeks depending on level and symptoms; recheck levels 2–4 weeks after change.\\n- If on injectable testosterone:\\n  * Hold next scheduled injection; consult provider regarding interval extension and dose reduction.\\n- If on pellet therapy:\\n  * Do NOT schedule the next pellet insertion. Notify provider urgently — pellets cannot be removed easily; plan to avoid further implants and consider transition to transdermal/gel at a lower starting dose after levels fall.\\n- Target levels:\\n  * Follow clinic HRT optimization target — aim to lower Total T toward the HRT optimization range (per clinic protocol ~75–125 ng/dL). If androgenic adverse effects are present or provider prefers physiologic female range, consider aiming closer to 30–70 ng/dL.\\n- Symptom management while testosterone is elevated:\\n  * For acne/hair changes: provider may consider spironolactone (e.g., 25–100 mg PO daily) after assessment and contraindication review — clinic provider decision required.\\n  * For mood/insomnia/heat intolerance: symptomatic management (sleep hygiene, short-term anxiolytic/sedative strategies per provider).\\n- Monitoring after adjustment:\\n  * Recheck Total T, Free T, SHBG 2–4 weeks after any dose reduction/hold; sooner if severe symptoms.\\n  * Re-check CBC, LFTs, and lipids at 4 weeks after change, then every 3–6 months as clinically indicated.\\n\\nProgesterone and Estradiol management (specific dose options for provider consideration)\\n- Progesterone is low (3.42 ng/mL) with adequate E2 — consider increasing progesterone for endometrial protection and symptom control.\\n- Provider options (choose based on cycle regularity and uterine status):\\n  * Micronized progesterone 100 mg PO nightly as initial dose; can titrate to 200 mg at bedtime if needed for symptom control or endometrial protection.\\n  * Cyclic dosing for women with regular cycles: days 14–28 (or last 14 days of cycle) with 100–200 mg nightly.\\n  * Continuous dosing for women with irregular cycles or on continuous estrogen: 100–200 mg nightly continuously.\\n- Monitoring:\\n  * Recheck serum progesterone 4 weeks after initiating or changing dose, ideally timed mid-luteal if cycling; assess symptom response (sleep, mood, bleeding).\\n  * Re-evaluate E2 6–8 weeks after any estrogen dose change.\\n\\nEstradiol adjustment\\n- E2 is slightly above optimization target (105 pg/mL vs goal 60–100 pg/mL).\\n- If patient is asymptomatic: consider modest reduction in estradiol dose (e.g., decrease transdermal patch dose by one strength or reduce oral dose per provider decision).\\n- If symptomatic improvement on current dose and no adverse signals: may continue and recheck E2 in 6–8 weeks after any change.\\n\\nPlatelets and iron evaluation\\n- Order ferritin, iron, TIBC and CRP immediately to evaluate for iron deficiency or inflammatory/reactive thrombocytosis.\\n- If ferritin <50 ng/mL or iron studies consistent with iron deficiency, start iron replacement per provider protocol (oral ferrous sulfate 325 mg PO once or twice daily or IV iron if indicated) — provider order required.\\n- Repeat CBC in 4–8 weeks after iron correction or if inflammatory marker abnormal.\\n\\nAdditional testing and referrals to consider\\n- If supraphysiologic testosterone and not on exogenous therapy: urgent pelvic ultrasound and adrenal imaging (CT) per provider.\\n- If on testosterone therapy with persistent androgen excess or dermatologic sequelae: consider dermatology referral or endocrinology consult.\\n- If fertility desired or irregular cycles with ovulatory concern: consider reproductive endocrinology referral and timed progesterone testing/ovulation monitoring.\\n- Mental health referral for significant anxiety/insomnia related to hormonal imbalance.\\n- Cardiology referral if lipids abnormal or cardiovascular risk elevated.\\n\\nLifestyle and patient counseling points for staff to communicate (staff-facing bullets to deliver in clinic)\\n- Ask and document last menstrual period and typical cycle length today — critical for hormone interpretation.\\n- Counsel patient to stop any additional testosterone products and to expect provider contact regarding next steps.\\n- Discuss symptoms to report urgently: rapid onset hirsutism, voice changes, marked acne, significant hair thinning, chest pain, severe mood changes.\\n- Lifestyle recommendations to discuss/encourage:\\n  * Sleep hygiene, stress reduction techniques (CBT, mindfulness) — beneficial for progesterone-related insomnia/anxiety.\\n  * Diet: reduce simple sugars, moderate alcohol intake (alcohol can affect estradiol/testosterone metabolism).\\n  * Weight management and regular aerobic/resistance exercise (improve insulin resistance, lipid profile).\\n  * Smoking cessation if applicable (risk management with HRT).\\n  * Iron-rich diet and consider oral iron if ferritin low — only start after provider order.\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (actionable checklist)\\n- Immediate/Today:\\n  1. Notify provider now of supraphysiologic Total T (254 ng/dL) and hold any further testosterone dosing.\\n  2. Document cycle day/last menstrual period in chart.\\n  3. Order iron studies (ferritin, iron, TIBC) and CRP/ESR; order baseline CBC, CMP/LFTs, fasting lipids, fasting glucose/A1c if not recent.\\n\\n- Within 48–72 hours:\\n  1. Provider appointment to determine testosterone plan (hold/decrease/transition) and progesterone initiation.\\n  2. If provider deems necessary, order pelvic ultrasound ± adrenal imaging (if no exogenous T).\\n\\n- 2–4 weeks after any testosterone dose change:\\n  1. Recheck Total T, Free T, SHBG.\\n  2. Recheck CBC and LFTs (and lipids if indicated).\\n\\n- 4 weeks after starting or changing progesterone:\\n  1. Reassess symptoms and re-measure progesterone (timed mid-luteal if cycling), adjust dose (100 → 200 mg nightly) per provider.\\n\\n- 6–8 weeks after any estradiol dose change:\\n  1. Recheck E2 level to confirm optimization.\\n\\n- 4–8 weeks for platelet follow-up:\\n  1. Repeat CBC; if platelets remain elevated, escalate to provider for further workup.\\n\\nPhysician notification requirements (explicit)\\n- Immediate: testosterone Total T >150 ng/dL (here 254 ng/dL) — provider must be notified now.\\n- Prompt: decision required on holding/adjusting testosterone, ordering imaging if not on exogenous therapy, and starting progesterone.\\n- Notify provider of ferritin/iron deficiency results or persistently rising platelets.\\n\\nNotes for providers/staff\\n- Clarify which testosterone formulation the patient is using (pellet vs transdermal vs injection) — this dictates immediate management steps.\\n- If initiating progesterone, counsel regarding sedation at bedtime and bleeding pattern changes; advise timing relative to cycles.\\n- Document all patient communications and instructions in the chart.\\n\\nIf you want, I can draft orders for: (A) immediate lab panel (CBC, ferritin, iron/TIBC, CRP, CMP/LFTs, fasting lipids, fasting glucose), (B) hormone repeats (Total T, Free T, SHBG, E2, P4 timed), and (C) imaging (pelvic ultrasound ± adrenal CT) for provider signature.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "SHBG", "value": 52.1, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 15, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Lab pattern consistent with relative estrogen excess or impaired estrogen clearance. Even without reported symptoms, supporting liver detoxification and estrogen metabolism pathways may be beneficial.", "supportingFindings": ["Estradiol:Progesterone imbalance (E2 105, P4 3.42)"]}, {"name": "Menopausal Transition", "confidence": "low", "description": "Clinical picture consistent with perimenopause or menopause. Hormonal shifts during this transition affect bone health, cardiovascular risk, mood, and quality of life.", "supportingFindings": ["Age 45 (perimenopause window)", "FSH 36.3 mIU/mL (elevated, menopausal range)"]}]}	2026-03-10 16:09:26.110776	\N
