er detoxification support with NAC, green tea extract, watercress, and B-vitamins. Designed for metabolic clearance support, biotransformation of hormones and environmental compounds, and hepatic stress. Appropriate when liver markers are borderline, estrogen clearance is sluggish, or metabolic dysfunction is present.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance pattern (hepatic detox pathways needed for clearance); Low energy (biotransformation burden); Brain fog (toxin clearance burden)", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement supports your liver's natural detoxification processes. Your results suggest your body could benefit from help processing hormones, metabolic byproducts, and other compounds. It provides nutrients that support both phases of your liver's detox pathways.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance pattern (hepatic detox pathways needed for clearance)", "Low energy (biotransformation burden)", "Brain fog (toxin clearance burden)"]}, {"dose": "1 soft chew during times of stress", "name": "HerWellness™ Rapid Stress Relief", "caution": "Fast-acting L-Theanine and Lactium formula. Non-drowsy. Promotes calm within 1 hour. Contains milk.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "low", "rationale": "HerWellness Rapid Stress Relief provides fast-acting stress support with L-Theanine (200mg) and Lactium. Promotes calm within 1 hour without drowsiness.", "indication": "Irritability reported", "phenotypes": ["Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation"], "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This fast-acting supplement helps your body respond to stress more calmly. It promotes relaxation within about an hour and can be taken as needed without causing drowsiness.", "supportingFindings": ["Irritability reported"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 11, "apoBValue": 121, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "borderline", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 0.9429414159677914, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": true}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "strongly_indicated", "indicated": true, "rationale": "LDL-C ≥190 mg/dL (204 mg/dL). High-intensity statin therapy strongly indicated per ACC/AHA guidelines. Consider familial hypercholesterolemia evaluation."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "consider", "rationale": "Lipid levels meet threshold for CAC consideration. CAC may be useful if risk level is uncertain or patient is hesitant about statin.", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\n- Your labs show several strengths (healthy blood sugar, good iron stores, normal thyroid) but also important areas to address: very high LDL cholesterol (204 mg/dL), low vitamin D (23.1 ng/mL), and elevated testosterone (Total 82.9 ng/dL; Free 8 pg/mL) with low progesterone (1 ng/mL) suggesting the start of perimenopause. These findings are manageable with a combination of lifestyle changes and follow-up with your clinician.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Hemoglobin 15.1 g/dL, Ferritin 97.7 ng/mL — iron stores look good for a woman.\\n- TSH 0.96 mIU/L — thyroid function is normal.\\n- Hemoglobin A1c 5.1% and Triglycerides 92 mg/dL — good blood sugar and triglyceride control.\\n- Lp(a) 11 mg/dL and hs-CRP 0.4 mg/L — low inflammation and favorable lipoprotein(a).\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + concrete actions)\\n1. LDL cholesterol 204 mg/dL (very high). Goal: ≥50% LDL reduction; target <100 mg/dL.\\n   - Start heart-healthy habits now: add 25–30 g fiber daily (oatmeal for breakfast, beans at lunch, vegetables at dinner).\\n   - Include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n   - Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter and high-fat cheeses.\\n   - Daily exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking (or equivalent).\\n   - Because LDL ≥190 mg/dL, guideline-based care typically recommends discussing a high-intensity statin (Atorvastatin 40–80 mg or Rosuvastatin 20–40 mg) with your provider—please review this as soon as possible.\\n\\n2. Vitamin D 25-OH: 23.1 ng/mL (insufficient).\\n   - Start vitamin D3 2,000 IU daily and increase dietary calcium (aim 1,000–1,200 mg/day through dairy or fortified alternatives).\\n   - Add weight-bearing exercise (walking, stairs, light resistance) 3x/week.\\n   - Recheck 25-OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks.\\n\\n3. Hormones / Perimenopause signals: Progesterone 1 ng/mL, Estradiol 119 pg/mL, FSH 10.5 mIU/mL — consistent with early perimenopause and luteal progesterone deficiency if sample was in luteal phase.\\n   - Track your cycle and note exact day of blood draw; if uncertain, repeat mid-luteal progesterone (about 7 days before expected period) or discuss cycle-targeted testing.\\n   - Lifestyle to help androgen symptoms (Total T 82.9 ng/dL, Free T 8 pg/mL): prioritize 7–8 hours sleep, strength training 2–3x/week, stress reduction (meditation, walking), and skin care for acne/hair concerns. Consider referral to gynecology/endocrinology to discuss progesterone options and evaluation for causes of high testosterone.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Schedule a visit within 2–4 weeks to discuss starting a statin and hormone management. Recheck fasting lipid panel 6–12 weeks after any medication change or intensive lifestyle efforts. Repeat vitamin D in 8–12 weeks. If hematocrit 46.9% persists, recheck after good hydration. If symptoms (irregular cycles, excess hair growth, acne) are bothersome, ask for gyne/endo referral. You’re doing well in several areas — these focused steps can make a big difference.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 15.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 46.9, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Slightly elevated hematocrit - may indicate dehydration.", "recommendation": "Encourage hydration. Repeat if persistent.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 249, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 119, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 10.5, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 82.9, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Elevated testosterone for females.", "recommendation": "Evaluate for PCOS, adrenal hyperplasia, or androgen-secreting tumor.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 0.96, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 97.7, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 23.1, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D deficiency (21-30 ng/mL). Suboptimal for hormone and bone health.", "recommendation": "Repletion: Metagenics D3 5,000 + K daily. Target ≥60 ng/mL. Recheck in 8-12 weeks.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 645, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 204, "status": "abnormal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Very high LDL - high cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Statin therapy recommended. Intensive lifestyle changes.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 51, "status": "borderline", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "HDL could be higher for optimal protection.", "recommendation": "Increase exercise. Moderate alcohol if appropriate.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 92, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 1.2689097661395692, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 1.3% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years\\n\\nSEVERE HYPERCHOLESTEROLEMIA: High-intensity statin therapy indicated (LDL >=190 mg/dL)", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.9429414159677914, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 0.9%", "recommendation": "HIGH-INTENSITY statin (Atorvastatin 40-80 mg or Rosuvastatin 20-40 mg) - LDL >=190 mg/dL", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.4265717611486817, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.4%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 10.105022636664025, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 10.1%, ASCVD 6.9%, Heart Failure 4.2%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 19, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 15, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 82, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 121, "status": "borderline", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Borderline elevated Apo B.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider pharmacotherapy if high ASCVD risk.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 11, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 331, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 89, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 7.32, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 0.4, "status": "normal", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Low cardiovascular inflammation risk.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1, "status": "borderline", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Low Progesterone (Phase Unknown)", "interpretation": "Progesterone is 1 ng/mL. Without cycle phase documentation, interpretation is limited. If drawn in luteal phase, this would indicate anovulation - a perimenopause marker.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Document cycle phase for accurate interpretation. If patient is mid-cycle or luteal, this suggests anovulation. Consider symptoms: sleep disruption, anxiety, irregular cycles. PATIENT EDUCATION: We need to know where you are in your menstrual cycle to fully interpret this result. Please note when your last period started.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase; <5 ng/mL suggests anovulation if luteal"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Relative Progesterone Deficiency", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 119 pg/mL with progesterone of only 1 ng/mL indicates relative progesterone deficiency. The estrogen is not being adequately opposed by progesterone, which can lead to estrogen dominance symptoms.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Consider progesterone supplementation to balance the E2:P4 ratio. Micronized progesterone 100-200 mg nightly can provide adequate opposition. If patient has symptoms (insomnia, anxiety, heavy bleeding, breast tenderness), progesterone should help. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen level is good, but your progesterone is not keeping up. This imbalance can cause sleep problems, mood changes, and heavy periods. Progesterone supplementation can help restore balance.", "referenceRange": "E2 >100 should have P4 ≥8 for adequate opposition"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Luteal Progesterone Deficiency", "interpretation": "Progesterone of 1 ng/mL with estradiol of 119 pg/mL in a woman 41 years old indicates luteal progesterone deficiency. Estrogen production is adequate but progesterone is not keeping pace - a hallmark of perimenopause.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Micronized progesterone 100-200 mg nightly. Dosing can be cyclic (days 14-28) or continuous depending on cycle regularity. Progesterone provides GABAergic support for sleep and anxiety relief. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your body is making enough estrogen, but progesterone has dropped. This is very common in perimenopause and causes sleep disruption, anxiety, and mood changes. Progesterone supplementation supports your nervous system and helps restore calm, restful sleep.", "referenceRange": "P4 ≥5 ng/mL expected with E2 ≥60 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 10.5, "status": "borderline", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Early Perimenopause", "interpretation": "FSH of 10.5 mIU/mL with progesterone of 1 ng/mL in a 41-year-old indicates early perimenopause. The brain is starting to signal harder (rising FSH) because ovarian response is becoming unpredictable. This is the beginning of the transition.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Progesterone is foundational at this stage: micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly for sleep and nervous system support. Begin the hormone continuity discussion - frame HRT as future-forward planning, not crisis intervention. Nervous system calming messaging matters. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your body is entering the perimenopause transition. Your brain is working a little harder to communicate with your ovaries, and ovulation is becoming less consistent. This can cause sleep changes, anxiety, and cycle irregularities. Progesterone support now can smooth this transition and protect your quality of life.", "referenceRange": "FSH 8-15 with P4 <5 indicates early transition"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 84, "status": "normal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 84 nmol/L is within normal range. SHBG regulates bioavailable sex hormones by binding testosterone and estradiol.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 8, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 8 pg/mL is elevated. May cause androgenic symptoms (acne, hirsutism, hair thinning).", "recommendation": "Evaluate for PCOS, exogenous testosterone, or low SHBG driving elevated free T.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- Key red flag: LDL 204 mg/dL — qualifies for FLAG to physician for consideration of immediate high-intensity statin therapy (primary prevention threshold ≥190 mg/dL).\\n- Concomitant cardiovascular risk markers: ApoB 121 mg/dL (borderline). HDL 51 mg/dL (borderline).\\n- Reproductive/endocrine: Total testosterone 82.9 ng/dL and free testosterone 8 pg/mL — both elevated for a female; concern for hyperandrogenism (PCOS, exogenous androgen, adrenal cause, or androgen-secreting tumor). FSH 10.5 mIU/mL, estradiol 119 pg/mL and progesterone 1 ng/mL in a 41 y/o — pattern consistent with early perimenopausal transition with luteal progesterone deficiency (relative estrogen excess).\\n- Vitamin D deficiency: 25-OH D 23.1 ng/mL — requires repletion.\\n- Hematocrit 46.9% borderline high — likely dehydration but repeat recommended.\\n- No abnormalities reported for hemoglobin, ferritin, B12, A1c, renal, hepatic markers (AST/ALT), hs-CRP; sleep apnea risk and 10-year risk calculators reportedly normal.\\n\\nPriority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL (Physician notification required immediately)\\n- LDL 204 mg/dL: Flag provider urgently for initiation of high-intensity statin therapy and cardiovascular risk discussion. Do not delay review.\\n  * Suggested initial options for provider consideration: atorvastatin 40–80 mg PO nightly OR rosuvastatin 20–40 mg PO nightly (start atorvastatin 40 mg is reasonable; up-titrate as needed).\\n  * Before initiation: confirm pregnancy status in women of childbearing potential (urine or serum hCG). Statins are contraindicated in pregnancy — discuss contraception if applicable.\\n  * Baseline labs prior to statin: ALT/AST (already normal per results), consider baseline CK if high risk or muscle symptoms history.\\n  * Follow-up lipid panel and ALT 6–12 weeks after statin start, then every 3–12 months per provider preference.\\n\\n- Elevated total + free testosterone: Flag provider for expedited endocrine/gyne evaluation.\\n  * If any signs of rapid/virilizing changes (voice deepening, clitoromegaly, rapid hirsutism), escalate urgently and consider imaging (pelvic ultrasound +/- CT/MRI abdomen-pelvis) and endocrine consult.\\n  * If patient is on exogenous testosterone, instruct staff to STOP the exogenous androgen immediately and notify provider.\\n\\nURGENT (to be actioned within 1–2 weeks)\\n- Start vitamin D repletion per protocol: Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily (or equivalent cholecalciferol regimen). Target 25-OH D ≥60 ng/mL. Recheck level 8–12 weeks after initiation.\\n- Progesterone support for perimenopausal symptoms and luteal deficiency (provider review required):\\n  * Provider recommendation options: micronized progesterone 100–200 mg PO at bedtime. Dosing strategy:\\n    - If cycles are regular and ovulatory: cyclic administration days 14–28 (or 12–14 days per cycle) to restore luteal support.\\n    - If cycles irregular/amenorrheic: nightly (continuous) dosing 100 mg at bedtime may be used for symptomatic relief; consider 200 mg at bedtime if inadequate response and as provider determines.\\n  * Counsel patient regarding sedation/somnolence as expected benefit. Provider to review contraindications (history of hormone-sensitive cancer, thromboembolic disease per clinic protocol).\\n  * Reassess symptoms 6–8 weeks after starting progesterone. Consider mid-luteal progesterone measurement (timed 7 days before expected menses) if objective ovulation confirmation is needed.\\n\\nROUTINE (to be actioned within 4–12 weeks)\\n- Hematocrit 46.9%: Advise hydration and repeat CBC in 2–4 weeks; if persistent or rising, investigate for secondary causes (smoking, OSA, EPO use, hemoconcentration) and notify provider.\\n- Lifestyle counseling: arrange diet/exercise counseling and consider referral to nutritionist or lifestyle coach (see Lifestyle interventions below).\\n- Document cycle day/phase at time of hormone draw in chart and ask patient to report last menstrual period dates for accurate interpretation and future testing timing.\\n\\nTestosterone-specific recommendations (staff-facing)\\n- Do NOT initiate testosterone therapy. Patient already has elevated total and free testosterone.\\n- If patient reports current exogenous androgen use:\\n  * Immediately stop exogenous testosterone and notify provider.\\n  * Repeat morning fasting total testosterone and free testosterone 4–6 weeks after cessation to allow clearance; sooner if severe virilization.\\n- Diagnostic workup to order/consider (discuss with provider):\\n  * Morning (0800–1000), fasting total testosterone repeated and free testosterone, ideally in early follicular phase (cycle day 2–7) if cycling.\\n  * DHEA-S, androstenedione, 17-hydroxyprogesterone, cortisol as indicated.\\n  * SHBG already reported normal — include in repeat panel as needed.\\n  * Pelvic ultrasound to assess ovaries (if PCOS suspected) and consider adrenal imaging (CT/MRI) if DHEA-S markedly elevated or rapid virilization.\\n- Therapeutic options for symptomatic hyperandrogenism (provider decision):\\n  * First-line medical management typically addressed after etiologic workup. For symptom control, the provider may consider combined OCPs or anti-androgens (e.g., spironolactone 50–100 mg PO daily) once pregnancy is ruled out and appropriate monitoring in place. Staff should not initiate without provider order.\\n\\nLifestyle interventions (specific, staff to counsel/implement)\\n- Cardiovascular risk reduction (immediate):\\n  * Diet: Mediterranean or portfolio diet focus — reduce saturated fat (aim <7% of calories), increase soluble fiber, nuts, plant sterols.\\n  * Exercise: Encourage ≥150 min/week moderate aerobic activity + 2 strength sessions/week.\\n  * Weight: Aim for 5–10% weight loss if BMI elevated — arrange nutrition referral.\\n  * Smoking cessation and alcohol moderation (limit alcohol; avoid binge consumption).\\n- Androgen management:\\n  * Weight optimization and exercise can reduce hyperandrogenic symptoms in PCOS-like physiology.\\n  * Hair care/referral dermatology for hirsutism/acne as needed.\\n- Vitamin D:\\n  * Encourage safe sun exposure and adherence to supplementation. Review calcium intake for bone health as needed.\\n- Sleep and hydration:\\n  * Address sleep hygiene (important with progesterone initiation) and advise adequate hydration to address borderline hematocrit.\\n\\nAdditional testing and referrals to consider\\n- Immediate:\\n  * Urine/serum pregnancy test prior to initiating statin or anti-androgen therapy in women of childbearing potential.\\n- Within 2–6 weeks:\\n  * Repeat fasting morning total/free testosterone (early follicular day 2–7 if cycling) to confirm elevation.\\n  * DHEA-S, androstenedione, 17-OH progesterone (screen for adrenal causes), AM cortisol if indicated.\\n  * Consider pelvic ultrasound to assess ovarian morphology if PCOS suspected.\\n- If rapid virilization or concerning labs:\\n  * Urgent endocrine referral and cross-sectional imaging (adrenal and pelvic) as directed by provider.\\n- Cardiology or lipid specialist referral if:\\n  * Persistent LDL >190 despite lifestyle and statin intolerance, or complex lipid profile requiring advanced therapy (PCSK9 inhibitors) — provider to consider.\\n- Other:\\n  * Repeat CBC for hematocrit in 2–4 weeks.\\n  * Recheck vitamin D in 8–12 weeks after supplementation.\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (action items for staff)\\n1. Immediate (Today)\\n   - Flag provider urgently for LDL 204 mg/dL and elevated testosterone results (total and free). Mark chart for prompt review.\\n   - If patient is of childbearing potential, obtain pregnancy test before any statin or anti-androgen orders and document contraception counseling.\\n   - Stop any reported exogenous testosterone and notify provider immediately.\\n\\n2. Within 1 week\\n   - Provider to decide on initiating high-intensity statin (suggest atorvastatin 40 mg nightly or rosuvastatin 20 mg nightly) and order baseline labs as per protocol.\\n   - Start vitamin D 5,000 IU D3 + K daily per protocol and schedule recheck in 8–12 weeks.\\n   - Document menstrual cycle day; if unknown, instruct patient to report last menstrual period date.\\n\\n3. Within 2–4 weeks\\n   - Repeat CBC for hematocrit; repeat morning fasting testosterone if provider orders (ideally day 2–7 of cycle).\\n   - If progesterone therapy to be started, provider to prescribe micronized progesterone 100–200 mg PO qHS; staff to counsel on dosing schedule (cyclic vs continuous) and expected effects; schedule symptom check at 6–8 weeks.\\n\\n4. At 6–12 weeks\\n   - Reassess lipids and ALT after statin initiation.\\n   - Recheck vitamin D level at 8–12 weeks.\\n   - Clinical follow-up for progesterone response at 6–8 weeks; consider repeating luteal progesterone if assessing ovulation.\\n\\n5. Ongoing\\n   - Coordinate referrals: Endocrinology/gynecology if hyperandrogenism workup required; nutrition for lipid/weight management; dermatology if acne/hirsutism refractory.\\n   - Document all counseling (statin risks/benefits, pregnancy risks, vitamin D, progesterone side effects, lifestyle advice).\\n\\nClinician safety/monitoring notes for staff\\n- Statin counseling: contraindicated in pregnancy; check and document pregnancy test; monitor LFTs per protocol; inform patient of possible myalgias — check CK only if symptomatic.\\n- Progesterone counseling: sedation likely — advise caution driving initially; review contraindications; discuss bleeding irregularities early in therapy.\\n- Hyperandrogenism: expedite evaluation if rapid virilization; do not initiate anti-androgen therapy without provider order and pregnancy exclusion.\\n\\nIf you want, I can draft specific order sets for:\\n- High-intensity statin initiation (medication, baseline labs, follow-up labs),\\n- Hormonal follow-up panel (timed testosterone, DHEA-S, 17-OHP, repeat progesterone for luteal confirmation),\\n- Imaging referrals (pelvic ultrasound, adrenal imaging) for provider signature.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 1.8, "detail": "Triglycerides 92 / HDL 51 = 1.8", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5.1%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 84, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 15, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "121 mg/dL", "detail": "Elevated atherogenic particle count", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "0.4 mg/L", "detail": "Low inflammatory burden", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 1, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (1/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Lab pattern consistent with relative estrogen excess or impaired estrogen clearance. Even without reported symptoms, supporting liver detoxification and estrogen metabolism pathways may be beneficial.", "supportingFindings": ["Estradiol:Progesterone imbalance (E2 119, P4 1)", "Progesterone 1 ng/mL (very low)", "Irritability reported"]}, {"name": "Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Pattern consistent with HPA-axis stress and cortisol dysregulation. Chronic stress depletes DHEA-S, disrupts sleep, and impairs cognitive and emotional function.", "supportingFindings": ["Irritability reported", "Low energy / fatigue reported", "Brain fog reported", "Vitamin D 23.1 ng/mL (low, stress resilience impact)"]}, {"name": "Menopausal Transition", "confidence": "low", "description": "Clinical picture consistent with perimenopause or menopause. Hormonal shifts during this transition affect bone health, cardiovascular risk, mood, and quality of life.", "supportingFindings": ["Age 41 (perimenopause window)", "FSH 10.5 mIU/mL (rising, perimenopause range)", "Irritability reported"]}]}	2026-03-10 18:43:24.078007	\N
48	Missy Maranto	female	2026-03-12 00:00:00	{"fsh": 22.3, "acne": false, "shbg": 75.3, "onHRT": true, "anxiety": true, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "hairLoss": false, "estradiol": 10, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": false, "lowLibido": false, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2026-03-12", "moodChanges": true, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Missy Maranto", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 45, "bmi": 23.9, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 130, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 2.98, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 13.8, "lowMotivation": false, "menstrualPhase": "follicular", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "freeTestosterone": 1}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History of present illness: Patient is a 45-year-old female presenting for review of recent hormone laboratory results and management of perimenopausal symptoms. She reports symptoms consistent with hypoestrogenia including intermittent hot flashes, irregular menstrual bleeding pattern over recent months, worsening sleep fragmentation, and decreased libido. No acute chest pain, shortness of breath, focal neurologic symptoms, or signs of thrombosis reported. No history of active liver disease or prior venous thromboembolism documented in chart today (confirm in chart review).\\n- Gynecologic/reproductive: Perimenopausal symptoms as above. Unknown current HRT regimen and last dose timing in chart — staff to document prior to any dose changes. Uterine status: not documented in chart today — confirm intact uterus prior to estrogen adjustments.\\n- Allergies: None documented in chart today.\\n- Medications: Not documented in chart today — verify current systemic or topical hormone therapies and other meds prior to any changes.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: None recorded in chart at time of encounter (BP, HR, weight/BMI not available; to be recorded at next visit).\\n- Risk scores: STOP-BANG for OSA = 0 (low risk).\\n- Hormone laboratory results (most recent):\\n  - Estradiol (E2): 10 pg/mL — ABNORMAL. (Clinic bone-protection threshold <40 pg/mL.)\\n  - FSH: 22.3 mIU/mL — BORDERLINE/ELEVATED.\\n  - Progesterone: 2.98 ng/mL — ABNORMAL (below clinic HRT therapeutic target when being used for endometrial protection).\\n  - Total Testosterone: 13.8 ng/dL — ABNORMAL (below clinic target for symptomatic benefit).\\n  - SHBG: 75.3 nmol/L — within reported normal range.\\n  - Free Testosterone: 1 pg/mL — within reported normal range.\\n- Other labs: CBC, CMP, LFTs, lipid panel, vitamin D, AMH, and DEXA results not available in this report — order as indicated below.\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1) Hypoestrogenic perimenopausal state (\\"crash phase\\"): Estradiol 10 pg/mL with FSH 22.3 mIU/mL consistent with markedly reduced ovarian estrogen production. Estradiol at this level is below bone-protective threshold and likely contributing to patient symptoms (hot flashes, sleep disturbance) and increased longer-term bone loss risk.\\n2) Inadequate progestogen exposure for endometrial protection: Progesterone 2.98 ng/mL is below clinic therapeutic target for progestin protection when uterus is intact; risk of endometrial stimulation if estrogen is increased without adequate progestogen.\\n3) Low total testosterone: Total T 13.8 ng/dL — below clinic symptomatic target; may contribute to low libido, low energy. SHBG and free T reported as normal but total T remains low.\\n4) Elevated FSH / diminished ovarian reserve: FSH 22.3 mIU/mL may indicate declining ovarian reserve consistent with perimenopause.\\n5) Bone health risk: Estradiol <40 pg/mL poses increased risk of accelerated bone resorption; consider bone density assessment if not recent.\\n6) Safety/administrative: Current HRT regimen, last dose timing, pregnancy status (if relevant) and contraindications (VTE, active liver disease, uncontrolled HTN) are not documented in chart today and must be verified prior to any medication changes.\\nRed flags requiring immediate physician review: decision to increase estrogen and progesterone doses (provider sign-off required). Contraindications to estrogen/progestogen must be ruled out prior to escalation; stop and notify provider for any new chest pain, neurologic deficit, or signs of VTE after dose change.\\n\\nPLAN:\\nGeneral:\\n- Results reviewed with patient. Discussed that labs show a hypoestrogenic perimenopausal pattern with estradiol 10 pg/mL and FSH 22.3 mIU/mL, inadequate progesterone level for endometrial protection, and low total testosterone. Discussed risks/benefits of treatment escalation, need for provider authorization, and safety monitoring. Patient verbalized understanding.\\n\\nImmediate actions (require provider sign-off prior to dispensing/titration):\\n1) Verify baseline information before any changes:\\n   - Document current HRT regimen (formulation, dose, route, exact last dose timing).\\n   - Confirm uterine status (intact vs hysterectomy).\\n   - Confirm pregnancy status if of childbearing potential.\\n   - Review contraindications to estrogen/progestogen/testosterone (personal/family VTE history, stroke, CHD, uncontrolled HTN, active liver disease, active breast cancer).\\n   - Obtain baseline CBC, CMP (including LFTs), and lipid panel if not recently available.\\n\\n2) Estradiol management (provider to authorize):\\n   - Goal: clinic target estradiol 60–100 pg/mL.\\n   - If patient is currently on transdermal 0.025 mg (25 mcg) patch → increase to transdermal 0.05 mg (50 mcg) patch.\\n   - If currently on 0.05 mg and still hypoestrogenic → consider 0.1 mg (100 mcg) patch.\\n   - If using transdermal gel, document product and dose; consider equivalent increase or switch to patch per provider preference.\\n   - Timing for recheck: recheck serum estradiol 4 weeks after dose change. Document last patch change and time of blood draw (for patches, standard clinic draw at trough 24 hours after patch change unless otherwise specified).\\n   - Provider to authorize escalation and confirm progestogen plan if uterus intact.\\n\\n3) Progesterone (endometrial protection) (provider to authorize):\\n   - If patient is on micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly or 100 mg x14 days/month → increase to 200 mg nightly (either cyclic 12–14 days/month for cyclic regimen or continuous 200 mg nightly if a continuous combined regimen is chosen).\\n   - Note: serum progesterone is timing-dependent — for therapeutic monitoring, draw level ~3–4 hours after an oral bedtime dose to approximate peak. Recheck progesterone 4 weeks after dosing change with timing documented.\\n   - If patient declines or has contraindication to micronized progesterone, discuss alternative progestogen options with provider.\\n\\n4) Testosterone management (provider to authorize if symptomatic and after review of contraindications):\\n   - If patient is already on testosterone: increase current dose by 25–50% as an initial titration; document current formulation and dose prior to change.\\n   - If initiating testosterone: provider consideration options:\\n     - IM: testosterone cypionate/enanthate 25–50 mg every 10–14 days (or 20–40 mg weekly), titratable to effect.\\n     - Transdermal: increase topical dose by approximately 25–50% per clinic product equivalence (document product).\\n   - Monitoring: recheck total testosterone, free T, SHBG, CBC (Hgb/Hct), LFTs 6–8 weeks after initiation/titration. Notify provider if total testosterone >150 ng/dL or if virilizing signs develop (voice change, clitoromegaly, severe acne, rapid hair growth).\\n\\nSafety monitoring and labs:\\n- Recheck schedule:\\n  - Estradiol and progesterone: 4 weeks after dose adjustments (document exact timing of last dose relative to blood draw).\\n  - Testosterone panel (total T, free T, SHBG), CBC, CMP/LFTs: 6–8 weeks after testosterone initiation/titration.\\n- Baseline labs if not available: pregnancy test (if applicable), CBC, CMP including LFTs, lipid panel, vitamin D level, TSH/free T4.\\n- Bone health: order DEXA if no DEXA within past 2 years or if fracture risk factors present.\\n- Consider AMH if fertility preservation or further ovarian reserve assessment desired.\\n\\nLifestyle and adjunctive measures:\\n- Bone health: recommend weight-bearing and resistance exercise, smoking cessation, limit alcohol to ≤1 drink/day. Ensure adequate calcium intake and vitamin D supplementation per clinic standards (check vitamin D level and supplement as indicated).\\n- Sleep: counsel on sleep hygiene; discuss that micronized progesterone can be sedating—advise bedtime dosing.\\n- Topical hormone safety education: if using transdermal hormones, advise on application sites, avoid skin-to-skin transfer, wash hands after application.\\n- Menstrual bleeding counseling: counsel that changes in HRT can alter bleeding patterns; instruct patient to report heavy bleeding or new severe pelvic pain. Consider endometrial evaluation only for abnormal bleeding after changes.\\n\\nFollow-up and timeline:\\n- Provider to review and authorize estrogen and progesterone dose changes today (03/19/2026).\\n- Implement dose changes within 1–2 weeks after provider approval and after verifying contraindications and current dosing/timing documentation.\\n- Recheck estradiol and progesterone 4 weeks after changes (ensure blood draw timed relative to last dose: estradiol trough for patch ~24 hours after change; progesterone ~3–4 hours after oral dose).\\n- Recheck testosterone panel and safety labs 6–8 weeks after testosterone change.\\n- Arrange DEXA within 4–8 weeks if indicated.\\n- Schedule clinician follow-up to review repeat lab results and symptoms at 4–8 weeks post-adjustment.\\n- Provider notification criteria: any contraindications identified, estradiol remains <20 pg/mL after two appropriate escalations, total T >150 ng/dL or virilization, new chest pain/neurologic deficits/shortness of breath or suspected VTE, significant abnormal LFTs or Hct rise >5% or Hct >52%.\\n\\nOrders placed / to be placed:\\n- Provider to sign orders for:\\n  - Estradiol/progesterone/testosterone dose changes as above (provider directive).\\n  - Labs: Estradiol, progesterone, total testosterone, free T, SHBG, CBC, CMP (incl LFTs), lipid panel, pregnancy test if applicable, vitamin D, TSH/free T4 as indicated.\\n  - DEXA if not recent.\\n  - AMH if fertility assessment desired.\\n\\nDocumentation notes:\\n- Staff to document current HRT regimen, last dose timing, uterine status, and pregnancy status prior to lab-based dose changes.\\n- All hormone dose changes require prescribing provider authorization and individualized risk assessment.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\nDate: 03/19/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 soft chew during times of stress", "name": "HerWellness™ Rapid Stress Relief", "caution": "Fast-acting L-Theanine and Lactium formula. Non-drowsy. Promotes calm within 1 hour. Contains milk.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "low", "rationale": "HerWellness Rapid Stress Relief provides fast-acting stress support with L-Theanine (200mg) and Lactium. Promotes calm within 1 hour without drowsiness.", "indication": "Anxiety reported; Mood changes reported", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This fast-acting supplement helps your body respond to stress more calmly. It promotes relaxation within about an hour and can be taken as needed without causing drowsiness.", "supportingFindings": ["Anxiety reported", "Mood changes reported"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\nYour hormones show a hypoestrogenic perimenopause pattern — an “ovarian crash” phase. Key numbers: estradiol 10 pg/mL and FSH 22.3 mIU/mL. Estradiol at 10 pg/mL is well below the bone-protection threshold (<40 pg/mL), which raises risk for increased bone breakdown. Progesterone is 2.98 ng/mL (below typical HRT therapeutic range), and total testosterone is low at 13.8 ng/dL despite current HRT. SHBG is 75.3 nmol/L (within normal range) and free testosterone is 1 pg/mL (within functional range). STOP-BANG sleep apnea score 0 points (low risk).\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- SHBG: 75.3 nmol/L — within normal limits, which helps regulate hormone availability.\\n- Free testosterone: 1 pg/mL — currently within a functional range.\\n- STOP-BANG: 0/8 — low risk for obstructive sleep apnea.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + actionable steps)\\n- Estradiol 10 pg/mL (target to reduce bone loss is >40–60 pg/mL):\\n  - Discuss adjusting estrogen therapy with your clinician soon — restoring estradiol above ~40 pg/mL helps protect bone.\\n  - Start bone-support actions now: weight-bearing aerobic activity 30 minutes most days (brisk walking), plus strength training 2–3x weekly.\\n  - Take calcium 1,000–1,200 mg/day via diet/supplements, and vitamin D 1,000–2,000 IU/day (check level).\\n  - Limit alcohol to <2 drinks/week and avoid smoking.\\n- Progesterone 2.98 ng/mL (below HRT therapeutic range):\\n  - Review timing/dose/form of progesterone with provider; consider adjusting HRT regimen.\\n- Total testosterone 13.8 ng/dL (low despite HRT):\\n  - Prioritize 7–8 hours quality sleep nightly.\\n  - Continue strength training 2–3x weekly to boost muscle and support testosterone function.\\n  - Manage stress with daily practices (10–20 minutes meditation, yoga, or brisk walking).\\n  - Ensure adequate protein each meal to support muscle (20–30 g/meal).\\n\\nAdditional female-specific checks to order\\n- Ferritin and CBC to screen for iron deficiency.\\n- TSH and free T4 to check thyroid function.\\n- 25-OH vitamin D.\\n- DEXA bone density scan given low estradiol (bone protection concern).\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Contact your provider within 2–4 weeks to review results and discuss HRT adjustments.\\n- If HRT is changed, recheck estradiol, FSH, progesterone, and total testosterone in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Arrange DEXA within 6–12 months and order ferritin/TSH/Vit D in the next 4–6 weeks.\\nYou’re taking important steps by reviewing these numbers — we can adjust therapy and lifestyle to protect your bones, hormones, and overall health.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 10, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol below bone protection threshold (<40 pg/mL). Bone resorption increases below 40-60 pg/mL.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase estrogen dose. Target 60-100 pg/mL. Current level inadequate for bone protection.", "referenceRange": "60-100 pg/mL (HRT goal, >40 for bone)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 2.98, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone below HRT therapeutic range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase progesterone dose. Target 8-10 ng/mL for optimal endometrial protection.", "referenceRange": "8-10 ng/mL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 22.3, "status": "borderline", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "Elevated FSH may indicate diminished ovarian reserve.", "recommendation": "Consider AMH testing if fertility is a concern.", "referenceRange": "3-10 mIU/mL (follicular phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 13.8, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Low testosterone despite HRT.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase testosterone dose. Target 75-125 ng/dL for optimal results.", "referenceRange": "75-125 ng/dL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 10, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Hypoestrogenic Pattern", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 10 pg/mL with FSH of 22.3 mIU/mL indicates hypoestrogenic perimenopause - the \\"crash phase.\\" Estrogen production has dropped significantly and the brain is signaling strongly (elevated FSH) trying to stimulate the ovaries.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Transdermal estradiol 0.025-0.05 mg is appropriate replacement therapy. Add micronized progesterone for uterine protection and sleep support. Frame this as hormone replacement, not stimulation - we are restoring what the body needs. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen has dropped to a level that can cause symptoms like hot flashes, night sweats, brain fog, and mood changes. Replacing estrogen with a patch or gel can relieve these symptoms and protect your long-term health. Progesterone is added to protect your uterus and help with sleep.", "referenceRange": "E2 <50 with FSH >10 indicates estrogen deficiency"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 75.3, "status": "normal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 75.3 nmol/L is within normal range. SHBG regulates bioavailable sex hormones by binding testosterone and estradiol.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 1 pg/mL is within normal functional range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- Labs indicate a hypoestrogenic perimenopausal pattern with estradiol 10 pg/mL and FSH 22.3 mIU/mL consistent with low ovarian estrogen production. Estradiol at this level is below the bone-protective threshold and is likely contributing to symptoms and long-term bone risk.\\n- Progesterone 2.98 ng/mL is below the clinic HRT therapeutic range for endometrial protection.\\n- Total testosterone 13.8 ng/dL is below the clinic target for symptomatic benefit.\\n- SHBG, free T, and STOP-BANG were within normal limits per the reported results.\\n- Action is required to restore estradiol to the clinic target (60–100 pg/mL), increase progesterone to therapeutic range (aim 8–10 ng/mL when measured appropriately), and raise testosterone toward the target (75–125 ng/dL). Timing of blood draws relative to HRT dosing must be documented for correct interpretation.\\n\\nPriority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL (requires provider sign-off before dispensing/titration)\\n- Estradiol: Patient estradiol 10 pg/mL → provider review and authorize estrogen dose increase to achieve target 60–100 pg/mL. If patient has intact uterus, ensure progestogen plan approved concurrently.\\n- Progesterone: Current value 2.98 ng/mL → provider approval to increase micronized progesterone for endometrial protection (see dosing options below).\\n- Safety checks before increases: confirm pregnancy status if of childbearing potential; review thromboembolic history, active liver disease, uncontrolled hypertension; notify provider immediately if any contraindications present.\\n\\nURGENT (to implement once provider approves)\\n- Estradiol dosing adjustment (transdermal preference per protocol):\\n  - If patient is currently on a 0.025 mg (25 mcg) transdermal patch → increase to 0.05 mg (50 mcg) patch.\\n  - If already on 0.05 mg (50 mcg) and still hypoestrogenic → consider 0.1 mg (100 mcg) patch.\\n  - If using transdermal gel: increase to the equivalent dose (clinic-specific conversion), or switch to patch per provider preference.\\n  - Recheck serum estradiol 4 weeks after dose change (draw timing: document last patch change/time of gel dose; for transdermal patches, steady state by 48–72 hours; standard practice is blood draw at trough 24 hours after patch change—document clinic protocol).\\n- Progesterone dosing adjustment:\\n  - If current micronized progesterone is 100 mg nightly or 100 mg x14 days/month → increase to 200 mg nightly (either cyclic 12–14 days/month if cyclic HRT, or continuous 200 mg nightly if a continuous combined regimen is chosen by provider).\\n  - Recheck progesterone 4 weeks after change. Note: serum progesterone is time-dependent—draw ~3–4 hours after an oral bedtime dose to approximate peak when measuring level for therapeutic monitoring (document time of last dose).\\n- Testosterone:\\n  - If patient already on testosterone: increase current dose by 25–50% and recheck levels.\\n  - If initiating or changing route, consider common clinic regimens (provider decision):\\n    - IM option: testosterone cypionate/enanthate 25–50 mg every 10–14 days (or 20–40 mg weekly) titratable to effect.\\n    - Transdermal option: increase topical dose per product equivalence by ~25–50% (clinic-specific topical concentrations vary—document current product and dose).\\n  - Recheck total testosterone (and free T, SHBG) 6–8 weeks after dose adjustment. Notify provider if total testosterone exceeds 150 ng/dL or if patient develops virilizing symptoms.\\n\\nROUTINE\\n- Bone health: order DEXA if no baseline within the last 2 years or if fracture risk factors present.\\n- Vitamin D, calcium counseling and supplementation as appropriate.\\n- Routine labs: CBC, CMP, lipid panel, LFTs at baseline and after testosterone escalation per clinic protocol.\\n- If patient desires fertility or fertility preservation counseling → order AMH and consider reproductive endocrine referral.\\n\\nTestosterone dose recommendations (staff-facing, for provider review)\\n- If patient already on testosterone:\\n  - Increase current dose by 25–50% as an initial step; document current formulation and dose prior to change.\\n  - Reassess labs 6–8 weeks after change; titrate further only under provider direction to reach total T 75–125 ng/dL.\\n- If patient not currently on testosterone and provider elects to start:\\n  - IM option for provider consideration: testosterone cypionate/enanthate 25–50 mg q10–14 days (or 20–40 mg weekly); recheck 6–8 weeks.\\n  - Transdermal option for provider consideration: increase topical formulation to a dose expected to achieve target total T (clinic-specific product dosing—start low and titrate).\\n- Monitoring: total testosterone, free testosterone, SHBG, LFTs, CBC (Hgb/Hct) at baseline and 6–8 weeks after initiation/titration. Watch for virilization; hold and notify provider if signs occur.\\n\\nLifestyle interventions to communicate to patient (staff to educate)\\n- Bone health:\\n  - Encourage weight-bearing exercise (resistance training, walking), smoking cessation, limit alcohol to ≤1 drink/day.\\n  - Ensure adequate calcium intake (dietary ± supplements) and vitamin D supplementation per clinic guidelines; consider vitamin D level if not recently checked.\\n- Cardiometabolic health:\\n  - Encourage Mediterranean-style diet, weight management, regular aerobic activity.\\n- Sleep and mood:\\n  - Discuss sleep hygiene; progesterone may help sleep but counsel on timing of dose (bedtime) and possible sedation.\\n- Safety with topical hormones:\\n  - If using transdermal/ topical testosterone or estrogen, counsel on application site, avoid skin-to-skin contact with others, wash hands after application.\\n- Menstrual bleeding:\\n  - Counsel that changes in HRT may alter bleeding patterns; instruct patient to report heavy bleeding or new severe pelvic pain.\\n\\nAdditional testing or referrals to consider\\n- AMH (anti-Müllerian hormone) if fertility is a current concern or to further assess ovarian reserve.\\n- DEXA scan for bone density baseline if not performed in past 2 years.\\n- Pregnancy test before increasing estrogen/testosterone if childbearing potential.\\n- Baseline and follow-up: CBC, CMP (incl. LFTs), lipid panel.\\n- TSH and free T4 if not recently checked (thyroid disease can impact symptoms).\\n- Reproductive endocrinology referral if fertility preservation or assisted reproduction is a patient goal.\\n- Endometrial evaluation (TVUS or consult) only if abnormal bleeding develops after HRT changes.\\n\\nPhysician notification criteria (immediate)\\n- Any contraindications to estrogen/progesterone identified (active VTE, stroke, uncontrolled HTN, active liver disease) — halt changes and notify provider.\\n- Estradiol remains <20 pg/mL after two appropriate escalations.\\n- Total testosterone >150 ng/dL or any virilizing symptoms (voice change, clitoromegaly, severe acne, rapid hair growth).\\n- New/worsening chest pain, neurologic deficits, shortness of breath, or signs of VTE after hormone dose change.\\n- Significant abnormal labs after dose change (elevated LFTs, Hct rise >5% or Hct >52%, markedly abnormal lipids).\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (concise)\\n- Immediate (today/after provider sign-off):\\n  - Provider to review and authorize estrogen increase and progesterone increase.\\n  - Verify pregnancy status and contraindications.\\n- Within 1–2 weeks:\\n  - Implement dose changes once provider approves (increase patch to 0.05 mg if appropriate; increase micronized progesterone to 200 mg nightly if indicated).\\n  - Document exact time of last HRT dose in chart for lab interpretation.\\n- 4 weeks after estrogen/progesterone adjustment:\\n  - Recheck serum estradiol and serum progesterone (draw timed relative to last dose—document timing).\\n  - Symptom review and adverse effects check.\\n- 6–8 weeks after testosterone adjustment:\\n  - Recheck total testosterone, free T, SHBG, CBC, LFTs; adjust dose per lab and symptoms.\\n- Within 4–8 weeks:\\n  - Order/arrange DEXA if indicated; order AMH if fertility concerned.\\n- Follow-up visit:\\n  - Schedule clinician follow-up after lab results (4–8 weeks) to review hormone levels, symptoms, bleeding pattern, and safety labs.\\n\\nDocumenting timing and current regimen\\n- IMPORTANT for staff: before changing doses or interpreting labs, document current HRT regimen (dose, route, last dose timing), contraceptive status, menstrual phase (if applicable), and whether the patient has an intact uterus. These details are necessary for correct lab interpretation and safe regimen adjustments.\\n\\nNote: These are clinic protocol–based management recommendations for provider review. Changes must be authorized by the prescribing provider and individualized to the patient’s full medical history and contraindications.", "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Menopausal Transition", "confidence": "high", "description": "Clinical picture consistent with perimenopause or menopause. Hormonal shifts during this transition affect bone health, cardiovascular risk, mood, and quality of life.", "supportingFindings": ["Age 45 (perimenopause window)", "FSH 22.3 mIU/mL (rising, perimenopause range)", "Estradiol 10 pg/mL (declining)", "Mood changes reported"]}]}	2026-03-19 13:57:49.728114	\N
