atocrit: 45.2 % — BORDERLINE (slightly elevated; may reflect mild hemoconcentration/dehydration)\\n  - Platelets: 285 K/uL (normal)\\n\\n- Chemistry / Renal:\\n  - Creatinine: 0.64 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - eGFR: 113 mL/min/1.73m2 (normal)\\n\\n- Liver enzymes:\\n  - AST: 17 U/L (normal)\\n  - ALT: 14 U/L (normal)\\n\\n- Thyroid:\\n  - TSH: 1.02 mIU/L (normal)\\n\\n- Vitamins / Nutritional:\\n  - Vitamin D (25-OH): 66.4 ng/mL (normal)\\n  - Vitamin B12: 592 pg/mL (normal)\\n\\n- Lipids / Cardiovascular markers:\\n  - Triglycerides: 70 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - HDL Cholesterol: 49 mg/dL — ABNORMAL (low for female; modestly increased CVD risk)\\n  - LDL Cholesterol: 102 mg/dL — BORDERLINE (near-optimal)\\n  - Apolipoprotein B: 87 mg/dL (normal)\\n\\n- Risk scores (AHA PREVENT 2023 / clinic utilities):\\n  - 10-year Total CVD risk (PREVENT): 0.693% (~0.7%) — LOW\\n  - 10-year ASCVD risk (PREVENT): 0.499% (~0.5%) — LOW\\n  - 10-year Heart Failure risk (PREVENT): 0.337% (~0.3%)\\n  - 30-year CVD risk (PREVENT): 6.03%\\n  - STOP-BANG Sleep Apnea Risk: 0 points\\n  - Clinic interpretation: 10-yr Total CVD 0.7%, 10-yr ASCVD 0.5%, Category: low. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.\\n\\n- Red flags: None.\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Borderline elevated hematocrit — 45.2%. Likely mild hemoconcentration/dehydration; not at threshold for polycythemia. If patient is receiving exogenous testosterone, this level does not currently require dose adjustment but requires monitoring.\\n   - Differential: dehydration/hemoconcentration, smoking, chronic hypoxia (unlikely without symptoms), relative polycythemia. No evidence of polycythemia vera at this level.\\n\\n2. Low HDL cholesterol — 49 mg/dL (female). Modestly increased long-term cardiovascular risk; triglycerides normal. Lifestyle modification recommended. No current indication for pharmacologic HDL-targeted therapy.\\n\\n3. Borderline LDL cholesterol — 102 mg/dL. Near-optimal; given very low 10-year ASCVD risk, statin therapy not indicated at this time. Continue lifestyle measures and reassess.\\n\\n4. Overall cardiovascular risk low per PREVENT tool (10-yr ASCVD 0.5%); no immediate pharmacologic intervention indicated. Continue risk-reduction counseling.\\n\\n5. Other labs within normal limits: Hemoglobin, platelets, renal function, liver enzymes, TSH, vitamin D, B12, apolipoprotein B, triglycerides. No acute abnormalities identified.\\n\\nPLAN:\\n1. Hematocrit 45.2% (Assessment #1)\\n   - Counseling: Advise oral hydration (increase fluids to at least 1.5–2 L/day unless contraindicated) and re-evaluate hydration status.\\n   - Orders:\\n     - Repeat CBC (Hgb/Hct) in 4–6 weeks to confirm persistence vs transient hemoconcentration.\\n     - If clinical dehydration suspected or symptoms present, repeat CBC in 1–2 weeks after rehydration.\\n   - Safety monitoring:\\n     - If patient is on exogenous testosterone: notify prescribing physician if Hct ≥50% (consider dose reduction/hold) or ≥54% (hold/stop and urgent evaluation).\\n     - Instruct patient to seek immediate care and notify clinic for new symptoms suggestive of thrombosis or cardiac ischemia (chest pain, sudden dyspnea, unilateral leg pain/swelling, focal neurologic deficits, visual changes, syncope).\\n\\n2. Low HDL 49 mg/dL (Assessment #2)\\n   - Lifestyle interventions (counseled today):\\n     - Aerobic exercise: target ≥150 min/week moderate-intensity (or 75 min vigorous) + resistance training 2x/week.\\n     - Diet: Mediterranean-style diet — increase fruits/vegetables, whole grains, legumes, fatty fish, nuts; reduce saturated/trans fats and refined carbohydrates; increase soluble fiber.\\n     - Weight: encourage weight loss if BMI >25 (5–10% weight loss beneficial).\\n     - Tobacco: counsel and offer cessation resources if patient smokes (smoking lowers HDL).\\n     - Alcohol: counsel moderation; do not begin alcohol for HDL effect.\\n   - Pharmacologic/supplement:\\n     - Do not start niacin for isolated low HDL without physician/cardiology approval due to adverse effect profile.\\n     - Recommend NutraGems® CoQ10 300 chewable gel, 1 daily (per clinic recommendation).\\n   - Follow-up testing:\\n     - Repeat fasting lipid panel in 3 months after lifestyle interventions.\\n\\n3. Borderline LDL 102 mg/dL (Assessment #3)\\n   - No statin recommended at current very low 10-year ASCVD risk after shared decision-making.\\n   - Continue above lifestyle measures targeted to lower LDL (soluble fiber, reduce saturated fats).\\n   - Repeat fasting lipid panel in 3 months to reassess LDL response.\\n   - If patient requests pharmacotherapy or risk profile changes, consider shared decision-making discussion and possible cardiology or preventive medicine referral.\\n\\n4. Overall cardiovascular risk low (Assessment #4)\\n   - Documented risk category and counseling provided.\\n   - No statin at this time; emphasize lifestyle modification and periodic monitoring.\\n   - Consider dietitian referral if patient desires structured dietary program.\\n\\n5. Other lab abnormalities / normal labs (Assessment #5)\\n   - No action needed for normal hemoglobin, platelets, creatinine, eGFR, AST/ALT, TSH, vitamin D, B12, triglycerides, apolipoprotein B.\\n   - Maintain routine health maintenance and annual monitoring per primary care.\\n\\n6. Testosterone/HRT status (Assessment #6)\\n   - Status unknown. Action: document current HRT/testosterone use in chart today.\\n   - If patient is on testosterone, ensure baseline and ongoing monitoring per clinic protocol: CBC prior to initiation, then at 3 months, 6 months, and annually (or more frequently if abnormal). Obtain total testosterone levels as clinically indicated (document cycle day and timing relative to dose when applicable).\\n\\n7. Referrals and additional testing (Assessment #7)\\n   - Consider dietitian referral for targeted lipid and weight management if patient interested.\\n   - Consider cardiology referral only if HDL remains low with additional risk factors or if ASCVD risk increases on reevaluation.\\n   - If menstrual irregularity or suspicion of iron deficiency arises on future visits, consider ferritin/iron studies.\\n\\nFollow-up orders and timing:\\n- Repeat CBC (Hgb/Hct): 4–6 weeks (sooner 1–2 weeks if clinically dehydrated). Note: if patient on testosterone, ensure adherence to monitoring schedule.\\n- Repeat fasting lipid panel: 3 months (specify fasting status). If original panel was non-fasting, order fasting panel at repeat.\\n- Routine clinic follow-up: 3–6 months for reassessment (recheck window suggested).\\n- Urgent actions: patient instructed to report immediately for thrombotic/ischemic symptoms.\\n\\nMedications / Supplements:\\n- No new prescription medications initiated.\\n- Supplement recommended: NutraGems® CoQ10 300, 1 chewable gel daily (high-priority supplement per clinic note).\\n- Statin therapy: not indicated at this time based on very low 10-year risk; document shared decision-making if patient requests treatment.\\n\\nDocumentation:\\n- I reviewed these lab results and risk assessment with the patient today. Counseling provided regarding hydration, lifestyle interventions for HDL/LDL improvement, monitoring plan, and when to seek urgent care.\\n- Patient advised to report current HRT/testosterone use so monitoring can be individualized.\\n\\nRe-check window / follow-up appointment:\\n- CBC repeat: 4–6 weeks (or 1–2 weeks if dehydrated).\\n- Fasting lipid panel: 3 months.\\n- Clinic follow-up: 3–6 months for routine continuity; sooner if symptoms or if on testosterone and monitoring schedule requires.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\n03/10/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": true, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "