icated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.3374712853548267, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.3%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 6.025418435895934, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 6.0%, ASCVD 4.0%, Heart Failure 3.4%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 17, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 14, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 113, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.64, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 87, "status": "normal", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Apo B at optimal level.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}], "aiRecommendations": "Clinical context note for staff: Labs show largely normal panel with three borderline/abnormal findings: hematocrit 45.2% (slightly high), LDL 102 mg/dL (near-optimal/borderline), HDL 49 mg/dL (low for female). There is no testosterone level or explicit HRT status provided. Recommendations below assume routine ambulatory female patient unless otherwise specified. Modify if patient is on testosterone or other HRT — see testosterone-specific section.\\n\\n1) Overall clinical assessment\\n- No immediate red flags. Most metabolic, hepatic, renal, thyroid, vitamin B12/D, and CBC indices are within normal limits.\\n- Hematocrit mildly elevated at 45.2% — likely minimal clinical significance (possible mild hemoconcentration/dehydration). Not consistent with polycythemia at this level.\\n- Lipid profile: LDL 102 mg/dL (near-optimal) and HDL 49 mg/dL (below desirable for female; increases long-term CVD risk modestly). Triglycerides normal.\\n- 10-year cardiovascular risk scores are within acceptable ranges per PREVENT results — no indication for statin initiation based solely on these values.\\n\\n2) Priority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL (immediate physician notification)\\n- None required based on current values.\\n\\nURGENT (notify physician if any of the following occur or if patient is on testosterone)\\n- If patient is on exogenous testosterone: notify physician if hematocrit increases to ≥50% (consider dose adjustment) or ≥54% (hold/stop per protocol and evaluate for venous thromboembolism risk).\\n- If symptoms of thrombosis, dyspnea, chest pain, visual changes, focal neurologic signs, or syncope occur — contact physician immediately.\\n\\nROUTINE (staff actions to implement)\\n- Hematocrit 45.2%:\\n  * Encourage oral hydration now and counsel on adequate fluid intake.\\n  * Repeat CBC (hematocrit/hemoglobin) in 4–6 weeks to confirm persistence vs transient hemoconcentration.\\n  * Sooner recheck (within 1–2 weeks) if clinical dehydration suspected or symptoms present.\\n  * If repeat Hct rises above ≥50%, notify physician per testosterone/hematocrit protocol.\\n\\n- Lipids:\\n  * No statin indicated at LDL 102 mg/dL with low 10-year ASCVD risk — document shared decision-making if patient requests treatment.\\n  * Arrange repeat fasting lipid panel in 3 months after lifestyle interventions to assess response.\\n  * If patient fasting status unknown, order fasting lipid panel for the repeat test.\\n\\n- HDL 49 mg/dL:\\n  * Provide lifestyle counseling to raise HDL (see lifestyle section).\\n  * Reassess with repeat lipid panel in 3 months. Consider primary care or cardiology referral if HDL remains low with other risk factors or if ASCVD risk becomes elevated.\\n\\n3) Testosterone dose recommendations (if applicable)\\n- If patient is NOT receiving testosterone: no testosterone dosing actions indicated. Obtain baseline total testosterone only if clinically indicated (e.g., symptoms of hyperandrogenism, transgender care initiation).\\n- If patient IS receiving testosterone (transgender or HRT protocol):\\n  * Baseline and monitoring guidance:\\n    - Baseline CBC including hematocrit prior to initiation, then at 3 months, 6 months, and annually thereafter (or more frequently if abnormal).\\n  * Dose adjustment criteria (clinic protocol):\\n    - If hematocrit ≥50%: consider holding dose or reducing dose; discuss with prescribing physician. Evaluate for dehydration and repeat CBC in 1–2 weeks.\\n    - If hematocrit ≥54%: stop testosterone immediately and notify prescribing physician; evaluate for polycythemia complications and consider hematology referral.\\n  * No change recommended solely for Hct 45.2% — continue current regimen and monitor per schedule.\\n  * When assessing testosterone levels for dose titration, document menstrual cycle day (if premenopausal) and time since last dose; obtain trough level (just before next dose) when applicable.\\n\\n4) Lifestyle interventions needed (specific, staff to counsel)\\n- Hydration:\\n  * Encourage at least 1.5–2 L/day fluid intake unless contraindicated; reinforce rehydration prior to repeat CBC.\\n\\n- Aerobic exercise:\\n  * Target ≥150 minutes/week moderate-intensity or 75 minutes/week vigorous aerobic exercise + 2 sessions/week resistance training.\\n  * Emphasize aerobic exercise specifically to help raise HDL.\\n\\n- Diet:\\n  * Recommend Mediterranean-style diet: increase fruits/vegetables, whole grains, legumes, fatty fish (omega-3s), nuts; reduce saturated fats and trans fats.\\n  * Increase soluble fiber (oats, beans) to help LDL reduction.\\n  * Limit refined carbohydrates and added sugars.\\n\\n- Weight and tobacco:\\n  * Encourage weight loss if BMI >25 (5–10% weight reduction can improve HDL and LDL).\\n  * Strongly advise smoking cessation (if applicable) — smoking lowers HDL.\\n\\n- Alcohol:\\n  * If patient drinks alcohol, advise moderation; small amounts may raise HDL, but risks often outweigh benefits — follow counseling per clinic policy.\\n\\n- Supplements/medications:\\n  * Do not initiate niacin without physician review: consider only if HDL remains very low despite lifestyle modification and after cardiology/physician approval due to side effects (flushing, hepatotoxicity, glucose intolerance).\\n\\n5) Additional testing or referrals to consider\\n- Repeat CBC (hematocrit) in 4–6 weeks after hydration.\\n- Repeat fasting lipid panel in 3 months.\\n- If patient is on or being considered for testosterone:\\n  * Obtain baseline total testosterone, estradiol (if indicated), and schedule ongoing monitoring per hormonal therapy protocol.\\n- If low HDL persists and multiple risk factors present:\\n  * Consider referral to cardiology or preventive medicine for advanced risk stratification and management.\\n- If heavy or irregular menses or concern for iron deficiency despite current labs:\\n  * Order ferritin, iron studies if symptomatic or if hemoglobin/Hct trend downward.\\n- Dietitian referral:\\n  * Consider referral if patient requires structured dietary intervention for lipids/weight management.\\n- If any new symptoms concerning for thrombotic event or cardiac symptoms — urgent evaluation/emergency referral.\\n\\n6) Summary of next steps and timeline (staff action checklist)\\n- Immediate (today/at visit):\\n  * Counsel patient on hydration and initiate lifestyle counseling (exercise, diet, smoking cessation).\\n  * Document current HRT/testosterone status in chart.\\n\\n- Short-term (1–6 weeks):\\n  * If dehydration suspected: repeat CBC in 1–2 weeks after hydration; otherwise repeat in 4–6 weeks.\\n  * If symptomatic (chest pain, dyspnea, neurologic symptoms) — escalate immediately.\\n\\n- Mid-term (3 months):\\n  * Repeat fasting lipid panel to reassess LDL and HDL after lifestyle measures.\\n  * If on testosterone, ensure scheduled 3-month CBC and testosterone level per protocol.\\n\\n- Ongoing (3–12 months):\\n  * Continue annual monitoring of lipids and CBC per routine care and HRT protocols.\\n  * Consider dietitian or cardiology referral if nonresponsive or risk profile changes.\\n\\nPhysician notification criteria for staff\\n- Notify prescribing physician immediately if:\\n  * Hematocrit ≥50% (if on testosterone) or ≥54% (critical).\\n  * New symptoms suggesting thrombotic event or cardiac ischemia.\\n  * LDL ≥190 mg/dL or sudden significant worsening of lipid profile.\\n  * Persistent HDL <40 mg/dL with other risk factors — discuss specialist referral.\\n\\nDocumentation reminders for staff\\n- Record counseling provided, recommended timeline for lab rechecks, and any plan for medication adjustments.\\n- If ordering repeat testing, specify fasting status for lipid panel and note cycle day/time of last HRT dose if applicable.\\n\\nIf you want, provide the patient’s HRT/testosterone status and whether the lipid panel was fasting so recommendations can be individualized (dose adjustments, monitoring cadence).", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 1.43, "detail": "Triglycerides 70 / HDL 49 = 1.43", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 14, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "87 mg/dL", "detail": "Normal atherogenic particle count", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": []}	2026-03-10 18:46:35.40348	\N
21	Leigh Wimberly 	female	2025-12-31 00:00:00	{"a1c": 5.1, "alt": 15, "ast": 19, "bun": 15, "co2": 18, "fsh": 10.5, "hdl": 51, "ldl": 204, "lpa": 11, "rbc": 4.99, "tsh": 0.96, "wbc": 8.1, "acne": false, "apoB": 121, "egfr": 82, "iron": 89, "shbg": 84, "tibc": 331, "hsCRP": 0.4, "onHRT": false, "folate": 7.32, "freeT4": 1.21, "sodium": 138, "albumin": 4.5, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 9.5, "glucose": 86, "bloating": false, "brainFog": true, "chloride": 107, "ferritin": 97.7, "hairLoss": false, "vitaminD": 23.1, "bilirubin": 0.4, "estradiol": 119, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": true, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 249, "potassium": 4.1, "creatinine": 0.9, "hematocrit": 46.9, "hemoglobin": 15.1, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "vitaminB12": 645, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2025-12-31", "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Leigh Wimberly ", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 41, "bmi": 35.97, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 111, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": true, "progesterone": 1, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 82.9, "totalProtein": 7.7, "lowMotivation": false, "triglycerides": 92, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "freeTestosterone": 8, "totalCholesterol": 273}	{"redFlags": [{"action": "Flag provider for consideration of statin therapy. Intensive lifestyle counseling.", "message": "LDL is 204 mg/dL (≥190 threshold).", "category": "Lipids - Severe LDL Elevation", "severity": "warning"}], "soapNote": "DATE: 03/10/2026\\n\\nSUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History/Symptoms: Patient is a 41-year-old female presenting for lab review and management of abnormal lipids and hormonal labs. Reports perimenopausal symptoms consistent with labs (subjective report documented in chart): irregular cycles and sleep disturbance. Reports no current pregnancy. No acute chest pain, dyspnea, syncope, or symptoms of rapid virilization (no voice change, clitoromegaly). Denies current use of testosterone or androgenic supplements (patient queried; no reported use). No current statin use. Last menstrual period not documented in lab report — cycle day needs confirmation.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: Not recorded at time of lab review in this entry (please update with current BP, BMI, HR if available).\\n- CBC:\\n  - Hemoglobin 15.1 g/dL — normal\\n  - Hematocrit 46.9% — borderline high (may reflect mild dehydration) [BORDERLINE]\\n  - Platelets 249 K/uL — normal\\n- Basic Metabolic / Renal:\\n  - Creatinine 0.9 mg/dL — normal\\n  - eGFR 82 mL/min — normal\\n- Liver:\\n  - AST 19 U/L — normal\\n  - ALT 15 U/L — normal\\n- Lipids:\\n  - Total cholesterol: (not provided)\\n  - LDL cholesterol 204 mg/dL — severely elevated; critical (LDL ≥190 mg/dL) [CRITICAL]\\n  - HDL cholesterol 51 mg/dL — borderline\\n  - Triglycerides 92 mg/dL — normal\\n  - Apolipoprotein B 121 mg/dL — borderline elevated\\n  - Lipoprotein(a) 11 mg/dL — normal\\n- Glycemic:\\n  - Hemoglobin A1c 5.1% — normal\\n- Inflammation:\\n  - hs-CRP 0.4 mg/L — normal\\n- Iron studies / Nutritional:\\n  - Ferritin 97.7 ng/mL — normal\\n  - Serum iron 89 ug/dL — normal\\n  - TIBC 331 ug/dL — normal\\n  - Folate 7.32 ng/mL — normal\\n  - Vitamin B12 645 pg/mL — normal\\n- Thyroid:\\n  - TSH 0.96 mIU/L — normal\\n- Renal/CVD risk scores:\\n  - 10-year Total CVD risk (PREVENT): 1.044% — low\\n  - 10-year ASCVD risk (PREVENT): 0.772% — low\\n  - 10-year HF risk (PREVENT): 0.351% — low\\n  - 30-year CVD risk (PREVENT): 8.521% — low\\n  - AHA PREVENT category: Low 10-yr risk; note LDL-based statin recommendation due to LDL ≥190 mg/dL\\n- Hormones:\\n  - Estradiol (E2) 119 pg/mL — normal for certain cycle phases\\n  - Progesterone (P4) 1 ng/mL — low/relative deficiency [ABNORMAL/BORDERLINE depending on cycle day]\\n  - FSH 10.5 mIU/mL — consistent with early perimenopause [BORDERLINE]\\n  - Total Testosterone 82.9 ng/dL — elevated for female [ABNORMAL]\\n  - Free Testosterone 8 pg/mL — elevated [ABNORMAL]\\n  - SHBG 84 nmol/L — normal\\n- Vitamin D:\\n  - 25-OH Vitamin D 23.1 ng/mL — deficient / suboptimal (21–30 ng/mL range) [ABNORMAL]\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Severe hypercholesterolemia — LDL 204 mg/dL. Very high atherogenic burden; ApoB 121 mg/dL borderline-high supporting need for pharmacotherapy. This meets guideline threshold for consideration of high-intensity statin regardless of 10-year ASCVD score. Red flag requiring immediate physician notification.\\n   - Clinical significance: High lifetime/atherosclerotic risk; consider familial hypercholesterolemia given LDL ≥190, age <50; confirm secondary contributors.\\n2. Early perimenopausal transition with relative luteal progesterone deficiency. Findings: FSH 10.5 mIU/mL (early perimenopause range), Estradiol 119 pg/mL with Progesterone 1 ng/mL → relative progesterone deficiency and luteal phase insufficiency likely contributing to perimenopausal symptoms (irregular cycles, sleep disturbance).\\n3. Androgen excess — Total testosterone 82.9 ng/dL and free T 8 pg/mL (both elevated for female). May account for acne, hair thinning, or hirsutism if present. Differential: PCOS spectrum (if menstrual irregularity), exogenous androgen exposure, adrenal source (DHEA-S), rare androgen-secreting tumor if markedly elevated or rapid virilization.\\n4. Vitamin D deficiency — 25-OH D 23.1 ng/mL. Suboptimal for bone and hormonal health; repletion indicated.\\n5. Hematocrit borderline high 46.9% — likely mild dehydration but needs reassessment; consider secondary causes (smoking, OSA, exogenous androgens) if persistent or rising.\\n6. Other labs within acceptable limits: Hemoglobin A1c 5.1%, TSH normal, LFTs normal, renal function normal, hs-CRP low, Lp(a) low; these support lower short-term ASCVD risk but do not negate need to address LDL.\\n\\nPLAN:\\n(General: Results reviewed with patient. Discussed priorities and safety considerations. Patient verbalized understanding; pregnancy status needs confirmation prior to certain therapies.)\\n\\nImmediate / Today (03/10/2026)\\n- Physician notified immediately regarding LDL 204 mg/dL and need for statin discussion and possible initiation. (Action taken: provider notified.)\\n- Confirm pregnancy status prior to initiating statin or anti-androgen therapy — order urine β-hCG today if childbearing potential and not sterilized.\\n- Document LMP and cycle day in chart today to guide hormone interpretation and timing of potential progesterone therapy and repeat labs.\\n- Start vitamin D repletion today: Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + Vitamin K daily with food (per clinic protocol). Counsel to take with a fat-containing meal. Recheck 25-OH Vitamin D in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Advise hydration and repeat CBC in 4 weeks to reassess hematocrit.\\n\\nStatin therapy (physician to prescribe if agreed)\\n- Recommend initiating high-intensity statin for LDL ≥190 mg/dL (unless contraindicated or pregnancy): options\\n  - Atorvastatin 40–80 mg PO nightly OR\\n  - Rosuvastatin 20–40 mg PO nightly\\n- Before starting: confirm pregnancy test negative and review contraception plans; discuss teratogenic risks.\\n- Baseline labs: current AST/ALT available and normal. No additional baseline LFTs required beyond current unless clinically indicated.\\n- Monitoring: fasting lipid panel 6–12 weeks after initiation or dose adjustment; then every 3–12 months once stable. Repeat LFTs only if symptomatic or per clinician preference (otherwise baseline is sufficient).\\n\\nHormonal management — progesterone and perimenopause\\n- Discussed options with patient; plan to document cycle day before prescribing.\\n- Provider consideration for micronized progesterone:\\n  - Consider starting micronized progesterone 100 mg PO nightly; may increase to 200 mg nightly based on symptoms (insomnia/anxiety) and provider judgment.\\n  - Cyclic dosing (approx. luteal phase, e.g., days 14–28) appropriate for women with some ovulatory cycles and for bleeding control; continuous nightly dosing may be used for anovulatory cycles or persistent sleep/anxiety issues.\\n- Symptom follow-up at 6–8 weeks post-start to assess response. Consider mid-luteal progesterone recheck (≈7 days post-ovulation) if ovulation confirmation needed.\\n\\nAndrogen excess evaluation and plan\\n- Do NOT start anti-androgen therapy until endocrine/gynecology review and pregnancy status confirmed.\\n- Order repeat AM fasting total and free testosterone (confirm elevated), plus DHEA-S and 17-hydroxyprogesterone (AM levels) within 1 week to evaluate adrenal contribution and to confirm persistent elevation.\\n- If repeat testosterone remains elevated or if clinical concern for virilization develops, refer to Endocrinology and consider pelvic ultrasound and/or adrenal imaging (CT) to evaluate for neoplasm.\\n- If symptoms (acne/hirsutism) are bothersome and pregnancy is not desired, discuss combined estrogen OCP and/or spironolactone with provider (typical spironolactone start 50–100 mg PO daily, titrate; monitor potassium/renal function; contraindicated in pregnancy).\\n\\nVitamin D\\n- Start Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily; recheck 25-OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks. Target per clinic: ≥60 ng/mL.\\n- Check baseline serum calcium only if history suggests risk for hypercalcemia or granulomatous disease.\\n\\nHematocrit\\n- Advise oral/IV hydration as tolerated and repeat CBC in 4 weeks. If hematocrit remains elevated or increases, evaluate for secondary causes (smoking, OSA, pulmonary disease, exogenous androgens) and notify provider.\\n\\nLifestyle and non-pharmacologic interventions\\n- Intensive lifestyle counseling for LDL lowering today:\\n  - Diet: Mediterranean or DASH-style diet; reduce saturated and trans fats, refined carbohydrates; increase fiber and soluble fiber; refer to dietitian for intensive counseling.\\n  - Exercise: at least 150 min/week moderate aerobic + strength training 2x/week.\\n  - Weight management: if overweight, target 5–10% weight loss.\\n  - Smoking cessation and alcohol moderation counseling as indicated.\\n- For perimenopausal symptoms: sleep hygiene, stress reduction, regular exercise, weight optimization.\\n\\nAdditional orders / labs and timeline\\n- Today:\\n  - Urine β-hCG (if childbearing potential) prior to statin/anti-androgen initiation.\\n  - Document LMP and cycle day in chart.\\n- Within 1 week:\\n  - Repeat AM fasting total and free testosterone.\\n  - DHEA-S, 17-OH progesterone (AM).\\n  - Consider SHBG recheck if clinically indicated (SHBG currently 84 nmol/L — normal).\\n- 4 weeks:\\n  - CBC repeat to reassess hematocrit.\\n- 6–12 weeks:\\n  - Fasting lipid panel 6–12 weeks after statin initiation (or sooner per clinician).\\n  - Recheck 25-OH vitamin D at 8–12 weeks after starting supplementation.\\n- Ongoing:\\n  - Symptom follow-up and medication tolerance checks at 6–8 weeks after initiating progesterone or statin.\\n  - Consider referral to Endocrinology for persistent/elevated testosterone or rapid virilization.\\n  - Consider lipid specialist referral for suspected familial hypercholesterolemia or inadequate LDL response on maximal therapy.\\n\\nReferrals recommended\\n- Endocrinology: if testosterone remains elevated or rapid virilization.\\n- Gynecology: for contraception counseling prior to statin if indicated and for perimenopause management if desired.\\n- Lipid clinic/cardiology: if familial hypercholesterolemia suspected or LDL remains very high despite therapy.\\n- Dietitian: for intensive LDL and weight management.\\n\\nPatient communication and education provided\\n- Discussed significance of LDL 204 mg/dL, need for prompt statin discussion, and pregnancy contraindication to statins. Patient aware pregnancy test is required prior to statin start.\\n- Reviewed vitamin D deficiency and repletion regimen, dosing, and recheck timeline.\\n- Reviewed need to document last menstrual period/cycle day to inform hormone therapy decisions.\\n- Advised hydration and plan for CBC repeat.\\n- Asked about signs of androgen excess and current supplement/medication use; patient denies androgen supplements.\\n\\nFollow-up:\\n- Provider to evaluate for statin initiation within 7 days.\\n- Labs and follow-up per timeline above.\\n- Clinic follow-up visit or telephone check 6–8 weeks after initiation of any new therapy (statin/progesterone) or sooner per provider discretion.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": true, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": true, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": true, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": true}, "preventRisk": {"ldlGoal": "Goal: >=50% LDL reduction (target <100 mg/dL)", "riskCategory": "low", "tenYearASCVD": 0.007720114329249718, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years\\n\\nSEVERE HYPERCHOLESTEROLEMIA: High-intensity statin therapy indicated (LDL >=190 mg/dL)", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0.010443587568500026, "thirtyYearASCVD": 0.05791235047392616, "ageValidFor30Year": true, "thirtyYearTotalCVD": 0.0852142540311626, "tenYearHFPercentage": "0.4%", "tenYearHeartFailure": 0.0035088812435596947, "statinRecommendation": "HIGH-INTENSITY statin (Atorvastatin 40-80 mg or Rosuvastatin 20-40 mg) - LDL >=190 mg/dL", "tenYearCVDPercentage": "1.0%", "tenYearASCVDPercen