ings": ["Vitamin D 23.1 ng/mL (deficient)"]}, {"dose": "3 capsules daily (divided doses)", "name": "Magtein® Magnesium L-Threonate", "caution": "L-Threonate form crosses blood-brain barrier for cognitive and sleep support. Well-tolerated; gentle on GI system.", "category": "mineral", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Magtein is the only magnesium form clinically shown to cross the blood-brain barrier. Supports quality sleep, cognitive function, GABA activity, and stress resilience.", "indication": "Low energy reported; Brain fog reported; Irritability reported", "phenotypes": ["Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "This special form of magnesium is designed to reach the brain, where it supports better sleep quality, mental clarity, and a calmer nervous system.", "supportingFindings": ["Low energy reported", "Brain fog reported", "Irritability reported"]}, {"dose": "2 tablets twice daily", "name": "EstroFactors™", "caution": "Nutritional support for estrogen balance and metabolism. Contains I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Supports healthy estrogen metabolite ratios.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "EstroFactors provides targeted nutritional support for estrogen metabolism and balance with I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Best suited for women with estrogen dominance patterns, PMS, perimenopausal hormone imbalance, or those needing broader estrogen metabolism support during hormonal transitions. Differs from Estrovera in that it addresses the underlying estrogen metabolism rather than vasomotor symptom relief.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected; Irritability reported; Age-appropriate for estrogen metabolism support", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement helps your body process and balance estrogen more effectively. It supports healthy hormone metabolism, which can help with PMS symptoms, bloating, mood changes, and hormonal balance during life transitions.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected", "Irritability reported", "Age-appropriate for estrogen metabolism support"]}, {"dose": "2 capsules twice daily", "name": "AdvaClear®", "caution": "Broad-spectrum phase I/II liver detoxification support. Contains NAC, green tea extract, milk thistle, and B-vitamins for biotransformation. Best taken with food.", "category": "detox", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "AdvaClear provides broad-spectrum phase I and phase II liver detoxification support with NAC, green tea extract, watercress, and B-vitamins. Designed for metabolic clearance support, biotransformation of hormones and environmental compounds, and hepatic stress. Appropriate when liver markers are borderline, estrogen clearance is sluggish, or metabolic dysfunction is present.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance pattern (hepatic detox pathways needed for clearance); Low energy (biotransformation burden); Brain fog (toxin clearance burden)", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement supports your liver's natural detoxification processes. Your results suggest your body could benefit from help processing hormones, metabolic byproducts, and other compounds. It provides nutrients that support both phases of your liver's detox pathways.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance pattern (hepatic detox pathways needed for clearance)", "Low energy (biotransformation burden)", "Brain fog (toxin clearance burden)"]}, {"dose": "1 soft chew during times of stress", "name": "HerWellness™ Rapid Stress Relief", "caution": "Fast-acting L-Theanine and Lactium formula. Non-drowsy. Promotes calm within 1 hour. Contains milk.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "low", "rationale": "HerWellness Rapid Stress Relief provides fast-acting stress support with L-Theanine (200mg) and Lactium. Promotes calm within 1 hour without drowsiness.", "indication": "Irritability reported", "phenotypes": ["Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation"], "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This fast-acting supplement helps your body respond to stress more calmly. It promotes relaxation within about an hour and can be taken as needed without causing drowsiness.", "supportingFindings": ["Irritability reported"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 11, "apoBValue": 121, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "borderline", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 0.7720114329249718, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": true}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "strongly_indicated", "indicated": true, "rationale": "LDL-C ≥190 mg/dL (204 mg/dL). High-intensity statin therapy strongly indicated per ACC/AHA guidelines. Consider familial hypercholesterolemia evaluation."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "consider", "rationale": "Lipid levels meet threshold for CAC consideration. CAC may be useful if risk level is uncertain or patient is hesitant about statin.", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\n- Your labs show a few important concerns to address right away and several reassuring results. Key issues: very high LDL cholesterol (204 mg/dL), vitamin D below optimal at 23.1 ng/mL, elevated total testosterone 82.9 ng/dL and free testosterone 8 pg/mL, and low progesterone 1 ng/mL with FSH 10.5 mIU/mL suggesting early perimenopause and luteal/progesterone deficiency. Your thyroid (TSH 0.96 mIU/L), iron stores (ferritin 97.7 ng/mL), hemoglobin 15.1 g/dL, glucose control (A1c 5.1%), and liver and kidney tests are within normal ranges.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Ferritin 97.7 ng/mL (good iron stores).\\n- TSH 0.96 mIU/L (normal thyroid function).\\n- Hemoglobin 15.1 g/dL, platelets 249 K/uL, eGFR 82 mL/min (normal).\\n- hs-CRP 0.4 mg/L (low inflammation).\\n- Lipoprotein(a) 11 mg/dL (optimal).\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + actions)\\n1. LDL cholesterol 204 mg/dL (very high). Your report recommends a HIGH‑INTENSITY statin (Atorvastatin 40–80 mg or Rosuvastatin 20–40 mg). Please contact your clinician urgently to discuss starting a statin. Lifestyle actions now:\\n   - Add 25–30 g fiber daily: try oatmeal for breakfast, beans with lunch, and vegetables at dinner.\\n   - Include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n   - Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter and cheese.\\n   - Daily exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking (or equivalent) most days.\\n   - Aim to recheck lipids 4–12 weeks after starting medication or after focused diet/exercise changes.\\n\\n2. Vitamin D (25-OH) 23.1 ng/mL (suboptimal for bone and hormonal health).\\n   - Start vitamin D3 1,000–2,000 IU daily (discuss exact dose with your clinician).\\n   - Add weight-bearing exercise 30 minutes most days and ensure ~1,000 mg calcium daily from diet/supplement.\\n   - Recheck vitamin D in 8–12 weeks.\\n\\n3. Sex hormones: Progesterone 1 ng/mL, Estradiol 119 pg/mL, FSH 10.5 mIU/mL — consistent with early perimenopause and luteal progesterone deficiency. Free testosterone 8 pg/mL and total testosterone 82.9 ng/dL are elevated and may cause acne, hair thinning, or excess hair.\\n   - Track menstrual cycles and consider a mid-luteal progesterone test (about 7 days before expected period) to confirm ovulation.\\n   - Lifestyle to help androgens: prioritize 7–8 hours quality sleep, include strength training 2–3× weekly, and manage stress with meditation/exercise/hobbies.\\n   - Discuss with your clinician options for symptom relief (progesterone therapy, further hormonal evaluation, or specialist referral).\\n\\nOther notes\\n- Hematocrit 46.9% slightly high; try rechecking after good hydration in the morning.\\n- Next steps: urgent clinician review for statin decision, start vitamin D, track cycle and repeat mid-luteal progesterone, consider endocrine/GYN follow-up for elevated testosterone and perimenopause symptoms. We’ll support you through each step.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 15.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 46.9, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Slightly elevated hematocrit - may indicate dehydration.", "recommendation": "Encourage hydration. Repeat if persistent.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 249, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 119, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 10.5, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 82.9, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Elevated testosterone for females.", "recommendation": "Evaluate for PCOS, adrenal hyperplasia, or androgen-secreting tumor.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 0.96, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 97.7, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 23.1, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D deficiency (21-30 ng/mL). Suboptimal for hormone and bone health.", "recommendation": "Repletion: Metagenics D3 5,000 + K daily. Target ≥60 ng/mL. Recheck in 8-12 weeks.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 645, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 204, "status": "abnormal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Very high LDL - high cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Statin therapy recommended. Intensive lifestyle changes.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 51, "status": "borderline", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "HDL could be higher for optimal protection.", "recommendation": "Increase exercise. Moderate alcohol if appropriate.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 92, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 1.0443587568500026, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 1.0% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years\\n\\nSEVERE HYPERCHOLESTEROLEMIA: High-intensity statin therapy indicated (LDL >=190 mg/dL)", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.7720114329249718, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 0.8%", "recommendation": "HIGH-INTENSITY statin (Atorvastatin 40-80 mg or Rosuvastatin 20-40 mg) - LDL >=190 mg/dL", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.35088812435596944, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.4%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 8.521425403116261, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 8.5%, ASCVD 5.8%, Heart Failure 3.6%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 19, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 15, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 82, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 121, "status": "borderline", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Borderline elevated Apo B.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider pharmacotherapy if high ASCVD risk.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 11, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 331, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 89, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 7.32, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 0.4, "status": "normal", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Low cardiovascular inflammation risk.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1, "status": "borderline", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Low Progesterone (Phase Unknown)", "interpretation": "Progesterone is 1 ng/mL. Without cycle phase documentation, interpretation is limited. If drawn in luteal phase, this would indicate anovulation - a perimenopause marker.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Document cycle phase for accurate interpretation. If patient is mid-cycle or luteal, this suggests anovulation. Consider symptoms: sleep disruption, anxiety, irregular cycles. PATIENT EDUCATION: We need to know where you are in your menstrual cycle to fully interpret this result. Please note when your last period started.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase; <5 ng/mL suggests anovulation if luteal"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Relative Progesterone Deficiency", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 119 pg/mL with progesterone of only 1 ng/mL indicates relative progesterone deficiency. The estrogen is not being adequately opposed by progesterone, which can lead to estrogen dominance symptoms.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Consider progesterone supplementation to balance the E2:P4 ratio. Micronized progesterone 100-200 mg nightly can provide adequate opposition. If patient has symptoms (insomnia, anxiety, heavy bleeding, breast tenderness), progesterone should help. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen level is good, but your progesterone is not keeping up. This imbalance can cause sleep problems, mood changes, and heavy periods. Progesterone supplementation can help restore balance.", "referenceRange": "E2 >100 should have P4 ≥8 for adequate opposition"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Luteal Progesterone Deficiency", "interpretation": "Progesterone of 1 ng/mL with estradiol of 119 pg/mL in a woman 41 years old indicates luteal progesterone deficiency. Estrogen production is adequate but progesterone is not keeping pace - a hallmark of perimenopause.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Micronized progesterone 100-200 mg nightly. Dosing can be cyclic (days 14-28) or continuous depending on cycle regularity. Progesterone provides GABAergic support for sleep and anxiety relief. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your body is making enough estrogen, but progesterone has dropped. This is very common in perimenopause and causes sleep disruption, anxiety, and mood changes. Progesterone supplementation supports your nervous system and helps restore calm, restful sleep.", "referenceRange": "P4 ≥5 ng/mL expected with E2 ≥60 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 10.5, "status": "borderline", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Early Perimenopause", "interpretation": "FSH of 10.5 mIU/mL with progesterone of 1 ng/mL in a 41-year-old indicates early perimenopause. The brain is starting to signal harder (rising FSH) because ovarian response is becoming unpredictable. This is the beginning of the transition.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Progesterone is foundational at this stage: micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly for sleep and nervous system support. Begin the hormone continuity discussion - frame HRT as future-forward planning, not crisis intervention. Nervous system calming messaging matters. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your body is entering the perimenopause transition. Your brain is working a little harder to communicate with your ovaries, and ovulation is becoming less consistent. This can cause sleep changes, anxiety, and cycle irregularities. Progesterone support now can smooth this transition and protect your quality of life.", "referenceRange": "FSH 8-15 with P4 <5 indicates early transition"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 84, "status": "normal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 84 nmol/L is within normal range. SHBG regulates bioavailable sex hormones by binding testosterone and estradiol.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 8, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 8 pg/mL is elevated. May cause androgenic symptoms (acne, hirsutism, hair thinning).", "recommendation": "Evaluate for PCOS, exogenous testosterone, or low SHBG driving elevated free T.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- Major cardiovascular red flag: LDL 204 mg/dL (≥190 mg/dL) — high atherogenic burden; ApoB 121 mg/dL borderline-high supporting need for pharmacotherapy.\\n- Reproductive endocrine: Evidence of early perimenopausal transition (FSH 10.5 mIU/mL) with relative luteal progesterone deficiency (P4 1 ng/mL) despite estradiol 119 pg/mL → functional estrogen:progesterone imbalance contributing to perimenopausal symptoms.\\n- Androgen excess: Total testosterone 82.9 ng/dL and free T 8 pg/mL — elevated for a female; requires evaluation for PCOS, exogenous androgen exposure, adrenal source, or less commonly an androgen-secreting neoplasm.\\n- Vitamin D deficiency: 25-OH Vit D 23.1 ng/mL — repletion indicated.\\n- Hematocrit borderline-high (46.9%) — likely mild/dehydration but reassess.\\n- Most other labs (CBC indices, metabolic panel, LFTs, kidney function, lipoprotein(a), iron studies, TSH, A1c, hs-CRP) are within protocol normal limits.\\n\\nPriority actions (organized by CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL (physician notification required immediately)\\n- Notify physician now regarding LDL 204 mg/dL (≥190 mg/dL). Provider to consider initiation of high-intensity statin therapy per clinic protocol and to assess for familial hypercholesterolemia.\\n- Before statin start: confirm pregnancy status (β-hCG) in women of childbearing potential and discuss contraception; statins contraindicated in pregnancy.\\n\\nURGENT (notify/act within 7 days)\\n- Endocrine evaluation plan for androgen excess: notify provider to order targeted testing and consider endocrine referral if clinically indicated (see Additional testing/referrals).\\n- Initiate vitamin D repletion per protocol (Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily) and schedule recheck 8–12 weeks.\\n- Document menstrual cycle day and date of last menstrual period in chart immediately to correctly interpret progesterone and plan progesterone therapy timing.\\n\\nROUTINE (address within 2–6 weeks)\\n- Hematocrit 46.9%: advise hydration and repeat CBC in 4 weeks. If persistent elevation or rising Hct, evaluate for secondary causes (smoking, exogenous androgens, OSA, pulmonary disease) and notify provider.\\n- Begin progesterone therapy discussion and plan (provider decision required) given relative progesterone deficiency and early perimenopause. See dosing guidance below.\\n- Lifestyle counseling for LDL lowering (diet, exercise, weight, tobacco cessation) to start immediately.\\n\\nSpecific medication/dose recommendations and monitoring (staff-facing)\\nStatin therapy (physician to order)\\n- Preferred initial options for LDL ≥190 mg/dL (unless contraindicated):\\n  - Atorvastatin 40–80 mg nightly OR\\n  - Rosuvastatin 20–40 mg nightly\\n- Baseline labs: use current AST/ALT (normal) as baseline; obtain pregnancy test if applicable prior to initiation.\\n- Monitoring: fasting lipid panel 6–12 weeks after initiation or dose change; then every 3–12 months once stable. Repeat LFTs at baseline and at 3 months only if symptomatic or clinician preference; otherwise annually per protocol.\\n- If LDL reduction inadequate on max tolerated statin, consider ezetimibe adjunct, PCSK9 inhibitor referral, or lipid specialist referral. Consider genetic testing/referral for familial hypercholesterolemia given LDL ≥190, especially if age <50 or family history of premature ASCVD.\\n\\nProgesterone (provider to prescribe; staff to prepare patient education)\\n- Recommended starting regimen (provider discretion based on cycle regularity and symptoms):\\n  - Micronized progesterone 100 mg orally nightly as first-line; increase to 200 mg nightly if insomnia/anxiety symptoms persist or if symptomatic response needed.\\n  - Dosing strategy: cyclic progesterone (days ~14–28) for women with (mostly) ovulatory cycles or irregular cycles where bleeding control desired; continuous nightly dosing may be used for persistent sleep/anxiety symptoms or anovulatory cycles.\\n- Monitoring and follow-up: symptom review at 6–8 weeks. Document cycle day and consider mid-luteal progesterone recheck (≈7 days post-ovulation) if confirmation of ovulation or titration needed.\\n\\nAndrogen management (if symptomatic) — evaluation precedes initiation of anti-androgenic therapy\\n- Do NOT start systemic anti-androgen therapy without provider review and appropriate reproductive counseling.\\n- If patient has bothersome acne/hirsutism and no pregnancy desire, provider may consider combined estrogen-containing OCP (if no contraindication) ± spironolactone:\\n  - Spironolactone 50–100 mg daily initially, titrate to 100–200 mg/day divided as needed (monitor potassium/renal function; avoid in pregnancy).\\n- If patient is trying to conceive, avoid anti-androgens and manage fertility planning with provider.\\n\\nVitamin D repletion\\n- Start Metagenics D3 5,000 IU daily + Vitamin K per clinic protocol.\\n- Recheck 25-OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks; target ≥60 ng/mL per clinic note.\\n- Check baseline serum calcium if clinically indicated (history of hypercalcemia or granulomatous disease).\\n\\nLifestyle interventions (staff to counsel and document)\\n- Cardiovascular risk/lipid lowering:\\n  - Intensive dietary counseling: reduce saturated fat, trans fats, refined carbs; recommend Mediterranean or DASH-style diet; refer to dietician if available.\\n  - Physical activity: target ≥150 min/week moderate aerobic exercise + 2 strength sessions/week.\\n  - Weight loss goal if overweight: 5–10% body weight reduction improves LDL and androgen status.\\n  - Smoking cessation counseling and resources if applicable.\\n  - Alcohol moderation.\\n- For androgen excess/perimenopause symptoms:\\n  - Sleep hygiene, stress reduction, regular exercise, and weight optimization to improve symptoms and metabolic profile.\\n- Vitamin D: advise taking with a fat-containing meal for absorption; counsel on adherence.\\n\\nAdditional testing and referrals to consider (staff to order per provider)\\n- Immediate/near-term labs:\\n  - Repeat fasting morning total and free testosterone (AM, fasting), document cycle day. Consider repeating to confirm elevation.\\n  - DHEA-S and 17-hydroxyprogesterone (AM samples) — to evaluate adrenal source/nonclassic CAH.\\n  - SHBG was reported normal; if not recently measured, confirm.\\n  - If imaging indicated: pelvic ultrasound (ovaries) and/or adrenal imaging (CT) if clinical concern for tumor or testosterone markedly elevated or rapid virilization.\\n  - Pregnancy test prior to statin or any anti-androgen therapy if of childbearing potential.\\n- Referrals:\\n  - Endocrinology if testosterone persistently elevated, markedly elevated (>150–200 ng/dL), or with virilization (rapid onset of voice changes, clitoromegaly, severe hirsutism).\\n  - Gynecology for management of perimenopausal bleeding patterns, HRT planning, and contraception counseling if statin planned.\\n  - Lipid clinic / cardiology if LDL remains >160 on therapy, family history of premature ASCVD, or suspected familial hypercholesterolemia.\\n  - Dietitian for intensive LDL and weight management support.\\n\\nWhen to escalate to urgent specialist evaluation\\n- Rapid virilization or total testosterone markedly elevated (>150–200 ng/dL) — urgent endocrine/imaging work-up.\\n- LD L remains very high despite maximal therapy or patient has family history of premature CVD — refer to lipid specialist.\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (staff action checklist)\\n- Immediate (today):\\n  - Notify provider re: LDL 204 mg/dL and need for statin discussion; confirm pregnancy test if applicable.\\n  - Document last menstrual period/cycle day in chart.\\n  - Start vitamin D repletion (Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K) and counsel on adherence.\\n  - Hydration advice and plan to repeat CBC in 4 weeks.\\n- Within 1 week:\\n  - Provider to decide on and, if agreed, prescribe high-intensity statin (atorvastatin 40–80 mg or rosuvastatin 20–40 mg) and counsel regarding pregnancy risk/contraception.\\n  - Order repeat morning fasting total + free testosterone, DHEA-S, 17-OH progesterone; order pregnancy test if indicated.\\n- 4–8 weeks:\\n  - Check adherence and symptoms for progesterone if started; symptom review at 6–8 weeks.\\n  - Repeat CBC for hematocrit if hydration recommended.\\n- 6–12 weeks:\\n  - Recheck fasting lipid panel 6–12 weeks after statin initiation or sooner if directed.\\n  - Recheck 25-OH vitamin D at 8–12 weeks; adjust dosing to reach target ≥60 ng/mL.\\n- Ongoing:\\n  - Monitor LFTs per protocol and symptom response to progesterone.\\n  - Consider specialty referrals (endocrinology, cardiology/lipid clinic, gynecology) as above.\\n\\nStaff education points to cover with patient (to be delivered by clinician/staff per protocol)\\n- Explain LDL result and need for prompt discussion about statin therapy; statins contraindicated in pregnancy — confirm pregnancy status and contraception plans prior to starting.\\n- Explain vitamin D repletion plan, dosing, expected recheck timeline, and to take with food.\\n- Request documentation of cycle day and last menstrual period to guide hormone interpretation and therapy timing.\\n- Advise on hydration and plan to repeat CBC to confirm hematocrit.\\n- Ask about symptoms of androgen excess (acne, hirsutism, hair thinning, voice changes) and any use of androgenic supplements/medications.\\n\\nKey thresholds and red flags for staff escalation\\n- LDL ≥190 mg/dL — immediate physician notification (done).\\n- Rapid virilization or total testosterone >150–200 ng/dL — urgent endocrine referral and imaging.\\n- Pregnancy positive — do not start statin or anti-androgen therapy; notify provider.\\n\\nNotes and clinical caveats\\n- Progesterone and testosterone interpretation are cycle-phase dependent — ensure cycle day is documented for any repeat testing.\\n- Do not start or adjust therapies (statin, progesterone, anti-androgens) without physician authorization; this guidance is for staff preparation and monitoring.\\n- All medication prescriptions require provider sign-off and contraception counseling where teratogenicity is relevant.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 1.8, "detail": "Triglycerides 92 / HDL 51 = 1.8", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5.1%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 84, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 15, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "121 mg/dL", "detail": "Elevated atherogenic particle count", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "0.4 mg/L", "detail": "Low inflammatory burden", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 1, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (1/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Lab pattern consistent with relative estrogen excess or impaired estrogen clearance. Even without reported symptoms, supporting liver detoxification and estrogen metabolism pathways may be beneficial.", "supportingFindings": ["Estradiol:Progesterone imbalance (E2 119, P4 1)", "Progesterone 1 ng/mL (very low)", "Irritability reported"]}, {"name": "Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Pattern consistent with HPA-axis stress and cortisol dysregulation. Chronic stress depletes DHEA-S, disrupts sleep, and impairs cognitive and emotional function.", "supportingFindings": ["Irritability reported", "Low energy / fatigue reported", "Brain fog reported", "Vitamin D 23.1 ng/mL (low, stress resilience