o new virilization, erythrocytosis symptoms, or thrombotic events).\\n- Medications/supplements: medication list reviewed (no prescription testosterone documented per chart). Patient is taking routine vitamins; specific supplements reviewed. (Staff to confirm and reconcile med list in chart.)\\n- Allergies: none documented in lab report.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: Not available in lab dataset / not documented today.\\n- CBC:\\n  - Hemoglobin: 14.7 g/dL — normal\\n  - Hematocrit: 45.2 % — BORDERLINE (high‑normal; may reflect mild hemoconcentration/dehydration)\\n  - Platelets: 285 K/uL — normal\\n- Basic chemistries / renal & liver:\\n  - Creatinine: 0.64 mg/dL — normal\\n  - eGFR: 113 mL/min/1.73 m2 — normal\\n  - AST: 17 U/L — normal\\n  - ALT: 14 U/L — normal\\n- Lipid profile / atherogenic markers:\\n  - HDL Cholesterol: 49 mg/dL — ABNORMAL (low for female; desirable >50 mg/dL)\\n  - LDL Cholesterol: 102 mg/dL — BORDERLINE (near‑optimal)\\n  - Triglycerides: 70 mg/dL — normal\\n  - Apolipoprotein B: 87 mg/dL — normal\\n- Endocrine / vitamins:\\n  - TSH: 1.02 mIU/L — normal\\n  - Vitamin D (25‑OH): 66.4 ng/mL — normal\\n  - Vitamin B12: 592 pg/mL — normal\\n- Risk scores (AHA PREVENT 2023 / STOP‑BANG):\\n  - 10‑year Total CVD risk (PREVENT): 0.53% — low\\n  - 10‑year ASCVD risk (PREVENT): 0.38% — low\\n  - 10‑year Heart Failure risk (PREVENT): 0.26% — low\\n  - 30‑year CVD risk (PREVENT): 4.72%\\n  - STOP‑BANG (sleep apnea risk): 0 points — low\\n- Other: No red flag/critical lab values reported.\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Low HDL cholesterol — 49 mg/dL (female; borderline low). Clinical significance: modestly increased cardiovascular risk marker. Triglycerides and ApoB are normal. Overall calculated 10‑yr ASCVD risk remains low.\\n   - Differential considerations: genetic baseline low HDL, suboptimal aerobic fitness, smoking (patient denies), diet, weight status. Pharmacologic HDL elevation not routinely recommended.\\n2. Borderline/high‑normal hematocrit — 45.2%. Clinical significance: may represent mild hemoconcentration/dehydration or other causes (smoking, exogenous androgens/testosterone, chronic hypoxia, polycythemia etiologies).\\n   - Differential considerations: transient dehydration, recent strenuous exercise, exogenous testosterone therapy (patient denies), smoking, sleep apnea (STOP‑BANG low), undiagnosed secondary causes less likely given normal hemoglobin and lack of symptoms.\\n   - Red flag: None at present. If symptomatic (headache, visual changes, dizziness, thrombotic symptoms) or if Hct rises to clinic thresholds (≥48% female → notify physician; ≥52–54% → hold testosterone and urgent review), urgent evaluation required.\\n3. Borderline LDL cholesterol — 102 mg/dL. Clinical significance: near‑optimal; with current low 10‑yr ASCVD risk, statin therapy not indicated. Lifestyle measures recommended.\\n4. Overall cardiovascular risk low per AHA PREVENT 2023 (10‑yr Total CVD 0.5%, ASCVD 0.4%) — continue risk­-factor modification and routine monitoring.\\n5. Other labs within normal limits (Hgb 14.7 g/dL, platelets 285 K/uL, creatinine 0.64 mg/dL, eGFR 113, AST/ALT normal, TSH normal, Vit D and B12 adequate).\\n\\nPLAN:\\n1. Low HDL (49 mg/dL)\\n   - Counseling: Advise aerobic exercise ≥150 minutes/week (moderate) or ≥75 minutes/week (vigorous) plus strength training 2×/week; Mediterranean‑style diet, increase soluble fiber, reduce refined carbohydrates and trans fats; alcohol moderation; smoking cessation if applicable.\\n   - Consider referral to nutritionist if dietary support desired.\\n   - Pharmacotherapy: Niacin or other HDL-targeted drugs are not routinely recommended. Statin therapy NOT indicated at current ASCVD risk.\\n   - Recheck: Lipid panel (fasting or non‑fasting per clinic protocol) in 6–12 months. Consider earlier (3 months) if patient initiates active lifestyle interventions or weight loss program.\\n   - Supplement: Recommend NutraGems® CoQ10 300 (1 chewable gel daily) as per clinic high‑priority supplement guidance.\\n\\n2. Borderline hematocrit (45.2%)\\n   - Immediate actions:\\n     - Instruct patient to increase oral hydration; avoid heavy exercise or dehydration prior to repeat CBC.\\n     - Confirm medication/supplement review: verify no exogenous testosterone, erythropoietic agents, or other agents that may raise Hct. (Staff to document current med list and smoking status.)\\n   - Lab follow‑up: Order CBC with differential (Hgb/Hct) in 2–4 weeks post‑hydration to assess persistence/change.\\n   - Contingency actions:\\n     - If Hct ≥48% (female) on repeat → urgent notification to prescribing clinician for review.\\n     - If Hct ≥52–54% → follow clinic protocol: hold testosterone (if on therapy), urgent physician notification and consider hematology referral.\\n   - Additional testing: Consider ferritin if history of heavy menses or clinical suspicion of iron deficiency despite normal Hgb.\\n\\n3. Borderline LDL (102 mg/dL) and overall CVD risk\\n   - Reinforce lifestyle modification as above for HDL; weight management if BMI elevated.\\n   - No statin initiation at this time given low 10‑yr ASCVD risk; document shared decision‑making and re‑evaluate if LDL rises or risk score changes.\\n   - Reassess lipids in 6–12 months or sooner if clinical circumstances change.\\n\\n4. Medication reconciliation and hormone therapy monitoring\\n   - Document and update medication list today; specifically confirm absence/presence of testosterone therapy, OCP/HRT, herbal supplements.\\n   - If patient is on testosterone therapy: notify prescribing clinician of current Hct 45.2% and plan for repeat CBC in 2–4 weeks; no dose change required at present per clinic thresholds.\\n   - Education provided regarding signs/symptoms that require immediate contact (thrombotic symptoms, severe headache, visual changes, neurological deficits).\\n\\n5. Other actions / monitoring\\n   - Routine labs otherwise within normal limits; no acute intervention required.\\n   - If ferritin indicated by menstrual history, order ferritin at next visit.\\n   - Provide smoking cessation resources if applicable.\\n   - Documented discussion of lifestyle recommendations and provided education on hydration and follow‑up testing.\\n\\n6. Orders placed / follow‑up timeline\\n   - CBC with differential (Hgb/Hct) — repeat in 2–4 weeks (post‑hydration).\\n   - Lipid panel — routine recheck in 6–12 months; optional recheck at 3 months if active interventions started.\\n   - Ferritin — as indicated based on menstrual history or clinical suspicion.\\n   - Medication reconciliation completed today; verify and document any exogenous androgen use.\\n   - Flag chart for testosterone monitoring protocol if patient is on therapy.\\n   - Routine clinic follow‑up: 3–6 months (or sooner if labs change or symptoms develop).\\n\\nAdministrative / education notes:\\n- Reviewed cardiovascular risk (AHA PREVENT 2023): 10‑yr total CVD 0.5%, 10‑yr ASCVD 0.4% — low. Discussed that statin therapy is generally not indicated at this risk level; emphasis on lifestyle modification.\\n- Provided patient education regarding mild hematocrit elevation, hydration, lifestyle measures to improve HDL, and when to seek urgent care.\\n- Supplement recommendation: NutraGems® CoQ10 300, 1 chewable gel daily (high‑priority per clinic guidance).\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow‑up as above.\\n\\n03/10/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": true, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "preventRisk": {"ldlGoal": "No specific LDL target; focus on risk-based statin intensity", "riskCategory": "low", "tenYearASCVD": 0.0037896110467327384, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "Heart