 improved with simple lifestyle steps.\\n\\nPositive findings (specific values)\\n- 10-year ASCVD risk: 0.4%; 10-year total CVD risk: 0.5%; 30-year CVD risk: 4.7% — LOW risk.\\n- LDL cholesterol: 102 mg/dL (near optimal).\\n- Triglycerides: 70 mg/dL (normal).\\n- Hemoglobin: 14.7 g/dL (normal for adult females).\\n- Platelets: 285 K/uL (normal).\\n- TSH: 1.02 mIU/L (euthyroid).\\n- Vitamin D (25-OH): 66.4 ng/mL (optimal range 60–100 ng/mL).\\n- Vitamin B12: 592 pg/mL (normal).\\n- Liver tests: AST 17 U/L and ALT 14 U/L (normal).\\n- Kidney: eGFR 113 mL/min, Creatinine 0.64 mg/dL (normal).\\n- Apolipoprotein B: 87 mg/dL (optimal).\\n- Sleep apnea risk (STOP-BANG): 0 points (low risk).\\n\\nAreas to improve (exact values + concrete actions)\\n1. HDL cholesterol: 49 mg/dL (marked as lower than ideal). Actions to raise protective HDL and keep heart risk low:\\n   - Add 25–30 g fiber daily: try oatmeal for breakfast, beans or lentils at lunch, and large servings of vegetables with dinner.\\n   - Include 2–3 servings of fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n   - Reduce saturated fat: choose lean cuts, swap butter for olive oil, limit high-fat cheeses.\\n   - Daily exercise: 30 minutes of brisk walking or equivalent most days.\\n2. Hematocrit: 45.2% (slightly elevated; often dehydration). Actions:\\n   - Increase fluid intake to about 2–2.5 L/day (more with exercise or heat).\\n   - Recheck CBC after improving hydration; if persistent, discuss causes (smoking, high altitude, or other).\\n\\nAdditional women’s health notes\\n- Bone health: Vitamin D is good at 66.4 ng/mL. Continue weight-bearing exercise 2–3x/week and aim for 1,000–1,200 mg calcium/day from diet/supplement as needed.\\n- Iron: Hemoglobin 14.7 g/dL is normal; no iron treatment needed now.\\n- Thyroid: TSH 1.02 mIU/L is normal.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Start the lifestyle steps above now. Recheck a fasting lipid panel and hematocrit in 6–12 months (sooner if you have symptoms). Continue annual preventive care with your clinician. If you want help building a meal plan, exercise routine, or have questions about supplements or hormones, plan a follow-up visit in the next 1–3 months.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 14.7, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 45.2, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Slightly elevated hematocrit - may indicate dehydration.", "recommendation": "Encourage hydration. Repeat if persistent.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 285, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.02, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 66.4, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D optimal (60-100 ng/mL). Supports hormone, bone, and immune health.", "recommendation": "Maintain current regimen. Monitor annually.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 592, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 102, "status": "borderline", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Near optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet and exercise.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 49, "status": "abnormal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Low HDL - increased cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Increase aerobic exercise. Consider niacin if very low.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 70, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.5291368631406987, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 0.5% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.3789611046732738, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 0.4%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.2579273408996752, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.3%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 4.71817966075982, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 4.7%, ASCVD 3.1%, Heart Failure 2.7%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 17, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 14, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 113, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.64, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 87, "status": "normal", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Apo B at optimal level.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- No acute red flags identified. Most labs within normal limits.\\n- Abnormal/borderline results summarized:\\n  - Hematocrit 45.2% — high‑normal/borderline. Most likely mild hemoconcentration/dehydration; requires repeat check and correlation with symptoms, medications, smoking status, and testosterone therapy status.\\n  - LDL 102 mg/dL — near‑optimal; no immediate pharmacologic indication given reported low 10‑yr CVD risk.\\n  - HDL 49 mg/dL — slightly low for a female (desirable >50 mg/dL); modestly increased CV risk marker but triglycerides and ApoB are normal.\\n\\nPRIORITY ACTIONS (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\n- CRITICAL\\n  - None required at present.\\n- URGENT\\n  - Hematocrit considerations:\\n    - If patient is symptomatic (headache, visual changes, dizziness, thrombosis) or reports new polycythemia risk factors (smoking, exogenous androgens), notify physician immediately.\\n    - If patient is on testosterone therapy, treat this as potentially therapy‑related and notify prescribing clinician per clinic testosterone monitoring protocol (see below).\\n- ROUTINE\\n  - Hydration: instruct patient to increase oral fluids prior to repeat CBC.\\n  - Repeat testing schedule:\\n    - CBC (Hct/Hgb) in 2–4 weeks after hydration to confirm whether elevation persists.\\n    - Lipid panel (fasting or non‑fasting per clinic protocol) in 6–12 months; sooner (3 months) if initiating lifestyle interventions or medication changes.\\n    - Consider targeted recheck of HDL and LDL at 3–6 months if active lifestyle interventions are started.\\n\\nTestosterone dose recommendations (if applicable)\\n- If patient is NOT on testosterone therapy: no testosterone dose action needed.\\n- If patient IS on testosterone therapy:\\n  - Current Hct 45.2% — no immediate dose reduction required.\\n  - Clinic notification thresholds (staff action):\\n    - Notify physician if Hct ≥48% (female threshold for closer review).\\n    - If Hct ≥52–54% (per clinic/heme protocol), stop testosterone pending physician review and consider hematology consult.\\n  - Typical interim actions to prepare physician decision:\\n    - Repeat CBC in 2–4 weeks.\\n    - Confirm hydration, smoking status, OCP/HRT use, recent altitude travel, or other causes.\\n    - If Hct rises and physician elects to modify therapy: follow clinic-specific dosing algorithm (e.g., reduce dose or increase interval) and document rationale — do not change dose without prescriber order.\\n\\nLifestyle interventions (specific, staff‑deliverable)\\n- Hydration: advise increased oral fluids and recheck CBC after 2–4 weeks.\\n- Cardiovascular risk modification:\\n  - Aerobic exercise ≥150 minutes/week (moderate) or ≥75 minutes/week (vigorous); strength training 2×/week.\\n  - Diet: Mediterranean‑style diet, increase soluble fiber (oats, legumes), reduce refined carbohydrates and trans fats.\\n  - Weight management: aim for 5–10% weight loss if BMI elevated.\\n  - Smoking cessation counseling and resources if current or recent smoker (nicotine replacement/cessation program referral).\\n  - Alcohol moderation (limit to ≤1 standard drink/day).\\n- HDL‑specific:\\n  - Emphasize regular aerobic exercise and smoking cessation as first‑line HDL raising strategies.\\n  - Discuss referral to nutritionist if dietary modification is needed.\\n  - Pharmacologic HDL therapy (niacin) not recommended routinely—consider only after physician evaluation and if HDL substantially low with other risk factors; be aware of adverse effects.\\n\\nAdditional testing or referrals to consider\\n- CBC with differential and repeat Hct in 2–4 weeks (after hydration).\\n- Ferritin if history of heavy menses or clinical suspicion for iron deficiency—even though Hgb is normal.\\n- Review medications and supplements (including testosterone, estrogen, OCPs, herbal products) that may affect Hct or lipids.\\n- If on testosterone and Hct rises: consider hematology referral per clinic protocol.\\n- If multiple CV risk factors or rising LDL/ASCVD risk on follow‑up: consider primary care or cardiology referral.\\n- If patient reports sleep symptoms despite STOP‑BANG low risk, consider sleep medicine referral as indicated.\\n\\nPatient education points for staff to cover\\n- Explain that hematocrit is slightly high but likely due to low fluid status; recommend hydration and repeat testing.\\n- Counsel on lifestyle steps to raise HDL and maintain LDL near goal (exercise, diet, smoking cessation).\\n- If on hormone therapy: explain the need for monitoring CBCs and possible adjustments if Hct rises.\\n- Advise to report any thrombotic symptoms, significant headaches, visual changes, or new neurologic symptoms promptly.\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (clear action items for staff)\\n1. Immediate (today)\\n   - Document review of current medications (testosterone, OCP/HRT), smoking status, and symptoms.\\n   - Provide hydration counseling to patient.\\n   - Flag chart for repeat CBC/Hct in 2–4 weeks.\\n   - If patient is on testosterone, notify prescribing clinician of Hct 45.2% and current plan to recheck in 2–4 weeks (no dose change required now).\\n2. Short term (2–6 weeks)\\n   - Repeat CBC with Hct; if Hct increases to ≥48% → urgent physician notification. If ≥52–54% → hold testosterone per protocol and notify physician immediately; consider hematology referral.\\n   - Begin/coach lifestyle interventions for HDL improvement and document counseling.\\n3. Medium term (3 months)\\n   - Reassess lipid panel if active interventions started or earlier if clinically indicated; otherwise routine lipid follow up at 6–12 months.\\n   - If ferritin indicated by menstrual history, obtain at next visit.\\n4. Ongoing\\n   - Continue routine monitoring per clinic protocols for patients on HRT/testosterone and for cardiovascular risk management.\\n\\nPhysician notification thresholds for staff\\n- Immediate notification: symptomatic patient or new concerning signs (see above).\\n- Urgent notification: Hct ≥48% (female) or rapidly rising Hct.\\n- Immediate hold and hematology notification: Hct ≥52–54% (follow clinic/hematology protocol).\\n\\nNote: Interpret hormone labs (if obtained later) in context of menstrual cycle phase and current HRT/birth control status. Follow clinic specific testosterone dosing algorithms for any dose adjustments — do not alter dosing without prescriber order.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 1.43, "detail": "Triglycerides 70 / HDL 49 = 1.43", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 14, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "87 mg/dL", "detail": "Normal atherogenic particle count", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": []}	2026-03-10 19:42:13.001199	\N
23	Leigh Wimberly 	female	2025-12-31 00:00:00	{"a1c": 5.1, "alt": 15, "ast": 19, "bun": 15, "co2": 18, "fsh": 10.5, "hdl": 51, "ldl": 204, "lpa": 11, "rbc": 4.99, "tsh": 0.96, "wbc": 8.1, "acne": false, "apoB": 121, "egfr": 82, "iron": 89, "shbg": 84, "tibc": 331, "hsCRP": 0.4, "onHRT": false, "folate": 7.32, "freeT4": 1.21, "sodium": 138, "albumin": 4.5, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 9.5, "glucose": 86, "bloating": false, "brainFog": true, "chloride": 107, "ferritin": 97.7, "hairLoss": false, "vitaminD": 23.1, "bilirubin": 0.4, "estradiol": 119, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": true, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 249, "potassium": 4.1, "creatinine": 0.9, "hematocrit": 46.9, "hemoglobin": 15.1, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "vitaminB12": 645, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2025-12-31", "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Leigh Wimberly ", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 41, "bmi": 35, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 108, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": true, "progesterone": 1, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 82.9, "totalProtein": 7.7, "lowMotivation": false, "triglycerides": 92, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "freeTestosterone": 8, "totalCholesterol": 273}	{"redFlags": [{"action": "Flag provider for consideration of statin therapy. Intensive lifestyle counseling.", "message": "LDL is 204 mg/dL (≥190 threshold).", "category": "Lipids - Severe LDL Elevation", "severity": "warning"}], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History/Symptoms: Patient is a 41-year-old female presenting for lab review and hormone assessment. Reports irregular menses consistent with perimenopausal symptoms (cycle timing not documented in lab submission). No acute chest pain, dyspnea, focal neurologic symptoms, or signs of acute illness reported in chart. Possible symptoms to assess further given labs: androgenic symptoms (acne, hirsutism, hair thinning), sleep disturbance, mood lability, abnormal uterine bleeding — patient counseled to report if present.\\n- Reproductive status: Presumed premenopausal/early perimenopausal based on labs; pregnancy status not documented — pregnancy test required prior to initiation of statin or hormone therapy.\\n- Medications/supplements: Patient reportedly taking clinic-recommended supplements (NutraGems CoQ10, D3 5,000 + K, magnesium L-threonate, EstroFactors™, AdvaClear®) per plan; confirm current self-supplied androgenic supplements or exogenous testosterone (explicitly ask and document).\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: Not available in chart today (BP, HR, BMI not provided).\\n- CBC:\\n  - Hemoglobin 15.1 g/dL — WNL\\n  - Hematocrit 46.9 % — BORDERLINE high (possible dehydration) [46.9%]\\n  - Platelets 249 K/uL — WNL\\n- Basic Metabolic / Renal / Hepatic:\\n  - Creatinine 0.9 mg/dL — WNL\\n  - eGFR 82 mL/min/1.73m2 — WNL\\n  - AST 19 U/L — WNL\\n  - ALT 15 U/L — WNL\\n- Iron studies / Nutritional:\\n  - Ferritin 97.7 ng/mL — WNL\\n  - TIBC 331 ug/dL — WNL\\n  - Serum iron 89 ug/dL — WNL\\n  - Folate 7.32 ng/mL — WNL\\n  - Vitamin B12 645 pg/mL — WNL\\n- Glucose metabolism:\\n  - Hemoglobin A1c 5.1% — WNL\\n- Lipids:\\n  - Total cholesterol: (value not provided) — see components below\\n  - Triglycerides 92 mg/dL — WNL\\n  - HDL cholesterol 51 mg/dL — BORDERLINE (could be higher for optimal protection)\\n  - LDL cholesterol 204 mg/dL — ABNORMAL / CRITICAL (LDL ≥190 mg/dL)\\n  - Apolipoprotein B 121 mg/dL — BORDERLINE elevated\\n  - Lipoprotein(a) 11 mg/dL — WNL\\n- Hormones:\\n  - Estradiol 119 pg/mL — WNL (note: interpretation depends on cycle day)\\n  - Progesterone 1 ng/mL — ABNORMAL / LOW (relative and luteal progesterone deficiency)\\n  - FSH 10.5 mIU/mL — BORDERLINE (consistent with early perimenopause)\\n  - Total testosterone 82.9 ng/dL — ABNORMAL (elevated for female)\\n  - Free testosterone 8 pg/mL — ABNORMAL (elevated)\\n  - SHBG 84 nmol/L — WNL\\n- Other:\\n  - 25-OH Vitamin D 23.1 ng/mL — ABNORMAL (suboptimal / deficient per clinic target)\\n  - hs-CRP 0.4 mg/L — WNL\\n- Risk scores:\\n  - 10-year Total CVD risk (AHA PREVENT 2023): 1.0% — Low\\n  - 10-year ASCVD risk: 0.8% — Low\\n  - 10-year Heart Failure risk: 0.3% — Low\\n  - 30-year CVD risk: 8.5%\\n  - STOP-BANG sleep apnea risk: 0 points\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Markedly elevated LDL cholesterol — LDL 204 mg/dL (critical). Meets independent indication for high-intensity statin therapy (LDL ≥190 mg/dL). High atherosclerotic risk marker despite low 10-year ASCVD due to age; immediate provider review required. Red flag: provider to be notified now. Baseline LFTs within normal limits (AST 19, ALT 15). Pregnancy status unknown — document prior to statin initiation.\\n2. Biochemical hyperandrogenism — Total testosterone 82.9 ng/dL and free testosterone 8 pg/mL (both elevated). May represent functional ovarian/adrenal androgen excess or exogenous androgen exposure. Differential: PCOS spectrum (though age 41 and perimenopausal transition), adrenal source (DHEA-S, 17-OHP assessment), exogenous androgen intake, androgen-secreting tumor (less likely but consider if virilization or rapidly rising levels). Requires provider review and further workup.\\n3. Early perimenopausal hormonal pattern with relative luteal progesterone deficiency — Progesterone 1 ng/mL, Estradiol 119 pg/mL, FSH 10.5 mIU/mL. Findings consistent with early perimenopause and luteal phase progesterone deficiency; correlate with menstrual cycle day and symptoms. If drawn in luteal phase, progesterone of 1 ng/mL suggests anovulation.\\n4. Vitamin D insufficiency/deficiency — 25-OH vitamin D 23.1 ng/mL. Suboptimal for bone and hormone health per clinic target (aim ≥60 ng/mL). Recommend repletion.\\n5. Borderline elevated hematocrit — Hct 46.9%. Possible mild dehydration or physiologic variation. If persists, evaluate for secondary causes (androgen exposure, smoking, OSA).\\n6. Borderline elevated ApoB and suboptimal HDL — ApoB 121 mg/dL; HDL 51 mg/dL. Contributes to lipid risk profile and supports aggressive LDL-lowering approach.\\n7. Other labs within acceptable limits — Hemoglobin A1c 5.1%, renal and hepatic function normal, ferritin and B12 adequate, hs-CRP low. SHBG elevated-normal (84 nmol/L).\\n\\nPLAN:\\nGeneral actions (today / immediate)\\n- Physician notification: Notify supervising physician immediately regarding LDL 204 mg/dL and elevated testosterone (total and free). Documented per clinic protocol as CRITICAL findings requiring provider review.\\n- Pregnancy testing: Obtain and document urine or serum pregnancy test prior to initiation of statin or progesterone therapy. Confirm contraceptive counseling if statin is started.\\n- Confirm cycle timing: Contact patient to obtain date of last menstrual period and cycle day to accurately interpret progesterone/estradiol; document in chart prior to any hormone decisions.\\n\\nProblem-specific plans\\n1. LDL 204 mg/dL (CRITICAL)\\n   - Provider to consider initiating high-intensity statin unless contraindicated:\\n     - Recommended options: Atorvastatin 40–80 mg nightly OR Rosuvastatin 20–40 mg nightly.\\n   - Pre-initiation requirements: confirm negative pregnancy test and counsel on teratogenic risk; review current/planned pregnancy or breastfeeding.\\n   - Baseline labs: AST/ALT are available and normal (AST 19, ALT 15). Proceed per provider order.\\n   - Monitoring: fasting lipid panel and LFTs 6–8 weeks after statin initiation; thereafter every 3–12 months per response and tolerability.\\n   - Counseling: intensive lifestyle modification (see Lifestyle below). Consider dietitian referral. Consider cardiology referral if provider deems appropriate.\\n   - Document: patient advised that immediate provider review was requested and pregnancy test will be obtained prior to statin start.\\n\\n2. Elevated total and free testosterone (urgent)\\n   - Do not initiate testosterone therapy. If patient is taking exogenous testosterone, DHEA, or androgenic supplements — instruct immediate discontinuation and notify provider.\\n   - Order androgen workup:\\n     - Serum DHEA-S\\n     - 17-hydroxyprogesterone\\n     - Repeat morning total testosterone (fasting, early AM) and free testosterone — document cycle day\\n     - Consider ACTH stimulation if 17-OHP abnormal\\n   - Imaging/referral: If clinical virilization, rapidly rising testosterone, or markedly elevated values on repeat testing — urgent endocrine and/or gynecology referral; consider pelvic/adrenal imaging (pelvic US and abdominal CT/MRI as indicated).\\n   - Timeline: order labs within 1–2 weeks; review results with provider and patient.\\n\\n3. Perimenopausal pattern / low luteal progesterone (progesterone 1 ng/mL)\\n   - Confirm cycle day/ovulatory status prior to definitive interpretation.\\n   - If symptomatic and provider approves, consider micronized progesterone:\\n     - Micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly (provider may choose 100–200 mg nightly). Options:\\n       - Cyclic luteal regimen for regular ovulatory cycles (e.g., start mid-luteal for ~14 days per cycle) OR\\n       - Continuous nightly dosing for irregular cycles/perimenopausal sleep/ mood benefits (100 mg nightly; 200 mg may be chosen for heavier bleeding).\\n   - Contraindications: evaluate for history of hormone-sensitive malignancy, active VTE, severe hepatic impairment; confirm pregnancy test negative.\\n   - Monitoring: reassess symptoms and bleeding pattern in 4–8 weeks; consider mid-luteal progesterone recheck only if needed after cycle documentation.\\n   - Provider authorization required before starting progesterone.\\n\\n4. Vitamin D deficiency (25-OH 23.1 ng/mL)\\n   - Initiate repletion per clinic protocol if provider/staff authorized:\\n     - Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily (or equivalent D3 5,000 IU daily).\\n   - Target per clinic: 25-OH ≥60 ng/mL. Recheck 25-OH in 8–12 weeks after initiation.\\n   - Counsel on dietary sources and safe sun exposure.\\n\\n5. Borderline elevated hematocrit 46.9%\\n   - Counsel on adequate hydration; repeat CBC/Hct in 4–6 weeks.\\n   - If Hct remains >48–50% or rising, evaluate for secondary causes (review smoking, androgen exposure, OSA—although STOP-BANG is 0, consider clinical correlation; consider overnight oximetry if indicated).\\n   - Document any symptoms of hyperviscosity (headache, visual changes, dizziness).\\n\\n6. Lifestyle and supplement recommendations\\n   - Intensive lifestyle counseling for lipid reduction:\\n     - Mediterranean-style diet, reduce saturated/trans fats, increase soluble fiber, consider plant sterols.\\n     - Physical activity: at least 150 minutes/week moderate-intensity + resistance training.\\n     - Weight optimization, smoking cessation if applicable, limit alcohol.\\n   - Continue recommended adjunct supplements per clinic plan (staff may start if protocol allows):\\n     - NutraGems CoQ10 300 (1 chewable gel daily)\\n     - D3 5,000 IU + K daily (start now for vitamin D repletion)\\n     - Magtein Magnesium L-Threonate (3 capsules daily divided)\\n     - EstroFactors™ (2 tablets twice daily)\\n     - AdvaClear® (2 capsules twice daily)\\n   - Note: Supplements do not replace statin for LDL ≥190 mg/dL.\\n\\n7. Follow-up testing and timeline\\n   - Immediate / within 24–48 hours: obtain urine or serum pregnancy test and document LMP/cycle day; notify provider of critical labs.\\n   - Within 48–72 hours: start vitamin D repletion if protocol allows; obtain orders for androgen panel (DHEA-S, 17-OHP, AM testosterone) if provider agrees.\\n   - 1–2 weeks: results review of androgen testing; consider pelvic ultrasound if clinically indicated.\\n   - 4–6 weeks: repeat CBC/Hct after hydration counseling.\\n   - 6–8 weeks: fasting lipid panel and LFTs if statin started.\\n   - 8–12 weeks: recheck 25-OH vitamin D after repletion.\\n   - 3 months: comprehensive reassessment of symptoms, lipid response, androgen workup results; consider referrals (endocrinology, gynecology, cardiology, dietitian) based on provider decision.\\n\\nDocumentation and communication\\n- Provider has been asked to review and authorize statin and progesterone orders (or provide alternate instructions).\\n- Patient was counseled on need for pregnancy testing prior to statin/hormone initiation and on urgent nature of LDL and elevated testosterone results.\\n- All abnormal/critical lab results flagged in chart for physician review.\\n\\nDisposition / Follow-up appointment\\n- Schedule follow-up visit or phone encounter per provider recommendation after initial actions (pregnancy test, statin decision, androgen testing). Recheck labs per timelines above. Routine recheck window: 3–6 months.\\n\\n03/10/2026\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": true, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": true, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": true, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": true}, "preventRisk": {"ldlGoal": "Goal: >=50% LDL reduction (target <100 mg/dL)", "riskCategory": "low", "tenYearASCVD": 0.0075920053106353445, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years\\n\\nSEVERE HYPERCHOLESTEROLEMIA: High-intensity statin therapy indicated (LDL >=190 mg/dL)", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0.010436476199027747, "thirtyYearASCVD": 0.05665472886803068, "ageValidFor30Year": true, "thirtyYearTotalCVD": 0.0846544769571736, "tenYearHFPercentage": "0.3%", "tenYearHeartFailure": 0.003378082512534623, "statinRecommendation": "HIGH-INTENSITY statin (Atorvastatin 40-80 mg or Rosuvastatin 20-40 mg) - LDL >=190 mg/dL", "tenYearCVDPercentage": "1.0%", "tenYearASCVDPercentage": "0.8%", "thirtyYearHFPercentage": "3.4%", "thirtyYearHeartFailure": 0.03447306936472355, "thirtyYearCVDPercentage": "8.5%", "thirtyYearASCVDPercentage": "5.7%"}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Essential for patients on statins.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "ApoB 121 mg/dL (cardiovascular support); LDL 204 mg/dL; TC 273 mg/dL; Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["ApoB 121 mg/dL (cardiovascular support)", "LDL 204 mg/dL", "TC 273 mg/dL", "Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)", "Age 41 (declining CoQ10 production)"]}, {"dose": "1 softgel daily with meal", "name": "D3 5,000 + K", "caution": "Repletion dose for deficiency/insufficiency. Contains vitamin K2 for optimal calcium metabolism. Recheck levels in 8-12 weeks.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "D3 5,000 + K provides vitamin D3 with K2 for repletion of deficiency/insufficiency. Target ≥60 ng/mL for bone, immune, and cardiovascular support.", "indication": "Vitamin D 23.1 ng/mL (deficient)", "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D is below optimal range. This supplement will help restore your levels to support bone health, immune function, and overall wellness.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 23.1 ng/mL (deficient)"]}, {"dose": "3 capsules daily (divided doses)", "name": "Magtein® Magnesium L-Threonate", "caution": "L-Threonate form crosses blood-brain barrier for cognitive and sleep support. Well-tolerated; gentle on GI system.", "category": "mineral", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Magtein is the only magnesium form clinically shown to cross the blood-brain barrier. Supports quality sleep, cognitive function, GABA activity, and stress resilience.", "indication": "Low energy reported; Brain fog reported; Irritability reported", "phenotypes": ["Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "This special form of magnesium is designed to reach the brain, where it supports better sleep quality, mental clarity, and a calmer nervous system.", "supportingFindings": ["Low energy reported", "Brain fog reported", "Irritability reported"]}, {"dose": "2 tablets twice daily", "name": "EstroFactors™", "caution": "Nutritional support for estrogen balance and metabolism. Contains I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Supports healthy estrogen metabolite ratios.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "EstroFactors provides targeted nutritional support for estrogen metabolism and balance with I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Best suited for women with estrogen dominance patterns, PMS, perimenopausal hormone imbalance, or those needing broader estrogen metabolism support during hormonal transitions. Differs from Estrovera in that it addresses the underlying estrogen metabolism rather than vasomotor symptom relief.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected; Irritability reported; Age-appropriate for estrogen metabolism support", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement helps your body process and balance estrogen more effectively. It supports healthy hormone metabolism, which can help with PMS symptoms, bloating, mood changes, and hormonal balance during life transitions.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected", "Irritability reported", "Age-appropriate for estrogen metabolism support"]}, {"dose": "2 capsules twice daily", "name": "AdvaClear®", "caution": "Broad-spectrum phase I/II liver detoxification support. Contains NAC, green tea extract, milk thistle, and B-vitamins for biotransformation. Best taken with food.", "category": "detox", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "AdvaClear provides broad-spectrum phase I and phase II liver detoxification support with NAC, green tea extract, watercress, and B-vitamins. Designed for metabolic clearance support, biotransformation of hormones and environmental compounds, and hepatic stress. Appropriate when liver markers are borderline, estrogen clearance is sluggish, or metabolic dysfunction is present.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance pattern (hepatic detox pathways needed for clearance); Low energy (biotransformation burden); Brain fog (toxin clearance burden)", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement supports your liver's natural detoxification processes. Your results suggest your body could benefit from help processing hormones, metabolic byproducts, and other compounds. It provides nutrients that support both phases of your liver's detox pathways.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance pattern (hepatic detox pathways needed for clearance)", "Low energy (biotransformation burden)", "Brain fog (toxin clearance burden)"]}, {"dose": "1 soft chew during times of stress", "name": "HerWellness™ Rapid Stress Relief", "caution": "Fast-acting L-Theanine and Lactium formula. Non-drowsy. Promotes calm within 1 hour. Contains milk.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "low", "rationale": "HerWellness Rapid Stress Relief provides fast-acting stress support with L-Theanine (200mg) and Lactium. Promotes calm within 1 hour without drowsiness.", "indication": "Irritability reported", "phenotypes": ["Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation"], "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This fast-acting supplement helps your body respond to stress more calmly. It promotes relaxation within about an hour and can be taken as needed without causing drowsiness.", "supportingFindings": ["Irritability reported"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 11, "apoBValue": 121, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "borderline", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 0.7592005310635345, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": true}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "strongly_indicated", "indicated": true, "rationale": "LDL-C ≥190 mg/dL (204 mg/dL). High-intensity statin therapy strongly indicated per ACC/AHA guidelines. Consider familial hypercholesterolemia evaluation."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "consider", "rationale": "Lipid levels meet threshold for CAC consideration. CAC may be useful if risk level is uncertain or patient is hesitant about statin.", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\nYour labs show a few important areas to address and many things working well. Notable concerns: LDL cholesterol 204 mg/dL (very high), total testosterone 82.9 ng/dL and free testosterone 8 pg/mL (both elevated for a female), vitamin D 25-OH 23.1 ng/mL (low), and low luteal/progesterone levels 1 ng/mL with estradiol 119 pg/mL and FSH 10.5 mIU/mL consistent with early perimenopause. Your 10-year ASCVD risk is low at 0.8%, but the very high LDL still needs treatment.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Hemoglobin 15.1 g/dL, ferritin 97.7 ng/mL, vitamin B12 645 pg/mL (iron and B12 status are good).\\n- TSH 0.96 mIU/L (thyroid function normal).\\n- eGFR 82 mL/min, AST 19 U/L, ALT 15 U/L (kidney and liver OK).\\n- hs-CRP 0.4 mg/L (low inflammation).\\n- 10-year Total CVD Risk 1.0% (low risk).\\n\\nAreas to improve (with specific values and actions)\\n1) LDL cholesterol 204 mg/dL; ApoB 121 mg/dL (borderline elevated):\\n   - Your LDL goal is a ≥50% reduction (target <100 mg/dL). Your report recommends a high-intensity statin (Atorvastatin 40-80 mg or Rosuvastatin 20-40 mg). Discuss this with your clinician—statins are strongly advised when LDL ≥190 mg/dL.\\n   - Lifestyle (start now): add 25–30 g fiber daily (oatmeal for breakfast, beans with lunch, vegetables at dinner); include 2–3 servings of fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines); reduce saturated fat (choose lean meats, limit butter and cheese); daily exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking or equivalent.\\n\\n2) Testosterone: Total 82.9 ng/dL, Free 8 pg/mL (both elevated):\\n   - Lifestyle: prioritize 7–8 hours sleep nightly, strength training 2–3×/week, and active stress management (walks, meditation, hobbies).\\n   - If you have acne, excess facial/body hair, or hair thinning, discuss referral to gynecology/endocrinology for evaluation and possible treatment (birth-control, anti-androgens, or other options).\\n\\n3) Vitamin D 23.1 ng/mL (suboptimal for bone and hormonal health):\\n   - Start vitamin D3 2,000 IU daily and increase weight-bearing exercise (walking, stairs, light resistance) to support bone health. Consider calcium intake (dairy or fortified alternatives).\\n   - Recheck 25-OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks.\\n\\n4) Progesterone 1 ng/mL with estradiol 119 pg/mL and FSH 10.5 mIU/mL:\\n   - These numbers suggest early perimenopause and possible luteal progesterone deficiency. Track menstrual cycles and symptoms (hot flashes, sleep changes, mood). Discuss with your clinician options such as cyclic progesterone support or symptom-focused therapies.\\n\\nOther notes\\n- Hematocrit 46.9% mildly high; stay well-hydrated and repeat fasting labs if concerned.\\n- Repeat lipid panel 6–12 weeks after starting any statin or major diet/exercise changes.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Schedule a visit to review statin recommendation and discuss testosterone/perimenopause management within 2–4 weeks.\\n- Start vitamin D 2,000 IU daily now and recheck in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Begin the dietary and exercise changes above today; repeat labs (lipids, progesterone if timed in luteal phase, and hematocrit) per your clinician’s plan. If you notice new symptoms (severe acne, rapid hair changes, chest pain), contact the clinic sooner.\\n\\nYou’re taking important steps by reviewing these results—small, consistent changes will help a lot.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 15.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 46.9, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Slightly elevated hematocrit - may indicate dehydration.", "recommendation": "Encourage hydration. Repeat if persistent.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 249, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 119, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 10.5, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 82.9, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Elevated testosterone for females.", "recommendation": "Evaluate for PCOS, adrenal hyperplasia, or androgen-secreting tumor.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 0.96, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 97.7, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 23.1, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D deficiency (21-30 ng/mL). Suboptimal for hormone and bone health.", "recommendation": "Repletion: Metagenics D3 5,000 + K daily. Target ≥60 ng/mL. Recheck in 8-12 weeks.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 645, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 204, "status": "abnormal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Very high LDL - high cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Statin therapy recommended. Intensive lifestyle changes.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 51, "status": "borderline", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "HDL could be higher for optimal protection.", "recommendation": "Increase exercise. Moderate alcohol if appropriate.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 92, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 1.0436476199027747, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 1.0% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years\\n\\nSEVERE HYPERCHOLESTEROLEMIA: High-intensity statin therapy indicated (LDL >=190 mg/dL)", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.7592005310635345, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 0.8%", "recommendation": "HIGH-INTENSITY statin (Atorvastatin 40-80 mg or Rosuvastatin 20-40 mg) - LDL >=190 mg/dL", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.3378082512534623, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.3%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 8.46544769571736, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 8.5%, ASCVD 5.7%, Heart Failure 3.4%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 19, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 15, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 82, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 121, "status": "borderline", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Borderline elevated Apo B.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider pharmacotherapy if high ASCVD risk.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 11, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 331, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 89, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 7.32, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 0.4, "status": "normal", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Low cardiovascular inflammation risk.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1, "status": "borderline", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Low Progesterone (Phase Unknown)", "interpretation": "Progesterone is 1 ng/mL. Without cycle phase documentation, interpretation is limited. If drawn in luteal phase, this would indicate anovulation - a perimenopause marker.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Document cycle phase for accurate interpretation. If patient is mid-cycle or luteal, this suggests anovulation. Consider symptoms: sleep disruption, anxiety, irregular cycles. PATIENT EDUCATION: We need to know where you are in your menstrual cycle to fully interpret this result. Please note when your last period started.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase; <5 ng/mL suggests anovulation if luteal"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Relative Progesterone Deficiency", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 119 pg/mL with progesterone of only 1 ng/mL indicates relative progesterone deficiency. The estrogen is not being adequately opposed by progesterone, which can lead to estrogen dominance symptoms.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Consider progesterone supplementation to balance the E2:P4 ratio. Micronized progesterone 100-200 mg nightly can provide adequate opposition. If patient has symptoms (insomnia, anxiety, heavy bleeding, breast tenderness), progesterone should help. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen level is good, but your progesterone is not keeping up. This imbalance can cause sleep problems, mood changes, and heavy periods. Progesterone supplementation can help restore balance.", "referenceRange": "E2 >100 should have P4 ≥8 for adequate opposition"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Luteal Progesterone Deficiency", "interpretation": "Progesterone of 1 ng/mL with estradiol of 119 pg/mL in a woman 41 years old indicates luteal progesterone deficiency. Estrogen production is adequate but progesterone is not keeping pace - a hallmark of perimenopause.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Micronized progesterone 100-200 mg nightly. Dosing can be cyclic (days 14-28) or continuous depending on cycle regularity. Progesterone provides GABAergic support for sleep and anxiety relief. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your body is making enough estrogen, but progesterone has dropped. This is very common in perimenopause and causes sleep disruption, anxiety, and mood changes. Progesterone supplementation supports your nervous system and helps restore calm, restful sleep.", "referenceRange": "P4 ≥5 ng/mL expected with E2 ≥60 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 10.5, "status": "borderline", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Early Perimenopause", "interpretation": "FSH of 10.5 mIU/mL with progesterone of 1 ng/mL in a 41-year-old indicates early perimenopause. The brain is starting to signal harder (rising FSH) because ovarian response is becoming unpredictable. This is the beginning of the transition.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Progesterone is foundational at this stage: micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly for sleep and nervous system support. Begin the hormone continuity discussion - frame HRT as future-forward planning, not crisis intervention. Nervous system calming messaging matters. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your body is entering the perimenopause transition. Your brain is working a little harder to communicate with your ovaries, and ovulation is becoming less consistent. This can cause sleep changes, anxiety, and cycle irregularities. Progesterone support now can smooth this transition and protect your quality of life.", "referenceRange": "FSH 8-15 with P4 <5 indicates early transition"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 84, "status": "normal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 84 nmol/L is within normal range. SHBG regulates bioavailable sex hormones by binding testosterone and estradiol.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 8, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 8 pg/mL is elevated. May cause androgenic symptoms (acne, hirsutism, hair thinning).", "recommendation": "Evaluate for PCOS, exogenous testosterone, or low SHBG driving elevated free T.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- Key laboratory concerns: markedly elevated LDL 204 mg/dL (meets threshold for statin consideration), elevated total testosterone 82.9 ng/dL and free testosterone 8 pg/mL (both above expected female range), vitamin D deficiency 25-OH 23.1 ng/mL, low luteal progesterone 1 ng/mL with estradiol 119 pg/mL and FSH 10.5 mIU/mL consistent with early perimenopausal biochemical pattern.\\n- Other findings: hematocrit borderline high 46.9% (possible dehydration or other cause), ApoB borderline 121 mg/dL, HDL borderline 51 mg/dL.\\n- Many items require provider review before initiation of pharmacologic therapy (statin, progesterone). Confirm menstrual cycle day and pregnancy status prior to HRT or statin initiation.\\n\\nPriority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL — Physician notification NOW\\n- LDL 204 mg/dL (critical): notify physician immediately for consideration of initiating high-intensity statin therapy per clinic protocol (LDL ≥190 mg/dL independent indication). Document pregnancy status (pregnancy test) and contraception counseling prior to statin start in person or by phone.\\n- Elevated total and free testosterone: notify physician for evaluation of androgen excess and to determine next steps (workup vs. referral).\\n\\nURGENT — Staff tasks to complete within 48–72 hours\\n- Obtain/confirm menstrual cycle timing and date of last menstrual period; document cycle phase in chart to correctly interpret progesterone and estradiol.\\n- Obtain pregnancy test (urine or serum) before initiating statin or any testosterone-suppressing therapy; document results.\\n- Review medication/supplement history with patient specifically asking about exogenous testosterone, DHEA, or androgenic supplements. Flag any positive exposures to provider.\\n\\nROUTINE — To be done within 1–4 weeks unless provider specifies otherwise\\n- Hydration counseling and repeat hematocrit in 4–6 weeks if clinically stable.\\n- Start vitamin D repletion per protocol and plan recheck 25-OH in 8–12 weeks.\\n- If provider approves progesterone therapy, initiate per below and arrange follow-up.\\n\\nSpecific medication recommendations and dose adjustments (staff-facing; provider review required)\\n- Statin (provider to order):\\n  - Recommend initiating high-intensity statin therapy unless contraindicated: atorvastatin 40–80 mg nightly OR rosuvastatin 20–40 mg nightly.\\n  - Baseline labs already include AST/ALT (normal) — document baseline LFTs in chart. Obtain pregnancy test prior to start. Counsel re: teratogenic risk; ensure contraception if of childbearing potential.\\n  - Monitoring: fasting lipid panel 6–8 weeks after start, then adjust dose as needed; thereafter every 3–12 months.\\n  - Notify provider if patient is pregnant, breastfeeding, or planning pregnancy—do not start statin.\\n\\n- Vitamin D repletion (per protocol; can be initiated by staff if protocol allows):\\n  - Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily.\\n  - Target 25-OH ≥60 ng/mL per clinic protocol. Recheck 25-OH in 8–12 weeks.\\n  - Counsel on safe sun exposure and dietary sources.\\n\\n- Progesterone (provider to authorize prior to dispensing/start):\\n  - Micronized progesterone 100–200 mg nightly.\\n  - Dosing considerations:\\n    - If cycles regular and ovulatory: consider cyclic luteal regimen (start ~day 14–28 of a 28-day cycle or for 14 days per cycle).\\n    - If cycles irregular/perimenopausal or for sleep/anxiolytic effects: consider nightly dosing (continuous) at 100 mg; 200 mg may be chosen for heavier bleeding or more symptomatic patients—physician discretion.\\n  - Contraindications/checks: confirm pregnancy status and review history of estrogen-sensitive malignancy, active VTE, severe hepatic impairment.\\n  - Monitoring: re-evaluate symptoms in 4–8 weeks; review bleeding patterns; consider mid-luteal progesterone re-check only if needed for diagnostic clarification after cycle documentation.\\n\\n- Testosterone dosing: NOT indicated — patient has elevated total and free testosterone. Do NOT initiate testosterone. If patient is currently on exogenous testosterone, STOP and notify provider. Further workup recommended below.\\n\\nLifestyle interventions to counsel patients (staff to deliver)\\n- Atherosclerotic risk & lipids:\\n  - Intensive lifestyle counseling: reduce saturated fat, trans fats; increase soluble fiber and plant sterols; encourage Mediterranean-style diet.\\n  - Weight optimization if overweight, structured moderate-to-vigorous exercise at least 150 min/week plus resistance training.\\n  - Smoking cessation and limit alcohol intake.\\n- Lipid-specific adjuncts: consider referral to dietitian for LDL-lowering nutrition counseling; encourage physical activity to raise HDL.\\n- Vitamin D: advise daily supplement as above, discuss safe sun exposure, dietary sources (fatty fish, fortified foods).\\n- Menopausal symptoms and progesterone expectations: explain goals (improved sleep, mood stabilization, decreased heavy bleeding), possible side effects (drowsiness, spotting, breast tenderness).\\n- Androgen excess: review hair, acne, voice changes, hair loss; counsel to discontinue exogenous androgenic supplements immediately pending provider review.\\n\\nAdditional testing and referrals to consider\\n- Androgen workup:\\n  - Serum DHEA-S.\\n  - Morning repeat total testosterone (fasting, early morning), and free testosterone if available — document cycle day.\\n  - 17-hydroxyprogesterone (baseline) to screen for nonclassic congenital adrenal hyperplasia; consider ACTH stimulation if abnormal.\\n  - Consider pelvic ultrasound if clinical virilization or if total testosterone markedly elevated or escalating; ovarian/adrenal imaging if suspicious for neoplasm (provider discretion).\\n  - Review SHBG (listed as normal) and insulin resistance markers (HgbA1c is normal per list but consider fasting glucose/insulin if metabolic concern).\\n  - If clinical virilization or rapidly rising testosterone, urgent referral to endocrinology or gynecology (adrenal/ovarian tumor evaluation).\\n- Hematocrit:\\n  - Repeat CBC in 4–6 weeks with hydration counseling; if persistent elevation (>48% or rising), assess for secondary polycythemia (review smoking, OSA risk—STOP-BANG is normal per chart, consider overnight oximetry, review medications including androgens).\\n- Cardiology/Preventive:\\n  - Given LDL ≥190 and ApoB 121, consider cardiology referral if additional risk factors or if provider desires multidisciplinary management.\\n- Reproductive/menopause:\\n  - If considering systemic HRT beyond progesterone, ensure that mammography and gynecologic screening are up to date and discuss risks/benefits with provider.\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (staff checklist)\\n1. Immediate (today/within 24–48 hours)\\n   - Notify physician of LDL 204 mg/dL and elevated testosterone results (documented provider flag).\\n   - Confirm last menstrual period/cycle day and pregnancy test result prior to any statin or hormone initiation.\\n   - Review and document current supplements/medications for exogenous androgens.\\n\\n2. Within 48–72 hours\\n   - If provider approves vitamin D repletion, start Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily; schedule 25-OH recheck in 8–12 weeks.\\n   - If provider approves progesterone, start micronized progesterone per provider-specified dose/regimen; document counseling.\\n\\n3. Within 1–2 weeks\\n   - Order androgen panel: DHEA-S, 17-OH progesterone, repeat morning total testosterone (document cycle day). Consider ordering pelvic ultrasound if clinically indicated or if provider requests.\\n\\n4. 4–8 weeks\\n   - Repeat hematocrit/CBC if hydration advice given.\\n   - If statin started: fasting lipid panel at 6–8 weeks and LFTs per protocol.\\n   - Reassess progesterone response/symptoms; document bleeding pattern.\\n\\n5. 3 months\\n   - Re-evaluate overall symptom control, lipid response, vitamin D level (if repleted per protocol), and androgen evaluation results. Arrange referrals (endocrinology/gynecology/cardiology/dietitian) as indicated by provider.\\n\\nPhysician notification requirements (explicit)\\n- Immediate provider notification required for LDL ≥190 mg/dL and for elevated testosterone results. Obtain orders before initiating statin or progesterone.\\n- Provider should be informed of any positive pregnancy test, any evidence of virilization, or if patient is taking exogenous androgens.\\n\\nNotes on interpretation and contraindications\\n- Interpretation of progesterone and estradiol requires documented cycle day — request this if missing.\\n- Statins are contraindicated in pregnancy and lactation; confirm reproductive plans before initiation.\\n- Micronized progesterone is generally well tolerated but review VTE, hepatic disease, and hormone-sensitive cancer history before start.\\n\\nConcise clinical prioritization summary\\n- CRITICAL: LDL 204 mg/dL — notify provider now; prepare to obtain pregnancy test and start high-intensity statin per provider order with follow-up lipid check at 6–8 weeks.\\n- URGENT: Elevated total and free testosterone — notify provider; stop any exogenous androgen intake; initiate androgen workup (DHEA-S, 17-OHP, AM testosterone repeat) and consider pelvic ultrasound if indicated.\\n- ROUTINE: Vitamin D replacement now (Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily) with recheck in 8–12 weeks; consider progesterone replacement 100–200 mg nightly after provider approval and cycle documentation; repeat hematocrit in 4–6 weeks after hydration counseling.\\n\\nPlease review and authorize the statin and progesterone orders (or provide alternate instructions).", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 1.8, "detail": "Triglycerides 92 / HDL 51 = 1.8", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5.1%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 84, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 15, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "121 mg/dL", "detail": "Elevated atherogenic particle count", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "0.4 mg/L", "detail": "Low inflammatory burden", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 1, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (1/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Lab pattern consistent with relative estrogen excess or impaired estrogen clearance. Even without reported symptoms, supporting liver detoxification and estrogen metabolism pathways may be beneficial.", "supportingFindings": ["Estradiol:Progesterone imbalance (E2 119, P4 1)", "Progesterone 1 ng/mL (very low)", "Irritability reported"]}, {"name": "Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Pattern consistent with HPA-axis stress and 