": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 17, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 64, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.94, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 9.25, "status": "borderline", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Early Perimenopause", "interpretation": "FSH of 9.25 mIU/mL with progesterone of 0.33 ng/mL in a 73-year-old indicates early perimenopause. The brain is starting to signal harder (rising FSH) because ovarian response is becoming unpredictable. This is the beginning of the transition.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Progesterone is foundational at this stage: micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly for sleep and nervous system support. Begin the hormone continuity discussion - frame HRT as future-forward planning, not crisis intervention. Nervous system calming messaging matters. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your body is entering the perimenopause transition. Your brain is working a little harder to communicate with your ovaries, and ovulation is becoming less consistent. This can cause sleep changes, anxiety, and cycle irregularities. Progesterone support now can smooth this transition and protect your quality of life.", "referenceRange": "FSH 8-15 with P4 <5 indicates early transition"}], "aiRecommendations": "Unable to generate recommendations. Please review lab findings manually.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 1.74, "detail": "Triglycerides 151 / HDL 87 = 1.74", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5.3%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 17, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Menopausal Transition", "confidence": "high", "description": "Clinical picture consistent with perimenopause or menopause. Hormonal shifts during this transition affect bone health, cardiovascular risk, mood, and quality of life.", "supportingFindings": ["Postmenopausal status"]}, {"name": "Oxidative Stress Burden", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Multiple markers suggest increased oxidative stress and cellular burden. Antioxidant and glutathione support may help reduce oxidative damage and support cellular energy production.", "supportingFindings": ["AST 24 U/L (cellular stress marker)", "Triglycerides 151 mg/dL (lipid peroxidation risk)", "Age 73 (increased oxidative stress risk)"]}]}	2026-03-10 20:24:05.049331	\N
25	Charlene Kennedy	female	2026-03-10 20:27:45.505	{"alt": 13, "ast": 16, "bun": 16, "co2": 25, "hdl": 88, "ldl": 85, "rbc": 3.37, "tsh": 1.41, "wbc": 6.7, "acne": false, "egfr": 78, "onHRT": false, "folate": 4.87, "sodium": 137, "albumin": 4.1, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 9.7, "glucose": 91, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "chloride": 100, "ferritin": 97.1, "hairLoss": false, "vitaminD": 14, "bilirubin": 0.3, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": false, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 279, "potassium": 5.2, "creatinine": 0.79, "hematocrit": 34.2, "hemoglobin": 11.1, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "vitaminB12": 292, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Charlene Kennedy", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 73, "bmi": 38.04, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": true, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 120, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "restlessLegs": false, "totalProtein": 6.4, "lowMotivation": false, "triglycerides": 85, "menstrualPhase": "postmenopausal", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "totalCholesterol": 190}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History of present illness: Patient is a 73-year-old female here for laboratory review and routine follow-up. She presents for discussion of recent lab abnormalities. Denies acute chest pain, palpitations, syncope, orthopnea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, new shortness of breath at rest, gastrointestinal bleeding, melena, hematochezia, or new focal neurologic symptoms. Denies significant new bone pain or history of recent falls. Postmenopausal; no menstrual history relevant. No new medication changes reported at time of visit.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\nLaboratory results (03/10/2026)\\n- CBC\\n  - Hemoglobin: 11.1 g/dL — LOW (anemia)\\n  - Hematocrit: 34.2 % — LOW\\n  - Platelets: 279 K/uL — WNL\\n  - (Note: WBC, MCV, reticulocyte not provided)\\n\\n- Iron / Stores\\n  - Ferritin: 97.1 ng/mL — WNL\\n\\n- Vitamin / Nutritional\\n  - Vitamin D (25-OH): 14 ng/mL — LOW (severe deficiency)\\n  - Vitamin B12: 292 pg/mL — Low-normal / borderline\\n  - Folate: 4.87 ng/mL — Low-normal / borderline\\n\\n- Thyroid / Metabolic\\n  - TSH: 1.41 mIU/L — WNL\\n\\n- Hepatic / Renal\\n  - AST: 16 U/L — WNL\\n  - ALT: 13 U/L — WNL\\n  - Creatinine: 0.79 mg/dL — WNL\\n  - eGFR: 78 mL/min/1.73m2 — WNL\\n\\n- Lipids / Cardiovascular risk\\n  - LDL-C: 85 mg/dL — WNL\\n  - HDL-C: 88 mg/dL — WNL\\n  - Triglycerides: 85 mg/dL — WNL\\n  - 10-year Total CVD Risk (AHA PREVENT 2023): 13.8% — INTERMEDIATE RISK\\n  - 10-year ASCVD Risk: 6.6%\\n  - 10-year Heart Failure Risk: 13.3%\\n  - Statin recommendation per PREVENT: moderate-to-high intensity based on shared decision-making\\n\\n- Sleep apnea risk\\n  - STOP-BANG: 2 points — low risk\\n\\n- Red flags: None identified from labs.\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Mild anemia (Hgb 11.1 g/dL, Hct 34.2%)\\n   - Clinical significance: Mild anemia in a 73-year-old female. Ferritin 97.1 ng/mL is not suggestive of iron-deficiency anemia, but MCV and reticulocyte index not available in current results. Borderline B12 (292 pg/mL) and borderline folate (4.87 ng/mL) could contribute. Differential: anemia of chronic disease, occult blood loss (GI), early nutritional deficiency (B12/folate), or mixed etiology.\\n   - Red flag: In older adults new anemia raises concern for GI blood loss and possible malignancy — requires evaluation.\\n\\n2. Severe Vitamin D deficiency (25-OH 14 ng/mL)\\n   - Clinical significance: Level ≤20 ng/mL consistent with deficiency; increased risk for bone loss, falls, muscle weakness, fatigue, immune effects.\\n\\n3. Borderline Vitamin B12 (292 pg/mL) and folate (4.87 ng/mL)\\n   - Clinical significance: Low-normal B12 may progress to deficiency; monitor for neurologic or hematologic manifestations. Folate low-normal—consider dietary insufficiency or medication effects.\\n\\n4. Intermediate 10-year cardiovascular risk (Total CVD 13.8%; ASCVD 6.6%; HF 13.3%)\\n   - Clinical significance: Risk in intermediate category; LDL 85 mg/dL and HDL high (88 mg/dL). Per guidelines/PREVENT, discuss moderate-to-high intensity statin therapy after shared decision-making. No current symptoms of ischemia or heart failure.\\n\\n5. Renal and hepatic function within normal limits\\n   - Clinical significance: Creatinine 0.79, eGFR 78 mL/min/1.73m2, AST/ALT normal — appropriate baseline for initiating supplements/statin if indicated.\\n\\nPLAN:\\n1. Anemia (Hgb 11.1 g/dL)\\n   - Orders:\\n     - CBC with differential and indices (including MCV) and reticulocyte count — repeat now to obtain full indices.\\n     - Peripheral smear if CBC indices are abnormal.\\n     - Fecal immunochemical test (FIT) x1 or stool occult blood testing given age and new anemia; refer to GI if positive or if concern for GI blood loss.\\n     - Consider iron studies already reviewed (ferritin 97.1 ng/mL). If ferritin falls or iron-deficiency pattern emerges, start oral iron at that time.\\n   - Therapy / Supplements:\\n     - Start Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support 1 tablet twice daily with meals (medium-priority supplement as recommended) to support hematologic status.\\n     - Start Intrinsi B12-Folate™ 1 tablet daily (addresses borderline B12/folate).\\n   - Monitoring / Follow-up:\\n     - Repeat CBC and reticulocyte in 8–12 weeks to assess response to supplements and to further evaluate anemia. Sooner if clinical status changes (worsening fatigue, syncope, overt bleeding).\\n     - If anemia worsens or GI symptoms occur, expedite GI referral for colon evaluation (age-appropriate cancer screening).\\n   - Patient counseling: Discussed potential causes and need to evaluate for occult blood loss vs nutritional causes. Patient advised to report any new bleeding, black stools, or marked fatigue.\\n\\n2. Vitamin D deficiency (25-OH 14 ng/mL)\\n   - Therapy:\\n     - Initiate Vitamin D3 10,000 IU + Vitamin K softgel daily with a meal (high-priority supplement recommendation).\\n     - Counsel on dietary calcium intake; ensure adequate dietary calcium (aim ~1,200 mg/day total intake unless contraindicated). Recommend calcium supplementation only if dietary intake is inadequate.\\n   - Monitoring:\\n     - Recheck 25-OH Vitamin D level in 12 weeks (3 months) after initiation to confirm response, then adjust maintenance dose as appropriate.\\n   - Additional:\\n     - Recommend fall prevention measures and weight-bearing exercise to support bone health.\\n     - Order or confirm date of most recent DEXA scan; if not done within last 2 years, schedule bone density assessment given age and severe vitamin D deficiency.\\n\\n3. Borderline B12 / Folate (B12 292 pg/mL; Folate 4.87 ng/mL)\\n   - Therapy:\\n     - Start Intrinsi B12-Folate™ 1 tablet daily as above.\\n     - If patient develops neurologic symptoms (paresthesias, gait instability), consider parenteral B12 and urgent evaluation.\\n   - Monitoring:\\n     - Recheck B12 and folate levels in 3 months.\\n     - If B12 declines or symptoms develop, consider IM B12 and workup for malabsorption (e.g., anti-intrinsic factor/parietal cell antibodies, celiac serologies if indicated).\\n\\n4. Cardiovascular risk (10-yr Total CVD 13.8%; ASCVD 6.6%)\\n   - Assessment/Discussion:\\n     - Reviewed risk estimate and that patient falls in intermediate risk category. Discussed benefits and risks of statin therapy, lifestyle modification, and blood pressure optimization.\\n   - Therapy recommendation:\\n     - Recommend shared decision-making regarding initiation of a moderate-intensity statin. If patient elects to start therapy, consider atorvastatin 20 mg nightly (example moderate-intensity regimen), adjust per tolerance and co-morbidities.\\n   - Orders / Monitoring:\\n     - If statin initiated: baseline LFTs are acceptable (AST/ALT normal). Recheck lipid panel and LFTs 6–12 weeks after initiation or dose change, then every 3–12 months per guidelines.\\n   - Lifestyle:\\n     - Reinforce heart-healthy diet (Mediterranean/DASH principles), regular aerobic exercise (as tolerated), weight management, blood pressure control, and smoking cessation if applicable.\\n\\n5. Heart failure risk (10-yr HF 13.3%)\\n   - Plan:\\n     - No signs/symptoms of heart failure at this visit. Reinforce control of cardiovascular risk factors (BP, lipids, glycemic control if applicable), sodium moderation, and monitoring for symptoms (edema, dyspnea, orthopnea).\\n     - Reassess at routine follow-up; consider BNP/NT-proBNP only if symptoms develop.\\n\\n6. Preventive care / Other\\n   - Bone health: order DEXA if not recent; counsel fall prevention, vitamin D and calcium as above.\\n   - Vaccination status not reviewed in chart — to be updated at next visit.\\n   - Sleep apnea: STOP-BANG 2 — low risk; no further testing indicated unless clinical suspicion increases.\\n   - Medication review: reconcile current meds at next visit; confirm no contraindications to supplements or statin.\\n\\nFollow-up / Recheck Window:\\n- Re-check labs in 3 months (recommended window 3–6 months):\\n  - CBC with indices and reticulocyte (8–12 weeks)\\n  - Vitamin D 25-OH (12 weeks)\\n  - Vitamin B12 and folate (12 weeks)\\n  - Lipid panel and LFTs 6–12 weeks after any statin initiation; otherwise repeat lipid panel in 6–12 months as routine\\n- Routine clinic follow-up in 3–6 months for review of repeat labs and management adjustments or sooner if new/worsening symptoms.\\n\\nDocumentation:\\n- Results reviewed and discussed with patient. Risks, benefits, and rationale for recommended supplements and possible statin therapy were discussed. Patient verbalized understanding and instructions. Questions answered. Follow-up as above.\\n\\nDate: 03/10/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "preventRisk": {"ldlGoal": "Goal: 30-50% LDL reduction", "riskCategory": "intermediate", "tenYearASCVD": 0.06618441252147342, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nINTERMEDIATE-HIGH RISK (7.5-20%): Moderate-to-high intensity statin recommended\\n\\nRisk-enhancing factors may favor more aggressive therapy", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0.13793284051841118, "ageValidFor30Year": false, "tenYearHFPercentage": "13.3%", "tenYearHeartFailure": 0.13311554180767934, "statinRecommendation": "MODERATE-TO-HIGH intensity statin based on shared decision-making", "tenYearCVDPercentage": "13.8%", "tenYearASCVDPercentage": "6.6%"}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 softgel daily with meal", "name": "D3 10,000 + K", "caution": "High-dose repletion therapy. Recheck vitamin D levels in 8-12 weeks. Contains vitamin K2 for calcium metabolism.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "high", "rationale": "D3 10,000 + K provides high-dose vitamin D3 with K2 for efficient repletion of severe deficiency. K2 ensures proper calcium utilization and prevents arterial calcification.", "indication": "Vitamin D 14 ng/mL (severe deficiency ≤20)", "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D level is very low, which can affect your bones, immune system, mood, and energy. This high-dose supplement will help bring your level up to optimal range, and the vitamin K2 helps your body use calcium properly.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 14 ng/mL (severe deficiency ≤20)"]}, {"dose": "1 tablet daily", "name": "Intrinsi B12-Folate™", "caution": "Contains intrinsic factor for enhanced B12 absorption. Ideal for patients with absorption concerns or vegetarian/vegan diets.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "high", "rationale": "Intrinsi B12-Folate provides methylated B12 and folate with intrinsic factor for superior absorption. Supports energy, cognition, methylation, and nerve function.", "indication": "B12 292 pg/mL (deficient <300); Folate 4.87 ng/mL (low)", "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "Your B12 and/or folate levels need support. This supplement provides highly absorbable forms of these essential vitamins to support your energy, brain function, and nervous system health.", "supportingFindings": ["B12 292 pg/mL (deficient <300)", "Folate 4.87 ng/mL (low)"]}, {"dose": "1 tablet twice daily with meals", "name": "Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support", "caution": "Contains iron, folate, and B12 for comprehensive RBC support. Avoid taking with calcium-rich foods or dairy. May cause mild GI upset initially.", "category": "iron", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Hemagenics provides highly absorbable iron bisglycinate with B12, B6, and folate for comprehensive red blood cell support. Indicated for functional iron deficiency, iron deficiency without anemia, and iron-responsive symptoms.", "indication": "Hemoglobin 11.1 g/dL (low)", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "Your lab results suggest your iron stores are lower than optimal. This supplement provides a gentle, well-absorbed form of iron along with B12 and folate to support your energy levels, hair health, and overall vitality.", "supportingFindings": ["Hemoglobin 11.1 g/dL (low)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "consider", "rationale": "Lipid levels meet threshold for CAC consideration. CAC may be useful if risk level is uncertain or patient is hesitant about statin.", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\n- You have mild anemia and a severe vitamin D deficiency that are likely contributing to fatigue and could affect bone health. Your 10-year cardiovascular risk is in the INTERMEDIATE range (13.8%), so we should work on heart-healthy habits and discuss medication options. Many other tests are normal.\\n\\nPositive findings (what is good right now)\\n- LDL cholesterol: 85 mg/dL (good)\\n- HDL cholesterol: 88 mg/dL (protective)\\n- Triglycerides: 85 mg/dL (normal)\\n- Ferritin: 97.1 ng/mL (adequate iron stores; >50 is optimal)\\n- Platelets: 279 K/uL (normal)\\n- TSH: 1.41 mIU/L (euthyroid)\\n- AST: 16 U/L, ALT: 13 U/L (liver tests normal)\\n- eGFR: 78 mL/min, Creatinine: 0.79 mg/dL (kidney function normal)\\n- Sleep Apnea risk (STOP-BANG): 2 points (LOW risk)\\n\\nAreas to improve (exact values + concrete actions)\\n1) Anemia\\n- Hemoglobin 11.1 g/dL and Hematocrit 34.2% (both low).\\n- Actionable steps: Track menstrual bleeding (note heavy or prolonged flows). Eat iron-rich meals: beef, chicken, lentils, beans, fortified cereal; add vitamin C (orange juice, bell peppers) with meals to boost absorption and avoid tea/coffee within 1 hour of iron foods. Because your ferritin is 97.1 ng/mL, your anemia may not be from low iron—do not start iron pills without discussing with your provider. Plan: repeat CBC in 8 weeks; if symptoms worsen (shortness of breath, fast heart rate, fainting) seek care sooner.\\n\\n2) Vitamin D — bone and immune health\\n- 25-OH Vitamin D: 14 ng/mL (severe deficiency; ≤20 ng/mL).\\n- Actionable steps: Start vitamin D3 supplementation—common options are 50,000 IU once weekly for 8–12 weeks followed by 2,000 IU daily maintenance, or 2,000–4,000 IU daily—please confirm exact dose with your provider. Increase dietary sources (fatty fish, fortified milk) and aim for 10–20 minutes sunlight on arms/face several times weekly if safe. Add calcium (diet and/or supplement) to reach 1,000–1,200 mg/day and do weight-bearing exercise (30 minutes, 3–5x/week).\\n\\n3) B12/Folate — borderline\\n- Vitamin B12: 292 pg/mL (low-normal). Folate: 4.87 ng/mL (low-normal).\\n- Actionable steps: Start oral B12 1,000 mcg daily or monthly B12 shots if recommended, and folic acid 400–800 mcg daily; increase B12 foods (eggs, dairy) and leafy greens/beans for folate. Recheck levels in 8–12 weeks.\\n\\nCardiovascular risk (PREVENT)\\n- 10-year total CVD risk: 13.8% (INTERMEDIATE), 10-year ASCVD: 6.6%, 10-year heart failure risk: 13.3%.\\n- What this means: About a 14% chance of a major heart-related event in 10 years—higher than average and worth action now.\\n- Required lifestyle plan (INTERMEDIATE risk): Increase aerobic activity to 200–300 minutes weekly (brisk walking, cycling, swimming) plus strength training 2×/week; focus on heart-healthy fats (avocado, nuts, olive oil, fatty fish 3×/week); reduce sodium to <2,300 mg/day (minimize processed foods); discuss quitting tobacco if you smoke; and work with your clinician about starting a moderate-to-high intensity statin to reach a 30–50% LDL reduction (current LDL 85 mg/dL → target roughly 42–60 mg/dL).\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Discuss starting vitamin D therapy and B12/folate supplements with your clinician this week.\\n- Repeat CBC, vitamin D, B12/folate in 8–12 weeks to check improvement.\\n- Discuss cardiovascular risk reduction and statin decision within 1–3 months.\\n- Seek urgent care for chest pain, severe shortness of breath, fainting, or very heavy bleeding.\\n\\nYou’re doing well in many areas. These targeted steps (supplements, diet, exercise, and follow-up tests) can improve energy, bone health, and heart risk — and we’ll adjust the plan based on your follow-up labs.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 11.1, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Low hemoglobin indicating anemia.", "recommendation": "Check iron studies, B12, folate. Evaluate menstrual blood loss if applicable.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 34.2, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Low hematocrit suggesting anemia.", "recommendation": "Evaluate for iron deficiency or other causes. Check iron studies.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 279, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.41, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 97.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, 