e, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "107 mg/dL", "detail": "Elevated atherogenic particle count", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "3.5 mg/L", "detail": "Elevated systemic inflammation", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "high", "phenotypes": [{"key": "visceral_metabolic", "name": "Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Recheck A1c, lipid panel, hs-CRP, ApoB in 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0"], "pathophysiology": "Early peripheral + hepatic insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia. Glucose may still be \\"normal.\\" A1c often rises last.", "triggerCriteria": ["A1c >= 5.7%", "TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0", "Central adiposity phenotype", "ALT above optimal range"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests your body may not be responding to insulin as efficiently as it should. Insulin is the hormone that helps move sugar from your bloodstream into your cells for energy.\\n\\nWhen cells become less sensitive to insulin:\\n- Blood sugar slowly rises over time\\n- Triglycerides increase\\n- Inflammation increases\\n- Weight gain around the midsection becomes easier\\n\\nThe good news? This stage is highly reversible with targeted nutrition, strength training, sleep optimization, and - when appropriate - medication support.\\n\\nWe'll focus on improving how your body processes fuel and reducing long-term cardiometabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward nutrition (>= 25-35g/meal)", "Carbohydrate quality + timing education", "Strength training 2-3x weekly", "10-15 min post-meal walking", "Sleep optimization", "Consider: GLP-1 therapy if obesity, appetite dysregulation, A1c >= 5.7, or inflammatory phenotype", "Consider: Metformin if prediabetes or strong family history", "Recheck metabolic panel in 3-6 months"]}, {"key": "hormonal_pcos", "name": "Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Monitor SHBG, androgens, A1c every 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["SHBG very low (<30)", "Elevated total testosterone", "Elevated free testosterone"], "pathophysiology": "Hyperinsulinemia suppresses SHBG and increases ovarian androgen production.", "triggerCriteria": ["Low SHBG (<50; strong if <30)", "Elevated total or free testosterone", "Irregular cycles", "A1c may still be normal", "TG/HDL may be borderline-high"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests insulin may be influencing your hormone balance.\\n\\nWhen insulin levels run high:\\n- The body makes less SHBG (a protective hormone-binding protein)\\n- Androgen levels can rise\\n- Cycles may become irregular\\n\\nThis pattern is common in PCOS and other hormone imbalance states.\\n\\nImproving insulin sensitivity often helps restore hormonal balance and reduce long-term metabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward, lower refined carb approach", "Strength training", "Consider metformin if cycles irregular or A1c trending", "Consider GLP-1 if obesity phenotype present", "Monitor SHBG, androgens, A1c"]}], "positiveCount": 3, "likelihoodLabel": "HIGH LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: HIGH LIKELIHOOD (3/6 markers positive). Identified phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance, Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance. Treat according to phenotype-specific protocol.", "confirmationTests": "Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose."}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Inflammatory Burden", "confidence": "high", "description": "Pattern of systemic inflammation indicated by elevated inflammatory markers and/or symptomatic presentation. May benefit from anti-inflammatory nutritional support.", "supportingFindings": ["hs-CRP 3.5 mg/L (elevated >3.0)", "ApoB 107 mg/dL (elevated)"]}, {"name": "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "confidence": "high", "description": "Metabolic pattern consistent with insulin resistance and/or visceral adiposity. This drives inflammation, hormonal imbalance, and cardiovascular risk.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance screening: HIGH LIKELIHOOD (3/6 markers positive)", "SHBG 16.4 nmol/L (low, associated with insulin resistance)", "ApoB 107 mg/dL (elevated)", "hs-CRP 3.5 mg/L (metabolic inflammation)", "Unexplained weight gain / central adiposity reported"]}, {"name": "Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Lab pattern consistent with relative estrogen excess or impaired estrogen clearance. Even without reported symptoms, supporting liver detoxification and estrogen metabolism pathways may be beneficial.", "supportingFindings": ["Progesterone 0.56 ng/mL (very low)", "Borderline liver markers (ALT 23, AST 25) suggesting possible impaired hepatic clearance"]}, {"name": "Oxidative Stress Burden", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Multiple markers suggest increased oxidative stress and cellular burden. Antioxidant and glutathione support may help reduce oxidative damage and support cellular energy production.", "supportingFindings": ["hs-CRP 3.5 mg/L (elevated, systemic inflammation)", "ALT 23 U/L (hepatic stress marker)", "AST 25 U/L (cellular stress marker)", "Insulin resistance pattern (metabolic oxidative driver)", "Low energy / fatigue (possible mitochondrial burden)"]}, {"name": "Gut-Microbiome Support", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Indicators suggest the gut and/or urogenital microbiome may benefit from targeted probiotic support and gut barrier maintenance.", "supportingFindings": ["Vaginal dryness (mucosal barrier may benefit from probiotic support)", "hs-CRP 3.5 mg/L (gut-driven inflammation possible)", "Vitamin D 20.8 ng/mL (impacts gut barrier integrity)"]}]}	2026-03-10 21:28:24.429877	\N
27	Laura Summerlin	female	2025-09-18 00:00:00	{"a1c": 5.1, "alt": 20, "ast": 20, "bun": 10, "co2": 18, "fsh": 8.14, "hdl": 39, "ldl": 110, "rbc": 5.05, "tsh": 1.59, "wbc": 7.5, "acne": false, "egfr": 124, "onHRT": false, "freeT3": 3.17, "sodium": 139, "albumin": 4.3, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 9.8, "glucose": 98, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "chloride": 106, "hairLoss": false, "vitaminD": 17.2, "bilirubin": 0.2, "estradiol": 32, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": false, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 408, "potassium": 4.5, "creatinine": 0.68, "hematocrit": 47.7, "hemoglobin": 14.5, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "vitaminB12": 333, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2025-09-18", "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Laura Summerlin", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 25, "bmi": 57.96, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 110, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 0.05, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 39.5, "totalProtein": 7.1, "lowMotivation": false, "triglycerides": 147, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "totalCholesterol": 178}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History of present illness: Patient presented for review of recent laboratory results. Patient reports fatigue and intermittent low mood; expresses concern about low vitamin D and overall energy. Denies chest pain, palpitations, syncope, focal weakness, bone pain, fractures, or bleeding. No acute illness reported.\\n- Menstrual/hormonal history: Menstrual cycle timing not documented in chart at time of visit; patient will provide cycle day if available. Noted low random progesterone (P4 0.05 ng/mL) — timing-dependent. Patient not currently requesting testosterone therapy.\\n- Allergies/medications: None documented in lab summary. (Verify active med/allergy list in chart.)\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: Not available in current lab-only encounter.\\n- CBC:\\n  - Hemoglobin: 14.5 g/dL (normal)\\n  - Hematocrit: 47.7 % (borderline high) — may indicate mild hemoconcentration/dehydration\\n  - Platelets: 408 K/uL (borderline high; 400–450 K/uL)\\n- Basic metabolic / kidney:\\n  - Creatinine: 0.68 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - eGFR: 124 mL/min (normal)\\n- Liver:\\n  - AST: 20 U/L (normal)\\n  - ALT: 20 U/L (normal)\\n- Lipid panel:\\n  - Total cholesterol: (not provided)\\n  - HDL cholesterol: 39 mg/dL (abnormal — low)\\n  - LDL cholesterol: 110 mg/dL (borderline/near optimal)\\n  - Triglycerides: 147 mg/dL (normal)\\n- Endocrine / hormones:\\n  - TSH: 1.59 mIU/L (normal)\\n  - Estradiol (E2): 32 pg/mL (within reported normal for unspecified cycle day)\\n  - Progesterone (P4): 0.05 ng/mL (low — interpret relative to cycle day)\\n  - FSH: 8.14 mIU/mL (normal)\\n  - Total Testosterone: 39.5 ng/dL (within reference range)\\n  - Testosterone pattern: Pattern D — Adequate androgens, persistent symptoms\\n- Vitamin / metabolic:\\n  - Vitamin D (25-OH): 17.2 ng/mL (abnormal — severe deficiency ≤20 ng/mL)\\n  - Vitamin B12: 333 pg/mL (normal)\\n  - Hemoglobin A1c: 5.1 % (normal)\\n- Calculated risk scores:\\n  - 10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT): 0 %\\n  - 10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT): 0 %\\n  - 10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT): 0 %\\n  - Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG): 0 points\\n  - Cardiovascular risk category: Low (Age outside statin-validated range; use clinical judgment)\\n- Other: No red-flag critical labs besides Vitamin D deficiency and low HDL identified.\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Severe Vitamin D deficiency — 25‑OH Vitamin D 17.2 ng/mL (≤20 ng/mL).\\n   - Clinical significance: Associated with fatigue, bone demineralization risk, immune dysfunction; requires repletion.\\n   - Red flag: Provider notification recommended for initiation of high-dose repletion and safety monitoring.\\n2. Low HDL cholesterol — 39 mg/dL.\\n   - Clinical significance: Increased cardiovascular risk marker despite low calculated 10‑yr risk given patient age; recommend lifestyle measures and recheck lipids.\\n3. Borderline thrombocytosis — platelets 408 K/uL.\\n   - Clinical significance: Mild, may be reactive (iron deficiency, inflammation, infection). Needs evaluation with iron studies and inflammatory markers.\\n   - Differential: Iron deficiency, recent infection/inflammation, reactive thrombocytosis.\\n4. Borderline elevated hematocrit — 47.7%.\\n   - Clinical significance: Slight elevation; may represent mild dehydration/hemoconcentration. Recheck after hydration.\\n   - Action threshold: Notify provider if hematocrit >50% or symptomatic.\\n5. Adequate total testosterone with persistent symptoms — Total T 39.5 ng/dL; Pattern D (Adequate androgens, persistent symptoms).\\n   - Clinical significance: Androgens unlikely primary driver of fatigue/mood; evaluate non-androgenic causes (vitamin D deficiency, thyroid, sleep, mood, HPA axis, estrogen/progesterone balance).\\n6. Low random progesterone — P4 0.05 ng/mL (timing-dependent).\\n   - Clinical significance: Interpretation requires cycle day; recommend mid-luteal progesterone if evaluating ovulation/fertility.\\n7. LDL 110 mg/dL (near optimal); Triglycerides 147 mg/dL (normal).\\n   - Clinical significance: Lifestyle modification first-line; recheck after 3 months.\\n8. Other labs within normal limits: Hemoglobin, TSH, B12, A1c, AST/ALT, creatinine/eGFR.\\n\\nPLAN:\\n1. Vitamin D deficiency (25‑OH D 17.2 ng/mL)\\n   - Provider notified for authorization to initiate repletion per clinic protocol.\\n   - Start Metagenics D3 10,000 IU + K PO daily x 8–12 weeks (high‑dose repletion), then transition to maintenance dosing per provider.\\n   - Baseline safety: serum calcium to be ordered now; renal function already acceptable (Cr 0.68, eGFR 124). Monitor for symptoms of hypercalcemia; counsel patient on signs (nausea, vomiting, polyuria, confusion).\\n   - Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D level in 8–12 weeks after initiation.\\n   - Document patient education on dosing and adherence.\\n2. Low HDL (39 mg/dL)\\n   - Lifestyle counseling provided:\\n     - Aerobic exercise: target ≥150 min/week moderate intensity.\\n     - Resistance training 2×/week.\\n     - Dietary: Mediterranean/DASH-style eating; reduce refined carbs and trans fats; increase omega‑3 fatty fish, fiber.\\n     - Limit alcohol; smoking cessation if applicable.\\n     - Weight management as indicated.\\n   - No pharmacologic HDL therapy indicated at this time. Repeat fasting lipid panel in 3 months after lifestyle intervention.\\n3. Borderline thrombocytosis (Platelets 408 K/uL) and hematocrit 47.7%\\n   - Order iron studies (ferritin, serum iron, TIBC/transferrin, transferrin saturation) and inflammatory marker (CRP or ESR) within 1 week.\\n   - Repeat CBC in 4–8 weeks after hydration and initial evaluation.\\n   - Hydration counseling given; advise patient to increase oral fluids and recheck CBC after improved hydration.\\n   - Do NOT start empiric iron until iron studies available. If ferritin <50 ng/mL and iron deficiency confirmed, consider oral iron therapy (e.g., ferrous sulfate 325 mg every other day with vitamin C 250 mg) and recheck ferritin at 8–12 weeks.\\n   - Consider hematology referral if platelets >450 K/uL persist or if workup is unrevealing.\\n4. Adequate testosterone with persistent symptoms; no testosterone therapy\\n   - No initiation of testosterone therapy based on current labs.\\n   - If fatigue/mood/sleep symptoms persist despite treating reversible causes, consider additional evaluation: DHEA‑S, Free T4/Free T3, anti‑TPO antibodies, sleep assessment, behavioral health referral.\\n5. Low random progesterone (P4 0.05 ng/mL)\\n   - Clarify menstrual cycle day and contraceptive/HRT status with patient. If assessing ovulation/fertility or luteal phase adequacy, schedule mid‑luteal progesterone (~7 days before menses; ~day 21 in a 28‑day cycle).\\n6. Lipids and cardiovascular risk\\n   - Lifestyle measures as above. Repeat fasting lipid panel in 3 months.\\n   - No statin indicated at this age by PREVENT calculation; discuss if other risk factors emerge.\\n7. Additional recommendations / supplements\\n   - High-priority supplement: D3 10,000 IU + K daily (as above) — provider authorization required.\\n   - Medium-priority: Intrinsi B12‑Folate™ 1 tablet daily may be considered for general support (patient already has B12 333 pg/mL — within range).\\n   - Counsel on interactions (vitamin D with calcium supplements) and monitoring.\\n8. Follow-up labs and timeline (actionable checklist)\\n   - Today / within 1 week:\\n     - Provider authorization for vitamin D repletion.\\n     - Order ferritin, serum iron, TIBC/transferrin, transferrin saturation, CRP or ESR.\\n     - Order serum calcium (baseline) and repeat CBC if clinically indicated.\\n     - Document cycle day or schedule mid‑luteal progesterone if indicated.\\n   - 4–8 weeks:\\n     - Repeat CBC to reassess platelets and hematocrit.\\n     - If iron therapy started, recheck ferritin at 8–12 weeks.\\n   - 8–12 weeks:\\n     - Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D and serum calcium as appropriate; transition to maintenance dosing if repleted.\\n   - 3 months:\\n     - Repeat fasting lipid panel after lifestyle interventions.\\n     - Re-evaluate symptoms; consider further endocrine testing (DHEA‑S, Free T4/Free T3, anti‑TPO) and referrals as indicated.\\n9. Safety thresholds / provider notification\\n   - Notify provider promptly if:\\n     - 25‑OH Vitamin D ≤20 ng/mL (initiation of repletion — action taken).\\n     - Platelets >450 K/uL or rising.\\n     - Hematocrit >50% or if symptomatic (headache, visual changes, thrombosis symptoms).\\n     - Ferritin <15 ng/mL, falling hemoglobin, or intolerance/nonresponse to iron therapy.\\n     - Any abnormal calcium or renal function during high‑dose vitamin D therapy.\\n10. Patient communication\\n   - Results reviewed and discussed with patient. Education provided on vitamin D repletion, hydration, timing of progesterone testing, lifestyle measures for lipids, and the plan to evaluate platelet elevation.\\n   - Patient instructed to report new symptoms (bone pain, significant fatigue worsening, palpitations, signs of hypercalcemia, bruising/bleeding, or thrombotic symptoms).\\n\\nFollow-up:\\n- Recheck window: 3–6 months for routine follow-up; specific labs as outlined above (CBC 4–8 weeks, iron studies now, 25‑OH D in 8–12 weeks, fasting lipids in 3 months).\\n- Provider to authorize vitamin D regimen and review iron/inflammatory studies when resulted.\\n\\n03/10/2026\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": true, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": true, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "preventRisk": {"riskCategory": "low", "tenYearASCVD": 0, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "PREVENT calculator is validated for ages 30-79 years. Patient is 25 years old. Use clinical judgment for risk assessment.", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0, "ageValidFor30Year": false, "tenYearHFPercentage": "N/A", "tenYearHeartFailure": 0, "statinRecommendation": "Age outside validated range - use clinical judgment", "tenYearCVDPercentage": "N/A", "tenYearASCVDPercentage": "N/A"}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 softgel daily with meal", "name": "D3 10,000 + K", "caution": "High-dose repletion therapy. Recheck vitamin D levels in 8-12 weeks. Contains vitamin K2 for calcium metabolism.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "high", "rationale": "D3 10,000 + K provides high-dose vitamin D3 with K2 for efficient repletion of severe deficiency. K2 ensures proper calcium utilization and prevents arterial calcification.", "indication": "Vitamin D 17.2 ng/mL (severe deficiency ≤20)", "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D level is very low, which can affect your bones, immune system, mood, and energy. This high-dose supplement will help bring your level up to optimal range, and the vitamin K2 helps your body use calcium properly.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 17.2 ng/mL (severe deficiency ≤20)"]}, {"dose": "1 tablet daily", "name": "Intrinsi B12-Folate™", "caution": "Contains intrinsic factor for enhanced B12 absorption. Ideal for patients with absorption concerns or vegetarian/vegan diets.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Intrinsi B12-Folate provides methylated B12 and folate with intrinsic factor for superior absorption. Supports energy, cognition, methylation, and nerve function.", "indication": "B12 333 pg/mL (suboptimal <400)", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "Your B12 and/or folate levels need support. This supplement provides highly absorbable forms of these essential vitamins to support your energy, brain function, and nervous system health.", "supportingFindings": ["B12 333 pg/mL (suboptimal <400)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\nYour major concerns are a low vitamin D level and a low HDL cholesterol. Your Vitamin D is 17.2 ng/mL (severe deficiency, ≤20 ng/mL) and your HDL is 39 mg/dL (below the target of 40 mg/dL). Most other results are reassuring: hemoglobin 14.5 g/dL, TSH 1.59 mIU/L, hemoglobin A1c 5.1%, AST 20 U/L, ALT 20 U/L, eGFR 124 mL/min, creatinine 0.68 mg/dL, and vitamin B12 333 pg/mL.\\n\\nPositive findings (what’s normal)\\n- Hemoglobin 14.5 g/dL — within normal range.\\n- TSH 1.59 mIU/L — thyroid function looks normal.\\n- Hemoglobin A1c 5.1% — blood sugar control is normal.\\n- AST 20 U/L and ALT 20 U/L — liver tests normal.\\n- eGFR 124 mL/min and creatinine 0.68 mg/dL — kidney function normal.\\n- Total testosterone 39.5 ng/dL — within expected female range; pattern suggests adequate androgens.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + concrete actions)\\n1. Vitamin D 25-OH 17.2 ng/mL (severe deficiency)\\n- Action: Discuss starting vitamin D3 with your provider. Options commonly used: prescription 50,000 IU weekly for 6–8 weeks OR over-the-counter 2,000 IU daily, then recheck in 8–12 weeks. Increase dietary sources: salmon, sardines, fortified milk/plant milk, eggs. Aim for 10–20 minutes of sun exposure to arms/face several times weekly if safe for your skin.\\n- Bone health: ensure 1,000–1,200 mg calcium/day from diet (dairy, leafy greens); do weight-bearing exercise 3–5x/week.\\n\\n2. HDL cholesterol 39 mg/dL (low) and LDL 110 mg/dL (near optimal), triglycerides 147 mg/dL\\n- Concrete lifestyle actions:\\n  - Add 25–30 g fiber daily: oatmeal for breakfast, beans with lunch, vegetables at dinner.\\n  - Include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n  - Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter and cheese.\\n  - Daily exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking or equivalent.\\n- Your 10-year ASCVD/PREVENT risk is 0% (LOW), which is good; still follow the steps above to raise HDL and improve overall heart health.\\n\\n3. Hematocrit 47.7% (slightly high) and platelets 408 K/uL (mildly elevated)\\n- Action: Drink an extra 1–2 cups of water the day before repeat labs (dehydration can raise hematocrit). Repeat CBC in 4–8 weeks. If platelets remain ≥400 K/uL, ask provider to check iron studies (ferritin, iron, TIBC) because reactive platelet elevations can follow iron deficiency or inflammation.\\n\\nHormones / cycle note\\n- Estradiol 32 pg/mL and progesterone 0.05 ng/mL depend on your menstrual phase. If you are premenopausal and concerned about cycles or ovulation, consider timing a progesterone test about 7 days before your expected period (mid-luteal) to confirm ovulation.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Discuss vitamin D replacement options with your clinician and start supplementation; recheck 25-OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Begin the cholesterol lifestyle changes now and recheck fasting lipids in 3–6 months.\\n- Repeat CBC in 4–8 weeks after staying well hydrated; obtain iron studies if platelets stay high or if you have symptoms.\\n- Book a follow-up visit to review progress and adjust plans. If you have new symptoms (worsening fatigue, bone pain, unusual bleeding, or any chest symptoms), contact the clinic sooner.\\n\\nYou’re doing well in many areas — these focused steps should help raise your vitamin D and HDL and address the mild CBC changes.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 14.5, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 47.7, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Slightly elevated hematocrit - may indicate dehydration.", "recommendation": "Encourage hydration. Repeat if persistent.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 408, "status": "borderline", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Mild platelet elevation (400-450). Commonly reactive to iron deficiency, inflammation, or infection.", "recommendation": "Check iron studies and inflammatory markers. Repeat CBC in 4-8 weeks.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 32, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.05, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 8.14, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 39.5, "status": "normal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone within normal female range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.59, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 17.2, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Severe Vitamin D deficiency (≤20 ng/mL). Associated with fatigue, bone loss, and immune dysfunction.", "recommendation": "High-dose repletion: Metagenics D3 10,000 + K daily for 8-12 weeks. Recheck levels after repletion.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 333, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 110, "status": "borderline", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Near optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet and exercise.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 39, "status": "abnormal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Low HDL - increased cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Increase aerobic exercise. Consider niacin if very low.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 147, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: N/A (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "PREVENT calculator is validated for ages 30-79 years. Patient is 25 years old. Use clinical judgment for risk assessment.", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): N/A", "recommendation": "Age outside validated range - use clinical judgment", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: N/A", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 20, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 20, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 124, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.68, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 39.5, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone Pattern D: Adequate Androgens, Persistent Symptoms", "interpretation": "Adequate Androgens pattern identified (Total T: 39.5 ng/dL (within range)). Total, free, and bioavailable testosterone are within normal ranges and SHBG is not extreme. If the patient reports fatigue, low mood, sleep disruption, or low libido, these symptoms are likely driven by non-androgenic factors. Findings to evaluate: Low Vitamin D (17.2 ng/mL) — contributes to fatigue and mood changes. Estrogen/Progesterone balance should be assessed (E2: 32 pg/mL, P4: 0.05 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Do not reflexively add testosterone when levels are adequate — look deeper. Evaluate iron status (ferritin target >50 for symptom resolution), thyroid function (optimize TSH), and sleep quality (consider sleep apnea screening with STOP-BANG). Assess estrogen and progesterone balance — E2/P4 imbalance is a common driver of persistent symptoms. Address CNS and psychosocial drivers: chronic stress, HPA axis dysregulation, mood disorders. Consider DHEA-S if not already checked. Support with lifestyle optimization: sleep hygiene, stress management, nutrition. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your testosterone levels are actually in a healthy range, which is good news. When symptoms persist despite normal testosterone, it usually means something else is contributing. Your provider will look at other factors like iron levels, thyroid function, sleep quality, and hormone balance to find what's driving your symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Total T 15-70 ng/dL, Free T 0.5-5.0 pg/mL (all within range)"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- Key abnormalities: severe vitamin D deficiency (25‑OH D 17.2 ng/mL), low HDL (39 mg/dL), borderline elevated platelets (408 K/uL) and hematocrit (47.7%). Total testosterone is in-range (39.5 ng/dL) with persistent symptoms — pattern consistent with “Adequate Androgens, Persistent Symptoms.”\\n- Most likely actionable drivers of symptoms to pursue first: vitamin D deficiency, possible iron deficiency/inflammation (given platelet elevation), and estrogen/progesterone balance (P4 0.05 ng/mL — interpret relative to cycle phase).\\n- No indication to initiate testosterone therapy based on current labs alone.\\n\\nPriority actions (organized CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL (notify provider immediately / start treatment now)\\n- Vitamin D deficiency: Notify provider for initiation of high‑dose repletion per clinic protocol.\\n  - Suggested regimen per protocol: Metagenics D3 10,000 IU + K daily x 8–12 weeks.\\n  - Baseline safety labs: confirm recent renal function (creatinine, eGFR — already normal) and consider baseline serum calcium. Arrange patient education regarding dosing and signs of hypercalcemia.\\n  - Recheck 25‑OH vitamin D level 8–12 weeks after start of repletion.\\n\\nURGENT (initiate testing/monitoring within 1–4 weeks)\\n- Iron studies and inflammatory markers: Order ferritin, serum iron, TIBC or transferrin, transferrin saturation, CRP or ESR now to evaluate cause of thrombocytosis.\\n  - If ferritin <50 ng/mL (symptom target >50): consider iron therapy (see below) and plan recheck ferritin in 8–12 weeks after therapy.\\n- CBC repeat: Repeat CBC in 4–8 weeks to reassess platelets and hematocrit after hydration and initial evaluation.\\n- Progesterone timing: Clarify menstrual cycle day and contraceptive/HRT status. If assessing ovulation/fertility or progesterone adequacy, order mid‑luteal progesterone (~7 days pre-menses; ~day 21 in a 28‑day cycle) rather than random level.\\n\\nROUTINE (follow-up in weeks–months)\\n- Lipids: Lifestyle intervention first; repeat fasting lipid panel in 3 months.\\n- Sleep/psychosocial assessment: If fatigue/mood/sleep symptoms persist, screen/referral for sleep study (if STOP‑BANG elevated or clinical suspicion) and consider behavioral health referral for mood/stress/HPA axis concerns.\\n- Consider DHEA‑S and full thyroid panel (Free T4, Free T3, anti‑TPO) if symptoms persist despite normal TSH.\\n\\nSpecific medication / dose recommendations and provider notification requirements\\n- Vitamin D:\\n  - Start Metagenics D3 10,000 IU + K PO daily x 8–12 weeks per protocol (provider must authorize).\\n  - Recheck 25‑OH in 8–12 weeks; if adequate, convert to maintenance dosing (e.g., 1,000–2,000 IU daily or as directed by provider).\\n  - Monitor serum calcium if symptoms or high-dose therapy prolonged.\\n- Iron:\\n  - Do NOT start empiric iron prior to iron studies. If ferritin is <50 ng/mL and iron deficiency is confirmed, recommended initial regimen for most patients: oral ferrous sulfate 325 mg (65 mg elemental iron) given every other day (or daily if protocol prefers) with vitamin C 250 mg to aid absorption, and counsel regarding GI side effects and interactions.\\n  - Reassess ferritin 8–12 weeks after starting iron. Target ferritin >50 ng/mL for symptomatic improvement.\\n  - Notify provider if ferritin very low (<15 ng/mL), hemoglobin falling, or intolerance/poor response.\\n- Testosterone:\\n  - No testosterone initiation or dose changes recommended — levels are adequate. If provider still considers androgen therapy after thorough evaluation, escalate to physician for individualized dosing and monitoring plan (not indicated from current data).\\n\\nLifestyle interventions to recommend to patient (staff to counsel)\\n- Hydration: Encourage increased oral fluids; recheck hematocrit after hydration.\\n- Vitamin D: Emphasize adherence to high‑dose repletion regimen and follow-up labs.\\n- Cardiovascular risk and lipids:\\n  - Aerobic exercise ≥150 minutes/week (aim for 150–300 minutes moderate intensity).\\n  - Resistance training 2x/week.\\n  - Dietary: Mediterranean‑style or DASH diet; reduce refined carbs/trans fats; increase omega‑3 fatty fish and fiber.\\n  - Weight loss target if overweight: aim for 5–10% to improve HDL/TG.\\n  - Smoking cessation if applicable.\\n  - Consider discussing alcohol moderation (excess lowers HDL).\\n- Sleep hygiene and stress management: sleep regularity, screen for obstructive sleep apnea if clinically indicated, refer behavioral health for stress/mood interventions if needed.\\n- Iron absorption counseling if iron started: take iron on an empty stomach or with vitamin C; avoid calcium, tea, coffee and antacids within 2 hours of iron dose.\\n\\nAdditional testing or referrals to consider\\n- Laboratory:\\n  - Ferritin, iron, TIBC/transferrin, transferrin saturation, CRP/ESR (now).\\n  - Repeat CBC in 4–8 weeks.\\n  - Repeat 25‑OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks post‑repletion.\\n  - Fasting lipid panel in 3 months after lifestyle intervention.\\n  - Mid‑luteal progesterone (timed) if evaluating ovulation/fertility or P4 adequacy.\\n  - Consider DHEA‑S, Free T4/Free T3 and thyroid antibodies if symptoms persist despite normal TSH.\\n  - Consider calcium and PTH if bone health concerns or if prolonged high‑dose vitamin D planned.\\n- Imaging / specialty:\\n  - Consider DEXA scan if other osteoporosis risk factors or clinical concern for low bone density (esp. with severe vitamin D deficiency and other risk factors).\\n  - Sleep medicine referral if high clinical suspicion for sleep apnea despite STOP‑BANG.\\n  - Behavioral health/psychology for mood/stress/HPA axis dysfunction if clinically indicated.\\n  - Hematology referral if platelets rise >450 K/uL persistently or if thrombocytosis unexplained after iron and inflammation work‑up.\\n\\nPhysician notification thresholds (staff action points)\\n- Notify provider promptly if:\\n  - 25‑OH vitamin D ≤20 ng/mL (start repletion) — action already indicated.\\n  - Platelets rise >450 K/uL or continue to trend upward on repeat CBC.\\n  - Hematocrit >50% or symptomatic (headache, visual changes, thrombosis symptoms).\\n  - Ferritin very low (<15 ng/mL) or hemoglobin falling.\\n  - Any abnormal calcium or renal function during high‑dose vitamin D therapy.\\n  - If considering pharmacologic HDL therapy (niacin) — requires provider discussion and monitoring.\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (actionable checklist)\\n- Today / within 1 week:\\n  - Notify provider to authorize vitamin D repletion (Metagenics D3 10,000 IU + K daily x 8–12 weeks); counsel patient on dosing and side‑effects.\\n  - Order ferritin, iron, TIBC/transferrin, transferrin saturation, CRP/ESR; clarify cycle day and contraceptive/HRT status.\\n  - Hydration counseling; document plan to repeat CBC.\\n- 4–8 weeks:\\n  - Repeat CBC to reassess platelets and hematocrit.\\n  - If iron therapy started, recheck ferritin at 8–12 weeks.\\n- 8–12 weeks:\\n  - Recheck 25‑OH vitamin D (and serum calcium if concerned); convert to maintenance dose if adequate.\\n  - Reassess symptoms; review iron study results and consider iron therapy if ferritin <50.\\n- 3 months:\\n  - Repeat fasting lipid panel after lifestyle interventions.\\n  - Re-evaluate symptoms; consider further testing (DHEA‑S, thyroid antibodies, mid‑luteal progesterone if not yet performed) and referrals as indicated.\\n\\nNotes for staff\\n- Do not start testosterone based solely on symptoms when total and free testosterone are in-range. Escalate to provider for a broader investigational approach.\\n- Ensure progesterone is interpreted relative to menstrual cycle timing; obtain cycle day or schedule mid‑luteal draw if ovulation assessment is needed.\\n- Document all patient education given (hydration, vitamin D dosing, iron absorption guidance, lifestyle recommendations) and flag abnormal results for provider review per above thresholds.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 3.77, "detail": "Triglycerides 147 / HDL 39 = 3.77", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5.1%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 20, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 1, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (1/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": []}	2026-03-10 21:37:51.600023	\N
62	Amanda Richardson	female	2026-03-26 19:28:33.843	{"fsh": 11.9, "mcv": 89.2, "rbc": 4.17, "wbc": 9.9, "acne": false, "shbg": 43.8, "onHRT": true, "anxiety": true, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "ferritin": 8.8, "hairLoss": false, "vitaminD": 31.3, "estradiol": 181, "headaches": true, "lowEnergy": true, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 279, "hematocrit": 37.2, "hemoglobin": 12, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Amanda Richardson", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 36, "bmi": 24, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 110, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 0.6, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 22.1, "lowMotivation": false, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "freeTestosterone": 3}	{"redFlags": [{"action": "Metagenics Hemagenics OR Prescription 65mg Elemental Iron. Evaluate for occult blood loss.", "message": "Ferritin is 8.8 ng/mL (<10 threshold).", "category": "Iron - Severe Deficiency", "severity": "urgent"}], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History/context: 36-year-old female established in Women's Hormone & Primary Care Clinic. Patient presents for review of recent laboratory results related to hormone therapy monitoring and general health screening.\\n- Reported symptoms consistent with lab abnormalities (patient endorses / was queried regarding typical estrogen-dominant symptoms): insomnia with early-morning waking (3 a.m.), intermittent anxiety, breast fullness/tenderness, and irregular/heavier menses. No syncope, chest pain, shortness of breath, melena, hematochezia, or overt GI bleeding reported. No known pregnancy. No current report of severe fatigue beyond baseline. Patient understands need for further evaluation for iron deficiency.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\nVital signs: BMI and BP not provided in lab packet. STOP-BANG sleep apnea risk: 0 points.\\n\\nLaboratory results (03/26/2026)\\n- Complete Blood Count (CBC)\\n  - Hemoglobin: 12.0 g/dL — within reference range (NORMAL)\\n  - Hematocrit: 37.2 % — NORMAL\\n  - MCV: 89.2 fL — NORMAL\\n  - Platelets: 279 K/uL — NORMAL\\n- Iron studies\\n  - Ferritin: 8.8 ng/mL — CRITICAL / Severe iron deficiency (FLAGGED; reference threshold for concern <10 ng/mL)\\n  - (No additional iron panel values provided)\\n- Hormones\\n  - Estradiol (E2): 181 pg/mL — ABNORMAL (elevated relative to typical HRT target range; consistent with estrogen dominance pattern)\\n  - Progesterone: 0.6 ng/mL — ABNORMAL (below expected HRT therapeutic/mid-luteal range)\\n  - Testosterone, Total: 22.1 ng/dL — ABNORMAL (low for age despite HRT)\\n  - Free Testosterone: 3 pg/mL — NORMAL (per report)\\n  - SHBG: 43.8 nmol/L — NORMAL\\n  - FSH: 11.9 mIU/mL — NORMAL for perimenopausal transition range\\n- Vitamin / Other\\n  - Vitamin D, 25-OH: 31.3 ng/mL — BORDERLINE / Insufficient (31–40 ng/mL)\\n- Risk scores: STOP-BANG 0 (low risk for obstructive sleep apnea)\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1) Severe iron deficiency — ferritin 8.8 ng/mL (CRITICAL). Iron stores severely depleted despite preserved hemoglobin (Hgb 12.0 g/dL) — iron deficiency without anemia at present. This is a high-priority red flag requiring timely treatment and evaluation for source of blood loss.\\n   - Clinical significance: symptomatic or progressive iron depletion; risk for developing iron-deficiency anemia; needs replacement and investigation for occult blood loss (GI vs gynecologic).\\n   - Differential: heavy menstrual blood loss (menorrhagia), occult GI bleeding, dietary insufficiency, malabsorption (less likely without other history), pregnancy (unlikely but must be excluded if applicable).\\n\\n2) Estrogen dominance pattern consistent with ovulatory dysfunction — Estradiol 181 pg/mL with low progesterone 0.6 ng/mL indicates continued estrogenic exposure without adequate luteal-phase progesterone. Correlates with reported symptoms (insomnia/3am waking, anxiety, breast tenderness, irregular/heavy cycles).\\n   - Clinical significance: symptoms of estrogen dominance; potential endometrial protection needed if on unopposed estrogen; abnormal uterine bleeding risk.\\n   - Differential: exogenous estrogen dose too high or inappropriate timing relative to progesterone; anovulatory cycles; perimenopausal endocrine variability.\\n\\n3) Low total testosterone — 22.1 ng/dL despite HRT (Free T 3 pg/mL reported as normal). Possible inadequate androgen replacement or altered bioavailability (SHBG 43.8 nmol/L is within normal range).\\n   - Clinical significance: may contribute to low libido, decreased energy, or mood changes (patient-reported symptoms correlate partially).\\n   - Differential: inadequate dosing/administration of testosterone therapy, medication interactions, lab variability.\\n\\n4) Vitamin D insufficiency — 25-OH Vitamin D 31.3 ng/mL (borderline insufficient).\\n   - Clinical significance: suboptimal for hormone optimization, bone health; supplementation indicated.\\n\\n5) Other labs normal — CBC indices otherwise normal (Hgb/Hct/MCV), platelets normal, SHBG and FSH are within expected ranges for perimenopausal transition. STOP-BANG low risk for sleep apnea.\\n\\nPLAN:\\nGeneral: Results reviewed and discussed with patient today (03/26/2026). Patient counseled on urgency of iron deficiency and need for further evaluation and prompt treatment. Follow-up and monitoring plans outlined below.\\n\\n1) Severe iron deficiency (Ferritin 8.8 ng/mL)\\n   - Treatment: Begin iron replacement promptly.\\n     - Preferred (non-prescription supplement option): Metagenics Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support — 1 tablet PO twice daily with meals.\\n     - Prescription option (if patient prefers Rx): Oral ferrous sulfate 325 mg (contains ~65 mg elemental iron) — dosing to be determined by prescribing clinician; common regimen is one tablet PO once to twice daily as tolerated. Recommend discussion with prescribing clinician for definitive Rx dosing.\\n   - Counseling: Take iron with vitamin C or orange juice to improve absorption; avoid calcium, tea, coffee, and high-fiber foods close to dosing. Expect GI side effects (constipation, nausea); consider stool softener or polyethylene glycol PRN for constipation. If Hemagenics chosen, review specific product directions and interactions.\\n   - Diagnostic evaluation for source of iron loss:\\n     - Order fecal immunochemical test (FIT) x1 (or stool guaiac) to evaluate occult GI bleeding. If positive or persistent iron deficiency, arrange GI referral for further evaluation (consider endoscopic workup as clinically indicated).\\n     - Arrange gynecologic evaluation for possible heavy menstrual bleeding (pelvic exam and transvaginal ultrasound as indicated, assessment for fibroids or structural causes). Consider menstrual blood loss quantification and review current contraception/HRT effects on bleeding.\\n   - Monitoring: Repeat labs in 2–4 weeks (urgent recheck window) — CBC (Hgb/Hct), ferritin, and iron studies to confirm response. Continue treatment until ferritin >50 ng/mL (typical target for repletion) and clinical improvement; adjust plan per results.\\n\\n2) Estrogen dominance / low progesterone (Estradiol 181 pg/mL; Progesterone 0.6 ng/mL)\\n   - Immediate: Discussed need to optimize progesterone to protect endometrium and address symptoms.\\n   - Management recommendation (to be implemented by HRT prescriber):\\n     - Consider increasing progesterone exposure. Options include micronized progesterone (e.g., 100–200 mg PO nightly for luteal support or cyclic dosing such as days 14–28 depending on regimen and patient preference). Alternatively, consider continuous combined therapy if appropriate.\\n     - Reassess estradiol regimen: consider lowering estradiol dose or altering route of administration if symptomatic or if clinician determines estradiol is supratherapeutic for patient goals.\\n   - Monitoring: Re-check serum estradiol and progesterone in 2–4 weeks after any HRT dose adjustments to confirm therapeutic progesterone and avoid sustained unopposed estrogen. If changes not made, re-check hormone panel in 2–4 weeks given abnormal values.\\n   - Additional: If heavy bleeding persists or endometrial concerns arise, obtain transvaginal ultrasound (endometrial thickness) and consider referral to gynecology.\\n\\n3) Low total testosterone (22.1 ng/dL) despite HRT\\n   - Plan: Review current testosterone formulation, dose, application technique/adherence, and timing of blood draw relative to dosing. Consider increasing dose or changing formulation if clinically indicated.\\n   - Action: Discuss with prescribing clinician/endocrinologist for potential adjustment. Repeat testosterone (total and free) with clear timing instructions relative to dosing in 2–4 weeks after any adjustment.\\n   - Consider checking additional labs if adjusting therapy (lipids, LFTs, CBC) per testosterone monitoring guidelines.\\n\\n4) Vitamin D insufficiency (25-OH 31.3 ng/mL)\\n   - Start Vitamin D3 5,000 IU daily with a meal plus K (as recommended): D3 5,000 + K — 1 softgel PO daily with a meal (medium-priority).\\n   - Re-check 25-OH Vitamin D in 8–12 weeks to confirm repletion; then adjust dose to maintenance (e.g., 1,000–2,000 IU daily) as appropriate.\\n\\n5) Adjunct supplement recommendations provided and counseling\\n   - Magtein® Magnesium L-Threonate — 3 capsules daily divided doses (medium priority); useful for sleep/mood/neurologic support.\\n   - EstroFactors™ — 2 tablets twice daily (medium priority) — as recommended by clinic for symptom management.\\n   - AdvaClear® — 2 capsules twice daily (medium priority) — for detoxification/adverse estrogen metabolites support per clinic protocol.\\n   - Review for possible interactions with iron and other meds/supplements; counsel on timing (separate iron and calcium/magnesium by 2 hours).\\n\\n6) Safety and red-flag actions\\n   - Urgent red flag: ferritin <10 ng/mL — requires prompt treatment and evaluation for blood loss. Ordered FIT stool test today (or will order per clinic protocol) and gynecologic referral as indicated. If patient develops melena, hematemesis, hematochezia, syncope, or progressive weakness, instructed to present to ER immediately.\\n   - Documented recommendation for GI evaluation if occult blood loss suspected or if iron deficiency persists despite supplementation.\\n\\n7) Follow-up and monitoring\\n   - Urgent recheck window for critical values: 2–4 weeks.\\n     - Labs to repeat at 2–4 weeks: CBC (Hgb/Hct), ferritin, iron studies (if available), estradiol, progesterone, total testosterone (timed appropriately relative to dosing), and basic metabolic panel if on oral iron and symptomatic.\\n   - Vitamin D recheck at 8–12 weeks.\\n   - Gynecology referral to be scheduled within 2–4 weeks for evaluation of heavy/irregular menses if patient agrees.\\n   - GI referral if FIT positive or if iron deficiency persists despite supplementation.\\n   - Clinic follow-up appointment / telephone check-in in 2–4 weeks to review repeat labs and finalize HRT adjustments.\\n\\nCommunication:\\n- Results reviewed and discussed with patient today (03/26/2026). Risks, benefits, and rationale for iron repletion and for adjusting HRT/progesterone were explained. Patient verbalized understanding and agrees with planned steps. Questions answered.\\n\\nDisposition:\\n- Start Hemagenics RBC Support 1 tablet PO twice daily with meals today (or prescription oral iron 65 mg elemental per prescribing clinician).\\n- Start Vitamin D3 5,000 IU + K daily.\\n- Begin other recommended supplements as desired and after reviewing interactions.\\n- Arrange FIT/stool occult blood testing and gynecology referral.\\n- Reassess labs in 2–4 weeks and follow up as above.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\nDate: 03/26/2026.", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 tablet twice daily with meals", "name": "Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support", "caution": "Contains iron, folate, and B12 for comprehensive RBC support. Avoid taking with calcium-rich foods or dairy. May cause mild GI upset initially.", "category": "iron", "priority": "high", "rationale": "Hemagenics provides highly absorbable iron bisglycinate with B12, B6, and folate for comprehensive red blood cell support. Indicated for functional iron deficiency, iron deficiency without anemia, and iron-responsive symptoms.", "indication": "Ferritin 8.8 ng/mL (severely depleted ≤20); Fatigue with iron depletion", "phenotypes": ["Iron Deficiency"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "Your lab results suggest your iron stores are lower than optimal. This supplement provides a gentle, well-absorbed form of iron along with B12 and folate to support your energy levels, hair health, and overall vitality.", "supportingFindings": ["Ferritin 8.8 ng/mL (severely depleted ≤20)", "Fatigue with iron depletion"]}, {"dose": "1 softgel daily with meal", "name": "D3 5,000 + K", "caution": "Repletion dose for deficiency/insufficiency. Contains vitamin K2 for optimal calcium metabolism. Recheck levels in 8-12 weeks.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "D3 5,000 + K provides vitamin D3 with K2 for repletion of deficiency/insufficiency. Target ≥60 ng/mL for bone, 