rrent testosterone therapy documented.\\n- Symptoms related to abnormal results: Patient reports intermittent low energy/fatigue. No GI upset, polyuria, polydipsia, muscle weakness, or neurocognitive changes reported at this visit.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\nLaboratory results (03/10/2026) — all values with units; abnormal/borderline values flagged.\\n\\nCBC:\\n- Hemoglobin (Hgb): 14.1 g/dL — within reference range\\n- Hematocrit (Hct): 42 % — within reference range\\n- Platelets: 406 K/uL — BORDERLINE (mild thrombocytosis; lab cutoff 400–450 K/uL)\\n\\nIron/Ferritin:\\n- Ferritin: 83.8 ng/mL — within reference range\\n\\nMetabolic / Renal / Hepatic:\\n- AST: 15 U/L — normal\\n- ALT: 13 U/L — normal\\n- Creatinine: 0.67 mg/dL — normal\\n- eGFR: 124 mL/min/1.73m2 — normal\\n- Serum calcium: not provided (ordered as baseline recommended)\\n\\nThyroid:\\n- TSH: 1.55 mIU/L — normal\\n\\nVitamins / Nutritional:\\n- Vitamin D (25‑OH): 15.9 ng/mL — ABNORMAL (Severe vitamin D deficiency; ≤20 ng/mL)\\n- Vitamin B12: 695 pg/mL — normal\\n- Folate: 2.01 ng/mL — ABNORMAL (folate deficiency)\\n\\nLipids / Cardiovascular:\\n- Triglycerides: 117 mg/dL — normal\\n- HDL cholesterol: 37 mg/dL — ABNORMAL (low HDL)\\n- LDL cholesterol: 127 mg/dL — BORDERLINE (near‑optimal)\\n- Apolipoprotein B (ApoB): 100 mg/dL — BORDERLINE\\n- Lipoprotein(a): 6 mg/dL — normal\\n- 10‑Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT): 0 %\\n- 10‑Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT): 0 %\\n- 10‑Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT): 0 %\\n- Cardiovascular category: Low; statin use: age outside validated range — use clinical judgment\\n\\nInsulin resistance screening / Metabolic phenotype:\\n- Insulin Resistance Screening: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD — 2 of 6 markers positive (phenotype: visceral / metabolic insulin resistance). Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA‑IR).\\n\\nOther:\\n- Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP‑BANG): 0 points — low risk\\n- LFTs, renal function listed above\\n- No vitals/BMI recorded in this encounter (not available)\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1) Severe Vitamin D deficiency — 25‑OH vitamin D 15.9 ng/mL.\\n   - Clinical significance: increased risk of bone demineralization, fatigue, immune modulation. Requires repletion.\\n   - Red flags: baseline hypercalcemia/renal impairment/granulomatous disease would alter management.\\n\\n2) Folate deficiency — serum folate 2.01 ng/mL.\\n   - Clinical significance: important for fertility/pregnancy prevention of neural tube defects; symptomatic deficiency can contribute to fatigue/macrocytosis (Hgb currently normal).\\n   - Differential/consideration: dietary insufficiency, malabsorption, medication effects (anticonvulsants, methotrexate), increased requirements (pregnancy).\\n\\n3) Dyslipidemia — Low HDL (37 mg/dL) with borderline LDL (127 mg/dL) and borderline ApoB (100 mg/dL).\\n   - Clinical significance: low HDL is an atherogenic feature despite low 10‑yr ASCVD risk; ApoB borderline may indicate atherogenic particle burden.\\n   - Differential/consideration: lifestyle factors, genetics, medication effects (OCP/HRT), insulin resistance.\\n\\n4) Insulin resistance — Screening shows moderate likelihood (2/6 markers positive); phenotype visceral/metabolic IR.\\n   - Clinical significance: hyperinsulinemia physiology; increased cardiometabolic risk; consider objective confirmation (fasting insulin, fasting glucose, HOMA‑IR).\\n   - Differential/consideration: early metabolic syndrome; assess weight, waist circumference, diet, activity.\\n\\n5) Borderline thrombocytosis — platelets 406 K/uL.\\n   - Clinical significance: mild reactive thrombocytosis possible (iron deficiency, inflammation, infection). Ferritin normal, but functional iron deficiency or inflammation still possible.\\n   - Plan considerations: repeat CBC; evaluate iron indices and inflammatory markers. Hematology referral if >450 K/uL or persistent/upward trend.\\n\\n6) Other labs within normal limits — Hgb 14.1 g/dL, ferritin 83.8 ng/mL, B12 695 pg/mL, TSH 1.55 mIU/L, AST/ALT normal, creatinine/eGFR normal, Lp(a) low.\\n\\nPLAN:\\nOverall approach: Initiate prioritized repletion and targeted testing now; implement lifestyle measures for cardiometabolic risk; arrange follow‑up labs and physician review per timeline below. Results reviewed with patient; pregnancy status confirmed as negative today verbally — confirm if planning pregnancy.\\n\\n1) Severe Vitamin D deficiency (25‑OH D 15.9 ng/mL)\\n   - Start Metagenics D3 10,000 IU + K (1 softgel PO daily with a meal) for 8–12 weeks per clinic repletion protocol.\\n   - Order baseline serum calcium prior to initiation (critical safety check).\\n   - Counsel patient on adherence, dietary sources of vitamin D, and signs of hypercalcemia (nausea, vomiting, polyuria, polydipsia, confusion) and instruct to discontinue and notify clinic if symptoms occur.\\n   - Contraindications/precautions reviewed: confirm no history of hypercalcemia, granulomatous disease, lymphoma, or stage 4–5 CKD. Patient reports no such history.\\n   - Recheck 25‑OH vitamin D level at 8–12 weeks after initiation. After repletion, transition to maintenance dosing per physician direction (typical maintenance 1,000–2,000 IU daily individualized).\\n\\n2) Folate deficiency (folate 2.01 ng/mL)\\n   - Start folic acid 1 mg PO daily.\\n   - If patient is pregnant or planning pregnancy: discuss with physician/OBGYN and consider high‑dose folic acid 4 mg PO daily per obstetric recommendations — physician notification required if pregnancy planned.\\n   - Recheck serum folate in 4–8 weeks.\\n   - Provide dietary counseling: increase leafy greens, legumes, citrus, fortified grains.\\n   - Consider evaluation for causes if folate remains low on recheck (malabsorption, medications).\\n\\n3) Dyslipidemia / low HDL / borderline ApoB (HDL 37 mg/dL; LDL 127 mg/dL; ApoB 100 mg/dL)\\n   - Initiate lifestyle interventions now:\\n     - Exercise: ≥150 minutes/week moderate aerobic activity + resistance training 2x/week to help raise HDL.\\n     - Diet: Mediterranean‑style eating pattern, reduce refined carbohydrates and trans fats, increase fatty fish/omega‑3 sources.\\n     - Weight management counseling if BMI elevated (not recorded today) — target 5–10% weight loss if applicable.\\n     - Smoking cessation counseling if applicable.\\n   - Recommend medium‑priority supplements: OmegaGenics® Fish Oil Neuro 1000 (1 softgel daily; may increase to 1–2 daily) as recommended by clinic.\\n   - Repeat fasting lipid panel (including ApoB if prior fasting) in 3 months after lifestyle changes. If LDL ≥160 mg/dL, ApoB persistently >100 mg/dL or other risk enhancers present, discuss pharmacotherapy with physician and consider cardiology referral.\\n   - Document medication list and review for OCP/HRT that may impact lipids; patient advised to inform clinic if taking hormonal therapy.\\n\\n4) Insulin resistance screening (moderate likelihood)\\n   - Order fasting glucose and fasting insulin to calculate HOMA‑IR for objective confirmation.\\n   - Consider hemoglobin A1c if not recently obtained (not provided here); order if clinically indicated.\\n   - Provide lifestyle counseling targeted to insulin sensitivity: reduce refined carbohydrate intake, fiber‑rich whole foods, regular physical activity, weight loss if indicated.\\n   - Consider referral to nutritionist for structured dietary plan and weight management.\\n   - Reassess after labs and lifestyle intervention; consider endocrine/metabolic referral if HOMA‑IR abnormal or clinical progression.\\n\\n5) Borderline thrombocytosis (platelets 406 K/uL)\\n   - Order iron studies: serum iron, TIBC or transferrin, transferrin saturation — to assess for functional iron deficiency despite normal ferritin.\\n   - Order inflammatory marker(s): CRP and/or ESR.\\n   - Repeat CBC (including platelets) in 4–8 weeks to reassess.\\n   - If platelets remain >450 K/uL or show upward trend, arrange hematology referral.\\n   - Notify physician urgently if platelets rise >450 K/uL or patient develops thrombosis/bleeding symptoms.\\n\\n6) Additional orders and testing\\n   - Baseline serum calcium (before high‑dose vitamin D) — order now.\\n   - Repeat 25‑OH vitamin D at 8–12 weeks post‑repletion.\\n   - Serum folate recheck in 4–8 weeks.\\n   - CBC with differential in 4–8 weeks.\\n   - Iron studies (serum iron, TIBC/transferrin, transferrin saturation) and CRP/ESR — order now.\\n   - Fasting lipid panel + ApoB in 3 months.\\n   - Fasting glucose and fasting insulin (for HOMA‑IR) to confirm insulin resistance — order now.\\n   - Pregnancy test if patient becomes pregnant or plans conception — urgent physician notification if pregnancy planned due to folate deficiency.\\n   - Medication reconciliation to document OCP/HRT use and medications that interact (e.g., thiazides) — confirm and document.\\n\\n7) Referrals and education\\n   - Referral to registered dietitian/nutritionist for folate optimization, weight/lipid management, and vitamin D dietary counseling — consider scheduling.\\n   - Consider cardiology referral if ApoB remains >100–130 mg/dL despite lifestyle therapy or if additional risk factors develop.\\n   - Hematology referral if platelets >450 K/uL persist or progressive cytopenias noted.\\n   - Provide patient education on diet, exercise, supplement adherence, and signs/symptoms that require urgent contact.\\n\\n8) Follow‑up timeline\\n   - Immediate (start within 1–7 days): begin vitamin D D3 10,000 IU + K daily and folic acid 1 mg daily. Order baseline calcium now.\\n   - 1–2 weeks: ensure iron studies and CRP/ESR are ordered and review; physician to review lab results within 1–2 weeks per clinic protocol.\\n   - 4–8 weeks: repeat CBC (platelets) and recheck serum folate.\\n   - 8–12 weeks: recheck 25‑OH vitamin D; transition to maintenance dosing per physician.\\n   - 3 months: repeat fasting lipid panel and ApoB to reassess need for pharmacotherapy; review fasting insulin/glucose results and calculate HOMA‑IR.\\n   - Recheck window for routine follow‑up labs: 3–6 months as outlined above.\\n\\nPhysician notification requirements:\\n- Notify physician immediately if:\\n  - Platelets >450 K/uL or rising trend.\\n  - Patient is pregnant or planning pregnancy (folate deficiency requires obstetric/physician input).\\n  - Baseline serum calcium is elevated prior to vitamin D initiation.\\n  - Any new symptoms of thrombosis, hypercalcemia, or adverse supplement reactions.\\n- Otherwise, place results and treatment plan in chart and request routine physician review within 1–2 weeks.\\n\\nDocumentation:\\n- Results discussed with patient today. Counseling provided regarding vitamin D repletion, folic acid supplementation, lifestyle modifications for lipids and insulin resistance, and signs/symptoms requiring urgent contact. Medication/supplement plan and laboratory follow‑up schedule reviewed. Patient verbalized understanding and agreed to start supplements and obtain recommended labs. Questions answered.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\nDate: 03/10/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": true, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": true, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": true, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "preventRisk": {"riskCategory": "low", "tenYearASCVD": 0, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "PREVENT calculator is validated for ages 30-79 years. Patient is 25 years old. Use clinical judgment for risk assessment.", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0, "ageValidFor30Year": false, "tenYearHFPercentage": "N/A", "tenYearHeartFailure": 0, "statinRecommendation": "Age outside validated range - use clinical judgment", "tenYearCVDPercentage": "N/A", "tenYearASCVDPercentage": "N/A"}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 softgel daily with meal", "name": "D3 10,0