ry": "metabolic", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "UltraFlora Healthy Weight with Akkermansia provides targeted probiotic support for the gut-metabolic axis. Akkermansia muciniphila supports gut barrier integrity, metabolic health, and healthy weight management. Indicated for insulin resistance, metabolic syndrome features, visceral adiposity, and weight loss resistance.", "indication": "Insulin resistance / visceral adiposity phenotype (gut-metabolic axis support); ApoB 100 mg/dL (atherogenic metabolic pattern)", "phenotypes": ["Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity"], "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This specialized probiotic contains Akkermansia, a beneficial gut bacteria linked to healthy metabolism and weight management. It supports your gut barrier, helps manage metabolic inflammation, and works with your body's natural weight regulation systems.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance / visceral adiposity phenotype (gut-metabolic axis support)", "ApoB 100 mg/dL (atherogenic metabolic pattern)"]}, {"dose": "1 tablet daily", "name": "Intrinsi B12-Folate™", "caution": "Contains intrinsic factor for enhanced B12 absorption. Ideal for patients with absorption concerns or vegetarian/vegan diets.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Intrinsi B12-Folate provides methylated B12 and folate with intrinsic factor for superior absorption. Supports energy, cognition, methylation, and nerve function.", "indication": "Folate 2.01 ng/mL (low)", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "Your B12 and/or folate levels need support. This supplement provides highly absorbable forms of these essential vitamins to support your energy, brain function, and nervous system health.", "supportingFindings": ["Folate 2.01 ng/mL (low)"]}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Essential for patients on statins.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "low", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "ApoB 100 mg/dL (cardiovascular support); LDL 127 mg/dL; HDL 37 mg/dL (low)", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["ApoB 100 mg/dL (cardiovascular support)", "LDL 127 mg/dL", "HDL 37 mg/dL (low)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 6, "apoBValue": 100, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "borderline", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 0, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": true}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "generally_recommended", "indicated": true, "rationale": "ApoB ≥90 mg/dL (100 mg/dL) with risk enhancers (low HDL, elevated ApoB). Statin discussion recommended."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall summary:\\nYour labs show some important areas to address, but many results are normal. Key concerns are severe vitamin D deficiency (25-OH vitamin D 15.9 ng/mL), low HDL cholesterol (37 mg/dL), and folate deficiency (2.01 ng/mL). Your 10-year cardiovascular risk is 0% (PREVENT), which means your short-term heart-attack/stroke risk is low. Other labs such as hemoglobin 14.1 g/dL, ferritin 83.8 ng/mL, TSH 1.55 mIU/L, and vitamin B12 695 pg/mL are within expected ranges.\\n\\nPositive findings:\\n- Hemoglobin 14.1 g/dL and hematocrit 42% — no current anemia.\\n- Ferritin 83.8 ng/mL — iron stores are adequate.\\n- TSH 1.55 mIU/L — thyroid function in the normal range.\\n- Kidney and liver function normal: eGFR 124 mL/min, AST 15 U/L, ALT 13 U/L.\\n- Lipoprotein(a) 6 mg/dL and triglycerides 117 mg/dL are good.\\n\\nAreas to improve (with concrete actions):\\n1) Vitamin D: 25-OH vitamin D 15.9 ng/mL (severe deficiency).\\n- Start vitamin D3 supplementation after discussing with your provider: options include 50,000 IU weekly for 8–12 weeks OR 4,000–5,000 IU daily, then recheck level in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Increase dietary calcium to 1,000–1,200 mg/day (dairy, fortified plant milk, leafy greens).\\n- Add 20–30 minutes of weight-bearing exercise 3–4 times weekly (walking, stairs, light strength training) to support bone health and reduce fracture risk.\\n- Aim for 10–20 minutes sunlight exposure on arms/legs several times per week when safe.\\n\\n2) Folate: 2.01 ng/mL (deficient).\\n- Begin folic acid 400–800 mcg daily (if pregnant or planning pregnancy, confirm optimal dose with your provider; many recommend 400–800 mcg or 1 mg depending on history).\\n- Add foods rich in folate: dark leafy greens, beans, lentils, fortified cereals.\\n- Recheck folate in 4–8 weeks after starting.\\n\\n3) Lipids: HDL 37 mg/dL (low); LDL 127 mg/dL; apolipoprotein B 100 mg/dL.\\n- Add 25–30 g fiber daily: oatmeal for breakfast, beans for lunch, vegetables at dinner.\\n- Include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n- Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter and high-fat cheeses.\\n- Daily exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking or equivalent.\\n- Repeat a fasting lipid panel in 3–6 months. Discuss the borderline ApoB/LDL with your clinician if family history or other concerns; statin decision should use clinical judgment.\\n\\nOther notes and next steps:\\n- Platelets 406 K/uL (mildly high). This can be reactive; repeat CBC in 6–12 weeks. Seek care sooner if you notice unusual bruising or bleeding.\\n- No sex hormones were measured; if you are evaluating menstrual symptoms, testing timing matters (we can plan tests by cycle phase).\\n- Please review any supplements/medications with your provider before starting high-dose vitamin D or folic acid.\\n\\nFollow-up timeline: start supplements/diet changes now, recheck vitamin D and folate in 8–12 weeks, repeat lipids in 3–6 months, and repeat CBC in 6–12 weeks. If you want, I can draft a discussion note for your provider to review these plans.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 14.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 42, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 406, "status": "borderline", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Mild platelet elevation (400-450). Commonly reactive to iron deficiency, inflammation, or infection.", "recommendation": "Check iron studies and inflammatory markers. Repeat CBC in 4-8 weeks.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.55, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 83.8, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 15.9, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Severe Vitamin D deficiency (≤20 ng/mL). Associated with fatigue, bone loss, and immune dysfunction.", "recommendation": "High-dose repletion: Metagenics D3 10,000 + K daily for 8-12 weeks. Recheck levels after repletion.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 695, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 127, "status": "borderline", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Near optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet and exercise.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 37, "status": "abnormal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Low HDL - increased cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Increase aerobic exercise. Consider niacin if very low.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 117, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: N/A (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "PREVENT calculator is validated for ages 30-79 years. Patient is 25 years old. Use clinical judgment for risk assessment.", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): N/A", "recommendation": "Age outside validated range - use clinical judgment", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: N/A", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 15, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 13, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 124, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.67, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 100, "status": "borderline", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Borderline elevated Apo B.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider pharmacotherapy if high ASCVD risk.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 6, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 2.01, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate deficiency.", "recommendation": "Folate supplementation. Critical if planning pregnancy.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "of 6 markers positive", "value": 2, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Insulin Resistance Screening", "recheckTiming": "3-6 months", "interpretation": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive). This combination points toward hyperinsulinemia physiology. Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR).", "recommendation": "Phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance. Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose.", "referenceRange": "0-1 Low, 2 Moderate, 3+ High Likelihood"}], "aiRecommendations": "1) Overall clinical assessment (summary of key abnormalities)\\n- Platelets 406 K/uL (borderline thrombocytosis; lab cutoff per protocol 400–450 K/uL for mild elevation). Possible reactive etiology (iron deficiency, inflammation, infection); ferritin is reported normal but further iron indices recommended to exclude functional iron deficiency.\\n- 25‑OH Vitamin D 15.9 ng/mL — severe deficiency (≤20 ng/mL). Action required per repletion protocol.\\n- Folate 2.01 ng/mL — low/deficient; clinically important for fertility/pregnancy planning.\\n- Lipids: LDL 127 mg/dL (near‑optimal/ borderline), HDL 37 mg/dL (low), ApoB 100 mg/dL (borderline). ASCVD 10‑yr risk reported normal; pharmacotherapy not mandatory now but risk mitigation indicated.\\n- Other labs within reference range (Hgb, Hct, ferritin, B12, TSH, AST/ALT, eGFR, Lp(a) etc.).\\n\\n2) Priority actions — organized CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE\\n\\nCRITICAL (needs physician notification immediately)\\n- None of the current values require immediate emergency action. HOWEVER notify the physician urgently if any of the following apply:\\n  - Platelet count increases to >450 K/uL or there are symptoms of thrombosis/bleeding.\\n  - Patient is pregnant or actively trying to conceive and folate deficiency confirmed (immediate clinician review for higher‑dose folate).\\n  - History of hypercalcemia, granulomatous disease, lymphoma, or severe renal impairment before starting high‑dose vitamin D.\\n\\nURGENT (action within 1–2 weeks)\\n- Start vitamin D repletion now:\\n  - Metagenics D3 10,000 IU + K oral daily for 8–12 weeks (per clinic protocol).\\n  - Order baseline serum calcium prior to starting high‑dose vitamin D (patient eGFR normal but check calcium to screen for hypercalcemia).\\n  - Document medication start and counsel patient on adherence and signs of hypercalcemia (nausea, polyuria, confusion) to report.\\n  - Notify physician if patient is pregnant, breastfeeding, or on interacting meds (e.g., thiazides).\\n\\n- Start folate replacement now:\\n  - Folate (folic acid) 1 mg PO daily (standard replacement dose). If patient is pregnant or has a history of neural tube defect: discuss with physician & consider 4 mg PO daily per obstetric guidance.\\n  - Recheck serum folate in 4–8 weeks.\\n\\nROUTINE (action within 4–12 weeks)\\n- CBC and iron/inflammatory evaluation:\\n  - Order iron studies: serum iron, TIBC or transferrin, transferrin saturation; confirm ferritin already normal but include to evaluate for functional iron deficiency.\\n  - Order inflammatory marker(s): CRP and/or ESR per protocol.\\n  - Repeat CBC (including platelets) in 4–8 weeks to reassess reactive thrombocytosis.\\n  - If platelets remain >450 K/uL or trending up, arrange hematology referral.\\n\\n- Lipid management:\\n  - Reinforce lifestyle therapy now; repeat fasting lipid panel (including ApoB if previously measured fasting) in 3 months after lifestyle intervention.\\n  - Escalate to pharmacotherapy only if LDL or ApoB remain elevated or if clinical risk increases — discuss with physician if ApoB >100 persistently or LDL ≥160 or other risk enhancers.\\n\\n3) Testosterone dose recommendations (if applicable)\\n- No testosterone lab values or current testosterone therapy listed → no dose recommendations indicated.\\n- If patient is on testosterone therapy, staff actions:\\n  - Obtain total testosterone (and SHBG/free T if available) timed per protocol (mid‑cycle or trough/peak depending on route). Note: interpret female androgen labs in relation to menstrual cycle phase; document cycle day.\\n  - Monitor hematocrit, lipids, LFTs at baseline and per protocol. Notify physician if hematocrit rises >52% or if notable lipid worsening.\\n  - If clinician requests adjustments, provide measured testosterone concentration and clinical symptoms for dosing decisions.\\n\\n4) Lifestyle interventions to recommend to patient (staff to counsel)\\n- Vitamin D:\\n  - Encourage safe sun exposure (brief, face/arms) and dietary sources (oily fish, fortified dairy) in addition to repletion therapy.\\n- Folate:\\n  - Increase dietary folate: leafy greens, legumes, citrus, fortified grains while on supplementation.\\n  - If pregnancy is planned: emphasize preconception folic acid use and early obstetric referral.\\n- Lipids / Cardiovascular:\\n  - Aerobic exercise ≥150 min/week moderate intensity plus resistance training to raise HDL.\\n  - Dietary counseling: Mediterranean-style diet, reduce refined carbs and trans fats, increase omega‑3 sources.\\n  - Smoking cessation (if applicable) — counseling and pharmacotherapy referral as needed.\\n  - Weight loss targets if BMI elevated (5–10% weight loss improves HDL/LDL).\\n- General:\\n  - Review medication adherence, alcohol moderation, and sleep hygiene.\\n\\n5) Additional testing or referrals to consider\\n- Lab tests to order:\\n  - Serum calcium (baseline) prior to high‑dose vitamin D.\\n  - Repeat 25‑OH vitamin D after completion of repletion (recheck at 8–12 weeks post‑start).\\n  - CBC + iron studies (serum iron, TIBC/transferrin, transferrin saturation) and CRP/ESR — order now and repeat CBC in 4–8 weeks.\\n  - Repeat serum folate in 4–8 weeks.\\n  - Fasting lipid panel + ApoB in 3 months after lifestyle measures.\\n  - If on HRT/OCP or suspected hormonal therapy interactions: obtain medication reconciliation and consider coagulation risk assessment if clinically indicated.\\n- Referrals:\\n  - Dietitian/nutritionist for folate, weight/lipid management, and vitamin D dietary counseling.\\n  - Cardiology if persistent ApoB >100–130 mg/dL or if multiple risk factors arise.\\n  - Hematology if platelets >450 K/uL persist, progressive thrombocytosis, or other cytopenias.\\n  - OB/Gyn or reproductive endocrinology if patient is planning pregnancy or infertile — urgent if folate deficiency and conception planned.\\n\\n6) Summary of next steps and timeline (action checklist for staff)\\n- Immediate (start within 1–7 days):\\n  - Start Metagenics D3 10,000 IU + K PO daily for 8–12 weeks; order baseline serum calcium.\\n  - Start folic acid 1 mg PO daily (discuss higher dose with physician if pregnant/planning pregnancy).\\n  - Document counseling points and contraindications; flag chart for physician review if pregnancy or comorbidities.\\n\\n- 1–2 weeks:\\n  - Ensure iron studies and inflammatory markers ordered (if not already) and reviewed.\\n  - Confirm medication list (OCPs/HRT) and assess for interaction/thrombosis risk.\\n\\n- 4–8 weeks:\\n  - Repeat CBC (platelets) and recheck serum folate.\\n  - Review iron studies/CRP results and notify physician if abnormalities or if platelets trending upward.\\n\\n- 8–12 weeks:\\n  - Recheck 25‑OH vitamin D level; transition to maintenance vitamin D dose per physician direction (commonly 1,000–2,000 IU daily or individualized).\\n\\n- 3 months:\\n  - Repeat fasting lipid panel and ApoB; reassess need for pharmacotherapy and consider cardiology referral if persistent abnormalities.\\n\\nPhysician notification requirements (explicit)\\n- Notify physician immediately if:\\n  - Platelets >450 K/uL or rising trend on repeat CBC.\\n  - Patient is pregnant or planning pregnancy (folate deficiency requires physician/OBGYN input).\\n  - Baseline calcium elevated prior to vitamin D repletion.\\n  - Any new symptoms suggesting thrombosis, hypercalcemia, or adverse reaction to supplements.\\n- Otherwise, place results and treatment plan in chart and request routine physician review within 1–2 weeks.\\n\\nNotes for staff interpretation\\n- Consider menstrual cycle phase when correlating any hormone assays (none supplied here) — document cycle day if future hormones ordered.\\n- HRT/OCPs can influence lipids, folate status, and coagulation risk—confirm current hormone therapy and factor into management plan.\\n- Do not make definitive diagnoses in chart notes; present findings and recommended protocolized actions for physician sign‑off.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 3.16, "detail": "Triglycerides 117 / HDL 37 = 3.16", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 13, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "100 mg/dL", "detail": "Elevated atherogenic particle count", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}], "likelihood": "moderate", "phenotypes": [{"key": "visceral_metabolic", "name": "Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Recheck A1c, lipid panel, hs-CRP, ApoB in 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL"], "pathophysiology": "Early peripheral + hepatic insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia. Glucose may still be \\"normal.\\" A1c often rises last.", "triggerCriteria": ["A1c >= 5.7%", "TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0", "Central adiposity phenotype", "ALT above optimal range"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests your body may not be responding to insulin as efficiently as it should. Insulin is the hormone that helps move sugar from your bloodstream into your cells for energy.\\n\\nWhen cells become less sensitive to insulin:\\n- Blood sugar slowly rises over time\\n- Triglycerides increase\\n- Inflammation increases\\n- Weight gain around the midsection becomes easier\\n\\nThe good news? This stage is highly reversible with targeted nutrition, strength training, sleep optimization, and - when appropriate - medication support.\\n\\nWe'll focus on improving how your body processes fuel and reducing long-term cardiometabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward nutrition (>= 25-35g/meal)", "Carbohydrate quality + timing education", "Strength training 2-3x weekly", "10-15 min post-meal walking", "Sleep optimization", "Consider: GLP-1 therapy if obesity, appetite dysregulation, A1c >= 5.7, or inflammatory phenotype", "Consider: Metformin if prediabetes or strong family history", "Recheck metabolic panel in 3-6 months"]}], "positiveCount": 2, "likelihoodLabel": "MODERATE LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive). This combination points toward hyperinsulinemia physiology. Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR).", "confirmationTests": "Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose."}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Metabolic pattern consistent with insulin resistance and/or visceral adiposity. This drives inflammation, hormonal imbalance, and cardiovascular risk.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance screening: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive)", "TG:HDL ratio 3.2 (≥3.0)", "HDL 37 mg/dL (low)", "ApoB 100 mg/dL (elevated)"]}]}	2026-03-10 21:41:12.12318	\N
