ovulatory luteal-phase value if obtained mid-luteal.\\n- Symptoms related to hormones: Not specifically documented in chart at time of visit. If patient reports fatigue, low mood, sleep disturbance, or low libido, note that androgen levels are within expected range and alternative causes should be evaluated.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: Not available in chart today (BMI, BP not documented).\\n- CBC\\n  - Hemoglobin: 15.1 g/dL (normal)\\n  - Hematocrit: 45.8 % (borderline high — may reflect mild hemoconcentration/dehydration)\\n  - Platelets: 264 K/uL (normal)\\n- Metabolic / Renal / Liver\\n  - Creatinine: 0.69 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - eGFR: 113 mL/min/1.73m2 (normal)\\n  - AST: 21 U/L (normal)\\n  - ALT: 23 U/L (normal)\\n- Lipid panel\\n  - Total cholesterol: not listed\\n  - LDL cholesterol: 183 mg/dL (ABNORMAL — high)\\n  - HDL cholesterol: 62 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - Triglycerides: 89 mg/dL (normal)\\n- Glucose / Diabetes screening\\n  - Hemoglobin A1c: 5.1 % (normal)\\n- Iron / Nutrition\\n  - Ferritin: 93.6 ng/mL (normal)\\n  - Vitamin B12: 779 pg/mL (normal)\\n- Vitamin D\\n  - 25‑OH Vitamin D: 32.7 ng/mL (BORDERLINE / insufficient per clinic protocol; target ≥60 ng/mL for hormone optimization)\\n- Thyroid\\n  - TSH: 2.98 mIU/L (normal)\\n- Hormones / Reproductive\\n  - Estradiol (E2): 97 pg/mL (normal for reported panel)\\n  - Progesterone (P4): 5.7 ng/mL (normal; consistent with ovulatory luteal value if timed appropriately)\\n  - FSH: 3.2 mIU/mL (normal)\\n  - Total Testosterone: 38 ng/dL (normal)\\n  - Pattern: Testosterone Pattern D — adequate androgens with persistent symptoms likely due to non-androgenic causes\\n- Risk scores / other\\n  - 10‑Year Total CVD Risk (AHA PREVENT 2023): 1.4% (low)\\n  - 10‑Year ASCVD Risk: 1.1% (low)\\n  - 10‑Year Heart Failure Risk: 0.5%\\n  - 30‑Year CVD Risk: 10.7%\\n  - Sleep Apnea risk (STOP‑BANG): 1 point (low)\\n- Critical flags: No laboratory critical-value flags reported (per chart). Elevated LDL flagged for provider notification per clinic protocol.\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1) Markedly elevated LDL cholesterol — LDL 183 mg/dL.\\n   - Clinical significance: LDL is substantially elevated despite low calculated 10‑year ASCVD risk. This is a clinically important lipid abnormality and is a risk enhancer that warrants same-day provider review for consideration of statin therapy versus aggressive lifestyle-first approach and further lipid evaluation (fasting confirmation, ApoB/advanced lipoprotein testing as indicated).\\n   - Differential/risk enhancers to review: family history of premature ASCVD, smoking status, blood pressure, menopausal status/hormone therapy, diabetes, inflammatory conditions, secondary causes (hypothyroidism, nephrotic syndrome) — chart does not currently document these risk enhancers.\\n\\n2) Borderline-high hematocrit — Hct 45.8%.\\n   - Clinical significance: Slightly above mid-normal; likely mild hemoconcentration/dehydration. Not at immediate hyperviscosity threshold.\\n   - Differential: Dehydration, smoking, exogenous androgens, erythropoietin use, chronic hypoxia; less likely polycythemia vera at this level but consider if persistent/increasing.\\n\\n3) 25‑OH Vitamin D insufficient — 32.7 ng/mL.\\n   - Clinical significance: Below clinic target for hormone optimization (target ≥60 ng/mL). Requires supplementation and recheck.\\n\\n4) Testosterone Pattern D — Total T 38 ng/dL; free/bioavailable and SHBG reported as not extreme.\\n   - Clinical significance: Androgens adequate per protocol; testosterone therapy not indicated based on labs alone. Persistent symptoms (if present) likely due to non-androgenic factors (estrogen/progesterone balance, thyroid, iron status, sleep, mood/stress).\\n\\n5) Other labs within expected limits.\\n   - Hemoglobin, platelets, ferritin, B12, A1c, TSH, AST/ALT, creatinine/eGFR, estradiol, progesterone, FSH, HDL, triglycerides are within normal limits and do not suggest acute end-organ dysfunction.\\n\\nPLAN:\\n(Overall: notify provider same day regarding elevated LDL; implement hydration and vitamin D supplement; do not initiate or escalate testosterone based on current labs. Follow-up intervals below.)\\n\\n1) Elevated LDL — LDL 183 mg/dL\\n   - Action: Notify treating provider same day per clinic protocol for statin decision.\\n   - Staff to prepare: recent lipid history, fasting status of current draw, current medications (including OCP/HRT and any over-the-counter supplements), smoking status, BP, family history of premature ASCVD, weight/BMI.\\n   - Orders pending provider decision:\\n     - If fasting status of current lipid panel unknown or nonfasting: order fasting lipid panel to confirm.\\n     - Consider ordering ApoB or advanced lipid particle testing (LDL‑P) as risk enhancers per provider discretion.\\n   - Treatment options to discuss with patient (provider decision required):\\n     - If statin initiated: typical options — Atorvastatin 20–40 mg nightly or Rosuvastatin 10–20 mg nightly (choose intensity based on provider/patient factors). Document shared decision-making regarding risks/benefits.\\n     - Baseline labs if starting statin: fasting lipid panel, ALT (already available), consider CK if history of myalgias or other risk factors.\\n     - Monitoring: repeat fasting lipid panel and ALT 6–12 weeks after statin initiation or dose change; then every 3–12 months for efficacy/tolerability.\\n     - If provider elects lifestyle-first: document specific lifestyle plan and recheck fasting lipids in 6–12 weeks after intensive lifestyle intervention.\\n   - Counseling: discuss dietary changes (Mediterranean pattern, reduce saturated/trans fats, increase soluble fiber), exercise goals (≥150 min/week moderate aerobic + resistance twice weekly), weight loss if applicable, smoking cessation, alcohol moderation. Offer nutrition referral.\\n\\n2) Borderline hematocrit — Hct 45.8%\\n   - Plan:\\n     - Counsel patient to increase oral hydration immediately and avoid dehydration prior to repeat testing.\\n     - Order repeat CBC (Hgb/Hct) in 2–4 weeks.\\n     - If repeat Hct remains elevated, increases, or >50% OR if patient develops symptoms (headache, visual changes, dizziness, focal deficits, thrombotic symptoms), notify provider urgently (within 24–48 hours).\\n     - Screen for potential causes: smoking status, exogenous androgen use, erythropoietin, pulmonary disease/chronic hypoxia; document in chart and discuss with patient.\\n     - Further workup (if persistent): consider erythropoietin level, oxygenation assessment, or hematology referral per provider.\\n\\n3) Vitamin D insufficiency — 25‑OH Vitamin D 32.7 ng/mL\\n   - Plan:\\n     - Start Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K (1 softgel daily with meal) per clinic protocol. Document start in chart and counsel on adherence and safe sun exposure.\\n     - Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D in ~12 weeks. Target level per clinic: ≥60 ng/mL for hormone optimization; adjust dose accordingly.\\n     - Provide patient education on supplementation and possible side effects.\\n\\n4) Testosterone / hormone management — Total T 38 ng/dL, Pattern D\\n   - Plan:\\n     - Do not initiate or increase testosterone therapy based solely on current labs.\\n     - If patient is currently receiving testosterone therapy: do not escalate dose without provider decision; obtain trough levels per clinic protocol if any adjustment planned.\\n     - If patient reports ongoing symptoms (fatigue, low mood, low libido): evaluate non-androgenic contributors — review estradiol/progesterone balance (E2 97 pg/mL; P4 5.7 ng/mL appear consistent with ovulatory luteal phase if timed correctly), assess thyroid function (TSH 2.98 normal; consider Free T4/Free T3 and anti‑TPO if indicated), assess iron status/ferritin (93.6 ng/mL normal), screen for sleep disorders, mood disorder screening, and review medications.\\n     - If hormonal timing uncertain, consider repeat mid-luteal progesterone (around cycle day 21–23 for a 28-day cycle) to confirm ovulation and verify cycle phase for interpretation of E2/P4.\\n\\n5) Additional testing / referrals\\n   - Order list for provider to consider/approve:\\n     - Fasting lipid panel (if current was nonfasting) — ASAP.\\n     - ApoB and/or LDL‑P if indicated for risk stratification.\\n     - Repeat CBC in 2–4 weeks (Hct/Hgb).\\n     - 25‑OH Vitamin D in 12 weeks after supplementation.\\n     - If starting statin: baseline ALT (already available), CK if indicated.\\n     - Consider thyroid panel (Free T4/Free T3) and anti‑TPO antibodies if persistent symptoms despite normal TSH.\\n     - Consider sleep evaluation (overnight oximetry/polysomnography) if symptoms suggestive of sleep apnea despite low STOP‑BANG.\\n   - Referrals as indicated:\\n     - Cardiology or lipid specialist if provider concerned about severe dyslipidemia or if statin intolerance/complex management anticipated.\\n     - Registered dietitian/nutritionist for intensive dietary therapy.\\n     - Behavioral health for mood/sleep interventions if indicated.\\n\\n6) Patient education and documentation\\n   - Reviewed results with patient: explained that LDL is significantly elevated and requires provider review for possible statin therapy; vitamin D is insufficient and supplementation is recommended; hematocrit borderline likely due to low-grade dehydration — advised hydration and repeat CBC.\\n   - Provided lifestyle recommendations (dietary modification, exercise, weight management, smoking cessation if applicable), hydration counseling, and instructions to start vitamin D supplement.\\n   - Documented plan to notify provider same day regarding LDL; patient provided opportunity for questions.\\n\\n7) Follow-up and timeline\\n   - Same day: Provider notified regarding LDL 183 mg/dL for decision on statin vs lifestyle-first. Staff to compile medication list, BP, smoking status, family history, weight/BMI and present to provider.\\n   - Within 1–7 days: Start Vitamin D 5,000 IU + K daily; document start. Hydration counseling initiated.\\n   - 2–4 weeks: Repeat CBC (Hct/Hgb). Notify provider if Hct remains elevated or >50% or symptomatic.\\n   - 6–12 weeks: If statin started, recheck fasting lipid panel and ALT at 6–12 weeks. If lifestyle-first, recheck fasting lipid panel at 6–12 weeks after intervention.\\n   - 12 weeks: Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D and adjust supplementation to achieve target ≥60 ng/mL.\\n   - Routine follow-up: Reassess symptoms and labs at next clinic visit per provider; re-evaluate need for cardiology or other specialty referral.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\nDate of visit / note: 03/12/2026.", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": true, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": true}, "preventRisk": {"ldlGoal": "No specific LDL target; focus on risk-based statin intensity", "riskCategory": "low", "tenYearASCVD": 0.010806890865484581, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0.0144277585626269, "thirtyYearASCVD": 0.07342888276756236, "ageValidFor30Year": true, "thirtyYearTotalCVD": 0.10694721753536701, "tenYearHFPercentage": "0.5%", "tenYearHeartFailure": 0.004842590714277369, "statinRecommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "tenYearCVDPercentage": "1.4%", "tenYearASCVDPercentage": "1.1%", "thirtyYearHFPercentage": "4.6%", "thirtyYearHeartFailure": 0.045842190326049984, "thirtyYearCVDPercentage": "10.7%", "thirtyYearASCVDPercentage": "7.3%"}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 softgel daily with meal", "name": "D3 5,000 + K", "caution": "Repletion dose for deficiency/insufficiency. Contains vitamin K2 for optimal calcium metabolism. Recheck levels in 8-12 weeks.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "D3 5,000 + K provides vitamin D3 with K2 for repletion of deficiency/insufficiency. Target ≥60 ng/mL for bone, immune, and cardiovascular support.", "indication": "Vitamin D 32.7 ng/mL (insufficient)", "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D is below optimal range. This supplement will help restore your levels to support bone health, immune function, and overall wellness.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 32.7 ng/mL (insufficient)"]}, {"dose": "2 capsules twice daily", "name": "AdvaClear®", "caution": "Broad-spectrum phase I/II liver detoxification support. Contains NAC, green tea extract, milk thistle, and B-vitamins for biotransformation. Best taken with food.", "category": "detox", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "AdvaClear provides broad-spectrum phase I and phase II liver detoxification support with NAC, green tea extract, watercress, and B-vitamins. Designed for metabolic clearance support, biotransformation of hormones and environmental compounds, and hepatic stress. Appropriate when liver markers are borderline, estrogen clearance is sluggish, or metabolic dysfunction is present.", "indication": "Borderline liver markers (ALT 23, AST 21) suggesting hepatic biotransformation load; Weight gain (metabolic clearance factor); Low energy (biotransformation burden)", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This supplement supports your liver's natural detoxification processes. Your results suggest your body could benefit from help processing hormones, metabolic byproducts, and other compounds. It provides nutrients that support both phases of your liver's detox pathways.", "supportingFindings": ["Borderline liver markers (ALT 23, AST 21) suggesting hepatic biotransformation load", "Weight gain (metabolic clearance factor)", "Low energy (biotransformation burden)"]}, {"dose": "2 tablets daily", "name": "Exhilarin®", "caution": "Ayurvedic adaptogenic formula. Supports mental clarity and emotional well-being. Takes 2-4 weeks for optimal benefits.", "category": "general", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Exhilarin provides adaptogenic support with Ashwagandha, Holy Basil, Bacopa, and Amla for mental energy, mood, and stress resilience. Complements nutrient repletion for comprehensive fatigue and mood management.", "indication": "Low energy reported; Mood changes reported", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This herbal formula supports mental energy, mood balance, and stress resilience. It works alongside your nutrient supplements to help restore your energy and emotional well-being.", "supportingFindings": ["Low energy reported", "Mood changes reported"]}, {"dose": "2 tablets twice daily", "name": "EstroFactors™", "caution": "Nutritional support for estrogen balance and metabolism. Contains I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Supports healthy estrogen metabolite ratios.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "EstroFactors provides targeted nutritional support for estrogen metabolism and balance with I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Best suited for women with estrogen dominance patterns, PMS, perimenopausal hormone imbalance, or those needing broader estrogen metabolism support during hormonal transitions. Differs from Estrovera in that it addresses the underlying estrogen metabolism rather than vasomotor symptom relief.", "indication": "Mood changes reported; Weight gain (estrogen metabolism factor); Age-appropriate for estrogen metabolism support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This supplement helps your body process and balance estrogen more effectively. It supports healthy hormone metabolism, which can help with PMS symptoms, bloating, mood changes, and hormonal balance during life transitions.", "supportingFindings": ["Mood changes reported", "Weight gain (estrogen metabolism factor)", "Age-appropriate for estrogen metabolism support"]}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Essential for patients on statins.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "low", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "LDL 183 mg/dL; TC 263 mg/dL; Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["LDL 183 mg/dL", "TC 263 mg/dL", "Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "generally_recommended", "indicated": true, "rationale": "Persistent non-HDL ≥160 mg/dL (201 mg/dL) indicates elevated atherogenic lipoprotein burden. Statin discussion recommended."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall summary (plain language)\\n- Your LDL (“bad”) cholesterol is high at 183 mg/dL. Most other tests are reassuring: your 10-year ASCVD (heart attack/stroke) risk is low at 1.1%, and many labs that matter for women’s health are within good ranges. A few areas to improve now (LDL, vitamin D, and possible mild dehydration) can make a big difference for long-term health.\\n\\nPositive findings (specific values)\\n- HDL (good) cholesterol: 62 mg/dL (protective).\\n- Triglycerides: 89 mg/dL (normal).\\n- Hemoglobin A1c: 5.1% (normal blood sugar control).\\n- Ferritin: 93.6 ng/mL (good iron stores).\\n- Hemoglobin: 15.1 g/dL (within female range).\\n- TSH: 2.98 mIU/L (thyroid function within expected range).\\n- AST/ALT: 21 / 23 U/L (liver tests normal).\\n- Total testosterone: 38 ng/dL (within expected female range). Estradiol: 97 pg/mL and progesterone: 5.7 ng/mL were measured — interpretation depends on where you are in your menstrual cycle.\\n\\nAreas to improve (exact values + concrete actions)\\n1. LDL cholesterol 183 mg/dL (high). Concrete steps to lower LDL:\\n   - Add 25–30 g fiber daily: e.g., oatmeal for breakfast, beans or lentils with lunch, vegetables and a whole grain at dinner.\\n   - Eat 2–3 servings of fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n   - Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, switch butter for olive oil, limit high-fat cheeses.\\n   - Exercise daily: 30 minutes brisk walking or equivalent.\\n   - Re-check a fasting lipid panel in 8–12 weeks after lifestyle changes; if LDL remains high, we will discuss medication (statin) options even though your 10-year ASCVD risk is currently low (1.1%).\\n\\n2. Vitamin D (25‑OH) 32.7 ng/mL (insufficient). Actions:\\n   - Start vitamin D3 supplement 1000–2000 IU daily (confirm dose with your clinician).\\n   - Include vitamin D foods (fortified milk, fatty fish) and aim for brief sun exposure when safe.\\n   - Recheck 25‑OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks.\\n\\n3. Hematocrit 45.8% (slightly elevated — may reflect dehydration). Actions:\\n   - Increase water intake (aim for ~2 liters/day unless otherwise directed).\\n   - Repeat CBC when well hydrated in 4–6 weeks.\\n\\nHormone note and next steps\\n- Total testosterone 38 ng/dL is normal, so symptoms like low mood, fatigue, or low libido are less likely from androgens. Estradiol 97 pg/mL and progesterone 5.7 ng/mL need cycle-day context — if we are assessing estrogen/progesterone balance, it’s best to time progesterone about 7 days before your expected period (mid-luteal). Discuss symptoms so we can target evaluation.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Begin the lifestyle actions above now. Repeat lipid panel in 8–12 weeks, vitamin D in 8–12 weeks, CBC after good hydration in 4–6 weeks. Schedule a follow-up appointment to review progress and discuss statin therapy or further hormone testing if symptoms persist.\\n\\nYou’re doing many things right — these focused steps should help reduce heart and bone-health risks. If you have questions or new symptoms, please reach out.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 15.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 45.8, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Slightly elevated hematocrit - may indicate dehydration.", "recommendation": "Encourage hydration. Repeat if persistent.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 264, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 97, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 5.7, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 3.2, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 38, "status": "normal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone within normal female range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 2.98, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 93.6, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 32.7, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D insufficient (31-40 ng/mL). Suboptimal for hormone optimization.", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 5,000 + K daily. Target ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 779, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 183, "status": "abnormal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "High LDL.", "recommendation": "Consider statin based on ASCVD risk. Lifestyle modifications.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 62, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 89, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 1.44277585626269, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 1.4% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 1.0806890865484582, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 1.1%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.4842590714277369, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.5%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (1/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 10.694721753536701, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 10.7%, ASCVD 7.3%, Heart Failure 4.6%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 21, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 23, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 113, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.69, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 38, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone Pattern D: Adequate Androgens, Persistent Symptoms", "interpretation": "Adequate Androgens pattern identified (Total T: 38 ng/dL (within range)). Total, free, and bioavailable testosterone are within normal ranges and SHBG is not extreme. If the patient reports fatigue, low mood, sleep disruption, or low libido, these symptoms are likely driven by non-androgenic factors. Findings to evaluate: Estrogen/Progesterone balance should be assessed (E2: 97 pg/mL, P4: 5.7 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Do not reflexively add testosterone when levels are adequate — look deeper. Evaluate iron status (ferritin target >50 for symptom resolution), thyroid function (optimize TSH), and sleep quality (consider sleep apnea screening with STOP-BANG). Assess estrogen and progesterone balance — E2/P4 imbalance is a common driver of persistent symptoms. Address CNS and psychosocial drivers: chronic stress, HPA axis dysregulation, mood disorders. Consider DHEA-S if not already checked. Support with lifestyle optimization: sleep hygiene, stress management, nutrition. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your testosterone levels are actually in a healthy range, which is good news. When symptoms persist despite normal testosterone, it usually means something else is contributing. Your provider will look at other factors like iron levels, thyroid function, sleep quality, and hormone balance to find what's driving your symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Total T 15-70 ng/dL, Free T 0.5-5.0 pg/mL (all within range)"}], "aiRecommendations": "Purpose: Clinical staff–facing synthesis and action plan for review by the treating provider. Follow clinic protocols and notify provider per items below.\\n\\n1) Overall clinical assessment (concise)\\n- Hematocrit 45.8% — borderline high; most likely mild hemoconcentration/dehydration but repeat needed to confirm persistence.\\n- 25‑OH Vitamin D 32.7 ng/mL — insufficient per clinic protocol (target ≥60 ng/mL for hormone optimization).\\n- LDL 183 mg/dL — markedly elevated and requires provider risk-based decision-making re: statin therapy and aggressive risk reduction.\\n- Testosterone Pattern D (Total T 38 ng/dL, free/bioavailable and SHBG not extreme) — androgens are adequate per protocol; persistent symptoms are likely due to non-androgenic drivers (estrogen/progesterone balance, iron/thyroid/sleep/psychosocial).\\n- Other labs listed as normal (including ferritin, TSH, estradiol, progesterone, A1c, renal/hepatic) — supports focusing on lipid and vitamin D issues and non-androgen causes of symptoms.\\n\\n2) Priority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\n\\nCRITICAL (immediate notification/action)\\n- LDL 183 mg/dL\\n  - Action: Notify provider same day for consideration of statin therapy and cardiovascular risk management (per protocol). Although 10‑year ASCVD risk is listed as normal, LDL is high enough to require review of risk enhancers and potential initiation of therapy.\\n  - Staff tasks: Prepare and present recent lipid panel, ASCVD risk estimate, current meds (including OCP/HRT), smoking status, blood pressure, family history, and recent weight/BMI for provider decision.\\n  - If provider recommends statin initiation: prescribe per provider choice (see URGENT for monitoring timeline).\\n\\nURGENT (within 1–7 days)\\n- Hematocrit 45.8%\\n  - Action: Counsel patient to increase oral hydration immediately. Repeat CBC (hematocrit/hemoglobin) in 2–4 weeks to assess persistence.\\n  - Notify provider if repeat hematocrit remains elevated or increases, or if hematocrit >50% (or patient develops symptoms: headache, visual changes, dizziness, thrombotic symptoms).\\n  - Consider screening questions re: smoking, exogenous androgens, erythropoietin use.\\n\\n- Vitamin D 32.7 ng/mL\\n  - Action: Start Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily per clinic protocol.\\n  - Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D in 12 weeks. Target ≥60 ng/mL.\\n  - Document supplementation start and counsel patient on adherence and safe sunlight exposure.\\n\\nROUTINE (within 4–12 weeks)\\n- Testosterone management\\n  - Action: Do not initiate or escalate testosterone therapy based solely on symptoms — Total T 38 ng/dL is within acceptable range (Pattern D). Do not reflexively add testosterone.\\n  - If patient is currently on testosterone: do not increase dose at this time; discuss with provider. If provider wishes to continue/adjust therapy, follow clinic testosterone dosing and monitoring protocol and obtain trough levels per protocol.\\n  - If provider later elects to trial testosterone (not currently recommended): ensure documented informed consent, baseline labs, and monitoring plan.\\n\\n3) Specific medication/dose recommendations and monitoring (staff tasks for provider)\\n- Statin consideration (provider decision required)\\n  - If provider elects to start statin given LDL 183 mg/dL:\\n    - Typical options per clinic protocol (provider choice): moderate- to high-intensity statin (examples: Atorvastatin 20–40 mg nightly or Rosuvastatin 10–20 mg nightly), adjusted for age, comorbidities, drug interactions.\\n    - Baseline labs: fasting lipid panel, ALT, CK if clinically indicated.\\n    - Recheck fasting lipid panel and ALT 6–12 weeks after initiation/dose change; then every 3–12 months for efficacy/tolerability.\\n    - Staff: counsel on statin side effects, liver warning signs, and drug interactions (including OCP/HRT interactions if present).\\n  - If provider prefers lifestyle first: document plan and timeline (see lifestyle interventions) and recheck fasting lipids in 6–12 weeks after lifestyle intensification.\\n\\n- Vitamin D\\n  - Start Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily per protocol.\\n  - Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D in 12 weeks. Adjust dose to reach target ≥60 ng/mL.\\n\\n- Testosterone\\n  - No dose initiation or increase recommended at this time.\\n  - If patient is on testosterone and provider decides to stop/adjust, staff should follow clinic-specific taper/stop guidance and monitoring.\\n\\n4) Lifestyle interventions to implement now (staff to counsel and document)\\n- Lipids\\n  - Diet: Mediterranean pattern / reduce saturated fat and trans fats; increase soluble fiber (oats, legumes), plant sterols if appropriate.\\n  - Exercise: Aim for ≥150 min/week moderate aerobic activity plus strength training twice weekly.\\n  - Weight management: Target modest weight loss if overweight (5–10%).\\n  - Smoking cessation if applicable.\\n  - Alcohol: Limit to recommended limits.\\n  - Reassess adherence at follow-up; refer to nutritionist if available.\\n\\n- Hydration\\n  - Encourage increased oral fluids; advise to avoid dehydration before repeat CBC.\\n\\n- Sleep & psychosocial factors\\n  - Review sleep hygiene; if daytime somnolence or snoring despite STOP‑BANG, consider referral for sleep medicine/overnight oximetry or polysomnography.\\n  - Screen for depression/anxiety and refer to behavioral health as indicated.\\n  - Recommend stress reduction practices (sleep routine, CBT, mindfulness) and document resources provided.\\n\\n- Vitamin D\\n  - Counsel on adherence to supplementation and safe sun exposure.\\n\\n5) Additional testing and referrals to consider (staff to arrange per provider order)\\n- Cardiovascular\\n  - If provider concerned about high LDL: fasting lipid panel confirmation if prior was nonfasting, ApoB or LDL‑P as risk enhancer, or referral to cardiology for severe dyslipidemia evaluation.\\n- Repeat labs\\n  - CBC (hematocrit/hemoglobin) in 2–4 weeks to reassess hematocrit.\\n  - 25‑OH Vitamin D in 12 weeks after starting supplementation.\\n  - Fasting lipid panel 6–12 weeks after therapeutic intervention (statin start or lifestyle intensification).\\n- Hormone workup (if symptoms persist)\\n  - If not already obtained or if timing unclear: ensure estradiol and progesterone were drawn in the appropriate cycle phase (document cycle day or per protocol). If ovulatory status unclear, repeat mid‑luteal progesterone (around day 21–23 if 28‑day cycle) to confirm ovulation.\\n  - Consider DHEA‑S, free testosterone, SHBG (if not already measured) and repeat if clinical suspicion persists.\\n- Thyroid\\n  - Although TSH is normal, if symptoms persist consider Free T4/Free T3 and thyroid antibodies (anti‑TPO) for subclinical thyroid disease per provider discretion.\\n- Sleep\\n  - If clinical concern despite STOP‑BANG, refer to sleep medicine for polysomnography.\\n- Referrals\\n  - Cardiology or lipid specialist if high LDL and high risk or if statin intolerance suspected.\\n  - Nutritionist/dietitian for intensive diet therapy.\\n  - Behavioral health for mood/sleep/stress management.\\n\\n6) Physician notification requirements\\n- Notify provider same day for LDL 183 mg/dL to decide statin vs lifestyle-first approach.\\n- Notify provider within 24–48 hours if there are symptoms concerning for hyperviscosity or thrombosis, or if hematocrit increases on repeat or exceeds clinic threshold (e.g., >50%).\\n- Notify provider if patient is taking exogenous androgens, erythropoietin, or other meds that could explain hematocrit or lipid abnormalities.\\n\\n7) Patient education points to cover (staff to document)\\n- Explain that testosterone level is within expected range and that adding testosterone is not recommended at this time; symptoms often have other causes.\\n- Explain need for statin discussion because LDL is significantly elevated and discuss risks/benefits if provider recommends medication.\\n- Instruct on starting Vitamin D 5,000 IU daily and importance of rechecking in ~12 weeks.\\n- Hydration advice and plan to repeat CBC in 2–4 weeks.\\n- Lifestyle counseling points (diet, exercise, sleep, smoking cessation).\\n- Provide written resources/referrals as available and document education provided.\\n\\n8) Summary of next steps and timeline (clear, actionable)\\n- Immediate / same‑day:\\n  - Notify provider re: LDL 183 mg/dL (staff prep: meds, risk factors, lipid history).\\n- Within 1–7 days:\\n  - Start Vitamin D 5,000 IU + K daily (document start).\\n  - Hydration counseling.\\n- 2–4 weeks:\\n  - Repeat CBC (Hct/Hgb). Notify provider if Hct remains elevated or >50% or if symptomatic.\\n- 6–12 weeks:\\n  - If statin started, recheck fasting lipid panel and ALT at 6–12 weeks; if lifestyle-first, recheck fasting lipids at 6–12 weeks.\\n- 12 weeks:\\n  - Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D level; adjust supplementation to reach clinic target ≥60 ng/mL.\\n- Ongoing:\\n  - Do not initiate or escalate testosterone based on current labs; pursue evaluation of other drivers (iron, thyroid, sleep, psychosocial). Arrange additional testing/referrals per provider decisions.\\n\\nIf additional patient context is available (menstrual cycle day when E2/P4 were drawn, current HRT or OCP use, current testosterone therapy status, smoking status, family history of premature ASCVD), include that to refine recommendations.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 1.44, "detail": "Triglycerides 89 / HDL 62 = 1.44", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5.1%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 23, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": []}	2026-03-12 15:37:27.87405	\N
