"indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "consider", "rationale": "Lipid levels meet threshold for CAC consideration. CAC may be useful if risk level is uncertain or patient is hesitant about statin.", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\nYour labs show a few clear areas to address and many strengths. Most routine tests are normal (good kidney function, liver tests, blood counts, and excellent cholesterol), but you have severe vitamin D deficiency (25-OH vitamin D 18 ng/mL) and hormonal signs consistent with perimenopause: low progesterone (0.28 ng/mL) with estradiol 78 pg/mL and very high SHBG (101 nmol/L). Ferritin is borderline low at 43.1 ng/mL and total testosterone is low at 13.5 ng/dL. Vitamin B12 is high at 2000 pg/mL (likely from supplements).\\n\\nPositive findings (what’s working well)\\n- Hemoglobin 12 g/dL, Hematocrit 36.1% — no anemia.\\n- LDL 72 mg/dL, HDL 73 mg/dL, Triglycerides 85 mg/dL — heart-healthy lipid profile.\\n- TSH 2.15 mIU/L — thyroid function within range.\\n- HbA1c 5% — good blood sugar control.\\n- AST 19 U/L, ALT 15 U/L — normal liver tests.\\n- eGFR 101 mL/min — normal kidney function.\\n- hs-CRP 0.6 mg/L — low inflammation.\\n- STOP-BANG 0 points — low sleep apnea risk.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + concrete actions)\\n1. Vitamin D 25-OH = 18 ng/mL (severe deficiency). Action: Start vitamin D3 and discuss dosing with your clinician — common options include prescription repletion (e.g., 50,000 IU weekly for 8–12 weeks) or over-the-counter 2000–5000 IU daily. Add dietary sources (fatty fish, fortified milk), aim for 10–20 minutes sun on arms/hands 3x/week, and recheck level in 8–12 weeks. Support bone health: calcium 1,000–1,200 mg/day from diet or supplements and weight-bearing exercise 30 minutes most days.\\n\\n2. Progesterone 0.28 ng/mL and Perimenopause pattern. If this blood draw was in the luteal phase, this low progesterone suggests anovulation/perimenopause. Action: Track cycles and, if possible, repeat progesterone 7 days before expected period (mid-luteal). Discuss symptoms and options (bioidentical or prescription progesterone, lifestyle measures) with your provider.\\n\\n3. SHBG 101 nmol/L and reduced bioavailable estrogen. High SHBG lowers free hormones. Action: Review medications/supplements (oral estrogen, OCPs, thyroid meds) with your clinician. Address modifiable factors (optimize liver health, maintain stable weight).\\n\\n4. Ferritin 43.1 ng/mL (optimal >50). Action: Increase iron-rich foods (red meat, poultry, lentils, beans, spinach) and take vitamin C with meals to boost absorption (glass of orange juice). If heavy periods or symptoms, consider oral iron and recheck ferritin in 8–12 weeks.\\n\\n5. Total testosterone 13.5 ng/dL (low). Action: Prioritize 7–8 hours sleep, add strength training 2–3x/week, and use stress-reduction (meditation, walking). Reassess levels after lifestyle changes or discuss medical options with provider.\\n\\n6. Vitamin B12 2000 pg/mL. Action: Likely from supplements — review and reduce high-dose B12 if not needed; recheck in 3 months.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Talk with your clinician about starting vitamin D repletion now and retesting 8–12 weeks.\\n- Repeat ferritin in 8–12 weeks after dietary changes or iron therapy.\\n- If perimenopause symptoms bother you, arrange a hormonal review and consider repeat mid-luteal progesterone testing.\\n- Review all medications/supplements (especially estrogen, B12) at your next visit.\\n\\nYou’re doing many things right; these targeted steps can boost bone health, hormonal balance, and energy. We can help you plan the next tests and treatments.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value