0–1,200 mg/day from diet or supplements and weight-bearing exercise 30 minutes most days.\\n\\n2. Progesterone 0.28 ng/mL and Perimenopause pattern. If this blood draw was in the luteal phase, this low progesterone suggests anovulation/perimenopause. Action: Track cycles and, if possible, repeat progesterone 7 days before expected period (mid-luteal). Discuss symptoms and options (bioidentical or prescription progesterone, lifestyle measures) with your provider.\\n\\n3. SHBG 101 nmol/L and reduced bioavailable estrogen. High SHBG lowers free hormones. Action: Review medications/supplements (oral estrogen, OCPs, thyroid meds) with your clinician. Address modifiable factors (optimize liver health, maintain stable weight).\\n\\n4. Ferritin 43.1 ng/mL (optimal >50). Action: Increase iron-rich foods (red meat, poultry, lentils, beans, spinach) and take vitamin C with meals to boost absorption (glass of orange juice). If heavy periods or symptoms, consider oral iron and recheck ferritin in 8–12 weeks.\\n\\n5. Total testosterone 13.5 ng/dL (low). Action: Prioritize 7–8 hours sleep, add strength training 2–3x/week, and use stress-reduction (meditation, walking). Reassess levels after lifestyle changes or discuss medical options with provider.\\n\\n6. Vitamin B12 2000 pg/mL. Action: Likely from supplements — review and reduce high-dose B12 if not needed; recheck in 3 months.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Talk with your clinician about starting vitamin D repletion now and retesting 8–12 weeks.\\n- Repeat ferritin in 8–12 weeks after dietary changes or iron therapy.\\n- If perimenopause symptoms bother you, arrange a hormonal review and consider repeat mid-luteal progesterone testing.\\n- Review all medications/supplements (especially estrogen, B12) at your next visit.\\n\\nYou’re doing many things right; these targeted steps can boost bone health, hormonal balance, and energy. We can help you plan the next tests and treatments.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 12, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 36.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 344, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 78, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.28, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 6.45, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 13.5, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Low testosterone.", "recommendation": "May contribute to low libido or fatigue. Consider HRT discussion.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 2.15, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 43.1, "status": "borderline", "category": "Ferritin"