2 capsules daily", "name": "GlutaClear®", "caution": "Glutathione and antioxidant support formula. Contains NAC, alpha-lipoic acid, and green tea catechins. Supports cellular defense against oxidative damage.", "category": "detox", "priority": "high", "rationale": "GlutaClear supports glutathione production and antioxidant defense with NAC, alpha-lipoic acid, and green tea catechins. Indicated when there is evidence of oxidative stress, inflammatory burden, or metabolic dysfunction requiring enhanced cellular defense. While AdvaClear provides broader liver detoxification support, GlutaClear specifically targets glutathione pathways and antioxidant capacity.", "indication": "Oxidative stress phenotype (glutathione/antioxidant support indicated); Inflammatory burden (antioxidant defense support); Insulin resistance (oxidative metabolic stress); hs-CRP 7.8 mg/L (high inflammatory/oxidative burden)", "phenotypes": ["Oxidative Stress Burden", "Inflammatory Burden", "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "This supplement boosts your body's master antioxidant, glutathione, which protects your cells from damage. Your results suggest increased oxidative stress, and this formula helps your body defend and repair at the cellular level.", "supportingFindings": ["Oxidative stress phenotype (glutathione/antioxidant support indicated)", "Inflammatory burden (antioxidant defense support)", "Insulin resistance (oxidative metabolic stress)", "hs-CRP 7.8 mg/L (high inflammatory/oxidative burden)", "A1c 6% (glycemic oxidative stress)", "Age 45 (declining glutathione production)"]}, {"dose": "2 capsules twice daily", "name": "AdvaClear®", "caution": "Broad-spectrum phase I/II liver detoxification support. Contains NAC, green tea extract, milk thistle, and B-vitamins for biotransformation. Best taken with food.", "category": "detox", "priority": "high", "rationale": "AdvaClear provides broad-spectrum phase I and phase II liver detoxification support with NAC, green tea extract, watercress, and B-vitamins. Designed for metabolic clearance support, biotransformation of hormones and environmental compounds, and hepatic stress. Appropriate when liver markers are borderline, estrogen clearance is sluggish, or metabolic dysfunction is present.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance pattern (hepatic detox pathways needed for clearance); Oxidative stress burden (liver biotransformation support); Inflammatory burden (metabolic clearance support); Insulin resistance / metabolic dysfunction (hepatic metabolic support)", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance", "Oxidative Stress Burden", "Inflammatory Burden", "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "This supplement supports your liver's natural detoxification processes. Your results suggest your body could benefit from help processing hormones, metabolic byproducts, and other compounds. It provides nutrients that support both phases of your liver's detox pathways.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance pattern (hepatic detox pathways needed for clearance)", "Oxidative stress burden (liver biotransformation support)", "Inflammatory burden (metabolic clearance support)", "Insulin resistance / metabolic dysfunction (hepatic metabolic support)", "hs-CRP 7.8 mg/L (inflammatory metabolic burden)"]}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Essential for patients on statins.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "ApoB 104 mg/dL (cardiovascular support); hs-CRP 7.8 mg/L; LDL 136 mg/dL; TC 218 mg/dL", "phenotypes": ["Inflammatory Burden", "Oxidative Stress Burden"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["ApoB 104 mg/dL (cardiovascular support)", "hs-CRP 7.8 mg/L", "LDL 136 mg/dL", "TC 218 mg/dL", "Lp(a) 71 (elevated)", "Age 45 (declining CoQ10 production)"]}, {"dose": "1 tablet daily", "name": "Intrinsi B12-Folate™", "caution": "Contains intrinsic factor for enhanced B12 absorption. Ideal for patients with absorption concerns or vegetarian/vegan diets.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Intrinsi B12-Folate provides methylated B12 and folate with intrinsic factor for superior absorption. Supports energy, cognition, methylation, and nerve function.", "indication": "B12 266 pg/mL (deficient <300)", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "Your B12 and/or folate levels need support. This supplement provides highly absorbable forms of these essential vitamins to support your energy, brain function, and nervous system health.", "supportingFindings": ["B12 266 pg/mL (deficient <300)"]}, {"dose": "2 tablets twice daily", "name": "EstroFactors™", "caution": "Nutritional support for estrogen balance and metabolism. Contains I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Supports healthy estrogen metabolite ratios.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "EstroFactors provides targeted nutritional support for estrogen metabolism and balance with I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Best suited for women with estrogen dominance patterns, PMS, perimenopausal hormone imbalance, or those needing broader estrogen metabolism support during hormonal transitions. Differs from Estrovera in that it addresses the underlying estrogen metabolism rather than vasomotor symptom relief.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected; Age-appropriate for estrogen metabolism support", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement helps your body process and balance estrogen more effectively. It supports healthy hormone metabolism, which can help with PMS symptoms, bloating, mood changes, and hormonal balance during life transitions.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected", "Age-appropriate for estrogen metabolism support"]}, {"dose": "1 tablet twice daily with meals", "name": "Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support", "caution": "Contains iron, folate, and B12 for comprehensive RBC support. Avoid taking with calcium-rich foods or dairy. May cause mild GI upset initially.", "category": "iron", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Hemagenics provides highly absorbable iron bisglycinate with B12, B6, and folate for comprehensive red blood cell support. Indicated for functional iron deficiency, iron deficiency without anemia, and iron-responsive symptoms.", "indication": "Ferritin 43.3 ng/mL (suboptimal ≤50)", "phenotypes": ["Iron Deficiency"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "Your lab results suggest your iron stores are lower than optimal. This supplement provides a gentle, well-absorbed form of iron along with B12 and folate to support your energy levels, hair health, and overall vitality.", "supportingFindings": ["Ferritin 43.3 ng/mL (suboptimal ≤50)"]}, {"dose": "1 tablet daily with meal", "name": "D3 2000 Complex", "caution": "Maintenance dose for suboptimal levels. Comprehensive vitamin D support with cofactors.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "low", "rationale": "D3 2000 Complex provides maintenance vitamin D with cofactors to reach and maintain optimal range ≥60 ng/mL.", "indication": "Vitamin D 41.7 ng/mL (suboptimal, target ≥60)", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D is close to optimal but could benefit from a maintenance dose to reach the ideal range for bone and immune health.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 41.7 ng/mL (suboptimal, target ≥60)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "phenotypes": ["Gut-Microbiome Support"], "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 71, "apoBValue": 104, "lpaStatus": "elevated", "apoBStatus": "borderline", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 1.1686710893573904, "hasElevatedLpa": true, "statinGuidance": "Possibly consider statin therapy given risk enhancement; shared decision-making recommended.", "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "reclassified_upward", "clinicalGuidance": "Lp(a) 71 mg/dL - RISK ENHANCER (genetic risk factor). RECLASSIFIED TO BORDERLINE RISK: Risk enhancer present. Shared decision-making regarding statin therapy.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "cacRecommendation": "CAC reasonable especially with strong family history and risk enhancement present.", "hasBorderlineApoB": true}, "cacStatinRec": {"lpaWarning": "Elevated Lp(a) of 71 mg/dL is a genetic cardiovascular risk marker. This level is associated with increased ASCVD risk independent of LDL. Since Lp(a) cannot be significantly lowered, focus on aggressive LDL/ApoB reduction and lifestyle optimization.", "statinDiscussion": {"strength": "generally_recommended", "indicated": true, "rationale": "ApoB ≥90 mg/dL (104 mg/dL) with risk enhancers (elevated hs-CRP, prediabetes/metabolic syndrome, elevated Lp(a), elevated ApoB). Statin discussion recommended."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "strongly_recommend", "rationale": "Elevated Lp(a) of 71 mg/dL is a genetic risk marker. CAC strongly recommended to assess arterial calcium burden and inform treatment intensity.", "recommended": true}}, "recheckWindow": "3 months (glycemic monitoring)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\n- Your tests show some important cardiovascular and hormone-pattern signals to address, plus a few borderline nutrition markers. Notable values: Lipoprotein(a) 71 mg/dL (elevated), hs‑CRP 7.8 mg/L (elevated inflammation), LDL 136 mg/dL (borderline high), Hemoglobin A1c 6.0% (prediabetes), ferritin 43.3 ng/mL (iron insufficiency), vitamin D 41.7 ng/mL (adequate but suboptimal), B12 266 pg/mL (low‑normal). Estradiol 128 pg/mL with progesterone 0.78 ng/mL suggests relative/luteal progesterone deficiency consistent with perimenopause (especially in a 45‑year‑old). TSH 1.6 mIU/L and hemoglobin 12 g/dL are normal.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- HDL 61 mg/dL (protective)\\n- Triglycerides 107 mg/dL (normal)\\n- eGFR 94 mL/min (kidney function normal)\\n- AST 18 U/L, ALT 13 U/L (liver enzymes normal)\\n- 10‑year ASCVD risk 1.2% (low short‑term risk), 30‑year CVD risk 12.8%\\n\\nAreas to improve + concrete actions\\n1. Heart risk & cholesterol (LDL 136 mg/dL, ApoB 104 mg/dL, Lp(a) 71 mg/dL, hs‑CRP 7.8 mg/L)\\n   - Diet: Add 25–30 g fiber daily (oatmeal for breakfast, beans at lunch, lots of vegetables at dinner).\\n   - Fish: Include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n   - Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter and cheese.\\n   - Move: 30 minutes brisk walking most days.\\n   - Inflammation: Follow a Mediterranean-style diet (olive oil, nuts, vegetables), aim for healthy weight, and prioritize sleep.\\n   - Lp(a) is largely genetic (71 mg/dL). Discuss with your clinician about family risk and whether referral to a cardiologist or specialized therapies is appropriate.\\n\\n2. Blood sugar (A1c 6.0% = prediabetes)\\n   - Reduce refined carbs: swap white bread for whole grains, avoid sugary drinks.\\n   - Include protein with each meal.\\n   - Walk 10–15 minutes after meals.\\n\\n3. Iron & B12 (Ferritin 43.3 ng/mL; target >50 ng/mL; B12 266 pg/mL)\\n   - Diet: eat iron‑rich foods (lean red meat, lentils, spinach) with vitamin C (citrus) to boost absorption.\\n   - If symptoms (fatigue, hair loss) or levels don’t improve: consider oral iron supplement (~45–65 mg elemental iron daily) and/or B12 supplement (methylcobalamin 1000 mcg daily). Recheck ferritin and B12 in 8–12 weeks.\\n\\n4. Vitamin D & bone health (25‑OH D 41.7 ng/mL; aim >50 ng/mL)\\n   - Supplement: consider vitamin D3 1,000–2,000 IU daily and recheck in 3 months.\\n   - Bone health: weight‑bearing exercise 3x/week, calcium 1,000–1,200 mg/day via diet/supplement as needed.\\n\\n5. Perimenopausal hormones (E2 128 pg/mL, P4 0.78 ng/mL)\\n   - If this blood draw was in the luteal phase, low progesterone suggests anovulatory cycles. Track cycles (period dates, ovulation kits or basal temp) and repeat mid‑luteal progesterone (~7 days before expected menses) or discuss with your clinician about perimenopausal symptom options (lifestyle, cyclical progesterone or other hormone therapies).\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Start the lifestyle changes now. Recheck fasting lipid panel, hs‑CRP, ferritin, vitamin D, B12 and A1c in 8–12 weeks to measure response. Repeat progesterone timed to your cycle if you want clearer perimenopause assessment. Contact your provider sooner if you experience chest pain, shortness of breath, very heavy bleeding, or severe symptoms. We’ll support you step‑by‑step.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 12, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 37.3, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 364, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 128, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.78, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 16.4, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 21.7, "status": "normal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone within normal female range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.6, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 43.3, "status": "borderline", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Iron insufficiency (ferritin 31-50). Optimal is >50.", "recommendation": "Provider protocol: Metagenics Hemagenics if symptomatic (fatigue, hair loss, restless legs, exercise intolerance).", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 41.7, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D adequate but suboptimal (41-59 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 2000 Complex daily to reach optimal range ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 266, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "Low-normal B12.", "recommendation": "Consider supplementation especially if symptomatic.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 6, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Prediabetes range (5.7-6.4%).", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider metformin if high risk.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 136, "status": "borderline", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Borderline high LDL.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider statin if additional risk factors.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 61, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 107, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 1.7202840244445763, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 1.7% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 1.1686710893573904, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 1.2%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 2.52192511994296, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 2.5%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 12.769261397613393, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 12.8%, ASCVD 8.0%, Heart Failure 17.6%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 18, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 13, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 94, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.79, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 104, "status": "borderline", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Borderline elevated Apo B.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider pharmacotherapy if high ASCVD risk.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 71, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Elevated Lp(a) - RISK ENHANCER. Increases CVD risk category.", "recommendation": "Lp(a) ≥50 mg/dL is a risk enhancer - consider upgrading CVD risk category. Aggressive LDL lowering indicated. Discuss hereditary nature.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 344, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 75, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 7.27, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 7.8, "status": "abnormal", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Elevated hs-CRP - increased cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Address cardiovascular risk factors. Consider repeat testing.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.78, "status": "borderline", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Low Progesterone (Phase Unknown)", "interpretation": "Progesterone is 0.78 ng/mL. Without cycle phase documentation, interpretation is limited. If drawn in luteal phase, this would indicate anovulation - a perimenopause marker.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Document cycle phase for accurate interpretation. If patient is mid-cycle or luteal, this suggests anovulation. Consider symptoms: sleep disruption, anxiety, irregular cycles. PATIENT EDUCATION: We need to know where you are in your menstrual cycle to fully interpret this result. Please note when your last period started.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase; <5 ng/mL suggests anovulation if luteal"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.78, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Relative Progesterone Deficiency", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 128 pg/mL with progesterone of only 0.78 ng/mL indicates relative progesterone deficiency. The estrogen is not being adequately opposed by progesterone, which can lead to estrogen dominance symptoms.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Consider progesterone supplementation to balance the E2:P4 ratio. Micronized progesterone 100-200 mg nightly can provide adequate opposition. If patient has symptoms (insomnia, anxiety, heavy bleeding, breast tenderness), progesterone should help. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen level is good, but your progesterone is not keeping up. This imbalance can cause sleep problems, mood changes, and heavy periods. Progesterone supplementation can help restore balance.", "referenceRange": "E2 >100 should have P4 ≥8 for adequate opposition"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.78, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Luteal Progesterone Deficiency", "interpretation": "Progesterone of 0.78 ng/mL with estradiol of 128 pg/mL in a woman 45 years old indicates luteal progesterone deficiency. Estrogen production is adequate but progesterone is not keeping pace - a hallmark of perimenopause.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Micronized progesterone 100-200 mg nightly. Dosing can be cyclic (days 14-28) or continuous depending on cycle regularity. Progesterone provides GABAergic support for sleep and anxiety relief. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your body is making enough estrogen, but progesterone has dropped. This is very common in perimenopause and causes sleep disruption, anxiety, and mood changes. Progesterone supplementation supports your nervous system and helps restore calm, restful sleep.", "referenceRange": "P4 ≥5 ng/mL expected with E2 ≥60 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 21.7, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone Pattern D: Adequate Androgens, Persistent Symptoms", "interpretation": "Adequate Androgens pattern identified (Total T: 21.7 ng/dL (within range); Free T: 3 pg/mL; SHBG: 45 nmol/L). Total, free, and bioavailable testosterone are within normal ranges and SHBG is not extreme. If the patient reports fatigue, low mood, sleep disruption, or low libido, these symptoms are likely driven by non-androgenic factors. Findings to evaluate: Low ferritin (43.3 ng/mL) — iron deficiency mimics androgen insufficiency. Estrogen/Progesterone balance should be assessed (E2: 128 pg/mL, P4: 0.78 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Do not reflexively add testosterone when levels are adequate — look deeper. Evaluate iron status (ferritin target >50 for symptom resolution), thyroid function (optimize TSH), and sleep quality (consider sleep apnea screening with STOP-BANG). Assess estrogen and progesterone balance — E2/P4 imbalance is a common driver of persistent symptoms. Address CNS and psychosocial drivers: chronic stress, HPA axis dysregulation, mood disorders. Consider DHEA-S if not already checked. Support with lifestyle optimization: sleep hygiene, stress management, nutrition. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your testosterone levels are actually in a healthy range, which is good news. When symptoms persist despite normal testosterone, it usually means something else is contributing. Your provider will look at other factors like iron levels, thyroid function, sleep quality, and hormone balance to find what's driving your symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Total T 15-70 ng/dL, Free T 0.5-5.0 pg/mL (all within range)"}, {"unit": "of 6 markers positive", "value": 4, "status": "critical", "category": "Insulin Resistance Screening", "recheckTiming": "3-6 months", "interpretation": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: HIGH LIKELIHOOD (4/6 markers positive). Identified phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance, Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance. Treat according to phenotype-specific protocol.", "recommendation": "Phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance; Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance. Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose.", "referenceRange": "0-1 Low, 2 Moderate, 3+ High Likelihood"}], "aiRecommendations": "Unable to generate recommendations. Please review lab findings manually.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 1.75, "detail": "Triglycerides 107 / HDL 61 = 1.75", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "6%", "detail": "Prediabetic range", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 45, "detail": "Low - hyperinsulinemia suppresses SHBG", "positive": true, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 13, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "104 mg/dL", "detail": "Elevated atherogenic particle count", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "7.8 mg/L", "detail": "Elevated systemic inflammation", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "high", "phenotypes": [{"key": "visceral_metabolic", "name": "Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Recheck A1c, lipid panel, hs-CRP, ApoB in 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["A1c >= 5.7%", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0"], "pathophysiology": "Early peripheral + hepatic insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia. Glucose may still be \\"normal.\\" A1c often rises last.", "triggerCriteria": ["A1c >= 5.7%", "TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0", "Central adiposity phenotype", "ALT above optimal range"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests your body may not be responding to insulin as efficiently as it should. Insulin is the hormone that helps move sugar from your bloodstream into your cells for energy.\\n\\nWhen cells become less sensitive to insulin:\\n- Blood sugar slowly rises over time\\n- Triglycerides increase\\n- Inflammation increases\\n- Weight gain around the midsection becomes easier\\n\\nThe good news? This stage is highly reversible with targeted nutrition, strength training, sleep optimization, and - when appropriate - medication support.\\n\\nWe'll focus on improving how your body processes fuel and reducing long-term cardiometabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward nutrition (>= 25-35g/meal)", "Carbohydrate quality + timing education", "Strength training 2-3x weekly", "10-15 min post-meal walking", "Sleep optimization", "Consider: GLP-1 therapy if obesity, appetite dysregulation, A1c >= 5.7, or inflammatory phenotype", "Consider: Metformin if prediabetes or strong family history", "Recheck metabolic panel in 3-6 months"]}, {"key": "hormonal_pcos", "name": "Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Monitor SHBG, androgens, A1c every 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["SHBG low (<50)", "A1c elevated"], "pathophysiology": "Hyperinsulinemia suppresses SHBG and increases ovarian androgen production.", "triggerCriteria": ["Low SHBG (<50; strong if <30)", "Elevated total or free testosterone", "Irregular cycles", "A1c may still be normal", "TG/HDL may be borderline-high"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests insulin may be influencing your hormone balance.\\n\\nWhen insulin levels run high:\\n- The body makes less SHBG (a protective hormone-binding protein)\\n- Androgen levels can rise\\n- Cycles may become irregular\\n\\nThis pattern is common in PCOS and other hormone imbalance states.\\n\\nImproving insulin sensitivity often helps restore hormonal balance and reduce long-term metabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward, lower refined carb approach", "Strength training", "Consider metformin if cycles irregular or A1c trending", "Consider GLP-1 if obesity phenotype present", "Monitor SHBG, androgens, A1c"]}], "positiveCount": 4, "likelihoodLabel": "HIGH LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: HIGH LIKELIHOOD (4/6 markers positive). Identified phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance, Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance. Treat according to phenotype-specific protocol.", "confirmationTests": "Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose."}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Inflammatory Burden", "confidence": "high", "description": "Pattern of systemic inflammation indicated by elevated inflammatory markers and/or symptomatic presentation. May benefit from anti-inflammatory nutritional support.", "supportingFindings": ["hs-CRP 7.8 mg/L (elevated >3.0)", "ApoB 104 mg/dL (elevated)"]}, {"name": "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "confidence": "high", "description": "Metabolic pattern consistent with insulin resistance and/or visceral adiposity. This drives inflammation, hormonal imbalance, and cardiovascular risk.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance screening: HIGH LIKELIHOOD (4/6 markers positive)", "A1c 6% (prediabetic range)", "SHBG 45 nmol/L (low, associated with insulin resistance)", "ApoB 104 mg/dL (elevated)", "hs-CRP 7.8 mg/L (metabolic inflammation)"]}, {"name": "Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Lab pattern consistent with relative estrogen excess or impaired estrogen clearance. Even without reported symptoms, supporting liver detoxification and estrogen metabolism pathways may be beneficial.", "supportingFindings": ["Estradiol:Progesterone imbalance (E2 128, P4 0.78)", "Progesterone 0.78 ng/mL (very low)"]}, {"name": "Oxidative Stress Burden", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Multiple markers suggest increased oxidative stress and cellular burden. Antioxidant and glutathione support may help reduce oxidative damage and support cellular energy production.", "supportingFindings": ["hs-CRP 7.8 mg/L (elevated, systemic inflammation)", "Insulin resistance pattern (metabolic oxidative driver)", "A1c 6% (glycemic stress)", "Age 45 (increased oxidative stress risk)"]}, {"name": "Gut-Microbiome Support", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Indicators suggest the gut and/or urogenital microbiome may benefit from targeted probiotic support and gut barrier maintenance.", "supportingFindings": ["hs-CRP 7.8 mg/L (gut-driven inflammation possible)", "B12 266 pg/mL (possible absorption issues)"]}, {"name": "Iron Deficiency", "confidence": "low", "description": "Iron stores are depleted or suboptimal. Iron is essential for energy, cognitive function, hair health, and oxygen transport.", "supportingFindings": ["Ferritin 43.3 ng/mL (suboptimal ≤50)"]}, {"name": "Menopausal Transition", "confidence": "low", "description": "Clinical picture consistent with perimenopause or menopause. Hormonal shifts during this transition affect bone health, cardiovascular risk, mood, and quality of life.", "supportingFindings": ["Age 45 (perimenopause window)", "FSH 16.4 mIU/mL (rising, perimenopause range)"]}]}	2026-03-16 16:42:32.584268	\N
41	Michelle Rivera	female	1977-05-04 00:00:00	{"a1c": 5, "alt": 11, "ast": 19, "bun": 18, "co2": 25, "fsh": 26.7, "hdl": 58, "ldl": 77, "rbc": 3.7, "tsh": 2.05, "wbc": 6.4, "acne": false, "egfr": 69, "onHRT": true, "freeT3": 2.58, "sodium": 140, "albumin": 4.3, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 8.9, "glucose": 45, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "chloride": 102, "hairLoss": false, "vitaminD": 54.9, "bilirubin": 0.3, "estradiol": 18, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": false, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 302, "potassium": 4.7, "creatinine": 1, "hematocrit": 38.8, "hemoglobin": 12.5, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "vitaminB12": 327, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "1977-05-04", "moodChanges": true, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Michelle Rivera", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 48, "bmi": 25.89, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": true, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 159, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 1.1, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 22.1, "totalProtein": 6.6, "lowMotivation": false, "triglycerides": 111, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "totalCholesterol": 157}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History of present illness: Patient Michelle Rivera is a 48-year-old female presenting for hormone lab review. Patient reports no specific vasomotor, sleep, mood, or libido complaints at this time. Menstrual status: perimenopausal pattern on labs; LMP and cycle day at time of blood draw not documented in chart. No reported history of VTE, breast cancer, stroke, or recent myocardial infarction in chart note; confirmatory review requested.\\n- Review of systems: negative for chest pain, dyspnea, leg swelling/pain, abnormal uterine bleeding, new virilization, or acute neurologic symptoms.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\nVitals: Not documented in chart today (BP, HR, BMI not available).\\n\\nLaboratory results (03/16/2026) — grouped by category. Abnormal/borderline values flagged.\\n\\nCBC:\\n- Hemoglobin 12.5 g/dL (normal)\\n- Hematocrit 38.8 % (normal)\\n- Platelets 302 K/uL (normal)\\n\\nMetabolic / Renal / Liver:\\n- AST 19 U/L (normal)\\n- ALT 11 U/L (normal)\\n- Creatinine 1.0 mg/dL (normal)\\n- eGFR 69 mL/min (normal)\\n\\nGlycemic:\\n- Hemoglobin A1c 5.0 % (normal)\\n\\nLipids / Cardiovascular:\\n- LDL cholesterol 77 mg/dL (normal)\\n- HDL cholesterol 58 mg/dL (borderline — could be higher for optimal protection)\\n- Triglycerides 111 mg/dL (normal)\\n\\nHormones:\\n- Estradiol (E2) 18 pg/mL — ABNORMAL (below bone-protective threshold <40 pg/mL)\\n- FSH 26.7 mIU/mL — ABNORMAL (perimenopausal/menopausal transition range)\\n- Progesterone (P4) 1.1 ng/mL — ABNORMAL / borderline for luteal-phase adequacy (possible anovulation if drawn in luteal phase)\\n- Testosterone, total 22.1 ng/dL — ABNORMAL/low relative to clinic therapeutic target (noted as “Adequate Androgens” pattern by protocol but low for clinic target)\\n- TSH 2.05 mIU/L (normal)\\n\\nVitamins / Micronutrients:\\n- Vitamin D, 25-OH 54.9 ng/mL — BORDERLINE (adequate but suboptimal; target ≥60 ng/mL per clinic)\\n\\nOther:\\n- Vitamin B12 327 pg/mL (normal)\\n- 10-year Total CVD risk (AHA PREVENT 2023) 4.03% (low) — normal\\n- 10-year ASCVD risk 2.33% (low) — normal\\n- 10-year Heart Failure risk 1.73% (low) — normal\\n- 30-year CVD risk 25.0% — BORDERLINE / elevated for long-term risk\\n- Sleep apnea risk (STOP‑BANG) 1 point (low)\\n\\nAbnormal / critical flags:\\n- Estradiol 18 pg/mL (E2 <40 pg/mL) — increased bone resorption risk.\\n- Progesterone 1.1 ng/mL — low; interpretation limited by unknown cycle timing; if luteal-phase, suggests anovulation.\\n- FSH 26.7 mIU/mL — supports perimenopausal transition.\\n- Total testosterone 22.1 ng/dL — below clinic therapeutic target; do not initiate/adjust testosterone without provider review.\\n- Vitamin D suboptimal at 54.9 ng/mL.\\n- 30-year CVD risk elevated at 25.0% — counsel on long-term risk reduction.\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Hypoestrogenic perimenopausal state (\\"crash phase\\") — E2 18 pg/mL with FSH 26.7 mIU/mL. Clinical significance: estradiol below bone-protective threshold (<40 pg/mL) → increased bone resorption risk and potential for vasomotor/cognitive symptoms. Differential: menopausal transition vs. ovarian insufficiency; confirm with clinical correlation and cycle timing.\\n   - Red flag: E2 <40 pg/mL requires provider notification for consideration of estrogen therapy and bone protection.\\n\\n2. Low progesterone 1.1 ng/mL — likely inadequate luteal progesterone. Clinical significance: if blood drawn in luteal phase, value suggests anovulation (common in perimenopause) and inadequate endometrial protection if exogenous estrogen is used. Differential: luteal-phase deficiency vs. blood drawn in follicular/random phase. Interpretation limited by lack of LMP/cycle day documentation.\\n   - Red flag: P4 <2 ng/mL if drawn mid-luteal should be escalated to provider.\\n\\n3. Relative androgen deficiency by clinic therapeutic target — Total testosterone 22.1 ng/dL. Laboratory range may be within reference, but low relative to clinic target (75–125 ng/dL). Clinical significance: consider only after estrogen/progesterone optimized and non-androgen drivers of symptoms evaluated.\\n   - Do NOT initiate or increase testosterone without provider authorization and confirmation of current regimen.\\n\\n4. Vitamin D sufficient but suboptimal — 25-OH D 54.9 ng/mL; target ≥60 ng/mL for bone/CV health per clinic protocol. Continue supplementation and recheck.\\n\\n5. Long-term cardiovascular risk elevated — 30-year CVD risk 25.0% (30-year ASCVD 13.9%, 30-year HF 13.8%). Short-term (10-year) risks are low. Clinical significance: implement lifestyle interventions; statin generally not indicated at current 10-year risk but monitor and manage risk factors.\\n\\n6. Other labs within expected ranges — CBC, LFTs, renal function, A1c, lipids (LDL 77), B12 adequate. No immediate concern on routine labs.\\n\\nPLAN:\\nGeneral immediate actions:\\n- Notify provider today regarding abnormal E2 18 pg/mL and P4 1.1 ng/mL (provider notification recommended per clinic protocol).\\n- Confirm contraindications to estrogen (review chart and ask patient): personal/family history of VTE, active or history of estrogen-sensitive malignancy (breast/endometrium), uncontrolled hypertension, recent stroke/MI. If any contraindication present, do not initiate systemic estrogen; discuss alternatives with provider.\\n- Obtain/record menstrual history and cycle timing: request LMP date and whether blood draw was mid-luteal, mid-follicular, or random. This is essential to interpret progesterone and to plan therapy.\\n\\nProblem-specific plans:\\n\\n1. Hypoestrogenic perimenopausal state (E2 18 pg/mL, FSH 26.7)\\n   - Provider decision requested: consider initiating or increasing transdermal estradiol to restore E2 to bone-protective range (target ~60–100 pg/mL).\\n   - Suggested regimen for provider consideration:\\n     • If not on estrogen: start transdermal estradiol patch 0.025 mg/day (25 mcg/day). If still symptomatic or lab response inadequate, consider increasing to 0.05 mg/day (50 mcg/day).\\n     • If currently on 0.025 mg/day patch: consider increasing to 0.05 mg/day.\\n   - Safety monitoring: confirm absence of contraindications before start; counsel on VTE/stroke symptoms and monitor BP.\\n   - Order baseline DEXA if not recent, and ensure mammogram up-to-date per age guidelines.\\n\\n2. Low progesterone (P4 1.1 ng/mL)\\n   - Before change: confirm cycle phase (obtain LMP).\\n   - If uterus intact and estrogen therapy initiated or ongoing: recommend micronized progesterone for endometrial protection:\\n     • Suggested dose for provider consideration: micronized progesterone 200 mg qHS (nightly). If on cyclic progesterone, discuss switching to nightly dosing or continuous per provider decision.\\n   - If drawn mid-luteal and P4 remains low, consider diagnosis of anovulation; discuss cyclic vs continuous progestogen strategy with provider.\\n   - Recheck progesterone after therapy/with cycle-timed draw as indicated.\\n\\n3. Testosterone (Total T 22.1 ng/dL)\\n   - Do NOT start or adjust testosterone without provider authorization.\\n   - If patient symptomatic and provider elects to treat after addressing estrogen/progesterone and excluding other causes: consider baseline SHBG and free testosterone or bioavailable T prior to therapy; initiate low-dose regimen with close monitoring. If already on testosterone, document current product/dose before any increase; incremental titration advised (e.g., +25–50% topical dose) and recheck levels in 4–8 weeks.\\n\\n4. Vitamin D 54.9 ng/mL\\n   - Continue current supplement: Metagenics D3 2000 Complex daily.\\n   - Recheck 25‑OH vitamin D in 3 months; target ≥60 ng/mL. Adjust dose if provider directs.\\n\\n5. Cardiovascular risk (30-year CVD 25.0%)\\n   - Lifestyle counseling: Mediterranean-style diet, ≥150 minutes/week moderate exercise, smoking cessation, weight optimization, limit alcohol.\\n   - Continue monitoring lipids annually; no statin indicated at this time given 10-year risk low; reassess if risk factors change.\\n   - Consider referral to primary care or cardiology for long-term risk management if patient has other risk factors or preference.\\n\\n6. Additional testing and monitoring\\n   - Hormone rechecks: If hormone therapy is initiated or doses changed, recheck E2, P4, and total testosterone in 4–8 weeks. For progesterone interpretation, document timing relative to dosing and cycle (mid-luteal if cycling).\\n   - Consider additional labs to refine therapy: SHBG, free testosterone or bioavailable T, DHEA‑S, ferritin/iron studies (if symptoms of fatigue or heavy menses), especially if testosterone therapy considered or fatigue persists.\\n   - Safety labs: LFTs and lipid panel as indicated per hormone therapy monitoring (baseline and periodically).\\n   - Imaging: DEXA scan (if not up-to-date) given hypoestrogenic state; baseline mammogram per screening guidelines.\\n\\nSupplement recommendations:\\n- Continue Intrinsi B12‑Folate™ (1 tablet daily) per protocol.\\n- Continue Metagenics D3 2000 Complex daily; reassess per above.\\n\\nLifestyle / nonpharmacologic interventions:\\n- Bone health: weight-bearing/resistance exercise ≥3 times weekly, ensure adequate dietary calcium and protein, smoking cessation if applicable.\\n- Cardiovascular: exercise program, Mediterranean diet, blood pressure control, address weight and diabetes prevention.\\n- Sleep/mental health: optimize sleep hygiene; primary care/behavioral health referral if mood or sleep symptoms develop.\\n\\nFollow-up and timeline:\\n- Today (03/16/2026): Provider notified of critical labs (E2, P4). Obtain LMP/cycle day and confirm contraindications to estrogen.\\n- Within 1–7 days: Provider decision regarding initiation/adjustment of transdermal estradiol and micronized progesterone. Document current HRT regimen if patient is already using hormones before any change.\\n- 4–8 weeks after any dose change: Recheck E2, P4, total testosterone (and free T/SHBG if indicated). Assess symptoms and adverse effects.\\n- 3 months: Recheck 25‑OH vitamin D; adjust supplementation to reach ≥60 ng/mL.\\n- 3–6 months: Routine clinic follow-up for hormone management (per clinic recheck window).\\n- 6–12 months: Consider DEXA if not performed recently; annual lipid reassessment.\\n\\nDocumentation:\\n- Do not initiate or change hormone therapy without provider authorization and without documenting current regimen and contraindications.\\n- Patient education materials to be provided after provider review/approval. Staff to discuss rationale for estrogen for bone protection, need for progesterone if uterus intact, and that testosterone will not be started reflexively.\\n\\nCommunication:\\n- Results flagged in chart and communicated to provider today. Patient requested to provide LMP date and any changes in personal/family history that would alter estrogen candidacy.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\n03/16/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "preventRisk": {"ldlGoal": "No specific LDL target; focus on risk-based statin intensity", "riskCategory": "low", "tenYearASCVD": 0.023274685201335313, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0.04032484666397683, "thirtyYearASCVD": 0.13909964261922594, "ageValidFor30Year": true, "thirtyYearTotalCVD": 0.2502245954030152, "tenYearHFPercentage": "1.7%", "tenYearHeartFailure": 0.017330996792488385, "statinRecommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "tenYearCVDPercentage": "4.0%", "tenYearASCVDPercentage": "2.3%", "thirtyYearHFPercentage": "13.8%", "thirtyYearHeartFailure": 0.1384254857523945, "thirtyYearCVDPercentage": "25.0%", "thirtyYearASCVDPercentage": "13.9%"}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 tablet daily", "name": "Intrinsi B12-Folate™", "caution": "Contains intrinsic factor for enhanced B12 absorption. Ideal for patients with absorption concerns or vegetarian/vegan diets.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Intrinsi B12-Folate provides methylated B12 and folate with intrinsic factor for superior absorption. Supports energy, cognition, methylation, and nerve function.", "indication": "B12 327 pg/mL (suboptimal <400); Mood changes with low B12", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "Your B12 and/or folate levels need support. This supplement provides highly absorbable forms of these essential vitamins to support your energy, brain function, and nervous system health.", "supportingFindings": ["B12 327 pg/mL (suboptimal <400)", "Mood changes with low B12"]}, {"dose": "1 tablet daily with meal", "name": "D3 2000 Complex", "caution": "Maintenance dose for suboptimal levels. Comprehensive vitamin D support with cofactors.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "low", "rationale": "D3 2000 Complex provides maintenance vitamin D with cofactors to reach and maintain optimal range ≥60 ng/mL.", "indication": "Vitamin D 54.9 ng/mL (suboptimal, target ≥60)", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D is close to optimal but could benefit from a maintenance dose to reach the ideal range for bone and immune health.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 54.9 ng/mL (suboptimal, target ≥60)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "consider", "rationale": "Lipid levels meet threshold for CAC consideration. CAC may be useful if risk level is uncertain or patient is hesitant about statin.", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\nYour labs show a perimenopausal, hypoestrogenic pattern: estradiol (E2) is 18 pg/mL and FSH is 26.7 mIU/mL, which indicates reduced ovarian estrogen production. Progesterone is low at 1.1 ng/mL and total testosterone is 22.1 ng/dL. Hemoglobin is 12.5 g/dL and TSH is 2.05 mIU/L (both normal). Your 10-year ASCVD risk is low at 2.3% but your 30-year CVD risk is 25.0%, so long‑term heart-health steps are important.\\n\\nPositive findings (what’s good)\\n- Hemoglobin 12.5 g/dL and hematocrit 38.8% — no current anemia.\\n- TSH 2.05 mIU/L — thyroid function in the normal range.\\n- LDL cholesterol 77 mg/dL and triglycerides 111 mg/dL — good current lipid numbers.\\n- Vitamin D 25‑OH is 54.9 ng/mL — adequate.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + actions)\\n- Low estrogen: Estradiol 18 pg/mL (below bone-protection threshold of ~40 pg/mL). Low E2 can increase bone loss. Actions:\\n  - Discuss hormone options with your clinician (e.g., transdermal or oral estrogen) to protect bones and relieve perimenopausal symptoms.\\n  - Start calcium 1,200 mg/day from diet and supplements as needed.\\n  - Weight-bearing aerobic exercise 30 minutes most days (brisk walking) + strength training 2x/week.\\n  - Consider baseline DEXA bone density scan within 3–6 months to assess bone health.\\n- Low progesterone: Progesterone 1.1 ng/mL. If you are still cycling, repeat a mid-luteal progesterone (~7 days before expected period) to confirm ovulation. If anovulatory, discuss cyclic/progestin therapy with your provider.\\n- Testosterone: Total T 22.1 ng/dL. If you are on HRT, this is lower than expected. Lifestyle measures that support healthy androgens and energy:\\n  - Prioritize 7–8 hours quality sleep nightly.\\n  - Strength training 2–3x weekly.\\n  - Stress reduction: daily 10–20 minute breathing/meditation or brisk walks.\\n- Bone/vitamin D: Vitamin D 54.9 ng/mL is adequate but suboptimal. Action: continue 1,000–2,000 IU vitamin D daily and recheck in 6–12 months aiming for ~60–80 ng/mL.\\n- Cardiovascular prevention (30-year CVD risk 25.0%): adopt long‑term heart-healthy habits:\\n  - Aim for 150 minutes/week moderate aerobic activity (brisk walking, cycling).\\n  - Follow a Mediterranean-style diet: vegetables, whole grains, olive oil, fish 2–3x/week, nuts.\\n  - Reduce processed foods and added sugars; limit sodium.\\n  - Maintain healthy weight and avoid smoking.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Book a visit in the next 4–8 weeks to review hormone options (estrogen/progestin management, adjust HRT if you’re already on it).\\n- If still cycling, repeat mid-luteal progesterone next cycle.\\n- Arrange DEXA scan within 3–6 months and repeat vitamin D in 6–12 months.\\n- Annual lipid panel and routine follow-up for CVD prevention. If you notice new symptoms (heavy bleeding, hot flashes, new fatigue, mood change, bone pain), contact the clinic sooner.\\n\\nYou’re doing many things well — these targeted steps can help protect your bones, hormones, and long-term heart health.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 12.5, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 38.8, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 302, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 18, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol below bone protection threshold (<40 pg/mL). Bone resorption increases below 40-60 pg/mL.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase estrogen dose. Target 60-100 pg/mL. Current level inadequate for bone protection.", "referenceRange": "60-100 pg/mL (HRT goal, >40 for bone)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1.1, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone below HRT therapeutic range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase progesterone dose. Target 8-10 ng/mL for optimal endometrial protection.", "referenceRange": "8-10 ng/mL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 26.7, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 22.1, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Low testosterone despite HRT.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase testosterone dose. Target 75-125 ng/dL for optimal results.", "referenceRange": "75-125 ng/dL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 2.05, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 54.9, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D adequate but suboptimal (41-59 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 2000 Complex daily to reach optimal range ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 327, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 77, "status": "normal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 58, "status": "borderline", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "HDL could be higher for optimal protection.", "recommendation": "Increase exercise. Moderate alcohol if appropriate.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 111, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 4.032484666397683, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 4.0% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 2.3274685201335314, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 2.3%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 1.7330996792488385, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 1.7%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (1/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 25.022459540301522, "status": "borderline", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 25.0%, ASCVD 13.9%, Heart Failure 13.8%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 19, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 11, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 69, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1.1, "status": "borderline", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Low Progesterone (Phase Unknown)", "interpretation": "Progesterone is 1.1 ng/mL. Without cycle phase documentation, interpretation is limited. If drawn in luteal phase, this would indicate anovulation - a perimenopause marker.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Document cycle phase for accurate interpretation. If patient is mid-cycle or luteal, this suggests anovulation. Consider symptoms: sleep disruption, anxiety, irregular cycles. PATIENT EDUCATION: We need to know where you are in your menstrual cycle to fully interpret this result. Please note when your last period started.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase; <5 ng/mL suggests anovulation if luteal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 18, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Hypoestrogenic Pattern", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 18 pg/mL with FSH of 26.7 mIU/mL indicates hypoestrogenic perimenopause - the \\"crash phase.\\" Estrogen production has dropped significantly and the brain is signaling strongly (elevated FSH) trying to stimulate the ovaries.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Transdermal estradiol 0.025-0.05 mg is appropriate replacement therapy. Add micronized progesterone for uterine protection and sleep support. Frame this as hormone replacement, not stimulation - we are restoring what the body needs. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen has dropped to a level that can cause symptoms like hot flashes, night sweats, brain fog, and mood changes. Replacing estrogen with a patch or gel can relieve these symptoms and protect your long-term health. Progesterone is added to protect your uterus and help with sleep.", "referenceRange": "E2 <50 with FSH >10 indicates estrogen deficiency"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 26.7, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Perimenopause Transition", "interpretation": "FSH of 26.7 mIU/mL indicates perimenopause (menopausal transition). No specific symptoms reported at this time. Elevated FSH reflects decreased ovarian function and reduced estrogen production. This is a normal part of reproductive aging but may benefit from hormone optimization if symptomatic.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: If symptomatic, consider low-dose transdermal estradiol + micronized progesterone (if uterus intact). Perimenopause is characterized by fluctuating hormones. Steady hormone delivery via patch can smooth out peaks and valleys. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your lab results show you are in perimenopause - the transition phase before menopause. Hormone levels can swing widely during this time, causing symptoms. Treatment options are available to help you feel better.", "referenceRange": "FSH <25 mIU/mL premenopausal; ≥25 suggests transition; ≥40 menopausal"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 22.1, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone Pattern D: Adequate Androgens, Persistent Symptoms", "interpretation": "Adequate Androgens pattern identified (Total T: 22.1 ng/dL (within range)). Total, free, and bioavailable testosterone are within normal ranges and SHBG is not extreme. If the patient reports fatigue, low mood, sleep disruption, or low libido, these symptoms are likely driven by non-androgenic factors. Findings to evaluate: Estrogen/Progesterone balance should be assessed (E2: 18 pg/mL, P4: 1.1 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Do not reflexively add testosterone when levels are adequate — look deeper. Evaluate iron status (ferritin target >50 for symptom resolution), thyroid function (optimize TSH), and sleep quality (consider sleep apnea screening with STOP-BANG). Assess estrogen and progesterone balance — E2/P4 imbalance is a common driver of persistent symptoms. Address CNS and psychosocial drivers: chronic stress, HPA axis dysregulation, mood disorders. Consider DHEA-S if not already checked. Support with lifestyle optimization: sleep hygiene, stress management, nutrition. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your testosterone levels are actually in a healthy range, which is good news. When symptoms persist despite normal testosterone, it usually means something else is contributing. Your provider will look at other factors like iron levels, thyroid function, sleep quality, and hormone balance to find what's driving your symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Total T 15-70 ng/dL, Free T 0.5-5.0 pg/mL (all within range)"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- Labs indicate a hypoestrogenic perimenopausal pattern with elevated FSH (26.7 mIU/mL) and low estradiol (E2 18 pg/mL) — level below bone-protective threshold (<40 pg/mL). Progesterone is low (P4 1.1 ng/mL). Total testosterone is low for the clinic’s therapeutic target (T total 22.1 ng/dL vs target 75–125 ng/dL per protocol). Vitamin D is adequate but suboptimal (25‑OH D = 54.9 ng/mL). 30‑year CVD risk is elevated (25.0%).\\n- Clinical priorities: restore estrogen to bone-protective range and provide endometrial protection with adequate progesterone; review need for androgen optimization only after addressing estrogen/progesterone and other drivers of symptoms. Confirm menstrual cycle phase before interpreting progesterone.\\n\\nPriority actions (organized CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL (notify provider now)\\n- Notify provider immediately for estradiol 18 pg/mL and progesterone 1.1 ng/mL given E2 <40 pg/mL → increased bone resorption risk and inadequate endometrial protection if uterus intact.\\n- Confirm whether patient has contraindications to estrogen (personal history of VTE, active breast cancer, uncontrolled HTN, recent stroke/MI). If yes, notify provider for alternative plan.\\n\\nURGENT (within 1–7 days)\\n- Document cycle status: obtain date of last menstrual period (LMP) and whether sample was drawn in follicular, mid-cycle, or luteal phase. If P4 was drawn in presumed luteal phase and remains 1.1 ng/mL, flag as probable anovulation/perimenopausal anovulation.\\n- Provider recommendation per protocol:\\n  * Begin/adjust transdermal estradiol: patch 0.025–0.05 mg/day (0.025–0.05 mg/day as per clinic protocol; typical product equivalents 25–50 mcg/day) to achieve target E2 60–100 pg/mL.\\n  * Add/adjust micronized progesterone for uterine protection and sleep support: consider increasing to 200 mg nightly (or 100–200 mg nightly depending on current dose and regimen). If patient is on cyclic progesterone, consider continuous or nightly dosing per provider decision.\\n- If patient is currently on HRT, do NOT make blind substitutions — confirm current doses and route, then make incremental increase as below.\\n\\nROUTINE (within 4–12 weeks and ongoing)\\n- Recheck hormones to assess response 4–8 weeks after dose adjustment: estradiol, progesterone, and total testosterone. For progesterone interpretation, document timing relative to dosing and cycle (mid-luteal if cycling).\\n- Vitamin D: continue Metagenics D3 2000 Complex daily as per protocol; recheck 25‑OH vitamin D in 3 months aiming for ≥60 ng/mL.\\n- Cardiovascular risk: schedule comprehensive risk factor modification and annual recheck of lipid panel and 30‑year risk.\\n\\nSpecific medication/dose recommendations for staff to present to provider\\n- Estradiol:\\n  * If not on estrogen: recommend initiation of transdermal estradiol patch 0.025 mg/day; provider may escalate to 0.05 mg/day if symptomatic or lab response inadequate. (Use lowest effective dose and monitor.)\\n  * If already on 0.025 mg patch: consider increasing to 0.05 mg patch.\\n- Progesterone (micronized):\\n  * If not on progesterone and uterus intact: recommend starting micronized progesterone 200 mg qHS (or 200 mg daily for continuous regimens) for endometrial protection.\\n  * If on 100 mg qHS: consider increasing to 200 mg qHS.\\n  * If using cyclic progesterone (e.g., 12–14 days/month), discuss switching to nightly dosing if persistent symptoms or anovulatory cycles.\\n- Testosterone (only if applicable / after provider review):\\n  * Do not reflexively start testosterone solely based on low total T if other causes of symptoms exist.\\n  * If provider elects to treat to clinic target (75–125 ng/dL) and patient is already on testosterone: consider incremental increase (e.g., +25–50% of current topical dose) with recheck in 4–8 weeks. Document current product and dose before adjustments.\\n  * If initiating testosterone de novo, recommend starting low and titrating: common starting regimens include transdermal testosterone preparations dosed to deliver ~300–600 µg/day (site-specific products vary) or low-dose injectable regimens per clinic protocol; provider to choose agent and precise dose. Recheck total testosterone 4–8 weeks after dose change to target 75–125 ng/dL.\\n- Safety monitoring to include: LFTs, lipid panel, and symptoms of virilization for testosterone; VTE/HTN surveillance for estrogen.\\n\\nPhysician notification requirements\\n- Immediate notification for any of the following: E2 <40 pg/mL with symptoms/risk for bone loss, P4 <2 ng/mL if patient is cycling in luteal phase (suggests anovulation), any contraindication to estrogen identified, or if provider-level authorization required to start/increase hormones.\\n- Notify provider before initiating or increasing testosterone.\\n\\nPatient education points for staff to cover (brief, to be reviewed/approved by provider)\\n- Explain that E2 of 18 pg/mL is below bone-protective levels and can cause vasomotor and cognitive symptoms; replacement with a transdermal patch is effective and commonly used.\\n- Explain the need for progesterone to protect the uterus when estrogen is used and that P4 will be increased to therapeutic levels.\\n- Clarify that testosterone is not automatically added — we will focus on estrogen/progesterone balance first; if testosterone is used, it will be low-dose and monitored.\\n- Request LMP date and advise patient to note cycle timing for future labs.\\n- Lifestyle basics to support bone and CV health: weight-bearing exercise, smoking cessation, alcohol moderation, dietary calcium and adequate protein, sleep optimization.\\n\\nLifestyle interventions (specific)\\n- Bone health: encourage weight-bearing/resistance exercise 3 times weekly; ensure calcium intake (dietary or supplementation to provider spec) and vitamin D adherence (Metagenics D3 2000 daily).\\n- Cardiovascular risk reduction: structured exercise (≥150 min moderate/week), Mediterranean-style diet, smoking cessation, weight optimization, blood pressure control; consider referral to primary care for CV risk management if uncontrolled risk factors present.\\n- Sleep and mental health: screen for sleep apnea (STOP‑BANG already normal), optimize sleep hygiene, consider behavioral strategies for stress/anxiety; refer to behavioral health if mood or sleep disorders suspected.\\n- Evaluate iron status and correct if low (see testing below), particularly if heavy menses.\\n\\nAdditional testing and referrals to consider\\n- Confirmatory/adjunct labs:\\n  * Ferritin, iron studies (CBC already normal): aim ferritin >50 ng/mL if symptomatic (fatigue, low libido).\\n  * SHBG, free testosterone or bioavailable testosterone if testosterone therapy is being considered or to better interpret total T.\\n  * DHEA-S if not measured and if adrenal contribution considered.\\n  * Repeat estradiol, progesterone, total testosterone after 4–8 weeks post-dose adjustment. For progesterone, time draw to mid-luteal if cycling.\\n  * Baseline mammogram (per age/screening guidelines) and DEXA scan given hypoestrogenic state and risk to bone — discuss with provider.\\n- Referrals:\\n  * Gynecology if uterus status uncertain, abnormal uterine bleeding, or for complex HRT regimens.\\n  * Endocrinology if hormone optimization is complex or if abnormal metabolic or adrenal labs.\\n  * Primary care/cardiology for elevated long-term CVD risk and risk-factor optimization.\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (concise actionable checklist)\\n- Now / today:\\n  1. Document LMP/date of sample and confirm whether sample was mid-luteal, mid-follicular, or random.\\n  2. Flag results for provider review: E2 18 pg/mL, P4 1.1 ng/mL, T total 22.1 ng/dL, Vitamin D 54.9 ng/mL, 30‑yr CVD risk 25%.\\n  3. Confirm contraindications to estrogen with chart review.\\n- Within 1–7 days (provider decision):\\n  4. If approved, initiate/adjust HRT per protocol:\\n     - Transdermal estradiol 0.025 → 0.05 mg/day as needed.\\n     - Micronized progesterone increase to ~200 mg qHS (or per provider specification).\\n     - Only adjust/add testosterone after provider decision and confirmation of current dose; if increasing, titrate and recheck.\\n- 4–8 weeks after dose change:\\n  5. Recheck E2, P4, total testosterone (and free T/SHBG if indicated). For progesterone, ensure timing relative to cycle/dose is documented.\\n  6. Monitor symptoms and adverse effects; report any concerns to provider immediately.\\n- 3 months:\\n  7. Recheck Vitamin D; target ≥60 ng/mL. Continue D3 2000 daily until goal.\\n- 6–12 months:\\n  8. Annual reassessment of lipids, 30‑year CV risk, and consider DEXA scanning if not up-to-date.\\n\\nNotes / caution\\n- Do not start or increase hormones without provider authorization and without confirming current regimen and contraindications.\\n- Interpretation of progesterone requires cycle-phase documentation; obtain LMP before making definitive changes based on P4.\\n- Monitor for adverse effects (VTE signs, abnormal bleeding, virilization) and notify provider immediately if they occur.\\n\\nProvide the patient-facing education only after provider review/approval; staff should use the scripted talking points above.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 1.91, "detail": "Triglycerides 111 / HDL 58 = 1.91", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 11, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Menopausal Transition", "confidence": "high", "description": "Clinical picture consistent with perimenopause or menopause. Hormonal shifts during this transition affect bone health, cardiovascular risk, mood, and quality of life.", "supportingFindings": ["Age 48 (perimenopause window)", "FSH 26.7 mIU/mL (elevated, menopausal range)", "Estradiol 18 pg/mL (declining)", "Mood changes reported"]}]}	2026-03-16 19:01:38.088606	\N
42	Michelle Rivera	female	1977-05-04 00:00:00	{"alt": 16, "ast": 20, "bun": 18, "co2": 24, "fsh": 10.5, "rbc": 3.67, "tsh": 1.69, "wbc": 6.2, "acne": false, "egfr": 95, "iron": 45, "shbg": 138, "tibc": 223, "onHRT": true, "folate": 20, "sodium": 138, "albumin": 4.6, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 9.4, "glucose": 87, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "chloride": 103, "ferritin": 149, "hairLoss": false, "bilirubin": 0.4, "estradiol": 104, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": false, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 318, "potassium": 4.6, "creatinine": 0.77, "hematocrit": 37.9, "hemoglobin": 12.6, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "vitaminB12": 803, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "1977-05-04", "moodChanges": true, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Michelle Rivera", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 48, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": true, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 141, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 1.48, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 54.9, "totalProtein": 6.7, "lowMotivation": false, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "freeTestosterone": 3}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow‑up / hormone management.\\n- HPI: Patient Michelle Rivera (48 y/o F) presenting for laboratory review and management of perimenopausal hormone results. Patient reports irregular menstrual cycles and intermittent sleep disturbance and night wakening over the past several months. Denies acute chest pain, shortness of breath, syncope, severe abdominal pain, or bleeding. No reported signs of virilization (no voice deepening, clitoromegaly, or rapid onset severe acne). No known personal history of hemochromatosis. Medications and exact timing of last progesterone dose / cycle day not documented in chart at time of review; instructed to provide cycle day/last progesterone dose timing for future labs.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: None available in chart today.\\n- Risk scores: STOP‑BANG (Sleep apnea risk) = 1 point.\\n\\nLaboratory results (03/16/2026)\\n- CBC:\\n  - Hemoglobin 12.6 g/dL (normal)\\n  - Hematocrit 37.9% (normal)\\n  - Platelets 318 K/uL (normal)\\n- Iron studies:\\n  - Serum iron 45 µg/dL (normal)\\n  - TIBC 223 µg/dL [BORDERLINE low]\\n  - Ferritin 149 ng/mL (normal)\\n  - Calculated transferrin saturation ≈ 20% (45/223) — within expected range\\n- Basic metabolic / renal:\\n  - Creatinine 0.77 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - eGFR 95 mL/min (normal)\\n- Liver tests:\\n  - AST 20 U/L (normal)\\n  - ALT 16 U/L (normal)\\n- Thyroid:\\n  - TSH 1.69 mIU/L (normal)\\n- Vitamin / nutrition:\\n  - Vitamin B12 803 pg/mL (normal)\\n  - Folate 20 ng/mL (normal)\\n- Hormones:\\n  - Progesterone 1.48 ng/mL [ABNORMAL] — below HRT therapeutic range; low for luteal support\\n  - Estradiol (E2) 104 pg/mL [BORDERLINE] — slightly above clinic optimization target (60–100 pg/mL)\\n  - FSH 10.5 mIU/mL (listed as borderline for early perimenopause in context)\\n  - Total Testosterone 54.9 ng/dL [BORDERLINE low for HRT optimization]\\n  - Free Testosterone 3 pg/mL (listed as within normal range)\\n  - SHBG 138 nmol/L [ABNORMAL, markedly elevated]\\n- Other:\\n  - Sleep apnea risk (STOP‑BANG) = 1 (low)\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Early perimenopausal transition with relative progesterone deficiency and probable luteal insufficiency/anovulation.\\n   - Supporting data: FSH 10.5 mIU/mL consistent with early transition; estradiol 104 pg/mL with progesterone 1.48 ng/mL indicates relative progesterone deficiency and possible luteal insufficiency if draw was expected to be luteal.\\n   - Caveat: Interpretation limited by lack of documented menstrual cycle day and timing of last progesterone dose.\\n2. Low serum progesterone (1.48 ng/mL).\\n   - Clinical significance: Below therapeutic/endometrial protection levels; may explain sleep disturbance, mood changes, or other perimenopausal symptoms.\\n3. Estradiol mildly above clinic optimization target (104 pg/mL).\\n   - Clinical significance: Slightly higher than target range (60–100 pg/mL); consider small dose reduction if clinically appropriate.\\n4. Elevated SHBG (138 nmol/L) with low total testosterone (54.9 ng/dL) — reduced bioavailable androgen.\\n   - Clinical significance: High SHBG will reduce bioavailable testosterone and may contribute to low libido, low energy; common causes include oral estrogen, certain medications, thyroid preparations, or hepatic factors.\\n5. Iron handling question — low TIBC (223 µg/dL) with normal ferritin (149 ng/mL) and transferrin saturation ≈20%.\\n   - Clinical significance: Low TIBC can reflect inflammatory state or altered iron handling; current TSat is not elevated but repeat and additional testing warranted if clinically indicated.\\n6. Other routine labs within acceptable limits (CBC, LFTs, renal function, B12, folate, TSH).\\n\\nRed flags requiring immediate provider notification:\\n- Any proposed increase in testosterone dosing must be reviewed and approved by prescribing provider.\\n- If transferrin saturation >45% on repeat testing or positive family history of hemochromatosis → notify provider for HFE genetic testing / hematology referral.\\n\\nPLAN:\\n(General note: All medication changes require provider sign‑off per clinic protocol. Document menstrual cycle day and exact timing of last progesterone dose prior to repeat hormone draws.)\\n\\n1. Progesterone deficiency (Progesterone 1.48 ng/mL)\\n   - Plan: Recommend initiation or escalation of micronized progesterone pending provider approval:\\n     - Suggested dose: Micronized progesterone 100–200 mg PO nightly.\\n     - Regimen selection:\\n       - If patient has regular cycles and prefers cyclic therapy: consider days 14–28 each cycle.\\n       - If cycles are irregular or if using continuous estradiol: consider nightly continuous dosing.\\n     - Rationale: Provide endometrial protection and symptomatic relief (sleep, anxiety, PMS symptoms).\\n   - Monitoring: Recheck serum progesterone 6–8 weeks after initiation/change. For accurate interpretation, document cycle day and time of last progesterone dose; if draw intended to assess luteal function, obtain mid‑luteal sample (≈7 days before expected menses) or 4–8 hours after nightly progesterone dose if on continuous therapy.\\n\\n2. Estradiol optimization (E2 104 pg/mL)\\n   - Plan: Consider modest reduction of estradiol per provider order:\\n     - If transdermal patch: reduce by one step (eg, move to next lower patch strength).\\n     - If oral estradiol: reduce dose by ~10–25% or consider switching to transdermal route to reduce SHBG.\\n   - Monitoring: Recheck estradiol 6–8 weeks after dose change.\\n\\n3. Testosterone / androgen status (Total T 54.9 ng/dL; Free T 3 pg/mL; SHBG 138 nmol/L)\\n   - Plan:\\n     - Obtain/confirm free and bioavailable testosterone if not recently measured (free T present at 3 pg/mL). Consider ordering calculated bioavailable testosterone and repeat free T concurrently with any dose change.\\n     - Any testosterone dose increase requires provider authorization. If provider elects to increase replacement, a conservative increment (eg, increase topical testosterone by ~25% or step up to next formulation strength) is reasonable; monitor for signs of virilization.\\n   - Monitoring: Recheck total T, free/bioavailable T, and SHBG 6–8 weeks after dose adjustment. Instruct patient to report any virilizing symptoms immediately.\\n\\n4. Elevated SHBG (138 nmol/L)\\n   - Plan:\\n     - Review medication list for drivers of SHBG (oral estrogen, oral contraceptives, thyroid hormone, anticonvulsants, hepatic disease).\\n     - If patient is on oral estrogen, discuss with provider switching to transdermal estradiol to reduce SHBG and improve bioavailable testosterone.\\n   - Monitoring: Reassess SHBG 6–8 weeks after any change in estrogen route/dose.\\n\\n5. Iron studies / low TIBC\\n   - Plan:\\n     - Order transferrin saturation (if not already reported by lab), repeat TIBC, and consider CRP and/or ESR to evaluate for inflammation.\\n     - If TSat >45% or concerning family history, refer for HFE genetic testing and hematology evaluation.\\n   - Monitoring: Repeat iron indices per results; act on elevated TSat or other abnormal findings.\\n\\n6. Supplements and lifestyle\\n   - Supplement: EstroFactors™ — staff recommendation medium priority: 2 tablets twice daily (documented as recommended).\\n   - Lifestyle counseling: reinforce regular aerobic exercise, weight management, balanced diet with adequate iron if indicated, sleep hygiene measures to assist with sleep disturbance, limit alcohol and tobacco. Counsel on signs of virilization and iron overload and instructed patient to report immediately if these occur.\\n\\n7. Orders and scheduling\\n   - Provider to review and sign orders for:\\n     - Initiation/increase of micronized progesterone 100–200 mg nightly (cyclic vs continuous per cycle regularity).\\n     - Estradiol dose adjustment or switch to transdermal (if applicable).\\n     - Testosterone adjustment only with provider approval.\\n   - Lab orders to place now:\\n     - Repeat hormones (Estradiol, Progesterone with documented cycle day/last dose timing, Total and Free Testosterone, SHBG) — schedule 6–8 weeks after any medication change or sooner if indicated.\\n     - Transferrin saturation (calculated) and repeat TIBC, CRP and/or ESR now.\\n     - Consider ordering calculated bioavailable testosterone if available.\\n   - Routine recheck window if no medication changes: 3–6 months per clinic protocol.\\n\\n8. Patient communication and education\\n   - Results reviewed and discussed with patient. Explained relative progesterone deficiency and rationale for progesterone supplementation, possible small estradiol reduction, and the significance of high SHBG.\\n   - Advised patient to provide exact menstrual cycle day and time of last progesterone dose before next labs.\\n   - Reviewed signs requiring urgent contact: virilization (voice change, clitoromegaly, rapid severe acne), symptoms suggesting iron overload, or other acute symptoms.\\n   - Documented that patient verbalized understanding.\\n\\nFollow‑up:\\n- Immediate: Document cycle day / date of last menstrual period and time of last progesterone dose (within 1 week).\\n- Short term: Provider review and orders for progesterone ± estradiol adjustment and any testosterone changes (within 1–2 weeks).\\n- Recheck labs: 6–8 weeks after any hormone change (Estradiol, Progesterone timed as above, Total/Free/Bioavailable T, SHBG). If no changes, routine follow‑up labs in 3–6 months.\\n- Escalation triggers: TSat >45% or new virilization → notify provider immediately.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow‑up as above.\\n\\nDate: 03/16/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "supplements": [{"dose": "2 tablets twice daily", "name": "EstroFactors™", "caution": "Nutritional support for estrogen balance and metabolism. Contains I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Supports healthy estrogen metabolite ratios.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "EstroFactors provides targeted nutritional support for estrogen metabolism and balance with I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Best suited for women with estrogen dominance patterns, PMS, perimenopausal hormone imbalance, or those needing broader estrogen metabolism support during hormonal transitions. Differs from Estrovera in that it addresses the underlying estrogen metabolism rather than vasomotor symptom relief.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected; Mood changes reported; Age-appropriate for estrogen metabolism support", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement helps your body process and balance estrogen more effectively. It supports healthy hormone metabolism, which can help with PMS symptoms, bloating, mood changes, and hormonal balance during life transitions.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected", "Mood changes reported", "Age-appropriate for estrogen metabolism support"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\n- Your results show early signs of perimenopause with low progesterone compared with estrogen. Key hormone numbers: progesterone 1.48 ng/mL, estradiol 104 pg/mL, FSH 10.5 mIU/mL. Your sex hormone binding globulin (SHBG) is high at 138 nmol/L, and total testosterone is a bit low at 54.9 ng/dL. Other routine labs look good: hemoglobin 12.6 g/dL, hematocrit 37.9%, ferritin 149 ng/mL, TSH 1.69 mIU/L, AST 20 U/L, ALT 16 U/L, eGFR 95 mL/min.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Iron stores: ferritin 149 ng/mL (healthy, above the commonly desired >50 ng/mL).\\n- Blood counts: hemoglobin 12.6 g/dL and hematocrit 37.9% are within normal range.\\n- Thyroid: TSH 1.69 mIU/L indicates normal thyroid function.\\n- Liver and kidney tests: AST 20 U/L, ALT 16 U/L, creatinine 0.77 mg/dL, eGFR 95 mL/min are normal.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + concrete actions)\\n- Progesterone is low at 1.48 ng/mL while estradiol is 104 pg/mL. If this blood draw was taken in the luteal phase (about 7 days before your expected period), progesterone 1.48 ng/mL suggests luteal phase deficiency or anovulation. Action: repeat a mid-luteal progesterone test (draw approximately 7 days before your next expected period) to confirm. Discuss with your clinician about progesterone support options (vaginal or oral progesterone or cyclical combined therapy) if symptoms (bloating, heavy/irregular bleeding, breast tenderness, mood swings) are present.\\n- SHBG is elevated at 138 nmol/L (this reduces bioavailable testosterone/estrogen). Action: review current medications with your provider — oral estrogen or some thyroid meds can raise SHBG. If you take oral estrogen (contraceptive or HRT), ask about switching to transdermal estrogen to lower SHBG impact.\\n- Total testosterone 54.9 ng/dL is below the commonly aimed HRT range (75–125 ng/dL). Actions to improve symptoms and support testosterone naturally: prioritize 7–8 hours quality sleep nightly, add strength training 2–3 times per week, and practice stress management (daily 10–20 min breathing, yoga, or walks).\\n\\nIron/TIBC note\\n- TIBC is low at 223 ug/dL while ferritin is 149 ng/mL and serum iron 45 ug/dL. This pattern can reflect adequate iron stores or chronic inflammation; with normal AST/ALT and no anemia, action: avoid additional iron supplements unless advised, and discuss repeating iron studies in 3–6 months or testing for hereditary hemochromatosis if there is a family history.\\n\\nBone health and general prevention\\n- Perimenopause can affect bone health. Actions: weight-bearing exercise 3+ times weekly, ensure 1,000–1,200 mg calcium daily from diet/supplements, get 800–1,000 IU vitamin D/day (check level if not measured), limit alcohol, and avoid smoking. Consider bone density (DEXA) if other risk factors are present.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Repeat mid-luteal progesterone next cycle (about 7 days before expected period). Review current medications with your clinician within 4–6 weeks. If you start any hormone therapy, plan labs and symptom check at 8–12 weeks. If you have family history of iron overload or ongoing symptoms, discuss additional testing (hemochromatosis genetic test or specialist referral).\\n\\nYou’re doing well on many measures — these targeted steps can help clarify hormones and protect long-term bone and metabolic health. Please bring these results to your visit and we can make a clear plan together.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 12.6, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 37.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 318, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 104, "status": "borderline", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol above HRT optimization goal (60-100 pg/mL).", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Consider reducing estrogen dose slightly if no symptoms warrant higher levels.", "referenceRange": "60-100 pg/mL (HRT goal, >40 for bone)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1.48, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone below HRT therapeutic range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase progesterone dose. Target 8-10 ng/mL for optimal endometrial protection.", "referenceRange": "8-10 ng/mL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 10.5, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 54.9, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone below optimal HRT goal (75-125 ng/dL).", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Consider increasing testosterone dose for optimized results if symptomatic.", "referenceRange": "75-125 ng/dL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.69, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 149, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 803, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 20, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 16, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 95, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.77, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 223, "status": "borderline", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "Low TIBC - possible iron overload or chronic disease.", "recommendation": "Evaluate for hemochromatosis or chronic inflammation.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 45, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 20, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1.48, "status": "borderline", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Low Progesterone (Phase Unknown)", "interpretation": "Progesterone is 1.48 ng/mL. Without cycle phase documentation, interpretation is limited. If drawn in luteal phase, this would indicate anovulation - a perimenopause marker.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Document cycle phase for accurate interpretation. If patient is mid-cycle or luteal, this suggests anovulation. Consider symptoms: sleep disruption, anxiety, irregular cycles. PATIENT EDUCATION: We need to know where you are in your menstrual cycle to fully interpret this result. Please note when your last period started.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase; <5 ng/mL suggests anovulation if luteal"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1.48, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Relative Progesterone Deficiency", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 104 pg/mL with progesterone of only 1.48 ng/mL indicates relative progesterone deficiency. The estrogen is not being adequately opposed by progesterone, which can lead to estrogen dominance symptoms.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Consider progesterone supplementation to balance the E2:P4 ratio. Micronized progesterone 100-200 mg nightly can provide adequate opposition. If patient has symptoms (insomnia, anxiety, heavy bleeding, breast tenderness), progesterone should help. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen level is good, but your progesterone is not keeping up. This imbalance can cause sleep problems, mood changes, and heavy periods. Progesterone supplementation can help restore balance.", "referenceRange": "E2 >100 should have P4 ≥8 for adequate opposition"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1.48, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Luteal Progesterone Deficiency", "interpretation": "Progesterone of 1.48 ng/mL with estradiol of 104 pg/mL in a woman 48 years old indicates luteal progesterone deficiency. Estrogen production is adequate but progesterone is not keeping pace - a hallmark of perimenopause.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Micronized progesterone 100-200 mg nightly. Dosing can be cyclic (days 14-28) or continuous depending on cycle regularity. Progesterone provides GABAergic support for sleep and anxiety relief. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your body is making enough estrogen, but progesterone has dropped. This is very common in perimenopause and causes sleep disruption, anxiety, and mood changes. Progesterone supplementation supports your nervous system and helps restore calm, restful sleep.", "referenceRange": "P4 ≥5 ng/mL expected with E2 ≥60 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 10.5, "status": "borderline", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Early Perimenopause", "interpretation": "FSH of 10.5 mIU/mL with progesterone of 1.48 ng/mL in a 48-year-old indicates early perimenopause. The brain is starting to signal harder (rising FSH) because ovarian response is becoming unpredictable. This is the beginning of the transition.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Progesterone is foundational at this stage: micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly for sleep and nervous system support. Begin the hormone continuity discussion - frame HRT as future-forward planning, not crisis intervention. Nervous system calming messaging matters. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your body is entering the perimenopause transition. Your brain is working a little harder to communicate with your ovaries, and ovulation is becoming less consistent. This can cause sleep changes, anxiety, and cycle irregularities. Progesterone support now can smooth this transition and protect your quality of life.", "referenceRange": "FSH 8-15 with P4 <5 indicates early transition"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 138, "status": "abnormal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 138 nmol/L is elevated. High SHBG reduces bioavailable testosterone and estrogen. Common causes: oral estrogen, oral contraceptives, thyroid medication, liver conditions.", "recommendation": "Evaluate SHBG drivers. Consider ordering Free Testosterone and Bioavailable Testosterone for complete androgen assessment.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 3, "status": "normal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 3 pg/mL is within normal functional range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (1/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}], "aiRecommendations": "1) Overall clinical assessment (concise)\\n- Hormone milieu consistent with early perimenopausal transition: FSH 10.5 mIU/mL and estradiol 104 pg/mL with low progesterone 1.48 ng/mL → relative/progesterone deficiency and probable luteal insufficiency/anovulation if sample was obtained in luteal phase. Do not diagnose—interpretation limited without documented cycle phase/timing of draw.\\n- Estradiol mildly above the clinic optimization target (104 pg/mL; target 60–100 pg/mL).\\n- Total testosterone low for HRT optimization (54.9 ng/dL; target 75–125 ng/dL); SHBG markedly elevated (138 nmol/L) which will reduce bioavailable androgen.\\n- Iron studies: low TIBC (223 µg/dL) with normal ferritin and serum iron — raises possibility of chronic inflammation or iron handling abnormality (less likely frank iron deficiency).\\n- Other basic labs listed as normal (Hgb/Hct/platelets, ferritin, B12, LFTs, creatinine, eGFR, folate, STOP‑BANG).\\n\\n2) Priority actions — CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE\\n\\nCRITICAL (notify provider immediately)\\n- Any proposed increase in testosterone dosing should be reviewed/approved by the prescribing provider before change.\\n- If transferrin saturation (TSat) (iron/TIBC) returns >45% or if there is a family history of hemochromatosis, notify provider for HFE genetic testing referral and hematology consideration.\\n\\nURGENT (address within 1–2 weeks / implement per standing HRT protocols)\\n- Progesterone deficiency: initiate or increase micronized progesterone per protocol for endometrial protection and symptomatic relief (see dosing below). Document cycle phase prior to dosing changes if possible.\\n- SHBG high: review current medications and estrogen route immediately (see recommendations below); if patient is on oral estrogen, consider switching to transdermal route (discuss with provider).\\n\\nROUTINE (follow-up lab scheduling and education)\\n- Slight reduction of estradiol per protocol if patient asymptomatic at higher E2 level — implement small dose decrease and recheck labs in 6–8 weeks.\\n- Adjust testosterone per protocol if patient symptomatic and after provider sign‑off; recheck hormones 6–8 weeks after dose change.\\n\\n3) Specific medication / dose recommendations (staff-facing, require provider sign-off)\\n- Progesterone\\n  - If not already on progesterone or dose is subtherapeutic: recommend micronized progesterone 100–200 mg nightly.\\n  - Regimen options: cyclic (days 14–28) for women with regular cycles OR continuous nightly dosing for irregular cycles or continuous estrogen therapy. Aim for serum progesterone in the 8–10 ng/mL range for adequate endometrial opposition.\\n  - If already on progesterone: increase dose to within 100–200 mg nightly range (document prior dose and response).\\n  - Recheck progesterone 6–8 weeks after change with correct timing (see monitoring timing below).\\n- Estradiol\\n  - Estradiol 104 pg/mL is just above goal (60–100). Per protocol, consider a modest reduction:\\n    - If transdermal patch: reduce by one standard step (eg, 50 µg → 25 µg patch) or reduce dose ~20–50% depending on current formulation.\\n    - If oral estradiol: reduce dose by ~10–25% or consider switching to transdermal if SHBG is contributing to androgen deficiency.\\n  - Implement change per provider order; recheck estradiol in 6–8 weeks.\\n- Testosterone\\n  - Because total T is below target and SHBG is high, assess free/bioavailable T first (if not already done). If patient is symptomatic and provider approves, consider increasing testosterone replacement by a modest increment (e.g., increase current regimen by ~25–50% or step up to next available preparation strength).\\n  - Example staff instruction (provider must authorize): increase topical testosterone dose by one formulation step (or increase dose by ~25%) and re-evaluate labs/symptoms in 6–8 weeks.\\n  - Monitor closely for signs of androgen excess/virilization; discontinue or reduce dose and notify provider if these occur.\\n- SHBG management\\n  - If patient is taking oral estrogen or oral contraceptives, discuss with provider the option to switch to transdermal estradiol to lower SHBG and improve bioavailable testosterone.\\n  - Review medication list for other SHBG drivers (thyroid meds, liver disease, anti-seizure drugs).\\n\\n4) Monitoring / lab timing and follow-up\\n- Document menstrual cycle day and time of last progesterone dose for all hormone draws. If patient is cycling and progesterone intent is to assess luteal function, draw mid‑luteal (approx 7 days before expected menses or ~day 21 in a 28‑day cycle). If on nightly micronized progesterone, document time of last dose and aim to standardize draw timing (ideally 4–8 hours after dose to reflect peak; document exact time).\\n- Recheck schedule after any hormone adjustment:\\n  - Progesterone: 6–8 weeks after dose initiation/change (timing of draw as above).\\n  - Estradiol: 6–8 weeks after dose reduction.\\n  - Total testosterone / free testosterone / SHBG: 6–8 weeks after testosterone dose change; order free and bioavailable testosterone if not already available.\\n- Iron workup follow-up:\\n  - Order transferrin saturation (iron/TIBC) and repeat TIBC if not already done. Also consider CRP or ESR to assess for inflammation.\\n  - If TSat >45% or persistently low TIBC with elevated iron indices, consider HFE gene testing / hematology referral.\\n\\n5) Additional testing or referrals to consider\\n- If not already obtained: Free testosterone and bioavailable testosterone (to assess androgen availability given elevated SHBG) — order prior to or concurrently with any testosterone dose changes.\\n- Transferrin saturation (calculated from serum iron and TIBC) and repeat TIBC, CRP/ESR.\\n- HFE gene testing and hematology referral if transferrin saturation elevated or clinical suspicion of hereditary hemochromatosis.\\n- Gynecology or menopause specialist referral if:\\n  - Patient has heavy uterine bleeding, abnormal uterine bleeding, or complex contraception/HRT needs.\\n  - Persistent symptoms despite optimized HRT.\\n- Consider endocrine consult if SHBG elevation unexplained by oral estrogen or medication list or if complex thyroid/androgen interplay suspected.\\n\\n6) Patient education points for staff to cover (document in chart)\\n- Ask and document: exact cycle day/date of last menstrual period and timing of blood draw relative to last progesterone dose — critical for interpretation.\\n- Explain that progesterone is low relative to estradiol and that supplementation usually improves sleep, anxiety, and cycle symptoms; discuss possible side effects (drowsiness, breast tenderness) and timing of benefit.\\n- Explain modest estradiol dose reduction rationale (if implemented): goal is optimization, not over-suppression; reinforce monitoring plan.\\n- Discuss SHBG drivers (oral estrogen, meds) and possible benefits of switching to transdermal estrogen if appropriate.\\n- Review signs to report urgently: rapid voice changes, clitoromegaly, severe acne, or other virilizing signs after testosterone changes; symptoms of iron overload (fatigue, arthralgia, skin hyperpigmentation) if relevant.\\n\\n7) Summary of next steps and timeline (concise action plan)\\n- Immediate (within 1 week)\\n  - Document menstrual cycle day and timing of last progesterone dose in chart.\\n  - Review medication list for oral estrogen or other SHBG drivers.\\n  - Provider review required: plan for progesterone initiation/increase and any testosterone change.\\n- Short term (implement within 1–2 weeks after provider sign‑off)\\n  - Start/increase micronized progesterone to 100–200 mg nightly (cyclic vs continuous per cycle regularity) — provider order required.\\n  - If on oral estradiol, discuss switching to transdermal with provider to lower SHBG.\\n  - If asymptomatic at E2 104 pg/mL and per protocol, reduce estradiol dose one step — provider order required.\\n- Recheck labs (6–8 weeks after any hormone change)\\n  - Estradiol, progesterone (with documented timing), total and free testosterone, SHBG.\\n  - If iron workup: order transferrin saturation and repeat TIBC ± CRP/ESR now.\\n- Escalation triggers (immediate provider notification)\\n  - TSat >45% or progressive iron indices suggesting iron overload, or unexpected LFT abnormalities.\\n  - Development of virilization after testosterone change or severe new symptoms after hormone adjustments.\\n\\nNotes / caveats\\n- Interpretation limited without cycle/timing documentation—prioritize obtaining cycle day and dose/timing of last hormone administration for accurate interpretation and correct timing for repeat labs.\\n- All dose changes must be signed/authorized by the prescribing provider per clinic protocol.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "SHBG", "value": 138, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 16, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Lab pattern consistent with relative estrogen excess or impaired estrogen clearance. Even without reported symptoms, supporting liver detoxification and estrogen metabolism pathways may be beneficial.", "supportingFindings": ["Estradiol:Progesterone imbalance (E2 104, P4 1.48)", "Progesterone 1.48 ng/mL (very low)", "Mood changes reported"]}, {"name": "Iron Deficiency", "confidence": "low", "description": "Iron stores are depleted or suboptimal. Iron is essential for energy, cognitive function, hair health, and oxygen transport.", "supportingFindings": ["Serum iron 45 µg/dL (low)"]}, {"name": "Menopausal Transition", "confidence": "low", "description": "Clinical picture consistent with perimenopause or menopause. Hormonal shifts during this transition affect bone health, cardiovascular risk, mood, and quality of life.", "supportingFindings": ["Age 48 (perimenopause window)", "FSH 10.5 mIU/mL (rising, perimenopause range)", "Mood changes reported"]}]}	2026-03-16 19:06:15.996136	\N
