on (hepatic metabolic support)"]}, {"dose": "1 soft chew during times of stress", "name": "HerWellness™ Rapid Stress Relief", "caution": "Fast-acting L-Theanine and Lactium formula. Non-drowsy. Promotes calm within 1 hour. Contains milk.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "low", "rationale": "HerWellness Rapid Stress Relief provides fast-acting stress support with L-Theanine (200mg) and Lactium. Promotes calm within 1 hour without drowsiness.", "indication": "Irritability reported", "phenotypes": ["Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation"], "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This fast-acting supplement helps your body respond to stress more calmly. It promotes relaxation within about an hour and can be taken as needed without causing drowsiness.", "supportingFindings": ["Irritability reported"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 6, "apoBValue": 123, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "borderline", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 2.1150703746797577, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": true}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "generally_recommended", "indicated": true, "rationale": "ApoB ≥90 mg/dL (123 mg/dL) with risk enhancers (low HDL, elevated triglycerides, elevated ApoB). Statin discussion recommended."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": true, "severity": "very_high", "recommendation": "Triglycerides ≥500 mg/dL (540 mg/dL) - URGENT: High risk for pancreatitis. Fibrate therapy indicated. Rule out secondary causes (diabetes, alcohol, medications)."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "consider", "rationale": "Borderline lipid levels with multiple risk enhancers (low HDL, elevated triglycerides, elevated ApoB). Consider CAC to refine risk assessment.", "recommended": true}}, "recheckWindow": "6-8 weeks (iron supplementation monitoring)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\n- Some tests are normal, but there are a few important problems to address right away: very high triglycerides (540 mg/dL), low iron stores (ferritin 20.6 ng/mL), and severe vitamin D deficiency (25-OH vitamin D 10.3 ng/mL). Your hormone pattern (progesterone 0.11 ng/mL in the luteal phase) suggests this cycle was likely anovulatory, which is common in perimenopause and can explain symptoms like night sweats.\\n\\nPositive findings (what’s OK)\\n- Hemoglobin 13.5 g/dL and hematocrit 42.3% are within normal range.\\n- TSH 2.34 mIU/L shows normal thyroid function.\\n- Hemoglobin A1c 5.2% indicates normal blood sugar control.\\n- eGFR 113 mL/min and creatinine 0.66 mg/dL show healthy kidney function.\\n- hs-CRP 0.9 mg/L indicates low inflammation.\\n- 10-year ASCVD risk 2.1% and 10-year total CVD risk 3.1% (LOW risk).\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + concrete actions)\\n- Triglycerides 540 mg/dL (VERY HIGH — risk of pancreatitis): Contact your provider today or within 24–48 hours. Until seen: stop alcohol completely, cut simple carbs and sugary drinks, follow a very low-fat meal plan, and avoid crash diets. Your clinician may start prescription therapy (fibrate or high-dose EPA) and order an urgent repeat fasting lipid panel.\\n- Ferritin 20.6 ng/mL (iron deficiency without anemia): Eat iron-rich foods (red meat, poultry, beans, lentils, iron-fortified cereal). If approved by your clinician, start an oral iron supplement (take with vitamin C, avoid taking with calcium or coffee/tea). Recheck ferritin and CBC in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Vitamin D 25-OH 10.3 ng/mL (severe deficiency): Discuss starting high-dose therapy with your clinician (common approaches: prescription high-dose vitamin D or 2,000–5,000 IU daily if approved). Take with a meal containing fat. Recheck level in 8–12 weeks. Add weight-bearing exercise and aim for 1,000–1,200 mg calcium daily for bone health.\\n- Lipids and heart health: HDL 40 mg/dL (low/borderline) and ApoB 123 mg/dL with 30-year CVD risk 20.8% (higher long-term risk) — intensify lifestyle now: add 25–30 g fiber daily (oatmeal, beans, vegetables), include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly, reduce saturated fat (choose lean meats, limit butter/cheese), and aim for 30–60 minutes moderate activity most days. Statin not automatically required now; discuss with your provider given long-term risk.\\n- Hormones: Progesterone 0.11 ng/mL and estradiol 42 pg/mL suggest anovulation/luteal insufficiency. Track cycles and symptoms; discuss perimenopause management (non-hormonal or hormonal options) with your clinician. For low testosterone (total 12.7 ng/dL), prioritize 7–8 hours sleep, strength training 2–3× weekly, and stress reduction.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Contact clinic within 24–48 hours for triglycerides 540 mg/dL (urgent). Book follow-up within 1–2 weeks for treatment planning (lipid meds, iron, vitamin D). Recheck fasting lipid panel soon after any medication change; repeat ferritin/CBC and vitamin D in 8–12 weeks. If you take B12 supplements/injections, review with your clinician given B12 1245 pg/mL.\\n\\nYou’re doing well in many areas — these focused steps can significantly reduce short-term risks and improve long-term health.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 13.5, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 42.3, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 89.2, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 89.2 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 347, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 42, "status": "borderline", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Low estradiol in luteal phase.", "recommendation": "May indicate corpus luteum dysfunction. Correlate with progesterone.", "referenceRange": "50-250 pg/mL (luteal phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.11, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Low progesterone in luteal phase - luteal phase defect.", "recommendation": "May affect fertility/early pregnancy. Consider progesterone support if TTC.", "referenceRange": "5-20 ng/mL (luteal phase)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 7.59, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 12.7, "status": "borderline", "ca