atient has uterus. Goal is symptom control through stable hormone delivery. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your hot flashes and night sweats may be caused by fluctuating hormone levels rather than consistently low estrogen. A steady-release estrogen patch can help stabilize your levels and reduce symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Symptoms indicate hormonal instability despite E2 level"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 49.8, "status": "normal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 49.8 nmol/L is within normal range. SHBG regulates bioavailable sex hormones by binding testosterone and estradiol.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 2, "status": "normal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 2 pg/mL is within normal functional range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "of 6 markers positive", "value": 3, "status": "critical", "category": "Insulin Resistance Screening", "recheckTiming": "3-6 months", "interpretation": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: HIGH LIKELIHOOD (3/6 markers positive). Identified phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance, Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance. Treat according to phenotype-specific protocol.", "recommendation": "Phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance; Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance. Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose.", "referenceRange": "0-1 Low, 2 Moderate, 3+ High Likelihood"}], "aiRecommendations": "Unable to generate recommendations. Please review lab findings manually.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 13.5, "detail": "Triglycerides 540 / HDL 40 = 13.5", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5.2%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 49.8, "detail": "Low - hyperinsulinemia suppresses SHBG", "positive": true, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 13, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "123 mg/dL", "detail": "Elevated atherogenic particle count", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "0.9 mg/L", "detail": "Low inflammatory burden", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "high", "phenotypes": [{"key": "visceral_metabolic", "name": "Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Recheck A1c, lipid panel, hs-CRP, ApoB in 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL"], "pathophysiology": "Early peripheral + hepatic insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia. Glucose may still be \\"normal.\\" A1c often rises last.", "triggerCriteria": ["A1c >= 5.7%", "TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0", "Central adiposity phenotype", "ALT above optimal range"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests your body may not be responding to insulin as efficiently as it should. Insulin is the hormone that helps move sugar from your bloodstream into your cells for energy.\\n\\nWhen cells become less sensitive to insulin:\\n- Blood sugar slowly rises over time\\n- Triglycerides increase\\n- Inflammation increases\\n- Weight gain around the midsection becomes easier\\n\\nThe good news? This stage is highly reversible with targeted nutrition, strength training, sleep optimization, and - when appropriate - medication support.\\n\\nWe'll focus on improving how your body processes fuel and reducing long-term cardiometabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward nutrition (>= 25-35g/meal)", "Carbohydrate quality + timing education", "Strength training 2-3x weekly", "10-15 min post-meal walking", "Sleep optimization", "Consider: GLP-1 therapy if obesity, appetite dysregulation, A1c >= 5.7, or inflammatory phenotype", "Consider: Metformin if prediabetes or strong family history", "Recheck metabolic panel in 3-6 months"]}, {"key": "hormonal_pcos", "name": "Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Monitor SHBG, androgens, A1c every 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["SHBG low (<50)", "TG/HDL borderline-high"], "pathophysiology": "Hyperinsulinemia suppresses SHBG and increases ovarian androgen production.", "triggerCriteria": ["Low SHBG (<50; strong if <30)", "Elevated total or free testosterone", "Irregular cycles", "A1c may still be normal", "TG/HDL may be borderline-high"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests insulin may be influencing your hormone balance.\\n\\nWhen insulin levels run high:\\n- The body makes less SHBG (a protective hormone-binding protein)\\n- Androgen levels can rise\\n- Cycles may become irregular\\n\\nThis pattern is common in PCOS and other hormone imbalance states.\\n\\nImproving insulin sensitivity often helps restore hormonal balance and reduce long-term metabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward, lower refined carb approach", "Strength training", "Consider metformin if cycles irregular or A1c trending", "Consider GLP-1 if obesity phenotype present", "Monitor SHBG, androgens, A1c"]}], "positiveCount": 3, "likelihoodLabel": "HIGH LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: HIGH LIKELIHOOD (3/6 markers positive). Identified phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance, Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance. Treat according to phenotype-specific protocol.", "confirmationTests": "Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose."}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "confidence": "high", "description": "Metabolic pattern consistent with insulin resistance and/or visceral adiposity. This drives inflammation, hormonal imbalance, and cardiovascular risk.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance screening: HIGH LIKELIHOOD (3/6 markers positive)", "TG:HDL ratio 13.5 (≥3.0)", "Triglycerides 540 mg/dL (elevated)", "HDL 40 mg/dL (low)", "SHBG 49.8 nmol/L (low, associated with insulin resistance)", "ApoB 123 mg/dL (elevated)"]}, {"name": "Inflammatory Burden", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Pattern of systemic inflammation indicated by elevated inflammatory markers and/or symptomatic presentation. May benefit from anti-inflammatory nutritional support.", "supportingFindings": ["ApoB 123 mg/dL (elevated)", "Triglycerides 540 mg/dL (elevated)"]}, {"name": "Iron Deficiency", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Iron stores are depleted or suboptimal. Iron is essential for energy, cognitive function, hair health, and oxygen transport.", "supportingFindings": ["Ferritin 20.6 ng/mL (suboptimal ≤50)", "Serum iron 48 µg/dL (low)"]}, {"name": "Oxidative Stress Burden", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Multiple markers suggest increased oxidative stress and cellular burden. Antioxidant and glutathione support may help reduce oxidative damage and support cellular energy production.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance pattern (metabolic oxidative driver)", "Triglycerides 540 mg/dL (lipid peroxidation risk)"]}, {"name": "Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Pattern consistent with HPA-axis stress and cortisol dysregulation. Chronic stress depletes DHEA-S, disrupts sleep, and impairs cognitive and emotional function.", "supportingFindings": ["Irritability reported", "Low motivation reported", "Vitamin D 10.3 ng/mL (low, stress resilience impact)"]}, {"name": "Menopausal Transition", "confidence": "low", "description": "Clinical picture consistent with perimenopause or menopause. Hormonal shifts during this transition affect bone health, cardiovascular risk, mood, and quality of life.", "supportingFindings": ["Age 41 (perimenopause wind