t will help restore your levels to support bone health, immune function, and overall wellness.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 23.4 ng/mL (deficient)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 115, "apoBValue": 94, "lpaStatus": "elevated", "apoBStatus": "borderline", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 0.25479505005970404, "hasElevatedLpa": true, "statinGuidance": "Possibly consider statin therapy given risk enhancement; shared decision-making recommended.", "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "reclassified_upward", "clinicalGuidance": "Lp(a) 115 mg/dL - RISK ENHANCER (genetic risk factor). RECLASSIFIED TO BORDERLINE RISK: Risk enhancer present. Shared decision-making regarding statin therapy.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "cacRecommendation": "CAC reasonable especially with strong family history and risk enhancement present.", "hasBorderlineApoB": true}, "cacStatinRec": {"lpaWarning": "Elevated Lp(a) of 115 mg/dL is a genetic cardiovascular risk marker. This level is associated with increased ASCVD risk independent of LDL. Since Lp(a) cannot be significantly lowered, focus on aggressive LDL/ApoB reduction and lifestyle optimization.", "statinDiscussion": {"strength": "generally_recommended", "indicated": true, "rationale": "ApoB ≥90 mg/dL (94 mg/dL) with risk enhancers (prediabetes/metabolic syndrome, elevated Lp(a), elevated ApoB). Statin discussion recommended."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "strongly_recommend", "rationale": "Elevated Lp(a) of 115 mg/dL is a genetic risk marker. CAC strongly recommended to assess arterial calcium burden and inform treatment intensity.", "recommended": true}}, "recheckWindow": "3 months (glycemic monitoring)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\nYour tests show that you are in menopause (Estradiol 5 pg/mL, FSH 122 mIU/mL) and experiencing symptoms consistent with a low-estrogen state (night sweats). Cardiovascular risk is low (10-year ASCVD risk 0.3%), but you have two important findings to address: low Vitamin D (25-OH 23.4 ng/mL) and a high Lipoprotein(a) (Lp(a) 115 mg/dL), which is a risk enhancer. A1c is 5.7% (prediabetes range). Many other results are reassuring.\\n\\nPositive findings (what’s healthy right now)\\n- Hemoglobin 13.2 g/dL and hematocrit 39.7% — no anemia.\\n- HDL 139 mg/dL and triglycerides 64 mg/dL — protective lipid picture.\\n- TSH 0.96 mIU/L — thyroid function normal.\\n- Vitamin B12 520 pg/mL and folate 8.47 ng/mL — adequate.\\n- eGFR 97 mL/min and creatinine 0.76 mg/dL — normal kidney function.\\n- 10-year total CVD risk 0.4% (ASCVD 0.3%) — overall low short-term heart risk.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + actions)\\n- Vitamin D 25-OH 23.4 ng/mL (deficient/suboptimal): Start Vitamin D3 2,000 IU daily, eat fatty fish (salmon, sardines), and get 10–20 minutes sun exposure on arms/legs several times weekly. Recheck 25-OH in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Lipoprotein(a) 115 mg/dL (elevated): This raises lifetime CVD risk even though your 10-year risk is low. Discuss Lp(a) with your clinician—consider family history review and possible referral to a lipid specialist. Maintain heart-healthy lifestyle: 25–30 g fiber/day (oatmeal breakfast, beans lunch, veggies dinner), 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly, reduce saturated fat (lean meats, limit butter/cheese), and 30 minutes daily brisk walking.\\n- A1c 5.7% (prediabetes): Reduce refined carbs (swap white bread for whole grain, limit sugary drinks), include protein with each meal, and walk 10–15 minutes after meals. Recheck A1c in 3–6 months.\\n- ALT 40 U/L (mildly high): Limit alcohol to <2 drinks/week, review supplements/medications that stress the liver, and work on weight management; repeat liver tests in 3 months.\\n- Ferritin 277 ng/mL and MCV 96.8 fL: Ferritin is high—stop any iron supplements until reviewed, and repeat ferritin and iron panel in 3 months to evaluate for inflammation or iron overload (hs-CRP is low at 0.7 mg/L).\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Discuss menopausal symptom options (non-drug measures and possible hormone therapy) with your clinician within 4–12 weeks.\\n- Start Vitamin D3 2,000 IU/day now and recheck in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Implement diet/exercise steps above today; recheck A1c in 3–6 months.\\n- Repeat ALT and ferritin in ~3 months; consider lipid specialist referral for Lp(a) discussion.\\n- Consider bone density (DEXA) given menopause and low Vitamin D—discuss scheduling within the next 3–6 months.\\n\\nYou’re doing well in many areas; these targeted steps can improve symptoms, bone health, and long-term heart health. If you’d like, we can help set up the vitamin re-checks and referrals.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 13.2, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 39.7, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult femal