.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle changes. Repeat in 4-6 weeks.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "score", "value": 0.91, "status": "normal", "category": "FIB-4 Score (Liver Fibrosis)", "recheckTiming": "", "interpretation": "FIB-4 score 0.91 indicates LOW risk of advanced fibrosis (F0-F1). Negative predictive value >90%.", "recommendation": "Advanced fibrosis unlikely. Continue lifestyle modifications and routine LFT monitoring. Address underlying cause of LFT elevation.", "referenceRange": "<1.30 low risk, 1.30-2.67 indeterminate, >2.67 high risk"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 97, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.76, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 94, "status": "borderline", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Borderline elevated Apo B.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider pharmacotherapy if high ASCVD risk.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 115, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Elevated Lp(a) - RISK ENHANCER. Increases CVD risk category.", "recommendation": "Lp(a) ≥50 mg/dL is a risk enhancer - consider upgrading CVD risk category. Aggressive LDL lowering indicated. Discuss hereditary nature.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 320, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 147, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 8.47, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 0.7, "status": "normal", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Low cardiovascular inflammation risk.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 5, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Hypoestrogenic Pattern", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 5 pg/mL with FSH of 122 mIU/mL indicates hypoestrogenic perimenopause - the \\"crash phase.\\" Estrogen production has dropped significantly and the brain is signaling strongly (elevated FSH) trying to stimulate the ovaries.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Transdermal estradiol 0.025-0.05 mg is appropriate replacement therapy. Add micronized progesterone for uterine protection and sleep support. Frame this as hormone replacement, not stimulation - we are restoring what the body needs. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen has dropped to a level that can cause symptoms like hot flashes, night sweats, brain fog, and mood changes. Replacing estrogen with a patch or gel can relieve these symptoms and protect your long-term health. Progesterone is added to protect your uterus and help with sleep.", "referenceRange": "E2 <50 with FSH >10 indicates estrogen deficiency"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 5, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Hypoestrogen State", "interpretation": "Low estradiol (5 pg/mL) with symptomatic presentation: night sweats. This pattern is consistent with a low estrogen state, especially in late perimenopause or postmenopause.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Consider transdermal estradiol initiation (product dependent). Add micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly if patient has uterus. Monitor symptoms and estradiol levels. PATIENT EDUCATION: Low estrogen can cause the symptoms you're experiencing. Hormone replacement therapy (HRT) with estradiol can help relieve these symptoms and protect bone and cardiovascular health.", "referenceRange": "Provider target: 60-100 pg/mL (>40 minimum)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 122, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Menopause", "interpretation": "FSH of 122 mIU/mL with low estradiol (5 pg/mL) confirms menopausal status. Symptoms present: night sweats. Elevated FSH reflects decreased ovarian function and reduced estrogen production. This is a normal part of reproductive aging but may benefit from hormone optimization if symptomatic.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: If symptomatic, consider hormone therapy (HT): transdermal estradiol + micronized progesterone (if uterus intact). Discuss benefits/risks of HT including cardiovascular, bone, and quality of life considerations. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your lab results confirm you are in menopause. Hormone therapy can help manage symptoms like hot flashes, sleep problems, and vaginal dryness while also protecting bone health.", "referenceRange": "FSH <25 mIU/mL premenopausal; ≥25 suggests transition; ≥40 menopausal"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 31.7, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone Pattern D: Adequate Androgens, Persistent Symptoms", "interpretation": "Adequate Androgens pattern identified (Total T: 31.7 ng/dL (within range); Free T: 5 pg/mL; SHBG: 46.4 nmol/L). Total, free, and bioavailable testosterone are within normal ranges and SHBG is not extreme. If the patient reports fatigue, low mood, sleep disruption, or low libido, these symptoms are likely driven by non-androgenic factors. Findings to evaluate: Low Vitamin D (23.4 ng/mL) — contributes to fatigue and mood changes. Estrogen/Progesterone balance should be assessed (E2: 5 pg/mL, P4: 0.19 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Do not reflexively add testosterone when levels are adequate — look deeper. Evaluate iron status (ferritin target >50 for symptom resolution), thyroid function (optimize TSH), and sleep quality (consider sleep apnea screening with STOP-BANG). Assess estrogen and progesterone balance — E2/P4 imbalance is a common driver of persistent symptoms. Address CNS and psychosocial drivers: chronic stress, HPA axis dysregulation, mood disorders. Consider DHEA-S if not already checked. Support with lifestyle optimization: sleep hygiene, stress management, nutrition. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your testosterone levels are actually in a healthy range, which is good news. When symptoms persist despite normal testosterone, it usually means something else is contributing. Your provider will look at other factors like iron levels, thyroid function, sleep quality, and hormone balance to find what's driving your symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Total T 15-70 ng/dL, Free T 0.5-5.0 pg/mL (all within range)"}, {"unit": "of 6 markers positive", "value": 4, "status": "critical", "category": "Insulin Resistance Screening", "recheckTiming": "3-6 months", "interpretation": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: HIGH LIKELIHOOD (4/6 markers positive). Identified phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance, Hepatic (Liver-Driven) Insulin Resistance, Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance. Treat according to phenotype-specific protocol.", "recommendation": "Phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance; Hepatic (Liver-Driven) Insulin Resistance; Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance. Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose.", "referenceRange": "0-1 Low, 2 Moderate, 3+ High Likelihood"}], "aiRecommendations": "Unable to generate recommendations. Please review lab findings manually.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 0.46, "detail": "Triglycerides 64 / HDL 139 = 0.46", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5.7%", "detail": "Prediabetic range", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 46.4, "detail": "Low - hyperinsulinemia suppresses SHBG", "positive": true, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 40, "detail": "Above optimal range - may indicate hepatic insulin resistance", "positive": true, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "94 mg/dL", "detail": "Elevated atherogenic particle count", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "0.7 mg/L", "detail": "Low inflammatory burden", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "high", "phenotypes": [{"key": "visceral_metabolic", "name": "Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Recheck A1c, lipid panel, hs-CRP, ApoB in 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["A1c >= 5.7%", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "ALT above optimal range"], "pathophysiology": "Early peripheral + hepatic insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia. Glucose may still be \\"normal.\\" A1c often rises last.", "triggerCriteria": ["A1c >= 5.7%", "TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0", "Central adiposity phenotype", "ALT above optimal range"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests your body may not be responding to insulin as efficiently as it should. Insulin is the hormone that helps move sugar from your bloodstream into your cells for energy.\\n\\nWhen cells become less sensitive to insulin:\\n- Blood sugar slowly rises over time\\n- Triglycerides increase\\n- Inflammation increases\\n- Weight gain around the midsection becomes easier\\n\\nThe good news? This stage is highly reversible with targeted nutrition, strength training, sleep optimization, and - when appropriate - medication support.\\n\\nWe'll focus on improving how your body processes fuel and reducing long-term cardiometabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward nutrition (>= 25-35g/meal)", "Carbohydrate quality + timing education", "Strength training 2-3x weekly", "10-15 min post-meal walking", "Sleep optimization", "Consider: GLP-1 therapy if obesity, appetite dysregulation, A1c >= 5.7, or inflammatory phenotype", "Consider: Metformin if prediabetes or strong family history", "Recheck metabolic panel in 3-6 months"]}, {"key": "hepatic", "name": "Hepatic (Liver-Driven) Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Monitor ALT, TG, ApoB, A1c every 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["ALT above optimal (>25 U/L)", "ApoB elevated", "A1c mildly elevated"], "pathophysiology": "Hepatic insulin resistance leads to increased hepatic glucose output + VLDL overproduction. Often early MASLD (metabolic-associated steatotic liver disease) physiology.", "triggerCriteria": ["ALT above optimal (>= 25 U/L)", "TG elevated or TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB elevated", "A1c may be normal or mildly elevated"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour labs suggest your liver may be under metabolic strain. The liver plays a major role in managing blood sugar and fat metabolism.\\n\\nWhen the liver becomes less responsive to insulin:\\n- Triglycerides rise\\n- Liver enzymes increase\\n- Fat can accumulate in the liver over time\\n\\nThis is common and often related to nutrition, stress, sleep, and weight distribution.\\n\\nThe encouraging part: liver-related metabolic changes are very responsive to targeted lifestyle changes and, if needed, medication support.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Weight reduction goal 5-10% if overweight", "Eliminate sugary beverages", "Alcohol reduction", "Protein-first nutrition", "Consider GLP-1 if weight loss appropriate", "Omega-3 if TG elevated"]}, {"key": "hormonal_pcos", "name": "Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Monitor SHBG, androgens, A1c every 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["SHBG low (<50)", "A1c elevated"], "pathophysiology": "Hyperinsulinemia suppresses SHBG and increases ovarian androgen production.", "triggerCriteria": ["Low SHBG (<50; strong if <30)", "Elevated total or free testosterone", "Irregular cycles", "A1c may still be normal", "TG/HDL may be borderline-high"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests insulin may be influencing your hormone balance.\\n\\nWhen insulin levels run high:\\n- The body makes less SHBG (a protective hormone-binding protein)\\n- Androgen levels can rise\\n- Cycles may become irregular\\n\\nThis pattern is common in PCOS and other hormone imbalance states.\\n\\nImproving insulin sensitivity often helps restore hormonal balance and reduce long-term metabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward, lower refined carb approach", "Strength training", "Consider metformin if cycles irregular or A1c trending", "Consider GLP-1 if obesity phenotype present", "Monitor SHBG, androgens, A1c"]}], "positiveCount": 4, "likelihoodLabel": "HIGH LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: HIGH LIKELIHOOD (4/6 markers positive). Identified phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance, Hepatic (Liver-Driven) Insulin Resistance, Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance. Treat according to phenotype-specific protocol.", "confirmationTests": "Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose."}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "confidence": "high", "description": "Metabolic pattern consistent with insulin resistance and/or visceral adiposity. This drives inflammation, hormonal imbalance, and cardiovascular risk.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance screening: HIGH LIKELIHOOD (4/6 markers positive)", "A1c 5.7% (prediabetic range)", "SHBG 46.4 nmol/L (low, associated with insulin resistance)", "ApoB 94 mg/dL (elevated)", "Unexplained weight gain / central adiposity reported", "ALT 40 U/L (above optimal for women >25)"]}, {"name": "Menopausal Transition", "confidence": "high", "description": "Clinical picture consistent with perimenopause or menopause. Hormonal shifts during this transition affect bone health, cardiovascular risk, mood, and quality of life.", "supportingFindings": ["Postmenopausal status", "FSH 122 mIU/mL (elevated, menopausal range)", "Estradiol 5 pg/mL (declining)", "Night sweats reported", "Mood changes reported"]}, {"name": "Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Lab pattern consistent with relative estrogen excess or impaired estrogen clearance. Even without reported symptoms, supporting liver detoxification and estrogen metabolism pathways may be beneficial.", "supportingFindings": ["Borderline liver markers (ALT 40, AST 29) suggesting possible impaired hepatic clearance", "ALT 40 U/L (elevated, may impair estrogen metabolism)", "Mood changes reported"]}, {"name": "Oxidative Stress Burden", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Multiple markers suggest increased oxidative stress and cellular burden. Antioxidant and glutathione support may help reduce oxidative damage and support cellular energy production.", "supportingFindings": ["ALT 40 U/L (hepatic stress marker)", "AST 29 U/L (cellular stress marker)", "Insulin resistance pattern (metabolic oxidative driver)", "A1c 5.7% (glycemic stress)", "Age 48 (increased oxidative stress risk)"]}]}	2026-03-18 17:40:23.089695	\N
