paValue": 132, "apoBValue": 98, "lpaStatus": "elevated", "apoBStatus": "borderline", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 1.9797779156748418, "hasElevatedLpa": true, "statinGuidance": "Possibly consider statin therapy given risk enhancement; shared decision-making recommended.", "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "reclassified_upward", "clinicalGuidance": "Lp(a) 132 mg/dL - RISK ENHANCER (genetic risk factor). RECLASSIFIED TO BORDERLINE RISK: Risk enhancer present. Shared decision-making regarding statin therapy.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "cacRecommendation": "CAC reasonable especially with strong family history and risk enhancement present.", "hasBorderlineApoB": true}, "cacStatinRec": {"lpaWarning": "Elevated Lp(a) of 132 mg/dL is a genetic cardiovascular risk marker. This level is associated with increased ASCVD risk independent of LDL. Since Lp(a) cannot be significantly lowered, focus on aggressive LDL/ApoB reduction and lifestyle optimization.", "statinDiscussion": {"strength": "generally_recommended", "indicated": true, "rationale": "ApoB ≥90 mg/dL (98 mg/dL) with risk enhancers (elevated hs-CRP, elevated Lp(a), elevated ApoB). Statin discussion recommended."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "strongly_recommend", "rationale": "Elevated Lp(a) of 132 mg/dL is a genetic risk marker. CAC strongly recommended to assess arterial calcium burden and inform treatment intensity.", "recommended": true}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\n- You have several important findings to address: severe vitamin D deficiency (25-OH vitamin D 13.8 ng/mL), a high sleep apnea risk (STOP-BANG 5/8), very high inflammation (hs-CRP 41 mg/L), and an elevated Lipoprotein(a) of 132 mg/dL. Your 10-year ASCVD risk is low at 1.98% but your 30-year CVD risk is 19.4%, so focused lifestyle changes and a few follow-ups are appropriate. You are menopausal based on FSH 75.7 mIU/mL and estradiol (E2) 17 pg/mL — this affects bone and symptom management.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Hemoglobin 15.2 g/dL, hematocrit 46.2% (slightly high), MCV 84.8 fL, platelets 357 K/uL.\\n- Ferritin 84.3 ng/mL (good iron stores).\\n- Hemoglobin A1c 5.2% (normal blood sugar).\\n- Lipids: LDL 120 mg/dL, HDL 52 mg/dL, triglycerides 91 mg/dL.\\n- Kidney and liver: eGFR 94 mL/min, creatinine 0.79 mg/dL, AST 25 U/L, ALT 21 U/L.\\n- Testosterone total 22.5 ng/dL (within female range).\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + actions)\\n- Vitamin D 25-OH = 13.8 ng/mL (severe deficiency). Start vitamin D3 therapy — discuss either prescription 50,000 IU once weekly for 8–12 weeks or vitamin D3 2,000–5,000 IU daily, then recheck level in 8–12 weeks. Increase dietary calcium and weight-bearing exercise (walks, stairs, resistance training) to support bone health; consider bone density (DEXA) discussion given menopausal status (FSH 75.7 mIU/mL, E2 17 pg/mL).\\n- hs-CRP = 41 mg/L (markedly elevated). Contact clinic within 1 week for urgent evaluation — this may reflect infection or inflammation. Recheck after any acute illness; if persistently high, work-up for sources of inflammation.\\n- STOP-BANG = 5 (high risk). Arrange a sleep medicine referral for home sleep apnea testing or polysomnography. In the meantime: avoid alcohol and sedatives before bed, sleep on your side, and aim for weight loss if applicable (target 5–10% weight reduction to help OSA).\\n- Lipoprotein(a) = 132 mg/dL (elevated). Discuss with your clinician/cardiologist — Lp(a) raises lifetime CVD risk and may change prevention strategies even with LDL 120 mg/dL.\\n- Vitamin B12 = 2000 pg/mL (high). Review supplements; stop high-dose B12 if unnecessary and recheck in 3 months.\\n- TSH = 3.95 mIU/L (upper-normal). If you have symptoms (fatigue, cold intolerance), recheck TSH with free T4 in 6–8 weeks.\\n\\nCardiovascular prevention (you): 10-year ASCVD risk 1.98% (low), 30-year CVD 19.4% (notable). Continue heart-healthy habits: aim for 150 minutes/week moderate aerobic activity (brisk walking), follow a Mediterranean-style diet (vegetables, whole grains, olive oil, nuts, fatty fish 2–3x weekly), limit processed foods and added sugars, and add 25–30 g fiber daily (oatmeal, beans, vegetables). These reduce long-term risk and help with Lp(a) impact.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Contact clinic within 1 week for hs-CRP evaluation.\\n- Start vitamin D per plan and recheck 8–12 weeks.\\n- Sleep medicine referral now for OSA testing.\\n- Review supplements (B12) now and recheck in 3 months.\\n- Consider cardiology discussion for Lp(a) and long-term prevention; consider bone density testing within 3 months given menopause and low vitamin D.\\n\\nYou’re doing well in many areas; these focused steps can improve energy, bone, sleep, and heart health. Please book follow-up to start these plans — we’re here to help.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 15.2, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 46.2, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Slightly elevated hematocrit - may indicate dehydration.", "recommendation": "Encourage hydration. Repeat if persistent.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 84.8, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 84.8 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 357, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 17, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol appropriate for postmenopausal status.", "recommendation": "Consider HRT if symptomatic for vasomotor symptoms, bone health, or quality of life.", "referenceRange": "<20 pg/mL (postmenopausal without HRT)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.26, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone appropriate for postmenopausal status.", "recommendation": "Expected finding.", "referenceRange": "<1 ng/mL (postmenopausal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 75.7, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "Elevated FSH consistent with menopause.", "recommendation": "Expected finding for postmenopausal women.", "referenceRange": ">25 mIU/mL (postmenopausal)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 22.5, "status": "normal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone within normal female range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 3.95, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 84.3, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 13.8, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Severe Vitamin D deficiency (≤20 ng/mL). Associated with fatigue, bone loss, and immune dysfunction.", "recommendation": "High-dose repletion: Metagenics D3 10,000 + K daily for 8-12 weeks. Recheck levels after repletion.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 2000, "status": "borderline", "cate