rs. Patient is 20 years old. Use clinical judgment for risk assessment.", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): N/A", "recommendation": "Age outside validated range - use clinical judgment", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: N/A", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 20, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 16, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 88, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.96, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 66, "status": "normal", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Apo B at optimal level.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 60, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Elevated Lp(a) - RISK ENHANCER. Increases CVD risk category.", "recommendation": "Lp(a) ≥50 mg/dL is a risk enhancer - consider upgrading CVD risk category. Aggressive LDL lowering indicated. Discuss hereditary nature.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 333, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 92, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 4.41, "status": "borderline", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Low-normal folate.", "recommendation": "Consider supplementation, especially if planning pregnancy.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 3.6, "status": "abnormal", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Elevated hs-CRP - increased cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Address cardiovascular risk factors. Consider repeat testing.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 25.4, "status": "normal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 25.4 nmol/L is within normal range. SHBG regulates bioavailable sex hormones by binding testosterone and estradiol.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 17, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 17 pg/mL is elevated. May cause androgenic symptoms (acne, hirsutism, hair thinning).", "recommendation": "Evaluate for PCOS, exogenous testosterone, or low SHBG driving elevated free T.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "of 6 markers positive", "value": 2, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Insulin Resistance Screening", "recheckTiming": "3-6 months", "interpretation": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive). This combination points toward hyperinsulinemia physiology. Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR).", "recommendation": "Phenotype(s): Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance. Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose.", "referenceRange": "0-1 Low, 2 Moderate, 3+ High Likelihood"}], "aiRecommendations": "Clinical purpose: staff-facing recommendations for provider review. Organized by priority with specific actions, doses, timelines, and when to notify the physician.\\n\\n1) Overall clinical assessment (concise)\\n- Key abnormalities: elevated total testosterone 86.4 ng/dL and free testosterone 17 pg/mL (both above female reference ranges), vitamin D deficiency 20.9 ng/mL, ferritin low-normal 37 ng/mL, elevated Lp(a) 60 mg/dL, hs-CRP 3.6 mg/L, low HDL 43 mg/dL, borderline folate 4.41 ng/mL, hematocrit 45.4% (borderline high), vitamin B12 high 902 pg/mL.\\n- Clinical concern priorities: hyperandrogenism (endogenous vs exogenous source) and cardiovascular risk enhancers (Lp(a) and hs-CRP). Iron and vitamin D insufficiency are addressable and may affect symptoms/long-term health.\\n\\n2) CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE prioritized actions\\n\\nCRITICAL (notify provider immediately)\\n- Elevated testosterone (total 86.4 ng/dL; free 17 pg/mL)\\n  - Notify provider now if patient reports virilization (voice changes, clitoromegaly, rapid/unusual hair growth), sudden worsening of acne, or rapid symptom onset.\\n  - If patient is currently using any exogenous androgen/testosterone (topical, intramuscular, pellets, compounded gels): instruct patient to HOLD further doses until provider review. Document route/dose/frequency and time of last dose for provider.\\n- Lipoprotein(a) 60 mg/dL and hs-CRP 3.6 mg/L\\n  - Notify provider for consideration of CVD risk reclassification and early specialist referral (lipid clinic/cardiology) if personal/family premature ASCVD.\\n\\nURGENT (action within 1–2 weeks; provider review)\\n- Vitamin D deficiency 20.9 ng/mL\\n  - Start repletion per clinic protocol: Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + vitamin K daily. Target 25-OH ≥60 ng/mL. Recheck level in 8–12 weeks.\\n  - Document supplementation history and calcium intake.\\n- Ferritin 37 ng/mL (iron insufficiency)\\n  - If symptomatic (fatigue, hair loss, RLS, exercise intolerance) or pregnancy/fertility planning: start iron per provider protocol (Metagenics Hemagenics as per clinic protocol). If asymptomatic, discuss benefits of empiric supplementation vs dietary measures.\\n  - Recheck ferritin in 8–12 weeks after starting supplementation.\\n- Free and total testosterone elevated — diagnostic evaluation:\\n  - If patient is not on exogenous testosterone: order targeted endocrine workup (see detailed testing section). Schedule in early follicular phase (cycle days 3–7) when possible for reproducible results. Consider pelvic ultrasound if PCOS suspected.\\n  - If on testosterone therapy: follow the “hold/reduce” guidance above and recheck levels (see monitoring).\\n\\nROUTINE (address within 4–12 weeks)\\n- HDL low (43 mg/dL): implement lifestyle measures (aerobic exercise, weight management, smoking cessation). Consider pharmacologic options only if indicated after risk reassessment.\\n- Folate low-normal (4.41 ng/mL): start folic acid 400–800 mcg daily if pregnancy planned or recommend standard multivitamin/prenatal. Recheck if clinically indicated.\\n- hs-CRP 3.6 mg/L: repeat after lifestyle interventions or treatment of any acute inflammation in 6–12 weeks to confirm persistent elevation before escalating therapy.\\n- Hematocrit 45.4%: encourage hydration, repeat CBC at next lab draw; notify provider if it increases or patient has polycythemia symptoms.\\n\\n3) Testosterone-specific recommendations (dose/action guidance for staff)\\n- If patient is receiving exogenous testosterone (any formulation)\\n  - Immediate action: HOLD next dose(s) and notify provider.\\n  - Suggested interim dosing plan for provider consideration:\\n    - For topical/gel/low-dose formulations: consider reducing dose by 50% or stopping temporarily pending re-evaluation and repeat levels in 4 weeks.\\n    - For long-acting IM injections or pellets: inform provider—levels may remain elevated for weeks; plan for monitoring and end-organ symptom assessment.\\n  - Monitoring after change: recheck total and free testosterone (morning draw) 4 weeks after stopping/reducing exogenous therapy. If therapy was stopped and levels normalize, discuss re-initiation at lower dose only after provider decision.\\n- If patient is NOT on exogenous testosterone\\n  - Do NOT initiate testosterone. Proceed with diagnostic evaluation for endogenous causes prior to any therapy.\\n  - Consider anti-androgen therapy only after provider-endocrine assessment and confirmation of source.\\n\\n4) Lifestyle and patient-education points for staff to cover (to be delivered by clinician/staff)\\n- Hydration: encourage increased oral fluids; repeat hematocrit with next labs.\\n- Vitamin D: start prescribed D3 5,000 IU + K daily; advise on safe sunlight exposure and dietary sources.\\n- Iron: if started on iron supplement per protocol, discuss GI side effects, take with vitamin C and between meals or per product directions; avoid concurrent calcium within 2 hours of iron.\\n- Cardiovascular risk: explain that elevated Lp(a) is often hereditary; emphasize smoking cessation, aerobic exercise (≥150 min/wk moderate), weight management, blood pressure control, and LDL optimization.\\n- Reproductive planning: if planning pregnancy, start folate 400–800 mcg/day and correct iron/vitamin D as above; avoid exogenous testosterone while attempting conception.\\n- Medication/supplement review: confirm and document all OTC supplements (especially B12, D3, and any testosterone products).\\n\\n5) Additional testing and referrals to consider\\n- Immediate/additional labs (timing in early follicular phase if patient is cycling):\\n  - Repeat total and free testosterone (morning fasting sample), SHBG (already normal per results), DHEA-S, 17-hydroxyprogesterone (AM), morning cortisol if clinical suspicion of adrenal source.\\n  - Pelvic ultrasound (transvaginal) if menstrual irregularity or clinical suspicion for PCOS.\\n  - If very rapid virilization or markedly higher testosterone (>150–200 ng/dL) or adrenal enzyme abnormalities: consider abdominal/pelvic imaging (CT/MRI) for androgen-secreting tumor—notify provider immediately.\\n- Cardiology/lipid clinic:\\n  - Refer for lipid specialist or cardiology consult if family or personal history of premature ASCVD, or if provider elects aggressive LDL lowering based on Lp(a) and hs-CRP.\\n  - Consider genetic counseling if strong family history of elevated Lp(a) or premature CVD.\\n- Endocrinology referral:\\n  - If endogenous hyperandrogenism confirmed or if diagnostic workup is unclear/abnormal, or if virilization present or resistant to initial management.\\n\\n6) Monitoring, re-check timeline, and who to notify\\n- Immediate (same day–48 hours)\\n  - Notify provider about elevated testosterone (86.4 ng/dL and free 17 pg/mL), Lp(a) 60 mg/dL, hs-CRP 3.6 mg/L, vitamin D deficiency.\\n  - If on exogenous testosterone: instruct patient to hold doses until provider direction; document and communicate to provider.\\n- Short-term (2–4 weeks)\\n  - If exogenous testosterone held/reduced: recheck total and free testosterone in 4 weeks (morning draw) and assess symptoms.\\n  - If starting iron for symptomatic ferritin 37: clinical follow-up and ferritin recheck in 8–12 weeks (but document start and schedule).\\n- Intermediate (6–12 weeks)\\n  - Vitamin D recheck at 8–12 weeks after starting D3 5,000 IU.\\n  - Ferritin recheck 8–12 weeks after starting iron.\\n  - Repeat hs-CRP in 6–12 weeks after addressing reversible causes/lifestyle changes.\\n  - If folate supplementation started for pregnancy planning, recheck per provider discretion.\\n- Routine (3–6 months)\\n  - Lipid panel and cardiovascular risk reassessment if lipid-lowering therapy is started or adjusted.\\n  - If diagnostic workup for hyperandrogenism is negative and symptoms persist, schedule endocrinology referral.\\n\\n7) Documentation and provider notification requirements\\n- Document: current medications/supplements (including topical/compounded testosterone gels), symptom inventory (hirsutism, acne, menstrual irregularity, voice changes), last menstrual period and cycle phase, pregnancy status/plans, family history of premature CVD.\\n- Mandatory notification to provider: elevated testosterone (both values), Lp(a) ≥50, hs-CRP >3, vitamin D <30, ferritin <50 with symptoms, and any report of virilization.\\n- Escalate to provider/emergency referral for rapid virilization or testosterone levels suggesting tumor (provider judgment; immediate imaging referral if concerned).\\n\\nConcise next-step summary for scheduling and orders\\n- Today:\\n  - Notify provider of testosterone, Lp(a), hs-CRP, vitamin D, ferritin results.\\n  - If on exogenous testosterone: instruct patient to hold medication pending provider review.\\n  - Start vitamin D3 5,000 IU + K daily per protocol; document start.\\n  - Begin folate 400–800 mcg if pregnancy planning.\\n- Orders to place per provider:\\n  - Repeat total and free testosterone (morning, fasting) — schedule in morning; if cycling, order for cycle day 3–7.\\n  - DHEA-S, 17-OH progesterone (AM), morning cortisol if indicated.\\n  - Pelvic ultrasound if PCOS suspected.\\n  - Ferritin recheck in 8–12 weeks if iron started.\\n  - Vitamin D 25-OH recheck in 8–12 weeks.\\n  - Repeat hs-CRP in 6–12 weeks.\\n  - Consider referral to lipid clinic/cardiology and/or endocrinology based on provider review.\\n\\nNote: Do not initiate androgen therapy. All medication starts/stops and imaging referrals require provider sign-off.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 1.65, "detail": "Triglycerides 71 / HDL 43 = 1.65", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "4.9%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 25.4, "detail": "Very low - strong IR signal", "positive": true, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 16, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "66 mg/dL", "detail": "Normal atherogenic particle count", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "3.6 mg/L", "detail": "Elevated systemic inflammation", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "moderate", "phenotypes": [{"key": "hormonal_pcos", "name": "Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Monitor SHBG, androgens, A1c every 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["SHBG very low (<30)", "Elevated total testosterone", "Elevated free testosterone"], "pathophysiology": "Hyperinsulinemia suppresses SHBG and increases ovarian androgen production.", "triggerCriteria": ["Low SHBG (<50; strong if <30)", "Elevated total or free testosterone", "Irregular cycles", "A1c may still be normal", "TG/HDL may be borderline-high"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests insulin may be influencing your hormone balance.\\n\\nWhen insulin levels run high:\\n- The body makes less SHBG (a protective hormone-binding protein)\\n- Androgen levels can rise\\n- Cycles may become irregular\\n\\nThis pattern is common in PCOS and other hormone imbalance states.\\n\\nImproving insulin sensitivity often helps restore hormonal balance and reduce long-term metabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward, lower refined carb approach", "Strength training", "Consider metformin if cycles irregular or A1c trending", "Consider GLP-1 if obesity phenotype present", "Monitor SHBG, androgens, A1c"]}], "positiveCount": 2, "likelihoodLabel": "MODERATE LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive). This combination points toward hyperinsulinemia physiology. Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR).", "confirmationTests": "Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose."}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Inflammatory Burden", "confidence": "high", "description": "Pattern of systemic inflammation indicated by elevated inflammatory markers and/or symptomatic presentation. May benefit from anti-inflammatory nutritional support.", "supportingFindings": ["hs-CRP 3.6 mg/L (elevated >3.0)", "WBC 13.5 K/uL (elevated)"]}, {"name": "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Metabolic pattern consistent with insulin resistance and/or visceral adiposity. This drives inflammation, hormonal imbalance, and cardiovascular risk.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance screening: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive)", "HDL 43 mg/dL (low)", "SHBG 25.4 nmol/L (low, associated with insulin resistance)", "hs-CRP 3.6 mg/L (metabolic inflammation)"]}, {"name": "Oxidative Stress Burden", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Multiple markers suggest increased oxidative stress and cellular burden. Antioxidant and glutathione support may help reduce oxidative damage and support cellular energy production.", "supportingFindings": ["hs-CRP 3.6 mg/L (elevated, systemic inflammation)", "Insulin resistance pattern (metabolic oxidative driver)"]}, {"name": "Iron Deficiency", "confidence": "low", "description": "Iron stores are depleted or suboptimal. Iron is essential for energy, cognitive function, hair health, and oxygen transport.", "supportingFindings": ["Ferritin 37 ng/mL (suboptimal ≤50)"]}]}	2026-03-19 19:14:45.873606	\N
55	Leigh Wiberly	female	2025-11-01 00:00:00	{"a1c": 5.1, "alt": 23, "ast": 21, "bun": 15, "co2": 22, "fsh": 3.2, "hdl": 62, "ldl": 183, "mcv": 102.2, "rbc": 4.48, "tsh": 2.98, "wbc": 8.3, "acne": false, "egfr": 113, "onHRT": false, "freeT3": 2.99, "sodium": 136, "albumin": 4.7, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 9.6, "glucose": 81, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "chloride": 102, "ferritin": 93.6, "hairLoss": false, "vitaminD": 32.7, "bilirubin": 0.4, "estradiol": 97, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": true, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 264, "potassium": 3.9, "creatinine": 0.69, "hematocrit": 45.8, "hemoglobin": 15.1, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "vitaminB12": 779, "weightGain": true, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2025-11-01", "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Leigh Wiberly", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 39, "bmi": 34, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 130, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 5.7, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 38, "totalProtein": 7.3, "lowMotivation": true, "triglycerides": 89, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "totalCholesterol": 263}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History of present illness: Patient Leigh Wiberly (39 y/o F) presents for review of recent laboratory results and hormone optimization. Patient reports fatigue and concern about low energy and libido (patient attributes symptoms to possible hormonal causes). Patient expresses concern about elevated LDL and asks about need for medication. No report of overt bleeding, syncope, or focal neurologic symptoms. No acute chest pain, shortness of breath, fever, or infection symptoms reported at this time.\\n- Menstrual/reproductive history: 39-year-old woman, presumed premenopausal. Cycle day/time of blood draw not documented in lab report — recommend documentation for future hormone interpretation.\\n- Social/medication history relevant to labs: Alcohol use and current medication list not provided in lab data; counsel given to limit/abstain from alcohol until macrocytosis evaluated. No current testosterone therapy indicated.\\n- Review of systems (focused): Positive for fatigue, low energy, low libido. Denies significant weight loss/gain, easy bruising, bleeding, palpitations, or syncope.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vital signs: Not provided in lab data (BMI, BP not available).\\n- Labs (03/26/2026) — grouped and flagged:\\n  - CBC:\\n    - Hemoglobin: 15.1 g/dL (normal)\\n    - Hematocrit: 45.8 % (borderline high)\\n    - MCV: 102.2 fL (ABNORMAL — macrocytosis)\\n    - Platelets: 264 K/uL (normal)\\n  - Reticulocyte count: not performed — order recommended\\n  - Peripheral smear: not performed — order recommended\\n  - Iron/Ferritin:\\n    - Ferritin: 93.6 ng/mL (normal)\\n  - Metabolic / Renal / Hepatic:\\n    - Creatinine: 0.69 mg/dL (normal)\\n    - eGFR: 113 mL/min (normal)\\n    - AST: 21 U/L (normal)\\n    - ALT: 23 U/L (normal)\\n  - Lipid panel:\\n    - LDL Cholesterol: 183 mg/dL (ABNORMAL — high)\\n    - HDL Cholesterol: 62 mg/dL (normal)\\n    - Triglycerides: 89 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - Glucose metabolism:\\n    - Hemoglobin A1c: 5.1 % (normal)\\n  - Thyroid:\\n    - TSH: 2.98 mIU/L (normal)\\n  - Hormones:\\n    - Estradiol (E2): 97 pg/mL (normal)\\n    - Progesterone (P4): 5.7 ng/mL (normal)\\n    - FSH: 3.2 mIU/mL (normal)\\n    - Testosterone, total: 38 ng/dL (normal) — Pattern D: Adequate androgens; persistent symptoms likely non-androgenic\\n  - Vitamins / other:\\n    - Vitamin B12: 779 pg/mL (normal)\\n    - Vitamin D (25‑OH): 32.7 ng/mL (BORDERLINE — insufficient per clinic optimization goal)\\n  - Calculated risk scores (AHA PREVENT 2023 / clinic tools):\\n    - 10-year Total CVD Risk: 1.0% (low)\\n    - 10-year ASCVD Risk: 0.8% (low)\\n    - 10-year Heart Failure Risk: 0.4%\\n    - 30-year CVD Risk: 7.8%\\n    - STOP‑BANG Sleep Apnea Risk: 0 points\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Macrocytosis — MCV 102.2 fL (ABNORMAL). Differential: B12 deficiency (less likely given B12 779 pg/mL), folate deficiency, functional B12 deficiency (consider MMA), alcohol use, medication effect (e.g., methotrexate, hydroxyurea, anticonvulsants, zidovudine), hypothyroidism (TSH currently normal), reticulocytosis, or bone marrow process. Red flag: MCV >100 fL — requires expedited evaluation and repeat testing.\\n2. Elevated LDL cholesterol — LDL 183 mg/dL (ABNORMAL). Despite low calculated 10-year ASCVD risk, LDL this high increases lifetime risk and warrants provider discussion regarding secondary causes, lifestyle intervention, and consideration of pharmacotherapy (shared decision-making). Red flag for provider review.\\n3. Vitamin D insufficiency — 25‑OH D 32.7 ng/mL (BORDERLINE). Suboptimal for clinic hormone optimization target (clinic target ≥60 ng/mL). Recommendation: initiate supplementation.\\n4. Borderline/high hematocrit — Hct 45.8% (BORDERLINE). May reflect mild hemoconcentration/dehydration; re-evaluate after hydration and repeat CBC.\\n5. Androgen status — Total testosterone 38 ng/dL (normal). Pattern D (adequate androgens). Patient’s fatigue/low libido likely from non-androgenic causes (sleep, mood, thyroid, iron status, estrogen/progesterone balance). Do NOT initiate testosterone based on current labs.\\n6. Other labs reassuring — Hemoglobin 15.1 g/dL, platelets 264 K/uL, ferritin 93.6 ng/mL, B12 779 pg/mL, HbA1c 5.1%, AST/ALT normal, creatinine/eGFR normal.\\n\\nPLAN:\\n1. Macrocytosis (MCV 102.2 fL)\\n   - Immediate: Staff to notify supervising provider today for expedited review and orders (done / documented).\\n   - Urgent labs (order now):\\n     - Repeat CBC with indices (confirm MCV) and include reticulocyte count.\\n     - Peripheral blood smear.\\n     - Serum folate (RBC folate preferred) and methylmalonic acid (MMA) to assess functional B12 status.\\n     - Review full medication list and alcohol use; document and escalate if potential culprit identified.\\n   - Short-term:\\n     - Advise patient to avoid alcohol until evaluation complete.\\n     - Encourage oral hydration; recheck CBC in 2–4 weeks after hydration.\\n   - If results indicate nutritional deficiency (low folate or elevated MMA) → provider-directed repletion:\\n     - Example regimens per provider: folate supplementation or B12 IM/PO replacement if indicated (provider to order).\\n   - If reticulocytosis or abnormal smear → consider hematology referral urgently.\\n2. Elevated LDL (183 mg/dL)\\n   - Immediate: Provider notified today for discussion of statin therapy vs lifestyle management (documented).\\n   - If fasting status uncertain, obtain repeat fasting lipid panel within 1–2 weeks to confirm.\\n   - Lifestyle counseling provided today:\\n     - Mediterranean-style diet, increase soluble fiber, reduce saturated/trans fats, increase dietary fish/omega-3s.\\n     - Aerobic exercise ≥150 minutes/week plus resistance training.\\n     - Weight management and smoking cessation counseling if applicable.\\n   - If provider elects to start statin:\\n     - Baseline LFTs already available (AST/ALT normal).\\n     - Plan repeat lipid panel 6–12 weeks after initiation and LFT monitoring per protocol.\\n   - Consider advanced lipid testing (ApoB, Lp(a)) if provider requests or if family history of premature CVD present.\\n3. Vitamin D insufficiency (25‑OH 32.7 ng/mL)\\n   - Start vitamin D3 5,000 IU daily with vitamin K (Metagenics D3 5,000 + K) per clinic protocol. No prior physician sign-off required unless contraindicated.\\n   - Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D in 8–12 weeks.\\n   - Counsel on dietary sources and safe sun exposure.\\n4. Borderline hematocrit (45.8%)\\n   - Counsel oral hydration; repeat CBC in 2–4 weeks after hydration.\\n   - If hematocrit remains elevated or hemoglobin rises, notify provider for further evaluation.\\n5. Androgen status / hormone optimization\\n   - Do NOT initiate testosterone based on current results (Total T 38 ng/dL; Pattern D = adequate).\\n   - Discuss non-androgenic contributors to fatigue and low libido: optimize sleep, assess mood/stress, review thyroid/iron status, evaluate estrogen/progesterone balance as needed.\\n   - Document menstrual cycle day/time for any future hormone testing; if provider requests repeat androgen panel, obtain on same cycle day for consistency.\\n   - If provider still considers testosterone trial, must document rationale and follow clinic testosterone initiation protocol (baseline lipids, LFTs, pregnancy test if applicable, informed consent).\\n6. Additional orders / referrals\\n   - Orders placed/ recommended:\\n     - CBC with indices + reticulocyte count (urgent)\\n     - Peripheral blood smear (urgent)\\n     - Serum folate (RBC folate if available) and methylmalonic acid (urgent)\\n     - Repeat fasting lipid panel (if initial was non-fasting or to confirm) within 1–2 weeks\\n     - Repeat CBC in 2–4 weeks after hydration\\n     - Recheck 25‑OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks after starting supplementation\\n   - Possible referrals (based on results):\\n     - Hematology if macrocytosis persists or smear/retic abnormal.\\n     - Preventive cardiology/cardiology if provider elects to escalate lipid management or if family history suggests premature CVD.\\n     - Endocrinology/reproductive endocrinology for complex hormone optimization if requested by provider.\\n     - Behavioral health or sleep medicine if indicated for mood, HPA axis concerns, or sleep disorder.\\n7. Patient education and lifestyle counseling provided today\\n   - Discussed importance of hydration, alcohol reduction, and review of current medications as potential causes of macrocytosis.\\n   - Discussed diet and exercise for LDL lowering; provided recommendation for Mediterranean-style diet and exercise goals.\\n   - Provided instructions for vitamin D supplementation (D3 5,000 IU daily with K) and plan for recheck.\\n   - Advised patient to report new symptoms (easy bruising/bleeding, syncope, worsening fatigue, chest pain, or focal neurologic symptoms).\\n8. Follow-up and timeline\\n   - Today (03/26/2026): Provider notified; vitamin D started; orders placed for urgent labs; patient counseled.\\n   - Within 1–2 weeks: Review urgent lab results with provider; confirm fasting lipid panel if needed; provider to determine need for statin or further testing.\\n   - 2–4 weeks: Repeat CBC after hydration to reassess hematocrit/MCV.\\n   - 6–12 weeks: If statin started → lipid panel at 6–12 weeks. If vitamin D started → recheck 25‑OH D at 8–12 weeks.\\n   - Further follow-up: Return to clinic or schedule telephone visit to discuss results and next steps after urgent labs are resulted; consider specialty referrals based on results.\\n- Documentation: Cycle day/time of hormone draw to be recorded for future hormone testing.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\nDate: 03/26/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": true, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": true}, "preventRisk": {"ldlGoal": "No specific LDL target; focus on risk-based statin intensity", "riskCategory": "low", "tenYearASCVD": 0.007508111608320002, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0.010129579899505324, "thirtyYearASCVD": 0.05323166809515259, "ageValidFor30Year": true, "thirtyYearTotalCVD": 0.07833851526309513, "tenYearHFPercentage": "0.4%", "tenYearHeartFailure": 0.003691608286975954, "statinRecommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "tenYearCVDPercentage": "1.0%", "tenYearASCVDPercentage": "0.8%", "thirtyYearHFPercentage": "3.6%", "thirtyYearHeartFailure": 0.035779505262264144, "thirtyYearCVDPercentage": "7.8%", "thirtyYearASCVDPercentage": "5.3%"}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 softgel daily with meal", "name": "D3 5,000 + K", "caution": "Repletion dose for deficiency/insufficiency. Contains vitamin K2 for optimal calcium metabolism. Recheck levels in 8-12 weeks.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "D3 5,000 + K provides vitamin D3 with K2 for repletion of deficiency/insufficiency. Target ≥60 ng/mL for bone, immune, and cardiovascular support.", "indication": "Vitamin D 32.7 ng/mL (insufficient)", "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D is below optimal range. This supplement will help restore your levels to support bone health, immune function, and overall wellness.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 32.7 ng/mL (insufficient)"]}, {"dose": "2 capsules twice daily", "name": "AdvaClear®", "caution": "Broad-spectrum phase I/II liver detoxification support. Contains NAC, green tea extract, milk thistle, and B-vitamins for biotransformation. Best taken with food.", "category": "detox", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "AdvaClear provides broad-spectrum phase I and phase II liver detoxification support with NAC, green tea extract, watercress, and B-vitamins. Designed for metabolic clearance support, biotransformation of hormones and environmental compounds, and hepatic stress. Appropriate when liver markers are borderline, estrogen clearance is sluggish, or metabolic dysfunction is present.", "indication": "Borderline liver markers (ALT 23, AST 21) suggesting hepatic biotransformation load; Weight gain (metabolic clearance factor); Low energy (biotransformation burden)", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This supplement supports your liver's natural detoxification processes. Your results suggest your body could benefit from help processing hormones, metabolic byproducts, and other compounds. It provides nutrients that support both phases of your liver's detox pathways.", "supportingFindings": ["Borderline liver markers (ALT 23, AST 21) suggesting hepatic biotransformation load", "Weight gain (metabolic clearance factor)", "Low energy (biotransformation burden)"]}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Essential for patients on statins.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "low", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "LDL 183 mg/dL; TC 263 mg/dL; Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["LDL 183 mg/dL", "TC 263 mg/dL", "Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "generally_recommended", "indicated": true, "rationale": "Persistent non-HDL ≥160 mg/dL (201 mg/dL) indicates elevated atherogenic lipoprotein burden. Statin discussion recommended."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall summary (plain language)\\n- Most results are reassuring: hemoglobin 15.1 g/dL, ferritin 93.6 ng/mL, HDL 62 mg/dL, triglycerides 89 mg/dL, A1c 5.1%, TSH 2.98 mIU/L, AST 21 U/L, ALT 23 U/L, creatinine 0.69 mg/dL and eGFR 113 mL/min. Your 10-year ASCVD (heart attack/stroke) risk is 0.8% and overall 10-year CVD risk is 1.0% — this is LOW risk.\\n\\nPositive findings (what’s good right now)\\n- Hemoglobin 15.1 g/dL (normal)\\n- Ferritin 93.6 ng/mL (good iron stores)\\n- HDL 62 mg/dL (protective)\\n- Triglycerides 89 mg/dL (normal)\\n- Hemoglobin A1c 5.1% (normal blood sugar control)\\n- Liver and kidney tests in normal range\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + concrete actions)\\n1) LDL cholesterol: 183 mg/dL (high)\\n- Lifestyle steps to lower LDL:\\n  - Add 25–30 g fiber daily: oatmeal for breakfast, beans with lunch, vegetables at dinner.\\n  - Include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n  - Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter and cheese.\\n  - Daily exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking or equivalent.\\n- Because your 10-year ASCVD risk is 0.8% (low), a statin is generally not indicated now; we will recheck after lifestyle changes and discuss medication only if needed.\\n\\n2) MCV 102.2 fL (macrocytosis)\\n- Possible causes: low folate, alcohol use, some medications, or less likely B12 (your B12 is 779 pg/mL).\\n- Actions:\\n  - Review any medications (e.g., methotrexate) and alcohol intake.\\n  - Start folate-rich foods (leafy greens, beans, fortified cereals).\\n  - Order a serum folate and repeat CBC and peripheral smear in 4–6 weeks. If unexplained, consider methylmalonic acid testing.\\n\\n3) Hematocrit 45.8% (slightly high — may reflect dehydration)\\n- Actions: drink 2–3 liters fluid daily for several days and repeat CBC in 1–2 weeks.\\n\\n4) Vitamin D (25-OH) 32.7 ng/mL (insufficient)\\n- Actions:\\n  - Start vitamin D3 2000 IU daily and get 10–20 minutes sun exposure 3–4 times weekly as feasible.\\n  - Include calcium-rich foods (dairy, fortified plant milks) and 2–3 sessions/week of weight-bearing exercise to support bone health.\\n  - Recheck vitamin D in 8–12 weeks.\\n\\nHormones and symptoms\\n- Total testosterone 38 ng/dL (within range) and your pattern suggests adequate androgens. If you have fatigue, low mood, poor sleep, or low libido, these are likely from non-androgen causes. Estradiol 97 pg/mL, progesterone 5.7 ng/mL and FSH 3.2 mIU/mL need interpretation based on where you are in your menstrual cycle — please note your cycle day/symptoms and we can interpret or retest timed to your cycle.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Implement cholesterol and lifestyle changes now. Repeat fasting lipid panel in ~12 weeks.\\n- Repeat CBC in 4–6 weeks (sooner if symptoms occur) and folate testing now.\\n- Start vitamin D3 2000 IU daily and recheck 8–12 weeks.\\n- Bring a list of current medications and alcohol use to your next visit. If LDL remains high despite lifestyle changes, we will discuss further options.\\n\\nIf you’d like, we can schedule follow-up to review progress and adjust the plan. You’re doing well in many areas — these focused steps should help improve the few abnormal results.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 15.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 45.8, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Slightly elevated hematocrit - may indicate dehydration.", "recommendation": "Encourage hydration. Repeat if persistent.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 102.2, "status": "abnormal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "Elevated MCV (102.2 fL) indicates macrocytic red blood cells. Common causes include B12 deficiency, folate deficiency, hypothyroidism, alcohol use, or certain medications (methotrexate, hydroxyurea).", "recommendation": "check serum B12, folate, TSH, reticulocyte count, and medication list. Rule out alcohol use..", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 264, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 97, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 5.7, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 3.2, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 38, "status": "normal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone within normal female range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 2.98, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 93.6, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 32.7, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D insufficient (31-40 ng/mL). Suboptimal for hormone optimization.", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 5,000 + K daily. Target ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 779, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 183, "status": "abnormal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "High LDL.", "recommendation": "Consider statin based on ASCVD risk. Lifestyle modifications.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 62, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 89, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 1.0129579899505323, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 1.0% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.7508111608320002, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 0.8%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.3691608286975954, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.4%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 7.8338515263095125, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 7.8%, ASCVD 5.3%, Heart Failure 3.6%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 21, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 23, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 113, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.69, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 38, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone Pattern D: Adequate Androgens, Persistent Symptoms", "interpretation": "Adequate Androgens pattern identified (Total T: 38 ng/dL (within range)). Total, free, and bioavailable testosterone are within normal ranges and SHBG is not extreme. If the patient reports fatigue, low mood, sleep disruption, or low libido, these symptoms are likely driven by non-androgenic factors. Findings to evaluate: Estrogen/Progesterone balance should be assessed (E2: 97 pg/mL, P4: 5.7 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Do not reflexively add testosterone when levels are adequate — look deeper. Evaluate iron status (ferritin target >50 for symptom resolution), thyroid function (optimize TSH), and sleep quality (consider sleep apnea screening with STOP-BANG). Assess estrogen and progesterone balance — E2/P4 imbalance is a common driver of persistent symptoms. Address CNS and psychosocial drivers: chronic stress, HPA axis dysregulation, mood disorders. Consider DHEA-S if not already checked. Support with lifestyle optimization: sleep hygiene, stress management, nutrition. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your testosterone levels are actually in a healthy range, which is good news. When symptoms persist despite normal testosterone, it usually means something else is contributing. Your provider will look at other factors like iron levels, thyroid function, sleep quality, and hormone balance to find what's driving your symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Total T 15-70 ng/dL, Free T 0.5-5.0 pg/mL (all within range)"}], "aiRecommendations": "CRITICAL SUMMARY (for clinic staff review)\\n- Key abnormal values: MCV 102.2 fL (macrocytosis); LDL 183 mg/dL (markedly elevated); Vitamin D 25‑OH 32.7 ng/mL (insufficient); Hematocrit 45.8% (borderline high). Total testosterone 38 ng/dL is within reference (Pattern D = adequate androgens) — do not reflexively start testosterone.\\n- Immediate staff actions: notify provider regarding macrocytosis (MCV >100 fL) and high LDL (183 mg/dL) for provider decision on workup and possible statin discussion. Begin targeted diagnostic testing and counseling per protocol.\\n\\n1) Overall clinical assessment\\n- Macrocytosis (MCV 102.2 fL): clinically significant — requires prompt evaluation for B12/folate deficiency, medication/alcohol effects, hypothyroidism (TSH listed as normal but confirm value), reticulocytosis, or less common marrow disorders.\\n- LDL 183 mg/dL: high. Although 10‑yr ASCVD risk calculators are listed as normal, LDL in this range warrants provider review for secondary causes and consideration of lipid‑lowering therapy based on overall risk and patient preferences.\\n- Vitamin D insufficient at 32.7 ng/mL: suboptimal for hormone optimization per clinic protocol.\\n- Hematocrit 45.8%: borderline high and may reflect mild hemoconcentration/dehydration; recheck after hydration.\\n- Testosterone pattern D (Total T 38 ng/dL): adequate — do not initiate testosterone based on level alone. Investigate non-androgenic drivers of symptoms (iron status, thyroid optimization, sleep, estrogen/progesterone balance, psychosocial/HPA axis issues).\\n\\n2) Priority actions (organized CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL (notify provider immediately)\\n- Notify supervising provider today for:\\n  - Macrocytosis (MCV 102.2 fL) — requires expedited evaluation and potential urgent workup.\\n  - LDL 183 mg/dL — provider discussion for statin consideration given LDL level despite low calculated 10‑yr risk.\\n\\nURGENT (within 1–2 weeks)\\n- Order the following tests now:\\n  - CBC with indices repeat (confirm MCV), reticulocyte count, peripheral blood smear.\\n  - Serum folate (or RBC folate), methylmalonic acid (MMA) if B12 borderline or to clarify B12 functional status.\\n  - Review current medication list (look for metformin, methotrexate, hydroxyurea, anticonvulsants, zidovudine) and recent alcohol use — document and escalate to provider if a culprit is identified.\\n  - Repeat fasting lipid panel (confirm LDL) and fasting glucose or repeat A1c only if clinically indicated (A1c listed as normal).\\n  - Basic LFTs already normal; if statin is started, baseline LFTs documented — repeat per protocol.\\n\\nROUTINE (within 4–12 weeks)\\n- Recheck vitamin D level 8–12 weeks after initiating replacement.\\n- Repeat CBC including MCV and hematocrit in 2–4 weeks after hydration; if persistent macrocytosis, follow more extensive workup per provider.\\n- If statin initiated, repeat lipid panel at 6–12 weeks post‑start.\\n\\n3) Testosterone dosing recommendations (IF APPLICABLE)\\n- Current data: Total testosterone 38 ng/dL = within reference; clinic protocol: do NOT initiate testosterone based on these results.\\n- Action for staff:\\n  - Do not prescribe or titrate testosterone for symptom management at this time.\\n  - If provider elects to trial testosterone despite protocol (provider must document rationale), escalate to supervising physician and follow the clinic’s formal testosterone initiation protocol and monitoring checklist (obtain baseline lipids, LFTs, pregnancy test if applicable, and informed consent). Do not initiate without physician sign‑off.\\n  - Document cycle day/time of blood draw in chart; repeat androgen panel on the same cycle day if clinician requests reassessment.\\n\\n4) Lifestyle interventions to communicate to patient (staff script / education points)\\n- Hydration: Encourage increased oral fluids today and before next lab draw; plan to repeat CBC after 2–4 weeks of adequate hydration.\\n- Diet and lipids:\\n  - Advise Mediterranean‑style diet: increase soluble fiber (oats, beans), reduce saturated fats, avoid trans fats, increase fish/omega‑3 intake.\\n  - Encourage weight management if overweight, smoking cessation if applicable, and moderate aerobic exercise (≥150 min/wk) and resistance training.\\n- Alcohol: Screen and counsel to reduce/abstain from alcohol until macrocytosis evaluated.\\n- Vitamin D: Start vitamin D3 supplement per protocol (see below) and counsel on safe sunlight exposure and dietary sources.\\n- Sleep and mood: Reinforce sleep hygiene and screen for sleep apnea/insomnia/depression as contributors to fatigue and libido changes.\\n\\n5) Additional testing and referrals to consider\\n- Laboratory:\\n  - Serum folate (RBC folate preferred), methylmalonic acid, repeat B12 if prior value borderline (current B12 listed as normal — still obtain folate/MMA given macrocytosis).\\n  - Reticulocyte count, peripheral blood smear.\\n  - If persistent macrocytosis without nutritional cause: consider bone marrow referral/hematology consult.\\n  - Repeat fasting lipid panel; consider ApoB and Lp(a) if provider requests advanced lipid testing.\\n- Referrals:\\n  - Hematology: if macrocytosis persists after initial workup or if smear/retic suggest marrow process.\\n  - Cardiology or preventive cardiology if provider decides therapy escalation for LDL or if family history / premature CVD.\\n  - Endocrinology or reproductive endocrinology if provider wants specialized hormone optimization or when fertility concerns exist.\\n  - Sleep medicine if high clinical suspicion for OSA despite normal STOP‑BANG score.\\n  - Behavioral health / psychology for mood/stress/HPA axis dysregulation if contributing symptoms.\\n- Medication/deprescribing review: pharmacy review for medications that can cause macrocytosis or interact with proposed therapies.\\n\\n6) Specific medication and monitoring recommendations (staff to implement per provider orders)\\n- Vitamin D supplementation (clinic protocol recommendation):\\n  - Initiate vitamin D3 5,000 IU daily with K (Metagenics D3 5,000 + K) per protocol; target level ≥60 ng/mL.\\n  - Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D in 8–12 weeks.\\n  - Physician notification not required prior to starting unless contraindicated.\\n- LDL management:\\n  - Notify provider for statin discussion. If provider elects to start a statin, follow usual initiation protocols (e.g., moderate to high‑intensity statin per provider decision), obtain baseline LFTs (already normal), and plan lipid panel at 6–12 weeks.\\n  - If lifestyle intervention only, repeat fasting lipid panel in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Macrocytosis workup:\\n  - Order folate/RBC folate and MMA now (urgent); order peripheral smear and retic count.\\n  - If B12 deficiency documented or MMA elevated — provider to decide on B12 repletion regimen (e.g., intramuscular B12 per clinic protocol) and follow‑up testing schedule.\\n- Hydration plan:\\n  - Instruct patient to hydrate and return for repeat CBC in 2–4 weeks; if hematocrit remains elevated or hemoglobin rises further, notify provider.\\n\\n7) Provider notification and escalation criteria\\n- Immediate provider notification today for:\\n  - MCV >100 fL (102.2 fL) — request expedited review and orders for folate/MMA/retic/smear.\\n  - LDL 183 mg/dL — request provider decision re: statin initiation or further testing.\\n- Notify provider if repeat CBC shows persistent macrocytosis or if reticulocytosis, abnormal smear, or worsening cell counts are present.\\n- Notify provider if vitamin D therapy intolerance or if patient is pregnant or lactating (special considerations for supplementation and testosterone decisions).\\n\\n8) Timeline and next steps (concise action plan)\\n- Today:\\n  - Staff: Notify provider re: MCV 102.2 and LDL 183 mg/dL.\\n  - Order: serum folate (RBC folate if available), methylmalonic acid, reticulocyte count, peripheral smear, repeat CBC.\\n  - Start vitamin D3 5,000 IU daily (per protocol) and document counseling.\\n  - Counsel patient on hydration, alcohol reduction, and LDL lifestyle measures; schedule follow‑ups.\\n- Within 1–2 weeks:\\n  - Review new lab results with provider. If MMA or folate abnormal → provider directs repletion therapy; if peripheral smear abnormal or retic high → consider hematology referral.\\n  - Repeat fasting lipid panel if initial was non-fasting or to confirm.\\n- 2–4 weeks:\\n  - Repeat CBC (after hydration) to confirm hematocrit/MCV changes.\\n- 6–12 weeks:\\n  - If statin started, lipid panel at 6–12 weeks post‑initiation.\\n  - If vitamin D started, recheck 25‑OH D at 8–12 weeks.\\n  - Reassess symptoms (fatigue, libido, mood) and laboratory trends; consider further referrals (endocrinology, hematology, sleep/behavioral health) based on results.\\n- Documentation:\\n  - Record cycle day/time of hormone draw in chart; if provider requests further hormone optimization, repeat sex steroid panel timed to cycle phase per protocol.\\n\\nClinical pearls for staff (brief)\\n- Do NOT initiate testosterone: Total T 38 ng/dL is within expected range and pattern indicates adequate androgens — evaluate non‑androgen drivers first.\\n- Macrocytosis requires immediate lab follow‑up even if B12 currently reported as normal — obtain folate/MMA and peripheral smear.\\n- LDL 183 mg/dL merits provider review—consider secondary causes and shared decision on statin therapy.\\n- Track and document menstrual cycle day for interpretation of estradiol/progesterone results when provider requests further hormone evaluation.\\n\\nIf you want, I can convert these actions into an orders checklist and patient instruction sheet for clinic use.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 1.44, "detail": "Triglycerides 89 / HDL 62 = 1.44", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5.1%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 23, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": []}	2026-03-26 12:47:50.405329	1
56	Leigh Wimberly 	female	2026-03-05 00:00:00	{"alt": 21, "ast": 21, "bun": 9, "co2": 21, "mcv": 98.2, "rbc": 4.53, "tsh": 1.21, "wbc": 6.7, "acne": false, "egfr": 85, "iron": 110, "shbg": 54.2, "tibc": 256, "onHRT": false, "freeT4": 1.26, "sodium": 138, "albumin": 4.8, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 9.7, "glucose": 86, "bloating": false, "brainFog": true, "chloride": 104, "ferritin": 145, "hairLoss": false, "vitaminD": 71.7, "bilirubin": 0.6, "estradiol": 61, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": true, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 255, "potassium": 4, "creatinine": 0.88, "hematocrit": 44.5, "hemoglobin": 15.4, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2026-03-05", "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Leigh Wimberly ", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 39, "bmi": 32, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": true, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 119, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 0.33, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 55.9, "totalProtein": 6.8, "lowMotivation": false, "tpoAntibodies": 15, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "freeTestosterone": 7}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History of present illness: Patient presented for review of recent laboratory results from 03/26/2026. Cycle day at time of blood draw is unknown (patient unable to provide exact cycle day/LMP at time of visit). Patient reports intermittent sleep disturbance and increased anxiety over recent months. Denies rapid-onset virilization (no voice change, clitoromegaly), no significant new hirsutism, no sudden hair loss, no chest pain or shortness of breath. Denies current use of prescribed testosterone; reports she will check and report any over-the-counter androgenic supplements (DHEA/“testosterone” creams).\\n- Menstrual history: Per patient, cycles have become irregular recently (patient reports change in regularity but did not provide exact cycle length). Fertility desires: not discussed/unknown at this visit.\\n- Allergies: None documented.\\n- Medications/Supplements: Patient to provide complete list; staff counseled to reconcile for exogenous androgens and DHEA. (No prescription testosterone reported by patient at time of visit.)\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: Not documented in chart today (BMI, BP not available).\\n- Risk scores: STOP-BANG Sleep Apnea Risk = 0 points.\\n- Laboratory results (03/26/2026) — grouped and flagged:\\n  CBC / Hematology\\n  - Hemoglobin (Hgb): 15.4 g/dL — normal\\n  - Hematocrit (Hct): 44.5 % — borderline high (may reflect dehydration)\\n  - Platelets: 255 K/uL — normal\\n  - MCV: 98.2 fL — high‑normal / borderline (early macrocytosis)\\n  Metabolic / Renal / Liver\\n  - Creatinine: 0.88 mg/dL — normal\\n  - eGFR: 85 mL/min — normal\\n  - AST: 21 U/L — normal\\n  - ALT: 21 U/L — normal\\n  Iron Studies / Nutritional\\n  - Ferritin: 145 ng/mL — normal\\n  - Serum Iron: 110 ug/dL — normal\\n  - TIBC: 256 ug/dL — normal\\n  Endocrine / Hormones\\n  - Estradiol (E2): 61 pg/mL — normal for age (adequate estrogen production)\\n  - Progesterone: 0.33 ng/mL — ABNORMAL / low relative to expected luteal values (if sample was luteal)\\n  - Perimenopause assessment (clinic algorithm): Luteal Progesterone Deficiency: 0.33 ng/mL — ABNORMAL (if drawn in luteal phase)\\n  - Perimenopause assessment (phase unknown): Low Progesterone (Phase Unknown): 0.33 ng/mL — BORDERLINE interpretation without cycle day\\n  - Testosterone (Total): 55.9 ng/dL — BORDERLINE / upper normal\\n  - Free Testosterone: 7 pg/mL — ABNORMAL / elevated\\n  - SHBG: 54.2 nmol/L — normal\\n  Thyroid / Vitamin\\n  - TSH: 1.21 mIU/L — normal\\n  - Vitamin D (25-OH): 71.7 ng/mL — normal / adequate\\n- Other: No imaging available. No pregnancy test result documented.\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Luteal phase progesterone deficiency / probable perimenopausal luteal insufficiency.\\n   - Data: Progesterone 0.33 ng/mL with estradiol 61 pg/mL in a 39‑year‑old. Per clinic algorithm, with luteal-phase draw this indicates luteal progesterone deficiency consistent with perimenopause. Interpretation limited by unknown cycle day. Clinical correlate: patient reports sleep disturbance and increased anxiety which can be associated with low progesterone.\\n   - Differential: True luteal insufficiency/anovulation (perimenopause), sample drawn in follicular phase (mis-timed), exogenous hormones altering levels (less likely per history), thyroid disease (TSH normal).\\n\\n2. Biochemical hyperandrogenism — elevated free testosterone; total testosterone borderline high.\\n   - Data: Free T 7 pg/mL (elevated); Total T 55.9 ng/dL (upper normal/borderline). SHBG normal.\\n   - Clinical significance: May correlate with androgenic symptoms (acne, hirsutism, hair thinning). No rapid virilization reported. Need to reconcile supplement/medication use (topical/oral testosterone, DHEA).\\n   - Differential: Endogenous ovarian or adrenal androgen excess (PCOS, adrenal hyperandrogenism), exogenous androgen exposure, lab variability.\\n\\n3. Borderline macrocytosis / potential early B12 or folate deficiency.\\n   - Data: MCV 98.2 fL (high-normal/borderline). Ferritin and iron indices normal.\\n   - Clinical significance: Early macrocytosis can precede overt B12/folate deficiency; consider alcohol use, medication effects, or marrow disorder less likely.\\n\\n4. Borderline elevated hematocrit.\\n   - Data: Hct 44.5% (borderline high).\\n   - Clinical significance: May reflect relative dehydration; recommend repeat after hydration. If persistent or rising, evaluate further.\\n\\n5. Other routine labs reassuring.\\n   - Data: Hgb, platelets, TSH, renal/liver function, vitamin D, ferritin within acceptable ranges.\\n\\nRed flags requiring immediate physician notification: any rapid virilization (voice change, clitoromegaly, rapid hair growth), pregnancy, chest pain/shortness of breath, or evidence of exogenous androgen exposure with virilization.\\n\\nPLAN:\\n1. Luteal progesterone deficiency / perimenopause management\\n   - Immediate: Document menstrual cycle day (LMP and typical cycle length) in chart today. Clarify whether progesterone sample was drawn luteal. Pregnancy status must be confirmed prior to initiating progesterone if therapy is considered.\\n   - Provider to review and decide regarding progesterone therapy. Suggested options per clinic protocol (provider order required):\\n     - If regular cycles and luteal deficiency suspected: Micronized progesterone 100 mg PO at bedtime nightly on days 14–28 of cycle; consider increasing to 200 mg nightly if inadequate symptom control after 6–12 weeks.\\n     - If irregular cycles/perimenopausal or patient prefers continuous dosing: Micronized progesterone 100–200 mg PO at bedtime nightly continuous. Counsel regarding sedation; advise taking at bedtime.\\n   - Follow-up: If started, clinical reassessment at 6–12 weeks for symptom response and bleeding pattern. Plan to re-evaluate labs as directed below.\\n\\n2. Elevated free testosterone / hyperandrogenism workup and management\\n   - Immediate: Reconcile medications and supplements today — instruct patient to report any topical/oral testosterone or DHEA use. If patient is on exogenous testosterone or androgenic supplements, instruct to hold or reduce dose (provider guidance recommended; if on testosterone, pause or reduce by 50% pending provider review).\\n   - Diagnostic: Order repeat total testosterone and free testosterone (AM draw, fasting if possible) and SHBG in 6–8 weeks after any medication/supplement changes. Consider additional labs if clinical hyperandrogenism or persistent elevation: DHEA-S, androstenedione, 17‑OH progesterone, fasting glucose/A1c, lipid panel.\\n   - Referral: Consider endocrinology or REI/OBGYN referral if marked biochemical hyperandrogenism, rapid virilization, or if androgen source unclear.\\n\\n3. Borderline macrocytosis (MCV 98.2 fL)\\n   - Orders: Send serum vitamin B12 and RBC folate (or RBC folate / methylmalonic acid) now.\\n   - Counseling: Discuss dietary intake and alcohol use. If B12/folate deficiency identified, treat per provider order. If borderline/low and treated, recheck CBC (Hct/MCV/Hgb) in 6–8 weeks.\\n   - If tests normal and MCV persists/worsens, consider further evaluation (medication review, hematology referral).\\n\\n4. Borderline elevated hematocrit (Hct 44.5%)\\n   - Immediate: Hydration counseling provided today; advise increased fluid intake and avoidance of significant diuretic or dehydrating behaviors prior to repeat labs.\\n   - Orders: Repeat CBC (Hct, Hgb, MCV) in 2–4 weeks after adequate hydration. If persistent or rising above provider threshold, consider further evaluation (sleep apnea re-evaluation despite STOP-BANG 0, smoking history, EPO use, hematology referral).\\n\\n5. Supplement recommendations (per clinic priority list)\\n   - High-priority (recommended):\\n     - AdvaClear®: 2 capsules twice daily (patient education provided regarding product; initiate per patient preference and provider approval).\\n     - NutraGems® CoQ10 300: 1 chewable gel daily.\\n   - Medium-priority:\\n     - GlutaClear®: 2 capsules daily.\\n     - EstroFactors™: 2 tablets twice daily.\\n     - Magtein® Magnesium L‑Threonate: 3 capsules daily in divided doses.\\n   - Note: Supplements documented and discussed; patient advised to list all OTC supplements/compounded creams. Provider to review and approve supplement regimen.\\n\\n6. Laboratory follow-up orders and timing\\n   - Now:\\n     - Vitamin B12 and RBC folate (or MMA if preferred).\\n     - Document LMP and cycle history in chart; pregnancy test if progesterone therapy being considered.\\n     - Medication/supplement reconciliation for androgenic products.\\n   - 2–4 weeks:\\n     - Repeat CBC (Hct, Hgb, MCV) after hydration.\\n   - 6–8 weeks:\\n     - Repeat total and free testosterone and SHBG (AM draw), especially if supplements/medications changed or stopped.\\n     - If B12/folate deficiency treated, recheck CBC at 6–8 weeks.\\n   - 6–12 weeks:\\n     - If progesterone started, clinical follow-up for symptom assessment and bleeding.\\n   - Additional testing as indicated by provider: DHEA‑S, androstenedione, 17‑OH progesterone, fasting glucose or HgbA1c, lipid panel, pelvic ultrasound if PCOS suspected.\\n\\n7. Patient education and counseling (documented discussion)\\n   - Explained abnormal results: low progesterone consistent with luteal deficiency/perimenopause if sample was luteal; elevated free testosterone may explain androgenic symptoms; borderline MCV may reflect early B12/folate issues; borderline Hct may reflect dehydration.\\n   - Counseled re: hydration and to avoid androgenic supplements (DHEA, topical testosterone) until evaluation complete.\\n   - Counseled on possible side effects of progesterone (drowsiness — take at bedtime, breast tenderness, irregular bleeding) and need to confirm pregnancy status prior to therapy.\\n   - Reviewed red flags requiring immediate contact: rapid virilization, pregnancy, chest pain, shortness of breath.\\n   - Lifestyle counseling: weight management, exercise, sleep hygiene, optimize dietary B12/folate intake; smoking cessation if applicable.\\n\\n8. Referrals and escalation\\n   - Consider endocrinology referral if marked hyperandrogenism or if adrenal source suspected.\\n   - Consider REI/OBGYN for complex cycle management or fertility wishes.\\n   - Consider hematology if persistent or progressive macrocytosis or rising hematocrit despite intervention.\\n   - Dermatology referral for refractory acne/hirsutism if needed.\\n\\n9. Administrative / charting actions\\n   - Staff to document LMP/cycle day and complete medication/supplement reconciliation in chart today.\\n   - Provider to review progesterone result in context of documented cycle day and authorize progesterone therapy if indicated.\\n   - Orders placed as above (B12, RBC folate, repeat CBC). (Note: provider to sign orders as applicable.)\\n\\nFollow-up: Recheck labs and revisit per timelines above; routine recheck window recommended 3–6 months unless earlier reassessment required.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\nDate: 03/26/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "supplements": [{"dose": "2 capsules twice daily", "name": "AdvaClear®", "caution": "Broad-spectrum phase I/II liver detoxification support. Contains NAC, green tea extract, milk thistle, and B-vitamins for biotransformation. Best taken with food.", "category": "detox", "priority": "high", "rationale": "AdvaClear provides broad-spectrum phase I and phase II liver detoxification support with NAC, green tea extract, watercress, and B-vitamins. Designed for metabolic clearance support, biotransformation of hormones and environmental compounds, and hepatic stress. Appropriate when liver markers are borderline, estrogen clearance is sluggish, or metabolic dysfunction is present.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance pattern (hepatic detox pathways needed for clearance); Oxidative stress burden (liver biotransformation support); Borderline liver markers (ALT 21, AST 21) suggesting hepatic biotransformation load; Low energy (biotransformation burden)", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance", "Oxidative Stress Burden"], "confidence