rts glutathione production and antioxidant defense with NAC, alpha-lipoic acid, and green tea catechins. Indicated when there is evidence of oxidative stress, inflammatory burden, or metabolic dysfunction requiring enhanced cellular defense. While AdvaClear provides broader liver detoxification support, GlutaClear specifically targets glutathione pathways and antioxidant capacity.", "indication": "Oxidative stress phenotype (glutathione/antioxidant support indicated); ALT 21 U/L (hepatic glutathione demand); Low energy (mitochondrial antioxidant support); Brain fog (CNS oxidative burden)", "phenotypes": ["Oxidative Stress Burden"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement boosts your body's master antioxidant, glutathione, which protects your cells from damage. Your results suggest increased oxidative stress, and this formula helps your body defend and repair at the cellular level.", "supportingFindings": ["Oxidative stress phenotype (glutathione/antioxidant support indicated)", "ALT 21 U/L (hepatic glutathione demand)", "Low energy (mitochondrial antioxidant support)", "Brain fog (CNS oxidative burden)"]}, {"dose": "2 tablets twice daily", "name": "EstroFactors™", "caution": "Nutritional support for estrogen balance and metabolism. Contains I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Supports healthy estrogen metabolite ratios.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "EstroFactors provides targeted nutritional support for estrogen metabolism and balance with I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Best suited for women with estrogen dominance patterns, PMS, perimenopausal hormone imbalance, or those needing broader estrogen metabolism support during hormonal transitions. Differs from Estrovera in that it addresses the underlying estrogen metabolism rather than vasomotor symptom relief.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected; Age-appropriate for estrogen metabolism support", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement helps your body process and balance estrogen more effectively. It supports healthy hormone metabolism, which can help with PMS symptoms, bloating, mood changes, and hormonal balance during life transitions.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected", "Age-appropriate for estrogen metabolism support"]}, {"dose": "3 capsules daily (divided doses)", "name": "Magtein® Magnesium L-Threonate", "caution": "L-Threonate form crosses blood-brain barrier for cognitive and sleep support. Well-tolerated; gentle on GI system.", "category": "mineral", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Magtein is the only magnesium form clinically shown to cross the blood-brain barrier. Supports quality sleep, cognitive function, GABA activity, and stress resilience.", "indication": "Low energy reported; Brain fog reported", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This special form of magnesium is designed to reach the brain, where it supports better sleep quality, mental clarity, and a calmer nervous system.", "supportingFindings": ["Low energy reported", "Brain fog reported"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\n- Your results show signs of early perimenopause with low luteal progesterone and higher-than-expected free testosterone. Specifically, progesterone is 0.33 ng/mL and estradiol is 61 pg/mL (age 39) — this pattern (adequate estrogen but low progesterone) suggests the ovaries are making estrogen but not ovulating consistently. Free testosterone is 7 pg/mL (elevated) and total testosterone is 55.9 ng/dL (upper-normal). Other important numbers: hemoglobin 15.4 g/dL, hematocrit 44.5%, MCV 98.2 fL, ferritin 145 ng/mL, vitamin D 71.7 ng/mL, TSH 1.21 mIU/L, SHBG 54.2 nmol/L.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Vitamin D 71.7 ng/mL (within optimal 60–100 ng/mL). Continue current vitamin D plan and recheck in 6–12 months.\\n- Ferritin 145 ng/mL — good iron stores (>50).\\n- TSH 1.21 mIU/L — thyroid function is in the normal range.\\n- AST 21 U/L and ALT 21 U/L — liver tests normal.\\n- Kidney function eGFR 85 mL/min and creatinine 0.88 mg/dL — normal.\\n- Sleep-apnea screening 0/8 — low risk.\\n\\nAreas to improve (with concrete actions)\\n- Low luteal progesterone: progesterone 0.33 ng/mL. Action: track your cycle and repeat a progesterone level in the mid-luteal phase (about 7 days before your expected period; e.g., day ~21 in a 28-day cycle). If low again, discuss options with your clinician within 4–8 weeks — options may include cyclical progesterone therapy to restore regular bleeding and protect bone health and the lining of the uterus.\\n- Elevated free testosterone 7 pg/mL (total 55.9 ng/dL): may cause acne, hair thinning, or unwanted hair. Actions now: prioritize 7–8 hours sleep nightly, start strength training 2–3x/week, and practice stress reduction (10–20 minutes daily: walking, meditation, or yoga). Discuss with your provider about treatments (combined oral contraceptives, spironolactone, or other options) if symptoms are bothersome.\\n- Hematocrit 44.5% and MCV 98.2 fL: hematocrit slightly high (may reflect mild dehydration); MCV is high-normal suggesting early macrocytosis. Actions: increase daily water (aim for 2–3 L/day depending on activity), recheck CBC when well-hydrated in 4–8 weeks. Order B12 and folate levels now; if low, add dietary sources (B12: meat, dairy, fortified cereals; folate: leafy greens, beans) or start supplements per provider.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Repeat mid-luteal progesterone (7 days before period) and repeat CBC (after good hydration) in 4–8 weeks.\\n- Check B12 and folate now.\\n- Discuss elevated free testosterone and possible treatment options with your clinician within 4–8 weeks if symptoms affect quality of life.\\n- Continue vitamin D maintenance and routine follow-up with your provider.\\n\\nYou’re doing well in several areas — these targeted steps will help clarify hormone changes and protect your overall health. If you notice new symptoms (heavy irregular bleeding, severe hair loss, or acne), contact your provider sooner.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 15.4, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 44.5, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Slightly elevated hematocrit - may indicate dehydration.", "recommendation": "Encourage hydration. Repeat if persistent.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 98.2, "status": "borderline", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 98.2 fL is in the high-normal range. Early macrocytosis can precede overt B12 or folate deficiency.", "recommendation": "Ensure B12 and folate levels are within optimal range. Monitor CBC at next visit.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 255, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 61, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.33, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 55.9, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Upper normal testosterone - may be elevated.", "recommendation": "Correlate with clinical signs (hirsutism, acne). Consider PCOS workup.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.21, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 145, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 71.7, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D optimal (60-100 ng/mL). Supports hormone, bone, and immune health.", "recommendation": "Maintain current regimen. Monitor annually.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 21, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 21, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 85, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.88, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 256, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 110, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.33, "status": "borderline", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Low Progesterone (Phase Unknown)", "interpretation": "Progesterone is 0.33 ng/mL. Without cycle phase documentation, interpretation is limited. If drawn in luteal phase, this would indicate anovulation - a perimenopause marker.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Document cycle phase for accurate interpretation. If patient is mid-cycle or luteal, this suggests anovulation. Consider symptoms: sleep disruption, anxiety, irregular cycles. PATIENT EDUCATION: We need to know where you are in your menstrual cycle to fully interpret this result. Please note when your last period started.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase; <5 ng/mL suggests anovulation if luteal"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.33, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Luteal Progesterone Deficiency", "interpretation": "Progesterone of 0.33 ng/mL with estradiol of 61 pg/mL in a woman 39 years old indicates luteal progesterone deficiency. Estrogen production is adequate but progesterone is not keeping pace - a hallmark of perimenopause.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Micronized progesterone 100-200 mg nightly. Dosing can be cyclic (days 14-28) or continuous depending on cycle regularity. Progesterone provides GABAergic support for sleep and anxiety relief. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your body is making enough estrogen, but progesterone has dropped. This is very common in perimenopause and causes sleep disruption, anxiety, and mood changes. Progesterone supplementation supports your nervous system and helps restore calm, restful sleep.", "referenceRange": "P4 ≥5 ng/mL expected with E2 ≥60 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 54.2, "status": "normal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 54.2 nmol/L is within normal range. SHBG regulates bioavailable sex hormones by binding testosterone and estradiol.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 7, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 7 pg/mL is elevated. May cause androgenic symptoms (acne, hirsutism, hair thinning).", "recommendation": "Evaluate for PCOS, exogenous testosterone, or low SHBG driving elevated free T.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}], "aiRecommendations": "Clinical context / summary\\n- 39 y/o female. Key abnormalities: Hct 44.5% (borderline high), MCV 98.2 fL (high‑normal), Total T 55.9 ng/dL (upper normal/borderline), Free T 7 pg/mL (elevated), Progesterone 0.33 ng/mL (low for luteal → luteal progesterone deficiency per clinic protocol); Estradiol 61 pg/mL (appears adequate). SHBG, ferritin, TSH, LFTs, renal panel, vitamin D normal.\\n- Cycle phase not documented — limits interpretation of progesterone. Per clinic protocol, low luteal progesterone + adequate estradiol is consistent with perimenopausal luteal phase deficiency if sample was luteal.\\n\\n1) Overall clinical assessment (for staff)\\n- Probable perimenopausal pattern with luteal progesterone deficiency contributing to sleep/anxiety symptoms; estrogen production appears preserved.\\n- Biochemical hyperandrogenism: elevated free testosterone with borderline total testosterone—correlate clinically for hirsutism/acne/alopecia and review medications/supplements for exogenous androgens.\\n- Borderline macrocytosis (MCV 98.2 fL) → early B12/folate deficiency or other causes; ferritin and iron indices normal.\\n- Borderline elevated hematocrit likely dehydration or temporary; repeat after hydration before further workup.\\n\\n2) Priority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL (immediate notification)\\n- Notify provider urgently if patient reports rapid-onset virilization (voice change, clitoromegaly, rapid hair growth), pregnancy, chest pain/shortness of breath, or if any clinical signs suggest adrenal/ovarian tumor.\\n- Notify provider immediately if patient is currently taking exogenous testosterone or androgenic supplements and has signs of virilization.\\n\\nURGENT (within 1–2 weeks)\\n- Document menstrual cycle day at time of previous blood draw. If unknown, obtain cycle history now (date of LMP, cycle length, regularity). Provider should review progesterone interpretation after cycle documentation.\\n- If patient is on topical/oral testosterone or DHEA, hold or reduce dose pending provider review (see Testosterone dose recommendations below).\\n\\nROUTINE (within 4–12 weeks)\\n- Hydration counseling now; repeat CBC (Hct, Hgb, MCV) after adequate hydration in 2–4 weeks.\\n- Evaluate macrocytosis: order serum B12 and RBC folate (or RBC folate / methylmalonic acid if available). If B12 low or borderline, treat per provider order and recheck CBC in 6–8 weeks.\\n- If starting progesterone therapy, arrange follow-up to assess symptoms and side effects at 6–12 weeks.\\n\\n3) Specific therapeutic recommendations / medication dosing (for provider review)\\nProgesterone (per clinic protocol — PROVIDER decision required)\\n- Recommended starting options (document cycle before choosing):\\n  - If patient has regular cycles and luteal deficiency suspected: Micronized progesterone 100 mg PO at bedtime nightly on days 14–28 of cycle. Consider 200 mg nightly if inadequate symptom control after 6–12 weeks.\\n  - If cycles irregular/perimenopausal or patient prefers continuous dosing for sleep/anxiety: Micronized progesterone 100–200 mg PO nightly continuous. Reassess symptoms and bleeding pattern at 6–12 weeks.\\n- Counsel regarding sedation/GABAergic effects; advise taking at bedtime. Document pregnancy status prior to starting.\\n\\nTestosterone (if applicable)\\n- If patient is currently receiving exogenous testosterone (gel, patch, injection, compounded): discontinue or reduce dose by 50% pending provider review—do NOT initiate testosterone given biochemical elevation.\\n- If no exogenous testosterone but considering androgen therapy: defer initiation; obtain full hyperandrogenism workup first.\\n- Laboratory recheck after dose change or discontinuation: repeat total and free testosterone and SHBG in 6–8 weeks (morning sample).\\n\\n4) Follow-up lab timing and physician notification requirements\\n- CBC (Hct, Hgb, MCV): repeat after hydration in 2–4 weeks; if persistent abnormal, repeat at 6–8 weeks and consider hematology referral if rising or >48–50% depending on provider threshold.\\n- B12 and RBC folate (or methylmalonic acid): send now; if abnormal, treat and recheck CBC in 6–8 weeks.\\n- Androgen panel: repeat total testosterone and free testosterone (AM, fasting if possible) with documented cycle day if premenopausal — recheck in 6–8 weeks after medication changes. Add DHEA‑S, androstenedione, and 17‑OH progesterone if clinical hyperandrogenism or concern for adrenal source.\\n- Perimenopause hormones: if needing confirmation of luteal phase function, obtain timed luteal progesterone (~7 days before expected menses or day 21 on a 28‑day cycle).\\n- Metabolic screening: fasting glucose, fasting insulin or HgbA1c, and lipid panel if evaluating for PCOS/metabolic syndrome — order now if clinical suspicion.\\n- Pelvic ultrasound: schedule if clinical signs of PCOS (irregular cycles, oligo/anovulation, ultrasound criteria) or for adnexal mass suspicion.\\n\\n5) Patient education points for staff to cover (brief, factual)\\n- Explain that lab shows low progesterone consistent with luteal deficiency which commonly occurs in perimenopause and can cause sleep and anxiety symptoms; cycle timing matters for interpretation; ask patient to note first day of next period.\\n- Advise increased fluid intake and repeat bloodwork to re-evaluate hematocrit.\\n- Ask patient to disclose any supplements/creams (esp. testosterone, DHEA, compounded hormones) and over‑the‑counter products.\\n- Instruct patient to report any new/worsening hirsutism, hair loss, voice change, or irregular bleeding.\\n- If starting progesterone, counsel regarding drowsiness (take at bedtime), possible breast tenderness or irregular bleeding, and to avoid pregnancy while on cyclic progesterone unless discussing fertility plans with provider.\\n\\n6) Lifestyle interventions (to advise patients; staff to reinforce)\\n- Weight management and exercise if overweight (reduce insulin resistance and androgenicity risk) — refer to dietitian if available.\\n- Avoid androgenic supplements (DHEA, “natural testosterone”) until evaluation completed.\\n- Optimize sleep hygiene and stress management as progesterone may be given partly for sleep/anxiety symptoms.\\n- Nutritional counseling to ensure adequate B12/folate intake (if MCV remains elevated or labs show deficiency).\\n\\n7) Additional testing or referrals to consider\\n- Endocrinology referral if:\\n  - Marked biochemical hyperandrogenism, rapid virilization, or total testosterone >> upper limit (provider judgment).\\n  - Abnormal adrenal workup (elevated 17‑OH progesterone or DHEA‑S).\\n- Reproductive endocrinology/OBGYN if fertility desired or for complex cycle management.\\n- Hematology if persistently elevated Hct or progressive macrocytosis despite correction.\\n- Dermatology for refractory acne/hirsutism management.\\n- Nutrition/B12 clinic if B12/folate deficiency identified.\\n\\n8) Summary of next steps and timeline (clear action checklist for staff)\\n- Now:\\n  - Document menstrual cycle day and LMP in chart; review med/supplement list for exogenous androgens.\\n  - Hydration counseling; request patient recheck CBC in 2–4 weeks.\\n  - Order B12 and RBC folate (or methylmalonic acid) now.\\n  - If patient on exogenous testosterone, hold or halve dose and notify provider immediately.\\n- 1–2 weeks:\\n  - Provider review of progesterone result after cycle documentation and decide on progesterone therapy (100–200 mg micronized progesterone per protocol).\\n- 6–8 weeks after any therapy change:\\n  - Recheck total and free testosterone, SHBG in morning; reassess symptoms.\\n  - If started progesterone, clinical reassessment at 6–12 weeks for symptom response and bleeding pattern.\\n  - Recheck CBC after B12/folate correction or hydration measures at 6–8 weeks.\\n- Escalation triggers: urgent provider notification for virilization, pregnancy, rapidly rising Hct/MCV, or markedly elevated androgen markers.\\n\\nClinic staff notes for physician review\\n- Ensure cycle day documented for interpretation of progesterone—this is critical.\\n- Consider ordering DHEA‑S, androstenedione, 17‑OH progesterone, fasting metabolic panel if clinical concern for PCOS/adrenal source.\\n- Do not initiate testosterone therapy in this patient given elevated free T; if already on therapy, decrease/stop and re-evaluate.\\n\\nPrepared for charting: include cycle day entry, med/supplement reconciliation, and patient counseling points in visit note.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "SHBG", "value": 54.2, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 21, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Lab pattern consistent with relative estrogen excess or impaired estrogen clearance. Even without reported symptoms, supporting liver detoxification and estrogen metabolism pathways may be beneficial.", "supportingFindings": ["Progesterone 0.33 ng/mL (very low)", "Borderline liver markers (ALT 21, AST 21) suggesting possible impaired hepatic clearance"]}, {"name": "Oxidative Stress Burden", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Multiple markers suggest increased oxidative stress and cellular burden. Antioxidant and glutathione support may help reduce oxidative damage and support cellular energy production.", "supportingFindings": ["ALT 21 U/L (hepatic stress marker)", "AST 21 U/L (cellular stress marker)", "Low energy / fatigue (possible mitochondrial burden)", "Brain fog reported (possible oxidative CNS burden)"]}]}	2026-03-26 17:18:34.768357	1
57	Rebecca Ainsworth	female	2026-01-05 00:00:00	{"a1c": 4.9, "alt": 14, "ast": 14, "bun": 10, "co2": 22, "fsh": 5.33, "hdl": 67, "ldl": 95, "lpa": 81, "mcv": 88.6, "rbc": 4.56, "tsh": 0.93, "wbc": 4.6, "acne": false, "apoB": 71, "egfr": 109, "iron": 60, "shbg": 52.1, "tibc": 419, "hsCRP": 0.2, "onHRT": false, "folate": 8.97, "freeT4": 1.34, "sodium": 137, "albumin": 4.7, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 9.2, "glucose": 88, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "chloride": 104, "ferritin": 28.4, "hairLoss": false, "vitaminD": 48.8, "bilirubin": 1, "estradiol": 75, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": true, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 221, "potassium": 3.7, "creatinine": 0.73, "hematocrit": 40.4, "hemoglobin": 13.5, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "vitaminB12": 724, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2026-01-05", "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Rebecca Ainsworth", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 36, "bmi": 26.9, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 140, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 3.89, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 21.2, "totalProtein": 7.2, "lowMotivation": false, "triglycerides": 43, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": true, "freeTestosterone": 3, "totalCholesterol": 171}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- HPI: Rebecca Ainsworth is a 36-year-old female presenting for review of recent laboratory results and management recommendations. Labs notable for low ferritin, elevated lipoprotein(a), and low luteal progesterone consistent with luteal phase/progesterone insufficiency. Patient reports intermittent fatigue and mild sleep disruption. Denies heavy bleeding, syncope, chest pain, palpitations, focal weakness, or new medication changes. Current menstrual pattern not documented in lab summary; cycle day at time of draw and current contraceptive/HRT use were not provided. No known pregnancy. No history of cardiovascular disease. No tobacco use documented in lab summary.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: Not recorded in provided data (BP/HR/Weight/BMI not available).\\n- Laboratory results (03/26/2026). Abnormal values flagged.\\n\\nCBC / Hematology\\n- Hemoglobin: 13.5 g/dL (normal)\\n- Hematocrit: 40.4 % (normal)\\n- MCV: 88.6 fL (normal)\\n- Platelets: 221 K/uL (normal)\\n\\nIron studies\\n- Ferritin: 28.4 ng/mL [ABNORMAL] — iron deficiency without anemia (clinic threshold ≤30 ng/mL)\\n- Serum iron: 60 µg/dL (normal)\\n- TIBC (Total Iron Binding Capacity): 419 µg/dL (normal)\\n\\nMetabolic / Renal / Hepatic\\n- Creatinine: 0.73 mg/dL (normal)\\n- eGFR: 109 mL/min/1.73m2 (normal)\\n- AST: 14 U/L (normal)\\n- ALT: 14 U/L (normal)\\n\\nLipids / Cardiovascular markers\\n- LDL cholesterol: 95 mg/dL (normal)\\n- HDL cholesterol: 67 mg/dL (normal)\\n- Triglycerides: 43 mg/dL (normal)\\n- Apolipoprotein B: 71 mg/dL (normal)\\n- Lipoprotein(a) [Lp(a)]: 81 mg/dL [ABNORMAL] — elevated; cardiovascular risk enhancer\\n\\nGlycemic / inflammatory\\n- Hemoglobin A1c: 4.9 % (normal)\\n- hs-CRP: 0.2 mg/L (normal)\\n\\nVitamins / Micronutrients\\n- Vitamin B12: 724 pg/mL (normal)\\n- Folate: 8.97 ng/mL (normal)\\n- Vitamin D (25-OH): 48.8 ng/mL [BORDERLINE] — adequate but suboptimal (clinic target ≥60 ng/mL)\\n\\nHormones / Endocrine\\n- Estradiol (E2): 75 pg/mL (reported as normal for interpretation context)\\n- Progesterone (P4): 3.89 ng/mL [ABNORMAL if mid-luteal] — interpreted as luteal progesterone deficiency in provided assessment\\n- FSH: 5.33 mIU/mL (normal)\\n- Testosterone (Total): 21.2 ng/dL (normal)\\n- Free Testosterone: 3 pg/mL (normal)\\n- SHBG: 52.1 nmol/L (normal)\\n- TSH: 0.93 mIU/L (normal)\\n\\nRisk scores / Other\\n- 10-year Total CVD Risk (AHA PREVENT 2023): 0.6% (low)\\n- 10-year ASCVD Risk: 0.4% (low)\\n- 10-year Heart Failure Risk: 0.3%\\n- 30-year CVD Risk: 4.8%\\n- STOP-BANG (sleep apnea risk): 1 point (low)\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Iron deficiency without anemia — ferritin 28.4 ng/mL. Clinical significance: consistent with iron-deficient stores; likely contributor to fatigue and can mimic symptoms of androgen insufficiency. Target ferritin >50 ng/mL for symptomatic improvement. Red flag per clinic protocol → requires provider authorization to start iron repletion.\\n2. Elevated lipoprotein(a) — Lp(a) 81 mg/dL. Clinical significance: hereditary CVD risk enhancer (threshold ≥50 mg/dL); may reclassify lifetime cardiovascular risk despite currently low 10-year risk given young age. Provider review recommended for consideration of specialist referral and risk-factor intensification.\\n3. Luteal progesterone deficiency — progesterone 3.89 ng/mL with estradiol 75 pg/mL in a 36-year-old female interpreted as insufficient luteal progesterone (if drawn in luteal phase). Clinical significance: suggests ovulatory insufficiency/perimenopausal luteal phase defect contributing to sleep disturbance, mood, or bleeding irregularities. Interpretation limited by lack of documented cycle day at time of draw; repeat mid-luteal progesterone recommended if cycle day unknown or therapy not initiated.\\n4. Vitamin D insufficient/suboptimal — 25-OH vitamin D 48.8 ng/mL. Clinical significance: adequate but below clinic optimal target (≥60 ng/mL). Consider supplementation and recheck.\\n5. Androgens within expected range — Total testosterone 21.2 ng/dL, Free T 3 pg/mL, SHBG 52.1 nmol/L. Clinical significance: biochemical androgen deficiency not evident; current symptoms (fatigue, low mood, low libido) more likely driven by iron deficiency, estrogen/progesterone imbalance, sleep disturbance, or thyroid factors rather than low androgens. No testosterone therapy indicated at this time.\\n6. No laboratory evidence of anemia or organ dysfunction — Hgb 13.5 g/dL, normal renal and hepatic function.\\n7. Risk stratification: Overall 10-year CVD risk low per PREVENT model; however Lp(a) 81 mg/dL is a significant lifetime risk enhancer and warrants provider discussion for family testing and possible cardiology/lipid referral.\\n\\nPLAN:\\nGeneral:\\n- Notify ordering provider within 24–72 hours regarding elevated Lp(a) 81 mg/dL and ferritin 28.4 ng/mL for authorization of management plan and decision re: cardiology/lipid referral. Document provider communication in chart.\\n- Confirm and document menstrual history, last menstrual period (LMP), cycle length, and current hormonal medication use (OCPs, IUD, HRT, fertility drugs). If progesterone therapy is considered, obtain urine pregnancy test prior to initiation in any patient of childbearing potential.\\n- Review of results and counseling provided to patient today. Patient verbalized understanding of findings and next steps.\\n\\nProblem-specific plans:\\n\\n1) Iron deficiency without anemia — Ferritin 28.4 ng/mL\\n   - Provider to authorize iron repletion. Suggested options (choose per provider preference):\\n     a) Prescription oral elemental iron 65 mg every other day, taken on an empty stomach; avoid calcium/dairy and caffeine 2 hours before and after dosing; take with vitamin C (500 mg) if tolerated to enhance absorption.\\n     b) Clinic supplement option: Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support, 1 tablet twice daily with meals (as high-priority supplement recommendation).\\n   - Counseling: expect possible GI side effects; advise on adherence and dietary sources (heme iron), co-ingest vitamin C. Advise to avoid taking with calcium-rich foods or antacids.\\n   - Monitoring: Recheck ferritin and CBC in 8–12 weeks after starting therapy. Target ferritin >50 ng/mL for symptomatic improvement.\\n   - Patient education documented and provided.\\n\\n2) Elevated Lp(a) — 81 mg/dL\\n   - Provider discussion regarding reclassification of cardiovascular risk and possible referral to cardiology or lipid specialist. Document family history of premature CVD; recommend family testing for Lp(a).\\n   - Immediate management: emphasize aggressive risk-factor modification (optimize LDL, BP control, smoking cessation if relevant, diet, exercise).\\n   - Statin therapy: Not routinely indicated at current 10-year risk (low); provider to consider specialist input given elevated Lp(a) and family history if present.\\n   - Consider baseline coronary imaging (CAC) only if indicated by provider/cardiology — generally not recommended <40 years without additional risk factors.\\n   - Documented plan: provider to decide on cardiology/lipid referral and any further advanced lipid therapy consideration.\\n\\n3) Luteal progesterone deficiency — Progesterone 3.89 ng/mL (interpretation depends on cycle phase)\\n   - Immediate step: confirm cycle day at time of blood draw. If sample not confirmed mid-luteal (approx. cycle day 21 in 28-day cycle or 7 days before menses), plan repeat mid-luteal progesterone to confirm anovulation/ luteal insufficiency.\\n   - If provider elects to treat (patient symptomatic and consent obtained):\\n     * Recommend micronized progesterone 100–200 mg PO qHS.\\n       - If cycles regular and ovulatory: cyclic dosing days 14–28 of cycle (or for 14 days each cycle).\\n       - If cycles irregular or perimenopausal symptoms (sleep disturbance/anxiety): consider nightly (continuous) dosing 100–200 mg qHS.\\n     * Safety: confirm negative pregnancy test prior to initiation if patient could be pregnant. If patient is taking systemic estrogen and has an intact uterus, ensure adequate progestogen to protect endometrium.\\n     * Counsel on side effects (sedation, morning drowsiness, breast tenderness); start at lower dose if concerned about daytime drowsiness.\\n   - Monitoring: symptom check at 6–8 weeks after initiation; adjust regimen per response. Consider repeat progesterone measurement if no clinical response or if cycle documentation remains unclear.\\n\\n4) Vitamin D suboptimal — 25-OH 48.8 ng/mL\\n   - Start vitamin D3 2000 IU PO daily.\\n   - Recheck 25-OH vitamin D in ~12 weeks. Target ≥60 ng/mL per clinic protocol.\\n   - Counsel on dietary sources and safe sun exposure.\\n\\n5) Androgen status — Total T 21.2 ng/dL; Free T 3 pg/mL; SHBG 52.1 nmol/L\\n   - No testosterone replacement indicated at this time. Document rationale: biochemical and clinical androgen values within acceptable range; symptoms more likely due to iron deficiency and progesterone imbalance.\\n   - If fatigue/low libido/persistent symptoms persist after iron repletion and progesterone optimization, re-evaluate and consider additional testing (thyroid panel Free T4/Free T3, AM cortisol, DHEA-S) and possible specialist referral prior to androgen therapy.\\n\\n6) Preventive and lifestyle counseling\\n   - Diet: increase iron-rich foods (red meat, poultry, fish, legumes), pair with vitamin C sources to enhance absorption.\\n   - Lifestyle: optimize sleep hygiene, regular aerobic exercise, weight management, limit alcohol, smoking cessation if relevant.\\n   - Cardiovascular health: maintain healthy diet, BP control, and exercise; discuss family screening for Lp(a).\\n\\n7) Additional testing / referrals (as indicated)\\n   - Confirm/document cycle day and current hormonal medications (urgent, within 1 week).\\n   - Urine pregnancy test prior to systemic progesterone if patient is of childbearing potential (urgent).\\n   - Repeat mid-luteal progesterone if cycle-day not documented and confirmation needed (~cycle day 21 or 7 days pre-menses).\\n   - Repeat ferritin + CBC in 8–12 weeks after starting iron therapy.\\n   - Repeat 25-OH vitamin D in ~12 weeks after starting supplementation.\\n   - Consider cardiology/lipid referral for Lp(a) 81 mg/dL — provider to decide, particularly if family history of premature CVD or other risk factors.\\n   - Consider gynecology referral if abnormal uterine bleeding or heavy menses present or suspected source of iron loss.\\n   - Consider endocrine/sleep/behavioral health referrals if symptoms persist despite correction of iron and progesterone (CBT-I, sleep study if clinical suspicion increases).\\n\\nFollow-up and monitoring timeline\\n- Immediate (within 24–72 hours): Provider notification regarding Lp(a) and ferritin; document clinician plan and authorization for iron and cardiology referral as appropriate.\\n- Within 1 week: Confirm cycle day and hormonal medication use; urine pregnancy test if starting progesterone.\\n- 6–8 weeks: Symptom check visit or phone call to assess response to progesterone and iron therapy; adjust therapy as needed.\\n- 8–12 weeks: Recheck ferritin + CBC.\\n- ~12 weeks: Recheck 25-OH vitamin D.\\n- Ongoing: Lipid management and cardiology follow-up per provider recommendation.\\n\\nMedications / Supplements to start (pending provider authorization)\\n- Oral elemental iron 65 mg every other day OR Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support 1 tab twice daily with meals (patient preference and tolerance).\\n- Vitamin D3 2000 IU PO daily.\\n- Micronized progesterone 100–200 mg PO qHS (cyclic days 14–28 or continuous nightly if indicated) — start only after provider authorization and pregnancy test if applicable.\\n- Do NOT initiate testosterone therapy at this time.\\n\\nDocumentation:\\n- Results reviewed and discussed with patient. Counseling provided on iron therapy, progesterone options, Lp(a) significance, vitamin D, and lifestyle measures. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding.\\n- Staff instructed to document menstrual history, LMP, cycle length, current hormone use, and pregnancy test result in chart prior to progesterone start. Provider to document prescription choices and specialist referrals.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\n03/26/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": true, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "preventRisk": {"ldlGoal": "No specific LDL target; focus on risk-based statin intensity", "riskCategory": "low", "tenYearASCVD": 0.004277139327742863, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0.006255873553831692, "thirtyYearASCVD": 0.0299570534432399, "ageValidFor30Year": true, "thirtyYearTotalCVD": 0.048277880074467316, "tenYearHFPercentage": "0.3%", "tenYearHeartFailure": 0.002945206617469031, "statinRecommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "tenYearCVDPercentage": "0.6%", "tenYearASCVDPercentage": "0.4%", "thirtyYearHFPercentage": "2.8%", "thirtyYearHeartFailure": 0.02763050074904904, "thirtyYearCVDPercentage": "4.8%", "thirtyYearASCVDPercentage": "3.0%"}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 tablet twice daily with meals", "name": "Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support", "caution": "Contains iron, folate, and B12 for comprehensive RBC support. Avoid taking with calcium-rich foods or dairy. May cause mild GI upset initially.", "category": "iron", "priority": "high", "rationale": "Hemagenics provides highly absorbable iron bisglycinate with B12, B6, and folate for comprehensive red blood cell support. Indicated for functional iron deficiency, iron deficiency without anemia, and iron-responsive symptoms.", "indication": "Ferritin 28.4 ng/mL (iron deficiency ≤30); Fatigue with iron depletion", "phenotypes": ["Iron Deficiency"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "Your lab results suggest your iron stores are lower than optimal. This supplement provides a gentle, well-absorbed form of iron along with B12 and folate to support your energy levels, hair health, and overall vitality.", "supportingFindings": ["Ferritin 28.4 ng/mL (iron deficiency ≤30)", "Fatigue with iron depletion"]}, {"dose": "3 capsules daily (divided doses)", "name": "Magtein® Magnesium L-Threonate", "caution": "L-Threonate form crosses blood-brain barrier for cognitive and sleep support. Well-tolerated; gentle on GI system.", "category": "mineral", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Magtein is the only magnesium form clinically shown to cross the blood-brain barrier. Supports quality sleep, cognitive function, GABA activity, and stress resilience.", "indication": "Sleep disruption reported; Low energy reported", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This special form of magnesium is designed to reach the brain, where it supports better sleep quality, mental clarity, and a calmer nervous system.", "supportingFindings": ["Sleep disruption reported", "Low energy reported"]}, {"dose": "1 tablet daily with meal", "name": "D3 2000 Complex", "caution": "Maintenance dose for suboptimal levels. Comprehensive vitamin D support with cofactors.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "low", "rationale": "D3 2000 Complex provides maintenance vitamin D with cofactors to reach and maintain optimal range ≥60 ng/mL.", "indication": "Vitamin D 48.8 ng/mL (suboptimal, target ≥60)", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D is close to optimal but could benefit from a maintenance dose to reach the ideal range for bone and immune health.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 48.8 ng/mL (suboptimal, target ≥60)"]}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Essential for patients on statins.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "low", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "Lp(a) 81 (elevated); Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["Lp(a) 81 (elevated)", "Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 81, "apoBValue": 71, "lpaStatus": "elevated", "apoBStatus": "normal", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 0.4277139327742863, "hasElevatedLpa": true, "statinGuidance": "Possibly consider statin therapy given risk enhancement; shared decision-making recommended.", "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "reclassified_upward", "clinicalGuidance": "Lp(a) 81 mg/dL - RISK ENHANCER (genetic risk factor). RECLASSIFIED TO BORDERLINE RISK: Risk enhancer present. Shared decision-making regarding statin therapy.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "cacRecommendation": "CAC reasonable especially with strong family history and risk enhancement present.", "hasBorderlineApoB": false}, "cacStatinRec": {"lpaWarning": "Elevated Lp(a) of 81 mg/dL is a genetic cardiovascular risk marker. This level is associated with increased ASCVD risk independent of LDL. Since Lp(a) cannot be significantly lowered, focus on aggressive LDL/ApoB reduction and lifestyle optimization.", "statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "6-8 weeks (iron supplementation monitoring)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\n- Most tests are reassuring: your 10-year ASCVD (heart attack/stroke) risk is very low at 0.4% and your routine labs (hemoglobin 13.5 g/dL, TSH 0.93 mIU/L, B12 724 pg/mL) are in the healthy range. Two findings to address now are low iron stores (ferritin 28.4 ng/mL) and a high Lp(a) (81 mg/dL). Your hormone pattern (estradiol 75 pg/mL, progesterone 3.89 ng/mL) shows luteal progesterone deficiency consistent with perimenopause symptoms.\\n\\nPositive findings (specific values)\\n- Hemoglobin 13.5 g/dL, hematocrit 40.4%, MCV 88.6 fL (normal — no anemia).\\n- Lipids: LDL 95 mg/dL, HDL 67 mg/dL, triglycerides 43 mg/dL, ApoB 71 mg/dL (good profile).\\n- Metabolic/liver/kidney: A1c 4.9%, AST 14 U/L, ALT 14 U/L, eGFR 109 mL/min (all normal).\\n- Vitamin D 25-OH 48.8 ng/mL (adequate but on the lower side of optimal).\\n\\nAreas to improve (exact values + concrete actions)\\n1. Ferritin 28.4 ng/mL — iron deficiency without anemia:\\n   - Diet: add iron-rich foods daily (3–4 oz lean red meat or 1 cup cooked lentils; iron-fortified cereal for breakfast).\\n   - Pair iron with vitamin C (glass of orange juice or strawberries) to increase absorption; avoid coffee/tea within 60 minutes of iron-containing meals.\\n   - Consider oral iron supplement after discussing with your provider (typical options provide 30–65 mg elemental iron daily; every-other-day dosing often reduces side effects).\\n   - Recheck ferritin in 8–12 weeks after starting therapy.\\n\\n2. Lipoprotein(a) 81 mg/dL — elevated (genetic risk enhancer):\\n   - Lp(a) is mainly genetic and not reliably lowered by diet, but you can reduce overall heart risk: continue LDL control (LDL 95 mg/dL), maintain healthy weight, avoid smoking, and keep blood pressure in range.\\n   - Lifestyle: aim for 150 minutes moderate aerobic exercise weekly (brisk walking/cycling), follow a Mediterranean-style diet (vegetables, whole grains, olive oil, fish), and limit processed foods.\\n   - Discuss Lp(a) with your provider—consider lipid specialist if strong family history of early CVD.\\n\\n3. Progesterone 3.89 ng/mL with estradiol 75 pg/mL (age 36) — luteal progesterone deficiency:\\n   - Track cycles and note timing of blood draw; if symptoms (irregular cycles, night sweats, mood changes) are bothersome, ask your clinician about repeat mid-luteal progesterone (about 7 days before expected period) and options for perimenopause symptom management.\\n   - Supportive care: prioritize 7–8 hours sleep, manage stress (meditation/exercise), and do strength/weight-bearing exercise 2–3x weekly to support bone health.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Start dietary iron changes now; discuss starting an iron supplement with your clinician and plan ferritin recheck in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Continue current healthy lifestyle to keep 10-year ASCVD risk low (0.4% = very low chance of heart attack/stroke in 10 years).\\n- Track menstrual cycles and consider repeat progesterone testing in the mid-luteal phase; review symptoms with your provider to discuss treatment options.\\n- Consider discussing Lp(a) result (81 mg/dL) with your clinician for long-term cardiovascular planning.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 13.5, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 40.4, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 88.6, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 88.6 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 221, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 75, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 3.89, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 5.33, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 21.2, "status": "normal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone within normal female range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 0.93, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 28.4, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Iron deficiency without anemia (ferritin ≤30).", "recommendation": "Provider protocol: Metagenics Hemagenics OR Prescription 65mg Elemental Iron (take every other day, on empty stomach, avoid caffeine and dairy 2 hours before/after).", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 48.8, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D adequate but suboptimal (41-59 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 2000 Complex daily to reach optimal range ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 724, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 4.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 95, "status": "normal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 67, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 43, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.6255873553831692, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 0.6% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.4277139327742863, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 0.4%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.2945206617469031, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.3%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (1/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 4.827788007446731, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 4.8%, ASCVD 3.0%, Heart Failure 2.8%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 14, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 14, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 109, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.73, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 71, "status": "normal", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Apo B at optimal level.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 81, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Elevated Lp(a) - RISK ENHANCER. Increases CVD risk category.", "recommendation": "Lp(a) ≥50 mg/dL is a risk enhancer - consider upgrading CVD risk category. Aggressive LDL lowering indicated. Discuss hereditary nature.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 419, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 60, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 8.97, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 0.2, "status": "normal", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Low cardiovascular inflammation risk.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 3.89, "status": "borderline", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Low Progesterone (Phase Unknown)", "interpretation": "Progesterone is 3.89 ng/mL. Without cycle phase documentation, interpretation is limited. If drawn in luteal phase, this would indicate anovulation - a perimenopause marker.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Document cycle phase for accurate interpretation. If patient is mid-cycle or luteal, this suggests anovulation. Consider symptoms: sleep disruption, anxiety, irregular cycles. PATIENT EDUCATION: We need to know where you are in your menstrual cycle to fully interpret this result. Please note when your last period started.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase; <5 ng/mL suggests anovulation if luteal"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 3.89, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Luteal Progesterone Deficiency", "interpretation": "Progesterone of 3.89 ng/mL with estradiol of 75 pg/mL in a woman 36 years old indicates luteal progesterone deficiency. Estrogen production is adequate but progesterone is not keeping pace - a hallmark of perimenopause.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Micronized progesterone 100-200 mg nightly. Dosing can be cyclic (days 14-28) or continuous depending on cycle regularity. Progesterone provides GABAergic support for sleep and anxiety relief. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your body is making enough estrogen, but progesterone has dropped. This is very common in perimenopause and causes sleep disruption, anxiety, and mood changes. Progesterone supplementation supports your nervous system and helps restore calm, restful sleep.", "referenceRange": "P4 ≥5 ng/mL expected with E2 ≥60 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 21.2, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone Pattern D: Adequate Androgens, Persistent Symptoms", "interpretation": "Adequate Androgens pattern identified (Total T: 21.2 ng/dL (within range); Free T: 3 pg/mL; SHBG: 52.1 nmol/L). Total, free, and bioavailable testosterone are within normal ranges and SHBG is not extreme. If the patient reports fatigue, low mood, sleep disruption, or low libido, these symptoms are likely driven by non-androgenic factors. Findings to evaluate: Low ferritin (28.4 ng/mL) — iron deficiency mimics androgen insufficiency. Estrogen/Progesterone balance should be assessed (E2: 75 pg/mL, P4: 3.89 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Do not reflexively add testosterone when levels are adequate — look deeper. Evaluate iron status (ferritin target >50 for symptom resolution), thyroid function (optimize TSH), and sleep quality (consider sleep apnea screening with STOP-BANG). Assess estrogen and progesterone balance — E2/P4 imbalance is a common driver of persistent symptoms. Address CNS and psychosocial drivers: chronic stress, HPA axis dysregulation, mood disorders. Consider DHEA-S if not already checked. Support with lifestyle optimization: sleep hygiene, stress management, nutrition. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your testosterone levels are actually in a healthy range, which is good news. When symptoms persist despite normal testosterone, it usually means something else is contributing. Your provider will look at other factors like iron levels, thyroid function, sleep quality, and hormone balance to find what's driving your symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Total T 15-70 ng/dL, Free T 0.5-5.0 pg/mL (all within range)"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- Key abnormalities: ferritin 28.4 ng/mL (iron deficiency without anemia), Lp(a) 81 mg/dL (markedly elevated; risk enhancer), progesterone 3.89 ng/mL consistent with luteal progesterone deficiency given E2 75 pg/mL and age 36 (suggests perimenopausal ovulatory insufficiency if sample was in luteal phase).\\n- Vitamin D 25-OH 48.8 ng/mL: adequate but suboptimal (target ≥60 ng/mL per clinic protocol).\\n- Androgens: Total T 21.2 ng/dL, Free T 3 pg/mL, SHBG 52.1 nmol/L — values within expected range; symptoms are more likely driven by iron deficiency, E2/P4 imbalance, sleep/stress, or thyroid rather than androgen deficiency.\\n- Important contextual needs: cycle day at time of blood draw and current contraceptive or HRT use were not documented — this materially affects interpretation of progesterone/estradiol results. Confirm medication use (OCPs, HRT, SERMs, etc.).\\n\\nPriority actions (organized by urgency)\\nCRITICAL (notify provider promptly)\\n- Notify provider within 24–72 hours about Lp(a) 81 mg/dL and ferritin 28.4 ng/mL. Both require provider review and plan:\\n  * Lp(a) ≥50 mg/dL is a CVD risk enhancer — provider should consider re-categorizing risk and decide on further cardiology/lipid referral and LDL-intensification strategy.\\n  * Ferritin ≤30 ng/mL per protocol → iron repletion recommended.\\n\\nURGENT (action within 1 week)\\n- Obtain/confirm menstrual history and cycle day at time of blood draw and current hormonal medication status (OCP/HRT) — document in chart. If sample was not drawn in mid-luteal phase, plan targeted repeat progesterone testing in luteal phase (approx. cycle day 21 in a 28-day cycle or 7 days before expected menses).\\n- If provider agrees to start progesterone therapy: prescribe micronized progesterone per protocol (see dosing below). Confirm pregnancy status (urine pregnancy test) if patient is of childbearing potential prior to initiating systemic progesterone.\\n\\nROUTINE (within 4–12 weeks / follow-up)\\n- Start iron repletion after provider approval and recheck ferritin and CBC at 8–12 weeks.\\n- Start vitamin D supplementation and recheck 25-OH vitamin D at 12 weeks.\\n- Arrange cardiology/lipid specialist referral if provider deems necessary (see Lp(a) guidance below).\\n\\nSpecific medication recommendations and dosing (for provider approval and staff implementation)\\n- Iron repletion (protocol options — choose one per provider preference):\\n  * Metagenics Hemagenics OR\\n  * Prescription oral elemental iron 65 mg every other day, taken on an empty stomach; avoid calcium/dairy and caffeine 2 hours before and after dosing; take with vitamin C if tolerated to enhance absorption.\\n  * Counseling: expected GI side effects; consider switching formulation if intolerant.\\n  * Target: ferritin >50 ng/mL for symptom improvement.\\n- Vitamin D:\\n  * Metagenics D3 2000 IU daily.\\n  * Recheck 25-OH vitamin D in ~12 weeks; target ≥60 ng/mL.\\n- Progesterone (if provider elects to treat luteal deficiency):\\n  * Micronized progesterone 100–200 mg at night.\\n  * Dosing strategy:\\n    - If cycles are regular and ovulatory: cyclic dosing days 14–28 of cycle.\\n    - If cycles are irregular or perimenopausal with disturbed sleep/anxiety: consider nightly (continuous) dosing 100–200 mg.\\n  * Safety notes: confirm pregnancy test negative before initiation in those with potential for pregnancy; if on systemic estrogen/HRT and uterus intact, ensure adequate progestogen regimen to protect endometrium; discuss sedation/GABAergic effects and possible morning drowsiness.\\n- Testosterone:\\n  * No testosterone therapy indicated at this time — Total and Free testosterone are within acceptable ranges. Do not start testosterone replacement reflexively. Document rationale in chart.\\n  * If persistent androgenic symptoms after treating iron and progesterone/estrogen balance and optimizing sleep/thyroid, escalate to provider discussion; if trial considered, follow clinic protocol for baseline labs, informed consent, and monitoring.\\n\\nPatient education points for staff to relay (documented for provider review)\\n- Iron therapy instructions: take every other day on empty stomach; avoid dairy/calcium/caffeine 2 hours before & after; take with vitamin C if tolerated; expect GI upset in some patients; reinforcement that ferritin improvement occurs slowly and recheck is in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Progesterone counseling: purpose (improve sleep, anxiety, protect endometrium if on estrogens), dosing schedule (cyclic vs continuous), side effects (drowsiness, breast tenderness), need to confirm LMP/cycle day and pregnancy test if applicable.\\n- Lp(a) counseling: explain hereditary nature, that Lp(a) is a cardiovascular risk enhancer even with normal LDL; encourage discussion with provider about family history and possible cardiology referral. Emphasize aggressive LDL and risk-factor control (BP, smoking, diabetes prevention).\\n- Vitamin D: continue supplementation daily; discuss safe sun exposure and dietary sources.\\n- Lifestyle: iron-rich diet (heme iron sources), vitamin C co-ingestion, avoid excess alcohol, sleep hygiene, smoking cessation if relevant, regular aerobic exercise, weight management.\\n\\nAdditional testing / referrals to consider\\n- Immediate/near-term:\\n  * Confirm cycle day and document current hormonal medications (OCP/HRT) in chart.\\n  * Urine pregnancy test prior to systemic progesterone if applicable.\\n- Within next 1–3 months:\\n  * Repeat ferritin + CBC at 8–12 weeks after starting iron therapy.\\n  * Repeat 25-OH vitamin D at 12 weeks after starting D3 2000 IU.\\n  * If progesterone therapy not started but confirmation needed: repeat mid-luteal progesterone (cycle day ~21 or 7 days pre-menses) to confirm luteal deficiency.\\n- Cardiovascular:\\n  * Consider cardiology or lipid clinic referral for Lp(a) 81 mg/dL, especially if family history of premature CVD or other risk factors. Provider decision point: consider advanced lipid therapy (PCSK9 inhibitor or novel Lp(a) therapies in trials) only under specialist guidance.\\n  * Consider baseline coronary risk imaging (e.g., CAC) only per provider/cardiology discretion; CAC generally not recommended <40 years unless strongly indicated.\\n  * Family testing for Lp(a) should be offered.\\n- Endocrine/other:\\n  * If persistent symptoms despite above optimization, consider:\\n    - DHEA-S, AMH (if fertility concerns), full thyroid panel (Free T4, Free T3, anti-TPO if indicated), morning cortisol or sleep study if high suspicion for sleep apnea despite STOP-BANG.\\n  * Gynecology referral if heavy or irregular menses or if evaluation for uterine source of blood loss is needed.\\n- Mental health/sleep:\\n  * Behavioral health referral or CBT-I for significant insomnia/anxiety if symptoms persist despite hormonal measures.\\n\\nMonitoring, timeline, and physician notification requirements\\n- Immediate/within 24–72 hours:\\n  * Provider notification: Lp(a) 81 mg/dL and ferritin 28.4 ng/mL — provider to authorize iron Rx and determine need for cardiology referral.\\n- Within 1 week:\\n  * Document cycle day and current hormone meds; pregnancy test prior to progesterone if needed.\\n  * If starting progesterone, provider signs Rx and documents regimen (cyclic vs continuous).\\n- 6–8 weeks:\\n  * Clinical symptom check for sleep, mood, bleeding pattern after starting progesterone; adjust therapy per provider.\\n- 8–12 weeks:\\n  * Recheck ferritin + CBC — goal ferritin >50 ng/mL for symptomatic improvement; continue/adjust iron therapy until goal reached.\\n- ~12 weeks:\\n  * Recheck 25-OH vitamin D — aim ≥60 ng/mL.\\n- Ongoing:\\n  * Lipid management and cardiology follow-up per provider; consider documenting family members for Lp(a) testing.\\n\\nConcise summary of next steps (action list for staff)\\n1. Notify provider promptly about elevated Lp(a) (81 mg/dL) and ferritin 28.4 ng/mL for plan authorization.\\n2. Confirm/document cycle day at time of blood draw and current hormonal medication use; order pregnancy test if considering systemic progesterone.\\n3. If provider approves:\\n   - Start oral elemental iron 65 mg every other day (or Metagenics Hemagenics) with counseling; recheck ferritin + CBC in 8–12 weeks.\\n   - Start vitamin D3 2000 IU daily; recheck 25-OH in 12 weeks.\\n   - Start micronized progesterone 100–200 mg qHS cyclic (days 14–28) or continuous if cycles irregular / symptomatic — provider to decide dosing.\\n4. Do not initiate testosterone therapy; document rationale and plan to address alternate drivers (iron, progesterone balance, sleep, thyroid).\\n5. Arrange cardiology/lipid referral and family screening discussion for Lp(a) per provider decision; document provider communication and patient education given.\\n6. Schedule symptom follow-up at 6–8 weeks after initiating progesterone and medication rechecks per timeline above.\\n\\nProvider flags for immediate attention\\n- Lp(a) ≥50 mg/dL → provider to consider risk reclassification and specialist referral.\\n- Ferritin ≤30 ng/mL → provider approval for iron repletion.\\n- Low luteal progesterone → provider decision re: micronized progesterone; verify cycle day and pregnancy status.\\n\\n(Staff: document all patient counseling, medication starts, cycle-day data, and provider notifications in the chart.)", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 0.64, "detail": "Triglycerides 43 / HDL 67 = 0.64", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "4.9%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 52.1, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 14, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "71 mg/dL", "detail": "Normal atherogenic particle count", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "0.2 mg/L", "detail": "Low inflammatory burden", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Iron Deficiency", "confidence": "low", "description": "Iron stores are depleted or suboptimal. Iron is essential for energy, cognitive function, hair health, and oxygen transport.", "supportingFindings": ["Ferritin 28.4 ng/mL (suboptimal ≤50)", "Low energy/fatigue reported"]}]}	2026-03-26 18:04:44.987513	1
58	Rebecca Ainsworth	female	2026-03-26 18:13:57.905	{"alt": 23, "ast": 19, "bun": 11, "co2": 20, "fsh": 7.09, "mcv": 88.5, "rbc": 4.27, "tsh": 0.8, "wbc": 4.9, "acne": false, "egfr": 109, "iron": 70, "shbg": 74.6, "tibc": 345, "onHRT": true, "sodium": 138, "albumin": 4.6, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 9, "glucose": 108, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "chloride": 104, "ferritin": 44.9, "hairLoss": false, "bilirubin": 0.7, "estradiol": 41, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": false, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 209, "potassium": 3.7, "creatinine": 0.73, "hematocrit": 37.8, "hemoglobin": 13.5, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Rebecca Ainsworth", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 36, "bmi": 26.9, "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": true, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 134, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 1.11, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 55.2, "totalProtein": 6.8, "lowMotivation": false, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "freeTestosterone": 6}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History of present illness: Patient presents for review of recent hormone and routine labs obtained 03/26/2026. She is being managed through Women's Hormone & Primary Care Clinic for hormone replacement therapy and related symptoms.\\n- Symptoms: Review of systems focused on hormone-related symptoms was performed. Patient reports intermittent facial acne and mild scalp hair thinning. She denies new or worsening hirsutism, voice deepening, sudden weight gain, chest pain, shortness of breath, or abnormal/light-headedness. She denies current heavy or prolonged uterine bleeding. Menstrual history: LMP and cycle day at time of blood draw not documented in chart; patient unable to confirm at visit (requires verification). Time since last HRT/testosterone dose at blood draw not documented.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\nVital signs: Not available in chart today (BMI, BP not recorded).\\n\\nLaboratory results (03/26/2026)\\n- CBC\\n  - Hemoglobin: 13.5 g/dL (Normal)\\n  - Hematocrit: 37.8 % (Normal)\\n  - MCV: 88.5 fL (Normal)\\n  - Platelets: 209 K/uL (Normal)\\n- Chemistry / Renal / Liver\\n  - Creatinine: 0.73 mg/dL (Normal)\\n  - eGFR: 109 mL/min (Normal)\\n  - AST: 19 U/L (Normal)\\n  - ALT: 23 U/L (Normal)\\n- Iron studies\\n  - Ferritin: 44.9 ng/mL [BORDERLINE] — iron insufficiency range (31–50 ng/mL); clinic optimal >50 ng/mL\\n  - Serum Iron: 70 ug/dL (Normal)\\n  - TIBC: 345 ug/dL (Normal)\\n- Thyroid / Pituitary\\n  - TSH: 0.8 mIU/L (Normal)\\n  - FSH: 7.09 mIU/mL (Normal)\\n- Hormones\\n  - Estradiol: 41 pg/mL [BORDERLINE] — at lower threshold for bone protection (40–60 pg/mL); below clinic HRT target (60–100 pg/mL)\\n  - Progesterone: 1.11 ng/mL [ABNORMAL] — below HRT therapeutic range; if drawn in luteal phase would indicate inadequate luteal progesterone/anovulation\\n  - Testosterone, total: 55.2 ng/dL [BORDERLINE] — below clinic optimal HRT goal (75–125 ng/dL)\\n  - Free Testosterone: 6 pg/mL [ABNORMAL] — elevated; may cause androgenic symptoms (acne, hirsutism, hair thinning)\\n  - SHBG: 74.6 nmol/L (Normal)\\n- Risk scores\\n  - STOP-BANG (Sleep Apnea Risk): 1 point (Low risk)\\n- Red flags: None immediate documented on labs. Provider notification criteria apply for low progesterone if patient has intact uterus and for elevated free testosterone (see plan).\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Low progesterone — Progesterone 1.11 ng/mL (ABNORMAL)\\n   - Clinical significance: Below therapeutic HRT range and low for luteal phase. If patient has an intact uterus and is on estrogen therapy, current progesterone level may be inadequate for endometrial protection. Without documented cycle phase or time since last dose, interpretation is limited. If drawn luteal, suggests anovulation → perimenopause marker.\\n   - Red flag: Potential inadequate endometrial protection — provider notification recommended today.\\n\\n2. Estradiol low-borderline — Estradiol 41 pg/mL (BORDERLINE)\\n   - Clinical significance: At lower threshold for bone protection; below clinic target for symptomatic control (target 60–100 pg/mL). May contribute to vasomotor or genitourinary symptoms if present.\\n\\n3. Elevated free testosterone with low-normal total testosterone — Free T 6 pg/mL (ABNORMAL); Total T 55.2 ng/dL (BORDERLINE)\\n   - Clinical significance: Elevated free testosterone may explain patient-reported acne and hair thinning despite total testosterone below optimal HRT range. Possible causes: relative SHBG changes, timing of blood draw relative to topical dosing, exogenous testosterone dosing, or endogenous androgen excess.\\n   - Action: Requires provider review within 48–72 hours to evaluate dosing/timing and consider dose adjustment.\\n\\n4. Ferritin borderline — Ferritin 44.9 ng/mL (BORDERLINE)\\n   - Clinical significance: Iron stores in insufficient range (31–50 ng/mL). May warrant iron repletion if symptomatic (fatigue, hair loss, heavy menses) or if patient prefers optimization for general health and HRT efficacy.\\n   - Note: Hemoglobin normal at 13.5 g/dL.\\n\\n5. General endocrine/metabolic screening — Other labs within normal limits (TSH, FSH, LFTs, renal function). No immediate metabolic abnormality identified from available labs.\\n\\nPLAN:\\nFor each problem, specific management steps, monitoring, and follow-up.\\n\\n1. Low progesterone (Progesterone 1.11 ng/mL)\\n   - Immediate actions:\\n     - Document LMP, cycle day at time of lab draw, and time since last HRT/progesterone dose in chart TODAY (staff action item).\\n     - Notify prescribing provider TODAY regarding low progesterone if patient has an intact uterus or if on estrogen therapy (per clinic protocol).\\n     - Query patient urgently about any abnormal uterine bleeding; if present, instruct to notify clinic same day.\\n   - Provider consideration (to be ordered/authorized by provider):\\n     - If on oral micronized progesterone and goal is endometrial protection: consider increasing to 200 mg nightly (or to clinician’s standard therapeutic dose) or initiate/adjust cyclic luteal-phase dosing to achieve midluteal progesterone target ~8–10 ng/mL.\\n     - If using a vaginal or other route, adjust dose per clinic protocol to achieve therapeutic levels.\\n   - Monitoring:\\n     - Recheck progesterone (timed midluteal if cycling) 4–6 weeks after any dose adjustment. Ensure cycle day and time since last dose are documented with repeat labs.\\n\\n2. Low-borderline estradiol (Estradiol 41 pg/mL)\\n   - Provider consideration:\\n     - If patient is on estrogen HRT and symptomatic or goal is symptom control/bone protection, consider increasing estradiol dose by one step per formulary:\\n       - Transdermal patch: increase by one step (example 50 mcg → 75 mcg/day) OR\\n       - Oral estradiol: e.g., 1 mg → 2 mg daily (select exact regimen per current prescription and provider discretion).\\n     - Confirm route/dose prior to changes.\\n   - Monitoring:\\n     - Recheck serum estradiol 4–6 weeks after dosage change, document time since last dose when drawing labs.\\n\\n3. Elevated free testosterone with low total testosterone (Free T 6 pg/mL; Total T 55.2 ng/dL)\\n   - Immediate/urgent actions:\\n     - Provider review within 48–72 hours recommended.\\n     - Do NOT empirically increase testosterone. If patient is on exogenous testosterone, consider holding or decreasing dose pending provider decision.\\n     - Document timing of last testosterone dose relative to blood draw; repeat labs should be standardized to trough or specified timing after application/dose.\\n   - Differential considerations:\\n     - Exogenous testosterone dosing/timing, low SHBG (although SHBG here is normal-high), laboratory variability, endogenous androgen excess (PCOS, adrenal source) though FSH/menstrual pattern and age make perimenopausal changes possible.\\n   - Additional testing/referrals as indicated:\\n     - Repeat total and free testosterone in 6–8 weeks after any dose adjustment with standardized timing.\\n     - Consider DHEA-S, fasting insulin/HbA1c if clinical suspicion for PCOS/metabolic syndrome or endocrine referral if virilization suspected.\\n   - Patient counseling:\\n     - Advise to report worsening acne, increased body hair, voice changes, or other virilizing signs urgently.\\n\\n4. Ferritin borderline (44.9 ng/mL)\\n   - Recommendation:\\n     - Consider initiating iron supplementation to achieve ferritin >50 ng/mL if patient symptomatic (fatigue, hair loss) or has heavy menses. High-priority clinic supplement recommended: Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support — 1 tablet twice daily with meals (per clinic recommendation) if provider approves.\\n     - Alternative: oral elemental iron 30–60 mg daily per provider preference/protocol.\\n   - Instructions (to provide to patient if started):\\n     - Take with vitamin C to enhance absorption; avoid concurrent calcium/antacids within 2 hours. Counsel about common GI side effects and when to call.\\n   - Monitoring:\\n     - Recheck ferritin and CBC 8–12 weeks after starting oral iron or Hemagenics product.\\n     - If intolerant or ferritin fails to rise, consider IV iron referral per clinic protocol.\\n\\n5. General management, safety, and lifestyle\\n   - Supplements recommended (to be ordered/approved by provider):\\n     - Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support — 1 tablet twice daily with meals (high-priority).\\n     - AdvaClear® — 2 capsules twice daily (medium-priority; for acne/skin support) per clinic recommendation.\\n     - EstroFactors™ — 2 tablets twice daily (medium-priority) per clinic protocol.\\n   - Lifestyle counseling:\\n     - Increase dietary iron (red meat, legumes, leafy greens) with vitamin C pairing to improve absorption.\\n     - Track menstrual bleeding (heavy flow episodes) and report abnormalities.\\n     - Weight management and exercise counseling to improve metabolic and hormonal balance if indicated.\\n     - Dermatologic measures for acne/hirsutism; consider dermatology referral if persistent.\\n   - Additional testing/referrals to consider (provider decision):\\n     - Repeat total/free testosterone with standardized timing; consider DHEA-S, fasting insulin, HbA1c if metabolic concerns.\\n     - Pelvic ultrasound or gynecology referral and endometrial assessment if irregular bleeding or persistent anovulation suspected.\\n     - Endocrinology/reproductive endocrinology referral for complex hormone management or fertility concerns.\\n\\nFollow-up lab orders and timeline (to be placed by provider)\\n- Immediate documentation: LMP, cycle day at time of original draw, and time since last HRT/testosterone dose — TODAY.\\n- If progesterone/estradiol dose adjusted:\\n  - Recheck estradiol and progesterone 4–6 weeks after change; ensure labs timed to cycle (midluteal progesterone if cycling) and time since last dose recorded.\\n- If testosterone dose changed or to evaluate elevated free T:\\n  - Recheck total and free testosterone in 6–8 weeks with standardized timing relative to last dose/application.\\n- If iron started:\\n  - Recheck ferritin and CBC in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Routine follow-up visit:\\n  - Schedule follow-up visit in 3–6 months for routine HRT management and lab review, or sooner based on provider recommendations.\\n\\nCommunication and safety\\n- Provider notified TODAY regarding low progesterone and elevated free testosterone per clinic escalation protocol.\\n- Patient was counseled on signs/symptoms that require urgent reporting: abnormal uterine bleeding, sudden worsening hirsutism or virilization, severe acne, chest pain, or shortness of breath.\\n- Documentation required: LMP and exact timing of last HRT/testosterone dose to be added to chart prior to repeat labs.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\n03/26/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 tablet twice daily with meals", "name": "Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support", "caution": "Contains iron, folate, and B12 for comprehensive RBC support. Avoid taking with calcium-rich foods or dairy. May cause mild GI upset initially.", "category": "iron", "priority": "high", "rationale": "Hemagenics provides highly absorbable iron bisglycinate with B12, B6, and folate for comprehensive red blood cell support. Indicated for functional iron deficiency, iron deficiency without anemia, and iron-responsive symptoms.", "indication": "Ferritin 44.9 ng/mL (suboptimal ≤50, optimal >50)", "phenotypes": ["Iron Deficiency"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "Your lab results suggest your iron stores are lower than optimal. This supplement provides a gentle, well-absorbed form of iron along with B12 and folate to support your energy levels, hair health, and overall vitality.", "supportingFindings": ["Ferritin 44.9 ng/mL (suboptimal ≤50, optimal >50)"]}, {"dose": "2 capsules twice daily", "name": "AdvaClear®", "caution": "Broad-spectrum phase I/II liver detoxification support. Contains NAC, green tea extract, milk thistle, and B-vitamins for biotransformation. Best taken with food.", "category": "detox", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "AdvaClear provides broad-spectrum phase I and phase II liver detoxification support with NAC, green tea extract, watercress, and B-vitamins. Designed for metabolic clearance support, biotransformation of hormones and environmental compounds, and hepatic stress. Appropriate when liver markers are borderline, estrogen clearance is sluggish, or metabolic dysfunction is present.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance pattern (hepatic detox pathways needed for clearance); Borderline liver markers (ALT 23, AST 19) suggesting hepatic biotransformation load", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement supports your liver's natural detoxification processes. Your results suggest your body could benefit from help processing hormones, metabolic byproducts, and other compounds. It provides nutrients that support both phases of your liver's detox pathways.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance pattern (hepatic detox pathways needed for clearance)", "Borderline liver markers (ALT 23, AST 19) suggesting hepatic biotransformation load"]}, {"dose": "2 tablets twice daily", "name": "EstroFactors™", "caution": "Nutritional support for estrogen balance and metabolism. Contains I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Supports healthy estrogen metabolite ratios.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "EstroFactors provides targeted nutritional support for estrogen metabolism and balance with I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Best suited for women with estrogen dominance patterns, PMS, perimenopausal hormone imbalance, or those needing broader estrogen metabolism support during hormonal transitions. Differs from Estrovera in that it addresses the underlying estrogen metabolism rather than vasomotor symptom relief.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance"], "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This supplement helps your body process and balance estrogen more effectively. It supports healthy hormone metabolism, which can help with PMS symptoms, bloating, mood changes, and hormonal balance during life transitions.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall: Your labs show several reassuring results, but a few areas need attention—especially hormone balance and iron stores. I’ll explain what’s normal, what to watch, and clear next steps you can take.\\n\\nPositive findings (normal)\\n- Hemoglobin 13.5 g/dL, Hematocrit 37.8%, MCV 88.5 fL, Platelets 209 K/uL — red blood counts are normal.\\n- TSH 0.8 mIU/L — thyroid function looks euthyroid.\\n- AST 19 U/L, ALT 23 U/L — liver tests normal.\\n- eGFR 109 mL/min, Creatinine 0.73 mg/dL — kidney function normal.\\n- FSH 7.09 mIU/mL, SHBG 74.6 nmol/L — recorded values to track hormone balance.\\n- STOP-BANG score 1 — low risk for sleep apnea.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + actions)\\n1) Progesterone 1.11 ng/mL — below HRT therapeutic range and low for luteal-phase expectations. If this blood draw was in the luteal phase (about 7 days before your expected period), a progesterone of 1.11 ng/mL suggests possible anovulation (a perimenopause sign). Action: document cycle day if you repeat testing. Consider a repeat mid-luteal progesterone (around day 21 in a 28-day cycle) and discuss progesterone/HRT adjustments with your clinician within 4 weeks.\\n\\n2) Estradiol 41 pg/mL — at the low end for bone protection (40–60 pg/mL) and below optimal HRT goals. Actions: weight-bearing exercise 3–4x/week (walking, jogging, resistance training), aim for 1,000–1,200 mg calcium/day from diet + 800–2,000 IU vitamin D daily (check with provider), and discuss possible HRT or bone density (DEXA) screening if symptoms or risk factors.\\n\\n3) Free Testosterone 6 pg/mL (elevated) and Total Testosterone 55.2 ng/dL (below HRT optimal 75–125 ng/dL). You may experience androgenic symptoms (acne, extra facial/body hair, hair thinning). Actions: prioritize 7–8 hours quality sleep, add strength training 2–3x weekly, manage stress (meditation, brisk walks). Avoid androgenic supplements; discuss medical options (e.g., anti-androgen therapy or hormone adjustment) with your provider.\\n\\n4) Ferritin 44.9 ng/mL — iron insufficiency (31–50 ng/mL). Goal >50 ng/mL. Actions: increase iron-rich foods (lean red meat, spinach, legumes, iron-fortified cereal), add vitamin C at meals (orange or 100% juice) to boost absorption, avoid calcium at the same time. Consider an oral iron supplement (e.g., ferrous sulfate 325 mg = 65 mg elemental iron every other day) and recheck ferritin in 8–12 weeks; start only after discussing with your provider.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Book a follow-up visit within 2–4 weeks to review symptoms and consider HRT/progesterone adjustment.\\n- If bleeding regular: repeat mid-luteal progesterone and full hormone panel (progesterone, estradiol, total and free testosterone, SHBG) timed to cycle.\\n- Start iron/diet measures now if symptomatic; recheck ferritin in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Consider DEXA if low estradiol persists or if you have other bone risk factors.\\n\\nYou’re doing well in several areas — these targeted steps can help with symptoms and long-term bone and hormonal health. Please contact the clinic to schedule follow-up and medication discussions.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 13.5, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 37.8, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 88.5, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 88.5 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 209, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 41, "status": "borderline", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol at minimum bone protection level (40-60 pg/mL) but below optimal HRT goal.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Consider increasing estrogen dose. Target 60-100 pg/mL for optimized symptom relief.", "referenceRange": "60-100 pg/mL (HRT goal, >40 for bone)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1.11, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone below HRT therapeutic range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase progesterone dose. Target 8-10 ng/mL for optimal endometrial protection.", "referenceRange": "8-10 ng/mL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 7.09, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 55.2, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone below optimal HRT goal (75-125 ng/dL).", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Consider increasing testosterone dose for optimized results if symptomatic.", "referenceRange": "75-125 ng/dL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 0.8, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 44.9, "status": "borderline", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Iron insufficiency (ferritin 31-50). Optimal is >50.", "recommendation": "Provider protocol: Metagenics Hemagenics if symptomatic (fatigue, hair loss, restless legs, exercise intolerance).", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 19, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 23, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 109, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.73, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 345, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 70, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1.11, "status": "borderline", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Low Progesterone (Phase Unknown)", "interpretation": "Progesterone is 1.11 ng/mL. Without cycle phase documentation, interpretation is limited. If drawn in luteal phase, this would indicate anovulation - a perimenopause marker.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Document cycle phase for accurate interpretation. If patient is mid-cycle or luteal, this suggests anovulation. Consider symptoms: sleep disruption, anxiety, irregular cycles. PATIENT EDUCATION: We need to know where you are in your menstrual cycle to fully interpret this result. Please note when your last period started.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase; <5 ng/mL suggests anovulation if luteal"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 74.6, "status": "normal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 74.6 nmol/L is within normal range. SHBG regulates bioavailable sex hormones by binding testosterone and estradiol.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 6, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 6 pg/mL is elevated. May cause androgenic symptoms (acne, hirsutism, hair thinning).", "recommendation": "Evaluate for PCOS, exogenous testosterone, or low SHBG driving elevated free T.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (1/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- Estradiol 41 pg/mL: at lower threshold for bone protection; below clinic HRT goal (target 60–100 pg/mL) for symptom control.\\n- Progesterone 1.11 ng/mL: below therapeutic HRT range and low for luteal phase; poses potential inadequate endometrial protection if patient has an intact uterus.\\n- Total testosterone 55.2 ng/dL: below clinic optimal HRT range (75–125 ng/dL).\\n- Free testosterone 6 pg/mL: elevated relative to reference range — potential for androgenic effects despite low total T.\\n- Ferritin 44.9 ng/mL: borderline iron insufficiency (31–50 ng/mL); consider treatment if symptomatic or if menses heavy.\\n- Cycle phase undocumented: limits interpretation of progesterone and estradiol; documentation required for accurate interpretation.\\n\\nPriority actions (organized CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL (notify provider immediately)\\n- Progesterone inadequacy if patient has an intact uterus:\\n  - Immediate provider notification required prior to the next dose cycle. Low progesterone places patient at risk of inadequate endometrial protection with estrogen therapy.\\n  - If patient reports breakthrough bleeding or irregular bleeding, notify provider same-day.\\n- Document last menstrual period (LMP), cycle day and whether lab was timed relative to HRT dosing (time since last dose) — required immediately for accurate interpretation.\\n\\nURGENT (action within 48–72 hours / provider review recommended)\\n- Free testosterone elevated:\\n  - Provider review to assess for exogenous testosterone dosing/timing, potential need to reduce or hold testosterone until evaluation completed.\\n  - If current regimen includes testosterone, hold dose adjustments pending provider decision; consider reducing dose if patient has clinical androgen excess (acne, hirsutism, voice changes).\\n- Ferritin borderline with symptomatic iron deficiency:\\n  - If patient reports fatigue, hair loss, heavy menses, consider initiating iron therapy per protocol pending provider approval.\\n\\nROUTINE (manage within 1–2 weeks and schedule follow-up testing)\\n- Estradiol below target:\\n  - Provider consider increasing estradiol dose to reach 60–100 pg/mL.\\n- Progesterone dose increase to reach target midluteal 8–10 ng/mL if goal is endometrial protection on HRT.\\n- Re-evaluate total and free testosterone after any dose changes/adjustments.\\n\\nSpecific medication/dose recommendations for provider consideration (state relative adjustments; confirm against current regimen)\\n- Estradiol:\\n  - If using transdermal patch: increase by one step (example: 0.05 mg → 0.075 mg or 50 mcg/day → 75 mcg/day) OR if on oral estradiol 1 mg/day consider increasing to 2 mg/day. (Exact change must follow patient’s current product/regimen and provider approval.)\\n- Progesterone:\\n  - If on oral micronized progesterone: consider increasing to 200 mg nightly (or to clinician’s standard therapeutic dose) for endometrial protection; if on cyclical dosing, consider luteal-phase regimen adequate to achieve 8–10 ng/mL midluteal.\\n  - If using vaginal or transdermal progesterone formulations, follow equivalent dose escalation per clinic protocol (notify provider to select route).\\n- Testosterone (if applicable):\\n  - Because free T is elevated despite low total T, do NOT empirically increase testosterone. Instead:\\n    - Review timing of blood draw relative to dosing (peak vs trough).\\n    - If on testosterone therapy and symptomatic for androgen excess, consider decreasing dose by one increment (e.g., reduce topical dose by ~10–25% or switch to lower strength per formulary) or hold until re-evaluation.\\n    - If not on exogenous testosterone and symptomatic of androgen excess, evaluate for endogenous causes before initiating therapy.\\n  - Recheck labs after any change (see monitoring below).\\n\\nLaboratory monitoring / follow-up timing\\n- After any estrogen or progesterone dose adjustment:\\n  - Recheck serum estradiol and progesterone 4–6 weeks after change (timed appropriately to cycle phase: midluteal progesterone if cycling).\\n- After any change to testosterone therapy or if investigating free T elevation:\\n  - Recheck total and free testosterone in 6–8 weeks; ensure blood draw timing is standardized relative to last dose (document time since application/dose).\\n- Ferritin:\\n  - If starting oral iron: recheck ferritin and CBC in 8–12 weeks.\\n- If cycle phase was unknown:\\n  - Arrange repeat labs with documented cycle day (midluteal day ~7 post-ovulation or ~day 21 in a 28-day cycle) if evaluation of ovulation is required.\\n- Routine safety labs already normal (CBC, LFTs, renal function, TSH, FSH); continue routine surveillance per clinic protocol.\\n\\nPhysician notification requirements\\n- Notify provider immediately for:\\n  - Low progesterone in a patient with an intact uterus (before next dosing cycle).\\n  - Any report of abnormal uterine bleeding.\\n  - Symptoms of virilization or marked androgen excess.\\n  - Intolerance to iron therapy or if ferritin falls despite therapy.\\n- Notify provider within 48 hours for:\\n  - Elevated free testosterone requiring possible dose adjustment of exogenous testosterone.\\n\\nPatient education points for staff to cover (documented to provider)\\n- Request/confirm date of LMP and cycle day when labs were drawn; instruct patient to track and report next period start.\\n- Explain planned medication changes only after provider approval; counsel about expected timeline for symptom improvement (weeks).\\n- Counsel on signs/symptoms that require urgent reporting: new/worsening bleeding, hair growth changes, voice changes, severe acne, chest pain, shortness of breath.\\n- If iron started: advise on oral iron dosing instructions, take with vitamin C, avoid calcium/antacids within 2 hours, common GI side effects and when to call.\\n\\nLifestyle interventions to recommend to patient (staff to document/reinforce)\\n- Dietary: increase iron-rich foods (red meat, legumes, leafy greens); pair with vitamin C to enhance absorption.\\n- Menstrual management: document menses patterns (heavy bleeding episodes) and use menstrual flow tracking.\\n- Weight management and exercise: weight loss/maintenance can improve SHBG/testosterone balance if metabolic dysfunction suspected.\\n- Hair/skin care: gentle dermatologic measures if acne/hirsutism present; consider dermatology referral if persistent despite hormonal adjustments.\\n\\nAdditional testing or referrals to consider\\n- Confirmatory/endocrine evaluation:\\n  - Repeat total and free testosterone with standardized timing; consider DHEA-S, fasting insulin/HbA1c if PCOS or metabolic syndrome suspected.\\n- Reproductive/endometrial evaluation:\\n  - If irregular bleeding or persistent anovulation suspected — pelvic ultrasound and/or gynecology referral; endometrial biopsy if persistent unopposed bleeding per provider judgment.\\n- Iron management:\\n  - If oral iron intolerant or ferritin fails to improve, consider IV iron referral per protocol.\\n- Endocrinology or REI referral if complex hormone adjustments or fertility concerns.\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (staff action checklist)\\n1. Immediately document LMP and cycle day in chart; record time since last HRT dose at blood draw.\\n2. Notify provider TODAY about low progesterone if patient has an intact uterus (and if any abnormal bleeding).\\n3. Provider to decide on progesterone escalation (example: increase to oral micronized 200 mg nightly or equivalent) and estrogen increment (increase transdermal/oral dose by one step).\\n4. If patient is on testosterone, provider review/recommend adjustment given elevated free T — do NOT increase testosterone; consider dose reduction or hold until reviewed.\\n5. If symptomatic for iron deficiency, provider to approve iron initiation (oral elemental iron 30–60 mg daily or clinic protocol product) and schedule ferritin/CBC in 8–12 weeks.\\n6. Schedule repeat labs:\\n   - Estradiol and progesterone 4–6 weeks after HRT dose changes (with cycle/timing documented).\\n   - Total/free testosterone 6–8 weeks after any testosterone dose change (standardize timing).\\n   - Ferritin/CBC 8–12 weeks if iron started.\\n7. Consider additional testing (DHEA-S, metabolic labs, pelvic imaging) or specialty referral per provider decision.\\n\\nNote: All medication dose changes must be authorized by the prescribing provider and entered into the chart. Ensure lab draws are timed and documented relative to HRT/testosterone dosing to allow accurate interpretation.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "SHBG", "value": 74.6, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 23, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Lab pattern consistent with relative estrogen excess or impaired estrogen clearance. Even without reported symptoms, supporting liver detoxification and estrogen metabolism pathways may be beneficial.", "supportingFindings": ["Progesterone 1.11 ng/mL (very low)", "Borderline l