well)\\n- Hemoglobin 13.1 g/dL and ferritin 138 ng/mL — good iron status.\\n- Hemoglobin A1c 5.1% — normal blood sugar control.\\n- HDL cholesterol 67 mg/dL and triglycerides 95 mg/dL — protective lipid values.\\n- TSH 1.33 mIU/L — thyroid function in the normal range.\\n- hs-CRP 0.7 mg/L — low inflammation for cardiovascular risk.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + concrete actions)\\n1) Vitamin D: 25-OH = 24.7 ng/mL (deficient, target >30 ng/mL).\\n- Start vitamin D3 2,000 IU daily and aim to recheck 25-OH in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Add 10–20 minutes sun exposure (arms/face) 2–3x weekly if safe, and include weight-bearing exercise (walking, stair climbing) 3x weekly for bone health.\\n- Ensure dietary calcium (dairy, fortified plant milk, leafy greens) ~1,000–1,200 mg/day.\\n\\n2) Cholesterol and ApoB: LDL = 149 mg/dL, ApoB = 104 mg/dL (borderline/high).\\n- Dietary steps: add 25–30 g fiber daily—oatmeal at breakfast, beans for lunch, vegetables at dinner.\\n- Include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n- Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter and cheese.\\n- Exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking (or equivalent) daily.\\n- Recheck fasting lipids in 3 months; statin is not generally recommended now given your 10-year risk (0.7%), but discuss Lp(a) and ApoB with your clinician if family history of early heart disease.\\n\\n3) Hormones / perimenopause: Estradiol = 28 pg/mL; Progesterone = 0.33 ng/mL; Total testosterone = 11.7 ng/dL (low).\\n- Track menstrual cycles and symptoms for 1–3 months; if progesterone was drawn in luteal phase, 0.33 ng/mL could indicate anovulation/perimenopause.\\n- Sleep and testosterone support: prioritize 7–8 hours sleep, strength training 2–3x weekly, and regular stress management (walking, meditation).\\n- Discuss perimenopause treatment options (sleep strategies, and if appropriate, menopausal hormone therapy) with your provider.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Start vitamin D 2,000 IU/day now; rec