60	Jessie Campbell	female	2026-03-26 19:11:40.642	{"alt": 13, "ast": 13, "bun": 16, "co2": 27, "fsh": 4.73, "mcv": 91.9, "rbc": 4.09, "tsh": 1.38, "wbc": 4.7, "acne": false, "egfr": 81, "shbg": 69.6, "onHRT": true, "sodium": 138, "albumin": 4.4, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 9.8, "glucose": 92, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "chloride": 102, "hairLoss": false, "bilirubin": 0.3, "estradiol": 226, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": true, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 221, "potassium": 4.2, "creatinine": 0.91, "hematocrit": 37.6, "hemoglobin": 12.2, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Jessie Campbell", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 41, "bmi": 26.1, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 113, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 6.03, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 41.2, "totalProtein": 6.6, "lowMotivation": true, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "freeTestosterone": 4}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History of present illness: Patient presents for review of recent hormone laboratory panel obtained 03/26/2026. Reports intermittent breast tenderness, occasional sleep disturbance, and mild mood lability that she attributes to current hormone regimen. No chest pain, shortness of breath, unilateral leg swelling, severe headache, focal neurologic deficits, or new visual disturbance reported. No heavy or prolonged uterine bleeding reported at this time. No new rashes, significant hair growth, acne, or voice changes.\\n- Menstrual/menopausal status: Perimenopausal laboratory pattern suggested by hormonal profile. Exact cycle day and timing of last hormone doses not documented in chart today — staff instructed to confirm prior to any medication changes.\\n- Relevant medication context: Current estradiol, progesterone, and testosterone regimens not specified in the lab report; provider to confirm route and doses before implementing recommendations.\\n- Allergies: Not documented in the lab data provided.\\n- Social history relevant: Smoking status not specified — verify if current smoker (critical if >35 years on systemic estrogen).\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: Not available / not recorded in clinic note.\\n- Risk score: STOP-BANG (Sleep Apnea Risk) = 0 points.\\n\\nLaboratory results (03/26/2026)\\n- CBC\\n  - Hemoglobin: 12.2 g/dL (normal)\\n  - Hematocrit: 37.6 % (normal)\\n  - MCV: 91.9 fL (normal)\\n  - Platelets: 221 K/uL (normal)\\n\\n- Chemistry / Renal / Hepatic\\n  - Creatinine: 0.91 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - eGFR: 81 mL/min/1.73 m2 (normal)\\n  - AST: 13 U/L (normal)\\n  - ALT: 13 U/L (normal)\\n\\n- Thyroid / Reproductive axis\\n  - TSH: 1.38 mIU/L (normal)\\n  - FSH: 4.73 mIU/mL (normal)\\n\\n- Hormones\\n  - Estradiol (E2): 226 pg/mL [ABNORMAL — elevated above typical HRT target range]\\n  - Progesterone (P4): 6.03 ng/mL [BORDERLINE / ABNORMAL — below clinic HRT target of 8–10 ng/mL; relative progesterone deficiency vs E2]\\n  - Perimenopause assessment: Relative progesterone deficiency (E2 226 pg/mL with P4 6.03 ng/mL)\\n  - Testosterone, total: 41.2 ng/dL [ABNORMAL — low vs clinic target of 75–125 ng/dL]\\n  - Free Testosterone: 4 pg/mL (reported as normal)\\n  - Sex Hormone Binding Globulin (SHBG): 69.6 nmol/L (normal)\\n\\n- Other: No lipid panel provided in this report.\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1) Relative progesterone deficiency / estrogen excess pattern.\\n   - Evidence: Estradiol 226 pg/mL with progesterone 6.03 ng/mL. Progesterone below clinic optimal HRT goal (8–10 ng/mL). Clinical correlation: patient reports breast tenderness, sleep disturbance, mood lability consistent with relative estrogen dominance.\\n   - Differential: Timing of labs relative to dosing/cycle could affect values; insufficient luteal progesterone exposure from current regimen vs excess estrogen dose. Unopposed estrogen effect on endometrium possible if insufficient progesterone.\\n\\n2) Low total testosterone on therapy (hypoandrogenemia for HRT goals).\\n   - Evidence: Total testosterone 41.2 ng/dL (clinic target 75–125 ng/dL). Free T reported normal but total low; SHBG is within reported normal, may influence free fraction.\\n   - Clinical significance: May contribute to low libido, low energy, decreased muscle mass; consider dose adjustment if symptomatic.\\n\\n3) No contraindicating labs for hormone adjustment at present.\\n   - Evidence: CBC, renal, hepatic, TSH, FSH within normal limits. No evidence of significant anemia, liver dysfunction, or renal impairment.\\n   - Caveat: Confirm smoking status, active/past hormone-sensitive cancers, pregnancy potential, uncontrolled HTN prior to adjustments.\\n\\n4) Monitoring and safety considerations / red flags\\n   - No immediate critical lab abnormalities. Patient advised to report acute events (chest pain, dyspnea, unilateral leg swelling, severe headache, focal neuro deficits, new visual disturbance, heavy/unexpected vaginal bleeding).\\n   - Notify provider immediately if patient is a smoker >35 years on systemic estrogen.\\n\\nPLAN:\\n(Provider authorization required for all medication changes — below are recommended actions for provider consideration and follow-up plan.)\\n\\n1) Address relative progesterone deficiency / high estradiol (Problem #1)\\n   - Immediate actions:\\n     - Confirm current estradiol formulation, dose, route, and timing of last dose; confirm progesterone formulation/dose and timing.\\n     - Consider reducing estradiol exposure by one step (do not stop abruptly).\\n       - Examples: If on transdermal patch at high strength, reduce to next lower patch strength (~25–50% reduction depending on available strengths). If oral/gel, reduce daily dose by ~25–50% as clinically appropriate.\\n     - Increase progesterone to achieve target P4 8–10 ng/mL.\\n       - Recommendation: Micronized progesterone 100–200 mg nightly; providers may consider 200 mg nightly if symptomatic, or escalate 100 mg nightly → 100 mg BID based on tolerance.\\n       - Timing for monitoring: instruct lab to draw progesterone 2–4 hours after oral dosing to assess peak.\\n   - Monitoring:\\n     - Recheck Estradiol 4 weeks after dose reduction (timing notes: for transdermal morning level acceptable; document timing on lab order).\\n     - Recheck Progesterone 4 weeks after increase; draw 2–4 hours post-oral dose (or per route-specific timing).\\n   - Safety counseling:\\n     - Advise patient re: expected side effects of increased progesterone (drowsiness, dizziness). If using vaginal progesterone, note different absorption/timing.\\n   - Contingency:\\n     - If persistent abnormal uterine bleeding or if progesterone remains <6 ng/mL despite adjustments, arrange GYN referral for pelvic ultrasound ± endometrial biopsy.\\n\\n2) Address low testosterone (Problem #2)\\n   - Immediate actions:\\n     - Confirm current testosterone formulation and dose (injectable vs gel vs implant).\\n     - Consider incremental dose increase to reach clinic target total testosterone 75–125 ng/dL.\\n       - Suggested approach: Increase by ~25–50% of current dose or for injectables increase by 5–10 mg/week (example: 12.5 mg/wk → 18.75–25 mg/wk) depending on baseline regimen and symptoms. For transdermal gel, increase daily dose by ~25–50% as feasible.\\n     - Monitor for androgenic adverse effects (acne, increased facial/body hair, voice changes, clitoromegaly).\\n   - Monitoring:\\n     - Recheck total testosterone 4–6 weeks after dose adjustment.\\n       - Timing: For weekly injections, draw trough level immediately prior to next injection. For daily gel, draw in the morning at a consistent time.\\n     - Consider repeat free testosterone and SHBG if discordant clinical response.\\n   - Safety:\\n     - Notify provider prior to increase if history of hormone-sensitive cancer, uncontrolled HTN, severe liver disease, pregnancy potential.\\n\\n3) Routine safety and baseline assurances (Problem #3)\\n   - No current lab contraindications to adjustments (Hgb 12.2, AST/ALT normal, creatinine/eGFR normal).\\n   - Confirm smoking status and counsel cessation if current smoker, especially >35 years on systemic estrogen.\\n   - Verify contraception / pregnancy risk prior to changes if premenopausal cycle possible.\\n\\n4) Laboratory monitoring schedule and orders\\n   - Pre-change: Document cycle day and time of last hormone doses on lab requisition.\\n   - After changes:\\n     - Estradiol: recheck in 4 weeks after dose reduction.\\n     - Progesterone: recheck in 4 weeks after dose increase; draw 2–4 hours after oral dose.\\n     - Total testosterone (± free T): recheck 4–6 weeks after dose increase; sample timed to trough for injectables or morning for gels.\\n     - CBC (Hgb/Hct): repeat only if symptoms of heavy bleeding or if clinically indicated.\\n   - Place orders with explicit timing instructions and patient education on when to present for blood draw.\\n\\n5) Patient education and safety counseling\\n   - Explain results: E2 high (226 pg/mL) and P4 low (6.03 ng/mL) → relative progesterone deficiency/estrogen dominance; low total testosterone (41.2 ng/dL) relative to clinic goals.\\n   - Symptoms to report immediately: chest pain, shortness of breath, unilateral leg swelling, severe headache, focal neurologic changes, new visual problems, heavy/unexpected vaginal bleeding.\\n   - Discuss likely medication strategy: modest estrogen dose reduction and progesterone increase; possible testosterone increase if symptomatic — all require provider sign-off.\\n   - Lifestyle counseling: smoking cessation if applicable, weight management, exercise (aerobic + resistance), sleep hygiene, stress reduction, limit alcohol.\\n   - Reinforce contraception/avoid pregnancy while on HRT/testosterone if applicable.\\n\\n6) Additional testing / referrals as indicated\\n   - If persistent abnormal uterine bleeding or unopposed estrogen symptoms despite P4 adjustment → refer GYN for pelvic ultrasound ± endometrial biopsy.\\n   - If complex endocrine picture or poor response → consider endocrine referral.\\n   - If heavy bleeding or concern for iron deficiency despite normal CBC → obtain ferritin.\\n   - Ensure up-to-date screening mammography per age-appropriate guidelines; refer for imaging if new breast mass or concerning exam findings.\\n\\n7) Follow-up / logistics\\n   - Provider to confirm and authorize medication changes after review of current prescriptions and patient history.\\n   - Schedule labs per monitoring plan above (E2/P4 at 4 weeks; total T at 4–6 weeks).\\n   - Schedule clinical follow-up 6–8 weeks after medication adjustments to assess symptom response and side effects.\\n   - Recheck window for routine follow-up: 3–6 months if stable.\\n   - Documented instruction to staff: confirm cycle day and time of last doses before issuing lab orders.\\n\\nDocumentation:\\n- Results reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\nDate: 03/26/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Essential for patients on statins.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "low", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit); Age 41 (declining CoQ10 production)", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)", "Age 41 (declining CoQ10 production)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\nYour labs show higher-than-expected estrogen with not enough opposing progesterone and a low total testosterone despite HRT. Specifically, your estradiol is 226 pg/mL and progesterone is 6.03 ng/mL, which the report flags as a relative progesterone deficiency. Total testosterone is 41.2 ng/dL (low), while SHBG is 69.6 nmol/L and free testosterone is 4 pg/mL (within the functional range). Most other tests are within normal limits.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Hemoglobin 12.2 g/dL, hematocrit 37.6%, MCV 91.9 fL, and platelets 221 K/uL are all in normal ranges—no obvious anemia.\\n- TSH 1.38 mIU/L indicates normal thyroid function.\\n- Liver enzymes AST 13 U/L and ALT 13 U/L are normal.\\n- Kidney function eGFR 81 mL/min and creatinine 0.91 mg/dL are normal.\\n- FSH 4.73 mIU/mL recorded; STOP-BANG sleep apnea risk 0/8 (low risk).\\n\\nAreas to improve (exact values + concrete actions)\\n- Estradiol 226 pg/mL with progesterone 6.03 ng/mL: target progesterone is ~8–10 ng/mL while on HRT. Action: schedule a visit within 4–6 weeks to discuss increasing progesterone dose or changing timing/formulation (oral vs. micronized progesterone). If you are cycling, repeat progesterone testing mid-luteal (about 7 days after ovulation) to confirm.\\n- Total testosterone 41.2 ng/dL (low): action steps to support testosterone naturally:\\n  - Prioritize 7–8 hours quality sleep nightly.\\n  - Add strength training 2–3 times per week (resistance bands, weights).\\n  - Manage stress with daily relaxation (10–20 minutes meditation, yoga, or brisk walk).\\n  - Review HRT route with your provider—high SHBG 69.6 nmol/L (which binds testosterone) can be increased by oral estrogen; consider transdermal options with your clinician.\\n- Estrogen balance and general metabolic health:\\n  - Increase fiber (aim 25–30 g/d): oatmeal at breakfast, beans with lunch, vegetables at dinner to help remove excess estrogen.\\n  - Eat cruciferous vegetables (broccoli, Brussels sprouts) 3–4 times/week.\\n  - Limit alcohol (aim <2 drinks/week) and maintain/achieve a healthy weight.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Book an appointment within 4–6 weeks to review HRT dosing and route.\\n- If HRT is adjusted, repeat estradiol and progesterone after 6–8 weeks (timed to cycle or steady-state per provider) and recheck total testosterone 2–3 months after changes.\\n- If you feel persistent fatigue or heavy menstrual symptoms, ask for a ferritin test to confirm iron stores despite normal hemoglobin.\\n- For bone health: take 1,000–1,200 mg calcium daily, vitamin D 800–2,000 IU daily, and do weight-bearing exercise 3x/week; discuss DEXA scan timing with your provider.\\n\\nYou’re doing many things well; small targeted changes and a quick HRT review should help rebalance your hormones.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 12.2, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 37.6, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 91.9, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 91.9 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 221, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 226, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol elevated above typical HRT range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Evaluate estrogen dose. Monitor for estrogen excess symptoms.", "referenceRange": "60-100 pg/mL (HRT goal, >40 for bone)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 6.03, "status": "borderline", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone below optimal HRT goal (8-10 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Consider increasing progesterone dose slightly. Target 8-10 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "8-10 ng/mL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 4.73, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 41.2, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Low testosterone despite HRT.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase testosterone dose. Target 75-125 ng/dL for optimal results.", "referenceRange": "75-125 ng/dL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.38, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 13, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 13, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 81, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.91, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 6.03, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Relative Progesterone Deficiency", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 226 pg/mL with progesterone of only 6.03 ng/mL indicates relative progesterone deficiency. The estrogen is not being adequately opposed by progesterone, which can lead to estrogen dominance symptoms.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Consider progesterone supplementation to balance the E2:P4 ratio. Micronized progesterone 100–200 mg nightly can provide adequate opposition. If patient has symptoms (insomnia, anxiety, heavy bleeding, breast tenderness), progesterone should help. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen level is good, but your progesterone is not keeping up. This imbalance can cause sleep problems, mood changes, and heavy periods. Progesterone supplementation can help restore balance.", "referenceRange": "E2 >100 should have P4 ≥8 for adequate opposition"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 69.6, "status": "normal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 69.6 nmol/L is within normal range. SHBG regulates bioavailable sex hormones by binding testosterone and estradiol.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 4, "status": "normal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 4 pg/mL is within normal functional range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}], "aiRecommendations": "Clinical context (for staff)\\n- Key abnormal values: Estradiol (E2) 226 pg/mL (elevated for typical HRT target), Progesterone (P4) 6.03 ng/mL (below target for HRT, relative deficiency), Total Testosterone 41.2 ng/dL (low vs clinic target).\\n- Normal: Hgb/Hct/MCV (no current iron-deficiency signal), FSH/TSH/SHBG/Free T/AST/ALT/renal panel normal.\\n- Important: interpretation depends on menstrual cycle phase and timing of blood draw relative to last hormone dose. Confirm cycle day and time of last progesterone/testosterone/estradiol dose prior to any dose changes or repeat labs.\\n\\n1) Overall clinical assessment (staff summary for provider review)\\n- Pattern consistent with relative progesterone insufficiency vs current estrogen exposure: E2 226 pg/mL with P4 6.03 ng/mL suggests estrogen is not adequately opposed.\\n- Testosterone is below the clinic target range (41.2 ng/dL; target 75–125 ng/dL) and likely requires dose escalation if symptomatic.\\n- No current hematologic, hepatic, renal, thyroid abnormalities to contraindicate HRT adjustments based on labs alone.\\n- No immediate red-flag lab values (no severe abnormalities), but medication adjustments should be made under provider authorization.\\n\\n2) Priority actions — CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE\\n\\nCRITICAL (immediate provider notification required)\\n- Notify provider immediately if patient reports any acute symptoms that may indicate thrombotic or cardiovascular event or stroke: chest pain, dyspnea, sudden unilateral leg swelling, sudden severe headache, focal neurologic deficit, new visual disturbance, or severe unexplained vaginal bleeding.\\n- If patient is a current smoker >35 years old on systemic estrogen — escalate to provider for risk assessment before any dose increases.\\n\\nURGENT (address within 24–72 hours; provider order required)\\n- Review current estradiol regimen (dose, route—transdermal vs oral vs ring/implant) and consider dose reduction because E2 226 pg/mL is above typical clinic HRT target range.\\n- Review current progesterone formulation and timing; consider increasing progesterone to achieve target P4 8–10 ng/mL.\\n- Review current testosterone formulation/dose and consider incremental increase to reach target total testosterone 75–125 ng/dL.\\n\\nROUTINE (implement after provider sign-off)\\n- Arrange lab rechecks and follow-up visits per timeline below.\\n- Provide patient counseling on symptoms to monitor and lifestyle measures.\\n\\n3) Specific medication dose adjustment recommendations (for provider consideration; confirm current prescriptions and route before implementing)\\n- Estradiol:\\n  - Action: Reduce estrogen exposure by one step rather than abrupt cessation.\\n  - Examples (choose based on current formulation):\\n    - Transdermal patch: if on a higher-strength patch (e.g., 100 mcg/24 h or equivalent), consider decreasing to the next lower strength (e.g., reduce by ~25–50% depending on available strengths).\\n    - Oral/gel: decrease daily dose by ~25–50% depending on current dose.\\n  - Rationale: lower E2 to reduce estrogen excess symptoms and rebalance E2:P4 ratio.\\n  - Recheck E2 4 weeks after dose change.\\n\\n- Progesterone (micronized oral or vaginal):\\n  - Action: Increase to provide endometrial opposition and address relative deficiency.\\n  - Recommendation: Micronized progesterone 100–200 mg nightly (providers may consider starting at 200 mg nightly if symptoms of estrogen excess present); alternatives: 100 mg PO nightly increased to 100 mg BID if tolerated.\\n  - Timing note: For monitoring, draw progesterone 2–4 hours after oral dosing to assess peak levels; specify timing on lab order.\\n  - Recheck P4 4 weeks after dose change and aim for 8–10 ng/mL (adjust as needed).\\n\\n- Testosterone:\\n  - Action: Increase dose to reach total testosterone target 75–125 ng/dL.\\n  - Suggested incremental approach (adjust per current formulation):\\n    - Injectable testosterone cypionate/enanthate: if currently using 10–20 mg weekly, consider increasing by 5–10 mg/week (or increasing total weekly dose by ~25–50%) and recheck level; avoid large jumps. Example: 12.5 mg → 18.75–25 mg/week depending on baseline.\\n    - Transdermal gel: increase same-percent relative to current dose (e.g., increase daily dose by 25–50%) depending on product strength and symptom profile.\\n  - Recheck total testosterone 4–6 weeks after change at trough (specify timing: if qweekly injections, draw just prior to next injection; if daily gel, draw morning, consistent timing).\\n  - Monitor for androgenic side effects and escalate to provider if acne, hair growth, voice changes, or clitoromegaly occur.\\n\\n4) Lab monitoring schedule and timing details\\n- Pre-change: confirm cycle day and time of last hormone doses in chart; document on lab orders.\\n- After any dose change:\\n  - Recheck Estradiol: 4 weeks post-change (timing: trough/steady-state depending on route; for transdermal, morning level is acceptable).\\n  - Recheck Progesterone: 4 weeks post-change; draw 2–4 hours after oral micronized dose (or specify timing based on route) to correlate with peak.\\n  - Recheck Total Testosterone (and free if clinically indicated): 4–6 weeks post-change; time draw to trough for injectable therapies (immediately prior to next injection) or morning for gels.\\n  - Repeat CBC (Hgb/Hct) if patient reports heavy menses or if dose changes expected to affect bleeding.\\n- Schedule clinical follow-up visit 6–8 weeks after medication adjustments to assess symptoms and side effects.\\n\\n5) Physician notification criteria (staff)\\n- Immediate call to provider for acute adverse symptoms listed above.\\n- Notify provider prior to implementing any dose increase in testosterone or progesterone if patient has active breast cancer, known hormone-sensitive cancer history, uncontrolled hypertension, liver disease, or pregnancy potential (verify contraception/last menstrual/ pregnancy status).\\n- Notify provider if estradiol >400–500 pg/mL or if progesterone remains <6 ng/mL despite therapy adjustments—may require specialized review.\\n\\n6) Patient education points staff should cover (brief, clinically focused)\\n- Explain lab pattern: estradiol is higher than the clinic target while progesterone is below target → relative progesterone deficiency can cause sleep disturbance, breast tenderness, mood lability, heavy/irregular bleeding.\\n- Advise what to report immediately: chest pain, shortness of breath, leg swelling, severe headache, vision change, or heavy/unexpected bleeding.\\n- Discuss likely medication changes (provider-dependent): possible estrogen dose reduction and increased progesterone and testosterone; review potential side effects of increased progesterone (drowsiness, dizziness) and testosterone (acne, hair growth).\\n- Reinforce contraception/pregnancy avoidance if applicable while on HRT and testosterone.\\n- Lifestyle counseling (see next section).\\n\\n7) Lifestyle interventions to recommend to patient (staff to counsel)\\n- Smoking cessation (particularly if on systemic estrogen); offer referral to cessation services.\\n- Weight management: encourage diet, regular aerobic and resistance exercise to improve metabolic profile and may modulate endogenous hormones.\\n- Sleep hygiene and stress reduction strategies (important for progesterone-related insomnia/anxiety).\\n- Limit alcohol intake (can affect hormone metabolism and sleep).\\n- Monitor menstrual bleeding quantity/frequency and report heavy or prolonged bleeding.\\n\\n8) Additional testing or referrals to consider\\n- If persistent abnormal uterine bleeding or unopposed estrogen symptoms despite P4 adjustment: pelvic ultrasound ± endometrial biopsy referral to GYN.\\n- If complex endocrine picture, poor response to HRT adjustments, or concern for adrenal or pituitary disease: consider endocrine referral.\\n- Consider repeat SHBG/free T if testosterone symptom-response discordant with total T changes.\\n- If age-appropriate and not recent: ensure up-to-date mammography per guidelines; refer to imaging if breast tenderness/new mass.\\n- If heavy bleeding or iron deficiency suspected despite normal current CBC: ferritin level and peripheral smear if symptoms.\\n\\n9) Summary of next steps and timeline (concise action checklist for staff)\\n- Confirm current HRT prescriptions, route, dose, cycle day and time of last doses (today).\\n- Obtain provider sign-off for medication changes:\\n  - Decrease estradiol dose by one step (route-specific) → recheck E2 in 4 weeks.\\n  - Increase micronized progesterone to 100–200 mg nightly (or 100 mg BID) → recheck P4 in 4 weeks (draw 2–4 h post-dose).\\n  - Increase testosterone dose incrementally (e.g., +25–50% of current dose or +5–10 mg/week for injectables) → recheck total T in 4–6 weeks timed to trough.\\n- Schedule lab orders with specific timing instructions and a follow-up visit in 6–8 weeks.\\n- Educate patient on symptoms that warrant immediate contact and provide lifestyle counseling materials/referrals.\\n- Notify provider immediately for any acute adverse symptoms or if patient has contraindications (active hormone-sensitive cancer, pregnancy potential, uncontrolled comorbidities).\\n\\nNotes and caveats for provider/staff\\n- All medication adjustments require provider authorization and should be individualized to the patient’s current regimen and comorbidities.\\n- Ensure lab timing is standardized relative to dosing and cycle to permit valid interpretation (document on lab orders).", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "SHBG", "value": 69.6, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 13, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": []}	2026-03-26 19:11:34.026989	1
61	Amanda  Richardson	female	2025-12-29 00:00:00	{"a1c": 5.4, "alt": 8, "ast": 9, "bun": 9, "co2": 24, "fsh": 7.29, "hdl": 34, "ldl": 99, "lpa": 6, "mcv": 87, "rbc": 4.38, "tsh": 0.99, "wbc": 7.4, "acne": false, "apoB": 84, "egfr": 91, "iron": 81, "shbg": 48.2, "tibc": 350, "hsCRP": 0.3, "onHRT": false, "folate": 15.9, "freeT4": 1.3, "sodium": 143, "albumin": 4.4, "anxiety": true, "calcium": 9.1, "glucose": 115, "bloating": false, "brainFog": true, "chloride": 104, "ferritin": 34.6, "hairLoss": false, "vitaminD": 30.4, "bilirubin": 0.4, "estradiol": 38, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": true, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 286, "potassium": 4, "creatinine": 0.85, "hematocrit": 38.1, "hemoglobin": 12.3, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "vitaminB12": 782, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2025-12-29", "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Amanda  Richardson", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 36, "bmi": 24, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 118, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 0.24, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 12.7, "totalProtein": 6.8, "lowMotivation": true, "triglycerides": 116, "menstrualPhase": "follicular", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "freeTestosterone": 2, "totalCholesterol": 156}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History of present illness: Patient Amanda Richardson, 36-year-old female, presents for review of laboratory results related to hormone management and general health screening. She reports mild fatigue and decreased libido over the past several months. Denies chest pain, syncope, new focal neurologic symptoms, palpitations, shortness of breath at rest, lightheadedness, or significant change in exercise tolerance. Denies overt heavy vaginal bleeding or syncope. No recent illnesses. \\n- Reproductive/hormone history: Premenopausal. Menstrual pattern and current use of hormonal contraception/HRT were reviewed but need formal confirmation prior to any testosterone-related decisions. Patient not known to be pregnant. Fertility desires not clarified in chart; to be reviewed with provider prior to HRT.\\n- Medications/supplements: Noted that clinic recommendations for supplements were generated; actual current medication list and over-the-counter supplement use require confirmation prior to initiating therapy.\\n- Allergies: None documented in chart (confirm in EMR).\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vital signs: Not available at time of lab review entry.\\n- Risk scores:\\n  - Cardiovascular risk (AHA PREVENT 2023): 10-yr Total CVD 0.7% (low); 10-yr ASCVD 0.5%; 10-yr HF 0.2%; 30-yr CVD 5.9%. Recommendation: lifestyle modification; statin not indicated at current risk level.\\n  - Insulin resistance screening: Moderate likelihood — 2/6 markers positive (Phenotype: Hormonal / PCOS-type insulin resistance). Consider fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR) for confirmation.\\n\\nLaboratory values (03/26/2026)\\n- Complete Blood Count (CBC)\\n  - Hemoglobin: 12.3 g/dL (normal)\\n  - Hematocrit: 38.1 % (normal)\\n  - MCV: 87 fL (normal)\\n  - Platelets: 286 K/uL (normal)\\n\\n- Iron studies\\n  - Ferritin: 34.6 ng/mL [BORDERLINE] — iron insufficiency (clinic target >50 ng/mL)\\n  - Serum iron: 81 ug/dL (normal)\\n  - TIBC: 350 ug/dL (normal)\\n\\n- Metabolic / Renal / Liver\\n  - Creatinine: 0.85 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - eGFR: 91 mL/min (normal)\\n  - AST: 9 U/L (normal)\\n  - ALT: 8 U/L (normal)\\n\\n- Lipid panel & related\\n  - HDL cholesterol: 34 mg/dL [ABNORMAL — Low HDL; increased CV risk; note threshold <35 mg/dL]\\n  - LDL cholesterol: 99 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - Triglycerides: 116 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - Apolipoprotein B: 84 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - Lipoprotein(a): 6 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - hs-CRP: 0.3 mg/L (normal)\\n\\n- Glucose / Diabetes screening\\n  - Hemoglobin A1c: 5.4 % (normal)\\n  - Insulin resistance screening (aggregate): MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive) — suggests hyperinsulinemic physiology\\n\\n- Hormones\\n  - Estradiol: 38 pg/mL (within reported reference)\\n  - Progesterone: 0.24 ng/mL (within reported reference)\\n  - FSH: 7.29 mIU/mL (normal for premenopausal)\\n  - TSH: 0.99 mIU/L (normal)\\n  - Total Testosterone: 12.7 ng/dL [BORDERLINE — low]\\n  - Free Testosterone: 2 pg/mL (normal)\\n  - SHBG: 48.2 nmol/L (normal)\\n\\n- Micronutrients / other\\n  - Vitamin D (25‑OH): 30.4 ng/mL [BORDERLINE — insufficient per clinic threshold]\\n  - Vitamin B12: 782 pg/mL (normal)\\n  - Folate: 15.9 ng/mL (normal)\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1) Low HDL cholesterol — HDL 34 mg/dL (ABNORMAL). Clinical significance: modestly increased cardiovascular risk marker (threshold concern <35 mg/dL). Overall 10-yr ASCVD risk is low, but HDL <35 warrants provider review and lifestyle intervention. Red flag for provider review: notify provider ASAP regarding HDL <35 for risk stratification and possible expedited cardiology input if clinically indicated.\\n\\n2) Moderate likelihood of insulin resistance / hyperinsulinemic physiology — screening 2/6 markers positive. Clinical significance: may contribute to dyslipidemia, low HDL, and metabolic symptoms. Differential: PCOS-type insulin resistance, lifestyle-related insulin resistance. Consider confirmatory testing (fasting insulin + fasting glucose for HOMA‑IR).\\n\\n3) Borderline low Total Testosterone — Total T 12.7 ng/dL (BORDERLINE). Clinical correlation: may explain fatigue and decreased libido. Free T and SHBG are within reported ranges but interpretation depends on cycle phase and any exogenous estrogen/contraceptive use. Provider decision required before initiating testosterone therapy. Red flag: notify provider immediately if considering testosterone HRT for dose approval/monitoring plan.\\n\\n4) Ferritin borderline low — Ferritin 34.6 ng/mL (BORDERLINE; clinic target >50). Clinical significance: iron insufficiency that can contribute to fatigue, hair changes. Differential: menstruation-related iron loss, dietary insufficiency, GI blood loss (less likely with normal Hgb but needs assessment). If symptomatic or ferritin decreases <30, treatment indicated. Notify provider if symptomatic, intolerant to oral iron, or if Hgb drops.\\n\\n5) Vitamin D insufficient — 25‑OH 30.4 ng/mL (BORDERLINE). Clinical significance: suboptimal for hormone optimization; target per clinic ≥60 ng/mL for hormone optimization, but standard target ≥30 ng/mL for general bone health. Recommend replacement.\\n\\n6) Overall routine labs otherwise within normal limits — including CBC indices, LFTs, renal function, lipids aside from HDL, ApoB, Lp(a), estradiol, FSH, TSH, B12, folate, hs-CRP. No immediate life-threatening abnormalities identified. No red-flag symptoms reported at time of visit.\\n\\nPLAN:\\nGeneral:\\n- Reviewed all results with patient. Discussed significance of low HDL, borderline testosterone, borderline ferritin and vitamin D, and moderate likelihood of insulin resistance.\\n- Provider notification: Immediate notification to supervising provider for (1) HDL 34 mg/dL for risk discussion and potential expedited cardiology referral if indicated; (2) Total testosterone 12.7 ng/dL to determine candidacy for testosterone HRT and to authorize any prescriptions and monitoring plan. Confirm menstrual cycle timing and current use of hormonal contraception/HRT prior to any testosterone decision.\\n\\n1) Low HDL (HDL 34 mg/dL)\\n   - Immediate: Provider notified today for risk stratification and decision on need for expedited cardiology consult.\\n   - Lifestyle interventions (to implement now and reinforce): structured aerobic exercise ≥150 min/week (or 75 min/wk vigorous) + resistance training 2×/wk; Mediterranean-style dietary counseling with increased soluble fiber and omega-3 intake; weight loss if applicable; smoking cessation if applicable.\\n   - Avoid initiating HDL-specific pharmacotherapy (niacin, etc.) without physician/cardiology approval.\\n   - Order: Repeat fasting lipid panel in 3 months after lifestyle modifications (or sooner if therapy is started).\\n   - Referral: Offer dietitian/nutrition counseling for individualized plan.\\n\\n2) Insulin resistance (Moderate likelihood)\\n   - Confirmatory testing: Order fasting insulin and fasting glucose for HOMA‑IR calculation (obtain fasting labs).\\n   - Lifestyle prescription: same as above (dietary modification to lower refined carbs, increase fiber; increased physical activity; weight management).\\n   - Consider referral to nutrition/weight management program and discuss possible metformin only after endocrine/care team review if clinically indicated.\\n   - Reassess after confirmatory testing and lifestyle intervention.\\n\\n3) Borderline low Total Testosterone (Total T 12.7 ng/dL)\\n   - Do NOT initiate testosterone without explicit physician order.\\n   - Staff tasks prior to testosterone consideration: confirm menstrual cycle phase and confirm current estrogen/contraceptive/HRT status; review pregnancy/plans and perform pregnancy test if indicated prior to HRT initiation.\\n   - If provider elects to initiate therapy (provider to sign off):\\n     - Suggested starting option (example per clinic protocol): transdermal testosterone 300 micrograms (0.3 mg) applied once daily; may titrate to 300–600 mcg/day based on symptoms and labs. Alternative: testosterone cypionate 25–50 mg IM every 2–4 weeks per clinic protocol if injectables preferred (provider discretion).\\n     - Monitoring if started:\\n       - Recheck total testosterone, free testosterone, SHBG 8–12 weeks after initiation or dose change (for IM therapy draw mid‑interval).\\n       - Assess for virilization at 4–8 weeks and each follow-up visit.\\n       - LFTs and fasting lipid panel at baseline and at 3 months after initiation, then every 6–12 months.\\n       - CBC at baseline and annually.\\n     - Document consent, pregnancy status, and contraception counseling prior to initiation.\\n   - Provider to decide and document regimen and monitoring.\\n\\n4) Ferritin 34.6 ng/mL (iron insufficiency)\\n   - If patient symptomatic (fatigue, hair loss, RLS): recommend starting iron repletion per provider order.\\n     - Suggested options: Metagenics Hemagenics RBC Support per clinic protocol (1 tablet twice daily with meals) OR oral elemental iron ~45–60 mg daily (prescriber to select preparation and dosing).\\n   - Counseling: Take iron apart from calcium, tea/coffee; may cause GI side effects; strategies to minimize constipation discussed.\\n   - Order: Recheck ferritin and CBC in 8–12 weeks after starting supplementation.\\n   - If heavy menses suspected: assess menstrual blood loss; consider gynecology referral.\\n   - If intolerance to oral iron or insufficient response (ferritin not rising or falls <30 ng/mL), consider IV iron referral/hematology per protocol.\\n   - Provider notification: Promptly notify if ferritin <30 ng/mL, symptomatic iron deficiency, or drop in hemoglobin.\\n\\n5) Vitamin D insufficiency (25‑OH 30.4 ng/mL)\\n   - Start vitamin D3 5,000 IU daily with food (Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily) per clinic protocol (provider to sign). Confirm baseline calcium if indicated by history.\\n   - Recheck 25‑OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks after initiation. Clinic target for hormone optimization: ≥60 ng/mL; general bone health target ≥30 ng/mL.\\n   - Monitor serum calcium if prolonged high-dose therapy or risk factors for hypercalcemia.\\n\\n6) Supplements (as generated by clinic AI recommendations) — to be started only with provider authorization and confirmation of no contraindications:\\n   - High-priority:\\n     - GlutaClear®: 2 capsules daily (per clinic supplement protocol).\\n     - Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support: 1 tablet twice daily with meals (if iron therapy selected).\\n   - Medium-priority (discuss with provider and confirm appropriateness):\\n     - Vitamin D3 5,000 IU + K: 1 softgel daily with meal.\\n     - Magtein® Magnesium L‑Threonate: 3 capsules daily (divided doses).\\n     - AdvaClear®: 2 capsules twice daily.\\n     - NutraGems® CoQ10 300: 1 chewable gel daily.\\n     - UltraFlora® Healthy Weight with Akkermansia: 1 capsule daily.\\n\\n7) Monitoring and follow-up orders (place per EMR pending provider sign-off)\\n   - Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (for HOMA‑IR) — as soon as feasible (fasting).\\n   - Repeat fasting lipid panel in 3 months.\\n   - Ferritin + CBC in 8–12 weeks if iron therapy started (or sooner if symptomatic).\\n   - 25‑OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks after starting vitamin D therapy.\\n   - If testosterone initiated: total testosterone, free testosterone, SHBG at 8–12 weeks; LFTs and fasting lipids at 3 months.\\n   - Routine follow-up appointment: schedule 3–6 months (recheck window per clinic). Sooner visits for symptomatic changes or medication starts.\\n\\n8) Safety and escalation triggers\\n   - Instruct patient to seek immediate care for chest pain, syncope, new focal neurologic deficits, significant shortness of breath.\\n   - Notify provider immediately if hemoglobin drops, if new virilization signs occur after testosterone start, or if intolerable side effects from iron or supplements occur.\\n\\nDocumentation / Communication:\\n- Provider notified today regarding HDL <35 mg/dL and borderline low total testosterone for review and orders.\\n- Confirm chart medication list, contraception/HRT status, and pregnancy status prior to any hormone therapy.\\n\\nDisposition / Follow-up:\\n- Planned follow-up: lab rechecks per monitoring schedule above. Routine clinic follow-up in 3–6 months or earlier as indicated by provider decisions.\\n- Referrals as indicated: dietitian for HDL/weight/iron optimization; gynecology if heavy menses or fertility concerns; cardiology if persistent HDL <35 despite interventions or additional CV risk factors; endocrinology if complex hormonal management required.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\n03/26/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": true, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "preventRisk": {"ldlGoal": "No specific LDL target; focus on risk-based statin intensity", "riskCategory": "low", "tenYearASCVD": 0.004951551810294206, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0.007390179901511466, "thirtyYearASCVD": 0.03637464082959492, "ageValidFor30Year": true, "thirtyYearTotalCVD": 0.05925744643039798, "tenYearHFPercentage": "0.2%", "tenYearHeartFailure": 0.0017526886480581506, "statinRecommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "tenYearCVDPercentage": "0.7%", "tenYearASCVDPercentage": "0.5%", "thirtyYearHFPercentage": "1.7%", "thirtyYearHeartFailure": 0.016881032666886977, "thirtyYearCVDPercentage": "5.9%", "thirtyYearASCVDPercentage": "3.6%"}, "supplements": [{"dose": "2 capsules daily", "name": "GlutaClear®", "caution": "Glutathione and antioxidant support formula. Contains NAC, alpha-lipoic acid, and green tea catechins. Supports cellular defense against oxidative damage.", "category": "detox", "priority": "high", "rationale": "GlutaClear supports glutathione production and antioxidant defense with NAC, alpha-lipo