d B-vitamins for biotransformation. Best taken with food.", "category": "detox", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "AdvaClear provides broad-spectrum phase I and phase II liver detoxification support with NAC, green tea extract, watercress, and B-vitamins. Designed for metabolic clearance support, biotransformation of hormones and environmental compounds, and hepatic stress. Appropriate when liver markers are borderline, estrogen clearance is sluggish, or metabolic dysfunction is present.", "indication": "Oxidative stress burden (liver biotransformation support); Insulin resistance / metabolic dysfunction (hepatic metabolic support); Low energy (biotransformation burden); Brain fog (toxin clearance burden)", "phenotypes": ["Oxidative Stress Burden", "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement supports your liver's natural detoxification processes. Your results suggest your body could benefit from help processing hormones, metabolic byproducts, and other compounds. It provides nutrients that support both phases of your liver's detox pathways.", "supportingFindings": ["Oxidative stress burden (liver biotransformation support)", "Insulin resistance / metabolic dysfunction (hepatic metabolic support)", "Low energy (biotransformation burden)", "Brain fog (toxin clearance burden)"]}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Essential for patients on statins.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "HDL 34 mg/dL (low); Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)", "phenotypes": ["Oxidative Stress Burden"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["HDL 34 mg/dL (low)", "Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Healthy Weight with Akkermansia", "caution": "Contains Akkermansia muciniphila and Bifidobacterium lactis B420 for metabolic and gut barrier support. Refrigerate after opening for maximum potency.", "category": "metabolic", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "UltraFlora Healthy Weight with Akkermansia provides targeted probiotic support for the gut-metabolic axis. Akkermansia muciniphila supports gut barrier integrity, metabolic health, and healthy weight management. Indicated for insulin resistance, metabolic syndrome features, visceral adiposity, and weight loss resistance.", "indication": "Insulin resistance / visceral adiposity phenotype (gut-metabolic axis support); SHBG 48.2 nmol/L (low, metabolic marker)", "phenotypes": ["Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity"], "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This specialized probiotic contains Akkermansia, a beneficial gut bacteria linked to healthy metabolism and weight management. It supports your gut barrier, helps manage metabolic inflammation, and works with your body's natural weight regulation systems.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance / visceral adiposity phenotype (gut-metabolic axis support)", "SHBG 48.2 nmol/L (low, metabolic marker)"]}, {"dose": "1 soft chew during times of stress", "name": "HerWellness™ Rapid Stress Relief", "caution": "Fast-acting L-Theanine and Lactium formula. Non-drowsy. Promotes calm within 1 hour. Contains milk.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "low", "rationale": "HerWellness Rapid Stress Relief provides fast-acting stress support with L-Theanine (200mg) and Lactium. Promotes calm within 1 hour without drowsiness.", "indication": "Anxiety reported", "phenotypes": ["Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation"], "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This fast-acting supplement helps your body respond to stress more calmly. It promotes relaxation within about an hour and can be taken as needed without causing drowsiness.", "supportingFindings": ["Anxiety reported"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 6, "apoBValue": 84, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "normal", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 0.49515518102942063, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": false}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\n- Most results are reassuring: your 10-year ASCVD risk is very low at 0.5% and many labs are in the healthy range. However, a few values could be improved to support heart health, iron stores, bone health, and hormone optimization.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- 10-year Total CVD Risk: 0.7%; 10-year ASCVD Risk: 0.5% (very low risk).\\n- LDL cholesterol: 99 mg/dL (optimal).\\n- Triglycerides: 116 mg/dL (normal).\\n- Hemoglobin: 12.3 g/dL and Hematocrit: 38.1% (both within normal range).\\n- TSH: 0.99 mIU/L (thyroid function normal).\\n- Estradiol: 38 pg/mL, Progesterone: 0.24 ng/mL, FSH: 7.29 mIU/mL — these are appropriate for the follicular / pre‑ovulatory phase of your cycle.\\n- hs‑CRP: 0.3 mg/L (low inflammation).\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + concrete actions)\\n1) Low HDL cholesterol: HDL is 34 mg/dL (goal is ≥40 mg/dL). To raise HDL and support heart health:\\n   - Add 25–30 g fiber daily: try oatmeal for breakfast, beans/lentils at lunch, and vegetables with dinner.\\n   - Eat 2–3 servings of fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n   - Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter and cheese.\\n   - Daily exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking (or equivalent) most days.\\n   - Consider weight-bearing exercise + strength training to improve lipid profile.\\n\\n2) Ferritin (iron stores) is low‑normal: ferritin 34.6 ng/mL (optimal >50 ng/mL). To boost iron:\\n   - Eat iron-rich foods: lean red meat, poultry, beans, lentils, spinach; pair with vitamin C (orange, bell pepper) to improve absorption.\\n   - If symptomatic (fatigue, hair loss) or if you’re pregnant/planning pregnancy, consider oral iron supplements—discuss with your provider (common tablets provide ~30–65 mg elemental iron). Recheck ferritin in 8–12 weeks if starting supplements.\\n\\n3) Vitamin D is slightly low: 25‑OH vitamin D = 30.4 ng/mL (insufficient; optimal >31–40 for hormone optimization). Actions:\\n   - Take vitamin D3 supplement (discuss dose with your clinician; many start 1,000–2,000 IU/day or a targeted loading dose as advised).\\n   - Aim for weight‑bearing activity and ensure dietary calcium (dairy or fortified alternatives) ~1,000–1,200 mg/day for bone health.\\n   - Recheck vitamin D in ~3 months after starting supplements.\\n\\n4) Testosterone (total) is low: total testosterone 12.7 ng/dL, but SHBG 48.2 nmol/L and free testosterone 2 pg/mL are within functional range. To support healthy testosterone:\\n   - Prioritize 7–8 hours quality sleep nightly.\\n   - Do strength training 2–3× weekly.\\n   - Manage stress with regular exercise, meditation, or hobbies.\\n   - Reassess symptoms (low libido, low energy) and recheck levels in 3 months if concerns persist.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Begin the above lifestyle steps now (dietary fiber, fatty fish, exercise, iron and vitamin D strategies).\\n- Recheck: lipid panel and HDL in ~3 months; ferritin in 8–12 weeks if supplementing iron; vitamin D in ~3 months after starting supplementation; repeat testosterone if symptoms persist in ~3 months.\\n- Statin therapy is not recommended now given your low 10‑year ASCVD risk (0.5%) and LDL 99 mg/dL; continue lifestyle focus.\\n- If you have symptoms (significant fatigue, heavy periods, new hair loss, low libido) or are considering pregnancy, make a follow-up appointment to discuss targeted treatment.\\n\\nYou’re doing well overall — these targeted, concrete steps can move key numbers into a healthier range. If you want, we can create a 4‑week nutrition and exercise plan together.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 12.3, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 38.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 87, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 87 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 286, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 38, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol appropriate for follicular phase.", "recommendation": "Normal finding. Continue monitoring if needed for fertility.", "referenceRange": "20-150 pg/mL (follicular phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.24, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone appropriate for pre-ovulatory phase.", "recommendation": "Normal finding.", "referenceRange": "<1.5 ng/mL (pre-ovulation)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 7.29, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH appropriate for follicular phase.", "recommendation": "Normal finding.", "referenceRange": "3-10 mIU/mL (follicular phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 12.7, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Low testosterone.", "recommendation": "May contribute to low libido or fatigue. Consider HRT discussion.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 0.99, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 34.6, "status": "borderline", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Iron insufficiency (ferritin 31-50). Optimal is >50.", "recommendation": "Provider protocol: Metagenics Hemagenics if symptomatic (fatigue, hair loss, restless legs, exercise intolerance).", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 30.4, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D insufficient (31-40 ng/mL). Suboptimal for hormone optimization.", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 5,000 + K daily. Target ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 782, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.4, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 99, "status": "normal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 34, "status": "abnormal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Low HDL - increased cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Increase aerobic exercise. Consider niacin if very low.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 116, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.7390179901511467, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 0.7% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.49515518102942063, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 0.5%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.17526886480581505, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.2%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.925744643039798, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 5.9%, ASCVD 3.6%, Heart Failure 1.7%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 9, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 8, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 91, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.85, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 84, "status": "normal", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Apo B at optimal level.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 6, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 350, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 81, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 15.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 0.3, "status": "normal", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Low cardiovascular inflammation risk.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 48.2, "status": "normal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 48.2 nmol/L is within normal range. SHBG regulates bioavailable sex hormones by binding testosterone and estradiol.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 2, "status": "normal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 2 pg/mL is within normal functional range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "of 6 markers positive", "value": 2, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Insulin Resistance Screening", "recheckTiming": "3-6 months", "interpretation": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive). This combination points toward hyperinsulinemia physiology. Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR).", "recommendation": "Phenotype(s): Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance. Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose.", "referenceRange": "0-1 Low, 2 Moderate, 3+ High Likelihood"}], "aiRecommendations": "Clinical assessment (concise)\\n- Overall: Mild androgen insufficiency (Total T 12.7 ng/dL), borderline iron stores (ferritin 34.6 ng/mL), borderline vitamin D insufficiency (25‑OH 30.4 ng/mL) and low HDL (34 mg/dL). Most other labs including estradiol, progesterone, FSH, SHBG, free T, CBC indices, LFTs and kidney function are within reference.\\n- Clinical implications: These abnormalities can contribute to fatigue, low libido, hair changes and modestly increased cardiovascular risk. None are immediately life‑threatening, but the HDL <35 mg/dL and symptomatic iron deficiency warrant prompt clinician review.\\n\\nPriority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\n- CRITICAL\\n  - Notify provider now if the patient is symptomatic for ischemic symptoms, syncope, chest pain, or if hemoglobin drops significantly (not the case here based on normal Hgb).\\n- URGENT (notify physician for expedited decision)\\n  - HDL 34 mg/dL (threshold <35 mg/dL): inform provider for review and to decide on risk stratification and whether expedited cardiology input is needed given clinical context and ASCVD score (currently reported as low).\\n  - Testosterone discussion: patient’s Total T 12.7 ng/dL is below typical female optimized range; notify provider to consider HRT candidacy and to review contraceptive/HRT status (OCPs/estrogen therapy raise SHBG and alter interpretation).\\n- ROUTINE (initiate per protocol and inform provider in routine queue)\\n  - Start vitamin D replacement per clinic protocol (Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily).\\n  - Consider iron repletion if symptomatic (follow Metagenics Hemagenics protocol).\\n  - Lifestyle counseling for low HDL (exercise, weight, diet, smoking cessation if applicable).\\n\\nTest-specific recommendations and actions\\n1) Testosterone (Total 12.7 ng/dL)\\n  - Staff actions before prescribing:\\n    - Confirm menstrual cycle phase and concurrent estrogen/contraceptive/HRT use (noted values interpreted in cycle phase; if possible, obtain baseline early follicular labs).\\n    - Confirm baseline LFTs and lipid panel (already normal LFTs; lipids show low HDL).\\n  - If provider elects to initiate testosterone HRT (provider decision required):\\n    - Suggested starting regimen (clinic staff: obtain physician sign‑off and follow clinic prescribing protocol):\\n      - Preferred initial: low‑dose transdermal testosterone (examples used in female practice): 300 micrograms/day (0.3 mg) applied once daily; may increase to 300–600 mcg/day based on symptoms and labs. OR\\n      - Alternative injectable option (if used per clinic protocol): testosterone cypionate 25–50 mg IM every 2–4 weeks (use clinic protocol for exact dosing).\\n    - Monitoring schedule:\\n      - Recheck total testosterone, free testosterone and SHBG at 8–12 weeks after initiation or dose change. For transdermal therapy any random level is acceptable; for IM therapy draw mid‑interval (midway between injections).\\n      - Assess for virilization symptoms at 4–8 weeks and at each follow‑up.\\n      - LFTs and fasting lipid panel at baseline and at 3 months after initiation, then every 6–12 months; CBC at baseline and annually.\\n    - Physician notification required immediately to approve starting HRT and to confirm dose and monitoring plan.\\n  - Contraindications/considerations:\\n    - Confirm pregnancy status or desire for fertility and discuss contraception if applicable.\\n    - Review interacting medications (estrogen/OCPs can raise SHBG and alter total T).\\n\\n2) Ferritin 34.6 ng/mL (borderline low)\\n  - Follow clinic protocol for iron insufficiency:\\n    - If symptomatic (fatigue, hair loss, RLS, exercise intolerance): initiate Metagenics Hemagenics per provider protocol.\\n    - If using elemental iron alternative: oral iron providing ~45–60 mg elemental iron daily is reasonable for mild insufficiency (obtain physician order).\\n  - Monitoring:\\n    - Recheck ferritin and CBC in 8–12 weeks after starting supplementation.\\n    - Goal ferritin >50 ng/mL per clinic target.\\n  - Additional actions:\\n    - Assess menstrual blood loss; if heavy menses, notify provider and consider gynecology referral.\\n    - If intolerance to oral iron or inadequate response (ferritin not rising or <30 despite therapy), consider IV iron referral per protocol.\\n  - Physician notification: inform provider if patient symptomatic, intolerant to oral iron, or if ferritin falls below 30 ng/mL.\\n\\n3) Vitamin D 25‑OH 30.4 ng/mL (borderline insufficient)\\n  - Start Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily per clinic protocol (notify provider per routine).\\n  - Recheck 25‑OH level in 8–12 weeks; target ≥60 ng/mL for hormone optimization per protocol.\\n  - Monitor serum calcium if high dose supplementation continued long‑term or if hypercalcemia risk factors present.\\n\\n4) HDL 34 mg/dL (low)\\n  - Immediate staff actions:\\n    - Document HDL <35 and notify provider for risk review.\\n    - Reinforce lifestyle interventions (below) and refer to nutrition/exercise counseling.\\n  - Pharmacologic therapy:\\n    - Do not start niacin or other HDL‑raising agents without physician/cardiology approval. Consider niacin only after specialist/physician discussion if HDL remains very low and patient at elevated CV risk.\\n  - Monitoring:\\n    - Repeat fasting lipid panel in 3 months after lifestyle interventions and any medication changes.\\n    - If persistent <35 despite lifestyle measures, escalate to provider/cardiology review.\\n\\nLifestyle interventions (to implement and document)\\n- Physical activity: prescribe structured aerobic exercise program (≥150 min/wk moderate or 75 min/wk vigorous) with resistance training 2×/wk; emphasize consistent sessions to raise HDL.\\n- Diet: recommend Mediterranean‑style eating pattern, increased soluble fiber, fatty fish (omega‑3), reduced refined carbs; consider referral to dietitian for personalized plan for HDL improvement and iron/vitamin D optimization.\\n- Weight management: encourage weight loss if BMI elevated (even modest loss improves HDL and iron status).\\n- Smoking cessation: if applicable, prioritize cessation (largest HDL beneficial effect).\\n- Iron and vitamin D practical points for staff to counsel (per protocol):\\n  - Iron: take apart from calcium, tea/coffee; expect GI side effects; advise how to reduce constipation (stool softener if ordered).\\n  - Vitamin D: take with food to improve absorption.\\n\\nAdditional testing and referrals to consider\\n- Gynecology: if heavy menstrual bleeding suspected or fertility concerns.\\n- Nutrition/Dietitian: for iron repletion diet, vitamin D, and HDL‑raising diet/exercise plan.\\n- Cardiology: if persistent HDL <35 after lifestyle measures or additional CV risk factors despite low 10‑yr ASCVD score.\\n- Endocrinology if complicated hormonal milieu, unclear low testosterone etiology, or if considering complex HRT regimens.\\n- Consider DEXA if age or risk factors for osteoporosis (especially given suboptimal vitamin D).\\n- If oral iron fails or ferritin declines: hematology or infusion clinic for IV iron.\\n\\nPhysician notification requirements (explicit)\\n- Notify provider immediately/ASAP for:\\n  - Decision on initiating testosterone HRT and confirmation of dosing/monitoring.\\n  - HDL <35 mg/dL for risk discussion and potential expedited cardiology referral.\\n- Notify provider promptly for:\\n  - Symptomatic iron deficiency, intolerance to oral iron, ferritin <30 ng/mL, or falling hemoglobin.\\n  - Any adverse events after initiating therapy (e.g., virilization signs, abnormal LFTs, new cardiac symptoms).\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (actionable checklist)\\n- Immediate (within 24–72 hours)\\n  - Notify provider of HDL 34 mg/dL and Total T 12.7 ng/dL for HRT consideration.\\n  - Review current meds/contraceptives and document menstrual cycle phase.\\n- Short term (start within 1–2 weeks after provider approval)\\n  - Vitamin D: start Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily per protocol; recheck 25‑OH in 8–12 weeks.\\n  - Iron: if symptomatic or provider authorizes, start Metagenics Hemagenics per protocol (or elemental iron 45–60 mg/day); recheck ferritin + CBC in 8–12 weeks.\\n  - Initiate lifestyle counseling referrals (dietitian, exercise program).\\n- If initiating testosterone (after physician order)\\n  - Start low‑dose transdermal T (example 300 mcg/day) or alternate per provider; schedule follow‑up visit.\\n  - Labs: recheck total T, free T, SHBG at 8–12 weeks; LFTs and fasting lipids at 3 months.\\n- Follow‑up monitoring\\n  - Ferritin/CBC: 8–12 weeks after iron start.\\n  - 25‑OH vitamin D: 8–12 weeks after supplementation start.\\n  - Lipids: 3 months after lifestyle interventions or medication change.\\n  - Testosterone labs and symptom assessment: 8–12 weeks after initiation or dose change, then q6–12 months.\\n- Escalation triggers\\n  - Notify provider immediately if symptoms worsen, new virilizing features, hemoglobin fall, intolerable side effects, or HDL remains <35 despite interventions.\\n\\nNotes and clinic protocol reminders\\n- All medication/supplement starts and dose changes require explicit physician order per clinic policy.\\n- Verify and document contraception/HRT status before interpreting testosterone and before initiating testosterone therapy.\\n- Tailor iron and vitamin D regimens per patient tolerance and clinic supplement availability; follow Metagenics product dosing per vendor/clinic protocol.\\n\\nProvide this summary to the supervising provider for approval of any therapy starts and for sign‑off on the outlined monitoring plan.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 3.41, "detail": "Triglycerides 116 / HDL 34 = 3.41", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5.4%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 48.2, "detail": "Low - hyperinsulinemia suppresses SHBG", "positive": true, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 8, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "84 mg/dL", "detail": "Normal atherogenic particle count", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "0.3 mg/L", "detail": "Low inflammatory burden", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "moderate", "phenotypes": [{"key": "hormonal_pcos", "name": "Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Monitor SHBG, androgens, A1c every 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["SHBG low (<50)", "TG/HDL borderline-high"], "pathophysiology": "Hyperinsulinemia suppresses SHBG and increases ovarian androgen production.", "triggerCriteria": ["Low SHBG (<50; strong if <30)", "Elevated total or free testosterone", "Irregular cycles", "A1c may still be normal", "TG/HDL may be borderline-high"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests insulin may be influencing your hormone balance.\\n\\nWhen insulin levels run high:\\n- The body makes less SHBG (a protective hormone-binding protein)\\n- Androgen levels can rise\\n- Cycles may become irregular\\n\\nThis pattern is common in PCOS and other hormone imbalance states.\\n\\nImproving insulin sensitivity often helps restore hormonal balance and reduce long-term metabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward, lower refined carb approach", "Strength training", "Consider metformin if cycles irregular or A1c trending", "Consider GLP-1 if obesity phenotype present", "Monitor SHBG, androgens, A1c"]}], "positiveCount": 2, "likelihoodLabel": "MODERATE LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive). This combination points toward hyperinsulinemia physiology. Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR).", "confirmationTests": "Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose."}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation", "confidence": "high", "description": "Pattern consistent with HPA-axis stress and cortisol dysregulation. Chronic stress depletes DHEA-S, disrupts sleep, and impairs cognitive and emotional function.", "supportingFindings": ["Anxiety reported", "Low energy / fatigue reported", "Low motivation reported", "Brain fog reported"]}, {"name": "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Metabolic pattern consistent with insulin resistance and/or visceral adiposity. This drives inflammation, hormonal imbalance, and cardiovascular risk.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance screening: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive)", "TG:HDL ratio 3.4 (≥3.0)", "HDL 34 m