ues.\\n- TSH 0.7 mIU/L — thyroid function is normal.\\n- 10-year ASCVD risk 1.6% — overall 10-year heart risk is low.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + concrete actions)\\n1) LDL cholesterol 171 mg/dL and ApoB 109 mg/dL; hs-CRP 3.2 mg/L:\\n   - Add 25–30 g fiber daily: oatmeal for breakfast, beans or lentils at lunch, vegetables at dinner.\\n   - Include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n   - Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter and high-fat cheeses.\\n   - Daily exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking (or equivalent) most days.\\n   - Because LDL is 171 mg/dL and hs-CRP is 3.2 mg/L, discuss lipid management with your clinician; with persistent LDL >160 despite lifestyle changes, a statin may be considered even if 10-year risk is low. Recheck fasting lipids in 6–12 weeks after lifestyle changes.\\n\\n2) Testosterone: Total T 179 ng/dL (HRT optimization goal 75–125 ng/dL); SHBG 158 nmol/L; Free T 10 pg/mL:\\n   - Priority: discuss reducing testosterone dose with your provider.\\n   - Lifestyle to support healthy hormones: prioritize 7–8 hours sleep nightly, strength training 2–3×/week, and daily stress management (walks, meditation, hobbies).\\n\\n3) Progesterone 1.84 ng/mL:\\n   - This is below typical HRT therapeutic targets but is unexpectedly above typical postmenopausal endogenous levels—please bring a current medication/supplement list to your appointment. Your clinician may repeat the test or adjust HRT dosing.\\n\\n4) MCV 96.1 fL (high-normal) and B12 2000 pg/mL (elevated):\\n   - If you take high-dose B12 supplements, consider lowering dose and review with your clinician. If macrocytosis progresses, testing methylmalonic acid may help.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Schedule a visit in the next 4–8 weeks to review HRT (reduce testosterone dose; review progesterone source) and to plan lipid strategy. Recheck lipids and hs-CRP in ~6–12 weeks after lifestyle changes. Continue vitamin D and bone-supporting habits (weight-bearing exercise, calcium ~1,000–1,200 mg/day from diet/supplements as advised). If you want, bring current supplements/medications to the visit so we can reconcile causes of the progesterone and high B12. You’re doing many things well — these targeted steps can lower heart risk and optimize hormone balance.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 13.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 39.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 96.1, "status": "borderline", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 96.1 fL is in the high-normal range. Early macrocytosis can precede overt B12 or folate deficiency.", "recommendation": "Ensure B12 and folate levels are within optimal range. Monitor CBC at next visit.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 377, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 57, "status": "borderline", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol at minimum bone protection level (40-60 pg/mL) but below optimal HRT goal.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Consider increasing estrogen dose. Target 60-100 pg/mL for optimized symptom relief.", "referenceRange": "60-100 pg/mL (HRT goal, >40 for bone)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1.84, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone below HRT therapeutic range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase progesterone dose. Target 8-10 ng/mL for optimal endometrial protection.", 