 Metabolic Insulin Resistance, Hepatic (Liver-Driven) Insulin Resistance. Treat according to phenotype-specific protocol.", "recommendation": "Phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance; Hepatic (Liver-Driven) Insulin Resistance. Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose.", "referenceRange": "0-1 Low, 2 Moderate, 3+ High Likelihood"}], "aiRecommendations": "Unable to generate recommendations. Please review lab findings manually.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 2.88, "detail": "Triglycerides 164 / HDL 57 = 2.88", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "6.1%", "detail": "Prediabetic range", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 73, "detail": "Above optimal range - may indicate hepatic insulin resistance", "positive": true, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "96 mg/dL", "detail": "Elevated atherogenic particle count", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "26.6 mg/L", "detail": "Elevated systemic inflammation", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "high", "phenotypes": [{"key": "visceral_metabolic", "name": "Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Recheck A1c, lipid panel, hs-CRP, ApoB in 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["A1c >= 5.7%", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0", "ALT above optimal range"], "pathophysiology": "Early peripheral + hepatic insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia. Glucose may still be \\"normal.\\" A1c often rises last.", "triggerCriteria": ["A1c >= 5.7%", "TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0", "Central adiposity phenotype", "ALT above optimal range"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests your body may not be responding to insulin as efficiently as it should. Insulin is the hormone that helps move sugar from your bloodstream into your cells for energy.\\n\\nWhen cells become less sensitive to insulin:\\n- Blood sugar slowly rises over time\\n- Triglycerides increase\\n- Inflammation increases\\n- Weight gain around the midsection becomes easier\\n\\nThe good news? This stage is highly reversible with targeted nutrition, strength training, sleep optimization, and - when appropriate - medication support.\\n\\nWe'll focus on improving how your body processes fuel and reducing long-term cardiometabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward nutrition (>= 25-35g/meal)", "Carbohydrate quality + timing education", "Strength training 2-3x weekly", "10-15 min post-meal walking", "Sleep optimization", "Consider: GLP-1 therapy if obesity, appetite dysregulation, A1c >= 5.7, or inflammatory phenotype", "Consider: Metformin if prediabetes or strong family history", "Recheck metabolic panel in 3-6 months"]}, {"key": "hepatic", "name": "Hepatic (Liver-Driven) Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Monitor ALT, TG, ApoB, A1c every 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["ALT above optimal (>25 U/L)", "ApoB elevated", "A1c mildly elevated"], "pathophysiology": "Hepatic insulin resistance leads to increased hepatic glucose output + VLDL overproduction. Often early MASLD (metabolic-associated steatotic liver disease) physiology.", "triggerCriteria": ["ALT above optimal (>= 25 U/L)", "TG elevated or TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB elevated", "A1c may be normal or mildly elevated"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour labs suggest your liver may be under metabolic strain. The liver plays a major role in managing blood sugar and fat metabolism.\\n\\nWhen the liver becomes less responsive to insulin:\\n- Triglycerides rise\\n- Liver enzymes increase\\n- Fat can accumulate in the liver over time\\n\\nThis is common and often related to nutrition, stress, sleep, and weight distribution.\\n\\nThe encouraging part: liver-related metabolic changes are very responsive to targeted lifestyle changes and, if needed, medication support.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Weight reduction goal 5-10% if overweight", "Eliminate sugary beverages", "Alcohol reduction", "Protein-first nutrition", "Consider GLP-1 if weight loss appropriate", "Omega-3 if TG elevated"]}], "positiveCount": 4, "likelihoodLabel": "HIGH LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: HIGH LIKELIHOOD (4/6 markers positive). Identified phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance, Hepatic (Liver-Driven) Insulin Resistance. Treat according to phenotype-specific protocol.", "confirmationTests": "Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose."}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Inflammatory Burden", "confidence": "high", "description": "Pattern of systemic inflammation indicated by elevated inflammatory markers and/or symptomatic presentation. May benefit from anti-inflammatory nutritional support.", "supportingFindings": ["hs-CRP 26.6 mg/L (elevated >3.0)", "ApoB 96 mg/dL (elevated)", "Triglycerides 164 mg/dL (elevated)"]}, {"name": "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "confidence": "high", "description": "Metabolic pattern consistent with insulin resistance and/or visceral adiposity. This drives inflammation, hormonal imbalance, and cardiovascular risk.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance screening: HIGH LIKELIHOOD (4/6 markers positive)", "A1c 6.1% (prediabetic range)", "Triglycerides 164 mg/dL (elevated)", "ApoB 96 mg/dL (elevated)", "hs-CRP 26.6 mg/L (metabolic inflammation)", "ALT 73 U/L (above optimal for women >25)"]}, {"name": "Oxidative Stress Burden", "confidence": "high", "description": "Multiple markers suggest increased oxidative stress and cellular burden. Antioxidant and glutathione support may help reduce oxidative damage and support cellular energy production.", "supportingFindings": ["hs-CRP 26.6 mg/L (elevated, systemic inflammation)", "ALT 73 U/L (hepatic stress marker)", "AST 41 U/L (cell