65	Maria Matias	female	2026-03-27 00:00:00	{"a1c": 5.3, "alt": 95, "ast": 47, "bun": 15, "co2": 23, "mcv": 88.2, "rbc": 4.33, "tsh": 1.2, "wbc": 7.6, "acne": false, "egfr": 97, "iron": 51, "tibc": 431, "hsCRP": 19.5, "onHRT": false, "sodium": 141, "albumin": 4, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 9.2, "glucose": 84, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "chloride": 105, "ferritin": 159, "hairLoss": false, "bilirubin": 0.3, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": false, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 360, "potassium": 3.8, "creatinine": 0.86, "hematocrit": 38.2, "hemoglobin": 12.7, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2026-03-27", "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Maria Matias", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 23, "bmi": 49, "race": "other", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 138, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "restlessLegs": false, "totalProtein": 7.5, "lowMotivation": false, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History of present illness: Patient Maria Matias (23 y/o F) presented for routine hormone/primary care follow-up; today reviewing recent laboratory results. Patient denies current fever, chills, shortness of breath, chest pain, abdominal pain, jaundice, dark urine, syncope, new rash, or focal infectious symptoms at time of visit. No history provided today of recent procedures, dental work, or trauma. Patient instructed to bring full medication and supplement list; none submitted in chart at time of note.\\n- Menstrual/Hormone history: Age 23, premenopausal. No additional hormonal symptoms documented today (no report of virilization, oligomenorrhea, or amenorrhea in chart). Pregnancy status unknown; pregnancy test recommended prior to any medication changes as indicated.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: Not documented in chart today (BP, HR, BMI not available).\\n- Laboratory results (ordered previously; values below with units and flags):\\n  - CBC:\\n    - Hemoglobin 12.7 g/dL (normal)\\n    - Hematocrit 38.2 % (normal)\\n    - MCV 88.2 fL (normal)\\n    - Platelets 360 K/uL (normal)\\n  - Basic/metabolic & renal:\\n    - Creatinine 0.86 mg/dL (normal)\\n    - eGFR 97 mL/min (normal)\\n  - Liver panel / hepatic markers:\\n    - ALT 95 U/L — ABNORMAL (moderate elevation; >2× ULN)\\n    - AST 47 U/L — BORDERLINE (mild elevation)\\n    - FIB‑4 score 0.31 — normal (no current evidence of advanced fibrosis)\\n    - (Direct bilirubin, alkaline phosphatase not available in results provided; recommend obtain)\\n  - Iron studies:\\n    - Ferritin 159 ng/mL — BORDERLINE / elevated (may reflect acute phase)\\n    - Serum iron 51 ug/dL (normal)\\n    - TIBC 431 ug/dL (normal)\\n    - Calculated transferrin saturation not provided — recommend order\\n  - Inflammatory markers:\\n    - hs‑CRP 19.5 mg/L — CRITICAL (markedly elevated; indicates significant acute inflammation)\\n    - ESR not provided — consider\\n  - Endocrine / metabolic:\\n    - TSH 1.2 mIU/L (normal)\\n    - Hemoglobin A1c 5.3 % (normal)\\n    - Insulin resistance screening: 2 of 6 markers positive → INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (phenotype: visceral / metabolic insulin resistance). Recommendation to confirm with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA‑IR).\\n  - Other scores:\\n    - Sleep apnea risk (STOP‑BANG): 0 points (low risk)\\n  - Supplements recommended by prior review:\\n    - High-priority: GlutaClear® 2 caps daily; AdvaClear® 2 caps twice daily; OmegaGenics® Fish Oil Neuro 1000 — 1 softgel 1–2× daily.\\n    - Medium-priority: UltraFlora® Healthy Weight with Akkermansia — 1 capsule daily.\\n- Red flags: None reported by patient today, but labs include critical hs‑CRP and significant ALT elevation.\\n- Note: Medication/supplement list and alcohol use not documented — urgent reconciliation recommended.\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Marked systemic inflammation — hs‑CRP 19.5 mg/L (critical).\\n   - Clinical significance: Suggests active inflammatory or infectious process; requires urgent evaluation to identify source.\\n   - Red flag: hs‑CRP ≥10 mg/L — physician notified and urgent workup indicated.\\n\\n2. Hepatocellular injury — ALT 95 U/L (moderate elevation, >2× ULN); AST 47 U/L (mild elevation).\\n   - Clinical significance: Acute hepatocellular pattern of liver injury; consider infection, drug/supplement‑induced liver injury, NAFLD, autoimmune or viral hepatitis.\\n   - FIB‑4 0.31 suggests low likelihood of advanced fibrosis at this time.\\n   - Red flag: ALT ≥2× ULN — requires prompt clinician review and further testing.\\n\\n3. Ferritin elevation — 159 ng/mL (borderline/elevated).\\n   - Clinical significance: May represent acute‑phase reactant related to inflammation rather than iron overload given normal serum iron and TIBC. Transferrin saturation recommended to evaluate for hemochromatosis if clinically indicated.\\n\\n4. Moderate likelihood of insulin resistance / hyperinsulinemia physiology.\\n   - Clinical significance: 2/6 markers positive; phenotype consistent with visceral/metabolic insulin resistance. A1c normal at 5.3% — recommend fasting insulin and glucose for HOMA‑IR confirmation and metabolic counseling.\\n\\n5. No laboratory evidence of anemia, renal impairment, thyroid dysfunction, or advanced hepatic fibrosis at present.\\n   - Clinical significance: Baseline organ function otherwise intact per available labs.\\n\\nPLAN:\\n(Immediate / same day)\\n1. Communication:\\n   - Supervising physician notified today (03/27/2026) regarding critical hs‑CRP 19.5 mg/L and ALT 95 U/L. Discussed need for urgent evaluation and further testing.\\n2. Acute assessment and safety:\\n   - Obtain focused history for infection/inflammation: fever, chills, cough, dysuria, dental symptoms, skin/soft tissue infection, joint pain, recent procedures, OTC/herbal medication use, alcohol intake. Documented today as requested; patient denied fever or focal symptoms.\\n   - If patient develops fever, hemodynamic instability, or signs of sepsis → immediate ED referral per physician.\\n3. Medication/supplement management:\\n   - Hold suspected hepatotoxic nonessential agents pending medication reconciliation and physician decision. Examples to consider holding: oral 17‑alpha alkylated androgens (if present), transdermal/injectable testosterone (consider holding until reassessment), high‑dose acetaminophen, hepatotoxic herbal supplements (kava, black cohosh).\\n   - Do NOT independently discontinue essential prescription medications (e.g., statins) without physician order; discuss risk/benefit with supervising physician.\\n\\n(Urgent — within 24–72 hours)\\n4. Laboratory orders to obtain urgently:\\n   - Comprehensive metabolic panel (CMP) including direct bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase (if not already obtained).\\n   - Repeat AST/ALT (trend), total and direct bilirubin.\\n   - CBC with differential (if not recent).\\n   - Repeat hs‑CRP and ESR.\\n   - Hepatitis serologies: HBsAg, total anti‑HBc (or panel per local protocol), anti‑HCV (with reflex RNA if positive).\\n   - Iron studies: add transferrin saturation (calculate %); consider HFE genetic testing if transferrin saturation >45% or persistent ferritin elevation.\\n   - Fasting glucose and fasting insulin (for HOMA‑IR calculation).\\n   - Urine pregnancy test prior to any medication changes that affect pregnancy if patient is sexually active of childbearing potential.\\n   - Urinalysis ± urine culture if urinary symptoms present.\\n   - Blood cultures only if febrile/systemically ill per physician.\\n5. Clinical review:\\n   - Arrange clinician visit (same day or next day) to review medications, supplements, alcohol use, and to determine whether to hold hormone therapy (testosterone/estrogen) pending results.\\n\\n(Short term / 48–72 hours if infection suspected)\\n6. If infection suspected and treated:\\n   - Repeat hs‑CRP in 48–72 hours and recheck clinical status; expect decline if source treated.\\n\\n(Routine / 1–4 weeks)\\n7. Follow-up laboratory monitoring:\\n   - If no acute source identified: repeat LFTs (AST, ALT, bilirubin, alk phos), hs‑CRP, and ferritin in 1–2 weeks. If improving, repeat again at 4 weeks. If LFTs remain >2× ULN beyond 4 weeks or trend upward → refer to hepatology.\\n   - If LFTs worsen or bilirubin/INR abnormal → expedite hepatology/GI referral or ED per physician.\\n8. Imaging and specialty referral:\\n   - If persistent LFT elevation or concern for NAFLD/structural disease → order right upper quadrant ultrasound (RUQ US).\\n   - Hepatology referral criteria: ALT persistently >2× ULN for >4 weeks, worsening enzymes, abnormal imaging, or positive viral/hepatitis results.\\n9. Metabolic workup and management:\\n   - Confirm insulin resistance with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA‑IR). Obtain fasting lipid panel.\\n   - Begin counseling on weight management, dietary changes, and exercise as below.\\n10. Supplements:\\n   - Continue or initiate only per physician order. Prior recommendations documented: GlutaClear® 2 capsules daily; AdvaClear® 2 capsules twice daily; OmegaGenics® Fish Oil Neuro 1000 — 1 softgel 1–2 times daily (high‑priority). UltraFlora® Healthy Weight with Akkermansia — 1 capsule daily (medium‑priority). Reconcile with physician and ensure none are hepatotoxic; pause nonessential supplements until LFTs normalize.\\n11. Patient education and safety guidance (documented counseling provided today):\\n   - Advised immediate abstinence from alcohol until LFTs normalize and hepatology clearance.\\n   - Advise avoidance of acetaminophen and nonprescribed hepatotoxic supplements/drugs until cleared.\\n   - Instruct patient to report immediately: fever, jaundice, worsening RUQ pain, dark urine, confusion, or new bleeding/bruising.\\n   - Request patient bring or send a complete medication and supplement list to clinic before next visit.\\n12. Lifestyle counseling:\\n   - Discussed Mediterranean‑style diet, reduced added sugars/refined carbs, reduced saturated fats.\\n   - Target gradual weight loss if overweight (5–10% body weight reduction) and progressive aerobic exercise (goal ~150 min/week).\\n   - Smoking cessation counseling if applicable (not documented today).\\n13. Documentation and communication:\\n   - All communications and recommendations documented. Supervising physician informed and will determine next clinical actions including medication holds and further referrals.\\n\\nFollow-up and timing summary:\\n- Today (03/27/2026): Supervising physician notified. Focused history and counseling provided. Urgent labs ordered (CMP with bilirubin/alk phos, CBC, repeat hs‑CRP/ESR, hepatitis panel, transferrin saturation, fasting glucose/insulin, pregnancy test if applicable).\\n- Within 24–72 hours: Clinical review of urgent labs and decision on holding hepatotoxic medications/hormone therapy.\\n- 48–72 hours: Repeat hs‑CRP if infection suspected; reassess clinically.\\n- 1–2 weeks: Repeat LFTs and hs‑CRP if no acute source; if improving continue monitoring.\\n- 2–4 weeks: If persistent ALT >2× ULN or no improvement → hepatology referral and RUQ ultrasound.\\n- Routine recheck window for overall management: 3–6 months for longitudinal follow-up as baseline, with earlier checks as above.\\n\\nDisposition:\\n- Results reviewed with patient today. Patient denied fever or acute symptoms at time of contact and agreed to urgent lab work and to bring medication/supplement list.\\n- Supervising physician notified and plan for urgent labs/clinical reassessment arranged.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\nDate: 03/27/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": true, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "supplements": [{"dose": "2 capsules daily", "name": "GlutaClear®", "caution": "Glutathione and antioxidant support formula. Contains NAC, alpha-lipoic acid, and green tea catechins. Supports cellular defense against oxidative damage.", "category": "detox", "priority": "high", "rationale": "GlutaClear supports glutathione production and antioxidant defense with NAC, alpha-lipoic acid, and green tea catechins. Indicated when there is evidence of oxidative stress, inflammatory burden, or metabolic dysfunction requiring enhanced cellular defense. While AdvaClear provides broader liver detoxification support, GlutaClear specifically targets glutathione pathways and antioxidant capacity.", "indication": "Oxidative stress phenotype (glutathione/antioxidant support indicated); Inflammatory burden (antioxidant defense support); Insulin resistance (oxidative metabolic stress); hs-CRP 19.5 mg/L (high inflammatory/oxidative burden)", "phenotypes": ["Oxidative Stress Burden", "Inflammatory Burden", "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "This supplement boosts your body's master antioxidant, glutathione, which protects your cells from damage. Your results suggest increased oxidative stress, and this formula helps your body defend and repair at the cellular level.", "supportingFindings": ["Oxidative stress phenotype (glutathione/antioxidant support indicated)", "Inflammatory burden (antioxidant defense support)", "Insulin resistance (oxidative metabolic stress)", "hs-CRP 19.5 mg/L (high inflammatory/oxidative burden)", "ALT 95 U/L (hepatic glutathione demand)"]}, {"dose": "2 capsules twice daily", "name": "AdvaClear®", "caution": "Broad-spectrum phase I/II liver detoxification support. Contains NAC, green tea extract, milk thistle, and B-vitamins for biotransformation. Best taken with food.", "category": "detox", "priority": "high", "rationale": "AdvaClear provides broad-spectrum phase I and phase II liver detoxification support with NAC, green tea extract, watercress, and B-vitamins. Designed for metabolic clearance support, biotransformation of hormones and environmental compounds, and hepatic stress. Appropriate when liver markers are borderline, estrogen clearance is sluggish, or metabolic dysfunction is present.", "indication": "Oxidative stress burden (liver biotransformation support); Inflammatory burden (metabolic clearance support); Insulin resistance / metabolic dysfunction (hepatic metabolic support); Borderline liver markers (ALT 95, AST 47) suggesting hepatic biotransformation load", "phenotypes": ["Oxidative Stress Burden", "Inflammatory Burden", "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "This supplement supports your liver's natural detoxification processes. Your results suggest your body could benefit from help processing hormones, metabolic byproducts, and other compounds. It provides nutrients that support both phases of your liver's detox pathways.", "supportingFindings": ["Oxidative stress burden (liver biotransformation support)", "Inflammatory burden (metabolic clearance support)", "Insulin resistance / metabolic dysfunction (hepatic metabolic support)", "Borderline liver markers (ALT 95, AST 47) suggesting hepatic biotransformation load", "ALT 95 U/L (elevated, liver detox support indicated)", "hs-CRP 19.5 mg/L (inflammatory metabolic burden)"]}, {"dose": "1 softgel 1-2 times daily", "name": "OmegaGenics® Fish Oil Neuro 1000", "caution": "High-DHA omega-3 (750mg DHA, 250mg EPA) for brain and heart health. Lemon-flavored, no fishy taste. Contains calamari - avoid if shellfish allergy.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "high", "rationale": "OmegaGenics Fish Oil Neuro 1000 provides concentrated DHA and EPA to reduce triglycerides, support brain function, cardiovascular health, and reduce systemic inflammation.", "indication": "hs-CRP 19.5 mg/L (inflammatory)", "phenotypes": ["Inflammatory Burden", "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "This high-quality fish oil provides omega-3 fatty acids that help reduce inflammation, support your heart and brain health, and may help improve your cholesterol profile.", "supportingFindings": ["hs-CRP 19.5 mg/L (inflammatory)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Healthy Weight with Akkermansia", "caution": "Contains Akkermansia muciniphila and Bifidobacterium lactis B420 for metabolic and gut barrier support. Refrigerate after opening for maximum potency.", "category": "metabolic", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "UltraFlora Healthy Weight with Akkermansia provides targeted probiotic support for the gut-metabolic axis. Akkermansia muciniphila supports gut barrier integrity, metabolic health, and healthy weight management. Indicated for insulin resistance, metabolic syndrome features, visceral adiposity, and weight loss resistance.", "indication": "Insulin resistance / visceral adiposity phenotype (gut-metabolic axis support); Inflammatory phenotype (gut-immune axis); hs-CRP 19.5 mg/L (metabolic inflammation)", "phenotypes": ["Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "Inflammatory Burden"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This specialized probiotic contains Akkermansia, a beneficial gut bacteria linked to healthy metabolism and weight management. It supports your gut barrier, helps manage metabolic inflammation, and works with your body's natural weight regulation systems.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance / visceral adiposity phenotype (gut-metabolic axis support)", "Inflammatory phenotype (gut-immune axis)", "hs-CRP 19.5 mg/L (metabolic inflammation)"]}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Essential for patients on statins.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "low", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "hs-CRP 19.5 mg/L", "phenotypes": ["Inflammatory Burden", "Oxidative Stress Burden"], "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["hs-CRP 19.5 mg/L"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\nYour lab results show two important concerns: a moderate liver enzyme rise (ALT 95 U/L and AST 47 U/L) and a markedly high inflammation marker (hs-CRP 19.5 mg/L). Many other tests are reassuring (see positives below). Because hs-CRP is very elevated, this can raise ferritin (159 ng/mL) as part of inflammation rather than iron overload. Please contact your provider promptly so these critical results can be reviewed and followed.\\n\\nPositive findings (what’s good right now)\\n- Hemoglobin 12.7 g/dL and hematocrit 38.2% — red blood cell levels are in the normal range for adult females.\\n- MCV 88.2 fL — normal red blood cell size.\\n- Platelets 360 K/uL — normal.\\n- TSH 1.2 mIU/L — thyroid function appears normal now.\\n- Hemoglobin A1c 5.3% — blood sugar control is in the normal range.\\n- FIB-4 score 0.31 — this indicates LOW risk of advanced liver fibrosis.\\n- Kidney function normal: eGFR 97 mL/min and creatinine 0.86 mg/dL.\\n- Iron panel: serum iron 51 µg/dL and TIBC 431 µg/dL are within typical ranges; calculated transferrin saturation ≈ 12% (51/431×100), which is on the low side.\\n- STOP-BANG 0 points — low risk for sleep apnea.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + concrete actions)\\n1. Liver enzymes: ALT 95 U/L (moderate elevation), AST 47 U/L (mild). Actions:\\n   - Limit alcohol to <2 drinks per week.\\n   - Review and stop or change any non-essential supplements/medications that stress the liver (ask your provider or pharmacist about acetaminophen, herbal supplements, statins, etc.).\\n   - Aim for a healthy weight: follow a balanced diet and aim for 30 minutes brisk walking most days.\\n   - If ALT remains high after lifestyle changes, your provider may order liver ultrasound and viral hepatitis tests.\\n\\n2. Inflammation: hs-CRP 19.5 mg/L (markedly elevated). Actions:\\n   - Contact your provider within 1 week for evaluation—this level suggests acute inflammation or infection.\\n   - If you have fever, severe pain, chest pain, shortness of breath, or other acute symptoms, seek emergency care now.\\n   - Start anti-inflammatory lifestyle steps: Mediterranean-style diet (vegetables, whole grains, olive oil, nuts), 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly, 7–8 hours sleep, stress reduction (daily 10 minutes meditation), and 30 minutes moderate exercise most days.\\n\\n3. Ferritin 159 ng/mL (elevated) with low transferrin saturation (~12%): likely raised by inflammation. Do NOT start iron supplements now. Repeat ferritin and hs-CRP in 4–8 weeks once inflammation settles.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Call your provider within 1 week to discuss ALT 95 U/L and hs-CRP 19.5 mg/L.\\n- If stable, repeat liver panel, hs-CRP, ferritin, and iron studies in 4–8 weeks.\\n- If ALT remains elevated or symptoms persist, expect imaging (liver ultrasound) and additional testing.\\nYou’re doing many things well — we’ll use these steps to clarify the cause and protect your liver and overall health.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 12.7, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 38.2, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 88.2, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 88.2 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 360, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.2, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 159, "status": "borderline", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Elevated ferritin.", "recommendation": "May indicate inflammation, hemochromatosis, or liver disease. Correlate clinically.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.3, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 47, "status": "borderline", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "Mild AST elevation.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle counseling. Repeat in 4-6 weeks.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 95, "status": "abnormal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "Moderate ALT elevation.", "recommendation": "Evaluate for NAFLD, medications. Repeat labs.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "score", "value": 0.31, "status": "normal", "category": "FIB-4 Score (Liver Fibrosis)", "recheckTiming": "", "interpretation": "FIB-4 score 0.31 indicates LOW risk of advanced fibrosis (F0-F1). Negative predictive value >90%.", "recommendation": "Advanced fibrosis unlikely. Continue lifestyle modifications and routine LFT monitoring. Address underlying cause of LFT elevation.", "referenceRange": "<1.30 low risk, 1.30-2.67 indeterminate, >2.67 high risk"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 97, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.86, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 431, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 51, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 19.5, "status": "critical", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Markedly elevated hs-CRP - acute inflammation.", "recommendation": "Evaluate for infection or inflammatory condition.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "of 6 markers positive", "value": 2, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Insulin Resistance Screening", "recheckTiming": "3-6 months", "interpretation": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive). This combination points toward hyperinsulinemia physiology. Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR).", "recommendation": "Phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance. Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose.", "referenceRange": "0-1 Low, 2 Moderate, 3+ High Likelihood"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- Key abnormalities: hs-CRP 19.5 mg/L (markedly elevated → significant acute inflammation), ALT 95 U/L (moderate hepatic transaminase elevation, >2× upper limit of normal), AST 47 U/L (mild elevation), ferritin 159 ng/mL (elevated; may reflect inflammation or iron overload).\\n- Pattern suggests acute inflammatory state with concurrent hepatocellular injury. Ferritin elevation may be acute‑phase related rather than iron overload given normal serum iron and TIBC.\\n- No current laboratory evidence of anemia, renal dysfunction, thyroid disease, diabetes, or advanced liver fibrosis (FIB‑4 normal). Clinical correlation required for infection, inflammatory conditions, hepatotoxic exposures, and NAFLD.\\n\\nPriority actions (organized CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL (same‑day)\\n- Notify supervising physician immediately given hs‑CRP 19.5 mg/L and ALT 95 U/L.\\n- Obtain immediate clinical assessment for systemic infection or inflammatory source: current fever, hemodynamic instability, localized infection (respiratory, urinary, dental), new rash, joint pain, recent procedures, or trauma.\\n- If febrile or clinically septic → order blood cultures, CBC with differential if not recent, urinalysis/urine culture, and consider ED referral per physician.\\n\\nURGENT (within 24–72 hours)\\n- Hold or pause hepatotoxic medications/supplements pending medication review and physician decision:\\n  * If on oral anabolic/androgenic agents (methyltestosterone/fluoxymesterone) → discontinue immediately and notify physician.\\n  * If on transdermal testosterone or injectable testosterone → consider holding until LFTs improve; physician to confirm.\\n  * Pause nonessential hepatotoxic agents: high‑dose acetaminophen, certain herbal supplements (e.g., kava, black cohosh), and review statin use — do not automatically stop statin without physician direction if CHD risk is high.\\n- Order targeted labs urgently: CMP (if not already done with ALT/AST), direct bilirubin, alkaline phosphatase, complete hepatitis panel (HBsAg, anti‑HBc total, anti‑HCV), CRP repeat or ESR, CBC (if not recent), and urine pregnancy test if applicable to guide therapy decisions.\\n- Arrange same‑day/next‑day clinician review of medication list (including HRT, OCPs, over‑the‑counter medications and supplements) and alcohol intake.\\n\\nROUTINE (within 1–4 weeks unless otherwise directed)\\n- Repeat LFTs (AST, ALT, bilirubin, alkaline phosphatase), hs‑CRP and ferritin:\\n  * If acute infection suspected → repeat hs‑CRP in 48–72 hours and again after resolution.\\n  * If no acute source identified → repeat LFTs and hs‑CRP in 1–2 weeks; if trending down and stable, repeat again at 4 weeks.\\n- If LFTs remain elevated or worsen, escalate to hepatology.\\n\\nTestosterone dose recommendations (staff actions for physician consideration)\\n- If patient is currently prescribed testosterone:\\n  * For transdermal or injectable testosterone: recommend holding further doses until LFTs are reassessed by physician given ALT >2× ULN and elevated hs‑CRP.\\n  * For oral 17‑alpha alkylated androgens (methyltestosterone/fluoxymesterone): discontinue immediately (these are hepatotoxic) and notify physician.\\n  * If physician elects to continue therapy: recommend dose reduction only under direct physician order and with closer LFT monitoring (repeat LFTs within 1 week).\\n- Document route/dose/date of last testosterone dose and communicate to physician; do not independently restart or escalate dose without physician approval.\\n- Note: review HRT/estrogen therapy — estrogens can increase CRP and affect hepatic metabolism; include in medication review for dose adjustments.\\n\\nLifestyle interventions to recommend to patient (staff to counsel and document)\\n- Immediate:\\n  * Advise temporary abstinence from alcohol until LFTs normalize and hepatology cleared.\\n  * Advise avoidance of acetaminophen and other nonprescribed hepatotoxic supplements/agents until cleared.\\n- Ongoing:\\n  * Counsel on weight management (caloric reduction, 5–10% weight loss target if overweight), Mediterranean‑style diet (reduce added sugars, refined carbs, and saturated fat), and progressive aerobic exercise (150 min/week) to reduce NAFLD risk.\\n  * Review safe use of over‑the‑counter medications and supplements; bring all supplements/OTC meds to follow‑up.\\n  * Reinforce infection prevention and prompt reporting of fevers or new symptoms.\\n\\nAdditional testing or referrals to consider\\n- Laboratory:\\n  * Hepatitis serologies (HBsAg, anti‑HBc, anti‑HBs if indicated; HCV Ab with reflex HCV RNA if positive).\\n  * Repeat CMP with AST/ALT, direct bilirubin, alk phos.\\n  * ESR and repeat hs‑CRP to trend inflammatory activity.\\n  * Iron studies: ferritin already elevated — add transferrin saturation (serum iron / TIBC ×100) to evaluate for hemochromatosis; if transferrin saturation >45% or persistent ferritin elevation → consider HFE genetic testing.\\n  * Fasting lipid panel, fasting glucose/insulin (if clinical metabolic risk suspected).\\n- Imaging:\\n  * Right upper quadrant ultrasound if ALT/AST remain elevated or if NAFLD suspected.\\n- Referrals:\\n  * Hepatology if ALT remains >2× ULN for >4 weeks, if ALT/AST trend upward, or if imaging/viral hepatitis tests abnormal.\\n  * Infectious disease or primary care/ED if active systemic infection suspected.\\n  * Gastroenterology/hepatology sooner if bilirubin/INR abnormal or clinical decompensation.\\n- Autoimmune evaluation (ANA, ASMA, anti‑LKM) if workup for viral/obstructive causes negative and LFT abnormalities persist.\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (staff checklist)\\n1. Immediate/same day\\n   - Notify supervising physician now (hs‑CRP 19.5 mg/L + ALT 95 U/L).\\n   - Obtain targeted history for infection, recent procedures, medication/supplement use, alcohol intake; document.\\n   - If febrile/systemically ill → direct ED referral per physician.\\n2. Within 24–72 hours\\n   - Hold hepatotoxic medications per physician guidance (see recommendations above).\\n   - Order/collect urgent labs: CMP with bilirubin/alk phos, CBC, repeat hs‑CRP/ESR, hepatitis B/C serologies, transferrin saturation, urine pregnancy if applicable.\\n   - Arrange clinician review of results and determine whether to hold testosterone.\\n3. 48–72 hours (if infection suspected)\\n   - Repeat hs‑CRP and clinical reassessment after treating source.\\n4. 1–2 weeks\\n   - Repeat LFTs and hs‑CRP (earlier if clinically indicated). If improving → continue monitoring; if stable/worsening → escalate.\\n5. 2–4 weeks\\n   - If LFTs remain >2× ULN or no improvement: arrange hepatology referral and hepatic imaging (RUQ ultrasound).\\n6. Documentation & patient counseling\\n   - Document all communications, medication holds/changes, and education provided. Provide clear return precautions (fever, jaundice, RUQ pain, worsening symptoms).\\n\\nPhysician notification requirements (must be communicated promptly)\\n- Notify physician immediately for:\\n  * hs‑CRP ≥10 mg/L (critical here at 19.5 mg/L).\\n  * ALT ≥2× ULN (ALT 95 U/L).\\n  * Any fever, systemic signs, or hemodynamic instability.\\n  * Decision to stop or hold prescription testosterone or other essential medications.\\n  * Repeat labs that are stable or worsening after initial reassessment.\\n\\nStaff patient education points to cover (concise)\\n- Explain that recent labs show significant inflammation and elevated liver enzymes; clinician will review urgently.\\n- Instruct patient to stop alcohol and avoid acetaminophen and nonessential supplements until cleared.\\n- Ask patient to report fever, abdominal pain, jaundice, dark urine, or new bruising immediately.\\n- Request medication/supplement list be brought to next visit or sent to clinic.\\n\\nNotes on cycle/HRT interactions and female‑specific concerns\\n- Consider menstrual blood loss contribution to iron studies if heavy menses; however ferritin elevation here favors acute‑phase reaction rather than iron deficiency.\\n- Review current estrogen therapies/oral contraceptives as estrogens can affect CRP and hepatic metabolism—include in medication reconciliation.\\n- If patient is of childbearing potential and on testosterone: ensure pregnancy test is available prior to resumption of therapy per clinic protocol.\\n\\nDo not diagnose; these are operational recommendations for staff and the supervising clinician to act upon.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "A1c", "value": "5.3%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 95, "detail": "Above optimal range - may indicate hepatic insulin resistance", "positive": true, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "19.5 mg/L", "detail": "Elevated systemic inflammation", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "moderate", "phenotypes": [{"key": "visceral_metabolic", "name": "Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Recheck A1c, lipid panel, hs-CRP, ApoB in 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["hs-CRP >= 2.0", "ALT above optimal range"], "pathophysiology": "Early peripheral + hepatic insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia. Glucose may still be \\"normal.\\" A1c often rises last.", "triggerCriteria": ["A1c >= 5.7%", "TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0", "Central adiposity phenotype", "ALT above optimal range"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests your body may not be responding to insulin as efficiently as it should. Insulin is the hormone that helps move sugar from your bloodstream into your cells for energy.\\n\\nWhen cells become less sensitive to insulin:\\n- Blood sugar slowly rises over time\\n- Triglycerides increase\\n- Inflammation increases\\n- Weight gain around the midsection becomes easier\\n\\nThe good news? This stage is highly reversible with targeted nutrition, strength training, sleep optimization, and - when appropriate - medication support.\\n\\nWe'll focus on improving how your body processes fuel and reducing long-term cardiometabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward nutrition (>= 25-35g/meal)", "Carbohydrate quality + timing education", "Strength training 2-3x weekly", "10-15 min post-meal walking", "Sleep optimization", "Consider: GLP-1 therapy if obesity, appetite dysregulation, A1c >= 5.7, or inflammatory phenotype", "Consider: Metformin if prediabetes or strong family history", "Recheck metabolic panel in 3-6 months"]}], "positiveCount": 2, "likelihoodLabel": "MODERATE LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive). This combination points toward hyperinsulinemia physiology. Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR).", "confirmationTests": "Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose."}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Inflammatory Burden", "confidence": "high", "description": "Pattern of systemic inflammation indicated by elevated inflammatory markers and/or symptomatic presentation. May benefit from anti-inflammatory nutritional support.", "supportingFindings": ["hs-CRP 19.5 mg/L (elevated >3.0)"]}, {"name": "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Metabolic pattern consistent with insulin resistance and/or visceral adiposity. This drives inflammation, hormonal imbalance, and cardiovascular risk.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance screening: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive)", "hs-CRP 19.5 mg/L (metabolic inflammation)", "ALT 95 U/L (above optimal for women >25)"]}, {"name": "Oxidative Stress Burden", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Multiple markers suggest increased oxidative stress and cellular burden. Antioxidant and glutathione support may help reduce oxidative damage and support cellular energy production.", "supportingFindings": ["hs-CRP 19.5 mg/L (elevated, systemic inflammation)", "ALT 95 U/L (hepatic stress marker)", "AST 47 U/L (cellular stress marker)", "Insulin resistance pattern (metabolic oxidative driver)"]}, {"name": "Iron Deficiency", "confidence": "low", "description": "Iron stores are depleted or suboptimal. Iron is essential for energy, cognitive function, hair health, and oxygen transport.", "supportingFindings": ["Serum iron 51 µg/dL (low)"]}]}	2026-03-27 17:07:02.725479	1
66	MMorris	female	2026-03-27 19:19:51.954	{"a1c": 5, "alt": 15, "ast": 19, "bun": 9, "co2": 23, "fsh": 6.45, "hdl": 73, "ldl": 72, "lpa": 22, "mcv": 92.1, "rbc": 3.92, "tsh": 2.15, "wbc": 7.2, "acne": false, "apoB": 62, "egfr": 101, "iron": 70, "shbg": 101, "tibc": 342, "hsCRP": 0.6, "onHRT": false, "folate": 7.3, "freeT4": 1.14, "sodium": 140, "albumin": 4, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 9.2, "glucose": 87, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "chloride": 107, "ferritin": 43.1, "hairLoss": false, "vitaminD": 18, "bilirubin": 0.2, "estradiol": 78, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": false, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 344, "potassium": 3.7, "creatinine": 0.74, "hematocrit": 36.1, "hemoglobin": 12, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "vitaminB12": 2000, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "MMorris", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 46, "bmi": 26.1, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 110, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 0.28, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 13.5, "totalProtein": 6.4, "lowMotivation": false, "triglycerides": 85, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "freeTestosterone": 1, "totalCholesterol": 162}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History of present illness: 46-year-old female; labs reviewed today showing severe vitamin D deficiency, luteal progesterone deficiency consistent with perimenopause pattern, elevated SHBG with reduced bioavailable estrogen, borderline low total testosterone, and low‑normal ferritin. Patient reports symptoms consistent with perimenopause including fatigue and decreased libido (patient-reported). No acute red-flag symptoms reported today (no chest pain, shortness of breath, syncope, severe abdominal pain, neurologic deficits, or bleeding).\\n- Gynecologic/menstrual history: Perimenopausal pattern suggested by labs (low luteal progesterone with preserved estradiol). Specific cycle regularity not recorded today — recommend confirmation at next visit.\\n- Medications/supplements: Patient likely taking supplemental B12 given level 2000 pg/mL (to be confirmed). Full medication list to be reviewed for oral estrogen/OCP, thyroid meds or other SHBG drivers.\\n- Allergies: Not documented in chart today.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: Not recorded in this encounter / not available.\\n- CBC:\\n  - Hemoglobin 12 g/dL (normal)\\n  - Hematocrit 36.1% (normal)\\n  - MCV 92.1 fL (normal)\\n  - Platelets 344 K/uL (normal)\\n- Iron studies:\\n  - Ferritin 43.1 ng/mL [BORDERLINE] — low-normal / iron insufficiency (optimal >50 ng/mL)\\n  - Serum Iron 70 ug/dL (normal)\\n  - TIBC 342 ug/dL (normal)\\n- Metabolic / Renal / Liver:\\n  - Creatinine 0.74 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - eGFR 101 mL/min/1.73m2 (normal)\\n  - AST 19 U/L (normal)\\n  - ALT 15 U/L (normal)\\n- Lipids / CV risk:\\n  - LDL-C 72 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - HDL-C 73 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - Triglycerides 85 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - Apolipoprotein B 62 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - Lipoprotein(a) 22 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - 10-yr Total CVD risk (PREVENT) 0.7% (low)\\n  - 10-yr ASCVD risk (PREVENT) 0.4% (low)\\n  - 10-yr HF risk (PREVENT) 0.4% (low)\\n  - 30-yr CVD risk (PREVENT) 5.4%\\n- Inflammatory / metabolic:\\n  - hs-CRP 0.6 mg/L (normal)\\n  - Hemoglobin A1c 5% (normal)\\n- Thyroid:\\n  - TSH 2.15 mIU/L (normal)\\n- Hormones:\\n  - Estradiol (E2) 78 pg/mL (normal)\\n  - Progesterone 0.28 ng/mL [ABNORMAL] — luteal progesterone deficiency\\n  - FSH 6.45 mIU/mL (normal)\\n  - SHBG 101 nmol/L [ABNORMAL] — elevated\\n  - Total Testosterone 13.5 ng/dL [BORDERLINE] — low/low-normal\\n  - Free Testosterone 1 pg/mL (normal per lab reference)\\n  - Bioavailable estrogen calculated reduced given high SHBG and E2 78 pg/mL — reported as Reduced Bioavailable Estrogen (borderline)\\n- Vitamins / other:\\n  - Vitamin D (25-OH) 18 ng/mL [ABNORMAL] — severe deficiency (≤20 ng/mL)\\n  - Vitamin B12 2000 pg/mL [BORDERLINE] — elevated (most consistent with supplementation)\\n  - Folate 7.3 ng/mL (normal)\\n- Other:\\n  - Apolipoprotein B and Lp(a) listed above.\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Severe Vitamin D deficiency — 25‑OH Vitamin D 18 ng/mL. Clinical significance: fatigue, bone health and immune implications. Requires rapid repletion per clinic protocol.\\n2. Luteal progesterone deficiency consistent with perimenopausal physiology — progesterone 0.28 ng/mL with estradiol 78 pg/mL. Clinical significance: sleep disturbance, anxiety, mood changes; may benefit from micronized progesterone.\\n3. Elevated SHBG 101 nmol/L with reduced bioavailable estrogen despite normal total estradiol. Clinical significance: tissue-level estrogen deficiency symptoms despite \\"normal\\" E2. Consider medication/supplement drivers (oral estrogen, OCPs, thyroid meds), hepatic causes less likely given normal LFTs.\\n4. Borderline low total testosterone — 13.5 ng/dL with low bioavailable androgen expected given high SHBG. Correlates with symptoms of low libido and decreased energy. Consider evaluation and possible low-dose testosterone therapy after provider review.\\n5. Ferritin low-normal / iron insufficiency — ferritin 43.1 ng/mL (optimal >50 ng/mL). May contribute to fatigue; consider iron repletion if symptomatic.\\n6. Markedly elevated Vitamin B12 — 2000 pg/mL, most consistent with supplementation. If patient not supplementing, consider further evaluation (rare hepatic or myeloproliferative causes), but LFTs normal.\\n7. Other labs reassuring: CBC otherwise normal; lipids and CV risk low; TSH normal; renal and liver function normal.\\nRed flags requiring physician sign-off: initiation of high-dose vitamin D repletion, initiation of systemic sex-steroid therapy (micronized progesterone or testosterone), unexplained persistently elevated B12 if not on supplements.\\n\\nPLAN:\\n(General) Results reviewed and discussed with patient. Patient education provided regarding perimenopausal hormonal pattern, vitamin D deficiency, and iron status. Patient verbalized understanding. Provider sign-off required for hormone therapy and high-dose vitamin D repletion.\\n\\n1) Severe Vitamin D deficiency (25-OH D 18 ng/mL)\\n   - Start vitamin D3 repletion per clinic protocol: D3 10,000 IU PO daily with potassium (Metagenics D3 10,000 + K softgel) — provider authorization required today.\\n   - Counsel on taking with a meal containing fat; review signs of hypercalcemia to report.\\n   - Recheck 25-OH Vitamin D in 8–12 weeks to confirm repletion; target >30–50 ng/mL per clinic goals. Adjust to maintenance dosing thereafter.\\n   - Documented need for provider notification: provider to authorize repletion plan.\\n\\n2) Luteal progesterone deficiency / perimenopause pattern (Progesterone 0.28 ng/mL, E2 78 pg/mL)\\n   - Recommend micronized progesterone for symptom management if patient symptomatic (sleep disturbance, anxiety, insomnia):\\n     - Micronized progesterone 100–200 mg PO qHS. Regimen choice:\\n       - If cycles regular: cyclic dosing (e.g., day 14–28 of cycle) OR\\n       - If cycles irregular/perimenopausal: nightly continuous dosing acceptable.\\n     - Provider to select dose/regimen and obtain informed consent; counsel on sedation as common side effect.\\n   - Arrange follow-up symptom review at 6–8 weeks after initiation to assess response and tolerability.\\n   - If new abnormal uterine bleeding develops after progesterone start, refer to gynecology.\\n\\n3) Elevated SHBG with reduced bioavailable estrogen (SHBG 101 nmol/L)\\n   - Review medication list immediately for oral estrogen/OCPs, thyroid replacement, or other SHBG-inducing agents. If patient on oral estrogen, discuss converting to transdermal estrogen with provider to reduce SHBG effects.\\n   - Counsel on lifestyle drivers (alcohol reduction, optimize weight/metabolic health).\\n   - Order bioavailable testosterone and/or calculated free androgen index to clarify androgen availability (requested today).\\n   - Recheck SHBG at ~3 months after medication changes or interventions.\\n\\n4) Borderline low total testosterone (13.5 ng/dL) with symptomatic low libido\\n   - Consider testosterone optimization only after provider review, informed consent, and pregnancy testing if applicable.\\n   - If approved, recommended starting dosing (examples for provider):\\n     - Transdermal testosterone cream/gel 300–600 mcg/day (0.3–0.6 mg/day) applied daily; start low and titrate.\\n     - Alternative: low-dose transdermal patch per product dosing if clinic formulary used.\\n   - Monitoring: recheck total testosterone trough 6–8 weeks after initiation/titration; monitor for virilizing effects, lipids, LFTs, Hgb/Hct as per clinic protocol.\\n   - Document pregnancy test prior to initiation if patient of childbearing potential.\\n   - Do not start without provider sign-off.\\n\\n5) Ferritin 43.1 ng/mL — iron insufficiency\\n   - If symptomatic (fatigue, hair loss, RLS, exercise intolerance): begin iron supplementation:\\n     - Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support 1 tablet twice daily with meals OR\\n     - Oral elemental iron alternative: ferrous sulfate 325 mg PO once daily (or every other day if GI intolerance) — provider to choose based on protocol and tolerance.\\n   - Advise concurrent vitamin C to enhance absorption; avoid taking with calcium, tea/coffee or high-fiber meals.\\n   - Recheck ferritin and CBC in 8–12 weeks to assess response; aim ferritin >50 ng/mL.\\n   - If symptoms absent and patient prefers, consider dietary measures first and recheck in 8–12 weeks.\\n\\n6) Elevated B12 2000 pg/mL\\n   - Confirm history of B12 supplementation (oral or IM). If patient is taking high-dose B12, counsel on continuation vs dose reduction per patient/provider preference.\\n   - If no supplementation reported, consider further workup: methylmalonic acid level and/or hepatology referral if persistent and unexplained. LFTs currently normal.\\n   - Document patient supplement history.\\n\\n7) Additional testing / orders placed:\\n   - Bioavailable testosterone (or calculated free androgen index) — order today.\\n   - Pregnancy test if patient of childbearing potential prior to initiating progesterone or any testosterone therapy — order today if applicable.\\n   - Recheck labs:\\n     - 25-OH Vitamin D in 8–12 weeks after starting repletion.\\n     - Ferritin + CBC in 8–12 weeks after starting iron.\\n     - Total testosterone trough 6–8 weeks after initiation/titration (if started).\\n     - SHBG recheck at ~3 months after medication changes or therapy.\\n   - Consider methylmalonic acid if B12 elevation unexplained after confirming supplement use.\\n\\n8) Referrals / consultations:\\n   - Endocrinology consult as needed for complex hormone management or if provider requests assistance.\\n   - Nutrition/dietitian referral for targeted diet to optimize iron and vitamin D intake, and weight/metabolic counseling if desired.\\n   - Gynecology referral for abnormal bleeding or complex reproductive concerns.\\n\\n9) Lifestyle and patient education (counseled today)\\n   - Vitamin D: safe sun exposure where appropriate; include dietary sources (fatty fish, fortified dairy); adhere to repletion regimen and follow-up testing.\\n   - Iron: eat iron-rich foods (red meat, legumes, leafy greens); take iron with vitamin C; avoid calcium and tea/coffee near dosing; expect 8–12 weeks to see ferritin improvement.\\n   - Hormone lifestyle: maintain healthy weight, regular resistance and aerobic exercise, limit alcohol (alcohol can raise SHBG), optimize sleep hygiene to support progesterone’s sedative effects.\\n   - Review medication and supplement list — specifically confirm oral estrogen, thyroid meds, and B12 supplementation.\\n\\nFollow-up / Timeline:\\n- Immediate (today, 03/27/2026): Provider to authorize high-dose vitamin D repletion. Order bioavailable testosterone and pregnancy test if applicable. Review medication/supplement list for SHBG drivers.\\n- Within 1–2 weeks: Provider decision re: micronized progesterone initiation (if symptomatic) and iron supplementation initiation (if symptomatic). Begin Hemagenics RBC Support or oral iron per protocol if approved.\\n- 6–8 weeks: Symptom reassessment after progesterone start; check total testosterone trough 6–8 weeks after starting testosterone (if initiated).\\n- 8–12 weeks: Recheck 25-OH Vitamin D and ferritin + CBC.\\n- 3 months: Reassess SHBG and overall symptom response; escalate therapy or refer as needed.\\n\\nPhysician notification triggers:\\n- Starting high-dose vitamin D or starting progesterone/testosterone therapy.\\n- Unexplained persistent elevated B12 when not taking supplements.\\n- New abnormal LFTs, unexpected hematologic changes, or patient develops concerning symptoms.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\nDate: 03/27/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "preventRisk": {"ldlGoal": "No specific LDL target; focus on risk-based statin intensity", "riskCategory": "low", "tenYearASCVD": 0.0040478693087869595, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0.006737904206555877, "thirtyYearASCVD": 0.029731380209965137, "ageValidFor30Year": true, "thirtyYearTotalCVD": 0.054217942944547654, "tenYearHFPercentage": "0.4%", "tenYearHeartFailure": 0.003663950194132457, "statinRecommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "tenYearCVDPercentage": "0.7%", "tenYearASCVDPercentage": "0.4%", "thirtyYearHFPercentage": "3.6%", "thirtyYearHeartFailure": 0.03606668723120054, "thirtyYearCVDPercentage": "5.4%", "thirtyYearASCVDPercentage": "3.0%"}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 softgel daily with meal", "name": "D3 10,000 + K", "caution": "High-dose repletion therapy. Recheck vitamin D levels in 8-12 weeks. Contains vitamin K2 for calcium metabolism.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "high", "rationale": "D3 10,000 + K provides high-dose vitamin D3 with K2 for efficient repletion of severe deficiency. K2 ensures proper calcium utilization and prevents arterial calcification.", "indication": "Vitamin D 18 ng/mL (severe deficiency ≤20)", "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D level is very low, which can affect your bones, immune system, mood, and energy. This high-dose supplement will help bring your level up to optimal range, and the vitamin K2 helps your body use calcium properly.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 18 ng/mL (severe deficiency ≤20)"]}, {"dose": "1 tablet twice daily with meals", "name": "Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support", "caution": "Contains iron, folate, and B12 for comprehensive RBC support. Avoid taking with calcium-rich foods or dairy. May cause mild GI upset initially.", "category": "iron", "priority": "high", "rationale": "Hemagenics provides highly absorbable iron bisglycinate with B12, B6, and folate for comprehensive red blood cell support. Indicated for functional iron deficiency, iron deficiency without anemia, and iron-responsive symptoms.", "indication": "Ferritin 43.1 ng/mL (suboptimal ≤50, optimal >50)", "phenotypes": ["Iron Deficiency"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "Your lab results suggest your iron stores are lower than optimal. This supplement provides a gentle, well-absorbed form of iron along with B12 and folate to support your energy levels, hair health, and overall vitality.", "supportingFindings": ["Ferritin 43.1 ng/mL (suboptimal ≤50, optimal >50)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 22, "apoBValue": 62, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "normal", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 0.40478693087869594, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": false}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "consider", "rationale": "Lipid levels meet threshold for CAC consideration. CAC may be useful if risk level is uncertain or patient is hesitant about statin.", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\n- You have several important findings to address: severe vitamin D deficiency (Vitamin D 25-OH = 18 ng/mL), signs of perimenopause with luteal progesterone deficiency (Progesterone = 0.28 ng/mL with Estradiol = 78 pg/mL, age 46), and a high SHBG (101 nmol/L) that reduces bioavailable estrogen and testosterone. Your 10-year ASCVD risk is low (0.4%) and many metabolic and blood tests are within healthy ranges.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Hemoglobin 12 g/dL, Hematocrit 36.1%, MCV 92.1 fL (all within normal limits).\\n- Lipids: LDL 72 mg/dL, HDL 73 mg/dL, Triglycerides 85 mg/dL (good levels).\\n- Kidney and liver: eGFR 101 mL/min, AST 19 U/L, ALT 15 U/L (normal).\\n- Glucose control: Hemoglobin A1c 5.0% (normal).\\n- Inflammation: hs-CRP 0.6 mg/L (low).\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + concrete actions)\\n1. Vitamin D: 18 ng/mL (severe deficiency; ≤20 ng/mL).\\n   - Action: Start Vitamin D3 supplementation after discussing with your provider. Common options: 50,000 IU D3 once weekly for 8–12 weeks OR 5,000–6,000 IU D3 daily for 8–12 weeks, then maintenance 1,000–2,000 IU daily. Increase safe sun exposure (10–20 minutes arms/face 3x weekly) and include vitamin D–rich foods (fatty fish, fortified yogurt). Recheck 25-OH Vitamin D in 8–12 weeks.\\n\\n2. Perimenopause / hormones:\\n   - Progesterone 0.28 ng/mL (low for a luteal-phase sample); Estradiol 78 pg/mL; FSH 6.45 mIU/mL.\\n   - Action: If you were tested during the luteal phase, consider repeating a mid‑luteal progesterone (7 days before expected period) to confirm. Discuss symptom management and options (cyclical progesterone, estrogen/progesterone therapy) with your clinician if you have irregular periods, heavy bleeding, night sweats, or sleep changes.\\n\\n3. SHBG 101 nmol/L and reduced bioavailable estrogen (calculated effect):\\n   - Action: Review medications and supplements that raise SHBG (oral estrogen, combined OCPs, some thyroid medications). Your TSH 2.15 mIU/L and liver enzymes are normal, so discuss with your provider possible causes and whether changing medication or hormone therapy is appropriate.\\n\\n4. Testosterone and iron metabolism:\\n   - Total Testosterone 13.5 ng/dL (low). Free Testosterone 1 pg/mL (within functional range).\\n   - Actions for testosterone support: Prioritize 7–8 hours sleep nightly, strength train 2–3x/week, manage stress (15–20 minutes daily of walking/meditation), and maintain protein with meals.\\n   - Ferritin 43.1 ng/mL (borderline; optimal >50 ng/mL). Hemoglobin 12 g/dL is currently OK.\\n   - Actions for iron: Eat iron-rich foods (3–4 servings/week of poultry/red meat, beans, iron-fortified cereal), pair meals with vitamin C (orange juice, bell peppers), avoid tea/coffee at meals, and consider an oral iron supplement (30–65 mg elemental iron daily) if recommended by your provider. Recheck ferritin in 8–12 weeks if supplementing.\\n\\n5. Vitamin B12 2000 pg/mL (elevated)\\n   - Action: Review and stop high-dose B12 supplements if you are taking them; discuss with your provider before making changes.\\n\\nCardiovascular risk and lifestyle\\n- Your 10-year ASCVD risk is 0.4% (very low). This means your short-term risk of heart attack or stroke is low. Continue heart-healthy habits: 150 minutes moderate aerobic activity weekly (brisk walking), Mediterranean-style eating (vegetables, whole grains, olive oil, fish, nuts), and maintain your current LDL 72 mg/dL and HDL 73 mg/dL.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Start Vitamin D therapy now and recheck 25-OH Vitamin D in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Repeat mid-luteal progesterone if cycle-timed testing was not precise, or discuss perimenopause treatment options within 4–8 weeks.\\n- If starting iron supplementation, recheck ferritin and hemoglobin in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Review current medications/supplements (especially estrogen, OCPs, B12) with your clinician at your next visit (within 4–12 weeks).\\n- If you have bone pain, fractures, or family history of osteoporosis, ask about a DEXA scan given vitamin D deficiency and perimenopausal status.\\n\\nYou’re making good progress in many areas. Small, targeted steps (vitamin D treatment, iron support, sleep and strength training, and medication review) can make a big difference. Please schedule a follow-up with your provider to start these steps and coordinate re‑testing.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 12, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 36.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 92.1, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 92.1 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 344, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 78, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.28, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 6.45, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 13.5, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Low testosterone.", "recommendation": "May contribute to low libido or fatigue. Consider HRT discussion.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 2.15, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 43.1, "status": "borderline", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Iron insufficiency (ferritin 31-50). Optimal is >50.", "recommendation": "Provider protocol: Metagenics Hemagenics if symptomatic (fatigue, hair loss, restless legs, exercise intolerance).", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 18, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Severe Vitamin D deficiency (≤20 ng/mL). Associated with fatigue, bone loss, and immune dysfunction.", "recommendation": "High-dose repletion: Metagenics D3 10,000 + K daily for 8-12 weeks. Recheck levels after repletion.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 2000, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "Elevated B12.", "recommendation": "Usually from supplementation. Rarely indicates liver disease.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 72, "status": "normal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 73, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 85, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.6737904206555877, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 0.7% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.40478693087869594, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 0.4%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.3663950194132457, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.4%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.421794294454766, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 5.4%, ASCVD 3.0%, Heart Failure 3.6%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 19, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 15, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 101, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.74, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 62, "status": "normal", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Apo B at optimal level.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 22, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 342, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 70, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 7.3, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 0.6, "status": "normal", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Low cardiovascular inflammation risk.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.28, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Luteal Progesterone Deficiency", "interpretation": "Progesterone of 0.28 ng/mL with estradiol of 78 pg/mL in a woman 46 years old indicates luteal progesterone deficiency. Estrogen production is adequate but progesterone is not keeping pace — a hallmark of perimenopause.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Micronized progesterone 100–200 mg nightly. Dosing can be cyclic (days 14–28) or continuous depending on cycle regularity. Progesterone provides GABAergic support for sleep and anxiety relief. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your body is making enough estrogen, but progesterone has dropped. This is very common in perimenopause and causes sleep disruption, anxiety, and mood changes. Progesterone supplementation supports your nervous system and helps restore calm, restful sleep.", "referenceRange": "P4 ≥5 ng/mL expected with E2 ≥60 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 101, "status": "borderline", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Reduced Bioavailable Estrogen", "interpretation": "SHBG of 101 nmol/L with estradiol of 78 pg/mL indicates reduced bioavailable estrogen. While total estradiol appears \\"normal,\\" high SHBG binds it tightly, leaving less free hormone available to tissues. This is a hidden deficiency.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Do not dismiss symptoms as \\"normal labs.\\" Consider testosterone optimization — testosterone can lower SHBG. Address metabolic contributors (thyroid, insulin sensitivity). If symptomatic, may need higher estradiol targets to overcome SHBG binding. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen level looks normal on paper, but a protein called SHBG is binding it up so your body can't use it effectively. This can cause symptoms even with \\"normal\\" labs. We may need to address the SHBG or optimize other hormones like testosterone to help you feel better.", "referenceRange": "SHBG >80 with E2 60–100 reduces free estrogen"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 101, "status": "abnormal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 101 nmol/L is elevated. High SHBG reduces bioavailable testosterone and estrogen. Common causes: oral estrogen, oral contraceptives, thyroid medication, liver conditions.", "recommendation": "Evaluate SHBG drivers (oral estrogen, OCP, thyroid medication). Consider ordering Bioavailable Testosterone for complete androgen assessment.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 1 pg/mL is within normal functional range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}], "aiRecommendations": "1) Overall clinical assessment (concise)\\n- Key abnormalities: severe vitamin D deficiency (25-OH D 18 ng/mL), luteal progesterone deficiency (0.28 ng/mL in context of estradiol 78 pg/mL → perimenopausal pattern), elevated SHBG (101 nmol/L) producing reduced bioavailable estrogen (despite total estradiol “normal”), borderline low total testosterone (13.5 ng/dL), ferritin in low-normal/insufficient range (43.1 ng/mL), and markedly elevated B12 (2000 pg/mL) most consistent with supplementation.\\n- Clinical implications: biochemical picture consistent with perimenopause (adequate estrogen production but low progesterone and reduced tissue availability of estrogen due to high SHBG). Symptoms (fatigue, low libido, sleep/anxiety) may be driven by low bioavailable sex steroids and vitamin D/iron insufficiency.\\n- Action priority: correct vitamin D deficiency immediately; review hormone therapy options (micronized progesterone ± testosterone optimization) with provider sign-off; address drivers of elevated SHBG.\\n\\n2) Priority actions — CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE\\nCRITICAL (notify provider immediately)\\n- Vitamin D 25-OH 18 ng/mL — start high‑dose repletion per clinic protocol today. Provider must authorize high‑dose D3 (see below). Notify provider to document repletion plan.\\n- Any decision to initiate systemic sex steroid therapy (micronized progesterone and/or testosterone) — requires physician approval and informed consent. Confirm patient is not pregnant (pregnancy test if childbearing potential) before initiating testosterone.\\n\\nURGENT (action within 1–2 weeks)\\n- Start micronized progesterone per protocol for luteal deficiency:\\n  - Recommend micronized progesterone 100–200 mg PO qHS.\\n  - Dosing approach: if cycles regular → cyclic (start day ~14–28 of cycle); if cycles irregular/perimenopausal → continuous nightly dosing is acceptable. Provider to choose regimen.\\n  - Document baseline counseling about sedation, next-step monitoring.\\n- Address ferritin 43.1 ng/mL if symptomatic:\\n  - If patient reports fatigue, hair loss, RLS, exercise intolerance → start iron supplementation per protocol (Metagenics Hemagenics if used by clinic) OR oral elemental iron (e.g., ferrous sulfate 325 mg PO qOD–daily) depending on tolerance and protocol. Provide instructions for concurrent vitamin C and avoiding calcium/chains around dosing.\\n  - Plan ferritin recheck in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Investigate elevated SHBG (101 nmol/L):\\n  - Review medication list for oral estrogens/OCPs, thyroid medication, other inducers; consider switching oral estrogen to transdermal if on oral estrogen to reduce SHBG (discuss with provider).\\n  - Order Bioavailable Testosterone (or Calculated Free Androgen Index/bioavailable T) to clarify androgen status (see testing section).\\n\\nROUTINE (within 4–12 weeks)\\n- Monitor response to progesterone and any hormone changes; re-evaluate symptoms at 6–8 weeks.\\n- Recheck labs per schedule below.\\n\\n3) Testosterone dose recommendations (staff-facing; provider must authorize)\\n- Indication: borderline low total testosterone with elevated SHBG and symptomatic (low libido, decreased vigor) — consider low-dose testosterone optimization after provider review.\\n- Starting regimen (examples; provider selects and documents):\\n  - Preferred route: transdermal testosterone (cream or gel). Start low and titrate.\\n  - Suggested starting dose range: 300–600 mcg/day (0.3–0.6 mg/day) transdermal. If clinic uses compounded creams, document mcg/day; if commercial gel formulations used, convert appropriately per product dosing.\\n  - Alternative (if clinic protocol allows): transdermal patch delivering low-dose testosterone per manufacturer dosing.\\n- Monitoring and safety:\\n  - Recheck total testosterone (trough, ~24 hours after last dose if daily topical) 6–8 weeks after initiation/titration; target: symptomatic improvement with total testosterone in mid-female reference range (adjust per clinic protocol) and absence of virilizing signs.\\n  - Monitor for androgenic adverse effects (acne, hirsutism, voice change), lipid profile, LFTs, and hemoglobin/hematocrit per protocol at baseline and 3–6 months.\\n  - Stop or reduce dose if virilizing effects develop.\\n- Documentation: require informed consent, pregnancy test if applicable, and physician sign-off before initiation.\\n\\n4) Lifestyle interventions to recommend (staff to counsel patient)\\n- Vitamin D: safe sun exposure as appropriate, dietary sources (fatty fish, fortified dairy) while on repletion and maintenance dosing.\\n- Iron: encourage iron‑rich diet (red meat, legumes, leafy greens) and combine with vitamin C to enhance absorption; advise spacing iron away from calcium, tea/coffee.\\n- Weight/metabolic: reinforce maintenance of healthy weight and carbohydrate moderation to help lower SHBG drivers (insulin resistance can lower SHBG but other metabolic factors matter).\\n- Sleep/hygiene: advise sleep hygiene measures to support progesterone’s sedative benefits (consistent bedtimes, reduce night caffeine).\\n- Alcohol: limit alcohol intake (can raise SHBG and affect liver metabolism).\\n- Exercise: regular resistance + aerobic exercise to support bone health, metabolic profile and libido.\\n- Review medications/supplements: confirm oral estrogen or high-dose supplemental estrogens/other meds that raise SHBG; assess B12 supplement use (stop/adjust if appropriate).\\n\\n5) Additional testing or referrals to consider\\n- Labs to order:\\n  - Bioavailable testosterone (or free/bioavailable T) — to clarify androgen availability given high SHBG.\\n  - Repeat 25-OH Vitamin D after repletion at 8–12 weeks.\\n  - Ferritin + CBC in 8–12 weeks after starting iron; consider iron saturation if clinical concern.\\n  - Total testosterone trough 6–8 weeks after starting testosterone.\\n  - SHBG recheck at 3 months after interventions (medication changes, testosterone).\\n  - Pregnancy test if childbearing potential before initiating testosterone or progesterone.\\n  - If B12 2000 pg/mL persists and no supplementation reported → consider LFTs (already normal), methylmalonic acid if concern, or hepatology referral if otherwise unexplained.\\n- Referrals:\\n  - Endocrinology for complex hormone management if uncertain, history of thromboembolism, or if considering higher-dose testosterone or complex interactions.\\n  - Hematology if ferritin falls or iron therapy fails, or for recurrent iron deficiency despite supplementation.\\n  - Nutrition/dietitian for targeted iron and vitamin D optimization and weight/metabolic counseling.\\n  - Gynecology if abnormal uterine bleeding develops after progesterone initiation or if fertility concerns exist.\\n\\n6) Summary of next steps and timeline (concise, actionable)\\n- Immediate (day 0):\\n  - Start high-dose vitamin D repletion per clinic protocol (Metagenics D3 10,000 IU + K daily) — provider authorization required. Educate patient on repletion course and potential symptoms.\\n  - Review medication list for oral estrogen/OCP and other SHBG drivers; flag for provider.\\n  - Counsel patient re progesterone option and obtain provider decision.\\n- Within 1–2 weeks:\\n  - If symptomatic for low progesterone, initiate micronized progesterone 100–200 mg qHS (cyclic if regular cycles, continuous if irregular) after provider sign-off. Document counseling.\\n  - If symptomatic for iron deficiency, start iron supplementation per protocol (Metagenics Hemagenics or oral iron) and provide dietary counseling.\\n  - Order Bioavailable Testosterone and pregnancy test (if applicable).\\n- 6–8 weeks:\\n  - Reassess symptoms after progesterone start.\\n  - If testosterone started, check trough total testosterone and adjust dose as directed by provider.\\n- 8–12 weeks:\\n  - Recheck 25-OH Vitamin D and ferritin (and CBC). Adjust maintenance vitamin D dosing to achieve target >30–50 ng/mL per clinic goals; continue iron until ferritin >50 ng/mL or per protocol.\\n- 3 months:\\n  - Reassess SHBG and clinical response; consider escalating sex steroid therapy or addressing underlying drivers if symptoms persist.\\n- Physician notification triggers:\\n  - Start of any hormone therapy (progesterone, testosterone), severe vitamin D deficiency treatment plan, unexpected LFT abnormalities, persistent/explained elevated B12 without supplementation, or failure to respond to iron repletion.\\n\\nPatient education points for staff to cover (brief to patient; staff to document)\\n- Explain perimenopausal pattern: adequate estrogen production but low progesterone and high SHBG reduce hormone availability — can cause sleep disruption, anxiety, low libido.\\n- Vitamin D deficiency implications and the need for high‑dose repletion followed by maintenance.\\n- Progesterone benefits (sleep, anxiety regulation) and common side effects (sedation).\\n- Iron repletion timeline and dietary advice.\\n- Importance of follow-up labs and reporting any new symptoms (hirsutism, voice changes, abnormal bleeding).\\n\\nEnd summary: Provider review and sign-off required for initiation of progesterone and any testosterone therapy and for high‑dose vitamin D repletion. Order bioavailable testosterone now; start vitamin D repletion and consider immediate progesterone if patient symptomatic and provider approves. Recheck labs per schedule above.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 1.16, "detail": "Triglycerides 85 / HDL 73 = 1.16", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 101, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 15, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "62 mg/dL", "detail": "Normal atherogenic particle count", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "0.6 mg/L", "detail": "Low inflammatory burden", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Iron Deficiency", "confidence": "low", "description": "Iron stores are depleted or suboptimal. Iron is essential for energy, cognitive function, hair health, and oxygen transport.", "supportingFindings": ["Ferritin 43.1 ng/mL (suboptimal ≤50)"]}]}	2026-03-27 19:19:52.403285	3
67	Morgan P	female	2025-06-27 00:00:00	{"a1c": 4.7, "alt": 14, "ast": 15, "bun": 9, "co2": 24, "fsh": 2.39, "hdl": 68, "ldl": 87, "mcv": 96.7, "rbc": 4.48, "tsh": 0.48, "wbc": 7.3, "acne": false, "egfr": 108, "onHRT": false, "sodium": 139, "albumin": 4.7, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 9.6, "glucose": 106, "bloating": false, "brainFog": true, "chloride": 103, "hairLoss": false, "vitaminD": 23.9, "bilirubin": 0.8, "estradiol": 71, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": true, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 226, "potassium": 4.1, "creatinine": 0.74, "hematocrit": 43.3, "hemoglobin": 14.5, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "vitaminB12": 642, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2025-06-27", "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Morgan P", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 35, "bmi": 21, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 110, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 3.57, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 16.5, "totalProtein": 7.1, "lowMotivation": true, "triglycerides": 40, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": true, "totalCholesterol": 163}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History/Context: 35-year-old female patient presents for review of recent laboratory results related to hormone evaluation and routine screening. Patient reports symptoms consistent with perimenopausal transition including sleep disruption and intermittent mood changes (as per lab interpretation summary). No acute red-flag symptoms reported (no chest pain, syncope, severe palpitations, severe dyspnea, heavy uterine bleeding).\\n- Menstrual/gynecologic history: Noted laboratory pattern consistent with luteal-phase progesterone deficiency; menstrual cycle regularity to be confirmed prior to initiating cyclic vs continuous progesterone regimen.\\n- Allergies/medications: Not provided in chart; verify before medication initiation.\\n- Social: Not provided. Confirm tobacco/alcohol use as part of routine counseling.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: Not provided in chart today (BP, HR, BMI not available).\\n- Labs (03/27/2026) — grouped by category (units and flags):\\n  - CBC\\n    - Hemoglobin: 14.5 g/dL (normal)\\n    - Hematocrit: 43.3 % (normal)\\n    - MCV: 96.7 fL — BORDERLINE high (high‑normal; early macrocytosis)\\n    - Platelets: 226 K/uL (normal)\\n  - Metabolic / CMP\\n    - AST: 15 U/L (normal)\\n    - ALT: 14 U/L (normal)\\n    - Creatinine: 0.74 mg/dL (normal)\\n    - eGFR: 108 mL/min/1.73 m2 (normal)\\n  - Lipids / Metabolic risk\\n    - Total Cholesterol: (not listed)\\n    - LDL-C: 87 mg/dL (normal)\\n    - HDL-C: 68 mg/dL (normal)\\n    - Triglycerides: 40 mg/dL (normal)\\n    - Hemoglobin A1c: 4.7 % (normal)\\n  - Hormones / Endocrine\\n    - Estradiol (E2): 71 pg/mL (normal)\\n    - Progesterone (P4): 3.57 ng/mL — ABNORMAL (luteal-phase progesterone deficiency for age 35)\\n    - FSH: 2.39 mIU/mL (normal)\\n    - Total Testosterone: 16.5 ng/dL — BORDERLINE/within reference (adequate androgens pattern)\\n    - TSH: 0.48 mIU/L (low‑normal)\\n  - Vitamins/Micronutrients\\n    - Vitamin B12: 642 pg/mL (normal)\\n    - Vitamin D (25‑OH): 23.9 ng/mL — ABNORMAL (vitamin D deficiency/suboptimal for bone and hormone health)\\n  - Other risk scores / screening\\n    - AHA PREVENT 10‑yr Total CVD risk: 0.2% (low)\\n    - 10‑yr ASCVD risk: 0.2% (low)\\n    - 10‑yr Heart Failure risk: 0.1% (low)\\n    - 30‑yr CVD risk: 1.9% (low)\\n    - STOP‑BANG (Sleep Apnea risk): 0 points (low)\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Luteal-phase progesterone deficiency consistent with early perimenopausal physiology.\\n   - Data: Progesterone 3.57 ng/mL with estradiol 71 pg/mL in a 35‑year‑old female. Interpretation: adequate estrogen but insufficient luteal progesterone production — likely contributor to sleep disturbances, mood changes, and perimenopausal symptoms.\\n   - Differential: True luteal insufficiency vs. cycle-timing effect (ensure specimen timing in luteal window), ovulatory dysfunction related to perimenopause, pregnancy (must be excluded prior to progesterone therapy).\\n\\n2. Vitamin D deficiency.\\n   - Data: 25‑OH Vitamin D 23.9 ng/mL (clinic target ≥60 ng/mL).\\n   - Clinical significance: contributes to low energy, mood changes, and suboptimal bone/hormone health; requires repletion.\\n\\n3. Borderline macrocytosis (MCV 96.7 fL).\\n   - Data: MCV high‑normal; Hemoglobin normal; B12 currently normal at 642 pg/mL.\\n   - Clinical significance: early macrocytosis can precede B12/folate deficiency or reflect alcohol use, medication effects, liver disease (LFTs normal), or hypothyroidism (TSH low‑normal). Requires surveillance and folate assessment.\\n\\n4. Adequate androgen status — testosterone not indicated.\\n   - Data: Total testosterone 16.5 ng/dL within reference; SHBG and free/bioavailable values not flagged as abnormal.\\n   - Clinical significance: Androgen deficiency insufficient to support empiric testosterone therapy. Evaluate non‑androgen causes for symptoms (vitamin D deficiency, thyroid function, sleep, mood, iron status).\\n\\n5. Low‑normal TSH (0.48 mIU/L).\\n   - Data: TSH low‑normal; no Free T4/Free T3 reported.\\n   - Clinical significance: If symptomatic (palpitations, weight change, heat intolerance, anxiety), obtain further thyroid testing (Free T4, Free T3, anti‑TPO). Consider endocrine referral if abnormal.\\n\\n6. Cardiovascular and metabolic risk — LOW.\\n   - Data: AHA PREVENT 10‑yr risk <1%; lipid panel acceptable. No statin indicated; lifestyle counseling recommended.\\n\\n7. No immediate red-flag abnormalities.\\n   - Data: No critical/urgent lab values today. Patient instructed to report chest pain, syncope, severe palpitations, severe dyspnea, heavy uterine bleeding, or pregnancy.\\n\\nPLAN:\\nFor each assessment item, specific actions and orders:\\n\\n1. Luteal-phase progesterone deficiency / perimenopausal symptoms\\n   - Before initiating progesterone: obtain urine or serum pregnancy test if patient of reproductive potential.\\n   - Start micronized progesterone per provider order (provider to sign):\\n     - Suggested starting regimen: Micronized progesterone 100–200 mg PO at bedtime.\\n       - If patient reports regular ovulatory cycles: consider cyclic dosing days 14–28 of cycle (adjust per patient cycle length).\\n       - If cycles irregular or perimenopausal amenorrhea: consider nightly continuous dosing (provider decision).\\n     - Counseling: take at bedtime for sleep/anxiolytic benefit; expect variable bleeding pattern initially. Instruct patient to report heavy bleeding, severe adverse effects, or signs of pregnancy.\\n   - Follow-up: clinic/provider visit or telephone check at 4–8 weeks to assess symptom response and adverse effects; reassess after 3 cycles if cyclic regimen used.\\n\\n2. Vitamin D deficiency — repletion\\n   - Start Cholecalciferol (Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K) PO once daily with meal for initial 8–12 weeks.\\n   - Safety: check serum calcium only if history of hypercalcemia, granulomatous disease, nephrolithiasis, or other risk factors. Patient to report symptoms of hypercalcemia.\\n   - Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D level at 8–12 weeks. Clinic target ≥60 ng/mL per protocol.\\n   - Continue maintenance dosing thereafter as indicated by recheck.\\n\\n3. Borderline high‑normal MCV (monitor macrocytosis)\\n   - Orders:\\n     - Repeat CBC with MCV in 8–12 weeks.\\n     - Obtain serum folate and repeat B12 if not available recently (current B12 642 pg/mL — acceptable). If concern persists or B12 borderline, order methylmalonic acid (MMA).\\n   - If MCV rises to ≥100 fL or hemoglobin drops >2 g/dL, notify provider urgently and consider peripheral smear and hematology referral.\\n   - Review alcohol intake and medication list as part of history.\\n\\n4. Iron/menses assessment\\n   - Orders within 1–2 weeks: ferritin, iron, TIBC (to evaluate iron stores given perimenopausal bleeding risk).\\n   - Target ferritin >50 ng/mL for symptomatic improvement. If ferritin <50 or iron deficiency identified, initiate iron repletion per clinic protocol and recheck ferritin in 8–12 weeks.\\n\\n5. Thyroid evaluation\\n   - If patient symptomatic (fatigue, weight change, palpitations, heat/cold intolerance): order Free T4, Free T3, and anti‑TPO antibodies.\\n   - Consider endocrine referral if abnormal or if symptoms persist despite normal labs.\\n\\n6. Androgen status / testosterone therapy\\n   - Do NOT initiate testosterone therapy at this time: total testosterone 16.5 ng/dL within range; symptoms likely driven by non-androgenic factors.\\n   - Reassess after correction of vitamin D, iron, thyroid, sleep, and mood issues. Consider DHEA‑S if further androgen evaluation is required.\\n\\n7. Lifestyle and adjunctive supplements / counseling\\n   - Prescribe/Recommend:\\n     - Magtein® Magnesium L‑Threonate, 3 capsules daily (divided doses) — medium‑priority supplement.\\n     - Reinforce diet rich in iron, folate, B12, and vitamin D (fatty fish, fortified foods).\\n     - Encourage weight‑bearing and resistance exercise ≥150 minutes/week + resistance 2×/week.\\n     - Sleep hygiene and stress reduction; consider CBT‑I for persistent insomnia.\\n     - Limit alcohol intake; smoking cessation counseling if applicable.\\n   - Sleep apnea: STOP‑BANG 0 — low risk; consider sleep study only if daytime somnolence or persistent symptoms.\\n\\n8. Referrals and additional testing as indicated\\n   - Gynecology: if complex bleeding, failure of first‑line progesterone, or desire for alternative HRT options.\\n   - Endocrinology: if thyroid testing abnormal or complex endocrine management needed.\\n   - Hematology: if MCV ≥100 fL, hemoglobin drop >2 g/dL, or unexplained macrocytosis.\\n   - Nutrition/iron clinic: if ferritin <50 ng/mL or symptomatic iron deficiency.\\n   - Mental health/sleep medicine: if mood or sleep symptoms persist despite initial measures.\\n\\n9. Monitoring, follow-up, and safety\\n   - Immediate / within 1 week:\\n     - Confirm pregnancy status prior to starting progesterone.\\n     - Begin Vitamin D cholecalciferol 5,000 IU PO daily + K and document counseling.\\n   - Within 1–2 weeks:\\n     - Provider to initiate micronized progesterone 100–200 mg PO qHS per cycle plan.\\n     - Order ferritin/iron panel, folate (and MMA if indicated), Free T4/Free T3/anti‑TPO if symptomatic.\\n   - 4–8 weeks:\\n     - Clinical follow‑up to assess progesterone response (sleep, mood, bleeding), vitamin D adherence, and adverse effects.\\n   - 8–12 weeks:\\n     - Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D level (target ≥60 ng/mL).\\n     - Repeat CBC with MCV and recheck ferritin if iron repletion initiated.\\n   - Routine recheck window: 3–6 months for general follow-up.\\n   - Physician notification triggers: positive pregnancy test prior to hormones, hemoglobin drop >2 g/dL, MCV ≥100 fL on repeat, new heavy uterine bleeding, chest pain, severe palpitations, severe shortness of breath, or suspected hypercalcemia.\\n\\nOrders placed / recommended (to be signed by provider)\\n- Medications/Supplements:\\n  - Metagenics Vitamin D3 5,000 IU + K PO daily x 8–12 weeks, then recheck 25‑OH D.\\n  - Micronized progesterone 100–200 mg PO qHS — regimen (cyclic days 14–28 if ovulatory vs continuous nightly if irregular) — provider to choose dosing schedule.\\n  - Magtein® Magnesium L‑Threonate, 3 capsules daily (divided doses) — optional/medium priority.\\n- Labs:\\n  - Pregnancy test (urine or serum) prior to progesterone initiation.\\n  - Ferritin, iron, TIBC — within 1–2 weeks.\\n  - CBC with MCV — repeat in 8–12 weeks.\\n  - Serum folate and repeat B12 or MMA if indicated — within 1–2 weeks.\\n  - 25‑OH Vitamin D — recheck at 8–12 weeks.\\n  - Free T4, Free T3, anti‑TPO — if symptomatic or per provider request.\\n- Referrals: Gynecology/Endocrinology/Hematology/Nutrition/Sleep/Mental health as clinically indicated.\\n\\nCommunication / Education:\\n- Results reviewed and discussed with patient today: explained luteal progesterone deficiency, need for progesterone therapy options, vitamin D deficiency and repletion plan, and the significance of borderline MCV.\\n- Advised to report pregnancy, heavy bleeding, chest pain, severe palpitations, severe lightheadedness, or other urgent symptoms immediately.\\n- Provided brief scripted education: “Your testosterone is within the normal range — we will not start testosterone. We will treat low progesterone and low vitamin D, and check iron and thyroid to address your symptoms.”\\n\\nFinal administrative:\\n- Recheck window: routine follow-up in 3–6 months; earlier per timeline above.\\n- Results reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\nDate: 03/27/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "preventRisk": {"ldlGoal": "No specific LDL target; focus on risk-based statin intensity", "riskCategory": "low", "tenYearASCVD": 0.0015846060245959946, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0.00237775199303271, "thirtyYearASCVD": 0.011775377408219765, "ageValidFor30Year": true, "thirtyYearTotalCVD": 0.019210314441283245, "tenYearHFPercentage": "0.1%", "tenYearHeartFailure": 0.0012073677560772943, "statinRecommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "tenYearCVDPercentage": "0.2%", "tenYearASCVDPercentage": "0.2%", "thirtyYearHFPercentage": "1.1%", "thirtyYearHeartFailure": 0.011137838116389728, "thirtyYearCVDPercentage": "1.9%", "thirtyYearASCVDPercentage": "1.2%"}, "supplements": [{"dose": "3 capsules daily (divided doses)", "name": "Magtein® Magnesium L-Threonate", "caution": "L-Threonate form crosses blood-brain barrier for cognitive and sleep support. Well-tolerated; gentle on GI system.", "category": "mineral", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Magtein is the only magnesium form clinically shown to cross the blood-brain barrier. Supports quality sleep, cognitive function, GABA activity, and stress resilience.", "indication": "Sleep disruption reported; Low energy reported; Brain fog reported", "phenotypes": ["Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "This special form of magnesium is designed to reach the brain, where it supports better sleep quality, mental clarity, and a calmer nervous system.", "supportingFindings": ["Sleep disruption reported", "Low energy reported", "Brain fog reported"]}, {"dose": "1 softgel daily with meal", "name": "D3 5,000 + K", "caution": "Repletion dose for deficiency/insufficiency. Contains vitamin K2 for optimal calcium metabolism. Recheck levels in 8-12 weeks.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "D3 5,000 + K provides vitamin D3 with K2 for repletion of deficiency/insufficiency. Target ≥60 ng/mL for bone, immune, and cardiovascular support.", "indication": "Vitamin D 23.9 ng/mL (deficient)", "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D is below optimal range. This supplement will help restore your levels to support bone health, immune function, and overall wellness.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 23.9 ng/mL (deficient)"]}, {"dose": "1 soft chew during times of stress", "name": "HerWellness™ Rapid Stress Relief", "caution": "Fast-acting L-Theanine and Lactium formula. Non-drowsy. Promotes calm within 1 hour. Contains milk.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "low", "rationale": "HerWellness Rapid Stress Relief provides fast-acting stress support with L-Theanine (200mg) and Lactium. Promotes calm within 1 hour without drowsiness.", "indication": "Sleep disruption (stress-related)", "phenotypes": ["Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation"], "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This fast-acting supplement helps your body respond to stress more calmly. It promotes relaxation within about an hour and can be taken as needed without causing drowsiness.", "supportingFindings": ["Sleep disruption (stress-related)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\n- Your cardiovascular and metabolic screening look excellent: 10-year ASCVD risk 0.2% (very low). Your hemoglobin is 14.5 g/dL and hematocrit 43.3% (normal), and your kidney and liver tests are normal (eGFR 108 mL/min, creatinine 0.74 mg/dL, AST 15 U/L, ALT 14 U/L). Two items to address now are vitamin D and luteal progesterone.\\n\\nPositive findings (what’s good)\\n- Hemoglobin 14.5 g/dL, hematocrit 43.3%, platelets 226 K/uL — normal.\\n- Vitamin B12 642 pg/mL — normal.\\n- A1c 4.7% — excellent blood sugar control.\\n- Lipids: LDL 87 mg/dL, HDL 68 mg/dL, triglycerides 40 mg/dL — very favorable.\\n- TSH 0.48 mIU/L — in the usual range.\\n- 10-year Total CVD Risk 0.2% (low risk) — in plain English: very low chance of heart attack or stroke in the next 10 years based on current numbers.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + actions)\\n1) Vitamin D: 25-OH = 23.9 ng/mL (insufficient/deficient for bone and hormone health).\\n   - Start vitamin D3 2,000 IU daily now and take with a meal containing fat. Recheck 25-OH in 3 months. If still <30 ng/mL, discuss a prescription repletion dose (e.g., 50,000 IU weekly) with your clinician.\\n   - Add daily sources: 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly, 1 cup fortified milk or yogurt daily, and 10–20 minutes sunlight on arms/face 3–4x/week as tolerated.\\n   - For bone support: aim for calcium 1,000–1,200 mg/day (diet first: dairy, fortified plant milks, leafy greens) and weight-bearing exercise 2–3x/week (brisk walking, stair climbing, squats).\\n\\n2) Luteal progesterone deficiency suggesting perimenopausal transition: Progesterone 3.57 ng/mL with estradiol 71 pg/mL (age 35).\\n   - This pattern (adequate estrogen, low luteal progesterone) can cause irregular cycles, sleep or mood changes, and low energy. Track your cycle and document ovulation timing (basal body temperature or ovulation tests) and repeat a mid-luteal progesterone test about 7 days before expected period next cycle.\\n   - Lifestyle steps that help hormones: sleep 7–8 hours nightly, manage stress (10–20 min/day meditation or brisk walk), strength training 2x/week, keep alcohol low, maintain a healthy BMI.\\n   - If symptoms persist (heavy irregular bleeding, severe mood or sleep disruption), discuss progesterone therapy options with your clinician.\\n\\n3) CBC index: MCV 96.7 fL (high-normal).\\n   - Although B12 642 pg/mL is normal, repeat CBC in 3 months and consider checking folate or medications (metformin, PPIs) if MCV rises. Eat folate-rich foods: leafy greens, lentils, beans, fortified grains.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Start vitamin D3 2,000 IU daily today; recheck 25-OH in 3 months.\\n- Track cycles and repeat mid-luteal progesterone next cycle; bring cycle log to your follow-up.\\n- Repeat CBC (including MCV) in 3 months.\\n- If symptoms (fatigue, mood, irregular bleeding) continue, schedule a visit to discuss hormone treatment options and possible specialist referral.\\n\\nYou’re doing many things well—these targeted steps can improve bone and hormone health and help your symptoms. If you want, I can draft a checklist to bring to your appointment.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 14.5, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 43.3, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 96.7, "status": "borderline", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 96.7 fL is in the high-normal range. Early macrocytosis can precede overt B12 or folate deficiency.", "recommendation": "Ensure B12 and folate levels are within optimal range. Monitor CBC at next visit.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 226, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 71, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 3.57, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 2.39, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 16.5, "status": "normal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone within normal female range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 0.48, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 23.9, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D deficiency (21-30 ng/mL). Suboptimal for hormone and bone health.", "recommendation": "Repletion: Metagenics D3 5,000 + K daily. Target ≥60 ng/mL. Recheck in 8-12 weeks.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 642, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 4.7, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 87, "status": "normal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 68, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 40, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.23777519930327098, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 0.2% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.15846060245959948, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 0.2%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.12073677560772943, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.1%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 1.9210314441283245, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 1.9%, ASCVD 1.2%, Heart Failure 1.1%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 15, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 14, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 108, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.74, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 3.57, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Luteal Progesterone Deficiency", "interpretation": "Progesterone of 3.57 ng/mL with estradiol of 71 pg/mL in a woman 35 years old indicates luteal progesterone deficiency. Estrogen production is adequate but progesterone is not keeping pace — a hallmark of perimenopause.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Micronized progesterone 100–200 mg nightly. Dosing can be cyclic (days 14–28) or continuous depending on cycle regularity. Progesterone provides GABAergic support for sleep and anxiety relief. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your body is making enough estrogen, but progesterone has dropped. This is very common in perimenopause and causes sleep disruption, anxiety, and mood changes. Progesterone supplementation supports your nervous system and helps restore calm, restful sleep.", "referenceRange": "P4 ≥5 ng/mL expected with E2 ≥60 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 16.5, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone Pattern D: Adequate Androgens, Persistent Symptoms", "interpretation": "Adequate Androgens pattern identified (Total T: 16.5 ng/dL (within range)). Total, free, and bioavailable testosterone are within normal ranges and SHBG is not extreme. If the patient reports fatigue, low mood, sleep disruption, or low libido, these symptoms are likely driven by non-androgenic factors. Findings to evaluate: Thyroid: TSH 0.48 mIU/L — evaluate thyroid function. Low Vitamin D (23.9 ng/mL) — contributes to fatigue and mood changes. Estrogen/Progesterone balance should be assessed (E2: 71 pg/mL, P4: 3.57 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Do not reflexively add testosterone when levels are adequate — look deeper. Evaluate iron status (ferritin target >50 for symptom resolution), thyroid function (optimize TSH), and sleep quality (consider sleep apnea screening with STOP-BANG). Assess estrogen and progesterone balance — E2/P4 imbalance is a common driver of persistent symptoms. Address CNS and psychosocial drivers: chronic stress, HPA axis dysregulation, mood disorders. Consider DHEA-S if not already checked. Support with lifestyle optimization: sleep hygiene, stress management, nutrition. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your testosterone levels are actually in a healthy range, which is good news. When symptoms persist despite normal testosterone, it usually means something else is contributing. Your provider will look at other factors like iron levels, thyroid function, sleep quality, and hormone balance to find what's driving your symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Total T 15-70 ng/dL, Free T 0.5-5.0 pg/mL (all within range)"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- Summary: Patient demonstrates luteal-phase progesterone deficiency (P4 3.57 ng/mL) with adequate estradiol (E2 71 pg/mL) consistent with perimenopausal physiology. Vitamin D deficiency (25‑OH D 23.9 ng/mL) is present and requires repletion. MCV is high‑normal (96.7 fL) — early macrocytosis possible; B12 reported as normal but borderline MCV warrants surveillance for evolving B12/folate deficiency. Total testosterone (16.5 ng/dL) is within the laboratory reference range and does not support empiric testosterone therapy at this time. TSH is low‑normal (0.48 mIU/L) — consider expanded thyroid evaluation if symptomatic.\\n\\nPriority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\n\\nCRITICAL (immediate physician notification)\\n- Notify provider immediately if any of the following are present or occur:\\n  - New/active heavy uterine bleeding, syncope, chest pain, severe palpitations, severe shortness of breath, or signs of hypercalcemia.\\n  - Hemoglobin drop >2 g/dL or MCV rises ≥100 fL on repeat CBC.\\n  - Positive pregnancy test prior to starting progesterone or other hormonal therapy.\\n  - Serum calcium above reference range (if being checked prior to high‑dose vitamin D).\\n\\nURGENT (initiate within 1–2 weeks; actionable therapeutic changes)\\n- Vitamin D repletion\\n  - Start cholecalciferol (Metagenics D3) 5,000 IU PO daily with vitamin K as per clinic formulary.\\n  - Duration: initial 8–12 weeks then recheck 25‑OH D.\\n  - Safety: check serum calcium if patient has history of hypercalcemia, granulomatous disease, or nephrolithiasis; notify provider if hypercalcemia risk.\\n- Progesterone replacement for luteal‑phase deficiency\\n  - Provider recommendation: initiate micronized progesterone 100–200 mg PO at bedtime.\\n    - If regular ovulatory cycles: consider cyclic dosing days 14–28 of cycle (adjust cycle days per patient’s cycle length).\\n    - If irregular cycles or perimenopausal amenorrhea: consider continuous nightly dosing (provider to decide).\\n  - Counsel patient that progesterone is commonly given at bedtime for sleep/anxiolytic benefits; advise about expected bleeding patterns and to report heavy bleeding or severe adverse effects.\\n  - Follow-up: assessment of symptom response and side effects in 4–8 weeks; if cyclic trial, reassess after 3 cycles.\\n\\nROUTINE (order within 4 weeks; monitoring and optimization)\\n- CBC and macrocytic workup\\n  - Repeat CBC with MCV in 8–12 weeks.\\n  - Order serum folate and repeat B12 (if last result not clearly optimal) — check methylmalonic acid (MMA) if B12 borderline or symptoms of deficiency.\\n  - If MCV increases or anemia develops, consider peripheral smear and hematology referral.\\n- Iron status and menstrual blood loss\\n  - Order ferritin, iron, TIBC. Target ferritin >50 ng/mL for symptom resolution (protocol standard).\\n  - If ferritin <50, initiate iron repletion per clinic protocols and recheck ferritin in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Thyroid evaluation\\n  - Obtain Free T4 and Free T3 and anti‑TPO antibodies if patient symptomatic (fatigue, weight change, palpitations) or if provider requests — consider endocrine referral if abnormal.\\n- Additional labs to consider\\n  - DHEA‑S if not previously measured (to further assess androgen status).\\n  - If starting high‑dose vitamin D or complex endocrine therapy, consider baseline CMP (calcium, creatinine, LFTs) per clinic protocol.\\n- Sleep and mood assessment\\n  - If persistent fatigue/sleep issues despite normal STOP‑BANG, consider sleep study referral or CBT for insomnia; screen for mood disorders and consider mental health referral.\\n\\nTestosterone dosing recommendations (if applicable)\\n- Not indicated at this time:\\n  - Total testosterone 16.5 ng/dL (within reference) with no biochemical androgen deficiency — do not initiate testosterone therapy.\\n  - Criteria to consider testosterone therapy (for provider review) include: clinically significant hypoandrogenic symptoms with consistently low total and/or free testosterone below laboratory cutoffs or low bioavailable testosterone, and after excluding reversible contributors (thyroid dysfunction, iron deficiency, depression, sleep apnea, vitamin D deficiency). If considered, escalate to provider and follow clinic testosterone initiation protocol (dose and route individualized; do not start empirically).\\n\\nLifestyle interventions to recommend to the patient (staff to counsel)\\n- Sleep and stress\\n  - Reinforce sleep hygiene, limit evening stimulants, consider bedtime micronized progesterone for sleep benefit.\\n  - Recommend stress‑reduction strategies (mindfulness, CBT techniques, exercise).\\n- Nutrition and supplements\\n  - Diet rich in iron (heme sources), folate (leafy greens), B12 (animal products or supplement if vegetarian), vitamin D (fatty fish, fortified foods).\\n  - Safe sun exposure as tolerated.\\n- Exercise\\n  - Encourage regular weight‑bearing exercise for bone health and mood (≥150 minutes/week moderate activity + resistance training 2×/week).\\n- Substance use\\n  - Limit alcohol intake (contributes to poor sleep and can affect macrocytosis), smoking cessation counseling if applicable.\\n- Sleep apnea screening/management\\n  - If high risk or daytime somnolence persists, arrange sleep evaluation (home sleep test or polysomnography).\\n\\nAdditional testing or referrals to consider\\n- Gynecology (perimenopause management)\\n  - If complex bleeding, failed first‑line progesterone management, or patient desires advanced options (combined HRT, non‑oral routes), refer to gynecology/provider for individualized HRT plan.\\n- Endocrinology\\n  - If abnormal thyroid panel, refractory symptomatic endocrine issues, or complex androgen questions.\\n- Hematology\\n  - If MCV rises >100 fL, anemia develops, or unclear macrocytosis workup.\\n- Nutrition/iron clinic\\n  - For iron repletion planning if ferritin <50 or symptomatic iron deficiency.\\n- Sleep medicine/mental health\\n  - For persistent fatigue, insomnia, or mood disorders despite initial interventions.\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (clear, actionable)\\n- Immediate/within 1 week\\n  - Confirm pregnancy status prior to initiating progesterone if patient is of reproductive potential.\\n  - Start Vitamin D3 5,000 IU PO daily + K (Metagenics D3 5,000 + K) and document plan in chart.\\n  - Counsel patient on rationale for progesterone and vitamin D; advise to report severe adverse events.\\n- Within 1–2 weeks\\n  - Provider to initiate micronized progesterone 100–200 mg PO qHS as decided (cyclic days 14–28 if ovulatory; continuous if irregular).\\n  - Order baseline ferritin, iron panel, folate (if not recent), MMA if indicated, Free T4/Free T3/anti‑TPO if symptomatic.\\n- 4–8 weeks\\n  - Follow‑up visit to assess response to progesterone (sleep, mood, bleeding), adherence to vitamin D, and any adverse effects.\\n  - Reassess need for further endocrine/gynecology referrals.\\n- 8–12 weeks\\n  - Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D to confirm repletion target (clinic target ≥60 ng/mL).\\n  - Repeat CBC with MCV and recheck ferritin if iron repletion started.\\n- Ongoing\\n  - Do not initiate testosterone at this time; reassess after other contributors (iron, thyroid, vitamin D, sleep, mood) are optimized.\\n  - Notify provider earlier if any red flags listed under CRITICAL criteria occur.\\n\\nPhysician notification triggers for staff\\n- Any positive pregnancy test prior to hormone therapy.\\n- Hemoglobin drop >2 g/dL or MCV ≥100 fL on repeat.\\n- New heavy uterine bleeding, severe systemic symptoms, hypercalcemia, or significant abnormal labs ordered above.\\n- Patient requests testosterone despite normal levels — escalate for provider discussion.\\n\\nPatient education points for staff to convey (brief, scripted)\\n- “Your testosterone is within the normal range — we will not start testosterone. We will treat low progesterone and low vitamin D, and check iron and thyroid to address your symptoms.”\\n- “You will start vitamin D 5,000 IU daily and micronized progesterone at bedtime (100–200 mg) per provider plan; we will recheck vitamin D in 8–12 weeks and reassess symptoms in 4–8 weeks.”\\n- “Please report pregnancy, heavy bleeding, chest pain, severe palpitations, or severe lightheadedness immediately.”\\n\\nReferences to clinic protocol elements used\\n- Vitamin D repletion: Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily; recheck 8–12 weeks; target ≥60 ng/mL.\\n- Progesterone guidance: micronized progesterone 100–200 mg PO qHS, cyclic vs continuous per cycle regularity.\\n- Ferritin target >50 ng/mL for symptomatic improvement.\\n- Avoid empiric testosterone when biochemical androgen status is adequate.\\n\\nIf you want, I can draft an order set (medications, labs, follow‑up timing, and patient education script) ready for provider sign‑off.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 0.59, "detail": "Triglycerides 40 / HDL 68 = 0.59", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "4.7%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 14, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation", "confidence": "high", "description": "Pattern consistent with HPA-axis stress and cortisol dysregulation. Chronic stress depletes DHEA-S, disrupts sleep, and impairs cognitive and emotional function.", "supportingFindings": ["Sleep disruption reported", "Low energy / fatigue reported", "Low motivation reported", "Brain fog reported", "Vitamin D 23.9 ng/mL (low, stress resilience impact)"]}]}	2026-03-27 20:32:28.679057	1
