simple next steps.\\n\\nPositive findings (specific values)\\n- Hemoglobin 15.9 g/dL (normal).\\n- MCV 94.7 fL (red blood cells normal size).\\n- TSH 1.45 mIU/L (thyroid function normal).\\n- Total testosterone 20.6 ng/dL, Free T 2 pg/mL, SHBG 73.9 nmol/L (androgens adequate).\\n- HDL 91 mg/dL (protective), LDL 104 mg/dL (near‑optimal), Triglycerides 46 mg/dL.\\n- A1c 4.6% (normal blood sugar).\\n- Vitamin B12 762 pg/mL and folate 12.9 ng/mL (adequate).\\n- AST 19 U/L, ALT 18 U/L (liver normal).\\n- hs-CRP 0.6 mg/L (low inflammation).\\n\\nAreas to improve (exact values + concrete actions)\\n1) Hydration and repeat blood count\\n   - Hematocrit 46.8% (slightly elevated). Drink an extra 1–2 liters of water daily for the next week and recheck a complete blood count when well-hydrated in 4–6 weeks.\\n\\n2) Iron stores\\n   - Ferritin 204 ng/mL (elevated). Your serum iron 145 ug/dL and TIBC 324 ug/dL give an estimated transferrin saturation ~45%. Action: If you take iron supplements, stop them and discuss with your clinician. Repeat ferritin in 3 months. If it stays >200 ng/mL, consider HFE genetic testing and specialist review.\\n\\n3) Hormones (possible exogenous hormones or timing)\\n   - Estradiol 111 pg/mL, Progesterone 10.8 ng/mL, FSH 3.99 mIU/mL — these are higher/lower than expected if you are postmenopausal. Action: Tell your provider about any hormone therapy, birth control, or progesterone creams. If not on hormones, repeat estradiol/progesterone/FSH in 4–8 weeks and consider checking adrenal sources. If you are cycling, timing matters—testing day of cycle changes results—please note the day of cycle for repeat tests.\\n\\n4) Bone health / Vitamin D\\n   - Vitamin D 44.1 ng/mL (adequate but suboptimal range 41–59 ng/mL). Action: Continue 800–1,000 IU vitamin D daily, include 1,000–1,200 mg calcium daily from diet/supplements, and do weight-bearing exercise 30 minutes 3–4x weekly. Discuss DXA screening with your clinician if you are >65 or have other risk factors.\\n\\nHeart-health takeaway (your numbers)\\n- 10-year ASCVD risk 0.2% (LOW). Keep up heart-healthy habits: 30 minutes brisk walking most days, Mediterranean-style meals (vegetables, whole grains, olive oil, fatty fish 2–3x/week), limit processed foods, and maintain a healthy weight.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Bring a list of all medications/supplements to your next visit.\\n- Rehydrate and recheck CBC in 4–6 weeks.\\n- Stop iron supplements (if taking) and recheck ferritin in 3 months.\\n- Discuss hormone therapy history and consider repeat estradiol/progesterone/FSH in 4–8 weeks with cycle timing noted.\\n- Follow bone-health and heart-health actions now; recheck Vitamin D in 3–6 months.\\n\\nIf you’d like, I can draft a short checklist to bring to your provider visit.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 15.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 46.8, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Slightly elevated hematocrit - may indicate dehydration.", "recommendation": "Encourage hydration. Repeat if persistent.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 94.7, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 94.7 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 276, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 111, "status": "borderline", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol elevated for postmenopausal status without HRT.", "recommendation": "Evaluate for exogenous estrogen source or ovarian pathology. Consider HRT if symptomatic.", "referenceRange": "<20 pg/mL (postmenopausal without HRT)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 10.8, "status": "borderline", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Elevated progesterone for postmenopausal status.", "recommendation": "Evaluate for exogenous source or adrenal production.", "referenceRange": "<1 ng/mL (postmenopausal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 3.99, "status": "borderline", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH lower than expected for postmenopausal status.", "recommendation": "Confirm menopausal status. Rule out pituitary dysfunction.", "referenceRange": ">25 mIU/mL (postmenopausal)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 20.6, "status": "normal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone within normal female range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.45, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "µg/dL", "value": 135, "status": "normal", "category": "DHEA-S", "interpretation": "DHEA-S within normal range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "65-400 µg/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 204, "status": "borderline", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Elevated ferritin.", "recommendation": "May indicate inflammation, hemochromatosis, or liver disease. Correlate clinically.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 44.1, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D adequate but suboptimal (41-59 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 2000 Complex daily to reach optimal range ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 762, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 4.6, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 104, "status": "borderline", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Near optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet and exercise.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 91, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 46, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.2503330055470075, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 0.3% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.17036079870166335, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 0.2%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.18335621907394553, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.2%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 2.0513645112751733, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 2.1%, ASCVD 1.3%, Heart Failure 1.7%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 19, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 18, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 80, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.95, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 85, "status": "normal", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Apo B at optimal level.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 17, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 324, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 145, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 12.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 0.6, "status": "normal", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Low cardiovascular inflammation risk.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 10.8, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Elevated Postmenopausal Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone of 10.8 ng/mL is unexpectedly elevated for postmenopausal status. This may indicate exogenous progesterone use, adrenal source, or lab timing issue.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Confirm patient is not on progesterone therapy. If no exogenous source, consider adrenal evaluation. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your progesterone level is higher than expected after menopause. We should confirm you're not taking any progesterone supplements.", "referenceRange": "<1 ng/mL expected postmenopausal"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 20.6, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone Pattern D: Adequate Androgens, Persistent Symptoms", "interpretation": "Adequate Androgens pattern identified (Total T: 20.6 ng/dL (within range); Free T: 2 pg/mL; SHBG: 73.9 nmol/L). Total, free, and bioavailable testosterone are within normal ranges and SHBG is not extreme. If the patient reports fatigue, low mood, sleep disruption, or low libido, these symptoms are likely driven by non-androgenic factors. Findings to evaluate: Estrogen/Progesterone balance should be assessed (E2: 111 pg/mL, P4: 10.8 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Do not reflexively add testosterone when levels are adequate — look deeper. Evaluate iron status (ferritin target >50 for symptom resolution), thyroid function (optimize TSH), and sleep quality (consider sleep apnea screening with STOP-BANG). Assess estrogen and progesterone balance — E2/P4 imbalance is a common driver of persistent symptoms. Address CNS and psychosocial drivers: chronic stress, HPA axis dysregulation, mood disorders. Consider DHEA-S if not already checked. Support with lifestyle optimization: sleep hygiene, stress management, nutrition. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your testosterone levels are actually in a healthy range, which is good news. When symptoms persist despite normal testosterone, it usually means something else is contributing. Your provider will look at other factors like iron levels, thyroid function, sleep quality, and hormone balance to find what's driving your symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Total T 15-70 ng/dL, Free T 0.5-5.0 pg/mL (all within range)"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- Key abnormalities: Hematocrit 46.8% (borderline high), Estradiol 111 pg/mL (unexpectedly high for postmenopausal), Progesterone 10.8 ng/mL (unexpectedly high for postmenopausal), FSH 3.99 mIU/mL (low for postmenopausal), Ferritin 204 ng/mL (elevated), Vitamin D 25-OH 44.1 ng/mL (adequate but below clinic target).\\n- Pattern suggests exposure to exogenous sex steroids (estrogen ± progesterone) or less commonly an endogenous source (ovarian functioning lesion, adrenal steroidogenesis, or pituitary abnormality). Elevated SHBG (73.9 nmol/L) supports the possibility of exogenous estrogen effect.\\n- No immediate life-threatening lab derangements identified. Several results warrant targeted confirmatory testing and provider review.\\n\\nPriority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL (immediate)\\n- Medication reconciliation: Immediately confirm with patient (phone call prior to provider review) whether they are taking any estrogen-containing HRT, transdermal or oral estradiol, combined products, topical creams, compounded bioidentical estrogens/progestogens, plant-based phytoestrogens, or progesterone preparations (oral, micronized, vaginal, injectable). Document specifics (agent, dose, route, last dose).\\n- Provider notification (same day) if patient reports current estrogen or progesterone use OR if patient denies exogenous hormones but E2 >100 pg/mL with low FSH and elevated progesterone — this combination requires prompt provider evaluation.\\n\\nURGENT (within 1–2 weeks)\\n- Repeat & confirm labs: order fasting morning labs off exogenous hormones if possible:\\n  - Estradiol, Progesterone, FSH, LH, SHBG (repeat full panel same lab)\\n  - Serum pregnancy test if childbearing potential and not definitively postmenopausal (to rule out pregnancy before imaging/ further testing)\\n  - Morning cortisol and ACTH and 17-hydroxyprogesterone if exogenous progesterone denied (to assess adrenal source)\\n  - Calculate transferrin saturation from serum iron/TIBC; if not automatically provided, request transferrin saturation\\n  - Repeat ferritin to confirm elevation\\n- Imaging/Referral triage:\\n  - If no exogenous hormones and repeat elevated estradiol/progesterone, arrange pelvic/transvaginal ultrasound (evaluate ovarian mass/functional cyst) and refer to Gynecology and/or Endocrinology for evaluation.\\n  - If adrenal source suspected (abnormal cortisol/17-OHP/DHEA-S or clinical features), refer to Endocrinology.\\n\\nROUTINE (within 4–12 weeks)\\n- Recheck hematocrit after counseling on hydration (see actions below); if persistent elevation or >50% notify provider.\\n- Recheck vitamin D in 8–12 weeks after supplementation adjustment.\\n- Reassess lipids with lifestyle intervention at 3–6 months.\\n\\nSpecific medication/dose recommendations\\n- Testosterone therapy: Not indicated\\n  - Rationale: Total testosterone 20.6 ng/dL, Free T 2 pg/mL, SHBG 73.9 nmol/L — androgen levels are within expected range. Do not initiate testosterone therapy for symptoms without documented deficiency and specialist review.\\n- Estrogen/progesterone: do not empirically adjust or stop exogenous therapy without provider review.\\n  - If patient is on HRT and symptomatic or labs concerning, notify provider for individualized HRT adjustment discussion.\\n- Vitamin D: follow clinic protocol\\n  - Recommend Metagenics D3 2000 Complex daily (per protocol) to reach target clinic range ≥60 ng/mL; recheck 25-OH Vitamin D in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Iron: do not prescribe iron supplementation\\n  - Ferritin 204 ng/mL indicates iron repletion or inflammatory state — do not treat with oral iron. If ferritin remains elevated and transferrin saturation >45%, consider HFE genetic testing per protocol.\\n\\nFollow-up timing and labs (specific)\\n- Immediate (today / same business day)\\n  - Confirm current meds/supplements and last dose timing; document and alert provider if on estrogen/progesterone.\\n- Within 72 hours\\n  - If on exogenous hormones: phone triage note to provider and plan whether to interpret labs in context of therapy versus hold therapy prior to repeat testing.\\n- Within 1–2 weeks\\n  - Repeat fasting morning: Estradiol, Progesterone, FSH, LH, SHBG\\n  - Serum iron, TIBC (if needed), calculate transferrin saturation; repeat ferritin\\n  - Morning cortisol, ACTH, 17-OH progesterone if exogenous progesterone denied\\n  - Order pelvic/transvaginal ultrasound if no exogenous hormones and elevated sex steroids confirmed\\n- 1–2 weeks: repeat hematocrit after hydration advice\\n- 8–12 weeks: Vitamin D 25-OH recheck after supplementation\\n- 3–6 months: lipid re-evaluation and routine preventive screening as indicated\\n\\nPhysician notification requirements\\n- Notify provider immediately if:\\n  - Patient admits current estrogen or progesterone use (report specifics).\\n  - Repeat labs confirm estradiol >100 pg/mL with low FSH (<10 mIU/mL) and elevated progesterone without exogenous source.\\n  - Ferritin persists >300 ng/mL or transferrin saturation >45% (concern for iron overload).\\n  - Hematocrit rises above 50% or patient symptomatic (headache, visual changes, thrombosis symptoms).\\n  - Any abnormal adrenal testing (elevated 17-OHP, abnormal cortisol/ACTH).\\n\\nPatient education points for staff to convey (documented counseling)\\n- Ask patient to bring or list all prescription medications, OTC supplements, topical creams, compounded hormones, and herbal products at next contact/visit.\\n- Counsel: hydrate well before repeat labs; avoid taking any hormone-containing meds on day(s) of testing if directed by provider.\\n- Do not start iron supplements given elevated ferritin; notify provider before starting any new supplement.\\n- Explain vitamin D plan: start Metagenics D3 2000 daily per protocol and recheck levels in ~8–12 weeks.\\n\\nLifestyle interventions to recommend (staff deliverable)\\n- Hydration: encourage increased oral fluids for 48–72 hours prior to repeat blood draw to reduce hemoconcentration.\\n- Cardiometabolic health: maintain Mediterranean-style diet, weight management, and ≥150 minutes/week moderate aerobic activity plus resistance training; recheck LDL in 3–6 months.\\n- Alcohol moderation and limit hepatotoxins (relevant for ferritin/liver health).\\n- Sleep hygiene and assess for contributors to fatigue (refer for behavioral health or sleep clinic if symptoms persist).\\n\\nAdditional testing / referrals to consider\\n- Lab:\\n  - Repeat estradiol, progesterone, FSH, LH, SHBG (fasting morning, off exogenous hormones if possible)\\n  - Transferrin saturation (calculate or request directly)\\n  - Morning cortisol, ACTH, 17-hydroxyprogesterone if progesterone elevation unexplained\\n  - HFE genetic testing if transferrin saturation >45% or ferritin persistently elevated\\n- Imaging:\\n  - Transvaginal pelvic ultrasound if no exogenous hormones and sex steroids remain elevated\\n- Referrals:\\n  - Gynecology for ovarian assessment if endogenous ovarian source suspected\\n  - Endocrinology for pituitary/adrenal evaluation if labs suggest non-ovarian source or if cortisol/17-OHP abnormal\\n  - Hepatology if ferritin elevation persists with liver enzyme changes (AST/ALT currently normal)\\n  - Sleep medicine or behavioral health if ongoing fatigue/sleep disturbance after metabolic/hormonal optimization\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (concise)\\n1. Today (immediate): Perform targeted medication/supplement reconciliation; notify provider if on any estrogen/progesterone products.\\n2. Within 72 hours: If on exogenous hormones, document and discuss interpretation plan with provider.\\n3. Within 1–2 weeks: Repeat fasting morning estradiol, progesterone, FSH, LH, SHBG; repeat ferritin and order transferrin saturation; repeat hematocrit after hydration; add morning cortisol/ACTH/17-OHP if no exogenous progesterone.\\n4. Within 2 weeks: If repeat abnormal and patient denies exogenous hormones, arrange pelvic ultrasound and refer to Gynecology ± Endocrinology.\\n5. 8–12 weeks: Recheck 25-OH Vitamin D after initiating Metagenics D3 2000 Complex.\\n6. 3–6 months: Reassess lipids and broader risk management.\\n\\nTestosterone dosing/action summary\\n- No testosterone initiation or dose change indicated at this time. Document rationale in chart and discuss alternative etiologies for symptoms with provider prior to any androgen therapy.\\n\\nDocument in chart: medication reconciliation outcome, patient counseling provided, labs ordered with timing, and provider notifications made.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 0.51, "detail": "Triglycerides 46 / HDL 91 = 0.51", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "4.6%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 73.9, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 18, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "85 mg/dL", "detail": "Normal atherogenic particle count", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "0.6 mg/L", "detail": "Low inflammatory burden", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Menopausal Transition", "confidence": "high", "description": "Clinical picture consistent with perimenopause or menopause. Hormonal shifts during this transition affect bone health, cardiovascular risk, mood, and quality of life.", "supportingFindings": ["Postmenopausal status", "Sleep disruption reported", "Irritability reported"]}, {"name": "Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation", "confidence": "high", "description": "Pattern consistent with HPA-axis stress and cortisol dysregulation. Chronic stress depletes DHEA-S, disrupts sleep, and impairs cognitive and emotional function.", "supportingFindings": ["Sleep disruption reported", "Irritability reported", "Low energy / fatigue reported", "Brain fog reported"]}]}	2026-03-27 20:40:51.465731	1
