ation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 41, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.24183404834260536, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 0.2% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.15880036233748904, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 0.2%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.12198763786252466, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.1%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 1.9614667235524217, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 2.0%, ASCVD 1.2%, Heart Failure 1.1%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 21, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 12, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 106, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.75, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 57, "status": "normal", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Apo B at optimal level.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 8, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 290, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 87, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 8.39, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 0.2, "status": "normal", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Low cardiovascular inflammation risk.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.36, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Luteal Progesterone", "interpretation": "Luteal progesterone of 0.36 ng/mL indicates anovulation or luteal failure. This cycle was likely anovulatory — a common perimenopause finding.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Anovulatory cycle confirmed. If recurrent, this signals advancing perimenopause. Consider progesterone for symptom management: micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly continuously, or cyclically if patient prefers withdrawal bleeds. Discuss HRT options if symptomatic. PATIENT EDUCATION: This level indicates you likely did not ovulate this cycle, which is common in perimenopause. This can cause irregular periods, heavy bleeding, and other symptoms. Treatment is available to help regulate your cycles and relieve symptoms.", "referenceRange": "≥10 ng/mL optimal, 8–10 borderline, <8 low, <5 anovulatory"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 28.3, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone Pattern D: Adequate Androgens, Persistent Symptoms", "interpretation": "Adequate Androgens pattern identified (Total T: 28.3 ng/dL (within range); Free T: 3 pg/mL; SHBG: 64.2 nmol/L). Total, free, and bioavailable testosterone are within normal ranges and SHBG is not extreme. If the patient reports fatigue, low mood, sleep disruption, or low libido, these symptoms are likely driven by non-androgenic factors. Findings to evaluate: Estrogen/Progesterone balance should be assessed (E2: 50 pg/mL, P4: 0.36 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Do not reflexively add testosterone when levels are adequate — look deeper. Evaluate iron status (ferritin target >50 for symptom resolution), thyroid function (optimize TSH), and sleep quality (consider sleep apnea screening with STOP-BANG). Assess estrogen and progesterone balance — E2/P4 imbalance is a common driver of persistent symptoms. Address CNS and psychosocial drivers: chronic stress, HPA axis dysregulation, mood disorders. Consider DHEA-S if not already checked. Support with lifestyle optimization: sleep hygiene, stress management, nutrition. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your testosterone levels are actually in a healthy range, which is good news. When symptoms persist despite normal testosterone, it usually means something else is contributing. Your provider will look at other factors like iron levels, thyroid function, sleep quality, and hormone balance to find what's driving your symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Total T 15-70 ng/dL, Free T 0.5-5.0 pg/mL (all within range)"}], "aiRecommendations": "1) Overall clinical assessment\\n- Current labs indicate an anovulatory/luteal-failure pattern this cycle: progesterone 0.36 ng/mL in the luteal window → consistent with luteal phase defect/anovulation (perimenopausal pattern).\\n- Vitamin D 25-OH = 30 ng/mL → insufficiency (21–30 ng/mL); suboptimal for bone and hormonal health. Target per clinic protocol ≥60 ng/mL with repletion.\\n- Androgen panel (Total T 28.3 ng/dL, Free T 3 pg/mL, SHBG 64.2 nmol/L) consistent with “Adequate Androgens” pattern. Do NOT reflexively start testosterone based on these values.\\n- Other routine labs listed as normal (including ferritin, TSH, estradiol), so alternate causes of fatigue/low libido should be prioritized before androgen therapy.\\n\\n2) Priority actions (organized CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL\\n- Physician notification required immediately if any of the following apply:\\n  * Patient is currently trying to conceive (TTC) — anovulation has direct fertility implications and may prompt immediate luteal/ovulation support and fertility referral.\\n  * Patient has acute heavy uterine bleeding, syncope, or other red-flag symptoms.\\n- If TTC and pregnancy is possible, confirm pregnancy status prior to initiating any hormonal therapy where clinically relevant (pregnancy test as appropriate).\\n\\nURGENT (action within 1–2 weeks)\\n- For infertility/TTC:\\n  * Discuss and consider initiating luteal support per protocol if advised by provider (see dosing below). Provider decision required for start/route/duration.\\n  * Order mid-luteal progesterone in the next cycle to confirm ovulation if the patient is not on exogenous progesterone: time specimen 7 days after confirmed ovulation or approximately cycle day 21 in a regular 28-day cycle (adjust per cycle length/ovulation timing).\\n- For symptomatic perimenopause (irregular menses, heavy bleeding, vasomotor or sleep disturbance) — notify provider to discuss HRT options; consider starting micronized progesterone for cycle regulation/symptom control per provider direction.\\n\\nROUTINE (action within 4–12 weeks)\\n- Vitamin D repletion per clinic protocol (Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily). Recheck 25-OH in 8–12 weeks to document rise toward target ≥60 ng/mL.\\n- If progesterone support is NOT started, re-evaluate menstrual pattern and repeat mid-luteal P4 in 1–3 cycles if irregularity persists to document ongoing anovulation.\\n- Document and counsel patient that testosterone therapy is not indicated based on current labs.\\n\\n3) Testosterone dose recommendations (if applicable)\\n- Recommendation: Testosterone therapy is NOT indicated at this time.\\n  * Rationale: Total and free testosterone are in acceptable ranges (Total T 28.3 ng/dL, Free T 3 pg/mL, SHBG 64.2 nmol/L) and symptoms are more likely related to estrogen/progesterone imbalance, sleep, iron/thyroid status, or psychosocial/CNS factors.\\n- If provider still considers androgen therapy later, require full reassessment (symptom correlation, baseline lipids, liver tests, pregnancy status if applicable, and informed consent) and discuss risks/monitoring—do not start without provider signoff.\\n\\n4) Specific medication recommendations / dose adjustments (per clinic protocol)\\n- Progesterone (provider decision; for symptomatic control or fertility support):\\n  * Micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly (oral) — clinic protocol option.\\n  * Dosing options to present to provider/patient:\\n    - Continuous: 100 mg PO qHS nightly continuously (used for perimenopausal symptom control/bleeding stabilization).\\n    - Cyclical: 100 mg PO qHS for a scheduled interval each month to induce withdrawal bleeds (discuss cycle days with patient; typical cycle-day scheduling should be individualized).\\n  * If used for luteal support in known conception/TTC, discuss duration with provider (common practice: continue through first trimester per provider decision; clinic protocol specifics to be followed).\\n  * Monitor bleeding pattern and side effects; reassess at 4–12 weeks.\\n- Vitamin D:\\n  * Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily as per protocol. Check 25-OH 8–12 weeks after initiation.\\n- Notify provider for any medication starts so they can document prescription and counseling.\\n\\n5) Patient education points to cover (staff to review with patient)\\n- Explain that this cycle’s progesterone (0.36 ng/mL) indicates probable anovulation — common in perimenopause and can cause irregular periods and fertility challenges.\\n- If TTC: explain options (luteal support, ovulation induction, referral) and the need for timely follow-up.\\n- If not TTC: explain progesterone therapy options (purpose, regimen alternatives — continuous vs cyclic, expected effects, side effects).\\n- Vitamin D: explain insufficiency, recommended supplement dose, expected timeline to recheck levels, and importance for bone and hormone health.\\n- Reassure patient that testosterone levels are within expected range and starting testosterone is not recommended at present.\\n- Lifestyle advice: sleep hygiene, stress mitigation, weight management and nutrition to help symptomatic improvement.\\n\\n6) Lifestyle interventions needed (specific, staff-facing)\\n- Sleep: reinforce sleep hygiene; if symptoms include daytime sleepiness or snoring despite low STOP-BANG, consider sleep medicine referral or further testing.\\n- Nutrition:\\n  * Maintain adequate dietary calcium and vitamin D; review calcium intake if patient is on replacement.\\n  * Ensure iron-rich diet if heavy menses present despite normal ferritin; recheck ferritin if bleeding pattern changes.\\n- Exercise: encourage weight-bearing exercise 3×/week for bone health and mood.\\n- Stress management: recommend CBT, mindfulness, or referral to behavioral health if chronic stress/mood symptoms.\\n- Substance use: counsel on smoking cessation and alcohol moderation if applicable.\\n\\n7) Additional testing or referrals to consider\\n- Recheck labs:\\n  * 25-OH Vitamin D in 8–12 weeks after supplementation.\\n  * Mid-luteal progesterone in next cycle (7 days post-ovulation or adjusted by cycle length) if not on exogenous progesterone, or 1 cycle after stopping progesterone if verifying endogenous ovulation.\\n- Consider if symptomatic or fertility concerns:\\n  * AMH, repeat FSH/E2 if fertility evaluation desired (age-dependent).\\n  * DHEA-S if adrenal androgen contribution suspected (not checked).\\n  * Pelvic ultrasound if abnormal uterine bleeding or suspicion of structural cause.\\n  * Endocrinology or REI referral for recurrent anovulation, infertility, or if ovulation induction is being considered.\\n  * Gynecology or menopause specialist consult for discussion of full HRT regimen (estrogen ± progesterone) if perimenopausal symptoms significant.\\n- Bone density (DEXA) assessment if additional risk factors for osteoporosis or as clinically indicated by age/FRAX/BMD guidelines.\\n\\n8) Summary of next steps and timeline (clear actionable checklist)\\n- Immediate:\\n  * Notify provider if patient is TTC or has heavy bleeding/other red flags.\\n  * If provider authorizes, initiate micronized progesterone 100 mg qHS (continuous or cyclic per shared decision-making) for symptomatic control or luteal support — document prescription and counseling.\\n  * Start Vitamin D 5,000 IU + K daily per protocol.\\n- Within 1–2 weeks:\\n  * If TTC: plan fertility counseling/possible referral; order pregnancy test if appropriate.\\n- Within next cycle (timing individualized):\\n  * Draw mid-luteal progesterone 7 days after ovulation (or cycle day 21 if regular 28-day cycle) to confirm ovulation if not on exogenous progesterone.\\n- 4–12 weeks:\\n  * Reassess progesterone-related symptoms and bleeding pattern if progesterone started.\\n  * Recheck Vitamin D 25-OH at 8–12 weeks.\\n- 3 months:\\n  * If anovulation persists or fertility goals unmet, discuss REI referral; if perimenopausal symptoms persist despite progesterone, discuss broader HRT options with provider.\\n- Ongoing:\\n  * No testosterone therapy at this time; reassess if new data or persistent symptoms after addressing thyroid, iron, sleep, and psychosocial factors.\\n\\nPhysician notification checklist for staff\\n- Notify physician now if: patient is actively trying to conceive, has significant/worrisome bleeding, plans to start progesterone, or requests testosterone despite normal labs.\\n- Document counseling provided about anovulation, progesterone options, Vitamin D therapy, and rationale for not starting testosterone.\\n\\nEnd of recommendations — please review and sign off for any medication starts (progesterone, Vitamin D) and for any referrals or additional testing.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 0.64, "detail": "Triglycerides 41 / HDL 64 = 0.64", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 64.2, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 12, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "57 mg/dL", "detail": "Normal atherogenic particle count", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "0.2 mg/L", "detail": "Low inflammatory burden", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Lab pattern consistent with relative estrogen excess or impaired estrogen clearance. Even without reported symptoms, supporting liver detoxification and estrogen metabolism pathways may be beneficial.", "supportingFindings": ["Progesterone 0.36 ng/mL (very low)", "Borderline liver markers (ALT 12, AST 21) suggesting possible impaired hepatic clearance"]}]}	2026-03-28 01:19:29.875886	1
72	Todd McAlpin	male	2025-05-01 00:00:00	{"a1c": 6, "alt": 42, "ast": 28, "bun": 16, "co2": 19, "hdl": 32, "ldl": 86, "mcv": 86.8, "rbc": 5.07, "tsh": 1.71, "wbc": 7.3, "egfr": 64, "sodium": 136, "albumin": 3.9, "calcium": 8.7, "glucose": 115, "chloride": 104, "vitaminD": 55.9, "bilirubin": 0.4, "estradiol": 15, "platelets": 239, "potassium": 4.5, "creatinine": 1.38, "hematocrit": 44, "hemoglobin": 13.2, "vitaminB12": 690, "labDrawDate": "2025-05-01", "patientName": "Todd McAlpin", "demographics": {"age": 46, "bmi": 30, "sex": "male", "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": true, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": true, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": true, "systolicBP": 123, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": true, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "testosterone": 337, "totalProtein": 6.4, "triglycerides": 253, "totalCholesterol": 169}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History of present illness: Patient Todd McAlpin, 46-year-old male, presents for review of recent laboratory results and discussion of hormone and metabolic management. Patient states he is concerned about low energy and decreased libido. He reports habitual snoring and daytime sleepiness; bed partner reports witnessed apneas. Denies chest pain, palpitations, syncope, dyspnea at rest, hematuria, melena, or overt bleeding. No acute infectious symptoms. No new medications started within the last 4 weeks reported by patient.\\n- Current therapies/supplements: Patient is being managed by Men's Hormone & Primary Care Clinic (specific current TRT regimen not documented in chart note). Patient reports taking vitamin D 2000 IU daily per prior recommendation. No reported use of aromatase inhibitors at this visit (to be confirmed in medication reconciliation).\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vital signs: Not available in chart at time of lab review; recommend obtaining in-clinic (BP, HR, weight, BMI).\\n- Laboratory results (03/28/2026):\\n  - Complete Blood Count (CBC)\\n    - Hemoglobin: 13.2 g/dL [ABNORMAL - low]\\n    - Hematocrit: 44 % (normal)\\n    - RBC count: 5.07 M/µL (normal)\\n    - MCV: 86.8 fL (normal)\\n    - WBC: 7.3 K/µL (normal)\\n    - Platelets: 239 K/µL (normal)\\n  - Metabolic panel / Renal\\n    - Sodium: 136 mEq/L (normal)\\n    - Potassium: 4.5 mEq/L (normal)\\n    - BUN: 16 mg/dL (normal)\\n    - Creatinine: 1.38 mg/dL [BORDERLINE ↑] — eGFR: 64 mL/min (normal range borderline)\\n  - Liver tests / Hepatic fibrosis\\n    - AST: 28 U/L (normal)\\n    - ALT: 42 U/L [BORDERLINE ↑]\\n    - Bilirubin, total: 0.4 mg/dL (normal)\\n    - FIB‑4 score: 0.83 (no advanced fibrosis)\\n  - Lipids\\n    - Total cholesterol: (not provided)\\n    - LDL-C: 86 mg/dL (normal)\\n    - HDL-C: 32 mg/dL [ABNORMAL - low]\\n    - Triglycerides: 253 mg/dL [BORDERLINE ↑]\\n  - Glucose metabolism\\n    - Fasting glucose: 115 mg/dL [BORDERLINE - impaired fasting glucose]\\n    - Hemoglobin A1c: 6.0 % [BORDERLINE - prediabetes]\\n    - Insulin resistance screening: 3 of 6 markers positive — HIGH LIKELIHOOD of insulin resistance. Phenotypes: Visceral/Metabolic IR and Hepatic (liver-driven) IR. [CRITICAL]\\n  - Hormones\\n    - Total Testosterone: 337 ng/dL [BORDERLINE low]\\n    - Estradiol (E2): 15 pg/mL [BORDERLINE low]\\n  - Vitamins / Micronutrients\\n    - Vitamin D (25‑OH): 55.9 ng/mL (adequate but suboptimal)\\n    - Vitamin B12: 690 pg/mL (normal)\\n  - Cardiovascular risk scores\\n    - 10‑yr Total CVD (AHA PREVENT 2023): 3.4% (low)\\n    - 10‑yr ASCVD: 2.2%\\n    - 10‑yr HF: 1.0%\\n    - 30‑yr CVD: 22.5% (elevated)\\n- RED FLAGS: None reported in supplied data besides lab critical flags.\\n- Other relevant calculated data: STOP‑BANG score = 5/8 → HIGH RISK for obstructive sleep apnea [CRITICAL].\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1) High risk obstructive sleep apnea (STOP‑BANG 5) — critical. Patient endorses snoring and daytime sleepiness. OSA increases cardiovascular risk and may impact safety of testosterone therapy. Requires expedited sleep evaluation.\\n2) Insulin resistance / metabolic syndrome phenotype — HIGH LIKELIHOOD (3/6 markers positive). Supporting labs: fasting glucose 115 mg/dL, A1c 6.0%, triglycerides 253 mg/dL, HDL 32 mg/dL. Elevated 30‑yr CVD risk 22.5%. Lifestyle-driven and hepatic/visceral phenotype likely contributors.\\n3) Dyslipidemia: Elevated triglycerides 253 mg/dL and low HDL 32 mg/dL — increases ASCVD and pancreatitis risk if triglycerides rise further. LDL 86 mg/dL currently acceptable; statin generally not indicated at current short-term risk but lifestyle intervention necessary; clinician review recommended for pharmacologic therapy consideration.\\n4) Borderline low testosterone 337 ng/dL and low estradiol 15 pg/mL — may correlate with symptoms of low energy and libido. Differential: age-related hypogonadism vs functional suppression from obesity/insulin resistance. If on aromatase inhibitor, low estradiol raises concern for AI dose reduction/cessation.\\n5) Borderline ALT elevation 42 U/L — hepatic transaminase mild elevation. Differential: nonalcoholic fatty liver disease related to metabolic syndrome, medication or supplement effect, alcohol. FIB‑4 low (0.83) argues against advanced fibrosis.\\n6) Borderline renal function / borderline creatinine 1.38 mg/dL, eGFR 64 mL/min — requires med review for nephrotoxic agents, hydration status, repeat BMP for trend.\\n7) Mild anemia: Hemoglobin 13.2 g/dL (low for male) — mild; warrants iron studies and follow-up CBC to evaluate etiology.\\n8) Prediabetes: A1c 6.0% and fasting glucose 115 mg/dL — risk for progression to type 2 diabetes; lifestyle intervention and repeat testing recommended.\\n9) Vitamin D: 25‑OH 55.9 ng/mL — adequate but below optimal range; continue current dosing and recheck per protocol.\\n10) Medication/supplement review needed — confirm current TRT regimen, use of aromatase inhibitors, supplements (notably those with hepatic/renal effects), and any nephrotoxic agents (NSAIDs, ACEi/ARB, diuretics).\\n\\nPLAN:\\nGeneral: Results reviewed with patient. Emphasized priority items: urgent sleep evaluation for high STOP‑BANG, cardiometabolic risk reduction, and kidney/liver monitoring. Patient education provided regarding need for expedited sleep testing, lifestyle measures, and laboratory rechecks. Patient verbalized understanding.\\n\\n1) High risk OSA (STOP‑BANG 5)\\n   - Action: Urgent sleep medicine referral placed today (HSAT or in‑lab polysomnography) — aim within 1–2 weeks. Flag as expedited.\\n   - Staff instructions: Provide HSAT instructions; advise patient to avoid driving if excessive daytime sleepiness; provide educational material on OSA and CPAP.\\n   - Physician notification: Immediate phone/pager notification to prescribing clinician regarding STOP‑BANG 5 and consideration whether to hold or adjust TRT pending sleep evaluation if moderate–severe OSA confirmed.\\n   - Follow-up: Sleep clinic will report results; if OSA confirmed, initiate CPAP therapy per sleep medicine; re-evaluate TRT appropriateness.\\n\\n2) Insulin resistance / prediabetes / dyslipidemia\\n   - Lifestyle: Intensive lifestyle intervention — weight loss goal ≥5–10% if overweight; Mediterranean or low-glycemic diet, reduction of refined carbohydrates and added sugars, alcohol reduction/abstinence until triglycerides and ALT improve, ≥150 min/week moderate aerobic exercise plus resistance training 2×/week.\\n   - Referral: Enroll in diabetes prevention program or nutrition counseling; consider referral to preventive cardiology or lipid clinic if clinician recommends.\\n   - Medications: No immediate initiation of antihyperglycemics indicated at this time. If after 3 months glycemic measures worsen, consider metformin per clinician discretion.\\n   - Monitoring orders:\\n     - Fasting lipid panel and fasting glucose: repeat in 3 months.\\n     - Hemoglobin A1c: repeat in 3 months (range 3–6 months acceptable).\\n   - Consider initiation of prescription omega‑3 (EPA) if triglycerides remain >200 mg/dL after lifestyle modification — clinician review required prior to prescribing.\\n\\n3) Dyslipidemia (Triglycerides 253 mg/dL; HDL 32 mg/dL)\\n   - Action: Lifestyle measures above prioritized.\\n   - Clinician notification: Electronic message within 24 hours recommending review for possible pharmacologic therapy if lifestyle measures insufficient.\\n   - Monitoring: Repeat fasting lipid panel in 3 months.\\n   - Supplements: Consider OmegaGenics Fish Oil EPA‑DHA 1000 (1 softgel 1–2 times daily with meals) per clinic supplement guidance (discuss with clinician).\\n\\n4) Testosterone 337 ng/dL / Estradiol 15 pg/mL (borderline)\\n   - Discussion: Discussed symptomatic status with patient (low energy, decreased libido). Plan contingent on clinician decision and sleep study results.\\n   - If symptomatic and clinician agrees, consider TRT dose increase by 20–30% per clinic protocol (examples provided for clinician: e.g., increase IM 100 mg q7d → 120–130 mg q7d; or topical gel 50 mg/day → 60–65 mg/day). Any change requires explicit clinician order.\\n   - If patient is taking an aromatase inhibitor and estradiol low, recommend clinician review for dose reduction or discontinuation of AI.\\n   - Monitoring if TRT adjusted:\\n     - Morning total testosterone and estradiol 8–12 weeks after any dose change (draw pre‑dose when applicable).\\n     - CBC/Hct at 4–6 weeks after dose change and then q3 months for first year to monitor for polycythemia.\\n     - Reassess symptoms at time of labs.\\n   - Current action: Electronic message to clinician recommending review of TRT and AI use; hold dose changes until sleep study results available or clinician directs otherwise.\\n\\n5) ALT 42 U/L (mild elevation)\\n   - Action: Review medication and supplement list for hepatotoxic agents; advise alcohol reduction/abstinence until repeat tests.\\n   - Orders: Repeat CMP with ALT in 4–6 weeks.\\n   - If ALT remains elevated or increases >2× ULN, order hepatitis serologies and consider hepatology referral and ultrasound for fatty liver assessment.\\n   - Note: FIB‑4 low (0.83) — low likelihood of advanced fibrosis.\\n\\n6) Creatinine 1.38 mg/dL, eGFR 64 mL/min (borderline)\\n   - Action: Review medications for nephrotoxins (NSAIDs, ACEi/ARB, diuretics, herbal supplements). Counsel hydration prior to repeat testing.\\n   - Orders: Repeat BMP/creatinine in 2–4 weeks. Obtain urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (spot ACR) with repeat BMP (if indicated).\\n   - Clinician notification: Urgent message within 48–72 hours for medication review and to determine need for expedited repeat labs or nephrology referral if trend persists.\\n\\n7) Hemoglobin 13.2 g/dL (male low)\\n   - Action: Order iron studies (ferritin, serum iron, TIBC, transferrin saturation) and repeat CBC in 4–6 weeks.\\n   - Clinician notification: Electronic message with iron studies ordered and request for anemia workup plan.\\n\\n8) Prediabetes (A1c 6.0%) and impaired fasting glucose\\n   - Action: Intensive lifestyle modification counseling (diet, exercise, weight loss).\\n   - Orders: Repeat fasting glucose and A1c in 3 months.\\n   - Consider enrollment in diabetes prevention program; consider metformin if further progression or per clinician judgment.\\n\\n9) Supplements recommended by clinic (per AI clinical recommendations)\\n   - High‑priority: Testralin® — 2 tablets twice daily (staff to verify appropriateness and drug interactions; clinician to approve).\\n   - Medium‑priority (discuss and consider prescribing/supplementing):\\n     - OmegaGenics Fish Oil EPA‑DHA 1000: 1 softgel 1–2 times daily with meals.\\n     - NutraGems CoQ10 300: 1 chewable gel daily.\\n     - Magtein Magnesium L‑Threonate: 3 capsules daily divided doses.\\n     - Adreset®: 2 capsules twice daily.\\n     - Exhilarin®: 2 tablets daily.\\n     - OmegaGenics Fish Oil Neuro 1000: 1 softgel 1–2 times daily with meals.\\n   - Note: All supplements require review for interactions with current medications and renal/hepatic dosing considerations; clinician approval required.\\n\\n10) Follow-up / timeline (summarized)\\n    - Immediate (within 24–72 hours): Notify clinician regarding STOP‑BANG 5, triglycerides/HDL, borderline creatinine and ALT. Place urgent sleep referral.\\n    - 1–2 weeks: Sleep testing to be completed.\\n    - 2–4 weeks: Repeat BMP/creatinine; urine ACR if indicated.\\n    - 4–6 weeks: Repeat CBC (hemoglobin), repeat CMP (ALT), review iron studies results.\\n    - 8–12 weeks: If TRT dose changed, recheck morning total testosterone and estradiol; CBC/Hct at 4–6 weeks post-change and at 8–12 weeks as indicated.\\n    - 3 months: Repeat fasting lipid panel, fasting glucose, reinforce lifestyle measures.\\n    - 3–6 months: Repeat A1c and reassess need for pharmacotherapy or specialty referral (cardiology/endocrinology/nephrology).\\n    - Annual: Routine preventive care and reassessment of 30‑yr CVD risk.\\n\\nDocumentation/communication:\\n- Results reviewed with patient today. Patient was counseled regarding urgency of sleep testing and importance of metabolic/lifestyle interventions. Patient instructed to report worsening sleepiness, new chest pain, dyspnea, syncope, significant edema, or signs of bleeding.\\n- Orders placed today: urgent sleep medicine referral (HSAT/polysomnography), repeat BMP in 2–4 weeks, CMP and CBC in 4–6 weeks, iron studies, urine ACR as indicated, fasting lipid and glucose in 3 months, A1c in 3 months, morning testosterone/estradiol after 8–12 weeks if dose changed.\\n- Clinician notifications: Immediate/urgent notifications to prescribing clinician regarding STOP‑BANG 5 (expedited sleep referral and TRT consideration), triglycerides/HDL, borderline creatinine and ALT. Electronic messages placed regarding hemoglobin and hormone panel for clinician review.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow‑up as above.\\n\\nDate: 03/28/2026", "preventRisk": {"ldlGoal": "No specific LDL target; focus on risk-based statin intensity", "riskCategory": "low", "tenYearASCVD": 0.021657116202000892, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0.03365956003244406, "thirtyYearASCVD": 0.13994754557387207, "ageValidFor30Year": true, "thirtyYearTotalCVD": 0.22467008990593682, "tenYearHFPercentage": "1.0%", "tenYearHeartFailure": 0.009842391442920647, "statinRecommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "tenYearCVDPercentage": "3.4%", "tenYearASCVDPercentage": "2.2%", "thirtyYearHFPercentage": "9.7%", "thirtyYearHeartFailure": 0.0967611515928371, "thirtyYearCVDPercentage": "22.5%", "thirtyYearASCVDPercentage": "14.0%"}, "supplements": [{"dose": "2 tablets twice daily", "name": "Testralin®", "caution": "Supports healthy testosterone levels and male vitality. Best taken with meals. May take 4-8 weeks for optimal effects.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "high", "rationale": "Testralin provides targeted botanical and nutrient support for healthy testosterone production and male hormonal balance. Supports energy, vitality, and healthy aging.", "indication": "Total T 337 ng/dL"}, {"dose": "1 softgel 1-2 times daily with meals", "name": "OmegaGenics® Fish Oil EPA-DHA 1000", "caution": "High-quality omega-3 (500mg EPA, 500mg DHA) for heart and brain health. Lemon-flavored for easy consumption. Contains fish oil.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "OmegaGenics Fish Oil EPA-DHA 1000 provides balanced omega-3 fatty acids to support cardiovascular health, cognitive function, and reduce inflammation. Recommended for all men 40+ for overall cardiac and neurologic protection.", "indication": "Age 46 - cardiac/neurologic support for patients 40+"}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone essential for mitochondrial energy production and cardiovascular protection. Recommended for all men 40+ for cardiac and neurologic health maintenance.", "indication": "Age 46 - cardiac/energy support for patients 40+"}, {"dose": "3 capsules daily (divided doses)", "name": "Magtein® Magnesium L-Threonate", "caution": "L-Threonate form crosses blood-brain barrier for cognitive and sleep support. Well-tolerated; gentle on GI system.", "category": "mineral", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Magtein is the only magnesium form shown to effectively cross the blood-brain barrier. Supports quality sleep, cognitive function, and hormonal balance.", "indication": "Multiple metabolic/hormonal stress indicators"}, {"dose": "2 capsules twice daily", "name": "Adreset®", "caution": "Adaptogenic formula with ginseng, rhodiola, and cordyceps. Best taken earlier in day. May take 2-4 weeks for full effect.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Adreset combines adaptogenic herbs to support healthy adrenal function, stress resilience, and energy. Helps restore HPA axis balance and supports testosterone production.", "indication": "Total T 337 ng/dL (low)"}, {"dose": "2 tablets daily", "name": "Exhilarin®", "caution": "Ayurvedic adaptogenic formula. Supports mental clarity and emotional well-being. Takes 2-4 weeks for optimal benefits.", "category": "general", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Exhilarin provides adaptogenic support for mental energy, mood, and stress resilience. Complements hormonal optimization for comprehensive fatigue management.", "indication": "Multiple fatigue factors: Total T 337, A1c 6%, Glucose 115"}, {"dose": "1 softgel 1-2 times daily", "name": "OmegaGenics® Fish Oil Neuro 1000", "caution": "High-DHA omega-3 (750mg DHA, 250mg EPA) for brain and heart health. Lemon-flavored, no fishy taste. Contains calamari - avoid if shellfish allergy.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "OmegaGenics Fish Oil Neuro 1000 provides concentrated DHA and EPA to reduce triglycerides, support brain function, cardiovascular health, and reduce inflammation.", "indication": "TG 253 mg/dL, HDL 32 mg/dL (low)"}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Probiotic", "caution": "Multi-strain probiotic for digestive and immune health. Shelf-stable formula. No refrigeration needed.", "category": "general", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete provides comprehensive multi-strain probiotic support for digestive health, immune function, and overall wellness.", "indication": "Men's health foundation"}, {"dose": "1 tablet daily with meal", "name": "D3 2000 Complex", "caution": "Maintenance dose for suboptimal levels. Comprehensive vitamin D support with cofactors.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "low", "rationale": "D3 2000 Complex provides maintenance vitamin D with cofactors to reach optimal range ≥60 ng/mL for testosterone and immune support.", "indication": "Vitamin D 55.9 ng/mL (suboptimal, target ≥60)"}], "recheckWindow": "8-12 weeks (after dose change or intervention)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall summary (plain language)\\nYour 10-year ASCVD risk is low at 2.2%, but your 30‑year CVD risk is elevated at 22.5% (meaning over the next 30 years you have a higher chance of heart disease without lifestyle changes). Important areas to address now are low HDL cholesterol (32 mg/dL), high triglycerides (253 mg/dL), borderline blood sugar (A1c 6.0%, fasting glucose 115 mg/dL), high sleep apnea risk (STOP‑BANG 5/8), low hemoglobin (13.2 g/dL), and borderline kidney/liver markers. Many of these improve with specific diet, activity, sleep, and follow-up testing.\\n\\nPositive findings (what’s OK)\\n- LDL cholesterol 86 mg/dL (within optimal range)\\n- Hematocrit 44% and MCV 86.8 fL (red cell indices OK for now)\\n- eGFR 64 mL/min (kidney function at/above 60)\\n- AST 28 U/L, FIB‑4 0.83 (low risk of advanced liver fibrosis)\\n- TSH 1.71 mIU/L, Vitamin B12 690 pg/mL, Vitamin D 55.9 ng/mL (adequate)\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + concrete actions)\\n- HDL 32 mg/dL (target >40 mg/dL) and Triglycerides 253 mg/dL:\\n  - Add 25–30 g fiber daily: oatmeal for breakfast, beans with lunch, vegetables at dinner.\\n  - Include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n  - Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter and cheese.\\n  - Daily exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking or equivalent.\\n- Blood sugar: Hemoglobin A1c 6.0% and fasting glucose 115 mg/dL:\\n  - Reduce refined carbs: swap white bread for whole grain, limit sugary drinks.\\n  - Include protein with each meal to stabilize blood sugar.\\n  - Walk 10–15 minutes after meals.\\n- Liver: ALT 42 U/L (mildly elevated):\\n  - Limit alcohol to <2 drinks per week.\\n  - Review supplements/medications that may stress the liver.\\n  - Maintain healthy weight through balanced diet.\\n- Testosterone estradiol: Total testosterone 337 ng/dL and estradiol 15 pg/mL (lower side):\\n  - Prioritize 7–8 hours quality sleep.\\n  - Include strength training 2–3× weekly.\\n  - Manage stress with exercise, meditation, or hobbies.\\n- Hemoglobin 13.2 g/dL (low for some men): check iron studies and ferritin—schedule CBC + iron panel.\\n- Kidney: Creatinine 1.38 mg/dL with eGFR 64 mL/min — repeat creatinine/eGFR in 3 months, avoid regular NSAIDs, control blood pressure.\\n\\nSleep apnea\\n- STOP‑BANG 5/8 (HIGH RISK). Arrange a sleep medicine referral for a formal sleep study (home or lab). If diagnosed, treatment (CPAP) often improves sleep, energy, and cardiovascular risk.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Start the diet/exercise steps today. Book a nutrition consult if possible.\\n- Repeat labs in 3 months: fasting lipid panel, A1c, fasting glucose, CBC with iron/ferritin, creatinine/eGFR, ALT.\\n- Arrange sleep study promptly (within 4–8 weeks).\\n- We do not recommend a statin now based on 10‑year ASCVD 2.2%; focus on the lifestyle steps above — we will reassess after follow-up labs.\\n\\nYou’re taking the right step by reviewing these results—small, specific changes can make a big difference. If you’d like, we can set up the follow-up orders and a brief visit to make a personalized plan.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 13.2, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "recheckTiming": "4-6 weeks", "interpretation": "Low hemoglobin may indicate anemia.", "recommendation": "Evaluate for iron deficiency or other causes of anemia.", "referenceRange": "13.5-17.5 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 44, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "recheckTiming": "Per standard monitoring schedule", "interpretation": "Hematocrit <50%. Within safe range for TRT continuation.", "recommendation": "Continue TRT with routine monitoring per protocol.", "referenceRange": "<50% (target), 50-52% (caution), 52-54% (high risk), ≥54% (critical)"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 86.8, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 86.8 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 337, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "recheckTiming": "8-12 weeks", "interpretation": "Testosterone below target range but may be adequate if asymptomatic.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: If symptomatic (fatigue, low libido), increase dose 20-30%.", "referenceRange": "400-700 ng/dL (trough target)"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 15, "status": "borderline", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol low (<20 pg/mL). Low estradiol can affect libido and bone health.", "recommendation": "Monitor. Low E2 may indicate need to increase testosterone dose or address over-aromatization blockade.", "referenceRange": "20-60 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 28, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "recheckTiming": "", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<40 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 42, "status": "borderline", "category": "ALT", "recheckTiming": "4-6 weeks", "interpretation": "Mild ALT elevation (<2× ULN).", "recommendation": "Lifestyle counseling (alcohol, weight). Recheck in 4-6 weeks.", "referenceRange": "<40 U/L"}, {"unit": "score", "value": 0.83, "status": "normal", "category": "FIB-4 Score (Liver Fibrosis)", "recheckTiming": "", "interpretation": "FIB-4 score 0.83 indicates LOW risk of advanced fibrosis (F0-F1). Negative predictive value >90%.", "recommendation": "Advanced fibrosis unlikely. Continue lifestyle modifications and routine LFT monitoring. Address underlying cause of LFT elevation.", "referenceRange": "<1.30 low risk, 1.30-2.67 indeterminate, >2.67 high risk"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 64, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "recheckTiming": "", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function (eGFR ≥60).", "recommendation": "Routine care and monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 1.38, "status": "borderline", "category": "Creatinine", "recheckTiming": "2-4 weeks", "interpretation": "Borderline creatinine elevation.", "recommendation": "Encourage hydration. Recheck in 2-4 weeks.", "referenceRange": "≤1.3 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 6, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "recheckTiming": "3-6 months", "interpretation": "A1c in prediabetic range (5.7-6.4%). Increased diabetes risk.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle intervention: diet, exercise, weight loss. Recheck in 3-6 months.", "referenceRange": "<5.7% (normal)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 86, "status": "normal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "LDL within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Maintain heart-healthy lifestyle. Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<130 mg/dL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 253, "status": "borderline", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Triglycerides elevated. Lifestyle and metabolic optimization needed.", "recommendation": "Reduce refined carbs, minimize alcohol. Increase physical activity. Omega-3 supplementation. Repeat in 3 months.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 3.365956003244406, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 3.4% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 2.1657116202000894, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 2.2%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.9842391442920647, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 1.0%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 22.46700899059368, "status": "borderline", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 22.5%, ASCVD 14.0%, Heart Failure 9.7%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 5, "status": "critical", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Follow-up in 1-3 months after sleep study", "interpretation": "HIGH RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (5/8 points)", "recommendation": "High probability of moderate-severe OSA. STRONGLY RECOMMEND sleep study (polysomnography or HSAT). Evaluate for CPAP therapy. Address cardiovascular risk factors. Consider holding or reducing TRT dose if elevated hematocrit.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.71, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Normal thyroid function. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 32, "status": "abnormal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Low HDL (<40 mg/dL). Increased cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle: aerobic exercise ≥150 min/wk, omega-3 fatty acids, limit alcohol. Consider niacin or fibrate if very low.", "referenceRange": "≥40 mg/dL (optimal ≥50)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 16, "status": "normal", "category": "BUN", "interpretation": "BUN within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "7-24 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.4, "status": "normal", "category": "Bilirubin (Total)", "recheckTiming": "", "interpretation": "Bilirubin within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.2 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "M/μL", "value": 5.07, "status": "normal", "category": "RBC Count", "interpretation": "RBC count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "4.5-5.5 M/μL"}, {"unit": "K/μL", "value": 7.3, "status": "normal", "category": "WBC Count", "interpretation": "WBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Normal immune function. Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "4.0-11.0 K/μL"}, {"unit": "K/μL", "value": 239, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelet Count", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Normal clotting function. Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/μL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 115, "status": "borderline", "category": "Glucose (Fasting)", "interpretation": "Impaired fasting glucose (100-125 mg/dL). Prediabetes range.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle intervention: diet, exercise, weight loss. Check A1c. Repeat in 3-6 months.", "referenceRange": "70-99 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mEq/L", "value": 136, "status": "normal", "category": "Sodium", "interpretation": "Sodium within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Normal electrolyte balance. Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "136-145 mEq/L"}, {"unit": "mEq/L", "value": 4.5, "status": "normal", "category": "Potassium", "interpretation": "Potassium within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Normal electrolyte balance. Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "3.5-5.0 mEq/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 55.9, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D adequate but suboptimal (41-59 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 2000 Complex daily to reach optimal range ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 690, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 optimal (400-1000 pg/mL). Adequate for neurological and red blood cell function.", "recommendation": "Continue current intake. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "400-1000 pg/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "of 6 markers positive", "value": 3, "status": "critical", "category": "Insulin Resistance Screening", "recheckTiming": "3-6 months", "interpretation": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: HIGH LIKELIHOOD (3/6 markers positive). Identified phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance, Hepatic (Liver-Driven) Insulin Resistance. Treat according to phenotype-specific protocol.", "recommendation": "Phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance; Hepatic (Liver-Driven) Insulin Resistance. Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose.", "referenceRange": "0-1 Low, 2 Moderate, 3+ High Likelihood"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- Summary: Patient has multiple cardiometabolic risk markers (A1c 6.0%, fasting glucose 115 mg/dL, triglycerides 253 mg/dL, HDL 32 mg/dL) and a high 30-year CVD risk (22.5%). High OSA risk (STOP‑BANG 5) is the most urgent finding. Testosterone total 337 ng/dL is borderline low; estradiol low at 15 pg/mL. Mild ALT elevation (42 U/L) and borderline creatinine (1.38 mg/dL). Hemoglobin mildly low at 13.2 g/dL. Vitamin D is adequate but below optimal. These require coordinated metabolic, sleep, and endocrine follow-up and targeted rechecks per protocol.\\n\\nPriority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL (notify physician immediately)\\n- STOP‑BANG 5 (high probability moderate–severe OSA):\\n  - Action: Notify prescribing physician/clinician immediately. Arrange expedited sleep medicine referral for diagnostic testing (HSAT or in-lab polysomnography) within 1–2 weeks.\\n  - Staff instructions: Place urgent referral order; instruct patient to avoid driving if symptomatic daytime sleepiness; provide CPAP information if OSA confirmed.\\n  - Physician decision required regarding continuation vs temporary reduction/holding of TRT if moderate–severe OSA is confirmed.\\n- Triglycerides 253 mg/dL and HDL 32 mg/dL:\\n  - Action: Notify clinician for risk review and possible escalation of therapy or specialist referral (cardiology/lipid clinic) depending on global risk and symptoms.\\n  - Consider prescription omega‑3 or pharmacologic therapy only after clinician review.\\n\\nURGENT (notify clinician within 48–72 hours)\\n- Creatinine 1.38 mg/dL:\\n  - Action: Notify clinician for medication review (NSAIDs, ACEi/ARB, diuretics, supplements) and plan for repeat BMP in 2–4 weeks.\\n  - Staff: Encourage hydration prior to recheck. Order repeat BMP and urine ACR if indicated.\\n- ALT 42 U/L:\\n  - Action: Notify clinician for review of hepatotoxic meds and alcohol use. If patient on oral hepatotoxic TRT formulations or hepatotoxic meds, escalate for physician review.\\n  - Staff: Order repeat CMP/ALT in 4–6 weeks; consider hepatitis serologies if persistent elevation.\\n- Hemoglobin 13.2 g/dL (low):\\n  - Action: Order iron studies (ferritin, serum iron, TIBC, transferrin saturation) and repeat CBC in 4–6 weeks. Notify clinician for anemia workup plan.\\n\\nROUTINE (address in normal workflow)\\n- Testosterone total 337 ng/dL and estradiol 15 pg/mL:\\n  - Action: Discuss with clinician whether patient is symptomatic. If symptomatic, protocol permits increasing TRT dose 20–30% (see dosing examples below). If patient is on an aromatase inhibitor, discuss reducing/stopping AI given low estradiol.\\n  - Recheck testosterone and estradiol 8–12 weeks after any dose adjustment.\\n- A1c 6.0%, fasting glucose 115 mg/dL:\\n  - Action: Lifestyle interventions; recheck A1c in 3–6 months. Place diabetes prevention counseling order or referral as indicated.\\n- Vitamin D 55.9 ng/mL:\\n  - Action: Continue Metagenics D3 2000 IU daily per protocol; recheck 25‑OH vitamin D in ~3 months.\\n\\nTestosterone dose recommendations (staff-facing, clinician approval required)\\n- Protocol rule: If symptomatic (fatigue, low libido, ED) and provider agrees, increase TRT dose 20–30%.\\n- If current regimen is known, use these examples as templates for provider order:\\n  - If on 100 mg IM q7d → increase to 120–130 mg IM q7d.\\n  - If on 150 mg IM q14d → increase total biweekly dose by 20–30% (to ~180–195 mg q14d) or convert to weekly injections for steadier levels (e.g., 90–100 mg q7d).\\n  - If topical gel 50 mg/day → increase to 60–65 mg/day.\\n- Monitoring after adjustment:\\n  - Recheck total testosterone and estradiol 8–12 weeks post-change (draw morning level, pre-dose if applicable).\\n  - CBC/Hct in 4–6 weeks then q3 months for first year to monitor for polycythemia.\\n- Aromatase inhibitor management:\\n  - If patient is taking an AI and estradiol is low (15 pg/mL) consider dose reduction or discontinuation — instruct staff to flag for clinician review before changing AI use.\\n\\nLifestyle interventions to counsel patients (staff to cover at visit)\\n- Weight reduction and waist circumference reduction (goal ≥5–10% body weight loss if overweight).\\n- Diet: reduce refined carbohydrates and added sugars; adopt Mediterranean-style or low-glycemic diet.\\n- Exercise: ≥150 minutes/week moderate aerobic activity + resistance training 2×/week.\\n- Alcohol: limit intake; advise abstinence or significant reduction until ALT normalizes and triglycerides improve.\\n- Sleep: counsel on sleep hygiene and importance of diagnostic sleep testing; weight loss and positional therapy as adjuncts.\\n- Hydration: encourage adequate oral fluids prior to repeat creatinine testing.\\n- Vitamin D: continue D3 2000 IU daily; counsel on adherence.\\n\\nAdditional testing, monitoring, and referrals to consider\\n- Immediate/near-term orders:\\n  - Sleep study referral (HSAT or polysomnography) — high priority.\\n  - Iron studies (ferritin, iron, TIBC, transferrin saturation) and repeat CBC in 4–6 weeks.\\n  - Repeat CMP (including ALT) in 4–6 weeks.\\n  - Repeat creatinine/BMP in 2–4 weeks; consider urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio.\\n  - Fasting lipid profile and fasting glucose in 3 months (or sooner if clinically indicated).\\n  - A1c repeat in 3–6 months.\\n  - Morning total testosterone and estradiol 8–12 weeks after any dose change.\\n  - Hepatitis panel if ALT elevation persists or risk factors present.\\n- Referrals:\\n  - Sleep medicine for diagnostic testing and CPAP management.\\n  - Primary care and/or cardiology for CVD risk optimization and management plan (statin consideration if indicated by clinician).\\n  - Endocrinology or clinic physician for complex hormone management if multiple adjustments or low estradiol with symptoms.\\n  - Nephrology if creatinine rises or eGFR declines on repeat testing.\\n\\nPhysician notification requirements (staff)\\n- Immediate phone/pager notification:\\n  - STOP‑BANG 5 (request expedited sleep referral and physician decision re: TRT).\\n  - Creatinine 1.38 mg/dL (if rising from baseline or on nephrotoxic meds).\\n  - ALT 42 U/L if on hepatotoxic meds or if clinician prefers immediate review.\\n  - Triglycerides 253 mg/dL + HDL 32 mg/dL (for consideration of lipid therapy).\\n- Electronic message within 24 hours:\\n  - Hemoglobin 13.2 g/dL with iron studies ordered.\\n  - Testosterone 337 ng/dL and estradiol 15 pg/mL with recommendation to consider dose change if symptomatic and review AI use.\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (ordered by timing)\\n- Immediate (within 24–72 hours)\\n  - Notify clinician of STOP‑BANG 5, triglycerides/HDL, borderline creatinine and ALT.\\n  - Place urgent sleep medicine referral for HSAT/polysomnography.\\n  - Review medication list for nephrotoxins/hepatotoxins.\\n- 1–2 weeks\\n  - Schedule and complete sleep testing.\\n  - If sleep study ordered, provide patient instructions and sleep-hygiene counseling.\\n- 2–4 weeks\\n  - Repeat BMP/creatinine.\\n  - Begin hydration counseling.\\n- 4–6 weeks\\n  - Repeat CBC (hemoglobin), CMP (ALT).\\n  - Iron studies results review and clinician-directed anemia management.\\n- 8–12 weeks (if TRT dose changed)\\n  - Recheck morning total testosterone and estradiol; review symptoms.\\n  - CBC/Hct (if dose increased) and monitor for polycythemia.\\n- 3 months\\n  - Repeat fasting lipid panel (triglycerides), fasting glucose; reinforce lifestyle measures.\\n  - Recheck vitamin D if indicated (or at clinician discretion).\\n- 3–6 months\\n  - Repeat A1c.\\n  - Reassess need for cardiology referral or initiation of pharmacotherapy for lipids/glycemic control.\\n- Annual\\n  - Reassess 30‑year CVD risk and routine preventive care.\\n\\nDirect staff talking points for patient education (brief, clinician-reviewed)\\n- Explain high risk for obstructive sleep apnea and the necessity of a sleep study; emphasize possible effects on energy, blood pressure, and heart health.\\n- Counsel on diet, exercise, alcohol reduction, and smoking cessation (if applicable) to improve triglycerides, HDL, A1c, and liver tests.\\n- Advise hydration prior to repeat creatinine and temporary alcohol avoidance until ALT rechecks.\\n- If TRT dose is changed, explain plan for rechecks (testosterone, estradiol, CBC) and what symptoms to report (excessive polycythemia symptoms, worsening sleepiness, edema).\\n\\nLast note for clinicians/staff\\n- Any TRT adjustment or changes to aromatase inhibitor therapy requires explicit clinician order. Monitor labs per protocol and escalate any rising ALT >2× ULN, creatinine trend upward, or new polycythemia promptly to the prescribing clinician.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 7.91, "detail": "Triglycerides 253 / HDL 32 = 7.91", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "6%", "detail": "Prediabetic range", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 42, "detail": "Above optimal range - may indicate hepatic insulin resistance", "positive": true, "threshold": "> 35 U/L (optimal)"}], "likelihood": "high", "phenotypes": [{"key": "visceral_metabolic", "name": "Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Recheck A1c, lipid panel, hs-CRP, ApoB in 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["A1c >= 5.7%", "TG/HDL >= 3", "ALT above optimal range"], "pathophysiology": "Early peripheral + hepatic insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia. Glucose may still be \\"normal.\\" A1c often rises last.", "triggerCriteria": ["A1c >= 5.7%", "TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0", "Central adiposity phenotype", "ALT above optimal range"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests your body may not be responding to insulin as efficiently as it should. Insulin is the hormone that helps move sugar from your bloodstream into your cells for energy.\\n\\nWhen cells become less sensitive to insulin:\\n- Blood sugar slowly rises over time\\n- Triglycerides increase\\n- Inflammation increases\\n- Weight gain around the midsection becomes easier\\n\\nThe good news? This stage is highly reversible with targeted nutrition, strength training, sleep optimization, and - when appropriate - medication support.\\n\\nWe'll focus on improving how your body processes fuel and reducing long-term cardiometabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward nutrition (>= 25-35g/meal)", "Carbohydrate quality + timing education", "Strength training 2-3x weekly", "10-15 min post-meal walking", "Sleep optimization", "Consider: GLP-1 therapy if obesity, appetite dysregulation, A1c >= 5.7, or inflammatory phenotype", "Consider: Metformin if prediabetes or strong family history", "Recheck metabolic panel in 3-6 months"]}, {"key": "hepatic", "name": "Hepatic (Liver-Driven) Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Monitor ALT, TG, ApoB, A1c every 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["ALT above optimal (>35 U/L)", "TG elevated / TG:HDL >= 3", "A1c mildly elevated"], "pathophysiology": "Hepatic insulin resistance leads to increased hepatic glucose output + VLDL overproduction. Often early MASLD (metabolic-associated steatotic liver disease) physiology.", "triggerCriteria": ["ALT above optimal (>= 35 U/L)", "TG elevated or TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB elevated", "A1c may be normal or mildly elevated"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour labs suggest your liver may be under metabolic strain. The liver plays a major role in managing blood sugar and fat metabolism.\\n\\nWhen the liver becomes less responsive to insulin:\\n- Triglycerides rise\\n- Liver enzymes increase\\n- Fat can accumulate in the liver over time\\n\\nThis is common and often related to nutrition, stress, sleep, and weight distribution.\\n\\nThe encouraging part: liver-related metabolic changes are very responsive to targeted lifestyle changes and, if needed, medication support.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Weight reduction goal 5-10% if overweight", "Eliminate sugary beverages", "Alcohol reduction", "Protein-first nutrition", "Consider GLP-1 if weight loss appropriate", "Omega-3 if TG elevated"]}], "positiveCount": 3, "likelihoodLabel": "HIGH LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: HIGH LIKELIHOOD (3/6 markers positive). Identified phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance, Hepatic (Liver-Driven) Insulin Resistance. Treat according to phenotype-specific protocol.", "confirmationTests": "Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose."}}	2026-03-28 01:50:58.531756	1
