Age 36; possible perimenopausal symptoms queried given low mid‑cycle hormones but cycle timing unknown. Patient not currently documented as trying to conceive.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: Not documented in chart at time of lab review (BMI/BP not available).\\n- CBC:\\n  - Hemoglobin 12.3 g/dL (normal)\\n  - Hematocrit 38.1% (normal)\\n  - MCV 87 fL (normal)\\n  - Platelets 286 K/uL (normal)\\n- Iron studies:\\n  - Ferritin 34.6 ng/mL — Borderline low (iron insufficiency; clinic protocol 31–50 ng/mL)\\n  - Serum iron 81 ug/dL (normal)\\n  - TIBC 350 ug/dL (normal)\\n- Metabolic / chemistries:\\n  - Creatinine 0.85 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - eGFR 91 mL/min/1.73 m2 (normal)\\n  - AST 9 U/L (normal)\\n  - ALT 8 U/L (normal)\\n- Glycemic / metabolic screening:\\n  - Hemoglobin A1c 5.4% (normal)\\n  - Insulin resistance screening: 2/6 markers positive — Moderate likelihood of insulin resistance; phenotype: Hormonal / PCOS-type insulin resistance (recommend confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose for HOMA-IR)\\n- Lipid panel / cardiovascular markers:\\n  - HDL cholesterol 34 mg/dL — Low (ABNORMAL)\\n  - LDL cholesterol 99 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - Triglycerides 116 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - Apolipoprotein B 84 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - Lipoprotein(a) 6 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - hs-CRP 0.3 mg/L (normal)\\n  - 10‑yr Total CVD Risk (AHA PREVENT 2023) 0.7% — Low\\n  - 10‑yr ASCVD Risk 0.5% — Low\\n  - 10‑yr HF Risk 0.2% — Low\\n  - 30‑yr CVD Risk 5.9%\\n  - STOP‑BANG Sleep Apnea Risk: 0 points\\n- Hormones / endocrine:\\n  - Estradiol 38 pg/mL (normal per dataset)\\n  - Progesterone 0.24 ng/mL — Borderline/low; interpretation limited without documented cycle phase. If luteal-phase draw, level suggests anovulation.\\n  - FSH 7.29 mIU/mL (normal)\\n  - Total Testosterone 12.7 ng/dL — Borderline low\\n  - Free Testosterone 2 pg/mL (normal)\\n  - SHBG 48.2 nmol/L (normal)\\n  - TSH 0.99 mIU/L (normal)\\n- Vitamins / other:\\n  - 25‑OH Vitamin D 30.4 ng/mL — Borderline low / insufficient for hormone optimization (clinic target ≥60 ng/mL)\\n  - Vitamin B12 782 pg/mL (normal)\\n  - Folate 15.9 ng/mL (normal)\\n\\nASSESSMENT: (numbered problem list)\\n1. Low HDL cholesterol — 34 mg/dL. Clinically significant low HDL increases cardiovascular risk despite low calculated 10‑yr ASCVD risk. Contributing factors may include lifestyle, weight, insulin resistance, medication effects, or genetic factors.\\n2. Insulin resistance — Insulin Resistance Screening moderate likelihood (2/6 markers positive). Phenotype consistent with hormonal/PCOS-type insulin resistance; hyperinsulinemic physiology suspected. Requires confirmation and targeted lifestyle intervention.\\n3. Ferritin low/iron insufficiency — Ferritin 34.6 ng/mL (protocol: 31–50 = iron insufficiency). Hemoglobin/MCV currently normal but may explain fatigue, hair loss, or exercise intolerance and can progress to iron deficiency anemia.\\n4. Vitamin D insufficiency — 25‑OH Vitamin D 30.4 ng/mL; below clinic optimization target (≥60 ng/mL). Replace and recheck.\\n5. Borderline low total testosterone — 12.7 ng/dL. May contribute to fatigue, low libido, or decreased well‑being despite normal free T and SHBG. Consider therapeutic trial only if symptomatic and after informed consent.\\n6. Low/low-normal progesterone — 0.24 ng/mL with unknown cycle phase. If drawn in mid‑luteal phase, this suggests anovulation and possible perimenopausal transition. Correlate with clinical symptoms and menstrual timing.\\n7. General lab review otherwise reassuring — CBC, renal/hepatic labs, lipids overall acceptable (LDL 99 mg/dL), A1c 5.4% normal, B12 and folate adequate.\\n\\nRed flags / physician notification requirements:\\n- No acute red-flag lab values (no critical values) at time of review.\\n- Physician must review and sign before initiating any hormone therapy (testosterone or progesterone).\\n- Urgent provider notification if patient develops symptoms of anemia (syncope, tachycardia, chest pain) or if hemoglobin drops significantly on subsequent labs.\\n\\nPLAN: (specific actions, monitoring, timeline)\\n1. Administrative / immediate:\\n   - Document and obtain date of last menstrual period (LMP) and cycle phase in chart today; request patient to report LMP and any recent bleeding pattern. This is required to accurately interpret progesterone and reproductive hormones. (Action: staff to contact patient today.)\\n   - Flag chart for provider review prior to initiating any hormone therapy.\\n\\n2. Low HDL (34 mg/dL) — problem #1:\\n   - Initiate structured lifestyle intervention now:\\n     - Aerobic exercise ≥150 min/week moderate OR ≥75 min/week vigorous + resistance training 2×/week.\\n     - Mediterranean-style diet: increase monounsaturated fats, fatty fish, fiber; reduce refined carbohydrates and trans fats.\\n     - Weight loss goal if overweight: 5–10% body weight as tolerated.\\n     - Alcohol moderation and smoking cessation counseling if applicable.\\n     - Sleep and stress optimization.\\n   - Review current medications and contraception/HRT status (document if on progestins or other agents that may alter lipids).\\n   - Reassess fasting lipid panel in 3 months after lifestyle intervention (sooner if medication is started).\\n   - Consider pharmacologic therapy only if HDL remains very low with increased ASCVD risk and after PCP/cardiology consultation (niacin rarely used; statin not indicated at current low ASCVD risk).\\n   - Provide patient education today about lifestyle measures and schedule follow-up.\\n\\n3. Insulin resistance — problem #2:\\n   - Order confirmatory labs: fasting glucose and fasting insulin to calculate HOMA‑IR (provider to sign).\\n   - Intensify lifestyle measures focusing on carbohydrate quality, weight management, and physical activity (as above).\\n   - Consider referral to nutritionist or lifestyle medicine program per patient preference.\\n   - Re-evaluate metabolic labs (A1c and fasting glucose/insulin) in 8–12 weeks or sooner based on confirmatory results.\\n\\n4. Iron insufficiency (Ferritin 34.6 ng/mL) — problem #3:\\n   - If patient symptomatic (fatigue, hair loss, RLS, exercise intolerance) or per provider preference, initiate oral iron therapy after provider sign-off:\\n     - Suggested options: Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support per clinic protocol OR elemental oral iron 45–65 mg daily (or 65 mg twice daily) depending on tolerance and provider preference.\\n   - Counseling points: take with vitamin C or orange juice to improve absorption; avoid concurrent calcium, milk, coffee, tea, and some antacids; expect GI side effects (nausea, constipation) and strategies to reduce (take with food, start low-dose and titrate).\\n   - Recheck ferritin and CBC in 8–12 weeks after initiation of therapy. Target ferritin >50 ng/mL for symptom improvement/optimal levels.\\n   - If intolerance or failure to respond → consider IV iron referral (hematology/infusion) per provider.\\n\\n5. Vitamin D insufficiency — problem #4:\\n   - Start vitamin D3 5,000 IU daily (Metagenics D3 5,000 + K suggested) after provider approval.\\n   - Recheck 25‑OH vitamin D in 12 weeks. Target ≥60 ng/mL per clinic protocol.\\n   - If patient has history of hypercalcemia or takes high-dose calcium supplements, notify provider before starting.\\n\\n6. Low/borderline total testosterone — problem #5:\\n   - Do not initiate testosterone without provider discussion and signed orders.\\n   - If patient reports clinically relevant symptoms and provider approves a trial, suggested starting options (provider to choose):\\n     - Transdermal: start low-dose topical testosterone equivalent to 300 mcg/day (0.3 mg/day) using clinic formulary or compounded preparation. Reassess and adjust per protocol.\\n     - Injectable option (if chosen): testosterone cypionate 25 mg subcutaneous (or IM) every 1–2 weeks (provider decision).\\n   - Monitoring if started:\\n     - Recheck total testosterone 8–12 weeks after initiation (timing depends on formulation: for topical obtain level per clinic guidance 2–4 hours post-application; for injections obtain mid-interval trough).\\n     - Baseline and periodic CBC, AST/ALT, lipid panel, fasting glucose/A1c; repeat CBC and LFTs at 3 months, then every 6–12 months.\\n     - Monitor for virilization (voice changes, acne, hirsutism), mood changes, and polycythemia (notify MD if Hgb rises >1.5 g/dL or >16 g/dL).\\n   - If no symptomatic benefit and low testosterone persists, consider dose titration per protocol after MD review.\\n\\n7. Low progesterone / possible anovulation / perimenopause — problem #6:\\n   - Obtain documented cycle timing. If assessing ovulatory status, order repeat progesterone in mid‑luteal phase (≈LH+7 or ~7 days before expected menses).\\n   - If patient is symptomatic (irregular menses, vasomotor symptoms, sleep disturbance) and provider elects to treat empirically, consider luteal-phase progesterone therapy per clinic protocol after MD approval.\\n   - Consider referral to Gynecology/Endocrinology if fertility desired or complex perimenopausal management required.\\n\\n8. Other testing and referrals:\\n   - Order fasting insulin + fasting glucose for HOMA‑IR calculation to confirm insulin resistance (provider sign).\\n   - Repeat ferritin + CBC in 8–12 weeks if iron therapy started.\\n   - Repeat 25‑OH vitamin D in 12 weeks after initiating D3 5,000 IU.\\n   - Repeat total testosterone 8–12 weeks if therapy is started or if baseline confirmation is needed prior to initiation.\\n   - Consider cardiology or PCP consultation if HDL remains very low despite lifestyle change or if pharmacotherapy is being considered.\\n   - Consider hematology referral for refractory iron deficiency or IV iron consideration.\\n   - Consider DEXA if additional bone risk factors or ongoing perimenopausal status.\\n\\n9. Supplements (per clinic AI recommendations; initiate only after provider review/sign-off):\\n   - High-priority: GlutaClear® 2 capsules daily; Hemagenics® Red Blood Cell Support 1 tablet twice daily with meals (if Hemagenics chosen for iron support).\\n   - Medium-priority: D3 5,000 IU + K daily; Magtein® Magnesium L‑Threonate 3 capsules daily (divided doses); AdvaClear® 2 capsules twice daily; NutraGems® CoQ10 300 1 chewable gel daily; UltraFlora® Healthy Weight with Akkermansia 1 capsule daily.\\n   - Staff to provide patient education regarding purpose, expected benefits, and potential side effects. Provider to approve supplements.\\n\\n10. Patient education and follow-up:\\n   - Reviewed lab results with patient today; discussed significance of low HDL, iron insufficiency, vitamin D insufficiency, possible insulin resistance, and borderline testosterone/progesterone findings.\\n   - Provided counseling on lifestyle interventions (diet, exercise, weight management, sleep, alcohol moderation) and importance of documenting LMP for hormone interpretation.\\n   - Follow-up plan:\\n     - Immediate: staff to document LMP and notify provider for hormone therapy consideration (today).\\n     - Within 1–2 weeks: provider to sign orders for any initiated therapies (iron, vitamin D, further testing). Start vitamin D 5,000 IU daily and iron if provider approves and patient symptomatic.\\n     - 8–12 weeks: recheck ferritin + CBC; recheck total testosterone if therapy started.\\n     - 12 weeks: recheck 25‑OH vitamin D and reassess fasting lipids after lifestyle interventions.\\n     - Ongoing: monitoring every 3 months initially for symptomatic management and lab checks; extend per provider.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\nDATE: 03/30/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": true, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHD