 support); Low energy (biotransformation burden); Brain fog (toxin clearance burden)", "phenotypes": ["Oxidative Stress Burden", "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement supports your liver's natural detoxification processes. Your results suggest your body could benefit from help processing hormones, metabolic byproducts, and other compounds. It provides nutrients that support both phases of your liver's detox pathways.", "supportingFindings": ["Oxidative stress burden (liver biotransformation support)", "Insulin resistance / metabolic dysfunction (hepatic metabolic support)", "Low energy (biotransformation burden)", "Brain fog (toxin clearance burden)"]}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Essential for patients on statins.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "HDL 34 mg/dL (low); Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)", "phenotypes": ["Oxidative Stress Burden"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["HDL 34 mg/dL (low)", "Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Healthy Weight with Akkermansia", "caution": "Contains Akkermansia muciniphila and Bifidobacterium lactis B420 for metabolic and gut barrier support. Refrigerate after opening for maximum potency.", "category": "metabolic", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "UltraFlora Healthy Weight with Akkermansia provides targeted probiotic support for the gut-metabolic axis. Akkermansia muciniphila supports gut barrier integrity, metabolic health, and healthy weight management. Indicated for insulin resistance, metabolic syndrome features, visceral adiposity, and weight loss resistance.", "indication": "Insulin resistance / visceral adiposity phenotype (gut-metabolic axis support); SHBG 48.2 nmol/L (low, metabolic marker)", "phenotypes": ["Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity"], "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This specialized probiotic contains Akkermansia, a beneficial gut bacteria linked to healthy metabolism and weight management. It supports your gut barrier, helps manage metabolic inflammation, and works with your body's natural weight regulation systems.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance / visceral adiposity phenotype (gut-metabolic axis support)", "SHBG 48.2 nmol/L (low, metabolic marker)"]}, {"dose": "1 soft chew during times of stress", "name": "HerWellness™ Rapid Stress Relief", "caution": "Fast-acting L-Theanine and Lactium formula. Non-drowsy. Promotes calm within 1 hour. Contains milk.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "low", "rationale": "HerWellness Rapid Stress Relief provides fast-acting stress support with L-Theanine (200mg) and Lactium. Promotes calm within 1 hour without drowsiness.", "indication": "Anxiety reported", "phenotypes": ["Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation"], "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This fast-acting supplement helps your body respond to stress more calmly. It promotes relaxation within about an hour and can be taken as needed without causing drowsiness.", "supportingFindings": ["Anxiety reported"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 6, "apoBValue": 84, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "normal", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 0.49515518102942063, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": false}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\nYour most important findings are a low HDL cholesterol (34 mg/dL), mild iron insufficiency (ferritin 34.6 ng/mL), vitamin D just below the optimal range (30.4 ng/mL), and a low total testosterone (12.7 ng/dL). Your overall 10-year cardiovascular risk is low: 0.7% total CVD risk and 0.5% ASCVD risk, which is good news. Thyroid function (TSH 0.99 mIU/L) and basic blood counts (hemoglobin 12.3 g/dL, hematocrit 38.1%) are normal.\\n\\nPositive findings (specific values)\\n- LDL cholesterol 99 mg/dL (optimal)\\n- Triglycerides 116 mg/dL (normal)\\n- hs-CRP 0.3 mg/L (low inflammation)\\n- TSH 0.99 mIU/L (euthyroid)\\n- Hemoglobin 12.3 g/dL, hematocrit 38.1%, MCV 87 fL (no anemia)\\n- Vitamin B12 782 pg/mL, folate 15.9 ng/mL (adequate)\\n\\nAreas to improve (exact values + concrete actions)\\n1) Low HDL: HDL 34 mg/dL (goal for women is >40 mg/dL)\\n- Add 25–30 g fiber daily: try oatmeal for breakfast, beans or lentils at lunch, and vegetables at dinner.\\n- Eat fatty fish 2–3 servings per week (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n- Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter and high-fat cheeses.\\n- Exercise daily: 30 minutes brisk walking or equivalent; include 2 strength sessions/week.\\n- Recheck a lipid panel in 3 months.\\n\\n2) Iron (ferritin 34.6 ng/mL; optimal >50 for symptom prevention and fertility/bone health)\\n- Eat iron-rich foods: 2–3 servings red meat/week or plant sources like spinach, beans, lentils.\\n- Add vitamin C with iron meals (orange, bell pepper) to improve absorption.\\n- Consider an iron supplement (e.g., ferrous sulfate 65 mg elemental iron daily) if dietary steps don’t raise ferritin — discuss with your clinician before starting.\\n- Recheck ferritin and hemoglobin in 8–12 weeks after treatment.\\n\\n3) Vitamin D (25-OH 30.4 ng/mL; insufficient)\\n- Start vitamin D3 1,000–2,000 IU daily and get 15 minutes sunlight on arms/legs several times weekly.\\n- Ensure calcium 1,000–1,200 mg/day from diet or supplements for bone health.\\n- Recheck 25-OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks.\\n\\n4) Sex hormones and perimenopause: Total testosterone 12.7 ng/dL (low); progesterone 0.24 ng/mL with cycle phase not documented; FSH 7.29 mIU/mL, estradiol 38 pg/mL.\\n- Interpretation depends on menstrual cycle day. If you are tracking cycles, repeat progesterone mid-luteal (~7 days before expected period) to check ovulation.\\n- Lifestyle to support hormones: prioritize 7–8 hours sleep, strength training 2–3x/week, and stress management (meditation, walking, hobbies).\\n- Discuss symptoms (mood, libido, irregular periods) with your clinician — they can review whether further testing or treatment is helpful.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Start lifestyle changes today (diet, exercise, vitamin D).\\n- Consider iron supplement if dietary changes are insufficient — recheck ferritin and Hb in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Repeat lipid panel in 3 months to monitor HDL.\\n- If you want hormone follow-up, repeat progesterone timed to your cycle next month or schedule a visit to discuss perimenopause symptoms and possible treatment.\\nIf you’d like, we can help arrange supplements, a nutrition plan, or a follow-up appointment. You’re doing well in many areas — these targeted steps can improve energy, bone health, and long-term heart health.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 12.3, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 38.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 87, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 87 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 286, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 38, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.24, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 7.29, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 12.7, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Low testosterone.", "recommendation": "May contribute to low libido or fatigue. Consider HRT discussion.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 0.99, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 34.6, "status": "borderline", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Iron insufficiency (ferritin 31-50). Optimal is >50.", "recommendation": "Provider protocol: Metagenics Hemagenics if symptomatic (fatigue, hair loss, restless legs, exercise intolerance).", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 30.4, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D insufficient (31-40 ng/mL). Suboptimal for hormone optimization.", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 5,000 + K daily. Target ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 782, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.4, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 99, "status": "normal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 34, "status": "abnormal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Low HDL - increased cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Increase aerobic exercise. Consider niacin if very low.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 116, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.7390179901511467, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 0.7% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.49515518102942063, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 0.5%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.17526633522816829, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.2%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.925744643039798, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 5.9%, ASCVD 3.6%, Heart Failure 1.7%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 9, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 8, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 91, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.85, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 84, "status": "normal", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Apo B at optimal level.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 6, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 350, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 81, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 15.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 0.3, "status": "normal", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Low cardiovascular inflammation risk.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.24, "status": "borderline", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Low Progesterone (Phase Not Documented)", "interpretation": "Progesterone is 0.24 ng/mL. Without documented cycle phase or sufficient estradiol/FSH context to identify a specific pattern, interpretation is limited. If drawn in the luteal phase, this level would indicate anovulation — a perimenopause marker.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Document cycle phase for accurate interpretation. If the patient is mid-cycle or in the luteal phase, this suggests anovulation. Consider symptoms: sleep disruption, anxiety, irregular cycles. If symptomatic, empiric progesterone (100 mg nightly) is reasonable. PATIENT EDUCATION: Knowing where you are in your menstrual cycle helps us fully interpret this result. Please note when your last period started so we can better understand your hormone picture.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase; <5 ng/mL suggests anovulation if luteal"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 48.2, "status": "normal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 48.2 nmol/L is within normal range. SHBG regulates bioavailable sex hormones by binding testosterone and estradiol.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 2, "status": "normal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 2 pg/mL is within normal functional range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "of 6 markers positive", "value": 2, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Insulin Resistance Screening", "recheckTiming": "3-6 months", "interpretation": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive). This combination points toward hyperinsulinemia physiology. Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR).", "recommendation": "Phenotype(s): Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance. Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose.", "referenceRange": "0-1 Low, 2 Moderate, 3+ High Likelihood"}], "aiRecommendations": "Clinical context reminder for staff: these recommendations are intended for clinician review and action only. Do not provide to patient without provider sign-off. Note cycle-phase documentation is missing and is required for accurate interpretation of progesterone and some reproductive hormones.\\n\\n1) Overall clinical assessment (synthesis)\\n- Low borderline total testosterone: 12.7 ng/dL — may contribute to symptoms such as low libido, decreased energy, or reduced well‑being despite normal free T and SHBG. Consider a therapeutic trial of testosterone if symptomatic and after informed consent.\\n- Ferritin 34.6 ng/mL — consistent with iron insufficiency (protocol range 31–50 ng/mL). Hemoglobin/MCV are normal now, but low ferritin may explain fatigue, hair loss, RLS, or exercise intolerance and can progress to iron deficiency anemia.\\n- Vitamin D 25‑OH 30.4 ng/mL — insufficient for hormone optimization per clinic protocol (target ≥60 ng/mL).\\n- HDL 34 mg/dL — low, increases cardiovascular risk even with otherwise normal lipid panel; address lifestyle and consider medical therapy if persistent or if additional risk factors present.\\n- Progesterone 0.24 ng/mL — interpretation limited by unknown cycle phase. If this sample was drawn in the luteal window, this level suggests anovulation (perimenopausal pattern). If patient is symptomatic, empiric luteal progesterone therapy is reasonable per provider protocol.\\n\\n2) Priority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL\\n- Notify MD before initiating any hormone therapy (testosterone or progesterone). Provider must review and sign orders.\\n- If patient has signs/symptoms of acute anemia (syncope, tachycardia, chest pain) or hemoglobin drop on record → urgent provider notification and immediate evaluation.\\n\\nURGENT (actions to complete within 1–2 weeks)\\n- Document menstrual cycle timing in the chart and ask patient to report the date of her last menstrual period to correctly interpret progesterone and potentially repeat progesterone testing in the mid‑luteal phase (≈7 days before expected menses) if clinically indicated.\\n- If patient is symptomatic from iron insufficiency (fatigue, hair loss, RLS, exercise intolerance) — initiate iron therapy per provider order (see below) and schedule recheck ferritin/CBC.\\n\\nROUTINE (standard follow-up)\\n- Implement vitamin D replacement per protocol and plan recheck at 12 weeks.\\n- Start lifestyle interventions for dyslipidemia now and re-evaluate lipids in 3 months.\\n- If initiating testosterone/progesterone, establish monitoring schedule (see monitoring below).\\n\\n3) Testosterone recommendations (if applicable)\\n- Indication: consider only if patient reports clinically relevant symptoms (low libido, fatigue, reduced quality of life) after informed discussion and provider approval.\\n- Suggested starting options (select per clinic standard and provider preference):\\n  - Preferred topical/transdermal option (lower risk/adjustable): start low-dose transdermal testosterone equivalent to 300 mcg/day (0.3 mg/day). Use compounded gel/cream or clinic formulary product per protocol. OR\\n  - Injection option (if clinic uses parenteral protocol): start testosterone cypionate 25 mg subcutaneous (or intramuscular) every 1–2 weeks (provider decision).\\n- Monitoring and targets:\\n  - Recheck total testosterone 8–12 weeks after initiation (for topical measure steady‑state trough, typically morning level 2–4 hours post‑application per clinic protocol; for injections obtain blood at mid‑interval trough per injection schedule). Aim for mid‑normal physiologic female total testosterone range for age (document clinic’s numeric target range) — do not exceed upper female reference limit.\\n  - Baseline and periodic monitoring: baseline CBC, LFTs (AST/ALT), lipid panel, fasting glucose/HbA1c; repeat CBC and LFTs at 3 months, then every 6–12 months. Monitor for virilization (voice changes, acne, hirsutism), mood changes, and polycythemia (notify provider if Hgb rises >1.5 g/dL or >16 g/dL).\\n- Dose adjustments:\\n  - If symptomatic improvement absent and testosterone remains below target without adverse effects, escalate per protocol (increase by small increments, e.g., +50% of initial topical dose) after MD review.\\n  - If signs of excess androgenicity or testosterone > upper limit, reduce dose or stop and notify MD immediately.\\n\\n4) Ferritin/iron management\\n- If symptomatic or per provider order: start Metagenics Hemagenics per clinic protocol (initiate only after provider sign-off). If clinic does not use Hemagenics, initiate oral elemental iron 45–65 mg daily (or 65 mg twice daily) for symptomatic iron insufficiency unless contraindicated.\\n- Administration counseling for staff to review with patient: take iron with vitamin C or orange juice for better absorption; avoid taking with calcium, milk, coffee, tea, and some antacids; advise about common GI side effects and strategies to minimize (take with food, start low and titrate).\\n- Recheck ferritin and CBC in 8–12 weeks after starting therapy. Target ferritin >50 ng/mL for symptom resolution/optimal levels.\\n- If intolerance to oral iron or ferritin decreases/worsens despite adherence, consider IV iron referral (hematology or infusion center) per provider order.\\n\\n5) Vitamin D management\\n- Start Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily per protocol after provider approval.\\n- Recheck 25‑OH vitamin D in 12 weeks. Target ≥60 ng/mL.\\n- If patient is taking calcium supplements or has hypercalcemia risk factors, notify provider prior to starting high‑dose D3.\\n\\n6) HDL and cardiovascular risk management\\n- Immediate actions:\\n  - Counsel patient on structured lifestyle interventions (see section 7).\\n  - Evaluate current medications: confirm no drugs are lowering HDL (e.g., certain beta blockers, progestins); document contraception/HRT status as they affect lipids.\\n- Consider pharmacologic therapy:\\n  - Discuss niacin only if persistent very low HDL with elevated ASCVD risk and after cardiology/PCP consultation. Niacin adverse effects (flushing, glucose intolerance, hepatic injury) require PCP/cardiology oversight.\\n- Reassess fasting lipid panel in 3 months after lifestyle interventions (and sooner if therapy initiated).\\n\\n7) Lifestyle interventions to recommend to patient (staff to counsel)\\n- Exercise: prescribe structured aerobic exercise ≥150 minutes/week moderate‑intensity OR ≥75 minutes/week vigorous plus resistance training 2×/week.\\n- Diet: recommend Mediterranean-style diet, increase monounsaturated fats, fatty fish (omega‑3s), whole grains, fiber; reduce refined carbohydrates and trans fats.\\n- Smoking cessation if applicable.\\n- Weight loss: aim for 5–10% loss if overweight — improves HDL and hormonal symptoms.\\n- Sleep optimization and stress management — relevant for perimenopausal symptoms and progesterone therapy response.\\n- Alcohol moderation — excessive alcohol lowers HDL quality and affects hormones.\\n\\n8) Additional testing or referrals to consider\\n- Repeat progesterone with documented cycle phase: request mid‑luteal (≈day LH+7 or ~7 days before menses) progesterone if evaluating ovulatory status.\\n- Repeat ferritin + CBC in 8–12 weeks after iron initiation.\\n- Repeat vitamin D in 12 weeks after D3 start.\\n- Repeat total testosterone 8–12 weeks after initiation or before initiating therapy to confirm baseline if starting treatment.\\n- Consider referral to:\\n  - Gynecology/Endocrinology for complex perimenopausal/androgen-related issues or if considering long‑term testosterone therapy.\\n  - Cardiology/Primary care for low HDL with other CV risk factors or if considering niacin/statin therapy.\\n  - Hematology for refractory iron deficiency or consideration of IV iron.\\n  - Fertility specialist if patient is attempting conception and progesterone/anovulation suspected.\\n- Consider DEXA scan if perimenopausal and any low bone density risk factors.\\n\\n9) Physician notification requirements (when to call MD)\\n- Prior to starting testosterone or progesterone therapy (requires provider order).\\n- If ferritin falls or hemoglobin drops significantly, or patient develops signs of anemia.\\n- If vitamin D >100 ng/mL or if hypercalcemia suspected.\\n- If testosterone therapy leads to virilizing signs, ALT/AST rise >2× ULN, or Hgb rises above 16 g/dL (or >1.5 g/dL increase).\\n- If HDL remains very low despite lifestyle measures and patient has intermediate/high ASCVD risk — discuss lipid therapy.\\n\\n10) Summary of next steps and timeline (action checklist)\\n- Immediately:\\n  - Document last menstrual period/cycle phase in chart.\\n  - Flag chart for provider review to consider testosterone/progesterone therapy.\\n- Within 1–2 weeks:\\n  - If symptomatic for iron insufficiency, initiate iron therapy per provider order.\\n  - Start vitamin D 5,000 IU daily per provider order.\\n  - Provide lifestyle counseling materials and schedule follow-up visit.\\n- 8–12 weeks:\\n  - Recheck ferritin + CBC.\\n  - Recheck total testosterone if therapy started (8–12 weeks) or earlier per product-specific timing.\\n- 12 weeks:\\n  - Recheck vitamin D level.\\n  - Reassess lipid panel after lifestyle intervention.\\n- Ongoing:\\n  - Monitor symptoms, side effects, and labs every 3 months initially, then extend interval per provider.\\n  - Repeat mid‑luteal progesterone with cycle documentation if ovulatory status clarification required.\\n\\nPatient education points for clinic staff to cover (brief)\\n- Ask and document date of last menstrual period.\\n- Explain purpose of iron and vitamin D supplementation, expected side effects, and when to stop and call.\\n- Explain that testosterone/progesterone require provider approval and monitoring; review possible benefits and adverse effects (virilization for testosterone; sedation/mood changes for progesterone).\\n- Lifestyle changes and timeline for expected lab follow-up.\\n\\nIf you want, I can draft specific order templates (medication orders, monitoring labs, and patient education scripts) for provider sign‑off based on the clinic’s formulary/protocol.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 3.41, "detail": "Triglycerides 116 / HDL 34 = 3.41", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5.4%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 48.2, "detail": "Low - hyperinsulinemia suppresses SHBG", "positive": true, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 8, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "84 mg/dL", "detail": "Normal atherogenic particle count", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "0.3 mg/L", "detail": "Low inflammatory burden", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "moderate", "phenotypes": [{"key": "hormonal_pcos", "name": "Hormonal / PCOS-Type Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Monitor SHBG, androgens, A1c every 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["SHBG low (<50)", "TG/HDL borderline-high"], "pathophysiology": "Hyperinsulinemia suppresses SHBG and increases ovarian androgen production.", "triggerCriteria": ["Low SHBG (<50; strong if <30)", "Elevated total or free testosterone", "Irregular cycles", "A1c may still be normal", "TG/HDL may be borderline-high"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests insulin may be influencing your hormone balance.\\n\\nWhen insulin levels run high:\\n- The body makes less SHBG (a protective hormone-binding protein)\\n- Androgen levels can rise\\n- Cycles may become irregular\\n\\nThis pattern is common in PCOS and other hormone imbalance states.\\n\\nImproving insulin sensitivity often helps restore hormonal balance and reduce long-term metabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward, lower refined carb approach", "Strength training", "Consider metformin if cycles irregular or A1c trending", "Consider GLP-1 if obesity phenotype present", "Monitor SHBG, androgens, A1c"]}], "positiveCount": 2, "likelihoodLabel": "MODERATE LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive). This combination points toward hyperinsulinemia physiology. Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR).", "confirmationTests": "Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose."}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Metabolic pattern consistent with insulin resistance and/or visceral adiposity. This drives inflammation, hormonal imbalance, and cardiovascular risk.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance screening: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive)", "TG:HDL ratio 3.4 (≥3.0)", "HDL 34 mg/dL (low)", "SHBG 48.2 nmol/L (low, associated with insulin resistance)"]}, {"name": "Oxidative Stress Burden", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Multiple markers suggest increased oxidative stress and cellular burden. Antioxidant and glutathione support may help reduce oxidative damage and support cellular energy production.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance pattern (metabolic oxidative driver)", "Low energy / fatigue (possible mitochondrial burden)", "Brain fog reported (possible oxidative CNS burden)"]}, {"name": "Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Pattern consistent with HPA-axis stress and cortisol dysregulation. Chronic stress depletes DHEA-S, disrupts sleep, and impairs cognitive and emotional function.", "supportingFindings": ["Anxiety reported", "Low energy / fatigue reported", "Brain fog reported"]}, {"name": "Iron Deficiency", "confidence": "low", "description": "Iron stores are depleted or suboptimal. Iron is essential for energy, cognitive function, hair health, and oxygen transport.", "supportingFindings": ["Ferritin 34.6 ng/mL (suboptimal ≤50)", "Low energy/fatigue reported"]}]}	2026-03-30 13:34:04.784896	1
