ce / impaired clearance phenotype detected"]}, {"dose": "1 soft chew during times of stress", "name": "HerWellness™ Rapid Stress Relief", "caution": "Fast-acting L-Theanine and Lactium formula. Non-drowsy. Promotes calm within 1 hour. Contains milk.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "low", "rationale": "HerWellness Rapid Stress Relief provides fast-acting stress support with L-Theanine (200mg) and Lactium. Promotes calm within 1 hour without drowsiness.", "indication": "Anxiety reported", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This fast-acting supplement helps your body respond to stress more calmly. It promotes relaxation within about an hour and can be taken as needed without causing drowsiness.", "supportingFindings": ["Anxiety reported"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "2-4 weeks (urgent values present)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\n- Your labs show three main issues to address: very low iron stores (ferritin 8.8 ng/mL), an “estrogen dominance” pattern (estradiol 181 pg/mL with low progesterone 0.6 ng/mL suggesting ovulatory dysfunction), and low total testosterone (22.1 ng/dL) despite ongoing HRT. Vitamin D is borderline (31.3 ng/mL). Your complete blood count is still within the normal range.\\n\\nPositive findings (exact values)\\n- Hemoglobin 12.0 g/dL (normal)\\n- Hematocrit 37.2% (normal)\\n- MCV 89.2 fL (normal-sized red blood cells)\\n- Platelets 279 K/uL (normal)\\n- FSH 11.9 mIU/mL\\n- SHBG 43.8 nmol/L\\n- Free testosterone 3 pg/mL\\n- STOP-BANG sleep apnea risk 0/8 (LOW risk)\\n\\nAreas to improve (values + concrete actions)\\n1. Ferritin 8.8 ng/mL (severely depleted)\\n   - Action: Discuss starting oral iron with your clinician (common option: ferrous sulfate 325 mg = ~65 mg elemental iron). Current best practice often uses every‑other‑day dosing to improve absorption and tolerance (e.g., one tablet every other day) or daily if recommended by your clinician.\\n   - Take iron with vitamin C (a glass of orange juice) and avoid tea/coffee or calcium within 1–2 hours of doses.\\n   - Eat iron-rich foods: 2–3 servings/week of lean red meat or iron-fortified cereals, add beans and dark leafy greens, and include vitamin C at meals.\\n   - If you have GI symptoms or can't tolerate oral iron, ask about IV iron. Repeat ferritin and CBC in 8–12 weeks after starting treatment. Also discuss testing for GI blood loss if no clear cause.\\n\\n2. Estradiol 181 pg/mL and Progesterone 0.6 ng/mL — “estrogen dominance”\\n   - Note cycle timing: if you are in the luteal phase we expect progesterone to be much higher; low progesterone suggests anovulatory cycles.\\n   - Action: Track ovulation (LH kits or basal body temp) and bring cycle information to your provider. Lifestyle: improve sleep hygiene (7–8 hrs nightly), reduce late-night caffeine/alcohol, stress reduction (meditation, yoga), and regular moderate exercise. Discuss with your provider about cyclic/progestogen therapy to balance estrogen and reduce symptoms (heavy/irregular flow, breast tenderness, insomnia).\\n\\n3. Testosterone (total) 22.1 ng/dL (low)\\n   - Action: Prioritize 7–8 hours sleep, start strength training 2–3x/week, and use stress management (walking, breathing exercises). Discuss testosterone assessment/therapy options with your clinician if symptoms persist.\\n\\n4. Vitamin D 25‑OH 31.3 ng/mL (insufficient)\\n   - Action: Start vitamin D3 2000 IU daily and recheck level in 8–12 weeks; add 10–20 minutes sunlight and fatty fish 1–2x/week.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- These critical findings require physician review before any medication changes. Schedule a visit within the next 2–4 weeks to review iron treatment and hormone adjustments. Recheck ferritin, CBC, and vitamin D in 8–12 weeks after starting therapy, and consider mid‑luteal progesterone testing if you are tracking ovulation. If you develop dizziness, rapid heartbeat, passage of large clots, or worsening symptoms, contact the clinic sooner.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 12, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 37.2, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 89.2, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 89.2 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 279, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 181, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol elevated above typical HRT range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Evaluate estrogen dose. Monitor for estrogen excess symptoms.", "referenceRange": "60-100 pg/mL (HRT goal, >40 for bone)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.6, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone below HRT therapeutic range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase progesterone dose. Target 8-10 ng/mL for optimal endometrial protection.", "referenceRange": "8-10 ng/mL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 11.9, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 22.1, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Low testosterone despite HRT.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase testosterone dose. Target 75-125 ng/dL for optimal results.", "referenceRange": "75-125 ng/dL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 8.8, "status": "critical", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Severely depleted iron stores without anemia.", "recommendation": "Provider protocol: Metagenics Hemagenics OR Prescription 65mg Elemental Iron (take every other day, on empty stomach, avoid caffeine and dairy 2 hours before/after). Evaluate for blood loss.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 31.3, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D insufficient (31-40 ng/mL). Suboptimal for hormone optimization.", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 5,000 + K daily. Target ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 181, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Estrogen Dominance Pattern", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 181 pg/mL with progesterone of only 0.6 ng/mL represents estrogen dominance. This is not excess estrogen production — it reflects ovulatory dysfunction where estrogen continues but ovulation (and thus progesterone) fails. Associated symptoms include insomnia or 3am waking, anxiety, breast tenderness, migraines, and heavy or irregular cycles.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Add progesterone first — do not attempt to suppress estrogen. Micronized progesterone 100–200 mg nightly provides opposition and symptom relief. Avoid language about \\"too much estrogen\\" — the issue is inadequate progesterone from anovulatory cycles. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen is in the higher range, but the real issue is that progesterone hasn't kept up. This happens when ovulation becomes irregular in perimenopause. Adding progesterone will help balance things out and relieve symptoms like heavy periods, breast tenderness, and mood swings.", "referenceRange": "E2 >150 with P4 <5 indicates unopposed estrogen"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 43.8, "status": "normal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 43.8 nmol/L is within normal range. SHBG regulates bioavailable sex hormones by binding testosterone and estradiol.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 3, "status": "normal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 3 pg/mL is within normal functional range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}], "aiRecommendations": "Unable to generate recommendations. Please review lab findings manually.", "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Iron Deficiency", "confidence": "high", "description": "Iron stores are depleted or suboptimal. Iron is essential for energy, cognitive function, hair health, and oxygen transport.", "supportingFindings": ["Ferritin 8.8 ng/mL (severely depleted ≤20)", "Low energy/fatigue reported"]}, {"name": "Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Lab pattern consistent with relative estrogen excess or impaired estrogen clearance. Even without reported symptoms, supporting liver detoxification and estrogen metabolism pathways may be beneficial.", "supportingFindings": ["Estradiol:Progesterone imbalance (E2 181, P4 0.6)", "Progesterone 0.6 ng/mL (very low)"]}]}	2026-03-30 13:42:25.085154	1
