"adjustedCategory": "reclassified_upward", "clinicalGuidance": "Lp(a) 71 mg/dL - RISK ENHANCER (genetic risk factor). RECLASSIFIED TO BORDERLINE RISK: Risk enhancer present. Shared decision-making regarding statin therapy.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "cacRecommendation": "CAC reasonable especially with strong family history and risk enhancement present.", "hasBorderlineApoB": true}, "cacStatinRec": {"lpaWarning": "Elevated Lp(a) of 71 mg/dL is a genetic cardiovascular risk marker. This level is associated with increased ASCVD risk independent of LDL. Since Lp(a) cannot be significantly lowered, focus on aggressive LDL/ApoB reduction and lifestyle optimization.", "statinDiscussion": {"strength": "generally_recommended", "indicated": true, "rationale": "ApoB ≥90 mg/dL (104 mg/dL) with risk enhancers (elevated Lp(a), elevated ApoB). Statin discussion recommended."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "strongly_recommend", "rationale": "Elevated Lp(a) of 71 mg/dL is a genetic risk marker. CAC strongly recommended to assess arterial calcium burden and inform treatment intensity.", "recommended": true}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall summary (plain language)\\nYour labs show some clear strengths and a few areas to address. Notable results: Vitamin D is low at 24.7 ng/mL, lipoprotein(a) is elevated at 71 mg/dL, and estradiol is low at 28 pg/mL with night sweats—consistent with a low-estrogen (late perimenopause/postmenopause) pattern. Your 10-year cardiovascular risk is low at 0.8%, but the high Lp(a) is a “risk enhancer” worth discussing with your clinician.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Hemoglobin 13.1 g/dL and hematocrit 40.6% are normal.\\n- Ferritin 138 ng/mL shows good iron stores.\\n- Hemoglobin A1c 5.1% (normal glucose control).\\n- HDL 67 mg/dL (protective), triglycerides 95 mg/dL, AST 15 U/L, ALT 16 U/L, TSH 1.33 mIU/L, Vitamin B12 603 pg/mL, eGFR 63 mL/min, hs-CRP 0.7 mg/L are all in healthy ranges.\\n\\nAreas to improve (with specific values and actions)\\n1) Vitamin D deficiency: 25-OH = 24.7 ng/mL (suboptimal for bone and hormone health).\\n   - Start Vitamin D3 1,000–2,000 IU daily (discuss exact dose with your provider).\\n   - Eat vitamin D–rich foods (fatty fish, fortified milk) and get 10–20 minutes sunlight on arms/face several times weekly.\\n   - Do weight-bearing exercise (walking, stair climbing, strength training 3x/week) and aim for calcium 1,000–1,200 mg/day from diet.\\n   - Recheck 25-OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks.\\n\\n2) Cholesterol pattern: LDL 149 mg/dL, ApoB 104 mg/dL (borderline), Lp(a) 71 mg/dL (elevated risk enhancer).\\n   - Lifestyle (start now): add 25–30 g fiber daily (oatmeal breakfast, beans at lunch, vegetables at dinner), include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines), reduce saturated fat (choose lean meats, limit butter and cheese), and do 30 minutes brisk walking daily.\\n   - Your 10‑year ASCVD risk is 0.6% (very low), so a statin is generally not recommended now; recheck fasting lipids in 3 months after lifestyle changes.\\n   - Discuss Lp(a) 71 mg/dL with your clinician—Lp(a) is largely genetic and may prompt specialist referral if you have a family history of early heart disease.\\n\\n3) Hormones and symptoms: Estradiol 28 pg/mL with night sweats; progesterone 0.33 ng/mL (phase not documented); total testosterone low at 11.7 ng/dL.\\n   - Track cycles and symptoms for 1–3 months. If bleeding is irregular or symptoms are bothersome, discuss menopause options (non-hormonal or estrogen therapy) with your provider.\\n   - For low testosterone: prioritize 7–8 hours sleep, strength training 2–3× weekly, and stress management (walking, meditation).\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Start vitamin D supplement and bone-strengthening exercise; recheck 25‑OH in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Begin the cholesterol diet/exercise plan now and repeat fasting lipids in 3 months.\\n- Track menstrual cycle and symptoms; consider a menopause visit within 4–8 weeks to discuss symptom relief and whether further hormone testing (timed progesterone/FSH) or treatment is right for you.\\n- Discuss Lp(a) 71 mg/dL and family history with your clinician; consider cardiology referral if there is premature CVD in relatives.\\n\\nYou’re doing well in many areas—these focused steps can improve bone, heart, and hormonal health. If you’d like, we can make a checklist and timeline together at your next visit.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 13.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 40.6, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 92.3, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 92.3 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 271, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 28, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.33, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", 