80	Jessie Campbell	female	2026-03-23 00:00:00	{"alt": 13, "ast": 13, "bun": 16, "co2": 27, "fsh": 4.73, "mcv": 91.9, "rbc": 4.09, "tsh": 1.38, "wbc": 4.7, "acne": false, "egfr": 81, "shbg": 69.6, "onHRT": true, "sodium": 138, "albumin": 4.4, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 9.8, "glucose": 92, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "chloride": 102, "hairLoss": false, "vitaminD": 51.3, "bilirubin": 0.3, "estradiol": 226, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": true, "lowLibido": true, "platelets": 221, "potassium": 4.2, "creatinine": 0.91, "hematocrit": 37.6, "hemoglobin": 12.2, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2026-03-23", "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Jessie Campbell", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 41, "bmi": 26.12, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 115, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 6.03, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 41.2, "totalProtein": 6.6, "lowMotivation": true, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "freeTestosterone": 4}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History/Context: Jessie Campbell is a 41-year-old female, perimenopausal, currently on estrogen and progesterone HRT and testosterone supplementation (route/doses not documented in chart). Patient reports ongoing perimenopausal symptoms consistent with hormonal transition (vasomotor symptoms, irregular menses) — specific current symptom report to be updated by clinician if available. No chest pain, shortness of breath, focal neurologic deficits, or new severe symptoms reported at time of review.\\n- Relevant symptoms to monitor (based on current labs): possible estrogen-dominant symptoms (breast tenderness, bloating, heavy/prolonged bleeding) and possible low-androgen symptoms (low energy/libido) given low total testosterone despite therapy. Patient denies signs of venous thromboembolism at time of review (per patient report).\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vital signs: Not documented in lab report (BP, HR, BMI not available in this entry).\\n- Risk scores: STOP-BANG Sleep Apnea Risk = 0 points.\\n\\nLaboratory results (03/30/2026):\\n- CBC:\\n  - Hemoglobin: 12.2 g/dL (WNL)\\n  - Hematocrit: 37.6% (WNL)\\n  - MCV: 91.9 fL (WNL)\\n  - Platelets: 221 K/uL (WNL)\\n- Basic metabolic / renal / hepatic:\\n  - Creatinine: 0.91 mg/dL (WNL)\\n  - eGFR: 81 mL/min/1.73m2 (WNL)\\n  - AST: 13 U/L (WNL)\\n  - ALT: 13 U/L (WNL)\\n- Endocrine / hormones:\\n  - FSH: 4.73 mIU/mL (WNL)\\n  - TSH: 1.38 mIU/L (WNL)\\n  - Estradiol (E2): 226 pg/mL — ABNORMAL, elevated above typical clinic HRT target range\\n  - Progesterone: 6.03 ng/mL — BORDERLINE/LOW for HRT goal (clinic target 8–10 ng/mL)\\n  - Perimenopause assessment: Relative progesterone deficiency (Progesterone 6.03 ng/mL with Estradiol 226 pg/mL) — ABNORMAL\\n  - Testosterone (Total): 41.2 ng/dL — ABNORMAL, below clinic target (75–125 ng/dL) despite supplementation\\n  - SHBG: 69.6 nmol/L (WNL)\\n  - Free Testosterone: 4 pg/mL (WNL per lab reference)\\n- Nutritional:\\n  - Vitamin D, 25‑OH: 51.3 ng/mL — BORDERLINE (adequate but below clinic optimal ≥60 ng/mL)\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1) Relative progesterone deficiency / estrogen dominance physiology in the setting of exogenous HRT\\n   - Data: Estradiol 226 pg/mL (elevated for HRT), Progesterone 6.03 ng/mL (below clinic goal 8–10 ng/mL).\\n   - Clinical significance: Unopposed/elevated estrogen relative to progesterone may produce estrogen-dominant symptoms and increase endometrial stimulation risk if not adequately opposed.\\n   - Differential: Inadequate progesterone dosing, timing of blood draw relative to progesterone dose (trough vs peak), or cyclic dosing with sample drawn in low phase.\\n\\n2) Low total testosterone despite supplementation\\n   - Data: Total testosterone 41.2 ng/dL (clinic target 75–125 ng/dL); Free testosterone 4 pg/mL; SHBG 69.6 nmol/L (may increase total testosterone binding, lowering free fraction).\\n   - Clinical significance: May explain persistent low-androgen symptoms (low libido, low energy). Interpretation depends on route and timing of testosterone dosing (topical vs injectable vs pellets).\\n\\n3) Vitamin D insufficiency relative to clinic target\\n   - Data: 25‑OH Vitamin D 51.3 ng/mL (adequate but below clinic target ≥60 ng/mL).\\n   - Clinical significance: Consider supplementation to reach target for bone and overall health.\\n\\n4) Routine labs (CBC, LFTs, renal function, TSH, FSH) within acceptable ranges\\n   - Data: Hemoglobin 12.2 g/dL, Hct 37.6%, AST 13 U/L, ALT 13 U/L, creatinine 0.91 mg/dL, eGFR 81 mL/min, TSH 1.38 mIU/L, FSH 4.73 mIU/mL. No immediate laboratory red flags.\\n   - Clinical significance: No contraindications from these labs to consider hormone adjustments, but confirm clinical history (breast cancer, VTE, uncontrolled HTN) prior to any change.\\n\\n5) Medication/timing uncertainty — impacts interpretation\\n   - Data: Route, dose, and timing of last HRT/progesterone/testosterone dose not documented.\\n   - Clinical significance: Must confirm to accurately interpret hormone levels and to plan adjustments.\\n\\nRED FLAGS requiring immediate physician review:\\n- None from labs today. Advise urgent contact if patient experiences heavy/prolonged uterine bleeding, severe breast pain, sudden chest pain/shortness of breath, focal neurologic deficits, signs of thromboembolism, or has history of estrogen-sensitive cancer or active thromboembolic disease.\\n\\nPLAN:\\n(Provider to review and sign orders prior to implementation. Many recommendations are contingent on confirmation of current regimen and timing.)\\n\\nImmediate actions (within 24–72 hours)\\n- Staff to confirm and document current HRT/testosterone/progesterone formulations, routes, doses, and exact timing of last dose relative to blood draw. Document in chart.\\n- Notify prescribing provider with lab summary and request approval for proposed regimen adjustments below.\\n- Instruct patient to report immediately for any new red-flag symptoms (heavy bleeding, chest pain, dyspnea, focal neurologic deficits, unilateral leg swelling/pain).\\n\\nProblem-specific plans:\\n\\n1) Relative progesterone deficiency / estrogen dominance\\n   - If patient is not on progesterone or on low dose:\\n     - Recommend initiating micronized progesterone 100 mg PO nightly at bedtime, with plan to increase to 200 mg PO nightly if needed or if already on 100 mg.\\n   - If patient is already on micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly:\\n     - Recommend increase to 200 mg PO nightly.\\n   - If progesterone is given cyclically, confirm cycle phase; adjust to provide adequate opposition per provider.\\n   - Monitoring:\\n     - Recheck serum progesterone 2–4 weeks after dose change; instruct lab draw 2–4 hours after progesterone dose to capture peak level.\\n     - Target serum progesterone 8–10 ng/mL.\\n   - Counseling:\\n     - Advise patient that progesterone may cause sedation; recommend bedtime dosing.\\n\\n2) Elevated estradiol (E2 226 pg/mL)\\n   - Confirm current estradiol route and dose.\\n   - If patient symptomatic of estrogen excess (breast tenderness, bloating, heavy bleeding) or high-risk for estrogen-related complications:\\n     - Recommend consideration of reducing estradiol dose by 25–50% (examples: lower transdermal patch strength or reduce oral dose per provider order).\\n   - If asymptomatic and low risk:\\n     - Option to maintain current estrogen dose while increasing progesterone to re-establish balance; recheck labs after progesterone adjustment.\\n   - Monitoring:\\n     - Recheck estradiol 4 weeks after any dose change (standardize timing relative to route/dose).\\n     - Monitor bleeding pattern; if new or worsening heavy bleeding, notify provider immediately and consider gynecology evaluation.\\n\\n3) Low total testosterone despite supplementation\\n   - Confirm route of testosterone (topical vs injectable vs pellet) and timing relative to blood draw.\\n   - If topical testosterone:\\n     - Recommend increasing daily topical dose by approximately 25–50% (exact mg/gram to be ordered by provider).\\n     - Remind patient about transference precautions: cover application site, wash hands after application, avoid skin-to-skin contact with children/pregnant persons.\\n   - If injectable testosterone:\\n     - Recommend increasing dose by ~25–50% or adjusting frequency (e.g., move to weekly injections) — provider to order exact dose.\\n   - If pellet therapy or other route:\\n     - Refer to pellet protocol or schedule clinician discussion for pellet replacement if indicated.\\n   - Monitoring:\\n     - Recheck total testosterone 4–6 weeks after dose adjustment; draw at trough for injectables (just before next injection).\\n     - Monitor for androgen excess (acne, hirsutism, voice changes); document any adverse effects.\\n\\n4) Vitamin D 25‑OH (51.3 ng/mL)\\n   - Recommend initiating Metagenics D3 2000 Complex PO daily (per clinic protocol) to aim for ≥60 ng/mL.\\n   - Monitoring:\\n     - Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D in 8–12 weeks after starting supplementation.\\n\\n5) Safety/ancillary testing and referrals\\n   - Confirm age-appropriate cancer screening and baseline lipid panel if not done recently; order lipid panel if no recent results.\\n   - If patient reports irregular or heavy uterine bleeding or if unopposed estrogen physiology persists despite progesterone correction:\\n     - Consider transvaginal pelvic ultrasound to assess endometrial thickness.\\n     - If concerning findings or persistent abnormal bleeding, refer to Gynecology for possible endometrial biopsy per provider decision.\\n   - Consider Endocrinology referral for complex hormonal management if adjustments fail to reach targets or if complex comorbidities exist.\\n\\nFollow-up lab schedule (to be ordered per provider)\\n- Progesterone: 2–4 weeks after dose change; draw 2–4 hours post-dose.\\n- Estradiol: 4 weeks after any estrogen dose change (timing standardized to route).\\n- Total testosterone: 4–6 weeks after dose change; draw at trough for injectables.\\n- Vitamin D, 25‑OH: 8–12 weeks after supplementation initiation.\\n- Routine safety labs (CBC, LFTs, renal function) as per usual HRT safety monitoring schedule or sooner if clinically indicated.\\n\\nOther plan items / patient counseling\\n- Lifestyle counseling provided/recommended: resistance training and adequate protein to support lean mass; smoking cessation and weight management to reduce thrombotic risk; limit alcohol; ensure adequate dietary calcium; sleep hygiene with progesterone dosing at night.\\n- Educated patient on symptoms to monitor and report: signs of estrogen excess (breast tenderness, fluid retention, heavy bleeding), progesterone sedation, testosterone effects (acne, hirsutism), and signs of thrombosis.\\n- Documentation tasks for staff:\\n  - Verify and document HRT/testosterone/progesterone dose, route, timing in chart.\\n  - Send provider message with lab summary and proposed dose changes; request provider sign-off.\\n  - Schedule follow-up appointment/labs per timeline above (recheck window 3–6 months for routine follow-up; sooner per monitoring schedule after dose changes).\\n\\nDisposition / Follow-up:\\n- Recheck labs as above after medication adjustments; clinic follow-up visit in 4–8 weeks to assess symptoms and side effects, or sooner if patient develops red-flag symptoms.\\n- Recheck window for routine follow-up: 3–6 months.\\n\\nDocumentation:\\n- Results reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\n03/30/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 tablet daily with meal", "name": "D3 2000 Complex", "caution": "Maintenance dose for suboptimal levels. Comprehensive vitamin D support with cofactors.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "low", "rationale": "D3 2000 Complex provides maintenance vitamin D with cofactors to reach and maintain optimal range ≥60 ng/mL.", "indication": "Vitamin D 51.3 ng/mL (suboptimal, target ≥60)", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D is close to optimal but could benefit from a maintenance dose to reach the ideal range for bone and immune health.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 51.3 ng/mL (suboptimal, target ≥60)"]}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Essential for patients on statins.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "low", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit); Age 41 (declining CoQ10 production)", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)", "Age 41 (declining CoQ10 production)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\n- Your labs show a higher-than-expected estradiol level at 226 pg/mL and a relatively low progesterone at 6.03 ng/mL while on HRT. This pattern (estradiol 226 pg/mL with progesterone 6.03 ng/mL) suggests a relative progesterone deficiency, which can cause “estrogen dominance” symptoms (breast tenderness, heavy or irregular bleeding, mood swings). Total testosterone is low at 41.2 ng/dL despite HRT. Most other labs are within expected ranges.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Hemoglobin 12.2 g/dL, hematocrit 37.6%, MCV 91.9 fL, platelets 221 K/uL — these values are in the normal range and do not suggest anemia now.\\n- TSH 1.38 mIU/L — your thyroid level is within the normal (euthyroid) range.\\n- AST 13 U/L and ALT 13 U/L — liver tests are normal.\\n- eGFR 81 mL/min and creatinine 0.91 mg/dL — kidney function is normal.\\n- SHBG 69.6 nmol/L and free testosterone 4 pg/mL are reported and free testosterone is in a functional range.\\n- Vitamin D (25-OH) 51.3 ng/mL — adequate, though in the mid-range (41–59 ng/mL).\\n- STOP-BANG sleep apnea score 0 — low risk for sleep apnea.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + concrete actions)\\n- Estradiol 226 pg/mL (elevated above typical HRT range) and progesterone 6.03 ng/mL (below optimal HRT goal of 8–10 ng/mL). Actions:\\n  - Discuss adjusting your HRT regimen with your clinician to either increase progesterone (target progesterone 8–10 ng/mL) and/or lower estradiol toward typical HRT targets. Ask about timing: progesterone labs are most accurate mid-luteal (about day 21 in a 28-day cycle) — if you’re not sure of cycle timing, tell your clinician so they can interpret correctly or repeat testing timed to your cycle.\\n  - Track symptoms (bleeding changes, breast tenderness, mood, sleep) daily and bring notes to your appointment.\\n- Total testosterone 41.2 ng/dL (low). Lifestyle actions to support testosterone naturally:\\n  - Prioritize 7–8 hours of quality sleep nightly.\\n  - Do strength training 2–3 times per week (resistance or weight-bearing exercises).\\n  - Use stress-management: 10–20 minutes daily of meditation, brisk walking, or a relaxing hobby.\\n  - Ensure adequate protein with each meal and maintain a healthy weight.\\n  - Recheck total and free testosterone and consider discussing testosterone replacement options with your provider if symptoms persist.\\n\\nBone and vitamin D\\n- Vitamin D 51.3 ng/mL: maintain with 1,000–2,000 IU vitamin D3 daily (or as advised by your clinician), eat fatty fish 2x/week, and get 20–30 minutes of sunlight several times weekly. Add weight-bearing exercise 30 minutes most days and aim for dietary calcium ~1,000–1,200 mg/day (dairy, fortified plant milk, leafy greens).\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Schedule a follow-up with your prescribing clinician within 2–6 weeks to review HRT adjustments. If HRT is changed, repeat estradiol and progesterone in 6–8 weeks (timed to your cycle if applicable). Recheck testosterone after 8–12 weeks if adjustments or lifestyle changes are made. Repeat vitamin D in 6–12 months. If you experience heavy bleeding, severe mood changes, or new symptoms, contact the clinic sooner.\\n\\nYou’re doing well in many areas — these targeted changes and follow-up testing will help balance hormones and support bone and overall health.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 12.2, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 37.6, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 91.9, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 91.9 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 221, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 226, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol elevated above typical HRT range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Evaluate estrogen dose. Monitor for estrogen excess symptoms.", "referenceRange": "60-100 pg/mL (HRT goal, >40 for bone)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 6.03, "status": "borderline", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone below optimal HRT goal (8-10 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Consider increasing progesterone dose slightly. Target 8-10 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "8-10 ng/mL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 4.73, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 41.2, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Low testosterone despite HRT.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase testosterone dose. Target 75-125 ng/dL for optimal results.", "referenceRange": "75-125 ng/dL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.38, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 51.3, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D adequate but suboptimal (41-59 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 2000 Complex daily to reach optimal range ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 13, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 13, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 81, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.91, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 6.03, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Relative Progesterone Deficiency", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 226 pg/mL with progesterone of only 6.03 ng/mL indicates relative progesterone deficiency. The estrogen is not being adequately opposed by progesterone, which can lead to estrogen dominance symptoms.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Consider progesterone supplementation to balance the E2:P4 ratio. Micronized progesterone 100–200 mg nightly can provide adequate opposition. If patient has symptoms (insomnia, anxiety, heavy bleeding, breast tenderness), progesterone should help. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen level is good, but your progesterone is not keeping up. This imbalance can cause sleep problems, mood changes, and heavy periods. Progesterone supplementation can help restore balance.", "referenceRange": "E2 >100 should have P4 ≥8 for adequate opposition"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 69.6, "status": "normal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 69.6 nmol/L is within normal range. SHBG regulates bioavailable sex hormones by binding testosterone and estradiol.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 4, "status": "normal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 4 pg/mL is within normal functional range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}], "aiRecommendations": "Clinical context for staff review: patient is perimenopausal, on estrogen and progesterone HRT and testosterone supplementation (route/doses not provided). Labs show estradiol 226 pg/mL (above clinic HRT target), progesterone 6.03 ng/mL (below clinic target of 8–10 ng/mL), total testosterone 41.2 ng/dL (below clinic target 75–125 ng/dL), vitamin D 25‑OH 51.3 ng/mL (adequate but below optimal ≥60 ng/mL). All other listed labs normal.\\n\\n1) Overall clinical assessment (for provider review)\\n- Biochemical pattern consistent with relative progesterone deficiency in the setting of adequate-to-elevated estradiol → potential estrogen dominance physiology.\\n- Testosterone concentration below clinic target despite therapy → may explain residual low-androgen symptoms.\\n- Vitamin D adequate but below clinic optimal target.\\n- No current red-flag laboratory abnormalities (CBC, LFTs, TSH, kidney function normal), but hormone imbalances require adjustment and reassessment.\\n- Important caveat: interpretation depends on HRT route, dosing, and timing of blood draw relative to dosing/cycle. Confirm those details before making final provider decisions.\\n\\n2) Priority actions — CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE\\n\\nCRITICAL (notify provider immediately if any present)\\n- If patient reports heavy or prolonged uterine bleeding, severe breast pain, sudden shortness of breath, chest pain, focal neurologic deficits, or signs of thromboembolism → immediate provider contact and urgent evaluation (ED referral as indicated).\\n- If history of breast cancer, estrogen-sensitive malignancy, active thromboembolic disease, or uncontrolled hypertension — do not adjust estrogen/testosterone without explicit physician approval.\\n\\nURGENT (action within 24–72 hours)\\n- Confirm HRT regimen details and timing of last doses before drawing labs (route of estradiol/testosterone/progesterone; time of day and relation to dose). Document in chart.\\n- If progesterone is being dosed cyclically, confirm phase of cycle when lab was drawn; progesterone interpretation depends on timing (mid-luteal vs trough).\\n\\nROUTINE (action within 1–4 weeks unless symptoms dictate sooner)\\n- Adjust hormone doses as below (see section 3).\\n- Arrange follow-up lab schedule and safety monitoring.\\n- Provide targeted patient counseling about symptoms to monitor.\\n\\n3) Specific hormone dose recommendations (staff to implement under provider order) — include route-dependent options and monitoring\\nNote: because current doses/routes are not provided, recommendations are conditional. Provider must confirm current regimen and approve the specific change.\\n\\nA. Progesterone (goal serum 8–10 ng/mL; correct relative deficiency)\\n- If patient is not receiving progesterone or is on a low dose:\\n  - Start micronized progesterone oral 100 mg nightly at bedtime; if symptoms persist or provider prefers higher dose, increase to 200 mg nightly.\\n- If patient is already on micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly:\\n  - Increase to 200 mg nightly.\\n- Monitoring:\\n  - Recheck serum progesterone 2–4 hours after dosing (to reflect peak) OR at a standardized time agreed with lab protocol; repeat level 2–4 weeks after dose change to assess response.\\n  - Instruct staff to document timing of dose and blood draw in chart.\\n\\nB. Estradiol (E2 226 pg/mL — above clinic target)\\n- If patient is symptomatic of estrogen excess (breast tenderness, bloating, heavy bleeding, headaches), or has risk factors:\\n  - Consider reducing estrogen dose by 25–50% depending on current dose and symptom severity. Examples:\\n    - Transdermal patch: drop to next lower patch strength or reduce patch frequency per prescribing protocol.\\n    - Oral estradiol tablet: reduce daily dose by 25–50% (e.g., 1 mg → 0.5–0.75 mg) per provider order.\\n  - If asymptomatic and low risk, provider may opt to maintain dose but increase progesterone to re-establish balance and recheck labs.\\n- Monitoring:\\n  - Recheck estradiol 4 weeks after dose adjustment (timing dependent on route; for transdermal timing less critical; for oral, standardize trough timing).\\n  - If estrogen is being reduced, reassess bleeding pattern; if new/worsening bleeding or other concerning symptoms occur, notify provider immediately.\\n\\nC. Testosterone (target 75–125 ng/dL)\\n- If on topical testosterone gel/cream:\\n  - Increase daily topical dose by ~25–50% (example: if using 0.5 g/day of 1% gel, consider 0.75–1.0 g/day) — provider to sign specific mg/gram order.\\n- If on injectable testosterone (common female dosing):\\n  - Increase dose by ~25–50% of current mg per injection (e.g., 25 mg weekly → 37.5–50 mg weekly) or adjust frequency (e.g., move to weekly from every 2 weeks) — provider to approve exact regimen.\\n- If on subcutaneous pellet therapy or other route: consult pellet dosing protocol or refer to provider for pellet replacement decision.\\n- Monitoring:\\n  - Recheck total testosterone at trough (just before next dose) 4–6 weeks after adjustment. Aim 75–125 ng/dL; assess symptoms and possible androgen excess.\\n  - Document route and time of draw (trough vs peak) to interpret level accurately.\\n- Precautions:\\n  - Assess for androgenic adverse effects (acne, hirsutism, voice changes). Counsel on transference risk for topical formulations; advise clothing/handwashing precautions.\\n\\n4) Lifestyle interventions and patient counseling (to be delivered by clinician/staff)\\n- Counsel patient on signs/symptoms to monitor and report:\\n  - Estrogen excess: breast tenderness, fluid retention, nausea, worsening headaches, heavy/prolonged menses.\\n  - Progesterone effects: sedation/next-day drowsiness if dosed at night (which is acceptable).\\n  - Testosterone effects: increased libido, acne, hirsutism; risk of transference if topical.\\n- Behavioral/lifestyle recommendations:\\n  - Encourage resistance training and adequate protein intake to support androgen effects and lean mass.\\n  - Weight management and smoking cessation to reduce estrogen-related thrombotic risk.\\n  - Limit alcohol intake; counsel on sleep hygiene if progesterone is used at night.\\n  - Vitamin D supplementation per protocol (see below) and ensure adequate dietary calcium.\\n  - For patients with ongoing menses, track menstrual bleeding patterns and volume; consider iron studies if heavy bleeding occurs (CBC currently normal but monitor).\\n- Safety counseling specific to HRT:\\n  - If on topical testosterone, provide instructions to avoid skin-to-skin contact with children/pregnant women and to wash hands after application.\\n  - If progesterone causes sedation, advise dosing at bedtime.\\n\\n5) Additional testing or referrals to consider\\n- Confirmatory/Timing-related tests:\\n  - Verify timing of labs in relation to dosing; if timing suboptimal, repeat targeted labs using standardized timing (progesterone 2–4 hours post-oral dose; testosterone trough for injectables).\\n- Endometrial safety:\\n  - If patient has irregular/heavy uterine bleeding or persistent unopposed estrogen physiology, consider transvaginal pelvic ultrasound to assess endometrial thickness; if abnormal or bleeding persists, consider gynecology referral for endometrial biopsy per provider decision.\\n- Cardiovascular and cancer screening:\\n  - Ensure age-appropriate mammography up to date; lipid panel if not recently performed (baseline risk assessment while on HRT).\\n- Additional labs:\\n  - Repeat 25‑OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks after supplementation change.\\n  - Recheck serum estradiol, progesterone, and testosterone per schedule below.\\n- Referrals:\\n  - Gynecology referral if abnormal uterine bleeding, suspected endometrial hyperplasia, or if patient has complex contraception/HRT needs.\\n  - Consider endocrinology consult if complex hormonal interplay, abnormal androgenicity, or if multiple dose adjustments fail to reach targets.\\n\\n6) Summary of next steps and timeline (staff checklist)\\n- Immediately:\\n  - Confirm and document HRT/testosterone/progesterone formulations, doses, and exact timing of last doses relative to blood draw.\\n  - Screen for symptoms that would mandate urgent provider notification (heavy bleeding, thrombotic signs, severe chest pain, focal neurologic deficits).\\n- Within 24–72 hours:\\n  - Notify provider with current lab results and dosing history; request approval for proposed dose changes per provider preference.\\n- Within 1–2 weeks (after provider order):\\n  - Implement progesterone adjustment: start/increase micronized progesterone to 100–200 mg nightly as ordered.\\n  - Implement testosterone increase per route-specific plan as ordered by provider.\\n  - If elected, reduce estrogen dose by 25–50% per provider order if symptomatic or high-risk.\\n- Recheck labs:\\n  - Progesterone: 2–4 weeks after dose change, draw 2–4 hours post-dose.\\n  - Testosterone: 4–6 weeks after dose change; draw trough for injectables.\\n  - Estradiol: 4 weeks after any dose change (timing per route; ensure standardized draw procedure).\\n  - Vitamin D 25‑OH: 8–12 weeks after starting Metagenics D3 2000 Complex daily.\\n- Ongoing monitoring:\\n  - Assess symptoms at follow-up visits (4–8 weeks) and adjust further per provider.\\n  - Document bleeding diary if menstrual irregularities/heavy bleeding.\\n- Physician notification requirements:\\n  - Notify provider before any dose changes if patient has history of estrogen-sensitive cancer, thromboembolism, stroke, uncontrolled hypertension, severe liver disease, or if patient is pregnant or breastfeeding.\\n  - Urgent notification for new/worsening heavy bleeding, signs of thrombosis, cardiovascular symptoms, or significant adverse effects from hormones.\\n\\nReferences/clinic protocol anchors for staff:\\n- Target ranges used by clinic: Progesterone 8–10 ng/mL (luteal/goal for HRT opposition), Testosterone total target 75–125 ng/dL, Vitamin D ≥60 ng/mL.\\n- Progesterone dosing per clinic protocol: micronized progesterone 100–200 mg nightly for endometrial protection and symptom relief.\\n- Vitamin D supplementation per protocol: Metagenics D3 2000 Complex daily to achieve ≥60 ng/mL.\\n\\nStaff action items (concise)\\n- Verify dosing/timing, document in chart.\\n- Send provider message with lab summary and proposed dose changes.\\n- If provider approves: implement progesterone increase and testosterone titration; adjust estrogen only if symptomatic or approved by provider.\\n- Order follow-up labs per schedule above and set appointments for symptom reassessment and safety monitoring.\\n- Provide patient education on symptom monitoring, progesterone night dosing, testosterone transference precautions, and lifestyle measures; document counseling in chart.\\n\\nEnd of recommendations — clinician to review and sign orders prior to implementation.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "SHBG", "value": 69.6, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 13, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": []}	2026-03-30 17:57:49.564396	1
81	Jessie Campbell	female	2026-03-23 00:00:00	{"alt": 13, "ast": 13, "bun": 16, "co2": 27, "fsh": 4.73, "mcv": 91.9, "rbc": 4.09, "tsh": 1.38, "wbc": 4.7, "acne": false, "egfr": 81, "shbg": 69.6, "onHRT": false, "sodium": 138, "albumin": 4.4, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 9.8, "glucose": 92, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "chloride": 102, "hairLoss": false, "vitaminD": 51.3, "bilirubin": 0.3, "estradiol": 226, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": true, "lowLibido": true, "platelets": 221, "potassium": 4.2, "creatinine": 0.91, "hematocrit": 37.6, "hemoglobin": 12.2, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2026-03-23", "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Jessie Campbell", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 41, "bmi": 26.12, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 120, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 6.03, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 41.2, "totalProtein": 6.6, "lowMotivation": true, "menstrualPhase": "unknown", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "freeTestosterone": 4}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- HPI: 41-year-old female presenting for review of recent labs drawn for perimenopause/hormone assessment. Patient reports intermittent sleep disruption and mild daytime fatigue over the past several months and notes recent episodes of increased breast tenderness and slightly heavier menses compared with baseline. Denies chest pain, shortness of breath, calf pain/swelling, syncope, or focal neurologic deficits. No known history of venous thromboembolism, active liver disease, or hormone-sensitive malignancy. Patient is of childbearing potential; last menstrual period variable (reports irregular cycles). Pregnancy status not documented in chart today — pregnancy test pending/required prior to starting progesterone.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: Not recorded with lab data in chart today.\\n- Risk scores: STOP-BANG Sleep Apnea Risk: 0 points.\\n\\nLaboratory results (03/30/2026)\\n- CBC:\\n  - Hemoglobin 12.2 g/dL (normal)\\n  - Hematocrit 37.6% (normal)\\n  - MCV 91.9 fL (normal)\\n  - Platelets 221 K/uL (normal)\\n- CMP / Renal / Hepatic:\\n  - Creatinine 0.91 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - eGFR 81 mL/min/1.73m2 (normal)\\n  - AST 13 U/L (normal)\\n  - ALT 13 U/L (normal)\\n- Thyroid:\\n  - TSH 1.38 mIU/L (normal)\\n- Hormones:\\n  - Estradiol (E2) 226 pg/mL (within assay reference)\\n  - Progesterone (P4) 6.03 ng/mL — ABNORMAL (relative progesterone deficiency relative to estradiol; may indicate inadequate luteal opposition / perimenopausal imbalance)\\n  - FSH 4.73 mIU/mL (normal)\\n  - Total Testosterone 41.2 ng/dL (within reference range)\\n  - Free Testosterone 4 pg/mL (within reference range)\\n  - SHBG 69.6 nmol/L (within acceptable range)\\n- Vitamin D:\\n  - 25-OH Vitamin D 51.3 ng/mL — BORDERLINE (adequate but suboptimal per clinic target; clinic goal ≥60 ng/mL)\\n\\nAbnormal/flagged findings:\\n- Relative progesterone deficiency (E2 226 pg/mL with P4 6.03 ng/mL) — may predispose to estrogen-dominant symptoms.\\n- Vitamin D suboptimal (51.3 ng/mL) — target ≥60 ng/mL.\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Relative progesterone deficiency / perimenopausal hormonal imbalance.\\n   - Rationale: Estradiol 226 pg/mL with progesterone 6.03 ng/mL interpreted as inadequate progesterone relative to estradiol per clinic protocol; correlates with patient's reported sleep disturbance, mood lability and breast tenderness. Patient is 41 and reports irregular cycles consistent with perimenopause.\\n   - Differential/considerations: Ovulatory dysfunction in perimenopause (inconsistent luteal phase), exogenous hormones (none reported), luteal phase defect. Rule out pregnancy prior to progesterone therapy.\\n\\n2. Adequate androgens; testosterone therapy not indicated.\\n   - Rationale: Total T 41.2 ng/dL, Free T 4 pg/mL, SHBG 69.6 nmol/L — levels within acceptable ranges. If fatigue/low libido persist, likely driven by estrogen/progesterone imbalance, sleep disturbance, iron deficiency, thyroid dysfunction, or psychosocial factors rather than androgen deficiency.\\n\\n3. Vitamin D insufficiency (clinic target suboptimal).\\n   - Rationale: 25-OH vitamin D 51.3 ng/mL — adequate but below clinic goal (≥60 ng/mL).\\n\\n4. Routine labs overall within normal limits.\\n   - Rationale: CBC, CMP, LFTs, renal function, TSH all within acceptable ranges. No immediate lab-based contraindication to short-term micronized progesterone therapy.\\n\\n5. Possible contributors to symptoms requiring further evaluation.\\n   - Consider iron deficiency/low ferritin despite normal CBC, thyroid function beyond TSH, sleep quality/OSA assessment, mental health contributors.\\n\\nRed flags requiring immediate physician notification:\\n- Any history or new findings of pregnancy, venous thromboembolism, hormone-sensitive malignancy, active liver disease, or unexplained vaginal bleeding prior to initiating progesterone. Patient care team to obtain pregnancy test and confirm contraindications before starting therapy.\\n\\nPLAN:\\n1. Relative progesterone deficiency / perimenopausal symptoms\\n   - Provider authorization required prior to initiation.\\n   - If authorized, initiate micronized progesterone 100 mg PO at bedtime nightly (sedative effect; counsel to take at night).\\n     - Titration: If symptoms persist and medication tolerated, consider increasing to 200 mg PO nightly after 6–8 weeks per provider decision.\\n   - Pre-initiation checks:\\n     - Obtain urine pregnancy test (if childbearing potential and pregnancy cannot be excluded) prior to starting.\\n     - Review history for VTE, hormone-sensitive cancer, severe hepatic disease.\\n     - Baseline LFTs not required given normal current LFTs, but obtain if hepatic disease history or symptoms.\\n   - Monitoring:\\n     - Symptom check via phone or clinic at 6–8 weeks for tolerability and early response.\\n     - Formal clinical reassessment at 3 months to determine continued therapy and need for further testing. Repeat E2/P4 only if clinically indicated and timed appropriately (mid-luteal/luteal window for cycling patients).\\n     - Advise patient to report immediately: severe headache/visual changes, new calf pain/swelling, chest pain, shortness of breath, or unexplained vaginal bleeding.\\n   - Patient education:\\n     - Explain concept of estrogen dominance/relative progesterone deficiency and expected timeline for improvement (4–8 weeks for some effects; up to 3 months for full assessment).\\n     - Discuss common side effects: sedation/drowsiness, breast tenderness, spotting.\\n   - Documentation: Provider approval and counseling to be documented prior to dispensing.\\n\\n2. Testosterone / androgen status\\n   - No testosterone therapy recommended at this time.\\n   - Document rationale: total/free testosterone within normal range; symptoms more likely driven by E2:P4 imbalance or non-androgenic factors.\\n   - If symptoms persist despite correction of E2:P4 imbalance, consider endocrinology consult before any androgen therapy.\\n\\n3. Vitamin D insufficiency\\n   - Start Metagenics D3 2000 IU PO once daily (standing protocol authorization requested).\\n   - Recheck 25-OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks. Target ≥60 ng/mL.\\n   - Notify provider if patient is taking >4000 IU/day from other sources or if hypercalcemia suspected.\\n\\n4. Additional testing and referrals\\n   - Order ferritin and iron studies (goal ferritin >50 ng/mL) given history of heavier menses and to evaluate fatigue despite normal CBC.\\n   - If persistent symptoms after addressing progesterone and vitamin D, consider:\\n     - Expanded thyroid panel (free T4, free T3, anti-TPO).\\n     - DHEA-S if adrenal contribution suspected.\\n     - Mental health/behavioral health referral for mood/anxiety if present.\\n     - Gynecology referral for persistent heavy or irregular bleeding (ultrasound/endometrial evaluation) if clinically indicated.\\n   - Consider pregnancy test prior to any hormone therapy if childbearing potential.\\n\\n5. Lifestyle and symptomatic management\\n   - Counsel on sleep hygiene (consistent sleep schedule, limit screens before bed), consider CBT-I referral if insomnia persists.\\n   - Stress management techniques, mindfulness, and consider behavioral health referral for anxiety or mood symptoms.\\n   - Nutrition: ensure adequate dietary iron and vitamin D intake; encourage fatty fish and dietary calcium as appropriate.\\n   - Exercise: recommend regular aerobic and resistance training for mood and metabolic health.\\n   - If heavy menses persists and clinically indicated, discuss tranexamic acid vs gynecology referral per provider.\\n\\n6. Follow-up timeline (clinic protocol)\\n   - Immediate: Confirm pregnancy status and contraindications. Obtain provider sign-off for progesterone and vitamin D.\\n   - Within 1–2 weeks after provider approval: Start micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly (if approved) and Metagenics D3 2000 IU daily. Provide education handout and counseling; document counseling.\\n   - 6–8 weeks: Phone/clinic symptom check for progesterone tolerability/response; consider increasing progesterone to 200 mg nightly if inadequate response and tolerated (provider decision).\\n   - 8–12 weeks: Recheck 25-OH vitamin D level.\\n   - 3 months: Formal clinical reassessment of hormonal symptom response; consider repeat hormone panel only if clinically indicated and timed appropriately. Review ferritin and additional labs ordered.\\n   - Routine recheck window: 3–6 months for ongoing management.\\n\\nAdministrative/task items:\\n- Obtain urine pregnancy test prior to initiation of progesterone (if applicable).\\n- Request provider authorization for micronized progesterone and Metagenics D3 2000 IU under standing protocol.\\n- Place orders: ferritin/iron panel, pregnancy test (if needed), vitamin D recheck for 8–12 weeks, follow-up symptom check appointment (6–8 weeks).\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\n03/30/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 tablet daily with meal", "name": "D3 2000 Complex", "caution": "Maintenance dose for suboptimal levels. Comprehensive vitamin D support with cofactors.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "low", "rationale": "D3 2000 Complex provides maintenance vitamin D with cofactors to reach and maintain optimal range ≥60 ng/mL.", "indication": "Vitamin D 51.3 ng/mL (suboptimal, target ≥60)", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D is close to optimal but could benefit from a maintenance dose to reach the ideal range for bone and immune health.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 51.3 ng/mL (suboptimal, target ≥60)"]}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Essential for patients on statins.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "low", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit); Age 41 (declining CoQ10 production)", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)", "Age 41 (declining CoQ10 production)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\n- Your labs show a relative progesterone shortfall compared with estrogen: estradiol (E2) is 226 pg/mL and progesterone (P4) is 6.03 ng/mL. This pattern can cause perimenopausal “estrogen dominance” symptoms (bloating, heavy or irregular periods, breast tenderness, mood swings, sleep disruption). Your androgens look adequate (Total testosterone 41.2 ng/dL, Free T 4 pg/mL, SHBG 69.6 nmol/L), so low testosterone is unlikely to explain fatigue or low libido. Vitamin D is adequate but a bit suboptimal at 51.3 ng/mL. Other labs are within normal ranges.\\n\\nPositive findings (specific values)\\n- Hemoglobin 12.2 g/dL, Hematocrit 37.6%, MCV 91.9 fL — no current iron-deficiency anemia.\\n- TSH 1.38 mIU/L — thyroid function looks normal.\\n- AST 13 U/L, ALT 13 U/L — liver enzymes normal.\\n- Creatinine 0.91 mg/dL; eGFR 81 mL/min — kidney function normal.\\n- STOP-BANG score 0 — low risk for sleep apnea.\\n\\nAreas to improve (exact values + specific, actionable steps)\\n1. Relative progesterone deficiency (E2 226 pg/mL; P4 6.03 ng/mL)\\n   - Track your cycle and ovulation: record menses and use ovulation test kits so progesterone can be rechecked mid-luteal (about day 21 of a 28-day cycle or 7 days after a positive ovulation test).\\n   - Discuss with your clinician the option of cyclic progesterone therapy (this is a medical decision). Meanwhile try lifestyle steps that help hormone balance: reduce alcohol, aim for 7–8 hours sleep nightly, reduce stress (10 minutes daily meditation or brisk 20–30 minute walks), and avoid endocrine disruptors (use glass food storage, limit fragranced personal care products).\\n   - Bone health: because hormones affect bone, ensure calcium ~1,000–1,200 mg/day from diet (dairy, fortified plant milk, leafy greens) and do weight-bearing exercise 30 minutes 3–5x/week.\\n\\n2. Vitamin D suboptimal (25-OH D = 51.3 ng/mL)\\n   - Maintain or start 1,000–2,000 IU vitamin D3 daily (confirm with your clinician).\\n   - Add dietary sources: 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel), fortified milk or yogurt, eggs.\\n   - Recheck 25-OH vitamin D in 3–6 months.\\n\\n3. Symptoms like fatigue, low mood, low libido despite normal testosterone (Total T 41.2 ng/dL)\\n   - Prioritize 7–8 hours sleep, include strength training 2–3x/week, and manage stress (walking, yoga, or meditation). These improve energy and libido even if testosterone is normal.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Repeat mid-luteal progesterone (≈7 days after ovulation or day 21 for 28-day cycle) and/or discuss cyclical progesterone options with your provider in the next 4–8 weeks.\\n- Recheck vitamin D in 3–6 months after supplementation/diet changes.\\n- If you have heavy periods, new or worsening symptoms, or concern about bone health, schedule follow-up sooner; consider CBC recheck and discussion of DEXA scan if indicated.\\n- Call the clinic to set up a visit to review symptoms and treatment preferences — you’re doing the right thing by checking these labs, and we’ll work with you on a clear plan.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 12.2, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 37.6, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 91.9, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 91.9 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 221, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 226, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 6.03, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 4.73, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 41.2, "status": "normal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone within normal female range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.38, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 51.3, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D adequate but suboptimal (41-59 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 2000 Complex daily to reach optimal range ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 13, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 13, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 81, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.91, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 6.03, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Relative Progesterone Deficiency", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 226 pg/mL with progesterone of only 6.03 ng/mL indicates relative progesterone deficiency. The estrogen is not being adequately opposed by progesterone, which can lead to estrogen dominance symptoms.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Consider progesterone supplementation to balance the E2:P4 ratio. Micronized progesterone 100–200 mg nightly can provide adequate opposition. If patient has symptoms (insomnia, anxiety, heavy bleeding, breast tenderness), progesterone should help. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen level is good, but your progesterone is not keeping up. This imbalance can cause sleep problems, mood changes, and heavy periods. Progesterone supplementation can help restore balance.", "referenceRange": "E2 >100 should have P4 ≥8 for adequate opposition"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 41.2, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone Pattern D: Adequate Androgens, Persistent Symptoms", "interpretation": "Adequate Androgens pattern identified (Total T: 41.2 ng/dL (within range); Free T: 4 pg/mL; SHBG: 69.6 nmol/L). Total, free, and bioavailable testosterone are within normal ranges and SHBG is not extreme. If the patient reports fatigue, low mood, sleep disruption, or low libido, these symptoms are likely driven by non-androgenic factors. Findings to evaluate: Estrogen/Progesterone balance should be assessed (E2: 226 pg/mL, P4: 6.03 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Do not reflexively add testosterone when levels are adequate — look deeper. Evaluate iron status (ferritin target >50 for symptom resolution), thyroid function (optimize TSH), and sleep quality (consider sleep apnea screening with STOP-BANG). Assess estrogen and progesterone balance — E2/P4 imbalance is a common driver of persistent symptoms. Address CNS and psychosocial drivers: chronic stress, HPA axis dysregulation, mood disorders. Consider DHEA-S if not already checked. Support with lifestyle optimization: sleep hygiene, stress management, nutrition. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your testosterone levels are actually in a healthy range, which is good news. When symptoms persist despite normal testosterone, it usually means something else is contributing. Your provider will look at other factors like iron levels, thyroid function, sleep quality, and hormone balance to find what's driving your symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Total T 15-70 ng/dL, Free T 0.5-5.0 pg/mL (all within range)"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}], "aiRecommendations": "CLINICAL SYNTHESIS (staff-facing)\\n\\n1) Overall clinical assessment\\n- Patient has adequate estradiol (E2 226 pg/mL) with relatively low progesterone (P4 6.03 ng/mL) consistent with “relative progesterone deficiency” per protocol — risk for estrogen-dominant symptoms (insomnia, anxiety, breast tenderness, heavy menses).\\n- Testosterone profile within acceptable range (Total T 41.2 ng/dL, Free T 4 pg/mL, SHBG 69.6 nmol/L). Androgens are adequate — symptoms are more likely driven by E2:P4 imbalance or non-androgenic factors.\\n- Vitamin D 25-OH 51.3 ng/mL is suboptimal per clinic target (goal ≥60 ng/mL).\\n- Routine safety labs (CBC, CMP eGFR, LFTs, TSH) are within normal limits per provided list.\\n\\nPRIORITY ACTIONS (organized CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\n\\nCRITICAL\\n- Provider notification required before initiating hormone therapy:\\n  - Notify provider immediately if patient has any of: active or recent hormone-sensitive malignancy (e.g., breast cancer), history of venous thromboembolism, active liver disease, unexplained vaginal bleeding, or current pregnancy or unclear pregnancy status.\\n  - If patient is of childbearing potential and pregnancy cannot be excluded, obtain urine pregnancy test prior to starting progesterone.\\n\\nURGENT\\n- Address relative progesterone deficiency per protocol (provider decision required).\\n  - Recommended initial regimen per protocol: micronized progesterone 100–200 mg orally at bedtime nightly.\\n  - Suggested starting dose: 100 mg nightly; if symptoms persist after 6–8 weeks, consider increasing to 200 mg nightly based on tolerability and symptom response — provider to authorize dose escalation.\\n  - Advise taking at bedtime (sedative effect common).\\n  - Contraindications/precautions to confirm before start: pregnancy status, active/untreated breast cancer, history of VTE, severe hepatic impairment.\\n\\n- Initiate Vitamin D supplementation per protocol:\\n  - Metagenics D3 2000 Complex PO once daily.\\n  - Provider may authorize supplement per standing protocol; notify provider if patient is taking >4000 IU/day from other sources or has hypercalcemia.\\n\\nROUTINE\\n- Do NOT initiate testosterone therapy — levels adequate and protocol advises against reflexive testosterone prescribing.\\n  - If provider is considering androgen therapy despite normal labs, consult endocrinology or senior clinician; if used, follow clinic testosterone dosing protocols and monitoring (not recommended here).\\n\\nSPECIFIC NEXT STEPS, DOSING, MONITORING AND TIMELINES\\n\\nA. Progesterone supplementation (if provider approves)\\n- Dose: micronized progesterone 100 mg PO nightly at bedtime; consider titration to 200 mg nightly if inadequate symptom response.\\n- Monitoring:\\n  - Symptom check at 6–8 weeks (clinic or phone triage); if improved, continue and reassess at 3 months.\\n  - Re-evaluate clinically at 3 months. If objective hormone testing desired, repeat E2 and P4 as clinically indicated; note timing issues — for cycling patients, draw P4 in luteal window (approx 7 days post-ovulation) for a true luteal P4; for perimenopausal/irregular cycles interpret with caution.\\n  - Baseline LFTs not routinely required for short-term micronized progesterone in patients with normal baseline LFTs, but obtain if history of hepatic disease or symptoms arise; notify provider for any new abnormal LFTs.\\n- Physician notification:\\n  - Provider sign-off required prior to initiation.\\n  - Urgent notification if patient develops new severe headaches, visual changes, calf pain/swelling (possible VTE), or unexplained vaginal bleeding.\\n\\nB. Vitamin D\\n- Dose: Metagenics D3 2000 IU PO daily.\\n- Monitoring:\\n  - Recheck 25-OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks after initiating supplementation.\\n  - Target ≥60 ng/mL per protocol.\\n  - Notify provider if recheck >100 ng/mL or if symptoms of hypercalcemia.\\n\\nC. Testosterone\\n- No testosterone therapy recommended at this time.\\n- If future consideration occurs, obtain specialist consult and follow clinic dosing/monitoring pathways; document rationale for deviation from protocol.\\n\\nPATIENT EDUCATION POINTS TO COVER (staff to deliver)\\n- Explain relative progesterone deficiency: estrogen is adequate but not opposed sufficiently by progesterone; this can cause sleep disruption, mood changes, heavy periods, breast tenderness.\\n- If starting progesterone: advise taking at bedtime due to sedative effect; counsel on expected time course (may take 4–8 weeks for symptom improvement); review common side effects (drowsiness, breast tenderness, spotting) and red-flag symptoms that require immediate attention (signs of VTE, severe chest pain, sudden shortness of breath, severe leg swelling).\\n- For Vitamin D: explain goal to raise level to ≥60 ng/mL; adherence to daily supplement and plan for repeat lab in ~8–12 weeks.\\n- Reassure regarding testosterone: levels are within normal limits and adding testosterone is not indicated—investigation will focus on other causes.\\n\\nLIFESTYLE INTERVENTIONS (staff to recommend)\\n- Sleep hygiene optimization (fixed sleep schedule, reduce screen time, consider CBT-I referral if insomnia persists).\\n- Stress management: recommend mindfulness/relaxation training, consider referral to behavioral health for anxiety/mood if present.\\n- Nutrition: ensure adequate dietary protein and iron intake; emphasize whole-food sources of vitamin D (fatty fish) and calcium if indicated.\\n- Exercise: encourage regular aerobic and resistance exercise for mood, sleep, and metabolic health.\\n- If heavy menses reported: discuss iron status evaluation and consider temporary tranexamic acid or referral to gynecology if heavy bleeding persists (provider decision).\\n\\nADDITIONAL TESTING / REFERRALS TO CONSIDER\\n- Ferritin and iron panel: target ferritin >50 ng/mL for symptom resolution — order ferritin even if CBC normal.\\n- Comprehensive thyroid evaluation if persistent symptoms despite normal TSH: free T4, free T3, anti-TPO as clinically indicated.\\n- DHEA-S measurement if adrenal contribution suspected.\\n- If irregular/heavy bleeding or persistent pelvic symptoms: refer to gynecology for evaluation (ultrasound, endometrial assessment) per protocol.\\n- Mental health referral for persistent mood/anxiety symptoms.\\n- Sleep medicine referral if clinical suspicion for OSA despite STOP-BANG (if symptoms persist).\\n- If considering prolonged progesterone or other systemic HRT, consider baseline lipid panel, CMP/LFTs, and pregnancy testing if applicable.\\n\\nSUMMARY OF NEXT STEPS WITH TIMELINE (staff checklist)\\n- Immediately:\\n  - Confirm contraindications and pregnancy status. Notify provider for approval to start progesterone and vitamin D.\\n- Within 1–2 weeks after provider approval:\\n  - Start micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly (if approved) and Metagenics D3 2000 IU daily.\\n  - Provide patient education handout and review side effects and red flags; document counseling.\\n- 6–8 weeks:\\n  - Phone/clinic symptom check for progesterone tolerability and early response. If inadequate response and tolerated, provider may increase progesterone to 200 mg nightly.\\n- 8–12 weeks:\\n  - Recheck 25-OH vitamin D level. Reassess need for continued dose or adjustment to reach ≥60 ng/mL.\\n- 3 months:\\n  - Formal clinical reassessment for hormone symptom response. Consider repeat hormone panel only if clinically indicated and timed appropriately (luteal P4 for cycling patients). Reassess ferritin, thyroid tests, and additional labs ordered.\\n- Ongoing:\\n  - Do not initiate testosterone therapy unless new clinical indications and documented specialist/provider rationale.\\n\\nPhysician notification criteria (immediate)\\n- Any contraindication discovered (pregnancy, VTE history, hormone-sensitive cancer, severe hepatic disease).\\n- New concerning symptoms after starting progesterone (signs of VTE, severe LFT abnormalities, severe unexplained bleeding).\\n- Failure of symptoms to improve after 3 months — consider escalated evaluation/referral.\\n\\nReferences to protocol points (staff)\\n- Progesterone: micronized progesterone 100–200 mg nightly to oppose E2.\\n- Vitamin D: Metagenics D3 2000 Complex daily; recheck 8–12 weeks; goal ≥60 ng/mL.\\n- Testosterone: do not add if total/free/bioavailable within target ranges; evaluate alternate drivers (iron, thyroid, sleep, psychosocial).\\n\\nNote: Timing of hormone testing is important — for cycle-dependent interpretation, ensure luteal-phase timing for progesterone in regularly cycling patients (approx 7 days post-ovulation or mid-luteal, typically cycle day ~21 in 28-day cycle). In perimenopausal/irregular cycles interpret single hormone values cautiously and prioritize clinical response to therapy.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "SHBG", "value": 69.6, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 13, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": []}	2026-03-30 18:05:56.314399	1
82	Morgan Charleigh	female	2026-01-14 00:00:00	{"a1c": 4.6, "alt": 18, "ast": 19, "bun": 14, "co2": 22, "fsh": 3.99, "hdl": 91, "ldl": 104, "lpa": 17, "mcv": 94.7, "rbc": 4.94, "tsh": 1.45, "wbc": 7.6, "acne": false, "apoB": 85, "egfr": 80, "iron": 145, "shbg": 73.9, "tibc": 324, "dheas": 135, "hsCRP": 0.6, "onHRT": false, "folate": 12.9, "freeT4": 1.27, "sodium": 138, "albumin": 5.3, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 9.9, "glucose": 78, "bloating": false, "brainFog": true, "chloride": 100, "ferritin": 204, "hairLoss": false, "vitaminD": 44.1, "bilirubin": 1.2, "estradiol": 111, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": true, "lowLibido": false, "platelets": 276, "potassium": 4.1, "creatinine": 0.95, "hematocrit": 46.8, "hemoglobin": 15.9, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "vitaminB12": 762, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2026-01-14", "moodChanges": true, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Morgan Charleigh", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 35, "bmi": 20, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 112, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": true, "progesterone": 10.8, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 20.6, "totalProtein": 8.3, "lowMotivation": true, "triglycerides": 46, "menstrualPhase": "luteal", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": true, "freeTestosterone": 2, "totalCholesterol": 204}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History/pertinent symptoms: Patient reports intermittent fatigue and occasional low mood. Denies syncope, visual changes, headaches, excessive thirst, palpitations, chest pain, or new focal symptoms. No reported heavy vaginal bleeding or recent significant blood loss. No current use of prescription testosterone. Unknown current use of combined hormonal contraception or systemic estrogen (staff to confirm medication/HRT status). Patient counseled regarding hydration and supplement use.\\n- Menstrual/hormone context: Labs include luteal-phase progesterone (P4 10.8 ng/mL) consistent with sampling in luteal phase. No current menopause symptoms reported. Patient age 35.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: Not available in lab report; vitals/BMI not recorded at time of chart entry.\\n- RED FLAGS: None identified on current labs.\\n- ABNORMAL / BORDERLINE (flagged)\\n  - Hematocrit (Hct): 46.8 % — BORDERLINE (mildly high; may reflect relative hemoconcentration/dehydration)\\n  - Ferritin: 204 ng/mL — BORDERLINE (elevated/high-normal)\\n  - Vitamin D, 25-OH: 44.1 ng/mL — BORDERLINE (adequate but suboptimal for clinic target ≥60 ng/mL)\\n  - LDL-C: 104 mg/dL — BORDERLINE (near optimal)\\n  - Testosterone Pattern D: Total T 20.6 ng/dL; Free T 2 pg/mL; SHBG 73.9 nmol/L — BORDERLINE/pattern: “Adequate androgens, persistent symptoms” (biochemical androgens within reference range; symptoms likely non-androgenic)\\n- CBC:\\n  - Hemoglobin: 15.9 g/dL (normal)\\n  - Hematocrit: 46.8 % (BORDERLINE)\\n  - MCV: 94.7 fL (normal)\\n  - Platelets: 276 K/uL (normal)\\n- Iron studies / related:\\n  - Serum Iron: 145 µg/dL (normal)\\n  - TIBC: 324 µg/dL (normal)\\n  - Ferritin: 204 ng/mL (BORDERLINE)\\n  - Calculated Transferrin Saturation (TSAT) = Serum iron / TIBC ×100 = 145/324 ×100 ≈ 44.8% (borderline, just below 45%)\\n- Metabolic / Renal / Hepatic:\\n  - AST: 19 U/L (normal)\\n  - ALT: 18 U/L (normal)\\n  - Creatinine: 0.95 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - eGFR: 80 mL/min (normal)\\n- Lipids / Cardiovascular:\\n  - Total cholesterol: (not separately listed)\\n  - LDL-C: 104 mg/dL (BORDERLINE)\\n  - HDL-C: 91 mg/dL (normal, cardioprotective)\\n  - Triglycerides: 46 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - Apolipoprotein B: 85 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - Lipoprotein(a): 17 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - hs-CRP: 0.6 mg/L (normal, low)\\n  - 10-yr Total CVD risk (PREVENT AHA 2023): 0.2% (low)\\n  - 10-yr ASCVD risk (PREVENT): 0.1% (low)\\n  - 10-yr Heart Failure risk (PREVENT): 0.1% (low)\\n  - 30-yr CVD risk (PREVENT): 1.6% (low)\\n  - Statin recommendation: Lifestyle modification; statin not generally indicated at current risk level\\n- Hormones:\\n  - Estradiol (E2): 111 pg/mL (measured luteal) — normal for luteal phase\\n  - Progesterone (P4): 10.8 ng/mL (luteal) — consistent with luteal sampling (Perimenopause assessment: luteal progesterone documented)\\n  - FSH: 3.99 mIU/mL (normal)\\n  - Total Testosterone: 20.6 ng/dL (normal)\\n  - Free Testosterone: 2 pg/mL (normal)\\n  - SHBG: 73.9 nmol/L (elevated-high side but not extreme; may reflect estrogen effect)\\n  - DHEA-S: 135 µg/dL (normal)\\n- Metabolic/Endocrine screening:\\n  - TSH: 1.45 mIU/L (normal)\\n  - Hemoglobin A1c: 4.6% (normal)\\n- Vitamins / Nutrition:\\n  - Vitamin B12: 762 pg/mL (normal)\\n  - Folate: 12.9 ng/mL (normal)\\n  - Vitamin D, 25-OH: 44.1 ng/mL (BORDERLINE)\\n- Other:\\n  - Sleep Apnea risk (STOP-BANG): 0 points (low)\\n  - Magtein® Magnesium L-Threonate and HerWellness™ supplements suggested as medium-priority (clinic recommendations)\\n- Summary: Overall labs largely within normal limits. Key borderline items: Hct 46.8%, Ferritin 204 ng/mL with TSAT ≈44.8% (borderline), Vitamin D 44.1 ng/mL, LDL 104 mg/dL. Androgen profile consistent with adequate androgens (Pattern D).\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Borderline elevated hematocrit 46.8% (with normal hemoglobin 15.9 g/dL)\\n   - Significance: Mild relative polycythemia/hemoconcentration possible (dehydration, recent exercise, smoking, exogenous androgens). No current hyperviscosity symptoms reported.\\n   - Red flags: Notify provider if Hct >52% (women) or if symptomatic.\\n\\n2. Elevated ferritin 204 ng/mL with TSAT ≈44.8% (borderline high)\\n   - Significance: Could represent iron loading (borderline TSAT near 45%), inflammation, liver contribution, or metabolic factors. LFTs normal and hs-CRP low, making overt inflammation less likely. Alcohol use/medications should be reviewed.\\n   - Differential: Early hereditary hemochromatosis (HFE) vs functional iron sequestration vs high iron stores; TSAT just under threshold—requires repeat fasting iron studies and trend.\\n   - Action threshold: HFE genetic testing and hepatology referral considered if TSAT >45% or ferritin persistently >200–300 ng/mL.\\n\\n3. Vitamin D insufficient for clinic target: 25-OH = 44.1 ng/mL\\n   - Significance: Adequate per general thresholds but below clinic goal (≥60 ng/mL) if pursuing optimization for symptoms/health.\\n\\n4. Lipids: LDL 104 mg/dL with excellent HDL 91 mg/dL; overall very low calculated 10-year CVD risk\\n   - Significance: Lifestyle optimization recommended; statin not indicated at current risk level.\\n\\n5. Androgen profile consistent with \\"Pattern D — adequate androgens\\" (Total T 20.6 ng/dL, Free T 2 pg/mL, SHBG 73.9 nmol/L)\\n   - Significance: Biochemical androgens are within reference limits; symptoms such as fatigue, low libido, low mood more likely from non-androgen causes (thyroid, iron status, sleep, psychosocial). Do not initiate testosterone replacement at this time.\\n   - Note: Elevated SHBG may be influenced by exogenous estrogen; confirm medication/HRT/OCP use.\\n\\n6. Overall preventive risk low (AHA PREVENT); routine preventive follow-up and lifestyle counseling appropriate.\\n\\nPLAN:\\n(Reviewed and discussed with patient today; see timeline below)\\n\\nImmediate / Today (within 1 week)\\n- Counseling provided:\\n  - Instruct patient to increase oral hydration for 48–72 hours to address potential hemoconcentration; document counseling.\\n  - Instruct patient to stop any over-the-counter iron supplements until further evaluation.\\n  - Review and document full medication/supplement list, specifically: estrogen-containing medications (OCPs/HRT), iron supplements, multivitamins with iron, alcohol use. If on systemic estrogen, flag for provider review given high SHBG.\\n  - Educated patient that current total and free testosterone are within normal range and testosterone therapy is not indicated.\\n- Start supplement per clinic protocol:\\n  - Vitamin D3 (Metagenics D3) 2000 IU PO daily — initiate now; recheck 25-OH in 12 weeks.\\n  - Medium-priority supplements (optional per clinic plan): Magtein® Magnesium L‑Threonate 3 capsules daily (divided doses); HerWellness™ Rapid Stress Relief 1 soft chew PRN for stress. Document patient preference/consent if started.\\n\\nShort-term (1–4 weeks)\\n- Labs/orders:\\n  - Repeat CBC (Hgb, Hct) in 4 weeks after hydration to reassess hematocrit. If Hct remains ≥previous level or rises, notify provider.\\n  - Order fasting iron panel (fasting serum iron, TIBC) and repeat ferritin now or within 1–2 weeks for TSAT confirmation and trend. (Note: existing values yield TSAT ≈44.8% borderline; fasting repeat recommended.)\\n  - Calculate TSAT from fasting iron/TIBC; if TSAT >45% or ferritin persistently >200–300 ng/mL, notify provider for HFE genetic testing (C282Y/H63D) and consider hepatology referral.\\n- Provider notification criteria:\\n  - Notify provider if repeat Hct remains elevated or if patient develops hyperviscosity symptoms.\\n  - Notify provider if TSAT >45% or ferritin rises >300 ng/mL or LFTs become abnormal.\\n\\nIntermediate (6–12 weeks)\\n- Labs/orders:\\n  - Recheck 25-OH Vitamin D in ~12 weeks after starting 2000 IU daily.\\n  - Repeat ferritin and iron studies in 8–12 weeks if initial repeat remains abnormal or TSAT borderline to trend iron stores.\\n  - Repeat fasting lipid panel in 3–6 months (earlier if clinical change); continue lifestyle interventions.\\n- Symptom management:\\n  - If patient’s fatigue, low mood, or low libido persist despite normal androgen levels, schedule provider visit for further evaluation: consider thyroid expanded testing (free T4, free T3, anti-TPO), sleep assessment (consider objective testing if clinically indicated), mental health screening/referral, and assessment for other reversible causes (iron, B12, adrenal function if indicated).\\n\\nChronic / Ongoing\\n- Do NOT initiate testosterone therapy at this time. Testosterone may be considered only by provider if biochemically warranted on repeat testing and after addressing reversible causes.\\n- Lifestyle counseling:\\n  - Hydration as above; reduce alcohol intake (review quantity and frequency).\\n  - Mediterranean-style diet, regular physical activity (≥150 minutes moderate aerobic activity weekly plus resistance), weight management as indicated.\\n  - Sleep hygiene counseling; if persistent daytime sleepiness or high clinical suspicion for OSA despite STOP-BANG = 0, escalate to provider.\\n  - Mental health resources/behavioral health referral for persistent mood/stress as needed.\\n- Monitoring thresholds:\\n  - Hct: notify provider if >52% or symptomatic.\\n  - Ferritin/TSAT: notify provider if ferritin rises >300 ng/mL or TSAT >45%.\\n\\nSpecific medication/supplement adjustments\\n- No prescription medication changes indicated today.\\n- Stop OTC iron supplements until iron studies/ferritin clarified.\\n- Start Vitamin D3 2000 IU PO daily (documented above).\\n- Continue other chronic meds unchanged unless reviewed separately.\\n\\nDocumentation / Communication\\n- Results reviewed and discussed with patient today. Patient advised of plan for repeat labs and reasons for withholding iron supplementation and testosterone therapy at this time.\\n- Staff to document medication/supplement list and confirm estrogen/HRT status; flag for provider review regarding SHBG and estrogen exposure.\\n\\nFollow-up / Appointment timing\\n- Repeat CBC (Hgb/Hct): in 4 weeks after hydration.\\n- Fasting iron panel + ferritin: within 1–2 weeks (or asap fasting), and repeat in 8–12 weeks if abnormal.\\n- 25-OH Vitamin D: recheck in ~12 weeks after starting D3 2000 IU daily.\\n- Lipid panel: repeat in 3–6 months or sooner if clinical changes.\\n- Follow-up appointment with provider: arrange after repeat labs or sooner if symptoms worsen or if lab thresholds for notification met.\\n- Provider to be notified promptly if Hct >52% (women), TSAT >45%, ferritin rises >300 ng/mL, or if new hepatic abnormalities/symptoms arise.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\nDate: 03/30/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "preventRisk": {"ldlGoal": "No specific LDL target; focus on risk-based statin intensity", "riskCategory": "low", "tenYearASCVD": 0.001310328809550072, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0.0019209559278461441, "thirtyYearASCVD": 0.009875775266890464, "ageValidFor30Year": true, "thirtyYearTotalCVD": 0.01571937290692691, "tenYearHFPercentage": "0.1%", "tenYearHeartFailure": 0.0014167521811229865, "statinRecommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "tenYearCVDPercentage": "0.2%", "tenYearASCVDPercentage": "0.1%", "thirtyYearHFPercentage": "1.3%", "thirtyYearHeartFailure": 0.01309092375810651, "thirtyYearCVDPercentage": "1.6%", "thirtyYearASCVDPercentage": "1.0%"}, "supplements": [{"dose": "3 capsules daily (divided doses)", "name": "Magtein® Magnesium L-Threonate", "caution": "L-Threonate form crosses blood-brain barrier for cognitive and sleep support. Well-tolerated; gentle on GI system.", "category": "mineral", "priori