n, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "LDL 104 mg/dL; TC 204 mg/dL; Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["LDL 104 mg/dL", "TC 204 mg/dL", "Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)"]}, {"dose": "1 tablet daily with meal", "name": "D3 2000 Complex", "caution": "Maintenance dose for suboptimal levels. Comprehensive vitamin D support with cofactors.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "low", "rationale": "D3 2000 Complex provides maintenance vitamin D with cofactors to reach and maintain optimal range ≥60 ng/mL.", "indication": "Vitamin D 44.1 ng/mL (suboptimal, target ≥60)", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D is close to optimal but could benefit from a maintenance dose to reach the ideal range for bone and immune health.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 44.1 ng/mL (suboptimal, target ≥60)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 17, "apoBValue": 85, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "normal", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 0.1310328809550072, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": false}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\nYour results are reassuring in many areas. Key normal values: hemoglobin 15.9 g/dL, MCV 94.7 fL, platelets 276 K/uL, TSH 1.45 mIU/L, Hemoglobin A1c 4.6%, AST 19 U/L, ALT 18 U/L, eGFR 80 mL/min, HDL 91 mg/dL, triglycerides 46 mg/dL, ApoB 85 mg/dL, Lp(a) 17 mg/dL. Your luteal hormones (estradiol 111 pg/mL and progesterone 10.8 ng/mL) indicate a healthy ovulation pattern.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- HDL 91 mg/dL (protective) and triglycerides 46 mg/dL (healthy).\\n- LDL 104 mg/dL (near optimal). At your 10-year ASCVD risk of 0.1% (very low), a statin is not indicated; continue lifestyle maintenance.\\n- Perimenopause assessment: luteal progesterone 10.8 ng/mL shows robust ovulation.\\n- Testosterone pattern: Total T 20.6 ng/dL, Free T 2 pg/mL, SHBG 73.9 nmol/L — androgens are adequate, so symptoms like fatigue or low mood are likely from other causes (consider estrogen/progesterone balance if symptoms persist).\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + actions)\\n- Hematocrit 46.8% (slightly high) — may reflect mild dehydration. Action: drink an extra 16–24 oz (500–700 mL) of water daily for 48–72 hours and repeat CBC. If it stays high, discuss with your provider.\\n- Ferritin 204 ng/mL (elevated). Action steps: stop any iron supplements until you speak with your provider; avoid elective iron tablets or high-dose iron-containing multivitamins. Your liver tests (AST 19, ALT 18) are normal, but elevated ferritin can reflect iron overload or inflammation. Ask your clinician to check transferrin saturation and consider HFE genetic testing or referral if sustained. Repeat ferritin and iron studies in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Vitamin D 44.1 ng/mL (adequate but suboptimal). Action: maintain vitamin D with 800–1,000 IU vitamin D3 daily, include fatty fish 1–2x weekly, and get 10–20 minutes of sun exposure a few times weekly as safe. Recheck 25-OH vitamin D in 6–12 months.\\n\\nHeart-health plan (you are low risk: 10-year ASCVD 0.1%)\\nWhat this means: your 10-year risk of heart attack/stroke is about 0.1% — very low. To maintain this: aim for 150 minutes of moderate aerobic activity weekly (e.g., 30 minutes brisk walking 5 days), follow a Mediterranean-style diet (vegetables, whole grains, olive oil, fish, nuts), and maintain a healthy weight.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Hydrate now and repeat CBC in 2–3 months.\\n- Stop iron supplements now; check transferrin saturation and repeat ferritin in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Start/continue vitamin D 800–1,000 IU daily and recheck in 6–12 months.\\n- If fatigue, mood, sleep, or libido concerns persist, book follow-up to review hormone balance (estrogen/progesterone timing) and broader evaluation.\\n\\nYou’re doing well overall — small targeted steps will address the few abnormal values. Contact your clinic if new symptoms arise or to schedule the follow-up labs.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 15.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 46.8, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Slightly elevated hematocrit - may indicate dehydration.", "recommendation": "Encourage hydration. Repeat if persistent.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 94.7, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 94.7 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 276, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 111, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol appropriate for luteal phase.", "recommendation": "Normal finding. Continue monitoring.", "referenceRange": "50-250 pg/mL (luteal phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 10.8, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone appropriate for luteal phase.", "recommendation": "Confirms ovulation occurred.", "referenceRange": "5-20 ng/mL (luteal phase)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 3.99, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 20.6, "status": "normal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone within normal female range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.45, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "µg/dL", "value": 135, "status": "normal", "category": "DHEA-S", "interpretation": "DHEA-S within normal range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "65-400 µg/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 204, "status": "borderline", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Elevated ferritin.", "recommendation": "May indicate inflammation, hemochromatosis, or liver disease. Correlate clinically.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 44.1, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D adequate but suboptimal (41-59 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 2000 Complex daily to reach optimal range ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 762, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 4.6, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 104, "status": "borderline", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Near optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet and exercise.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 91, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 46, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.1920955927846144, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 0.2% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.1310328809550072, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 0.1%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.14167521811229863, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.1%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 1.5719372906926912, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 1.6%, ASCVD 1.0%, Heart Failure 1.3%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 19, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 18, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 80, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.95, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 85, "status": "normal", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Apo B at optimal level.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 17, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 324, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 145, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 12.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 0.6, "status": "normal", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Low cardiovascular inflammation risk.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 10.8, "status": "normal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Luteal Progesterone", "interpretation": "Luteal progesterone of 10.8 ng/mL indicates robust ovulation. This is an optimal level suggesting good ovarian function.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Ovulatory function appears intact. Continue monitoring as part of routine perimenopause surveillance. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your progesterone level shows healthy ovulation this cycle.", "referenceRange": "≥10 ng/mL optimal, 8–10 borderline, <8 low, <5 anovulatory"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 20.6, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone Pattern D: Adequate Androgens, Persistent Symptoms", "interpretation": "Adequate Androgens pattern identified (Total T: 20.6 ng/dL (within range); Free T: 2 pg/mL; SHBG: 73.9 nmol/L). Total, free, and bioavailable testosterone are within normal ranges and SHBG is not extreme. If the patient reports fatigue, low mood, sleep disruption, or low libido, these symptoms are likely driven by non-androgenic factors. Findings to evaluate: Estrogen/Progesterone balance should be assessed (E2: 111 pg/mL, P4: 10.8 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Do not reflexively add testosterone when levels are adequate — look deeper. Evaluate iron status (ferritin target >50 for symptom resolution), thyroid function (optimize TSH), and sleep quality (consider sleep apnea screening with STOP-BANG). Assess estrogen and progesterone balance — E2/P4 imbalance is a common driver of persistent symptoms. Address CNS and psychosocial drivers: chronic stress, HPA axis dysregulation, mood disorders. Consider DHEA-S if not already checked. Support with lifestyle optimization: sleep hygiene, stress management, nutrition. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your testosterone levels are actually in a healthy range, which is good news. When symptoms persist despite normal testosterone, it usually means something else is contributing. Your provider will look at other factors like iron levels, thyroid function, sleep quality, and hormone balance to find what's driving your symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Total T 15-70 ng/dL, Free T 0.5-5.0 pg/mL (all within range)"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- Labs show generally normal panel with a few borderline/abnormal findings: Hematocrit 46.8% (mildly high), Ferritin 204 ng/mL (high-normal/elevated), Vitamin D 25-OH 44.1 ng/mL (adequate but suboptimal), LDL 104 mg/dL (near-optimal). Androgen profile consistent with “Pattern D — adequate androgens” (Total T 20.6 ng/dL, Free T 2 pg/mL, SHBG 73.9 nmol/L). Luteal progesterone (P4 10.8 ng/mL) suggests sampling was in luteal phase — interpret estrogen/progesterone balance in that context (E2 111 pg/mL measured in same phase).\\n- Key clinical points for provider review: do not reflexively treat with testosterone (androgens are adequate). Elevated ferritin needs correlation for inflammation, liver disease, iron overload; elevated hematocrit may reflect relative hemoconcentration/dehydration or other causes. Vitamin D is sufficient but below target if aiming for ≥60 ng/mL.\\n\\nPriority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL (notify provider immediately if any of these occur)\\n- Notify provider immediately if:\\n  * Hematocrit rises to >52% (women) or if patient develops symptoms of hyperviscosity (headache, visual changes, syncope).\\n  * Ferritin increases to >300 ng/mL or transferrin saturation (TSAT) >45% (suggestive of possible hereditary hemochromatosis).\\n  * New hepatic symptoms or AST/ALT become abnormal.\\n\\nURGENT (action within 1–2 weeks)\\n- Hematocrit 46.8%:\\n  * Staff action: Advise increased oral hydration now; document instructions and reassess.\\n  * Repeat serum hematocrit/hemoglobin in 4 weeks after hydration and any recent activity/blood loss corrections.\\n  * Provider notification if repeat hematocrit still elevated or rising.\\n- Ferritin 204 ng/mL:\\n  * Staff action: Do not start oral iron supplementation; review medication list and recent supplements (stop any over-the-counter iron until further evaluation).\\n  * Order fasting iron studies to calculate transferrin saturation (if not already calculated): serum iron and TIBC/ transferrin saturation; confirm if prior serum iron and TIBC values available — calculate TSAT now.\\n  * If TSAT >45% or ferritin remains >200–300 on repeat, notify provider for HFE genetic testing (C282Y/H63D) and consider hepatology referral.\\n  * Review alcohol intake and hepatotoxic medication use and document.\\n- Review estrogen therapy / combined hormonal contraception status:\\n  * Staff: Document whether patient is on exogenous estrogen or combined OCP/HRT (estrogen increases SHBG). If on estrogen and symptomatic, flag for provider to consider dose/formulation change or progesterone optimization.\\n\\nROUTINE (action within 4–12 weeks)\\n- Vitamin D 25-OH 44.1 ng/mL:\\n  * Start/continue Metagenics D3 2000 IU PO daily per clinic protocol.\\n  * Recheck 25-OH vitamin D in 12 weeks.\\n  * Counsel on safe sun exposure and dietary sources.\\n- Lipids:\\n  * LDL 104 mg/dL — reinforce diet/exercise; repeat lipid panel in 6–12 months or sooner if other CVD risk factors change.\\n- Androgens / Symptoms:\\n  * No testosterone replacement indicated based on current androgen values. Do not initiate testosterone without provider-level discussion and documented indication.\\n  * If symptoms (fatigue, low libido, low mood) persist, staff should arrange provider evaluation to pursue non-androgenic causes (iron optimization if low, thyroid workup, sleep evaluation, psychosocial assessment).\\n\\nTestosterone dose recommendations (if applicable)\\n- Not applicable at this time: do not prescribe testosterone. Specific guidance:\\n  * Provider-level decision only if repeated testing shows biochemical androgen deficiency or if symptoms persist with documented low total/free T and low SHBG after correcting reversible causes.\\n  * If provider elects to trial testosterone in the future, follow clinic protocols for dosing, baseline labs (liver, lipids), and monitoring of total T, hematocrit, lipids, and pregnancy testing if applicable — but do not start now.\\n\\nLifestyle interventions for staff to counsel patients on (to cover during visit)\\n- Hydration: encourage increased oral fluids for 48–72 hours and reassess hematocrit.\\n- Alcohol: limit alcohol intake (document baseline use) given ferritin elevation and potential liver contribution.\\n- Diet: reinforce Mediterranean-style diet for LDL management; avoid unnecessary iron-fortified supplements until ferritin/TSAT clarified.\\n- Sleep: review sleep hygiene; if clinical suspicion despite STOP-BANG result, escalate to provider for possible sleep medicine referral or objective testing.\\n- Stress and mental health: screen for depression/anxiety; offer behavioral health referral for persistent mood/stress issues.\\n- Exercise: encourage regular moderate aerobic activity 150 min/week and resistance training; discuss weight management if applicable.\\n\\nAdditional testing or referrals to consider\\n- Immediate additional tests:\\n  * Calculate transferrin saturation (TSAT) from existing serum iron/TIBC; if not available/accurate, order fasting serum iron, TIBC and repeat ferritin.\\n  * Repeat hematocrit/hemoglobin in 4 weeks after hydration.\\n  * Recheck Vitamin D in 12 weeks after supplementation.\\n  * Confirm medication list (estrogen, OCPs, HRT, supplemental iron) and stop exogenous iron until reviewed.\\n- Conditional/if indicated:\\n  * HFE genetic testing (C282Y and H63D) if TSAT >45% or ferritin persistently >200–300 ng/mL on repeat.\\n  * Hepatic panel and ultrasound if ferritin elevated and LFTs abnormal or significant alcohol history.\\n  * Inflammatory evaluation: repeat hs-CRP (already normal) or other inflammatory markers if clinical suspicion.\\n  * Thyroid panel expansion (free T4, free T3, anti-TPO antibodies) if symptoms suggest thyroid dysfunction despite normal TSH or if provider planning therapy adjustments.\\n  * DHEA-S if not already checked (noted as normal in this panel).\\n- Referrals:\\n  * Endocrinology for complex iron overload/hemochromatosis or for complex hormone balance if provider deems necessary.\\n  * Sleep medicine if high clinical suspicion for OSA despite STOP-BANG or persistent daytime somnolence.\\n  * Behavioral health/psychology for stress, mood, or HPA axis-related symptoms.\\n\\nPatient education points for staff to deliver\\n- Explain that current testosterone levels are within expected range and adding testosterone is not indicated at this time.\\n- Advise increased fluid intake and avoidance of unnecessary iron supplements pending ferritin/TSAT clarification.\\n- Explain vitamin D supplementation plan (Metagenics D3 2000 IU daily) and plan to recheck in ~12 weeks.\\n- Reinforce lifestyle measures (diet, exercise, sleep, limit alcohol) and that these can improve LDL and symptoms.\\n- Inform patient that provider will review ferritin and hematocrit results and may order follow-up testing; provide timeframe for follow-up labs.\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (staff actions)\\n1. Immediate (today / within 1 week)\\n   - Document medication/supplement list; instruct patient to stop OTC iron until reviewed.\\n   - Counsel on hydration; record counseling in chart.\\n   - Start vitamin D 2000 IU daily (if protocol permits immediate start) and document.\\n   - Flag chart for provider review regarding elevated ferritin and elevated SHBG in context of estrogen status.\\n2. Short-term (1–4 weeks)\\n   - Order/calculate TSAT (fasting iron + TIBC if needed). If TSAT >45% or ferritin increases, notify provider for HFE testing.\\n   - Repeat hematocrit/hemoglobin in 4 weeks after hydration.\\n   - Provider notified if repeat hematocrit remains elevated or if ferritin >300 or TSAT >45%.\\n3. Intermediate (8–12 weeks)\\n   - Recheck 25-OH vitamin D in 12 weeks after supplementation.\\n   - Repeat ferritin (and iron studies) in 8–12 weeks if initial abnormality unresolved.\\n   - Repeat lipid panel in 3–6 months per routine preventive care or sooner if clinical changes.\\n4. Ongoing\\n   - Do not initiate testosterone therapy without provider-level decision.\\n   - If symptoms persist despite normal androgen levels, schedule provider visit to evaluate thyroid optimization, iron status, sleep, mental health and consider targeted referrals (endocrinology, sleep medicine, behavioral health).\\n\\nPhysician notification thresholds (staff)\\n- Notify provider promptly for:\\n  * Hematocrit >52% (women) or symptomatic hyperviscosity\\n  * Ferritin rising >300 ng/mL or TSAT >45%\\n  * New abnormal LFTs or hepatic symptoms\\n  * Persistent abnormal labs on repeat testing or unresolved concerning symptoms\\n\\nNote to provider reviewers\\n- Androgen panel supports conservative management; consider evaluating estrogen/progesterone balance (E2/P4 ratio in luteal sampling) and review SHBG-raising factors (exogenous estrogen, thyroid status, hepatic function) before considering androgen therapy.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 0.51, "detail": "Triglycerides 46 / HDL 91 = 0.51", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "4.6%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 73.9, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 18, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "85 mg/dL", "detail": "Normal atherogenic particle count", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "0.6 mg/L", "detail": "Low inflammatory burden", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation", "confidence": "high", "description": "Pattern consistent with HPA-axis stress and cortisol dysregulation. Chronic stress depletes DHEA-S, disrupts sleep, and impairs cognitive and emotional function.", "supportingFindings": ["Sleep disruption reported", "Irritability reported", "Low energy / fatigue reported", "Low motivation reported", "Brain fog reported"]}]}	2026-03-30 19:44:07.670942	1
83	Morgan Charleigh	female	2026-01-18 00:00:00	{"fsh": 6.39, "acne": false, "iron": 79, "tibc": 241, "onHRT": false, "anxiety": false, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "ferritin": 217, "hairLoss": false, "estradiol": 50, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": true, "lowLibido": true, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2026-01-18", "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Morgan Charleigh", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 35, "bmi": 20, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 110, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 1.04, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 13.2, "lowMotivation": true, "menstrualPhase": "follicular", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": true}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- HPI: Morgan Charleigh is a 35-year-old female presenting for review of recent laboratory results obtained 03/30/2026. Patient reports intermittent fatigue and decreased libido. No jaundice, abdominal pain, arthralgias, syncope, or overt bleeding. No current complaints of virilization (voice change, clitoromegaly, severe acne). Menstrual history: not documented in chart today — clinician to confirm cycle day and current use of hormonal therapy (OCP/HRT) as this affects hormone interpretation. No reported use of iron supplements at time of visit (patient counseled to stop if taking).\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: Not recorded in chart for this visit. BMI not available.\\n- Risk scores: STOP-BANG sleep apnea risk = 0 (low).\\n\\nLaboratory results (03/30/2026)\\n- Hormones\\n  - Estradiol: 50 pg/mL (within reference range)\\n  - Progesterone: 1.04 ng/mL (within reference range)\\n  - FSH: 6.39 mIU/mL (within reference range)\\n  - Total Testosterone: 13.2 ng/dL — BORDERLINE low\\n- Iron studies / related\\n  - Serum Iron: 79 µg/dL (reported as normal)\\n  - TIBC (total iron binding capacity): 241 µg/dL — BORDERLINE low\\n  - Ferritin: 217 ng/mL — BORDERLINE high\\n  - Calculated Transferrin Saturation (TSAT): 79 / 241 × 100 ≈ 32.8% (intermediate; not >45%)\\n- Other labs\\n  - CBC: Not available / not provided\\n  - CMP / LFTs (AST, ALT, ALP, bilirubin): Not available / not performed in this lab set\\n  - Lipids, fasting glucose / HbA1c: Not available / not performed in this lab set\\n  - CRP / ESR: Not available\\n  - Hepatitis serologies: Not available\\n- Notes: No abnormal/critical labs flagged. No red flags present at time of review.\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1) Borderline low total testosterone — 13.2 ng/dL.\\n   - Clinical significance: May contribute to decreased libido and fatigue. Need correlation with symptoms, menstrual status, and concurrent estrogen/progestin therapy (estrogen increases SHBG and lowers free testosterone).\\n   - Differential: physiologic variation, effects of exogenous estrogen/OCP/HRT, SHBG elevation, adrenal causes less likely without other signs.\\n   - Red flags: none currently. Baseline safety labs required prior to any consideration of testosterone therapy.\\n\\n2) Elevated ferritin 217 ng/mL with low TIBC 241 µg/dL; TSAT ≈ 33%.\\n   - Clinical significance: Pattern (high ferritin, low TIBC, TSAT in intermediate range) suggests possible inflammation/chronic disease or hepatic contribution rather than classic hereditary hemochromatosis (TSAT >45% expected in iron overload). Ferritin is an acute phase reactant.\\n   - Differential: inflammation/chronic disease, hepatic injury, metabolic syndrome, non-hemochromatosis iron loading, early hereditary hemochromatosis (less likely given TSAT ≈33%), recent iron supplementation.\\n   - Red flags requiring prompt clinician notification: new/worsening jaundice, RUQ pain, marked fatigue with abnormal LFTs, or if repeat TSAT >45% or ferritin increases significantly.\\n\\n3) Other reproductive hormones (estradiol, progesterone, FSH) within reported ranges.\\n   - Clinical significance: Results must be interpreted in context of cycle day and exogenous hormone use. No immediate action required unless symptoms or cycle timing suggest otherwise.\\n\\n4) Sleep apnea risk low (STOP-BANG = 0).\\n   - Clinical significance: No further sleep apnea workup indicated based on score.\\n\\nPLAN:\\nProblem 1 — Borderline low total testosterone (13.2 ng/dL)\\n- Actions:\\n  1. Correlate with documented symptoms (decreased libido, fatigue) and confirm menstrual cycle day and any current estrogen/progestin therapy. Document in chart.\\n  2. If considering diagnosis/treatment of androgen deficiency, obtain baseline labs prior to any testosterone therapy: morning (AM) fasting total testosterone, free testosterone, SHBG, LFTs (AST, ALT), fasting glucose or HbA1c, lipid panel, and pregnancy test if applicable. Order these at next visit or prior to initiation.\\n  3. For clinician review only — suggested initial therapeutic options if after shared decision-making testosterone trial is pursued:\\n     - Preferred: Transdermal testosterone gel delivering 300 mcg/day (0.3 mg/day) applied daily; reassess clinically and with labs.\\n     - Alternative: Compounded transdermal gel 300–500 mcg/day if clinic protocol supports.\\n     - IM option (less preferred for physiologic dosing): Testosterone cypionate 25–50 mg IM every 2–4 weeks — only with physician/endocrinology approval.\\n  4. Monitoring if therapy initiated: check morning total testosterone and LFTs at 3 months after steady state, then at 6 months, then annually if stable. Monitor for virilization, lipid/glucose changes; stop and notify physician for virilization or marked lab abnormalities.\\n- Timing: Baseline testosterone/SHBG and metabolic/liver labs prior to any therapy; monitoring per schedule above.\\n\\nProblem 2 — Elevated ferritin 217 ng/mL with low TIBC 241 µg/dL (TSAT ≈ 32.8%)\\n- Immediate / urgent actions (staff/clinician):\\n  1. Advise patient to stop any iron supplementation if currently taking and avoid additional dietary iron loading and high-dose vitamin C with meals until workup complete. Document counseling provided.\\n  2. Order repeat iron studies with numeric results and TSAT calculation: Serum iron, TIBC, transferrin saturation (TSAT = serum iron/TIBC ×100). Include ferritin on repeat.\\n  3. Order baseline LFTs (AST, ALT, ALP, total bilirubin) and inflammatory marker (CRP or ESR) within 1–2 weeks.\\n  4. Order CBC (hemoglobin, hematocrit) to assess for anemia/erythrocytosis.\\n  5. Consider hepatitis B/C serologies if LFTs abnormal or risk factors present.\\n  6. If repeat TSAT persistently >45% or ferritin continues to rise, consider HFE genetic testing (C282Y, H63D) and referral to hematology for evaluation ± therapeutic phlebotomy; consider hepatic MRI for iron quantification and hepatology referral if LFTs abnormal.\\n- Timing: Repeat iron panel and LFTs within 1–2 weeks. CBC and CRP/ESR within 1–2 weeks.\\n\\nProblem 3 — Other reproductive hormones within reported ranges\\n- Actions:\\n  1. Document menstrual cycle day and any HRT/OCP use on future lab requisitions.\\n  2. No immediate intervention; correlate clinically as needed.\\n- Timing: As part of baseline endocrine evaluation if considering hormonal therapy.\\n\\nProblem 4 — Sleep apnea risk low\\n- Actions:\\n  - No further sleep apnea evaluation indicated based on STOP-BANG score of 0. Address sleep hygiene as part of fatigue management if clinically appropriate.\\n\\nSupplements and lifestyle\\n- Supplements:\\n  - Magtein® Magnesium L-Threonate recommended (medium-priority): 3 capsules daily divided doses (documented recommendation for clinician/staff to provide). Verify formulation and allergy history prior to recommending.\\n- Lifestyle counseling provided (documented):\\n  - Avoid iron supplementation and high-dose vitamin C with meals until iron workup complete.\\n  - Limit alcohol intake pending LFTs.\\n  - Encourage weight optimization, regular exercise (≥150 min/week moderate), balanced diet, and sleep hygiene for fatigue management.\\n  - Counsel regarding interaction between estrogen therapy and SHBG/free testosterone; document any HRT/OCP use.\\n- Orders placed / to be placed:\\n  1. Repeat iron panel with numeric serum iron, TIBC, ferritin and automatic TSAT calculation — within 1–2 weeks.\\n  2. LFT panel (AST, ALT, ALP, total bilirubin) — within 1–2 weeks.\\n  3. CRP or ESR — within 1–2 weeks.\\n  4. CBC (Hgb/Hct) — within 1–2 weeks.\\n  5. Hepatitis serologies if LFTs abnormal or risk factors present — clinician discretion.\\n  6. Baseline endocrine/metabolic labs if testosterone therapy being considered: AM fasting total and free testosterone, SHBG, fasting glucose or HbA1c, lipid panel, pregnancy test if applicable — prior to initiation.\\n- Monitoring / follow-up:\\n  - Urgent labs (iron panel + LFTs + CBC + CRP/ESR): within 1–2 weeks.\\n  - Clinician to review repeat results promptly; if repeat TSAT >45% or ferritin rising → urgent hematology/hepatology referral.\\n  - If testosterone therapy initiated: recheck morning total testosterone and LFTs at 3 months after steady state, then 6 months, then annually if stable.\\n  - Routine follow-up visit recommended within 2 weeks to review repeat labs and counseling, or sooner if new/worsening symptoms.\\n  - Routine recheck window for broader care: 3–6 months.\\n\\nCommunication / education:\\n- Results reviewed and discussed with patient today. Patient counseled to stop iron supplements if taking, to avoid iron-loading behaviors, and to limit alcohol pending LFTs. Discussed need for repeat iron studies and LFTs and potential next steps depending on results. Patient verbalized understanding and agreed to recommended testing and follow-up.\\n- Chart flagged for clinician review with recommendation set and symptom checklist (fatigue, libido changes, family history of hemochromatosis).\\n\\nDate: 03/30/2026\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "supplements": [{"dose": "3 capsules daily (divided doses)", "name": "Magtein® Magnesium L-Threonate", "caution": "L-Threonate form crosses blood-brain barrier for cognitive and sleep support. Well-tolerated; gentle on GI system.", "category": "mineral", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Magtein is the only magnesium form clinically shown to cross the blood-brain barrier. Supports quality sleep, cognitive function, GABA activity, and stress resilience.", "indication": "Sleep disruption reported; Low energy reported", "phenotypes": ["Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "This special form of magnesium is designed to reach the brain, where it supports better sleep quality, mental clarity, and a calmer nervous system.", "supportingFindings": ["Sleep disruption reported", "Low energy reported"]}, {"dose": "1 soft chew during times of stress", "name": "HerWellness™ Rapid Stress Relief", "caution": "Fast-acting L-Theanine and Lactium formula. Non-drowsy. Promotes calm within 1 hour. Contains milk.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "low", "rationale": "HerWellness Rapid Stress Relief provides fast-acting stress support with L-Theanine (200mg) and Lactium. Promotes calm within 1 hour without drowsiness.", "indication": "Sleep disruption (stress-related)", "phenotypes": ["Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation"], "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This fast-acting supplement helps your body respond to stress more calmly. It promotes relaxation within about an hour and can be taken as needed without causing drowsiness.", "supportingFindings": ["Sleep disruption (stress-related)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\n- Most of your reproductive hormones look appropriate for the follicular / pre‑ovulatory phase of your cycle, which is good. A few blood tests are borderline and worth following: your total testosterone is low at 13.2 ng/dL, your ferritin is high at 217 ng/mL, and your TIBC is low at 241 µg/dL. Serum iron is 79 µg/dL and your calculated transferrin saturation is about 32.8% (79 ÷ 241 × 100). No critical findings.\\n\\nPositive findings (what’s normal)\\n- Estradiol 50 pg/mL — appropriate for follicular phase.\\n- Progesterone 1.04 ng/mL — appropriate for the pre‑ovulatory phase.\\n- FSH 6.39 mIU/mL — appropriate for follicular phase.\\n- Serum iron 79 µg/dL — within the normal range.\\n- STOP‑BANG sleep apnea score 0/8 — LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea.\\n\\nAreas to improve (exact values + concrete actions)\\n1. Low total testosterone: 13.2 ng/dL (suboptimal)\\n   - Sleep: prioritize 7–8 hours nightly (consistent bed/wake times).\\n   - Strength training: do resistance workouts 2–3 times per week (squats, lunges, free weights or machines).\\n   - Stress management: add daily 10–20 minute stress reduction (walk, meditation, gentle yoga).\\n   - Recheck testosterone in ~3 months after lifestyle changes and discuss symptoms (low energy, low libido, irregular periods) with your provider.\\n\\n2. Elevated ferritin: 217 ng/mL and low TIBC: 241 µg/dL (possible inflammation or iron handling issue)\\n   - Stop any iron supplements and avoid high‑dose vitamin C with meals until clarified.\\n   - Limit alcohol to <2 drinks/week while we evaluate causes of high ferritin.\\n   - Do not start new iron-containing vitamins without testing.\\n   - Next tests to consider: repeat ferritin and TIBC in 6–12 weeks, C‑reactive protein (CRP) or ESR (to check inflammation), liver panel, and possibly HFE genetic testing or referral to hematology if ferritin remains high or transferrin saturation increases (>45%).\\n\\nBone health and thyroid (female-focused)\\n- Because hormones and iron can affect bone and energy, ensure weight‑bearing exercise (30 min most days), aim for 1,000–1,200 mg calcium/day from food/supplements and 800–1,000 IU vitamin D daily or check vitamin D level. If you have fatigue, hair loss, cold intolerance or heavy periods, ask your provider to check TSH and free T4.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Stop iron supplements now. Schedule follow-up blood work: repeat ferritin/TIBC and CRP and a liver panel in 6–12 weeks. Recheck testosterone after ~3 months of lifestyle changes or sooner if symptoms are significant. Discuss HFE testing or hematology referral if ferritin stays high. Contact your provider sooner for new or worsening symptoms. You’re doing well with cycle hormones and sleep apnea risk — small lifestyle steps can help the borderline results; we’ll track them together.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 50, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol appropriate for follicular phase.", "recommendation": "Normal finding. Continue monitoring if needed for fertility.", "referenceRange": "20-150 pg/mL (follicular phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1.04, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone appropriate for pre-ovulatory phase.", "recommendation": "Normal finding.", "referenceRange": "<1.5 ng/mL (pre-ovulation)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 6.39, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH appropriate for follicular phase.", "recommendation": "Normal finding.", "referenceRange": "3-10 mIU/mL (follicular phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 13.2, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Low testosterone.", "recommendation": "May contribute to low libido or fatigue. Consider HRT discussion.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 217, "status": "borderline", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Elevated ferritin.", "recommendation": "May indicate inflammation, hemochromatosis, or liver disease. Correlate clinically.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 241, "status": "borderline", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "Low TIBC - possible iron overload or chronic disease.", "recommendation": "Evaluate for hemochromatosis or chronic inflammation.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 79, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- Key abnormal labs: Total testosterone 13.2 ng/dL (borderline low); Ferritin 217 ng/mL (mildly elevated); TIBC 241 µg/dL (low).\\n- Interpretation for staff:\\n  * Low borderline total testosterone may contribute to symptoms such as low libido or fatigue—correlate with documented symptoms, menstrual status, and current hormone therapies (HRT/OCP).\\n  * Elevated ferritin with low TIBC raises concern for iron overload or an acute/chronic inflammatory state rather than iron deficiency. Serum iron reported as \\"normal\\" (no numeric value provided) — need transferrin saturation (TSAT) to help differentiate.\\n  * Other reproductive hormones listed as normal (estradiol, progesterone, FSH) — consider cycle phase when last measured (note for clinician review).\\n\\nPriority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\n- CRITICAL (notify physician immediately)\\n  * If patient has symptoms of liver disease (jaundice, right upper quadrant pain), significant fatigue with abnormal LFTs, or if transferrin saturation (TSAT) >45% on repeat labs → notify physician immediately for expedited evaluation/referral.\\n- URGENT (action within 1–2 weeks)\\n  * Stop any iron supplementation and advise patient to avoid additional dietary iron loading (no iron-rich supplements, avoid high-dose vitamin C with meals) until iron workup is complete.\\n  * Order repeat iron panel to include numeric values and calculate TSAT: Serum iron, TIBC, transferrin saturation (TSAT = serum iron / TIBC ×100).\\n  * Order baseline LFTs (AST, ALT, ALP, bilirubin) and inflammatory marker (CRP or ESR) to assess for hepatic causes or inflammation.\\n  * Obtain hepatitis serologies if clinically indicated or if LFTs abnormal.\\n- ROUTINE (within 4–12 weeks)\\n  * If considering testosterone therapy, confirm baseline safety labs (lipid panel, fasting glucose or HbA1c, LFTs, SHBG, morning total and free testosterone) prior to initiation.\\n  * Document menstrual cycle day and concurrent estrogen/progestin therapies on lab requisition for hormone interpretation.\\n\\nTestosterone recommendations (FOR CLINIC STAFF / for clinician review)\\n- Indication: Consider only if patient reports clinically significant symptoms consistent with androgen deficiency and after shared decision-making with the clinician.\\n- Baseline prerequisites before initiation:\\n  * Morning total and free testosterone (fasting AM draw), SHBG, LFTs, fasting glucose/HbA1c, lipid panel, pregnancy test if applicable.\\n  * Document current estrogen therapies (estrogen increases SHBG and lowers free testosterone).\\n- Suggested starting regimen (presented as protocol options for physician to order):\\n  * Preferred initial trial: Transdermal testosterone gel 300 mcg/day (0.3 mg/day) applied daily; reassess.\\n  * Alternative: Transdermal 1% compounded gels delivering 300–500 mcg/day if clinic uses compounded formulations.\\n  * IM option (less preferred for physiologic female dosing): Testosterone cypionate 25–50 mg IM every 2–4 weeks — only if clinic protocol supports and with endocrinology/physician approval.\\n- Monitoring after initiation:\\n  * Check morning total testosterone and LFTs at 3 months after steady state, then at 6 months, then annually if stable.\\n  * Target: total testosterone within mid-normal physiologic female range per clinic reference; adjust dose up or down in 100–200 µg increments only after clinical review if symptoms persist or if levels outside target.\\n  * Monitor for adverse effects (virilization, acne, voice change, abnormal lipids, LFT elevation). If virilization signs occur, stop therapy and notify physician urgently.\\n- Physician notification criteria during therapy:\\n  * Total testosterone above upper female reference range, new/worsening virilization, significant LFT elevations, or lipid/glucose abnormalities requiring intervention.\\n\\nLifestyle interventions to recommend to patient (staff to counsel)\\n- Immediate:\\n  * Cease iron supplements and avoid iron-fortified supplements until evaluation completed.\\n  * Limit alcohol intake pending LFTs (alcohol can increase ferritin and cause hepatic injury).\\n- General:\\n  * Encourage weight optimization (weight loss if BMI elevated) and structured exercise (≥150 min/week moderate activity) — both reduce inflammatory markers and improve metabolic profile.\\n  * Advise balanced diet; avoid unnecessary red meat and iron-fortified foods if ferritin remains elevated pending definitive workup.\\n  * Address sleep hygiene and stress reduction for fatigue management.\\n- Reproductive-specific:\\n  * Document and counsel regarding HRT or OCP interactions: ongoing estrogen therapy increases SHBG and may lower free testosterone—review timing of labs relative to hormone therapy.\\n\\nAdditional testing and referrals to consider\\n- Immediate/additional labs:\\n  * Repeat iron studies with numeric serum iron, TIBC, and calculate TSAT.\\n  * LFTs (AST, ALT, ALP, bilirubin), CRP or ESR.\\n  * HFE gene testing (C282Y, H63D) if ferritin remains elevated or TSAT >45%.\\n  * Hepatitis B/C serology if LFTs abnormal or risk factors present.\\n  * Serum hemoglobin/hematocrit to assess for erythrocytosis or anemia.\\n  * If considering fertility assessment or if patient has reproductive concerns, consider AMH and pelvic ultrasound / antral follicle count per clinician decision.\\n- Imaging/referral:\\n  * If TSAT persistently >45% or ferritin significantly elevated on repeat testing → refer to hematology for evaluation ± therapeutic phlebotomy and consider hepatic MRI for iron quantification.\\n  * If LFTs abnormal → refer to hepatology.\\n  * Consider endocrinology referral if complex hormone optimization or if considering higher-dose testosterone regimens or if abnormal adrenal/other endocrine labs.\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (staff action checklist)\\n- Within 48–72 hours:\\n  1. Stop any patient-reported iron supplements; counsel avoidance of iron loading and high alcohol intake until workup.\\n  2. Flag chart for clinician review due to ferritin 217 ng/mL and low TIBC; include symptom checklist (fatigue, arthralgias, abdominal pain, family history of hemochromatosis).\\n- Within 1–2 weeks (urgent orders):\\n  3. Order repeat iron panel with numeric serum iron, TIBC, and calculate TSAT; order LFTs and CRP/ESR; order hepatitis panel if indicated.\\n  4. If patient is symptomatic or if repeat TSAT >45% or ferritin increases, notify physician for expedited referral to hematology/hepatology.\\n- Within 4 weeks:\\n  5. If testosterone therapy being considered, obtain baseline AM/fasting total and free testosterone, SHBG, lipids, fasting glucose/HbA1c, LFTs; schedule clinician visit for shared decision-making.\\n- At 3 months after any testosterone start:\\n  6. Recheck morning total testosterone and LFTs; assess symptoms and side effects; adjust dose per clinician.\\n- Ongoing:\\n  7. Document all education provided to patient (stop iron, lifestyle advice, expected monitoring) in chart and ensure follow-up appointment scheduled.\\n\\nNotes for staff to include in clinician handoff\\n- Provide exact numeric values available (include morning draw time and cycle day if known).\\n- Document current HRT/OCP status, symptom inventory (libido, energy, mood), family history of hemochromatosis or early liver disease.\\n- Recommend clinician review prior to initiating testosterone; provide options above for physician ordering and monitoring.\\n\\n(Do not issue patient-facing guidance from this note. These items are clinical recommendations for physician review and staff action.)", "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Pattern consistent with HPA-axis stress and cortisol dysregulation. Chronic stress depletes DHEA-S, disrupts sleep, and impairs cognitive and emotional function.", "supportingFindings": ["Sleep disruption reported", "Low energy / fatigue reported", "Low motivation reported"]}]}	2026-03-30 19:55:53.247086	1
