libido, hair loss, or hirsutism. Menses: patient reports regular cycles; timing of blood draw relative to cycle reviewed (luteal-phase progesterone reported). No current use of testosterone therapy. No recent illness reported.\\n- Relevant review of systems: Negative for fevers, weight loss, night sweats, easy bruising or bleeding, significant menstrual heaviness reported. Sleep apnea screening (STOP‑BANG) low risk per prior assessment.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- No vital signs documented in chart at time of visit.\\n- Labs (03/30/2026) — all values, units, and flags noted:\\n\\nCBC\\n- Hemoglobin: 15.9 g/dL (normal)\\n- Hematocrit: 46.8 % — BORDERLINE (slightly elevated; may reflect dehydration)\\n- MCV: 94.7 fL (normal)\\n- Platelets: 276 K/uL (normal)\\n\\nIron studies / related\\n- Serum Iron: 145 µg/dL (normal)\\n- TIBC: 324 µg/dL (normal)\\n- Transferrin saturation (calculated TSAT = serum iron / TIBC ×100): 145 / 324 = 44.8 % — BORDERLINE (just below 45% threshold)\\n- Ferritin: 204 ng/mL — BORDERLINE (elevated)\\n\\nVitamin / metabolic\\n- Vitamin D (25‑OH): 44.1 ng/mL — BORDERLINE (adequate but below clinic optimal)\\n- Vitamin B12: 762 pg/mL (normal)\\n- Folate: 12.9 ng/mL (normal)\\n\\nLipids / cardiovascular\\n- Total cholesterol: (not listed)\\n- LDL cholesterol: 104 mg/dL — BORDERLINE (near optimal)\\n- HDL cholesterol: 91 mg/dL (normal, protective)\\n- Triglycerides: 46 mg/dL (normal)\\n- Apolipoprotein B: 85 mg/dL (normal)\\n- Lipoprotein(a): 17 mg/dL (normal)\\n- 10‑yr Total CVD Risk (AHA PREVENT): 0.18% (0.2%), 10‑yr ASCVD: 0.12% (0.1%), 10‑yr HF: 0.13% (0.1%); 30‑yr CVD 1.49% (low risk). Statin not indicated; lifestyle measures recommended.\\n\\nHormones / endocrine\\n- Estradiol (E2): 111 pg/mL (reported; interpret with cycle timing) — listed normal\\n- Progesterone (P4): 10.8 ng/mL — listed normal (luteal progesterone)\\n- FSH: 3.99 mIU/mL (normal)\\n- TSH: 1.45 mIU/L (normal)\\n- DHEA‑S: 135 µg/dL (normal)\\n- Testosterone, Total: 20.6 ng/dL (normal)\\n- Free Testosterone: 2 pg/mL (normal)\\n- SHBG: 73.9 nmol/L (normal)\\n- Androgen interpretation: Pattern D — Adequate androgens with persistent symptoms possible from non‑androgenic causes. (Total T 20.6 ng/dL, Free T 2 pg/mL, SHBG 73.9 nmol/L)\\n\\nGlucose / metabolic\\n- Hemoglobin A1c: 4.6 % (normal)\\n\\nLiver / renal / inflammation\\n- AST: 19 U/L (normal)\\n- ALT: 18 U/L (normal)\\n- Creatinine: 0.95 mg/dL (normal)\\n- eGFR: 80 mL/min (normal)\\n- hs‑CRP: 0.6 mg/L (normal)\\n\\nOther\\n- Perimenopause assessment: Luteal progesterone 10.8 ng/mL (adequate)\\n- Sleep apnea risk (STOP‑BANG): 0 points (low)\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1) Borderline elevated hematocrit 46.8% — likely mild relative elevation; most likely due to dehydration at time of draw. Clinical significance: low immediate concern but warrants recheck after hydration to exclude persistent erythrocytosis. Red flags: notify provider if repeat Hct ≥48–50% or patient symptomatic (thrombotic symptoms, severe headache, visual changes, focal neuro deficits, chest pain).\\n2) Elevated ferritin 204 ng/mL with calculated TSAT 44.8% — borderline for elevated iron saturation (just below 45% threshold). Differential: early iron overload (hereditary hemochromatosis), inflammation/chronic disease, hepatic causes, metabolic syndrome (LFTs currently normal), or lab variability. Clinical significance: not diagnostic of hemochromatosis but requires close follow‑up given ferritin >200 ng/mL and TSAT ~45% threshold.\\n3) Vitamin D (25‑OH) 44.1 ng/mL — adequate but below clinic optimal target (recommended ≥60 ng/mL). Plan to supplement and recheck.\\n4) LDL cholesterol 104 mg/dL — near optimal; overall CVD risk low (AHA PREVENT 10‑yr risk <1%); lifestyle modifications recommended; statin not indicated at present.\\n5) Androgen status: Pattern D — adequate androgens (Total T 20.6 ng/dL, Free T 2 pg/mL, SHBG 73.9 nmol/L). Patient’s fatigue/low energy unlikely from androgen deficiency. Consider non‑androgen contributors (iron handling, thyroid, sleep, stress/HPA axis, estrogen/progesterone balance). E2 111 pg/mL and luteal P4 10.8 ng/mL appear consistent with luteal-phase; confirm cycle timing if clinical questions persist.\\n6) Routine labs otherwise within normal limits (HbA1c, LFTs, renal function, hs‑CRP, platelets, vitamin B12, folate). No immediate critical abnormalities.\\n\\nPLAN:\\n1) Borderline elevated hematocrit (46.8%)\\n   - Patient education: advised that mild Hct elevation frequently reflects relative dehydration. Counsel to increase oral fluids today and prior to repeat blood draw.\\n   - Orders: Repeat CBC (Hct/Hb) in 2–4 weeks with instructions to hydrate and obtain morning sample. Mark as routine but prompt.\\n   - Thresholds for escalation: Notify provider same day if repeat Hct ≥48–50% or if patient develops symptoms suggestive of thrombosis or hyperviscosity.\\n   - If Hct remains elevated after repeat and hydration, evaluate for secondary causes (smoking, OCP/HRT/testosterone exposure, exogenous EPO, living at altitude, undiagnosed OSA) and consider hematology referral and JAK2 testing if primary polycythemia suspected.\\n\\n2) Elevated ferritin 204 ng/mL and TSAT 44.8%\\n   - Orders: Confirm TSAT on labs — TSAT already calculated from current serum iron/TIBC = 44.8% (borderline). Place order for repeat iron studies (serum iron, TIBC, ferritin) if clinical concern or if initial draw may have been affected by acute factors.\\n   - Plan: Do not start iron supplementation.\\n   - Thresholds: If TSAT ≥45% or ferritin persistently rising or >300 ng/mL → order HFE genetic testing (C282Y, H63D) and consider hematology referral. If LFTs abnormal in future, consider hepatology.\\n   - Patient education: Explained that elevated ferritin often reflects inflammation or iron loading and requires evaluation before giving iron.\\n\\n3) Vitamin D 25‑OH 44.1 ng/mL\\n   - Treatment: Start Vitamin D3 (Metagenics D3 or equivalent) 2000 IU PO once daily.\\n   - Orders: Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D in 8–12 weeks after starting supplementation.\\n   - Patient education: Discussed target levels (clinic prefers ≥60 ng/mL), safe sun exposure and adherence.\\n\\n4) Lipids / cardiovascular risk (LDL 104 mg/dL)\\n   - Management: Reinforce lifestyle: Mediterranean/heart‑healthy diet, regular aerobic exercise, weight optimization, limit saturated fats.\\n   - No statin currently indicated given low 10‑yr ASCVD risk; document rationale.\\n   - Orders: Repeat fasting lipid panel in 6–12 months (earlier if clinical change).\\n\\n5) Androgen/hormone symptom evaluation\\n   - Recommendation: Do NOT initiate testosterone therapy now. Documented rationale: biochemical levels adequate; symptoms likely non‑androgenic.\\n   - Further evaluation: If fatigue/low mood/low libido persist after optimizing iron, sleep, thyroid and vitamin D, consider cycle‑timed reassessment of E2 and luteal progesterone (confirm mid‑luteal draw) and consider referral to reproductive endocrinology/endocrinology if persistent hormonal concern.\\n   - Consider other contributors: screen for sleep disorders (despite low STOP‑BANG), mood disorders, HPA dysfunction if clinically indicated.\\n\\n6) Additional routine surveillance and referrals\\n   - If persistent or worsening ferritin/TSAT → HFE genetic testing and hematology referral.\\n   - If persistent abnormal hematocrit despite hydration → hematology referral and workup (JAK2).\\n   - If sleep symptoms persist despite low STOP‑BANG → consider sleep medicine referral.\\n   - If mood/stress symptoms significant → consider behavioral health referral.\\n\\n7) Medications / supplements documented\\n   - Start Vitamin D3 2000 IU PO daily (documented start today).\\n   - Do NOT start iron or testosterone at this time.\\n   - Clinic suggested medium‑priority supplements (patient counseled): Magtein® magnesium L‑threonate and HerWellness™ Rapid Stress Relief as adjuncts if patient elects; patient to discuss over‑the‑counter supplements with clinician.\\n\\n8) Patient education and lifestyle counseling (documented discussion today)\\n   - Hydration before repeat labs; avoid starting iron supplements.\\n   - Heart‑healthy diet and exercise to maintain LDL and overall cardiovascular health.\\n   - Sleep hygiene and stress reduction strategies; return for evaluation if excessive daytime sleepiness or other sleep concerns.\\n   - Return sooner if acute symptoms (chest pain, focal neurologic signs, severe headaches, vision changes, swelling/pain suggesting thrombosis).\\n\\n9) Follow‑up and timeline\\n   - Immediate (today 03/30/2026): Results reviewed with patient; Vitamin D3 2000 IU started; hydration counseling given; plan documented and orders placed.\\n   - Short term (2–4 weeks): Repeat CBC (hematocrit/hemoglobin) after hydration. Repeat iron studies/TSAT if not already obtained or if results change.\\n   - Intermediate (8–12 weeks): Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D after supplementation; reassess symptoms and determine need for cycle‑timed hormone testing.\\n   - Routine (6–12 months): Repeat fasting lipid panel; ongoing symptom‑based follow‑up and specialty referrals as indicated.\\n\\nOrders placed today:\\n- CBC (repeat in 2–4 weeks) — draw morning, patient hydrated.\\n- Iron studies / TSAT (if not already posted as discrete order) — review current values; repeat if clinician requests.\\n- 25‑OH Vitamin D (recheck in 8–12 weeks after starting D3).\\n- Fasting lipid panel (routine interval 6–12 months).\\n\\nCommunication:\\n- Provider alerted via chart message summarizing plan and thresholds for urgent notification (repeat Hct ≥48–50%, TSAT ≥45% or ferritin rising >300 ng/mL, new concerning symptoms).\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\n03/30/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "preventRisk": {"ldlGoal": "No specific LDL target; focus on risk-based statin intensity", "riskCategory": "low", "tenYearASCVD": 0.0012382538136250993, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0.0018184615249065002, "thirtyYearASCVD": 0.009368751710347104, "ageValidFor30Year": true, "thirtyYearTotalCVD": 0.014920477814400507, "tenYearHFPercentage": "0.1%", "tenYearHeartFailure": 0.0013414408144334054, "statinRecommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "tenYearCVDPercentage": "0.2%", "tenYearASCVDPercentage": "0.1%", "thirtyYearHFPercentage": "1.2%", "thirtyYearHeartFailure": 0.012449248045959664, "thirtyYearCVDPercentage": "1.5%", "thirtyYearASCVDPercentage": "0.9%"}, "supplements": [{"dose": "3 capsules daily (divided doses)", "name": "Magtein® Magnesium L-Threonate", "caution": "L-Threonate form crosses blood-brain barrier for cognitive and sleep support. Well-tolerated; gentle on GI system.", "category": "mineral", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Magtein is the only magnesium form clinically shown to cross the blood-brain barrier. Supports quality sleep, cognitive function, GABA activity, and stress resilience.", "indication": "Sleep disruption reported; Low energy reported; Brain fog reported; Irritability reported", "phenotypes": ["Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "This special form of magnesium is designed to reach the brain, where it supports better sleep quality, mental clarity, and a calmer nervous system.", "supportingFindings": ["Sleep disruption reported", "Low energy reported", "Brain fog reported", "Irritability reported"]}, {"dose": "1 soft chew during times of stress", "name": "HerWellness™ Rapid Stress Relief", "caution": "Fast-acting L-Theanine and Lactium formula. Non-drowsy. Promotes calm within 1 hour. Contains milk.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "HerWellness Rapid Stress Relief provides fast-acting stress support with L-Theanine (200mg) and Lactium. Promotes calm within 1 hour without drowsiness.", "indication": "Sleep disruption (stress-related); Irritability reported; Mood changes reported", "phenotypes": ["Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This fast-acting supplement helps your body respond to stress more calmly. It promotes relaxation within about an hour and can be taken as needed without causing drowsiness.", "supportingFindings": ["Sleep disruption (stress-related)", "Irritability reported", "Mood changes reported"]}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Essential for patients on statins.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "low", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "LDL 104 mg/dL; TC 204 mg/dL; Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["LDL 104 mg/dL", "TC 204 mg/dL", "Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)"]}, {"dose": "1 tablet daily with meal", "name": "D3 2000 Complex", "caution": "Maintenance dose for suboptimal levels. Comprehensive vitamin D support with cofactors.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "low", "rationale": "D3 2000 Complex provides maintenance vitamin D with cofactors to reach and maintain optimal range ≥60 ng/mL.", "indication": "Vitamin D 44.1 ng/mL (suboptimal, target ≥60)", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D is close to optimal but could benefit from a maintenance dose to reach the ideal range for bone and immune health.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 44.1 ng/mL (suboptimal, target ≥60)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 17, "apoBValue": 85, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "normal", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 0.12382538136250992, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": false}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\nYour results are generally healthy. Key numbers: LDL 104 mg/dL, HDL 91 mg/dL, triglycerides 46 mg/dL, hemoglobin A1c 4.6%, TSH 1.45 mIU/L. Your 10-year ASCVD (heart attack/stroke) risk is 0.1% and overall 10-year CVD risk is 0.2% — this is very low. Sex hormones (Estradiol 111 pg/mL and Progesterone 10.8 ng/mL) are consistent with the luteal phase of your cycle, showing robust ovulation.\\n\\nPositive findings (what’s good)\\n- HDL cholesterol 91 mg/dL (protective).\\n- Triglycerides 46 mg/dL (normal).\\n- Hemoglobin 15.9 g/dL and MCV 94.7 fL (normal red blood cell size and oxygen-carrying capacity).\\n- TSH 1.45 mIU/L (thyroid function normal).\\n- Vitamin B12 762 pg/mL and folate 12.9 ng/mL (adequate).\\n- hs-CRP 0.6 mg/L (low inflammation).\\n- Luteal progesterone 10.8 ng/mL indicates good ovulation.\\n\\nAreas to improve (with exact values and concrete actions)\\n1. Hematocrit 46.8% (slightly elevated) — may reflect dehydration.\\n   - Action: Increase fluid intake to ~2–3 liters/day (water, herbal tea). Recheck CBC when well-hydrated in 4–8 weeks.\\n2. Ferritin 204 ng/mL (elevated).\\n   - Action: Stop any iron supplements unless prescribed. Ask your clinician for a transferrin saturation test and repeat ferritin in 6–12 weeks. If transferrin saturation is high or ferritin stays >200 ng/mL, your provider may evaluate for iron overload (HFE testing) or other causes.\\n3. Vitamin D (25-OH) 44.1 ng/mL (adequate but suboptimal).\\n   - Action: If you are not supplementing, start vitamin D3 1,000–2,000 IU daily and include 10–20 minutes sun exposure several times weekly and oily fish (salmon) 2x/week. Recheck level in 3 months.\\n4. LDL cholesterol 104 mg/dL (near optimal).\\n   - Action: Maintain heart-healthy habits: aim for 150 minutes/week of moderate activity (brisk walking), follow a Mediterranean-style diet (vegetables, whole grains, olive oil, nuts, fatty fish 2–3x/week), and keep sodium low by limiting processed foods.\\n\\nHormones and symptoms\\n- Total testosterone 20.6 ng/dL, Free T 2 pg/mL, SHBG 73.9 nmol/L — within expected ranges. If you have fatigue, low mood, sleep problems, or low libido, these are more likely due to non-androgen causes. Practical steps: prioritize 7–8 hours sleep, strength training 2–3x/week, stress management (meditation or counseling), and discuss mood/sleep with your clinician.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Follow above lifestyle steps now. Repeat CBC and ferritin in 4–12 weeks (based on hydration and testing plan). Recheck vitamin D in 3 months after starting/adjusting supplements. Discuss transferrin saturation/HFE testing with your provider if ferritin remains elevated. Routine follow-up with your clinician in 3 months or sooner for any new/worsening symptoms.\\n\\nYou’re doing well overall — small, specific changes (hydrate, stop unnecessary iron, add modest vitamin D, maintain exercise and diet) should address the borderline items. If you want, I can help you plan meals, a vitamin D schedule, or an exercise plan.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 15.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 46.8, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Slightly elevated hematocrit - may indicate dehydration.", "recommendation": "Encourage hydration. Repeat if persistent.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 94.7, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 94.7 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 276, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 111, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol appropriate for luteal phase.", "recommendation": "Normal finding. Continue monitoring.", "referenceRange": "50-250 pg/mL (luteal phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 10.8, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone appropriate for luteal phase.", "recommendation": "Confirms ovulation occurred.", "referenceRange": "5-20 ng/mL (luteal phase)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 3.99, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 20.6, "status": "normal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone within normal female range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.45, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "µg/dL", "value": 135, "status": "normal", "category": "DHEA-S", "interpretation": "DHEA-S within normal range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "65-400 µg/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 204, "status": "borderline", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Elevated ferritin.", "recommendation": "May indicate inflammation, hemochromatosis, or liver disease. Correlate clinically.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 44.1, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D adequate but suboptimal (41-59 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 2000 Complex daily to reach optimal range ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 762, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 4.6, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 104, "status": "borderline", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Near optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet and exercise.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 91, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 46, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.18184615249065, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 0.2% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.12382538136250992, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 0.1%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.13414408144334056, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.1%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 1.4920477814400508, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 1.5%, ASCVD 0.9%, Heart Failure 1.2%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 19, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 18, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 80, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.95, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 85, "status": "normal", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Apo B at optimal level.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 17, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 324, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 145, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 12.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 0.6, "status": "normal", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Low cardiovascular inflammation risk.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 10.8, "status": "normal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Luteal Progesterone", "interpretation": "Luteal progesterone of 10.8 ng/mL indicates robust ovulation. This is an optimal level suggesting good ovarian function.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Ovulatory function appears intact. Continue monitoring as part of routine perimenopause surveillance. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your progesterone level shows healthy ovulation this cycle.", "referenceRange": "≥10 ng/mL optimal, 8–10 borderline, <8 low, <5 anovulatory"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 20.6, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone Pattern D: Adequate Androgens, Persistent Symptoms", "interpretation": "Adequate Androgens pattern identified (Total T: 20.6 ng/dL (within range); Free T: 2 pg/mL; SHBG: 73.9 nmol/L). Total, free, and bioavailable testosterone are within normal ranges and SHBG is not extreme. If the patient reports fatigue, low mood, sleep disruption, or low libido, these symptoms are likely driven by non-androgenic factors. Findings to evaluate: Estrogen/Progesterone balance should be assessed (E2: 111 pg/mL, P4: 10.8 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Do not reflexively add testosterone when levels are adequate — look deeper. Evaluate iron status (ferritin target >50 for symptom resolution), thyroid function (optimize TSH), and sleep quality (consider sleep apnea screening with STOP-BANG). Assess estrogen and progesterone balance — E2/P4 imbalance is a common driver of persistent symptoms. Address CNS and psychosocial drivers: chronic stress, HPA axis dysregulation, mood disorders. Consider DHEA-S if not already checked. Support with lifestyle optimization: sleep hygiene, stress management, nutrition. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your testosterone levels are actually in a healthy range, which is good news. When symptoms persist despite normal testosterone, it usually means something else is contributing. Your provider will look at other factors like iron levels, thyroid function, sleep quality, and hormone balance to find what's driving your symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Total T 15-70 ng/dL, Free T 0.5-5.0 pg/mL (all within range)"}], "aiRecommendations": "1) Overall clinical assessment (concise)\\n- Labs show mostly normal results. Abnormal/borderline issues: borderline elevated hematocrit (46.8%), elevated ferritin (204 ng/mL), vitamin D suboptimal (25-OH = 44.1 ng/mL), LDL marginally above optimal (104 mg/dL). \\n- Androgen panel consistent with “Pattern D — adequate androgens” (Total T 20.6 ng/dL, Free T 2 pg/mL, SHBG 73.9 nmol/L). Symptoms of fatigue/low libido are unlikely to be driven by androgen deficiency based on current labs; evaluate other contributors (iron, thyroid, sleep, estrogen/progesterone balance). \\n- No immediate, unequivocal red flags on liver enzymes, renal function, glucose control, or inflammatory marker (hs-CRP listed as normal) per provided data.\\n\\n2) Priority actions — CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE\\nCRITICAL (notify provider immediately)\\n- None of the provided values constitute immediate life‑threatening abnormalities. However, instruct staff to notify the provider immediately if the patient reports new concerning symptoms (thrombosis, severe headache, focal neuro deficits, chest pain) or if repeat hematocrit rises above 50% or the patient becomes symptomatic.\\n\\nURGENT (action within days — notify provider)\\n- Borderline elevated ferritin (204 ng/mL): arrange calculations and additional labs urgently if not already available — obtain transferrin saturation (TSAT = serum iron / TIBC ×100) and review current serum iron and TIBC numbers. If TSAT ≥ 45% or ferritin continues to rise >300 ng/mL, notify provider for consideration of HFE genetic testing and hematology/hepatology referral.\\n- Hematocrit 46.8%: counsel on immediate hydration and repeat CBC promptly (see timeline below). If repeat Hct remains elevated (≥48–50%), notify provider for further evaluation (smoking history, OCP/HRT/testosterone exposure, EPO sources, altitude, sleep apnea); consider hematology referral if persistent.\\n\\nROUTINE (actionable within weeks to months)\\n- Vitamin D 25-OH 44.1 ng/mL: start supplementation per clinic protocol (see dosing below) and recheck in 8–12 weeks.\\n- LDL 104 mg/dL: reinforce lifestyle measures; recheck fasting lipid panel per routine interval (see timeline).\\n- Androgen findings: Do not initiate testosterone therapy (see direct recommendation below). Review estrogen/progesterone relationship in cycle context (E2 111 pg/mL, P4 10.8 ng/mL) and consider cycle-timed reassessment if clinically indicated.\\n\\n3) Testosterone recommendations (for provider/staff)\\n- Current data: Total T 20.6 ng/dL, Free T 2 pg/mL, SHBG 73.9 nmol/L = adequate. \\n- Recommendation: DO NOT START or escalate testosterone therapy based on these results. Document rationale in chart: “Androgen levels adequate; symptoms likely non-androgenic. Further evaluation of iron, thyroid, sleep, HPA, and estrogen/progesterone balance recommended prior to considering androgen therapy.”\\n- If provider later elects to trial testosterone (only after documented deficiency + consistent symptoms and failed optimization of other contributors), follow clinic’s therapeutic protocols and inform staff to prepare monitoring plan (baseline CBC, LFTs, lipids, repeat T levels and Hct at 8–12 weeks, then every 3–6 months). (No initial dose changes recommended now.)\\n\\n4) Specific next steps — tests, dose adjustments, provider notifications, patient education, lifestyle\\nA. Tests / labs to order\\n- Immediate/near-term:\\n  - Repeat CBC (hematocrit/hemoglobin) in 2–4 weeks after ensuring good hydration. If initial sample was non-fasting or patient dehydrated, re-draw in morning with hydration.\\n  - Calculate/obtain transferrin saturation (TSAT). If TSAT not available, order serum iron and TIBC (if not already on chart with values).\\n- If warranted by TSAT/ferritin trend:\\n  - HFE gene testing (C282Y/H63D) if TSAT ≥45% or ferritin persistently elevated >300 ng/mL.\\n  - Consider JAK2 mutation testing and hematology referral if polycythemia suspected and Hct remains elevated despite correction of hydration.\\n- Routine follow-up:\\n  - 25-OH vitamin D recheck in 8–12 weeks after starting supplementation.\\n  - Lipid panel in 6–12 months (earlier if clinical risk changes).\\n  - If symptoms persist: order cycle‑timed E2 and luteal progesterone if not already performed in correct phase (confirm sample timing relative to cycle); consider repeat morning cortisol or DHEA-S if clinical suspicion of HPA dysfunction remains (DHEA-S reported normal here).\\n  - If sleep symptoms persist despite STOP‑BANG negative, consider formal sleep study referral per provider discretion.\\n\\nB. Medication/supplement doses (specific)\\n- Vitamin D: Initiate Metagenics D3 2000 IU once daily (per clinic protocol) until target ≥60 ng/mL; recheck 25‑OH in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Iron: DO NOT prescribe iron supplementation for this patient based on a ferritin of 204 ng/mL (not iron deficient). If heavy menses causes symptomatic iron deficiency in future, target ferritin >50 ng/mL for symptom resolution; only treat iron deficiency after confirming low ferritin and low TSAT.\\n- Testosterone: No dose indicated. Document “no testosterone therapy”.\\n\\nC. Provider notification thresholds\\n- Notify provider ASAP if:\\n  - Repeat hematocrit ≥48–50% or patient symptomatic (thrombotic signs, severe headache, visual changes).\\n  - TSAT ≥45% or ferritin rising or >300 ng/mL.\\n  - Any new concerning symptoms (chest pain, neurologic deficits).\\n- Routine notification: abnormal results placed in chart and message to provider with proposed plan (repeat labs, supplements started, timeline).\\n\\nD. Patient education points for staff to convey\\n- Hematocrit: “Your hematocrit is slightly high — often due to dehydration. Please increase oral fluids today and we will repeat the blood count in 2–4 weeks.”\\n- Ferritin: “Your ferritin is higher than expected; this often reflects inflammation or other causes rather than iron deficiency. We will check additional iron studies to evaluate this further before giving iron.”\\n- Vitamin D: “Your vitamin D is adequate but below our optimal target. We will start a daily vitamin D3 supplement and recheck in ~8–12 weeks.”\\n- Testosterone: “Your testosterone is within the expected range; we do not recommend starting testosterone. We will look for other causes of symptoms such as sleep, thyroid, iron, or hormone balance.”\\n\\nE. Lifestyle recommendations to give patients (staff to counsel)\\n- Hydration: encourage increased fluid intake prior to repeat CBC.\\n- Diet: heart-healthy diet (vegetables, whole grains, lean protein, limit saturated/trans fats) to maintain LDL; avoid unnecessary iron supplementation if ferritin is elevated.\\n- Vitamin D: safe sun exposure per local guidance and adherence to supplementation.\\n- Sleep & stress: reinforce sleep hygiene, screen for OSA if symptoms present despite normal STOP-BANG, and provide resources for stress management.\\n- Menstrual blood loss: if heavy menses, document and escalate to provider since heavy bleeding affects iron status.\\n\\n5) Additional testing/referrals to consider (staff to arrange per provider order)\\n- Hematology referral if ferritin persistently elevated with TSAT ≥45% or if hematocrit remains elevated despite hydration.\\n- HFE genetic testing if transferrin saturation elevated.\\n- Hepatology if ferritin elevation accompanied by abnormal LFTs (AST/ALT) or clinical liver disease features (LFTs currently normal).\\n- Endocrinology or reproductive endocrinology referral if ongoing symptomatic hormone imbalance after initial optimization and cycle-timed hormone reassessment.\\n- Sleep medicine referral if clinical suspicion for OSA persists despite STOP-BANG.\\n- Behavioral health / psychiatry referral if mood, chronic stress, or HPA-axis related symptoms significant.\\n\\n6) Summary of next steps and timeline (clear, actionable)\\n- Immediate (today)\\n  - Counsel patient on hydration and education points (hematocrit, ferritin, vitamin D, no testosterone).\\n  - Start Metagenics D3 2000 IU daily per protocol; document start in chart.\\n\\n- Short term (2–4 weeks)\\n  - Repeat CBC (hematocrit/hemoglobin) after hydration. If Hct ≥48–50% or symptomatic → notify provider same day.\\n  - Order transferrin saturation (or serum iron and TIBC if TSAT not available) now; review results on receipt. If TSAT ≥45% → notify provider for HFE testing / hematology referral.\\n\\n- Intermediate (8–12 weeks)\\n  - Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D after supplementation.\\n  - Reassess symptoms; if persistent and androgen deficiency remains unlikely, plan cycle‑timed estrogen/progesterone evaluation or other targeted testing per provider.\\n\\n- Routine (6–12 months)\\n  - Repeat fasting lipid panel (or earlier if clinical change).\\n  - Ongoing symptom-based follow-up; consider specialty referrals if multi-system contributors identified or if abnormalities persist.\\n\\nDocumentation and physician communication\\n- Enter a problem-oriented note summarizing: borderline Hct with plan to repeat after hydration, ferritin elevated with plan for TSAT and threshold criteria for HFE/heme/ hepatology referral, vitamin D supplementation started, no testosterone initiation, and the timeline above. Send secure message/alert to provider with the plan and pending labs.\\n\\nNotes on interpretation\\n- Consider menstrual cycle timing when interpreting E2 and progesterone; confirm that the progesterone was a mid-luteal draw to assess luteal sufficiency. If timing unclear and symptoms persist, order cycle-timed testing per protocol.\\n- Ferritin elevation often reflects inflammation or iron overload; interpret in clinical context (hs-CRP normal here favors against acute inflammation but does not exclude chronic or other causes).\\n- Avoid empirical iron or testosterone therapy without matching biochemical deficiency and clinical correlation.\\n\\nIf you want, I can generate the exact orders/template text for the EHR (repeat CBC, TSAT, D3 Rx, labs timing) and the provider message summarizing these points.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 0.51, "detail": "Triglycerides 46 / HDL 91 = 0.51", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "4.6%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 73.9, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 18, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "85 mg/dL", "detail": "Normal atherogenic particle count", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "0.6 mg/L", "detail": "Low inflammatory burden", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation", "confidence": "high", "description": "Pattern consistent with HPA-axis stress and cortisol dysregulation. Chronic stress depletes DHEA-S, disrupts sleep, and impairs cognitive and emotional function.", "supportingFindings": ["Sleep disruption reported", "Irritability reported", "Low energy / fatigue reported", "Low motivation reported", "Brain fog reported"]}]}	2026-03-30 20:18:50.464407	1
85	Morgan Charleigh	female	2026-01-18 00:00:00	{"fsh": 6.39, "acne": false, "iron": 79, "tibc": 241, "onHRT": false, "anxiety": false, "bloating": false, "brainFog": true, "ferritin": 217, "hairLoss": false, "estradiol": 50, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": true, "lowLibido": false, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2026-01-18", "moodChanges": true, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Morgan Charleigh", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 35, "bmi": 20, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 110, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 1.04, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 13.2, "lowMotivation": false, "menstrualPhase": "follicular", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": true}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History of present illness: Patient presents for review of recent laboratory results completed as part of hormone/iron evaluation. Patient reports intermittent fatigue and decreased libido over recent months (subjective; quantify if needed). No acute symptoms such as syncope, chest pain, shortness of breath, abdominal pain, or jaundice. No known recent infections. No current pregnancy. Patient reports no current use of oral iron supplements. Current use of hormonal contraception/HRT not documented in chart today — needs confirmation prior to any hormone therapy decisions.\\n- Menstrual history: Premenopausal, age 35. Menstrual cycle regularity and cycle day at time of blood draw not documented — recommend documentation when repeating hormone testing.\\n- Medications/supplements: Patient denies prescription testosterone. Reports taking OTC magnesium formulation (Magtein® recommended). No current oral iron reported. No other medications documented in provided data.\\n- Allergies: Not provided in chart.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: Not provided in lab data (BP, HR, BMI not available).\\n- Risk scores: STOP‑BANG sleep apnea risk = 0 (low risk).\\n\\nLaboratory results (03/30/2026) — grouped by category; abnormal values flagged:\\n- Iron studies\\n  - Serum iron: 79 µg/dL — within reported normal range\\n  - TIBC (total iron binding capacity): 241 µg/dL — BORDERLINE LOW\\n  - Ferritin: 217 ng/mL — BORDERLINE HIGH (female)\\n  - Calculated transferrin saturation (TSAT) = (serum iron / TIBC) × 100 = 79 / 241 × 100 = 32.8% — within expected range (<45%); does not meet TSAT criteria for iron overload.\\n- Hormones\\n  - Estradiol (E2): 50 pg/mL — within reported reference range (normal)\\n  - Progesterone: 1.04 ng/mL — normal\\n  - FSH: 6.39 mIU/mL — normal for premenopausal range\\n  - Testosterone, total: 13.2 ng/dL — BORDERLINE LOW for symptomatic cis-female\\n- Other\\n  - Sleep apnea risk (STOP-BANG): 0 points — low risk\\n- Labs not provided / not available in current dataset: CBC (complete), CMP (including AST/ALT, bilirubin, alk phos), lipids, fasting glucose or HbA1c, SHBG, free testosterone. Recommend ordering/confirming as indicated below.\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1) Borderline low total testosterone — total T 13.2 ng/dL.\\n   - Clinical significance: May contribute to symptoms such as decreased libido and fatigue if present and unexplained by other causes. Need to confirm cycle phase, SHBG/free T, and exclude reversible contributors before considering androgen therapy.\\n   - Differential: physiologic low-normal state, effect of exogenous estrogen/OCPs (if used), assay timing/cycle variability, nondiagnostic low free testosterone due to binding proteins.\\n\\n2) Elevated ferritin with low TIBC — ferritin 217 ng/mL; TIBC 241 µg/dL; serum iron 79 µg/dL; calculated TSAT 32.8%.\\n   - Clinical significance: Pattern more suggestive of acute phase / chronic inflammatory state or hepatic disease than classic hereditary hemochromatosis (TSAT <45%). Ferritin mildly elevated for a woman (>200 ng/mL threshold warrants attention). Need to evaluate for inflammation, liver disease, recent illness, alcohol use, metabolic syndrome, and exclude iron overload when clinically indicated.\\n   - Differential: inflammation/chronic disease, hepatic dysfunction, metabolic syndrome, iron loading (less likely with TSAT 32.8%), recent iron supplementation.\\n\\n3) Other reproductive hormones within reported normal limits — estradiol 50 pg/mL, progesterone 1.04 ng/mL, FSH 6.39 mIU/mL.\\n   - Clinical significance: No biochemical evidence of menopause or ovarian failure based on provided values; interpret in context of cycle day and exogenous hormones.\\n\\n4) Low sleep apnea risk — STOP‑BANG 0 points.\\n   - Clinical significance: Low pretest probability for obstructive sleep apnea.\\n\\n5) Supplementation recommendation — Magtein® Magnesium L‑Threonate (medium priority).\\n   - Clinical significance: Tolerability and interactions generally favorable; counsel on timing and GI effects.\\n\\nRed flags requiring immediate physician notification:\\n- TSAT >45% (not present) or ferritin markedly rising or >500 ng/mL (not present at this time).\\n- Abnormal liver enzymes (AST/ALT) — LFTs not provided; order and notify if abnormal.\\n- Pregnancy or breastfeeding if testosterone therapy under consideration.\\n- Development of virilizing signs if androgen therapy started.\\n\\nPLAN:\\nAssessment 1 — Borderline low total testosterone (13.2 ng/dL)\\n- Immediate:\\n  - Do not initiate androgen therapy without physician discussion and patient counseling.\\n  - Confirm menstrual cycle day at time of blood draw and document current use of hormonal contraception or HRT (staff to verify and document today).\\n  - Perform pregnancy test prior to any consideration of androgen therapy.\\n- Diagnostic:\\n  - Order SHBG and free testosterone (or calculated free testosterone) to better assess bioavailable androgen status.\\n  - Order baseline labs if testosterone therapy is considered: fasting lipids, LFTs (AST, ALT), fasting glucose or HbA1c, CBC.\\n- Therapeutic (physician decision required):\\n  - If physician approves a trial and patient meets criteria: suggested starting regimen — transdermal testosterone gel 300 mcg (0.3 mg) daily. Note: physician must enter medication order.\\n  - If started, monitoring: recheck total testosterone (and free if available), LFTs, lipids, and symptom assessment at 3 months after initiation or dose change. Target mid‑female physiologic range; avoid supraphysiologic levels. Continue ongoing monitoring every 6–12 months thereafter.\\n- Counseling:\\n  - Discuss benefits/risks, contraception/pregnancy avoidance while on therapy, and skin transfer precautions for topical preparations.\\n  - Instruct patient to report any signs of virilization, mood changes, or pregnancy immediately.\\n\\nAssessment 2 — Ferritin 217 ng/mL with low TIBC; TSAT 32.8%\\n- Immediate:\\n  - Calculated TSAT = 32.8% (using serum iron 79 µg/dL and TIBC 241 µg/dL) — does not meet >45% threshold for iron overload at present.\\n  - Instruct patient to stop any OTC oral iron supplements until further evaluation completed; document counseling today.\\n- Diagnostic orders (urgent within 1–2 weeks):\\n  - CRP and/or ESR to assess for inflammatory state.\\n  - LFTs: AST, ALT, alkaline phosphatase, total bilirubin.\\n  - If any LFT abnormalities or clinical risk: Hepatitis B and C serologies.\\n  - Repeat iron panel (fasting serum iron numeric, TIBC) and ferritin in 4–8 weeks if inflammation suspected or after resolution of any acute illness.\\n  - Consider HFE gene testing (C282Y/H63D) if ferritin remains >200 ng/mL on repeat testing or TSAT >45% on repeat testing.\\n- Referral triggers:\\n  - Notify physician and consider hematology/hepatology referral if TSAT >45% on repeat testing, ferritin trend rising or >500 ng/mL, or if LFTs abnormal.\\n\\nAssessment 3 — Other reproductive hormones within normal limits\\n- Plan:\\n  - Document cycle day and contraceptive/HRT status when repeating hormone labs.\\n  - No immediate endocrine referral indicated based on current results; consider endocrine or sexual medicine referral if persistent symptomatic hypoactive sexual desire disorder after basic workup.\\n\\nAssessment 4 — Low sleep apnea risk (STOP‑BANG 0)\\n- Plan:\\n  - No further sleep apnea testing indicated based solely on STOP‑BANG score. Address fatigue via broader evaluation (thyroid testing, sleep quality, mood).\\n\\nAssessment 5 — Magnesium supplement (Magtein®)\\n- Plan:\\n  - Recommend Magtein® Magnesium L‑Threonate: 3 capsules daily divided (per clinic medium-priority recommendation). Confirm exact formulation and dosing with patient; document use and tolerability.\\n  - Counsel on timing (bedtime may aid sleep/cognition), GI side effects, and to notify clinic of any new symptoms.\\n\\nLifestyle and general counseling (staff to review with patient today)\\n- Avoid oral iron supplements until iron overload ruled out.\\n- Advise moderation of alcohol intake (alcohol can raise ferritin and affect LFTs).\\n- Counsel on weight management, regular aerobic exercise, anti-inflammatory diet (reduce processed foods), and sleep hygiene to address fatigue and reduce inflammatory markers.\\n- Screen for other contributors to fatigue/decreased libido: thyroid disease (order TSH if not recently done), depression/anxiety, sleep disorders.\\n\\nFollow-up orders and timeline\\n- Within 48 hours:\\n  - Calculate and document TSAT (done — 32.8%). Staff to file calculation in chart.\\n  - Order CRP (or ESR) and LFTs (AST, ALT, alk phos, bilirubin).\\n  - Verify and document current contraceptive/HRT use and pregnancy status; perform urine pregnancy test if considering testosterone.\\n  - Counsel and document cessation of OTC iron.\\n- Within 1–2 weeks:\\n  - Provide urgent notification to physician if any of: TSAT >45% on new calculations, ferritin rising, ferritin >500 ng/mL, abnormal LFTs, or positive pregnancy if testosterone considered.\\n- 4–8 weeks:\\n  - Repeat ferritin and iron studies (fasting iron, TIBC) if inflammation suspected or to confirm trend.\\n- If testosterone therapy is initiated (physician decision):\\n  - Baseline labs prior to start: total and free testosterone, SHBG, lipids, LFTs, fasting glucose/HbA1c, CBC.\\n  - Reassess at 3 months post-initiation: total (and free) testosterone, LFTs, lipids, symptom review; then every 6–12 months or per physician direction.\\n- Routine follow-up:\\n  - Routine clinic follow-up recommended in 3–6 months (per recheck window) to reassess labs and symptoms unless earlier concerns arise.\\n\\nDocumentation and communication\\n- All patient counseling (stop iron, lifestyle, supplement use), verification of contraception/pregnancy status, and plans for repeat labs will be documented in the chart.\\n- Physician review requested for potential testosterone therapy and for review of elevated ferritin and LFTs when available.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\nDate: 03/30/2026.", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "supplements": [{"dose": "3 capsules daily (divided doses)", "name": "Magtein® Magnesium L-Threonate", "caution": "L-Threonate form crosses blood-brain barrier for cognitive and sleep support. Well-tolerated; gentle on GI system.", "category": "mineral", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Magtein is the only magnesium form clinically shown to cross the blood-brain barrier. Supports quality sleep, cognitive function, GABA activity, and stress resilience.", "indication": "Sleep disruption reported; Low energy reported; Brain fog reported", "phenotypes": ["Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "This special form of magnesium is designed to reach the brain, where it supports better sleep quality, mental clarity, and a calmer nervous system.", "supportingFindings": ["Sleep disruption reported", "Low energy reported", "Brain fog reported"]}, {"dose": "1 soft chew during times of stress", "name": "HerWellness™ Rapid Stress Relief", "caution": "Fast-acting L-Theanine and Lactium formula. Non-drowsy. Promotes calm within 1 hour. Contains milk.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "low", "rationale": "HerWellness Rapid Stress Relief provides fast-acting stress support with L-Theanine (200mg) and Lactium. Promotes calm within 1 hour without drowsiness.", "indication": "Sleep disruption (stress-related); Mood changes reported", "phenotypes": ["Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation"], "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This fast-acting supplement helps your body respond to stress more calmly. It promotes relaxation within about an hour and can be taken as needed without causing drowsiness.", "supportingFindings": ["Sleep disruption (stress-related)", "Mood changes reported"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\n- Overall, most hormone and iron measures are okay for your reported cycle phase, but there are two borderline findings to follow: low total testosterone and an elevated iron store picture (high ferritin with low TIBC). Sleep apnea risk is low.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Estradiol 50 pg/mL — appropriate for the follicular phase of the cycle.\\n- Progesterone 1.04 ng/mL — appropriate for the pre‑ovulatory phase.\\n- FSH 6.39 mIU/mL — appropriate for the follicular phase.\\n- Serum iron 79 µg/dL — within the expected range.\\n- STOP‑BANG sleep apnea score 0/8 — LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + actions)\\n1) Testosterone (Total) 13.2 ng/dL — low for many women and can contribute to low energy, low libido, or decreased muscle mass.\\n   - Actions: prioritize 7–8 hours of quality sleep nightly; add strength training 2–3 times per week (squats, lunges, resistance bands); manage stress with daily 10–20 minute practices (walking, guided breathing, or meditation). \\n   - Follow up: discuss symptoms with your provider; lab repeat or further testing (free testosterone, DHEA, thyroid) may be needed in ~3 months.\\n\\n2) Ferritin 217 ng/mL (elevated) and TIBC 241 µg/dL (low).\\n   - I calculated transferrin saturation = (Serum iron 79 / TIBC 241) ×100 ≈ 32.8%.\\n   - Interpretation: high ferritin with low TIBC can mean iron overload or chronic inflammation. This is not the same as iron deficiency.\\n   - Immediate actions: if you take iron supplements, stop them now. Avoid high‑iron foods for now (limit organ meats and excessive red meat) and avoid taking vitamin C supplements with meals that contain iron because vitamin C increases iron absorption. Limit alcohol while this is evaluated.\\n   - Next tests: repeat ferritin, TIBC, and CRP/liver tests in 6–8 weeks. If ferritin stays >200 ng/mL or rises, your clinician may order HFE genetic testing or refer to hematology; therapeutic phlebotomy or other treatments may be considered if iron overload is confirmed.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Stop any iron supplements today and schedule a follow-up visit in 4–8 weeks.\\n- Repeat ferritin, TIBC, CRP, and basic liver tests in 6–8 weeks. Recheck testosterone or expand hormone testing in ~3 months if symptoms persist.\\n- Bone health: maintain weight‑bearing exercise and ensure calcium ~1,000–1,200 mg/day plus vitamin D 800–2,000 IU/day as appropriate — discuss supplementation with your provider.\\n- Contact us sooner if you have new symptoms (unusual fatigue, joint pain, darkening of skin, or irregular bleeding). You’re doing well with hormones for this cycle phase — these targeted steps will help clarify and improve the borderline results.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 50, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol appropriate for follicular phase.", "recommendation": "Normal finding. Continue monitoring if needed for fertility.", "referenceRange": "20-150 pg/mL (follicular phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1.04, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone appropriate for pre-ovulatory phase.", "recommendation": "Normal finding.", "referenceRange": "<1.5 ng/mL (pre-ovulation)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 6.39, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH appropriate for follicular phase.", "recommendation": "Normal finding.", "referenceRange": "3-10 mIU/mL (follicular phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 13.2, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Low testosterone.", "recommendation": "May contribute to low libido or fatigue. Consider HRT discussion.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 217, "status": "borderline", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Elevated ferritin.", "recommendation": "May indicate inflammation, hemochromatosis, or liver disease. Correlate clinically.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 241, "status": "borderline", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "Low TIBC - possible iron overload or chronic disease.", "recommendation": "Evaluate for hemochromatosis or chronic inflammation.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 79, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}], "aiRecommendations": "Clinical context: Patient lab abnormalities — Total testosterone 13.2 ng/dL (borderline/low); Ferritin 217 ng/mL (mildly elevated); TIBC 241 µg/dL (low). Estradiol, progesterone, FSH, serum iron and STOP‑BANG are reported normal. Recommendations below are STAFF‑FACING only; physician review required before implementing medication changes.\\n\\n1) Overall clinical assessment\\n- Findings:\\n  - Total testosterone 13.2 ng/dL — low/borderline for a symptomatic cis-female; may contribute to low libido/fatigue if symptoms present.\\n  - Ferritin 217 ng/mL with low TIBC (241 µg/dL) and reported normal serum iron — pattern can reflect inflammation/chronic disease (acute phase response) or iron loading; additional data needed to distinguish.\\n- Immediate interpretation caveats:\\n  - Confirm menstrual cycle phase and exogenous hormone use (OCPs, HRT) because these alter testosterone and binding proteins.\\n  - Serum iron value was reported as “normal” but not quantified — calculate/transferrin saturation (TSAT = serum iron/TIBC ×100) to assess for iron overload.\\n\\n2) Priority actions (organized CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL (notify physician immediately)\\n- If transferrin saturation (TSAT) >45% OR ferritin markedly rises (>500 ng/mL) OR AST/ALT abnormal: phone physician for expedited review and consider urgent hematology referral.\\n- If patient is pregnant or breastfeeding: do not start androgen therapy; notify physician before any hormonal treatment.\\n\\nURGENT (action within 1–2 weeks)\\n- Calculate TSAT now (use numeric serum iron value). If TSAT unavailable, order fasting serum iron, TIBC, and calculate TSAT.\\n- Order inflammatory and liver workup to contextualize elevated ferritin:\\n  - CRP and/or ESR\\n  - AST, ALT, alkaline phosphatase, bilirubin\\n  - Hepatitis B/C serology if clinical risk or LFTs abnormal\\n- Review and document current medications (combined oral contraceptives, transdermal/pill estrogen, glucocorticoids, iron supplements) and recent illnesses that could raise ferritin. Stop any oral iron supplements until iron overload excluded.\\n\\nROUTINE (action within 4–12 weeks unless above thresholds met)\\n- Repeat ferritin and iron studies in 4–8 weeks if inflammation suspected or after treating active illness; sooner if clinical concern.\\n- If ferritin remains >200 ng/mL in a woman or TSAT >45% on repeat testing → order HFE gene testing (C282Y/H63D) and consider hematology referral for possible hemochromatosis or secondary iron overload.\\n- If ferritin elevated with raised CRP/ESR → pursue evaluation/treatment of inflammatory source (rheumatology or primary care as indicated).\\n\\n3) Testosterone — medication considerations and monitoring (for physician discussion and potential initiation)\\n- Assessment prerequisites before initiating testosterone therapy:\\n  - Confirm symptomatic indication (clinically significant reduced libido/fatigue not better explained by other causes).\\n  - Confirm patient is not pregnant/planning immediate pregnancy and not breastfeeding.\\n  - Review current estrogen/OCP/HRT use (these influence total and free testosterone).\\n  - Baseline labs: total testosterone, free testosterone or calculated free testosterone (if available), SHBG, baseline lipids, LFTs, fasting glucose or HbA1c, CBC.\\n- Suggested starting regimen (physician order required):\\n  - Preferred initial option: low‑dose transdermal testosterone gel 300 mcg (0.3 mg) daily (commonly used starting dose for female hypoactive sexual desire trial). If clinic formulary differs, document rationale.\\n  - Alternative escalation: increase to 400–600 mcg/day only after 3 months if inadequate symptom response and no adverse effects and total testosterone remains below upper target.\\n- Monitoring schedule after initiation:\\n  - Recheck total testosterone (and free if available), LFTs, lipids, and symptom assessment at 3 months after start or dose change.\\n  - If on therapy, target total testosterone in the mid‑female physiologic range; avoid supraphysiologic levels—notify physician if total testosterone exceeds male reference or if clinical virilization occurs.\\n  - Ongoing monitoring every 6–12 months thereafter or per physician direction.\\n- Immediate physician notification criteria related to testosterone therapy:\\n  - Signs of virilization (voice change, clitoromegaly, significant hirsutism), major mood changes, or discontinuation if pregnant.\\n\\n4) Lifestyle interventions to recommend to the patient (staff to counsel)\\n- Avoid iron supplementation until iron overload excluded.\\n- Counsel on alcohol moderation (excess alcohol can raise ferritin and affect liver tests).\\n- Advise weight management, regular aerobic exercise, and healthy diet to reduce chronic inflammation and metabolic risk (monitor lipids and glucose).\\n- If symptoms include fatigue, screen/treat sleep, mood disorders, and other contributors (thyroid disease already partially assessed; TSH not provided—confirm if done).\\n- Discuss safe use of topical testosterone (skin transfer precautions) if therapy initiated — provide patient instructions if prescribed.\\n\\n5) Additional testing and referrals to consider\\n- Immediate tests:\\n  - Fasting serum iron (numeric), transferrin saturation (TSAT), repeat TIBC if needed, CRP or ESR, AST/ALT.\\n  - SHBG and free testosterone if total testosterone low and decision to treat is contemplated.\\n  - Pregnancy test if of childbearing potential prior to androgen therapy.\\n- Conditional tests/referrals:\\n  - HFE gene testing if TSAT >45% or ferritin persistently >200 ng/mL in a woman.\\n  - Hepatology or hematology referral if iron overload suspected or if LFT abnormalities present.\\n  - Rheumatology or infectious disease if inflammatory markers elevated and source not clear.\\n  - Sexual medicine, endocrine, or reproductive specialist referral for complex hypoactive sexual desire disorder or fertility concerns.\\n- Documentation: record menstrual cycle day and any hormonal contraception/HRT use when repeating hormone labs.\\n\\n6) Summary of next steps and timeline (concise action list for staff)\\n- Within 48 hours:\\n  - Calculate TSAT (use available serum iron value). If not available, order fasting serum iron and repeat TIBC.\\n  - Order CRP (or ESR) and LFTs (AST, ALT).\\n  - Verify and document current contraceptive/HRT use and pregnancy status.\\n  - Advise patient to stop any OTC iron supplements; document counseling.\\n- Within 1–2 weeks:\\n  - Provide results to physician if TSAT >45% or if LFTs abnormal or ferritin rising; obtain physician direction for HFE testing/hematology referral.\\n- If considering testosterone therapy:\\n  - Ensure baseline labs (total & free testosterone, SHBG, lipids, LFTs, glucose/HbA1c, CBC) completed prior to initiation.\\n  - If physician approves trial: start transdermal testosterone gel 300 mcg daily (physician order required).\\n  - Recheck testosterone, LFTs, lipids, and symptom review at 3 months; adjust dose per physician and symptoms.\\n- Routine follow-up:\\n  - Repeat ferritin and iron studies in 4–8 weeks if inflammation/acute illness suspected; sooner if clinically indicated.\\n  - Continue monitoring per specialist/physician instructions.\\n\\nPhysician notification checklist for staff\\n- Notify physician now if:\\n  - TSAT >45% (once calculated)\\n  - Ferritin trend rising or >500 ng/mL\\n  - Abnormal LFTs\\n  - Patient is pregnant/breastfeeding and testosterone therapy considered\\n  - Patient has significant symptoms and requests testosterone trial (to discuss risks/benefits and sign-off)\\n\\nDocument all patient counseling, medication holds (iron), menstrual phase at time of hormone testing, and plan for repeat labs in the chart.", "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Pattern consistent with HPA-axis stress and cortisol dysregulation. Chronic stress depletes DHEA-S, disrupts sleep, and impairs cognitive and emotional function.", "supportingFindings": ["Sleep disruption reported", "Low energy / fatigue reported", "Brain fog reported"]}]}	2026-03-30 20:21:13.48105	1
