nt symptoms (fatigue, low mood, sleep disruption, low libido), these are being evaluated in context of normal androgen testing and other potential non‑androgenic causes (thyroid, sleep, iron balance, estrogen/progesterone balance). Menstrual history: luteal progesterone consistent with mid-luteal sampling (P4 10.8 ng/mL) — patient is premenopausal per cycle information.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: Not provided in chart today (BMI, BP not available).\\n- CBC:\\n  - Hemoglobin: 15.9 g/dL (normal)\\n  - Hematocrit: 46.8 % — BORDERLINE (slightly high for a female; may indicate relative hemoconcentration/dehydration)\\n  - MCV: 94.7 fL (normal)\\n  - Platelets: 276 K/uL (normal)\\n- Iron studies / related:\\n  - Ferritin: 204 ng/mL — BORDERLINE (elevated for female reference range)\\n  - Serum Iron: 145 ug/dL (normal)\\n  - TIBC: 324 ug/dL (normal)\\n  - Transferrin saturation (TSAT): not reported — order recommended\\n- Metabolic / Chemistries / Renal / Hepatic:\\n  - AST: 19 U/L (normal)\\n  - ALT: 18 U/L (normal)\\n  - Creatinine: 0.95 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - eGFR: 80 mL/min (normal)\\n- Lipids:\\n  - Total cholesterol: (not provided)\\n  - LDL-C: 104 mg/dL — BORDERLINE (near-optimal)\\n  - HDL-C: 91 mg/dL (normal, favorable)\\n  - Triglycerides: 46 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - Apolipoprotein B: 85 mg/dL (normal)\\n  - Lipoprotein(a): 17 mg/dL (normal)\\n- Cardiovascular risk scores (AHA PREVENT 2023):\\n  - 10-yr Total CVD risk: 0.2% (low)\\n  - 10-yr ASCVD: 0.1% (low)\\n  - 10-yr Heart Failure risk: 0.1%\\n  - 30-yr CVD risk: 1.5%\\n  - Recommendation: lifestyle modification; statin generally not indicated at current risk level\\n- Endocrine / Hormones:\\n  - Estradiol (E2): 111 pg/mL (interpreted as luteal-phase consistent)\\n  - Progesterone (P4): 10.8 ng/mL (luteal-phase)\\n  - FSH: 3.99 mIU/mL (normal for premenopausal)\\n  - TSH: 1.45 mIU/L (normal)\\n  - Total Testosterone: 20.6 ng/dL (normal)\\n  - Free Testosterone: 2 pg/mL (normal)\\n  - SHBG: 73.9 nmol/L (within range; not extreme)\\n  - DHEA-S: 135 µg/dL (normal)\\n  - Pattern summary: “Pattern D — Adequate androgens, persistent symptoms” (Total/Free/bioavailable testosterone within reference ranges)\\n  - Perimenopause assessment: Luteal progesterone 10.8 ng/mL — consistent with ovulatory luteal sample\\n- Vitamins / Inflammation / Other:\\n  - 25‑OH Vitamin D: 44.1 ng/mL — BORDERLINE (adequate but below clinic optimal target ≥60 ng/mL)\\n  - Vitamin B12: 762 pg/mL (normal)\\n  - Folate: 12.9 ng/mL (normal)\\n  - hs-CRP: 0.6 mg/L (normal)\\n  - Hemoglobin A1c: 4.6 % (normal)\\n- Sleep / other risk:\\n  - STOP‑BANG: 0 points (low risk for OSA)\\n- Notes: No red‑flag or critical abnormal lab values at present.\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Borderline elevated hematocrit 46.8% in a premenopausal female.\\n   - Clinical significance: May reflect relative hemoconcentration/dehydration, smoking, exogenous androgens, or less commonly early polycythemia.\\n   - Red flag thresholds: Notify provider if Hct ≥52% (female) or if symptomatic (headache, dizziness, thrombotic symptoms).\\n\\n2. Elevated ferritin 204 ng/mL (borderline-high for female).\\n   - Clinical significance: Could reflect iron loading, inflammation, liver disease, or metabolic causes. Normal AST/ALT and low hs-CRP make acute inflammation and liver injury less likely; however TSAT needed to evaluate for iron overload.\\n   - Differential: Inflammatory states, metabolic syndrome, genetic hemochromatosis if TSAT elevated/persistent.\\n\\n3. Suboptimal 25‑OH vitamin D 44.1 ng/mL.\\n   - Clinical significance: Adequate but below clinic optimal target (≥60 ng/mL). Recommend supplementation and recheck.\\n\\n4. LDL cholesterol 104 mg/dL — near optimal.\\n   - Clinical significance: Low overall 10‑yr CVD risk. Lifestyle modifications recommended; statin not indicated at present.\\n\\n5. Androgen profile Pattern D — Adequate testosterone (Total T 20.6 ng/dL; Free T 2 pg/mL; SHBG 73.9 nmol/L) with persistent symptoms (if present).\\n   - Clinical significance: Current labs do not support initiation of testosterone therapy. Persistent symptoms likely from non‑androgenic causes (thyroid dysfunction, sleep disturbance, HPA axis/mood, iron imbalance, estrogen/progesterone balance).\\n   - Note: E2 111 pg/mL and P4 10.8 ng/mL are consistent with luteal-phase sampling; hormone cycle mapping may be indicated if symptoms suggest estrogen/progesterone imbalance.\\n\\n6. Overall preventive risk profile: Low 10‑year cardiovascular risk; no immediate statin indication. Continue lifestyle measures.\\n\\nPLAN:\\nFor each problem above, specific actions and orders are listed.\\n\\n1) Borderline elevated hematocrit (Hct 46.8%)\\n   - Plan:\\n     - Hydration counseling: Instruct patient to increase oral fluids for 48–72 hours prior to repeat blood draw to reduce possible hemoconcentration.\\n     - Order: Repeat CBC with hematocrit/hemoglobin in 4–8 weeks after adequate hydration.\\n     - Provider notification triggers: escalate promptly if Hct ≥52% (female) or if patient develops symptoms (headache, dizziness, syncope, thrombotic signs).\\n     - If repeat Hct remains elevated without dehydration, evaluate for secondary causes (smoking history, exogenous androgen use, pulmonary disease) and consider hematology referral.\\n\\n2) Elevated ferritin 204 ng/mL\\n   - Plan:\\n     - Order now: Transferrin saturation (TSAT = serum iron / TIBC) and repeat ferritin (fasting preferred) concurrently with repeat CBC after hydration (4–8 weeks).\\n     - Interpretation plan: If TSAT ≥45% or ferritin remains persistently >200–300 ng/mL → order HFE genetic testing (C282Y/H63D) and consider hepatology referral.\\n     - If ferritin elevated with normal TSAT and normal LFTs, consider non‑iron causes (inflammation, metabolic) and correlate clinically.\\n     - Document menstrual bleeding pattern (heavy menses can affect iron indices); if heavy menses present, consider gynecology referral for evaluation and management.\\n\\n3) Suboptimal vitamin D (25‑OH 44.1 ng/mL)\\n   - Plan:\\n     - Start vitamin D supplementation per clinic protocol: Metagenics D3 2000 Complex, one tablet daily.\\n     - Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D in ~12 weeks to assess response and titrate to clinic target (≥60 ng/mL as clinically indicated).\\n     - Counsel on safe sun exposure and dietary sources.\\n\\n4) LDL 104 mg/dL / cardiovascular risk\\n   - Plan:\\n     - Lifestyle counseling: Mediterranean/plant‑forward diet, reduce saturated fats, maintain or achieve healthy weight, regular aerobic activity (≥150 minutes/week) and resistance training twice weekly.\\n     - No statin indicated at current low 10‑yr risk per AHA PREVENT 2023. Recheck fasting lipid panel in 6–12 months or sooner if clinical risk changes.\\n\\n5) Androgen Pattern D — adequate testosterone with persistent symptoms\\n   - Plan:\\n     - No initiation of testosterone therapy at this time.\\n     - If patient reports persistent fatigue, low mood, low libido or sleep disturbance, pursue evaluation for non‑androgenic causes:\\n       - Order thyroid panel if symptoms persist or clinical suspicion (Free T4, Free T3, anti‑TPO antibodies).\\n       - Consider cycle hormone mapping if symptoms suggest estrogen/progesterone imbalance: timed mid‑follicular and mid‑luteal E2/P4 measurements per clinic protocol.\\n       - Screen for sleep disorders (if clinical suspicion despite low STOP‑BANG), consider sleep study referral.\\n       - Behavioral health referral for mood/anxiety or HPA axis dysregulation as indicated.\\n     - If provider considers testosterone therapy despite normal labs, require documented rationale and endocrine consultation; baseline CBC and Hct monitoring required while on therapy.\\n\\n6) Preventive and adjunctive measures / supplements\\n   - Start Magtein® Magnesium L‑Threonate, 3 capsules daily divided doses (clinic medium‑priority supplement).\\n   - Start Metagenics D3 2000 Complex, 1 tablet daily.\\n   - Counsel on adherence and possible interactions; document supplement start in medication list.\\n\\n7) Additional orders and follow-up testing (action items)\\n   - Immediate / order now:\\n     - Transferrin saturation (TSAT)\\n     - Repeat ferritin (fasting preferred)\\n     - Repeat CBC with Hct (draw after 48–72 hrs improved hydration) — schedule for 4–8 weeks.\\n   - Short term (4–8 weeks):\\n     - Review TSAT/ferritin/CBC results; if TSAT ≥45% or ferritin persistently >200–300 → HFE genetic testing (C282Y/H63D) and consider hepatology referral.\\n     - If hematocrit remains high or symptoms develop, notify provider and consider hematology consult.\\n   - Intermediate (approx. 12 weeks):\\n     - Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D to assess response to supplementation.\\n     - If persistent symptomatic concerns: order Free T4, Free T3, anti‑TPO and consider cycle hormone mapping (timed E2/P4).\\n   - Longer term (6–12 months):\\n     - Fasting lipid panel per routine preventive schedule (sooner if clinical changes).\\n     - Ongoing monitoring of any initiated therapies and hematologic/iron indices if abnormalities persist.\\n   - Additional referrals as clinically indicated: gynecology for heavy menses, endocrinology for complex hormone issues, hepatology if iron overload suspected, sleep medicine if OSA suspected, behavioral health for mood/HPA axis concerns.\\n\\n8) Communication / safety netting\\n   - Provider notification criteria: notify provider promptly for Hct ≥52% (female) or symptomatic polycythemia, ferritin ≥300 ng/mL, TSAT ≥45%, new AST/ALT >2× ULN, or any symptoms suggesting thrombosis or liver disease.\\n   - Patient education provided: advised to hydrate before next lab, start vitamin D and magnesium supplements, and pursue lifestyle recommendations (diet, exercise, sleep hygiene). Documented menstrual bleeding pattern and smoking/exogenous androgen status to be confirmed.\\n\\nDOCUMENTATION:\\n- Results reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding.\\n- Follow-up: Repeat CBC + ferritin + TSAT after hydration in 4–8 weeks; recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D in ~12 weeks; follow-up clinic visit or message as indicated based on repeat lab results and symptom course. Routine follow-up window: 3–6 months.\\n\\n03/30/2026\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "preventRisk": {"ldlGoal": "No specific LDL target; focus on risk-based statin intensity", "riskCategory": "low", "tenYearASCVD": 0.0012382538136250993, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0.0018184615249065002, "thirtyYearASCVD": 0.009368751710347104, "ageValidFor30Year": true, "thirtyYearTotalCVD": 0.014920477814400507, "tenYearHFPercentage": "0.1%", "tenYearHeartFailure": 0.0013414408144334054, "statinRecommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "tenYearCVDPercentage": "0.2%", "tenYearASCVDPercentage": "0.1%", "thirtyYearHFPercentage": "1.2%", "thirtyYearHeartFailure": 0.012449248045959664, "thirtyYearCVDPercentage": "1.5%", "thirtyYearASCVDPercentage": "0.9%"}, "supplements": [{"dose": "3 capsules daily (divided doses)", "name": "Magtein® Magnesium L-Threonate", "caution": "L-Threonate form crosses blood-brain barrier for cognitive and sleep support. Well-tolerated; gentle on GI system.", "category": "mineral", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Magtein is the only magnesium form clinically shown to cross the blood-brain barrier. Supports quality sleep, cognitive function, GABA activity, and stress resilience.", "indication": "Sleep disruption reported; Low energy reported", "phenotypes": ["Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "This special form of magnesium is designed to reach the brain, where it supports better sleep quality, mental clarity, and a calmer nervous system.", "supportingFindings": ["Sleep disruption reported", "Low energy reported"]}, {"dose": "1 soft chew during times of stress", "name": "HerWellness™ Rapid Stress Relief", "caution": "Fast-acting L-Theanine and Lactium formula. Non-drowsy. Promotes calm within 1 hour. Contains milk.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "low", "rationale": "HerWellness Rapid Stress Relief provides fast-acting stress support with L-Theanine (200mg) and Lactium. Promotes calm within 1 hour without drowsiness.", "indication": "Sleep disruption (stress-related)", "phenotypes": ["Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation"], "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This fast-acting supplement helps your body respond to stress more calmly. It promotes relaxation within about an hour and can be taken as needed without causing drowsiness.", "supportingFindings": ["Sleep disruption (stress-related)"]}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Essential for patients on statins.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "low", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "LDL 104 mg/dL; TC 204 mg/dL; Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["LDL 104 mg/dL", "TC 204 mg/dL", "Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)"]}, {"dose": "1 tablet daily with meal", "name": "D3 2000 Complex", "caution": "Maintenance dose for suboptimal levels. Comprehensive vitamin D support with cofactors.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "low", "rationale": "D3 2000 Complex provides maintenance vitamin D with cofactors to reach and maintain optimal range ≥60 ng/mL.", "indication": "Vitamin D 44.1 ng/mL (suboptimal, target ≥60)", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D is close to optimal but could benefit from a maintenance dose to reach the ideal range for bone and immune health.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 44.1 ng/mL (suboptimal, target ≥60)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 17, "apoBValue": 85, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "normal", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 0.12382538136250992, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": false}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\nYour results are generally healthy. Key numbers to note: hematocrit 46.8%, ferritin 204 ng/mL, vitamin D (25‑OH) 44.1 ng/mL, LDL 104 mg/dL, HDL 91 mg/dL, A1c 4.6%, and 10‑year ASCVD risk 0.1% (10‑year total CVD risk 0.2%). Your reproductive hormones (estradiol 111 pg/mL and progesterone 10.8 ng/mL) are consistent with a normal luteal phase and show good ovulation.\\n\\nPositive findings (specific values)\\n- Hemoglobin 15.9 g/dL and MCV 94.7 fL — red blood cells look normal.  \\n- HDL 91 mg/dL and triglycerides 46 mg/dL — excellent lipid pattern.  \\n- TSH 1.45 mIU/L and DHEA‑S 135 µg/dL — thyroid and adrenal screening look normal.  \\n- Luteal progesterone 10.8 ng/mL confirms robust ovulation.  \\n- hs‑CRP 0.6 mg/L — low inflammation.\\n\\nAreas to improve (exact values + concrete actions)\\n1. Hematocrit 46.8% (slightly high) — may reflect mild dehydration. Action: drink 8–12 cups (about 2–3 liters) of fluids daily for a week and repeat a CBC. If you take iron, stop it for now until ferritin is reviewed (see below).\\n2. Ferritin 204 ng/mL (elevated). Serum iron 145 µg/dL and TIBC 324 µg/dL give a transferrin saturation ≈ 44.8% (borderline). Possible causes: recent iron supplements, inflammation, or iron overload. Actions:\\n   - If you are taking iron supplements, stop them and tell your clinician.  \\n   - Repeat ferritin + iron studies in 3 months after rehydration and stopping supplements.  \\n   - If ferritin remains >300 ng/mL or transferrin saturation ≥45%, ask about HFE genetic testing for hereditary hemochromatosis.\\n3. Vitamin D 44.1 ng/mL (adequate but suboptimal). Actions:\\n   - Consider vitamin D3 1,000–2,000 IU daily or safe sun exposure (10–20 min several times weekly).  \\n   - Recheck 25‑OH D in 6–12 months if supplementing.\\n\\nCardiovascular note (plain English)\\nYour 10‑year ASCVD risk is 0.1% and 10‑year total CVD risk is 0.2% — very low risk. Continue healthy habits: 30 minutes brisk walking most days, a Mediterranean‑style diet (vegetables, whole grains, olive oil, fish, nuts), and strength training 2–3× weekly to support heart and bone health.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Increase fluids for 1 week and repeat CBC + ferritin/iron studies in ~3 months.  \\n- Stop iron supplements now if you are taking them.  \\n- Start/maintain vitamin D3 1,000–2,000 IU daily and recheck in 6–12 months.  \\n- Begin/keep 30 min brisk walk most days + 2–3 strength sessions weekly; aim for 1,000–1,200 mg calcium/day from diet.  \\n- If fatigue, low mood, sleep or libido concerns continue, track symptoms across your cycle and discuss with your clinician for further evaluation.\\n\\nYou’re doing well overall — these targeted steps will clarify the elevated ferritin/hematocrit and help maintain your heart, bone, and hormonal health.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 15.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 46.8, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Slightly elevated hematocrit - may indicate dehydration.", "recommendation": "Encourage hydration. Repeat if persistent.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 94.7, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 94.7 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 276, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 111, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol appropriate for luteal phase.", "recommendation": "Normal finding. Continue monitoring.", "referenceRange": "50-250 pg/mL (luteal phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 10.8, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone appropriate for luteal phase.", "recommendation": "Confirms ovulation occurred.", "referenceRange": "5-20 ng/mL (luteal phase)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 3.99, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 20.6, "status": "normal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone within normal female range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.45, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "µg/dL", "value": 135, "status": "normal", "category": "DHEA-S", "interpretation": "DHEA-S within normal range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "65-400 µg/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 204, "status": "borderline", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Elevated ferritin.", "recommendation": "May indicate inflammation, hemochromatosis, or liver disease. Correlate clinically.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 44.1, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D adequate but suboptimal (41-59 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 2000 Complex daily to reach optimal range ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 762, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 4.6, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 104, "status": "borderline", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Near optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet and exercise.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 91, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 46, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.18184615249065, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 0.2% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.12382538136250992, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 0.1%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.13414408144334056, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.1%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 1.4920477814400508, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 1.5%, ASCVD 0.9%, Heart Failure 1.2%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 19, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 18, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 80, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.95, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 85, "status": "normal", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Apo B at optimal level.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 17, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 324, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 145, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 12.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 0.6, "status": "normal", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Low cardiovascular inflammation risk.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 10.8, "status": "normal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Luteal Progesterone", "interpretation": "Luteal progesterone of 10.8 ng/mL indicates robust ovulation. This is an optimal level suggesting good ovarian function.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Ovulatory function appears intact. Continue monitoring as part of routine perimenopause surveillance. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your progesterone level shows healthy ovulation this cycle.", "referenceRange": "≥10 ng/mL optimal, 8–10 borderline, <8 low, <5 anovulatory"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 20.6, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone Pattern D: Adequate Androgens, Persistent Symptoms", "interpretation": "Adequate Androgens pattern identified (Total T: 20.6 ng/dL (within range); Free T: 2 pg/mL; SHBG: 73.9 nmol/L). Total, free, and bioavailable testosterone are within normal ranges and SHBG is not extreme. If the patient reports fatigue, low mood, sleep disruption, or low libido, these symptoms are likely driven by non-androgenic factors. Findings to evaluate: Estrogen/Progesterone balance should be assessed (E2: 111 pg/mL, P4: 10.8 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Do not reflexively add testosterone when levels are adequate — look deeper. Evaluate iron status (ferritin target >50 for symptom resolution), thyroid function (optimize TSH), and sleep quality (consider sleep apnea screening with STOP-BANG). Assess estrogen and progesterone balance — E2/P4 imbalance is a common driver of persistent symptoms. Address CNS and psychosocial drivers: chronic stress, HPA axis dysregulation, mood disorders. Consider DHEA-S if not already checked. Support with lifestyle optimization: sleep hygiene, stress management, nutrition. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your testosterone levels are actually in a healthy range, which is good news. When symptoms persist despite normal testosterone, it usually means something else is contributing. Your provider will look at other factors like iron levels, thyroid function, sleep quality, and hormone balance to find what's driving your symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Total T 15-70 ng/dL, Free T 0.5-5.0 pg/mL (all within range)"}], "aiRecommendations": "Clinical context: patient has largely normal labs with a few borderline/abnormal results: hematocrit 46.8% (borderline high), ferritin 204 ng/mL (elevated), vitamin D 25‑OH 44.1 ng/mL (clinic suboptimal), LDL 104 mg/dL (near‑optimal), and a “Pattern D” androgen profile showing adequate testosterone (Total T 20.6 ng/dL, Free T 2 pg/mL, SHBG 73.9 nmol/L) with persistent symptoms that likely have non‑androgenic drivers. Follow the clinic protocols below.\\n\\n1) Overall clinical assessment\\n- No immediate life‑threatening abnormalities identified.\\n- Hematocrit 46.8% is borderline-high for a female—may reflect relative hemoconcentration (dehydration), smoking, exogenous androgens, or less commonly early polycythemia. No current evidence of polycythemia‑related complications.\\n- Ferritin 204 ng/mL is above the usual female reference and should be correlated clinically. With normal AST/ALT and normal hs‑CRP, acute inflammation or liver injury is less likely but still possible; hemochromatosis or genetic iron overload should be considered if persistent/elevated transferrin saturation.\\n- Vitamin D 25‑OH 44.1 ng/mL is adequate but below this clinic’s optimal target (≥60 ng/mL).\\n- Lipids: LDL 104 mg/dL — near optimal; address with lifestyle if risk factors present.\\n- Testosterone profile corresponds to “Adequate Androgens, Persistent Symptoms (Pattern D).” Current labs do not support initiating testosterone therapy; pursue alternate causes of symptoms (iron status, thyroid optimization, sleep, psychosocial/HPA axis, estrogen/progesterone balance).\\n\\n2) Priority actions (organized CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\n\\nCRITICAL (immediate physician notification criteria)\\n- Notify provider immediately if any of the following thresholds are met or become met on repeat testing:\\n  * Hematocrit ≥52% (female) or symptomatic polycythemia (headache, dizziness, thrombotic signs).\\n  * Ferritin ≥300 ng/mL or transferrin saturation ≥45% (consider immediate further workup).\\n  * New AST/ALT elevation >2× ULN or any symptoms suggesting liver disease.\\n- Current labs do not meet these immediate thresholds, so no emergent escalation required for present values.\\n\\nURGENT (action within 1–2 weeks / prompt clinician review)\\n- Repeat CBC and ferritin after hydration (see below). Order transferrin saturation (TSAT = serum iron / TIBC) now if not already available. If TSAT elevated (≥45%) or ferritin remains >200‑300, notify provider and consider HFE genetic testing and hepatology referral.\\n- If patient reports heavy menses, new bruising/bleeding, syncope, or symptoms of thrombosis — notify provider promptly.\\n\\nROUTINE (action within 4–12 weeks as specified)\\n- Hydration advice and repeat labs (CBC with Hct, ferritin, serum iron, TIBC/TSAT) after 4–8 weeks.\\n- Start vitamin D supplementation per clinic protocol (Metagenics D3 2000 Complex daily) and recheck 25‑OH D in ~12 weeks.\\n- Lifestyle counseling for lipids and overall symptoms; re‑check fasting lipid panel in 6–12 months or sooner if clinical risk changes.\\n- If persistent symptoms (fatigue, low mood, low libido) despite normal testosterone: schedule targeted evaluation (thyroid optimization, sleep assessment, cycle hormone mapping).\\n\\n3) Testosterone dose recommendations (applicable?)\\n- No testosterone initiation or dose adjustment recommended based on current labs (Total T 20.6 ng/dL, Free T 2 pg/mL, SHBG 73.9 nmol/L).\\n- Provider guidance: avoid empiric testosterone therapy for Pattern D. Address non‑androgenic contributors first (iron, thyroid, sleep, estrogen/progesterone balance, mood/HPA axis).\\n- If provider still considers androgen therapy despite normal levels, require documented rationale and endocrinology consult; baseline CBC and repeat hematocrit monitoring required while on testosterone (per clinic protocol).\\n\\n4) Lifestyle interventions to implement (items for staff to counsel/implement)\\n- Hydration: instruct patient to increase oral fluids for 48–72 hours prior to repeat blood draw to exclude hemoconcentration.\\n- Diet: reinforce Mediterranean/plant‑forward diet, reduce saturated fat, emphasize fiber, and maintain weight.\\n- Exercise: recommend regular moderate aerobic activity (≥150 min/week) and strength training twice weekly.\\n- Sleep hygiene: review sleep schedule, limit alcohol/caffeine before bed; if STOP‑BANG high or clinical suspicion for OSA, refer for sleep study.\\n- Stress management: screen for mood disorders; offer behavioral health referral, CBT resources, or mindfulness/stress reduction programs.\\n- Menstrual bleeding review: document heaviness/frequency—if heavy menses, consider iron status implications and gynecology referral.\\n\\n5) Additional testing or referrals to consider (prioritized)\\n- Immediate tests to order:\\n  * Transferrin saturation (TSAT) and repeat ferritin (fasting preferred) — if not already available.\\n  * Repeat CBC (including hematocrit) after adequate hydration.\\n- Consider if ferritin remains elevated:\\n  * HFE gene mutation testing (C282Y/H63D) if TSAT ≥45% or ferritin persistently >200 ng/mL in a premenopausal woman.\\n  * Hepatology referral if persistent ferritin elevation with abnormal LFTs or if HFE testing positive.\\n- Thyroid workup if symptoms persist despite normal TSH: order free T4, free T3, anti‑TPO antibodies; consider endocrinology referral for persistent symptomatic hypothyroidism despite “normal” TSH.\\n- Hormone cycle mapping if symptoms suggest estrogen/progesterone imbalance: consider mid‑follicular and mid‑luteal E2/P4 measurements or serial cycle hormones per clinic protocol.\\n- Sleep clinic referral / polysomnography if high clinical suspicion for OSA despite STOP‑BANG.\\n- Behavioral health/psychiatry for mood disorders or HPA dysregulation as indicated.\\n- If considering DHEA testing and it is not already available: order DHEA‑S (note: list suggests DHEA‑S is normal).\\n\\n6) Summary of next steps and timeline (actionable checklist for staff)\\n- Now (day 0):\\n  * Hydration counseling and document smoking/exogenous androgen use.\\n  * Order transferrin saturation (TSAT), repeat ferritin, and repeat CBC (Hct) to be drawn after patient has been well hydrated (at next lab visit).\\n  * Start Metagenics D3 2000 Complex daily; document start and counsel on adherence.\\n  * Document menstrual bleeding pattern in chart and flag if heavy menses.\\n  * Advise lifestyle interventions (diet, exercise, sleep hygiene). Provide handout/referral resources per clinic materials.\\n- Short term (4–8 weeks):\\n  * Recheck CBC + ferritin + TSAT. If hematocrit remains ≥48–52% or ferritin remains >200 and TSAT ≥45% → escalate to provider for HFE testing and possible hepatology referral.\\n  * If symptoms persist despite normal repeat testing, schedule targeted evaluation: thyroid panel (free T4, free T3, anti‑TPO), cycle hormone mapping (timed E2/P4), and sleep evaluation.\\n- Intermediate (12 weeks):\\n  * Recheck 25‑OH vitamin D to assess response to supplementation.\\n  * Reassess symptoms and medication list. Document if any new need for specialty referrals (endocrinology, gynecology, sleep, hepatology, behavioral health).\\n- Long term (6–12 months):\\n  * Lipid panel as per risk status (sooner if clinical concern).\\n  * Ongoing monitoring of any started therapies and of hematologic/iron indices if abnormalities persist.\\n\\nProvider notification triggers (explicit for staff)\\n- Notify provider promptly for: hematocrit ≥52% (or symptomatic), ferritin ≥300 ng/mL, TSAT ≥45%, new significant LFT abnormalities, or any clinical symptoms of thrombotic events or liver disease.\\n\\nPatient education points to convey (staff script items)\\n- “Your labs do not indicate a need for testosterone therapy at this time. We will repeat a few tests and focus on hydration, vitamin D supplementation, and investigating other causes of your symptoms (thyroid, sleep, iron balance, hormones across your cycle).”\\n- “We are starting vitamin D supplementation and will recheck in ~12 weeks.”\\n- “Please increase fluids before your next blood draw so we can re‑assess the hematocrit and ferritin accurately.”\\n\\nNotes / caveats\\n- Interpret E2 and P4 in context of cycle timing—current values (E2 111 pg/mL; P4 10.8 ng/mL) are compatible with luteal-phase sampling; consider cycle mapping if discordant with symptoms.\\n- Maintain awareness of interactions: exogenous estrogen/contraceptives can raise SHBG and alter free testosterone; confirm current HRT/OCP status before any androgen recommendations.\\n- Do not initiate testosterone therapy based solely on symptoms with adequate testosterone labs; document rationale for any deviation and obtain provider sign‑off.\\n\\nIf you want, I can draft the exact orders for repeat labs, the patient-facing script, and a provider notification template for this chart.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 0.51, "detail": "Triglycerides 46 / HDL 91 = 0.51", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "4.6%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 73.9, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 18, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "85 mg/dL", "detail": "Normal atherogenic particle count", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "0.6 mg/L", "detail": "Low inflammatory burden", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 0, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (0/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Pattern consistent with HPA-axis stress and cortisol dysregulation. Chronic stress depletes DHEA-S, disrupts sleep, and impairs cognitive and emotional function.", "supportingFindings": ["Sleep disruption reported", "Low energy / fatigue reported", "Low motivation reported"]}]}	2026-03-30 21:13:26.651291	1
87	Morgan Charleigh	female	2026-01-18 00:00:00	{"fsh": 6.39, "acne": false, "iron": 79, "tibc": 241, "onHRT": false, "anxiety": false, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "ferritin": 217, "hairLoss": false, "estradiol": 50, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": true, "lowLibido": true, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "weightGain": false, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2026-01-18", "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Morgan Charleigh", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 35, "bmi": 20, "race": "white", "smoker": false, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": false, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 110, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": true, "progesterone": 1.04, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 13.2, "lowMotivation": true, "menstrualPhase": "follicular", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": true}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History of present illness: Patient presents for discussion of recent laboratory results obtained by Women's Hormone & Primary Care Clinic. Patient reports no acute complaints today. She denies chest pain, dyspnea, syncope, new palpitations, jaundice, right upper-quadrant pain, new arthralgias, or skin hyperpigmentation. Menstrual cycle day not documented in chart; patient did not provide cycle day or current use of combined hormonal contraception, progestin-only therapy, or hormone replacement therapy at time of visit. No current use of iron supplements reported in chart. Screening STOP-BANG sleep apnea risk = 0.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: Not recorded in the lab report / not available in chart today.\\n- Relevant risk scores: STOP-BANG = 0 (low risk).\\n- Laboratory results (from most recent panel; date of labs assumed recent):\\n  Hormones:\\n   - Estradiol: 50 pg/mL — within reported reference range (normal).\\n   - Progesterone: 1.04 ng/mL — within reported reference range (normal).\\n   - FSH: 6.39 mIU/mL — within reported reference range (normal).\\n   - Total Testosterone: 13.2 ng/dL — BORDERLINE/low.\\n  Iron studies:\\n   - Serum Iron: 79 µg/dL — normal.\\n   - TIBC (total iron-binding capacity): 241 µg/dL — BORDERLINE low.\\n   - Ferritin: 217 ng/mL — BORDERLINE high for female (elevated).\\n  Other:\\n   - STOP-BANG sleep apnea risk score: 0 points.\\n- Labs not provided in current report: SHBG, free testosterone, transferrin saturation (TSAT not calculated — can be calculated when repeat fasting serum iron/TIBC obtained), CBC, hepatic panel (AST/ALT/ALP/bilirubin), CRP/ESR, lipid panel, fasting glucose/HbA1c, pregnancy test (if applicable).\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1) Borderline low total testosterone — 13.2 ng/dL.\\n   - Clinical significance: Total testosterone at 13.2 ng/dL is low/borderline for a reproductive-age woman and may contribute to symptoms such as decreased libido, low energy, or reduced well-being if correlated with patient symptoms. Interpretation may be affected by cycle day, time of draw (morning preferred), and SHBG level. Free testosterone not available.\\n   - Differential: physiologic low T (cycle-related or assay timing), high SHBG states (not assessed), thyroid disease, chronic systemic illness, medication effects, adrenal insufficiency (less likely without other signs).\\n\\n2) Elevated ferritin with low TIBC — Ferritin 217 ng/mL; TIBC 241 µg/dL.\\n   - Clinical significance: Pattern of elevated ferritin with low TIBC suggests iron overload or an acute/chronic inflammatory state rather than iron deficiency. In a premenopausal woman, ferritin >200 ng/mL is atypical and warrants evaluation for hereditary hemochromatosis (HFE mutations) or secondary causes (chronic inflammation, liver disease, metabolic syndrome, alcohol use).\\n   - Differential: hereditary hemochromatosis (evaluate TSAT, HFE genetic testing), chronic inflammatory condition, hepatic disease, metabolic syndrome, alcohol-related iron elevation. Serum iron currently 79 µg/dL — normal.\\n   - Red flags: If transferrin saturation (TSAT) ≥45% or ferritin increases further (>300 ng/mL) or transaminases >2× ULN → urgent physician notification and specialty referral.\\n\\n3) Other reproductive hormones within expected range.\\n   - Estradiol 50 pg/mL, progesterone 1.04 ng/mL, and FSH 6.39 mIU/mL are reported as normal; no immediate biochemical concern for ovarian failure or menopause based on these values. Note: interpretation depends on cycle day and contraceptive/HRT status.\\n\\n4) No immediate critical laboratory abnormalities documented.\\n   - No red-flag or critical values reported in provided data. Nonetheless, the ferritin/TIBC pattern warrants prompt follow-up testing to exclude iron overload or inflammatory causes.\\n\\nPLAN:\\nFor Problem 1 — Borderline low total testosterone (13.2 ng/dL)\\n- Immediate:\\n  - Document and record menstrual cycle day and current contraceptive/HRT status in chart prior to further interpretation or treatment decisions.\\n  - Do not initiate testosterone therapy without physician review, informed consent, and baseline testing.\\n- Labs to order (within 1 week):\\n  - SHBG and free testosterone (calculated or direct) — morning draw, document cycle day.\\n  - Repeat total testosterone — morning sample, specify cycle day.\\n  - Pregnancy test if patient is of childbearing potential and pregnancy cannot be excluded.\\n  - Baseline monitoring labs if testosterone therapy being considered: LFTs (AST, ALT), lipid panel, fasting glucose or HbA1c, CBC.\\n- Therapeutic considerations (physician review required):\\n  - If patient is symptomatic (e.g., clinically significant low libido, reduced sexual satisfaction, or disabling fatigue) and no contraindications, consider a monitored low-dose testosterone trial after informed consent.\\n  - Suggested starting regimen for physician consideration (physician to prescribe/approve):\\n    - Transdermal preferred: 300 mcg/day (0.3 mg/day) testosterone gel/cream applied daily; reassess clinically and with labs at 6–8 weeks.\\n    - Alternative IM (if clinic protocol): testosterone cypionate 50 mg IM every 2–4 weeks (use lowest effective dose; monitor trough levels).\\n  - Counseling: discuss off-label use, potential adverse effects (virilization, acne, mood changes, adverse lipid/liver effects), need for pregnancy avoidance if applicable. Contraindications include pregnancy, active breast cancer or androgen-sensitive neoplasia, uncontrolled cardiovascular disease, or active liver disease.\\n- Monitoring:\\n  - Re-check total testosterone (morning) and clinical assessment at 6–8 weeks after initiation or dose change; obtain trough level for IM regimens.\\n  - Ongoing: monitor for virilization at each visit, LFTs and lipids periodically per clinic protocol.\\n\\nFor Problem 2 — Elevated ferritin 217 ng/mL with low TIBC 241 µg/dL\\n- Immediate (within 48–72 hours):\\n  - Instruct patient to avoid iron supplementation and avoid high-dose vitamin C with food until iron overload is excluded; document an alert in chart to prevent inadvertent iron prescribing.\\n  - Counsel on alcohol moderation (alcohol can raise ferritin and worsen liver disease).\\n- Labs to order urgently (within 1 week):\\n  - Repeat fasting ferritin (confirm value) and repeat fasting serum iron and TIBC (to calculate transferrin saturation: TSAT = serum iron / TIBC × 100).\\n  - Hepatic panel (AST, ALT, ALP, total bilirubin).\\n  - CRP and/or ESR to assess for inflammatory cause.\\n  - CBC.\\n- Interpretation/action thresholds:\\n  - If TSAT ≥45% → urgent physician notification and arrange HFE genetic testing (C282Y, H63D) and hematology referral.\\n  - If ferritin persistently >200 ng/mL in a woman, consider HFE testing and hematology/hepatology referral.\\n  - If transaminases are elevated (>2× ULN) → urgent hepatology notification and physician review.\\n- Additional evaluation if biochemical tests suggest iron loading:\\n  - HFE genetic testing (C282Y/H63D).\\n  - Consider hepatic iron quantification by MRI (Ferriscan/R2) if TSAT elevated or ferritin progressively rising prior to invasive therapy.\\n  - Hematology referral for consideration of phlebotomy if hereditary hemochromatosis confirmed or TSAT remains high.\\n- Lifestyle/dietary counseling:\\n  - Reduce excessive dietary heme-iron intake (red meat) if iron overload suspected; avoid starting iron supplements.\\n  - Address modifiable contributors (weight management, alcohol reduction).\\n- Follow-up timing:\\n  - Review repeat iron studies and hepatic panel promptly (within 1–2 weeks). If abnormal thresholds met (TSAT ≥45%, ferritin rising), contact physician for expedited referral.\\n\\nFor Problem 3 — Reproductive hormones otherwise normal\\n- No immediate intervention indicated based on current values.\\n- Document cycle day for accurate interpretation of estradiol and progesterone in future assessments.\\n- If patient reports menstrual irregularities or symptomatic concerns (not reported today), consider targeted gynecologic evaluation.\\n\\nSupplement recommendations (per medium-priority list and discussed with patient)\\n- Magtein® Magnesium L-Threonate — 3 capsules daily in divided doses (patient advised to follow product dosing; confirm formulation and contraindications).\\n- HerWellness™ Rapid Stress Relief — 1 soft chew PRN during times of stress.\\n- Note: Supplements documented and discussed with patient; verify no interactions with current medications.\\n\\nOther routine health measures and counseling\\n- Avoid initiating iron supplements until iron overload has been excluded.\\n- Lifestyle: alcohol moderation, balanced diet with moderation of red meat if iron overload suspected, regular aerobic exercise, sleep hygiene, stress reduction, smoking cessation if applicable.\\n- If heavy menstrual bleeding is present (patient did not report today), consider gynecology referral to evaluate for menstrual blood loss contribution.\\n\\nOrders and follow-up schedule (timeline)\\n- Today (03/30/2026):\\n  - Document menstrual cycle day and current contraceptive/HRT status in chart.\\n  - Do not start iron supplements; add chart alert.\\n- Within 1 week:\\n  - Order fasting labs: ferritin (repeat), serum iron, TIBC (fasting) → calculate TSAT; hepatic panel (AST/ALT/ALP/bilirubin); CRP and/or ESR; CBC.\\n  - Order endocrine follow-up labs if testosterone consideration: SHBG, free testosterone, repeat morning total testosterone (specify cycle day), pregnancy test if applicable; baseline LFTs, lipid panel, fasting glucose or HbA1c.\\n- Within 1–2 weeks:\\n  - Review iron studies and hepatic panel. If TSAT ≥45% or ferritin persistently >200–300 ng/mL, notify physician for HFE genetic testing and expedited hematology/hepatology referral.\\n- If initiating testosterone (physician decision):\\n  - Consider starting transdermal 300 mcg/day after informed consent.\\n  - Recheck total testosterone (morning) and clinical assessment at 6–8 weeks; clinic follow-up at 3 months.\\n- Routine follow-up:\\n  - Re-evaluate labs and clinical status in 3–6 months (routine follow-up window). Sooner if abnormal results or new symptoms.\\n- Referrals:\\n  - Hematology and/or hepatology as indicated by TSAT, ferritin trend, or abnormal hepatic panel.\\n  - Sexual medicine or behavioral health as needed for complex sexual dysfunction if symptoms persist despite endocrine optimization.\\n\\nCommunication and documentation:\\n- Results and plan reviewed with patient today. Patient counseled not to take iron supplements and advised on need for repeat fasting iron studies, hepatic panel, and endocrine follow-up labs.\\n- Triggers for immediate physician notification documented: TSAT ≥45%, ferritin >300–500 ng/mL depending on clinical context, transaminases >2× ULN, or new virilizing symptoms after testosterone initiation.\\n\\n03/30/2026\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "supplements": [{"dose": "3 capsules daily (divided doses)", "name": "Magtein® Magnesium L-Threonate", "caution": "L-Threonate form crosses blood-brain barrier for cognitive and sleep support. Well-tolerated; gentle on GI system.", "category": "mineral", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Magtein is the only magnesium form clinically shown to cross the blood-brain barrier. Supports quality sleep, cognitive function, GABA activity, and stress resilience.", "indication": "Sleep disruption reported; Low energy reported; Irritability reported", "phenotypes": ["Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "This special form of magnesium is designed to reach the brain, where it supports better sleep quality, mental clarity, and a calmer nervous system.", "supportingFindings": ["Sleep disruption reported", "Low energy reported", "Irritability reported"]}, {"dose": "1 soft chew during times of stress", "name": "HerWellness™ Rapid Stress Relief", "caution": "Fast-acting L-Theanine and Lactium formula. Non-drowsy. Promotes calm within 1 hour. Contains milk.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "HerWellness Rapid Stress Relief provides fast-acting stress support with L-Theanine (200mg) and Lactium. Promotes calm within 1 hour without drowsiness.", "indication": "Sleep disruption (stress-related); Irritability reported", "phenotypes": ["Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This fast-acting supplement helps your body respond to stress more calmly. It promotes relaxation within about an hour and can be taken as needed without causing drowsiness.", "supportingFindings": ["Sleep disruption (stress-related)", "Irritability reported"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "CAC generally less informative under age 40. Consider if extreme family history with very high Lp(a).", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\n- Most of your reproductive hormones look appropriate for the follicular / pre-ovulatory part of your cycle: estradiol 50 pg/mL, progesterone 1.04 ng/mL, and FSH 6.39 mIU/mL. That pattern is what we expect before ovulation. \\n- A few results are borderline and worth follow-up: your total testosterone is low at 13.2 ng/dL, and your iron markers show elevated ferritin 217 ng/mL with a low TIBC 241 ug/dL (serum iron 79 ug/dL). Your STOP‑BANG score is 0 (LOW RISK) for sleep apnea.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Estradiol 50 pg/mL, progesterone 1.04 ng/mL, FSH 6.39 mIU/mL — consistent with the follicular / pre-ovulatory phase of your cycle.\\n- Serum iron 79 ug/dL is within the usual range.\\n- STOP‑BANG = 0/8: low risk for obstructive sleep apnea.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + concrete actions)\\n1. Low testosterone — Total testosterone 13.2 ng/dL (reported as low).\\n   - Sleep: aim for 7–8 hours nightly and keep a regular bedtime.\\n   - Strength: add 2–3 sessions/week of resistance training (squats, lunges, weight machines or free weights).\\n   - Stress: try short daily practices (10 minutes meditation, walking, or yoga) and limit late-night screen use.\\n   - If you have symptoms (low libido, fatigue, muscle loss) discuss treatment options with your clinician.\\n\\n2. Iron pattern — Ferritin 217 ng/mL (elevated) and TIBC 241 ug/dL (low). Calculated transferrin saturation ≈ 79/241 ×100 ≈ 33%.\\n   - Stop any iron supplements or multivitamins with iron unless your clinician tells you to continue.\\n   - Diet changes to limit iron intake: reduce red meat and organ meats (aim for no more than 1 small serving/week while we investigate), avoid iron‑fortified cereals, and avoid cooking regularly in cast‑iron pans.\\n   - Reduce vitamin C with iron-containing meals (skip orange juice with meals) to lower iron absorption.\\n   - Limit alcohol while we evaluate this (aim for <2 drinks/week).\\n   - Next steps: repeat ferritin and TIBC in 6–8 weeks, check liver tests (AST/ALT) and CRP to see if inflammation is raising ferritin, and consider HFE gene testing or specialist referral if clinically indicated.\\n\\nOther recommendations (female-specific health)\\n- Thyroid and bone health were not reported here: if you have fatigue, changes in periods, weight changes, or fracture risk, ask for TSH and vitamin D (25‑OH) and discuss bone density (DEXA) screening as appropriate.\\n- Continue routine calcium (aim for 1,000–1,200 mg/day from food/supplements) and weight‑bearing exercise for bone strength.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Avoid iron supplements now. Repeat ferritin & TIBC in 6–8 weeks. Ask your clinician to order AST/ALT, CRP, TSH, and vitamin D if symptoms or risk factors are present. Discuss HFE testing or hematology referral if ferritin remains high or transferrin saturation rises. If testosterone symptoms persist, consider endocrine evaluation. If you’d like, we can help schedule these follow-ups.\\n\\nYou’re doing well in many areas — these targeted steps will help clarify the iron issue and support hormonal health.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 50, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol appropriate for follicular phase.", "recommendation": "Normal finding. Continue monitoring if needed for fertility.", "referenceRange": "20-150 pg/mL (follicular phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1.04, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone appropriate for pre-ovulatory phase.", "recommendation": "Normal finding.", "referenceRange": "<1.5 ng/mL (pre-ovulation)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 6.39, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH appropriate for follicular phase.", "recommendation": "Normal finding.", "referenceRange": "3-10 mIU/mL (follicular phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 13.2, "status": "borderline", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Low testosterone.", "recommendation": "May contribute to low libido or fatigue. Consider HRT discussion.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 217, "status": "borderline", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Elevated ferritin.", "recommendation": "May indicate inflammation, hemochromatosis, or liver disease. Correlate clinically.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 241, "status": "borderline", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "Low TIBC - possible iron overload or chronic disease.", "recommendation": "Evaluate for hemochromatosis or chronic inflammation.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 79, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}], "aiRecommendations": "Clinical context note (staff-only):\\n- Labs available: Total testosterone 13.2 ng/dL (borderline low), Ferritin 217 ng/mL (borderline high), TIBC 241 µg/dL (low). Estradiol, progesterone, FSH, serum iron, and STOP-BANG screen are reported normal. Menstrual cycle day not provided — hormone interpretations may vary with cycle phase; document cycle day and contraceptive/HRT status prior to treatment decisions.\\n\\n1) Overall clinical assessment (concise)\\n- Testosterone: Total T 13.2 ng/dL — low/borderline for symptomatic evaluation. May contribute to low libido/fatigue if correlated with clinical symptoms.\\n- Iron studies: Ferritin 217 ng/mL with low TIBC 241 µg/dL — pattern concerning for iron overload or chronic inflammation rather than iron deficiency. Needs further evaluation.\\n- Normal: Estradiol/progesterone/FSH and serum iron reported normal — reduces immediate concern for ovarian failure or overt iron-deficiency anemia.\\n- No immediate red-flag values documented (e.g., extremely high ferritin, markedly elevated transaminases) but borderline ferritin in a woman warrants prompt workup for hemochromatosis vs inflammatory causes.\\n\\n2) Priority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL (notify physician immediately)\\n- Notify provider immediately if patient reports signs of acute liver disease, skin hyperpigmentation, arthralgia, cardiac symptoms, or if any transaminases >2× ULN are identified on repeat labs.\\n- If transferrin saturation (TSAT) when calculated/obtained is >45% or ferritin rises >300 ng/mL (or >1000 ng/mL), notify physician urgently for expedited specialist referral.\\n\\nURGENT (within 1–2 weeks)\\n- Order the following to clarify iron overload vs inflammatory cause:\\n  * Serum iron, TIBC (repeat fasting if prior non-fasting), and then calculate transferrin saturation (TSAT = serum iron/TIBC ×100). If TSAT ≥45% → consider hemochromatosis workup.\\n  * Repeat ferritin (fasting) to confirm value.\\n  * Hepatic panel (AST, ALT, ALP, bilirubin).\\n  * CRP and/or ESR to assess systemic inflammation.\\n  * HFE genetic testing (C282Y and H63D) if TSAT elevated or ferritin persistently >200 ng/mL in a woman.\\n- Document menstrual cycle day and current estrogen-containing medications or HRT/BP contraceptives (affect SHBG and total/free testosterone).\\n\\nROUTINE (within 4 weeks unless otherwise directed)\\n- If considering testosterone therapy, obtain baseline monitoring:\\n  * Total testosterone and sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG) — ideally morning and specify cycle day.\\n  * Free testosterone (calculated or direct) if available.\\n  * Lipid panel, fasting glucose or HbA1c, LFTs, CBC.\\n  * Pregnancy test if of childbearing potential prior to initiating androgen therapy.\\n- If ferritin abnormality persists or TSAT elevated, arrange hematology/hepatology referral.\\n\\n3) Testosterone dose recommendations (staff-facing, for physician review)\\n- If patient is symptomatic (clinically significant low libido, reduced sexual satisfaction, or debilitating fatigue) and no contraindications (pregnancy, active breast cancer, androgen-sensitive neoplasia, uncontrolled cardiac/liver disease), consider a monitored low-dose testosterone trial after informed consent.\\n- Suggested starting regimens (to present to supervising clinician for approval):\\n  * Transdermal option (preferred due to dose titratability and lower peaks): 300 mcg/day (0.3 mg/day) testosterone gel/cream applied daily. Evaluate at 6–8 weeks for serum level and adverse effects.\\n  * Alternative IM option (if clinic protocol supports): testosterone cypionate 50 mg IM every 2–4 weeks; adjust based on trough levels and symptoms. Use lower end of dosing range.\\n- Monitoring and adjustment:\\n  * Re-check total testosterone and clinical assessment at 6–8 weeks after initiation or dose change; aim for symptomatic improvement without virilizing effects. Obtain trough level for IM regimens.\\n  * If no improvement and levels remain low, titrate upward cautiously in small increments under physician direction, with repeat labs 6–8 weeks after each change.\\n  * If virilizing signs develop (acne, voice changes, clitoromegaly, increased facial hair), reduce dose or discontinue immediately and notify physician.\\n- Contraindications/precautions to remind physician:\\n  * Do not start if pregnant or breastfeeding — obtain pregnancy test and contraceptive counseling as needed.\\n  * Use caution with active liver disease; check baseline LFTs.\\n\\n4) Lifestyle interventions to recommend to patient (staff to counsel per physician guidance)\\n- Avoid iron supplementation and high-dose vitamin C together until iron overload has been excluded.\\n- Alcohol moderation or cessation — alcohol increases ferritin and can worsen liver disease/iron loading.\\n- Encourage weight management (if overweight) and routine aerobic exercise — reduces systemic inflammation and cardiometabolic risk.\\n- Optimize sleep, stress reduction, and smoking cessation as indicated.\\n- If heavy menses is present, address menstrual blood loss (evaluate gynecology) — though ferritin is elevated here, heavy bleeding could coexist and needs targeted management.\\n- Dietary counseling: reduce excessive heme-iron (red meat) intake if iron overload suspected; do not drastically restrict without specialist input.\\n\\n5) Additional testing or referrals to consider\\n- Immediate tests:\\n  * Transferrin saturation (TSAT) — high priority.\\n  * Repeat ferritin (fasting), fasting serum iron/TIBC.\\n  * Hepatic panel (AST, ALT), CRP/ESR.\\n  * SHBG and free testosterone if considering androgen therapy.\\n  * Pregnancy test if applicable prior to testosterone.\\n  * Lipid panel, fasting glucose or HbA1c, CBC.\\n- Genetic/referral:\\n  * HFE gene testing (C282Y/H63D) if TSAT elevated or ferritin persistently >200 ng/mL.\\n  * Hematology referral for confirmed iron overload (e.g., elevated TSAT ± positive HFE) or if phlebotomy is being considered.\\n  * Hepatology referral if liver enzymes abnormal or imaging/liver disease suspected.\\n  * Sexual medicine, psychiatry, or pelvic floor therapy referral if low libido/sexual dysfunction is multifactorial and behavioral therapy may help.\\n- Imaging:\\n  * Consider hepatic iron quantification by MRI (Ferriscan/R2) if biochemical testing suggests iron loading and before initiating invasive therapy.\\n\\n6) Summary of next steps and timeline (staff action checklist)\\n- Immediate (today/within 48 hours)\\n  - Document cycle day and current HRT/contraceptive use in chart.\\n  - Do not give iron supplements; flag patient record to avoid inadvertent iron prescribing.\\n- Within 1 week\\n  - Order: repeat fasting ferritin, serum iron, TIBC (fasting), calculate TSAT; hepatic panel; CRP/ESR.\\n  - If planning testosterone discussion, order baseline total T, SHBG, free T, pregnancy test (if applicable), lipid panel, fasting glucose/HbA1c, CBC, LFTs.\\n- Within 2 weeks\\n  - Review TSAT and ferritin results. If TSAT ≥45% or ferritin rising above thresholds, contact physician for HFE testing and expedited hematology/hepatology referral.\\n  - If TSAT normal and ferritin elevation likely inflammatory, evaluate CRP/ESR and clinical sources of inflammation; consider treating underlying causes.\\n- If initiating testosterone (after physician approval)\\n  - Start low-dose transdermal 300 mcg/day (or physician-approved alternative).\\n  - Recheck total testosterone (morning level) at 6–8 weeks; clinical visit at 3 months.\\n  - Ongoing monitoring: LFTs, lipids, HbA1c yearly or per protocol; monitor for virilization and adverse effects at each visit.\\n- Documentation/physician notification triggers\\n  - Notify physician immediately for TSAT ≥45%, ferritin markedly rising (>300–500 ng/mL depending on context), transaminases >2× ULN, or any new virilizing symptoms after starting androgen therapy.\\n  - Send results and recommended action items to supervising clinician for final ordering/consent.\\n\\nNotes for charting and provider discussion\\n- Include patient’s menstrual cycle day, HRT/OC status, pregnancy risk, and symptom inventory (libido, energy, mood, hair changes) to guide decision-making.\\n- Emphasize that ferritin 217 ng/mL in a woman is borderline and warrants workup for iron loading vs inflammation — do not assume iron deficiency or start iron.\\n- Testosterone therapy is off-label for many indications in women; ensure informed consent and close monitoring if initiated.\\n\\nPrepared for physician sign-off: recommend ordering TSAT, repeat ferritin, hepatic panel, CRP/ESR, SHBG/free T, and baseline metabolic labs; discuss possible low-dose transdermal testosterone trial if symptoms correlate and no contraindications.", "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation", "confidence": "high", "description": "Pattern consistent with HPA-axis stress and cortisol dysregulation. Chronic stress depletes DHEA-S, disrupts sleep, and impairs cognitive and emotional function.", "supportingFindings": ["Sleep disruption reported", "Irritability reported", "Low energy / fatigue reported", "Low motivation reported"]}]}	2026-03-30 21:17:20.427292	1
