or estrogen metabolism and balance with I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Best suited for women with estrogen dominance patterns, PMS, perimenopausal hormone imbalance, or those needing broader estrogen metabolism support during hormonal transitions. Differs from Estrovera in that it addresses the underlying estrogen metabolism rather than vasomotor symptom relief.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected; Age-appropriate for estrogen metabolism support", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement helps your body process and balance estrogen more effectively. It supports healthy hormone metabolism, which can help with PMS symptoms, bloating, mood changes, and hormonal balance during life transitions.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected", "Age-appropriate for estrogen metabolism support"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 11, "apoBValue": 121, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "borderline", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 0.9429414159677914, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": true}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "strongly_indicated", "indicated": true, "rationale": "LDL-C ≥190 mg/dL (204 mg/dL). High-intensity statin therapy strongly indicated per ACC/AHA guidelines. Consider familial hypercholesterolemia evaluation."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "consider", "rationale": "Lipid levels meet threshold for CAC consideration. CAC may be useful if risk level is uncertain or patient is hesitant about statin.", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall summary in plain language:\\nYou have some important findings to address and many healthy results. Your LDL (“bad”) cholesterol is high at 204 mg/dL, and your vitamin D is low at 23.1 ng/mL. Your total testosterone is elevated at 82.9 ng/dL with free testosterone 8 pg/mL, and your progesterone is low at 1 ng/mL while estradiol is 119 pg/mL — this pattern is consistent with luteal progesterone deficiency and perimenopause. Your 10‑year cardiovascular risk is low (Total CVD 1.3%, ASCVD 0.9%), but the very high LDL means we should act now.\\n\\nPositive findings (what’s good right now):\\n- Hemoglobin 15.1 g/dL, ferritin 97.7 ng/mL, MCV 94 fL — iron stores and red blood cells look healthy.\\n- TSH 0.96 mIU/L — thyroid function is in the normal range.\\n- Hemoglobin A1c 5.1% and triglycerides 92 mg/dL — blood sugar and triglycerides are normal.\\n- hs-CRP 0.4 mg/L — low inflammation for heart disease risk.\\n\\nAreas to improve (specific values + actions):\\n1. LDL cholesterol 204 mg/dL (very high). Your cardiology note recommends a high‑intensity statin (Atorvastatin 40–80 mg or Rosuvastatin 20–40 mg) because LDL ≥190 mg/dL. Also start these lifestyle steps now:\\n   - Add 25–30 g fiber daily: oatmeal for breakfast, beans or lentils with lunch, plenty of vegetables at dinner.\\n   - Eat fatty fish 2–3 times weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n   - Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter, fatty cheeses.\\n   - Daily exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking (or equivalent) most days.\\n   Please discuss statin options with your clinician as soon as possible.\\n\\n2. Vitamin D 23.1 ng/mL (suboptimal for bone/hormone health). Actions:\\n   - Aim for brief sun exposure when safe (10–20 minutes several times/week).\\n   - Consider vitamin D3 supplementation (discuss dose with your clinician; common starting doses are 1,000–2,000 IU/day or a higher short course if advised).\\n   - Add weight‑bearing exercise (walking, squats) and calcium‑rich foods to protect bone health.\\n   Recheck 25‑OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks after starting supplements.\\n\\n3. Reproductive hormones: Progesterone 1 ng/mL with estradiol 119 pg/mL and FSH 10.5 mIU/mL — suggests luteal progesterone deficiency (perimenopause). Elevated testosterone (total 82.9 ng/dL; free 8 pg/mL) may cause acne, hair thinning, or excess hair. Actions:\\n   - Prioritize 7–8 hours sleep, add strength training 2–3×/week, and use stress‑management (walking, meditation).\\n   - Discuss with your gynecologist/endocrinologist about hormone management (bioidentical progesterone, cyclic progesterone, or other options) and testing timed to your cycle.\\n\\nOther notes:\\n- Hematocrit 46.9% slightly high — may reflect dehydration. Increase fluids and recheck in 1–2 weeks.\\n- HDL 51 mg/dL could be higher — exercise and healthy fats help. ApoB 121 mg/dL is borderline.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline:\\n1. Schedule a physician visit urgently to review LDL 204 mg/dL and discuss starting a high‑intensity statin.\\n2. Start the cholesterol lifestyle changes immediately and plan a lipid recheck 4–12 weeks after any medication start (or 3 months after lifestyle changes).\\n3. Begin vitamin D measures and recheck in 8–12 weeks.\\n4. Book follow‑up with your gynecologist/endocrinologist to address perimenopausal symptoms and elevated testosterone; they may repeat hormones timed to your cycle and discuss treatment.\\n5. Recheck hematocrit after hydrating in 1–2 weeks.\\n\\nYou’re doing many things well; these targeted steps can improve symptoms and long‑term health. Please review these results with your clinician before making medication changes.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 15.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 46.9, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Slightly elevated hematocrit - may indicate dehydration.", "recommendation": "Encourage hydration. Repeat if persistent.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 94, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 94 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 249, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 119, "status": "normal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol level recorded. Interpretation depends on menstrual phase.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle day or menstrual phase.", "referenceRange": "20-500 pg/mL (varies by cycle phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 10.5, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 82.9, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Elevated testosterone for females.", "recommendation": "Evaluate for PCOS, adrenal hyperplasia, or androgen-secreting tumor.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 0.96, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 97.7, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 23.1, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D deficiency (21-30 ng/mL). Suboptimal for hormone and bone health.", "recommendation": "Repletion: Metagenics D3 5,000 + K daily. Target ≥60 ng/mL. Recheck in 8-12 weeks.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 645, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 204, "status": "abnormal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Very high LDL - high cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Statin therapy recommended. Intensive lifestyle changes.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 51, "status": "borderline", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "HDL could be higher for optimal protection.", "recommendation": "Increase exercise. Moderate alcohol if appropriate.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 92, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 1.2689097661395692, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 1.3% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years\\n\\nSEVERE HYPERCHOLESTEROLEMIA: High-intensity statin therapy indicated (LDL >=190 mg/dL)", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.9429414159677914, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 0.9%", "recommendation": "HIGH-INTENSITY statin (Atorvastatin 40-80 mg or Rosuvastatin 20-40 mg) - LDL >=190 mg/dL", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.4265717611486817, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 0.4%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 0, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (0/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 10.105022636664025, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 10.1%, ASCVD 6.9%, Heart Failure 4.2%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 19, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 15, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 82, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 121, "status": "borderline", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Borderline elevated Apo B.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider pharmacotherapy if high ASCVD risk.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 11, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 331, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 89, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 7.32, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 0.4, "status": "normal", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Low cardiovascular inflammation risk.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Luteal Progesterone Deficiency", "interpretation": "Progesterone of 1 ng/mL with estradiol of 119 pg/mL in a woman 41 years old indicates luteal progesterone deficiency. Estrogen production is adequate but progesterone is not keeping pace — a hallmark of perimenopause.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Micronized progesterone 100–200 mg nightly. Dosing can be cyclic (days 14–28) or continuous depending on cycle regularity. Progesterone provides GABAergic support for sleep and anxiety relief. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your body is making enough estrogen, but progesterone has dropped. This is very common in perimenopause and causes sleep disruption, anxiety, and mood changes. Progesterone supplementation supports your nervous system and helps restore calm, restful sleep.", "referenceRange": "P4 ≥5 ng/mL expected with E2 ≥60 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 84, "status": "normal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 84 nmol/L is within normal range. SHBG regulates bioavailable sex hormones by binding testosterone and estradiol.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 8, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 8 pg/mL is elevated. May cause androgenic symptoms (acne, hirsutism, hair thinning).", "recommendation": "Evaluate for PCOS, exogenous testosterone, or low SHBG driving elevated free T.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}], "aiRecommendations": "For clinic staff use only — clinical synthesis and actionable recommendations based on the provided labs.\\n\\n1) Overall clinical assessment (concise)\\n- Critical cardiovascular risk marker: LDL 204 mg/dL with ApoB 121 mg/dL — consistent with very high atherogenic lipid burden (LDL ≥190 mg/dL). Requires provider review for statin initiation and rapid risk-reduction.\\n- Androgen excess: Total testosterone 82.9 ng/dL and free testosterone 8 pg/mL — both elevated for a female; correlate with symptoms (acne, hirsutism, hair thinning, voice changes). Requires further evaluation for PCOS, adrenal source, exogenous androgen use, or neoplasm depending on clinical context and pace of onset.\\n- Perimenopausal pattern: Luteal progesterone 1 ng/mL with estradiol 119 pg/mL (age 41) suggests luteal progesterone deficiency consistent with perimenopause.\\n- Vitamin D deficiency: 25-OH D = 23.1 ng/mL (insufficient/deficient).\\n- Hematocrit borderline high: 46.9% — likely mild/dehydration; correlate clinically.\\n- Other routine labs (TSH, FSH, ferritin, A1c, LFTs, creatinine, hs-CRP, lipoprotein(a)) are within reported normal limits.\\n\\n2) Priority actions — CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE\\n\\nCRITICAL (Physician notification immediately)\\n- LDL 204 mg/dL (≥190): Flag provider immediately for initiation of high‑intensity statin therapy per protocol.\\n  * Rationale: LDL ≥190 mg/dL indicates primary severe hypercholesterolemia with high lifetime ASCVD risk. ApoB 121 supports atherogenic particle burden.\\n  * Action for provider: consider starting high‑intensity statin without delay (see dosing below). Ensure patient not pregnant and counsel re: statin teratogenicity.\\n- Testosterone (total 82.9 ng/dL; free 8 pg/mL): Notify provider for expedited evaluation.\\n  * Rationale: Elevated androgens require assessment to exclude PCOS, exogenous androgen exposure, late‑onset CAH, or androgen‑secreting tumor — especially urgent if rapid virilization (voice change, clitoromegaly, marked hirsutism).\\n  * Action for provider: review history for exogenous testosterone/androgenic medications, assess for virilizing signs; consider urgent endocrine workup/imaging if rapid progression.\\n\\nURGENT (prompt staff action, notify provider per clinic protocol)\\n- Start Vitamin D repletion and schedule recheck.\\n- Address perimenopausal luteal progesterone deficiency with provider-confirmed progesterone therapy plan.\\n- Ensure pregnancy test status documented before initiating statin, spironolactone (if considered), or progesterone as clinically indicated.\\n\\nROUTINE\\n- Repeat hematocrit after hydration and reassess.\\n- Lifestyle counseling (diet, exercise, weight, alcohol, smoking cessation) coordinated at visit.\\n\\n3) Medication / dose recommendations (specific, for provider review)\\n\\nStatin (for provider order)\\n- Recommend initiating high‑intensity statin therapy given LDL 204 mg/dL:\\n  * Preferred options: Atorvastatin 40–80 mg nightly OR Rosuvastatin 20–40 mg nightly.\\n  * Baseline: LFTs are reported normal (AST/ALT normal) — acceptable to start. Obtain baseline ALT/AST (already available). Do pregnancy test for women of childbearing potential prior to prescribing.\\n  * Follow-up lipid panel 4–12 weeks after initiation to assess response and tolerance; adjust dose or add ezetimibe if LDL reduction inadequate.\\n  * Consider adding ezetimibe 10 mg daily if on maximal statin and LDL remains >100 mg/dL or if provider wants greater LDL lowering.\\n\\nAndrogen management (if treatment indicated after provider assessment)\\n- If patient is taking exogenous testosterone: discontinue or reduce dose per provider; recheck T levels after appropriate washout (4–6 weeks).\\n- If endogenous hyperandrogenism requires pharmacologic anti‑androgen therapy and pregnancy is excluded:\\n  * Consider spironolactone 50 mg PO twice daily (start 50 mg BID → titrate to 100–200 mg/day as needed and tolerated). Monitor BMP (potassium, creatinine) and blood pressure at baseline, 1–2 weeks after initiation, and at 1 month.\\n  * Alternate options per provider decision: combined oral contraceptive (if contraception acceptable) or finasteride for hair loss (specialist decision). Avoid starting anti‑androgens until pregnancy excluded.\\n- For symptomatic progesterone deficiency (provider order required):\\n  * Micronized progesterone 100–200 mg PO nightly. Options:\\n    - Cyclic dosing: days 14–28 of cycle if cycles regular.\\n    - Continuous nightly dosing if cycles irregular/continuous symptoms.\\n  * Counsel regarding somnolence/dizziness (take at bedtime). Reassess symptoms in 6–12 weeks.\\n\\nVitamin D\\n- Initiate repletion per clinic protocol: D3 5,000 IU daily with vitamin K (Metagenics D3 5,000 + K) — provider may prescribe. Target 25‑OH D ≥60 ng/mL. Recheck 25‑OH D in 8–12 weeks.\\n\\nHematocrit\\n- No pharmacotherapy indicated. Encourage hydration; repeat CBC in 4 weeks if clinically stable or sooner if symptoms.\\n\\n4) Monitoring and follow-up timing (specific)\\n- Lipids/statin:\\n  * Baseline LFTs available; repeat fasting lipid panel 4–12 weeks after statin initiation, then every 3 months until stable, then every 6–12 months.\\n  * Check ALT/AST at 6–12 weeks if clinically indicated or if symptoms; CK only if muscle symptoms.\\n- Testosterone/hereditary androgen workup:\\n  * If exogenous testosterone stopped, recheck total and free testosterone after 4–6 weeks.\\n  * If evaluating endogenous source, order additional labs (see section 5) and recheck total/free T in early follicular phase if premenopausal and cycling (cycle days 3–7) when feasible.\\n  * If starting spironolactone: BMP at baseline, 1–2 weeks after start, and 1 month; then every 3–6 months.\\n- Progesterone therapy:\\n  * Reassess symptoms at 6–12 weeks. Consider repeat luteal progesterone after 2–3 cycles if clinically indicated.\\n- Vitamin D:\\n  * Recheck 25‑OH D in 8–12 weeks after starting D3 5,000 IU daily.\\n- Hematocrit:\\n  * Repeat CBC after hydration within 2–4 weeks.\\n\\n5) Additional testing and referrals to consider (with priorities)\\n- Immediate/urgent endocrine testing if elevated androgens:\\n  * DHEA‑S, androstenedione, 17‑hydroxyprogesterone (17‑OH P) (to screen for CAH), repeat total and free testosterone (ideally days 3–7 if cycling), SHBG already reported normal.\\n  * If rapid virilization or markedly elevated DHEA‑S: obtain adrenal imaging (CT abdomen) per provider decision.\\n  * Pelvic transvaginal ultrasound if PCOS suspected or if ovarian source suspected.\\n- Lipid-related:\\n  * Confirm fasting lipid panel prior to treatment if current sample was non-fasting.\\n  * Consider fasting lipid panel + direct LDL and ApoB confirmatory repeat. If family history of premature ASCVD or very high LDL, consider referral to lipid specialist/genetic testing for familial hypercholesterolemia.\\n- Reproductive safety:\\n  * Pregnancy test prior to starting statin or anti‑androgen therapy; document contraception discussion.\\n- Cardiology referral:\\n  * Consider cardiology if ASCVD risk factors, symptoms, or if provider wants specialist input for aggressive LDL lowering.\\n- Endocrinology referral:\\n  * Consider endocrinology if diagnostic uncertainty about androgen source, inadequate response to first-line therapy, or suspicion for adrenal/ovarian tumor.\\n\\n6) Patient education points for staff to cover (for provider review)\\n- LDL: Explain that LDL is very high and that a statin is recommended to lower cardiovascular risk; discuss need to confirm pregnancy status and potential side effects (myalgias, LFT abnormalities). Emphasize diet, exercise, weight loss.\\n- Progesterone/perimenopause: Explain perimenopausal progesterone deficiency is a common cause of sleep disturbance and mood/anxiety; progesterone supplementation is an option and commonly causes sedation (take at bedtime). Clarify cyclic vs continuous dosing based on cycle regularity.\\n- Vitamin D: Explain the need for repletion and sun exposure moderation; take supplement daily and recheck levels in ~8–12 weeks.\\n- Androgens: Ask about current supplements/medications (testosterone gels/injections), document any androgenizing symptoms; emphasize the need for further testing and potential therapies that require pregnancy exclusion.\\n- Hematocrit: Encourage adequate hydration and report symptoms such as headache, dizziness.\\n\\n7) Summary of next steps and timeline (clear actionable checklist)\\n- Immediate (today / same visit)\\n  * Notify provider urgently: LDL 204 mg/dL and elevated testosterone (total and free).\\n  * Obtain/confirm pregnancy test for women of childbearing potential before starting statin, spironolactone, or progesterone.\\n  * Provide patient education about LDL, androgen findings, vitamin D plan, hydration for Hct.\\n  * Order Vitamin D replacement (D3 5,000 IU + K) and document plan to recheck in 8–12 weeks.\\n  * If provider agrees, initiate micronized progesterone 100–200 mg nightly (cyclic or continuous per provider order).\\n  * Arrange fasting lipid panel confirmation if index lipid was not fasting.\\n\\n- Short term (within 1–4 weeks)\\n  * If statin prescribed: start high‑intensity statin (atorvastatin 40–80 mg or rosuvastatin 20–40 mg) — order and schedule lipid check at 4–12 weeks.\\n  * If spironolactone considered: obtain baseline BMP and BP; schedule BMP at 1–2 weeks and 1 month.\\n  * Order androgen panel: DHEA‑S, androstenedione, 17‑OH progesterone, repeat total/free testosterone (preferably cycle days 3–7 if cycling), and consider pelvic ultrasound if PCOS features.\\n  * Repeat CBC for Hct after hydration within 2–4 weeks.\\n\\n- Follow‑up (6–12 weeks)\\n  * Recheck Vitamin D level (8–12 weeks).\\n  * Reassess progesterone response at 6–12 weeks and adjust regimen if needed.\\n  * Recheck fasting lipid panel 4–12 weeks after statin initiation; adjust therapy per LDL/ApoB response and tolerance.\\n  * Reassess androgen labs and review imaging results if obtained; escalate to endocrinology/gynecology referral as indicated.\\n\\nPhysician notification summary: Alert provider immediately for LDL ≥190 mg/dL (statin consideration) and for elevated testosterone (possible expedited endocrine workup; urgent if rapid virilization). Staff to ensure pregnancy test completed prior to initiating statin/anti‑androgen therapy and to obtain baseline BMP if spironolactone is being considered.\\n\\nIf you want, I can draft specific order sets (statin start orders, baseline labs, androgen panel, progesterone prescription wording, and monitoring schedule) for provider signature.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 1.8, "detail": "Triglycerides 92 / HDL 51 = 1.8", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5.1%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 84, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 15, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "121 mg/dL", "detail": "Elevated atherogenic particle count", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "0.4 mg/L", "detail": "Low inflammatory burden", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 1, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (1/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Lab pattern consistent with relative estrogen excess or impaired estrogen clearance. Even without reported symptoms, supporting liver detoxification and estrogen metabolism pathways may be beneficial.", "supportingFindings": ["Estradiol:Progesterone imbalance (E2 119, P4 1)", "Progesterone 1 ng/mL (very low)"]}, {"name": "Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Pattern consistent with HPA-axis stress and cortisol dysregulation. Chronic stress depletes DHEA-S, disrupts sleep, and impairs cognitive and emotional function.", "supportingFindings": ["Low energy / fatigue reported", "Brain fog reported", "Vitamin D 23.1 ng/mL (low, stress resilience impact)"]}, {"name": "Menopausal Transition", "confidence": "low", "description": "Clinical picture consistent with perimenopause or menopause. Hormonal shifts during this transition affect bone health, cardiovascular risk, mood, and quality of life.", "supportingFindings": ["Age 41 (perimenopause window)", "FSH 10.5 mIU/mL (rising, perimenopause range)"]}]}	2026-03-30 22:32:27.950622	1
