 Actions: discuss estrogen optimization with your clinician (transdermal or other HRT options) because estrogen helps prevent bone loss; start weight-bearing and resistance exercise 3x/week (walking, stair-climbing, squats) and ensure dietary calcium ~1,000–1,200 mg/day from dairy or supplements.\\n2) Progesterone 0.41 ng/mL and anovulation risk: If you were in the luteal phase when tested, this suggests anovulatory cycles. Action: document cycle day for future tests and review HRT dosing with your provider within 4–8 weeks.\\n3) Low testosterone: Total testosterone 16.7 ng/dL. Actions: prioritize 7–8 hours sleep nightly, add strength training 2–3x/week, and use stress reduction (15–20 minutes/day meditation or brisk walking). Discuss hormone options with your clinician if symptoms persist.\\n4) Vitamin D deficiency: 25-OH Vitamin D 18.3 ng/mL. Action: start repletion—either vitamin D3 50,000 IU weekly for 6–8 weeks OR 5,000–6,000 IU daily, then maintenance (1,000–2,000 IU/day). Recheck level in 8–12 weeks.\\n5) Hematocrit 45.7%: hydrate well (aim for 2–3 L fluids/day) and repeat CBC in 2–4 weeks.\\n6) B12 232 pg/mL (low-normal): consider oral B12 1,000 mcg daily if fatigue or deficiency symptoms; recheck in 8–12 weeks.\\n\\nCardiovascular plan (low risk 10-year ASCVD 0.6%): Continue heart-healthy habits—aim for 150 minutes/week moderate aerobic activity (brisk walking), Mediterranean-style eating (veg, whole grains, olive oil, fish), limit processed foods, maintain healthy weight.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Review HRT and bone protection with clinician in 4–8 weeks.\\n- Start vitamin D repletion now; recheck 25-OH D in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Repeat hormones with documented cycle day or after any HRT changes in 6–12 weeks.\\n- Repeat CBC after hydration in 2–4 weeks; check B12 after supplementation in 8–12 weeks.\\n\\nYou’re doing many things well—these targeted steps can help your symptoms, bone health, and energy. Please call to schedule a follow-up to make a clear plan.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 15.1, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 45.7, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Slightly elevated hematocrit - may indicate dehydration.", "recommendation": "Encourage hydration. Repeat if persistent.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 93.6, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 93.6 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 209, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 32, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol below bone protection threshold (<40 pg/mL). Bone resorption increases below 40-60 pg/mL.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase estrogen dose. Target 60-100 pg/mL. Current level inadequate for bone protection.", "referenceRange": "60-100 pg/mL (HRT goal, >40 for bone)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.41, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone below HRT therapeutic range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase progesterone dose. Target 8-10 ng/mL for optimal endometrial protection.", "referenceRange": "8-10 ng/mL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 39.3, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 16.7, "status": "abnormal", "ca