mits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 43, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 20, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 3.3, "status": "abnormal", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Elevated hs-CRP - increased cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Address cardiovascular risk factors. Consider repeat testing.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 66.9, "status": "normal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 66.9 nmol/L is within normal range. SHBG regulates bioavailable sex hormones by binding testosterone and estradiol.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 1 pg/mL is within normal functional range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "of 6 markers positive", "value": 2, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Insulin Resistance Screening", "recheckTiming": "3-6 months", "interpretation": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive). This combination points toward hyperinsulinemia physiology. Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR).", "recommendation": "Phenotype(s): Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance. Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose.", "referenceRange": "0-1 Low, 2 Moderate, 3+ High Likelihood"}], "aiRecommendations": "Clinical context (for staff review)\\n- Labs of concern: Total testosterone 13.3 ng/dL (borderline low), 25-OH vitamin D 37.2 ng/mL (insufficient per clinic targets), ApoB 117 mg/dL (borderline elevated), hs-CRP 3.3 mg/L (elevated → higher CV inflammatory risk).\\n- Other relevant normals: SHBG and free testosterone normal, estradiol/progesterone/FSH normal, ferritin and CBC normal, TSH normal, lipid panel LDL/HDL/triglycerides normal, 10-year ASCVD risk normal. Confirm whether patient is taking systemic estrogen, combined OCP, or other HRT (affects interpretation of testosterone and SHBG).\\n\\n1) Overall clinical assessment (concise)\\n- No acute/critical laboratory emergency.\\n- Findings suggest: borderline low total testosterone (but free T and SHBG normal → biochemical androgen deficiency not definitive); suboptimal vitamin D for hormone optimization; evidence of low-grade systemic inflammation (hs-CRP >3 mg/L) and atherogenic particle signal (ApoB 117 mg/dL) that warrant cardiovascular risk optimization despite normal calculated 10-year ASCVD score.\\n- Management options: conservative risk-factor modification and re-evaluation; consider a symptom-guided, physician-approved trial of low-dose testosterone if patient has clinically significant symptoms (low libido, decreased sexual function, persistent fatigue) after counseling.\\n\\n2) Priority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL (immediate physician notification)\\n- Notify physician if:\\n  - New or worsening constitutional symptoms, signs of infection/inflammation, or any acute cardiovascular symptoms.\\n  - Patient is pregnant, breastfeeding, or of childbearing potential without reliable contraception and testosterone therapy is being considered.\\n\\nURGENT (within 1–2 weeks)\\n- Review elevated hs-CRP (3.3 mg/L) with physician:\\n  - Check for acute causes of inflammation (recent infection, dental work, injury, autoimmune flare). If symptoms/signs present, defer CV-risk interpretation until resolved and repeat hs-CRP after recovery.\\n  - If no acute trigger, escalate CV risk counseling and consider earlier re-check and lipid/ApoB reassessment.\\n\\nROUTINE (initiate within 0–4 weeks)\\n- Start vitamin D supplementation per protocol: Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily. Document patient allergy/intolerance review and counsel about adherence and sun exposure.\\n- Lifestyle counseling for CV risk and ApoB reduction (see section 4).\\n- If patient symptomatic and interested in androgen therapy, discuss trial with physician; obtain informed consent including side effects and monitoring plan.\\n\\n3) Testosterone recommendations (staff-facing; physician approval required)\\n- Indication: Consider only if patient has persistent, clinically significant symptoms attributable to androgen deficiency (e.g., decreased libido, sexual dysfunction, refractory fatigue) and after discussion of risks/benefits.\\n- Recommended initial regimen (follow clinic HRT order set; physician must sign):\\n  - Preferred first-line: low‑dose transdermal testosterone trial.\\n    - Clinic example starting dose: transdermal testosterone 300 mcg (0.3 mg) daily (apply to abdomen/thigh/gluteal area per product instructions). If clinic formulary uses a different concentration, match to an approximate delivered dose of 300 mcg/day.\\n    - Alternative transdermal starting range (if protocol permits): 300–600 mcg/day.\\n  - Injectable alternative (only if clinic protocol supports): testosterone cypionate 10–25 mg IM or subcutaneous every 7–14 days as a low-dose protocol (physician to order exact dose/frequency).\\n- Monitoring and titration:\\n  - Re-check total testosterone, free testosterone, SHBG 8–12 weeks after initiation or dose change, and again at 6 months. Aim for symptomatic improvement without supraphysiologic levels; avoid levels that produce virilization.\\n  - Assess for adverse effects (acne, hirsutism, voice change, hair loss, mood changes) at 4–8 week intervals initially; document virilizing signs at each visit.\\n  - If no symptomatic improvement by 12 weeks and levels in target range, re-evaluate diagnosis and consider referral to endocrinology.\\n- Contraindications/precautions to review before starting:\\n  - Pregnancy/breastfeeding → do not start; ensure effective contraception if applicable.\\n  - Active hormone-sensitive malignancy → discuss with physician.\\n  - Liver disease, uncontrolled CVD → consult physician.\\n\\n4) Lifestyle interventions (specific staff instructions)\\n- Cardiovascular risk reduction (priority given elevated hs-CRP and ApoB):\\n  - Diet: Recommend Mediterranean-style diet (reduce processed carbs/trans fats; emphasize vegetables, fatty fish, fiber, nuts). Consider referral to dietitian.\\n  - Weight: Target 5–10% weight loss if BMI elevated; document baseline weight and set realistic goals.\\n  - Exercise: At least 150 minutes/week moderate-intensity aerobic activity + 2 sessions/week resistance training.\\n  - Smoking cessation: Offer counseling and pharmacotherapy if applicable.\\n  - Alcohol reduction: Limit to guideline amounts.\\n  - Sleep and stress: address sleep hygiene and stress reduction (CBT, mindfulness referrals as needed).\\n- Vitamin D: Instruct patient to take Metagenics D3 5000 IU + K daily with a meal; counsel on signs of vitamin D excess (rare) and calcium intake.\\n- General hormone health: If symptomatic and considering testosterone, counsel on potential side effects (acne, oily skin, hirsutism, voice deepening) and on expected timeline for benefit (~8–12 weeks for libido/fatigue improvement).\\n\\n5) Additional testing or referrals to consider (ordered based on physician direction)\\n- Repeat testing:\\n  - Vitamin D 25-OH: recheck in 8–12 weeks after starting supplementation. Target per clinic: ≥60 ng/mL.\\n  - Total testosterone, free testosterone, SHBG: recheck 8–12 weeks after any testosterone start/change; otherwise repeat in 3 months if untreated but symptomatic.\\n  - hs-CRP: repeat in 6–12 weeks after addressing acute causes/lifestyle changes to confirm persistence.\\n  - ApoB + fasting lipid panel: re-evaluate in 3 months; ensure fasting status for accurate triglyceride assessment.\\n- Additional labs if indicated:\\n  - High-sensitivity troponin/ECG only if cardiac symptoms.\\n  - Evaluate inflammatory sources if hs-CRP persists (CBC, ESR, consider dental/ENT/GI review).\\n  - If patterns suggest adrenal contribution to androgen status (e.g., signs of hyperandrogenism despite low total T), consider DHEA-S and 17‑OH progesterone.\\n- Referrals:\\n  - Cardiology or lipidology if ApoB remains ≥110–120 mg/dL or if there is persistent hs-CRP elevation >3 despite lifestyle measures and patient has other risk factors — for consideration of statin therapy or PCSK9/other lipid-lowering strategies.\\n  - Endocrinology if complex androgen deficiency, unclear source of androgen changes, or difficulty titrating testosterone.\\n  - Gynecology if fertility concerns or contraception counseling before HRT initiation.\\n  - Dietitian and behavioral health as needed for lifestyle interventions.\\n\\n6) Summary of next steps and timeline (actionable checklist)\\n- Immediate (today)\\n  - Confirm documentation of current medications (OCP/HRT, supplements) and pregnancy status; notify physician of hs-CRP 3.3 and ApoB 117.\\n  - Counsel patient on starting Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K daily (if no contraindication) and document counseling.\\n  - Provide cardiovascular lifestyle counseling and offer referrals (dietitian, smoking cessation) as appropriate.\\n\\n- Short term (within 2 weeks)\\n  - Physician to decide on trial of testosterone therapy if patient symptomatic and consents.\\n  - If testosterone to be started: place prescription per clinic protocol (transdermal 300 mcg/day suggested starting dose) and schedule monitoring.\\n\\n- Follow-up testing schedule (if interventions started)\\n  - 6–12 weeks: repeat hs-CRP (if no acute infection), symptom check.\\n  - 8–12 weeks: recheck total testosterone, free testosterone, SHBG after starting or changing testosterone dose.\\n  - 8–12 weeks: recheck 25-OH vitamin D level after supplementation.\\n  - 3 months: fasting lipid panel + ApoB recheck; review need for lipid-lowering therapy if ApoB remains elevated.\\n  - Ongoing: clinic visit/calls at 4–8 weeks for adverse effect screening if on testosterone; reassess need to continue/titrate at 12 weeks.\\n\\nPhysician notification requirements (explicit)\\n- Notify ordering physician or supervising clinician:\\n  - Prior to initiating testosterone therapy (required sign-off).\\n  - For persistent hs-CRP >3.0 mg/L without an acute explanation.\\n  - If ApoB remains ≥110–120 mg/dL despite lifestyle changes or if family history/personal history raises risk.\\n  - For any adverse events or signs of virilization after starting testosterone.\\n\\nPatient education points to cover (staff script items)\\n- Explain borderline low total testosterone vs normal free testosterone and that treatment is symptom-driven; risks/benefits of a trial of low-dose testosterone.\\n- Explain vitamin D insufficiency, rationale for 5,000 IU daily, expected timeline to normalization (8–12 weeks) and need for re-check.\\n- Communicate the meaning of elevated hs-CRP and ApoB as markers of inflammation and atherogenic particle burden and emphasize lifestyle modification as first-line.\\n- Discuss contraception and pregnancy avoidance if testosterone therapy is being considered.\\n\\nNotes specific to cycle/HRT interactions\\n- Document cycle day when hormones drawn; if premenopausal, interpretation of testosterone/estradiol/progesterone can vary by cycle phase—note for physician review.\\n- If patient is taking estrogen-containing HRT or OCPs, SHBG may be elevated and total testosterone interpretation altered; confirm medication use prior to decisions.\\n\\nEndorsement\\n- All therapeutic decisions (initiation/titration of testosterone, starting statin or other lipid-lowering therapy) require physician ordering and informed consent. Follow clinic HRT and lipid management protocols for exact medication selection and dose adjustments.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 1, "detail": "Triglycerides 61 / HDL 61 = 1", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "4.9%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 66.9, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 14, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "117 mg/dL", "detail": "Elevated atherogenic particle count", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "3.3 mg/L", "detail": "Elevated systemic inflammation", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "moderate", "phenotypes": [{"key": "visceral_metabolic", "name": "Visceral / Metabolic Insulin Resistance", "monitoringPlan": "Recheck A1c, lipid panel, hs-CRP, ApoB in 3-6 months", "matchedCriteria": ["ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0"], "pathophysiology": "Early peripheral + hepatic insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia. Glucose may still be \\"normal.\\" A1c often rises last.", "triggerCriteria": ["A1c >= 5.7%", "TG/HDL >= 3", "ApoB >= 90 mg/dL", "hs-CRP >= 2.0", "Central adiposity phenotype", "ALT above optimal range"], "patientExplanation": "What This Means\\n\\nYour lab pattern suggests your body may not be responding to insulin as efficiently as it should. Insulin is the hormone that helps move sugar from your bloodstream into your cells for energy.\\n\\nWhen cells become less sensitive to insulin:\\n- Blood sugar slowly rises over time\\n- Triglycerides increase\\n- Inflammation increases\\n- Weight gain around the midsection becomes easier\\n\\nThe good news? This stage is highly reversible with targeted nutrition, strength training, sleep optimization, and - when appropriate - medication support.\\n\\nWe'll focus on improving how your body processes fuel and reducing long-term cardiometabolic risk.", "treatmentRecommendations": ["Protein-forward nutrition (>= 25-35g/meal)", "Carbohydrate quality + timing education", "Strength training 2-3x weekly", "10-15 min post-meal walking", "Sleep optimization", "Consider: GLP-1 therapy if obesity, appetite dysregulation, A1c >= 5.7, or inflammatory phenotype", "Consider: Metformin if prediabetes or strong family history", "Recheck metabolic panel in 3-6 months"]}], "positiveCount": 2, "likelihoodLabel": "MODERATE LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: MODERATE LIKELIHOOD (2/6 markers positive). This combination points toward hyperinsulinemia physiology. Consider confirmation with fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR).", "confirmationTests": "Optional Confirmation: Fasting insulin + fasting glucose (HOMA-IR), OR fasting C-peptide + glucose."}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Inflammatory Burden", "confidence": "high", "description": "Pattern of systemic inflammation indicated by elevated inflammatory markers and/or symptomatic presentation. May benefit from anti-inflammatory nutritional support.", "supportingFindings": ["hs-CRP 3.3 mg/L (elevated >3.0)", "ApoB 117 mg/dL (elevated)"]}, {"name": "Menopausal Transition", "confidence": "high", "description": "Clinical picture consistent with perimenopause or menopause. Hormonal shifts during this transition affect bone health, cardiovascular risk, mood, and quality of life.", "supportingFindings": ["FSH 10.3 mIU/mL (rising, perimenopause range)", "Estradiol 38 pg/mL (declining)"]}, {"name": "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Metabolic pattern consistent with insulin resistance and/or visceral adiposity. This drives inflammation, hormonal imbalance, and cardiovascular risk.", "supportingF