report specific details of menses or menopausal symptoms during today’s encounter; no new bleeding reported.\\n- Medications/supplements: Not specifically provided in chart. (Recommend reconciliation at provider visit.)\\n- Allergies: Not provided.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vital signs: Not available in chart for today.\\n- Relevant calculated scores:\\n  * 10-year Total CVD risk (PREVENT): 0.3% (low)\\n  * 10-year ASCVD risk (PREVENT): 0.2%\\n  * 10-year Heart Failure risk (PREVENT): 0.4%\\n  * 30-year CVD risk (PREVENT): 2.1%\\n  * STOP‑BANG (sleep apnea risk): 0 points\\n  * FIB‑4 score: 0.86\\n\\nLaboratory data (04/01/2026):\\n- CBC\\n  * Hemoglobin 13 g/dL — normal\\n  * Hematocrit 39.3% — normal\\n  * MCV 95.4 fL — normal\\n  * Platelets 275 K/uL — normal\\n- Metabolic / Renal / Liver\\n  * Creatinine 0.8 mg/dL — normal\\n  * eGFR 92 mL/min/1.73 m2 — normal\\n  * AST 37 U/L — borderline high (reference-dependent; mild elevation)\\n  * ALT 52 U/L — borderline high (mild elevation) — provider review recommended prior to oral estrogen therapy\\n  * FIB‑4 score 0.86 — low (low likelihood advanced fibrosis)\\n- Lipid panel\\n  * LDL‑C 105 mg/dL — borderline (near optimal)\\n  * HDL‑C 144 mg/dL — high/optimal\\n  * Triglycerides 60 mg/dL — normal\\n- Glycemic / Metabolic\\n  * Hemoglobin A1c 5.4% — normal\\n- Micronutrients / Other labs\\n  * 25‑OH Vitamin D 31.5 ng/mL — borderline insufficient (31–40 ng/mL)\\n  * Vitamin B12 600 pg/mL — normal\\n- Hormones\\n  * Estradiol (E2) 5 pg/mL — low/ABNORMAL (hypoestrogenic)\\n  * FSH 104 mIU/mL — elevated/ABNORMAL (consistent with menopause)\\n  * Progesterone (P4) 0.05 ng/mL — low/consistent with postmenopausal state\\n  * Total Testosterone 24.3 ng/dL — within expected range (adequate androgen status)\\n  * TSH 1.17 mIU/L — normal\\n\\nASSESSMENT (numbered problem list):\\n1. Biochemical menopause / hypoestrogenic state (\\"crash\\" perimenopausal → menopausal pattern).\\n   - Basis: Estradiol 5 pg/mL (low) with FSH 104 mIU/mL (markedly elevated) — consistent with ovarian failure/menopause.\\n   - Clinical significance: Explains decreased estrogen production; patient is at risk for vasomotor symptoms, genitourinary syndrome of menopause, bone density loss long-term. Patient currently reports no specific menopausal symptoms.\\n   - Red flag: None immediate, but estrogen therapy decisions require review given mild LFT abnormalities (see #3).\\n2. Vitamin D insufficiency.\\n   - Basis: 25‑OH Vitamin D 31.5 ng/mL (borderline insufficient; target for hormone optimization higher, goal ≥60 ng/mL per clinic protocol).\\n   - Clinical significance: Suboptimal for bone health and hormone optimization.\\n3. Mild transaminitis (borderline AST/ALT elevations).\\n   - Basis: AST 37 U/L, ALT 52 U/L (ALT mildly elevated).\\n   - Clinical significance: Mild hepatic enzyme elevations; FIB‑4 low and eGFR normal. Possible etiologies to consider: nonalcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD), medication/supplement effect, alcohol use, viral hepatitis; requires follow-up.\\n   - Red flag: Provider notification required prior to initiating any oral estrogen therapy. If ALT/AST rise >2× baseline or patient develops hepatic symptoms, urgent evaluation/referral needed.\\n4. Borderline elevated LDL cholesterol.\\n   - Basis: LDL 105 mg/dL (near optimal but above ideal for some risk profiles).\\n   - Clinical significance: Global 10-year CVD risk is low (ASCVD 0.2%); statin generally not indicated at this risk level. Lifestyle measures recommended.\\n5. Adequate androgen status / testosterone not low.\\n   - Basis: Total testosterone 24.3 ng/dL within expected range; SHBG not extreme per report.\\n   - Clinical significance: Symptoms such as fatigue, low libido, low mood are unlikely to be primarily due to androgen deficiency. Avoid initiating testosterone therapy now.\\n6. Other routine labs normal.\\n   - Basis: CBC, creatinine, eGFR, HbA1c, B12, triglycerides, HDL, FIB‑4 within acceptable ranges.\\n\\nPLAN:\\nGeneral:\\n- Discussed results with patient today. Reviewed that labs confirm menopausal biochemical status and described implications and management options. Patient verbalized understanding. Provider-staff coordination items below for orders and provider decision-making.\\n\\n1) Management of biochemical menopause / hypoestrogenic state\\n   - If patient is symptomatic and desires hormone therapy (HT), recommend transdermal estradiol as first-line because it bypasses first-pass hepatic metabolism given the mild ALT elevation.\\n     * Suggested regimen for provider consideration: Transdermal estradiol patch 25–50 mcg/day (0.025–0.05 mg patch) — provider decision required.\\n     * If uterus intact, add micronized progesterone for endometrial protection:\\n       - Option A (continuous): Micronized progesterone 100–200 mg PO qHS continuous.\\n       - Option B (cyclic): Micronized progesterone 100–200 mg PO qHS x12–14 days/month.\\n     * Provider to document chosen regimen and counsel on expected benefits/side effects.\\n   - Do NOT initiate oral estrogen without provider approval and after reviewing LFT trend.\\n   - If patient is asymptomatic and prefers no HT, discuss non‑hormonal options and monitoring for bone health and genitourinary symptoms.\\n\\n2) Vitamin D replacement\\n   - Start Vitamin D3 5,000 IU + K daily (Metagenics D3 5,000 IU + K or equivalent) — order placed.\\n   - Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D in 8–12 weeks aiming for ≥60 ng/mL; adjust dose as needed.\\n   - Counsel on dietary calcium intake and safe sun exposure.\\n\\n3) Elevated AST/ALT — hepatic evaluation and monitoring\\n   - Repeat AST, ALT in 4–6 weeks to assess trend prior to/after HT initiation.\\n   - If ALT/AST increase >2× baseline or become >2–3× ULN, or if patient develops symptoms (jaundice, RUQ pain), notify provider immediately and arrange hepatitis panel (HBsAg, HCV Ab) and hepatic imaging (ultrasound) as indicated; consider GI/hepatology referral.\\n   - Lifestyle counseling: reduce alcohol intake, review and stop hepatotoxic supplements/medications, weight optimization, Mediterranean-style diet, limit added sugars.\\n   - Document orders: repeat LFTs scheduled in 4–6 weeks.\\n\\n4) Lipid management\\n   - Lifestyle modifications recommended: Mediterranean-style diet, reduce saturated fat, increase fiber, regular aerobic exercise (≥150 min/week) + resistance training 2×/week.\\n   - Repeat fasting lipid panel in 3 months after lifestyle interventions. Statin therapy not indicated at present given 10-year ASCVD risk low; reassess if risk factors change.\\n\\n5) Androgen status / testosterone\\n   - No testosterone therapy indicated at this time given total testosterone 24.3 ng/dL within range.\\n   - If persistent sexual dysfunction or low libido after estrogen/progesterone optimization, pursue broader evaluation (iron studies, thyroid function, sleep, mood) before considering testosterone. Consider DHEA‑S if adrenal contribution suspected and per provider discretion.\\n\\n6) Iron studies and fatigue evaluation\\n   - Order ferritin, iron, TIBC now to evaluate for iron deficiency as contributor to fatigue/low libido; target ferritin >50 ng/mL if symptomatic.\\n   - If ferritin low and iron deficiency confirmed, consider oral iron therapy per provider decision and recheck in 6–8 weeks after initiation.\\n\\n7) Additional testing / referrals\\n   - Order ferritin/iron studies today.\\n   - Order repeat AST/ALT in 4–6 weeks.\\n   - If transaminases remain elevated, order hepatitis B and C serologies and consider liver ultrasound for steatosis.\\n   - Consider nutritionist referral for weight and lipid optimization if indicated.\\n   - Consider behavioral health referral if mood, anxiety, or sleep symptoms become prominent.\\n\\n8) Supplements (per clinic recommendations)\\n   - Start Vitamin D3 5,000 IU + K daily (as above).\\n   - Optional medium-priority supplements for consideration: CoQ10 300 mg chewable gel daily; AdvaClear® 2 capsules twice daily — discuss with provider and reconcile with current medications prior to starting.\\n\\n9) Follow-up and timeline\\n   - Immediate (today): Orders placed for Vitamin D supplement, ferritin/iron studies, and to schedule repeat LFTs in 4–6 weeks. Flag chart for provider decision regarding HT (transdermal estradiol ± progesterone).\\n   - 1–2 weeks: Provider to decide on initiating HT and document chosen regimen and contraindications review.\\n   - 4–6 weeks: Repeat AST/ALT. If persistent elevation, proceed with hepatitis panel and imaging as indicated.\\n   - 6–12 weeks after HT initiation: Clinical follow-up to assess symptom response, side effects, blood pressure, and consider further labs as needed.\\n   - 8–12 weeks: Recheck 25‑OH Vitamin D and adjust dosing to achieve target ≥60 ng/mL.\\n   - 3 months: Repeat lipid panel and routine metabolic assessment.\\n   - Routine follow-up window: 3–6 months for ongoing management.\\n\\n10) Notifications / safety triggers\\n   - Notify provider prior to initiating any oral estrogen.\\n   - Notify provider immediately if ALT/AST rise >2× baseline, show sustained elevation, or if patient develops hepatic symptoms.\\n   - Notify provider if ferritin <50 with symptomatic anemia.\\n\\nDocumentation:\\n- Results were reviewed with the patient today and discussed. Counseling provided on lifestyle interventions (alcohol moderation, diet, exercise), rationale for transdermal estrogen preference given LFTs, and vitamin D replacement. Risks, benefits, and need for follow-up labs were reviewed.\\n- Orders placed: Vitamin D3 5,000 IU + K daily (supplement recommendation), ferritin/iron studies, repeat AST/ALT in 4–6 weeks, lipid panel in 3 months (per clinic protocol). Provider flagged for HT decision.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\n04/01/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "preventRisk": {"ldlGoal": "No specific LDL target; focus on risk-based statin intensity", "riskCategory": "low", "tenYearASCVD": 0.0017420263766603375, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0.002644062144138707, "thirtyYearASCVD": 0.012811161057946196, "ageValidFor30Year": true, "thirtyYearTotalCVD": 0.021323373908753933, "tenYearHFPercentage": "0.4%", "tenYearHeartFailure": 0.00426692576779784, "statinRecommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "tenYearCVDPercentage": "0.3%", "tenYearASCVDPercentage": "0.2%", "thirtyYearHFPercentage": "3.9%", "thirtyYearHeartFailure": 0.03850438007082117, "thirtyYearCVDPercentage": "2.1%", "thirtyYearASCVDPercentage": "1.3%"}, "supplements": [{"dose": "1 softgel daily with meal", "name": "D3 5,000 + K", "caution": "Repletion dose for deficiency/insufficiency. Contains vitamin K2 for optimal calcium metabolism. Recheck levels in 8-12 weeks.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "D3 5,000 + K provides vitamin D3 with K2 for repletion of deficiency/insufficiency. Target ≥60 ng/mL for bone, immune, and cardiovascular support.", "indication": "Vitamin D 31.5 ng/mL (insufficient)", "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D is below optimal range. This supplement will help restore your levels to support bone health, immune function, and overall wellness.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 31.5 ng/mL (insufficient)"]}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ1