). Take these concrete steps now:\\n  - Add 25–30 g fiber daily: oatmeal for breakfast, beans with lunch, vegetables at dinner.\\n  - Include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n  - Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter and cheese.\\n  - Daily exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking or equivalent.\\n- Triglycerides 195 mg/dL (borderline high) — the same changes above help; also avoid sugary drinks and refined carbs.\\n- Apolipoprotein B 146 mg/dL and Lipoprotein(a) 74 mg/dL are risk enhancers. Discuss with your clinician about whether to start a statin or refer to cardiology for further evaluation.\\n- Because your 30-year CVD risk = 22.1% (meaning higher lifetime risk), consider more intensive heart-healthy changes and discussion of medications if lifestyle alone doesn’t lower LDL.\\n\\nOther women’s health notes\\n- Bone health: consider weight-bearing exercise and ensure calcium (1,000–1,200 mg/day) and vitamin D (800–2,000 IU/day) intake; discuss DEXA if additional risk factors.\\n- No sex-hormone tests were done. If future hormone testing is needed, timing in your menstrual cycle can change results—ask your clinician when to schedule.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Start the lifestyle actions today.\\n- Schedule a follow-up visit in 4–8 weeks to review progress and discuss starting a statin given LDL 189 mg/dL, ApoB 146, and Lp(a) 74.\\n- Recheck fasting lipid panel in 6–12 weeks after lifestyle changes or sooner if a medication is started.\\n- If you notice symptoms like chest pain, shortness of breath, or sudden swelling, seek urgent care.\\n\\nYou’re doing well in many areas — these targeted changes can lower your long-term risk. Your care team is here to help you make a plan.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 14.2, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 