hese occur).\\n- Menstrual history / menopausal status: Perimenopausal pattern on labs (FSH elevated). Date of last menstrual period (LMP) and cycle day not documented in chart at time of lab review — requested and pending; cycle-phase timing unknown. Current HRT regimen (route, dose, timing) not documented in chart — requested and pending confirmation prior to any medication changes.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vitals: Not recorded in lab report (BP, HR, BMI not available in chart entry).\\n- Laboratory results (04/02/2026). Abnormal / flagged values noted.\\n\\nCBC\\n- Hemoglobin 13.6 g/dL (normal)\\n- Hematocrit 42.2 % (normal)\\n- MCV 95.3 fL (normal)\\n- Platelets 235 K/uL (normal)\\n\\nBasic metabolic / renal / liver\\n- Creatinine 0.66 mg/dL (normal)\\n- eGFR 109 mL/min/1.73 m2 (normal)\\n- AST 19 U/L (normal)\\n- ALT 22 U/L (normal)\\n\\nIron studies / nutrition\\n- Ferritin 140 ng/mL (normal)\\n- Serum iron 75 ug/dL (normal)\\n- TIBC 346 ug/dL (normal)\\n- Folate 20 ng/mL (normal)\\n- Vitamin B12 847 pg/mL (normal)\\n\\nGlycemic\\n- Hemoglobin A1c 5.5 % (normal)\\n\\nLipid profile / cardiovascular markers\\n- LDL cholesterol 51 mg/dL (normal)\\n- HDL cholesterol 45 mg/dL [ABNORMAL — low]\\n- Triglycerides 113 mg/dL (normal)\\n- Apolipoprotein B 57 mg/dL (normal)\\n- Lipoprotein(a) 20 mg/dL (normal)\\n- hs-CRP 1 mg/L [BORDERLINE]\\n\\nHormones\\n- FSH 27.1 mIU/mL [ABNORMAL] — consistent with perimenopausal transition\\n- Estradiol (E2) 57 pg/mL [BORDERLINE] — at lower end of bone protection range\\n- Progesterone 1.18 ng/mL [ABNORMAL] — below luteal/HRT therapeutic target; may indicate inadequate luteal progesterone or anovulation if drawn in luteal phase\\n- Testosterone, total 24.8 ng/dL [ABNORMAL — low vs clinic target]\\n- Free testosterone 4 pg/mL (normal)\\n- SHBG 31.9 nmol/L (normal)\\n\\nVitamin / other\\n- Vitamin D (25-OH) 37.2 ng/mL [BORDERLINE — insufficient per optimization target]\\n\\nRisk scores / calculated results\\n- 10-year Total CVD risk (AHA PREVENT 2023): 1.6% (low)\\n- 10-year ASCVD risk: 1.0% (low)\\n- 10-year Heart Failure risk: 1.5% (low)\\n- 30-year CVD risk: 12.5%\\n- Sleep apnea risk (STOP-BANG): 1 point (low)\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1) Perimenopausal transition with vasomotor symptoms.\\n   - Evidence: FSH 27.1 mIU/mL (elevated for reproductive-aged reference; consistent with menopausal transition) and symptomatic hot flashes/night sweats.\\n   - Estradiol 57 pg/mL is borderline (at minimal bone protection range); symptoms likely due to fluctuating estrogen typical of perimenopause.\\n\\n2) Low progesterone — inadequate luteal/progestogen exposure or anovulation.\\n   - Progesterone 1.18 ng/mL (low). Interpretation limited by unknown cycle phase/LMP and timing of last progesterone dose if on HRT.\\n   - Clinical significance: If uterus present and patient receiving estrogen or at risk of unopposed estrogen exposure, endometrial protection is required. If drawn in luteal phase, this level suggests anovulation — consistent with perimenopause.\\n\\n3) Low total testosterone despite HRT (if on testosterone).\\n   - Total testosterone 24.8 ng/dL — below clinic optimization target (75–125 ng/dL). May explain persistent symptoms affecting libido, energy, or muscle mass if present.\\n   - Free testosterone within reference; however clinic targets for symptomatic benefit rely on total testosterone.\\n\\n4) Borderline vitamin D insufficiency — suboptimal for hormone optimization.\\n   - 25-OH Vitamin D 37.2 ng/mL (target per protocol ≥60 ng/mL for optimization).\\n\\n5) Low HDL (45 mg/dL) and borderline hs-CRP (1 mg/L) — cardiovascular risk factors to address.\\n   - Overall 10-year risk low; still recommend lifestyle interventions given low HDL.\\n\\n6) No acute laboratory red flags.\\n   - CBC, CMP, LFTs, creatinine, renal function within normal limits. No critical findings requiring emergent action documented.\\n   - Red flag reminders: notify provider immediately if patient has uterus and is/will be exposed to unopposed estrogen; notify provider for any new abnormal uterine bleeding, chest pain, or neurologic symptoms.\\n\\nPLAN:\\n(General: Confirm LMP/cycle day and current HRT regimen [route, dose, frequency, timing of last dose] before implementing medication changes. Provider authorization required for all prescription changes. Patient counseling provided; documented questions to be addressed at follow-up.)\\n\\nA. Perimenopause / vasomotor symptoms / estradiol optimization\\n   - Action: Request and document LMP/cycle day and confirm current estradiol formulation and dose.\\n   - If patient is on transdermal estradiol and symptomatic or goal estradiol not achieved: recommend provider consider increasing transdermal estradiol one step (example protocol: 0.0375 mg → 0.05–0.075 mg/day or 0.05 → 0.075 mg/day). Provider to confirm exact dose changes.\\n   - If patient is on oral estradiol: consider switching to transdermal route for steadier levels if appropriate.\\n   - Labs/follow-up: Recheck serum estradiol 4–6 weeks after any dose change; ensure timing of draw standardized for route used.\\n   - Education: Discuss rationale for steady-delivery estradiol (patch) to reduce fluctuations and vasomotor symptoms.\\n\\nB. Low progesterone — endometrial protection / anovulation management\\n   - Urgent action: Confirm whether patient has a uterus. If uterus present and not currently on adequate progestogen, notify provider immediately to initiate/adjust progestogen to avoid unopposed estrogen.\\n   - If not currently on progesterone and uterus intact: recommend provider consider starting micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly (empiric) OR medroxyprogesterone acetate 10 mg daily x12–14 days/month. Provider to authorize regimen.\\n   - If already on micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly and progesterone remains low: propose increasing to 200 mg nightly or altering timing; provider decision.\\n   - Important: Document timing of last progesterone dose relative to lab draw (blood should be timed appropriately for interpretation).\\n   - Labs/follow-up: Recheck serum progesterone 4–8 weeks after initiation or dose adjustment; document cycle phase and last dose timing.\\n\\nC. Low total testosterone (if symptomatic or on testosterone replacement)\\n   - Action: Obtain current testosterone route and dose. Provider to consider adjusting per protocol:\\n     - Topical: increase dose by ~25–50% (e.g., example adjustment; exact dose requires provider confirmation).\\n     - IM: consider increase +10–20 mg per injection or shorten interval (e.g., q14 → q10 days) — provider to specify mg and interval.\\n     - Pellets: consider earlier additional pellet placement per provider.\\n   - Labs/follow-up: Recheck total testosterone (timed as trough for injectable or consistent timing for topical) 6–8 weeks after change.\\n   - Monitor for side effects (acne, virilization, lipid changes, liver enzymes) and notify provider if occur.\\n\\nD. Vitamin D repletion\\n   - Start Vitamin D3 5,000 IU daily with K (e.g., D3 5,000 IU + K supplement) per clinic protocol; provider to review/authorize.\\n   - Recheck 25-OH Vitamin D in 12 weeks; target ≥60 ng/mL for optimization.\\n\\nE. Lipids / cardiovascular risk reduction\\n   - Counseling: Lifestyle modifications prioritized — aerobic exercise ≥150 min/week, resistance training 2×/week, Mediterranean-style diet, reduce refined carbs/trans fats, limit alcohol, smoking cessation if applicable.\\n   - Given low 10-year ASCVD risk, statin therapy not indicated at this time per PREVENT estimate; reassess if risk factors change.\\n   - Consider referral to primary care or cardiology if HDL remains low despite lifestyle changes or if hs-CRP rises or other risk factors develop.\\n   - Labs/follow-up: Repeat lipid panel and hs-CRP in 3 months after lifestyle interventions.\\n\\nF. Supplements (clinic recommendations; discuss with provider)\\n   - High-priority: HerWellness™ Estrovera® 1 tablet daily; AdvaClear® 2 capsules twice daily; UltraFlora® Healthy Weight with Akkermansia 1 capsule daily; NutraGems® CoQ10 300 1 chewable gel daily.\\n   - Medium-priority: Vitamin D3 5,000 IU + K daily; EstroFactors™ 2 tablets twice daily; GlutaClear® 2 capsules daily.\\n   - Note: Supplements discussed as recommendations; patient instructed to obtain provider approval prior to starting to ensure no interactions with medications.\\n\\nG. Additional testing / referrals\\n   - If no recent bone density (DEXA) and patient meets risk criteria or if prolonged low estradiol anticipated: consider baseline DEXA per provider decision.\\n   - If abnormal uterine bleeding occurs or persists after HRT changes: consider transvaginal ultrasound and/or endometrial sampling.\\n   - Consider endocrinology or reproductive endocrinology referral if achieving testosterone/progesterone targets is difficult or for complex hormone management.\\n   - Pregnancy test if still menstruating and contraception status unclear prior to initiating or changing estrogen/testosterone therapy.\\n\\nH. Patient education / documentation\\n   - Education provided regarding importance of documenting LMP/cycle day and timing of blood draws relative to progesterone/testosterone dosing.\\n   - Counseling provided on HRT rationale, need for progesterone if uterus intact, potential side effects of progesterone and testosterone, and lifestyle measures to improve HDL and reduce CVD risk.\\n   - Patient instructed to report immediately if abnormal uterine bleeding, chest pain, shortness of breath, or neurologic symptoms.\\n\\nI. Follow-up and orders\\n   - Immediate (today): Request documentation of LMP/cycle day and current HRT regimen. Notify provider if uterus present and progesterone absence/inadequacy.\\n   - Within 1 week: Present proposed medication adjustments to provider for authorization (estradiol dose increase or route change; progesterone initiation or escalation; testosterone adjustment). Do not change prescriptions without provider sign-off.\\n   - 4–6 weeks after estradiol change: Recheck estradiol.\\n   - 4–8 weeks after progesterone change or initiation: Recheck progesterone (document timing).\\n   - 6–8 weeks after testosterone adjustment: Recheck total testosterone (timed appropriately).\\n   - 12 weeks after starting Vitamin D: Recheck 25-OH Vitamin D.\\n   - 3 months after lifestyle interventions: Repeat lipid panel and hs-CRP.\\n   - Schedule follow-up visit or phone check within 4 weeks after any HRT initiation/change to assess symptoms and side effects. Routine recheck window: 3–6 months.\\n\\nPhysician notification summary (urgent items placed in chart for sign-off):\\n- Any initiation or escalation of progestogen if uterus present.\\n- Any estradiol dose increase or route change.\\n- Any testosterone dose increase (need specific dosing order).\\n- New abnormal uterine bleeding or acute cardiopulmonary/neurologic symptoms.\\n\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.\\n\\nDate: 04/02/2026", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": true, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": false, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": false}, "preventRisk": {"ldlGoal": "No specific LDL target; focus on risk-based statin intensity", "riskCategory": "low", "tenYearASCVD": 0.0098659554889008, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0.015939138359336497, "thirtyYearASCVD": 0.07067296272106392, "ageValidFor30Year": true, "thirtyYearTotalCVD": 0.12504839579127017, "tenYearHFPercentage": "1.5%", "tenYearHeartFailure"