ggish, or metabolic dysfunction is present.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance pattern (hepatic detox pathways needed for clearance); Insulin resistance / metabolic dysfunction (hepatic metabolic support); Borderline liver markers (ALT 22, AST 19) suggesting hepatic biotransformation load; Weight gain (metabolic clearance factor)", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance", "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "This supplement supports your liver's natural detoxification processes. Your results suggest your body could benefit from help processing hormones, metabolic byproducts, and other compounds. It provides nutrients that support both phases of your liver's detox pathways.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance pattern (hepatic detox pathways needed for clearance)", "Insulin resistance / metabolic dysfunction (hepatic metabolic support)", "Borderline liver markers (ALT 22, AST 19) suggesting hepatic biotransformation load", "Weight gain (metabolic clearance factor)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Healthy Weight with Akkermansia", "caution": "Contains Akkermansia muciniphila and Bifidobacterium lactis B420 for metabolic and gut barrier support. Refrigerate after opening for maximum potency.", "category": "metabolic", "priority": "high", "rationale": "UltraFlora Healthy Weight with Akkermansia provides targeted probiotic support for the gut-metabolic axis. Akkermansia muciniphila supports gut barrier integrity, metabolic health, and healthy weight management. Indicated for insulin resistance, metabolic syndrome features, visceral adiposity, and weight loss resistance.", "indication": "Insulin resistance / visceral adiposity phenotype (gut-metabolic axis support); Weight gain / weight loss resistance; SHBG 31.9 nmol/L (low, metabolic marker)", "phenotypes": ["Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "This specialized probiotic contains Akkermansia, a beneficial gut bacteria linked to healthy metabolism and weight management. It supports your gut barrier, helps manage metabolic inflammation, and works with your body's natural weight regulation systems.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance / visceral adiposity phenotype (gut-metabolic axis support)", "Weight gain / weight loss resistance", "SHBG 31.9 nmol/L (low, metabolic marker)"]}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Essential for patients on statins.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "high", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "On statin therapy (CoQ10 depletion); HDL 45 mg/dL (low); Age 46 (declining CoQ10 production)", "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["On statin therapy (CoQ10 depletion)", "HDL 45 mg/dL (low)", "Age 46 (declining CoQ10 production)"]}, {"dose": "1 softgel daily with meal", "name": "D3 5,000 + K", "caution": "Repletion dose for deficiency/insufficiency. Contains vitamin K2 for optimal calcium metabolism. Recheck levels in 8-12 weeks.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "D3 5,000 + K provides vitamin D3 with K2 for repletion of deficiency/insufficiency. Target ≥60 ng/mL for bone, immune, and cardiovascular support.", "indication": "Vitamin D 37.2 ng/mL (insufficient)", "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D is below optimal range. This supplement will help restore your levels to support bone health, immune function, and overall wellness.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 37.2 ng/mL (insufficient)"]}, {"dose": "2 tablets twice daily", "name": "EstroFactors™", "caution": "Nutritional support for estrogen balance and metabolism. Contains I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Supports healthy estrogen metabolite ratios.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "EstroFactors provides targeted nutritional support for estrogen metabolism and balance with I3C, DIM, and calcium D-glucarate. Best suited for women with estrogen dominance patterns, PMS, perimenopausal hormone imbalance, or those needing broader estrogen metabolism support during hormonal transitions. Differs from Estrovera in that it addresses the underlying estrogen metabolism rather than vasomotor symptom relief.", "indication": "Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected; Weight gain (estrogen metabolism factor); Age-appropriate for estrogen metabolism support", "phenotypes": ["Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement helps your body process and balance estrogen more effectively. It supports healthy hormone metabolism, which can help with PMS symptoms, bloating, mood changes, and hormonal balance during life transitions.", "supportingFindings": ["Estrogen dominance / impaired clearance phenotype detected", "Weight gain (estrogen metabolism factor)", "Age-appropriate for estrogen metabolism support"]}, {"dose": "2 capsules daily", "name": "GlutaClear®", "caution": "Glutathione and antioxidant support formula. Contains NAC, alpha-lipoic acid, and green tea catechins. Supports cellular defense against oxidative damage.", "category": "detox", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "GlutaClear supports glutathione production and antioxidant defense with NAC, alpha-lipoic acid, and green tea catechins. Indicated when there is evidence of oxidative stress, inflammatory burden, or metabolic dysfunction requiring enhanced cellular defense. While AdvaClear provides broader liver detoxification support, GlutaClear specifically targets glutathione pathways and antioxidant capacity.", "indication": "Insulin resistance (oxidative metabolic stress); ALT 22 U/L (hepatic glutathione demand); Age 46 (declining glutathione production)", "phenotypes": ["Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity"], "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This supplement boosts your body's master antioxidant, glutathione, which protects your cells from damage. Your results suggest increased oxidative stress, and this formula helps your body defend and repair at the cellular level.", "supportingFindings": ["Insulin resistance (oxidative metabolic stress)", "ALT 22 U/L (hepatic glutathione demand)", "Age 46 (declining glutathione production)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 20, "apoBValue": 57, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "normal", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 0.9865955488900799, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": false}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "none", "indicated": false, "rationale": ""}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "none", "rationale": "", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment (plain language)\\n- Your labs show that you are in perimenopause. Your FSH is 27.1 mIU/mL, which supports the menopausal transition and matches your symptoms of hot flashes and night sweats. Some hormone levels are lower than we’d expect for symptom control: progesterone is 1.18 ng/mL (below the HRT therapeutic range) and total testosterone is 24.8 ng/dL (low despite HRT). Estradiol is 57 pg/mL — in the lower end of the “bone protection” range (40–60 pg/mL). Vitamin D is 37.2 ng/mL (insufficient). Your heart disease risk is low: 10-year ASCVD risk is 1.0% (about 1 in 100 chance of heart attack or stroke over 10 years), and overall 10-year CVD risk is 1.6%.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Hemoglobin 13.6 g/dL and hematocrit 42.2% are normal.\\n- Ferritin 140 ng/mL indicates good iron stores.\\n- TSH 1.15 mIU/L shows normal thyroid function.\\n- LDL 51 mg/dL, Apo B 57 mg/dL, and Lipoprotein(a) 20 mg/dL are all in optimal ranges.\\n- Hemoglobin A1c 5.5% shows normal blood sugar control.\\n\\nAreas to improve (with exact values + concrete actions)\\n- Progesterone 1.18 ng/mL (below HRT therapeutic range): Ask to review your HRT regimen. If you are still having menstrual cycles, we should recheck progesterone in the luteal phase (about 7 days before expected period) to assess ovulation. If anovulatory, discuss changing progestogen dose or schedule with your clinician.\\n- Total testosterone 24.8 ng/dL (low): Prioritize sleep and strength training to support hormones: get 7–8 hours of quality sleep nightly; do resistance/strength training 2–3 times per week; manage stress with regular movement, meditation, or hobbies. Discuss with your provider whether testosterone dose/formulation needs adjustment.\\n- Estradiol 57 pg/mL & FSH 27.1 mIU/mL: Because estradiol is only at minimum bone protection, consider hormone optimization discussion with your clinician to reduce vasomotor symptoms and protect bone.\\n- Vitamin D 25-OH 37.2 ng/mL (insufficient): Start vitamin D3 1,000–2,000 IU daily (I suggest 2,000 IU daily given perimenopausal bone protection needs). Recheck level in 8–12 weeks.\\n- HDL 45 mg/dL (lower than ideal for women): Implement heart-healthy diet and activity:\\n  - Add 25–30 g fiber daily: oatmeal for breakfast, beans for lunch, veggies at dinner.\\n  - Include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n  - Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter and cheese.\\n  - Daily exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking or equivalent.\\n- hs-CRP 1 mg/L (borderline inflammation): Lower inflammation with regular exercise, Mediterranean-style eating (olive oil, vegetables, nuts, fish), maintain healthy weight, and avoid smoking.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Schedule a visit within 4 weeks to review HRT and symptoms. If HRT is changed, recheck progesterone and estradiol 8–12 weeks after adjustment (timed to cycle if applicable).\\n- Start vitamin D 2,000 IU daily now and recheck 25-OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Begin the exercise and diet items above now; reassess lipids and hs-CRP at routine follow-up (typically 6–12 months) unless otherwise advised.\\n- Consider a DEXA bone density check in the next 6–12 months if you are over 50 or have additional risk factors — discuss with your clinician.\\n\\nYou’re doing well in many areas (iron, thyroid, LDL, blood sugar). Small focused changes—adjusting HRT with your provider, improving sleep and resistance exercise, raising vitamin D, and heart-healthy eating—should help your symptoms and long-term bone and heart health. If symptoms worsen or you have new concerns, contact the clinic sooner.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 13.6, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 42.2, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 95.3, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 95.3 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 235, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 57, "status": "borderline", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol at minimum bone protection level (40-60 pg/mL) but below optimal HRT goal.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Consider increasing estrogen dose. Target 60-100 pg/mL for optimized symptom relief.", "referenceRange": "60-100 pg/mL (HRT goal, >40 for bone)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1.18, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone below HRT therapeutic range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase progesterone dose. Target 8-10 ng/mL for optimal endometrial protection.", "referenceRange": "8-10 ng/mL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 27.1, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "FSH level recorded.", "recommendation": "For accurate interpretation, document cycle phase.", "referenceRange": "3-20 mIU/mL (varies by phase)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 24.8, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Low testosterone despite HRT.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase testosterone dose. Target 75-125 ng/dL for optimal results.", "referenceRange": "75-125 ng/dL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.15, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 140, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 37.2, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D insufficient (31-40 ng/mL). Suboptimal for hormone optimization.", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 5,000 + K daily. Target ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 847, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.5, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 51, "status": "normal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Optimal LDL.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 45, "status": "abnormal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Low HDL - increased cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Increase aerobic exercise. Consider niacin if very low.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 113, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 1.5939138359336495, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 1.6% (LOW RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nLOW RISK (<5%): Focus on lifestyle modifications\\n\\nReassess risk in 4-6 years", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 0.9865955488900799, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 1.0%", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications recommended. Statin generally not indicated at current risk level.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 1.4973710901483195, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 1.5%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 1, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (1/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 12.504839579127017, "status": "normal", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 12.5%, ASCVD 7.1%, Heart Failure 12.1%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 19, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 22, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 109, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.66, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 57, "status": "normal", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Apo B at optimal level.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 20, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 346, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 75, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 20, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 1, "status": "borderline", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Borderline hs-CRP - moderate cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Continue monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 1.18, "status": "borderline", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Low Progesterone (Phase Not Documented)", "interpretation": "Progesterone is 1.18 ng/mL. Without documented cycle phase or sufficient estradiol/FSH context to identify a specific pattern, interpretation is limited. If drawn in the luteal phase, this level would indicate anovulation — a perimenopause marker.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Document cycle phase for accurate interpretation. If the patient is mid-cycle or in the luteal phase, this suggests anovulation. Consider symptoms: sleep disruption, anxiety, irregular cycles. If symptomatic, empiric progesterone (100 mg nightly) is reasonable. PATIENT EDUCATION: Knowing where you are in your menstrual cycle helps us fully interpret this result. Please note when your last period started so we can better understand your hormone picture.", "referenceRange": "Varies by cycle phase; <5 ng/mL suggests anovulation if luteal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 57, "status": "borderline", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Vasomotor Symptoms with Fluctuating Estrogen", "interpretation": "Vasomotor symptoms (hot flashes, night sweats) present despite estradiol level of 57 pg/mL. In perimenopause, estrogen can be spiky and fluctuating, causing vasomotor symptoms even when levels appear normal or high at time of draw.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Consider steady delivery: transdermal estradiol patch starting at 0.0375 mg/day (common starting dose for twice-weekly systems). Add micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly if patient has uterus. Goal is symptom control through stable hormone delivery. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your hot flashes and night sweats may be caused by fluctuating hormone levels rather than consistently low estrogen. A steady-release estrogen patch can help stabilize your levels and reduce symptoms.", "referenceRange": "Symptoms indicate hormonal instability despite E2 level"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 27.1, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Perimenopause Transition", "interpretation": "FSH of 27.1 mIU/mL indicates perimenopause (menopausal transition). Symptoms present: hot flashes, night sweats. Elevated FSH reflects decreased ovarian function and reduced estrogen production. This is a normal part of reproductive aging but may benefit from hormone optimization if symptomatic.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: If symptomatic, consider low-dose transdermal estradiol + micronized progesterone (if uterus intact). Perimenopause is characterized by fluctuating hormones. Steady hormone delivery via patch can smooth out peaks and valleys. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your lab results show you are in perimenopause — the transition phase before menopause. Hormone levels can swing widely during this time, causing symptoms. Treatment options are available to help you feel better.", "referenceRange": "FSH <25 mIU/mL premenopausal; ≥25 suggests transition; ≥40 menopausal"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 31.9, "status": "normal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 31.9 nmol/L is within normal range. SHBG regulates bioavailable sex hormones by binding testosterone and estradiol.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 4, "status": "normal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 4 pg/mL is within normal functional range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}], "aiRecommendations": "Clinical context reminder for staff: These recommendations are for clinic staff and require provider review/authorization before medication changes. Confirm current HRT regimen (route, dose, timing) and date of last menstrual period (LMP) / cycle phase prior to implementing adjustments.\\n\\n1) Overall clinical assessment\\n- Labs consistent with perimenopausal transition (FSH 27.1 mIU/mL) with symptomatic vasomotor complaints despite estradiol 57 pg/mL (borderline, at minimum bone protection level).\\n- Progesterone 1.18 ng/mL is below expected luteal/HRT therapeutic range → inadequate endometrial protection if patient has uterus and/or anovulatory cycles if drawn in luteal phase.\\n- Total testosterone 24.8 ng/dL is below the clinic target for symptomatic optimization (target 75–125 ng/dL per protocol).\\n- Vitamin D 37.2 ng/mL is insufficient for optimization (protocol target ≥60 ng/mL).\\n- Cardiovascular markers: HDL low at 45 mg/dL and hs-CRP 1 mg/L (borderline) — lifestyle and risk modification indicated.\\n- No acute safety laboratory alarms (CBC, CMP, liver enzymes, renal function normal).\\n\\n2) Priority actions — CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE\\nCRITICAL (require immediate provider notification)\\n- If patient has a uterus and is on/starting unopposed estrogen or if any change will lead to unopposed estrogen exposure: notify provider to ensure prompt addition/adjustment of progestogen for endometrial protection.\\n- If patient reports abnormal uterine bleeding, chest pain, sudden neurological symptoms, or other acute symptoms — stop HRT pending provider evaluation and urgent workup.\\n\\nURGENT (address within 1–2 weeks; provider sign-off required for medication changes)\\n- Document cycle phase/LMP immediately. If progesterone was drawn in the luteal window and reported level 1.18 ng/mL → consider anovulation/perimenopause and start/adjust progesterone empirically per provider decision.\\n- Review current estrogen/testosterone/progesterone regimen with provider; propose dosing adjustments below. Obtain provider approval prior to implementing.\\n\\nROUTINE (follow schedule below)\\n- Recheck hormone levels after dose changes per timeline section.\\n- Initiate Vitamin D repletion and lifestyle interventions now; recheck Vitamin D at 12 weeks.\\n\\n3) Specific medication / dose recommendations (staff to present to provider; adjust based on current route/dose)\\nNote: These are protocol-based suggested adjustments. Confirm current regimen and allergies; provider must approve.\\n\\nA. Estradiol (target 60–100 pg/mL)\\n- If patient is on transdermal estradiol:\\n  - If on 0.0375 mg/day patch: consider increasing to 0.05 mg/day or 0.075 mg/day (one-step upward change). Provider approval required.\\n  - If on 0.05 mg: consider increasing to 0.075 mg/day.\\n- If patient is on oral estradiol: consider switching to transdermal for steadier levels (if suitable) or increase oral dose per provider preference.\\n- If using pellets: consult provider to consider earlier reinsertion or additional pellet placement.\\n- Recheck serum estradiol 4–6 weeks after dose change (draw timing consistent with steady-state timing for route used).\\n\\nB. Progesterone (target 8–10 ng/mL for endometrial protection)\\n- If not on progesterone and patient has a uterus: start micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly (empiric) OR medroxyprogesterone 10 mg daily x12–14 days/month as alternate—provider choice.\\n- If already on 100 mg nightly and serum progesterone remains low: consider increasing to 200 mg nightly or adjust timing (ensure blood draw is 6–8 hours post-dose for trough/peak interpretation) — provider decision.\\n- If using cyclical progesterone: ensure adequate luteal-phase dosing (e.g., micronized progesterone 100–200 mg nightly x12–14 days/month) per provider preference.\\n- Recheck serum progesterone 4–8 weeks after change; document cycle phase and timing of last dose relative to draw.\\n\\nC. Testosterone (target total 75–125 ng/dL)\\n- Obtain current testosterone route/dose. Suggested stepwise adjustments per common routes:\\n  - Topical testosterone cream/gel: increase dose by ~25–50% (e.g., 0.5 mg → 0.625–0.75 mg/day or higher depending on clinic formulation). Provider must confirm absolute dose.\\n  - IM testosterone cypionate/enantate: consider increasing dose by 10–20 mg per injection or shortening interval (e.g., from q14 days to q10 days) to reduce troughs; provider to authorize specific mg and interval.\\n  - Pellets: consider additional pellet placement earlier than planned; discuss with provider.\\n- Recheck total testosterone (trough level for injectable or consistent timing for topical) 6–8 weeks after adjustment.\\n- Notify provider if patient has acne, virilizing symptoms, or abnormal labs (liver enzymes, lipids) after initiation/increase.\\n\\nD. Vitamin D\\n- Start Vitamin D3 + K2 regimen per protocol: Metagenics D3 5,000 IU daily with K if available (provider review).\\n- Recheck 25-OH Vitamin D in 12 weeks aiming for ≥60 ng/mL.\\n\\nE. Lipids / CVD risk markers\\n- Emphasize nonpharmacologic first-line measures (see lifestyle). Consider referral to primary care or cardiology if persistent low HDL or additional ASCVD risk factors despite lifestyle changes.\\n- Medication consideration (e.g., niacin) only after provider assessment and if lifestyle interventions insufficient.\\n\\n4) Laboratory recheck and follow-up timeline (staff to schedule)\\n- Hormone panel (Estradiol, Progesterone, Total Testosterone): 4–8 weeks after any dose change (estradiol 4–6 weeks; testosterone 6–8 weeks; progesterone 4–8 weeks depending on timing).\\n- Vitamin D (25-OH): 12 weeks after starting supplementation.\\n- Lipid panel and hs-CRP: re-evaluate in 3 months after lifestyle intervention.\\n- If initiating/changing HRT, schedule follow-up visit/call within 4 weeks to assess symptom response and adverse effects; provider to review labs when available.\\n\\n5) Patient education points for staff to cover (document in chart)\\n- Ask and document date of LMP / cycle day; explain why cycle timing matters for interpreting progesterone and estradiol.\\n- Explain rationale for steady-delivery estradiol (patch) if proposed: reduces fluctuation-induced vasomotor symptoms.\\n- Explain need for progesterone if uterus intact to protect endometrium; discuss schedule (nightly vs. cyclical) and side effects (drowsiness with micronized progesterone).\\n- Discuss testosterone goals and symptoms targeted (libido, energy, muscle mass) and potential masculinizing side effects; instruct on application and avoidance of skin transfer if topical.\\n- Counsel on Vitamin D supplementation, lifestyle measures for HDL improvement and CVD risk reduction, and importance of lab follow-up.\\n\\n6) Lifestyle interventions (staff to document counseling and provide resources)\\n- Aerobic exercise: at least 150 minutes/week moderate intensity; add resistance training 2×/week to improve HDL and metabolic health.\\n- Diet: emphasize Mediterranean-style diet with emphasis on monounsaturated fats, fatty fish (omega-3), fiber; reduce refined carbohydrates and trans fats.\\n- Weight management: targeted BMI reduction if overweight; smoking cessation (if applicable).\\n- Sleep hygiene and stress reduction (CBTi, mindfulness) for vasomotor and perimenopausal symptoms.\\n- Limit alcohol (can lower HDL and aggravate vasomotor symptoms).\\n\\n7) Additional testing or referrals to consider\\n- If not done within last 2–5 years and perimenopausal with borderline estrogen: consider baseline DEXA (BMD) if risk factors for osteoporosis or symptoms of bone loss.\\n- If abnormal uterine bleeding or persistent bleeding after starting/modifying HRT: transvaginal ultrasound and/or endometrial sampling per provider decision.\\n- Cardiology referral if multiple ASCVD risk factors or persistent low HDL and elevated hs-CRP despite lifestyle modifications.\\n- Endocrinology or reproductive endocrinology referral for complex androgen or hormone optimization cases where achieving target testosterone/progesterone levels is difficult.\\n- Pregnancy test if still menstruating and contraception status unclear prior to initiating estrogen/testosterone therapy.\\n\\n8) Summary of next steps and timeline (to place on chart and communicate to provider)\\n- Immediately: Document LMP/cycle day and confirm current HRT regimen (route/dose/timing). Notify provider if uterus present and progesterone absent or inadequate.\\n- Within 1 week: Present proposed stepwise medication adjustments to provider for approval:\\n  - Estradiol: increase transdermal dose one step (e.g., 0.0375 → 0.05–0.075 mg/day) OR switch route if indicated.\\n  - Progesterone: start micronized progesterone 100 mg nightly if not on progestogen and uterus intact; if already on 100 mg nightly and still low, propose increase to 200 mg nightly pending provider approval.\\n  - Testosterone: increase by 25–50% (topical) or +10–20 mg per IM injection / shorten injection interval — provider to confirm exact dosing based on current regimen.\\n  - Vitamin D: start D3 5,000 IU daily with K; recheck at 12 weeks.\\n- 4–8 weeks after any HRT adjustment: recheck estradiol, progesterone, total testosterone (timing standardized relative to dose/route), assess symptoms and side effects; provider to review and sign off on next steps.\\n- 3 months: reassess lipids/hs-CRP and lifestyle adherence; consider further workup or referral if metrics not improving.\\n- Document all patient education provided and obtain provider authorization for medication changes before dispensing/initiating.\\n\\nPhysician notification summary (place urgent flags in chart)\\n- Notify physician now to authorize:\\n  - Any progesterone initiation or escalation if uterus present.\\n  - Proposed estradiol dose increase or route change.\\n  - Proposed testosterone dose increase and specific dosing plan.\\n  - If abnormal uterine bleeding or new/worrisome symptoms occur.\\n\\nProvide these recommendations and the patient’s current HRT regimen to the provider for authorization and then implement per the above timelines.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 2.51, "detail": "Triglycerides 113 / HDL 45 = 2.51", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5.5%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 31.9, "detail": "Low - hyperinsulinemia suppresses SHBG", "positive": true, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 22, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "57 mg/dL", "detail": "Normal atherogenic particle count", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "1 mg/L", "detail": "Low inflammatory burden", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 1, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (1/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Metabolic pattern consistent with insulin resistance and/or visceral adiposity. This drives inflammation, hormonal imbalance, and cardiovascular risk.", "supportingFindings": ["HDL 45 mg/dL (low)", "SHBG 31.9 nmol/L (low, associated with insulin resistance)", "Unexplained weight gain / central adiposity reported"]}, {"name": "Estrogen Dominance / Impaired Clearance", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Lab pattern consistent with relative estrogen excess or impaired estrogen clearance. Even without reported symptoms, supporting liver detoxification and estrogen metabolism pathways may be beneficial.", "supportingFindings": ["Progesterone 1.18 ng/mL (very low)", "Borderline liver markers (ALT 22, AST 19) suggesting possible impaired hepatic clearance"]}, {"name": "Menopausal Transition", "confidence": "low", "description": "Clinical picture consistent with perimenopause or menopause. Hormonal shifts during this transition affect bone health, cardiovascular risk, mood, and quality of life.", "supportingFindings": ["Age 46 (perimenopause window)", "FSH 27.1 mIU/mL (elevated, menopausal range)", "Hot flashes reported", "Night sweats reported"]}]}	2026-04-02 13:32:49.252021	1
104	Stephanie Sinclair	female	2025-07-24 00:00:00	{"a1c": 5.4, "alt": 28, "ast": 34, "bun": 14, "co2": 22, "fsh": 41.6, "hdl": 86, "ldl": 159, "mcv": 98.5, "rbc": 4.58, "tsh": 0.98, "wbc": 9.7, "acne": false, "egfr": 78, "onHRT": false, "sodium": 138, "albumin": 4.3, "anxiety": false, "calcium": 9.6, "glucose": 96, "bloating": false, "brainFog": false, "chloride": 102, "hairLoss": false, "vitaminD": 52.9, "bilirubin": 0.5, "estradiol": 56, "headaches": false, "lowEnergy": true, "lowLibido": true, "platelets": 320, "potassium": 4.6, "creatinine": 0.86, "hematocrit": 45.1, "hemoglobin": 14.2, "hotFlashes": false, "jointAches": false, "vitaminB12": 276, "weightGain": true, "heavyMenses": false, "labDrawDate": "2025-07-24", "moodChanges": false, "nightSweats": false, "patientName": "Stephanie Sinclair", "pmsSymptoms": false, "demographics": {"age": 58, "bmi": 30.8, "race": "white", "smoker": true, "snoring": false, "diabetic": false, "onBPMeds": true, "bmiOver35": false, "onStatins": false, "tiredness": false, "systolicBP": 137, "familyHistory": false, "observedApnea": false, "neckCircOver40cm": false}, "frequentUTIs": false, "irritability": false, "progesterone": 0.26, "restlessLegs": false, "testosterone": 29.2, "totalProtein": 7, "lowMotivation": false, "triglycerides": 123, "menstrualPhase": "postmenopausal", "onBirthControl": false, "vaginalDryness": false, "sleepDisruption": false, "totalCholesterol": 270}	{"redFlags": [], "soapNote": "SUBJECTIVE:\\n- Chief complaint: Lab review / follow-up / hormone management.\\n- History of present illness: Patient is a 58-year-old woman presenting for review of recent labs obtained for routine hormone management and cardiovascular risk assessment. She reports no current vasomotor symptoms (hot flashes, night sweats), no new fatigue beyond baseline, no syncope, no chest pain, no dyspnea, and no focal neurologic complaints. No history provided of use of hormone replacement therapy. No report of alcohol excess or recent illness. No new medications reported.\\n- Gynecologic/menopausal history: Laboratory pattern consistent with menopause; patient is postmenopausal by history/age. No vaginal bleeding reported.\\n\\nOBJECTIVE:\\n- Vital signs: Not provided in lab data. (Recommend BP and BMI documented at next visit.)\\n- Labs (grouped by category). Values listed with units; flagged as ABNORMAL, BORDERLINE, or NORMAL per provided interpretation.\\n\\nCBC:\\n- Hemoglobin: 14.2 g/dL [NORMAL]\\n- Hematocrit: 45.1 % [BORDERLINE] — mildly high; consider dehydration or relative erythrocytosis\\n- MCV: 98.5 fL [BORDERLINE] — high-normal; early macrocytosis\\n- Platelets: 320 K/uL [NORMAL]\\n\\nMetabolic / Renal / Liver:\\n- Creatinine: 0.86 mg/dL [NORMAL]\\n- eGFR: 78 mL/min/1.73 m2 [NORMAL]\\n- AST (SGOT): 34 U/L [BORDERLINE] — mild elevation\\n- ALT (SGPT): 28 U/L [NORMAL]\\n- FIB-4 Score (Liver Fibrosis): 1.16 [NORMAL]\\n\\nGlycemic:\\n- Hemoglobin A1c: 5.4 % [NORMAL]\\n\\nLipids:\\n- LDL cholesterol: 159 mg/dL [BORDERLINE] — borderline high\\n- HDL cholesterol: 86 mg/dL [NORMAL, protective]\\n- Triglycerides: 123 mg/dL [NORMAL]\\n\\nHormones:\\n- FSH: 41.6 mIU/mL [ABNORMAL] — consistent with menopause\\n- Estradiol (E2): 56 pg/mL [BORDERLINE for postmenopausal without HRT]\\n- Progesterone (P4): 0.26 ng/mL [NORMAL for postmenopausal]\\n- Testosterone, total: 29.2 ng/dL [NORMAL/adequate androgens pattern]\\n\\nVitamins / Micronutrients:\\n- Vitamin B12: 276 pg/mL [BORDERLINE/low-normal]\\n- Vitamin D (25-OH): 52.9 ng/mL [BORDERLINE — adequate but suboptimal (41–59 ng/mL)]\\n\\nRisk scores / Other:\\n- 10-year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT): 7.1% [BORDERLINE]\\n- 10-year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT): 4.3% [BORDERLINE/low]\\n- 30-year CVD Risk (PREVENT): 28.2% (30-yr ASCVD 16.7%, HF 23.4%) [BORDERLINE]\\n- 10-year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT): 4.9% [NORMAL]\\n- Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG): 2 points [LOW RISK]\\n\\nASSESSMENT:\\n1. Menopausal status — laboratory confirmation (FSH 41.6 mIU/mL). E2 56 pg/mL is borderline for a postmenopausal woman in the absence of exogenous estrogen. Patient reports no bothersome vasomotor or genitourinary symptoms at this time. Consider hormone therapy only if symptomatic after shared decision-making.\\n   - Clinical significance: Elevated FSH consistent with decreased ovarian function and reduced estrogen production. No acute red flags.\\n\\n2. Borderline-elevated LDL cholesterol, hyperlipidemia — LDL 159 mg/dL with 10-year ASCVD risk 4.3% and 10-year total CVD risk 7.1%.\\n   - Clinical significance: LDL is above goal for primary prevention in most guidelines. Absolute 10-year ASCVD risk is <5% but total CVD risk is borderline. Presence of risk-enhancing factors (age, LDL level, family history if present) may shift management toward pharmacotherapy.\\n   - Differential/considerations: Evaluate for additional risk enhancers (family history premature ASCVD, chronic inflammatory disease, CAC score) prior to statin initiation.\\n\\n3. Borderline vitamin B12 (276 pg/mL) and borderline macrocytosis (MCV 98.5 fL).\\n   - Clinical significance: B12 in low-normal range and rising MCV raise concern for early B12 deficiency or folate deficiency. Could explain mild fatigue if present.\\n   - Differential: Nutritional deficiency, pernicious anemia, medication effects (metformin, PPI), alcohol, hypothyroidism. Peripheral smear/MMA helpful.\\n\\n4. Borderline elevated hematocrit 45.1%.\\n   - Clinical significance: Mildly high; could reflect hemoconcentration/dehydration or early erythrocytosis. Correlate with symptoms, smoking status, and repeat labs when euvolemic.\\n\\n5. Borderline AST 34 U/L with normal ALT (28 U/L) and FIB-4 1.16.\\n   - Clinical significance: Mild AST elevation only; likely not clinically significant but monitor. Evaluate for alcohol use, medications, metabolic fatty liver disease given LDL/triglycerides.\\n\\n6. Vitamin D adequate but suboptimal (25-OH 52.9 ng/mL).\\n   - Clinical significance: Level is acceptable; repletion may not be necessary if not deficient; aim for 30–60 ng/mL depending on practice; continue maintenance if on supplementation.\\n\\n7. Androgen status — Total testosterone 29.2 ng/dL (adequate); symptoms if present are unlikely androgen-driven.\\n   - Clinical significance: If patient reports low libido, fatigue, or mood changes, evaluate other etiologies (sleep, thyroid, mood disorders, menopausal estrogen/progesterone balance).\\n\\n8. Preventive health: Age-appropriate bone health concerns given postmenopausal status.\\n   - Clinical significance: Consider DEXA scan if not up to date; fracture risk assessment (FRAX) as indicated.\\n\\nPLAN:\\n1. Menopause / Hormone management (FSH 41.6 mIU/mL; E2 56 pg/mL).\\n   - Discussed that labs are consistent with menopause. Because patient reports no bothersome vasomotor or genitourinary symptoms, no hormonal therapy is recommended at this time.\\n   - If symptoms develop or patient desires symptom management, discuss options including systemic estrogen ± progesterone (if uterus present) vs non-hormonal therapies; proceed with shared decision-making and appropriate counseling on risks/benefits.\\n\\n2. Borderline hyperlipidemia — LDL 159 mg/dL.\\n   - Lifestyle interventions: dietary counseling (Mediterranean or plant-forward diet, reduce saturated fat and trans fats), increase soluble fiber, limit simple carbohydrates, weight management if applicable, regular aerobic exercise ≥150 min/week, smoking cessation if applicable.\\n   - Shared decision-making regarding statin therapy: given 10-year ASCVD risk 4.3% (<5%) but total CVD risk 7.1% and LDL 159 mg/dL, discuss potential benefit of statin if additional risk enhancers present (family history of premature ASCVD, CAC score, metabolic syndrome, inflammatory conditions). Recommend consideration of coronary artery calcium (CAC) scoring to refine risk if patient and clinician desire clarification.\\n   - If patient elects pharmacotherapy after discussion, consider initiating a moderate-intensity statin (example: atorvastatin 10–20 mg nightly OR rosuvastatin 5–10 mg nightly) with plan to recheck lipids 6–12 weeks after initiation and adjust dose to achieve LDL reduction goals. Do shared decision-making prior to initiation.\\n   - If no statin started now: recheck fasting lipid panel in 6–12 months.\\n\\n3. Borderline vitamin B12 and MCV (B12 276 pg/mL; MCV 98.5 fL).\\n   - Start high-priority supplement: Intrinsi B12-Folate™ 1 tablet daily (as recommended).\\n   - Order additional testing: methylmalonic acid (MMA) and serum folate; consider anti-intrinsic factor/parietal cell antibodies if concern for pernicious anemia; peripheral smear if indicated.\\n   - Repeat CBC with MCV and serum B12 in 3 months to document response.\\n   - If patient is taking medications that lower B12 (metformin, PPI), consider review and mitigation.\\n\\n4. Hematocrit mild elevation (Hct 45.1%).\\n   - Recommend repeat CBC when euvolemic to assess persistence (repeat with morning labs). If persistent elevation or symptoms (headache, erythrocytosis), evaluate for secondary causes including smoking, sleep apnea, EPO-secreting lesions, and consider arterial blood gas if indicated.\\n   - Encourage adequate hydration.\\n\\n5. Liver enzymes and fatty liver risk (AST 34 U/L; ALT 28 U/L; FIB-4 1.16).\\n   - No acute action required; borderline AST only. Counsel on alcohol moderation and weight optimization.\\n   - If metabolic risk factors or persistent abnormal LFTs, consider hepatic ultrasound and/or referral for NAFLD evaluation.\\n   - Repeat basic metabolic panel and liver panel in 3–6 months or earlier if clinically indicated.\\n\\n6. Vitamin D (25-OH 52.9 ng/mL).\\n   - Level is in adequate but suboptimal range. If patient not on vitamin D supplementation, may consider maintenance dosing (e.g., vitamin D3 1000–2000 IU daily) depending on dietary intake and fracture risk. If already taking supplementation, continue and recheck in 6–12 months.\\n   - Recommend DEXA bone density scan if not performed within recommended interval for age/postmenopausal status.\\n\\n7. Androgen status and symptoms.\\n   - Testosterone total is within expected range (29.2 ng/dL). If patient reports fatigue, low mood, sleep disruption, or low libido in the future, evaluate for non-androgen causes (sleep disorders, depression, thyroid dysfunction, medication side effects). Androgen therapy is not indicated based on current labs.\\n\\n8. Preventive and lifestyle recommendations.\\n   - Encourage exercise, heart-healthy diet, weight management, smoking cessation if applicable, and moderation of alcohol.\\n   - Recommend routine age-appropriate preventive care: DEXA screening if not up to date, colorectal cancer screening per guidelines, review immunizations (influenza, pneumococcal if indicated, Tdap), and blood pressure monitoring.\\n\\n9. Supplements (per clinic recommendations).\\n   - High-priority: Intrinsi B12-Folate™ 1 tablet daily (start now).\\n   - Medium-priority (optional): AdvaClear® 2 capsules twice daily; UltraFlora® Healthy Weight with Akkermansia 1 capsule daily; GlutaClear® 2 capsules daily; NutraGems® CoQ10 300 chewable gel 1 daily; OmegaGenics® Fish Oil Neuro 1000 1 softgel 1–2 times daily. Discuss potential benefits/risks and medication interactions prior to initiating.\\n\\n10. Follow-up labs and timing.\\n    - Repeat CBC with differential and MCV, serum B12 (and MMA if ordered), and folate in 3 months after initiating B12/folate supplementation.\\n    - Repeat fasting lipid panel in 6–12 weeks if statin started; otherwise repeat fasting lipid panel in 6–12 months.\\n    - Repeat LFTs and basic metabolic panel in 3–6 months.\\n    - Repeat 25-OH vitamin D in 6–12 months if supplementation initiated or dose changed.\\n    - Consider ordering CAC scoring if uncertainty regarding statin initiation; refer patient for imaging if desired.\\n    - Schedule routine clinical follow-up in 3–6 months for ongoing hormone management and risk review or sooner if new symptoms arise.\\n\\n11. Red flags: None identified that require urgent evaluation. No critical lab values at this time.\\n\\nPatient communication:\\n- Results reviewed and discussed with patient today, including menopausal interpretation, lipid risk and options, B12 implications and supplementation, and plans for follow-up testing and lifestyle measures. Questions were answered and patient verbalized understanding.\\n\\nFollow-up:\\n- Return visit or telehealth follow-up in 3–6 months or sooner if symptoms develop or if initiating a statin (follow-up per lipid-check schedule above).\\n\\n04/02/2026\\nResults reviewed and discussed with patient. Questions answered. Patient verbalized understanding. Follow-up as above.", "cvRiskFlags": {"CKD": false, "high_TG": false, "low_HDL": false, "diabetes": false, "high_ApoB": false, "high_Lp_a": false, "hsCRP_high": false, "high_nonHDL": true, "prediabetes": false, "very_high_TG": false, "family_history": false, "very_high_ApoB": false, "very_high_Lp_a": false, "very_high_nonHDL": true}, "preventRisk": {"ldlGoal": "No specific LDL target; focus on risk-based statin intensity", "riskCategory": "borderline", "tenYearASCVD": 0.04334949694469105, "calculatorUsed": "PREVENT", "recommendations": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nBORDERLINE RISK (5-7.5%): Consider coronary artery calcium (CAC) scoring\\n\\nStatin therapy based on shared decision-making if risk-enhancing factors present", "tenYearTotalCVD": 0.0708675620577023, "thirtyYearASCVD": 0.1669985407990691, "ageValidFor30Year": true, "thirtyYearTotalCVD": 0.28232419115579843, "tenYearHFPercentage": "4.9%", "tenYearHeartFailure": 0.04871227736411019, "statinRecommendation": "Consider statin if risk-enhancing factors present. CAC scoring may help guide decision.", "tenYearCVDPercentage": "7.1%", "tenYearASCVDPercentage": "4.3%", "thirtyYearHFPercentage": "23.4%", 