tablet daily with meal", "name": "D3 2000 Complex", "caution": "Maintenance dose for suboptimal levels. Comprehensive vitamin D support with cofactors.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "low", "rationale": "D3 2000 Complex provides maintenance vitamin D with cofactors to reach and maintain optimal range ≥60 ng/mL.", "indication": "Vitamin D 52.9 ng/mL (suboptimal, target ≥60)", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D is close to optimal but could benefit from a maintenance dose to reach the ideal range for bone and immune health.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 52.9 ng/mL (suboptimal, target ≥60)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 6, "apoBValue": 94, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "borderline", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 3.883700304601588, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": true}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "generally_recommended", "indicated": true, "rationale": "ApoB ≥90 mg/dL (94 mg/dL) with risk enhancers (elevated ApoB). Statin discussion recommended."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": false, "severity": "normal", "recommendation": "Triglycerides within normal range."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "consider", "rationale": "Lipid levels meet threshold for CAC consideration. CAC may be useful if risk level is uncertain or patient is hesitant about statin.", "recommended": false}}, "recheckWindow": "3-6 months (routine follow-up)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\nYour results show a menopausal hormone pattern with signs of a recent “crash” phase: FSH 67.7 mIU/mL (consistent with menopause) and estradiol 40 pg/mL (low for premenopause and somewhat higher than expected for untreated postmenopause). SHBG is high at 128 nmol/L, which lowers the amount of free sex hormones available. Cardiovascular risk is borderline: 10-year total CVD risk 6.7% and 10-year ASCVD (heart attack/stroke) risk 3.9%. Your LDL cholesterol is 145 mg/dL and ApoB is 94 mg/dL. Vitamin D is 52.9 ng/mL (adequate but suboptimal).\\n\\nPositive findings (what’s good right now)\\n- Hemoglobin 13.9 g/dL, hematocrit 41.7%, ferritin 74 ng/mL — iron stores and blood counts are healthy.\\n- TSH 0.98 mIU/L — thyroid function is normal.\\n- HDL 80 mg/dL and triglycerides 85 mg/dL — protective cholesterol pattern.\\n- eGFR 100 mL/min, AST 25 U/L, ALT 19 U/L — kidneys and liver OK.\\n- Hemoglobin A1c 5.4% — blood sugar in normal range.\\n- Lipoprotein(a) 6 mg/dL — low and favorable.\\n\\nAreas to improve + concrete actions\\n1) LDL 145 mg/dL and ApoB 94 mg/dL (borderline): \\n- Add 25–30 g fiber/day: oatmeal for breakfast, beans at lunch, vegetables at dinner.\\n- Include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n- Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter and cheese.\\n- Daily exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking (or equivalent).\\n2) Cardiovascular risk (10-year total CVD 6.7% — BORDERLINE): \\n- Aim for 150 minutes moderate aerobic activity weekly (brisk walking, cycling, swimming).\\n- Follow a Mediterranean-style diet: vegetables, whole grains, olive oil, nuts, fish; limit processed foods and added sugars.\\n- Recheck fasting lipid panel in 3 months; consider coronary artery calcium (CAC) scoring to guide statin decision — discuss with your clinician.\\n3) Hormones and bone health:\\n- FSH 67.7 mIU/mL and estradiol 40 pg/mL suggest menopause with hypoestrogenic symptoms possible. If you have hot flashes, vaginal dryness, low mood or sleep problems, discuss hormone therapy options (risks/benefits) with your provider.\\n- SHBG 128 nmol/L — please tell us if you take oral estrogen, oral contraceptives, thyroid medication, or have liver disease.\\n- Vitamin D 52.9 ng/mL: continue supplementation (typical 1,000–2,000 IU vitamin D daily) and recheck in 6–12 months. Ensure dietary calcium ~1,000–1,200 mg/day and do weight-bearing exercise; consider DEXA scan within 6–12 months given menopausal status.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Schedule a visit to discuss menopausal symptoms, HRT options, and whether to pursue CAC or statin therapy.\\n- Lifestyle changes now (fiber, fish, exercise) and repeat fasting lipids in 3 months.\\n- Repeat vitamin D in 6–12 months; consider DEXA in 6–12 months.\\nIf you have symptoms (hot flashes, heavy bleeding, new fatigue, liver symptoms) or questions about medications that affect SHBG, contact us sooner. You’re doing well in many areas — these targeted steps can lower your cardiovascular risk and support bone and hormonal health.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 13.9, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 41.7, "status": "normal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Hematocrit within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 95.2, "status": "normal", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 95.2 fL is within normal range (80–100 fL), indicating normal-sized red blood cells.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 277, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 40, "status": "borderline", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol elevated for postmenopausal status without HRT.", "recommendation": "Evaluate for exogenous estrogen source or ovarian pathology. Consider HRT if symptomatic.", "referenceRange": "<20 pg/mL (postmenopausal without HRT)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.39, "status": "normal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone appropriate for postmenopausal status.", "recommendation": "Expected finding.", "referenceRange": "<1 ng/mL (postmenopausal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 67.7, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "Elevated FSH consistent with menopause.", "recommendation": "Expected finding for postmenopausal women.", "referenceRange": ">25 mIU/mL (postmenopausal)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 28.7, "status": "normal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone within normal female range.", "recommendation": "No intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "15-70 ng/dL"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 0.98, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 74, "status": "normal", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Ferritin within optimal range (>50).", "recommendation": "Adequate iron stores. No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 52.9, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D adequate but suboptimal (41-59 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 2000 Complex daily to reach optimal range ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 856, "status": "normal", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "B12 within normal range.", "recommendation": "No supplementation needed.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.4, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 145, "status": "borderline", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Borderline high LDL.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider statin if additional risk factors.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 80, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 85, "status": "normal", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Normal triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Maintain healthy diet.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 6.733174146948976, "status": "borderline", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 6.7% (BORDERLINE RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nBORDERLINE RISK (5-7.5%): Consider coronary artery calcium (CAC) scoring\\n\\nStatin therapy based on shared decision-making if risk-enhancing factors present", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 3.883700304601588, "status": "borderline", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 3.9%", "recommendation": "Consider statin if risk-enhancing factors present. CAC scoring may help guide decision.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 4.093634564874444, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 4.1%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 2, "status": "normal", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "LOW RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (2/8 points)", "recommendation": "Low probability of moderate-severe OSA. Continue routine monitoring. Consider sleep study if persistent symptoms of excessive daytime sleepiness, loud snoring, or cardiovascular risk factors.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 26.8117257892958, "status": "borderline", "category": "30-Year CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "30-year risks: CVD 26.8%, ASCVD 15.0%, Heart Failure 19.5%", "recommendation": "Long-term lifestyle modifications and risk factor management are critical for reducing lifetime cardiovascular risk.", "referenceRange": "Long-term cardiovascular risk"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 25, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 19, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 100, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.69, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 94, "status": "borderline", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Borderline elevated Apo B.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider pharmacotherapy if high ASCVD risk.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 6, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 293, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 114, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 40, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Hypoestrogenic Pattern", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 40 pg/mL with FSH of 67.7 mIU/mL indicates hypoestrogenic perimenopause — the \\"crash phase.\\" Estrogen production has dropped significantly and the brain is signaling strongly (elevated FSH) trying to stimulate the ovaries.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Transdermal estradiol 0.025–0.05 mg is appropriate replacement therapy. Add micronized progesterone for uterine protection and sleep support. Frame this as hormone replacement, not stimulation — we are restoring what the body needs. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen has dropped to a level that can cause symptoms like hot flashes, night sweats, brain fog, and mood changes. Replacing estrogen with a patch or gel can relieve these symptoms and protect your long-term health. Progesterone is added to protect your uterus and help with sleep.", "referenceRange": "E2 <50 with FSH >10 indicates estrogen deficiency"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 67.7, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Menopause", "interpretation": "FSH of 67.7 mIU/mL is consistent with menopause. No specific symptoms reported at this time. Elevated FSH reflects decreased ovarian function and reduced estrogen production. This is a normal part of reproductive aging but may benefit from hormone optimization if symptomatic.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: If symptomatic, consider hormone therapy (HT): transdermal estradiol + micronized progesterone (if uterus intact). Discuss benefits/risks of HT including cardiovascular, bone, and quality of life considerations. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your lab results confirm you are in menopause. Hormone therapy can help manage symptoms like hot flashes, sleep problems, and vaginal dryness while also protecting bone health.", "referenceRange": "FSH <25 mIU/mL premenopausal; ≥25 suggests transition; ≥40 menopausal"}, {"unit": "nmol/L", "value": 128, "status": "abnormal", "category": "SHBG", "interpretation": "SHBG 128 nmol/L is elevated. High SHBG reduces bioavailable testosterone and estrogen. Common causes: oral estrogen, oral contraceptives, thyroid medication, liver conditions.", "recommendation": "Evaluate SHBG drivers (oral estrogen, OCP, thyroid medication). Consider ordering Bioavailable Testosterone for complete androgen assessment.", "referenceRange": "24-122 nmol/L"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 2, "status": "normal", "category": "Free Testosterone", "interpretation": "Free testosterone 2 pg/mL is within normal functional range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "0.5-5.0 pg/mL"}], "aiRecommendations": "FOR CLINIC STAFF ONLY — Clinical synthesis and action plan\\n\\n1) Overall clinical assessment (concise)\\n- Labs consistent with menopause/perimenopause: FSH 67.7 mIU/mL (menopausal range) with an estradiol of 40 pg/mL — hypoestrogenic “crash” pattern. Patient likely menopausal and may benefit from hormone therapy if symptomatic.\\n- Estradiol 40 pg/mL is borderline-high for a postmenopausal patient who is not on HRT → must exclude exogenous estrogen exposure and consider ovarian source if medication/supplement review is negative.\\n- SHBG markedly elevated (128 nmol/L) — will reduce bioavailable sex steroids; common causes include oral estrogen, OCPs, thyroid meds, hepatic dysfunction. Liver enzymes are normal (AST/ALT normal), but medication review is essential.\\n- Lipid risk: LDL 145 mg/dL, ApoB 94 mg/dL, 10-yr CVD risk 6.7% (borderline). Lifestyle management recommended; consider CAC and statin shared decision-making if risk-enhancers present.\\n- Vitamin D 25-OH 52.9 ng/mL — adequate but suboptimal for clinic protocol; supplementation recommended to target ≥60 ng/mL.\\n\\n2) Priority actions — CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE\\n\\nCRITICAL (do now / immediate provider notification)\\n- Confirm current medication and supplement list immediately for any exogenous estrogen sources (oral estrogen, combined OCPs, topical/creams, compounded products, phytoestrogen supplements). Staff: call patient or review EHR med list; report findings to provider today.\\n- If patient is not taking exogenous estrogen and has unexplained vaginal bleeding, pelvic pain, or clinical signs concerning for ovarian/uterine pathology → urgent gynaecology referral and pelvic/transvaginal ultrasound; notify provider immediately.\\n- Notify provider prior to any initiation of HRT (transdermal estradiol ± progesterone) or if planning to start statin.\\n\\nURGENT (within 1–2 weeks)\\n- Order Bioavailable Testosterone (or calculated bioavailable testosterone) and re-check SHBG drivers (confirm current thyroid medication dosing, liver function recently normal but review for hepatic disease). Send morning, fasting sample if lab requires.\\n- If patient is symptomatic and provider elects to treat hypoestrogenic symptoms, prepare for initiation of transdermal estradiol 25–50 mcg/day (see dosing below) and micronized progesterone for uterine protection — obtain consent and document counseling on risks/benefits. Provider review required before prescribing.\\n- For cardiovascular risk borderline (10-yr 6.7%), discuss CAC scoring as next step in shared decision-making; arrange referral/order if provider and patient agree.\\n\\nROUTINE (within 4–12 weeks)\\n- Start Metagenics D3 2000 IU daily; recheck 25-OH vitamin D in 8–12 weeks.\\n- Lifestyle counseling (diet, exercise, smoking cessation) and schedule follow-up lipid panel in 3 months after lifestyle interventions or 6–12 weeks after any statin start.\\n- If starting HRT, schedule follow-up visit at 6–12 weeks post-initiation to assess symptoms, side effects, and consider labs per protocol.\\n\\n3) Testosterone (androgen) recommendations (if applicable)\\n- Current Total and Free testosterone listed as normal; SHBG high may reduce bioavailable testosterone — do NOT start testosterone replacement until bioavailable testosterone is measured and symptoms correlate.\\n- If bioavailable testosterone is low and patient has persistent clinical symptoms (low libido, low energy, reduced well-being) after addressing estrogen deficiency:\\n  - Consider a therapeutic trial of transdermal testosterone. Suggested starting dose (clinic protocol): 300 mcg (0.3 mg) daily transdermal gel. Alternative starting ranges 300–600 mcg/day depending on symptom severity and monitoring capability.\\n  - Monitoring: total testosterone at baseline and 3 months (draw morning sample), evaluate for virilizing effects (acne, hirsutism, voice change), monitor lipids and LFTs. Discontinue if adverse effects.\\n- Physician must approve testosterone therapy; document informed consent and follow-up plan.\\n\\n4) Lifestyle interventions to implement now (staff to counsel and document)\\n- Diet: Mediterranean-style diet, reduce saturated fat, emphasize vegetables, whole grains, legumes, fish, olive oil. Specific target: reduce LDL through diet and weight optimization.\\n- Exercise: ≥150 minutes/week moderate aerobic activity + twice-weekly resistance training.\\n- Weight: support weight loss if BMI >25 (5–10% weight loss improves lipids).\\n- Smoking cessation counseling and referral if positive.\\n- Sleep hygiene and alcohol moderation.\\n- Bone health: encourage calcium intake (dietary) and weight-bearing exercise; consider DEXA per age/risk factors (see additional testing).\\n- Vitamin D: instruct take Metagenics D3 2000 IU daily with food; document adherence plan.\\n\\n5) Additional testing and referrals to consider (staff order items per provider)\\n\\nImmediate/near-term\\n- Medication/supplement review documented and reported to provider (critical).\\n- Bioavailable testosterone (and repeating SHBG if indicated) — morning draw.\\n- If unexplained elevated estradiol and no exogenous source: transvaginal pelvic ultrasound (evaluate ovaries/uterus) and consider gynecology consult.\\n- If symptomatic and planning HRT: ensure up-to-date mammogram per age/guidelines before initiation if due.\\n- CBC and ferritin already normal; order DEXA if age ≥65 or <65 with risk factors for osteoporosis (fragility fracture, long-term glucocorticoids, smoking, low body weight).\\n- Consider Coronary Artery Calcium (CAC) score if shared decision-making for statin initiation given 10-yr CVD risk 6.7% (borderline).\\n\\nRoutine monitoring\\n- Fasting lipid panel (and ApoB) in 3 months after lifestyle interventions or 6–12 weeks after statin initiation.\\n- Vitamin D recheck 8–12 weeks after starting D3 2000 IU.\\n- If HRT started: symptom review and safety check at 6–12 weeks; estradiol level not routinely required but can be rechecked at provider discretion if clinical concern. Re-check FSH not necessary for routine follow-up.\\n- If testosterone trial started: total testosterone and safety labs at baseline and 3 months, then as clinically indicated.\\n\\n6) Physician notification requirements\\n- Notify provider immediately if:\\n  - Patient denies exogenous estrogen but estradiol elevation unexplained.\\n  - Patient reports vaginal bleeding, pelvic pain, or other concerning symptoms.\\n  - Staff proposes initiation of HRT (transdermal estradiol ± progesterone) or testosterone — provider sign-off required.\\n  - Considering starting statin therapy (provider decision) or ordering CAC.\\n- Provide provider with medication/supplement list and bioavailable testosterone/SHBG results when available.\\n\\n7) Summary of next steps and timeline (clear, actionable)\\n- Today (same day):\\n  - Review and document full medication/supplement list; report to provider.\\n  - Confirm presence/absence of uterine status (intact vs hysterectomy) in chart.\\n- Within 1 week:\\n  - Order morning Bioavailable Testosterone and re-check SHBG drivers; order pelvic ultrasound only if provider directs or if symptoms present.\\n  - Start Metagenics D3 2000 IU daily (if not contraindicated); counsel patient on adherence.\\n  - Begin lifestyle counseling and provide written resources/referrals.\\n- 1–2 weeks:\\n  - Provider decision point: initiate HRT (if symptomatic) — transdermal estradiol 25–50 mcg/day + micronized progesterone 100–200 mg nightly (if uterus intact). Document counseling and contraindications. If patient has hysterectomy, progesterone not required.\\n  - If starting testosterone trial (only after bioavailable testosterone low and provider approves): initiate transdermal testosterone 300 mcg/day.\\n- 6–12 weeks:\\n  - If HRT started: clinic follow-up to assess symptom response and adverse effects; consider estradiol level if clinically indicated.\\n  - If testosterone started: measure total testosterone, review for adverse effects, check lipids/LFTs.\\n- 8–12 weeks:\\n  - Recheck 25-OH Vitamin D.\\n- 3 months:\\n  - Recheck fasting lipid panel and ApoB after lifestyle changes or 6–12 weeks after statin start if initiated. Reassess need for CAC if still undecided on statin.\\n- Annual:\\n  - Annual lipid risk assessment and ASCVD risk recalculation; routine preventive care per age.\\n\\nPatient education points for staff to cover (scripted, to be delivered by clinician/staff)\\n- Explain menopausal status and that estradiol has fallen; if symptomatic, HRT can relieve vasomotor and sleep symptoms and protect bone health — discuss risks and benefits with provider.\\n- Ask about current medications/supplements explicitly (including topical creams and compounded products).\\n- Advise to report any new vaginal bleeding immediately.\\n- Explain rationale for vitamin D supplementation and timeline for rechecking.\\n- Discuss lifestyle changes and resources for diet/exercise/smoking cessation.\\n\\nNote on contraindications/caveats\\n- Do not start oral estrogen if risk factors for VTE, active or past estrogen-dependent malignancy, uncontrolled hypertension, or other contraindications — provider must review history.\\n- Transdermal estrogen preferred over oral when concerned about SHBG and thrombotic risk.\\n- All medication starts require provider sign-off and documentation of informed consent.\\n\\nIf you want, I can prepare:\\n- A checklist for the intake call to confirm med/supplement exposures today.\\n- Pre-populated order sets: bioavailable testosterone, pelvic ultrasound, CAC referral, D3 refill, and transdermal estradiol ± micronized progesterone prescription for provider signature.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 1.06, "detail": "Triglycerides 85 / HDL 80 = 1.06", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5.4%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 128, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 19, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "94 mg/dL", "detail": "Elevated atherogenic particle count", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 1, "likelihoodLabel": "LOW LIKELIHOOD", "providerSummary": "INSULIN RESISTANCE SCREENING: LOW LIKELIHOOD (1/6 markers positive). No significant insulin resistance pattern detected at this time.", "confirmationTests": ""}, "clinicalPhenotypes": [{"name": "Menopausal Transition", "confidence": "high", "description": "Clinical picture consistent with perimenopause or menopause. Hormonal shifts during this transition affect bone health, cardiovascular risk, mood, and quality of life.", "supportingFindings": ["Postmenopausal status", "FSH 67.7 mIU/mL (elevated, menopausal range)", "Estradiol 40 pg/mL (declining)"]}, {"name": "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity", "confidence": "moderate", "description": "Metabolic pattern consistent with insulin resistance and/or visceral adiposity. This drives inflammation, hormonal imbalance, and cardiovascular risk.", "supportingFindings": ["ApoB 94 mg/dL (elevated)", "Unexplained weight gain / central adiposity reported"]}]}	2026-04-02 16:13:50.127931	3
