cillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 12, "apoBValue": 113, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "borderline", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 3.6826270253998294, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": true}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "generally_recommended", "indicated": true, "rationale": "ApoB ≥90 mg/dL (113 mg/dL) with risk enhancers (elevated triglycerides, elevated ApoB). Statin discussion recommended."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": true, "severity": "borderline", "recommendation": "Triglycerides 150-199 mg/dL (150 mg/dL). Lifestyle modifications recommended (weight loss, exercise, reduce refined carbs/alcohol)."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "consider", "rationale": "Borderline lipid levels with multiple risk enhancers (elevated triglycerides, elevated ApoB). Consider CAC to refine risk assessment.", "recommended": true}}, "recheckWindow": "2-4 weeks (urgent values present)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall assessment\\nYour results show confirmed menopause (FSH 155 mIU/mL, estradiol 11 pg/mL) and a few important abnormalities to address: elevated LDL cholesterol (166 mg/dL), high total testosterone for a woman (139 ng/dL with free T 17 pg/mL and SHBG 60.3 nmol/L), and elevated hematocrit (48%). Your 10-year total cardiovascular risk is 5.3% (PREVENT), which places you in the BORDERLINE risk category.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Hemoglobin 15.5 g/dL (normal)\\n- HDL cholesterol 73 mg/dL (protective)\\n- TSH 1.05 mIU/L (euthyroid)\\n- Hemoglobin A1c 5.4% (normal blood sugar control)\\n- Vitamin D 25-OH 52.1 ng/mL (adequate, 41–59 ng/mL)\\n- eGFR 69 mL/min (kidney function acceptable)\\n\\nAreas to improve (with specific actions)\\n1. LDL cholesterol 166 mg/dL, triglycerides 150 mg/dL, ApoB 113 mg/dL:\\n   - Add 25–30 g fiber daily: oatmeal for breakfast, beans with lunch, vegetables at dinner.\\n   - Include 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n   - Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter and cheese.\\n   - Daily exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking or equivalent.\\n   - Because LDL is 166 mg/dL and your 10-year risk is 5.3% (BORDERLINE), follow the above and discuss statin therapy with your clinician; coronary artery calcium (CAC) scoring can help guide this decision.\\n\\n2. Testosterone total 139 ng/dL (supraphysiologic for women) with free T 17 pg/mL:\\n   - Prioritize 7–8 hours quality sleep nightly.\\n   - Include strength training 2–3 times weekly.\\n   - Manage stress with regular exercise, meditation, or hobbies.\\n   - If you are using testosterone pellets or replacement, schedule a medication review—these levels commonly occur with pellet therapy and may need dose adjustment or cessation.\\n\\n3. Hematocrit 48% (elevated):\\n   - Stay well hydrated and avoid tobacco; elevated hematocrit can reflect polycythemia.\\n   - Because your STOP-BANG score is 4/8 (INTERMEDIATE RISK for obstructive sleep apnea), arrange a sleep evaluation (home sleep test or sleep lab) within the next 4–8 weeks—sleep apnea can raise hematocrit.\\n   - If hematocrit stays high, further testing (oxygenation, EPO level, hematology referral) may be needed.\\n\\nOther notes\\n- Ferritin 229 ng/mL and B12 1664 pg/mL are high; avoid iron supplements and discuss iron overload testing (repeat ferritin, consider genetic testing) if persistent.\\n- Menopause confirmed (FSH 155 mIU/mL, estradiol 11 pg/mL) — consider bone health screening (DEXA) within 6–12 months because low estrogen increases bone loss.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n- Urgent physician review recommended for the critical findings (hematocrit, testosterone, LDL). Please book a clinic visit within 1–2 weeks.\\n- Arrange sleep study within 4–8 weeks for STOP-BANG 4/8.\\n- Start lifestyle changes now; recheck lipids and hematocrit in ~3 months after starting diet/exercise and any therapy changes. Consider bone density testing and discussion of hormone or other treatments for menopause symptoms as needed.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 15.5, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 48, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Elevated hematocrit - evaluate for polycythemia.", "recommendation": "Consider hematology referral. Evaluate for underlying causes.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 97.4, "status": "borderline", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 97.4 fL is in the high-normal range. Early macrocytosis can precede overt B12 or folate deficiency.", "recommendation": "Ensure B12 and folate levels are within optimal range. Monitor CBC at next visit.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 247, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/