: "AdvaClear®", "caution": "Broad-spectrum phase I/II liver detoxification support. Contains NAC, green tea extract, milk thistle, and B-vitamins for biotransformation. Best taken with food.", "category": "detox", "priority": "high", "rationale": "AdvaClear provides broad-spectrum phase I and phase II liver detoxification support with NAC, green tea extract, watercress, and B-vitamins. Designed for metabolic clearance support, biotransformation of hormones and environmental compounds, and hepatic stress. Appropriate when liver markers are borderline, estrogen clearance is sluggish, or metabolic dysfunction is present.", "indication": "Inflammatory burden (metabolic clearance support); Insulin resistance / metabolic dysfunction (hepatic metabolic support); Borderline liver markers (ALT 6, AST 23) suggesting hepatic biotransformation load; Weight gain (metabolic clearance factor)", "phenotypes": ["Inflammatory Burden", "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "This supplement supports your liver's natural detoxification processes. Your results suggest your body could benefit from help processing hormones, metabolic byproducts, and other compounds. It provides nutrients that support both phases of your liver's detox pathways.", "supportingFindings": ["Inflammatory burden (metabolic clearance support)", "Insulin resistance / metabolic dysfunction (hepatic metabolic support)", "Borderline liver markers (ALT 6, AST 23) suggesting hepatic biotransformation load", "Weight gain (metabolic clearance factor)", "Low energy (biotransformation burden)"]}, {"dose": "1 soft chew during times of stress", "name": "HerWellness™ Rapid Stress Relief", "caution": "Fast-acting L-Theanine and Lactium formula. Non-drowsy. Promotes calm within 1 hour. Contains milk.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "HerWellness Rapid Stress Relief provides fast-acting stress support with L-Theanine (200mg) and Lactium. Promotes calm within 1 hour without drowsiness.", "indication": "Anxiety reported; Sleep disruption (stress-related); Mood changes reported", "phenotypes": ["Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This fast-acting supplement helps your body respond to stress more calmly. It promotes relaxation within about an hour and can be taken as needed without causing drowsiness.", "supportingFindings": ["Anxiety reported", "Sleep disruption (stress-related)", "Mood changes reported"]}, {"dose": "1 chewable gel daily", "name": "NutraGems® CoQ10 300", "caution": "Chewable 300mg CoQ10 in emulsified form for enhanced absorption. Supports heart muscle function, energy production, and antioxidant protection. Essential for patients on statins.", "category": "cardiovascular", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "NutraGems CoQ10 300 provides high-potency ubiquinone for cardiovascular protection, cellular energy production, and antioxidant support. Essential for patients on statin therapy, which depletes CoQ10.", "indication": "ApoB 113 mg/dL (cardiovascular support); LDL 166 mg/dL; TC 269 mg/dL; Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)", "phenotypes": ["Inflammatory Burden"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "CoQ10 is a vital nutrient your body uses for energy production and heart health. This chewable supplement supports your cardiovascular system and helps maintain cellular energy, especially important as natural production declines with age.", "supportingFindings": ["ApoB 113 mg/dL (cardiovascular support)", "LDL 166 mg/dL", "TC 269 mg/dL", "Low energy (mitochondrial support benefit)", "Age 64 (declining CoQ10 production)"]}, {"dose": "3 capsules daily (divided doses)", "name": "Magtein® Magnesium L-Threonate", "caution": "L-Threonate form crosses blood-brain barrier for cognitive and sleep support. Well-tolerated; gentle on GI system.", "category": "mineral", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Magtein is the only magnesium form clinically shown to cross the blood-brain barrier. Supports quality sleep, cognitive function, GABA activity, and stress resilience.", "indication": "Sleep disruption reported; Low energy reported; Anxiety reported (magnesium supports GABA)", "phenotypes": ["Stress / Cortisol Dysregulation"], "confidenceLevel": "high", "patientExplanation": "This special form of magnesium is designed to reach the brain, where it supports better sleep quality, mental clarity, and a calmer nervous system.", "supportingFindings": ["Sleep disruption reported", "Low energy reported", "Anxiety reported (magnesium supports GABA)"]}, {"dose": "1 tablet daily", "name": "HerWellness™ Estrovera®", "caution": "Clinically studied ERr 731 rhubarb extract for vasomotor symptom relief. Non-hormonal option. Effects typically noticed within 4 weeks.", "category": "hormone-support", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "Estrovera provides clinically studied ERr 731 Siberian rhubarb extract specifically for vasomotor symptom relief (hot flashes, night sweats). Best suited for women with active vasomotor symptoms who need non-hormonal support.", "indication": "Postmenopausal status; Sleep disruption (vasomotor-related); Mood changes reported", "phenotypes": ["Menopausal Transition"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This plant-based supplement is clinically proven to help with hot flashes, night sweats, and related sleep and mood changes. It works without hormones and most women notice improvement within 4 weeks.", "supportingFindings": ["Postmenopausal status", "Sleep disruption (vasomotor-related)", "Mood changes reported"]}, {"dose": "2 capsules daily", "name": "GlutaClear®", "caution": "Glutathione and antioxidant support formula. Contains NAC, alpha-lipoic acid, and green tea catechins. Supports cellular defense against oxidative damage.", "category": "detox", "priority": "medium", "rationale": "GlutaClear supports glutathione production and antioxidant defense with NAC, alpha-lipoic acid, and green tea catechins. Indicated when there is evidence of oxidative stress, inflammatory burden, or metabolic dysfunction requiring enhanced cellular defense. While AdvaClear provides broader liver detoxification support, GlutaClear specifically targets glutathione pathways and antioxidant capacity.", "indication": "Inflammatory burden (antioxidant defense support); Insulin resistance (oxidative metabolic stress); Low energy (mitochondrial antioxidant support); Age 64 (declining glutathione production)", "phenotypes": ["Inflammatory Burden", "Insulin Resistance / Visceral Adiposity"], "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "This supplement boosts your body's master antioxidant, glutathione, which protects your cells from damage. Your results suggest increased oxidative stress, and this formula helps your body defend and repair at the cellular level.", "supportingFindings": ["Inflammatory burden (antioxidant defense support)", "Insulin resistance (oxidative metabolic stress)", "Low energy (mitochondrial antioxidant support)", "Age 64 (declining glutathione production)"]}, {"dose": "1 tablet daily with meal", "name": "D3 2000 Complex", "caution": "Maintenance dose for suboptimal levels. Comprehensive vitamin D support with cofactors.", "category": "vitamin", "priority": "low", "rationale": "D3 2000 Complex provides maintenance vitamin D with cofactors to reach and maintain optimal range ≥60 ng/mL.", "indication": "Vitamin D 52.1 ng/mL (suboptimal, target ≥60)", "confidenceLevel": "moderate", "patientExplanation": "Your vitamin D is close to optimal but could benefit from a maintenance dose to reach the ideal range for bone and immune health.", "supportingFindings": ["Vitamin D 52.1 ng/mL (suboptimal, target ≥60)"]}, {"dose": "1 capsule daily", "name": "UltraFlora® Complete Women's Probiotic", "caution": "5-in-1 multi-benefit probiotic with Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 for vaginal and urinary health. Increase to 2 daily for urogenital irritation.", "category": "probiotic", "priority": "low", "rationale": "UltraFlora Complete Women's provides 5-in-1 support for vaginal, urinary, digestive, and immune health with clinically studied Lactobacillus GR-1 and RC-14 strains.", "indication": "Foundational women's probiotic support", "confidenceLevel": "supportive", "patientExplanation": "This women-specific probiotic supports digestive health, vaginal and urinary tract balance, and immune function with clinically proven strains.", "supportingFindings": ["Foundational women's probiotic support"]}], "adjustedRisk": {"lpaValue": 12, "apoBValue": 113, "lpaStatus": "normal", "apoBStatus": "borderline", "riskCategory": "low", "baseASCVDRisk": 3.6826270253998294, "hasElevatedLpa": false, "hasElevatedApoB": false, "adjustedCategory": "low", "clinicalGuidance": "Low risk (<5%) without risk enhancers. Focus on lifestyle modifications; reassess in 4-6 years.", "hasBorderlineLpa": false, "hasBorderlineApoB": true}, "cacStatinRec": {"statinDiscussion": {"strength": "generally_recommended", "indicated": true, "rationale": "ApoB ≥90 mg/dL (113 mg/dL) with risk enhancers (elevated triglycerides, elevated ApoB). Statin discussion recommended."}, "triglycerideMgmt": {"elevated": true, "severity": "borderline", "recommendation": "Triglycerides 150-199 mg/dL (150 mg/dL). Lifestyle modifications recommended (weight loss, exercise, reduce refined carbs/alcohol)."}, "cacRecommendation": {"priority": "consider", "rationale": "Borderline lipid levels with multiple risk enhancers (elevated triglycerides, elevated ApoB). Consider CAC to refine risk assessment.", "recommended": true}}, "recheckWindow": "2-4 weeks (urgent values present)", "patientSummary": "Here is a copy of your recent lab results, along with the recommendations.\\n\\nOverall summary (plain language)\\nYour results show that you are in menopause: FSH 155 mIU/mL and estradiol 11 pg/mL confirm low ovarian estrogen. Important findings to address now are: LDL cholesterol 166 mg/dL, hematocrit 48% (elevated), high risk for sleep apnea (STOP-BANG 5/8), and hormone levels that may need adjustment (progesterone 0.19 ng/mL low for HRT; total testosterone 139 ng/dL, above the HRT optimization goal of 75–125 ng/dL; SHBG 60.3 nmol/L; free T 17 pg/mL elevated). Your hemoglobin 15.5 g/dL, TSH 1.05 mIU/L, HDL 73 mg/dL, A1c 5.4%, and eGFR 69 mL/min are within expected ranges.\\n\\nPositive findings\\n- Hemoglobin 15.5 g/dL (normal)\\n- HDL 73 mg/dL (protective)\\n- TSH 1.05 mIU/L (thyroid normal)\\n- A1c 5.4% (normal blood sugar)\\n- Vitamin D 25-OH 52.1 ng/mL (adequate)\\n\\nAreas to improve (with exact values and concrete actions)\\n1) Cholesterol and heart risk: LDL 166 mg/dL, triglycerides 150 mg/dL, ApoB 113 mg/dL, 10-year CVD risk 5.3% (borderline).\\n- Diet: add 25–30 g fiber/day (oatmeal for breakfast, beans at lunch, vegetables at dinner).\\n- Eat 2–3 servings fatty fish weekly (salmon, mackerel, sardines).\\n- Reduce saturated fat: choose lean meats, limit butter/cheese.\\n- Exercise: 30 minutes brisk walking daily; aim for 150 minutes moderate activity per week and follow a Mediterranean-style diet.\\n- Recheck fasting lipids in 3 months; discuss statin options with your clinician given LDL 166 mg/dL.\\n\\n2) Bone health & menopause: Estradiol 11 pg/mL (below bone protection threshold <40 pg/mL).\\n- Lifestyle: weight-bearing exercise 3x/week, 1,000–1,200 mg calcium daily from diet, maintain Vitamin D 1,000–2,000 IU daily.\\n- Testing: schedule a DEXA bone density scan within 3–6 months and discuss menopausal hormone therapy with your provider for symptom and bone protection options.\\n\\n3) Hematocrit and sleep: Hematocrit 48% (elevated); STOP-BANG 5 (high risk for obstructive sleep apnea).\\n- Stop/avoid tobacco and limit alcohol.\\n- Arrange a sleep medicine referral for a diagnostic sleep study (within 1–3 months). Treating OSA may lower hematocrit.\\n- Repeat CBC (hematocrit) in 4–6 weeks or sooner after any treatment change.\\n\\n4) Hormone therapy adjustments: Progesterone 0.19 ng/mL (below HRT therapeutic); total testosterone 139 ng/dL (above HRT optimization goal).\\n- Discuss HRT regimen with your clinician: consider lowering testosterone dose or changing delivery method and adjusting progesterone. Lifestyle: prioritize 7–8 hours sleep, strength training 2–3x weekly, stress reduction (meditation, walks).\\n\\nOther notes\\n- Ferritin 229 ng/mL is elevated — stop any iron supplements until reviewed; your serum iron 92 and TIBC 296 are normal. Discuss testing for causes (inflammation, liver disease, or hemochromatosis).\\n- Vitamin B12 1664 pg/mL is high — review B12 supplements/injections.\\n\\nNext steps and timeline\\n1) Book an appointment with your clinician/OBGYN within 4 weeks to review HRT and lipids.\\n2) Arrange sleep study referral in 1–3 months for STOP-BANG 5.\\n3) Start the diet/exercise steps today; recheck fasting lipids in 3 months and repeat CBC in 4–6 weeks.\\n4) Schedule DEXA in 3–6 months and discuss bone protection options.\\n\\nIf you are currently taking iron, B12, or hormone pills/injections, bring them to your visit so we can review dosing. If any new or severe symptoms (chest pain, sudden breathlessness, fainting, heavy bleeding), seek care immediately.", "interpretations": [{"unit": "g/dL", "value": 15.5, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin", "interpretation": "Hemoglobin within normal range for adult females.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "12-16 g/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 48, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hematocrit", "interpretation": "Elevated hematocrit - evaluate for polycythemia.", "recommendation": "Consider hematology referral. Evaluate for underlying causes.", "referenceRange": "36-44%"}, {"unit": "fL", "value": 97.4, "status": "borderline", "category": "MCV", "interpretation": "MCV 97.4 fL is in the high-normal range. Early macrocytosis can precede overt B12 or folate deficiency.", "recommendation": "Ensure B12 and folate levels are within optimal range. Monitor CBC at next visit.", "referenceRange": "80-100 fL"}, {"unit": "K/uL", "value": 247, "status": "normal", "category": "Platelets", "interpretation": "Platelet count within normal range.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "150-400 K/uL"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 11, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Estradiol", "interpretation": "Estradiol below bone protection threshold (<40 pg/mL). Bone resorption increases below 40-60 pg/mL.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase estrogen dose. Target 60-100 pg/mL. Current level inadequate for bone protection.", "referenceRange": "60-100 pg/mL (HRT goal, >40 for bone)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 0.19, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Progesterone", "interpretation": "Progesterone below HRT therapeutic range.", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Increase progesterone dose. Target 8-10 ng/mL for optimal endometrial protection.", "referenceRange": "8-10 ng/mL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 155, "status": "normal", "category": "FSH", "interpretation": "Elevated FSH consistent with menopause.", "recommendation": "Expected finding for postmenopausal women.", "referenceRange": ">25 mIU/mL (postmenopausal)"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 139, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Testosterone (Total)", "interpretation": "Testosterone above HRT optimization goal (75-125 ng/dL).", "recommendation": "Provider recommendation: Consider reducing testosterone dose. Monitor for androgenic side effects.", "referenceRange": "75-125 ng/dL (HRT goal)"}, {"unit": "mIU/L", "value": 1.05, "status": "normal", "category": "TSH", "interpretation": "TSH within normal range - euthyroid.", "recommendation": "No thyroid intervention needed.", "referenceRange": "0.4-4.5 mIU/L"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 229, "status": "borderline", "category": "Ferritin", "interpretation": "Elevated ferritin.", "recommendation": "May indicate inflammation, hemochromatosis, or liver disease. Correlate clinically.", "referenceRange": ">50 ng/mL (optimal)"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 52.1, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin D (25-OH)", "interpretation": "Vitamin D adequate but suboptimal (41-59 ng/mL).", "recommendation": "Metagenics D3 2000 Complex daily to reach optimal range ≥60 ng/mL.", "referenceRange": "≥60 ng/mL optimal"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 1664, "status": "borderline", "category": "Vitamin B12", "interpretation": "Elevated B12.", "recommendation": "Usually from supplementation. Rarely indicates liver disease.", "referenceRange": "200-900 pg/mL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.4, "status": "normal", "category": "Hemoglobin A1c", "interpretation": "Normal glycemic control.", "recommendation": "Continue healthy lifestyle.", "referenceRange": "<5.7%"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 166, "status": "abnormal", "category": "LDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "High LDL.", "recommendation": "Consider statin based on ASCVD risk. Lifestyle modifications.", "referenceRange": "<100 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 73, "status": "normal", "category": "HDL Cholesterol", "interpretation": "Good HDL level - protective.", "recommendation": "Maintain with regular exercise.", "referenceRange": "≥50 mg/dL (optimal ≥60)"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 150, "status": "borderline", "category": "Triglycerides", "interpretation": "Borderline high triglycerides.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Reduce sugar and alcohol.", "referenceRange": "<150 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 5.341445006655916, "status": "borderline", "category": "10-Year Total CVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year cardiovascular disease risk: 5.3% (BORDERLINE RISK). Includes ASCVD and heart failure.", "recommendation": "Heart-healthy lifestyle: Mediterranean diet, regular exercise (150 min/week), maintain healthy weight, smoking cessation\\n\\nBORDERLINE RISK (5-7.5%): Consider coronary artery calcium (CAC) scoring\\n\\nStatin therapy based on shared decision-making if risk-enhancing factors present", "referenceRange": "Low <5%, Borderline 5-7.4%, Intermediate 7.5-19.9%, High ≥20%"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 3.6826270253998294, "status": "borderline", "category": "10-Year ASCVD Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year ASCVD risk (heart attack, stroke): 3.7%", "recommendation": "Consider statin if risk-enhancing factors present. CAC scoring may help guide decision.", "referenceRange": "Heart attack & stroke risk"}, {"unit": "%", "value": 3.125309574314699, "status": "normal", "category": "10-Year Heart Failure Risk (PREVENT)", "recheckTiming": "Annual", "interpretation": "10-year heart failure risk: 3.1%", "recommendation": "Monitor for symptoms: shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling.", "referenceRange": "Heart failure risk"}, {"unit": "points", "value": 5, "status": "critical", "category": "Sleep Apnea Risk (STOP-BANG)", "recheckTiming": "Follow-up in 1-3 months after sleep study", "interpretation": "HIGH RISK for obstructive sleep apnea (5/8 points)", "recommendation": "High probability of moderate-severe OSA. STRONGLY RECOMMEND sleep study (polysomnography or HSAT). Evaluate for CPAP therapy. Address cardiovascular risk factors. Consider holding or reducing TRT dose if elevated hematocrit.", "referenceRange": "Low 0-2, Intermediate 3-4, High 5-8"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 23, "status": "normal", "category": "AST", "interpretation": "AST within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "U/L", "value": 6, "status": "normal", "category": "ALT", "interpretation": "ALT within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<32 U/L"}, {"unit": "mL/min", "value": 69, "status": "normal", "category": "eGFR", "interpretation": "Normal kidney function.", "recommendation": "Routine care.", "referenceRange": "≥60 mL/min"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 0.92, "status": "normal", "category": "Creatinine", "interpretation": "Creatinine within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "≤1.0 mg/dL"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 113, "status": "borderline", "category": "Apolipoprotein B", "interpretation": "Borderline elevated Apo B.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Consider pharmacotherapy if high ASCVD risk.", "referenceRange": "<90 mg/dL optimal"}, {"unit": "mg/dL", "value": 12, "status": "normal", "category": "Lipoprotein(a)", "interpretation": "Lp(a) within optimal range.", "recommendation": "Continue current lifestyle. Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<29 mg/dL normal, ≥29 elevated, ≥50 risk enhancer"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 296, "status": "normal", "category": "TIBC (Iron Binding Capacity)", "interpretation": "TIBC within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "250-450 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ug/dL", "value": 92, "status": "normal", "category": "Serum Iron", "interpretation": "Serum iron within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Routine monitoring.", "referenceRange": "40-170 ug/dL"}, {"unit": "ng/mL", "value": 20, "status": "normal", "category": "Folate", "interpretation": "Folate within normal limits.", "recommendation": "Continue folic acid if reproductive age.", "referenceRange": ">5 ng/mL"}, {"unit": "mg/L", "value": 1.1, "status": "borderline", "category": "hs-CRP", "interpretation": "Borderline hs-CRP - moderate cardiovascular risk.", "recommendation": "Lifestyle modifications. Continue monitoring.", "referenceRange": "<1.0 mg/L low risk, 1.0-3.0 mg/L moderate, >3.0 mg/L high"}, {"unit": "pg/mL", "value": 11, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Perimenopause Assessment: Hypoestrogenic Pattern", "interpretation": "Estradiol of 11 pg/mL with FSH of 155 mIU/mL indicates hypoestrogenic perimenopause — the \\"crash phase.\\" Estrogen production has dropped significantly and the brain is signaling strongly (elevated FSH) trying to stimulate the ovaries.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Transdermal estradiol 0.025–0.05 mg is appropriate replacement therapy. Add micronized progesterone for uterine protection and sleep support. Frame this as hormone replacement, not stimulation — we are restoring what the body needs. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your estrogen has dropped to a level that can cause symptoms like hot flashes, night sweats, brain fog, and mood changes. Replacing estrogen with a patch or gel can relieve these symptoms and protect your long-term health. Progesterone is added to protect your uterus and help with sleep.", "referenceRange": "E2 <50 with FSH >10 indicates estrogen deficiency"}, {"unit": "mIU/mL", "value": 155, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Hormone Pattern: Menopause", "interpretation": "FSH of 155 mIU/mL with low estradiol (11 pg/mL) confirms menopausal status. Symptoms present: sleep disruption. Elevated FSH reflects decreased ovarian function and reduced estrogen production. This is a normal part of reproductive aging but may benefit from hormone optimization if symptomatic.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: If symptomatic, consider hormone therapy (HT): transdermal estradiol + micronized progesterone (if uterus intact). Discuss benefits/risks of HT including cardiovascular, bone, and quality of life considerations. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your lab results confirm you are in menopause. Hormone therapy can help manage symptoms like hot flashes, sleep problems, and vaginal dryness while also protecting bone health.", "referenceRange": "FSH <25 mIU/mL premenopausal; ≥25 suggests transition; ≥40 menopausal"}, {"unit": "ng/dL", "value": 139, "status": "abnormal", "category": "Testosterone Pattern C: Above HRT Optimization Goal", "interpretation": "Testosterone above HRT optimization goal (Total T: 139 ng/dL (above HRT optimization goal of 75–125 ng/dL); SHBG: 60.3 nmol/L; Free T: 17 pg/mL (elevated)). The target range for testosterone on HRT is 75–125 ng/dL. At 139 ng/dL, the patient is above goal — consider dose reduction or reassessment of delivery method.", "recommendation": "PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION: Testosterone is above the HRT optimization goal of 75–125 ng/dL. Reduce the testosterone dose — do NOT add additional testosterone. Manage downstream symptoms if present: spironolactone for acne or hair thinning, sleep support for insomnia, anxiety management as needed. If using pellet therapy, consider transitioning to a titratable form (transdermal cream or gel) to allow precise dose adjustments. Monitor CBC, lipids, and liver function. PATIENT EDUCATION: Your testosterone is above the target range for your hormone therapy. Your provider will adjust your dose to bring it back into the optimal range (75–125 ng/dL) and manage any related symptoms.", "referenceRange": "HRT optimization goal: 75–125 ng/dL"}], "aiRecommendations": "Overall clinical assessment\\n- Labs consistent with biochemical menopause: FSH 155 mIU/mL with estradiol 11 pg/mL — symptomatic hypoestrogenic state requiring consideration of hormone replacement for symptom control and bone protection.\\n- Red-flag hematologic and sleep findings: Hematocrit 48% (threshold for physician notification) and STOP‑BANG 5 (high OSA risk). Concurrent supratherapeutic testosterone (Total T 139 ng/dL) likely contributes to erythrocytosis risk.\\n- Cardiometabolic profile: LDL 166 mg/dL, triglycerides 150 mg/dL, ApoB 113 mg/dL, hs‑CRP 1.1 mg/L → borderline-moderate CVD risk (10‑yr total CVD 5.3%).\\n- Other notable: Ferritin 229 ng/mL (elevated — correlate clinically), B12 high (likely supplementation), vitamin D adequate but below clinic target (52.1 ng/mL), MCV high‑normal (97.4 fL).\\n\\nPriority actions (CRITICAL → URGENT → ROUTINE)\\nCRITICAL (Physician notification now)\\n- Notify provider immediately about hematocrit 48% and STOP‑BANG score 5. These require expedited evaluation because of elevated erythrocytosis risk and high probability of OSA.\\n\\nURGENT (action within days to 2 weeks)\\n- Sleep evaluation: Arrange expedited referral to sleep medicine for HSAT or in‑lab polysomnography. Document STOP‑BANG findings on referral.\\n  * If OSA confirmed, initiate CPAP therapy and coordinate follow-up for adherence and effect on hematocrit.\\n  * Recheck Hct 1 month after CPAP initiation and after any testosterone dose change.\\n- Testosterone management: Hold or reduce testosterone pending evaluation (see Testosterone dose recommendations below). Provider to authorize immediate change.\\n- Hematology/hematology testing:\\n  * Order repeat CBC with Hct/Hgb within 1–2 weeks to confirm elevation and exclude preanalytic causes (ensure adequate hydration prior to draw).\\n  * If Hct remains ≥48% after reassessment and reversible causes addressed (dehydration, OSA, exogenous testosterone), refer to hematology for evaluation (consider EPO, peripheral smear, reticulocyte count, JAK2 V617F testing and possible bone marrow workup).\\n  * If symptomatic or Hct >50% consider urgent hematology/phlebotomy per physician discretion.\\n\\nROUTINE (within 4–12 weeks unless otherwise noted)\\n- Estradiol/progesterone optimization: Provider to initiate or increase HT (see Hormone therapy below). Recheck levels and symptom response per schedule.\\n- Lipids/CVD prevention: Discuss shared decision-making regarding statin therapy given LDL 166 mg/dL, ApoB 113 mg/dL, borderline 10‑yr CVD risk. Consider CAC scoring if shared decision unclear.\\n- Iron/ferritin workup: Order transferrin saturation (TSAT = serum iron/TIBC) now; repeat ferritin in 3 months and evaluate for inflammatory or hepatic causes if persistently elevated. Consider HFE testing if TSAT >45% or ferritin persistently high.\\n- Vitamin D: Increase supplementation; recheck 8–12 weeks.\\n\\nTest- and dose-specific recommendations\\n1) Testosterone dose recommendations (IMMEDIATE provider action)\\n- Current: Total T 139 ng/dL (above HRT target 75–125 ng/dL) with Free T 17 pg/mL and SHBG 60.3 nmol/L.\\n- If on pellets:\\n  * Do not place additional pellets; consider discontinuing next pellet cycle and transitioning to a titratable delivery (transdermal cream/gel) to allow dose reduction.\\n- If transdermal (cream/gel):\\n  * Reduce dose by approximately 20–30% (e.g., reduce applied amount to 70–80% of current daily dose) OR change frequency to every other day for short-term titration — provider to specify exact mg.\\n- If injectable:\\n  * Reduce dose by 25%–50% or extend injection interval by 25%–50% (provider to choose based on regimen).\\n- Monitoring after change:\\n  * Recheck Total testosterone, Free T, SHBG, and CBC (Hct/Hgb) 4–8 weeks after dose adjustment and again at 3 months.\\n  * If testosterone remains above 125 ng/dL, further downward adjustments required.\\n- Interim safety step:\\n  * Consider holding all testosterone until immediate repeat CBC and sleep evaluation are completed if hematocrit rising or OSA suspected/confirmed.\\n\\n2) Estradiol and progesterone (PROVIDER RECOMMENDATION)\\n- Estradiol:\\n  * Start/adjust transdermal estradiol patch 0.025–0.05 mg (25–50 mcg) daily per protocol to restore estradiol to target 60–100 pg/mL for bone protection.\\n  * Recheck serum estradiol 4–8 weeks after initiation/dose change and adjust to achieve target.\\n- Progesterone (if uterus intact):\\n  * Initiate micronized progesterone for endometrial protection and sleep support. Typical starting regimens: 100–200 mg oral nightly (continuous) or 200 mg nightly for 12 days/month if cyclic — provider to select regimen.\\n  * Aim for serum progesterone in therapeutic range per clinic protocol (target 8–10 ng/mL if measuring). Recheck level 4–8 weeks after dose change.\\n- Patient education points (staff to cover):\\n  * Explain menopausal labs and rationale for transdermal estradiol + micronized progesterone as replacement and uterine protection.\\n  * Discuss risks/benefits of HT, timeframe to symptom relief (weeks), and monitoring plan.\\n\\nFollow-up lab schedule and monitoring (specific timing)\\n- CBC with differential, Hct/Hgb:\\n  * Repeat within 1–2 weeks to confirm Hct 48% and after correcting preanalytic variables.\\n  * Recheck at 4–8 weeks after any testosterone dose change or CPAP initiation, and again at 3 months.\\n- Testosterone (Total and Free) and SHBG:\\n  * Recheck 4–8 weeks after dose adjustment; thereafter every 3 months until stable, then every 6–12 months.\\n- Estradiol and progesterone:\\n  * Recheck 4–8 weeks after initiation/dose change; thereafter per symptom control and stability (every 3–6 months).\\n- Lipid panel:\\n  * If initiating lifestyle changes only: recheck in 6–12 weeks.\\n  * If starting statin: baseline LFTs before initiation; lipid panel 6–12 weeks after start, then every 3–12 months.\\n- Vitamin D 25‑OH:\\n  * Recheck 8–12 weeks after increased supplementation.\\n- Ferritin, iron studies (TSAT):\\n  * Obtain TSAT now; repeat ferritin and iron panel in 3 months if abnormal or if inflammatory markers stable.\\n- hs‑CRP:\\n  * Consider repeating 3–6 months to track inflammatory/CVD risk trends.\\n\\nMedication and referral recommendations\\n- Immediate referrals:\\n  * Sleep medicine/pulmonology for diagnostic sleep study (HSAT or polysomnography).\\n  * Hematology if Hct persists ≥48% after reversible causes addressed or if additional concerning indices (high RBC mass, abnormal EPO, positive JAK2).\\n  * Cardiology if shared decision supports statin initiation and high concern, or for CAC scoring referral if indicated.\\n- Consider transition of testosterone delivery to transdermal form to allow titration if currently on non‑titratable method (pellets); discuss with provider.\\n- Statin discussion:\\n  * Offer shared decision-making for moderate-intensity statin (e.g., atorvastatin 10–20 mg or rosuvastatin 5–10 mg) given LDL 166 mg/dL and other risk markers — provider to decide.\\n\\nLifestyle interventions for staff to counsel (standardize patient messages)\\n- Cardiometabolic:\\n  * Mediterranean dietary pattern; reduce saturated fat and simple sugars; limit alcohol (especially relevant to triglycerides).\\n  * Increase aerobic activity to ≥150 minutes/week and resistance exercise twice weekly.\\n  * Weight management to BMI goal individualized.\\n  * Smoking cessation resources if applicable.\\n- Sleep/OSA:\\n  * Promote sleep hygiene; advise on positional measures; emphasize urgency of sleep testing and adherence to CPAP if prescribed.\\n- Bone health and vitamin D:\\n  * Increase vitamin D dosing per protocol (e.g., Metagenics D3 2000 Complex daily as suggested) to reach ≥60 ng/mL; counsel on calcium intake and weight‑bearing exercise.\\n- Iron/ferritin:\\n  * No empirical iron supplementation given elevated ferritin; avoid unnecessary iron until evaluation complete.\\n\\nAdditional testing to consider\\n- Immediate: Repeat CBC, TSAT (serum iron/TIBC), fasting lipid panel (if previous non‑fasting), repeat estradiol/progesterone/testosterone after dose adjustments.\\n- If ferritin/TSAT abnormal: HFE genetic testing, liver function panel (already normal), CRP/ESR if inflammation suspected.\\n- If persistent erythrocytosis and negative JAK2: consider RBC mass testing per hematology.\\n- Consider CAC scoring for coronary risk clarification (borderline 5.3% 10‑yr risk).\\n\\nSummary of next steps and timeline (concise action list for staff)\\n1. Immediate (today):\\n   - Notify physician about Hct 48% and STOP‑BANG 5.\\n   - Arrange urgent sleep medicine referral for HSAT/polysomnography.\\n   - Order repeat CBC with Hct/Hgb within 1–2 weeks; ensure patient well hydrated before draw.\\n   - Order transferrin saturation (TSAT) now.\\n2. Within 48–72 hours (after physician directs):\\n   - Implement testosterone dose change per provider (hold or reduce 20–30% or modify regimen). Document exact new dose and plan for recheck.\\n3. 1–4 weeks:\\n   - Recheck CBC (Hct/Hgb). If Hct persists ≥48%, arrange hematology referral.\\n4. 4–8 weeks:\\n   - Recheck hormone levels (Total T, Free T, SHBG, estradiol, progesterone) after dose changes.\\n   - If initiating/adjusting estradiol and progesterone, re-evaluate symptom control and safety labs.\\n   - Recheck vitamin D at 8–12 weeks after supplementation increase.\\n5. 6–12 weeks:\\n   - Reassess lipids after lifestyle intervention or statin initiation.\\n6. Ongoing:\\n   - Continue monitoring per clinic protocol and escalate to hematology/cardiology/sleep medicine as indicated.\\n\\nKey physician notification triggers for staff\\n- Hematocrit ≥48% (now) — notify immediately (already flagged).\\n- STOP‑BANG ≥5 (now) — notify and refer urgently for sleep study.\\n- Any recommendation to hold or substantially reduce testosterone — communicate to provider to authorize.\\n- Hct that rises above 50% or symptomatic erythrocytosis, or abnormal JAK2/EPO results — urgent hematology contact.\\n\\nUse this plan as a draft for provider sign-off. Staff should implement orders and referrals only after the supervising provider authorizes medication changes and specialty referrals.", "insulinResistance": {"markers": [{"name": "TG:HDL Ratio", "value": 2.05, "detail": "Triglycerides 150 / HDL 73 = 2.05", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 3.0"}, {"name": "A1c", "value": "5.4%", "detail": "Normal glycemic control", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 5.7%"}, {"name": "SHBG", "value": 60.3, "detail": "Within normal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "< 50 nmol/L"}, {"name": "ALT", "value": 6, "detail": "Within optimal range", "positive": false, "threshold": "> 25 U/L (optimal)"}, {"name": "ApoB", "value": "113 mg/dL", "detail": "Elevated atherogenic particle count", "positive": true, "threshold": ">= 90 mg/dL"}, {"name": "hs-CRP", "value": "1.1 mg/L", "detail": "Low inflammatory burden", "positive": false, "threshold": ">= 2.0 mg/L"}], "likelihood": "none", "phenotypes": [], "positiveCount": 1, "like